window.RV_LOADED['ib-mathematics-aa-hl'] = {"name":"IB Mathematics AA HL","topics":[{"name":"Calculus I","subtopics":[{"name":"Differentiation Fundamentals","questions":[{"ref":"AA074","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA074\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x^2 + a)^5$.\n\nIn the expansion of the derivative, $f'(x)$, the coefficient of the term\nin $x^5$ is $960$. Find the possible values of $a$.","markScheme":"Using the chain rule, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= 5(x^2 + a)^4(2x) \\\\[6pt]\n&= 10x(x^2 + a)^4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the binomial theorem, we\nhave\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} 960x^5 &= 10x\\binom{4}{2}\\big[\\big(x^2\\big)^{4-2}\\big]\\big[a^2\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, if we solve the equation $(1)$ for $a$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n960x^5 &= 10x\\big[6\\big]\\big[x^4\\big]\\big[a^2\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n960 &= 60\\hspace{0.05em}a^2 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{7.4em} a = \\pm 4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1040","marks":"20","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1040\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined as $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-(a+3)x+3a}{x-2}$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}, x\\neq 2$.\nThe parameter $a$ can be any real number.\n\n1.  For this part of the question $a=4$.\n\n    1.  Find the equations of the vertical and oblique asymptotes of $f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Justify that $f$ has two stationary points. Note: you do not need to find the coordinates of these points.\n\n    3.  Hence, sketch $f(x)$ and identify the asymptotes and axis intercepts.[9 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $f(x)$ will always have at least one root for any value of $a$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the range of values of $a$ for which $f$ has no stationary points. [4 marks]\n\n4.  Show that the root of the equation of the oblique asymptote is always exactly 1 unit away from one of the roots of $f(x)$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  The vertical asymptote occurs when the denominator is equal to zero\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x-2&=0\\\\\n            x&=2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore the equation of the vertical asymptote is $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{x=2}$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        To find the oblique asymptote we use algebraic long division\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/a1040-1.png\" alt=\"a1040-1\">\n\n        Using this result we can rewrite $f(x)$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f(x)&=x-5+\\frac{2}{x-2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can see that as $x$ tends to positive or negative infinity $f(x)$ tends to $x-5$.\n\n        Therefore the equation of the oblique asypmpote is $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{y=x-5}$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    2.  To find stationary points we need to solve the equation $f'(x)=0$. Using the quotient rule we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f'(x)&=\\frac{(2x-7)(x-2)-(1)(x^2-7x+12)}{(x-2)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            &=\\frac{2x^2-7x-4x+14-x^2+7x-12)}{(x-2)^2)}\\\\\n            &=\\frac{x^2-4x+2}{(x-2)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        By solving $f'(x)=0$ we form a quadratic equation\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x^2-4x+2&=0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The discriminant of the equation is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            (-4)^2-4(1)(2)&=8\\\\\n            &>0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The discriminant is greater than zero,\n\n        therefore$f(x) \\text{ has two stationary points}$.\n\n    3.  To find the $y-$intercepts we evaluate $f(0)=\\tfrac{12}{-2}$, therefore the $y-$axis intercept occurs at $(0, -6)$\n\n        For the $x-$intercepts we solve $f(x)=0$. Hence $(x-3)(x-4)=0$, the $x-$intercepts occur at $(3, 0)$ and $(4, 0)$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1040new.png\" alt=\"AA1040new\">\n\n        $\\text{Correct asymptotes}$\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        $\\text{Correct intercepts}$\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        $\\text{Correct shape}$\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n2.  We must show that the equation $f(x)=0$ has at least one solution for all values of $a$. For that we can find the discriminant of the numerator\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\triangle&=(-(a+3))^2-4(1)(3a) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n        &=a^2+6a+9-12a\\\\\n        &=a^2-6a+9\\\\\n        &=(a-3)^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As $(a-3)^2\\geq 0$ for all values of $a$ then\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    $f(x) \\text{ will always have at least one root.}$\n\n3.  Find the derivative of $f(x)$ using the quotient rule\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x)&=\\frac{(2x-(a+3))(x-2)-(1)(x^2-(a+3)x+3a)}{(x-2)^2}\\\\\n        &=\\frac{2x^2-4x\\cancel{-ax}+2a\\cancel{-3x}+6-x^2\\cancel{+ax}\\cancel{+3x}-3a}{(x-2)^2}\\\\\n        &=\\frac{x^2-4x-a+6}{(x-2)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We must find the range of values of $a$ for which there are no solutions to the equation $f'(x)= 0$. We can find the discriminant of the numerator found above\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\triangle&=(-4)^2-4(1)(6-a) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n        &=4a-8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore there are no solutions to $f'(x)= 0$ when $4a-8<0$, so we have no stationary points for $a<2$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Also, when $a=2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\frac{x^2-4x+4}{(x-2)^2}\\\\\n        &= \\frac{(x-2)^2}{(x-2)^2}\\\\\n        &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence when $a=2$, we have a constant gradient of $1$, and $f(x)$ is a linear function (with a hole at $x=2$) and hence has no stationary point.\n\n    Hence $f$ has no stationary points for $a \\leq 2.$\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n4.  We can find the equation of the oblique asymptote in terms of $a$ using algebraic long division\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/a1040-3.png\" alt=\"a1040-3\">\n\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    As $x$ tends to a large number we can see that the equation of the oblique asymptote is $y=x-a-1$.\n\n    The root of the oblique asymptote occurs when $y=0$, therefore the root is $x=a+1$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Now lets consider the roots of $f(x)$, you may notice that you can factorise $f(x)$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x)&=(x-a)(x-3)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    However, if you did not realise this you could use the quadratic formula (or complete the square) to find the roots of $f$.\n\n    Hence, $f$ has the two roots $x_1=a$ and $x_2=3$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Therefore the root of the oblique aysmptote, $x=a+1$\n\n    $\\text{ is always one more than } x_1$.\n\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$"},{"ref":"AA1153","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1153\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nHelen is working with the function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&=e^{-x}(x-2)^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $x>0$.\n\nShe wants to learn more about its behaviour.\n\n1.  Show that $f'(x)=-e^{-x}(x^2-6x+8)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Help Helen by finding $f''(x)$.[1 mark]\n\n3.  Hence, or otherwise find\n    1.  the exact coordinates of any stationary points.\n\n    2.  the nature of these stationary points.[6 marks]\n\nThe function is concave down between $a<x<b$.\n\n4.  Find $a$ and $b$ in the form $s \\pm \\sqrt{t}$ where $s, t \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [4 marks]\n\nHelen begins looking at another function $g(x)$.\n\nShe knows that $g(2)=5$ and $g'(2)=3$.\n\n5.  Find the exact value of $[f(g(2))]'$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can differentiate the function using the product rule\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x)&=(e^{-x})'(x-2)^2+e^{-x}((x-2)^2)'\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-e^{-x}(x-2)^2+2e^{-x}(x-2) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Factorise out the $e^{-x}$ term and simplify\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=-e^{-x}((x-2)^2-2(x-2))\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-e^{-x}(x^2-4x+4-2x+4)\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.25em}=-e^{-x}(x^2-6x+8) \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  We can use the product rule again\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(x)&=(-e^{-x})'(x^2-6x+8)+(-e^{-x})(x^2-6x+8)'\\\\[6pt]\n        &=e^{-x}(x^2-6x+8)-e^{-x}(2x-6)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.25em}=e^{-x}(x^2-8x+14)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3.  1.  To find the $x-$coordinate of stationary points we must solve $f'(x)=0$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            0&=-e^{-x}(x^2-6x+8)\\\\[6pt]\n            0&=-e^{-x}(x-2)(x-4)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        With the function fully factorised we can solve for $x$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -e^{-x}=0&&\\hspace{1.8em} &x-2=0&x-4=0&\\\\[6pt]\n            \\text{no solution}&& &\\hspace{1.8em}x=2&x=4& \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We can see that there are $2$ stationary points at $x=2$ and $x=4$.\n\n        Next we can find the $y-$coordinate of these two points.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f(2)=e^{-2}(2-2)^2 & &f(4)=e^{-4}(4-2)^2\\\\[6pt]\n            f(2)=0 & &f(4)=4e^{-4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The exact coordinates of the two stationary points are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.525em}(2, 0) \\text{ and } (4, 4e^{-4}) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  To classify the stationary points we can evaluate $f''(x)$ at the stationary points.\n\n        If $ f''(x_{\\text{stat point}}) > 0 $, the S.P. is a minimum, and if $ f''(x_{\\text{stat point}}) < 0 $, the S.P. is a maximum.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f''(2)&=e^{-2}(2^2-8\\times 2+14) & &f''(4)=e^{-4}(4^2-8\\times 4+14)\\\\[6pt]\n            f''(2)&=2e^{-2} & &f''(4)=-2e^{-4}\\\\[6pt]\n            f''(2)&>0 \\hspace{1em} \\text{... minimum}& &f''(4)<0 \\hspace{1em} \\text{... maximum}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.525em}(2, 0) \\text{ is a minimum and } (4, 4e^{-4}) \\text{ is a maximum.} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  When a function changes concavity we know that $f''(x)=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n       f''(x)&=e^{-x}(x^2-8x+14)\\\\[6pt]\n       &=0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $e^{-x}\\neq 0$ therefore we can use $x^2-8x+14$ to learn about the concavity.\n\n    The original function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x)<0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can make a rough sketch of $y=x^2-8x+14$, recognising the region of the graph which is below zero. This is shown as the solid line.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1153_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1153_a\">\n\n    Hence, between the roots, $a$ and $b$, will be the region for which $f(x)$ is concave down.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    To find $a$ and $b$ we can use the quadratic formula.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n       0&=x^2-8x+14\\\\[6pt]\n       x&=\\frac{-(-8)\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^2-4\\times1 \\times 14}}{2\\times 1}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n       x&=\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{8}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n       x&=4\\pm \\sqrt{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As $b>a$ then we can say that $f(x)$ is concave down in the region\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.525em}4-\\sqrt2 < x< 4+\\sqrt 2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Using the chain rule we can differentiate $f(g(x))$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        [f(g(x))]'&=f'(g(x))\\times g'(x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        [f(g(2))]'&=f'(g(2))\\times g'(2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We were given $g'(2)=3$ and $g(2)=5$, hence\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        [f(g(2))]' &=f'(5)\\times 3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using part (b) we can find $f'(5)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(5)&=-e^{-5}(5^2-6\\times 5+8)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=-e^{-5}(3)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=-3e^{-5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we can evaluate $[f(g(x))]'$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    [f(g(x))]'&=f'(5)\\times 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &=-3e^{-5}\\times 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace*{-4.35em}[f(g(x))]'=-9e^{-5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1156","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1156\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=5xe^x+1$.\n\nThe function has a single point of inflexion at $A$.\n\n1.  Show that the point of inflexion  of $f(x)$ occurs when $x=-2$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Sketch $f(x)$ on the grid below, label point $A$ and any asymptotes.[1 mark]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1156_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1156_q\">\n\n3.  The line $g(x)=mx+c$ passes through $A$ for all values of $m$ and $c$.\n\n    1.  Evaluate $m$ when $g(x)$ is tangent to $f(x)$ at point $A$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the range of values of $m$ for which $f(x)=g(x)$ has exactly $3$ solutions. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Recognise that inflexion points occur when $f''(x)=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Use the product rule to differentiate $f(x)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x)&=(5x)'e^x+5x(e^x)'\\\\[6pt]\n        &=5e^x+5xe^x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Now we use a similar method to find $f''(x)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(x)&=5e^x+5e^x+5xe^x\\\\[6pt]\n        &=10e^x+5xe^x\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we solve the equation $f''(x)=0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=10e^x+5xe^x\\\\[6pt]\n        &=5e^x(2+x) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        2+x&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{x=-2} \\hspace{2em} \\text{...as required.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Here is the sketch of $f(x)$ with the required elements highlighted\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1156_s0.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1156_s0\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3.  1.  Recognise that $m=f'(-2)$.\n\n        Using the G.D.C. we can find $m$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f'(-2)&=-0.6767...\\\\[6pt]\n            &\\hspace{-0.8em}\\boxed{m=-0.677} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  The tangent at $A$ intersects $f(x)$ at one point.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1156_s1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1156_s1\">\n\n        Therefore when $m=-0.6767...$ there is one solution for $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n        As $m$ approaches $0$ we see below that there are $3$ solutions to $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1156_s2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1156_s2\">\n\n        When $m=0$ $g(x)$ is parallel to the asymptote, we can see below that there are $2$ solutions to $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1156_s3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1156_s3\">\n\n        Therefore there are $3$ solutions to $f(x)=g(x)$ when $-0.677<m<0$\n\n        __A1A1__ Correct end points \\\n        __A1__ $\\hspace{1.5em}$Correct inequality signs"},{"ref":"AA1258","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1258\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the curve with equation $y = (x + 2)\\mathrm{e}^{kx}$, where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. The tangent to the curve at the point where $x = 0$ is parallel to the line $y = -3x$. Find the value of $k$.","markScheme":"Differentiating using the **product rule (5.6)**, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(uv) = u\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} + v\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, with\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    u &= x + 2, & \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    v &= \\mathrm{e}^{kx}, & \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= k\\mathrm{e}^{kx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwe have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= (x+2)(k\\mathrm{e}^{kx}) + \\mathrm{e}^{kx}(1) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)\\;A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\mathrm{e}^{kx}\\bigl(1 + k(x+2)\\bigr)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nEvaluating the derivative at $x = 0$, the gradient of the tangent is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\left.\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right|_{x=0} &= \\mathrm{e}^{0}\\bigl(1 + k(0+2)\\bigr) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1(1 + 2k) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 + 2k \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince the tangent is parallel to $y = -3x$, its gradient is $-3$, so we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    1 + 2k &= -3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for $k$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    2k &= -4 \\\\[6pt]\n    k &= \\boxed{-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1262","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1262\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe tangent to the curve $y = x^3 - 3x$ at the point $G(2, 2)$ meets the $x$-axis at $H$ and the $y$-axis at $J$. Find the ratio $GH : HJ$.","markScheme":"Differentiating to find the gradient function of the tangent, we have $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 3x^2 - 3$. Evaluating at $x = 2$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\left.\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right|_{x=2} &= 3(2)^2 - 3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(4) - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the **equation of a straight line (2.1)**, $y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$, with $m = 9$ and $(x_1, y_1) = (2, 2)$, the tangent at $G$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    y - 2 &= 9(x - 2) \\\\[6pt]\n    y-2 &= 9x - 18 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 9x - 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFinding $H$ by setting $y = 0$ gives $0 = 9x - 16$, so $x = \\dfrac{16}{9}$. Finding $J$ by setting $x = 0$ gives $y = -16$. Hence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    H = \\left(\\dfrac{16}{9},\\,0\\right), \\qquad J = (0,\\,-16) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $G$, $H$ and $J$ are collinear, the ratio of the segments equals the ratio of the differences in their $x$-coordinates. Hence we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    GH : HJ &= \\left(2 - \\dfrac{16}{9}\\right) : \\left(\\dfrac{16}{9} - 0\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{9} : \\dfrac{16}{9} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2 : 16 \\\\[6pt]\n    GH : HJ &= \\boxed{1 : 8} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1264","marks":"17","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1264\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nConsider the function $f$ such that $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^2-10x+20}{x-3}$.\n\n1. Write down the equation of the vertical asymptote.[1 mark]\n\n2. Find the coordinates of any maxima and minima on the graph of $f$. [6 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ approaches an oblique asymptote as $x\\rightarrow \\pm \\infty$.\n\n3. Find the equation of the oblique asymptote.[4 marks]\n\n4. Sketch the graph of $f$ on the axes provided, clearly indicating any asymptotes, axes intercepts, maxima and minima. [5 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/1264-1.png\" alt=\"1264-1\">\n\n5. State the range of $f$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Since the function is undefined when the denominator is zero, and the numerator is non-zero at this value, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x-3 &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Therefore the equation of the vertical asymptote is\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\boxed{x = 3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using **the quotient rule (5.6)**, with $u=2x^2-10x+20$ and $v=x-3$, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\frac{v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}-u\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}{v^2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(x) &= \\frac{(x-3)(4x-10)-(2x^2-10x+20)(1)}{(x-3)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\frac{4x^2-22x+30-2x^2+10x-20}{(x-3)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(x) &= \\frac{2x^2-12x+10}{(x-3)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Setting $f'(x)=0$ to find the stationary points, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2x^2-12x+10 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    x^2-6x+5 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n    (x-1)(x-5) &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    So the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points are\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= 1 \\text{ or } x = 5 \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting these values into $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(1) &= \\frac{2(1)^2-10(1)+20}{1-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= -6 \\\\[12pt]\n\n    f(5) &= \\frac{2(5)^2-10(5)+20}{5-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= 10\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We therefore have two stationary points, $(1,-6)$ and $(5,10)$. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By performing a sign-change test, we can determine whether these are maxima or minima. Let's find the value of the gradient at $x=0$, $x=2$, $x=4$ and $x=6$. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f'(0)&= \\dfrac{2(0)^2-12(0)+10}{9} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(0)>0\\right]} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(2) &= \\dfrac{2(2)^2-12(2)+10}{1} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(2)<0\\right]}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    f'(4) &= \\dfrac{2(4)^2-12(4)+10}{1} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(4)<0\\right]}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(6) &= \\dfrac{2(6)^2-12(6)+10}{9} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(6)>0\\right]} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Before $(1,-6)$, the gradient is **positive** and immediately after, the gradient is **negative**. Before $(5,10)$, the gradient is **negative** and immediately after, the gradient is **positive**. Therefore we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\text{maximum at } (1,\\,-6) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\text{minimum at } (5,\\,10)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Because we are dividing a degree 2 polynomial by a degree 1 polynomial, we know that $f(x)$ can be written in the form $f(x)=ax+b+\\dfrac{c}{x-3}$, where $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{R}$. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(R1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{2x^2-10x+20}{x-3} &= ax+b+\\dfrac{c}{x-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    2x^2 - 10x + 20 &= ax(x-3) + b(x-3) + c \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= ax^2 -3ax + bx -3b + c \\\\[6pt]\n\n    2x^2 - 10x + 20 &= ax^2 - (3a-b)x + (c - 3b)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Equating the coefficients on the LHS with the RHS, we must have $a=2$.\n\n    We must have $3a-b=10$ and therefore $b=-4$.\n\n    We must have $c-3b = 20$ and therefore $c=8$.\n\n    Hence, $f(x)=2x-4+\\dfrac{8}{x-3}$.\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    As $x\\rightarrow\\pm\\infty$, we have $\\dfrac{8}{x-3}\\rightarrow0$, so the graph approaches the line $y=2x-4$.\n\n    Therefore the equation of the oblique asymptote is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{y = 2x-4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. To find the $y$-intercept, we set $x=0$, so we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(0) &= \\frac{2(0)^2-10(0)+20}{0-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= -\\frac{20}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    To find any $x$-intercepts, we set $f(x)=0$, which requires the numerator to be zero. Using \\textbf{the formula for the discriminant (2.7)} with $a=2$, $b=-10$ and $c=20$, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    b^2-4ac &= (-10)^2-4(2)(20) \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= -60\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Since the discriminant is negative, the graph has no $x$-intercepts. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the asymptotes, the maximum and minimum from the previous parts, and the $y$-intercept, we obtain the following sketch.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/1264-2.png\" alt=\"1264-2\">\n\n    $\\text{Correct asymptotes}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\\text{Correct maximum, minimum and $y$-intercept}$$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Correct curve}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. From the sketch in part (d), the maximum value on the left-hand branch is $-6$ and the minimum value on the right-hand branch is $10$, so the range is\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{f(x)\\leq-6 \\text{ or } f(x)\\geq10} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1317","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1317\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nLet $f(x)=4(x-1)^2$.\n\n1.    Write down the range of $f$. [1 mark]\n\nConsider another function $g$ such that $g(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{e^x}$ for $x\\geq 0$.\n\n2.    Find the $y$-intercept of $g$. [1 mark]\n\n3.    Show that $g'(x)=\\dfrac{-4(x^2-4x+3)}{e^x}$. [3 marks]\n\nThe graph of $g$ has two stationary points.\n\n4.    \n\n    1.    Find the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $g$. \n\n    \n\n    2.    Given that $g''(x)=\\dfrac{4(x^2-6x+7)}{e^x}$, determine the nature of the stationary points. [5 marks]\n\n5.    \n\n    1.    Find the values of $x$ for which the graph of $g$ is concave-down. \n\n    2.    Hence, write down the region for which $g$ is increasing and concave-down. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. The given function $f$ is a quadratic function. As the $x^2$ term is positive it is concave-up (opens upwards).\n\n    Therefore the vertex is the the minimum point and with the $y$-coordinate of this point we can write down the range.\n\n    \n\n    The vertex form of a quadratic is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y=a(x-h)^2+k\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    where the vertex is $(h,k)$.\n\n    Our quadratic is already written in vertex form. We can see this more clearly if it is written like this\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y=4(x-1)^2+0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence the minimum point is $(1,0)$ which means the range is $f(x)\\geq0$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Substituting $f$ into the new function $g$ we have $g(x)=\\dfrac{4(x-1)^2}{e^x}$.\n\n    To find the $y$-intercept we evaluate the function at $x=0$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    g(0)&=\\frac{4(0-1)^2}{e^0}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=4\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,4)$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. The function $g$ is a quotient. Hence we will use the quotient rule to differentiate it.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{If }& &\\hspace{-5em}&y&=\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{u}}{\\textcolor{blue}{v}}&\\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{then }&&\\hspace{-5em}&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&= \\frac{v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}-u\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    For $g(x)=\\dfrac{\\textcolor{red}{4(x-1)^2}}{\\textcolor{blue}{e^x}}$ we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u&=4(x-1)^2&v&=e^{x}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2\\times4(x-1)^1&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=e^{x}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=8(x-1)&&\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can substitute these expressions into the quotient rule formula giving\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    g'(x)&= \\frac{e^x(8(x-1))-4(x-1)^2(e^x)}{(e^x)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By factorising the numerator we can cancel the common term and then simplify the numerator\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    g'(x)&= \\frac{4\\cancel{e^x}(2(x-1)-(x-1)^2)}{(e^x)^{\\cancel{2}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &= \\frac{4(2(x-1)-(x-1)^2)}{e^x}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &= \\frac{4(2x-2-(x^2-2x+1))}{e^x}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &= \\frac{4(2x-2-x^2+2x-1))}{e^x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &= \\frac{4(-x^2+4x-3)}{e^x}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{-4(x^2-4x+3)}{e^x}\\hspace{3em}\\text{...as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4.  1. To find $x$-coordinates of the stationary points we set the derivative equal to zero.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{-4(x^2-4x+3)}{e^x}&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Solving for $x$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        -4(x^2-4x+3)&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n        x^2-4x+3&=0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        This quadratic can be factorised\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (x-3)(x-1)&=0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence by the null factor theorem,  $x=1$ and $x=3$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. As we are given $g''(x)$ let's conduct a second-derivative test to determine the nature of the stationary points. \n\n        To do this we evaluate the second derivative using the $x$-values of the stationary points we found in part (d)(i).\n\n        If $g''(x)<0$ then the point is a maximum.\n\n        If $g''(x)>0$ then the point is a minimum.\n\n        Substituting $x=1$ and $x=3$ into the second derivative\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g''(1)&=\\frac{4((1)^2-6(1)+7)}{e^{(1)}}&&g''(3)=\\frac{4((3)^2-6(3)+7)}{e^{(3)}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{4(2)}{e}&&\\hspace{2.1em}=\\frac{4(-2)}{e^3}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &>0&&\\hspace{2.1em}<0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Note that we do not need to know the exact values here. It is enough to know whether the final outcome is positive or negative.\n\n        As $g''(1)>0$, there is a $\\text{local minimum at }x=1$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As $g''(3)<0$ there is a $\\text{local maximum at }x=3$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n5.  1. A function is concave-down if $g''(x)<0$ (and concave-up if $g''(x)>0$).\n\n        Hence we can form the inequality\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g''(x)&<0\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\frac{4(x^2-6x+7)}{e^x}&<0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Solving for $x$ we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        4(x^2-6x+7)&<0\\\\[6pt]\n\n        x^2-6x+7&<0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        To solve this inequality we need to find the roots of $x$.\n\n        The roots are the $x$-intercepts which are the points where the quadratic changes from positive to negative and vice versa.\n\n        Hence the graph changes concavity when $g''(x)=0$. Note, these are known as points of inflexion.\n\n        Hence we must solve\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x^2-6x+7=0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The quadratic cannot be easily factorised thus we will use the quadratic formula.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{If}\\hspace{1em}\\,ax^2+bx+c&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\text{then}\\hspace{4.7em}x&=\\dfrac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        From the equation $a=1$, $b=-6$ and $c=7$ so\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x&=\\dfrac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2-4(1)(7)}}{2(1)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\dfrac{6\\pm\\sqrt{8}}{2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=\\dfrac{6\\pm2\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=3\\pm\\sqrt{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We now have the two roots but we still need to decide for what region of $x$ is $x^2-6x+7<0$.\n\n        Note that $y=x^2-6x+7$ is concave-up for all $x$, this is because the coefficient of $x^2$ is positive.\n\n        As the graph is U-shaped the region below the $x$-axis is when $x^2-6x+7<0$. This occurs when $x$ is $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{between}}$ the two roots $x^2-6x+7<0$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1317a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1317a1\">\n\n        Hence the desired region is $3-\\sqrt{2}<x<3+\\sqrt{2}$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. In part (d) we found that the graph of $g$ has a minimum at $x=1$ and a maximum at $x=3$.\n\n        We are told that $g$ has only $2$ stationary points, therefore the function must be $\\textcolor{green}{\\text{increasing}}$ on the interval $1<x<3$.\n\n        In part (e)(i) we found that the function is $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{concave-down}}$ between $3-\\sqrt{2}<x<3+\\sqrt{2}$.\n\n        As we want the area of the curve which is increasing __and__ concave-down, we must consider the $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{overlapping region}}$ of these intervals, this is shown below.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/AA1317a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1317a2\">\n\n        Hence $3-\\sqrt{2}<x<3$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A2}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1343","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1343\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nA scientist is studying the effect of a supplement on the growth of a certain species of bacteria in a container. \n\nA function is found which models the population $P$ of bacteria after $t$ minutes such that $P_A(t)=a\\times 3^{kt}$ where $t\\geq0$ and $a,k\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe initial population of the bacteria is known to be $600$ and the scientist finds that when $t=3$ the population has tripled.\n\n1.    \n\n    1.    Write down the value of $a$. \n\n    2.    Hence show that the value of $k=\\dfrac{1}{3}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.    Calculate the population after $10$ minutes. Give your answer to the nearest integer. [1 mark]\n\n3.    Use your model to determine the rate of change of $P_A$ when $t=20$ minutes. [2 marks]\n\nAt $10$ minutes the scientist adds a supplement to the container. \n\nThe model after adding the supplement can be expressed in the form $P_B(t)=be^{c(t-10)}$ where $b,c\\in\\mathbb{R}$ and $t>10$.\n\n4.    Write down the value of $b$. [1 mark]\n\nThe scientist calculates that the population when $t=15$ minutes is decreasing by $74$ bacteria per minute.\n\n5.    Given that $c<-1$, find the value of $c$. [4 marks]\n\n6.    Determine the time when the population of bacteria first drops below $1\\%$ of the maximum population. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. To find $a$ we can use the initial population $P=600$ which is when $t=0$. Hence \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        600&=a(3^{k(0)})\\\\[6pt]\n\n        600&=a(1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        a&=600\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. Using the value from part (a)(i) and that the population has tripled when $t=3$, we can solve for $k$. \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        P_A(t)&=600(3)^{kt}\\\\[10pt]\n\n        3(600)&=600(3)^{3k}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As this is a 'show that' question we need to show algebraic working.\n\n        Diving by $600$ and using the exponent of $3$ we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        3&=3^{3k}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        3^1&=3^{3k}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        1&=3k\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        k&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. To find the population when $t=10$ we substitute this into the function using the parameters we found in part (a). We must also remember to round our answer to the nearest integer.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P_A(10)&=600(3)^{\\normalsize\\frac{1}{3}(10)}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=23\\,364.4...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=23\\,364\\text{ bacteria}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. We are not expected to know how to find the derivative of the given function but we can use our G.D.C. to help.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the derivative function on the G.D.C. and evaluating when $t=20$ we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P_A'(20)&=333\\,182.9...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=333\\,000\\text{ bacteria per minute}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. We know the population of bacteria in the container when the supplement is added is $23\\,364$, from part (b).\n\n    Hence we can substitute this value and the initial value of $t=10$ into the equation to solve for $b$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    23\\,364&=be^{c(10-10)}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    23\\,364&=be^0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    b&=23\\,364\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. We are given that the population is $\\textbf{decreasing}$ when $t=15$. \n\n    The given value is the $\\textit{rate of change}$ of $P_B$ so we need to find the derivative function.\n\n    Using the chain rule with the standard derivative of $y=e^x$ we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P_B'(t)&=23\\,364e^{c(t-10)}(c)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=23\\,364c\\cdot e^{c(t-10)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Setting this equal to $-74$ when $t=15$ we can solve for $c$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    -74&=23\\,364c\\cdot e^{c(15-10)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    -74&=23\\,364c\\cdot e^{5c}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    c=-1.184...&\\hspace{3em}c=-0.00322...\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    As $c<-1$ then our answer is $c=-1.18$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n6. As this is an exponential function and the population is decreasing after the supplement is added to the container, the maximum value is the initial value. \n\n    Hence $1\\%$ of $23\\,364$ is $233.64...$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Setting the equation equal to this value we can solve for $t$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    233.364...&=23\\,364...e^{-1.184...(t-10)}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0.01&=e^{-1.184...(t-10)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the G.D.C. we can solve for $t$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    t&=13.88...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=13.9\\text{ minutes}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1640","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1640\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the function $y = \\dfrac{2x + 3}{x + 1}$. The graph of this function has a vertical and a horizontal asymptote.\n\n1.  Write down the equations of the asymptotes.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, giving your answer in the form $\\dfrac{a}{(x+b)^2}$ where $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.[3 marks]\n\n3.  State the number of points of inflexion on the graph of this function, justifying your answer.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The vertical asymptote occurs where the denominator is zero, $x + 1 = 0$, so we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\boxed{x=-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As $x \\to \\pm\\infty$ the function approaches the ratio of leading coefficients, so the horizontal asymptote is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\boxed{y = 2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Applying the **quotient rule (5.6)**, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{u}{v}\\right) = \\dfrac{v\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} - u\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}{v^2}$, with\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        u &= 2x + 3, & \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n        v &= x + 1, & \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{(x+1)(2) - (2x+3)(1)}{(x+1)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)\\;A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Simplifying the numerator, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{2x + 2 - 2x - 3}{(x+1)^2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\boxed{\\dfrac{-1}{(x+1)^2}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    so $a = -1$ and $b = 1$.\n\n3.  Differentiating the result from part (b), we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2 y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} &= \\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since the numerator is a non-zero constant, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2 y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}$ is never equal to zero for any $x$ in the domain of $y$, so there can be no point of inflexion.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the number of points of inflexion is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\boxed{0} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1641","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1641\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = 2x^3 - 4x$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Find $f'(x)$ from first principles.","markScheme":"Applying the **derivative of $f(x)$ from first principles (5.12)**\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) = \\displaystyle\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwith $f(x) = 2x^3 - 4x$, and $f(x+h) = 2(x+h)^3 - 4(x+h)$ we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\bigl[2(x+h)^3 - 4(x+h)\\bigr] - \\bigl[2x^3 - 4x\\bigr]}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nExpanding $(x+h)^3$ using the **binomial theorem (1.9)**,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    (a+b)^n = \\displaystyle\\sum_{r=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{r} a^{n-r} b^r,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwith $n = 3$, we expand to get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    (x+h)^3 &= x^3 + 3x^2 h + 3xh^2 + h^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting this in and simplifying the numerator gives us\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{2x^3 + 6x^2 h + 6xh^2 + 2h^3 - 4x - 4h - 2x^3 + 4x}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{6x^2 h + 6xh^2 + 2h^3 - 4h}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nDividing each term by $h$, we then have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{6x^2 h}{h} + \\dfrac{6xh^2}{h} + \\dfrac{2h^3}{h} - \\dfrac{4h}{h}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\bigl(6x^2 + 6xh + 2h^2 - 4\\bigr) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTaking the limit as $h \\to 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\boxed{6x^2 - 4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1643","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1643\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = -x^3 + 3x + 5$.\n\n1.  Expand $(x + h)^3$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Use the formula $f'(x) = \\displaystyle\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}$ to show that the derivative of $f(x)$ is $-3x^2 + 3$.[4 marks]\n3.  The tangent to the curve of $f$ at the point $A(2,\\,3)$ is parallel to the tangent at a point $B$. Find the coordinates of $B$.[4 marks]\n4.  The graph of $f$ is increasing for $p < x < q$. Find the value of $p$ and of $q$.[3 marks]\n5.  Write down the range of values for the gradient of $f$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Applying the **binomial theorem (1.9)**, $(a+b)^n = \\displaystyle\\sum_{r=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{r} a^{n-r} b^r$, with $n = 3$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (x+h)^3 &= \\binom{3}{0}x^3 + \\binom{3}{1}x^2 h + \\binom{3}{2}x h^2 + \\binom{3}{3}h^3 \\\\[14pt]\n        &= \\boxed{x^3 + 3x^2 h + 3xh^2 + h^3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1\\;A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  Applying the **derivative of $f(x)$ from first principles (5.12)**,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) = \\displaystyle\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h},\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    with $f(x) = -x^3 + 3x + 5$ and $f(x+h) = -(x+h)^3 + 3(x+h) + 5$, subbing in we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\bigl[-(x+h)^3 + 3(x+h) + 5\\bigr] - \\bigl[-x^3 + 3x + 5\\bigr]}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting the expansion of $(x+h)^3$ from part (a) and simplifying the numerator, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{-x^3 - 3x^2 h - 3xh^2 - h^3 + 3x + 3h + 5 + x^3 - 3x - 5}{h} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{-3x^2 h - 3xh^2 - h^3 + 3h}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Factoring $h$ from the numerator and cancelling, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{h(-3x^2 - 3xh - h^2 + 3)}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} (-3x^2 - 3xh - h^2 + 3) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Taking the limit as $h \\to 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\boxed{-3x^2 + 3}\\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required.} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3.  Evaluating the derivative at $A$, the gradient of the tangent at $A$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(2) &= -3(2)^2 + 3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -9 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Setting $f'(b) = -9$ to find the $x$-coordinate of $B$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -3b^2 + 3 &= -9 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n        b^2 &= 4 \\\\[6pt]\n        b &= \\pm 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since $B \\neq A$, we take $b = -2$, and $f(-2) = 8 - 6 + 5 = 7$, so\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        B &= \\boxed{(-2,\\, 7)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n4.  The graph is increasing where $f'(x) > 0$. Setting up the inequality, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -3x^2 + 3 &> 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Dividing by $-3$ (and flipping the inequality), we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x^2 &< 1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[12pt]\n        -1 &< x < 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    therefore we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        p = \\boxed{-1}, \\quad q = \\boxed{1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n5.  The gradient function $f'(x) = -3x^2 + 3$ is an inverted parabola with maximum at $x = 0$, giving $f'(0) = 3$. Since $f'(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to \\pm\\infty$, the range of the gradient is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\boxed{f'(x) \\leq 3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1\\;A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1645","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1645\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = px^3 + qx$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $p$ and $q$ are constants.\n\nFind $f'(x)$ from first principles.","markScheme":"Applying the **derivative of $f(x)$ from first principles (5.12)**,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) = \\displaystyle\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwith $f(x) = px^3 + qx$ and $f(x+h) = p(x+h)^3 + q(x+h)$, subbing in we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\bigl[p(x+h)^3 + q(x+h)\\bigr] - \\bigl[px^3 + qx\\bigr]}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nExpanding $(x+h)^3$ using the **binomial theorem (1.9)**, $(a+b)^n = \\displaystyle\\sum_{r=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{r} a^{n-r} b^r$, with $n = 3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    (x+h)^3 &= \\binom{3}{0}x^3 h^0 + \\binom{3}{1}x^2 h^1 + \\binom{3}{2}x^1 h^2 + \\binom{3}{3}x^0 h^3 \\\\[16pt]\n    &= x^3 + 3x^2 h + 3xh^2 + h^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting into the definition and simplifying the numerator, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{px^3 + 3px^2 h + 3pxh^2 + ph^3 + qx + qh - px^3 - qx}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{3px^2 h + 3pxh^2 + ph^3 + qh}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFactoring $h$ from the numerator and dividing, we now have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{h(3px^2 + 3pxh + ph^2 + q)}{h} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\lim_{h \\to 0} (3px^2 + 3pxh + ph^2 + q) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTaking the limit as $h \\to 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\boxed{3px^2 + q} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA208","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA208\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = e^{2x}-4e^x+2$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\leq a$, where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nPart of the graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/dc14321f698d30780f4ecaf20c082a22181a9565.svg\" alt=\"dc14321f698d30780f4ecaf20c082a22181a9565.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the largest value of $a$ such that $f$ has an inverse function.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  For this value of $a$, find an expression for $f^{-1}(x)$, stating\n    its domain. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  From the diagram, we see that the $x$-coordinate of the local\n    minimum point on the graph of $y = f(x)$ is the largest value of $a$\n    such that $f$ has an inverse function.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[e^{2x}-4e^x+2\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2e^{2x}-4e^x \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\big[2e^x\\big]\\big[e^x - 2\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we solve the equation $f'(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\big[2e^x\\big]\\big[e^x - 2\\big] &= 0 \\hspace{5.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    e^x - 2 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n    e^x &= 2 \\\\[7pt]\n    x &= \\ln 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = \\ln 2$\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y =  f^{-1}(x)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\hspace{3em} [\\text{where $-2 \\leq x < 2$}] \\\\[9pt]\n    f(y) &= x \\hspace{3em} [\\text{where $y \\leq \\ln 2$}] \\\\[7pt]\n    e^{2y}-4e^y+2 &= x \\\\[7pt]\n    \\big[(e^y)^2-4e^y\\big]+2 &= x \\\\[7pt]\n    \\hspace{7.1em} \\big[(e^y-2)^2 - 4\\big]+2 &= x \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by completing the square}] \\\\[7pt]\n    (e^y-2)^2 &= x+2 \\\\[7pt]\n    e^y-2 &= \\pm\\sqrt{x+2} \\\\[7pt]\n    e^y-2 &= -\\sqrt{x+2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $y \\leq \\ln 2$}] \\\\[7pt]\n    e^y &= 2-\\sqrt{x+2} \\\\[7pt]\n    y &= \\ln (2-\\sqrt{x+2})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln (2-\\sqrt{x+2}), \\text{ for } x \\in \\mathbb{R}, -2 \\leq x < 2$"},{"ref":"AA464","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA464\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = -x^5 + e^{-x} + 2$,\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. By considering $f'(x)$, determine whether $f$ is a\none-to-one or many-to-one function.","markScheme":"For any $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.4em} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}- x^5 + e^{-x} + 2\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n&= -5x^4 - e^{-x} \\\\[8pt]\n&= -\\big[5x^4 + e^{-x}\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n&< 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence $f$ is strictly decreasing on $\\mathbb{R}$.\nTherefore $f$ is a\n $\\text{one-to-one}$\nfunction."},{"ref":"AA467","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA467\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of all the points on the curve\n$y = 2x^4 - \\dfrac{34}{3}x^3 + \\dfrac{41}{2}x^2 - 10x$\n\nat which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the tangent at\nP$\\bigg(1,\\dfrac{7}{6}\\bigg)$.","markScheme":"Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[2x^4 - \\dfrac{34}{3}x^3 + \\dfrac{41}{2}x^2 - 10x\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n&= 8x^3 - 34x^2 + 41x - 10 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nEvaluating the gradient of\nthe tangent to the curve at P, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg|_{x=1} &= 8(1)^3 - 34(1)^2 + 41(1) - 10 \\\\[5pt]\n&= 8 - 34 + 41 - 10 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 5 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTangents to the curve that are parallel have the\nsame gradient.\n\nHence we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg|_{x=1} \\\\[6pt]\n8x^3 - 34x^2 + 41x - 10 &= 5 \\\\[10pt]\n\\hspace{2em} 8x^3 - 34x^2 + 41x - 15 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we let $p(x) = 8x^3 - 34x^2 + 41x - 15$, we obtain $p(1) = 0$.\n\nHence, by the factor theorem, $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x)$.\n\nUsing the long division method, we have\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $x$, we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1)(8x^2 - 26x +15) &= 0 \\hspace{8.2em} \\\\[10pt]\n(x-1)(4x-3)(2x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{3.7em} x = \\dfrac{3}{4},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNote: $x=1$ is a solution of this polynomial but we already knew this value and need the other x values where this tangent value occurs."},{"ref":"AA468","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA468\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the curve $y = \\dfrac{2x}{1+x}$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$,\n$x \\neq -1$.\n\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Determine the equation of the normal to the curve at the point\n    P$(-2,4)$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.5em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2x}{1+x}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(2)(1+x) - (2x)(1)}{(1+x)^2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by quotient rule}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(2 + \\cancel{2x}) - \\cancel{2x}}{(1+x)^2} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{(1+x)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating the gradient of the tangent to the curve at P, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg|_{x=-2} &= \\dfrac{2}{(1+(-2))^2} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{(-1)^2} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 2 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the gradient of the normal to the curve\n    at P is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the point-gradient formula\n    with P$(-2,4)$ and $m = -1/2$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[10pt]\n    y - 4 &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}(x - (-2)) \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 4 &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.6em} y =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}x + 3\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA478","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA478\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x+1)e^{-2x}$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Prove by induction that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\big[n(-2)^{n-1} + (-2)^n(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}$\n    for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.[7 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of any local minimum and maximum points on the\n    graph of $y = f(x)$. Justify whether any such point is a minimum or\n    a maximum. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of any points of inflexion on the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$. Justify whether any such point is a point of inflexion.\n    [5 marks]\n\n5.  Hence sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, indicating clearly the points\n    found in parts (c) and (d) and any intercepts with the axes.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the product rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[(x+1)e^{-2x}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= e^{-2x} - 2(x+1)e^{-2x} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $\\mathrm{P}(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.9em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\big[n(-2)^{n-1} + (-2)^n(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that $\\mathrm{P}(n)$\n    holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, from part (a), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that $\\mathrm{P}(k)$ holds\nfor some $k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^kf}{\\mathrm{d}x^k} = \\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\hspace{0.5em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that $\\mathrm{P}(k+1)$ holds, we\nneed to show that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.6em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} = \\big[(k+1)(-2)^k + (-2)^{(k+1)}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we differentiate both sides of the equation (IH), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.55em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\Big[\\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\\Big] \\\\[14pt]\n&= \\big[(-2)^k\\big]e^{-2x} - 2\\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\[16pt]\n&= \\big[(-2)^k\\big]e^{-2x} + \\big[k(-2)^k + (-2)^{k+1}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\[16pt]\n&= \\big[(k+1)(-2)^k + (-2)^{(k+1)}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $P(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $f'(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    1 - 2(x+1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    - 2x - 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -0.5 \\hspace{0.6em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $-0.5$ is the only\n    critical number of $f$.\n\n    Using the ID Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/6db5903b56fcbc8c908bce990a2d28a2f4d84f81.svg\" alt=\"6db5903b56fcbc8c908bce990a2d28a2f4d84f81.svg\">\n\n    The sign of $f'$ changes from positive to negative at $-0.5$.\n    Hence, by the First Derivative Test, $f$ has a local maximum at\n    $-0.5$.\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = -0.5$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} y &= f(-0.5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-0.5+1)e^{-2(-0.5)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 0.5e\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has a local\n    maximum at\n     $\\mathrm{A}(-0.5,0.5e)$\n\n4.  If we solve the equation $f''(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\big[2(-2) + (-2)^2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} &= 0 \\hspace{3.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-4 + 4(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    -4 + 4(x+1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence there is at most one point of inflexion\n    on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    Using the Concavity Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/4ab9272c6eeca47267a3b6db4da801aa3659585d.svg\" alt=\"4ab9272c6eeca47267a3b6db4da801aa3659585d.svg\">\n\n    The graph of $y = f(x)$ changes concavity from downward to upward at\n    $0$.\n\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has an inflexion point at $0$.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0+1)e^{-2(0)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has the inflexion\n    point at\n     $\\mathrm{B}(0,1)$\n\n5.  We first note that the domain of the function\n    $f(x) = (x+1)e^{-2x}$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\n    Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (x+1)e^{-2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x+1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -1 \\hspace{1.7em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{C}(-1,0)$\n    is the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    From part (d), we know $y = 1$ when $x = 0$.\n\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{B}(0,1)$\n    is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\big[(x+1)e^{-2x}\\big] \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\infty\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\big[(x+1)e^{-2x}\\big] \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the $x$-axis is a horizontal asymptote of\n    the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ indicating the points A, B, &\n    C, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/109a725ff8e3517bb8bd68e412ea7f4366d78a1c.svg\" alt=\"109a725ff8e3517bb8bd68e412ea7f4366d78a1c.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA500","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA500\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}$, for $x > 0$.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  The graph of $f$ has a maximum at point P. Find the coordinates of\n    P.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the quotient rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) & =  \\dfrac{(\\ln x)'(x) - (\\ln x)(x)'}{x^2} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(x) - (\\ln x)(1)}{x^2} \\\\[8pt]\n    &  \\hspace{.275em}=  \\dfrac{1 - \\ln x}{x^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving $f'(x) = 0$ for stationary points, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1 - \\ln x}{x^2} & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n    1 - \\ln x & =  0 \\\\[9pt]\n    1 & =  \\ln x \\\\[9pt]\n    e^1 & =  x \\\\[9pt]\n    x & =  e.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since $f'(x) > 0$ for $x < e$ and\n    $f'(x) < 0$ for $x > e$, $f$ has a maximum when $x = e$.\n\n    The $y$-coordinate of P is $f(e) = \\dfrac{1}{e}$. Hence $f$ has a\n    maximum at   $\\textrm{P}\\Big(e,\\dfrac{1}{e}\\Big)$"},{"ref":"AA501","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA501\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{-3x}$.\n\n1.  Write down $f'(x), f''(x)$ and $f'''(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for $f^{(n)}(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4mm}f'(x) = -3 e^{-3x} \\\\[8pt]\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    f''(x) =  9 e^{-3x} \\\\[8pt]\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4mm}f'''(x) = -27 e^{-3x}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6mm}f^{(n)}(x) =  (-3)^n e^{-3x}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA502","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA502\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = g(x)h(x)$, where $g(3) = 6$, $h(3) = 2$, $g'(3) = 4$ and\n$h'(3) = 1$.\n\nFind the equation of the normal to the graph of $f$ at $x = 3$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny   & =  f(x) \\\\[5pt]\n& =  f(3) \\\\[5pt]\n& =  g(3)h(3) \\\\[5pt]\n& =  6\\cdot 2 \\\\[5pt]\n& =  12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.3cm}\nf'(3)       & =  g'(3)h(3) + g(3)h'(3) \\\\[5pt]\n& = 4\\cdot2 + 6\\cdot 1 \\\\[5pt]\n& =  14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the slope of the normal to the\ncurve at $(x_0,y_0) = (3,12)$ is $m = -\\dfrac{1}{14}$ and the equation\nof the normal is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_0     & =  m(x - x_0) \\hspace{4mm}\\\\[8pt]\ny - 12  & =   -\\dfrac{1}{14}(x - 3) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{.7cm}y      =  -\\dfrac{1}{14}x + \\dfrac{171}{14}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA518","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA518\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the curve $y = \\dfrac{9}{5-x} + \\dfrac{1}{x-1}$.\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve where the gradient\nis zero.","markScheme":"If we calculate the derivative of $y$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\dfrac{9}{5-x} + \\dfrac{1}{x-1}\\bigg] \\\\[8pt]\n&= \\dfrac{9}{(5-x)^2} - \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)^2} \\hspace{3em}  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we solve the equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0$ for $x$,\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{9}{(5-x)^2} - \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)^2} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n9(x-1)^2 &= (5-x)^2 \\\\[10pt]\n9x^2-18x+9 &= 25-10x+x^2 \\\\[10pt]\n8x^2-8x-16 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\nx^2-x-2 &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\n(x+1)(x-2) &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\nx&= -1,\\,2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the $x$-coordinates of the points on the\ncurve where the gradient is zero are\n\n$x = -1\\ \\mbox {and}\\  x = 2$"},{"ref":"AA521","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA521\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^3+x-2}{2x}$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\neq 0$.\n\n1.  The graph of $y = f(x)$ has a local minimum at A. Find the\n    coordinates of A.[5 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that there is exactly one point of inflexion, B, on the\n        graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    2.  The coordinates of B can be expressed in the form B$(2^p, 2^q)$,\n        where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. Find the value of $p$ and the value\n        of $q$. [8 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ showing clearly the position of the\n    points A and B. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we calculate the $1$st derivative of $f$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5cm}\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^3+x-2}{2x}\\bigg] \\\\[4pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(3x^2+1)(2x) - (x^3+x-2)(2)}{(2x)^2} \\hspace{1.5em} [\\text{by quotient rule}]\\\\[4pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{6x^3+\\cancel{2x} - 2x^3-\\cancel{2x}+4}{4x^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4x^3 + 4}{4x^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{x^3+1}{x^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we solve the equation\n    $f'(x) = 0$ for $x \\neq 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{x^3+1}{x^2} &= 0  \\\\[5.5pt]\n    x^3+1 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    x &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $-1$ is the only critical number of\n    $f$.\n\n    Using the ID Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/9e92d9e245c8060950431511bb61a4076fa9daaf.svg\" alt=\"9e92d9e245c8060950431511bb61a4076fa9daaf.svg\">\n\n    The sign of $f'$ changes from negative to positive at $-1$.\n\n    Hence, by the First Derivative Test, $f$ has a local minimum at\n    $-1$.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = -1$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= f(-1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(-1)^3+(-1)-2}{2(-1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has a local\n    minimum at  $ \\mbox A(-1,2)$\n\n2.\n    1.  If we calculate the $2$nd derivative of $f$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.7cm}\n        f''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^3+1}{x^2}\\bigg] \\\\[4pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{(3x^2)(x^2) - (x^3+1)(2x)}{(x^2)^2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by quotient rule}] \\\\[4pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{x^4 - 2x}{x^4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        If we solve the equation\n        $f''(x) = 0$ for $x \\neq 0$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{x^4 - 2x}{x^4} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x^4 - 2x &= 0 \\\\[9.5pt]\n        x^3 - 2 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x \\neq 0$}] \\\\[8pt]\n        x &= \\sqrt[3]{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence there is at most one point\n        of inflexion on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n        Using the Concavity Test, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/8d86187a467f2a2771ca3a277926d4698a2c343b.svg\" alt=\"8d86187a467f2a2771ca3a277926d4698a2c343b.svg\">\n\n        The graph of $y = f(x)$ changes from concave downward to concave\n        upward at $\\sqrt[3]{2}$.\n\n        Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has an inflexion point at\n        $\\sqrt[3]{2}$.\n\n        $\n        \\mbox{Hence there is exactly one point of inflexion on the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\).}$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = \\sqrt[3]{2}$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        y &= f(\\sqrt[3]{2}) \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{(\\sqrt[3]{2})^3+\\sqrt[3]{2}-2}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}} \\\\[4pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}+\\sqrt[3]{2}-\\cancel{2}}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}} \\\\[4pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has\n        the inflexion point at B$(2^\\frac{1}{3},2^{-1})$.\n\n        $\n        \\mbox{Hence \\(p = \\dfrac 1 3\\) and \\(q = -1\\)}$\n\n3.  We first note that the domain of the function $f$ is\n    $\\mathbb{R}/\\{0\\} = (-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,+\\infty)$.\n\n    Since $f(0)$ is not defined, the graph of $y = f(x)$ has no\n    $y$-intercept.\n\n    Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x\\neq 0$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{x^3+x-2}{2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x^3+x-2 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    x &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point P$(1,0)$ is the $x$-intercept\n    of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0^-} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0^-} \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^2 + 1}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{x}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= +\\infty\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0^+} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0^+} \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^2 + 1}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{x}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\infty\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the line $x = 0$ (or the $y$-axis)\n    is a vertical asymptote of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    Since\n    $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty} f(x) = + \\infty$, the\n    graph of $y = f(x)$ has no horizontal asymptotes.\n\n    If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ showing the position of the\n    points A and B, we obtain\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/fcf5f7edc26498711f659b8034d315b54cc4c103.svg\" alt=\"fcf5f7edc26498711f659b8034d315b54cc4c103.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA526","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA526\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = kx^3$.\n\n1.  Show that the point P$(2,8k)$ lies on the curve of $f$. [1 mark]\n\nAt P, the normal to the curve is parallel to $y = \\dfrac{1}{6}x$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $k$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To show that the point P lies on the curve we substitute $x = 2$ to\n    the equation of $f$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(2)        & = k (2)^3\\\\[3pt]\n    & = 8 k\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    as required.\n\n2.  Let's find $f'(2)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)       & = 3k x^2\\\\[3pt]\n    f'(2)       & = 3k(2^2)\\\\[3pt]\n    & = 12k\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the slope of of the normal to\n    the curve at P is $m = -\\dfrac 1 {12k}$. On the other hand we know\n    that the normal is parallel to $y = \\dfrac 1 6 x$, hence\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -\\dfrac 1 {12k}         & = \\dfrac 1 6\\hspace{8mm} \\\\[10pt]\n    & \\hspace{-.5em}k = -\\dfrac 1 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA528","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA528\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $f'$, the derivative of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/5fc6d51a5be97f36e791638f80bec9eadd8df94b.svg\" alt=\"5fc6d51a5be97f36e791638f80bec9eadd8df94b.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f'$ has a local maximum at A, a local minimum at B and\npasses through P$(2,\\hspace{-0.1em}-3)$.\n\n1.  The point Q$(2,6)$ lies on the graph of the function $f$.\n    1.  Write down the gradient of the curve of $f$ at Q.\n\n    2.  Find the equation of the normal to the curve of $f$ at Q.\n        [4 marks]\n\n2.  Determine the concavity of the graph of $f$ when $2 < x < 3$ **and**\n    justify your answer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The gradient of the curve of $f$ at Q is\n          $f'(2) = -3$\n\n    2.  The slope of the normal to the curve at Q$(2,6)$ is\n        $m = \\dfrac 1 3$ (from part (i)) and the equation of the normal\n        is given by\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        y - y_1 & = m (x - x_1)\\\\[5pt]\n        y - 6       & = \\dfrac 1 3(x - 2)\\\\[5pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2mm}y = \\dfrac 1 3 x + \\dfrac {16} 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  From the diagram, we see that $f'$ is decreasing on $(2,3)$, hence\n    $f''(x) < 0$ on this interval, therefore we conclude that $f$ is\n    concave down on $(2,3)$."},{"ref":"AA532","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA532\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider $f(x),\\, g(x)$ and $h(x) = (f\\circ g)(x)$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Given that $g(5) = 8,\\, g'(5) = 2$ and $f'(8) = -4$,\nfind the gradient of the normal to the curve $y = h(x)$ at $x = 5$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh'(x)       & = \\dfrac {\\textrm d } {\\textrm d x}f(g(x))\\\\[3pt]\n& = f'(g(x))\\cdot g'(x)\\\\[8pt]\nh'(5)       & = f'(g(5)) \\cdot g'(5)\\\\[3pt]\n& = f'(8)\\cdot 2\\\\[3pt]\n& = -4\\cdot 2\\\\[3pt]\n& = -8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the gradient of the normal to the\ncurve $y = h(x)$ at $x = 5$ is\n $m = \\dfrac 1 8$"},{"ref":"AA533","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA533\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3+2x^2+kx-3$. Part of the graph of $f$ is shown in the\nfollowing diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/8a31bfc24db155e41ebd397f7655348ba4cac6ac.svg\" alt=\"8a31bfc24db155e41ebd397f7655348ba4cac6ac.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ crosses the $y$-axis at the point Q. The line $L$ is\ntangent to the graph of $f$ at Q.\n\n1.  Find the coordinates of Q. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find $f'(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the equation of $L$ in terms of $k$. [4 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ has a local maximum at the point P. The line $L$ passes\nthrough P.\n\n3.  Find the value of $k$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The point Q lies on $f(x)$ and has $x = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5cm}\n    f(0)        & = 0^3 + 2(0^2) + k(0) - 3\\\\[3pt]\n    & = -3.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence Q has coordinates\n       $(0,-3)$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n           $f'(x)  = 3x^2 + 4 x + k$\n\n    2.  By part (i) we have $f'(0) = k$. Hence the equation of $L$ is\n        $y = kx + b$. To find $b$ we substitute the coordinates of Q\n        into the equation\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        -3  & = k(0) + b\\hspace{1cm}\\\\\n        b   & = -3.\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The equation of L is\n          $y = kx -3$\n\n3.  Let's find the $x$-coordinate of P\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^3+2x^2+kx-3   & = kx - 3\\\\[3pt]\n    x^3 + 2x^2          & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n    x^2(x + 2)          & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n    x               & = 0,\\,-2.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The $x$-coordinate of P\n    is $-2$. We know that P is a local maximum, hence $f'(-2) = 0$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3(-2)^2 + 4 (-2) + k    & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n    12 - 8 + k          & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}k = -4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA536","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA536\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = 6x - \\ln x$, for $x > 0$.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $f''(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Solve $f'(x) = f''(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Differentiating $f(x)$ with respect to $x$, we get\n    $$\n    f'(x)  = 6 - \\dfrac 1 x\n    $$\n\n2.  Differentiating $f'(x)$ with respect to $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{.5cm}\n    f''(x)      & = - (-1) \\dfrac 1 {x^2}\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = \\dfrac 1 {x^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Equating the answers to part (a) and part (b), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1cm}\n    6 - \\dfrac 1 x  & = \\dfrac 1 {x^2} \\\\[8pt]\n    6 x^2 - x       & = 1\\\\[8pt]\n    6x^2 - x - 1    & = 0\\\\[8pt]\n    x           & = \\dfrac {1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4(6)(-1)}} {2(6)}\\\\[2pt]\n    & = \\dfrac 1 2,\\,-\\dfrac 1 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since\n    $x > 0$ the only solution is   $ x = \\dfrac 1 2$"},{"ref":"AA547","marks":"17","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA547\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nConsider a function $f$. The line $L_1$ with equation $y = 2x - 1$ is a\ntangent to the graph of $f$ when $x = 3$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down $f'(3)$.\n\n    2.  Find $f(3)$. [4 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = f(x^2 - 1)$ and P be the point on the graph of $g$ where\n$x = 2$.\n\n2.  Show that the graph of $g$ has a gradient of $8$ at P.\n    [5 marks]\n\nLet $L_2$ be the tangent to the graph of $g$ at P. The line $L_1$\nintersects $L_2$ at the point Q.\n\n3.  Find the $y$-coordinate of Q.[8 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The slope of the tangent line $L_1$ at $x = 3$ is $m = 2$, hence\n\n          $$\n          \\hspace{-1.1cm}f'(3) = 2\n          $$\n\n    2.  Since $L_1$ and $f$ have a common point at $x = 3$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(3)       & = 2(3) - 1\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{.275em} = 5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.9cm}\n    g'(x)       & = f'(x^2 - 1) \\cdot 2 x\\\\[6pt]\n    g'(2)       & = f'(2^2 - 1)\\cdot 2 (2)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = f'(3)\\cdot 4\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2(4)\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  First we find the $y$-coordinate of P. We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3mm}\n    g(2)    & = f(2^2 - 1)\\\\[5pt]\n    & = 5.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence P has coordinates $(2,5)$.\n\n    Next, we find the equation of $L_2$. Using the equation of the\n    tangent $y - y_1 = m (x - x_1)$, with $m = 8$, $x_1 = 2$, and\n    $y_1 = 5$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y - 5   & = 8(x - 2)\\\\[6pt]\n    y       & = 8x - 11\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's find the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point of $L_1$ and\n    $L_2$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x - 1     & = 8x - 11\\hspace{2mm}\\\\[6pt]\n    10         & = 6x\\\\[6pt]\n    x      & = \\dfrac 5 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting $x = \\dfrac 5 3$ into the equation of\n    $L_1$, we find the $y$-coordinate of Q\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1cm}\n    y       & = 2\\left(\\dfrac 5 3\\right) - 1\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {10} 3 - \\dfrac 3 3\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= \\dfrac 7 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA550","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA550\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{3x}$. The line $L$ is the tangent to the curve of $f$ at\n$(0,1)$.\n\nFind the equation of $L$ in the form $y = mx + c$.","markScheme":"First we find $m$. We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)   & = 3 e^{3x}\\hspace{.7cm}\\\\[6pt]\nm   & = f'(0)\\\\[6pt]\n& = 3e^{3(0)}\\\\[6pt]\n& = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe equation of $L$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_1     & = m(x - x_1)\\\\[6pt]\ny - 1       & = 3(x - 0)\\\\[6pt]\n& \\hspace{-1.8mm}y = 3x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA551","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA551\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe diagram shows part of the graph of $y = f'(x)$. The $x$-intercepts\nare at points $A$ and $C$. There is a minimum at point $B$ and a maximum\nat point $D$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/80f456da3204172f8b19b401e13f9469b7dc48f1.svg\" alt=\"80f456da3204172f8b19b401e13f9469b7dc48f1.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $f'(x)$ at $A$.\n\n    2.  Hence, show that $A$ corresponds to a maximum on the graph of\n        $f$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Which of the points $A,B,C,D$ corresponds to a minimum on the graph\n    of $f$.[1 mark]\n\n3.  Which of the points $A,B,C,D$ corresponds to a point of inflection\n    of $f$. Justify your answer. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  $f'(x) = 0$\n\n    2.  Since $f'(x)$ is positive for $x < A$ and negative for $x > A$,\n        we conclude that $f(A)$ is a maximum.\n\n2.  $\\mbox{Point C}$\n\n3.  The function $f$ has an inflection point at $x$, if $f''(x) = 0$.\n    The second derivative is zero at points of local extrema of $f'$.\n\n    From the diagram $f'$ has its local extrema at\n      $\\mbox{B and D}$"},{"ref":"AA555","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA555\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram show part of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/dbdc7b0d9715bcdcbe90ca12f9685be23c9b6d26.svg\" alt=\"dbdc7b0d9715bcdcbe90ca12f9685be23c9b6d26.svg\">\n\nThe graph has a local maximum at A, where $x = -2$, and a local minimum\nat B, where $x = 8$.\n\n1.  On the graph above, sketch the graph of $y = f'(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Write down in order from least to greatest: $f(-2)$, $f'(8)$,\n    $f''(-2)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Since $f$ has extrema at $x = -2$ and $x = 8$, that means $f'$ has\n    $x$-intercepts at those points, and the vertex in between, which is\n    at $x = 3$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/9941fb47747408bde670b0c57078a69678df2459.svg\" alt=\"9941fb47747408bde670b0c57078a69678df2459.svg\">\n\n2.  We see in the diagram that $f(-2) > 0$, $f'(8) = 0$, and $f$ is\n    concave down around $x = -2$, which means $f''(-2) <0$. Finally\n\n    $$\n    f''(-2),\\ f'(8),\\ f(-2)\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA556","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA556\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider $f(x) = x^3 - \\dfrac p x$, $x \\neq 0$, where $p$ is a constant.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  There is a minimum value of $f(x)$ when $x = 1$. Find the value of\n    $p$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    f'(x)  = 3x^2 + \\dfrac p {x^2}\\hspace{.8cm}\n    $$\n\n2.  A minimum occurs when $f'(x) = 0$. Using $f'(1) = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0   & = 3(1^2) + \\dfrac p {1^2}\\\\[6pt]\n    0   & = 3 + p\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{-1.5mm}p = -3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA559","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA559\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = 0.5 x^3 - 3.2 x$. There is a local maximum at point A and a\nlocal minimum at point B.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find the coordinates of point A.\n\n    2.  Find the coordinates of point B.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of the point of inflection of $f$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the values of $x$ for which the graph of $f$ has a\n    negative rate of change. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Graphing $f$, the coordinates of the local maximum are\n\n        $\\text{A}(-1.46,3.12)$\n    2.  Graphing $f$, the coordinates of the local minimum are\n\n        $\\text{B}(1.46,-3.12)$\n\n2.  A point of inflection occurs when $f''(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)   & = 1.5 x^2 -3.2\\\\[5pt]\n    f''(x)  & = 3 x\\\\[5pt]\n    0       & = 3x\\\\[5pt]\n    x       & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n    f(0)        & = 0.5(0^3) - 3.2(0)\\\\[5pt]\n    & = 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the point of\n    inflection are   $(0,0)$\n\n3.  $f$ has a negative rate of change, when $f'(x) < 0$. It happens\n    between the\n\n    maximum and minimum   $-1.46 < x < 1.46$"},{"ref":"AA563","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA563\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f'(x) = x^3 - 9 x^2 + 24 x + 3$.\n\n1.  There are two points of inflection on the graph of $f$. Write down\n    the $x$-coordinates of these points. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Let $h(x) = f''(x)$. Explain why the graph of $h$ has no points of\n    inflection. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  A point of inflection occurs when $f''(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f''(x)  & = 3x^2 - 18 x + 24\\\\[6pt]\n    0       & = 3x^2 - 18 x + 24\\\\[6pt]\n    0       & = x^2 - 6 x + 8\\\\[6pt]\n    0       & = (x-2)(x-4)\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{-2mm}  x = 2,\\, 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  From (a) we get $h(x) = 3x^2 - 18 x + 24$. Let's find $h''(x)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h'(x)   & = 6 x - 18\\hspace{1.1cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    h''(x)  & = 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since $6 \\neq 0$ we conclude that\n    $h(x)$ has no points of inflection."},{"ref":"AA571","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA571\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = p x^3 - qx$. At $x = 0$, the gradient of the curve of $f$ is\n$2$.\n\nGiven that\n$f^{-1} (12) = 2$, find the value of $p$ and $q$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)       & = 3 p x^2 - q\\\\[8pt]\n2       & = 3 p (0)^2 - q \\hspace{.8cm}\\\\[12pt]\n&\\hspace{-1.5mm}q = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\\\nSince $f^{-1}(12) = 2$, it follows $f(2) = 12$. Substituting $f(2) =12$,\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12  & = p(2^3) - 2(-2)\\\\[5pt]\n12  & = 8 p +4\\\\[5pt]\n8   & = 8p\\\\[6pt]\n& \\hspace{-1.5mm}p = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA572","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA572\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = \\ln(2x - 1)$. Let point A be the point on\nthe curve where $x = 3$.\n\n1.  Write down the gradient of the curve at A. [2 marks]\n\n2.  The normal to the curve at A cuts the $x$-axis at P. find the\n    coordinates of P. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $$\n    f'(3) = 0.4 \\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    $$\n\n2.  First, we find the equation of the normal. Using the equation of the\n    normal $y - y_1 = m_n(x - x_1)$ with $m_n = -\\dfrac 1 {0.4}$,\n    $x_1 = 3$, and $y_1 = \\ln(5)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y - \\ln(5)  & = - \\dfrac 1 {0.4} (x - 3)\\\\[6pt]\n    y       & = - 2.5(x - 3) + \\ln(5)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting\n    $y = 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0   & = - 2.5(x - 3) + \\ln(5)\\\\[5pt]\n    x   & = 3.64\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The\n    coordinates of P are   $(3.64,0)$"},{"ref":"AA574","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA574\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe values of the functions $f$ and $g$ and their derivatives for\n$x = 3$ and $x = 7$ are shown in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/c8404d702928a0dd31b44066b9bd662f1194c9ac.svg\" alt=\"c8404d702928a0dd31b44066b9bd662f1194c9ac.svg\">\n\nLet $h(x) = f(x) g(x)$.\n\n1.  Find $h(3)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the normal to $h$ when $x = 7$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(3)        & = f(3) g(3)\\\\[5pt]\n    & = 3 (-2)\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = -6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  First, we find the gradient of the tangent to $h$ at $x = 7$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2cm}\n    m_T         & = h'(7)\\\\[5pt]\n    & = f(7)g'(7) + f'(7) g(7)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 5(3) + 1(6)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 21\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, the gradient of the normal to\n    $h$ at $x = 7$ is $m_n = -\\dfrac 1 {21}$.\n\n    The equation of the normal is given by $y - y_1 = m_n(x - x_1)$.\n    Here $x_1 = 7$, hence we find $y_1$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y_1 & = h(7)\\hspace{2mm}\\\\[5pt]\n    & = 5(6)\\\\[5pt]\n    & = 30\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1cm}\n    y - 30  & = -\\dfrac 1 {21} (x - 7)\\\\[6pt]\n    y       & = -\\dfrac 1 {21} x + \\dfrac 1 {3} + 30\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.5mm}  y  = -\\dfrac 1 {21} x + \\dfrac {91} {3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA576","marks":"14","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA576\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac {5x} {e^{3x}}$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 10$.\n\n1.  Sketch the graph of $f$. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/bcb38cd407da88a3ed46eea88b881a18ac54f09f.svg\" alt=\"bcb38cd407da88a3ed46eea88b881a18ac54f09f.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Write down the $x$-coordinate of the maximum point on the graph\n        of $f$.\n\n    2.  Write down the interval where $f$ is increasing. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find $f'(x)$ using the quotient rule. [4 marks]\n\n4.  Find the interval where the rate of change of $f$ is increasing.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The graph of $f$ is\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/6d4afc798ed69cbfb4b452835400774dc507b8ac.svg\" alt=\"6d4afc798ed69cbfb4b452835400774dc507b8ac.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  $x = 0.333$\n\n    2.  $0 \\leq x < 0.333$\n\n3.  Using the quotient rule\n    $\\left(\\dfrac u v\\right)' = \\dfrac {v u ' - u v'} {v^2}$ with\n    $u = 5x$ and $v = e^{3x}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    f'(x)   = \\dfrac {5 e^{3x} - 15x e^{3x}} {e^{6x}}\n    $$\n\n4.  From part (c) by using G.D.C. we get\n      $0.667 < x \\leq 10$"},{"ref":"AA581","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA581\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 e^x$ and $g(x) = 4x - x^2$.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $x$-coordinate where the tangents of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are\n    parallel. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the product rule $uv = u'v + uv'$ with $u = x^2$ and\n    $v = e^x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)       & = 2 x e^x + x^2 e^x\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}  = x e^x (x + 2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The tangents are parallel when the gradients are the same.\n    First, let's find $g'(x)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g'(x)   & = 4 - 2x\\hspace{.8cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7cm}\n    f'(x)           & = g'(x)\\\\[6pt]\n    x e^x (x + 2)   & = 4 - 2x\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{-2mm} x = 0.596 \\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA586","marks":"15","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA586\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 - 9x + k$. Part of the graph of $f$ is shown\nbelow. The graph of $f$ has a local maximum at A, a local minimum at B\nand a point of inflexion at C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/82b794a48ab65bd95cc0c0e1dcc2dc5ac6c03744.svg\" alt=\"82b794a48ab65bd95cc0c0e1dcc2dc5ac6c03744.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Find $f'(x)$.\n\n    2.  Find $f''(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflexion at C. [2 marks]\n\nGiven that $f(-1) = 14$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Find $f(0)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, find the coordinates of the local maximum A$(x,y)$ and\n        justify your answer. [7 marks]\n\n4.  Given that the $x-$coordinate of $\\text{B}$ is $x_\\text{B}$ write down in order from least to greatest $f''(x_{\\text{B}})$,\n    $f'( x_{\\text{B}})$, $f(x_{\\text{B}})$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $$\n        f'(x) = 3x^2 + 6x - 9\\hspace{1cm}\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $$\n        f''(x) = 6x + 6\\hspace{2cm}\n        $$\n\n2.  A point of inflection occurs when $f''(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    6x + 6  & = 0\\hspace{2.2cm}\\\\[5pt]\n    & \\hspace{-1.9mm}x = -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.  Substituting $f(-1) = 14$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.8cm}\n        14  & = (-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 - 9(-1) + k\\\\[6pt]\n        14  & = -1 + 3 + 9 +k\\\\[6pt]\n        k   & = 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{.6cm}\n        f(0)    & = (0)^3 + 3(0)^2 - 9(0) + 3\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-6.4mm}f(0)  = 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  A local maximum occurs when $f'(x) = 0$, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        3x^2 + 6x - 9   & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n        x^2 + 2x - 3    & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n        (x + 3)(x - 1)  & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n        x           & = -3,\\,1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Substituting $x = -3$ into $f''(x)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(-3)     & = 6(-3) + 6\\\\[5pt]\n        & = -12\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Since $f''(-3) < 0$, we conclude\n        that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x= -3$. Then\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(-3)   & = (-3)^3 + 3(-3)^2 - 9(-3) + 3\\\\[5pt]\n        & = 30\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Finally\n          $\\mbox A(-3,30)$\n\n4.  We see from the diagram that $f''( x_{\\text{B}}) > 0$, $f'( x_{\\text{B}}) = 0$,\n    and $f(x_{\\text{B}}) < 0$, hence\\\n    $f(x_{\\text{B}}), \\ f'(x_{\\text{B}}),\\ f''(x_{\\text{B}})$"},{"ref":"AA587","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA587\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sqrt{2x + 1}$ for $x \\geq -0.5$.\n\n1.  Find\n    1.  $f(12)$;\n\n    2.  $f'(12)$ [4 marks]\n\nConsider another function $g(x)$. Let P be a point on the graph $g$. The\n$x$-coordinate of P is $12$. The equation of the tangent to the graph at\nP is $y = x + 3$.\n\n2.  Write down $g'(12)$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $g(12)$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Let $h(x) = f(x) \\times g(x)$. Find the equation of the\n    tangent of $h$ at the\\\n    point where $x = 12$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Substituting $x=12$ into $f(x)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(12)   & = \\sqrt{2(12) + 1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{.275em} = 5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $f'(x)$ at the point $x=12$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{.7cm}\n        f'(x)       & = \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{2x + 1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        f'(12)      & = \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt{2(12) + 1}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{.275em} = \\dfrac 1 5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    $g'(12) = 1$\n\n3.  Substituting $x=12$ into $g(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(12)   & = 12 + 3\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 15\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8cm}\n    h(12)   & = f(12) \\times g(12)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 5 ( 15) = 75\\\\[6pt]\n    h'(12)  & = f(12) \\times g'(12) + g(12)\\times f'(12)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 5 (1) + 15\\left( \\dfrac 1 5\\right) = 8\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the equation of the tangent $y - y_1 = m (x - x_1)$ with\n    $m= 8$, $x_1 = 12$, and $y_1 = 75$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5mm}\n    y - 75  & = 8 (x - 12)\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.7mm}y = 8x - 21\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA588","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA588\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider $f(x) = x\\ln(x) - x$, for $x>0$\n\n1.  Find $f(1)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Solve $f(x) = 0$. [3 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ has a local minimum at point P$(x,y)$.\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of point P and explain why it is a\n    local minimum. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Find the set of $x$ values for which $f$ is increasing.\n    [2 marks]\n\n5.  Hence, sketch the graph of $f$, for $x>0$.[3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/bcb38cd407da88a3ed46eea88b881a18ac54f09f.svg\" alt=\"bcb38cd407da88a3ed46eea88b881a18ac54f09f.svg\">","markScheme":"1.  Substituting $x = 1$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4cm}\n    f(1)        & = 1 \\ln (1) - 1\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x\\ln x - x      & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n    x(\\ln x - 1)    & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n    \\ln x - 1       & = 0 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\ln x           & = 1\\\\[5pt]\n    & \\hspace{-1.8mm}x  = e\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  A local minimum occurs when $f'(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)       & = x\\,\\dfrac 1 x + \\ln x - 1\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\ln x\\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln x       & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    x       & = e^0\\\\[6pt]\n    x       & = 1\\\\[6pt]\n    f(1)        & = -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n      $\\mbox P(1,-1)$\n\n    To show that $x = 1$ is a local minimum we need to check the sign of\n    the second derivative of $f$ at this point. We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f''(x)  & = \\dfrac 1 x \\\\[6pt]\n    f''(1)      & = 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since $f''(1) > 0$, we conclude\n    that P is a local minimum.\n\n4.  $f$ is increasing when $f'(x) > 0$. We know that $f'(x) = 0$ at\n    $x = 1$. Hence, we\n\n    find that $f'(x) > 0$ for   $x > 1$\n\n5.  The graph of $f$ can be seen\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/bccbedd0f163e5c2dcc1c4256e83b215caff0dfb.svg\" alt=\"bccbedd0f163e5c2dcc1c4256e83b215caff0dfb.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA593","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA593\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac {2x + 1} {x - 3}$, $x \\in \\mathbb R$, $x \\neq 3$\n\n1.\n    1.  Find the equation of the vertical and horizontal asymptote of\n        $f$.\n\n    2.  Write down the coordinates of the point Q at which the\n        asymptotes intersects.\n\n    3.  Find the $x$ and $y$ intercepts of $f$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $f'(x) = -\\dfrac 7 {(x-3)^2}$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence, find the equation of the tangent at point\n    P$(4,y)$.[3 marks]\n\n4.  The point S also lies on the graph of $f$. The tangent to S is\n    parallel to the\\\n    tangent at point P. Find the coordinates of S. [4 marks]\n\n5.  Show that Q is the midpoint of \\[PS\\]. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Vertical asymptote:   $ x = 3$\\\n        Horizontal asymptote:   $ y = 2$\n\n    2.    $\\text{Q}(3,2)$\n    3.  To find the $y$-intercept of $f$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        y   & = f(0)\\\\[8pt]\n        y   & = \\dfrac {2(0) + 1} {0 - 3}\\hspace{1cm}\\\\[10pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2mm}y = -\\dfrac 1 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        To find the $x$-intercepts of $f$, we solve $f(x)=0$\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac {2x + 1} {x - 3}     & = 0\\hspace{3cm}\\\\[8pt]\n        2x + 1              & = 0\\\\[10pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2mm}x = -\\dfrac 1 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the quotient rule, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{.9cm}\n    f'(x)       & = \\dfrac {2(x - 3) - (2x + 1)} {(x - 3)^2}\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = -\\dfrac 7 {(x - 3)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  First we find the $y$ coordinate of P$(4,y)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3mm}\n    y   & = \\dfrac {2(4) + 1} {4 - 3}\\\\[5pt]\n    y   & = 9\\\\[1pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The gradient of the tangent of $f$\n    at P$(4,9)$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4mm}\n    m   & = f'(4)\\\\[5pt]\n    & = -\\dfrac 7 {(4 - 3)^2}\\\\[5pt]\n    & = -7\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the equation of the tangent\n    $y - y_1 = m (x - x_1)$ with $m = -7$, $x_1 = 4$, and $y_1 = 9$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y - 9   & = -7 (x - 4)\\\\[9pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2mm} y = -7x + 37\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Parallel lines have the same gradient. Hence, the gradient of the\n    tangent at\\\n    both P and S is $m = -7$.\\\n    Hence, solving the equation $f'(x) = -7$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -\\dfrac 7 {(x - 3)^2}   & = -7\\\\[6pt]\n    (x - 3)^2           & = 1\\\\[6pt]\n    x - 3           & = \\pm 1\\\\[6pt]\n    x               & = 2\\\\[6pt]\n    f (2)               & = \\dfrac {2(2) + 1}{2 - 3}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = -5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n      $\\mbox S(2,-5)$\n\n5.  A midpoint has the coordinates\n    M$\\left(\\dfrac {x_1 + x_2} 2, \\dfrac {y_1 + y_2} 2\\right)$.\\\n    Substituting the coordinates of P and S into M, we get\n    M$\\left(\\dfrac {4 + 2} 2, \\dfrac {9 + (-5)} 2\\right)$.\\\n    Therefore, we find M$(3,2)$ is the midpoint of \\[PS\\].\\\n    $\\mbox{Hence, Q\\((3,2)\\) is the midpoint of [PS]}$"},{"ref":"AA599","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA599\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 3 x^3 + 2x^2 - 5x + 10$.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ has horizontal tangents at the points where $x = a$ and\n$x = b$, $a <b$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.[3 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Sketch the graph of $y = f'(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence explain why the graph of $f$ has a local maximum point at\n        $x = a$.[2 marks]\n\n4.\n    1.  Find $f''(b)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, use your answer to part (d) (i) to show that the graph of\n        $f$\\\n        has a local minimum point at $x = b$. [4 marks]\n\nThe tangent to the graph of $f$ at $x = a$ and the normal to the graph\nof $f$ at $x = b$ intersect at the point $(p,q)$.\n\n5.  Find the value of $p$ and the value of $q$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $f'(x) = x^2 + 4x - 5$\n\n2.  Horizontal tangents occur when $f'(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 + 4x - 5    & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    (x - 1)(x+5)    & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    x           & = -5,\\,1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n      $a = -5,\\ b = 1$\n\n3.\n    1.  The sketch of $f'$ is\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/1841a9522e22450b6ba89a4cef01640f127977b9.svg\" alt=\"1841a9522e22450b6ba89a4cef01640f127977b9.svg\">\n\n    2.  The first derivative changes from positive to negative at\n        $x = a$ so the local maximum is at $x = a$.\n\n4.\n    1.  The second derivative of $f$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(x)  & = 2 x +4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Substituting $b = 1$ into\n        $f''$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(1)  & = 2(1) + 4\\\\[5pt]\n        &\\hspace{.275em} = 6\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  $f''(b)$ is positive, therefore the function is concave up at\n        $x = b$ and, by the second derivative test, we conclude that $f$\n        has a local minimum at $x = b$.\n\n5.  The tangent to $f$ at $x = a$ is the horizontal line $y = f(a)$. We\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(a)    & = \\dfrac 1 3(-5)^3 + 2(-5)^2 - 5(-5) + 10\\\\[5pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {130}3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The normal to $f$ at $x = b$ is the vertical line $x = 1$. Hence the\n    intersection is at\n    $$\n    \\left(1,\\dfrac{130} 3\\right).\n    $$\n\n    It follows that\n      $p = 1,\\ \\ q = \\dfrac {130}3$"},{"ref":"AA772","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA772\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x)=2x^2-5x+7$. The line $L$ intersects $f$ at P$(3,10)$ and is\nperpendicular to the tangent to the curve of $f$ at P. Find the equation\nof $L$ in the form $y = mx + c$.","markScheme":"First we find the slope ($m$) of the tangent to $f$ at P. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)   & = 4x-5\\\\[12pt]\nf'(3)   & = 4(3)-5\\\\[12pt]\nm   & = 7\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope ($m_{\\perp}$) of $L$, perpendicular\nto the tangent is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_\\perp     & = -\\dfrac{1}{m}\\hspace{.7cm}\\\\[12pt]\n& = -\\dfrac{1}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe find the equation of $L$ that\npasses through P$(3,10)$ with slope $m_{\\perp}=-\\dfrac{1}{7}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_1     & = m_{\\perp}(x - x_1)\\\\[12pt]\ny - 10      & = -\\dfrac{1}{7}(x - 3)\\\\[12pt]\n& \\hspace{-1.8mm}y = -\\dfrac{1}{7}x +\\dfrac{73}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA774","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA774\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nNote: In this question, distance is in metres and time is in seconds.\n\nA tennis ball is thrown in the air. Its height $h$ above the ground\nafter time $t$ is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\\\\nh(t)  = -5t^2+20t+4, \\textrm { for  } 0 \\leq t \\leq 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Find $h'(t)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the maximum height attained by the ball. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-2em}h'(t)     = -10t+20\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The maximum height occurs when $h'(t) = 0$. Therefore, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -10t + 20 & = 0\\\\[12pt]\n    t &= 2\\\\\n    % t & \\hspace{-1.5mm}p = -3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The maximum\n    height occurs at $t=2$. Therefore, evaluating $h(2)$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(2)    & = -5(2)^2 + 20(2)+4\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{1mm}=24 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA782","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA782\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{g(x)}{h(x)}+15$, where $g(5) = 30$, $h(5) = 10$,\n$g'(5) = 20$ and $h'(5) = 5$.\n\nFind the equation of the normal to the graph of $f$ at $x = 5$.","markScheme":"First we find the $y$-coordinate of the graph of $f$ at $x=5$, as\nfollows\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny   & =  f(5) \\\\[12pt]\n& =  \\dfrac{g(5)}{h(5)}+15 \\\\[12pt]\n& =  \\dfrac{30}{10}+15  \\\\[12pt]\n& =  18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand the gradient of the tangent line of the\ngraph at $x=5$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(5) & =  \\dfrac{h(5)g'(5) - g(5)h'(5)}{[h(5)]^2}+0 \\\\[12pt]\n& = \\dfrac{(10)(20)-(30)(5)}{(10)^2} \\\\[12pt]\n% & =  \\dfrac{200-150}{100} \\\\[7pt]\n& =\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, the slope of the normal to the\ncurve at $(5,18)$ is $m = -2$ and the normal line is given by the\nequation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n%y - y_1    & =  m(x - x_1) \\hspace{4mm}\\\\[8pt]\ny - 18  & =   -2(x - 5) \\\\[13pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.5em}y   =  -2x +28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA784","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA784\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $f'$, the derivative of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/43a326bcac6e745530fb72b906e2afd3683a2a60.svg\" alt=\"43a326bcac6e745530fb72b906e2afd3683a2a60.svg\">\n\nThe points M$(-\\sqrt{2},-60)$ and N$(\\sqrt{2},-60)$ lie on the graph of\n$f'$.\n\nThe point P$(\\sqrt{2},40)$ lies on the graph of the function $f$.\n\n1.  Write down the gradient of the curve of $f$ at P. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the tangent to the curve of $f$ at P.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The gradient of the curve of $f$ at P is\n    $f'(\\sqrt{2}) = -60$\n\n2.  Using the point-gradient formula with $\\text{P}(\\sqrt{2},40)$ and\n    $m = -60$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    %y - y_1    &=  m(x - x_1) \\hspace{8em}\\\\[12pt]\n    y - 40 &= -60(x - \\sqrt{2}) \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}y=-60x+60\\sqrt{2}+40\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA796","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA796\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=e^{2x-4} - x$ at the\npoint where $x=2$.","markScheme":"Differentiating $y=e^{2x-4} - x$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &=  2 e^{2x-4} - 1 \\hspace{0.5em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the gradient of the tangent to the curve at the point where\n$x = 2$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= 2e^{2(2)-4} - 1 \\\\[12pt]\n%      &= 2e^0 - 1 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2(1) - 1 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 1 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe $y$-coordinate of the point at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= e^{2(2)-4} - 2 \\\\[12pt]\n&= e^0 - 2 \\\\[12pt]\n&= -1 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the point-gradient formula with\n$\\text{P}(2,-1)$ and $m = 1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - (-1) &= 1(x - 2) \\\\[12pt]\ny + 1 &= x -2 \\\\[12pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.525em}y = x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA805","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA805\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{4x^2+px}{x+1}$, where $x\\neq -1$ and $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nFind the set of values of $p$ for which the graph of $f$ has exactly two stationary points.","markScheme":"Using the **quotient rule (5.6)**, to differentiate $f(x)$, $y = \\dfrac{u}{v} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{v\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} - u\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}{v^2}$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{(x+1)(8x+p) - (4x^2+px)(1)}{(x+1)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n      &= \\dfrac{8x^2 + px + 8x + p - 4x^2 - px}{(x+1)^2} \\\\[12pt]\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{4x^2 + 8x + p}{(x+1)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor $f$ to have exactly two stationary points, the quadratic $4x^2 + 8x + p = 0$ must have two distinct real roots, and neither root can equal $-1$ (which is excluded from the domain).\n\nApplying the **discriminant (2.7)**, with the condition $\\Delta > 0$ for two distinct real roots, $\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8^2 - 4(4)(p) &> 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n64 - 16p &> 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving the inequality gives $p < 4$.\n\nChecking whether $x = -1$ can be a root of $4x^2 + 8x + p = 0$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(-1)^2 + 8(-1) + p &= p - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $p < 4$ gives $p - 4 < 0$, the value $x = -1$ is not a root of the quadratic, so both stationary points lie in the domain.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence $\\boxed{p < 4}$.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA821","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA821\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$. The tangent to\nthe graph of $f$ at $x=3$ has equation $y=4x-2$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $f'(3)$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Find $f(3)$.[1 mark]\n\nThe function $g$ is defined for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ where\n$g(x)=11-2x^2$ and $h(x)=f(g(x))$.\n\n3.  Find $h(2)$.[2 marks]\n\n4.  Hence, find the equation of the tangent to the graph of $h$\n    at $x=2$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  As the gradient of the tangent line is $4$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-2.15em}f'(3) = 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  As the line passes through $(3,f(3))$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(3) &= 4(3)-2 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.82em}f(3) = 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(2) &= f(g(2)) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(11-2(2)^2) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(3) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The slope of the tangent line to $h$ at $x=2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h'(2) &= f'(g(2))\\times g'(2) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f'(3)\\times-4(2)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=(4)(-8)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=-32\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the point-gradient formula with $\\text{P}\\left(2, 10 \\right)$\n    and $m = -32$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y-10 &= -32(x-2) \\\\[12pt]\n    y-10 &= -32x+64 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}y=-32x+74\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA863","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA863\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $f$ defined by $f(x) = \\ln \\left( 4x^2 - 9\\right)$\nfor $x > \\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\nThe following diagram shows part of the graph of $f$ which crosses the\n$x$-axis at point A, with coordinates $(a,0)$. The line $L$ is the\ntangent to the graph of $f$ at the point B.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Differentiation%20Fundamentals/images/6a470451e3d20e2264b1afd77591f138e5b01904.svg\" alt=\"6a470451e3d20e2264b1afd77591f138e5b01904.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the exact value of $a$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Given that the gradient of $L$ is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$, find the\n    $x$-coordinate of B. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $f(a) = 0$ for $a$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln \\left(4a^2 - 9\\right) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    4a^2 - 9 &= 1 \\\\[5pt]\n    4a^2 &= 10 \\\\[5pt]\n    a^2 &= \\dfrac{5}{2} \\\\[5pt]\n    a &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{2}} \\hspace{3em}[a>0]\\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em}a = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{2} \\hspace{5.25em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Differentiating $f(x) = \\ln \\left(4x^2 - 9\\right)$, we get\n    $f'(x) = \\dfrac{8x}{4x^2 - 9}$\n\n    If we solve $f'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ for $x$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{8x}{4x^2 - 9} &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    4x^2 - 9 &= 16x \\\\[6pt]\n    4x^2 - 16x - 9 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{6.75em} (2x + 1)(2x - 9) &= 0 \\\\[2pt]\n    2x - 9 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} \\left[\\text{since } 2x + 1 \\neq 0 \\text{ for } x > \\dfrac 3 2 \\right]\\\\[4pt]\n    x &= \\dfrac{9}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the $x$-coordinate of B is\n     $x = \\dfrac{9}{2}$"},{"ref":"AA898","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA898\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nConsider the function defined by $f(x) = (2x-6)\\ln(x+3)+x$ for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x>p$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for $f'(x)$. [3 marks]\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ has no points of\ninflexion.\n\n3.  Determine if the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down or\n    concave up over its domain. [3 marks]\n\nThe function $g$ is defined by $g(x)=3\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x+3}\\right)+x$,\nfor $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x>-3$.\n\n4.  Find an expression for $g'(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n5.  Find the horizontal and vertical asymptotes of\n    $g'(x)$.[2 marks]\n\n6.  Find the exact value of the minimum of $y=g(x)$.[3 marks]\n\n7.  Solve $f(x)<g(x)$ for $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x>-3$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Due to the natural log term, $x+3>0$. Therefore\n    $ p=-3$\n\n2.  Differentiating $f$ with respect to $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[(2x-6)\\ln(x+3)+x\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n    & =\\dfrac{2x-6}{x+3}+2\\ln(x+3)+1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Differentiating $f'$ with respect to $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[\\dfrac{2x-6}{x+3}+2\\ln(x+3)+1\\right] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(x+3)(2)-(2x-6)}{\\left(x+3\\right)^2} + \\dfrac{2}{x+3} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2x+18}{(x+3)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, over $x>-3$, we have $f''(x)>0$. Therefore,\n    $f \\text{ is concave up}$\n\n4.  Differentiating $g$ with respect to $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[3\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x+3}\\right)+x\\right] \\\\[12pt]\n    &=3(x+3)\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{(x+3)^2}\\right)+1  \\\\[8pt]\n    & =\\dfrac{-3}{x+3}+1 \\\\[10pt]\n    & =\\dfrac{x}{x+3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  The denominator of $g'$ is zero when $x=-3$.\\\n    Hence,\n    $\\text{the line \\(x=-3\\) is a vertical asymptote of the graph of \\(y=g'(x)\\)}$\\\n    If we take the limit as $x$ tends to $\\infty$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} g'(x) &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac{x}{x+3}%\\cdot \\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{x}}{\\dfrac{1}{x}}\n    \\\\[8pt]\n    & = \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{1+\\dfrac{3}{x}\\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{7.2mm}} \\\\[6pt]\n    & = 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    $\\text{the line \\(y=1\\) is a horizontal asymptote of the graph of \\(y=g'(x)\\)}$\n\n6.  From part (d) we obtain a stationary point at $x=0$.\n    The second derivative of $g$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\dfrac{x}{x+3}\\right] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{(x+3)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, $g''(x)>0$ which implies that at $x=0$ the function $g$\n    reaches its minimum. Therefore, the minimum value of $g$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(0) &= 3\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)+0 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -3\\ln(3)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n7.  Using logarithm properties, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &< g(x) \\\\[12pt]\n    (2x-6)\\ln(x+3) + x &< 3\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x+3}\\right) + x \\\\[12pt]\n    (2x-6)\\ln(x+3) &< -3\\ln(x+3) \\\\[15pt]\n    (2x-3)\\ln(x+3) &< 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Thus, we have two options\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left[2x-3<0\\enskip\\&\\enskip \\ln(x+3)>0\\right] &\\text{  or  } \\left[2x-3>0 \\enskip\\&\\enskip \\ln(x+3)<0\\right] \\\\[12pt]\n    \\left[x<1.5\\enskip\\&\\enskip\\ln(x+3)>0\\right]&\\text{ or } \\left[x>1.5\\enskip\\&\\enskip \\ln(x+3)<0\\right] \\\\[12pt]\n    x < 1.5\\enskip & \\&\\enskip 1<x+3 \\hspace{2em}[\\text{since }\\ln(x+3)>0\\text{ for }x>1.5]\\\\[8pt]\n    -2 <& \\hspace{0.1em}x < 1.5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore,\n    $f(x)<g(x)$ over $-2 <  x < 1.5$"},{"ref":"AA921","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA921\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the curve $y=(kx-1)\\ln(2x)$ where $k\\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $x>0$. \n\nThe tangent to the curve at $x=2$ is perpendicular to the line $y=\\dfrac{2}{5+4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4}x$. \n\nFind the value of $k$.","markScheme":"First we find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ using the product rule.\n\nLet $u=kx-1$ and $v=\\mathrm{ln}(2x)$ so that $y=uv$. Therefore $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} = k$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}$.\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= u\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} + v\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\[10pt]\n\n&= (kx-1)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x} + \\mathrm{ln}(2x)\\cdot k \\\\[10pt]\n\n&= k - \\dfrac{1}{x} + k\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{ln}(2x)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThus, the derivative at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg\\lvert_{x=2} &= k-\\dfrac{1}{2}+k\\ln 4\\\\[-13pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThe line $y=\\dfrac{2}{5+4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4}x$ has gradient $m=\\dfrac{2}{5+4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4}$. \n\nGiven that the tangent to the curve at $x=2$ is perpendicular to this line, the tangent must have a gradient of $m=-\\dfrac{5+4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4}{2}$. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg\\lvert_{x=2} &= -\\dfrac{5+4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n\nk-\\dfrac{1}{2}+k\\ln 4 &= \\dfrac{-5-4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n2k-1+2k\\ln 4 &= -5-4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4 \\\\[10pt]\n\n2k+2k\\ln 4 &= -4-4\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4 \\\\[10pt]\n\nk(2+2\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4) &= -2(2+2\\hspace{0.15em}\\ln 4) \\\\[10pt]\n\n& \\hspace{-0.528em}\\boxed{k = -2}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"}]},{"name":"Implicit Differentiation","questions":[{"ref":"AA1259","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1259\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nConsider the curve $y^2 - xy = x^3$, where $x \\neq 0$ and $y \\neq 0$.\n\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of the point on the curve where the tangent is parallel to the $x$-axis.[3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of the point on the curve where the tangent is parallel to the $y$-axis.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Differentiating both sides implicitly with respect to $x$ and applying the **product rule (5.6)**, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(uv) = u\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} + v\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, to the term $xy$ with\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        u &= x, & \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        v &= y, & \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - \\left(x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + y\\right) &= 3x^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Collecting the derivative terms on one side, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (2y - x)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 3x^2 + y \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Making the derivative the subject, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\boxed{\\dfrac{3x^2 + y}{2y - x}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The tangent is parallel to the $x$-axis where $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0$, so we let the numerator $3x^2 + y = 0$,  giving $y = -3x^2$. Substituting into the equation of the curve, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (-3x^2)^2 - x(-3x^2) &= x^3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n        9x^4 + 3x^3 &= x^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Rearranging, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        9x^4 + 2x^3 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x^3(9x + 2) &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since $x \\neq 0$, we have $x = -\\dfrac{2}{9}$. Substituting into $y = -3x^2$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        y &= -3\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{9}\\right)^2 \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -3 \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{81} \\\\[10pt]\n         y &= -\\dfrac{4}{27}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    so the point is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\boxed{\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{9},\\,-\\dfrac{4}{27}\\right)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The tangent is parallel to the $y$-axis where $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ is undefined, so we let the denominator $2y - x = 0$, giving $y = \\dfrac{x}{2}$. Substituting into the equation of the curve, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2 - x\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) &= x^3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[10pt]\n        \\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{x^2}{2} &= x^3 \\\\[10pt]\n        -\\dfrac{x^2}{4} &= x^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Rearranging, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x^3 + \\dfrac{x^2}{4} &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n        x^2\\left(x + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since $x \\neq 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting into $y = \\dfrac{x}{2}$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        y &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{1}{8}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    so the point is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\boxed{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,-\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1345","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1345\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe curve $C$ has equation $2x^2-xy+y^2+ax=3$. Where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\nThis is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Implicit%20Differentiation/images/AA1345-q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1345-q\">\n\n1.    Show that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{y-4x-a}{2y-x}$ [3 marks]\n\nThe domain of $C$ is $p \\leq x \\leq q$ where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nGiven that $q-p=\\dfrac{20}{7}$\n\n2.    find the value of $a$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. This is an implicitly defined relation.\n\n    Hence, to differentiate it, we must use implicit differentiation.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall, that if $y=f(x)$ then due to the chain rule, when we differentiate a function of $y$ we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}[f(y)]&=f'(y)\\times\\frac {\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using this, we differentiate the relation term by term, to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(2x^2)-\\textcolor{red}{\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(xy)}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(y^2)+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(ax)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(3)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note we will need to use the product rule for the term in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4x-\\left(y+x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right)+2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+a&=0\\\\[12pt]\n    4x-y-x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+a&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next we collect the terms involving $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ to one side and factorise\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=y-4x-a\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}(2y-x)&=y-4x-a\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.7em}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{y-4x-a}{2y-x}\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. The first important thing to note about curve $C$ is that the boundaries of its domain occur when $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ is undefined.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(R1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Implicit%20Differentiation/images/AA1345-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1345-a1\">\n\n    Here, $p$ and $q$ represent the lower and upper boundary respectively.\n    Using the answer from part (a)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{y-4x-a}{2y-x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    we can see that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ is undefined when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2y-x&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    y&=\\frac{1}{2}x\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To find the $x-$coordinates of the domain boundary we must substitute $ y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ into the equation for $C$ and solve for $x$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x^2-x\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)^2+ax&=3\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    2x^2-\\frac{1}{2}x^2+\\frac{1}{4}x^2+ax-3&=0\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{7}{4}x^2+ax-3&=0\\\\[12pt]\n    7x^2+4ax-12&=0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the quadratic formula we can find the two roots of $x$ in terms of $a$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x_{1,2}&=\\frac{-4a\\pm\\sqrt{(4a)^2-4 \\times 7 \\times (-12)}}{2 \\times 7}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{-4a\\pm\\sqrt{16a^2+336}}{14}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In the question, we are told that $q-p=\\dfrac{20}{7}$.\n    The smaller root is $p$, and the larger one is $q$.\n    We can find the difference of the two roots\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    q-p&=x_2-x_1\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\frac{-4a+\\sqrt{16a^2+336}}{14}-\\left(\\frac{-4a-\\sqrt{16a^2+336}}{14}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{-4a+\\sqrt{16a^2+336}+4a+\\sqrt{16a^2+336}}{14}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{\\sqrt{16a^2+336}}{7}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using this result with the information given, we form the equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\sqrt{16a^2+336}}{7}&=\\frac{20}{7}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    which we can solve using a G.D.C., using $x$ instead of $a$, to get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Implicit%20Differentiation/images/AA1345-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1345-a3\">\n\n    Recall that $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$, hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a&=2\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA469","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA469\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe curve $C$ is defined by the equation $x^2y+\\ln(xy)=1$, $x>0$, $y>0$.\n\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$ and $y$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Determine the equation of the tangent to $C$ at the point P$(1,1)$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using implicit differentiation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[x^2y+\\ln (xy)\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[1\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    2xy + x^2y' + \\dfrac{y + xy'}{xy} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by differentiation rules}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} 2x^2y^2 + x^3yy' + y + xy' &= 0 \\\\[15pt]\n    (x + x^3y)y' &= -(y + 2x^2y^2) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.9em} y' = -\\dfrac{y + 2x^2y^2}{x + x^3y}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating the gradient of the tangent to $C$ at P, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg|_{\\substack{x=1\\\\[1pt]\\hspace{0.025em}y=1}} &= -\\dfrac{1 + 2(1)^2(1)^2}{1 + (1)^3(1)} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1+2}{1+1} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the point-gradient\n    formula with P$(1,1)$ and $m = -3/2$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.3em} y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[10pt]\n    y - 1 &= -\\dfrac{3}{2}(x - 1) \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 1 &= -\\dfrac{3}{2}x + \\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.9em} y =  -\\dfrac{3}{2}x + \\dfrac{5}{2}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA471","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA471\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nA curve is defined by $x^2 + 10xy + y^2 = 48$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -\\dfrac{2x + 10y}{10x + 2y}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the normal to the curve at the point\n    P$(-2,22)$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find the distance between the two points on the curve where each\n    tangent is parallel to the line $y = -x$.\n    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using implicit differentiation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.6em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[x^2 + 10xy + y^2\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[48\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n    2x + 10y + 10xy' + 2yy' &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by differentiation rules}] \\\\[15pt]\n    (10x + 2y)y' &= -(2x + 10y) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.4em} y' = -\\dfrac{2x + 10y}{10x + 2y}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating the gradient of the tangent to the curve at P, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.1em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg|_{\\substack{x=-2\\\\[1pt]y=22\\hspace{0.2em}}} &= -\\dfrac{2(-2) + 10(22)}{10(-2) + 2(22)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= - 9 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the gradient of the normal to the curve at P is\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{1.5em} m &= -\\dfrac{1}{(-9)} \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{1}{9}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n     Hence, using the point-gradient\n    formula with P$(-2,22)$ and $m = 1/9$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[11pt]\n    y - 22 &= \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - (-2)) \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 22 &= \\dfrac{1}{9}x + \\dfrac{2}{9} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.1em} y = \\dfrac{1}{9}x + \\dfrac{200}{9}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= -1 \\\\[7pt]\n    -\\dfrac{2x + 10y}{10x + 2y} &= -1 \\\\[10pt]\n    2x + 10y &= 10x + 2y \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[15pt]\n    8y &= 8x \\\\[15pt]\n    y &= x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $y = x$ in the equation of the curve, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 + 10x^2 + x^2 &= 48 \\\\[6pt]\n    12x^2 &= 48 \\\\[6pt]\n    x^2 &= 4 \\\\[7pt]\n    x &= \\pm 2 \\hspace{5.85em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the two points are\n    A$(-2,-2)$ and B$(2,2)$.\n\n    Finally, using the distance between two points formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7em}\\mathrm{AB} &= \\sqrt{(2-(-2))^2 + (2-(-2))^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{4^2 + 4^2} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 4\\sqrt{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA472","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA472\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the curve defined by the equation $x^5 + y^5 = 5x^2y$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2xy - x^4}{y^4 - x^2}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\nThe normal to this curve is parallel to the $y$-axis at the point where\n$x = h$, $h > 0$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $h$, giving your answer correct to two\n    decimal places. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using implicit differentiation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[x^5 + y^5\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[5x^2y\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n    5x^4 + 5y^4y' &= 5(2xy + x^2y') \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by differentiation rules}] \\\\[12pt]\n    x^4 + y^4y' &= 2xy + x^2y' \\\\[12pt]\n    (y^4 - x^2)y' &= 2xy - x^4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.15em} y' = \\dfrac{2xy - x^4}{y^4 - x^2}\n    $$\n\n2.  The normal to the curve that is parallel to the $y$-axis has\n    gradient $0$.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{2xy - x^4}{y^4 - x^2} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{4.3em} 2xy - x^4 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $x \\neq 0$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $x = h$ and $y = h^3\\hspace{-0.15em}/2$ in the\n    equation of the curve, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^5 + y^5 &= 5x^2y \\\\[10pt]\n    \\hspace{4.7em}  h^5 + \\dfrac{h^{15}}{32} &= \\dfrac{5h^5}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    1 + \\dfrac{h^{10}}{32} &= \\dfrac{5}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $h > 0$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11.3em} h \\approx 1.47 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA473","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA473\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe double folium is a curve defined by the equation\n$(x^2 + y^2)^2 = 8xy^2$, shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Implicit%20Differentiation/images/552dc05568884371101edad81b0dc8a6f51cb176.svg\" alt=\"552dc05568884371101edad81b0dc8a6f51cb176.svg\">\n\nDetermine the exact coordinates of the point P on the curve where the\ntangent line is parallel to the $x$-axis.","markScheme":"Using implicit differentiation, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.6em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[(x^2 + y^2)^2\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[8xy^2\\big] \\\\[9pt]\n2(x^2 + y^2)(2x + 2yy') &= 8(y^2 + 2xyy') \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by chain rule}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient of the tangent to the curve at P is $y' = 0$.\n\nHence, substituting $y' = 0$ in the equation $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{7em} 4x(x^2 + y^2) &= 8y^2 \\\\[12pt]\nx(x^2 + y^2) &= 2y^2 \\\\[12pt]\nx^3 + xy^2 &= 2y^2 \\\\[12pt]\nx^3 &= (2-x)y^2 \\\\[6pt]\ny^2 &= \\dfrac{x^3}{2-x} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{$0 < x < 2$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, substituting the expression $(2)$ in the equation of the curve,\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^2 + y^2)^2 &= 8xy^2 \\\\[9pt]\n\\bigg(x^2 + \\dfrac{x^3}{2-x}\\bigg)^2 &= 8x\\bigg(\\dfrac{x^3}{2-x}\\bigg) \\hspace{0.4em}\\\\[3pt]\nx^4\\bigg(1+ \\dfrac{x}{2-x}\\bigg)^2 &= \\dfrac{8x^4}{2-x} \\\\[3pt]\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{2-x}\\bigg)^2 &= \\dfrac{8}{2-x} \\\\[7pt]\n\\dfrac{4}{(2-x)^2} &= \\dfrac{8}{2-x} \\\\[7pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2} &= 2-x \\\\[10pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFinally, solving the equation $(2)$\n\\[where $x = 3/2$\\] for $y$, we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.7em} y^2 &= \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.25em}{$\\big(\\frac{3}{2}\\big)^3$}}{\\raisebox{-0.2em}{$2-\\frac{3}{2}$}} \\\\[5pt]\ny^2 &= \\dfrac{27}{4} \\\\[5pt]\ny &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the exact coordinates\nof P are\n $\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\bigg)$"},{"ref":"AA474","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA474\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA ladder of length $13$ m rests on horizontal\nground and leans against a vertical wall. The bottom of the ladder is\npulled away from the wall at a constant speed of\n$1.2\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{s}^{-1}$. Calculate the\nspeed of descent of the top of the ladder when the bottom is\n$5$ m away from the wall.","markScheme":"Let $y$ be the distance of the top of the ladder from the ground at time\n$t$ seconds.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2 + y^2 &= 13^2 \\\\[6pt]\nx^2 + y^2 &= 169\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing implicit\ndifferentiation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\big[x^2 + y^2\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\big[169\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{5.4em} 2x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}  + 2y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by chain rule}] \\\\[6pt]\nx\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}  + y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, substituting $x = 5$ in the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{7.8em} 25 + y^2 &= 169 \\\\[6pt]\n y^2 &= 144 \\\\[7pt]\n y &= 12 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $y > 0$}]\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n Hence,\nsubstituting $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 1.2$, $x = 5$ and\n$y = 12$ and in the equation $(2)$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5)(1.2) + 12\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n6 + 12\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= - 0.5 \\hspace{3.2em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the speed of descent of the top of the ladder when $x = 5$ is\n $0.5\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"AA475","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA475\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAn oval-shaped superspeedway can be described by the curve\n$x^2 + 12y^2 = 1 + 10x^2y^2$,\n\nshown in the diagram below. A F$1$ racing car is moving along the track\nwith\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 240\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km}\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{h}^{-1}$\nwhen $x = 0.67\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Implicit%20Differentiation/images/1ca7c44c955ccd2b9c01d16c7fb83b17ecb1d9a3.svg\" alt=\"1ca7c44c955ccd2b9c01d16c7fb83b17ecb1d9a3.svg\">\n\nFind the value of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}$, giving your answer\nto the nearest km h$^{-1}$.","markScheme":"Using implicit differentiation, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{10.6em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\big[x^2 + 12y^2\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\big[1 + 10x^2y^2\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n2x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}  + 24y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 20xy^2\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20x^2y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by chain rule}] \\\\[6pt]\nx\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}  + 12y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 10xy^2\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10x^2y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\\\[6pt]\n(12y - 10x^2y)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= (10xy^2 - x)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{10xy^2 - x}{12y - 10x^2y}\\bigg]\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 0.67$ in the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2 + 12y^2 &= 1 + 10x^2y^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{6.1em} (0.67)^2 + 12y^2 &= 1 + 10(0.67)^2y^2 \\\\[7pt]\ny &\\approx 0.271 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, evaluating $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $x = 0.67$,\n$y = 0.271$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 240$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{7.5em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{10(0.67)(0.271)^2 - 0.67}{12(0.271) - 10(0.67)^2(0.271)}\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.15em}\\times240 \\\\[12pt]\n&\\approx -21 \\text{ km\\hspace{0.1em}h$^{-1}$}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA503","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA503\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA curve has equation $2y^2e^{x+1} - 5x^2 = 3$.\n\n1.  Find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms\n    of $x$ and $y$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the equations of the tangents to this curve when $x = -1$.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using implicit differentiation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left(4ye^{x+1}\\right)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + 2y^2e^{x+1} - 10x & =  0 \\\\[3pt]\n    \\left(4ye^{x+1}\\right)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =  10x - 2y^2e^{x+1} \\\\[3pt]\n    \\left(2ye^{x+1}\\right)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =  5x - y^2e^{x+1} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.8cm}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}  =  \\dfrac{5x - y^2e^{x+1}}{2ye^{x+1}}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substituting $x = -1$ into the following equation and solving it for\n    $y$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2y^2e^{x+1} - 5x^2 &= 3 \\\\[3pt]\n    2y^2e^{-1+1} - 5\\cdot(-1)^2 &= 3 \\\\[3pt]\n    2y^2e^0 - 5\\cdot1 &= 3 \\\\[3pt]\n    2y^2 - 5 & =  3 \\\\[3pt]\n    2y^2 & =  8 \\\\[3pt]\n    y^2 & =  4 \\\\[3pt]\n    y & =  \\pm 2.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting $(x,y) = (-1,\\pm2)$ into\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, we get the slopes of the\n    tangents as follows:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m & =  \\dfrac{5\\cdot(-1) - (\\pm2)^2e^{-1+1}}{2\\cdot(\\pm2)e^{-1+1}} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{-5 - 4}{(\\pm4)} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{9}{4} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{for $y = -2$}] \\\\[5pt]\n    & =  -\\dfrac{9}{4} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{for $y = 2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the equations of the tangents to the curve at\n    $(x_0,y_0) = (-1,\\pm2)$ are given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y - y_0  & =  m(x - x_0) \\\\[10pt]\n    y + 2 = \\dfrac{9}{4}(x + 1) &\\textrm{ and } y - 2 = -\\dfrac{9}{4}(x + 1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    y = \\dfrac{9}{4}x + \\dfrac{1}{4} \\textrm{ and } y = -\\dfrac{9}{4}x - \\dfrac{1}{4}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA511","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA511\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA curve is defined by $x^2 + y^2 - 3xy + 45 = 0$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y-2x}{2y-3x}$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the normal to the curve at the point $(21,9)$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find the distance between the two points on the curve where each\n    tangent is perpendicular to the $y$-axis. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Applying implicit differentiation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 + y^2 - 3xy + 45    & =  0 \\\\[12pt]\n    2x + 2y\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - 3y - 3x\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}             & =  0 \\qquad \\quad [\\text{by chain and product rules}]\\\\[6pt]\n    (2y - 3x)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}  & = 3y - 2x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}        & =  \\dfrac{3y-2x}{2y-3x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    as required.\n\n2.  The slope of the tangent to the curve at $(x,y) = (21,9)$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    & =  \\dfrac{3\\cdot9-2\\cdot21}{2\\cdot9-3\\cdot21} \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the slope of the\n    normal to the curve at $(x_0,y_0) = (21,9)$ is $m = -3$ and the\n    equation of the normal is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y-y_0       & =  m(x - x_0) \\\\[6pt]\n    y-9             & =  -3(x-21) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5mm}y =  -3x + 72\n    $$\n\n3.  The slope of the tangents must be zero. Hence setting the equation\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{3y-2x}{2y-3x} = 0,\n\n    $$\n    we obtain $3y = 2x$. Now substituting\n    $3y = 2x$ into original equation and solving for $x$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 + y^2 - 3xy + 45\n    & =  0 \\qquad \\quad [\\text{multiply by $9$}]\\\\[3pt]\n    9x^2 + (3y)^2 - (9x)\\cdot(3y) + 405\n    & =  0 \\\\[3pt]\n    9x^2 + (2x)^2 - (9x)\\cdot(2x) + 405\n    & =  0 \\\\[3pt]\n    9x^2 + 4x^2 - 18x^2 + 405\n    & =  0   \\\\[3pt]\n    - 5x^2 + 405\n    & =  0 \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{divide by $-5$}] \\\\[3pt]\n    x^2 - 81    & =  0 \\\\[3pt]\n    x       & = \\pm 9.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the two points are $(-9,-6)$\n    and $(9,6)$. The distance between them is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sqrt{18^2+12^2}    & =  \\sqrt{36\\cdot(9+4)} \\\\[10pt]\n    &   =  6\\sqrt{13}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA517","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA517\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nThe curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y^2 + 4xy + e^x = 10$. The\npoint P$(0,b)$ lies on $C$ where $b < 0$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Show that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = - \\dfrac{4y+e^x}{2y+4x}$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find the equation of the normal to $C$ at the point P.[3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of the second point at which the normal found\n    in part (c) intersects $C$.[4 marks]\n\n5.  Given that $u = y^4$,  $y < 0$,  find\n     $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}$  at  $x = 0$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  When $x = 0$, we have $y = b$. Hence, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y^2 + 4xy + e^x     & =  10\\hspace{3.5cm} \\\\[3pt]\n    b^2 + 4(0)(b) + e^0     & =  10 \\\\[3pt]\n    b^2 + 1         & = 10\\\\[3pt]\n    b^2                 & =  9 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.5cm}b  =  -3 \\qquad  \\quad [\\text{since $b < 0$}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using implicit differentiation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y^2 + 4xy + e^x     & =  10 \\\\[12pt]\n    2y\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + 4\\bigg(y + x\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg) + e^x\n    & =  0  \\qquad \\quad [\\text{by chain and product rules}] \\\\[4pt]\n    (2y + 4x)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    & = -4y - e^x \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    & = -\\dfrac{4y + e^x}{2y + 4x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    as\n    required.\n\n3.  The slope of the tangent to $C$ at $(0,-3)$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    & = -\\dfrac{4\\cdot(-3) + 1}{2\\cdot(-3) + 0} \\\\[6pt]\n    & = -\\dfrac{11}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the slope of the\n    normal to $C$ at $(x_0,y_0) = (0,-3)$ is $m = \\dfrac{6}{11}$ and the\n    equation of the normal line is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y-y_0   & = m(x - x_0) \\hspace{.5cm}\\\\[8pt]\n    y-(-3)  & = \\dfrac{6}{11}x \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1cm}y  = \\dfrac{3}{11}(2x-11)\n    $$\n\n4.  Substituting $y = \\dfrac{3}{11}(2x-11)$ into the equation of the\n    curve $C$ and solving for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y^2 + 4xy + e^x     & = 10 \\\\[3pt]\n    \\dfrac{9}{121}(2x-11)^2 + \\dfrac{12x}{11}(2x-11) + e^x & =  10 \\\\[4pt]\n    x               & = 0, \\,3.19808\\dots  \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[5pt]\n    x               & \\approx  3.20\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting\n    $x \\approx 3.1981$ into the equation of the normal line, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y       & = \\dfrac{3}{11}(2x-11) \\\\[3pt]\n    y       & \\approx  \\dfrac{3}{11}(3\\cdot3.1981-11) \\\\[10pt]\n    y       & \\approx  -1.26  \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the other point on $C$ has coordinates\n     $(3.20, -1.26)$\n\n5.  Evaluating derivative of $u = y^4$ at $x = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =  4y^3\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  4\\cdot(-3)^3\\cdot\\bigg(-\\dfrac{11}{6}\\bigg) \\\\[9pt]\n    & =  198  \\qquad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA520","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA520\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2$ defined as follows\n $$\n \\begin{array}{rl}\n C_1: & 3y^2 + 2x^2 = 5,\\hspace{0.25em} y > 0, \\\\[10pt]\n C_2: & y^2 - 5x^3 = 0,\\hspace{0.25em} y > 0. \\\\\n \\end{array}\n $$\n\n1.  Using implicit dif$\\text{f}$erentiation, or otherwise, find\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for each curve in terms of $x$\n    and $y$. [4 marks]\n\nLet P$(a,b)$ be the unique point where the curves $C_1$ and $C_2$\nintersect.\n\n2.  Show that the tangent to $C_1$ at P is perpendicular to the\n    tangent to $C_2$ at P.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using implicit dif$\\text{f}$erentiation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[3y^2 + 2x^2\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[5\\big] \\\\[7pt]\n    6y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + 4x &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by chain rule}]\n    \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -\\dfrac{2x}{3y}\n    $$\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[y^2 - 5x^3\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[0\\big] \\\\[7pt]\n    2y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - 15x^2 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by chain rule}]  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n     \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{15x^2}{2y}\n    $$\n\n2.  The slope of the tangent to $C_1$ at P is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m_1 &= -\\dfrac{2a}{3b} \\hspace{0.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The slope of\n    the tangent to $C_2$ at P is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m_2 &= \\dfrac{15a^2}{2b}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the product of the\n    slopes at P is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.6em} m_1m_2 &= \\bigg(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{2a}{3b}\\bigg)\\bigg(\\dfrac{15a^2}{2b}\\bigg) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{5a^3}{b^2} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{5a^3}{5a^3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $b^2 = 5a^3$}] \\\\[14pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the tangent to $C_1$ at P is\n    perpendicular to the tangent to $C_2$ at P."},{"ref":"AA524","marks":"19","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA524\n\n**Maximum mark: 19**\n\nA curve $C$ is given by the implicit equation $x - y + \\sin(xy) = 0$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1+y\\cos(xy)}{1-x\\cos(xy)}$.\n    [5 marks]\n\n2.  The curve $xy = \\pi$ intersects $C$ at P and Q.\n    1.  Find the coordinates of P and Q.\n\n    2.  Given that the gradients of the tangents to $C$ at P and Q are\n        $m_1$ and $m_2$ respectively, show that\n        $m_1\\cdot\\hspace{0.1em} m_2 = 1$. [7 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of the three points on $C$, nearest to the\n    origin, where the tangent $\\text{is~parallel}$ to the line\n    $y = x$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we dif$\\text{f}$erentiate the equation of the curve $C$\n    implicitly with respect to $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[x - y + \\sin(xy)\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[0\\big] \\\\[8pt]\n    \\hspace{0.3em} 1 - \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + \\cos(xy)\\bigg[y + x\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg] &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by chain rule}] \\\\[6pt]\n    (1 + y\\cos(xy)) &= (1 - x\\cos(xy))\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.3em}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1 + y\\cos(xy)}{1 - x\\cos(xy)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  If we substitute $xy = \\pi$ in the equation of the curve $C$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x - y + \\sin(xy) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x - y + \\sin(\\pi) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x - y &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x^2 - xy &= x(0) \\\\[6pt]\n        x^2 - \\pi &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= \\pm\\sqrt{\\pi} \\hspace{3.2em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence,\n        evaluating $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{x}$ for $x = \\pm\\sqrt{\\pi}$, we\n        obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} y &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\pm \\sqrt{\\pi}} \\\\[6pt]\n        y &= \\pm\\sqrt{\\pi}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we find\n          $\\mbox P \\left(-\\sqrt{\\pi},-\\sqrt{\\pi}\\right) \\mbox{ and Q}\\left(\\sqrt{\\pi},\\sqrt{\\pi}\\right)$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at P and Q, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        m_1 &= \\dfrac{1 - \\sqrt{\\pi}\\cos(\\pi)}{1 + \\sqrt{\\pi}\\cos(\\pi)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1+\\sqrt{\\pi}}{1-\\sqrt{\\pi}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        and\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        m_2 &= \\dfrac{1 + \\sqrt{\\pi}\\cos(\\pi)}{1 - \\sqrt{\\pi}\\cos(\\pi)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1 - \\sqrt{\\pi}}{1+\\sqrt{\\pi}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the\n        product of the gradients $m_1$ and $m_2$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} m_1\\cdot\\hspace{0.1em} m_2 &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\cancel{1+\\sqrt{\\pi}}}{\\cancel{1-\\sqrt{\\pi}}}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.04em}\\cdot\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\cancel{1 - \\sqrt{\\pi}}}{\\cancel{1+\\sqrt{\\pi}}}\\bigg) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Let A, B and C be the three points on $C$, nearest to the origin,\n    where the tangent $\\text{has gradient \\(1\\)}$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{1+y\\cos(xy)}{1-x\\cos(xy)} &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\hspace{4.1em} (x+y)\\cos(xy) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by rearranging terms}] \\\\[16pt]\n    x + y = 0 \\hspace{0.5em} &\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{or} \\hspace{0.5em} \\cos(xy) = 0 \\\\[11pt]\n    y = -x \\hspace{0.5em} &\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{or} \\hspace{0.5em} xy = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we substitute $y = -x$ in the equation of the curve $C$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x - y + \\sin(xy) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x - (-x) + \\sin(x(-x)) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    2x + \\textstyle \\sin(-x^2) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= 0 \\hspace{3.3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find  $\\mbox A (0,0)$\\\n    If we substitute $xy = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ in the equation of the curve\n    $C$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x - y + \\sin(xy) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{6.6em} x - y + \\sin(\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    x - y + 1 &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    x^2 - xy + x &= x(0) \\\\[7pt]\n    x^2 + x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x&\\approx -1.85,\\hspace{0.4em} 0.849 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, evaluating $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2x}$ for\n    $x = -1.85,\\hspace{0.4em} 0.849$, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{13.2em} y &\\approx -0.849,\\hspace{0.4em} 1.85 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    Hence we find\n     $\\mbox B (-1.85,-0.849) \\mbox{ and C}(0.849,1.85)$"},{"ref":"AA978","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA978\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the curve, $C$, described by the equation: \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n(x^2+y^2)(y^2-9) + 9y^2=0  \\text{ where }x > 0 \\text{ and } y > 0.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nShow that the curve has no local maximum or local minimum points.","markScheme":"Expanding the brackets on the left hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    x^2y^2 - 9x^2 + y^4 \\cancel{- 9y^2 + 9y^2} = 0\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe can differentiate implicitly, term by term, to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^2y^2 - 9x^2 + y^4\\right) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2xy^2 + 2x^2y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - 18x + 4y^3\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(2x^2y + 4y^3\\right) &= 18x - 2xy^2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{18x - 2xy^2}{2x^2y + 4y^3}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nIn order for a local maximum or local minimum to exist, we must have $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=0$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{18x - 2xy^2}{2x^2y + 4y^3} &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    18x - 2xy^2 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2x(9-y^2) &= 0\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThis means possible maxima or minima when $2x=0$ or $9-y^2=0$. But $x>0$ hence $2x\\neq0$, so the only possibility is $9-y^2=0$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    y^2 &= 9 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y &= 3 \\hspace{3em}\\text{[$y>0$]}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=3$ into the original equation, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    x^2(3)^2 - 9x^2 + (3)^4 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    9x^2 - 9x^2 + 81 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    81 &\\neq 0\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe have a contradiction. \n\nTherefore $\\boxed{\\text{the curve has no local maximum or local minimum points.}}$"}]},{"name":"Kinematics I- Differentiation","questions":[{"ref":"AA1276","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1276\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nConsider the function\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}(9x^2-8x+3)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n1.  Show that $\\displaystyle f'(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}(15x^2-8x+1)$. [4 marks] \n\n2.  Hence find the $x-$coordinates of the two stationary points of $f(x)$.[3 marks]\n\nA particle, $P$, is moving along the $x-$axis. Its position $s$, in metres, relative to the origin after time $t$, measured in seconds, is given by \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    s(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{t}(9t^2-8t+3)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWhere $t \\geq 0$.\n\nThe particle is moving to the left for $t=k\\,$seconds.\n\n3.  Hence find $k$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  There are a number of ways to differentiate this function. \n\n    We could expand the brackets using index laws then differentiate each term.\n\n    However, in this solution we will use the product rule.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{If }& &\\hspace{-5em}&y&=uv&\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{then }&&\\hspace{-5em}&\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&= v\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}+u\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    With $u=\\frac{2}{3}x^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ and $v=9x^2-8x+3$.\n\n    Using the power rule, we get $\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$ and $\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=18x-8$.\n\n    By substituting these results into the formula we can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f'(x)&=(9x^2-8x+3)\\times \\tfrac{1}{3}x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+\\tfrac{2}{3}x^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\times (18x-8)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    If we look at the requested form of the answer we can see that $x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$ is a common factor.\n\n    \n\n    Note that for the second term of the derivative we can use the following to help us factorise $x^{\\frac{1}{2}} = x^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\times x$.\n\n    \n\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\left[\\tfrac{1}{3}(9x^2-8x+3)+\\tfrac{2}{3} \\times x(18x-8)\\right]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can expand the brackets and simplify \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\left[3x^2-\\tfrac{8}{3}x+1+12x^2-\\tfrac{16}{3}x\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\left[15x^2-\\tfrac{24}{3}x+1)\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}(15x^2-8x+1)\\hspace{2em}\\text{... as required}\n\n    \\end{aligned}  \n\n    $$  \n\n  \n\n    \n\n2.  Stationary points occur when $f'(x)=0$.\n\n    Hence we can form an equation\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}(15x^2-8x+1)&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\neq 0$ for any value of $x$, therefore we only need to consider the term in the brackets when solving the equation above. Thus we need to solve\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        15x^2-8x+1&=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}  \n\n    $$ \n\n    We can attempt to factorise this. Note that $15x^2=3x \\times 5x$ and the only combinations that give $+1$ are $1 \\times 1$ or $(-1) \\times (-1)$. Hence through inspection we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (5x-1)(3x-1)&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$ \n\n    The $x-$coordinates of the stationary points are $x=\\tfrac{1}{5}, \\tfrac{1}{3}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$ \n\n3.  The particle is moving left when the velocity is negative. Hence we need to find the interval for $t$ which satisfies\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v(t)&<0\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can note that $v(t)=s'(t)$ and that the function given for $s(t)$ is of the same form as $f(x)$ from part (a).\n\n    Hence, using the answer from part (a) we can say that \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v(t)&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}(15t^2-8t+1)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can realise that if $t \\geq 0$ then $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}>0$. Therefore the expression in the bracket will determine the sign of $v(t)$.\n\n    To help us here we can make a sketch of $y=15t^2-8t+1$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/1276.JPEG\" alt=\"1276\">\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$ \n\n    \n\n    Notice the red part of the curve is when $y<0$ and therefore $v(t)<0$.\n\n    Using our answers from part (b) we can write that $v(t)<0$ when $\\tfrac{1}{5}<t<\\tfrac{1}{3}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$ \n\n    However, the question doesn't ask for an interval it asks for a total time the particle is moving left, hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k&=\\tfrac{1}{3}-\\tfrac{1}{5}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\tfrac{5}{15}-\\tfrac{3}{15}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\tfrac{2}{15}\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{seconds}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1394","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1394\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graphs of the displacement, velocity and acceleration of a moving object as functions of time, $t$ where $0\\leq t \\leq 10$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA1394.png\" alt=\"AA1394\">\n\n1. Complete the following table by noting which graph A, B or C corresponds to each function.[2 marks]\n\n|Function|Graph|\n\n|:-:|:-:|\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$displacement$\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{11em}$|\n\n|acceleration||\n\n2. Write down the values of $t$ when the object is at rest.[2 marks]\n\n3. Hence write down the values of $t$ for which the object is moving in the negative direction.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. We have\n\n|Function|Graph|\n\n|:-:|:-:|\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$displacement$\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{A}$ $\\hspace{1em}$|\n\n|acceleration|$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{B}$ $\\hspace{1em}$|\n\n$$\\\\[-4.8em]$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n2. Graph C is the graph of velocity. The particle is at rest when $v(t)=0$. \n\n  \n\n    Hence we need the roots of Graph C which are when $\\hspace{0.2em}t=1\\hspace{0.2em}$ and $\\hspace{0.2em}t=7\\hspace{0.2em}$.\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. The object is moving in the negative direction when the velocity is less than zero. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA1394c.png\" alt=\"AA1394c\">\n\n    Hence we can use the roots from part (b) and the endpoints of the given domain to write the time intervals where Graph C is negative.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{0.525em}0 \\leq t < 1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{0.525em}7 < t \\leq 10 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1396","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1396\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe displacement of a car, $s$ metres, from a fixed point O after $t$ seconds is modelled by\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    s(t) = t^3 - 6t^2 + 9t, \\quad \\text{ where }0 \\leq t \\leq 5.\n\n\\end{aligned}  \n\n$$ \n\n1. Find an expression for the velocity of the car, $v(t)$.[2 marks]\n\n2. Find the values of $t$ when the car is at rest.[2 marks]\n\n3. Find the displacement of the car when the acceleration is equal to $12\\,\\text{m\\,s}^{-2}$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. To find the velocity function we need to differentiate $s(t)$ with respect to $t$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v(t) &= s'(t) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        v(t) &= \\hspace{0.2em}3t^2 - 12t + 9 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. The car is at rest when $v(t) = 0$. Hence we can set the equation from part (a) equal to zero and solve for $t$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        3t^2 - 12t + 9 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        3(t - 1)(t - 3) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        t = \\hspace{0.2em}1\\text{ s} \\hspace{1em} &\\text{or} \\hspace{1em} t = \\hspace{0.2em}3\\text{ s} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. We are given information about the acceleration. Hence we need a equation for acceleration. We obtain this by differentiating $v(t)$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a(t)&=v'(t)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        a(t) &= 6t - 12 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Next we can set the equation equal to 12 to find the time when $a(t)=12\\,\\text{m\\,s}^{-2}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        6t - 12 &= 12 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        t &= 4 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Finally, substituting $t = 4$ into $s(t)$ we find the displacement when $a(t)=12$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        s(4) &= (4)^3 - 6(4)^2 + 9(4) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 64 - 96 + 36 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\hspace{0.2em}4\\text{ m} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1397","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1397\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe displacement of the particle, $s$ metres, from a fixed origin O after $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ns(t) = t\\,e^{2-t}, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n1. Show that $v(t) = e^{2-t}(1 - t)$.[3 marks]\n\n2. Write down the initial velocity of the particle.[1 mark]\n\n3. Find the displacement of the particle when it is at rest.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. To differentiate $s(t)$ we need to use the $\\textbf{product rule (5.6)}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= u\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    If we let $u=t$ and $v=e^{2-t}$ we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}t}=1\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-e^{2-t}  \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting these results into the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v(t)&= t \\cdot \\left(-e^{2-t}\\right)+e^{2-t}\\cdot 1 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\hspace{0.2em}e^{2-t}(1 - t)\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using the result from part (a) we can substitute $t = 0$ to find the initial velocity. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v(0) &= e^{2}(1 - 0)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= e^2\\text{ m\\,s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. First we need to find the time when the particle is at rest. Setting $v(t) = 0$ we can solve for $t$. Recall that $e^{2-t} > 0$ for all $t$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        e^{2-t}(1 - t) &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall that $e^{2-t} > 0$ for all $t$, hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        1 - t &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        t &= 1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can substitute $t = 1$ into $s(t)$ to find the displacement when the particle is at rest.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        s(1) &= 1 \\cdot e^{2-1} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= e\\text{ metres} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1398","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1398\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA weight on the end of a spring moves along a straight vertical rod. The diagram below shows the velocity, $v$ $\\text{ms}^{-1}$, of the weight at time $t$ seconds, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 5$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA1398A.png\" alt=\"AA1398A\">\n\nThe graph intersects the $x$-axis at $C$, $E$ and $G$, where $t = 2$, $t = 3.5$ and $t = 5$ respectively. There are local maxima at $A$ and $D$ and local minima at $B$ and $F$.\n\n1. Write down the velocity at point $C$.[1 mark]\n\n2. Write down the values of $t$, for $0 < t < 5$, at which the weight changes direction.[2 marks]\n\n3. State the value of the acceleration of the weight at point $D$, justifying your answer.[2 marks]\n\n4. Explain why the weight is slowing down for $D < t < E$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Reading the $y$-value at point $C$ from the graph.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v = \\hspace{0.2em}0 \\text{ m\\,s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Identifying where $v$ changes sign by reading the $t$-intercepts ($x$-axis) within the open interval $0 < t < 5$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        t = \\hspace{0.2em}2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = \\hspace{0.2em}3.5 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note: $t = 5$ is not included as the domain given in this question part was $0<t<5$.\n\n3. Point $D$ is a local maximum of $v$, so the graph of $v$ has a horizontal tangent there and $v'(t) = 0$. $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Since $a(t) = v'(t)$ we can conclude\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a = \\hspace{0.2em}0 \\text{ m\\,s}^{-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. For $C < t < E$, the graph shows $v > 0$, so the weight is moving in the positive direction. $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    From $D$ to $E$, $v$ is decreasing towards zero, so the gradient values are less than zero. $(a < 0)$. \n\n    Since $\\hspace{0.2em}v\\text{ and }a\\text{ have opposite signs}\\hspace{0.2em}$ the speed is decreasing (i.e. the weight is slowing down). $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1399","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1399\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nTwo athletes, Mateo and Diego, compete in a $250$ metres race along a straight track.\n\nMateo's displacement, in metres, from the starting line during the race can be modelled by\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n  s_M(t) = 9.5\\,t + 6e^{-t/2} - 6, \\quad \\text{where } t \\geq 0.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTime, $t$, is measured in seconds from when the race starts.\n\n1.  1. Find an expression for Mateo's velocity, $v_M(t)$.\n\n    2. Hence show that when Mateo's velocity is $9\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$, then $t=2\\ln6$ seconds.[5 marks]\n\n2.  1. Find an expression for Mateo's acceleration, $a_M(t)$.\n\n  \n\n    2. Hence show that Mateo is always speeding up during the race.[3 marks]\n\nDiego's displacement, in metres, from the starting line during the race can be modelled by\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n  s_D(t) = 9\\,t + 4e^{-t/3} - 4, \\quad \\text{where } t \\geq 0.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nMateo crosses the finishing line ahead of Diego.\n\n3. Find the time at which Mateo overtakes Diego.[2 marks]\n\n4. Find the distance Diego is from the finishing line at the moment Mateo crosses the finishing line.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. Differentiating $s_M(t)$ term by term and applying the chain rule to the exponential term.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v_M(t) &= 9.5 + 6 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)e^{-t/2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\hspace{0.2em}9.5 - 3e^{-t/2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    2. Setting $v_M(t) = 9$ and rearranging to isolate the exponential term.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            9.5 - 3e^{-t/2} &= 9 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n            - 3e^{-t/2} &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            e^{-t/2} &= \\frac{1}{6}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Taking the natural logarithm of both sides\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\ln(e^{-t/2})&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            -\\frac{t}{2}&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n\n            t&=-2\\left(\\ln\\left(6^{-1}\\right)\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            t &= \\hspace{0.2em}2\\ln6\\,\\text{ s}\\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n2.  1. We can differentiate $v_M(t)$ using the **chain rule (5.6)** to obtain Mateo's acceleration.\n\n        When we let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ then $v=-3e^u$.\n\n    \n\n        Differentiating both we have $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-3e^u$.\n\n    \n\n        Then using the formula\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\times\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            a_M(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= -3 e^{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            a_M(t) &= \\hspace{0.2em}1.5\\,e^{{-t}/_{2}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. First let's note the conditions for which an object is speeding up or slowing down.\n\n        - If the velocity and acceleration both have the same sign ($v(t) \\text{ and }a(t)>0$ or $v(t) \\text{ and } a(t)<0$), an object is **speeding up**\n\n        - If the velocity and acceleration have opposite signs ($v(t)>0 , a(t)<0$ or $v(t)<0 , a(t)>0$), an object is **slowing down**\n\n        Since $e^{-t/2} > 0$ for all real values of $t$, we know that $a_M(t) = 1.5\\,e^{-t/2} > 0$ for all $t \\geq 0$. \n\n        Similarly, we have our expression for Mateo's velocity from part $\\text{(a)}\\,\\text{(i)}$, $v_M(t)=9.5 - 3e^{-t/2}$. By examining this function on the G.D.C. we can see that $v_M(t)>0$ for all $t \\geq 0$ also.\n\n        $$\\text{Both velocity and acceleration are positive throughout the race}$$ \n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Therefore Mateo is continually speeding up. \n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{AG}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n3. Mateo overtakes Diego when their displacements are equal, so we set up the equation\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    9.5\\,t + 6e^{-t/2} - 6 &= 9\\,t + 4e^{-t/3} - 4 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    On the G.D.C., we can sketch the graphs of $\\color{B52209}{y = 9.5x + 6e^{-x/2} - 6}$ (Mateo's displacement) and $\\color{039855}{y = 9x + 4e^{-x/3} - 4}$ (Diego's displacement), using $x$ in place of $t$, and find the point of intersection. This is shown on the following axes.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA1399c.png\" alt=\"AA1399c\">\n\n    From the graph, $t = 4.521\\ldots$, so\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    t &= 4.521\\ldots \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{4.52\\,\\text{ s}}\\hspace{1em}\\text{(3 s.f.)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. First we should find the time when Mateo crosses the finish line. We can do that by setting Mateo's displacement equal to $250$ metres.\n\n    Using the GDC, we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        9.5\\,t + 6e^{{-t}/_{2}} - 6 &= 250 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        t &= 26.95\\ldots\\,\\text{s} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Then we substitute this time into Diego's displacement function and subtract the value from the $250$ metres. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d &= 250 - \\left[\\,9(26.95\\ldots) + 4e^{{-26.95\\ldots}/_{3}} - 4\\,\\right] \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 11.47\\ldots \\\\[6pt]\n\n        d&= \\hspace{0.2em}11.5\\,\\text{m} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1400","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1400\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nA swimmer is swimming back and forth along a straight lane in a pool, completing a turn each time she reaches a wall. The swimmer moves at a minimum velocity while executing a turn and reaches a maximum velocity midway between the two walls.\n\nHer velocity, in $\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$, can be modelled by the function\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n  v(t) = -0.5\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right) + 2.5, \\qquad t \\geq 0,\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\n1.  1. Find the maximum velocity of the swimmer.\n\n    2. Hence find the time at which the swimmer first reaches her maximum velocity.[4 marks]\n\n2. Find the number of turns the swimmer executes in the first two minutes of motion.[3 marks]\n\n3. 1. Find $v'(t)$.\n\n    2. Hence, or otherwise, write down the maximum acceleration of the swimmer.[3 marks]\n\n4. Show that the length of the pool is $50$ metres.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. We know that the range of cosine is $-1\\leq \\cos\\theta \\leq 1$. \n\n        Considering the boundaries, we can determine that the swimmer reaches her maximum velocity when $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right)=-1$ (since $-0.5\\cos(\\theta)$ is maximised when cosine equals $-1$). \n\n        $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n        \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence we obtain\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            v_{\\max} &= -0.5(-1)+2.5 \\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= \\hspace{0.2em}3\\text{ m\\,s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. Setting $v(t)=3$ \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            3&=-0.5\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right)+2.5\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            -1&=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right)\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We know that $\\cos\\theta=-1$ when $\\theta=\\pi$. Hence \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4) &= \\pi  \\\\[6pt]\n\n            t-4&=10\\\\[6pt]\n\n            t &= \\hspace{0.2em}14\\text{ seconds} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. The swimmer turns at each minimum of $v(t)$ so we want to know the length of time between each minimum. By finding the length of one period, we can use that information to find the number of turns that occur in two minutes. \n\n    We find the period by using the relationship that $\\text{period}=\\frac{2\\pi}{b}$. In our equation, $b=\\frac{\\pi}{10}$. Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{period} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{^{\\pi}/_{10}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 20\\text{ seconds} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The first maximum occurred at $t=14$ so we can subtract half of the period to determine that the first minimum occurred at $t=4$.\n\n    Then by adding $20$ second intervals, we can determine how many minima there are. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        t=4,\\,24,\\,44,\\,64,\\,84,\\,104,\\,\\textcolor{RVRed}{124},\\ldots\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The turn after $t=104$ occurs at $t=124$, which is outside the first two minutes. \n\n    Hence the swimmer makes $6\\text{ turns}$ in the first two minutes.\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3.  1. To find $v'(t)$ we can differentiate $v(t)$ using the $\\textbf{chain rule (5.6)}$ where \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\times\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    When we let $u=\\frac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)$ then $v=-0.5\\cos u+2.5$.\n\n    \n\n    Hence we have $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi}{10}$ and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-0.5(-\\sin u)$.\n\n    \n\n    Then using the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v'(t) &= 0.5\\sin u\\times\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{10}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 0.5\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{10}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        v'(t)&= \\hspace{0.em}\\dfrac{\\pi}{20}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    2. Considering the maximum value of $\\sin(\\theta)=1$, hence the maximum acceleration is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a_{\\max} &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{20}\\cdot1\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\hspace{0.em}\\dfrac{\\pi}{20}\\text{ m\\,s}^{-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. The length of the pool, $L$, can be found by determining the distance travelled by the swimmer between two consecutive turns, and we are told these coincide with minima of the function. \n\n    From part (b), the first two minima occur at $t=4$ and $t=24$, so using **the formula for distance travelled from $\\bm{t_1}$ to $\\bm{t_2}$ (5.9)**, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{distance} &= \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} |v(t)| \\, \\mathrm{d}t \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt] \n\n        L &= \\int_{4}^{24} \\left[-0.5\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right)+2.5\\right]\\mathrm{d}t \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that $v(t)>0$ for all values of $t$, therefore the absolute value signs are not required.\n\n    Integrating term by term\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        L &= \\left[-0.5\\cdot\\dfrac{10}{\\pi}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right)+2.5t\\right]_{4}^{24} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\left[-\\dfrac{5}{\\pi}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}(t-4)\\right)+2.5t\\right]_{4}^{24} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\left(-\\dfrac{5}{\\pi}\\sin(2\\pi)+60\\right)-\\left(-\\dfrac{5}{\\pi}\\sin(0)+10\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 60-10 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        L &= \\hspace{0.em}50\\text{ m}\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA491","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA491\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe displacement, $s$, in metres, of a particle $t$ seconds after it\npasses through the origin is given by the expression\n$s = \\ln\\left(3+t-2e^{-t}\\right)$,  $t \\geq 0$.\n\n1.  Find an expression for the velocity, $v$, of the particle at time\n    $t$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for the acceleration, $a$, of the particle at\n    time $t$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the acceleration of the particle at time $t=0$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The velocity of the particle at time $t$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(t) & =  s'(t) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} =  \\dfrac{1+2e^{-t}}{3+t-2e^{-t}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The acceleration of the particle at time $t$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(t) & =  v'(t) \\\\[4pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{\\left(-2e^{-t}\\right)\\left(3+t-2e^{-t}\\right) - \\left(1+2e^{-t}\\right)^2}{\\left(3+t-2e^{-t}\\right)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} =  -\\dfrac{1 + (10+2t)e^{-t}}{\\left(3+t-2e^{-t}\\right)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Substituting $t = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(0) & =  -\\dfrac{1 + (10+0)\\cdot1}{\\left(3+0-2\\cdot1\\right)^2} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} =  -11 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{s}^{-2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA493","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA493\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA particle moves such that its velocity\n$v$ m s$^{-1}$ is related\nto its displacement $s$ m by the equation\n$v(s) = 2\\arctan\\left(\\cos s\\right)$,  $0 \\leq s \\leq \\pi$.\n\n1.  Find the particle's acceleration\n    $a$ m s$^{-2}$ in\n    terms of $s$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Using an appropriate graph, find the particle's displacement when\n    its acceleration is\n    $0.5$ m s$^{-2}$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The particle's acceleration\n    $a$ m s$^{-2}$ in\n    terms of $s$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a (s)       & =  v\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm ds} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  2\\arctan\\left(\\cos s \\right) \\left[-\\dfrac{2\\sin s}{\\cos^2 s + 1}\\right] \\\\[8pt]\n    &   \\hspace{.275em}= - \\dfrac{4\\sin s \\arctan\\left(\\cos s \\right)}{\\cos^2 s + 1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the following sketch of $y  = a(s)$, we find that\n     $\\hspace{.275em}s \\approx 1.70\\hspace{0.25em}\\mbox{m}$  and\n\n    $\\hspace{.275em}s \\approx 2.82\\hspace{0.25em}\\mbox{m}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/ea424853a1966ff77cdfece65136c789023e5909.svg\" alt=\"ea424853a1966ff77cdfece65136c789023e5909.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA516","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA516\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe displacement, $s$, in metres, of a particle $t$ seconds after it\npasses through the origin is given by the expression\n$s = \\ln\\big(1+ t\\hspace{0.025em}e^{-t}\\big)$,  $t \\geq 0$.\n\n1.  Find an expression for the velocity, $v$, of the particle at time\n    $t$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for the acceleration, $a$, of the particle at\n    time $t$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the acceleration of the particle at time $t=0$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The velocity of the particle at time $t$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(t) & =  s'(t) \\\\[4pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{0 + \\big(e^{-t} - t\\hspace{0.025em}e^{-t}\\big)}{1 +  t\\hspace{0.025em}e^{-t}}  \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{(1- t)\\hspace{0.025em}e^{-t}}{1 +  t\\hspace{0.025em}e^{-t}}\\cdot\\dfrac{e^t}{e^t}\\\\[10pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{1 - t}{e^t + t}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The acceleration of the particle at time $t$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(t) & =  v'(t) \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{(-1)(e^t + t) - (1-t)(e^t + 1)}{\\big(e^t+t\\big)^2} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by quotient rule}] \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{-e^t - \\cancel{t} - e^t - 1 + t\\hspace{0.05em}e^t + \\cancel{t}}{\\big(e^t+t\\big)^2} \\\\[10pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{(t-2)\\hspace{0.025em}e^t - 1}{\\big(e^t+t\\big)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Substituting $t = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(0) & =  \\dfrac{(0-2)\\cdot1 - 1}{\\big(1+0\\big)^2} \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  -3 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.1em}s^{-2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA565","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA565\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA particle moves in a straight line with velocity\n$v(t) = 2 t - 0.3 t^3 + 2$, for $t \\geq 0$, where $v$ is in ms$^{-1}$\nand $t$ in seconds.\n\n1.  Find the acceleration of the particle after $2.2$ seconds.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the time when the acceleration is zero.\n\n    2.  Find the velocity when the acceleration is zero. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(t)        & = v'(t)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2 - 0.9 t^2\\\\[6pt]\n    a(2.2)  & = 2 - 0.9(2.2^2)\\hspace{7mm}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}  = -2.36\\,\\mbox{ms}^{-2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  $a(t) = 0 \\,\\Longrightarrow \\,v'(t) = 0$, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{7mm}\n        2 - 0.9 t^2 & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n        & \\hspace{-1.5mm}  t = 1.49\\,\\mbox{s}\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  By part (i), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        v(1.49)     & = 2(1.49) - 0.3(1.49^3) + 2\\\\[6pt]\n        & \\hspace{.275em} = 3.99\\,\\mbox {ms}^{-1}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA597","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA597\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe displacement, in centimeters, of a particle from an origin, O, at\ntime $t$ seconds, is given by $s(t) = t \\sin2t - 7 \\sin t \\cos t$,\n$0 \\leq t \\leq 3$.\n\n1.  Find the maximum distance of the particle from O. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the acceleration of the particle at the instant it changes\n    direction for the the second time. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The maximum distance occurs when \\|$s(t)$\\| is at its maximum.\\\n    The maximum distance from O is\n      $2.76 \\ \\mbox{cm}$\n\n2.  The particle changes direction for the second time at $t = 2.18$\n    seconds.\\\n    The acceleration is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em}\n    a(t)        & = s''(t)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 4 \\cos 2t - 2(2t - 7)\\sin 2t\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.1cm} a(2.18)= -6.37\\ \\mbox{cm\\,s}^{-2}     \\qquad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA936","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA936\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe velocity, $v\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{s}^{-1}$, at time $t$ seconds, of a particle moving in a straight line is given by\n\n$v=\\dfrac{(t^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\hspace{0.15em}t}{2}\\,$,\n\nfor $0 \\leq t \\leq 2$.\n\n1. Determine when the particle changes direction for the first time. [2 marks]\n\n2. Find the times when the acceleration of the particle is $1.4\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{s}^{-2}$. [3 marks]\n\n3. Find the acceleration of the particle when its speed is at its greatest. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the G.D.C. to sketch the graph of $v=\\dfrac{(t^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\hspace{0.15em}t}{2}\\,$, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA936a.PNG\" alt=\"AA936a\">\n\n    At time $t=1 \\text{ second}\\,$, $v=0$ and the velocity has changed from negative to positive. Therefore the particle has changed direction for the first time.\n\n2. Acceleration is the derivative of velocity, therefore we must find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}$. Using the product rule, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{2t\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\,t+(t^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{cos}\\,t}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We must solve the equation\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    1.4 &= \\dfrac{2t\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\,t+(t^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{cos}\\,t}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the G.D.C. to sketch the graphs of $a=\\dfrac{2t\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\,t+(t^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{cos}\\,t}{2}$ and $a=1.4$, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA936b.PNG\" alt=\"AA936b\">\n\n    The acceleration of the particle is $1.4\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}$ at $t\\approx1.34 \\text{ seconds}\\,$ and $t\\approx1.88 \\text{ seconds}\\,$.\n\n3. From the graph in part (a) we see that the particle's speed is greatest at time $t=2$ seconds. Substituting this value into the acceleration equation from part (b), we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a &= \\dfrac{2t\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\,t+(t^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{cos}\\,t}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{2(2)\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\,(2)+(2^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{cos}\\,(2)}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    a &\\approx 1.194\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    When the particle's speed is at its greatest, the acceleration is approximately $1.19\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}\\,$."},{"ref":"AA939","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA939\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA physicist is studying the motion of two separate particles moving in a straight line. She measures the displacement of each particle from a fixed origin over the course of 10 seconds.\n\nThe physicist found that the displacement of particle $A$, $s_A$ cm, at time $t$ seconds can be modelled by the function $s_A(t)=7t+9$, where $0 \\leq t \\leq 10$.\n\nThe physicist found that the displacement of particle $B$, $s_B$ cm, at time $t$ seconds can be modelled by the function $s_B(t)=\\mathrm{cos}(3t+5)+8t+4$.\n\n1. Use the physicist's models to find the initial displacement of\n\n    1. Particle $A$;\n\n    2. Particle $B$ correct to three significant figures. [3 marks]\n\n2. Find the values of $t$ when $s_A(t)=s_B(t)$. [3 marks]\n\n3. For $t>6$, prove that particle $B$ was always further away from the fixed origin than particle $A$. [3 marks]\n\n4. For $0 \\leq t \\leq 10$, find the total amount of time that the velocity of particle $A$ was greater than the velocity of particle $B$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. The initial displacement of particle $A$ is found by substituting the value $t=0$ into $s_A(t)$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        s_A(t) &= 7t+9 \\\\[10pt]\n\n        s_A(0) &= 7(0)+9\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The initial displacement of particle $A$ is $9\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\,$.\n\n    \n\n    2. The initial displacement of particle $B$ is found by substituting the value $t=0$ into $s_B(t)$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        s_B(t) &= \\mathrm{cos}(3t+5)+8t+4 \\\\[10pt]\n\n        s_B(0) &= \\mathrm{cos}(3(0)+5)+8(0)+4 \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &\\approx 4.284\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The initial displacement of particle $B$ is approximately $4.28\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\,$.\n\n2. Using the G.D.C. to sketch the graphs of $s_A(t)$ and $s_B(t)$ for $0 \\leq t \\leq 10$, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA939a.PNG\" alt=\"AA939a\">\n\n    The graphs of $s_A(t)$ and $s_B(t)$ intersect at points A, B and C. \n\n    Therefore, the particles have the same displacement at approximately $4.34\\text{ seconds, }5.21\\text{ seconds and }5.85\\text{ seconds}$\n\n3. We must prove that for $t > 6$, $s_B(t) > s_A(t)$. Therefore we consider\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{cos}(3t+5)+8t+4 &> 7t + 9\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Subtracting $(7t + 4)$ from both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{cos}(3t+5)+t &> 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The cosine function has a minimum value of $-1$ and a maximum value of $1$. \n\n    Therefore, for any value of $t$, $\\mathrm{cos}(3t+5) \\geq -1$.\n\n    Given that $t>6$, the sum $\\mathrm{cos}(3t+5) + t$ must be greater than 5.\n\n    Hence we have proven that for $t>6$,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{cos}(3t+5)+t &> 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Adding $7t + 4$ to both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{cos}(3t+5)+8t+4 &> 7t + 9 \\text{ , for } t>6\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    as required.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    & \\hspace{-0.575em}\\boxed{\\text{For }t>6\\text{ , }s_B(t) > s_A(t)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. Velocity is the derivative of displacement, therefore we must find expressions for $s_A'(t)$ and $s_B'(t)$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    s_A(t) &= 7t+9 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    s_A'(t) &= 7 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    s_B(t) &= \\mathrm{cos}(3t+5)+8t+4 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    s_B'(t) &= -3\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{sin}(3t+5)+8\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence $v_A=7$ and $v_B=-3\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{sin}(3t+5)+8$. We must determine the values of $t$ for which $v_A > v_B$. Using the G.D.C. to sketch the graphs of $v_A=7$ and $v_B=-3\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{sin}(3t+5)+8$ for $0 \\leq t \\leq 10$, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Kinematics%20I-%20Differentiation/images/AA939b.PNG\" alt=\"AA939b\">\n\n    Between points A and B, $v_A > v_B$. There are in total 5 periods during which $v_A > v_B$, and due to the periodic nature of $v_B$, each has the same duration.\n\n    The total time for which $v_A > v_B$ is therefore found by:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    5 \\times (1.3616 - 0.54101) &= 4.103\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    For $0 \\leq t \\leq 10$, the total time that the velocity of particle $A$ is greater than the velocity of particle $B$ is approximately $4.10\\text{ seconds}$"}]},{"name":"L'Hopital's Rule","questions":[{"ref":"AA1182","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1182\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the function $y=x^{-2x}$.\n\n1.  Show $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2x^{-2x}(\\ln{x}+1)$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 1} \\left(\\frac{1-\\left(\\tfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)^x}{\\tfrac{1}{2}x(x-1)}\\right)$ [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let's first take the natural logarithm of both sides\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        y&=x^{-2x}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\ln{y}&=\\ln{x^{-2x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\ln{y}&=-2x\\ln{x} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now implicitly differentiate also using the product rule for the R.H.S.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{1}{y}\\times \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-2\\ln{x}-2x\\times\\frac{1}{x} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-2y(\\ln{x}+1)\\\\[12pt]\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{-2x^{-2x}(\\ln{x}+1)}\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  First attempt to evaluate the limit by substituting in $x=1$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\lim_{x \\to 1} \\left(\\frac{1-\\left(\\tfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)^x}{\\tfrac{1}{2}x(x-1)}\\right)&=\\left(\\frac{1-\\left(\\tfrac{1}{1^2}\\right)^1}{\\tfrac{1}{2}(1-1)}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\left(\\frac{1-1}{\\tfrac{1}{2}(1-1)}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\frac{0}{0} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We have an indeterminate form, therefore we can use L'Hôpital's rule, we also notice that we can rewrite the numerator in a form similar to part (a)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\lim_{x \\to 1} \\left(\\frac{1-\\left(\\tfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)^x}{\\tfrac{1}{2}x(x-1)}\\right)&=\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\left(\\frac{(1-x^{-2x})'}{\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}x(x-1)\\right)'}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the result of part (a) to differentiate the numerator. The derivative of the denominator can be found by expanding and then using the power rule.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\left(\\frac{0-(-2x^{-2x}(\\ln{x}+1))}{x-\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now attempt to evaluate the limit at $x=1$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\frac{0-(-2(1)^{-2(1)}(\\ln{1}+1))}{1-\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{2}{\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1278","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1278\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{\\cos(mx)-\\cos(nx)}{x^2}$ where $m,n \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe function has a maximum value of $f_{\\text{max}}$ and it is known that $\\displaystyle f_{\\text{max}}= \\lim_{x \\to 0} f(x)$.\n\n1.  Show that $\\displaystyle f_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{n^2-m^2}{2}$. [6 marks]\n\nIt is now known that $m>0$ and $n=4\\sqrt{m}$.\n\n2.  Hence, using these conditions find the largest possible value of $f_{\\text{max}}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To find $f_{\\text{max}}$ we need to evaluate $f(x)$ as $x \\rightarrow 0$. \n\n    \n\n    Hence we can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f_{\\text{max}}&=\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(\\frac{\\cos(mx)-\\cos(nx)}{x^2}\\right)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can begin by attempting to evaluate the limit using direct substitution of $x=0$, recall $\\cos{0}=1$.    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f_{\\text{max}}&= \\frac{\\cos(0)-\\cos(0)}{(0)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &= \\frac{1-1}{(0)^2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{0}{0}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$    \n\n    This is an indeterminate form. Hence we can use L'Hôpital's Rule to attempt to find the limit.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the standard derivative for cosine and the power rule we can differentiate the numerator and denominator to get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f_{\\text{max}}&=\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(\\frac{-m\\sin(mx)-(-n\\sin(nx))}{2x}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(\\frac{-m\\sin(mx)+n\\sin(nx)}{2x}\\right)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Again we can attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution of $x=0$\n\n    $$\n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f_{\\text{max}}&=\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(\\frac{-m\\sin(0)+n\\sin(0))}{2(0)}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{0}{0}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Again we have an indeterminate form. Let's try L'Hôpital's Rule one more time.\n\n    $$\n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f_{\\text{max}}&=\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(\\frac{-m^2\\cos(mx)+n^2\\cos(nx)}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    As $\\cos{0}=1$ we get \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\frac{-m^2+n^2}{2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\dfrac{n^2-m^2}{2} \\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  We can take the result from part (b) and replace $n$ with $4\\sqrt{m}$, this gives $f_{\\text{max}}$ in terms of $m$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f_{\\text{max}}&=\\frac{(4\\sqrt{m})^2-m^2}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This is a quadratic function in $m$. \n\n    \n\n    By completing the square we can write $f_\\text{max}$ in vertex form and find the coordinates of the maximum point. \n\n    \n\n    Note, although we are told to look for a maximum value we can also see that as the coefficient of $m^2$ is negative then the parabola will be concave down hence the vertex will be a maximum point.\n\n    When completing the square it is often easier to work with a positive squared term hence we will factorise by $-\\tfrac{1}{2}$ which gives \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f_{\\text{max}}&=-\\frac{1}{2}(m^2-16m)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can complete the square\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\left[(m-8)^2-64\\right]\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=-\\frac{1}{2}(m-8)^2+32\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence the largest value of $f_\\text{max}$ is $32$    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1404","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1404\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nUse L'Hópital's rule to determine the value of \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned} \n\n    \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{5\\left(x-\\arctan(x)\\right)}{x^3}\n\n\\end{aligned} \n\n$$","markScheme":"As we have been told to use L'Hópital's rule, we can go ahead and use it straight away.\n\nUsing L'Hópital's rule and independently differentiating the numerator and denominator, we get \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{5\\left(x-\\arctan(x)\\right)}{x^3}  &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{5\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\right)}{3x^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{5\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{1+x^2}\\right)}{3x^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\\\\[-10pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow through direct substitution we get \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{5x^2}{1+x^2} &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} 3x^2 &= 0 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWhich leads to $\\frac{0}{0}$ which is indeterminate form, hence, we can use L'Hópital's rule again with the quotient rule to obtain $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\tag*{{\\textbf{M1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{\\dfrac{5x^2}{1+x^2}}{3x^2}  &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{\\dfrac{(1+x^2)10x-5x^2(2x)}{(1+x^2)^2}}{6x} \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{\\dfrac{10x+10x^3-10x^3}{(1+x^2)^2}}{6x} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{\\dfrac{10x}{(1+x^2)^2}}{6x} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{10}{6(1+x^2)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{10}{6} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{5\\left(x-\\arctan(x)\\right)}{x^3} &=\\hspace{0.2em}\\dfrac{5}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA601","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA601\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1.  Use L'Hôpital's rule to determine the value of\n    $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{e^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5x) + 1}{4x^2}$.\n    [6 marks]\n\n2. Hence find $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{{\\displaystyle \\int_0^x \\hspace{-0.2em}\\big(e^{-5t^2} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5t) + 1\\big) \\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}t}}{{\\displaystyle \\int_0^x 4t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t}}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\big[e^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5x) + 1\\big] &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\big[4x^2\\big] &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    using L'Hôpital's rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{e^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5x) + 1}{4x^2}  &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{\\big[e^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5x) + 1\\big]'}{\\big[4x^2\\big]'} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{-10xe^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} + 10\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(5x)}{8x} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\big[\\hspace{-0.2em}-10xe^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} + 10\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(5x)\\big] &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\big[8x\\big] &= 0 \\hspace{1.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using L'Hôpital's rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{-10xe^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} + 10\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(5x)}{8x}  &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{\\big[\\hspace{-0.2em}-10xe^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} + 10\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(5x)\\big]'}{\\big[8x\\big]'} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{-10e^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} + 100x^2e^{-5x^2} + 50\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5x)}{8} \\\\[15pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-10(1) + 100(0)(1) + 50(1)}{8} \\\\[19pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\bigg[\\int_0^x \\hspace{-0.2em}\\Big(e^{-5t^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5t) + 1\\Big) \\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}t\\bigg] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\bigg[\\int_0^x 4t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\bigg] &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using L'Hôpital's rule, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.7em}{$\\displaystyle \\int_0^x \\hspace{-0.2em}\\left(e^{-5t^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5t) + 1\\right) \\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}t$}}{\\raisebox{-0.7em}{$\\displaystyle \\int_0^x 4t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$}} &=  \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.7em}{$\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\int_0^x \\hspace{-0.2em}\\left(e^{-5t^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5t) + 1\\right) \\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}t\\bigg]$}}{\\raisebox{-0.7em}{$\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\int_0^x 4t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\bigg]$}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{e^{-5x^2} \\hspace{-0.2em} - 2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(5x) + 1}{4x^2} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by FTC}] \\\\[24pt]\n    &= 5 \\hspace{2.75em} [\\text{by part (a)}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA604","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA604\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by\n$$\nf(t) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\nat^2 + bt, & t \\leq 4, \\\\[9pt]\n|t-4| + 2, & t > 4.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants.\n\nGiven that both $f$ and its derivative are continuous at $t = 4$, find\nthe value of $a$ and the value of $b$.","markScheme":"Evaluating the left-sided limit of $f$ at $t = 4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.2em} \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^-} f(t) &= \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^-} (at^2 + bt) \\\\[6pt]\n&= a(4)^2 + b(4) \\\\[11pt]\n&= 16a + 4b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nEvaluating the right-sided limit of $f$ at\n$t = 4$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.25em} \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^+} f(t) &= \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^+} (|t-4| + 2) \\\\[6pt]\n&= |4-4| + 2 \\\\[11pt]\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the continuity of $f$ at $t = 4$, we\ndeduce\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^-} f(t) &= \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^+} f(t) \\\\[6pt]\n16a + 4b &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor $t > 4$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.2em} |t-4| + 2 &= (t-4) + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= t-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, differentiating $f$ with respect to $t$, we\nfind\n$$\nf'(t) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\n2at + b, & t \\leq 4, \\\\[9pt]\n1, & t > 4.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nEvaluating the left-sided limit of $f'$ at\n$t = 4$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.6em} \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^-} f'(t) &= \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^-} (2at + b) \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2a(4) + b \\\\[11pt]\n&= 8a + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nEvaluating the right-sided limit of $f'$ at\n$t = 4$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^+} f'(t) &= \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^+} 1 \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the continuity of $f'$ at $t = 4$, we\ndeduce\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^-} f'(t) &= \\lim_{t \\rightarrow 4^+} f'(t) \\\\[6pt]\n8a + b &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, solving the system of\nlinear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$ and $b$, we find\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n a = \\dfrac{1}{8}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}b = 0\n \\end{aligned}\n $$"},{"ref":"AA609","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA609\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nUse L'Hôpital's rule to determine the value of\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\sin^2(x)}{\\ln(1+x^2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} 2\\sin^2(x)&= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\ln(1+x^2) &= 0\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using\nL'Hôpital's rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{2\\sin^2(x)}{\\ln(1+x^2)}  &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{4\\sin(x)\\cos(x)}{\\dfrac{2x\\rule[0pt]{0pt}{3mm}}{1+x^2}} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{(1+x^2)2\\sin(2x)}{2x} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} (1+x^2)2\\sin(2x) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} 2x &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using L'Hôpital's rule, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{(1+x^2)2\\sin(2x)}{2x}  &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{(1+x^2)4\\cos(2x)+4x\\sin(2x)}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{(1+0^2)4\\cos(0)+4(0)\\sin(0)}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{4}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA812","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA812\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUse L'Hôpital's rule to determine the value of\n$\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{x^2e^{2x}}{1 - \\cos x}$","markScheme":"Since $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} x^2e^{2x} = 0$ and\n$\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} (1 - \\cos x) = 0$, we use L'Hôpital's rule\nand get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{x^2e^{2x}}{1 - \\cos x} &= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left[x^2e^{2x}\\right]'}{[1 - \\cos x]'} \\\\[12pt]\n&= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2xe^{2x} + x^2 \\cdot 2e^{2x}}{-(-\\sin x)} \\\\[12pt]\n&= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^{2x} (2x + 2x^2)}{\\sin x}\\\\[5pt]\\\\\n\\intertext{Since $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  e^{2x} (2x + 2x^2) = 0$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\sin x = 0$, we use L'H\\^opital's rule again:}\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^{2x} (2x + 2x^2)}{\\sin x} &= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left[e^{2x} (2x + 2x^2)\\right]'}{[\\sin x]'} \\\\[12pt]\n&= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2e^{2x}(2x+2x^2) + e^{2x} (2 + 4x) }{\\cos x} \\\\[12pt]\n&= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^{2x} (4x + 4x^2 + 2 + 4x)}{\\cos x} \\\\[12pt]\n&= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^{2x} (4x^2 + 8x + 2)}{\\cos x} \\\\[12pt]\n&= \\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^{2(0)} \\left[4(0)^2 + 8(0) + 2 \\right]}{\\cos (0)} \\\\[12pt]\n&\\hspace{0.525em}= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA885","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA885\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nUse L'Hôpital's rule to find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{\\arctan(3x)}{\\tan(4x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\hspace{0.25em}\\arctan(3x)&= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\hspace{0.25em}\\tan(4x) &= 0\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using L'Hôpital's rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0}\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{\\arctan(3x)}{\\tan(4x)} &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0}\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{\\left[\\arctan(3x)\\right]'}{\\left[\\tan(4x)\\right]'}\\\\[12pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0}\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{\\dfrac{3}{1+9x^2}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9.5mm}}{4\\sec^2(4x)} \\\\[12pt]\n&= \\dfrac{\\hspace{-0.4em}\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0}\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{3}{1+9x^2}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9.5mm}}{\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0}\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{4}{\\cos^2(4x)}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9.5mm}} \\\\[13pt]\n&\\hspace{0.3em} =\\dfrac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA938","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA938\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider \n\n$\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0}} \\dfrac{2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^x)-c}{3x}$\n\nwhere $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. Show that a finite limit only exists for $c = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Using l'H$\\hat{\\text{o}}$pital's rule, show algebraically that the value of the limit is $\\dfrac{1}{3}.$ [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Given that $3x\\rightarrow{0}$ as $x\\rightarrow{0}$, a finite limit can only exist if the numerator also approaches $0$, so that the limit takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}$.\n\n    We must have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x \\to 0} 2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^x)-c &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting the value $x=0$ directly, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^0)-c &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(1)-c &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(1) &= c \\\\[10pt]\n\n    2\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\bigg) &= c \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &  \\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{c = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. According to l'H$\\hat{\\text{o}}$pital's rule, for the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}$,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x \\to c} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} &= \\lim_{x \\to c} \\dfrac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Letting $f(x)=2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^x)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $g(x)=3x$, we find the derivatives.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x) &= 2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^x)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{2e^x}{1+e^{2x}} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    g(x) &= 3x \\\\[8pt]\n\n    g'(x) &= 3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^x)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}{3x} &= \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\dfrac{2e^x}{1+e^{2x}}}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{2e^x}{3(1+e^{2x})}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting the value $x=0$ directly on the RHS, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^x)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}{3x} &= \\dfrac{2e^0}{3(1+e^{2(0)})} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3(1+1)} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &  \\hspace{-9.25em}\\boxed{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(e^x)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}{3x} = \\dfrac{1}{3}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Optimization","questions":[{"ref":"AA1049","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1049\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA semicircle with centre $(0,0)$ and radius $4$ has a triangle, $\\triangle$\\,ABC, inscribed as shown in the figure below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/question.png\" alt=\"question\">\n\nThe equation of the curve is given by $y=\\sqrt{a-x^2}$, where $a \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1. Find the coordinates of point B in terms of $x$ only. [2 marks]\n\n2. Hence, find an expression for the area of triangle ABC, in terms of $x$. [2 marks]\n\n3. Show that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}(\\text{Area})}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{8-2x-x^2}{\\sqrt{16-x^2}}$. [3 marks]\n\n4. Hence or otherwise, find the coordinates for B that maximize the area. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  First lets find $a$, we can see from point $A$ that when $x=4$ then $y=0$. Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=\\sqrt{a-(-4)^2}\\\\\n        a&=(-4)^2\\\\\n        a&=16 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $y=\\sqrt{16-x^2}$\n\n    Hence, point B has coordinates $(x,y)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\quad \\text{B}\\left(x,\\sqrt{16-x^2} \\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Area }\\triangle \\text{ABC} &= \\tfrac{1}{2}\\text{base} \\times \\text \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\tfrac{1}{2}\\big(x-(-4)\\big)\\left( \\sqrt{16-x^2} \\right) \\\\[7pt]\n    & \\hspace{0.28em}= \\tfrac{1}{2}(x+4)\\left( \\sqrt{16-x^2} \\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We need to use the product rule, first differentiate both functions of\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (x+4)'&=1 &\\left((16-x^2)^{\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\right)'=-2x\\times\\tfrac{1}{2}(16-x^2)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\textcolor{white}{gap} &=-x(16-x^2)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now using the product rule\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}(\\text{Area})}{\\mathrm{d}x} &=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left(1\\times(16-x^2)^{\\tfrac{1}{2}}+-x(16-x^2)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}(x+4)\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{(16-x^2)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}}{2}\\left((16-x^2)-x(x+4)\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{(16-x^2)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}}{2}\\left(16-4x-2x^2\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.28em}= \\dfrac{8-2x-x^2}{\\sqrt{16-x^2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4. The extreme is attained at points where the derivative is zero. Hence,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{8-2x-x^2}{\\sqrt{16-x^2}} &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[7pt]\n        -(x^2+2x-8) &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[7pt]\n        -(x+4)(x-2) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n        x &= -4,2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Making a sketch of $y=-(x+4)(x-2)$ we can see that for the red part of the curve $y$ is positive and the green part $y$ is negative.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/Screenshot%202024-03-08%20at%2010.43.09%20AM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-03-08 at 10.43.09 AM\">\n\n    Using the first derivative test, we get\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/Screenshot%202024-03-08%20at%2010.43.12%20AM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-03-08 at 10.43.12 AM\">\n\n    The sign of the first derivative changes from positive to negative at $x=2$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $y$ for $x=2$, we find $y=\\sqrt{16-2^2} = \\sqrt{12} = 2\\sqrt{3}$.\n\n    Therefore, the are is maximum at $\\text{B}\\left(2,2\\sqrt{3}\\right)$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1414","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1414\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe diagram shows a cone with a volume of $\\displaystyle \\frac{8\\pi}{3}\\,\\text{cm}^3$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/cone.JPEG\" alt=\"cone\">\n\nThe radius of the circular base is $r\\,$cm and the vertical height is $h\\,$cm.\nThe cone is open, so only the curved surface is present.\nThe curved surface area of the cone is $A\\,\\text{cm}^2$.\n\n1.    Show that $\\displaystyle A=\\pi\\sqrt{r^4+\\frac{64}{r^2}}$. [3 marks]\n\nThe minimum value of $A$ occurs when $r=2^k$, where $k \\in \\mathrm{Q}$.\n\n2.    Find $k$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have been given a cone with a volume of\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{8\\pi}{3}\\,\\text{cm}^3,\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    radius $r\\,\\text{cm}$, and height $h\\,\\text{cm}$.\n\n    The __formula for the volume of a cone (3.1)__ is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V\n    =\n    \\frac13\\pi r^2h.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the volume given in the question, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{8\\pi}{3}\n    &=\n    \\frac13\\pi r^2h.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Making $h$ the subject gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h\n    =\n    \\frac{8}{r^2}.\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The cone is open, so only the curved surface contributes to the surface area.\n\n    Using the __formula for the curved surface area of a cone (3.1)__\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A\n    =\n    \\pi rl,\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $l$ is the slant height.\n    Using Pythagoras' theorem,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    l\n    &=\n    \\sqrt{r^2+h^2}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting for $h$ gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=\\pi r \\sqrt{r^2+\\left( \\frac{8}{r^2} \\right)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\pi\\sqrt{r^2\\left(r^2+\\frac{64}{r^4}\\right)}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\displaystyle \\pi\\sqrt{r^4+\\frac{64}{r^2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. To find the minimum value of $A$ we must solve\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm dA}{\\mathrm dr}\n    =\n    0\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let us begin by differentiating\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A\n    =\n    \\pi\n    \\left(\n    r^4+\\frac{64}{r^2}\n    \\right)^{1/2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The function is raised to the power $\\dfrac12$, therefore we must use the __chain rule (5.6)__.\n    Before doing so, let\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u\n    &=\n    r^4+\\frac{64}{r^2}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\n    r^4+64r^{-2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Then\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A\n    =\n    \\pi u^{1/2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the __formula for the chain rule (5.6)__\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm dA}{\\mathrm dr}\n    =\n    \\frac{\\mathrm dA}{\\mathrm du}\n    \\times\n    \\frac{\\mathrm du}{\\mathrm dr}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Differentiating $u^{1/2}$ with respect to $u$ using the __power rule (5.3)__ gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm du}\n    \\left(\n    \\pi u^{1/2}\n    \\right)\n    =\n    \\frac{\\pi}{2}u^{-1/2}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm du}{\\mathrm dr}\n    &=\n    4r^3+64(-2)r^{-3}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\n    4r^3-\\frac{128}{r^3}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting these results into the chain rule formula gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm dA}{\\mathrm dr}\n    &=\n    \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n    u^{-1/2}\n    \\left(\n    4r^3-\\frac{128}{r^3}\n    \\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\n    \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\left(\n    r^4+\\frac{64}{r^2}\n    \\right)^{-1/2}\n    \\left(\n    4r^3-\\frac{128}{r^3}\n    \\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we need to solve\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\left(\n    r^4+\\frac{64}{r^2}\n    \\right)^{-1/2}\n    \\left(\n    4r^3-\\frac{128}{r^3}\n    \\right)\n    =\n    0\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The first two factors can never be zero, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4r^3-\\frac{128}{r^3}\n    &=\n    0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving this gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4r^6\n    &=\n    128\\\\[6pt]\n    r^6\n    &=\n    32\\\\[6pt]\n    r^6\n    &=2^5\\\\[6pt]\n    r&=2^{\\frac{5}{6}}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since the question states that there is a minimum value of $A$, and $r>0$ then this stationary point must correspond to the minimum.\n\n    Therefore the minimum value of $A$ occurs when $r=2^{\\frac{5}{6}}$.\n\n    Therefore $k=\\dfrac{5}{6}$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-30pt]\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textcolor{black}{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA242","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA242\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nIn a triangle ABC, $\\text{B\\^{A}C} = \\ang{60}$, $\\text{AB} = (1-x)$ cm,\n$\\text{AC} = (x + 3)^2$ cm, $-3 < x < 1$.\n\n1.  Show that the area, $A$ cm$^2$, of the triangle is given by\n    $$\n    A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(9 - 3x - 5x^2 - x^3).\n\n    $$\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n    2.  Verify that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0$ when\n        $x = -\\dfrac{1}{3}$.  [3 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2A}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}$ and hence verify\n        that $x = -\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives the maximum area of\n\n        triangle ABC.\n\n    2.  Calculate the maximum area of triangle ABC.\n    3.  Find the length of \\[BC\\] when the area of triangle ABC is a\n        maximum. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} A &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(1-x)(x+3)^2 \\sin \\ang{60} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(1-x)(x^2+6x+9) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[9-3x-5x^2-x^3\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Differentiating the expression $(1)$ with respect to $x$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.1em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[9-3x-5x^2-x^3\\big]\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[\\hspace{-0.1em}-3-10x-3x^2\\big] \\\\[9pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[3 + 10x + 3x^2\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating the expression $(2)$ for $x = -1/3$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.7em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[3 + 10(-1/3) + 3(-1/3)^2\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[3 - 10/3 + 1/3\\big] \\\\[14pt]\n        &= 0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  Differentiating the expression $(2)$ with respect to $x$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2A}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\bigg[\\hspace{-0.1em}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[3 + 10x + 3x^2\\big]\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\big[10+6x\\big] \\\\[9pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\big[5+3x\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Evaluating the expression $(3)$ for $x = -1/3$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2A}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\big[5 + 3(-1/3)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\[11.5pt]\n        &< 0 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, by the Second Derivative Test, $x = -1/3$ gives the maximum area of triangle ABC.\n\n    2.  Evaluating the expression $(1)$ for $x = -1/3$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.6em} A &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(1-x)(x+3)^2 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(1-(-1/3))(-1/3+3)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\approx 4.11 \\text{ cm}^2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the cosine rule (with $x = -1/3$), we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\text{BC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{AC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{(1-x)^2 + (x+3)^4 - 2(1-x)(x+3)^2\\cos \\ang{60}} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{(4/3)^2 + (8/3)^4 - 2(4/3)(8/3)^2\\cos \\ang{60}} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\text{BC} \\approx 6.55 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{13.75em}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA477","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA477\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nFarmer Thomas wants to build a sheep farming field in the shape of a\nrectangle with semicircles of radius $r$ on two sides, as shown on the\ndiagram. He has decided to use in total $350$ metres of wooden fencing.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/3892b0192f3c881f07dfd8fd9a4e829c08a254c5.svg\" alt=\"3892b0192f3c881f07dfd8fd9a4e829c08a254c5.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Find an expression for the area of the farming field in terms of\n        $r$.\n\n    2.  Find the width of the farming field when the area is a maximum.\n        [9 marks]\n\n2.  Show that in this case the length of the rectangle is equal to zero\n    and the farming field is the\n    circle of radius $175\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi^{-1}$ metres. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Let $l$ and $w$ be the length and width of the rectangular part\n        of the field.\n\n        The perimeter of the field is fixed at $350$ metres.\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        P &= 2\\pi r + 2l \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        350 &= 2\\pi r + 2l \\\\[6pt]\n        175 &= \\pi r + l \\\\[6pt]\n        l &= 175 - \\pi r\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the area of the field is\n        given by\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.5em} A &= \\pi r^2 + lw \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\pi r^2 + (175 - \\pi r)(2r) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\pi r^2 + 350r - 2\\pi r^2 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 350r - \\pi r^2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Differentiating $A$ with respect to $r$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.4em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}r}\\big[350r - \\pi r^2\\big] \\\\[9pt]\n        &= 350 - 2\\pi r\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Solving the equation\n        $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r} = 0$ for $r$, we obtain\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        350 - 2\\pi r &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        175 - \\pi r &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        r &= \\dfrac{175}{\\pi}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence\n        $r = 175\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi^{-1}$ is the only critical number of\n        $A$.\n\n        Therefore, $A$ is a maximum when\n        $r = 175\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi^{-1}$.\n        Evaluating $w = 2r$ for $r = 175\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi^{-1}$, we\n        find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        w &= 2\\times \\dfrac{175}{\\pi} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{350}{\\pi} \\text{ metres} \\hspace{0.65em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $l = 175 - \\pi r$ for $r = 175\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi^{-1}$,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} l &= 175 - \\pi \\times \\dfrac{175}{\\pi} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 175 - 175 \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 0 \\text{ metres} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the length of the rectangular part of the field is zero.\n\n    Therefore the field is a circle of radius\n    $175\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi^{-1}$ metres."},{"ref":"AA538","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA538\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA closed cylindrical can with radius $r$ cm and height $h$ cm has a\nvolume of $24\\pi$ cm$^3$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/b2d9004fe101620336600f8d88cc9fbb24300180.svg\" alt=\"b2d9004fe101620336600f8d88cc9fbb24300180.svg\">\n\n1.  Express $h$ in terms of $r$. [2 marks]\n\nThe material for the base and top of the can costs $15$ cents per cm$^2$\nand the material for the curved side costs $10$ cents per cm$^2$. The\ntotal cost of the material, in cents, is $C$.\n\n2.  Show that $C = 30\\pi r^2 + \\dfrac{480\\pi}{r}$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Given that there is a minimum value for $C$, find this\n    minimum value in terms of $\\pi$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the volume formula for the cylinder $V = \\pi r^2 h$ with\n    $V = 24 \\pi$ cm$^3$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    24 \\pi  & = \\pi r^2 h\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-.2cm} h = \\dfrac {24} {r^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The area of the base and top is $2 \\pi r^2$ cm$^2$ and the area of\n    the curved side is $2\\pi r h$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8cm}\n    C   & = 15 (2\\pi r^2) + 10(2 \\pi r h)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 30 \\pi r^2 + 20 \\pi r h\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 30 \\pi r^2 + 20 \\pi r \\left (\\dfrac {24} {r^2}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} = 30 \\pi r^2 + \\dfrac {480 \\pi} r\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let's differentiate $C$ as a function of $r$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    C' (r)      & = 60 \\pi r - \\dfrac {480 \\pi}{r^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    0       & = 60 \\pi r - \\dfrac {480 \\pi}{r^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac {480 \\pi} {r^2}  & = 60 \\pi r\\\\[6pt]\n    r^3                     & = 8\\\\[6pt]\n    r                   & = 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, the minimum value of\n    $C(r)$ occurs when $r = 2$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5mm}\n    C(2)        & = 30 \\pi (2^2) + \\dfrac {480 \\pi} 2\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 120\\pi + 240 \\pi\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}  = 360\\pi\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA545","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA545\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nJack makes an open container in the shape of a cuboid with square base,\nas shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/7c5e6537084929c30c7c2d4d621b774227d60004.svg\" alt=\"7c5e6537084929c30c7c2d4d621b774227d60004.svg\">\n\nThe container has base length $x$ m and height $y$ m. The volume is $32$\nm$^3$.\n\nLet $A(x)$ be the outside surface area of the container.\n\n1.  Show that $A(x) = \\dfrac{128}{x} + x^2$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $A'(x)$.  [2 marks]\n\n3.  Given that the outside surface area is a minimum, find the base\n    length of the container.  [4 marks]\n\n4.  Jack coats the outside of the container with waterproof resin. A can\n    of resin covers a surface area of $5$ m$^2$ and costs\n    $$\\$\\hspace{0.05em}15$$. Find the total cost of the cans needed to\n    coat the container. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The volume of the cuboid is $x^2 y = 32$ m$^3$, hence\n    $y = \\dfrac {32} {x^2}$.\n\n    The outside area of the container consists of a square base and four\n    equal sides (rectangles), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A(x)        & = x^2 + 4 xy\\\\[6pt]\n    & = x^2 + 4x \\dfrac {32} {x^2}\\\\[8pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} = \\dfrac {128} x + x^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n     A'(x)  = 2 x - \\dfrac {128} {x^2}\\hspace{2mm}\n    $$\n\n3.  The minimum occurs when $A'(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2 x - \\dfrac {128} {x^2}    & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    2 x                     & = \\dfrac {128} {x^2}\\hspace{1.6cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    x ^3                    & = 64\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2mm}x = 4\\,\\mbox{m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The area that Jack needs to cover is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A(4)        & = 4^2 + \\dfrac {128} 4\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 48\\,\\mbox {m}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This area requires\n    $\\dfrac {48} 5 \\approx 10$ cans of resin, which cost is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mbox{Cost}     & = 10 (15)\\hspace{3mm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} = \\$150\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA578","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA578\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA farmer wants to build a rectangular enclosure for his chickens. The\narea of the enclosure must be $350$ m$^2$. The fencing used for the side\n$AB$ costs \\$13 per metre. The fencing for the other three sides costs\n\\$4 per metre. The farmer wants the cost of the enclosure to be a\nminimum. Find the minimum cost.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/85385e28e826f6afebd300b35460cb2bf58db5e7.svg\" alt=\"85385e28e826f6afebd300b35460cb2bf58db5e7.svg\">","markScheme":"Let's denote $x = AB = DC$ and $y = AD = BC$. The area is $xy = 350$\nand\\\n$y = \\dfrac {350} x$. Then the cost, $C$, is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC   & = 13x + 4x + 4y + 4y\\\\[6pt]\nC   & = 17x + 8y\\\\[6pt]\nC   & = 17x + 8 \\left(\\dfrac{350}x\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-7mm}C_{\\min}      = \\$436\n\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA590","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA590\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = 24 - 2 x^2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb R$. The following diagram\nshows part of the graph of $f$ and the rectangle OPQR, where R is on the\npositive $x$-axis, P is on the positive $y$-axis and Q is on the graph of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/fc8d9b54a495190d0f0425a2c9aaaafa45786244.svg\" alt=\"fc8d9b54a495190d0f0425a2c9aaaafa45786244.svg\">\n\nFind the coordinates of point R$(x,y)$ that gives the maximum area of\nOPQR.","markScheme":"Let's denote $x =$ OR and $y =$ OP. Hence the area is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5mm}\nA(x)        & = xy\\\\[6pt]\n& = x(24 - 2x^2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe maximum of $A$ occurs when\n$A'(x) = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA'(x)   & = 24 - 6 x^2\\\\[6pt]\n0       & = 24 - 6x^2\\\\[6pt]\n6x^2    & = 24\\\\[6pt]\nx^2         & = 4\\\\[6pt]\nx       & = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore we find\n  $\\mbox R(2,0)$"},{"ref":"AA655","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA655\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 16-x^2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find the $x$-intercepts of the graph of $f$. [2 marks]\n\nThe following diagram shows part of the graph of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Optimization/images/d5c58a7cfd2c4ba4ba9bf55d5d266b5503182c5c.svg\" alt=\"d5c58a7cfd2c4ba4ba9bf55d5d266b5503182c5c.svg\">\n\nRectangle ABCD is drawn with A & B on the $x$-axis and C & D on the\ngraph of $f$.\n\nLet $\\text{OA} = a$.\n\n2.  Show that the area of ABCD is $32a - 2a^3$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence find the value of $a > 0$ such that the area of ABCD\n    is a maximum. [4 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = (x-4)^2 + k$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $k$ is a\nconstant.\n\n4.  Show that when the graphs of $f$ and $g$ intersect,\n    $2x^2-8x+k = 0$. [2 marks]\n\n5.  Given that the graphs of $f$ and $g$ intersect only once,\n    find the value of $k$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    16 - x^2 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    (4 - x)(4 + x) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= \\pm 4 \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the $x$-intercepts\n    are\n     $\\text{P}(-4,0)$\n    and  $\\text{Q}(4,0)$\n\n2.  Using the area of a rectangle formula, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\text{shaded}} &= \\text{(AB)(AD)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.05em}\\big(2a\\big)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(16-a^2\\big) \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 32a - 2a^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Hence, solving the equation\n    $\\big[A_{\\text{shaded}}\\big]' \\hspace{-0.15em}= 0$ for $a > 0$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\big[32a - 2a^3\\big]' \\hspace{-0.15em} &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\n    32 - 6a^2 &= 0  \\\\[11pt]\n    \\dfrac{32}{6} &= a^2 \\\\[11pt]\n    a &= \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16}{3}} \\\\[11pt]\n    a &= \\pm {\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}} \\\\[11pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.75em} a = \\dfrac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $a > 0$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Rearranging terms in the equation $f(x) = g(x)$, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} 16 - x^2 &= (x-4)^2 + k \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cancel{16} - x^2 &= (x^2 - 8x + \\cancel{16}) + k \\\\[6pt]\n    0 &= (x^2 - 8x) + x^2 + k \\\\\n    &\\hspace{-5.8em}2x^2 - 8x + k = 0 \\hspace{4.95em} \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Given that the graphs of $f$ & $g$ intersect exactly once, from part\n    (d), we know the equation $(1)$ has exactly one solution.\n\n    Hence the quadratic equation $2x^2 - 8x + k=0$ has only one\n    solution.\n\n    Therefore we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $2x^2 - 8x + k$}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    (-8)^2 - 4(2)(k) &= 0 \\hspace{3em}  [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n    64 - 8k &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    64 &= 8k \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5.1em} k = 8\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Related Rates of Change","questions":[{"ref":"AA1066","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1066\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nThe shape $OAB$ shows a quarter circle with a radius of $8$ centimetres. The vertex $C$ lies on $OB$ and the vertex $D$ lies on the arc $AB$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/aa1066a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1066a\">\n\n$\\angle OCD$ is $90^\\circ$ and $\\angle COD$ is $\\theta$.\n\nThe length $CB$ is $h$.\n\n \n\n1. Find the following in terms of $h$.\n\n    \n\n    1. $OC$\n\n    \n\n    2. $CD$[3 marks]\n\n    \n\n2. Hence, show that the area of the region $CBD$ is \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A_{CDB}&=32\\arccos{\\left(\\frac{8-h}{8}\\right)}+\\tfrac{1}{2}(h-8)\\sqrt{16h-h^2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    [5 marks]\n\nA rain drainage gutter is modelled by a prism with the cross-sectional area $A_{CBD}$ and a length of $1\\textbf{ metre}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/aa1066b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1066b\">\n\nOn a dry day, in which it rained over night, the gutter was partially filled with water and the depth of it was is given by $h$. \n\nDuring the day water flowed out of the bottom of the gutter at a rate of $250\\sqrt{h}$ cm$^3$ per second.\n\n3. Find the rate of change of $h$ when the depth of the water is $3$ cm.[9 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. We have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            OB &= 8 \\hspace{2em} \\text{ therefore } \\hspace{2em} OC = \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{8-h} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. We know that $OD=8$, therefore using Pythagoras' theorem we can write\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        CD^2&=8^2-(8-h)^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        CD&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\sqrt{16h-h^2}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. We must find the area of sector $ODB$ then subtract from it the area of $\\triangle OCD$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the the results from part (a) we can know the area of $\\triangle OCD=\\tfrac{1}{2}(8-h)\\sqrt{16h-h^2}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The area of the sector $ODB$ is $\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 8^2 \\times \\theta$, hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A_{CDB} &=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 8^2 \\times \\theta - \\tfrac{1}{2}(8-h)\\sqrt{16h-h^2}\\\\\n\n        &=32\\theta + \\tfrac{1}{2}(h-8)\\sqrt{16h-h^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using $\\triangle OCD$ we can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\cos{\\theta}&=\\frac{8-h}{8} \\hspace{2em} \\text{ hence}\\\\\n\n        \\theta&=\\arccos{\\left(\\frac{8-h}{8}\\right)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\hspace{0.525em}&\\boxed{A_{CDB}=32\\arccos{\\left(\\frac{8-h}{8}\\right)} + \\tfrac{1}{2}(h-8)\\sqrt{16h-h^2}} \\hspace{2em} \\text{as required} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. First we should find the volume of rain in gutter in terms of $h$. \n\n    \n\n    The gutter is a prism with a cross-sectional area $CDB$ therefore, using the depth of the prism to be $100$ cm we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        V&=100 \\times A_{CDB}\\\\\n\n        &=3200\\arccos{\\left(\\frac{8-h}{8}\\right)} + 50(h-8)\\sqrt{16h-h^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We want to find $\\frac{dh}{dt}$ when $h=3$ cm. \n\n    Using the chain rule we can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{dh}{dt}&=\\frac{dh}{dV} \\times \\frac{dV}{dt} \\tag*{(1)}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    When $h=3$, we know that\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tfrac{dV}{dt}&=-250\\sqrt{3}\\\\\n\n        &=-433.0127...\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence substituting this into equation $(1)$ we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{dh}{dt}&=\\frac{dh}{dV} \\times -433.0127...\\tag*{(2)}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the G.D.C and the formula we found for $V$ we can find \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\left. \\frac{dV}{dh} \\right|_{h=3}=624.49... \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{dh}{dV} &=\\frac{1}{624.49...}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &=0.0016012...\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting this into equation $(2)$ gives\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{dh}{dt}&=0.0016012... \\times -433.0127...\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n\n        &=-0.693375...\\\\\n\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{-0.693 \\text{ cm/second}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1386","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1386\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nAn inverted conical funnel is being filled with water. The funnel has a semi-vertical angle of $30^{\\circ}$. Let $h$ be the depth of the water and $r$ be the radius of the water surface at time $t$.\n\nThis is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/AA1386.png\" alt=\"AA1386\">\n\nWater flows into the funnel at a constant rate of $\\pi \\text{ cm}^3 \\text{s}^{-1}$.\n\n1. Show that the volume of water in the funnel, $V$, at time $t$, can be expressed as $V = \\frac{\\pi}{9}h^3$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Find the rate of change of the water's depth when the depth is $3 \\text{ cm}$. [5 marks]\n\n3. Let $A$ be the area of the curved surface of the cone that is in contact with the water. Find the rate of change of $A$ when the depth is $3 \\text{ cm}$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. We can start by using **the formula for the volume of a right cone (3.1)**.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        V &= \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This is expressed in terms of two variables, $r$ and $h$. We need an expression in terms of $h$ only, so we need to express $r$ in terms of $h$.\n\n    To do this, we could note from the right-angled triangle in the diagram that the ratio of $r$ to $h$ is always $\\tan 30^{\\circ}$. That is, \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tan 30^{\\circ} &= \\frac{r}{h}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$  \n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        r &= h \\tan 30^{\\circ}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Further, we could note that $\\tan 30^{\\circ} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ so we have $r = \\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{3}}$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting this expression into our volume formula for the cone in place of $r$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    V &= \\frac{1}{3}\\pi \\left(\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2 h \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    V &= \\frac{1}{3}\\pi \\left(\\frac{h^2}{3}\\right) h \\\\[6pt]\n\n    V &= \\hspace{0.5em} \\frac{\\pi}{9}h^3 \\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required.} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. We want to find a value for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $h=3$.\n\n    We were given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\pi$ and we have an expression for $V$ in terms of $h$ from part (a). We can differentiate this expression with respect to $h$ to obtain an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h}$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        V &= \\frac{\\pi}{9}h^3 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h} &= \\frac{\\pi}{3}h^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\frac{\\pi h^2}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can now use related rates of change to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t}$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}V} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\pi \\times \\frac{3}{\\pi h^2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{3}{h^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that we took the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h}$ to use in this equation.\n\n    Substituting $h=3$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{3}{3^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\hspace{0.5em}\\frac{1}{3}\\, \\text{cm}\\,\\text{s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using \\textbf{the formula for the curved surface area of a cone (3.1)}, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A &= \\pi r l\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This is at present expressed in terms of the variables $r$ and $l$, but ideally we'd like it expressed in terms of the variable $h$ only. \n\n    Referring to our diagram,\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/aa1386c.png\" alt=\"aa1386c\">\n\n    We could use the Pythagorean theorem to express $l$ in terms of $h$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        l &= \\sqrt{h^2 + r^2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\sqrt{h^2 + \\left(\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\sqrt{h^2 + \\dfrac{h^2}{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4h^2}{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        l &= \\dfrac{2h}{\\sqrt{3}} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We know from our work in part (a) that $r=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\n\n    Substituting our expressions for $r$ and $l$ into the area formula, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A &= \\pi \\left(\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\left(\\frac{2h}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\[6pt] \n\n        &= \\frac{2 \\pi h^2}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    If we differentiate this with respect to $h$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n       \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}h} &= \\frac{4 \\pi h}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    When $h=3$,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}h} &= \\frac{4 \\pi (3)}{3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 4\\pi \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We are looking for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $h=3$. As we did in part (b), we can set up a related rates equation. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}h} \\times \\frac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting in our value for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}h}$, and our value for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ from part (b), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 4\\pi \\times \\frac{1}{3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\hspace{0.5em}\\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\text{ cm}^2 \\text{s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1387","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1387\n\n**Maximum mark: 13** \n\nAn observer stands at point $O$, $300 \\text{ m}$ horizontally from a launch pad $L$. A rocket is launched vertically upwards from $L$. Let $h$ be the height of the rocket above the ground and $\\theta$ be the angle of elevation of the rocket from the observer at time $t$.\n\nThe rocket rises such that its vertical speed is constant at $40 \\text{ m s}^{-1}$.\n\n1. Express $h$ in terms of $\\theta$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Find the rate of change of the angle of elevation, $\\theta$, when the rocket is at a height of $400 \\text{ m}$. Give your answer in radians per second. [7 marks]\n\n3. Let $s$ be the straight-line distance from the observer to the rocket. Find the rate of change of $s$ when the height of the rocket is $400 \\text{ m}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. From the right-angled triangle formed by the observer, the launch pad, and the rocket:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/AA1387a.png\" alt=\"AA1387a\">\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tan \\theta &= \\frac{h}{300} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    h &= 300 \\tan \\theta \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. We want to find a value for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $h=400$.\n\n    We were given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 40$ and we have an expression for $h$ in terms of $\\theta$ from part (a). Using **the formula for the derivative of tan *x* (5.15)**, we can differentiate this expression with respect to $\\theta$ to obtain an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        h &= 300 \\tan \\theta \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta} &= 300 \\sec^2 \\theta \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can now use related rates of change to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t}$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}h} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 40 \\times \\frac{1}{300 \\sec^2 \\theta} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{2 \\cos^2 \\theta}{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that we took the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}$ to use in this equation.\n\n    To substitute $h=400$, we first need to evaluate $\\cos^2 \\theta$ at this height. \n\n    When $h = 400$, the right-angled triangle has sides adjacent $= 300$ and opposite $= 400$.\n\n    By the Pythagorean theorem, the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{300^2 + 400^2} = 500$ (a 3-4-5 triangle). \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\cos \\theta &= \\frac{\\text{adj}}{\\text{hyp}} = \\frac{300}{500} = \\frac{3}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\cos^2 \\theta &= \\frac{9}{25} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $\\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{9}{25}$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{2}{15} \\left(\\frac{9}{25}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\frac{18}{375} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{6}{125} \\text{ rad s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using the Pythagorean theorem, we can relate distance $s$ and height $h$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        s^2 &= 300^2 + h^2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Differentiating with respect to time $t$ (implicit differentiation), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2s \\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 2h \\frac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        s \\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= h \\frac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    When $h = 400$, we know from part (b) that $s = 500$.\n\n    We are given $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 40$.\n\n    Substituting these values:\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        500 \\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 400(40) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        500 \\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 16000 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{16000}{500} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 32 \\text{ m s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1388","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1388\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA hemispherical bowl of radius $6 \\text{ cm}$ is initially empty. Water is poured into the bowl at a constant rate of $2\\pi \\text{ cm}^3 \\, \\text{s}^{-1}$.\n\nThe volume of water, $V \\text{ cm}^3$, in a hemispherical bowl of radius $R$ when the water depth is $h$ is given by the formula $V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi h^2 (3R - h)$.\n\nLet $A$ be the area of the flat circular surface of the water at time $t$. Find the rate at which $A$ is increasing when the depth of the water is $2 \\text{ cm}$.","markScheme":"Let's first of all write down what we know.\n\n- We were given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t}=2\\,\\pi$.\n\n- In this case, $R=6$, hence \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        V&=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi h^2\\left(18-h\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 6\\pi h^2 - \\frac{1}{3}\\pi h^3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n- Letting $r$ be the radius of the flat circular surface of the water at time $t$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A &= \\pi r^2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\nWhat we are looking for is the value of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $h=2$.\n\nUsing related rates of change, we can form the following equation\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}h} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}V} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe have $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 2 \\pi$.\n\nGiven that $A = \\pi r^2$, we can say that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r} = 2 \\pi r$.\n\nGiven that $V = 6\\pi h^2 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi h^3$ we can say that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h} = 12\\pi h - \\pi h^2$, and therefore\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}V} &= \\dfrac{1}{12\\pi h - \\pi h^2}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our related rates equation, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 2 \\pi r \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}h} \\times \\dfrac{1}{12\\pi h - \\pi h^2} \\times 2 \\pi \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWhat we still require is an equation relating $h$ and $r$, so that we can differentiate it to get an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}h}$. \n\nTo do this, let's consider the hemispherical bowl, and note that the distance from the centre of a sphere to any point on the surface is equal to the radius, in this case, $6\\,\\text{cm}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/AA1388.png\" alt=\"AA1388\">\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    r^2 + (6-h)^2 &= 6^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nRearranging and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    r^2 &= 6^2 - (6-h)^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= 36 - \\left(36 - 12h + h^2\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n\n    r^2 &= 12h - h^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe can differentiate this equation with respect to $h$, to obtain an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}h}$. We have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}h}\\left(r^2\\right) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}h}\\left(12h - h^2\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt] \n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}r}\\left(r^2\\right) \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}h} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}h}\\left(12h - h^2\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n\n    2r\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}h} &= 12 - 2h \\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}h} &= \\dfrac{12 - 2h}{2r} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{6-h}{r} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNote that we differentiated the left hand side implicitly.\n\nNow we can substitute this into our related rates equation. We have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 2 \\pi r \\times \\dfrac{6-h}{r} \\times \\dfrac{1}{12\\pi h - \\pi h^2} \\times 2 \\pi \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{4\\pi^2 r(6-h)}{\\pi rh(12-h)} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{4\\pi (6-h)}{h(12-h)}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe have been asked to find the rate\n\nat which $A$ is increasing when the depth of the water is $2\\,\\text{cm}$, that is, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $h=2$. Substituting $h=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{4\\pi (6-2)}{2(12-2)} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{16\\pi}{20} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\frac{4\\pi}{5} \\text{ cm}^2 \\, \\text{s}^{-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA214","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA214\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nA water truck tank which is $3$ metres long has a uniform cross-section\nin the shape of a major segment. The tank is divided into two equal\nparts and is partially filled with water as shown in the following\ndiagram of the cross-section. The centre of the circle is O, the angle\nAOB is $\\alpha$ radians, and the angle AOF is $\\beta$ radians.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/5ea5dc5dff760c4e76844ca31202e2b591b61f26.svg\" alt=\"5ea5dc5dff760c4e76844ca31202e2b591b61f26.svg\">\n\n1.  Given that $\\alpha = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, calculate the amount of water,\n    in litres, in the right part\n\n    of the water tank. Give your answer correct to **the nearest\n    integer**. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for the volume of water $V$, in m$^3$, in the\n    left part of the water tank in\n    terms of $\\beta$. [3 marks]\n\nThe left part of the tank is now being filled with water at a constant\nrate of\n$\\text{\\(0.001\\)\\hspace{0.25em}m\\(^3\\)}$ per second.\n\n3.  Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when\n    $\\beta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{5}$. Round your answer to $3$ significant\n    figures.[4 marks]\n\n4.  Calculate the amount of time it will take for the left part\n    of the tank to be fully filled with water. Give your answer in\n    minutes and correct to **the nearest integer**. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/0d7f0df5c42b7d349ae8aa91d1b35d3e63931aef.svg\" alt=\"0d7f0df5c42b7d349ae8aa91d1b35d3e63931aef.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the area of a sector and the area of a triangle\n    formulas, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} V_{\\text{water\\hspace{0.2em}(right)}} &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OB}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^{O}B}) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{BG})(\\text{OG})\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\cos \\alpha)(\\sin \\alpha)\\bigg] \\\\[4pt]\n    &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\pi}{8} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\bigg)\\bigg] \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\pi}{8} - \\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.428 \\text{ m}^3 \\\\[10pt]\n    V_{\\text{water\\hspace{0.2em}(right)}}&\\approx428 \\text{ litres}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the right triangle EOD, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{D\\^OE}) &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{3.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{D\\^OE} &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the left part of\n    the tank is fully filled with water when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\beta &= \\pi - \\text{D\\^OE} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= \\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We must consider 2 cases:\n    1. $0 \\leq \\beta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n    2. $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < \\beta \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$\n\n    In the first case, similar to part (a) using **the area of a sector formula (3.4)**, $A=\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta$ and **the area of a triangle formula (3.PL)**, $A=\\frac{1}{2}bh$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{water\\hspace{0.2em}(left)}} &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\beta - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\cos \\beta \\times \\sin \\beta)\\bigg] \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 3\\bigg[\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\times2\\bigg)\\beta - \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\times2\\bigg)(\\cos  \\beta \\times \\sin \\beta)\\bigg] \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\\bigg[2\\beta - 2\\cos  \\beta \\sin \\beta\\bigg]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using **the double angle identity (3.6)**, $\\sin2\\theta=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{water\\hspace{0.2em}(left)}} &= \\dfrac{3}{4}(2\\beta - \\sin 2\\beta)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    In the second case, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/aa214c.PNG\" alt=\"aa214c\">\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{water\\hspace{0.2em}(left)}} &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\beta + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\cos (\\pi - \\beta) \\times \\sin (\\pi - \\beta))\\bigg]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Noting that $\\cos (\\pi - \\beta) = -\\cos \\beta$ and $\\sin (\\pi - \\beta) = \\sin \\beta$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{water\\hspace{0.2em}(left)}} &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\beta - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\cos  \\beta \\times \\sin \\beta)\\bigg] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\\bigg[2\\beta - 2\\cos  \\beta \\sin \\beta\\bigg]\\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{4}(2\\beta - \\sin 2\\beta)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence for all valid values of $\\beta$, $0 \\leq \\beta \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$,\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\boxed{V = \\dfrac{3}{4}(2\\beta - \\sin 2\\beta)}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n3.  From part (b)\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V = \\dfrac{3}{4}(2\\beta - \\sin 2\\beta)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using implicit differentiation and adapting **the chain rule (5.6)**, we can differentiate the volume of water function with respect to $t$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Chain Rule}\\hspace{2em}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\times\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    To differentiate the RHS of the volume formula with respect to $t$, this becomes\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(f(\\beta))=f'(\\beta)\\times\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\\bigg[2\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t} -  2(\\cos 2\\beta)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\bigg]\\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{3}{2}(1 - \\cos 2\\beta)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Rearranging to make $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ the subject\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3(1 - \\cos 2\\beta)}\\bigg]\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0.001$ and $\\beta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{5}$ in the expression, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.4em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\beta}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3(1 - \\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\frac{6\\pi}{5}\\big))}\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.2em}\\times0.001 \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx\\boxed{0.000369 \\text{ rad s}^{-1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  From part (b)\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V = \\dfrac{3}{4}(2\\beta - \\sin 2\\beta)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Evaluating the volume of water function for $\\beta = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} V_{\\text{full\\hspace{0.2em}water\\hspace{0.2em}(left)}} &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - \\sin \\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 4.577 \\text{ m}^3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 4577 \\text{ litres}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence initially there was approximately $4577 - 1351 = 3226$ litres of empty space in the left part of the water tank. With the rate of $0.001$m$^3$ per second or $1$ litre per second, this empty space will be filled with water in\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3226\\text{ seconds} &= \\dfrac{3226}{60} \\text{ minutes} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx\\boxed{54 \\text{ minutes}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA476","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA476\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nAir is being pumped into a spherical balloon so that its volume is\nincreasing at a constant rate of\n$15\\hspace{0.3em}\\text{cm}^3\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{min}^{-1}$.\n\nThe surface area $S$ and the volume $V$ of a sphere of radius $r$ are given by $S = 4\\pi r^2$ and $V = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$.\n\nFind the\nrate at which the surface area of the balloon is increasing when its\nradius hits $10$ cm.","markScheme":"Differentiating $S$ and $V$ with respect to $t$, we have\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{10.6em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}S}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 8\\pi r\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\\\[9pt]\n \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= 4\\pi r^2\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by chain rule}]\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\nHence we deduce\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.2em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}S}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{2}{r}\\bigg[4\\pi r^2\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\bigg] \\\\[9pt]\n&= \\dfrac{2}{r}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}S}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $r = 10$ and\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 15$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.1em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}S}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{2}{10}\\cdot15 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 3 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the surface area is increasing at the rate\nof\n $3 \\text{ cm\\(^2\\)\\hspace{0.1em}min\\(^{-1}\\)}$\nwhen $r = 10\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$."},{"ref":"AA505","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA505\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA bird is $4$ km East and $6$ km North of its nest. It is flying East at a rate of $14$ km h$^{-1}$ and North at a rate of $18 \\text{ km\\,h}^{-1}.$\n\nCalculate the rate that its distance from the nest is changing.","markScheme":"The distance from the nest is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr   & = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\\\[3pt]\n& = \\big(x^2 + y^2\\big)^\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the rate of change of above distance function is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n& = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(x^2 + y^2\\big)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\Big(2x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\Big) \\\\[8pt]\n& = \\dfrac{x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} + y\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}}{\\big(x^2 + y^2\\big)^\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[8pt]\n& = \\dfrac{\\big(4\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km}\\big)\\big(14\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km\\hspace{0.1em}h}^{-1}\\big) + \\big(6\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km}\\big)\\big(18\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km\\hspace{0.1em}h}^{-1}\\big)}{\\Big((4\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km})^2 + (6\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km})^2\\Big)^\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[16pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em} \\approx 22.7\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km\\hspace{0.1em}h}^{-1}  \\qquad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA991","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA991\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAn inverted cone is being filled with water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Related%20Rates%20of%20Change/images/cone.png\" alt=\"cone\">\n\nInitially, the cone is empty. The empty cone has a radius of $4\\,$cm and a height of $20\\,$cm.\n\nLiquid is poured into the cone at a rate of $28.5\\, \\mathrm{cm}^3\\, \\mathrm{s}^{-1}$.\n\nThe volume of water in the cone is $V$ at any particular time $t$, and $h$ is the height of the water above the vertex of the cone as shown in the diagram.\n\nYou may assume the liquid is always horizontal as it fills.\n\n1. Show that $V=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\pi r^3$. [2 marks]\n2. Find the rate at which the radius is increasing when $h=12\\,$cm. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the concept of similar triangles we can see that $h=5r$.\n\n    Using the formula for the volume of a cone\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_{cone}&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2h\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2(5r)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\pi r^3\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    As required.\n\n2. We want to find out the value of $\\dfrac{dr}{dt}$.\nHence, using the chain rule we can write a relation linking the three variables $V$, $r$ and $t$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{dr}{dt}&= \\dfrac{dr}{dV}\\times \\dfrac{dV}{dt}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The value of $\\dfrac{dV}{dt}$ is given in the question as  $28.5\\, \\mathrm{cm}^3$.\n\n    We can find $\\dfrac{dr}{dV}$ by differentiating the formula from part (a). Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{dV}{dr}&= \\dfrac{d\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\pi r^3\\right)}{dr}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=5\\pi r^2\\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{dr}{dV}&=\\dfrac{1}{5\\pi r^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We know when $h=12$ that $r=2.4\\,$cm. Putting all of this together\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{dr}{dt}&=\\dfrac{1}{5\\pi (2.4)^2}\\times 28.5\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.315 \\mathrm{cm}\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Trigonometric Differentiation","questions":[{"ref":"AA1151","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1151\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the curve $y=e^x\\sin{x}$.\n\n1.  Show that when $x=\\pi$ the gradient of the curve is $-e^{\\pi}$[3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, find the equation of the normal to the curve when $x=\\pi$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To find the gradient we need the derivative, and for this function we must use **the product rule formula (5.6)** to find the derivative.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=u\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}+v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we let $u=e^x$ and $v= \\sin x$, then $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=e^x$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x$, using **the standard derivatives for $\\bm{e^x}$ and $\\bm{\\sin x}$ (5.6)**. We have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=e^x\\sin{x}+e^x\\cos{x}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Now we should evaluate the derivative when $x=\\pi$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=e^{\\pi}\\sin{\\pi}+e^{\\pi}\\cos{\\pi}\\\\[12pt]\n       &=e^{\\pi}\\times 0+e^{\\pi}\\times (-1)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n       \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\boxed{-e^{\\pi}} \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  The normal is perpendicular to the tangent which means we can use the fact that $m_1\\times m_2=-1$.\n\n    Using the gradient value from part (a) we can solve for the gradient of the normal at $x=\\pi$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -e^{\\pi}\\times m_2 &= -1\\\\[12pt]\n        m_2&=\\frac{1}{e^\\pi}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To use the line equation, we must also find the $y$-coordinate of the point when $x=\\pi$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        y&=e^\\pi\\sin{\\pi}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we can use the point $(\\pi, 0)$, the gradient $m_2$, and **the formula for the equation of a straight line (2.1)**.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[6pt]\n        y-0&=\\frac{1}{e^\\pi}(x-\\pi)\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.9em}\\boxed{\\displaystyle y=\\frac{x-\\pi}{e^\\pi}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1158","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1158\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nAn isosceles trapezium is inscribed inside a semi-circle of radius $r\\,$centimetres, where $r>0$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/AA1158_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1158_q\">\n\nThe angle $\\angle BOC$ is $\\theta$, where $\\theta<\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nThe area of the trapezium is $A\\,$ cm$^2$.\n\n1.  Show  $A=\\tfrac{1}{2}r^2(\\sin{2\\theta}+2\\sin{\\theta})$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the maximum value of $A$ in terms of $r$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The area of a trapezium formula requires the lengths of the two parallel sides and the perpendicular distance between them.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/AA1158_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1158_a\">\n\n    $h$ is distance between the sides $AB$ and $CD$ and $x$ is half of $CD$.\n\n    Therefore we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=\\left(\\tfrac{2x+2r}{2}\\right)h\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\left(x+r\\right)h\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the trigonometric ratios we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x&=r\\cos{\\theta}\\\\[6pt]\n        h&=r\\sin{\\theta} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using those results we can write $A$ in terms of $r$ and $\\theta$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A&=\\left(r\\cos{\\theta}+r\\right)r\\sin{\\theta}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=r^2(\\cos{\\theta}\\sin{\\theta}+\\sin{\\theta})\\\\[6pt]\n    &=r^2(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin{2\\theta}+\\sin{\\theta})\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    A&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\tfrac{1}{2}r^2(\\sin{2\\theta}+2\\sin{\\theta})} \\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  To find the maximum area we should differentiate $A$ with respect to $\\theta$ treating $r$ as a constant.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}&=\\tfrac{1}{2}r^2\\left(2\\cos{2\\theta+2\\cos{\\theta}}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    At the maximum value $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}=0$, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=\\tfrac{1}{2}r^2\\left(2\\cos{2\\theta}+2\\cos{\\theta}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        0&=2\\cos{2\\theta}+2\\cos{\\theta}&\\hspace{2em}&\\text{as }r\\text{ is a constant and }r>0\\\\[6pt]\n        0&=2(2\\cos^2{\\theta}-1)+2\\cos{\\theta}\\\\[6pt]\n        0&=2\\cos^2{\\theta}-1+\\cos{\\theta}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=(2\\cos{\\theta}-1)(\\cos{\\theta}+1)\\\\[6pt]\n        \\cos{\\theta}&=\\tfrac{1}{2}&\\hspace{-6em}&\\cos{\\theta}=-1&\\\\[6pt]\n        \\theta&=\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}&&\\hspace{1.7em}\\theta=\\pi&\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore the maximum value of $A$ must occur when $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$ as $\\theta<\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{\\text{max}}&=\\tfrac{1}{2}r^2(\\sin{\\left(\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)}+2\\sin{\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)})\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+2\\times \\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4}r^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1347","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1347\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the function $g(x)=15\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{20}\\right)-18$ where $0\\leq x\\leq 20$ and $x$ is measured in radians.\n\n1.    \n\n    1.    Show that $g'(x)=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\pi\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{20}\\right)$\n\n    2.    Hence find the equation of the tangent line at $x=14$. [4 marks]\n\nThe tangent line intersects the $x$-axis forming an angle of $\\theta$. This is shown in the diagram. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/AA1347.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1347\">\n\n2.    Find $\\theta$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. To find the derivative of this function we will need to use the chain rule.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}(g(u))\\times\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        If we set $u=\\dfrac{\\pi x}{20}$ then $g(u)=15\\sin u -18$.\n\n        Hence $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{20}$ and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}(g(u))=15\\cos u$.\n\n        Substituting these into the formula\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g'(x))&=15\\cos\\left(u\\right)\\times\\frac{\\pi}{20}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=15\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{20}\\right)\\times\\frac{\\pi}{20}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{20}\\right)\\hspace{2em}\\text{as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. To find the equation of the tangent line we can use the equation \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        y=mx+c\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Substituting $x$ into the $g(x)$ equation we can find the associated $y$-value.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(14)&=15\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi (14)}{20}\\right)-18\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=-5.864...\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        To find the gradient, $m$, we substitute $x=14$ into the derivative equation from part (a)(i).\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m&=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi (14)}{20}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=-1.384...\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can substitute $x$, $y$, and $m$ into the line equation to solve for $c$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        -5.864...&=-1.384...(14)+c\\\\[6pt]\n\n        c&=13.52...\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the equation of the tangent line is\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}y=-1.38x+13.5\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. To find theta we recall that the gradient of a line is equal to $\\tan\\theta$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/AA1347a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1347a2\">\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    m=\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\hspace{3em}\n\n    \\tan\\theta&=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tan\\theta&=m\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We found the gradient in part (a) was $m=-1.384...$ so let's substitute this value and solve for $\\theta$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tan\\theta&=-1.384...\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\theta&=\\tan^{-1}(-1.384...)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=-0.9454...\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Our answer is an acute angle, which makes sense in context. However, as $\\theta$ is the $\\textit{measure}$ of the angle, we should take the absolute value as our final answer.\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\theta&=0.945\\text{ radians}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA244","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA244\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIn a triangle ABC, $\\text{AB} = 2$ cm,\n$\\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\text{B\\^{A}C} = \\theta$.\n\n1.  Show that $\\text{AC} = \\dfrac{2}{\\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta}$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that AC has a minimum value, find the value of $\\theta$ for\n    which this occurs.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch triangle ABC, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/9801060c55056d1d9b63618f5ec449a71b496347.svg\" alt=\"9801060c55056d1d9b63618f5ec449a71b496347.svg\">\n\n    Using the sine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A})} &= \\dfrac{\\text{AB}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B})} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4}} &= \\dfrac{2}{\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\frac{3\\pi}{4} - \\theta\\big)} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} &= \\dfrac{2}{\\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\cos \\theta - \\cos \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\sin \\theta}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\cancel{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}} &= \\frac{2}{\\cancel{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\cos \\theta + \\cancel{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\sin \\theta} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AC} = \\dfrac{2}{\\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta} \\hspace{2.9em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the quotient rule, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (\\text{AC})' &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{\\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0 - 2\\hspace{0.05em}(-\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta)}{(\\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta)^2} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\sin \\theta - 2\\cos \\theta}{(\\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $(\\text{AC})' = 0$ for $\\theta$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{2\\sin \\theta - 2\\cos \\theta}{(\\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta)^2} &= 0 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2\\sin \\theta - 2\\cos \\theta &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\tan \\theta &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{12.8em} \\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < \\theta < \\pi$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA287","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA287\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nThe diagram below shows the graph of\n$f(x) = a\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(k(x-d)) + c$, for $2 \\leq x \\leq 14$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/4b63d8795f4a8298ac4b50b87dc5e3453b8a7026.svg\" alt=\"4b63d8795f4a8298ac4b50b87dc5e3453b8a7026.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ has a maximum at P$(5,15)$ and a minimum at Q$(11,-5)$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of:\n    1.  $a$;\n\n    2.  $c$. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that $k = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\n    2.  Find the smallest possible value of $d$, given $d > 0$.\n        [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find $f'(x)$.  [3 marks]\n\n4.  At a point R, the gradient is $-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$. Find the\n    $x$-coordinate of R. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        a &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\hspace{0.75em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{15 - (-5)}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 10\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        c &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} + f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\hspace{0.65em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{15 + (-5)}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The period of $f$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= 2(11-5) \\hspace{4.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 12\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        k &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{3.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{12} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the coordinates of P, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(5) &= 15 \\\\[12pt]\n        \\hspace{0.3em} 10\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/6)(5-d)) + 5 &= 15 \\\\[12pt]\n        \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/6)(5-d)) &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(5-d) &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi \\hspace{2.55em} [\\sin^{-1} (1) = \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2] \\\\[8pt]\n        5 - d &= 3 + 12n \\\\[12pt]\n        d &= 2 - 12n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the smallest possible (positive) value of $d$ is\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.8em} d &= 2-12(0) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using the chain rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\Big[10\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/6)(x-2)) + 5\\Big] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 10\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big)\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/6)(x-2)) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/6)(x-2))\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Solving the equation $f'(x) = -5\\pi/3$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.85em} \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/6)(x-2)) &= - \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/6)(x-2)) &= -1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(x-2) &= \\pi + 2n\\pi \\hspace{2.8em} [\\cos^{-1} (-1) = \\pi] \\\\[8pt]\n    x-2 &= 6 + 12n \\\\[12pt]\n    x &= 8 + 12n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the $x$-coordinate of R is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.1em} x &=  8 + 12(0) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA295","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA295\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of\n$f(x) = a\\sin kx + c$ , for $0 \\leq x \\leq 16$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/0c46c1625d0d181b69606fe9f859ac512028fc3e.svg\" alt=\"0c46c1625d0d181b69606fe9f859ac512028fc3e.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ has a minimum at P$(4,8)$ and a maximum at Q$(12,16)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find the value of $c$.\n\n    2.  Show that $k = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$.\n    3.  Find the value of $a$. [6 marks]\n\nThe graph of $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a translation of\n$\\begin{pmatrix} d \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThe minimum point on the graph of $g$ has coordinates $(6.5,8)$.\n\n2.\n    1.  Write down the value of $d$.\n\n    2.  Find $g(x)$. [3 marks]\n\nThe graph of $g$ changes from concave-up to concave-down when $x = \\nu$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Find $\\nu$.\n\n    2.  Hence, or otherwise, find the maximum positive rate of change of\n        $g$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.65em} c &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} + f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{16 + 8}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 12\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The period of $f$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= 2(12-4) \\hspace{2.0em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 16\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        k &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{16} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the coordinates of P, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(4) &= 8 \\\\[5pt]\n        a\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{8}\\Big) + 12 &= 8 \\\\[6pt]\n        a + 12 &= 8 \\hspace{9.45em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = -4 \\hspace{2.5em}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/4c9ebf6623a7cbaa7d18df1ccb4a6937f54d7676.svg\" alt=\"4c9ebf6623a7cbaa7d18df1ccb4a6937f54d7676.svg\">\n\n        Hence, using the $x$-coordinates of P and R, we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        d &= 6.5 - 4 \\\\[7pt]\n        &= 2.5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{7em} g(x) &= f(x - d) \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -4\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/8)(x - d)) + 12 \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -4\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/8)(x - 2.5)) + 12 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b) (i)}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/16601e340ebe52d1691c9411518b8b6028078a08.svg\" alt=\"16601e340ebe52d1691c9411518b8b6028078a08.svg\">\n\n        Hence, using the $x$-coordinates of R and S, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.8em} \\nu &= \\dfrac{14.5 + 6.5}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 10.5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the chain rule, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.1em} g'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\Big[\\hspace{-0.2em}-4\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/8)(x-2.5)) + 12\\Big] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/8)(x-2.5)) \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, evaluating the derivative of $g$ for $x = \\nu$, we obtain\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\hspace{2.75em} g'(\\nu) &= g'(10.5) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (c) (i)}] \\\\[7pt]\n         &= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/8)(10.5-2.5)) \\\\[6pt]\n         &\\approx 1.57\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$"},{"ref":"AA465","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA465\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA curve is given by the equation $y = \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin x)$.\n\nFind the coordinates of all the points on the curve for which\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0$,\n$0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$.","markScheme":"Using the chain rule, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin x)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin x)\\big]\\big[2\\pi\\cos x\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n&= - 2\\pi \\cos x \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\nwe deduce\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0 &\\hspace{0.5em}\\Leftrightarrow\\hspace{0.5em} \\cos x = 0 \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{or}\\hspace{0.5em} \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin x) = 0 \\\\[7pt]\n&\\hspace{0.5em}\\Leftrightarrow\\hspace{0.5em} \\cos x = 0 \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{or}\\hspace{0.5em} \\sin x = 0 \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{or}\\hspace{0.5em} \\sin x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{0.5em}\\Leftrightarrow\\hspace{0.5em} x = 0,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\hspace{0.25em}\\pi \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $y = \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin x)$ for the values of $x$\ngiven by $(1)$, we get\n$$\n\n\\hspace{2em} \\begin{array}{rcccccl}\ny &=& \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin 0) &=& \\cos 0 &=& 1 \\\\[6pt]\ny &=& \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin \\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}) &=& \\cos \\pi &=& -1 \\\\[6pt]\ny &=& \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2}) &=& \\cos 2\\pi &=& 1 \\\\[6pt]\ny &=& \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{6}) &=& \\cos \\pi &=& -1 \\\\[6pt]\ny &=& \\cos\\hspace{0.1em}(2\\pi \\sin \\textstyle \\pi) &=& \\cos 0 &=& 1\n\\end{array}\n$$\nHence we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} (0,1),\\hspace{0.25em} \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},-1\\Big),\\hspace{0.25em} \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},1\\Big),\\hspace{0.25em} \\Big(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},-1\\Big),\\hspace{0.25em} (\\pi,1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA466","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA466\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe function $h$ is defined by $h(x) = \\cos(\\pi\\cos x)$,\n $-\\pi \\leq x \\leq \\pi$.\n\n1.  Determine whether $h$ is even, odd or neither even nor odd.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $h''(0) = 0$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Jack states that, because $h''(x) = 0$, the graph of $h$ has an\n    inflexion at the point P$(0,-1)$. Explain briefly whether Jack's\n    statement is correct or not. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  For any $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.9em} h(-x) &= \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(-x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi\\cos x) \\hspace{3em}  [\\text{since $x \\mapsto\\cos x$ is even}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= h(x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $\\text{\\(h\\) is even}$\n\n2.  Differentiating $h$ with respect to $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.4em} h'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi\\cos x)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[\\hspace{-0.15em} -\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x)\\big]\\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\pi \\sin x\\big] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by chain rule}] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\pi \\sin x \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Differentiating $h'$ with respect to $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[\\pi \\sin x \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    [\\text{by product rule}] \\hspace{2em} &= \\pi \\cos x \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x) + \\pi \\sin x \\big[\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x)\\big]' \\\\[10pt]\n    [\\text{by chain rule}] \\hspace{2em} &= \\pi \\cos x \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x) + \\pi \\sin x\\big[\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x)\\big]\\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\pi\\sin x\\big] \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\pi \\cos x \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x) - (\\pi \\sin x)^2 \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, evaluating $h''(x)$ for $x = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} h''(0) &= \\pi \\cos 0 \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos 0) - (\\pi \\sin 0)^2 \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi \\cos 0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\pi(1)(0) - (0)^2(-1) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  :::answer\n    Jack's statement is incorrect. We know $h$ is even and therefore symmetrical about the $y$-axis. Hence the stationary point P$(0,-1)$ is either a maximum or a minimum."},{"ref":"AA513","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA513\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA curve is given by the equation $y = \\cos(2\\pi \\cos x)$.\n\nFind the coordinates of all the points on the curve for which\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0$,  $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$.","markScheme":"Applying the chain rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =  -\\sin(2\\pi \\cos x)\\cdot(-2\\pi\\sin x) \\\\[3pt]\n& =  2\\pi \\sin x\\hspace{0.05em}\\sin(2\\pi \\cos x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\nwe obtain\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 2\\pi \\sin x \\sin(2\\pi \\cos x) = 0$\n$\\Longrightarrow$ either $\\sin x = 0$ or\n\n$\\sin(2\\pi \\cos x) = 0$. Then:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x = 0 &\\Longrightarrow  x = 0, \\pi; \\\\[18pt]\n\\sin(2\\pi \\cos x) = 0 &\\Longrightarrow \\text{either } 2\\pi \\cos x = 0 \\text{ or } 2\\pi \\cos x = \\pm \\pi; \\\\[18pt]\n2\\pi \\cos x = 0 &\\Longrightarrow \\cos x = 0 \\\\[12pt]\n&\\Longrightarrow x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}; \\\\[8pt]\n2\\pi \\cos x = \\pm \\pi &\\Longrightarrow \\cos x = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[3pt]\n&\\Longrightarrow  x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the coordinates of all points on the curve for which\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0$ are\n\n$$\n\\Big(0,1\\Big),\\, \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},-1\\bigg),\\, \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},1\\bigg), \\,\\bigg(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},-1\\bigg), \\,\\Big(\\pi,1\\Big)\n$$"},{"ref":"AA560","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA560\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) =\\dfrac {2x} {\\cos x}$. Find $f'(\\pi)$.","markScheme":"Using the quotient rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)       & = \\dfrac {2 \\cos x + 2 x \\sin x} {\\cos^2 x}\\\\[6pt]\nf'(\\pi) & = \\dfrac {2 \\cos \\pi + 2 \\pi \\sin \\pi} {\\cos^2 \\pi}\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\dfrac {2(-1) + 2 \\pi (0)} {(-1)^2}\\\\[6pt]\n& \\hspace{.275em}= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA566","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA566\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = 4 x \\sin (2x)$.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the gradient of the curve when $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the product rule with $u = 4 x$ and $v = \\sin (2x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)   & = u' v + u v'\\\\[5pt]\n    & = (4x)' \\sin (2x) + 4x(\\sin(2x))'\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 4 \\sin (2x) + 8 x \\cos(2x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We must find the value of $f'(x)$ when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right)    & = 4 \\sin \\left(2 (\\dfrac \\pi 2)\\right)\n    + 8 \\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)\\cos\\left(2 (\\dfrac \\pi 2)\\right)\n    \\\\[6pt]\n    & = 4(0) + 4 \\pi (-1)\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{-3mm}m = -4\\pi\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA585","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA585\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sin (3x)$ and $g(x) = \\ln (2x + 1)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find $f'(x)$;\n\n    2.  Find $g'(x)$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Let $h(x) = f(x) \\times g(x)$. Find $h'\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)$.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $$\n        f'(x) =3 \\cos (3x)\\hspace{2cm}\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        g'(x) = \\dfrac 2 {2x + 1}\\hspace{2cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the product rule with $u = f(x)$ and $v = g(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3cm}\n    h'(x)   & = 3 \\cos (3x)\\times \\ln(2 x + 1) + \\sin (3x) \\times \\dfrac 2 {2x + 1}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 3 \\cos (3x) \\times \\ln ( 2x + 1) + \\dfrac {2 \\sin(3x)}{2x + 1}\\\\[6pt]\n    h'\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right) & = 3 (0) \\times \\ln (\\pi +1) + \\dfrac {2(-1)}{\\pi +1}\\\\[8pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= -\\dfrac 2 {\\pi +1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA591","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA591\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sin x - \\sqrt 3 \\cos x$, $0 \\leq x \\leq 2 \\pi$.\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/9ae852b18e9905650e6f91259600e3211743364b.svg\" alt=\"9ae852b18e9905650e6f91259600e3211743364b.svg\">\n\nThe curve crosses the $x$-axis at $A$ and $C$ and has a maximum at point\n$B$.\n\n1.  Find the exact coordinates of $A$ and of $C$. [6 marks]\n\n2.  Find $f'(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of $B$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Points A and C are $x$-intercepts, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin x - \\sqrt 3 \\cos x    & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin x              & = \\sqrt 3 \\cos x\\hspace{1cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac {\\sin x} {\\cos x}    & = \\sqrt 3\\\\[6pt]\n    \\tan x              & = \\sqrt 3\\\\[6pt]\n    x                   & = \\dfrac \\pi 3,\\,\\dfrac {4\\pi}3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have\n      $\\mbox{A}\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 3,0\\right) \\mbox{ and C}\\left(\\dfrac {4\\pi} 3,0\\right)$\n\n2.\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.7cm}\n    f'(x)  = \\cos x + \\sqrt 3 \\sin x\n    $$\n\n3.  B is the maximum and it occurs when $f'(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos x + \\sqrt 3 \\sin x    & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\sqrt 3 \\sin x             & = -\\cos x\\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac {\\sin x} {\\cos x}       & = -\\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt 3}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\tan x                 & = -\\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt 3}\\\\[6pt]\n    x                  & = \\dfrac {5\\pi} 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting\n    the above result into the expression for $f$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f\\left(\\dfrac {5\\pi} 6\\right)  & = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac {5\\pi} 6\\right)\n    - \\sqrt 3 \\cos \\left(\\dfrac {5\\pi} 6\\right)\\\\[6pt   ]\n    & = \\dfrac 1 2 - \\sqrt 3 \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt 3} 2\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally\n      $\\mbox B \\left(\\dfrac {5\\pi} 6,2\\right)$"},{"ref":"AA819","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA819\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = -2\\sin^2 x + 3\\sin 2x + \\tan x - 3$,\n$0 \\leq x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Determine an expression for $f'(x)$ in terms of $x$.\n\n    2.  Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$ for $0\\leq x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n    3.  Find the $x$-coordinate(s) of the point(s) of inflexion of the\n        graph of $y=f(x)$, labelling these clearly on the graph of\n        $y=f'(x)$.[5 marks]\n\n2.  Let $u=\\tan x$.\n    1.  Express $\\sin x$ in terms of $u$.\n\n    2.  Express $\\sin 2x$ in terms of $u$.\n    3.  Show that $f(x)=0$ can be expressed as\n        $u^3-5u^2+7u-3=0$.[9 marks]\n\n3.  Solve the equation $f(x)=0$, giving your answers in the form\n    $\\arctan p$, where $p \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Taking the derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\big[-2\\sin^2 x + 3\\sin 2x + \\tan x - 3\\,\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -4\\sin x\\cos x + 6\\cos 2x + \\sec^2 x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using G.D.C., we get\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/33031ee8e485fec825d6edb17e4448dc539308eb.svg\" alt=\"33031ee8e485fec825d6edb17e4448dc539308eb.svg\">\n\n    3.  $x=0.98$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using trigonometric identities, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos x &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sec x} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1+\\tan^2 x}}\\hspace{2em} \\left[\\hspace{0.1em} \\text{since } \\sec x > 0 \\text{ on } 0\\leq x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1+u^2}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now, we can\n        express $\\sin x$ in terms of $u$ as follows\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        u &= \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\sin x \\hspace{0.1em} \\sqrt{1+u^2} \\hspace{3em}[\\hspace{0.1em} \\text{by (1)} \\hspace{0.1em}]\\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-6.9mm}\\sin x = \\dfrac{u}{\\sqrt{1+u^2}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using (1) and part (i), we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sin 2x &= 2\\sin x \\cos x \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 2 \\left(\\dfrac{u}{\\sqrt{1+u^2}}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1+u^2}}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2u}{1+u^2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Substituting the results in part (i) and (ii) into the\n        expression of $f(x)$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        %-2\\sin^2x + 3\\sin 2x + \\tan x - 3 \\\\[12pt]\n        -2\\left(\\dfrac{u}{\\sqrt{1+u^2}}\\right)^2 + 3\\left(\\dfrac{2u}{{1+u^2}}\\right) + u - 3 &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        \\dfrac{-2u^2 + 6u + (1+u^2)(u-3)}{1+u^2} &= 0\\\\[12pt]\n        \\dfrac{u^3 - 5u^2 + 7u -3}{1+u^2} &= 0\\\\[12pt]\n        u^3 - 5u^2 + 7u - 3 &= 0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using part (b) (iii), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u^3 - 5u^2 + 7u - 3 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    u &= 1,\\, 3 \\hspace{1.2em} [\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{by using G.D.C.}\\hspace{0.1em}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$ are\n\n    $$\n    \\qquadx = \\arctan 1, \\arctan 3\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA902","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA902\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nA function $f(x)$ is defined by\n$f(x)=\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)$, $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that $f$ is an even function.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote to the graph of\n    $y=f(x)$.[2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Show that $f'(x) =- \\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{x^2}\\left(x^2+1\\right)}$\n        for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, $x\\neq 0$.\n\n    2.  Using the expression for $f'(x)$ and the result\n        $\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|$, show that $f$ is increasing for\n        $x<0$.[9 marks]\n\nA function $g$ is defined by\n$g(x)=\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)$, $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$,\n$x\\geq0$.\n\n4.  Find the range of $g$. [2 marks]\n\n5.  Find an expression for $g^{-1}(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n6.  State the domain of $g^{-1}(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n7.  Sketch the graph of $y=g^{-1}(x)$. Clearly indicating any\n    asymptotes with their equations and stating the values of any axes\n    intercepts.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(-x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(-x) &= \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{(-x)^2-1}{(-x)^2+1}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(x)\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Therefore, \\(f(x)\\) is an even function}$\n\n2.  Taking the limit as $x$ tends to $\\pm \\infty$, using the continuity\n    of the $\\arccos$ function, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty} \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right) &= \\arccos\\left(\\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty}\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\arccos\\left(\\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty}\\dfrac{1-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}}{1+\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\arccos(1) \\\\[16pt]\n    &= 0\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Therefore \\(y=0\\) is a horizontal asymptote for the graph of \\(y=f(x)\\)}$\n\n3.\n    1.  If we calculate the derivative of $f$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+1}\\right)^2}} \\left(\\dfrac{4x}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^2}\\right) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by chain rule}]\\\\[12pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^2-\\left(x^2-1\\right)^2}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^2}}} \\left(\\dfrac{4x}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4x^2}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^2}}} \\left(\\dfrac{4x}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^2}\\right)\\\\[14pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{x^2+1}{2\\sqrt{x^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{4x}{\\left(x^2+1\\right)^2}\\\\[18pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{x^2}\\left(x^2+1\\right)}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  $f$ is increasing when $f'(x)>0$. Using that $\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= -\\dfrac{2x}{\\sqrt{x^2}\\left(x^2+1\\right)} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{2x}{|x|\\left(x^2+1\\right)} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\left\\lbrace\\begin{array}{ll} \\phantom{-}\\dfrac{2}{x^2+1}&x<0 \\\\[12pt]\n        -\\dfrac{2}{x^2+1}&x> 0\n        \\end{array}\\right.\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $\\text{Therefore \\(f\\) is increasing over \\(x<0\\)}$\n\n4.  Using G.D.C., we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/f0c0fbdf08255f11d554923de27f12f77e5813a5.svg\" alt=\"f0c0fbdf08255f11d554923de27f12f77e5813a5.svg\">\n\n    Hence,$\\text{range of \\(g\\)\\,:\\,} (0,\\pi]$\n\n5.  If we solve the equation $\\left(g\\circ g^{-1}\\right)(x)=x$ for\n    $y=g^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g\\left(g^{-1}(x)\\right) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    g(y) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{y^2-1}{y^2+1}\\right) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{y^2-1}{y^2+1} &= \\cos x\\\\[12pt]\n    (y^2+1)\\cos x&=y^2-1\\\\[12pt]\n    y^2\\cos x-y^2&=-1-\\cos x\\\\[12pt]\n    y^2(\\cos x-1)&=-1-\\cos x\\\\[12pt]\n    y^2&=\\dfrac{1+\\cos x}{1-\\cos x}\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2mm}g^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+\\cos x}{1-\\cos x}} \\hspace{2em} \\cos x \\neq 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n6.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{domain of $g^{-1}$} &= \\text{range of $g$} \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= (0,\\pi]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n7.  Using G.D.C., we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Trigonometric%20Differentiation/images/5eccef056ae5f9c36509f6f503f953d242605d5c.svg\" alt=\"5eccef056ae5f9c36509f6f503f953d242605d5c.svg\">"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA1172","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1172\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA company is designing an animated logo for its website.\n\nThe logo is created by drawing a rectangle around a sector with radius $r$ centimetres and angle $\\theta$ radians. This is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Unassigned/images/AA1172_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1172_q\">\n\nLet the area of rectangle $RSTU$ be $A$.\n\n1.  Show that $A=r\\sqrt{2r^2(1-\\cos{\\theta})}$ [2 marks]\n\nTo animate the logo the designer increases the angle $\\theta$ by a constant rate of $0.5$ radians per second.\n\nFor the final logo design $r$ is fixed at $4\\,$cm.\n\n2.  If\n    $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}=a\\sqrt{b}$ where $a, b\\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n    Find $a$ and $b$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Recognise that the area of the rectangle is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=VW\\times r \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the Cosine rule to find $VW$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        VW^2&=r^2+r^2-2r^2\\cos{\\theta}\\\\[6pt]\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        VW&=\\sqrt{2r^2(1-\\cos{\\theta})}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=r\\sqrt{2r^2(1-\\cos{\\theta})} \\hspace{2em} \\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We can recognise that the three quantities $A, \\theta$ and $t$ are changing.\n\n    Hence we can relate them using the chain rule.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\\times \\frac{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We know from the question that $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t}=0.5$ for all $\\theta$.\n\n    We need to find the value of $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n    Recall that $r=4$ hence $A=4\\sqrt{32(1-\\cos{\\theta})}$\n\n    We can use the power and chain rule to differentiate $A$ with respect to $\\theta$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A&=4(32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))^{\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}&=4(32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times (32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))' \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The derivative of the inner function of $A$ shown above is $(32-32\\cos{\\theta})'=32\\sin{\\theta}$\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}&=4(32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times 32\\sin{\\theta}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{64\\sin{\\theta}}{\\sqrt{32(1-\\cos{\\theta})}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We must evaluate this expression when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}&=\\frac{64\\sin{\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}}}{\\sqrt{32(1-\\cos{\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}})}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{64\\times \\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\sqrt{32(1-\\tfrac{1}{2})}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=8\\sqrt{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Recall\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\\times \\frac{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=8\\sqrt{3}\\times 0.5\\\\[6pt]\n        &=4\\sqrt{3} \\,\\text{cm}^2\\text{s}^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $a=4 \\text{ and } b=3$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA770","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA770\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nConsider the function $g(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $a \\neq 0$ and\n$a$, $b$, $c$, $d \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down an expression for $g'(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, given that $g^{-1}$ does not exist, show that\n        $b^2-3ac>0.$[3 marks]\n\nConsider the function\n$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2+6x+\\dfrac{9}{2}$\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that $f^{-1}$ exists.\n\n    2.  $f(x)$ can be written in the form $p\\hspace{0.05em}(x+2)^3+q$,\n        where $p$, $q\\in \\mathbb{R}$. Find the value of $p$ and the\n        value of $q$.\n\n    3.  Hence, find $f^{-1}(x)$.[7 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f(x)$ may be obtained by transforming the graph of $y=x^3$\nusing a sequence of three transformations.\n\n3.  State each of the transformations in the order in which\n    they are applied.[3 marks]\n\n4.  Sketch the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=f^{-1}(x)$ on the same\n    set of axes, indicating the points where each graph crosses the\n    coordinate axes.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Taking the derivative with respect to $x$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        g'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\hspace{0.1em} \\big[ ax^3+bx^2+cx+d \\big] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 3ax^2+2bx+c\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Since $a \\neq 0$, $g(x)$ is a cubic polynomial. For $g^{-1}$ not to exist, $g(x)$ cannot be one-to-one. Thus, $g'(x)$ must have **two distinct real roots** (so that the graph has two stationary points, a local maximum and a local minimum) which requires the discriminant of $g'(x)$ to be strictly positive $\\left(\\triangle > 0 \\right)$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\big[\\text{discriminant of $g'$}\\big] &> 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        (2b)^2 - 4(3a)(c) &> 0 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac] \\\\[12pt]\n        4b^2 - 12ac &> 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-1.25cm}b^2-3ac > 0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using part (a) (ii), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (3)^2-3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(6\\right) & \\ngtr 0.\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore, by the contrapositive statement to part (a) (ii), we\n        deduce $f^{-1}$ exists.\n\n    2.  Factorizing the expression for $y=f(x)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(x^3+6x^2+12x+9\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(x^3+6x^2+12x+8+1\\right)  \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(x+2\\right)^3 + \\dfrac{1}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore\n        $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\, q=\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n    3.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n        $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n        f(y) &= x \\\\[10pt]\n        \\dfrac{1}{2}(y+2)^3+\\dfrac{1}{2} &= x \\\\[10pt]\n        (y+2)^3 &= 2x - 1 \\\\[12pt]\n        y+2 &= \\sqrt[3]{2x-1} \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-10.5mm}f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt[3]{2x-1} - 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We get the following transformations\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}&: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{horizontal translation $2$ units left} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}&:\n    \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical stretch by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$}\\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{transformation $3$}&: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical translation $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ units up}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    *Note:* The horizontal translation can be performed in any step but\n    the\\\n    vertical stretch should be performed before the vertical\n    translation.\n\n4.  Using the shape of a cubic function, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20I/Unassigned/images/79b23cf27cdbbbaba59cb5e2eee4c41cd47b395a.svg\" alt=\"79b23cf27cdbbbaba59cb5e2eee4c41cd47b395a.svg\">"}]}]},{"name":"Calculus II","subtopics":[{"name":"Differential Equations","questions":[{"ref":"AA1256","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1256\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA ball is dropped from a certain height and falls vertically. The velocity $v$ of the ball at time $t$ is such that the rate of change of velocity is proportional to the difference between the terminal velocity $v_T$ and the current velocity $v$. This is modelled by the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = k(v_T - v)$, where $k$ is a positive constant. The ball is released from rest, so $v = 0$ when $t = 0$.\n\n1.  Solve the differential equation to find an expression for $v$ in terms of $v_T$, $k$ and $t$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the time taken for the velocity to reach $\\dfrac{3}{4}v_T$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Treating this as a first-order differential equation, separating out the variables, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= k(v_T - v)\\\\[4pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{v_T - v} &= k\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Integrating both sides using the **standard integral (5.10)** $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\ln|x| + C$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -\\ln|v_T - v| &= kt + C \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting the initial condition $v = 0$ when $t = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -\\ln|v_T - 0| &= k(0) + C \\\\[6pt]\n        C &= -\\ln v_T\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Rearranging, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{v_T}{v_T - v}\\right) &= kt \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Exponentiating both sides and isolating $v$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{v_T}{v_T - v} &= \\mathrm{e}^{kt} \\\\[10pt]\n        \\dfrac{v_T - v}{v_T} &= \\mathrm{e}^{-kt} \\\\[6pt]\n        v &= \\boxed{v_T\\left(1 - \\mathrm{e}^{-kt}\\right)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Setting $v = \\dfrac{3}{4}v_T$ in the result from part (a), we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{3}{4}v_T &= v_T\\left(1 - \\mathrm{e}^{-kt}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Dividing through by $v_T$ and rearranging, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{e}^{-kt} &= \\dfrac{1}{4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Taking natural logarithms of both sides, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\ln\\left(\\mathrm{e}^{-kt}\\right) &= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Applying the **laws of logarithms (1.7)**, $\\log_a x^m = m\\log_a x$, to bring the power to the front, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -kt\\ln(\\mathrm{e}) &= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n        -kt &= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Dividing both sides by $-k$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        t &= -\\dfrac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n        t &= \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\ln 4}{k}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1294","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1294\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the differential equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d^2}y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}&=-10xe^{-x^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $y=-1$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2$ when $x=0$.\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Use Euler's method with a step length of $0.1$ to estimate a value for $y$ when $x=0.5$.[4 marks]\n\n3.  Hence justify whether your answer to part (b) is an overestimate or an underestimate. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can find the first derivative by integrating the second derivative.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int{\\frac{\\mathrm{d^2}y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}}&=\\int{-10xe^{-x^2}}\\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using integration by substitution\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Let }u&=-x^2\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{Therefore }\\mathrm{d}u&=-2x\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{and }\\mathrm{d}x&=-\\frac{1}{2x}\\mathrm{d}u\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Replacing  $\\mathrm{d}x$ we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int{\\frac{\\mathrm{d^2}y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}}&=\\int{-10xe^{u}\\times -\\frac{1}{2x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\int{5e^{u}\\mathrm{d}u}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=5e^u+c\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    To find the value of $c$, we can use the given information of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2$ when $x=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2&=5e^0+c\\\\[6pt]\n    2&=5+c\\\\[6pt]\n    c&=-3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=5e^{-x^2}-3\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=5e^{-x^2}-3 \\hspace{1.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{6.25em} y(0) &= -1 \\\\[6pt]\n    h &= 0.1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the formulae we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} x_{n+1} &= x_n + 0.1 \\\\[2pt]\n    y\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} y_{n+1} &= y_n +0.1\\times\\bigg[\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=5e^{-(x_n)^2}-3 \\bigg] \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$}] \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\\\\[-18pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using Euler's method, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA1294-table.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1294-table\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find the estimate of $y$ when $x=0.5$ to be $-0.142$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We can use the second derivative to decide if our value is an over or underestimate.\n\n    We need to decide what sign the second derivative takes near to our estimated value.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d^2}y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}&=-10xe^{-x^2}\\hspace{2em}\\text{as }x>0 \\,\\text{ and }\\,e^{-x^2}>0\\\\[6pt]\n        &<0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we know that $f(x)$ is concave-down for all $x>0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Here is a visual of a function that is concave down. Note, this is not to scale.\n\n    Notice how the estimated point, in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$, is vertically above the actual value on the curve.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/1294.JPEG\" alt=\"1294\">\n\n    The $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$ solid line is the difference between the estimated and the actual value.\n\n    Therefore Euler's method has produced an\n    $\\text{overestimate}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1323","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1323\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nConsider the differential equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}-2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $0<x<\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\n1.    Show that $x^2$ is an integrating factor for this differential equation. [3 marks]\n\n2.    Hence, find the general solution to the differential equation, giving your answer in the form $y=g(x)+\\dfrac{C}{x^2}$, where $C$ is the constant of integration. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. The question is asking us to find an integrating factor for this differential equation.\n\n    The following, from the formula booklet, is the form of such a differential equation.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and the integrating factor, $f(x)$, is given by $f(x)=e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n    Hence, our first step is to rearrange the given equation to match this form and then identify $P(x)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}-2y\\\\[12pt]\n    x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2y&=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\frac{2y}{x}&=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    From this, we can see that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\textcolor{red}{\\,+\\,P(x)}y&=Q(x)\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\textcolor{red}{\\,+\\,\\frac{2}{x}}y&=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, $P(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can now find the integrating factor, $f(x)$.\n\n    The integrating factor is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&=e^{\\int\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=e^{2\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=e^{2\\ln{x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=e^{\\ln{x^2}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=x^2\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. The question asks us to write the solution in the form of $y=g(x)+\\dfrac{C}{x^2}$.\n\n    The first step, when using an integrating factor, is to multiply the entire equation by the integrating factor and then simplify.\n\n    This gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 \\times \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+ x^2 \\times \\frac{2y}{x}&= x^2 \\times \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\textcolor{red}{x^2 \\times \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+ 2xy}&= \\textcolor{blue}{x^2 \\times \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}}\\hspace{3em}[1]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The next step is crucial when solving differential equations of this form.\n\n    To understand why it works, we will first use the product rule to find the derivative of $f(x)y$. This is the derivative of the integrating factor multiplied by $y$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{blue}{\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^2y\\right)}&=\\textcolor{red}{x^2 \\times \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+ 2xy}\\hspace{3em}[2]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Notice how the L.H.S. of $[1]$ is equivalent to the R.H.S. of $[2]$, shown in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$.\n\n    This means we can equate the  R.H.S. of $[1]$ with the  L.H.S. of $[2]$, shown in $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$, and form the following equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{blue}{\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^2y\\right)}&=\\textcolor{blue}{x^2 \\times \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The next step is to integrate both sides with respect to $x$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^2y\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int \\frac{4x^2}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[12pt]\n    x^2y&=\\int\\frac{4x^2}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\hspace{2em} [3]\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We now have a rather challenging integral.\n\n    Would a substitution of $u=x^2$ work? You could try this, but you would be left with an integral with both $x$ and $u$ in it. We need another strategy.\n\n    Let's try $u=9-4x^2$, which forms part of the denominator of the quotient.\n\n    We can then see that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-8x$ noting that $-8x$ is a multiple of $4x$.\n\n    By rewriting the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\int \\textcolor{red}{x} \\times \\textcolor{blue}{\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    as the product of two functions we can use integration by parts. As we then have a function ($\\textcolor{red}{x}$) which we can differentiate and a function $\\left(\\textcolor{blue}{\\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}}\\right)$ we can integrate.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Consider the formula for integration by parts.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\textcolor{red}{u} \\times \\textcolor{blue}{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=uv-\\int v\\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The first decision we make is which part of integral should be $\\textcolor{red}{u}$ and which part should be $\\textcolor{blue}{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}$.\n\n    As mentioned previously we should choose\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u&=x&\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&= \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we will find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ and $v$.\n\n    Let's consider $u$ first\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u&=x\\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=1\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now onto $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v&=\\int{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\int \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As mentioned we can use a substitution, let $k=9-4x^2$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}k}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-8x\\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{d}x&=-\\frac{1}{8x}\\mathrm{d}k\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Rewriting $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $k$ we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{k^3}}\\times\\frac{-1}{8x}\\mathrm{d}k\\\\[12pt]\n    &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\int k^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}k\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the inverse power rule to integrate we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\div \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}+1\\right)k^{-\\frac{3}{2}+1}\\\\[12pt]\n    v&=k^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now substitute all of the elements into the formula for integration by parts.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{4x^2}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=x \\times \\left(k^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)-\\int k^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\times 1 \\, \\mathrm{d}x\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}-\\int \\textcolor{red}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}} \\, \\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    You may recognise $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{this}}$ integral from the formula booklet.\n    It is of the form\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a^2-x^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)}+C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $a$ is $3$ and $x$ is $2x$.\n\n    Because we are working with $2x$ (and not $x$), we need to use a substitution to make sure we don't make any errors when integrating.\n\n    Let $p=2x$. Therefore $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}p}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2$. Hence $\\mathrm{d}x=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathrm{d}p$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Rewriting our integral in terms of $p$ we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3^2-p^2}} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}p\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3^2-p^2}}\\, \\mathrm{d}p\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now apply the standard integral from the formula booklet\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{p}{3}\\right)}+C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Rewriting back in terms of $x$ we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note, we can omit the constant of integration at this stage.\n\n    Substituting the elements we have found into the integration by parts formula we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{4x^2}{\\sqrt{(9-4x^2)^3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)} + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall $[3]$, we can replace the L.H.S. of the above to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2y&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\arcsin{\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)} + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To write the answer in the desired form we must divide by $x^2$ which gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}y=\\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}-\\dfrac{1}{2x^2}\\arcsin{\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{3}\\right)} + \\dfrac{C}{x^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1336","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1336\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2=\\dfrac{3}{x^2}(2xy+y^2)$, where $y\\geq 0$ and $4 \\leq x<6$.\n\nIt is known that $y=0$ when $x=4$, and the solution is of the form $y=f(x)$.\n\n1.    Use Euler's method with a step length of $0.25$, to find an approximate value for $y$ when $x=5.5$. [4 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.    Use the substitution $y=vx$ to show that $x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3v^2+5v+2$.\n\n    2.    Hence, by solving the differential equation, show that\\\n        $f(x)=\\dfrac{x(0.5x-2)}{3-0.5x}$. [10 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.    Find the actual value of $y$ when $x=5.5$.\n\n    2.    By considering the gradient of the curve $y=f(x)$, suggest a reason why Euler's method does not give a good approximation for the value of $y$ found in part (a). [2 marks]\n\nThe voltage along a wire is given by $V(d)=\\dfrac{d(0.5d-2)}{3-0.5d}$, where $d \\geq 0$ and $d\\neq a$.\n\n$V$, measured in volts, models the voltage at a point $d$ meters from a fixed point on the wire.\n\n4.    Write down the value of $a$. [1 mark]\n\nA scientist, looking for a fault, carefully measures the voltage along the wire and is surprised to find that no readings fall within the interval $s<V<t$, where $s, t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and are measured in volts.\n\n5.    Find $s$ and $t$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. First rearrange the differential equation to get it in the form $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=h(x,y)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{3}{x^2}(2xy+y^2)+2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The initial value and step length are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y(4) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    h &= 0.25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Consider the formula for Euler's method where $h$ is the step length\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x_{n+1}&=x_n+h\\\\[6pt]\n    y_{n+1}&=y_n+h\\times f(x_n,y_n)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    It is important to note that we use $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=h(x,y)$ for the function $f(x_n,y_n)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} x_{n+1} &= x_n + 0.25 \\\\[2pt]\n    y\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} y_{n+1} &= y_n +0.25\\times\\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{x_n^2}(2x_ny_n+y_n^2)+2 \\bigg] \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using Euler's method, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    | &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;$\\textcolor{red}{n}$&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; | &nbsp;&nbsp;$x_n$&nbsp;&nbsp; | $y_n$        | $\\textcolor{red}{\\Delta y_n}$ |\n    |-------|--------|--------------|------------------|\n    | $\\textcolor{red}{\\;\\;0\\;\\;}$ | 4      | 0            | $\\textcolor{red}{0.5}$        |\n    | $\\textcolor{red}{\\;\\;1\\;\\;}$ | 4.25   | 0.5          | $\\textcolor{red}{0.6868...}$  |\n    | $\\textcolor{red}{\\;\\;2\\;\\;}$ | 4.5    | 1.186...     | $\\textcolor{red}{0.9477...}$  |\n    | $\\textcolor{red}{\\;\\;3\\;\\;}$ | 4.75   | 2.134...     | $\\textcolor{red}{1.325...}$   |\n    | $\\textcolor{red}{\\;\\;4\\;\\;}$ | 5      | 3.460...     | $\\textcolor{red}{1.897...}$   |\n    | $\\textcolor{red}{\\;\\;5\\;\\;}$ | 5.25   | 5.357...     | $\\textcolor{red}{2.811...}$   |\n    | $\\textcolor{red}{\\;\\;6\\;\\;}$ | 5.5    | 8.169...     | $\\textcolor{red}{4.382...}$   |\n\n    Note, your solution must include the values in the middle two columns. The first and fourth column, in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$, are here for your reference.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, we have found that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y(5.5)&=8.169...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=8.17\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  1. First, consider the differential equation (D.E.).\n\n        We have been given $y=vx$, which we can rearrange to $v=\\dfrac{y}{x}$.\n\n        Hence, we can write the D.E. in terms of $v$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2&=\\dfrac{3}{x^2}(2xy+y^2)\\\\[12pt]\n        &=6\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{y^2}{x^2}\\right)+2\\\\[12pt]\n        \\textcolor{red}{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}}&=\\textcolor{blue}{3v^2+6v+2}\\hspace{2em}[1]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Note that, since $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=f(v)$ then we can say this equation is homogeneous.\n\n        Next, use the given substitution. Let's use the product rule to differentiate that to get an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $v$ and $x$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=x \\times  \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} + v \\times  \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\textcolor{red}{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}}&= \\textcolor{blue}{x   \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} + v }\\hspace{2em}[2]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Notice how the $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{L.H.S.}}$ of both [1] and [2] are equivalent, hence we can equate the $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{R.H.S.'s}}$ of [1] and [2]. This gives\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x   \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} + v&=3v^2+6v+2\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2.3em}x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3v^2+5v+2\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2. A substitution ($y=vx$) can be used to transform a homogeneous D.E. into a separable form.\n\n        In the previous part we began this process.\n\n        Reflecting on that answer, we can separate the variables $v$ and $x$, giving\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{1}{3v^2+5v+2}\\times \\mathrm{d}v&=\\frac{1}{x}\\times \\mathrm{d}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now we integrate both sides to get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int\\frac{1}{3v^2+5v+2} \\mathrm{d}v&=\\int\\frac{1}{x} \\mathrm{d}x\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The R.H.S. is a standard integral.\n\n        But the L.H.S. is a little trickier.\n\n        We can't integrate it directly or use a substitution.\n\n        But we can factorise the denominator to get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{L.H.S.}&=\\int\\frac{1}{3v^2+5v+2} \\mathrm{d}v\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\int\\frac{1}{(3v+2)(v+1)} \\mathrm{d}v\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        This is useful, as it suggests we can use partial fractions to split the fraction into two parts such as\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{1}{(3v+2)(v+1)}&\\equiv\\frac{A}{3v+2}+\\frac{B}{v+1}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        where $A$ and $B$ are to be determined.\n\n        Note, we have used the equivalence symbol. This means the L.H.S. and the R.H.S. are equal for __all__ values of $v$. In other words, this is an identity.\n\n        If we multiply the structure above by $(3v+2)(v+1)$ we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        1&\\equiv A(v+1)+B(3v+2)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        As this is true for all $v$ then we can pick any value we want for $v$ to find $A$ and $B$. Therefore it is a good strategy to pick a value that will cancel out either $A$ or $B$.\n        Hence, let's pick $v=-1$ first\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        1&= A(-1+1)+B(3(-1)+2)\\\\[6pt]\n        1&= -B\\\\[6pt]\n        B&=-1\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now let's pick $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        1&= A\\left(\\left(-\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)+1\\right)+B(3\\left(-\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)+2)\\\\[6pt]\n        1&= \\frac{1}{3}A\\\\[6pt]\n        A&=3\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Putting those results together we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{1}{(3v+2)(v+1)}&=\\frac{3}{3v+2}-\\frac{1}{v+1}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we can rewrite the integral which gives\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int\\left(\\frac{3}{3v+2}-\\frac{1}{v+1}\\right) \\mathrm{d}v&=\\int\\frac{1}{x} \\mathrm{d}x\\\\[12pt]\n        3\\int \\frac{1}{3v+2} \\mathrm{d}v-\\int \\frac{1}{v+1} \\mathrm{d}v&=\\int\\frac{1}{x} \\mathrm{d}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using the standard integral\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}{x}&=\\ln{|x|}+C\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        3 \\times \\textcolor{red}{\\frac{1}{3}} \\times \\ln{|3v+2|}- \\ln{|v+1|}&=\n        \\ln x + \\ln A\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Notice the additional factor of $\\textcolor{red}{\\frac{1}{3}}$, we need this to compensate for the chain rule as $(3v+2)'=3$.\n\n        As this solution involves logarithms it is good practice to give the constant of integration as $\\ln{A}$. We'll see why in a moment.\n\n        Remember we need the solution to be in the form $y=f(x)$.\n        To get to this let's rewrite both sides as a single logarithm.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\ln{|3v+2|}- \\ln{|v+1|}&=\n        \\ln x + \\ln A\\\\[6pt]\n        \\ln\\left(\\frac{3v+2}{v+1}\\right)&=\n        \\ln Ax\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        By choosing the constant as $\\ln{A}$, it allows us to quickly write the R.H.S. as a single logarithm.\n\n        Additionally, we removed the absolute value signs from both $\\ln{|3v+2|}$ and $\\ln{|v+1|}$ as $x,y>1$ means $v>1$ therefore the inner functions of both logarithms are greater than zero. Hence $|3v+2|=(3v+2)$ and $|v+1|=(v+1)$.\n\n        As we have the equation in the form\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\ln{\\big[g(x)\\big]}&=\\ln{\\big[h(x)\\big]}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        then we can equate the inner functions\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        g(x)&=h(x)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{3v+2}{v+1}&=Ax\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Let's make $v$ the subject of the equation\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        3v+2&=Avx+Ax\\\\[6pt]\n        3v-Avx&=Ax-2\\\\[6pt]\n        v(3-Ax)&=Ax-2\\\\[6pt]\n        v&=\\frac{Ax-2}{3-Ax}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now let's reuse the substitution $v=\\frac{y}{x}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{y}{x}&=\\frac{Ax-2}{3-Ax}\\\\[6pt]\n        y&=\\frac{x(Ax-2)}{3-Ax}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We still have the constant of integration.\n        Using the condition given in the question $(4,0)$ we can form an equation and solve it on our G.D.C. to find $A$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=\\frac{4(4A-2)}{3-4A}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        A&=0.5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using this value for $A$ we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &y=\\dfrac{x(0.5x-2)}{3-0.5x}\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  1. To find the actual value of $y$ when $x=5.5$ we evaluate $f(5.5)$ on a G.D.C.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(5.5)&=\\frac{(5.5)(0.5(5.5)-2)}{3-0.5(5.5)}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=16.5\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2. We found the actual value, in the previous part, when $x=5.5$, to be $y=16.5$.\n\n        There is a large difference between this and the estimate of $y=8.17$, we found in part (a).\n\n        To help explain this large difference let's examine a sketch of $y=f(x)$ on our G.D.C.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA1336-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1336-a1\">\n\n        The $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$ line, drawn at $x=5.5$ shows the gradient at this point.\n\n        Notice that the gradient around $x=5.5$ increases very rapidly.\n\n        Euler's method involves using the gradient at a previous point to estimate the location of the next point. As the gradient increases rapidly our estimate is significantly less than the actual value ($8.17<16.5$).\n\n        Therefore, as the gradient increases\n        $\\text{very rapidly around }x=5.5$ Euler's method does not give a good approximation for the value of $y$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4. The function for $V$ is a rational function.\n\n    It is undefined if the denominator is equal to zero, therefore the value $d=a$ must be excluded from the function.\n\n    We must solve\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3-0.5a&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    a&=6\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5. The image below shows $y=V(d)$ for $d\\geq 0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA1336-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1336-a2\">\n\n    The points $A$ and $B$ are a local minimum and maximum, respectively.\n    Using a G.D.C., we find their coordinates to be\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A(2.535...,-1.071...)&&B(9.464...,-14.92...)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note that no part of the curve exists in the vertical region between these two points.\n    This means the scientist will not detect any voltage between these two values.\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    s&=-14.9&&&t&=-1.07\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note that the scientist should look at the wire where $d=6$, it looks like fault might be there!"},{"ref":"AA1379","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1379\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - \\dfrac{2}{x}\\,y = x^2 e^{x}$.\n\n1. Show that $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ is an integrating factor for this differential equation.[4 marks]\n\n2. Given that $y=2$ when $x=1$, solve the differential equation, giving your answer in the form $y = f(x)$.[6 marks]","markScheme":"1. This differential equation can be rewritten in the standard linear form.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + P(x)y &= Q(x)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} +\\bigg(\\textcolor{RVGreen}{- \\dfrac{2}{x}}\\bigg)\\,y &= x^2 e^{x}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Then we can identify $P(x) = -\\dfrac{2}{x}$.\n\n    Applying the $\\textbf{integrating factor (5.18)}$ we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        I(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        I(x) &= e^{\\int -\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Computing the integral in the exponent, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\int -\\dfrac{2}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= -2\\ln|x| \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting and applying the laws of exponents and logarithms, we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        I(x) &= e^{-2\\ln|x|}  \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\left(e^{\\ln|x|}\\right)^{-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= |x|^{-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        I(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Multiplying the differential equation through by $I(x)$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - \\dfrac{2}{x}\\,y &= x^2 e^{x}\\bigg)\\\\[12pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - \\dfrac{2}{x^3}\\,y &= e^{x}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recognising the left-hand side as the derivative of a product, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right) &= e^{x} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Then integrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\int\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right) &=\\int e^{x}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{y}{x^2} &= e^{x} + C  \\\\[9pt]\n\n        y &= x^2\\left(e^{x} + C\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    To solve for $C$ we can apply the initial condition $y = 2$ when $x = 1$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2 &= 1^2\\left(e^{1} + C\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        C &= 2 - e \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence the solution is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        y &= \\hspace{0em}x^2\\left(e^{x} + 2 - e\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1380","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1380\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x + 2} = x$, $x > -2$, which passes through the point $(0, 2)$.\n\n1. Using Euler's method with a step length of $h = 0.5$, find an approximation for the value of $y$ when $x = 1$. Give your answer to two decimal places.[3 marks]\n\nThe exact solution of the differential equation is $y = f(x)$, where\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x + 2}\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{3} + x^2 + 4\\right).\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n2.  1. Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$.[2 marks]\n\n    2. Hence determine whether your answer in part (a) is an overestimate or an underestimate of the true value of $y(1)$. Justify your answer.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. First we should rewrite the equation as $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = x - \\dfrac{y}{x + 2}$.\n\n    Then we can apply **Euler's method (5.18)** with $x_0 = 0$, $y_0 = 2$ and $h = 0.5$. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{{\\textbf{M1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the formulae we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} x_{n+1} &= x_n + 0.5 \\\\[2pt]\n\n        y\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} y_{n+1} &= y_n +0.5\\times\\bigg[x_n-\\frac{y_n}{x_n+2} \\bigg] \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$}] \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[-18pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we obtain\n\n    |$\\hspace{1em}n\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}x_n\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}y_n\\hspace{1em}$|\n\n    |:-:|:-:|:-:|\n\n    |0|0|2|\n\n    |1|0.5|1.5|\n\n    |2|1|1.45|\n\n    Hence we find $y(1)= 1.45$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  1. Plotting the exact solution for $0 \\le x \\le 1$, which passes through $(0, 2)$ and $\\left(1, \\tfrac{16}{9}\\right)$, gives the curve below.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA1380A.png\" alt=\"AA1380A\">\n\n        $\\text{Correct shape with minimum near x = 0.6}$\n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        $\\text{Correct endpoints}$\n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. The exact value $y(1) = \\dfrac{16}{9} = 1.777\\ldots$ is indicated in $\\textcolor{Green}{\\text{green}}$. \n\n        This exceeds the Euler estimate of $1.45$ indicated in $\\textcolor{darkred}{\\text{red}}$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA1380B.png\" alt=\"AA1380B\">\n\n        Hence the method gives an $\\hspace{0em}\\text{underestimate}$.\n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        This is due to the fact that the solution curve is concave up on $[0, 1]$, so each tangent-line step of Euler's method lies below the curve.\n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1381","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1381\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    (1+x^2)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4xy^2\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nfor $y$, which satisfies the initial condition $y(0)=-1$.","markScheme":"Rewriting the differential equation and integrating with respect to $x$ and $y$, we have \n\n$$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{M1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{y^2} &=\\dfrac{4x}{1+x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\int\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{y^2} &=\\int \\dfrac{4x}{1+x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[4pt]\n\n    -\\dfrac{1}{y} &= 2\\ln(1+x^2)+C \\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\quad [\\hspace{0.01em}\\text{since }1+x^2>0]\\hspace{2em}[1] \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ and $y=-1$ in [1], we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{M1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    -\\dfrac{1}{(-1)} &= 2\\ln(1+(0)^2)+C \\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n\n    1 &= \\cancel{2\\ln(1)}+C\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\n\n    C &= 1\\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $C$ in [1], we get\n\n$$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    -\\dfrac{1}{y} &= 2\\ln(1+x^2)+1 \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[2pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3mm}y = -\\dfrac{1}{2\\ln(1+x^2)+1}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1383","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1383\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    (1+x^2)\\hspace{0.15em}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=1+y^2\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nfor $y$, which satisfies the initial condition $y(0)=\\sqrt{3}$.","markScheme":"Rewriting the differential equation and integrating with respect to $x$ and $y$, we have \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{1+y^2} &=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{1+x^2}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\int\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{1+y^2} &=\\int\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{1+x^2}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[4pt]\n\n    \\arctan y &= \\arctan x+C \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm} \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\\\\\n\n    y &= \\tan(\\arctan x + C)\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm} \\hspace{1em}[1]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ and $y=\\sqrt{3}$ in [1], we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\sqrt{3} &= \\tan(\\arctan{0}+C) \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n\n    \\sqrt{3} &= \\tan (C)\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n\n    C &= \\arctan{\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n\n    C &= \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    y=\\tan\\left(\\arctan x+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1385","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1385\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nThe mass $M$ (in kilograms) of a growing crystal after $t$ hours can be modelled by the differential equation  \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\hspace{5mm}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}M}{\\mathrm{d}t} = kM^{1/3}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}, \\qquad k,\\,t>0\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n1. Show that the mass of the crystal is given by  \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    M(t)=\\left(\\dfrac{2kt}{3}+M_0^{2/3}\\right)^{3/2}\\rule[-4.5mm]{0pt}{10.5mm}, \\quad t>0\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- where $M_0$ is the initial mass of the crystal.  [4 marks]\n\nIt is known that the initial mass of the crystal was $64$ kg, and that after $9$ hours the mass had doubled.\n\n2. Find the value of $k$ to three significant figures.  [2 marks]\n\n3. Estimate the mass of the crystal after $15$ hours to the nearest kilogram.  [2 marks]\n\nAfter further analysis, a refined model for the mass of the crystal is given by  \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}M_2}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t}\n\n=\n\n\\big(1.6+2\\sin(0.25\\pi t)\\big)M_2^{1/3}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is measured in hours, $t \\geq 0$.\n\n4. Verify that  \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\nM_2=\\left(\\dfrac{2(1.6)t}{3}-\\dfrac{16\\cos(0.25\\pi t)}{3\\pi}+64^{2/3}\\right)^{3/2}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- is a solution of this new di\\-ffe\\-ren\\-tial equation. [5 marks] \n\n5. Sketch the graph of $M_2(t)$ and the graph of $M(t)$ found in parts (a) and (b) on the same axes, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 40$.  [3 marks]\n\n6. Use $M_2(t)$ to estimate the number of **whole** hours it takes for the mass of the crystal to reach $150$ kg. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Solving the separable differential equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}M}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t} &= kM^{1/3} \\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[12pt] \n\n    \\int M^{-1/3}\\,\\mathrm{d}M &= \\int k \\, \\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t \\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{3}{2}M^{2/3} &= k\\hspace{0.05em}t+C \\\\[12pt]\n\n    M^{2/3} &= \\dfrac{2k\\hspace{0.05em}t}{3} + A \\hspace{2em}[1]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    M &= \\left(\\dfrac{2k\\hspace{0.05em}t}{3}+A\\right)^{3/2} \\hspace{2em}[2]\\\\[-10pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- Solving [1] for the initial value $M(0)=M_0$, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    A &= M_0^{2/3} \\hspace{2em}[3]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- Substituting [3] in [2], we obtain\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-6.65mm}M(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{2k\\hspace{0.05em}t}{3}+M_0^{2/3}\\right)^{3/2} \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n2. Solving for $k$, we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n   128 &= \\left(\\dfrac{2k\\hspace{0.05em}(9)}{3}+64^{2/3}\\right)^{3/2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}} \\\\[12pt]\n\n   k&=1.566...\\\\[6pt]\n\n   &\\hspace{-0.9em}k = 1.57 \\hspace{4em}[\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n3. Evaluating $M(t)$ at $t=15$, we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n   M(15) &= \\left(\\dfrac{2(1.566...)(15)}{3}+64^{2/3}\\right)^{3/2} \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n   &=178.1...\\\\[6pt]\n\n   &\\hspace{0.em}= 178 \\hspace{5em}[\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n4. Taking the power $\\tfrac{2}{3}$ of both sides of the proposed solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    M_2^{2/3} &= \\dfrac{2(1.6)t}{3}-\\dfrac{16\\cos(0.25\\pi t)}{3\\pi}+64^{2/3} \\hspace{2em}[1]\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- Differentiating both sides of [1] with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\!\\left(M_2^{2/3}\\right)\n\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n    \\left(\\dfrac{2(1.6)t}{3}-\\dfrac{16\\cos(0.25\\pi t)}{3\\pi}+64^{2/3}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{2}{3}M_2^{-1/3}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}M_2}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n    &= \\dfrac{3.2}{3}+\\dfrac{4\\sin(0.25\\pi t)}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- Multiplying both sides by $\\dfrac{3}{2}M_2^{1/3}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}M_2}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n    &= \\left(1.6+2\\sin(0.25\\pi t)\\right)M_2^{1/3}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{R.H.S.}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- This matches the given differential equation, hence the proposed function is a solution.\n\n5. Using G.D.C., we sketch $M(t)$ using\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    y=\\left(\\dfrac{2(1.566...)x}{3}+(64)^{2/3}\\right)^{3/2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- and $M_2(t)$ using\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n   y=\\left(\\dfrac{2(1.6)x}{3}-\\dfrac{16\\cos(0.25\\pi x)}{3\\pi}+64^{2/3}\\right)^{3/2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n- to get\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA1385e.png\" alt=\"AA1385e\">\n\n6. Using G.D.C., we get\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA1385f.png\" alt=\"AA1385f\">\n\n- We must give the next whole number of hours, hence,\n\n    $$\\text{the mass reaches $150$ kg after 11 hours.}$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1655","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1655\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2y}{1 + x^2}$, where $y=1$ when $x=0$.\n\nSolve the differential equation, giving your answer in the form $y=f(x)$.","markScheme":"In this differential equation, we can separate the variables. Hence, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y &= \\dfrac{2}{1 + x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNext we need to integrate both sides. Notice that we can use the standard integral $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{1}{1 + x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\arctan x$ on the R.H.S. giving\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{1}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y &= \\int \\dfrac{2}{1 + x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\ln|y| &= 2\\arctan x + C \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $y = 1$, we may drop the modulus, and then raise $e$ to the power of both sides giving\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    e^{\\ln y} &= e^{(2\\arctan x + C)}\\\\[6pt]\n    y &= e^{(2\\arctan x + C)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTo find the value of $C$ we can apply the initial condition $y = 1$ when $x = 0$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    1&=e^{(2\\arctan0+C)}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n    1 &= e^{2(0) + C} \\\\[6pt]\n    1 &= e^{C} \\\\[6pt]\n    C &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, writing the result in the required form,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\boxed{y=e^{2\\arctan x}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1656","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1656\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 3x^2 y^3$.\n\nGiven that $y = 2$ when $x = 1$, solve the differential equation, giving your answer in the form $y = f(x)$.","markScheme":"Let's start by separating the variables and then integrate both sides.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{1}{y^3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=3x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[10pt]\n    \\int y^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y &= \\int 3x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[10pt]\n    -\\dfrac{1}{2y^2} &= x^3 + C \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen we can apply the initial condition $y = 2$ when $x = 1$ to solve for $C$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    -\\dfrac{1}{2(2)^2} &= 1 + C \\tag*{(\\textbf{(M1)})} \\\\[6pt]\n    C &= -\\dfrac{9}{8} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    -\\dfrac{1}{2y^2} &= x^3 - \\frac{9}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTo give the equation in the required form we need to isolate $y$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1}{y^2} &= \\dfrac{9}{4} - 2x^3 \\\\[10pt]\n    y^2&=\\frac{4}{9-8x^3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTaking the positive root, since $y(1) = 2 > 0$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\boxed{y=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{9 - 8x^3}}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA602","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA602\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$, where\n$x > 0$.\n\n1.  Given that $y(1) = 2$, use Euler's method with step length $h = 0.5$\n    to find an approximation for $y(3)$. Give your answer correct to two\n    significant figures. [6 marks]\n\n2.  Solve the equation\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ for\n    $y(1) = 2$.  [6 marks]\n\n3.  Find the percentage error when $y(3)$ is approximated by the final\n    rounded value found in part (a). Give your answer correct to two\n    significant figures. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{y}{x} \\hspace{3em} [x > 0] \\hspace{1.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{6.55em} y(1) &= 2 \\\\[10pt]\n    h &= 0.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7em} x\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} x_{n+1} &= x_n + 0.5 \\\\[4pt]\n    y\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} y_{n+1} &= y_n +0.5\\times\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{y_n}{x_n}\\bigg] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using Euler's method, we obtain\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/74696bc6706165e57bfc152ad13c76ba39254d2a.svg\" alt=\"74696bc6706165e57bfc152ad13c76ba39254d2a.svg\">\n\n    Hence we find\n     $y(3) \\approx 7.4$\n\n2.  An integrating factor is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    I(x) &= e^{\\footnotesize\\raisebox{0.2em}{\\mbox{$\\int-\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}}} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= e^{-\\ln x} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, multiplying both sides of the\n    differential equation by $1/x$, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - \\dfrac{y}{x^2} &= \\dfrac{1}{2x} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\dfrac{y}{x}\\bigg] &= \\dfrac{1}{2x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Integrating both sides of the equation $(1)$ with respect to $x$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.7em} \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\dfrac{y}{x}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int\\dfrac{1}{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{y}{x} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln x + C \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x > 0$}] \\\\[8pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{1}{2}x\\ln x + x\\hspace{0.05em}C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $x = 1$ and $y = 2$ in the equation $(2)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.4em} 2 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(1)(0) + (1)C \\\\[6pt]\n    C &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.15em} y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x\\ln x + 2x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Evaluating $y(x)$ for $x = 3$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} y(3) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(3)\\ln 3 + 2(3) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 7.6479\\dots \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the percentage error\n    formula\n    $\\epsilon = \\bigg|\\dfrac{v_\\mathrm{A} - v_\\mathrm{E}}{v_\\mathrm{E}}\\bigg|\\times 100\\hspace{0.05em}\\%$\n    with $v_\\mathrm{E} = (7.6479\\dots)$ and $v_\\mathrm{A} = 7.4$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\epsilon &= \\bigg|\\dfrac{7.4 - (7.6479\\dots)}{(7.6479\\dots)}\\bigg|\\times 100\\hspace{0.05em}\\% \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 3.2\\hspace{0.05em}\\%\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA603","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA603\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve the differential equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1+x^2)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2xy^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfor\n$y$, which satisfies the initial condition $y(0)=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$.","markScheme":"In this equation, it is possible to separate the variables.\n\nRewriting the differential equation and integrating with respect to $x$\nand $y$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{y^2} &=\\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[8pt]\n\\int\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{y^2} &=\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[4pt]\n-\\dfrac{1}{y} &= \\ln(1+x^2)+C \\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\quad [\\hspace{0.01em}\\text{since }1+x^2>0\\hspace{0.01em}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ and $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ in (1), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} &= \\ln(1+(0)^2)+C \\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n2 &= \\cancel{\\ln(1)}+C\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\nC &= 2\\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{7mm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting the value of\n$C$ in (1), we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\dfrac{1}{y} &= \\ln(1+x^2)+2 \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[2pt]\n&\\hspace{-2mm}y = -\\dfrac{1}{\\ln(1+x^2)+2}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA606","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA606\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nConsider the differential equation\n$x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+y=x^{p+1}$ where $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$,\n$x\\neq 0$ and $p$ is a positive integer, $p>0$.\n\n1.  Solve the differential equation given that $y=1$ when $x=1$. Give\n    your answer in the form $y=f(x)$.[8 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that the $x$-coordinate(s) of the points on the curve\n        $y=f(x)$ where $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=0$ satisfy the\n        equation $x^{p+2}=1$.\n\n    2.  Deduce the set of values for $p$ such that there are two points\n        on the curve $y=f(x)$ where\n        $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=0$. Give a reason for your\n        answer.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The differential equation is equivalent to\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\dfrac{y}{x}=x^p \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula to find an integrating factor of (1).\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    I(x) &= e^{\\footnotesize\\raisebox{0.3em}{\\mbox{$\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\, \\mathrm{d}x$}}}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n    &= e^{\\ln x}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    &= x\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Multiplying both sides\n    of (1) by the integrating factor $I(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+y&=x^{p+1}\\\\\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}[xy] &= x^{p+1}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}[xy]\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int x^{p+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    xy &= \\dfrac{1}{p+2}x^{p+2}+C\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    y &= \\dfrac{1}{p+2}x^{p+1}+\\dfrac{C}{x}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $x=1$ and $y=1$ in equation (2), we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 &= \\dfrac{1}{p+2}+C \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    C &= 1-\\dfrac{1}{p+2}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &= \\dfrac{p+1}{p+2}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore\n    the solution to the differential equation is\n\n    $$\n    y=\\dfrac{1}{p+2}\\left(x^{p+1}+\\dfrac{p+1}{x}\\right)\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The derivative of $y$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{1}{p+2}\\left((p+1)x^{p}-\\dfrac{p+1}{x^2}\\right)\\rule[-4mm]{0mm}{4mm}\\\\\n        &= \\dfrac{p+1}{p+2}\\left(x^p-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{6mm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Setting the derivative equal to zero in (3), we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        0 &= \\dfrac{p+1}{p+2}\\left(x^p-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)\\rule[-4mm]{0mm}{4mm}\\\\\n        0&= x^p-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\rule[-4mm]{0mm}{9mm}\\\\\n        x^p &= \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\rule[-4mm]{0mm}{9mm}\\\\\n        &\\hspace{-7.25mm}x^{p+2} = 1\\rule[0mm]{0mm}{5mm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The equation (4) has two solutions if and only if $p+2$ is even\n        and therefore (4) has two solutions only when $p$ is even."},{"ref":"AA607","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA607\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nConsider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\dfrac{2x^3}{1+x^4}y=4x^3$ where $y=1$\nwhen $x=0$.\n\n1.  Show that $\\sqrt{1+x^4}$ is an integrating factor for this\n    differential equation.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Solve the differential equation giving your answer in the form\n    $y=f(x)$.[6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula to find an integrating factor, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     I(x)&=e^{\\raisebox{0.35em}{\\mbox{$\\int$}}\\hspace{-0.1em}\\footnotesize\\raisebox{0.42em}{\\mbox{$ \\frac{2x^3\\rule[-0.5mm]{0pt}{2.25mm}}{1+x^4\\rule[-0.5mm]{0pt}{2.3mm}}\\, \\mathrm{d}x$}}}\\\\\n     &= e^{\\raisebox{0.1em}{\\footnotesize\\mbox{$\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(1+x^4\\right)$}}}\\\\\n     &= e^{\\raisebox{0.1em}{\\footnotesize\\mbox{$\\ln\\left(1+x^4\\right)^{1/2}$}}}\\\\\n     &\\hspace{0.3em}= \\sqrt{1+x^4}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  Multiplying both sides of the differential equation by the\n    integrating factor, $I(x)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sqrt{1+x^4}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\dfrac{2x^3y\\sqrt{1+x^4}}{1+x^4}&=4x^3\\sqrt{1+x^4}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{3.5mm}\\\\\n    \\sqrt{1+x^4}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\dfrac{2x^3y}{\\sqrt{1+x^4}}&=4x^3\\sqrt{1+x^4}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[y\\sqrt{1+x^4}\\right]&=4x^3\\sqrt{1+x^4}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[y\\sqrt{1+x^4}\\right]\\, \\mathrm{d}x &=\\int 4x^3\\sqrt{1+x^4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\n    y\\sqrt{1+x^4} &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\left(1+x^4\\right)^{3/2}+C\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\n    y &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\left(1+x^4\\right)+\\dfrac{C}{\\sqrt{1+x^4}}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{6mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ in equation (1), we have as expression\n    for $y$\n\n    $$\n    y=\\dfrac{2}{3}\\left(1+x^4\\right)+\\dfrac{1}{3\\sqrt{1+x^4}}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA611","marks":"13","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA611\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1.  Consider the differential equation\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = f\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right), \\quad x>0.\n\n    $$\n    Use the substitution $v=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ to show that\n    the general solution of this\\\n    differential equation is\n\n    $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{f(v)-v} = \\ln x + C.$[4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve the differential\n    equation\n\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{4x^2+5xy+y^2}{x^2}, \\quad x>0,\n\n    $$\n    given that $y=2$ when $x=1$. Give your answer in\n    the form $y=g(x)$.[9 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Introducing the new variable $v=y/x$ or $y=vx$ and taking the\n    derivative with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}  (vx) \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    &= v +x \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Then, the differential equation becomes\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= f(v) \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    v+x\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= f(v) \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{f(v)-v}&= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{x} \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\[4pt]\n    \\int\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{f(v)-v}&= \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{x}\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{9mm} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-18mm}\\int\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{f(v)-v}= \\ln x+c \\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  First we rearrange the differential equation\n    $$\n    \\hspace{14mm}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 4+5\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)+\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)^2\n    $$\n\n    Then, $f(v)=4+5v+v^2$ and using the general solution from (a)\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{4+5v+v^2-v} &= \\ln x+C \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{(2+v)^2} &= \\ln x+C \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    -\\dfrac{1}{2+v} &= \\ln x+C \\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using that $y=2$ when $x=1$ and therefore $v=2$, we get\n\n    $$\n    C=-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n    $$\n    Substituting the variable $v$ we obtain an expression for $y$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln x-\\dfrac{1}{4} &= -\\dfrac{1}{2+v}\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n    2+v &= \\dfrac{1}{\\frac{1}{4}-\\ln x}\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{10mm} \\\\\n    2+\\dfrac{y}{x} &= \\dfrac{1}{\\frac{1}{4}-\\ln x}\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\[4pt]\n    y &= x \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{1}{4}-\\ln x}-2\\right)\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2mm}y = x \\left(\\dfrac{\\frac{1}{2}+2\\ln x}{\\frac{1}{4}-\\ln x}\\right)\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA612","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA612\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\bigg(\\dfrac{6x}{3x^2-2}\\bigg)\\,y=4x$, given that $y=4$ when $x=0$.\n\n1.  Show that $3x^2-2$ is an integrating factor for this differential\n    equation.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence solve this differential equation. Give the answer in the form\n    $y=f(x)$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula to find an integrating factor\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    I(x) &= e^{\\footnotesize\\raisebox{0.5em}{\\mbox{$\\int\\frac{6x}{3x^2-2}\\, \\mathrm{d}x$}}}\\rule[-1mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n    &= e^{\\ln(3x^2-2)}\\rule[-1mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n    &= 3x^2-2\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{6mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $3x^2-2$\n    is an integrating factor.\n\n2.  Multiplying both sides of the differential equation by the\n    integrating factor, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left(3x^2-2\\right)\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+6xy &= 12x^3-8x \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\left(3x^2-2\\right)y\\right] &= 12x^3-8x \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\left(3x^2-2\\right)y\\right] \\mathrm{d}x &= \\int (12x^3-8x) \\mathrm{d}x \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    \\left(3x^2-2\\right)y &= 3x^4-4x^2+C\\rule[-2.5mm]{0pt}{8mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $x=0$ and $y=4$ in (1), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (3(0)^2 -2)(4) &= 3(0)^4-4(0)^2+C \\\\[7pt]\n    C &= -8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, the solution is\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.7em}y = \\dfrac{3x^4-4x^2-8}{3x^2-2}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA617","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA617\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe curves $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ both pass through the point $(1,0)$ and\nare defined by the differential equations\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2x-y^2$ and\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3y-\\dfrac{x}{2}$\nrespectively.\n\n1.  Show that the tangent to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$ is\n    normal to the curve $y=g(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $g(x)$.[7 marks]\n\n3.  Use Euler's method with steps of $0.2$ to estimate $f(2)$ to 5\n    decimal places.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The gradient of $f$ at $(1,0)$ is $2(1)-(0)^2=2$ whereas the\n    gradient of $g$ at $(1,0)$ is\n    $3(0)-\\dfrac{1}{2}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{5mm}$. Therefore,\n    as one gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other, we know\n    that $f(x)$ is normal to $g(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$.\n\n2.  An integrating factor for\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-3y=-\\dfrac{x}{2}$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    I(x) &= e^{\\footnotesize\\mbox{$\\int -3\\, \\mathrm{d}x$}}\\\\[7pt]\n    &= e^{-3x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Multiplying both sides by the integrating factor, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    e^{-3x}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-3y\\right)&= e^{-3x}\\left(-\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\\\[2pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[ye^{-3x}\\right] &= -\\dfrac{xe^{-3x}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Integrating both sides of the equation (1) with respect to $x$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[ye^{-3x}\\right] \\mathrm{d}x &= \\int -\\dfrac{xe^{-3x}}{2}\\, \\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n    ye^{-3x} &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int xe^{-3x}\\, \\mathrm{d}x \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[-\\dfrac{1}{3}xe^{-3x}+\\dfrac{1}{3}\\int e^{-3x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right]\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &=\\dfrac{1}{6}xe^{-3x}-\\dfrac{1}{6}\\int e^{-3x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &=\\dfrac{1}{6}x e^{-3x}+\\dfrac{1}{18}e^{-3x}+C\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{18} e^{-3x}(3x+1)+C\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $x=1$ and $y=0$ in (2), we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0 &= \\dfrac{1}{18} e^{-3}(4)+C \\\\[3pt]\n    C &= -\\dfrac{4}{18} e^{-3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore an\n    expression for $g(x)$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11.5mm}y=\\dfrac{e^{-3x}(3x+1)-4e^{-3}}{18e^{-3x}}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 2x-y^2 \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    y(1) &= 0 \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    h &= 0.2\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5.5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7em} x\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} x_{n+1} &= x_n + 0.2 \\\\[2pt]\n    y\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} y_{n+1} &= y_n +0.2\\times\\big[2x_n-y_n^2\\big]\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using Euler's method, we obtain\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/68cb167d79a9e181f6de8c131e519dc8f409146a.svg\" alt=\"68cb167d79a9e181f6de8c131e519dc8f409146a.svg\">\n\n    Hence we find\n     $y(2)\\approx 1.80239$"},{"ref":"AA666","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA666\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nConsider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} -(\\tan x)\\hspace{0.05em}y = 1$, where\n$x \\neq \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}$, for any integer $n$.\n\n1.  Given that $y(0) = 1$, use Euler's method with step length $h = 0.2$\n    to find an approximation for $y(1)$. Give your answer correct to two\n    decimal places. [6 marks]\n\n2.  Solve the equation\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} -(\\tan x)\\hspace{0.05em}y = 1$.\n    Give your answer in the form $y=f(x)$.\n    [6 marks]\n\n3.  Find the percentage error when $y(1)$ is approximated by the final\n    rounded value found in part (a). Give your answer correct to two\n    significant figures. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= 1 + (\\tan x)y \\hspace{3em} \\bigg[x \\neq \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}\\bigg] \\hspace{1.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{6.25em} y(0) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    h &= 0.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} x_{n+1} &= x_n + 0.2 \\\\[2pt]\n    y\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} y_{n+1} &= y_n +0.2\\times\\bigg[1 + (\\tan x_n)y_n \\bigg] \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using Euler's method, we obtain\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/ba982c50d6f744daecad4d742b810439a420b395.svg\" alt=\"ba982c50d6f744daecad4d742b810439a420b395.svg\">\n\n    Hence we find\n     $y(1) \\approx 2.87$\n\n2.  An integrating factor is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    I(x) &= e^{\\footnotesize\\raisebox{0.2em}{\\mbox{$\\int-\\tan x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}}} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= e^{\\ln (\\cos x)} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\cos x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, multiplying both sides of the\n    differential equation by $\\cos x$, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos x\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - (\\cos x)(\\tan x)y &= \\cos x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos x\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + (-\\sin x)y &= \\cos x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[(\\cos x)y\\bigg] &= \\cos x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Integrating both sides of the equation $(1)$ with respect to $x$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[(\\cos x)y\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    (\\cos x)y &= \\sin x + C  \\\\[8pt]\n    y &= \\tan x + C\\sec x\\hspace{0.05em} \\hspace{1.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    the last equation is possible since $x\\neq \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}$.\n\n    Substituting $x = 0$ and $y = 1$ in the equation $(2)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.4em} 1 &= \\tan (0)+C\\sec(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    C &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.15em} y = \\tan x +\\sec x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Evaluating $y(x)$ for $x = 1$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y(1) &= \\tan(1)+\\sec(1) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 3.408\\dots \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the percentage error\n    formula\n    $\\epsilon = \\bigg|\\dfrac{v_\\mathrm{A} - v_\\mathrm{E}}{v_\\mathrm{E}}\\bigg|\\times 100\\hspace{0.05em}\\%$\n    with $v_\\mathrm{E} = (3.408\\dots)$ and $v_\\mathrm{A} = 2.87$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\epsilon &= \\bigg|\\dfrac{2.87 - (3.408\\dots)}{(3.408\\dots)}\\bigg|\\times 100\\hspace{0.05em}\\% \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 16\\hspace{0.05em}\\%\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA698","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA698\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} - e^x = 1$.\n\nGiven that $y\\left(0\\right) = 1$, use Euler's method with step length\n$h = 0.25$ to find an approximation for $y\\left(1\\right)$. Give your\nanswer correct to two decimal places.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= e^x +1  \\hspace{1.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{6.25em} y(0) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\nh &= 0.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} x_{n+1} &= x_n + 0.25 \\\\[2pt]\ny\\text{-iteration:}\\hspace{0.75em} y_{n+1} &= y_n +0.25\\times\\bigg[e^{x_n} + 1 \\bigg] \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's method, we obtain\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/5012cde401b92b0132542335f99910ec80cc28af.svg\" alt=\"5012cde401b92b0132542335f99910ec80cc28af.svg\">\n\nHence we find\n $y(1) \\approx 3.51$"},{"ref":"AA900","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA900\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nThe population $P$ of fish in a lake after $t$ weeks can be modelled by\nthe differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5mm}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = k\\sqrt{P}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}, \\qquad k,\\,t>0\n$$\n\n1.  Show that the population of fish is given by\n    $$\n    P(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{kt}{2}+\\sqrt{P_0}\\right)^2\\rule[-4.5mm]{0pt}{10.5mm}, \\quad t>0\n    $$\n    where $P_0$ is the initial fish population.[4 marks]\n\nIt is known that the initial fish population was $3\\hspace{0.1em}000$,\nand that $24$ weeks later the population had doubled in\nsize.\n\n2.  Find the value of $k$ to three significant\n    figures.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Estimate the number of fish after $30$ weeks to the nearest\n    integer.[2 marks]\n\nAfter a careful adjustment it is found that the model that best\ndescribes the fish population is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P_2}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t} = \\big(1.89+3\\cos(0.2\\pi t)\\big)\\sqrt{P_2}%\\rule[-4.5mm]{1pt}{10mm}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time measured in weeks,\n$t \\geq 0$.\n\n4.  Verify that\n    $P_2=\\left(\\dfrac{1.89\\hspace{0.05em}t}{2}+\\dfrac{30\\sin(0.2\\pi t)}{4\\pi}+\\sqrt{3\\hspace{0.15em}000}\\right)^2$\n    is the solution of this new\n    differential equation.\n    [5 marks]\n\n5.  Sketch the graph of $P_2(t)$ and the graph of $P(t)$ found\n    in parts (a) and (b) on the same axes, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 50$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n6.  Use $P_2(t)$ to estimate the number of **whole** weeks it\n    takes for the population to reach $5\\,000$ fish.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Solving the separable differential equation, we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t} &= k\\sqrt{P} \\\\[12pt] \n\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\sqrt{P}} &= \\int k \\, \\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t \\\\[12pt]\n\n    2\\sqrt{P} &= k\\hspace{0.05em}t+C \\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\sqrt{P} &= \\dfrac{k\\hspace{0.05em}t}{2} + A \\hspace{4.85em}{(1)}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    P &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{k\\hspace{0.05em}t}{2}+A\\bigg)^2 \\hspace{3em}{(2)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Solving (1) for the initial value $P(0)=P_0$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A &= \\sqrt{P_0} \\hspace{8.2em}{(3)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting (3) in (2), we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &P(t) = \\bigg(\\dfrac{kt}{2}+\\sqrt{P_0}\\bigg)^2 \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Solving for $k$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    6\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= \\left(\\dfrac{k\\hspace{0.05em}(24)}{2}+\\sqrt{3\\hspace{0.15em}000}\\right)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n\n   &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{k \\approx 1.89} \\hspace{4em}[\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Evaluating $P(t)$ at $t=30$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(30) &= \\left(\\dfrac{(1.89)(30)}{2}+\\sqrt{3\\hspace{0.15em}000}\\right)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n\n   &\\hspace{0.285em}\\boxed{= 6\\hspace{0.15em}909} \\hspace{5em}[\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. Taking the square root of both sides and differentiating implicitly, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\sqrt{P_2} &= \\dfrac{1.89\\hspace{0.05em}t}{2}+\\dfrac{30\\sin(0.2\\pi t)}{4\\pi}+\\sqrt{3\\hspace{0.15em}000} \\hspace{5em}{(4)}\\\\[12pt]\n\n   \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t} \\sqrt{P_2} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t} \\left(\\dfrac{1.89\\hspace{0.05em}t}{2}+\\dfrac{30\\sin(0.2\\pi t)}{4\\pi}+\\sqrt{3\\hspace{0.15em}000}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n\n   \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{P_2}}\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P_2}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t} &= \\dfrac{1.89}{2} + \\dfrac{3\\cos(0.2\\pi t)}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n\n   \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P_2}{\\mathrm{d}\\hspace{0.05em}t} &= \\left(1.89+3\\cos(0.2\\pi t)\\right) \\sqrt{P_2} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. Using G.D.C., we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA900e.PNG\" alt=\"AA900e\">\n\n6. Using G.D.C., we get \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA900f.PNG\" alt=\"AA900f\">\n\n    $\\text{Therefore the population reaches 5000 fish after 19 weeks.}$"},{"ref":"AA904","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA904\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nThe acceleration, $a\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{ms}^{-2}$ of a particle\nmoving in a vertical trajectory at time $t$ seconds, $t\\geq 0$, is given\nby $a(t)=-(3+v)$ where $v$ is the particle's velocity in ms$^{-1}$.\n\nAt $t=0$, the particle is at a fixed origin O and has an initial\nvelocity of $v_0\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{ms}^{-1}$.\n\n1.  By solving an appropriate differential equation, show that the\n    particle's\\\n    velocity is given by $v(t)=(v_0+3)e^{-t}-3$.[6 marks]\n\nThe particle initially moves upwards until it reaches its maximum height\nfrom O, and then returns to O.\n\nLet $s$ metres represent the particle's displacement from O, and\n$s_{\\text{max}}$ the maximum displacement from\nO.\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that the time $T$ taken for the particle to reach\n        $s_\\text{max}$ satisfies the equation\n        $e^{-T} = \\dfrac{3\\rule[-0mm]{0pt}{4.5mm}}{v_0+3}.$\n    2.  Hence, solve for $T$ in terms of $v_0$.\n\n    3.  By solving an appropriate differential equation and using the\n        results from **part (b) (i)** and **(ii)**, find an expression\n        for $s_{\\text{max}}$ in terms of $v_0$.[8 marks]\n\nLet $v(T-k)$ represent the particle's velocity $k$ seconds before it\nreaches $s_{\\text{max}}$, where\n\n$$\nv(T-k) = (v_0+3)e^{-(T-k)}-3\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\n$$\n\n3.  By using the result from **part (b) (i)**, show that\n    $v(T-k) = 3e^k-3$.[2 marks]\n\nSimilarly, let $v(T+k)$ represent the particle's velocity $k$ seconds\nafter it reaches $s_{\\text{max}}$.\n\n4.  Deduce a similar expression for $v(T+k)$ in terms of $k$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n5.  Hence, show that $v(T-k)+v(T+k)\\geq 0$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Solving the separable differential equation for $v(t)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a &= -(3+v) \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= -(3+v) \\\\[12pt]\n    \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{3+v} &= - \\int \\mathrm{d}t \\\\[12pt]\n    \\ln(3+v) &= -t + C\\\\[12pt]\n    3 + v &= Ae^{-t} \\\\[12pt]\n    v &= Ae^{-t}-3\\\\[8pt]\\\\\n    \\intertext{Using the initial value $v(0)=v_0$, we get}\\\\\n    v_0 &= A-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    A &= v_0+3\\\\[8pt]\\\\\n    \\intertext{Hence, the particle's velocity is}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-1.87mm}v(t) = \\left(v_0+3\\right)e^{-t}-3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The time $T$ satisfies $v(T)=0$, hence, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (v_0+3)e^{-T} - 3 &= 0 \\\\[13pt]\n        (v_0+3)e^{-T} &= 3 \\\\[10pt]\n        &\\hspace{-6mm}e^{-T} = \\dfrac{3}{v_0+3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Solving equation (1) for $T$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        e^{-T} &= \\dfrac{3}{v_0+3} \\\\[12pt]\n        e^T &= \\dfrac{v_0+3}{3} \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2.6mm} T = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{v_0+3}{3}\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Solving the differential equation for $s(t)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        v &= (v_0+3)e^{-t} - 3 \\\\[12pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= (v_0+3)e^{-t} - 3 \\\\[12pt]\n        s &= \\int \\big[(v_0+3)e^{-t} - 3\\big]\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\[12pt]\n        s &= -(v_0+3)e^{-t}-3t + C\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using the initial condition $s(0)=0$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        s(0) &= -(v_0+3) + C \\\\[12pt]\n        C &= v_0+3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Substituting the value of $C$ in (2), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        s &= -(v_0+3)e^{-t}-3t + v_0+3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Evaluating at $T$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        s_{\\text{max}} &= -(v_0+3)e^{-T}-3T+v_0+3 \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -3-3\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{v_0+3}{3}\\right)+v_0+3\\hspace{2em}\\big[\\text{by (b) (i) and (ii)}\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-6.69mm} s_{\\text{max}} =v_0-3\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{v_0+3}{3}\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using part (b) (i), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(T-k) &= (v_0+3)e^{-T}e^k-3\\\\[12pt]\n    &= (v_0+3)\\left(\\dfrac{3}{v_0+3}\\right)e^k-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3e^{k}-3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using part (b) (i), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(T+k) &=(v_0+3)e^{-(T+k)}-3\\\\[12pt]\n    &=(v_0+3)e^{-T}e^{-k}-3\\\\[12pt]\n    &= (v_0+3)\\left(\\dfrac{3}{v_0+3}\\right)e^{-k}-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3e^{-k}-3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Using parts (c) and (d), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(T-k) + v(T+k)  &= 3e^{k}-3 + 3e^{-k}-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3e^{k}-6 + 3e^{-k} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3e^{2k}-6e^{k} + 3}{e^{k}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3(e^{2k}-2e^{k} + 1)}{e^{k}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3(e^{k}-1)^2}{e^{k}} \\geq 0 \\hspace{3em} \\big[\\text{since }e^{k}> 0\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA940","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA940\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nA video streaming service company are monitoring their market share in a region in which they have recently commenced operations.\n\nThe number of households, $N$, they predict will subscribe to the streaming service can be modelled by the logistic differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\dfrac{3kN(L-N)}{2L}$\n\nwhere $t$ is time measured in years and $k$, $L$ are positive constants.\n\nThe constant $L$ represents the total number of households in the region who could possibly subscribe to the streaming service.\n\n1. Show that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}=\\bigg(\\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\bigg)^2(N)(L-N)(L-2N)$. [4 marks]\n\n2. Hence show that the number of households subscribing to the streaming service is predicted to increase at its maximum rate when $N=\\dfrac{L}{2}$. [5 marks]\n\n3. Hence determine the maximum value of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ in terms of $k$ and $L$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $N_0$ be the number of households who have subscribed to the streaming service at the time the company start monitoring their market share. \n\n4. By solving the logistic differential equation, show that its solution can be expressed in the form\n\n    $kt=\\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg)\\mathrm{ln}\\Bigg(\\dfrac{N(L-N_0)}{N_0(L-N)}\\Bigg)$\n\n    [7 marks]\n\nAfter 12 years, the number of subscribed households is predicted to be $4N_0$. It is known that $L=5N_0$.\n\n5. Find the value of $k$ for this model. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the product rule, we can differentiate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ implicitly.\n\n    Let $u = \\dfrac{3kN}{2L}$ and $v = L - N$, so that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t} = uv$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}{t}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}N} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}{t}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}N} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= -1\\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting into the product rule formula, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} &= u \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}{t}} + v \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}{t}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{3kN}{2L}\\bigg)\\bigg(-1\\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\bigg) + (L - N)\\bigg(\\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\bigg)(L-2N)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can also substitute in $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\dfrac{3kN(L-N)}{2L}$, giving\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\times \\dfrac{3kN(L-N)}{2L}\\bigg)(L-2N) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &  \\hspace{-2.1em}\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = \\bigg(\\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\bigg)^2(N)(L-N)(L-2N)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. The maximum value for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ will occur when $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0$ and changes sign from positive to negative. There are three values of $N$ for which $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0$:\n\n    $\\bullet$ When $N=0$ $\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\bullet$ When $L - N = 0 \\hspace{0.3em}\\implies N = L$ $\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\bullet$ When $L - 2N = 0 \\hspace{0.3em}\\implies N = \\dfrac{L}{2}$ $\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    The following table shows how the sign of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}$ changes between these values:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Differential%20Equations/images/AA940a.PNG\" alt=\"AA940a\">\n\n    When $N=\\dfrac{L}{2}$, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2N}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0$ and changes sign from positive to negative.\n\n    Therefore $\\text{the maximum value for }\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\text{ occurs at }N=\\dfrac{L}{2}$\n\n3. When $N=\\dfrac{L}{2}$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{3kN(L-N)}{2L} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{3k\\bigg(\\dfrac{L}{2}\\bigg)\\bigg(L-\\dfrac{L}{2}\\bigg)}{2L} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{3k\\bigg(\\dfrac{L}{2}\\bigg)\\bigg(\\dfrac{L}{2}\\bigg)}{2L} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{3kL^2}{8L} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{3kL}{8}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The maximum value of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ is $\\dfrac{3kL}{8}$\n\n4. By separating the variables, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{N(L-N)}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}N &= \\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}t\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    And therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\int \\dfrac{1}{N(L-N)}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}N &= \\int \\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}t\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The left hand side can be expressed in partial fraction form:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{N(L-N)} &\\equiv \\dfrac{A}{N} + \\dfrac{B}{L-N} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    1 &\\equiv A(L-N) + BN\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $N=0$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    1 &= AL \\\\[10pt]\n\n    A &= \\dfrac{1}{L}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $N=L$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    1 &= A(L-L) + BL \\\\[10pt]\n\n    1 &= BL \\\\[10pt]\n\n    B &= \\dfrac{1}{L}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{N(L-N)} &= \\dfrac{1}{L}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{N} + \\dfrac{1}{L-N}\\bigg)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{L}\\hspace{0.15em}\\int \\dfrac{1}{N} + \\dfrac{1}{L-N}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}N &= \\int \\dfrac{3k}{2L}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\int \\dfrac{1}{N} + \\dfrac{1}{L-N}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}N &= \\int \\dfrac{3k}{2}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}t\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Integrating both sides of the equation, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{ln}|N| - \\mathrm{ln}|L-N| &= \\dfrac{3kt}{2} + c \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N}{L-N}\\bigg) &= \\dfrac{3kt}{2} + c \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since }N>0 \\text{ and }L>N]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that $N=N_0$ when $t=0$, we can find the value of c:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N_0}{L-N_0}\\bigg) &= c\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N}{L-N}\\bigg) &= \\dfrac{3kt}{2} + \\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N_0}{L-N_0}\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{3kt}{2} &= \\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N}{L-N}\\bigg) - \\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N_0}{L-N_0}\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N(L-N_0)}{N_0(L-N)}\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &  \\hspace{-0.9em}\\boxed{kt = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{N(L-N_0)}{N_0(L-N)}\\bigg)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. Substituting $t=12$, $N=4N_0$ and $L=5N_0$ into the equation from part (d), we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    12k &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{4N_0(5N_0-N_0)}{N_0(5N_0-4N_0)}\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{ln}\\bigg(\\dfrac{4(4N_0)}{(N_0)}\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{ln}\\hspace{0.15em}16 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    k &= \\dfrac{1}{18}\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{ln}\\hspace{0.15em}16 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &  \\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{k \\approx 0.154}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Integration by Parts and by Substitution","questions":[{"ref":"AA1041","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1041\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nConsider the function $f_n(x)=e^{x}\\cos{nx}$, where $n\\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  1.  Show that $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f_2(x) \\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\dfrac{1}{5}(e^{2\\pi}-1)$.\n\n    2.  Show that $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f_n(x) \\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\dfrac{1}{1+n^2}(e^{2\\pi}-1)$. [7 marks]\n\n2.  1.  Expand and simplify $\\cos(A+B)+\\cos(A-B)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, write $\\cos 3x\\cos x$ as the sum of two cosine functions.\n\n    3.  Hence, find $a$, where $a\\in \\mathbb{Q}$, if $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^{x}\\cos{3x}\\cos{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=a(e^{2\\pi}-1)$. [6 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $b$, where $b\\in \\mathbb{Q}$, if $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^{x}\\sin{4x}\\sin{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=b(e^{2\\pi}-1)$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  As $n=2$, we need to integrate the following function\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f_2(x) \\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The integral is a product of two functions of $x$, this suggests we use integration by parts (IbP).\n\n        We need to choose a function for $u$ and $v'$, it is a good strategy to pick a function that is easy to integrate for $v'$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            u&=\\cos{2x} \\hspace{2em} \\text{ therefore } u'=-2\\sin{2x} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            v'&=e^x \\hspace{3.6em} \\text{ therefore } v=e^x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can then substitute these expressions into the formula for $\\textbf{integration by parts}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\Big[e^x\\cos{2x}\\Big]_{0}^{2\\pi}-\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}-2e^x\\sin{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Next resolve the limits and simplify the integral\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\Big[e^{2\\pi}\\cos4\\pi -e^0\\cos{0}\\Big]+2\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\sin{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)+2\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\sin{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now we need to use (repeat) integration by parts again.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            u&=\\sin{2x} \\hspace{2em} \\text{ therefore } u'=2\\cos{2x} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            v'&=e^x \\hspace{3.6em} \\text{ therefore } v=e^x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Substituting them into the integral we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)+2\\left(\\Big[e^x\\sin{2x}\\Big]_{0}^{2\\pi}-2\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        In a similar fashion to earlier, we can resolve the limits and simplify\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)+2\\left(\\Big[e^{2\\pi}\\sin{4\\pi}-e^{0}\\sin{0}\\Big]-2\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n        &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)-4\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Notice that the integral is a multiple of our original integral\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)-4\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $\\displaystyle5\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)$\n\n        $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{2x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right) \\hspace{2em}$ ...as required\n\n    2.  The integral for $n$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f_n(x) \\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{nx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can use a very similar method to part (a). Notice the coefficient changes to $n$. Use previous workings to help\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\left[e^{2\\pi}\\cos2n\\pi -e^0\\cos{0}\\right]+n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\sin{nx} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[6pt]\n            &(\\cos{2n\\pi=1} \\text{ for all }n\\in \\mathbb{Z})\\\\\n            &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)+n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\sin{nx} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We again repeat IbP, in a similar fashion to part (a)\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)+n\\left(\\Big[e^x\\sin{nx}\\Big]_{0}^{2\\pi}-n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{nx} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Resolve the limits and simplify\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)+n\\left(\\Big[e^{2\\pi}\\sin{2n\\pi}-e^{0}\\sin{0}\\Big]-n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{nx} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n        &(\\sin{2n\\pi=0} \\text{ for all }n\\in \\mathbb{Z})\\\\\n        &=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)-n^2\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{nx} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $\\displaystyle(1+n^2)\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{nx} \\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right)$\n\n        $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^x\\cos{nx} \\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{1+n^2}\\left(e^{2\\pi}-1\\right) \\hspace{2em}$ ...as required\n\n2.  1.  Using the compound angle formulae for cosine\n\n        $\\cos{(A+B)}+\\cos{(A-B)}$\n\n        $=\\cos A\\cos B -\\sin A\\sin B+(\\cos A\\cos B+\\sin A\\sin B)$\n\n        $=\\cos A\\cos B-\\cancel{\\sin A\\sin B}+\\cos A\\cos B+\\cancel{\\sin A\\sin B}$\n\n        $=2\\cos A\\cos B$\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    2.  By comparing $\\cos 3x\\cos x$ with the previous result we can see that $A=3x$ and $B=x$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            2\\cos 3x\\cos x &= \\cos(3x+x)+\\cos(3x-x) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n            \\cos 3x\\cos x &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos{4x}+\\cos{2x}\\right)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  Use the previous answer to rewrite the integral\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^{x}\\cos{3x}\\cos{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\tfrac{1}{2}e^{x}\\left(\\cos{4x}+\\cos{2x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Integrate each term individually\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^{x}\\cos{4x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^{x}\\cos{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now use the formula from (a)(ii), where $n=4$ and $n=2$ respectively\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{1+4^2}(e^{2\\pi}-1)+ \\dfrac{1}{1+2^2}(e^{2\\pi}-1)\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            &\\text{(Write $\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{17}+\\tfrac{1}{5}\\right)$ as a single fraction)}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            &=\\tfrac{11}{85}(e^{2\\pi}-1)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore $\\hspace{0.525em}a=\\tfrac{11}{85}$\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n3.  In a similar way to part (b) we can find $\\sin{4x}\\sin{2x}$ as the sum of two cosine functions.\n\n    $\\cos{(A+B)}-\\cos{(A-B)}=\\cos A\\cos B -\\sin A\\sin B-(\\cos A\\cos B+\\sin A\\sin B)$\n\n    $=\\cancel{\\cos A\\cos B}-\\sin A\\sin B-\\cancel{\\cos A\\cos B}-\\sin A\\sin B$\n\n    $=-2\\sin A\\sin B$\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Comparing this result to the expression $\\sin{4x}\\sin{2x}$, we can see that $A=4x$ and $B=2x$, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -2\\sin 4x\\sin 2x &= \\cos(4x+2x)-\\cos(4x-2x)\\\\[6pt]\n         \\sin 4x\\sin 2x &= -\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos6x-\\cos2x\\right)  \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the integral becomes\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}e^{x}\\sin{4x}\\sin{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}-\\tfrac{1}{2}e^{x}\\left(\\cos6x-\\cos2x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now use the formula from (a)(ii), where $n=6$ and $n=2$ respectively to evaluate the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{1+6^2}(e^{2\\pi}-1)- \\dfrac{1}{1+2^2}(e^{2\\pi}-1)\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{16}{185}(e^{2\\pi}-1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\hspace{0.525em}b=\\tfrac{16}{185}$\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$"},{"ref":"AA1046","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1046\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows part of the graph of $y=\\dfrac{6x^2}{1+x^3}$ for $x\\geq 0$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20by%20Parts%20and%20by%20Substitution/images/Screenshot%202024-03-08%20at%2010.24.07%20AM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-03-08 at 10.24.07 AM\">\n\nThe area bounded by the curve, the $x$-axis and the line $x=a$, shaded in grey, equals $2\\ln(65)$.\n\nFind the value of $a$.","markScheme":"The area of the region shaded is given by the integral\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\int_0^a \\dfrac{6x^2}{1+x^3} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[9pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can integrate this quotient using a substitution of $u=1+x^3$.\n$$\n\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 3x^2$, hence $\\mathrm{d}x=\\tfrac{1}{3x^2}\\mathrm{d}u$\n\nWe can also transform the limits. When $x=0$, $u=1$ and when $x=a$, $u=1+a^3$.\n\nHence, the integral becomes\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    &=  \\int_1^{1+a^3} \\dfrac{6x^2}{u}\\times \\dfrac{1}{3x^2}\\mathrm{d}u\\hspace{3em}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[9pt]\n    &=  2\\int_1^{1+a^3} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\mathrm{d}u\\hspace{3em}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[9pt]\n    &= 2\\big[\\ln(1+a^3)-\\ln(1)\\,\\big] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2\\ln(1+a^3)\\\\[-16pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, solving for $a$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\cancel{2}\\ln(1+a^3) &= \\cancel{2}\\ln(65) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[8pt]\n    1+a^3 &= 65 \\\\[8pt]\n    a^3 &= 64 \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.53em}a = 4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1062","marks":"12","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1062\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe function $f(x)$ lies entirely to the right of the $y-$axis, it has no $y-$intercept and it has a derivative of $f'(x)=\\dfrac{x^3}{\\sqrt{x^4-27}}$.\n\n1. 1. Show that $f''(x)=\\dfrac{x^2(x^4-81)}{\\sqrt{(x^4-27)^3}}$.\n\n    \n\n    2. Hence justify that $f$ has a single point of inflection when $x=3$. [6 marks]\n\n    \n\n2. Find $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{x^3}{\\sqrt{x^4-27}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [3 marks]\n\n    \n\nIt is known that $\\displaystyle\\int_{3}^{6} f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=18.33$, to four significant figures.\n\n3. Find $f(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Use the quotient rule to differentiate $f'(x)$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            f''(x)&=\\frac{3x^2\\sqrt{x^4-27}-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times4x^3\\times(x^4-27)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}} \\times x^3}{x^4-27} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n            &=\\frac{3x^2\\sqrt{x^4-27}-2x^6(x^4-27)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}}{x^4-27} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n            &=\\frac{x^2(x^4-27)^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}(3(x^4-27)-2x^4)}{x^4-27} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n            &\\boxed{=\\dfrac{x^2(x^4-81)}{\\sqrt{(x^4-27)^3}}} \\hspace{3em}\\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. At a point of inflection $f''(x)=0$ and the concavity changes.\n\n        $$                        \n\n        \\begin{aligned}                \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}            \n\n        \\end{aligned}                \n\n        $$\n\n        Using the numerator from the previous result we can write\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x^2(x^4-81)&=0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        \\implies \\,\\, x^2&=0 &x^4-81=0\\\\[3pt]\n\n        x&=0, \\pm 3.\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As $f(x)$ lies entirely to the right of the $y-$axis and has no $y-$interpect the only solution to $f''(x)=0$ is $x=3$. \n\n        $$                        \n\n        \\begin{aligned}                \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}            \n\n        \\end{aligned}                \n\n        $$\n\n        By graphing $f''(x)$ on the G.D.C., we can see that $f''(x)<0$ for $x<3$ (red part) and $f''(x)>0$ for $x>3$ (blue part), confirming a change in concavity.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20by%20Parts%20and%20by%20Substitution/images/AA1062.png\" alt=\"AA1062\">\n\n        $$\\text{Hence $f(x)$ has a single point of inflection at $x=3$.}$$\n\n    \n\n2. We can use a substitution of $u=x^4-27$ to find the integral.\n\n    Hence $\\tfrac{du}{dx}=4x^3$, therefore $dx=\\tfrac{1}{4x^3}du$. We can now form the integral using this information\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\int \\frac{\\cancel{x^3}}{\\sqrt{u}}\\times\\frac{1}{4\\cancel{x^3}}du \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{1}{4}\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}du\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can now integrate the expression ($u^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$) using the inverse power rule.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\frac{1}{2}u^{\\tfrac{1}{2}}+c \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x^4-27}+c} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Form an equation from the information given using the result from part (c)\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\int_{3}^{6} \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x^4-27}+c\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=18.33 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Use the G.D.C. to solve the equation, hence $c=-4.00$.\n\n    $$                        \n\n    \\begin{aligned}                \n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}            \n\n    \\end{aligned}                \n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\boxed{f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x^4-27}-4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA483","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA483\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nUsing integration by parts find\n$\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\cos x\\, \\textrm dx$.","markScheme":"Integrating by parts twice, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int x^2 \\cos x\\,\\textrm  dx    & =  x^2\\sin x - 2 \\int x\\sin x\\,\\textrm dx \\quad \\quad \\quad\n[\\text{with $u = x^2$, $\\textrm dv = \\cos x\\,\\textrm dx$}]\\\\[6pt]\n& =  x^2\\sin x - 2 \\left[x(-\\cos x) + \\int \\cos x\\,\\textrm dx\\right]\n\\quad   [\\text{with $u = x$, $\\textrm dv = \\sin x\\,\\textrm dx$}]\\\\[8pt]\n& \\hspace{.275em}= x^2\\sin x + 2x\\cos x - 2\\sin x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA484","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA484\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind $\\displaystyle \\int \\arccos x\\, \\textrm dx$.","markScheme":"Integrating by parts with $u = \\arccos x$ and $\\textrm dv =\\textrm  dx$,\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\arccos x\\,\\textrm  dx     & =  x\\arccos x + \\int \\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,\\textrm dx \\\\[6pt]\n& =  x\\arccos x - \\int \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\textrm du  \\qquad                              [\\text{with $u = 1 - x^2$}]  \\\\[6pt]\n& =  x\\arccos x - u^{\\frac{1}{2}} + C \\\\[8pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em} =  x\\arccos x - \\sqrt{1-x^2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA488","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA488\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUsing the substitution $u = 1-\\sqrt{2x}$, find\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x}}{1-\\sqrt{2x}}\\,\\textrm dx$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu   & =  1-\\sqrt{2x} \\\\[6pt]\n\\textrm du  & =  -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}}\\,\\textrm dx \\\\[6pt]\n-\\sqrt{2x}\\,\\textrm du & =  \\textrm dx \\\\[6pt]\n(u-1)\\,\\textrm du   & =  \\textrm dx. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the\nsubstitution $u = 1-\\sqrt{2x}$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x}}{1-\\sqrt{2x}}\\,\\textrm dx & =  -\\int \\dfrac{(u-1)^2}{u}\\,\\textrm du \\\\[3pt]\n& =  - \\int \\dfrac{u^2 - 2u + 1}{u}\\,\\textrm du \\\\[6pt]\n& =  \\int \\left[-u + 2 - \\dfrac{1}{u} \\right]\\textrm du \\\\[6pt]\n& =  2u - \\dfrac{u^2}{2} - \\ln |u| + C \\\\[6pt]\n& =  2(1 - \\sqrt{2x}) - \\dfrac{(1-\\sqrt{2x})^2}{2} - \\ln|1-\\sqrt{2x}| + C \\\\[6pt]\n\\int \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x}}{1-\\sqrt{2x}}\\,\\textrm dx&=\\dfrac{3}{2} - \\sqrt{2x} - x - \\ln|1 - \\sqrt{2x}| + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n__Note, we could omit the $\\bm{\\frac{3}{2}}$ term from our answer as this value could be absorbed by the constant $\\bm{C}$.__\n\nSo a correct final answer is also\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x}}{1-\\sqrt{2x}}\\,\\textrm dx & =- \\sqrt{2x} - x - \\ln|1 - \\sqrt{2x}| + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA498","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA498\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUsing integration by parts, find\n$\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sin x\\, \\textrm dx$.","markScheme":"Integrating by parts twice, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int x^2 \\sin x\\, \\textrm dx    & =  x^2(-\\cos x) + 2 \\int x\\cos x\\,\\textrm dx \\qquad  \\quad\n[\\text{$u = x^2$, $\\textrm dv = \\sin x\\,\\textrm dx$}]\\\\[3pt]\n& =  -x^2\\cos x + 2 \\left[x\\sin x - \\int \\sin x\\,\\textrm dx\\right] \\qquad \\quad\n[\\text{$u = x$, $\\textrm dv = \\cos x\\,\\textrm dx$}]\\\\[8pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em} =  -x^2\\cos x + 2x\\sin x + 2\\cos x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA507","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA507\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nShow that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_1^3 x^2\\ln x\\,\\mathrm{d} x = 9\\ln 3 - \\dfrac{26}{9}$.","markScheme":"Integrating by parts, with $u = \\ln x$ and\n$\\mathrm{d}v = x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^3 x^2\\ln x\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n& = \\dfrac{x^3\\ln x}{3}\\bigg|_1^3 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\int_1^3 x^2 \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\hspace{4cm}\\\\[10pt]\n& =  \\left[\\dfrac{27\\ln 3}{3} - 0\\right] - \\dfrac{x^3}{9}\\bigg|_1^3 \\\\[10pt]\n& =  9\\ln 3 - \\left[\\dfrac{27}{9} - \\dfrac{1}{9}\\right] \\\\[12pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em}=  9\\ln 3 - \\dfrac{26}{9} \\quad \\quad \\text{as required.}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA515","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA515\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of\n$y = x\\hspace{0.05em}(\\ln x)^2$,\n $x > 0$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20by%20Parts%20and%20by%20Substitution/images/878824d0096f82bb6a1a431149415223a532a0b9.svg\" alt=\"878824d0096f82bb6a1a431149415223a532a0b9.svg\">\n\n1.  Given that the curve passes through the point $(p,0)$, state the\n    value of $p$. [1 mark]\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed by the curve ($p \\leq x \\leq e$), the\n$x$-axis and the line $x = e$.\n\n2.  Integrate by parts twice to find the area of the region\n    $R$. [5 marks]\n\nLet $I_n = \\displaystyle \\int_1^e x^2(\\ln x)^n\\,\\mathrm{d}x$,\n $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Find the value of $I_0$.\n\n    2.  Show that $I_n = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big(e^3 - nI_{n-1}\\big)$.\n    3.  Hence find the values of $I_1, I_2$ and $I_3$. [7 marks]\n\nThe region $R$ is rotated through $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n4.  Find the volume of the solid formed. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  $\\hspace{.275em}p = 1$\n\n2.  Integrating by parts twice, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Area}     & =  \\int_1^e x\\hspace{0.05em}(\\ln x)^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\quad \\quad \\quad \\big[\\text{let $u = (\\ln x)^2,$ $\\mathrm{d}v = x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\big[uv\\big]\\Big|_1^e - \\int_1^e v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\quad \\quad \\quad \\big[\\text{$\\Rightarrow v = \\dfrac{x^2}{2},$ $\\mathrm{d}u = \\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[x^2(\\ln x)^2\\big]\\Big|_1^e - \\int_1^e x \\ln x \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\quad \\quad \\quad \\big[\\text{let $u = \\ln x,$ $\\mathrm{d}v = x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{e^2}{2} - \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[x^2 \\ln x\\big]\\Big|_1^e - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_1^e x\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\bigg) \\quad \\quad \\quad \\big[\\text{$\\Rightarrow v = \\dfrac{x^2}{2},$ $\\mathrm{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\cancel{\\dfrac{e^2}{2}} - \\bigg(\\cancel{\\dfrac{e^2}{2}} - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\big[x^2\\big]\\Big|_1^e\\bigg) \\\\[8pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}=  \\dfrac{1}{4}\\big[e^2 - 1\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        I_0         & =  \\int_1^e x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big[x^3\\big]\\Big|_1^e \\\\[8pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big[e^3 - 1\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Integrating by parts, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        I_n         & =  \\int_1^e x^2(\\ln x)^n\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\quad \\quad \\big[\\text{let $u = (\\ln x)^n,$ $\\mathrm{d}v = x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}\\big] \\\\[3pt]\n        & = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big[x^3(\\ln x)^n\\big]\\Big|_1^e - \\dfrac{n}{3}\\int_1^e x^2(\\ln x)^{n-1} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\quad \\quad \\big[\\text{$ v = \\dfrac{x^3}{3},$ $\\mathrm{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.05em}n\\hspace{0.05em}(\\ln x)^{n-1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$}\\big] \\\\[5pt]\n        & = \\dfrac{e^3}{3} - \\dfrac{n}{3}\\hspace{0.05em}I_{n-1} \\\\[8pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big[e^3 - n\\hspace{0.05em}I_{n-1}\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        as required.\n\n    3.  Using parts (c)\n        (i) - (ii), we\n        get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        I_1     & =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big[e^3 - I_0\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{e^3}{3} - \\dfrac{1}{9}\\big[e^3 - 1\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{1}{9}\\big[2\\hspace{0.05em}e^3 + 1\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        I_2     & =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big[e^3 - 2I_1\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{e^3}{3} - \\dfrac{2}{27}\\big[2e^3 + 1\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n        &  =  \\dfrac{1}{27}\\big[5\\hspace{0.05em}e^3 - 2\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        I_3     & =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\big[e^3 - 3I_2\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{e^3}{3} - \\dfrac{1}{27}\\big[5e^3 - 2\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n        &  =  \\dfrac{1}{27}\\big[4\\hspace{0.05em}e^3 + 2\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  The volume of the solid formed is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Volume}   & = \\pi  \\int_1^e \\big[x\\hspace{0.05em}(\\ln x)^2\\big]^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\pi  \\int_1^e x^2(\\ln x)^4\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[10pt]\n    & = \\pi I_4 \\\\[10pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\big[e^3 - 4I_3\\big] \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by part (c) (ii)}] \\\\[8pt]\n    & = \\pi\\bigg(\\dfrac{e^3}{3} - \\dfrac{4}{81}\\big[4\\hspace{0.05em}e^3 + 2\\big]\\bigg) \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by part (c) (iii)}] \\\\[10pt]\n    &  = \\dfrac{\\pi}{81}\\big[11\\hspace{0.05em}e^3 - 8\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA519","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA519\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the functions $f, g$, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, given by\n$f(x) = e^{2x}\\sin x$ and $g(x) = e^{2x}\\cos x$.\n\n1.  Find\n    1.  $f'(x)$;\n\n    2.  $g'(x)$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, find\n    $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\pi e^{2x}\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the product rule, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[e^{2x}\\sin x\\big] \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 2e^{2x}\\sin x + e^{2x}\\cos x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  and\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         g'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[e^{2x}\\cos x\\big] \\\\[8pt]\n         &= 2e^{2x}\\cos x - e^{2x}\\sin x\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n2.  From part (a), we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) + 2g'(x) &= 5e^{2x}\\cos x \\\\[6pt]\n    e^{2x}\\cos x &= \\dfrac{1}{5}\\big[f'(x) + 2g'(x)\\big] \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, integrating both sides of the equation ($\\ast$) with respect\n    to $x$ from $0$ to $\\pi$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^\\pi e^{2x}\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int_0^\\pi \\dfrac{1}{5}\\big[f'(x) + 2g'(x)\\big]\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{5}\\big[f(\\pi) + 2g(\\pi)\\big] - \\dfrac{1}{5}\\big[f(0) + 2g(0)\\big] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by FTC}]\\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{5}\\big[0 + 2(-e^{2\\pi})\\big] - \\dfrac{1}{5}\\big[0 + 2(1)\\big] \\\\[9pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{2}{5}\\big[e^{2\\pi}+1\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA542","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA542\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int 6x^2e^{x^3+8}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $f(x)$, given that $f'(x) = 6x^2e^{x^3+8}$ and $f(-2) = 5$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using integration by substitution with $u = x^3 + 8$ and\n    $\\dfrac {\\textrm d u} {3x^2} = \\textrm d x$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int 6x^2e^{x^3+8}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    & = \\int 6 \\cancel{x^2} e^u \\dfrac {\\textrm d u} {3\\cancel{x^2}}\\hspace{1cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\int 2 e^u \\textrm d u\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2 e^u + C\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 2 e^{x^3+8} + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We know that $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int f'(x) \\textrm \\,d x$ and\n    from part (a) $f(x)  = 2 e^{x^3+8} + C$. Let's determine $C$. We\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.3cm}\n    5   & = 2 e^{(-2)^3+8} + C\\\\[6pt]\n    5   & = 2 + C\\\\[8pt]\n    C   & = 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We found\n      $f(x) = 2 e^{x^3 + 8} +3$"},{"ref":"AA543","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA543\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int 4xe^{x^2-9}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $f(x)$, given that $f'(x) = 4xe^{x^2-9}$ and $f(3) = 7$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using integration by substitution with $u = x^2 - 9$ and\n    $\\dfrac {\\textrm d u} {2x} = \\textrm d x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int 4x e^{x^2 - 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    & = \\int 4 \\cancel{x} e^u \\dfrac {\\textrm d u} {2 \\cancel{x}}\\hspace{1cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\int 2 e^u \\textrm d u\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2 e^u + C\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 2 e^{x^2 - 9} + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We know that $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int f'(x) \\textrm \\,d x$ and\n    from part (a) $f(x)  = 2 e^{x^2 - 9} + C$. Let's determine $C$. We\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.3cm}\n    7   & = 2 e^{(3)^2 - 9} + C\\\\[6pt]\n    7   & = 2 + C\\\\[6pt]\n    C   & = 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We found\n      $f(x) = 2 e^{x^2 - 9} +5$"},{"ref":"AA564","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA564\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac {\\ln x} x$\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int f(x)\\,\\textrm d x$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\displaystyle\\int_1^e f(x) \\,\\textrm d x$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $u = \\ln x$ then $x\\hspace{0.1em}\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x$ and\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int f(x)\\,\\textrm d x  & = \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} x \\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\int u\\,\\textrm d u\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {u^2}2 + C\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}  = \\dfrac {(\\ln x)^2} 2 + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.4cm}\n    \\int_1^e \\dfrac {\\ln x} x\\,\\textrm d x  & = \\left[\\dfrac {(\\ln x)^2} 2\\right]_1^e\\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\left[\\dfrac {(\\ln e)^2} 2\\right]\n    -  \\left[\\dfrac {(\\ln 1)^2} 2\\right]\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac  1 2  - 0\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} = \\dfrac 1 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA569","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA569\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f'(x) = \\dfrac x {\\sqrt{x^2 + 1}}$. Given that $f(0) = 7$, find\n$f(x)$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.5cm}\nf(x)        & = \\int f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int \\dfrac x {\\sqrt {x^2 + 1}}\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int \\dfrac 1 {2\\sqrt u} \\,\\textrm d u\\qquad\\quad\n\\mbox{[where $u = x^2 + 1$, $\\textrm d u = 2 x \\,\\textrm d x$]} \\\\[6pt]\n& = \\dfrac 1 2 \\dfrac {u^{1/2}} {\\frac 1 2} + C\\\\[6pt]\n& = u^{1/2} + C\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\sqrt{x^2 + 1} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $f(0) = 7$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7   & = \\sqrt {0^2 + 1} + C\\hspace{1.5cm}\\\\[5pt]\n7   & = 1 + C\\\\[5pt]\nC   & = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n  $f(x) = \\sqrt {x^2 + 1} + 6$"},{"ref":"AA570","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA570\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f'(x)  = \\dfrac {12x} {(x^2 - 1)^4}$. Given that $f(0) = 3$, find\n$f(x)$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.5cm}\nf(x)        & = \\int f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int \\dfrac {12x} {(x^2 - 1)^4}\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int \\dfrac 6 {u^4} \\,\\textrm d u\\qquad\\quad\n\\mbox{[where $u = x^2 - 1$, $\\textrm d u = 2 x \\,\\textrm d x$]} \\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int 6 u^{-4}\\,\\textrm d u\\\\[6pt]\n& =  \\dfrac {6 u^{-3}} {-3} + C\\\\[6pt]\n& = -2u^{-3} + C\\\\[6pt]\n& = -\\dfrac 2 {(x^2 - 1)^3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $f(0) = 3$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3   & = -\\dfrac 2 {(0^2 - 1)^3} + C\\hspace{.7cm}\\\\[5pt]\n3   & = -\\dfrac 2 {(-1)^3} + C\\\\[5pt]\n3   & = 2 + C\\\\[6pt]\nC   & = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n  $f(x) = -\\dfrac 2 {(x^2 - 1)^3} + 1$"},{"ref":"AA573","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA573\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac {4x}{x^2 + 1}$,\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 6$, and the line $x = 6$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20by%20Parts%20and%20by%20Substitution/images/2c4cfb84f6a2023641db5a3e6fb90100b872411a.svg\" alt=\"2c4cfb84f6a2023641db5a3e6fb90100b872411a.svg\">\n\nLet $R$ be the region enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis and the\nline $x = 6$.\n\nFind the area of $R$.","markScheme":"The area of $R$ is given by the integral\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_R         & = \\int_0^6 f(x)\\,\\textrm dx\\\\[8pt]\n& = \\int_0^6 \\dfrac {4x} {x^2 + 1}\\,\\textrm d x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\\\nIntegrating by substitution with $u = x^2 + 1$ and\n$\\textrm du = 2 x \\,\\textrm d x$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5mm}\nA_R         & = \\int \\dfrac 2 u \\,\\textrm du\\\\[8pt]\n& =  2 \\ln (u)\\\\[6pt]\n& =  2 \\ln (x^2 + 1)  \\Big|_0^{6}\\\\[8pt]\n& = 2 \\ln(37) - 2 \\ln(1)\\\\[10pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em} = 2 \\ln 37\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA620","marks":"13","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA620\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1.  Use L'Hôpital's rule to find\n    $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} x^3 e^{-x}$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Show that the proper integral\n    $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty x^3e^{-x}\\, \\mathrm{d}x$ converges, and\n    state its value.[9 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using L'Hôpital's rule repeatedly, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} x^3 e^{-x}&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac{x^3}{e^x}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n    &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^2}{e^x} \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac{6x}{e^x}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{6}{e^x}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &=0\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{6mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Integrating from $0$ to $R$, by parts, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^R x^3 e^{-x} \\, \\mathrm{d}x &= -e^{-x}x^3\\bigg\\lvert_0^R +3\\int_0^R e^{-x}x^2 \\, \\mathrm{d}x\\rule[-6mm]{0pt}{12mm}\\\\\n    &=-e^{-R}R^3+3\\left[-e^{-x}x^2 \\bigg\\lvert_0^R + 2\\int_0^R xe^{-x}\\, \\mathrm{d}x\\right]\\rule[-6mm]{0pt}{12mm}\\\\\n    &= -e^{-R}R^3-3e^{-R}R^2+6\\left[-xe^{-x}\\bigg\\lvert_0^R+\\int_0^R e^{-x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right]\\rule[-6mm]{0pt}{12mm}\\\\\n    &= -e^{-R}R^3-3e^{-R}R^2-6e^{-R}R-6e^{-R}+6\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, by the definition of a proper integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^\\infty x^3e^{-x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\lim_{R\\to\\infty} \\int_0^R x^3e^{-x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n    &= \\lim_{R\\to \\infty} \\left[ -e^{-R}R^3-3e^{-R}R^2-6e^{-R}R-6e^{-R}+6 \\right]\\quad\\quad(1)\\\\\n    &=6\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{6mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Note each term multiplied by $e^{-R}$ in (1) vanishes by the\n    intermediate steps in the solution of (a)."},{"ref":"AA650","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA650\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nLet $y = (x^2+x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}$, for $x \\geq 0$.\n\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find\n    $\\displaystyle \\int \\textstyle (2x+1)\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n    [3 marks]\n\nConsider the functions $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sqrt{x^2+x}$ and\n$g(x) = 5 - \\dfrac{x}{2}\\sqrt{x^2+x}$, for $x \\geq 0$.\n\nThe graphs of $f$ and $g$ are shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20by%20Parts%20and%20by%20Substitution/images/b13d6c069881bca9dffc49b90d0991e7e07b6530.svg\" alt=\"b13d6c069881bca9dffc49b90d0991e7e07b6530.svg\">\n\nThe shaded region $S$ is enclosed by the graph of\n$f$ , the graph of $g$, the\n$y$-axis and the line $x = 2$.\n\n3.  Write down an expression for the area of $S$. [1 mark]\n\n4.  Hence find the exact area of $S$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the chain rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\Big[(x^2+x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\Big] \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{2}(x^2+x)^{\\frac{1}{2}}(2x+1) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{2}(2x+1)\\textstyle \\sqrt{x^2+x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\int \\textstyle (2x+1)\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{2}\\textstyle (2x+1)\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\bigg]\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3} \\int \\bigg[\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\Big[(x^2+x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\Big]\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg]\\mathrm{d}x \\hspace{2.2em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3}(x^2+x)^{\\frac{3}{2}} + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    $\\int_0^2 \\big[g(x) - f(x)\\big]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x$\n\n4.  Hence, evaluating the definite integral, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.9em} \\text{Area of $S$} &= \\int_0^2 \\bigg[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big[5 - \\textstyle\\dfrac{x}{2}\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\Big]\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big[\\textstyle\\dfrac{1}{4}\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\Big]\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg] \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\int_0^2 \\bigg[5 - \\textstyle\\dfrac{x}{2}\\sqrt{x^2+x} - \\textstyle\\dfrac{1}{4}\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\bigg] \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\int_0^2 \\bigg[5 - \\dfrac{1}{4}(2x+1)\\textstyle\\sqrt{x^2+x}\\bigg]\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 5\\int_0^2 1 \\,\\mathrm{d}x  - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\int_0^2(2x+1)\\textstyle\\sqrt{x^2+x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 5\\Big[x\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}(x^2+x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\bigg]\\bigg|_0^2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b)}] \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 5\\Big[2-0\\Big]\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}(2^2+2)^{\\frac{3}{2}}-0\\bigg] \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 10 - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}(6\\textstyle\\sqrt{6})\\bigg] \\\\[14pt]\n    &= 10 - \\textstyle\\sqrt{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA673","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA673\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{20x}{\\sqrt{(5x^2+4)^3}}$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_0^3 f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the integration by substitution method with $u=5x^2+4$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{20x}{\\sqrt{(5x^2+4)^3}} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\hspace{0.5em} & = \\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{u^3}} \\,\\textrm{d}u \\hspace{3em} [\\text{using substitution}] \\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2\\int u^{-\\frac{3}{2}} \\,\\textrm{d}u \\\\[4pt]\n    &= 2\\left[\\dfrac{u^{-\\frac{1}{2}}}{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\right] + C \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-4}{\\sqrt{u}} + C \\\\[10pt]\n    & = \\textstyle \\dfrac{-4}{\\sqrt{5x^2+4}} + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating the definite integral, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^3 \\dfrac{20x}{\\sqrt{(5x^2+4)^3}} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\hspace{0.5em} & = \\left[\\textstyle \\dfrac{-4}{\\sqrt{5x^2+4}}\\right]_0^3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left[\\textstyle \\dfrac{-4}{\\sqrt{5(3)^2+4}}\\right] \\hspace{0.2em} - \\hspace{0.2em} \\left[\\textstyle \\dfrac{-4}{\\sqrt{5(0)^2+4}}\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-4}{7} \\hspace{0.2em} - \\hspace{0.2em} \\dfrac{-4}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-4}{7} \\hspace{0.2em} + \\hspace{0.2em} \\dfrac{14}{7} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10}{7}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA919","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA919\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nUsing the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}-1$, find the value of $\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{\\sqrt{x}-1}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","markScheme":"Using integration by substitution, let $u = \\sqrt{x}-1$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\nu &= x^{1/2} - 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n2\\hspace{0.15em}\\mathrm{d}u &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe also note that when $x=1$, $u=0$ and when $x=4$, $u=1$.\n\nSubstituting $u = \\sqrt{x}-1$ and $2\\,\\mathrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}}$ , and changing the limits to $u=0$ and $u=1$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\displaystyle\\int_{x=1}^{x=4} \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{\\sqrt{x}-1}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}x & = \\displaystyle\\int_{u=0}^{u=1} 4\\sqrt{u} \\,\\textrm{d}u \\\\[10pt] %\\hspace{3em} [\\hspace{0.05em}\\text{using substitution}\\hspace{0.05em}] \\\\[6pt]\n\n& = 4 \\displaystyle\\int_{u=0}^{u=1} u^{1/2} \\,\\textrm{d}u \\\\[10pt]\n\n&= 4\\Big[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,u^{3/2}\\Big]_{u=0}^{u=1} \\\\[10pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{8}{3}\\Big(1^{3/2} - 0^{3/2}\\Big) \\\\[10pt]\n\n&\\hspace{-8em}\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{\\sqrt{x}-1}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\dfrac{8}{3}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA976","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA976\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the function $f$ such that $f'(x)=\\dfrac{8x}{x^2+5}$ and the graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(2,9\\ln 3)$. \n\nFind an expression for $f(x).$","markScheme":"We have that $f(x) = \\int f'(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x)&=\\int \\dfrac{8x}{x^2+5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=4 \\int \\dfrac{2x}{x^2+5} \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 4\\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\hspace{3em} [\\text{using }u=x^2+5, \\,\\mathrm{d}u=2x\\,\\mathrm{d}x]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 4\\ln\\,u + C\\\\[8pt]\n\n    f(x) &= 4 \\ln(x^2+5)+C\\hspace{3em}[\\text{substituting back}]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSince the graph of the function passes through the point $(2,9\\ln 3)$, we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    9\\ln 3 &= 4\\ln(2^2+5)+C\\\\[9pt]\n\n    9\\ln 3 &= 4\\ln 9 + C \\\\[9pt]\n\n    C &= 9\\ln 3 -4\\ln(3^2)\\\\[9pt]\n\n    C &= 9\\ln 3 -8\\ln 3\\\\[9pt]\n\n    C &= \\ln 3\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n Hence $\\boxed{f(x)=4\\ln(x^2+5)+\\ln 3}$  or  $\\boxed{f(x)=\\ln\\left(3(x^2+5)^4\\right)}$"}]},{"name":"Integration Fundamentals and Areas","questions":[{"ref":"AA1036","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1036\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nThe quadratic function $f(x)$ has a vertex at $(h, -2)$.\n\n1.  The function can be written in the form of $f(x) = a(x-h)^2+k$.\n\n    1.  If the equation of the axis of symmetry of $f(x)$ is $x=3$, write down the values of $h$ and $k$.\n\n    2. If the $y-$intercept of $f(x)$ is $-11$, find $a$. [4 marks]\n\nA new function $g(x)$ is defined such that $g'(x)=f(x)$.\n\n2.  1.  Show that the function $f(x)$ has no real roots.\n\n    2.  Hence, or otherwise, justify that $g(x)$ is a decreasing function. [3 marks]\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is vertically translated such that it now has $2$ distinct roots, $x_1$ and $x_2$, where $x_2>x_1$. The coordinates of the new vertex is $(3, m)$. Where $m>0$.\n\n3.  Show that $x_1=3- \\sqrt{m}$ and $x_2=3+ \\sqrt{m}$. [2 marks]\n\nThe area enclosed by $f(x)$ and the $x-$axis is $\\frac{32}{3}$ units.\n\n4.  1.  Use the substitution $u=x-3$ to find $\\displaystyle\\int f(x)\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ in terms of $m$.\n\n    2.  Hence find $m$. [6 marks]\n\n5.  Hence, or otherwise, find the region(s) of $x$, for which $g(x)$ is a decreasing function. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Written in vertex form the point $(h, k)$ is the location of the vertex, additionally, the vertex lies on the axis of symmetry.\n\n        Hence $\\hspace{0.525em}h=3\\hspace{0.525em}$ and $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{k=-2}\\hspace{0.525em}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  From the question we are told $f(0)=-11$, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -11&=a(0-3)^2-2  \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n            -9&=9a\\\\[6pt]\n            &\\hspace{-0.525em}a=-1\\hspace{0.525em}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  1.  We can use the discriminant to show that $f(x)$ has no real roots.\n\n        But first, we need to expand and simplify $f(x)$ into the general form.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f(x)&=-(x-3)^2-2\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-(x^2-6x+9)-2\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-x^2+6x-11\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now using the coefficients $a, b$ and $c$ we can find the value of the discriminant\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\triangle &= b^2-4ac  \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &= (6)^2-4(-1)(-11)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=36-44\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-8\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $\\hspace{0.525em}f(x) \\text{ has no real roots as } \\triangle < 0$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    2.  As $f(x)$ has no real roots and $a<0$ we can say that $f(x)$ is entirely below the $x-$axis.\n\n        Here is a sketch to help visualise the function\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/a1036-1.png\" alt=\"a1036-1\">\n\n        As $g'(x)=f(x)$, $\\text{the gradient of } g(x) \\text{ is always negative}$ making $g(x)$ a decreasing function.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n3.  After the translation we can write $f(x)=-(x-3)^2+m$.\n\n    Here is a new sketch of $f(x)$ with the new features identified.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/a1036-2.png\" alt=\"a1036-2\">\n\n    The roots are found by solving $f(x)=0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=-(x-3)^2+m \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        (x-3)^2&=m\\\\[6pt]\n        x-3&=\\pm \\sqrt{m}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}x_1=3 - \\sqrt{m} \\text{ and } x_2=3 + \\sqrt{m}\\hspace{0.525em} \\hspace{3em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n4.  1.  Using the substitution $u=x-3$ we find that $\\frac{du}{\\mathrm{d}x}=1$, hence $\\mathrm{d}u=\\mathrm{d}x$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n           \\int f(x)\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}x&= \\int \\left(-(x-3)^2+m\\right)\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n           &=\\int \\left(-u^2+m\\right)\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\[6pt]\n           &=-\\frac{1}{3}u^3+mu+c\\\\[6pt]\n           &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)^3+m(x-3)+c} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  We are told that the area enclosed by $f(x)$ and the $x-$axis is $\\frac{32}{3}$ we can visualise this, with the shaded area below being equal to $\\dfrac{32}{3}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/a1036-3.png\" alt=\"a1036-3\">\n\n        hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{x_1}^{x_2}f(x)\\,\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{32}{3} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using the integral we found in part (i) and the roots we find in part (c) we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\left[-\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)^3+m(x-3)\\right]_{3-\\sqrt{m}}^{3+\\sqrt{m}}&=\\dfrac{32}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Note, we do not need to include the constant of integration $c$, as this is a definite integral.\\\\[6pt]\n        Next we can substitute in the limits of integration, simplify and then solve for $m$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -\\frac{1}{3}(3+\\sqrt{m}-3)^3+m(3+\\sqrt{m}-3) -&\\\\[6pt]\n            \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}(3-\\sqrt{m}-3)^3+m(3-\\sqrt{m}-3)\\right) &=\\frac{32}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n            -\\frac{1}{3}(\\sqrt{m})^3+m\\sqrt{m}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}(-\\sqrt{m})^3-m\\sqrt{m}\\right)&=\\frac{32}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n            -\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{m})^3+2m\\sqrt{m}&=\\frac{32}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Rewrite the radicals into index form\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -\\frac{2}{3}m^{\\frac{3}{2}}+2m^{\\frac{3}{2}}&=\\frac{32}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n            -2m^{\\frac{3}{2}}+6m^{\\frac{3}{2}}&=32\\\\[6pt]\n            4m^{\\frac{3}{2}}&=32\\\\[6pt]\n            m^{\\frac{3}{2}}&=8\\\\[6pt]\n            m&=8^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{m=4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n5.  Using the answer from part (c) and (d)(ii) we know that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x_1&=3-\\sqrt{4}\\\\\n        &=1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x_2&=3+\\sqrt{4}\\\\\n        &=5 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $g(x)$ is a decreasing function when the value of its gradient is negative.\n\n    Here is a sketch of $f(x)$ with the roots identified\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/a1036-4.png\" alt=\"a1036-4\">\n\n    As $g'(x)=f(x)$  and $f(x)$ is negative to the left of $x_1$ and to the right of $x_2$, then $g(x)$ is a decreasing function for\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n         \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{x<1}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{x>5}\\hspace{0.525em} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1064","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1064\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the two curves $y=2x^2$ and $y=x^2$.\n\nRegion $A$ is the area enclosed by the two curves, from the origin to the line $x=a$, where $a\\in \\mathbb{R^+}$. This is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/aa1064a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1064a\">\n\n1. Show that the area of $A$ is $\\frac{1}{3}a^3$.[1 mark]\n\nConsider a third curve $y=kx^2$,  where $k>2, k\\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nRegion $B$ is the  area enclosed by the new curve and $y=2x^2$, from the origin to the line $y=b$, where  $b\\in \\mathbb{R}$. This is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/aa1064b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1064b\">\n\n2. Find $k$, where $k \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, such that the area of region B equals the area of region A for all values of $a$.[7 marks]","markScheme":"1. We can find area $A$ by evaluating the following definite integral\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\int_{0}^{a} \\left(2x^2-x^2\\right)dx&= \\int_{0}^{a} x^2dx\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &=\\left[\\frac{1}{3}x^3\\right]_{0}^{a} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\tfrac{1}{3}a^3} \\hspace{3em}\\text{...as required}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. We need to find a function for the area of region $B$ in terms of $a$.\n\n    To do this we need to integrate up the $y-$axis. We can find $x(y)$ for the two curves forming region $B$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        y&=2x^2 \\hspace{3em} \\text{becomes } x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\\\\\n\n        y&=kx^2 \\hspace{3em} \\text{becomes } x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{k}}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we can represent the area of region $B$ with the following integral\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\int_{0}^{b} \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{y}{k}}\\right)dy \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    However, before we integrate we need to change the limits so that they are in terms of $a$.\n\n    The point $(a, b)$ is on the curve $y=2x^2$, hence $b=2a^2$. We can now evaluate the definite integral\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\int_{0}^{2a^2} \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{y}{k}}\\right)dy \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &=\\int_{0}^{2a^2} \\left(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{y} - \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\sqrt{y}\\right)dy\\\\[3pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By factorising the integral it is easier to integrate\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\left(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}- \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\right)\\int_{0}^{2a^2} \\sqrt{y}\\,\\,dy\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &=\\left(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}- \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\right)\\left[\\tfrac{2}{3}y^{\\tfrac{3}{2}}\\right]_{0}^{2a^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can substitute the limits into the integral to find the area of region $B$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\left(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}- \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\right)\\left(\\tfrac{4}{3}\\sqrt{2}a^3\\right)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Form an equation such that the area of region $A$ equals the area of region $B$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{1}{3}a^3&=\\left(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}- \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\right)\\left(\\tfrac{4}{3}\\sqrt{2}a^3\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Solve for $k$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{1}{3}a^3&=\\frac{4}{3}a^3- \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}a^3}{3\\sqrt{k}} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Rearrange and factorise\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        0&=a^3-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}a^3}{3\\sqrt{k}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        0&=a^3\\left(1-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{k}}\\right)\\\\[3pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We are told in the question that $a\\neq0$ therefore the only solution is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        1-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{k}}&=0\\\\[3pt]\n\n        1&=\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{k}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        3\\sqrt{k}&=4\\sqrt{2}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        9k&=16\\times 2\\\\[3pt]\n\n        k&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{32}{9}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1160","marks":"17","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1160\n\n**Maximum mark: 19**\n\nConsider the function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&=\\tfrac{1}{4}x^2-x+3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Write $f(x)$ in the form $a(x-h)^2+k$. Where $a, h, k \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, find $\\displaystyle \\int f(x)\\,dx$. [2 marks]\n\nA rectangle $ABCD$ has vertices shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/grid.JPEG\" alt=\"grid\">\n\n$f(x)$ cuts the rectangle into two parts.\n\nThe part above the curve is labelled $U$ and the part below the curve is labelled $L$.\n\n3.  1.  Verify the point (4,3) lies on $f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, or otherwise, sketch $f(x)$ on the diagram above and identify the parts $U$ and $L$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  1.  Write down the area of rectangle $ABCD$.\n\n    2.  Hence, show that the area of $U$ is twice the area of $L$. [5 marks]\n\nThe rectangle $ABCD$ is modified such that point $C(b, f(b))$ is a variable point on the function $f(x)$. Where $b>2$ and $b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe point $A(2, 2)$ is fixed in the same position as previously.\n\n5.  Find the area of the new rectangle in terms of $b$.[2 marks]\n\n6.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,dx$ in terms of $b$.[1 mark]\n\n7.  Hence, or otherwise, prove that the area of $U$ is twice the area of $L$ for any position of point $C$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  By completing the square, we can rewrite $f(x)$ into the desired form\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x)&=\\tfrac{1}{4}(x^2-4x)+3\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{4}[(x-2)^2-2^2]+3\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{4}(x-2)^2-1+3\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.2em}=\\tfrac{1}{4}(x-2)^2+2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We can use the inverse power rule to integrate the function\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int f(x)\\,dx&=\\int {\\left[\\tfrac{1}{4}(x-2)^2+2\\right]}\\,dx\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{12}(x-2)^3+2x+C \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3.  1.  Evaluate the function at $x=4$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f(4)&=\\tfrac{1}{4}(4-2)^2+2\\\\[6pt]\n            &=1+2\\\\[6pt]\n            &\\hspace{-2em}f(4)=3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore $\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{the point} (4,3) \\text{ lies on } f(x)$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  Using our answers from part (a) and (c)(i) Recognise that the vertex lies on $A(2,2)$ and the curve passes through $C(4,3)$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1160_a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1160_a1\">\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  1.  $2 \\text{ units}^2$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  We can recognise that the area of region $U$ can be found by subtracting the area under the curve from $A$ to $C$ from the red dashed rectangle, this is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1160_a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1160_a2\">\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_U&=6-\\int_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,dx\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the answer from part (b) and remembering that for definite integrals the constant $C$ cancels out\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=6-\\left[\\tfrac{1}{12}(x-2)^3+2x\\right]_{2}^{4}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=6-\\left[\\left(\\tfrac{1}{12}(4-2)^3+2\\times 4\\right)-\\left(\\tfrac{1}{12}(2-2)^3+2\\times 2\\right)\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n        &=6-\\left[\\left(\\tfrac{8}{12}+8\\right)-4\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n        &=6-\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}+4\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{4}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can find the area of $L$ in a similar way\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_L&=\\left[\\tfrac{1}{12}(x-2)^3+2x\\right]_{2}^{4}-4 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}+4\\right)-4\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{2}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\hspace{0.525em}A_U=2\\times A_L\\hspace{0.525em}$ as required.\n\n5.  Here is a sketch of the new rectangle with the variable point $C$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1160_q%20%281%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1160_q (1)\">\n\n    Let the area of the new rectangle be $A_{R}$.\n\n    The width of the rectangle is $b-2$ and the height is $f(b)-2$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{R}&=(f(b)-2)(b-2)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=(\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^2+2-2)(b-2)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^2(b-2)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^3\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n6.  Using the answer to part (b) we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\left[\\tfrac{1}{12}(x-2)^3+2x\\right]_{2}^{b}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\left(\\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3+2b\\right)-\\left(\\tfrac{1}{12}(2-2)^3+2\\times 2\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3+2b-4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n7.  Consider the red rectangle shown in the following image\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1160_a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1160_a3\">\n\n    Using our answer from part (a) we can evaluate $f(b$) and see that the area of the red rectangle in terms of $b$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (b-2)f(b)&=(b-2)\\left(\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^2+2\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^3+2(b-2)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^3+2b-4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the answer from part (f) we can now write an expression for the area of region $U$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_U&=\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^3+2b-4 - \\int_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^3+2b-4 - \\left(\\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3+2b-4\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{4}(b-2)^3\\cancel{+2b-4} - \\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3\\cancel{-2b+4}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{6}(b-2)^3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Consider the blue rectangle shown in the image below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1160_a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1160_a4\">\n\n    We can see that the area is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2(b-2)&=2b-4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can find the area of region $L$ by subtracting the area of the blue triangle from the area under the curve.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_L&=\\int_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x - (2b-4)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\left(\\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3+2b-4\\right)-(2b-4)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3\\cancel{+2b-4}\\cancel{-2b+4}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We now have expressions for $A_U$ and $A_L$ in terms of $b$, which is the $x-$coordinate of point $C$. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tfrac{1}{6}(b-2)^3&=2 \\times \\tfrac{1}{12}(b-2)^3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        A_U&=2 \\times A_L\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    $\\text{The area of region }U\\text{ is twice the area of region }L\\text{ for any position of point C.}$"},{"ref":"AA1176","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1176\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nThe following implicit relation describes the ellipse shown below\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    x^2-2xy+4y^2&=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_a\">\n\nThe ellipse is centered on the origin and has rotational symmetry of order two.\n\n1.  Show that $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{x-y}{x-4y}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  1.  Hence show that the equation of the horizontal lines which are tangent to the ellipse are $y=\\pm\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{6}$.\n\n    2.  Use a similar method to find the equation of the  vertical lines which are tangent to the ellipse. [4 marks]\n\nConsider the top part of the ellipse shown below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_q2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_q2\">\n\n3.  Show that this can be explicitly defined as the following function of $x$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x) = \\frac{x}{4} + \\sqrt{\\frac{32-3x^2}{16}} \\tag*{[4]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\nRegion $A$ is above the $y-$axis enclosed by the ellipse, this is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_q3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_q3\">\n\n4.  Find the area of region $A$. [3 marks]\n\nA company wants to create a logo by inscribing the ellipse inside a rectangle.\n\nThe region inside the rectangle but outside of the ellipse is labeled as region $B$, this is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_q4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_q4\">\n\n5.  Find the size of region $B$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We must use implicit differentiation and the product rule\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (x^2-2xy+4y^2)'&=(8)'\\\\[6pt]\n        2x-\\left(2y+2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right)+8y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        2x-2y-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+8y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}(8y-2x)&=2y-2x\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{2y-2x}{8y-2x}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\displaystyle\\frac{x-y}{x-4y} \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  1.  Recognise that for horizontal tangents $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=0$. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{x-y}{x-4y}&=0\\\\[12pt]\n        x-y&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        x&=y \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can substitute this into the relation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y^2-2y \\times y+4y^2&=8\\\\[6pt]\n    y^2-2y^2+4y^2&=8 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3y^2&=8\\\\[6pt]\n    y&=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{3}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em}y=\\pm \\tfrac{2}{3}\\sqrt{6} \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Both of these tangents are shown in the image below, recall that $x=y$ when $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=0$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_a1\">\n\n    2.  Recognise that for vertical tangents $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ is undefined. This means the denominator of the quotient must be zero, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x-4y&=0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            y&=\\tfrac{1}{4}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Again, lets substitute this into the relation\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x^2-2x \\times \\left(\\tfrac{1}{4}x\\right)+4\\left(\\tfrac{1}{4}x\\right)^2&=8 \\\\[6pt]\n        x^2-\\tfrac{x^2}{2}+\\tfrac{x^2}{4}&=8\\\\[6pt]\n        4x^2-2x^2+x^2&=32\\\\[6pt]\n        x&=\\pm\\sqrt{\\tfrac{32}{3}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=3.265...\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.555em}x=\\pm \\,3.27 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        These two new tangents are added to the previous image and shown again below, recall that $y=\\tfrac{1}{4}x$ when $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ is undefined.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_a2\">\n\n3.  Considering the original relation, we need to make $y$ the subject\n     $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x^2-2xy+4y^2&=8\\\\[6pt]\n        4y^2-2xy&=8-x^2\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now complete the square on the L.H.S. to isolate the $y$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        4\\left(y^2-\\tfrac{1}{2}xy\\right)&=8-x^2\\\\[6pt]\n        4\\left[\\left(y-\\tfrac{x}{4}\\right)^2-\\tfrac{x^2}{16}\\right]&=8-x^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        4\\left(y-\\tfrac{x}{4}\\right)^2-\\tfrac{x^2}{4}&=8-x^2\\\\[6pt]\n        4\\left(y-\\tfrac{x}{4}\\right)^2&=8-\\tfrac{3}{4}x^2\\\\[6pt]\n        \\left(y-\\tfrac{x}{4}\\right)^2&=2-\\tfrac{3}{16}x^2\\\\[6pt]\n        \\left(y-\\tfrac{x}{4}\\right)^2&=\\frac{32-3x^2}{16}\\\\[6pt]\n        y-\\frac{x}{4}&=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{32-3x^2}{16}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As we want the upper part of the ellipse we must take the positive square root, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{x}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{32-3x^2}{16}} \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Consider the following image which has the two points on the vertical tangents labelled $S$ and $T$ and it shows the area enclosed by $f(x)$ and the $x-$axis.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_a3\">\n\n    If we integrate $f(x)$ from $S$ to $T$ we obtain the signed area. Notice in the image below that the red region will be negative and the blue region positive.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1176_a4%20%281%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1176_a4 (1)\">\n\n    As the ellipse is symmetrical centred at the origin the red region is the same size as the blue one.\n\n    Therefore the red region cancels out the blue region. Meaning the signed area is the area of region $A$. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=\\int_{-\\sqrt{\\tfrac{32}{3}}}^{\\sqrt{\\tfrac{32}{3}}}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\int_{-\\sqrt{\\tfrac{32}{3}}}^{\\sqrt{\\tfrac{32}{3}}}\\left(\\frac{x}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{32-3x^2}{16}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=7.255...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=7.26 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n5.  To find the area of region $B$ we must subtract the area of the ellipse from the area of the rectangle.\n\n    Using the answers to part (b) we can find the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/Screenshot%202024-08-28%20at%2010.51.58%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-08-28 at 10.51.58 PM\">\n\n    The width of the rectangle is $2 \\times \\sqrt{\\frac{32}{3}}$ and the height is $2 \\times \\tfrac{2}{3}\\sqrt{6}$. Therefore we can find the area\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{\\text{rectangle}}&=2 \\times \\sqrt{\\tfrac{32}{3}} \\times 2 \\times \\tfrac{2}{3}\\sqrt{6} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=6.531... \\times 3.265...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=21.33...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the answer to part (d) we can find the area of the ellipse.\n\n    Due to symmetry the area of the ellipse is $2\\times A$. Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        B&=A_{\\text{rectangle}}-2 \\times A\\\\[6pt]\n        &=21.33... - 2 \\times 7.255... \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=6.822...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=6.82\\hspace{0.525em} \\,\\text{units}^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1290","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1290\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=4x-x^2-1$.\n1.  Write $f(x)$ in the form $(x-h)^2+k$.[2 marks]\n\nA line is drawn through the points $A(0, f(0))$ and $B(2, f(2))$.\n\n2.  1.  Write down the coordinates of points $A$ and $B$.\n\n    2.  Find $g(x)$, the equation of the line passing through points $A$ and $B$.\n\n    3.  Hence, show that the area enclosed by $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ is $\\dfrac{4}{3}$ units$^2$. [4 marks]\n\nA horizontal line is drawn through the points $C(1, f(1))$ and $D(3, f(3))$.\n\n3.  Show that the area enclosed by $f(x)$ and line $CD$ is $\\dfrac{4}{3}$ units$^2$.[2 marks]\n\nConsider the two points $E(a, f(a))$ and $F(a+2, f(a+2))$.\n\n4.  Show that the area enclosed by the function $f$ and the line $EF$ is $\\dfrac{4}{3}$ units$^2$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To write $f$ in the desired form we must complete the square\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x)&=4x-x^2-1\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-(x^2-4x)-1\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-[(x-2)^2-(2)^2]-1\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-(x-2)^2+4-1\\\\[6pt]\n        &=3-(x-2)^2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  1.  We can find the $y-$coordinate by evaluating the function at both $x=0$ and $x=2$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &&f(0)&=3-(0-2)^2&\\hspace{-1em} &&f(2)&= 3-(2-2)^2&  \\\\[6pt]\n            &&&=-1&\\hspace{-1em} && &= 3&\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates are $A(0,-1)\\text{ and }B(2,3)$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We can make a quick sketch of $f$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1290-a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1290-a\">\n\n        We have the $y-$intercept already.\n\n        And the gradient of the line through $A$ and $B$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            m&=\\dfrac{3-(-1)}{2-0}\\\\[6pt]\n            m&=2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore, the line through $A$ and $B$ is $y=2x-1$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  The shaded area, seen below, shows the area we need to find\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1290-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1290-a2\">\n\n        To find the area we evaluate the following definite integral, where $A_x$ and $B_x$ are the $x-$coordinates of points $A$ and $B$ respectively\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\int_{A_x}^{B_x} (\\mathrm{f}(x)-\\mathrm{g}(x)) \\,dx \\\\[4pt]\n            &=\\int_{0}^{2} (4x-x^2-1-(2x-1)) \\,dx\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[4pt]\n            &=\\int_{0}^{2} (2x-x^2) \\,dx\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using the inverse power rule and substituting in the limits we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\left[x^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}x^3\\right]_{0}^{2}\\\\[4pt]\n             &=(2)^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}(2)^3-\\left((0)^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}(0)^3\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[4pt]\n             &=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\text{ units}^2\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We can make a quick sketch of the new region enclosed by $f(x)$ and the new (horizontal) $g(x)$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1290-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1290-a3\">\n\n    We now evaluate the definite integral, where $C_x$ and $D_x$ are the $x-$coordinates of points $C$ and $D$ respectively\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\int_{C_x}^{D_x} (\\mathrm{f}(x)-\\mathrm{g}(x)) \\,dx\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\int_{1}^{3} (4x-x^2-1-(2)) \\,dx\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\int_{1}^{3} (4x-x^2-3) \\,dx \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[3pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the inverse power rule, to integrate each term and substitute in the limits, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\left[2x^2-\\frac{1}{3}x^3-3x\\right]_{1}^{3}\\\\[3pt]\n            &=2(3)^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}(3)^3-3(3)-\\left((2(1)^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}(1)^3-3(1)\\right) \\\\[3pt]\n            &=18-9-9-(2-\\frac{1}{3}-3)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[3pt]\n            &=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\text{ units}^2\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The diagram below shows a region bound by $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and the points $E(a, f(a))$ and $F(a+2, f(a+2)))$.\n\n    The function $f$ needs to be used to calculate the $y-$coordinate values of $E$ and $F$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/AA1290-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1290-a4\">\n\n    To find the area we need to find the linear function $g(x)$ in terms of $a$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $\\mathrm{g}(x)$ is of the form $g(x)=m_gx+c_g$.\n\n    Where the gradient, $m_g$, is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        m_g&=\\dfrac{y_2+y_1}{x_2-x_1}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{f}(a+2)-\\mathrm{f}(a)}{a+2-a}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{(3-a^2)-(3-(a-2)^2))}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Simplifying we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\dfrac{-a^2+(a-2)^2}{2}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{-a^2+a^2-4a+4}{2}\\\\[3pt]\n        m_g&=2-2a \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[3pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the point $A(a, f(a))$ we can find the $y-$intercept, $c_g$, of $g(x)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        y&=(2-2a)x+c_g\\\\[3pt]\n        \\mathrm{f}(a)&=(2-2a)a+c_g\\\\[3pt]\n        3-(a-2)^2&=(2-2a)a+c_g\\\\[3pt]\n        c_g&=3-(a-2)^2-2a+2a^2\\\\[3pt]\n        c_g&=a^2+2a-1 \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[3pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The function $g(x)$ in terms of $a$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{g}(x)=(2-2a)x+a^2+2a-1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The shaded area is given by the following definite integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\int_{E_x}^{F_x} (f(x)-g(x)) \\,dx\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\int_{a}^{a+2} (4x-x^2-1-((2-2a)x+a^2+2a-1)) \\,dx \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Simplifying we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\int_{a}^{a+2} (4x-x^2-1-(2x-2ax+a^2+2a-1)) \\,dx\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\int_{a}^{a+2} (2x-x^2+2ax-a^2-2a) \\,dx\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\int_{a}^{a+2} (-x^2+(2a+2)x-a^2-2a) \\,dx\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can integrate each term, using the inverse power rule, and substitute in the limits.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    =\\left[-\\dfrac{1}{3}x^3+\\dfrac{2a+2}{2}x^2-x(a^2+2a)\\right]_{a}^{a+2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    =-\\dfrac{1}{3}(a+2)^3+(a+1)(a+2)^2-(a+2)(a^2+2a)-...\\\\\n    ...\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}a^3+(a+1)a^2-a(a^2+2a)\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Expanding and simplifying we gradient\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    =-\\dfrac{1}{3}(a^3+6a^2+12a+8)+a^3+5a^2+8a+4-(a^3+4a^2+4a)-...\\\\\n    ...\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}a^3+a^3+a^2-a^3-2a^2\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We continue to collect any like terms and simplify to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    =\\cancel{-\\dfrac{1}{3}a^3}-2a^2-4a-\\dfrac{8}{3}+a^2+4a+4-...\\\\\n    ...\\left(\\cancel{-\\dfrac{1}{3}a^3}-a^2\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\cancel{-2a^2}-\\cancel{4a}-\\dfrac{8}{3}+\\cancel{a^2}+\\cancel{4a}+4+\\cancel{a^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n    &=-\\dfrac{8}{3}+4\\\\\n    &=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\,\\text{units}^2\\hspace{3em}\\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1314","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1314\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the functions $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}$ and $g(x)=6-8e^{-x}$, where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nIt is known when $x=a$, where $a>0$, that $f'(a)=g'(a)$.\n\n1.    Show that $a=\\ln 2$. [3 marks]\n\nThe functions $f$ and $g$ have one intersection point at $x=\\ln 2$.\n\n2.    Find the area enclosed by the line $x=0$, the graph of  $f$ and the graph of $g$.\n\n     Express your answer in the form $a-b\\ln{c}$ where $a \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ and $b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. First let's differentiate $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x)&=\\tfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}&&g(x)=6-8e^{-x}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(x)&=\\tfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}\\times 2&&g'(x)=-8e^{-x}\\times (-1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=e^{2x}&&\\hspace{1em}=8e^{-x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now, because we know that $f'(a)=g'(a)$, lets set the derivative equations equal and substitute $x=a$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    e^{2a}&=8e^{-a}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    To solve for $a$ let's apply the natural logarithm to both sides.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\ln e^{2a}&=\\ln (8e^{-a})\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Then we can simplify using logarithm rules.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2a&=\\ln 8+\\ln e^{-a}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2a&=\\ln 8-a\\\\[6pt]\n\n    3a&=\\ln 8\\\\[6pt]\n\n    3a&=\\ln 2^3\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    3a&=3\\ln 2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    a&=\\ln 2\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. To find the area between two curves we use the formula \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A=\\int_a^b{(\\text{upper curve}-\\text{lower curve})}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    where $a$ and $b$ are the upper and lower limits.\n\n    As we are told that the two curves intersect only once, at $x=\\ln{2}$, we can be confident that one curve consistently lies above the other between the limits of $x=0$ and $x=\\ln{2}$.\n\n    This means the graphs do not cross again between $x=0$ and $x=\\ln2$ and we do not need to worry about the area being partially negative, the difference between the upper and lower curves will remain positive throughout.\n\n    Let's consider the $y$-values at $x=0$ so we can determine which function is the upper curve.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(0)&=\\tfrac{1}{2}e^{2(0)}&&g(0)=-8e^{-(0)}+6\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{2}&&\\hspace{1.7em}=-2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Since $f(0)>g(0)$ this means $f(x)$ is the upper function.\n\n    Now we can write the equation to find the area.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A&=\\int_a^b{\\bigg(f(x)-g(x)}\\bigg)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\int_0^{\\ln2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}-(-8e^{-x}+6)\\right)\\mathrm{d}x\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1A1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\int_0^{\\ln 2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}+8e^{-x}-6\\right)\\mathrm{d}x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Next we can integrate using the inverse power rule and the standard integral\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\int e^{x}\\ \\mathrm{d}x&=e^{x}+c\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    and accounting for the coefficients in the exponents we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A&=\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)e^{2x}+8\\left(\\frac{1}{-1}\\right)e^{-x}-6x\\right]_0^{\\ln 2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Evaluating the upper and lower boundaries\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A&=\\left[\\frac{1}{4}e^{2(\\ln2)}-8e^{-\\ln2}-6(\\ln2)\\right]-\\left[\\frac{1}{4}e^{2(0)}-8e^{0}-6(0)\\right]\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Applying the logarithm rule from part (a) to the exponents\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\left[\\frac{1}{4}e^{\\ln2^2}-8e^{\\ln2^{-1}}-6\\ln2\\right]-\\left[\\frac{1}{4}-8\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\left[\\frac{1}{4}e^{\\ln4}-8e^{\\ln{\\normalsize\\frac{1}{2}}}-6(\\ln2)\\right]-\\left[-\\frac{31}{4}\\right]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the fact that $e^{\\ln a}=a$ we can simplify further\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\frac{1}{4}(4)-8\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right)-6\\ln2+\\frac{31}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=1-4-6\\ln2+\\frac{31}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{19}{4}-6\\ln2\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This is the form that the question has asked for therefore $a=\\tfrac{19}{4}$, $b=6$ and $c=2$."},{"ref":"AA183","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA183\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln(x+2)}{2}$, for $x > -2$. The diagram below shows\npart of the graph of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/0b26b0e195f4a087c10216491d624f903712efff.svg\" alt=\"0b26b0e195f4a087c10216491d624f903712efff.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the coordinates of:\n    1.  the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the vertical asymptote to the graph of $f$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the area of the region enclosed by the graph of $f$, the\n    $x$-axis and\\\n    the $y$-axis. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.8em} \\dfrac{\\ln(x+2)}{2} &= 0 \\\\[4pt]\n        x &= -1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are\n         $(-1,0)$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $f(x)=\\dfrac{\\ln(x+2)}{2}$ for $x = 0$, we have\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\hspace{0.4em} f(0) &= \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n         &\\approx 0.347\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n         Hence the coordinates of the\n        $y$-intercept are\n         $(0,0.347)$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0^+} \\ln x &= -\\infty \\hspace{3em} \\\\[7pt]\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -2^+} \\ln (x+2) &= -\\infty \\\\[6pt]\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -2^+} f(x) &= -\\infty \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    the equation of the vertical asymptote is\n     $x = -2$\n\n3.  The area of the region enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis\n    and the $y$-axis is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} A &= \\int_{-1}^0 \\dfrac{\\ln(x+2)}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 0.193 \\text{ units}^2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA480","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA480\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nConsider the function defined by $f(x) = (1-x)\\sqrt{2x - x^2}$ where\n$0 \\leq x \\leq 2$.\n\n1.  Show that $f(1 - x) = - f(1 + x)$, for $-1 \\leq x \\leq 1$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $f'(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Hence find the $x$-coordinates of any local minimum or maximum\n    points. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the range of $f$. [3 marks]\n\n5.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, indicating clearly the coordinates\n    of\n    $\\text{the \\(x\\)-intercepts}$ and any local maximum or minimum\n    points. [3 marks]\n\n6.  Find the area of the region enclosed by the graph of $y = f(x)$ on\n    the $x$-axis, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$. [2 marks]\n\n7.  Show that\n    $\\displaystyle \\int_0^2 \\left|f(x)\\right|\\,dx > \\left|\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx\\right|$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  For any $-1 \\leq x \\leq 1$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} f(1-x) &= (1-(1-x))\\sqrt{2(1-x) - (1-x)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= x\\sqrt{(2-2x) - (1-2x + x^2)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= x\\sqrt{1 - x^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Similarly, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} f(1+x) &= (1-(1+x))\\sqrt{2(1+x) - (1+x)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -x\\sqrt{(2+2x) - (1+2x + x^2)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -x\\sqrt{1- x^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, for any $-1 \\leq x \\leq 1$,\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(1-x) &= x\\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-x\\sqrt{1 - x^2}\\big] \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= - f(1+x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Differentiating $f$ with respect to $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[(1-x)\\sqrt{2x - x^2}\\big] \\\\[10pt]\n    [\\text{by differentiation rules}] \\hspace{2em} &= -\\sqrt{2x-x^2} + \\dfrac{(1-x)(2-2x)}{2\\sqrt{2x-x^2}} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\sqrt{2x-x^2} + \\dfrac{(1-x)^2}{\\sqrt{2x-x^2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    [\\text{since $0 < x < 2$}] \\hspace{2em} &= \\dfrac{-(2x-x^2) + (1-2x + x^2)}{\\sqrt{2x-x^2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2x^2 - 4x + 1}{\\sqrt{2x-x^2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $f'(x) = 0$ for $x$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{2x^2 - 4x + 1}{\\sqrt{2x-x^2}} &= 0 \\hspace{9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2x^2 - 4x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11.1em} x \\approx 0.293,\\hspace{0.25em} 1.71 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n4.  Evaluating $f(x)$ for the critical numbers $x = 0.293$ and\n    $x = 1.71$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} f(0.293) &= (1-0.293)\\sqrt{2(0.293) - (0.293)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.5 \\\\[12pt]\n    f(1.71) &= (1-1.71)\\sqrt{2(1.71) - (1.71)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx -0.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $f(x)$ for the end points\n    $x = 0$ and $x = 2$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} f(0) &= (1-0)\\sqrt{2(0) - (0)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    f(2) &= (1-2)\\sqrt{2(2) - (2)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the range of $f$ is\n     $[-0.5, 0.5]$\n\n5.  The local maximum and minimum points on the graph of $y = f(x)$\n    are $\\mathrm{P}(0.293,0.5)$ and\n    $\\mathrm{Q}(1.71,-0.5)$, respectively.\n    Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} (1-x)\\sqrt{2x - x^2} &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    x &= 0,\\hspace{0.25em} 1,\\hspace{0.25em} 2 \\hspace{2.8em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the $x$-intercepts of the graph of $y = f(x)$ are\n    $\\mathrm{A}(0,0)$, $\\mathrm{B}(1,0)$\n    and $\\mathrm{C}(2,0)$.\n\n    If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, indicating the points A, B, C,\n    P and Q, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/9dd83089e114d237eeecf0520f20db531d26c1c0.svg\" alt=\"9dd83089e114d237eeecf0520f20db531d26c1c0.svg\">\n\n6.  Evaluating the integral of $f(x)$ from $a = 0$ to $b = 1$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &= \\int_0^1 (1-x)\\sqrt{2x-x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n7.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx &= \\int_0^2 (1-x)\\sqrt{2x-x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0 \\hspace{3.45em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.2em} \\int_0^2 |f(x)|\\,dx &= \\int_0^2 |1-x|\\sqrt{2x-x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^2 \\left|f(x)\\right|\\,dx = \\dfrac{2}{3} > \\left|\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx\\right| = 0 \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA489","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA489\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x + 2}{4x + 1}$,\n$x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\neq -\\dfrac{1}{4}$.\n\n1.  Find an expression for $f^{-1}(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $f(x)$ can be written in the form\n    $f(x) = A + \\dfrac{B}{4x + 1}$, find the values of the constants $A$\n    and $B$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence write down\n    $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x + 2}{4x + 1}\\,\\textrm dx$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Setting $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\\\\n    f(f^{-1}(x))    & =  x \\\\[6pt]\n    f(y)            & =  x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3y + 2}{4y + 1}  & =  x \\qquad \\quad [\\text{solve for $y$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    3y + 2      & =  4xy + x \\\\[6pt]\n    y(3 - 4x)       & =  x - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y           & =  \\dfrac{x - 2}{3 - 4x}.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we have\n      $\\hspace{.275em} f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x - 2}{3 - 4x}$\n\n2.  Solving the following equation for $A$ and $B$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{3x + 2}{4x + 1} & =  A + \\dfrac{B}{4x + 1} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{3x + 2}{4x + 1} & =  \\dfrac{4Ax + A + B}{4x + 1} \\\\[10pt]\n    & \\Longrightarrow  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n     A  =  \\dfrac{3}{4},\\ \\  B  =  \\dfrac{5}{4}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using part (b), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{3x + 2}{4x + 1}\\, \\textrm dx & =  \\int \\left[\\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{5}{4}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{4x + 1}\\right]\\textrm dx \\\\[14pt]\n    &  \\hspace{.275em}=  \\dfrac{3}{4}x + \\dfrac{5}{16}\\ln|4x+1| + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA499","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA499\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int_0^6 \\dfrac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\textrm dx = \\ln k$,\nfind the value of $k$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^6 \\dfrac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\textrm dx & =  \\ln k \\hspace{5.2cm}\\\\[8pt]\n\\ln(3x+2)\\big|_0^6 & =  \\ln k \\\\[11pt]\n\\ln 20 - \\ln 2 & =  \\ln k \\\\[9pt]\n\\ln \\Big(\\frac{20}{2}\\Big) & =  \\ln k \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2cm}k  =  10 \\qquad\\quad[\\text{since $x \\mapsto \\ln x$ is one-to-one}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA504","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA504\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{ae^{-x}}{b - ae^{-x}}$ where\n$a > 0$, $b > 0$.\n\n1.  Show that $f'(x) = \\dfrac{-abe^{-x}}{(b-ae^{-x})^2}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Explain why $f''(x)$ is never zero. [5 marks]\n\n3.  Find the equation of:\n    1.  the vertical asymptote of $f$;\n\n    2.  the horizontal asymptote of $f$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Draw a sign diagram for $f'(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n5.  If $a = 3$ and $b = 1$,\n    1.  sketch the graph of $f$ labelling all asymptotes;\n\n    2.  find the area of the region enclosed by $f$, the $x$ and $y$\n        axes and the line $x = \\ln 2$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the quotient rule to differentiate $f(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)       & = \\dfrac{(-ae^{-x})(b-ae^{-x}) - (ae^{-x})(ae^{-x})}{(b-ae^{-x})^2} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{-abe^{-x} + \\cancel{a^2e^{-2x}} - \\cancel{a^2e^{-2x}}}{(b-ae^{-x})^2} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{-abe^{-x}}{(b-ae^{-x})^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the quotient rule to differentiating $f'(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f''(x)      & = \\dfrac{(abe^{-x})(b-ae^{-x})^2 - (-abe^{-x})\\big[2(b-ae^{-x})(ae^{-x})\\big]}{(b-ae^{-x})^4} \\\\[3pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{(abe^{-x})(b-ae^{-x}) - (-abe^{-x})(2ae^{-x})}{(b-ae^{-x})^3} \\\\[3pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{ab^2e^{-x} - a^2be^{-2x} + 2a^2be^{-2x}}{(b-ae^{-x})^3} \\\\[3pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{ab^2e^{-x} + a^2be^{-2x}}{(b-ae^{-x})^3} \\\\[3pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{abe^{-x}(b + ae^{-x})}{(b-ae^{-x})^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Since $a > 0$, $b > 0$, $e^{-x} > 0$, we can see that $f''(x)$\n    cannot be zero for any $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3.\n    1.  The equation of the vertical asymptote of $f$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        b - ae^{-x}         & = 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        b               & = ae^{-x} \\\\[6pt]\n        be^x            & = a \\\\[6pt]\n        e^{x}           & = \\dfrac{a}{b} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{1.4cm}x  = \\ln\\Big(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\Big)\n        $$\n\n    2.  Note that\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x)        & = \\dfrac{ae^{-x}}{b - ae^{-x}} \\cdot \\dfrac{e^x}{e^x} \\\\[3pt]\n        & = \\dfrac{a}{be^x - a}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x) \\rightarrow 0  &\\text{ as } x \\rightarrow \\infty \\\\[3pt]\n        &\\text{ and } \\\\[3pt]\n        f(x) \\rightarrow -1     &\\text{ as }  x \\rightarrow -\\infty\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the equations of the horizontal asymptotes are\n          $y = 0 \\mbox{ and } y = -1$\n\n4.  Here is the sign diagram for $f'(x)$:\n\n    |   $x$   | $-\\infty < x < \\ln\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ | $\\ln\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) < x < \\infty$ |\n    |:-------:|:-----------------------------------------------------------:|:----------------------------------------------------------:|\n    | $f'(x)$ |                             $-$                             |                            $-$                             |\n\n5.  For $a = 3$ and $b = 1$, we have\n    $\\boxed{f(x) = \\dfrac{3e^{-x}}{1 - 3e^{-x}}}$\n\n    1.  Here is the sketch of the graph of $y = f(x)$:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/112ee9b78075e5e4511dd3aaefa8ffc03c70a8f1.svg\" alt=\"112ee9b78075e5e4511dd3aaefa8ffc03c70a8f1.svg\">\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        {\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Area}     & =  - \\int_0^{\\ln 2} f(x)\\, \\textrm dx \\\\[3pt]\n        & =  -\\int_0^{\\ln 2} \\dfrac{3e^{-x}}{1 - 3e^{-x}}\\, \\textrm dx \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{let $u = 1 - 3e^{-x}$}] \\\\[3pt]\n        & = -\\int_{-2}^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\, \\textrm du \\\\[3pt]\n        & =  - \\Big[\\ln |u|\\Big] \\Big|_{-2}^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[8pt]\n        %           & = - \\Big[\\ln \\Big(\\frac{1}{2}\\Big) - \\ln 2\\Big] \\\\[9pt]\n        & = \\ln 2 - \\ln \\Big(\\frac{1}{2}\\Big) \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{.275em} = \\ln 4 \\text{ unit}^2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        }\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA522","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA522\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA function $f$ satisfies the conditions $f(0) = \\ln 2$, $f(1) = 0$ and\nits second derivative is\n$f''(x) = 40x^\\frac{2}{3} + (x+1)^{-2}$,\n$x \\geq 0$.\n\nFind $f(x)$.","markScheme":"If we integrate $f''$ with respect to $x$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.75em} \\int f''(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&= \\int \\Big[40x^\\frac{2}{3} + (x+1)^{-2}\\Big] \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\nf'(x)       &= 40\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^\\frac{5}{3}}{\\frac{5}{3}}\\bigg] + \\left[-\\dfrac{1}{x+1}\\right] + A \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by FTC}] \\\\[8pt]\nf'(x)       &= 24x^\\frac{5}{3} - \\dfrac{1}{x+1} + A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we\nintegrate $f'$ with respect to $x$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.15em} \\int f'(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&= \\int \\Big[24x^\\frac{5}{3} - \\dfrac{1}{x+1} + A\\Big] \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\nf(x)        &= 24\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^\\frac{8}{3}}{\\frac{8}{3}}\\bigg] - \\ln(x+1) + Ax + B \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by FTC}] \\\\[11pt]\nf(x)        &= 9x^\\frac{8}{3} - \\ln(x+1) + Ax + B \\tag*{$(\\ast)$}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, substituting $x = 0$ in $(\\ast)$ and using the condition\n$f(0) = \\ln 2$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0)        &= 9(0)^\\frac{8}{3} - \\ln(0+1) + A(0) + B \\hspace{0.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\ln 2       &= 0 - \\ln 1 + 0 + B \\\\[6pt]\nB       &= \\ln 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSimilarly, substituting $x = 1$ in\n$(\\ast)$ and using the condition $f(1) = 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.1em} f(1)         &= 9(1)^\\frac{8}{3} - \\ln(1+1) + A(1) + \\ln 2 \\\\[6pt]\n0                   &= 9 - \\cancel{\\ln 2} + A + \\cancel{\\ln 2} \\\\[6pt]\nA                   &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{.275em}f(x) = 9x^\\frac{8}{3} - \\ln(x+1) - 9x + \\ln 2 \\hspace{1.5em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA525","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA525\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = 3x^2 - 4x$. The graph of $f$ is shown in the following\ndiagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/e2c3c697e83174aad434bec213190a266a8c7cf8.svg\" alt=\"e2c3c697e83174aad434bec213190a266a8c7cf8.svg\">\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int (3x^2 - 4x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the region enclosed by the graph of $f$, the\n    $x$-axis and the lines $x = 2$ and $x = 4$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Integrating $f(x)$ term by term, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int (3x^2 - 4x) \\,\\textrm d x  & = \\int 3x^2 \\,\\textrm d x + \\int (-4 x)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[4pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {3x^3} 3 - \\dfrac {4x^2} 2 + C\\\\[7pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = x^3 - 2x^2 + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The area enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis and the lines\n    $x = 2$ and $x = 4$ is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6cm}\n    A       & = \\int_2^4 (3x^2 - 4x) \\,\\textrm d x \\\\[4pt]\n    & = (x^3 - 2x^2)\\Big|_2^4\\\\[6pt]\n    & = [4^3 - 2(4^2)] - [2^3 - 2(2^2)]\\\\[7pt]\n    & = [64 - 32] - [8 - 8]\\\\[7pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 32\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA530","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA530\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2e^{\\frac{x}{5}}$ and $g(x) = mx$, where $m\\geq0$, and\n$-6\\leq x \\leq 6$. Let $R$ be the region enclosed by the $y$-axis, the\ngraph of $f$, and the graph of $g$.\n\n1.  Let $m=2$.\n    1.  Sketch the graphs of $f$ and $g$ on the same axes.\n\n    2.  Find the area of $R$. [7 marks]\n\n2.  Consider all values of $m$ such that the graphs of $f$ and $g$\n    intersect. Find the value of $m$ that gives the greatest value for\n    the area of $R$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Graphics of $f$ and $g$ are shown in the diagram below\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/07778bd6161f620485d0a1023e79e4d2a671e73f.svg\" alt=\"07778bd6161f620485d0a1023e79e4d2a671e73f.svg\">\n\n    2.  First let's find the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection\n        of $f$ and $g$, A, shown in the diagram\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3cm}\n        2 e^{\\frac x 5}         & = 2 x\\\\[5pt]\n        x               & = 1.296 \\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We choose the value of $x$ that is closer to the $y$-axis,\n        $x = 1.296$.\n        Hence, the area of $R$ is given by the integral\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{.5cm}\n        A_R         & = \\int_0^{1.296} \\left(2 e^{\\frac x 5} - 2x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[8pt]\n        &\\hspace{.275em} = 1.28\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  The area of $R$ gets its maximum when $g$ is a tangent line to $f$\n    and $m = f'(x_1)$, where $(x_1,y_1)$ is a point on $f$ and $g$. We\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)       & = \\dfrac 2 5 e^{\\frac x 5}\\hspace{1.6cm}\\\\[5pt]\n    m       & = \\dfrac 2 5 e^{\\frac {x_1} 5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the fact that $f(x_1) = g(x_1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2cm}\n    2e^{\\frac {x_1} 5}  & = \\dfrac 2 5 e^{\\frac {x_1} 5}x_1\\\\[5pt]\n    x_1             & = 5\\\\[5pt]\n    m               & = \\dfrac 2 5 e^{\\frac 5 5}\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}  = 1.09\n    \\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA531","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA531\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $g(x) = \\dfrac{8-2x}{\\sqrt{9+8x-x^2}}$. The following diagram shows\npart of the graph of $g$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/5fa0f1e41fdbe2be7df3ce222628aa65ca20900b.svg\" alt=\"5fa0f1e41fdbe2be7df3ce222628aa65ca20900b.svg\">\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed by the graph of $g$, the $x$-axis, and the\n$y$-axis. Find the area of $R$.","markScheme":"First, we need to find a point where $g$ intersects the $x$-axis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{8-2x}{\\sqrt{9+8x-x^2}}   & = 0\\hspace{2.5cm}\\\\[3pt]\n8 - 2 x                     & = 0\\\\[3pt]\nx                       & = 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the area of $R$ is\ngiven by the integral\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5cm}\nA       & = \\int_0^4 \\dfrac{8-2x}{\\sqrt{9+8x-x^2}}\\,\\textrm dx\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int_0^4 u^{-1/2}\\,\\textrm d u \\qquad\\quad\n[\\mbox{with } u = 9+8x-x^2]\\\\[6pt]\n& = 2 u^{1/2}\\Big|_0^4\\\\[6pt]\n& = 2 \\sqrt{9+8x-x^2}\\Big|_0^4\\\\[6pt]\n& = 2 \\sqrt{25} - 2 \\sqrt 9\\\\[10pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em} = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA537","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA537\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA function $f(x)$ has derivative $f'(x) = 6x^2-24x$. The graph of $f$\nhas an $x$-intercept at $x = 1$.\n\n1.  Find $f(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  The graph of $f$ has a point of inflexion at $x = k$. Find $k$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the values of $x$ for which the graph of $f$ is concave-up.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)        & = \\int (6x^2 - 24x) \\textrm d x\\\\[4pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {6x^3} 3 - \\dfrac {24 x^2} 2 + C\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2 x^3 - 12 x^2 + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since the\n    $x$-intercept is at $x = 1$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{.9cm}\n    0       & = 2(1^3) - 12 (1^2) + C\\\\[6pt]\n    C       & = 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n      $f(x) = 2x^3 - 12 x^2 + 10$\n\n2.  The second derivative is zero at the point of inflection, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f''(x)  & = 12 x - 24\\hspace{1.4cm}\\\\[4pt]\n    0       & = 12 x - 24\\\\[4pt]\n    x       & = 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence   $k = 2$\n\n3.  The function $f$ is concave-up when $f''(x) > 0$, then\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    12 x - 24       & > 0 \\hspace{2.8cm}\\\\[4pt]\n    12x             & > 24\\\\[4pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2mm}x > 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA544","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA544\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 - 3x + 2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The following diagram\nshows part of the graph of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/d183574a6a692dbdc1dc3e0fc2c20b11fc58cca9.svg\" alt=\"d183574a6a692dbdc1dc3e0fc2c20b11fc58cca9.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ crosses the $x$-axis at the point P$(1,0)$ and at the\npoint Q$(2,0)$.\n\n1.  Show that $f'(1) = -1$. [3 marks]\n\nThe line $L$ is the normal to the graph of $f$ at P.\n\n2.  Find the equation of $L$ in the form $y = mx + c$.\n    [3 marks]\n\nThe line $L$ intersects the graph of $f$ at another point R, as shown in\nthe following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/34a951b9f58cf4f39cef2ae8f1bab13da582edcc.svg\" alt=\"34a951b9f58cf4f39cef2ae8f1bab13da582edcc.svg\">\n\n3.  Find the $x$-coordinate of R. [4 marks]\n\n4.  Find the area of the region enclosed by the graph of $f$\n    and the line $L$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)   & = 2 x - 3\\\\[6pt]\n    f'(1)   & = 2(1) - 3\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The equation of the normal $L$ is $y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$ with\n    $m = -\\dfrac {1}{-1}$ (by part (a)), $y_1 = 0$, and $x_1 = 1$, hence\n      $y = x - 1$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 - 3x + 2    & = x - 1\\\\[6pt]\n    x^2 - 4x + 3    & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    (x - 3) (x - 1)     & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    x           & = 3,\\,1\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}x = 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The area of the required region is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A       & = \\int_1^3 (x - 1) - (x^2 - 3x + 2)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\int_1^3 -x^2 + 4x - 3\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\left[-\\dfrac {x^3} 3 + 2x^2 - 3 x\\right]_1^3\\\\[6pt]\n    & = [-9 + 18 - 9] - \\left[-\\dfrac 1 3 + 2 - 3 \\right]\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} = \\dfrac 4 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA548","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA548\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3$. Given that $f(2) = 1$, find $f(x)$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)        & = \\int f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int(3x^2 - 3)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = x^3 - 3x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(2) = 1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1   & = 2^3 - 3(2) + C\\\\[6pt]\n1   & = 8 - 6 +C\\\\[6pt]\nC   & = -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n  $f(x) = x^3 - 3 x - 1$"},{"ref":"AA549","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA549\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f'(x) = 6x^2 + 2x - 1$. Given that $f(2) = 5$, find $f(x)$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)        & = \\int f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int(6x^2 + 2x - 1)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = 2x^3 + x^2 - x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(2) = 5$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5   & = 2(2)^3 + 2^2 - 2 + C\\\\[6pt]\n5   & = 16  + 4 - 2 + C\\\\[6pt]\nC   & = -13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n  $f(x) = 2x^3 + x^2 - x - 13$"},{"ref":"AA553","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA553\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider a function $f(x)$ such that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_1^5 f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 6$.\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_1^5 2 f (x)\\,\\textrm dx$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_1^5(f(x) + 3)\\,\\textrm dx$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Removing the constant from the integral, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_1^5 2f(x)\\,\\textrm d x         & =  2\\int_1^5 f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\hspace{3mm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2(6)\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}  = 12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Writing as the sum of two integrals, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4mm}\n    \\int_1^5 (f(x) + 3)\\,\\textrm d x    & = \\int_1^5 f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\int_1^5 3 \\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 6 + \\left[3x\\right]\\Big|_1^5\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 6 + [15 - 3]\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}  = 18\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA558","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA558\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int \\dfrac 8 {2x - 1}\\,\\textrm d x$, for\n$x > \\dfrac 1 2$. The graph of $f$ passes through $(1,5)$.\n\nFind $f(x)$.","markScheme":"We integrate\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)        & = \\int \\dfrac 8 {2x - 1}\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\dfrac {8 \\ln (2x - 1)} 2 + C\\\\[6pt]\n& = 4 \\ln(2x - 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's determine $C$. Substituting $(1,5)$ into the expression for $f$,\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5mm}\n5       & = 4 \\ln (2(1) - 1) + C\\\\[6pt]\n5       & = 4\\ln (1) + C\\\\[6pt]\nC       & = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\n  $f(x) = 4 \\ln(2x - 1) + 5$"},{"ref":"AA568","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA568\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2 x e^x$ and $g(x) = - 2 f(x) -1$.\n\nThe graphs of $f$ and $g$ intersect at $x = a$ and $x = b$, where\n$a< b$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$ and of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, find the area of the region enclosed by the graphs of $f$ and\n    $g$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let's find $g$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(x)    & = -2 f(x) - 1\\hspace{1cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = -2 (2 x e^x) - 1\\\\[6pt]\n    & = -4 x e^x - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next, we find where $f$ and $g$ intersect\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3cm}\n    2 x e ^ x   & = -4 x e^x - 1\\\\[6pt]\n    x       & = -2.83,\\,-0.204\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence,   $a = -2.83\\ \\mbox{and } b = -0.204$\n\n2.  The area is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4cm}\n    A       & = \\int_a^b (g(x) - f(x))\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\int_{-2.83}^{-0.204} (-4x e^x - 1 - 2 x e ^ x)\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= 1.91\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA575","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA575\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\ln x$ and $g(x) = 2 + 3 \\ln (x - 1)$, for $x > 1$.\n\nThe graph of $g$ can be obtained from the graph of $f$ by two\ntransforms:\n\na vertical stretch of scale factor $q$\n\na translation of $\\binom h k$\n\n1.  Write down the value of\n    1.  $q$;\n\n    2.  $h$;\n    3.  $k$.[3 marks]\n\nLet $h(x) = g(x) \\cdot \\cos(0.1x)$, for $1 < x < 8$. The following\ndiagram shows the graph of $h$ and the line $y = x$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/cb7483cb56f1d952788497253145250d6c19a449.svg\" alt=\"cb7483cb56f1d952788497253145250d6c19a449.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_{2.02}^{5.57} (h(x) - x)\\,\\textrm d x$.\n\n    2.  Hence, find the area of the region enclosed by the graphs of $h$\n        and $h^{-1}$.[5 marks]\n\n3.  Let $d$ be the vertical distance from a point on the graph\n    of $h$ to the line $y = x$. There is a point $Q(x,y)$ on the graph\n    of $h$ where $d$ is a maximum. Find the coordinates of $Q$, where\n    $2.02 < x < 5.57$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  $ q = 3$\n\n    2.  $h = 1$\n\n    3.  $k = 2$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1cm}\\int_{2.02}^{5.57} (h(x) - x)\\,\\textrm dx\n        & = \\int_{2.02}^{5.57} [(2 + 3 \\ln(x - 1)) \\cos(0.1 x) - x]\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n        & \\hspace{.275em}  = 2.28\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  By part (b) (i), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        2 \\int_{2.02}^{5.57} (h(x) - x)\\,\\textrm dx & = 2(2.28)\\hspace{4.2cm}\\\\[6pt]\n        & \\hspace{.275em} = 4.56\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  The distance is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d   & = h(x) - x\\\\[6pt]\n    d   & = (2 + 3 \\ln(x - 1))\\cos(0.1 x) - x\\\\[6pt]\n    x   & = 3.44\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\\\[6pt]\n    h(3.44)     & = 4.40\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n      $\\mbox Q (3.44,4.40)$"},{"ref":"AA582","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA582\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_3^7 g(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 8$.\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_3^7 5 g (x)\\,\\textrm dx$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_4^8(g(x-1) + 2)\\,\\textrm dx$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_3^7 5g(x)\\,\\textrm d x         & =  5\\int_3^7 g(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\hspace{3mm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 5(8)\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}  = 40\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4mm}\n    \\int_4^8 (g(x-1) + 2)\\,\\textrm d x  & = \\int_4^8 g(x-1)\\,\\textrm d x + \\int_4^8 2 \\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\int_3^7 g(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\int_4^8 2 \\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 8 + \\left[2x\\right]\\Big|_4^8\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 8 + [16 - 8]\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}  = 16\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA595","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA595\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac {\\ln (8 x^3)} {k x}$ where $x > 0$,\n$k \\in \\mathbb R^+$.\n\n1.  Show that $f'(x) = \\dfrac {3 - \\ln(8 x^3)} {k x^2}$. [4 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ has exactly one maximum point A.\n\n2.  Find the $x$-coordinate of A.[3 marks]\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ is given by\n$f''(x) = \\dfrac {2 \\ln (8 x^3) - 9} {kx^3}$. The graph of $f$ has\nexactly one point of inflexion B.\n\n3.  Show that the $x$-coordinate of B is\n    $\\dfrac {e^{3/2}} {2}$. [3 marks]\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis, and the\nvertical lines through the maximum point A and the point of inflexion B.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/b5db3dac8f98ade85e6b992c4d531177ea19402b.svg\" alt=\"b5db3dac8f98ade85e6b992c4d531177ea19402b.svg\">\n\n4.  Given that the area of $R$ is $5$, find the value of $k$.\n    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the quotient rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)       & = \\dfrac {kx \\cdot \\dfrac {8(3x^2)} {8 x^3} - k \\cdot \\ln(8x^3)} {(kx)^2}\\\\[8pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {3k - k \\ln(8x^3)} {k^2x^2}\\\\[15pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}  = \\dfrac {3 - \\ln(8x^3)} {kx^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  A maximum on $f$ occurs when $f'(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac {3 - \\ln(8x^3)} {kx^2}   & = 0\\hspace{2.6cm}\\\\[8pt]\n    \\ln(8x^3)                   & = 3\\\\[8pt]\n    8x^3                    & = e^3\\\\[8pt]\n    x^3                     & = \\dfrac {e^3} {8}\\\\[10pt]\n    & \\hspace{-2mm} x = \\dfrac e 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The point of inflection occurs when $f''(x) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac {2 \\ln (8 x^3) - 9} {kx^3}  & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    2 \\ln (8 x^3) - 9  & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln(8x^3)                      & = \\dfrac 9 2\\\\[6pt]\n    8 x^3                      & = e^{9/2}\\\\[6pt]\n    x^3                            & = \\dfrac {e^{9/2}} 8\\\\[10pt]\n    &  \\hspace{-2mm}\n    x = \\dfrac {e^{3/2}} 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  An expression for the area of $R$ can be found by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_R         & = \\int_{\\frac e 2}^{\\frac {e^{3/2}}2} \\dfrac {\\ln(8x^3)} {kx}\\,\n    \\textrm d x \\hspace{3cm}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Integrating by\n    substitution with $u = \\ln(8x^3)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_R     & = \\int_3^{\\frac9 2} \\dfrac u {3k}\\,\\textrm d u \\hspace{4cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac 1 {3k} \\left[\\dfrac {u^2}2\\right]_3^{\\frac 9 2}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac 1 {3k} \\left[ \\dfrac {81}8 - \\dfrac 9 2\\right]\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {15} {8k}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    It was given that $A_R = 5$. Hence we can solve for $k$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac {15} {8k}    & = 5\\hspace{5cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    8k              & = 3\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2mm}  k  = \\dfrac 3 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA727","marks":"18","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA727\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nA cannonball is fired from the top of a tower. The rate of change of the height, $h$, of the cannonball above the ground is modelled by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh'(t) = -4t + 20, \\hspace{0.5em} t \\geq 0,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $h$ is in metres and $t$ is the time, in seconds, since the moment the cannonball was fired.\n\n1.  Determine the time $t$ at which the cannonball reached its maximum height. [2 marks]\n\nAfter one second, the cannonball is 26 metres above the ground.\n\n2.  1. Find an expression for $h(t)$, the height of the cannonball above the ground at time $t$.\n\n    2. Hence, determine the maximum height reached by the cannonball. [5 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the height of the tower. [1 mark]\n\n4.  Calculate the height of the cannonball $4$ seconds after it was fired. [2 marks]\n\nThe cannonball hits its target on the ground $n$ seconds after it was fired.\n\n5.  Find the value of $n$. [2 marks]\n\n6.  Determine the total time the cannonball was above the height of the tower.[3 marks]\n\nA second cannonball is fired from exactly halfway up the tower, with the same projectile motion as the first cannonball.\n\n7. Given that both cannonballs land at the same time, determine the length of time between the first cannonball and the second cannonball being fired. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The time at which the cannonball reaches maximum height is found by solving $h'(t)=0$. We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h'(t) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    -4t + 20 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.275em}\\boxed{t = 5 \\text{ s}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  1. Given that $h(t)=\\int h'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        h(t) &= \\int{-4t+20}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -2t^2 +20t + C\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Given that $h=26$ when $t=1$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        h(1) &= -2(1)^2 +20(1) + C \\\\[6pt]\n        26 &= -2 + 20 + C \\\\[6pt]\n        C &= 8\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore $\\hspace{0.575em}\\boxed{h(t) = -2t^2 +20t + 8}$.\n\n    2. We must calculate $h(5)$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        h(5) &= -2(5)^2 +20(5) + 8 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 58\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The maximum height reached is $\\hspace{0.575em}\\boxed{58 \\text{ metres}}$.\n\n3.  Evaluating $h(t)$ for $t = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} h(0) &= -2(0)^2+20(0)+8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{= 8 \\text{ m}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Evaluating $h(t)$ for $t = 4$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} h(5) &= -2(4)^2+20(4)+8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{= 56 \\text{ m}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Solving the equation $h(n) = 0$ for $n > 0$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -2n^2+20n+8 &= 0 \\hspace{9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}\\boxed{n \\approx 10.4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n6.  Solving the equation $h(t) = 8$ for $t > 0$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -2t^2+20t+8 &= 8 \\hspace{8.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    -2t^2+20t &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em} \\boxed{t = 10 \\text{ seconds}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n7. Given that the second cannonball follows the same projectile motion as the first cannonball, the equation is the same as $h(t)$, but with a vertical translation of 4 units down (the second cannonball is launched from a height of 4 metres instead of 8 metres). We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h_2(t) &= -2t^2 +20t + 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Where $h_2(t)$ is the height of the second cannonball above the ground, in metres, at time $t$ seconds.\n\n    Solving the equation $h_2(t) = 0$ for $t > 0$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -2t^2+20t+4 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    t &\\approx 10.20 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The second cannonball will take $10.2$ seconds to land, whereas the first cannonball will take $10.4$ seconds.\n\n    Therefore, the second cannonball was fired $\\hspace{0.25em}\\boxed{0.2 \\text{ seconds later}}$."},{"ref":"AA780","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA780\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = -x^2+2x+3$. The graph of $f$ is shown in the following\ndiagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/9b151d369be7a9d5947f25e37a1d88fcf6e38455.svg\" alt=\"9b151d369be7a9d5947f25e37a1d88fcf6e38455.svg\">\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int (-x^2+2x+3) \\,\\mathrm{d}x$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Integrating term by term, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\displaystyle \\int (-x^2+2x+3) \\,\\mathrm{d}x & = -\\dfrac {x^3} 3 + \\dfrac {2x^2} 2 + 3x + C \\\\[12pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= -\\dfrac {x^3} 3 +x^2 + 3x + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_{1}^{2} (-x^2+2x+3) \\,\\textrm d x &=\\left(-\\dfrac{x^3} 3 +x^2+ 3x\\right)\\Bigg|_{1}^{2}  \\\\[12pt]\n    & = \\left[-\\dfrac{(2)^3} 3 + (2)^2+3(2)\\right]-\\left[-\\dfrac{(1)^3} 3 + (1)^2 +3(1)\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{22}{3} - \\dfrac{11}{3} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}=\\dfrac{11}{3}\n    %&\\hspace{.275em} = 32\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA785","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA785\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe values of the functions $f$ and $g$ and their derivatives for\n$x = 2$, $x=3$ and $x = 6$ are shown in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/d0a6640466a6441c708713c23e21d5cb05c7fd52.svg\" alt=\"d0a6640466a6441c708713c23e21d5cb05c7fd52.svg\">\n\n1.  Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} g''(x)  \\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Let $k(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}$. Find $k'(2)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Let $h(x) = f(g(x))$. Find the equation of the tangent to $h$ at\n    $x=6$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} g''(x)  \\,\\mathrm{d}x &= g'(x) \\Big|_2^3\\hspace{3.5cm}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= g'(3)-g'(2)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2-0\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.5em}=2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the quotient rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k'(2) &=    \\dfrac{g(2)f'(2)-f(2)g'(2)}{[g(2)]^2} \\hspace{2em}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{(-3)(-2)-(2)(0)}{(-3)^2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  First, we find the gradient of the tangent to $h$ at $x = 6$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m   & = h'(6) \\hspace{8em}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= f'(g(6))\\cdot g'(6)\\\\[12pt]\n    &= f'(3)\\cdot(-1)\\\\[12pt]\n    &= -7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finding the $y$-coordinate on $h$ at\n    $x=6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= h(6) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(g(6)) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(3)\\\\[12pt]\n    & = 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the point-gradient formula with\n    $\\text{P}(6,8)$ and $m=-7$, we get Hence, finding the equation of\n    the tangent to $h$ at $x=6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    %y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\hspace{7em}\\\\[8pt]\n    y-8 &= -7(x-6)\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}y = -7x+50\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA789","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA789\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph of a function $f$ passes through the point $(\\ln 2,15)$.\\\nGiven that $f'(x)=9e^{3x}$, find $f(x)$.","markScheme":"An expression for $f(x)$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)& = \\int f'(x)  \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[10pt]\n& = \\int9e^{3x}  \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[10pt]\n& =  \\dfrac{9}{3}e^{3x} +C \\\\[12pt]\n& =  3\\hspace{0.05em}e^{3x} +C \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nas the graph of $f$\npasses through the point $(\\ln 2,15)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 &= 3e^{3(\\ln2)}+C \\\\[12pt]\n15 &= 3e^{\\ln \\left(2^3\\right)}+C \\\\[12pt]\n15 &= 3e^{\\ln 8}+C \\\\[12pt]\n15&=3(8)+C\\\\[12pt]\nC&=-9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\hspace{-1.95em}f(x)=3e^{3x}-9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA816","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA816\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1.  Show that $3\\log_{a^3}x = \\log_a x$ where $a$, $x\\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n    [2 marks]\n\nIt is given that\n$\\log_2 y + \\log_8 4x^2 + \\log_8 2x = 0$.\n\n2.  Express $y$ in terms of $x$. Give your answer in the form\n    $y=bx^c$ where $b$, $c$ are constants.[6 marks]\n\nThe region $R$, is bounded by the graph of the function found in part\n(b), the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=1$ and $x=k$ where $k>1$. The area\nof $R$ is $\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\n3.  Find the value of $k$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3\\log_{a^3}x &= 3 \\, \\dfrac{\\log_a x}{\\log_a a^3} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\cancel{3} \\,\\dfrac{\\log_a x}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &=\\log_a x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms and part (a), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_2 y + \\log_8 4x^2 + \\log_8 2x  &= 0  \\\\[8pt]\n    \\log_2 y + \\log_8 8x^3 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\log_2 y + 3 \\log_8 2x &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\log_2 y +  \\log_2 2x &= 0  \\\\[8pt]\n    \\log_2 y &= -\\log_2 2x   \\\\[8pt]\n    \\log_2 y &= \\log_2 (2x)^{-1}   \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  First we find the area of $R$ using integration, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Area of }R &= \\int_1^k \\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{0.05em}x^{-1} \\, \\mathrm{d} x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln x \\right]_1^k \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln k \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2}&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln k \\\\[8pt]\n    \\ln k &= 3 \\\\[8pt]\n    & \\hspace{-0.5em} k = e^3  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA817","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA817\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nGiven that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^{e^k} \\dfrac{2}{1+2x}\\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\ln 3$, find\nthe value of $k$.","markScheme":"Using integration by inspection or substitution, we have\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\int_0^{e^k} \\dfrac{2}{1+2x}\\, \\mathrm{d}x &= \\big[ \\ln ( 1+ 2x)\\big]_0^{e^k} \\\\[12pt]\n &= \\ln \\left(1+2e^k\\right) - \\ln (1) \\\\[12pt]\n &= \\ln \\left(1+2e^k\\right)\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n Hence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln \\left(1+2e^k\\right) &= \\ln 3 \\hspace{5.6em}\\\\[12pt]\n1+2e^k &= 3 \\\\[12pt]\n2e^k &= 2 \\\\[12pt]\ne^k &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.525em}k = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA825","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA825\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider a function with domain $a< x < b$. The following diagram shows\nthe graph of $f'$, the derivative of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/ffaa11a4b19671c0de5905aef42c6954ba3def71.svg\" alt=\"ffaa11a4b19671c0de5905aef42c6954ba3def71.svg\">\n\nFrom the graph above $p$, $0$ and $s$ are $x$-intercepts of $f'$, and\nthere is a local minimum at $x=q$ and a local maximum at\n$x=r$.\n\n1.  Find all the values of $x$ where the graph of $f$ is increasing.\n    Justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $x$ where the graph of $f$ has a local minimum.\n    Justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $x$ where the graph of $f$ has a local maximum.\n    Justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Find the values of $x$ where the graph of $f$ has points of\n    inflexion. Justify your answer.[4 marks]\n\nThe total area of the region enclosed by the graph of $f'$ and the\n$x$-axis between $x=p$ and $x=s$ is $25$.\n\n5.  Given that $f(p)+f(s)=13$, find the value of\n    $f(0)$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  $f(x)$ is increasing when $f'(x)>0$. Therefore\n    $f \\text{ is increasing on interval } (0,s) $\n\n2.  $f(x)$ has a local minimum when the function changes from decreasing\n    to increasing.\n\n    Therefore,\n    $f \\text{ has a local minimum at } x=0 $\n\n3.  $f(x)$ has a local maximum when the function changes from increasing\n    to decreasing.\n\n    Therefore,\n    $f \\text{ has a local maximum at } x=s $\n\n4.  The graph of $f$ has an inflection point at $x$ if $f''(x)=0$ and\n    $f'$ changes from increasing to decreasing or vice-versa.\n\n    Therefore,\n    $f \\text{ has inflection points at } x=p, x=q \\text{ and } x=r $\n\n5.  Using the formula for the total area enclosed by a function and the\n    $x$-axis, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Total area} &= \\int_p^s |f'(x)| \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\int_p^0 -f'(x) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\int_0^s f'(x) \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -f(x)\\Big|_{p}^{0} + f(x)\\Big|_{0}^{s}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    So, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Total area} &= -f(0) + f(p) + f(s) - f(0) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -2f(0) + f(p) + f(s)\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using that the total\n    area is $25$ and $f(p)+f(s)=13$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -2f(0) + f(p) + f(s) &= 25 \\\\[12pt]\n    -2f(0) + 13  &= 25 \\\\[12pt]\n    -2f(0) &= 12 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.88em}f(0) = -6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA873","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA873\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider the function $f$ defined by $f(x) = 2\\ln (24-1.5x)$ for\n$x < 16$.\n\nThe line $L_1: y = x$ intersects the graph of $f$ at point P.\n\nThe line $L_2$ is perpendicular to $L_1$ and tangent to the graph of $f$\nat point Q.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/44437a6d91aede9e10da2b2d5464a439b2f48901.svg\" alt=\"44437a6d91aede9e10da2b2d5464a439b2f48901.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the $x$-coordinate of point P, to three significant figures.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the exact coordinates of point Q.\n\n    2.  Show that the equation of $L_2$ is $y = -x + 2\\ln 3 + 14$.\n        [6 marks]\n\nThe shaded region $A$, as shown in the previous diagram, is enclosed by\nthe graph of $f$, the line $L_1$ and the line $L_2$\n\n3.\n    1.  Find the exact $x$-coordinate of the point where $L_2$\n        intersects $L_1$.\n\n    2.  Hence, find the area of $A$, to two decimal places. [5 marks]\n\nThe line $L_2$ is also tangent to the graph of the inverse function\n$f^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Integration%20Fundamentals%20and%20Areas/images/7e7a6456e0689f23b9b11eaa2d64983fc0f6ebb9.svg\" alt=\"7e7a6456e0689f23b9b11eaa2d64983fc0f6ebb9.svg\">\n\n4.  Find the shaded area enclosed by the graphs of $f$,\n    $f^{-1}$ and the line $L_2$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $y = f(x)$ with $y = x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= x \\\\[8pt]\n    \\hspace{5em} 2\\ln (24-1.5x) &= x \\\\[8pt]\n    x &\\approx 5.51 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the $x$-coordinate of point P is\n     $5.51$\n\n2.\n    1.  The gradient of the line $L_1$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        m_{L_1} &= 1 \\hspace{4.25em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Since the line\n        $L_2$ is perpendicular to the line $L_1$, we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        m_{L_2} &= -\\dfrac{1}{m_{L_1}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{1}{1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -1 \\hspace{3.25em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Since the line\n        $L_2$ is tangent to the graph of $f$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{6.75em} f'(x) &= -1 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{2(-1.5)}{24-1.5x} &= -1 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{-3}{24-1.5x} &= -1 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= 14 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Substituting $x = 14$ in the equation $y = f(x)$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.25em} y &= 2\\ln (24 - 1.5(14)) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2\\ln 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Thus, the coordinates of point Q are\n         $(14, 2\\ln 3)$\n\n    2.  Using the equation of a line in the form $y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$\n        with $(x_1,y_1) = (14, 2\\ln 3)$ and $m = -1$, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{7em} y - 2\\ln 3 &= -1(x-14) \\\\[6pt]\n        y - 2\\ln 3 &= -x+14 \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}y = -x + 2\\ln 3 + 14\\hspace{3em} [\\text{Shown}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  Substituting $y = x$ in the equation of the line $L_2$, we\n        obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.5em} x &= -x + 2\\ln 3 + 14 \\\\[8pt]\n        2x &= 2\\ln 3 + 14 \\\\[8pt]\n        x &= \\ln 3 + 7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, $L_1$ and $L_2$ intersect at\n         $x=\\ln 3 + 7$\n\n    2.  Hence, the area of $A$ is defined by\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        A &= \\displaystyle \\int_{5.51}^{\\ln 3 + 7} \\left[x - f(x) \\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ \\displaystyle \\int_{\\ln 3 + 7}^{14} \\left[(-x +2\\ln 3 + 14) - f(x) \\right] \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n        %     &= \\displaystyle \\int_{5.51}^{8.10} \\left[x - 2\\ln (24-1.5x)\\right] \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[1pt]\n        %     & \\hspace{1em}+ \\displaystyle \\int_{8.10}^{14} \\left[(-x +2\\ln 3 + 14) - 2\\ln (24-1.5x)\\right] \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n        &\\approx 4.05 + 7.50  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[10pt]\n        &\\approx 11.55 \\text{ unit}^2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  Since the graphs of $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are symmetric about the line\n    $y = x$, the line splits the shaded region into two equal areas, one\n    of which is $A$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\text{shaded region}} &= 2A \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2(11.55) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 23.1 \\text{ unit}^2 \\hspace{7.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Kinematics II- Integration and Mixed","questions":[{"ref":"AA1043","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1043\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/q1043.png\" alt=\"q1043\">\n\nA boat is moored to a wooden pier. The boat travels up and down as the tide rises and falls.\n\nThe height of the boat above the sea floor is modelled by the following function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    h(t)=30+10\\cos{\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}t-1\\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $t$ is measured in hours and $t\\geq0$.\n\n1.  Find the initial height, velocity and acceleration of the boat. [3 marks]\n\nAfter $k$ hours the boat has travelled up and down a total distance of  $60\\,$metres.\n\n2.  Find $k$. [2 marks]\n\nThere is an interval of time,  $a\\leq t \\leq b$ within the first $8$ hours, for which the speed of the boat is increasing.\n\n3.  Find $a$ and $b$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula we recall\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        v(t)&=h'(t) &a(t)=h''(t)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, we can use a G.D.C. to sketch $h$, $v$ and $a$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/a1043-1.png\" alt=\"a1043-1\">\n\n    Evaluating each function for $t=0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        h(0)=35.4\\, m \\hspace{2em} v(0)=4.21\\, ms^{-1} \\hspace{2em} a(0)=-1.35\\, ms^{-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the total distance travelled we can write the following equation.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int_{0}^{k} |v(t)|dt&=60 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[4pt]\n        k&=18.8495...\\hspace{2em}\\text{[by using G.D.C.]}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{18.8\\,\\text{hours}}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If the velocity and the acceleration have the same sign then the speed of the boat is increasing.\n\n    Here is a graph showing the first $8$ hours.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/a1043-2.png\" alt=\"a1043-2\">\n\n    The blue dashed line shows the only region in which the acceleration and velocity have the same sign, i.e. they are both negative.\n\n    Using the G.D.C. we can identify $a$, this is when $v(t)=0$ and $b$, when $a(t)=0$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Therefore the speed increases in the following region\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2<t<5.14 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1056","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1056\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe equation of the movement of a bouncing ball is given by the equation\n$$\nh(t) = \\left\\lvert 3e^{-t}\\cos\\left(\\pi t + \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right\\rvert.\n$$\nThe graph of $h$ is shown on the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/Screenshot%202024-03-06%20at%208.03.02%20PM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-03-06 at 8.03.02 PM\">\n\n1. Find the first two times $t_1$ and $t_2$ at which the ball hits the ground. [2 marks]\n\n2. Determine an expression for $h(t)$ **without** modulus signs for $t_1<t<t_2$.[2 marks]\n\nLet $t_M$ be the time at which the maximum between $t_1$ and $t_2$ occurs.\n\n3. Using the G.D.C., find the value of $t_M$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the G.D.C., we can sketch the graph of $h$ and find the coordinates of the first two zeroes.\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/1056-1.png\" alt=\"1056-1\">\n\n    Therefore, $t_1 = 0.25$ and $t_2 = 1.25$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Let's remove the modulus signs from $h(t)$ and call this $g(t)$. Therefore\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(t) &= 3e^{-t}\\cos\\left(\\pi t + \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    On the G.D.C. we can compare $h(t)$ with our new function $g(t)$ for $t_1 < t < t_2$. We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/1056-2.png\" alt=\"1056-2\">\n\n    The parts of the graph in red and blue show where $g(t)$ and $h(t)$ are different.\n\n    The parts in green show where the graphs are the same.\n\n    Between $t_1$ and $t_2$, $h(t)$ is a reflection of $g(t)$ in the horizontal axis. This is the effect of the modulus signs.\n\n    That is, for $t_1 < t < t_2$, $h(t) = -g(t)$.\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(R1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\boxed{h(t) = -3e^{-t}\\cos\\left(\\pi t + \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\enskip\\,\\,  \\text{for}\\enskip\\,  t_1<t<t_2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Using the G.D.C.,\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/1056-3.png\" alt=\"1056-3\">\n\n    Hence, the maximum is obtained at $t_M = 0.652$ (3 s.f.)\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1166","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1166\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA particle is travelling along the $x-$axis.\n\nIts position in centimetres relative to the origin after $t$ seconds is given by the function $s(t)=2t^4-3t^3-t+1$. Where $t>0$.\n\nThe particle changes direction at $k\\,$seconds.\n\n1.  Find $k$. [2 marks]\n\nAt $t=1\\,$s an observer begins measuring the distance travelled by the particle.\n\nThey stop observing after $b\\,$seconds when the particle has travelled a further $3\\,$cm, where $b>1$.\n\n2.  Find $b$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Recall that the velocity is the rate of change of displacement. Therefore $v=s'(t)$.\n\n    We can use the G.D.C. to sketch the graph of $s(t)$ and $v=s'(t)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA1166_a%20%282%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1166_a (2)\">\n\n    At $t=0$ the particle is at $x=1$, then it moves to the left of the origin and stops. After this it moves to the right of the origin.\n\n    The particle changes direction when the velocity is zero and this can be found using the G.D.C. when $s'(t)=0$.\n\n    $\\text{The particle changes direction at }t=1.21\\,\\text{seconds.}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the distance travelled we know\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        D&=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}|v(t)|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The initial time is $t_1=1\\,$s, $D=3$ and $t_2=b$. Hence we need to solve the following equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        3&=\\int_{1}^{b}|s'(t)|\\,\\mathrm{d}t  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can also show this graphically\n\n    Solving for $b$ on the G.D.C. gives $\\hspace{0.525em}b=1.72\\,s$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Note: When solving on the G.D.C. you may need to specify that $b>1$."},{"ref":"AA1287","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1287\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA particle $P$ moves along a straight line such that its displacement, in metres, after $t$ seconds, from a fixed point $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    s(t)&=3e^{-\\left(t+1\\right)}\\sin{(4t+4)},\\hspace{1em} 0\\leq t \\leq 2\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n1.  Sketch the graph of the velocity of $P$ against $t$ on the axes below.[2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA1287.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1287\">\n\n$P$ reaches its maximum speed when $t=a\\,$seconds.\n\n2.  Find $a$.[1 mark]\n\n3.  Hence or otherwise, find the distance travelled whilst the acceleration of $P$ is negative.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Recall\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        v(t)&=s'(t)\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Hence, we can use the calculator to graph both $s(t)$ and $v(t)$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA1287a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1287a\">\n\n    $\\boxed{\\text{Correct endpoints}}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $\\boxed{\\text{Correct shape}}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  Recall speed$=|v(t)|$.\n\n    The maximum speed occurs when $|v(t)|$ is at a maximum.\n\n    In this case, the maximum speed is also the maximum positive value of $v(t)$, which we find using a calculator to be $(0.4483..., 2.819...)$. \n\n    Hence, the maximum speed is $2.82\\text{ m\\,s}^{-1}$ and this occurs at $t=0.448\\,\\text{s}$\n\n    Therefore $a=0.448$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3.  Recall\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, the acceleration of $P$ is negative when the gradient of the velocity function, $v(t)$, is negative.\n\n    From part (b) we know the local maximum occurs when $t=0.448$ seconds. \n\n    Using technology we find the local minimum point of the velocity function to be $(1.233...,-1.285...)$. Hence, the minimum occurs when $t=1.23$ seconds.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The period that the acceleration of $P$ is negative is shown in blue below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA1287b.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1287b\">\n\n    The distance travelled is calculated using the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{distance travelled}&=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}|v(t)|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can graph this function on the calculator\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA1287c.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1287c\">\n\n    Using the calculator\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{distance travelled}&=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}|v(t)|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=\\int_{0.4483...}^{1.233...}|v(t)|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[8pt]\n\n        &=1.049...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=1.05\\text{ m}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1341","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1341\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA particle moves in a straight line about a fixed point $O$.  The particle's velocity, $v$ m\\,s$^{-1}$, after $t$ seconds is given by $v(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}t\\right)^3+\\sin(3t)$ for $0\\leq t \\leq 3$.\n\n1.    Given that the initial displacement is $-\\frac{1}{6}$ metres find an expression for the displacement, $s$ m, of the particle at time $t$. [4 marks]\n\n2.    Hence determine the total time that the particle is moving toward the fixed point $O$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. To find an expression for the displacement $s(t)$ we need to integrate the velocity function.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    s(t)&=\\int\\left[\\left(\\frac{1}{2}t\\right)^3+\\sin(3t)\\right]\\mathrm{d}t\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Let's rewrite the integral and then use the inverse power rule and the integral of sine to integrate.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    s(t)&=\\int\\frac{1}{8}t^3\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int\\sin(3t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)t^4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos(3t)+C\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We know that the initial displacement is $-\\frac{1}{6}$ metres. This means that when $t=0$ then $s(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}$.\n\n    We can use this information to solve for $C$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    -\\frac{1}{6}&=\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}(0)^4\\right)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos(3(0))+C\\\\[6pt]\n\n    -\\frac{1}{6}&=-\\frac{1}{3}+C\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{1}{6}&=C\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence the expression for displacement at time $t$ seconds is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}s(t)=\\dfrac{1}{32}t^4-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(3t)+\\dfrac{1}{6}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Now that we have an expression for displacement, let's use our G.D.C. to graph it.\n\n    The particle is moving in a straight line in relation to the point $O$. This point is represented by the $x$-axis (i.e. when $y=0$).\n\n    When the graph is below the axis it is on one side of point $O$. When the graph is above the axis it is on the other side of point $O$.\n\n    Hence, for the function to be moving toward the $x$-axis the function needs to be negative and increasing or positive and decreasing.\n\n    The intervals where the function demonstrates this behavior are indicated on the diagram below.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA1341a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1341a1\">\n\n    The function is negative and increasing from $t=0$ until it crosses the $x$-axis.\n\n    Using the G.D.C. to find the first $x$-intercept we get $t=0.3485...$.\n\n    The function is positive and decreasing between the local maximum and local minimum shown above. Using the G.D.C. we find the local maximum occurs when $t=1.103...$ and the local minimum occurs when $t=1.813...$.\n\n    \n\n    Hence the total time moving toward the point $O$ can be written\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    T&=(0.3458...-0)+(1.813...-1.103...)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=1.058...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=1.06\\text{ s}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA184","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA184\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(t) = 3t^2 + 27$, where $t > 0$.\n\nThe graph of a function $g$ is obtained when the graph of $f$ is\ntransformed by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{a stretch by a scale of\\hspace{0.05em} $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ \\hspace{0.05em}parallel to the $y$-axis, followed by} \\\\\n&\\text{a horizontal translation of 4 and a vertical translation of -5}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Find $g(t)$, giving your answer in the form $a(t - b)^2 + c$.\n    [4 marks]\n\nA particle moves along a straight line so that its velocity in\nm s$^{-1}$, at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n$g(t)$.\n\n2.  Find the distance the particle travels between $t = 7$ and\n    $t = 10$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} g(t) &= \\dfrac{1}{9}f(t - 4) - 5  \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{9}\\big[3(t - 4)^2 + 27\\big] - 5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3}(t - 4)^2 -2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The distance the particle travels between $t = 7$ and $t = 10$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.8em} \\text{distance} &= \\int_7^{10} \\left|g(t)\\right| \\mathrm{d}t \\\\[2pt]\n    &= \\int_7^{10} \\left|\\dfrac{1}{3}(t - 4)^2 -2\\right| \\mathrm{d}t \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 15 \\text{ m}  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA490","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA490\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA particle can move along a straight line from a point P. The velocity\n$v$, in m s$^{-1}$, is given by the function\n$v(t) = 2 - e^{-\\sin t^3}$ where $t \\geq 0$ is measured in seconds.\n\n1.  Write down the first two times $t_1,t_2 > 0$ when the particle\n    changes direction.[2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the time $0 < t < t_2$ when the particle has a maximum\n        velocity.\n\n    2.  Find the time $0 < t < t_2$ when the particle has a minimum (most negative)\n        velocity. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the distance travelled by the particle between times $t = t_1$\n    and $t = t_2$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The particle change direction when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(t)    & =  0 \\hspace{8.4cm}\\\\[4pt]\n    2 - e^{-\\sin t^3} & =  0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{.275em}t  \\approx  1.58 \\text{ sec and } 1.77 \\text{ sec} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using G.D.C., we get\n           $t_\\text{velocity-max} \\approx 1.16\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{sec}.$\n    2.  Using G.D.C., we\n        get   $t_\\text{velocity-min} \\approx 1.68\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{sec}.$\n\n3.  The distance $d$ travelled by the particle is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d & =   \\left|\\int_{1.58}^{1.77} \\left[2 - e^{-\\sin t^3}\\right] \\textrm dt \\right| \\\\[12pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}\\approx  0.0877\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA492","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA492\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA particle moves in a straight line such that at time $t$ seconds\n($t \\geq 0$), its velocity is given by $v = 18t^3e^{-3t^2}$. Find the\nexact distance travelled by the particle in the first two seconds.","markScheme":"The distance $d$ travelled by the particle in the first two seconds is\ngiven by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd & =  \\int_0^2 v(t)\\,\\textrm dt \\\\[8pt]\n& =  18\\int_0^2 \\left[t^3e^{-3t^2}\\right]\\textrm dt \\quad \\quad \\quad \\Big[\\text{let $u = t^2$ and $\\textrm dv/\\textrm dt = te^{-3t^2}$}\\Big] \\\\[8pt]\n& =  18 \\left[-\\dfrac{1}{6}\\left(t^2e^{-3t^2}\\right)\\Big|_0^2  + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\int_0^2 te^{-3t^2}\\textrm dt \\right] \\\\[8pt]\n& =  18\\left[-\\dfrac{2}{3}e^{-12}  - \\dfrac{1}{18}\\left(e^{-3t^2}\\right)\\Big|_0^2 \\right] \\\\[8pt]\n& =  18\\left[-\\dfrac{2}{3}e^{-12}  - \\dfrac{1}{18}\\left(e^{-12} - 1\\right) \\right] \\\\[10pt]\n& =  -12e^{-12}  - \\left(e^{-12} - 1\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em}  =  1 -13e^{-12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA494","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA494\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA particle moves in a straight line such that its velocity,\n$v$ m s$^{-1}$, at time\n$t$ seconds, is given by\n$$\nv(t) = \\left\\{\\begin{array}{clc}\n6t - t^2, &\\text{for}& 0 \\leq t \\leq 5, \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2}(15 - t), &\\text{for}& t > 5.\n\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\n1.  Find the value of $t$, for $t>0$, when the particle is\n    instantaneously at rest. [2 marks]\n\nThe particle returns to its initial position at $t = T$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $T$. Give your answer correct to three\n    significant figures. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The particle is instantaneously at rest when\\\n    $v(t) = 0$ $\\Longrightarrow$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}(15 - t) = 0$\n    $\\Longrightarrow$\n    $\\hspace{.275em}t = 15\\hspace{0.25em}\\mbox s$\n\n2.  Let $d$ be the distance travelled before coming to rest. We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d   & =  \\int_0^5 (6t - t^2)\\,\\textrm dt + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_5^{15}(15-t)\\,\\textrm dt \\\\[8pt]\n    & \\approx  58.333\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Solving the following equation for $T$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{15}^{T}|(15 - t)|\\,\\textrm dt     & =  58.333\\hspace{6cm} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.2cm}T     \\approx  30.3\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{s}  \\qquad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA495","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA495\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nJohn drops a stone from the top of a cliff which is $h$ metres above sea\nlevel. The stone strikes the water surface after 9 seconds.\n\nThe velocity of the falling stone,\n$v$ m s$^{-1}$, $t$\nseconds after John releases it, can be modelled by the function\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\left\\{\\begin{array}{clc}\n9.8t, &\\text{for}& 0 \\leq t \\leq 9, \\\\[6pt]\n88.2e^{\\left(3-\\frac{t}{3}\\right)}, &\\text{for}& t > 9.\n\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\n1.  Find the velocity of the stone when $t = 12$, giving your answer to\n    the\\\n    nearest m s$^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the value of $h$, giving your answer to the nearest metre.\n    [2 marks]\n\nThe velocity of the stone when it reaches the bottom of sea is\n$10$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\n3.  Determine the depth of sea near the cliff, giving your\n    answer to the\\\n    nearest metre. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The velocity of the stone when $t = 12$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(12) & =  88.2e^{\\left(3-\\frac{12}{3}\\right)} \\\\[9pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{88.2}{e} \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} \\approx  32 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m\\hspace{0.1em}s}^{-1} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The height of the cliff above sea level is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h & =  \\int_0^9 9.8 t\\, \\textrm dt \\\\[8pt]\n    %& =  \\left(4.9\\,t^2\\right)\\big|_0^9 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} \\approx  397 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $v(t) = 10$ for $t$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    88.2e^{\\left(3-\\frac{t}{3}\\right)} & =  10 \\\\[9pt]\n    t & \\approx  15.531 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{s}. \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the depth of sea near the cliff is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d & =  88.2\\int_9^{15.531} e^{\\left(3-\\frac{t}{3}\\right)}\\textrm dt \\\\[8pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}\\approx  235 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA512","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA512\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA particle moves back and forth in a straight line. Its velocity\n$v$ m s$^{-1}$ at time\n$t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv = 3\\hspace{0.025em}t - \\dfrac{3}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}t^2, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 7.\n$$\n\nAt time $t = 0$, the displacement $s$ of the particle from the starting\npoint is $1$ m.\n\n1.  Find the displacement of the particle when $t = 5$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Sketch a displacement/time graph for the particle,\n    $0 \\leq t \\leq 7$, showing clearly where the curve meets the axes\n    and the coordinates of the points where the displacement takes the\n    greatest and least values. [5 marks]\n\nFor $t > 7$, the displacement of the particle is given by\n\n$$\ns = \\alpha + \\beta \\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{2\\pi t}{7}\\bigg)\n$$\nsuch that $s$\nis continuous for all $t \\geq 0$.\n\n3.  Given that $s = 9$ when $t = 10.5$, find the values of\n    $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the times $t_1$ and $t_2$ $(0 < t_1 < t_2 < 12)$ when\n    the particle returns to its starting point. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The displacement of the particle is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    s & =  \\int 3\\hspace{0.05em}t - \\dfrac{3}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}t^2\\, \\mathrm{d}t \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{3}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}t^2 - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}t^3 + c.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $t = 0$ and $s = 1$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    s & =  \\dfrac{3}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}t^2 - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}t^3 + c \\\\[3pt]\n    1 & =  \\dfrac{3}{2}(0)^2 - \\dfrac{1}{4}(0)^3 + c \\\\[10pt]\n    c & =  1.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, substituting $t = 5$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    s & =  \\dfrac{3}{2}\\cdot5^2 - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot5^3 + 1 \\\\[10pt]\n    & =  7.25 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Here is a sketch of displacement/time graph for the particle:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/c48bb76b8c3fc2aaf78a8fef30dce4bdfe8db748.svg\" alt=\"c48bb76b8c3fc2aaf78a8fef30dce4bdfe8db748.svg\">\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -11.25  & = \\alpha + \\beta \\cos 2\\pi  \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{for $t = 7$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    9       & = \\alpha + \\beta \\cos 3\\pi \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{for $t = 10.5$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\text{or} \\\\[6pt]\n    -11.25  & = \\alpha + \\beta \\\\[6pt]\n    9       & = \\alpha - \\beta\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Solving the system of equations given above for $\\alpha$ and\n    $\\beta$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\alpha = -1.125 \\ \\  \\mbox{and} \\ \\ \\beta = -10.125\n    $$\n\n4.  Solving the equation $s(t) = 1$ for $0 < t < 7$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 + \\dfrac{3}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}t^2 - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}t^3 & = 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}t^2 - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}t^3 & = 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}t & = 0 \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{since $t > 0$}] \\\\[8pt]\n    t & = 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving the equation $s(t) = 1$ for\n    $7 < t < 12$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -1.125 - 10.125 \\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{2\\pi t}{7}\\bigg)  & =  1 \\\\[5pt]\n    t & \\approx  8.99 \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence  $t_1 = 6\\hspace{0.25em}\\mbox s$\n      and\n     $t_2 \\approx 8.99\\hspace{0.25em}\\mbox s$"},{"ref":"AA529","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA529\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA particle moves along a straight line so that its velocity,\n$v$ ms$^{-1}$, after $t$ seconds is given by\n$v(t) = 1.5^t - 4.9$, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 6$.\n\n1.  Find when the particle is at rest. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the acceleration of the particle when $t = 3$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the total distance travelled by the particle. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The particle is at rest when $v(t) = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1.5^t - 4.9     & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n    & \\hspace{-1.5mm}t = 3.92\\,\\mbox{s}\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The acceleration is $a(t) = v'(t)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5cm}\n    a(3) & = v'(3)\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= 1.37\\,\\mbox{ms}^{-2}\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The total distance travelled by the particle is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5cm}\n    d   & =\\int_0^6|1.5^t - 4.9| \\,\\textrm dt\\\\[6pt]\n    %   & = \\left[\\dfrac {1.5^t} {\\ln1.5} - 4.9 t\\right] \\Bigg|_0^6\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= 15.4\\,\\mbox{m}\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA534","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA534\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe acceleration,\n$a$ m s$^{-2}$, of a\nparticle at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{3}{t} + 5\\cos 2t, \\hspace{0.4em}\\text{for}\\hspace{0.5em}t\\geq1.\n$$\n\nThe particle is at rest when $t=1$.\n\nFind the velocity of the particle when $t=4$.","markScheme":"The velocity is given by the integral\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t)    & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{3}{t} + 5\\cos 2t\\right)\\textrm d t\\\\[10pt]\n& = 3 \\ln t + \\dfrac 5 2 \\sin 2 t + C\\\\[10pt]\n0    & = 3 \\ln 1 + \\dfrac 5 2 \\sin 2 (1) + C \\qquad [\\mbox{as $v(1)=0$ was given}]\\\\[10pt]\n0    & = 3(0) + \\dfrac 5 2 \\sin (2) + C\\\\[10pt]\nC    & = -\\dfrac 5 2 \\sin (2)\\\\[10pt]\nv(t)    & = 3 \\ln t + \\dfrac 5 2 \\sin 2 t - \\dfrac 5 2 \\sin (2)\\\\[10pt]\nv(4)    & = 3 \\ln 4 + \\dfrac 5 2 \\sin 2 (4) - \\dfrac 5 2 \\sin (2)\\\\[14pt]\n& \\hspace{.275em}  = 4.36\\,\\mbox{ms}^{-1}\\qquad\\quad[\\mbox{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA552","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA552\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA particle travels with velocity $v$ ms$^{-1}$ for $9$ seconds. This is\nshown in the graph below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/cf002adef4ecbdd36c9bfaf67f9b2bb8d9bd2905.svg\" alt=\"cf002adef4ecbdd36c9bfaf67f9b2bb8d9bd2905.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the car's velocity at $t = 4$.\n\n2.  Find the car's acceleration at $t = 2$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the total distance travelled. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $v(4) = 6 \\mbox{ m/s}$\n\n2.  The car's acceleration at $t=2$ is equal to the slope, $m$, of\n    $v(t)$ at $t=2$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m   & = \\dfrac 6 3\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 2\\,\\mbox{m/s}^2\\hspace{1cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The total distance, $d$, is equal to the area under the curve\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2cm}\n    d   & = \\dfrac 1 2 (6)(3) + 2(6) + \\dfrac 1 2 (4)(6)\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 33\\,\\mbox{m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA561","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA561\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA particle moves along a straight line such that its velocity, $v$,\nms$^{-1}$, is given by $v(t) = 5 t e^{-1.2t}$, for $t \\geq 0$.\n\n1.  On the grid below, sketch the graph of $v$, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 3$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/9e220f7900c7ae0744aee17bcbc0834aa0ea52fb.svg\" alt=\"9e220f7900c7ae0744aee17bcbc0834aa0ea52fb.svg\">\n\n2.  Find the distance travelled by the particle in the first $3$\n    seconds.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the maximum velocity of the particle in the first $3$ seconds.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have the following sketch.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/7bc45070b48f8dedd825164c932e2bb4061749da.svg\" alt=\"7bc45070b48f8dedd825164c932e2bb4061749da.svg\">\n\n2.  The distance travelled by the particle is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d       & = \\int_0^3 |5 t e^{-1.2t}|\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 3.04\\,\\mbox{m}\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The maximum velocity occurs when $t = 0.833$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    & \\hspace{-1.2cm}v(0.833) = 1.53\\,\\mbox{ms}^{-1}\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA577","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA577\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA particle $P$ moves along a straight line so that its velocity, $v$\nms$^{-1}$, after $t$ seconds, is given by $v = \\sin 3 t - 2 \\cos t -2$,\nfor $0 \\leq t \\leq 6$. The initial displacement of $P$ from a fixed\npoint $O$ is $5$ metres.\n\n1.  Find the displacement of $P$ from $O$ after $6$ seconds.[5 marks]\n\nThe following sketch shows the graph of $v$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/ad441389a1006d49da4e5d171d3673ad636a23b9.svg\" alt=\"ad441389a1006d49da4e5d171d3673ad636a23b9.svg\">\n\n2.  Find when the particle is first at rest.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the number of times the particle changes\n    direction.[2 marks]\n\n4.  Find the acceleration of $P$ after $2$ seconds.[2 marks]\n\n5.  Find the maximum speed of $P$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  the displacement form $O$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4mm}\n    d       & = 5 + \\int_0^6 (\\sin 3 t - 2 \\cos t -2)\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 5 - 11.3\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = -6.30\\,\\mbox m\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The particle is at rest when $v(t) = 0$ hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{3cm} t = 2.32\\,\\mbox s\n    \\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\hspace{2.4cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The particle changes direction when $v(t) = 0$, hence\n      $\\mbox{two times}$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(t)    & = v'(t)\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 3\\cos 3 t + 2 \\sin t \\\\[6pt]\n    a(2)    & = 3 \\cos 3(2) + 2 \\sin 2\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 4.70\\,\\mbox{ms}^{-2}\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  The maximum speed occurs when $v'(t) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3\\cos 3 t + 2 \\sin t    & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    t               & = 5.85\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.25cm} |v(5.85)|     = 4.78\\,\\mbox{ms}^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA580","marks":"15","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA580\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA particle $P$ starts from a point $O$ and moves along a horizontal\nstraight line. Its velocity $v$ ms$^{-1}$ after $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) =\n\\begin{cases}\n3t + 1, &\\textrm {for } 0 \\leq t \\leq 1\\\\\n2 \\sqrt t - \\dfrac 1 {t^2} + 3,& \\textrm {for } 1 \\leq t \\leq 8\n\\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $v$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/81dd5c33bf6112288e7fb373e8824b738373898c.svg\" alt=\"81dd5c33bf6112288e7fb373e8824b738373898c.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the initial velocity of particle $P$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the acceleration of the particle in the first\n    second.[3 marks]\n\n3.  How many times does the particle change direction in the first $8$\n    seconds. Explain your answer. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the total distance travelled by the particle in the first $8$\n    seconds.[6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Substituting $t=0$ into $v(t)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(0)        & = 3(0) + 1\\hspace{2cm}\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}= 1\\,\\mbox {ms}^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Acceleration is the derivative of velocity.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(t)        & = v'(t)\\hspace{2.2cm}\\\\[5pt]\n    & = 3\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{-6.5mm}a(0\\rightarrow1) = 3\\,\\mbox{ms}^{-2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The particle changes direction when $v(t) = 0$. From the diagram we\n    see\n\n    that $v(t)$ does not intercept the $x$-axis, hence\n      $\\mbox{no change in direction}$\n\n4.  The total distance is given by the sum of integrals\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4cm}\n    d       & = \\int_0^1 (3t + 1)\\,\\textrm dt\n    + \\int_1^8 \\left(2 \\sqrt t - \\dfrac 1 {t^2} + 3\\right)\\textrm d t\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 51.5\\,\\mbox m\n    \\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA584","marks":"13","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA584\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA particle moves in a straight line and its velocity, $v$ ms$^{-1}$, at\ntime $t$ seconds, is given by $v(t) = (t^2 - 2)^2$, for\n$0 \\leq t \\leq 2$.\n\n1.  Find the initial velocity of the particle.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $t$ for which the particle is at rest. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the total distance travelled by the particle in the first $2$\n    seconds.[3 marks]\n\n4.  Show that the acceleration of the particle is given by\n    $a(t) = 4 t^3 - 8t$. [3 marks]\n\n5.  Find the values of $t$ for which the velocity is positive and the\n    acceleration is negative. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(0)        & = (0^2 - 2)^2\\hspace{2.4cm}\\\\[5pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} = 4 \\,\\mbox{ms}^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The particle is at rest when $v(t) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (t^2 - 2)^2 & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    &  \\hspace{-1mm} t = \\sqrt 2 \\,\\mbox s \\hspace{0.2cm}$or$\\hspace{0.2cm} 1.41 \\,\\mbox s\n    \\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The distance is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1cm}\n    d   & = \\int_0^2|(t^2 - 2)^2|\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} = 3.73   \\,\\mbox m\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(t)        & = v'(t)\\\\[5pt]\n    & = 2(t^2 - 2)(2t)\\hspace{1.9cm}\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}= 4t^3 - 8t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Using G.D.C. we find   $0 < t < 1.41$"},{"ref":"AA592","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA592\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nA particle P moves along a straight line so that its velocity, $v$,\nms$^{-1}$, after $t$ seconds, is given by\n$v = 2 \\sin t - \\cos 5 t + 0.1$, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 4$. The initial\ndisplacement of P from a fixed point O is $2$ metres.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/9ef1e30cd19bb1c81d67684bb5cdd6acbc3c0de8.svg\" alt=\"9ef1e30cd19bb1c81d67684bb5cdd6acbc3c0de8.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the displacement of P from O after $4$ seconds. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Find the second time for $t$, when the particle is at rest.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the number of times P changes direction. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Write down the number of times P is neither accelerating or\n    decelerating. [2 marks]\n\n5.  Find the maximum distance of P from O during the time\n    $0\\leq t \\leq 4$ and justify your answer. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The displacement of P from O is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5cm}\n    s   & = 2 + \\int_0^4 v(t)\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2 + \\int_0^4(2 \\sin t - \\cos 5 t + 0.1)\\,\\textrm dt\\\\[6pt]\n    & =     2 + 3.52\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}= 5.52\\,\\mbox{m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The particle at rest when $v(t) = 0$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2 \\sin t - \\cos 5 t + 0.1   & = 0\\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1mm}t = 3.38\\,\\mbox{s}\n    \\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\hspace{1.8cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The particle changes direction, when $v(t)$ changes its sign\n    (crosses the $t$-axis).\n\n    From the diagram we see that the particle changes direction\n      $\\mbox{two times}$\n\n4.  The particle is neither accelerating / decelerating when\n    $a(t) = v'(t)= 0$.\n\n    From the diagram we see that it happens\n      $6\\mbox{ times}$\n\n5.  The displacement is given by the integral\n    $\\displaystyle \\int_0^t v(t)\\,\\textrm dt$.\\\n    From the diagram, we see that this integral is at a maxiumum when\n    $v(t)$\\\n    crosses the $t$-axis for the second time, at $t = 3.38$ s (part\n    (b)). We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d_{\\max}        & = 2 + \\int_0^{3.38} v(t)\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 2 + \\int_0^{3.38}(2 \\sin t - \\cos 5 t + 0.1)\\,\\textrm dt\\\\[6pt]\n    & =  2 + 4.47\\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}= 6.47\\,\\mbox{m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA594","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA594\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\n**Note: In this question, distance is in metres and time is in\nseconds.**\n\nA particle P moves in a straight line for six seconds. Its acceleration\nduring this period is given by $a(t) = -2t^2 + 13 t - 15$, for\n$0 \\leq t \\leq 6$.\n\n1.  Write down the values of $t$ when the particle's acceleration is\n    zero.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence or otherwise, find all possible values of $t$ for which the\n    velocity of P is increasing. [2 marks]\n\nThe particle has an initial velocity of $7$ ms$^{-1}$.\n\n3.  Find an expression for the velocity of P at time\n    $t$.[6 marks]\n\n4.  Find the total distance travelled by P when its velocity is\n    decreasing. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -2t^2 + 13 t - 15   & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.5mm} t = 1.5,\\,5\\ \\mbox{sec}\n    \\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The velocity is increasing on   $1.5 < t < 5$\n\n3.  The velocity is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v(t)    & = \\int a(t)\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\int (-2t^2 + 13 t - 15)\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & = -\\dfrac {2 t^3} 3 + \\dfrac {13t^2} 2 - 15 t + C\\hspace{.5cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Since $v(0) = 7$ we have $C = 7$.\\\n    Hence, we get\n      $v(t) = -\\dfrac {2 t^3} 3 + \\dfrac {13t^2} 2 - 15 t + 7$\n\n4.  From (b) we know that the velocity is decreasing on $0 < t < 1.5$\n    and $5 < t < 6$. Hence the distance is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.3cm}\n    d       & = \\int_0^{1.5} |v(t)|\\,\\textrm d t +  \\int_5^{6} |v(t)|\\,\\textrm d t\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 13.6\\,\\mbox{m} \\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA660","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA660\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n**In this question distance is in centimetres and time is in seconds.**\n\nParticle X is moving along a straight line such that its displacement\nfrom a point A, after $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$s_{\\text{X}} = 24-t-5t^3e^{-0.6t}$  , $\\hspace{3em} 0 \\leq t \\leq 30$.\n\nThis is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/57dc605df0f876326f48c9f31184664f7f576253.svg\" alt=\"57dc605df0f876326f48c9f31184664f7f576253.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $t$ when particle X first changes direction.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total distance travelled by particle X in the first $3.5$\n    seconds. [2 marks]\n\nAnother particle, Y, moves along the same line, starting at the same\ntime as particle X. The velocity of\nparticle Y is given by $v_{\\text{Y}} = 5 - t$,\n $0 \\leq t \\leq 30$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Given that particle X and Y start at the same\n        point, find the displacement\n        function $s_{\\text{Y}}$ for particle Y.\n\n    2.  Find the other value of $t$ when particles X and Y\n        meet.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Solving the equation $s'_{\\text{X}} = 0$ for $t$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\big[24-t-5t^3e^{-0.6t}\\big] &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\hspace{4.6em} -1 - 15t^2e^{-0.6t} + 3t^3e^{-0.6t} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by derivative rules}] \\\\[10pt]\n    t &\\approx 5.28,\\hspace{0.25em} 14.5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find\n     $t\\approx5.28 \\text{ seconds}$\n\n2.  Evaluating $s_{\\text{X}}$ for $t=0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.4em} s_{\\text{X}}(0) &= 24-0-5(0)^3e^{-0.6(0)} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 24\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $s_{\\text{X}}$ for $t=3.5$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} s_{\\text{X}}(3.5) &= 24-3.5-5(3.5)^3e^{-0.6(3.5)} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx -5.75\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{total distance} &= 24+5.75 \\hspace{9.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 29.8 \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.  Integrating $v_{\\text{Y}}$ with respect to $t$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{8.6em} s_{\\text{Y}} &= \\int v_{\\text{Y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\int (5-t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\[5pt]\n        &= 5t - \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2 + C \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for some $C \\in \\mathbb{R}$}] \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Solving the equation $s_{\\text{X}}(0) = s_{\\text{Y}}(0)$ for\n        $C$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.2em} 24 &= 5(0) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(0)^2 + C \\\\[8pt]\n        24 &= C\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we obtain\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        s_{\\text{Y}} = 5t - \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2 + 24 \\hspace{1.5em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Solving the equation $s_{\\text{X}} = s_{\\text{Y}}$ for $t > 0$,\n        we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cancel{24} -t-5t^3e^{-0.6t} &= 5t - \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2 + \\cancel{24} \\hspace{9.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2 - 6t - 5t^3e^{-0.6t} &= 0 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{9.9em} t \\approx 12.8 \\text{ seconds} \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA823","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA823\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nA particle moves along the $x$-axis with a velocity, $v$ ms$^{-1}$, at\ntime $t$ seconds given by the function\n\n$v(t)=3+8t-3t^2 \\hspace{2em}$\n\nFor $0\\leq t \\leq 4$. The particle is initially at the origin.\n\n1.  Find the value of $t$ when the particle reaches its maximum\n    velocity.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Sketch a graph of $v$ against $t$ showing any points of intersection\n    with the axes.[5 marks]\n\n3.  Find the distance of the particle from the origin after 3 seconds.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The particle reaches its maximum velocity when $v'(t) = 0$. So, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v'(t) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    8-6t &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    t &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since $v''(t)=-6$ we conclude\n    that $v_{max}$ is at\n    $t=\\dfrac{4}{3}$\n\n2.  We get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/4314915e54d9507109b4e530e22327467bbd38e0.svg\" alt=\"4314915e54d9507109b4e530e22327467bbd38e0.svg\">\n\n3.  We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{dist} &= \\int_0^{3}v(t) \\, \\mathrm{d}t \\\\[10pt]\n    &=\\int_0^{3}  (3+8t-3t^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}t\\\\[10pt]\n    &=3 t + 4 t^2 - t^3 \\Big|_{0}^{3}\\\\[10pt]\n    &=3\\left(3\\right)+4\\left(3\\right)^2-\\left(3\\right)^3\\\\[10pt]\n    &=9+36-27\\\\[10pt]\n    &=18 \\hspace{0.2em}\\text{m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA942","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA942\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nA particle moves along the $x$-axis with a velocity, $v\\,\\text{ms}^{-1}\\,$, at time $t$ seconds given by the function\n\n$v(t) = 2 + 7t -4t^2$\n\nFor $0 \\leq t \\leq 3$. The particle is initially at the origin.\n\n1. Find the value of $t$ when the particle reaches its maximum velocity. [3 marks]\n\n2. Sketch a graph of $v$ against $t$ showing any points of intersection with the axes. [5 marks]\n\n3. Find the displacement of the particle from the origin after 2 seconds. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. The particle reaches its maximum velocity when $v'(t)=0$. So, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    v'(t) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n\n    7 - 8t &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n\n    t &= \\dfrac{7}{8}\\\\[-9pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Since $v''(t) = -8$ we conclude that $v_{max}$ is at $\\hspace{0.25em}t = \\dfrac{7}{8}$\n\n2. We get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA942a.PNG\" alt=\"AA942a\">\n\n3. We get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{displacement} &= \\int_0^{2}v(t) \\, \\mathrm{d}t \\\\[8pt]\n\n     &=\\int_0^{2}  (2+7t-4t^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}t\\\\[8pt]\n\n     &= 2t + \\dfrac{7t^2}{2} - \\dfrac{4t^3}{3} \\Bigg|_{0}^{2}\\\\[8pt]\n\n     &=2\\left(2\\right)+\\dfrac{7\\left(2\\right)^2}{2}-\\dfrac{4\\left(2\\right)^3}{3}\\\\[10pt]\n\n     &=4+14-\\dfrac{32}{3}\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{=\\dfrac{22}{3} \\hspace{0.2em}\\text{m}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA969","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA969\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nConsider a particle P, moving in a straight line, relative to an origin O.\n\nThe displacement, in metres, of the particle from the origin at time $t$ seconds is given by $s(t) = t \\sin 2t - 7 \\sin t \\cos t$, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 4$.\n\nThis is shown in the following diagram. Point A shows the particle's displacement after $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ seconds and point B shows the particle's displacement after $t=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ seconds.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/q.png\" alt=\"q\">\n\n1. Show that the displacement of P from O can be expressed as $\\left(\\sin 2t \\right)\\left(t - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right)$. [3 marks]\n\n2. Show that for $t>0$, the particle first passes through the origin\nat $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [4 marks]\n\n3. Find the velocity of the particle at this time. [4 marks]\n\n4. Find the total distance travelled by P between point A and point B. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the double angle identity for sine, $\\sin\\,2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    s(t) &= t \\sin 2t - 7 \\sin t \\cos t \\\\[8pt]\n    &= t \\sin 2t - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\left(2 \\sin t \\cos t\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= t \\sin 2t - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\sin 2t  \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.535em}\\boxed{s(t) = \\left(\\sin 2t \\right)\\left(t - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. If the particle is at the origin, $s(t)=0$. We must solve\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        s(t) &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n        \\left(\\sin 2t \\right)\\left(t - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore either $\\sin 2t=0$ or $t - \\dfrac{7}{2}=0$.\n\n    For $0 < t \\leq 4$, we have $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} , \\pi$ or $\\dfrac{7}{2}$.\n\n    $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < \\pi < \\dfrac{7}{2}$ therefore,\n\n    The first time the particle passes through the origin for $t>0$ is when $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n3. Given that $v = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t}$, we must find the value of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        v(t) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\bigg[\\left(\\sin 2t \\right)\\left(t - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right)\\bigg] \\\\[8pt]\n        v(t) &= 2\\cos 2t \\left(t - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) + \\sin 2t \\hspace{3em}\\text{[By the chain rule]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        v\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) &= 2\\cos \\left(2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right) \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) + \\sin \\left(2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 2\\cos \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi - 7}{2}\\right) + \\sin \\pi \\\\[8pt]\n        &= -2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi - 7}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2.7em}\\boxed{v\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 7 - \\pi}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4. Given that between point A and point B, $s'(t)>0$, the particle is moving in the same direction for all of this time. Hence the total distance travelled in this time is the difference in displacement between points A and B.\n\n    Given that at point A, $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$, and at point B, $t=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Total distance} &= s\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) - s\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\left(\\sin 2\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\sin 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) - \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ , and in general $\\sin(\\pi + \\theta) = -\\sin\\theta$ and $sin\\left(\\pi - \\theta\\right) = \\sin\\theta$.\n\n    Hence, $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Total distance} &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) - \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\left(-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{7}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{7}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        &\\hspace{-6.2em}\\boxed{\\text{Total distance} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(7 - \\pi)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA995","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA995\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nA particle moves in a straight line.\nIts displacement, $s$ centimetres, from the origin at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    s(t)=(3t-\\frac{1}{2}t^3)\\sin^2(2t) \\text{ for } 0 \\leq t \\leq \\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs shown on below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/q1.png\" alt=\"q1\">\n\n1. What is the furthest distance the particle is from the origin? [2 marks]\n\n2. For how many seconds is the particle further than $1\\,$cm from the origin? [3 marks]\n\n3. For what interval(s) of $t$ is the particle moving away from the origin? [3 marks]\n\n4. Find the particle's velocity when $t=1$. [1 mark]\n\n5. What is the maximum speed of the particle? [3 marks]\n\nThe particle arrives back to the origin for the first time at point $A$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/AA995a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA995a2\">\n\nIt takes the particle $a$ seconds to travel a further distance of $2.5\\,$cm.\n\n6. Find $a$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. The point shown below is the furthest from the origin. Using the G.D.C. we can find the $y$-coordinate.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/a1.png\" alt=\"a1\">\n\n    The furthest the particle gets from the origin is $2.22\\,\\text{cm}$\n\n2. The three regions shown below is the total time when the particle is over $1\\,$cm from the origin.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/a2.png\" alt=\"a2\">\n\n    Use your G.D.C. to find intersection points and sum the three times\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{T} &= (1.248...-0.496...)+(2.2522...-1.945...)+(2.8151...-2.676...)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1.1985...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The particle is further than $1$cm from the origin for $1.20\\,\\text{seconds}$.\n\n3. The following graph identifies the regions in which the particle is moving away from the origin.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/a3.png\" alt=\"a3\">\n\n    By using a G.D.C. the three regions can be found.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\boxed{0<x<0.883 \\text{ and }1.57<x<2.10 \\text{ and }2.45<x<2.75}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4. Recall $v(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t}$, use G.D.C. to plot the function $v(t)$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/a4.png\" alt=\"a4\">\n\n    Use your G.D.C. to resolve $v(1)=-2.54\\,\\text{cm}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$.\n\n5. Recall the formula $\\text{speed}=|v(t)|$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/a5.png\" alt=\"a5\">\n\n    The highest speed occurs at the point identified on the diagram above. The coordinates are $(2.44, 5.80)$.\n\n    Therefore the maximum speed of the particle is $5.80\\,\\text{cm}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$\n\n6. The formula for the distance travelled from time $t_1$ to $t_2$ is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        D=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}|v(t)|dt\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $t_1$ is the $x$-coordinate of $A$.\n\n    Using a G.D.C. the coordinates of $A$ can be found to be $(1.57..., 0)$.\n\n    This information is shown below on the graph of $y=|v(t)|$. The area under this graph is the distance travelled **from** point $A$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Kinematics%20II-%20Integration%20and%20Mixed/images/Screenshot%202023-09-16%20at%208.22.28%20AM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-09-16 at 8.22.28 AM\">\n\n    To find $t_2$ solve the following equation. Note, the function $|v(t)|$ is found using the G.D.C.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2.5&=\\int_{1.57...}^{t_2}|v(t)|dt\\\\[6pt]\n            t_2&=2.4431.... &\\text{[By using G.D.C.]}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    To find $a$ we must calculate the time **after** point $A$. Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        a&=2.4431....-1.57...\\\\[3pt]\n        a&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{0.872\\,\\text{s}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Maclaurin Series","questions":[{"ref":"AA1059","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1059\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin series for $\\ln(1+3x)\\sin{x}$.[3 marks]\n\n2. Hence find $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\ln(1+3x)\\sin{x}-3x^2}{x^3}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the formula booklet we can write down\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\ln(1+3x)\\sin{x}&=\\left(3x-\\tfrac{(3x)^2}{2}+\\tfrac{(3x)^3}{3}+...\\right)\\left(x-\\tfrac{x^3}{3!}+\\tfrac{x^5}{5!}-...\\right)\\\\\n\n    &=\\left(3x-\\tfrac{9x^2}{2}+9x^3+...\\right)\\left(x-\\tfrac{x^3}{3!}+\\tfrac{x^5}{5!}-...\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recognise that we need, up to and including, the $x^4$ terms\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(R1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=3x^2-\\tfrac{9x^3}{2}+9x^4-\\tfrac{3x^4}{3!}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{3x^2-\\tfrac{9x^3}{2}+\\tfrac{17x^4}{2}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using the result from part (a) we can rewrite the quotient\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\ln(1+3x)\\sin{x}-3x^2}{x^3}&=\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{3x^2-\\tfrac{9x^3}{2}+\\tfrac{17x^4}{2}-3x^2}{x^3} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{-\\tfrac{9x^3}{2}+\\tfrac{17x^4}{2}}{x^3}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-9}{2}+\\frac{17x}{2}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    All of the remaining terms of the expansion will evaluate to zero hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{-\\frac{9}{2}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1173","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1173\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nConsider the following differential equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=x-2xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is known that the point $(0, 2)$ lies on the solution curve.\n\n1.  1.  Show that $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}=1-2y-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n    2.  Hence write down the value of $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}$ at $x=0$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  1.  Find the values of $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^3y}{\\mathrm{d}x^3}$ and $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^4y}{\\mathrm{d}x^4}$ at $x=0$.\n\n    2.  Hence, find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin Series for the solution curve of the differential equation. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  We can use the product rule to differentiate\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}&=(x-2xy)' \\\\[6pt]\n        &=1-\\left(2y+2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1-2y-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    2.  We know from the question that when $x=0, y=2$. We can also see that $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=0$ when $x=0$. Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}\\bigg\\lvert_{x=0,y=2}&=1-2\\times 2- 2\\times 0 \\times 0\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  1.  To find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^3y}{\\mathrm{d}x^3}$ we can use the product rule to differentiate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^3y}{\\mathrm{d}x^3}&=\\left(1-2y-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\right)'\\\\[12pt]\n            &=-2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-\\left(2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n            &=-2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-4\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n           \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^3y}{\\mathrm{d}x^3}\\bigg\\lvert_{x=0,y=2} &=-4\\times 0 - 2 \\times 0 \\times -3 \\\\[6pt]\n           &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{0} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We use a similar method to find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^4y}{\\mathrm{d}x^4}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^4y}{\\mathrm{d}x^4}&=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^3y}{\\mathrm{d}x^3}\\right)'\\\\[12pt]\n           &=\\left(-4\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}\\right)'\\\\[12pt]\n           &=-4\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}-\\left(2\\frac{d^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}+2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^3y}{\\mathrm{d}x^3}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n           &=-6\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}-2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^3y}{\\mathrm{d}x^3}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n           &=-6\\times (-3)-2\\times 0 \\times 0\\\\[12pt]\n           &=18 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Here is a summary of what we have found in the previous part.\n\n        Assuming the solution curve is of the form $y=f(x)$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f(0)=2 \\hspace{2em} f'(0)=0 \\hspace{2em} f''(0)=-3 \\hspace{2em} f'''(0)=0 \\hspace{2em} f''''(0)=18\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now using the definition of the Maclaurin Series from the formula booklet we can write the first three non-zero terms\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f(x)&=2+x \\times 0 + \\frac{x^2}{2!}\\times (-3) + \\frac{x^3}{3!}\\times 0+ \\frac{x^4}{4!}\\times 18 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=2-\\frac{3}{2}x^2+\\frac{3}{4}x^4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1344","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1344\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=m+\\dfrac{\\sin{3x}}{x^3}+\\dfrac{n}{x^2}$ where, $m,n \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.    Show that a finite limit for $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}[f(x)]$ can only exist when $n=-3$. [3 marks]\n\n2.    Find the value of $m$ for which $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}[f(x)]=5$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. Let's begin by trying to find the limit of $f$ as $x \\rightarrow 0$ by direct substitution.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}[f(x)]&=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left[m+\\dfrac{\\sin{3x}}{x^3}+\\dfrac{n}{x^2}\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n    &=m+\\dfrac{\\sin{3(0)}}{(0)^3}+\\dfrac{n}{(0)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The limit is undefined as we are dividing by zero.\n\n    However, we may be able to use $\\text{L'H\\^opital's Rule}$.\n\n    Let's write $f$ as a single fraction.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)=\\frac{mx^3+\\sin{3x}+nx}{x^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now try to find the limit as $x\\rightarrow 0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}[f(x)]&=\\frac{m(0)^3+\\sin{3(0)}+n(0)}{(0)^3}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\frac{0}{0}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This is an indeterminate form. Therefore we can attempt to find a limit by using $\\text{L'H\\^opital's Rule}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(R1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Differentiating the numerator and denominator independently and evaluating the limit we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left[\\frac{3mx^2+3\\cos{3x}+n}{3x^2}\\right]\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{3m(0)^2+3\\cos{3(0)}+n}{3(0)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{3+n}{0}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Again, as we are dividing by zero, a finite limit can only exist if the fraction is in an indeterminate form.\n    Hence we can say that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{3+n}{0}&\\equiv\\frac{0}{0}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $n=-3$ ...as required.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. In this part, we must use the value of $n=-3$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(R1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using part of our previous answer we can say that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}[f(x)]&=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left[\\frac{3mx^2+3\\cos{3x}-3}{3x^2}\\right]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As shown previously, this leads to an indeterminate form hence we can use $\\text{L'H\\^opital's Rule}$ and evaluate the limit to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}[f(x)]&=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left[\\frac{6mx-9\\sin{3x}}{6x}\\right]\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{6m(0)-9\\sin{3(0)}}{6(0)}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{0}{0}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Once again we use \\text{L'H\\^opital's Rule} and evaluate the limit.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}[f(x)]&=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left[\\frac{6m-27\\cos{3x}}{6}\\right]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{6m-27\\cos{3(0)}}{6}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{6m-27}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now use the information given in the question to form an equation which we can solve to find $m$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{6m-27}{6}&=5\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    m&=9.5\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA596","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA596\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = e^{\\arctan x}$.\n\n1.  Find the first two derivatives of $f(x)$ and hence find the\n    Maclaurin series for $f(x)$ up to and including the $x^2$ term.\n    [6 marks]\n\n2.  Show that the coefficient of $x^3$ in the Maclaurin series for\n    $f(x)$ is $-\\dfrac 1 6$. [6 marks]\n\n3.  Using the Maclaurin series for $\\sin x$ and $\\ln(2x + 1)$, find the\n    Maclaurin series for $\\sin(\\ln(2x + 1))$ up to and including the\n    $x^3$ term. [6 marks]\n\n4.  Hence, or otherwise, find\n    $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\, \\dfrac {f(x) - 1} {\\sin(\\ln(2x + 1))}$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the chain and quotient rules, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)       & = \\dfrac {e^{\\arctan x}} {1 + x^2}\\\\[6pt]\n    f''(x)  & =     \\dfrac {e^{\\arctan x} - 2 x e^{\\arctan x}} {(1 + x^2)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence $f(0) = 1$, $f'(0) = 1$, $f''(0)    = 1$.\n\n    Substituting the above result into the Maclaurin formula\n\n    $$\n    f(x) = f(0) + x f'(0) + \\dfrac {x^2} {2!} f''(0) + \\dots,\n\n    $$\n    we get\n    the Maclaurin series for $f(x)$ up to and including the $x^2$ term\n\n    $$\n    1 +  x + \\dfrac {x^2} 2\n    $$\n\n2.  Let's find $f'''(0)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'''(x)     & = \\dfrac {(6x^2 - 6x - 1) e^{\\arctan x}} {(1 + x^2)^3}\\\\[6pt]\n    f'''(0) & = -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, the coefficient of $x^3$ in the\n    Maclaurin series is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    c_3     & = \\dfrac {-1} {3!} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = -\\dfrac 1 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Substituting $2x$ into the Maclaurin series for $\\ln(1 + x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln(1 + 2x)     & = 2x - \\dfrac {(2x)^2} {2} + \\dfrac {(2x)^3} {3} + \\dots\\\\[4pt]\n    & = 2x - 2x^2 + \\dfrac {8x^3} 3 + \\dots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $\\ln(1 + 2x)$ into the Maclaurin series for $\\sin x$,\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin (\\ln(1 + 2x))  & = \\ln(1 + 2x)  - \\dfrac {(\\ln(1 + 2x) )^3} {3!} + \\dots\\\\[4pt]\n    & = \\left(2x - 2x^2 + \\dfrac {8x^3} 3 + \\dots\\right)\n    - \\dfrac {\\left(2x - 2x^2 + \\dfrac {8x^3} 3\\right)^3} 6 + \\dots\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\left(2x - 2x^2 + \\dfrac {8x^3} 3\\right) - \\dfrac{(2x)^3} 6 + \\dots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the Maclaurin series for $\\sin(\\ln(2x + 1))$ up to and\n    including the $x^3$ term is\n\n    $$\n    2 x - 2x^2 + \\dfrac {4x^3} 3\n    $$\n\n4.  Substituting the results from parts (a) and (c), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\,\\dfrac {f(x) - 1} {\\sin(\\ln(2x + 1))}\n    & = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\,\\dfrac {x + \\dfrac {x^2} 2 + \\dots} {2x - 2x^2 + \\dots}\\hspace{4em}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\,\\dfrac {1 + \\dfrac {x} 2 + \\dots} {2 - 2x + \\dots}\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = \\dfrac 1 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA605","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA605\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=(\\arccos x)^2$,\n$-1\\leq x\\leq 1$.\n\n1.  Show that $f'(0)=-\\pi$ [2 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1-x^2)f''(x)-xf'(x)=2.\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n2.  By differentiating the above equation twice, show that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (1-x^2)f^{(4)}(x)-5xf'''(x)=4f''(x)\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $f^{(n)}(x)$\n    denotes the $n$th derivative of $f(x)$.[4 marks]\n\n3.  Hence show the Maclaurin series for $f(x)$ up to and\n    including the term in $x^4$ is\n    $\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{4}-\\pi x+x^2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}x^3+\\dfrac{x^4}{3}$.[5 marks]\n\n4.  Use this series approximation for $f(x)$ with\n    $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ to find an approximate value for\n    $25\\pi-\\dfrac{20}{3}\\pi^2$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The first derivative of $f(x)$ and its value at $0$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= 2\\arccos{x}(\\arccos(x))'\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n    &=-\\dfrac{2\\arccos{x}}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{8.5mm}\\\\\n    f'(0) &= -2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{8.5mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= -\\pi\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Differentiating both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[(1-x^2)f''(x)-xf'(x)\\right] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} [2]\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    (1-x^2)f'''(x)-3xf''(x)-f'(x) &=0\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Differentiating again with respect to $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[(1-x^2)f'''(x)-3xf''(x)-f'(x)\\right] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} [0]\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    (1-x^2)f^{(4)}(x)-5xf'''(x)-4f''(x) &=0\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    (1-x^2)f^{(4)}(x)-5xf'''(x)=4f''(x)\\hspace{-14.6mm}&\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\hspace{15mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  By direct evaluation we have\n    $f(0)=(\\arccos{0})^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2=\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{4}$.\n\n    From (a) we have $f'(0)=-\\pi$ and substituting $x=0$ in the\n    equation\\\n    $f$ satisfies, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (1-(0)^2)f''(0)-(0)f'(0) &= 2 \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{2mm}\\\\\n    f''(0)&= 2\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{4mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting $x=0$\n    in equation (1), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (1-0^2)f'''(0)-3(0)f''(0)-f'(0)&= 0 \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{2mm}\\\\\n    f'''(0)-(-\\pi) &= 0 \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{6mm}\\\\\n    f'''(0) &= -\\pi\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{4mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally,\n    substituting $x=0$ in equation (2), we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (1-(0)^2)f^{(4)}(0)-5(0)f'''(0)&= 4f''(0)\\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{2mm}\\\\\n    f^{(4)}(0) &= 4(2) \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{6mm}\\\\\n    &= 8\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{4mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, the Maclaurin\n    series for $f(x)$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= f(0)+\\dfrac{f'(0)}{1!}x+\\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^2+\\dfrac{f'''(0)}{3!}x^3+\\dfrac{f^{(4)}(0)}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{4} -\\pi x+x^2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}x^3+\\dfrac{1}{3}x^4+\\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Substituting $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ in equation (3), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) &= \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{4} -\\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3+\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4+\\cdots \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    \\left(\\arccos{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)^2 &\\approx \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{4}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{4}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{48}+\\dfrac{1}{48}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)^2&\\approx \\dfrac{12\\pi^2-25\\pi}{48}+\\dfrac{13}{48}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    \\dfrac{48\\pi^2}{9}-12\\pi^2+25\\pi &\\approx 13\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    25\\pi-\\dfrac{20}{3}\\pi^2 \\approx 13\\hspace{-8mm}&\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5.5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA608","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA608\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=\\cos(p\\arccos x)$, $-1<x<1$ and\n$p\\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $f'(0)=p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)$.[2 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ and its derivative satisfy\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1-x^2)f^{(n+2)}(x)=(2n+1)xf^{(n+1)}(x)+\\left(n^2-p^2\\right)f^{(n)}(x), \\quad n\\in \\mathbb{Z^+}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f^{(n)}(x)$ denotes the $n$th derivative of $f(x)$ and\n$f^{(0)}(x)$ is $f(x)$.\n\n2.  Show that $f^{(n+2)}(0)=(n^2-p^2)f^{(n)}(0)$.[1 mark]\n\n3.  For $p\\in\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}$, show\n    that the Maclaurin series for $f(x)$, up to and including the $x^4$\n    term, is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-6em}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)+p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)x-\\dfrac{p^2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2}x^2\\\\[12pt]\n    &+\\dfrac{(1-p^2)p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{6}x^3-\\dfrac{(4-p^2)p^2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{24}x^4\\hspace{8mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    [5 marks]\n\n4.  Hence or otherwise, find\n    $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\cos(p\\arccos(x))}{x}$ where $p$\n    is an odd integer. [3 marks]\n\n5.  If $p$ is an integer, prove the Maclaurin series for $f(x)$\n    is a polynomial of degree $p$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Differentiating with respect to $x$ and evaluating at $x=0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{p\\sin(p\\arccos x)}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n    f'(0) &= \\dfrac{p\\sin(p\\arccos(0))}{\\sqrt{1-(0)^2}}\\rule[-5mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $x=0$ in the equation $f$ satisfy, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (1-(0)^2)f^{(n+2)}(0)&=(2n+1)(0)f^{(n+1)}(0)+\\left(n^2-p^2\\right)f^{(n)}(0)\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-14mm}f^{(n+2)}(0) = (n^2-p^2)f^{(n)}(0)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Considering $f$ and its derivatives at $x=0$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= \\cos(p\\arccos(0))=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f'(0) &= p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)&[\\text{by part (a)}]\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f''(0)&=(0^2-p^2)f^{(0)}(0)=(-p^2)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right) &[\\text{by part (b)}]\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f'''(0) &= (1^2-p^2)f^{(1)}(0)=(1-p^2)p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)&[\\text{by part (b)}]\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f^{(4)}(0)&= (2^2-p^2)f^{(2)}(0) = (4-p^2)(-p^2)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right) &[\\text{by part (b)}]\\rule[-0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, the Maclaurin series for $f(x)$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&= f(0)+f'(0)x+\\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^2+\\dfrac{f'''(0)}{3!}x^3+\\dfrac{f^{(4)}(0)}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\\\\n    &= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)+p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)x-\\dfrac{p^2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2}x^2\\\\\n    &\\hspace{5.5em}+\\dfrac{(1-p^2)p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{6}x^3-\\dfrac{(4-p^2)p^2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{24}x^4+\\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the Maclaurin series for $f(x)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(p\\arccos(x))}{x} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\, \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}+p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)x-\\dfrac{p^2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2}x^2+\\cdots}{x}\\\\\n    &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\  p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\dfrac{p^2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2}x+\\cdots\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= p\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  From the recursive equation (1) we deduce $f^{p+2}(0)=0$ and\n    therefore $f^{(n)}=0$ for all $n\\geq p+2$. For the $p+1$ term we\n    have two options:\n\n    -   $p$ is odd. Then\n        $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is zero and\n        $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is non zero,\n        therefore the $p+1$ coefficient is zero and the $p$ term is non\n        zero.\n\n    -   $p$ is even. Then\n        $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is non zero\n        and $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{p\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is zero,\n        therefore the $p+1$ coefficient is zero and the $p$ term is non\n        zero.\n\n    In both cases the $n+1$ term vanishes and the $n$ term does not.\n    Therefore $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $p$."},{"ref":"AA610","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA610\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet the Maclaurin series for $\\cot x$ be\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{a_1}{x}+a_2x+a_3x^3+\\cdots\n$$\nwhere $a_1$, $a_2$ and\n$a_3$ are non zero constants.\n\n1.  Find the series for $\\csc^2 x$, in terms of $a_1$, $a_2$ and $a_3$,\n    up to and including the $x^2$ term\n    1.  by differentiating the above series for $\\cot x$;\n\n    2.  by using the relationship $\\csc^2 x= 1+\\cot^2 x$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, by comparing your two series, determine the values of $a_1$,\n    $a_2$ and $a_3$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Differentiating the Maclaurin series for $\\cot x$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\cot x  &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{a_1}{x}+a_2x+a_3x^3+\\cdots\\right)\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n        -\\csc^2 x &= -\\dfrac{a_1}{x^2}+a_2+3\\hspace{0.15em}a_3x^2+\\cdots\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\[3pt]\n        &\\hspace{-6mm}\\csc^2 x = \\dfrac{a_1}{x^2}-a_2-3\\hspace{0.15em}a_3x^2+\\cdots\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Considering only products of terms that will result in powers\n        less than or equal to $2$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\csc^2 x &= 1+\\left(\\dfrac{a_1}{x}+a_2x+a_3x^3+\\cdots\\right)^2\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.3em}= 1+\\dfrac{a_1^2}{x^2}+2a_1a_2+(a_2^2+2a_1a_3)x^2+\\cdots\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Comparing the two series we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a_1^2 &= a_1 \\\\\n    1+2a_1a_2 &= -a_2 \\\\\n    a_2^2+2a_1a_3 &= -3a_3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    From (1) it is clear that\n    $a_1=1$, substituting $a_1=1$ into (2) gives us $a_2=-1/3$ and\n    substituting $a_1$ and $a_2$ into (3) gives us $a_3=-1/45$.\n\n    Therefore the values of the constants are\n    $$\n\n    a_1 = 1, \\, a_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\, \\text{and} \\, a_3 = -\\dfrac{1}{45}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA613","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA613\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1.  By successive differentiation find the first five non-zero terms in\n    the Maclaurin series for $f(x)=(2-2x)\\ln(1-x)+2x$.[8 marks]\n\n2.  Deduce that, for $n\\geq 2$, the coefficient of $x^n$ in this series\n    is $\\dfrac{2}{n(n-1)}$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We find the first six derivatives and their evaluations at $x=0$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= -\\dfrac{2-2x}{1-x}-2\\ln(1-x)+2=-2\\ln(1-x) & f'(0)&= 0 \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    f''(x) &= \\dfrac{2}{1-x} & f''(0)&=2\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f'''(x)&=\\dfrac{2}{(1-x)^2} & f'''(0) &= 2=2(1)\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f^{(4)}(x) &= \\dfrac{4}{(1-x)^3} & f^{(4)}(0)&=4=2(2)\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f^{(5)}(x) &= \\dfrac{12}{(1-x)^4} & f^{(5)}(0) &= 12=2(6)\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    f^{(6)}(x) &= \\dfrac{48}{(1-x)^5} & f^{(6)}(0)&= 48=2(24)\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    thus, the first five non-zero terms are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{-1.4em}f(x) &= \\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^2+\\dfrac{f'''(0)}{3!}x^3+\\dfrac{f^{(4)}(0)}{4!}x^4+\\dfrac{f^{(5)}(0)}{5!}x^5+\\dfrac{f^{(6)}(0)}{6!}x^6+\\cdots \\\\[8pt]\n    \\hspace{-0.3em}&= x^2+\\dfrac{1}{3}x^3+\\dfrac{1}{6}x^4+\\dfrac{1}{10}x^5+\\dfrac{1}{15}x^6\\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  From the terms of $f^{(n)}(0)$ in (a) we can infer the $n$-th term\n    is $2(n-2)!$. Therefore, the coefficient of $x^n$ in the Maclaurin\n    series is\n\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{2(n-2)!}{n!} =\\enskip\\dfrac{2}{n(n-1)}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA614","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA614\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=e^x\\cos x$, $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  By finding a suitable number of derivatives of $f$, determine the\n    Maclaurin series for $f(x)$ as far as the term\n    $x^4$.[6 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, determine the exact value of $\\displaystyle\n    \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^x\\cos x-x-1}{x^3}$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Taking the first four derivatives using the product rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)&=e^x\\cos x-e^x\\sin x & f'(0)&= 1\\rule[-1.5mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\[1pt]\n    f''(x)&=-2e^x\\sin x & f''(0)&= 0\\rule[-1.5mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\[1pt]\n    f'''(x)&=-2e^x\\cos x-2e^x\\sin x & f'''(0)&= -2\\rule[-1.5mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\[1pt]\n    f^{(4)}(x)&= -4e^x\\cos x&f^{(4)}(0)&=-4\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{3.75mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the first terms of the Maclaurin series are\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&= f(0)+\\dfrac{f'(0)}{1!}x+\\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^2+\\dfrac{f'''(0)}{3!}x^3+\\dfrac{f''''(0)}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= 1+x-\\dfrac{1}{3}x^3-\\dfrac{1}{6}x^4+\\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  **Method 1:** Using the Maclaurin series found in (a), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{e^x\\cos x-x-1}{x^3} &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{1}+\\cancel{x}-\\dfrac{1}{3}x^3-\\dfrac{1}{6}x^4+\\cdots -\\cancel{x}-\\cancel{1}}{x^3} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^x\\cos x-x-1}{x^3} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}-\\dfrac{1}{6}x+\\cdots\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    \\\n    **Method 2:** Using L'Hôpital's rule repeatedly, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow0} \\dfrac{e^x\\cos x-x-1}{x^3} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^x\\cos x-e^x\\sin x-1}{3x^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{-2e^x\\sin x}{6x}\\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{-2e^x\\cos x-2e^x\\sin x}{6} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{2}{6}=-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, the exact\n    value of $\\displaystyle\n    \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{e^x\\cos x-x-1}{x^3}$ is\n    $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$"},{"ref":"AA616","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA616\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nLet $f(x)=e^x\\cos x$.\n\n1.  Show that $f''(x)=2\\left(f'(x)-f(x)\\right)$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  By further differentiation of the result in part (a), find the\n    Maclaurin\\\n    expansion of $f(x)$, as far as the term in $x^5$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Differentiating with respect to $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= -e^x\\sin x + e^x\\cos x \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{2mm}\\\\\n    f''(x)&= -\\cancel{e^x\\cos x}-e^x\\sin x-e^x\\sin x+\\cancel{e^x\\cos x} \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{6mm}\\\\\n    &= 2(-e^x\\sin x) \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{6mm}\\\\\n    &= 2(-e^x\\sin x+e^x\\cos x-e^x\\cos x)\\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{6mm}\\\\\n    &= 2(f'(x)-f(x))\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{4mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Given that $f(0)=1$, $f'(0)=1$ and $f''(0)=0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'''(0) &= 2(f''(0)-f'(0)) = 2(0-1) = -2\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{2mm}\\\\\n    f^{(4)}(0) &= 2(f'''(0)-f''(0)) = 2(-2-0)=-4 \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n    f^{(5)}(0)&=2(f^{(4)}(0)-f'''(0))=2(-4-(-2))=-4\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, the Maclaurin expansion of $e^x\\cos x$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= f(0)+\\dfrac{f'(0)}{1!}x+\\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^2 + \\dfrac{f'''(0)}{3!}x^3+\\dfrac{f^{(4)}(0)}{4!}x^4+\\dfrac{f^{(5)}(0)}{5!}x^5+\\cdots\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= 1+x-\\dfrac{1}{3}x^3-\\dfrac{1}{6}x^4-\\dfrac{1}{30}x^5+\\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA618","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA618\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=e^{-x} \\sin x -x+x^2$.\n\nBy finding a suitable number of derivatives of $f$, determine the first\nnon-zero term in its Maclaurin series.","markScheme":"Differentiating with respect to $x$, and evaluating the first\nderivatives at $0$ we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=e^{-x}\\sin x-x+x^2 & f(0)&=0\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\nf'(x)&=-e^{-x}\\sin x + e^{-x}\\cos x-1+2x & f'(0)&=0\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\nf''(x)&=\\cancel{e^{-x}\\sin x}-e^{-x}\\cos x-e^{-x}\\cos x-\\cancel{e^{-x}\\sin x} + 2 & f''(0)&=0\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\nf'''(x)&= 2e^{-x}\\cos x + 2e^{-x}\\sin x & f'''(0)&=2\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{4mm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first non-zero term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3!}x^3=\\hspace{0.4em}\\dfrac{1}{3}x^3\n$$"}]},{"name":"Trigonometric Integration","questions":[{"ref":"AA1274","marks":"23","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1274\n\n**Maximum mark: 23**\n\nConsider the function $\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{\\cos{x}}{2+\\sin{x}}$ for $-\\pi \\leq x \\leq \\pi$.\n\n1.  Evaluate $f(0)$.[1 mark]\n2.  Find all possible values of $a$ if $f(a)=0$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  1.  Show that $\\displaystyle f'(x)=-\\frac{2\\sin{x}+1}{(2+\\sin{x})^2}$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the $x-$coordinates of any stationary points of $f$.[7 marks]\n\n4.  Given that $\\displaystyle f''(x)=-\\frac{2\\cos{x}(1-\\sin{x})}{(2+\\sin{x})^3}$ find the nature of any stationary points of $f$.[5 marks]\n\n5.  Hence sketch the graph of $f$, clearly showing the values of the axes intercepts and the $x-$coordinates of any stationary points.[3 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ is positive and decreasing in the region $s<x<t$.\n\nThe area enclosed by $f$ and the $x-$axis from $x=s$ to $x=t$ is $\\ln{c}$ where $c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n6.  Find $c$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To evaluate the function we substitute in $x=0$ which gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(0)&=\\frac{\\cos{0}}{2+\\sin{0}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the fact that $\\cos{0}=1$ and $\\sin{0}=0$ we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{1}{2+0}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  Using $x=a$ and the function $f$ we can form an equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(a)&=\\frac{\\cos{a}}{2+\\sin{a}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Only the numerator can provide solutions to the equation, hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos{a}&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Recall, the domain of $f$ is $-\\pi \\leq x \\leq \\pi$, we can use a sketch of $\\cos{x}$ to find all solutions of $a$ in that domain\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1274-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a1\">\n\n    We know that the principal angle of $\\cos{\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}}$ is $0$, which is shown in $\\textcolor{green}{\\text{green}}$, and using the symmetries of the cosine curve we can see that $-\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$, shown in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$, is also a solution.\n\n    Hence $\\displaystyle a=\\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  1.  To differentiate $f$ we need to use the quotient rule.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        For this we need the derivative of both the numerator and the denominator\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(\\cos{x})&= -\\sin{x}&\\hspace{-1em} &&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(2+\\sin{x})&=\\cos{x}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now we can use these results and the quotient rule to form an expression for $f'(x)$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            f'(x)&=\\frac{(2+\\sin{x})(-\\sin{x})-(\\cos{x})(\\cos{x})}{(2+\\sin{x})^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n            &=\\frac{-2\\sin{x}-\\sin^2{x}-\\cos^2{x}}{(2+\\sin{x})^2}\\\\[12pt]\n            &=\\frac{-2\\sin{x}-(\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x})}{(2+\\sin{x})^2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Notice we can use a trigonometric identity to simplify the numerator\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\frac{-2\\sin{x}-1}{(2+\\sin{x})^2}\\\\[12pt]\\\n            &=-\\dfrac{2\\sin{x}+1}{(2+\\sin{x})^2}   \\hspace{2em}\\text{... as required.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  To find any stationary points we must solve $f'(x)=0$, hence we can form an equation using the result from part (c)(i)\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -\\frac{2\\sin{x}+1}{(2+\\sin{x})^2}&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We only need to consider the numerator, hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -2\\sin{x}-1&=0\\\\[6pt]\n            \\sin{x}&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        The principal solution (this means the solution in the first quadrant) is $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\n        However, if we make a sketch of $\\sin{x}$ with the same domain as $f$ we can see that the solutions to $\\sin{x}=-\\tfrac{1}{2}$ are both negative\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1274-a22.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a22\">\n\n        Due to the symmetries of the graph we can see that the two angles, $x_1$ and $x_2$, marked in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$ are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &&x_1&= -\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{6}&\\hspace{-1em} && x_2&= 0-\\frac{\\pi}{6}& \\\\[6pt]\n            &&&= -\\frac{5\\pi}{6}&\\hspace{-1em} && x_2&=-\\frac{\\pi}{6}&\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence there are two stationary points with $x-$coordinates of $x=-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  We can use the second derivative to determine the nature of the stationary points.\n\n    If $f''(x)>0$ the point is a minimum and if $f''(x)<0$ it is a maximum.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's first test the point $x=-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})&=-\\frac{2\\cos{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}(1-\\sin{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})})}{(2+\\sin{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})})^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To evaluate the expression above we need the exact values of $\\sin{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}$ and $\\cos{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}$.\n\n    From previous work we know that $\\sin{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}=-\\tfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    We can use the symmetry of the cosine curve\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1274-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a3\">\n\n    to realise that $\\cos{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}=\\cos{(\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}$.\n\n    Hence $\\cos{(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\n    Now let's substitute the two values into the earlier expression we had $f''(x)$ to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})&=-\\frac{2\\times\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\times(1-(-\\tfrac{1}{2}))}{(2+(-\\tfrac{1}{2}))^3}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We don't need to evaluate the expression exactly we just need to know if it is positive or negative, let's do a little simplification\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=-\\frac{2\\times\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(1+\\tfrac{1}{2})}{(\\tfrac{3}{2})^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now see that quotient will result in a positive value. Hence, as the entire quotient is being multiplied by $-1$, the result is negative.\n\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})&<0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $x=-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\text{ is a maximum value}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use a similar process for the other stationary point $x=-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})&=-\\frac{2\\cos{(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})}(1-\\sin{(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})})}{(2+\\sin{(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})})^3}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    From previous work we know that $\\sin{(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})}=-\\tfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    And using the symmetries of the cosine curve we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos{(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})}&=\\cos{(\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-\\cos{(\\tfrac{\\pi}{6})}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now substitute these two values into the second derivative to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})&=-\\frac{2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(1-(-\\tfrac{1}{2}))}{(2+(-\\tfrac{1}{2})^3}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=-\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}(1+\\tfrac{1}{2})}{(\\tfrac{3}{2})^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can see that the quotient will now be negative and hence the overall value will be positive.\n\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f''(-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6})&>0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $x=-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\text{ is a minimum value}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  To help us with the sketch we can summarise our findings so far.\n\n    From parts (a) and (b) we found the axes intercepts $(0, \\tfrac{1}{2})$, $(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0)$ and $(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0)$.\n\n    We have also found a maximum point at $x=-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and a minimum point at $x=-\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}$.\n\n    We should also remind ourselves that the domain is $-\\pi \\leq x \\leq \\pi$.\n\n    Let's first sketch the domain and the axes intercepts\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1274-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a4\">\n\n    We can now add the stationary points\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1274-a5.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a5\">\n\n    Finally we can use the plotted points to fit the function. Being careful to stop at the end-points!\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1274-a6.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a6\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\text{Correct axes intercepts}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\text{Correct position of two stationary points}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\text{Correct shape and end-points}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n6.  We need to identity the region in which $f$ is positive (above the $x-$axis) and is decreasing (which means the gradient is negative).\n\n    This region is the highlighted red part of $f$ shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1274-a7.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a7\">\n\n    Hence we can see that the value of $s=-\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n    To find the area under the curve between these two values we need to evaluate the definite integral of $f$ from $-\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ to $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Area}&=\\int_{\\textstyle -\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{2}}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x  \\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\int_{\\textstyle -\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\frac{\\cos{x}}{2+\\sin{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This integral is a quotient. Often a good strategy to integrate a quotient is to use a substitution for the denominator.\n\n    Hence let $u=2+\\sin{x}$.\n\n    To rewrite the integral we will need an expression for $\\mathrm{d}x$ in terms of $\\mathrm{d}u$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\cos{x}\\\\[12pt]\n        \\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{1}{\\cos{x}}\\mathrm{d}u\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let's now replace the original integral so that it is in terms of $u$. We will omit the limits for now.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\int \\frac{\\cos{x}}{2+\\sin{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &=\\int \\frac{\\cancel{\\cos{x}}}{u}\\times\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\cos{x}}}\\mathrm{d}u\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\mathrm{d}u\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\ln|u|+C\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let's now substitute $u=2+\\sin{x}$ and reintroduce the limits.\n\n    As the integral is now definite we can omit the constant.\n\n    This gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Area}&=\\bigg[\\ln{|2+\\sin{x}|}\\bigg]_{\\textstyle -\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As $2+\\sin{x}>0$ for all $x$ we can remove the absolute value signs.\n\n    Substituting in the upper and lower limit we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\ln{(2+\\sin{\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)})}-\\ln{(2+\\sin{\\left(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)})}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Recall $\\sin{\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}=1$ and $\\sin{\\left(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}=-\\tfrac{1}{2}$, hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\ln{(2+1)}-\\ln{(2+-(\\tfrac{1}{2}))}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\ln{3}-\\ln{\\tfrac{3}{2}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\ln{\\tfrac{3}{\\tfrac{3}{2}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\ln{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $c=2$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1318","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1318\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\n1.    \n\n    1.    Expand and simplify $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}-k\\right)^3$ in ascending powers of $k$. \n\n    2.    By considering a double angle identity, use a suitable substitution\n\nfor $k$ to show that $\\cos^3{2x}=1-6\\sin^2{x} +12\\sin^4{x}-8\\sin^6{x}$ [6 marks]\n\nConsider the function $f$ such that $f(x)=16\\sin{2x}\\left(1-6\\sin^2{x} +12\\sin^4{x}-8\\sin^6{x}\\right)$.\n\n2.    \n\n    1.    Show that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=2-2\\cos^4{2a}$, where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+.$ \n\n    \n\n    2.    It is given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{a}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-1$ where $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}<a<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n    \n\n        Find $a$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. Using the binomial theorem\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (a+b)^n&=a^n+\\binom{n}{1}a^{n-1}b+...+\\binom{n}{r}a^{n-r}b^r+...+b^n\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        with $a=\\tfrac{1}{2}$, $b=-k$ and $n=3$ we expand $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-k\\right)^3$ to get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3+\\binom{3}{1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}(-k)+\\binom{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{1}(-k)^2+\\binom{3}{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{0}(-k)^3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We can use the third row of Pascal's triangle to help us.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1318a0.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1318a0\">\n\n        Replacing the binomial coefficients with the values in the third row and simplifying we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\frac{1}{8}+3\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)(-k)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\times k^2-k^3\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{8}-\\dfrac{3k}{4}+\\dfrac{3k^2}{2}-k^3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. The given relation has both a $\\cos{2x}$ term and $\\sin^2{x}$ terms.\n\n        Considering the trigonometric identities in the formula booklet we can relate the two terms using\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\cos{2x}&=1-2\\sin^2{x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Dividing this by $2$ we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\cos{2x}}{2}&=\\frac{1}{2}-\\sin^2{x}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}-k\\right)\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Comparing the R.H.S. to the binomial in part (a)(i) we can see that $k=\\sin^2{x}$ would be a suitable substitution.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence using the answer from part (a) with $k=\\sin^2{x}$ we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}-\\sin^2{x}\\right)^3&=\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{3(\\sin^2{x})}{4}+\\frac{3(\\sin^2{x})^2}{2}-(\\sin^2{x})^3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Simplifying the R.H.S. we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{3\\sin^2{x}}{4}+\\frac{3\\sin^4{x}}{2}-\\sin^6{x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now, considering the L.H.S., we can use the identity to rewrite the contents of the bracket, to get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}-\\sin^2{x}\\right)^3&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\cos{2x}}{2}\\right)^3\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\dfrac{\\cos^3{2x}}{8}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Putting the two results together we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{\\cos^3{2x}}{8}&=\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{3\\sin^2{x}}{4}+\\frac{3\\sin^4{x}}{2}-\\sin^6{x}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Multiplying by $8$ we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\cos^3{2x}&=1-\\frac{3\\sin^2{x}}{4} \\times 8+\\frac{3\\sin^4{x}}{2}\\times 8-\\sin^6{x}\\times 8\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-3.4em}\\cos^3{2x}=1-6\\sin^2{x} +12\\sin^4{x}-8\\sin^6{x}\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2.  1. The first thing to notice in this question is that part of the function $f$ is the same as the R.H.S. of the relation from part (a)(ii).\n\n        It makes sense to replace this part of $f$ with the L.H.S. of the relation in part (a)(ii) as this will make $f$ much simpler.\n\n        This gives\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(x)&=16\\sin{2x}\\cos^3{2x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        To integrate this expression we can first notice that it is of the form\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(x)&=g'(x)\\times [g(x)]^n\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        That means we can use a substitution of $u=g(x)$.\n\n        Hence, for $f$, we use $u=\\cos{2x}$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n        We can find an expression for $\\mathrm{d}x$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-2\\sin{2x}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        \\mathrm{d}x&=-\\frac{1}{2\\sin{2x}}\\mathrm{d}u\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Rewriting the integral in terms of $u$, without limits for now, we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\int16\\sin{2x} \\times u^3\\times -\\frac{1}{2\\sin{2x}}\\mathrm{d}u\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Assuming we picked the correct substitution, the functions of $x$ should cancel out\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\int16\\cancel{\\sin{2x}} \\times u^3\\times -\\frac{1}{2\\cancel{\\sin{2x}}}\\mathrm{d}u\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\int-8u^3\\,\\mathrm{d}u\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        and they do!\n\n        We can now integrate this function of $u$, with respect to $u$, using the inverse power rule to get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=\\frac{-8}{4}u^4\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=-2u^4\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can reintroduce the limits, factorise the $-2$, and write the result in terms of $x$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=-2\\bigg[\\cos^4{2x}\\bigg]_0^a\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Substituting in the limits we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=-2\\bigg[\\cos^4{2(a)}-(\\cos^4{2(0)})\\bigg]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Recall $\\cos{0}=1$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=-2\\bigg[\\cos^4{2(a)}-1\\bigg]\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=2-2\\cos^4{2a}\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. In this question we need to find the value of $a$ such that the definite integral is equal to $-1$.\n\n        To do this we can form an equation, using our previous result, which we can solve to find $a$.\n\n        But first let's rewrite the integral into a form so that we can use our previous answer.\n\n        As $a>\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$ we can say\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\int_{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{a}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x-\\int_{0}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using the answer from part (b)(i) we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=(2-2\\cos^4{2a})-\\left(2-2\\cos^4{\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{6}\\right)}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Recall $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=2-2\\cos^4{2a}-2+2\\times \\frac{1}{16}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{1}{8}-2\\cos^4{2a}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We can now use this simplified result to form an equation which we can solve to find $a$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        -1&=\\frac{1}{8}-2\\cos^4{2a}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        2\\cos^4{2a}&=\\frac{9}{8}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\cos^4{2a}&=\\frac{9}{16}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        \\cos{2a}&=\\sqrt[\\scriptsize{4}]{\\frac{9}{16}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        &=\\pm\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The principal angle for $\\cos{\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\n        Using a sketch (or the unit circle) of the cosine curve along with the $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{principal}}$ value we can identify the $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{other}}$ solutions.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA1318a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1318a1\">\n\n        From these values we can find four possible values of $a$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2a&=\\frac{\\pi}{6},&\\frac{5\\pi}{6},&&\\frac{7\\pi}{6},&&\\frac{11\\pi}{6}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        a&=\\frac{\\pi}{12},&\\frac{5\\pi}{12},&&\\frac{7\\pi}{12},&&\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Recall the interval for $a$,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\frac{\\pi}{6}&<a<\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n        \\frac{2\\pi}{12}&<a<\\frac{6\\pi}{12}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using this we can identify the only valid value for $a$. Therefore\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a&=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1327","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1327\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1.    Show that $\\sin^3{\\theta} = \\sin{\\theta} - \\sin{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}$. [1 mark]\n\nThe function $f$ is defined as $f(x) = \\sin^3{2x}$.\n\n2.    Hence, show that $\\displaystyle \\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\frac{1}{6}\\cos^3{2x} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos{2x} + C$. [4 marks]\n\nA continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $g$ defined by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    g(x) =\n    \\begin{cases}\n        af(x), & 0 \\leq x \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\\\\n        0, & \\text{otherwise}\n    \\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3.    Find $\\mathrm{P}\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}{6} < X < \\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Rewriting and then using the Pythagorean identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin^3{\\theta} &= \\sin{\\theta} \\times \\sin^2{\\theta} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sin{\\theta}(1 - \\cos^2{\\theta}) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sin{\\theta}- \\sin{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. We need to integrate the function $f$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\sin^3{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Notice how the question uses the word _hence_; this means we need to use the previous part to help us.\n\n    Let’s rewrite $\\sin^3{2x}$, with $\\theta = 2x$, using the identity from part (a)(i).\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\sin^3{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\left( \\textcolor{red}{\\sin{2x}} \\textcolor{blue}{\\,-\\,\\sin{2x}\\cos^2{2x}} \\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now integrate both terms separately.\n    For the first term, we use the standard integral for sine\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\textcolor{red}{\\sin{2x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\cos{2x} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    And for the second term, $\\textcolor{blue}{\\,-\\,\\sin{2x}\\cos^2{2x}}$, we use the substitution\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u &= \\cos{2x} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= -2 \\sin{2x} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{d}x &= -\\dfrac{1}{2 \\sin{2x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, writing the second term in terms of $u$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\textcolor{blue}{\\,-\\,\\sin{2x}\\cos^2{2x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int -\\sin{2x} \\times u^2 \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2 \\sin{2x}} \\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Notice, we can cancel out the sine functions! But be careful with the two negative signs.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\frac{1}{2} \\int u^2\\,\\mathrm{d}u\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We now have a single function of $u$, which we are integrating with respect to $u$. Excellent!\n    Using the inverse power rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{3} u^3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\frac{1}{6} u^3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\frac{1}{6} \\cos^3{2x} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We will add the constant of integration in the next step.\n    Putting the results for the two terms back together, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\frac{1}{6} \\cos^3{2x} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos{2x} + C\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have been asked to find the probability of the continuous random variable  $X$ taking a value from $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$ to $\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\n    As this is a continuous distribution, we need to find the area under the probability density function from $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$ to $\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\n    Hence, we can say that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}\\left( \\tfrac{\\pi}{6} < X < \\tfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right) &= \\int_{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{4}} g(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let’s write $g$ in terms of $f$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\int_{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{4}} a f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, we need to evaluate the definite integral shown above.\n\n    However, we don't know the value of $a$.\n\n    But we do know that the area under a valid probability distribution must be equal to $1$.\n\n    Hence, using the given domain of $g$ which is $0 < x < \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ we can form an equation which we can solve to find $a$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{4}} a f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = 1 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using our answer from part (b), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a \\left[ \\frac{1}{6} \\cos^3{2x} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos{2x} \\right]_0^{\\textstyle\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting in the limits and solving for $a$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a \\left[ \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\cos^3{(2 \\times \\tfrac{\\pi}{4})} - \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\cos{(2 \\times \\tfrac{\\pi}{4})} \\right) - \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\cos^3{(2 \\times 0)} - \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\cos{(2 \\times 0)} \\right) \\right] &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    a \\left[ \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\cos^3{ \\tfrac{\\pi}{2}} - \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\cos{\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}} \\right) - ... \\\\\n    ...\\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\cos^3{0} - \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\cos{0} \\right) \\right] &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    a \\left[ \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times 0^3 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0 \\right) - \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times 1^3 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\right) \\right] &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    -a \\left( \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{3}{6} \\right) &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{2a}{6} &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    a &= 3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we have $a$, we can find the probability.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}\\left( \\tfrac{\\pi}{6} < X < \\tfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)&=\\int_{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{4}}3 f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3 \\left[ \\frac{1}{6} \\cos^3{2x} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos{2x} \\right]_{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting in the limits and simplifying, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &= 3 \\left[ \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times ( 0 )^3 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times ( 0)  \\right) - \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^3 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\right] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -3 \\left( \\frac{1}{48} - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -\\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\left( \\tfrac{\\pi}{6} < X < \\tfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)&= \\dfrac{11}{16} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA481","marks":"24","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA481\n\n**Maximum mark: 24**\n\nLet $y = e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)$.\n\n1.  Find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Show that\n    $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2y}{\\textrm dx^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)$.\n    [2 marks]\n\nConsider the function $f$ defined by\n$f(x) =  e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)$,\n$-\\pi \\leq x \\leq \\pi$.\n\n3.  Show that the function $f$ has a local maximum value when\n    $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [4 marks]\n\n4.  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflexion of the\n    graph of $y = f(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n5.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, clearly indicating the\n    positions of the\\\n    local maximum point, the point of inflexion and the intercepts with\\\n    the axes. [3 marks]\n\n6.  Find the area of the region enclosed by the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$ and\\\n    the $x$-axis.[6 marks]\n\nThe curvature at any point $(x,y)$ on a graph is defined as\n$\\kappa = \\dfrac{\\left|\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2y} {\\textrm dx^2} \\right|} {\\left[1 + \\left[ \\dfrac{\\textrm dy} {\\textrm dx}\\right]^2\\right]^{\\frac{3}{2}}}$.\n\n7.  Find the value of the curvature of the graph of $y = f(x)$\n    at the local maximum point. [3 marks]\n\n8.  Find the value of $\\kappa$ for $x = 0$ and comment on its\n    meaning with respect to the shape of the graph. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Calculating the $1$st derivative of $y$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}  & =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) - \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n    &  =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Calculating the $2$nd derivative of $y$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\textrm d^2y}{\\textrm dx^2} & =  \\dfrac{1}{4}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\left[ \\cancel{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)} + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] - \\dfrac{1}{4}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\left[-\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) + \\cancel{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)}\\right] \\\\[8pt]\n    &  =  \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    as required.\n\n3.  Solving $f'(x) = 0$ for the stationary points, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}}\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) & =  -\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) & =  -1 \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad   [\\text{for $x \\neq 0$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{x}{2} & =  -\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n    x & =  -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Evaluating $f''(x)$ at $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f''\\Big(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\Big) & =  \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}e^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}}{4} \\\\[10pt]\n    & <  0.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence  $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$  is the\n    $x$-coordinate of a local maximum point.\n\n4.  Solving the equation $f''(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)     & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)                          & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{x}{2}                                    & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x                                           & =  0.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Since $f''(x)$ has opposite signs on either side of $x = 0$,\n     $x = 0$  is the $x$-coordinate of a\n    point of inflexion on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n5.  Solving $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    e^{-\\frac{x}{2}} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{x}{2} & =  \\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    x & =  \\pm \\pi\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $f(x)$ at $x = 0$, we get\n    $f(0) = 1$. Hence the points $(\\pm \\pi, 0)$ are the $x$-intercepts\n    and the point $(0,1)$ is the $y$-intercept of the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$.\n\n    Here is the sketch of the graph:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/a2af7476e7f6ea761930ebe4c1784f3fa7423cd9.svg\" alt=\"a2af7476e7f6ea761930ebe4c1784f3fa7423cd9.svg\">\n\n6.  The area of the region enclosed by the graph of $y = f(x)$ and the\n    $x$-axis is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A & =  \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi e^{-\\frac{x}{2}} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\, \\textrm dx \\quad \\quad \\quad \\left[\\text{let $u = -\\dfrac{x}{2}$}\\right]\\\\[6pt]\n    A & =  -2\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}} e^u \\cos(-u)\\, \\textrm du \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{A}{2} & =  \\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} e^u \\cos u\\, \\textrm du \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{integrate by parts}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{A}{2} & =  e^u \\sin u \\Big|_{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}  -  \\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} e^u \\sin u\\, \\textrm du \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{A}{2} & =  \\big(e^u \\sin u\\big)\\Big|_{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}  - \\Big[\\big(-e^u \\cos u\\big) \\Big|_{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} + \\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} e^u \\cos u\\,\\textrm du \\Big] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{A}{2} & =  \\big(e^\\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\big) - \\left[0 +\\dfrac{A}{2}\\right]\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    A  =  e^\\frac{\\pi}{2} + e^{-\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\n    $$\n\n7.  The value of the curvature of the graph of $y = f(x)$ at\n    $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\kappa &= \\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}} \\left|\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right|}{\\left[1 + \\dfrac{1}{4}e^{-x}\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right]^2\\right]^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\[6pt]\n    %\n    \\end{aligned}\n    %\\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\left|\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right|}{\\left[1 + \\dfrac{1}{4}e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\cos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right]^2\\right]^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\[6pt]\n    %\n    \\end{aligned}\n    %\\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\dfrac{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}e^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}}{\\left[1 + \\dfrac{1}{4}e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right]^2\\right]^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\[10pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}e^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n8.  The value of the curvature of the graph of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\kappa &= \\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-\\frac{x}{2}} \\left|\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right|}{\\left[1 + \\dfrac{1}{4}e^{-x}\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right]^2\\right]^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left|\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right|}{\\left[1 + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\left[\\cos\\left(0\\right) + \\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right]^2\\right]^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &  =  0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph\n    of $y = f(x)$ is a straight line in the neighborhood of the point\n    $(0,1)$."},{"ref":"AA485","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA485\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nUsing the substitution $x^3 = 3\\sec \\theta$, show that\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{x\\sqrt{x^6 - 9}} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x^3}\\right) + C$.","markScheme":"Using the substitution $x^3 = 3\\sec \\theta$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{x\\sqrt{x^6 - 9}}    & =  \\int \\dfrac{x^2\\,\\textrm dx}{x^3\\sqrt{x^6 - 9}}                                     \\quad \\qquad [\\text{replace $x^2\\,\\textrm dx = \\tan \\theta \\sec \\theta\\,\\textrm d\\theta$}]  \\\\[8pt]\n& =  \\int \\dfrac{\\tan \\theta \\cancel{\\sec \\theta}\\,\\textrm d\\theta}{3\\cancel{\\sec\\theta}\\sqrt{9\\sec^2\\theta - 9}}   \\quad \\qquad [\\text{replace $\\sec^2 \\theta -1 = \\tan^2 \\theta$}]  \\\\[8pt]\n& =  \\int \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\tan \\theta}\\,\\textrm d\\theta}{9\\cancel{\\tan \\theta}} \\\\[8pt]\n& =  \\dfrac{\\theta}{9} + C \\qquad  \\Big[\\text{replace $\\theta = \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x^3}\\right)$} \\Big]  \\\\[10pt]\n&\\hspace{.275em} =  \\dfrac{1}{9} \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{3}{x^3}\\right) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA486","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA486\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Using the substitution $x = \\cot \\theta$, show that\n    $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{(x^2+1)^2}\\,\\textrm dx = \\int_\\frac{\\pi}{4}^\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\sin^2\\theta \\,\\textrm d\\theta$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the value of\n    $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{(x^2+1)^2}\\,\\textrm dx$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the substitution $x = \\cot \\theta$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{(x^2+1)^2}\\,\\textrm dx   & =  -\\int_\\frac{\\pi}{2}^\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\dfrac{\\csc^2 \\theta}{(\\cot^2 \\theta+1)^2}\\,\\textrm d\\theta \\qquad \\quad  [\\text{since }\\textrm  dx = -\\csc^2 \\theta\\,\\textrm d\\theta] \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\int_\\frac{\\pi}{4}^\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\dfrac{\\csc^2 \\theta}{\\csc^4 \\theta}\\,\\textrm d\\theta \\qquad \\quad [\\text{since } \\cot^2 \\theta + 1 = \\csc^2 \\theta] \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\int_\\frac{\\pi}{4}^\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\dfrac{1}{\\csc^2 \\theta}\\,\\textrm d\\theta \\\\[10pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em} =  \\int_\\frac{\\pi}{4}^\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\sin^2\\theta \\,\\textrm d\\theta\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using double-angle formula $\\cos 2\\theta  = 1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{(x^2+1)^2}\\,\\textrm dx  & =  \\int_\\frac{\\pi}{4}^\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\sin^2\\theta \\,\\textrm d\\theta \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_\\frac{\\pi}{4}^\\frac{\\pi}{2} (1-\\cos 2\\theta)\\,\\textrm d\\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big(\\theta-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin 2\\theta\\Big)\\Big|_\\frac{\\pi}{4}^\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big(\\Big[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin(\\pi) \\Big] - \\Big[\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\Big]\\Big) \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}  =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{8} + \\dfrac{1}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA487","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA487\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUsing the substitution $u = e^x - 4$, find\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{e^x}{e^{2x} - 8e^x + 25}\\,\\textrm dx$.","markScheme":"Using the substitution $u = e^x - 4$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\dfrac{e^x}{e^{2x} - 8e^x + 25}\\,\\textrm dx & =  \\int \\dfrac{e^x}{(e^x - 4)^2 - 16 + 25}\\,\\textrm dx \\\\[6pt]\n& =  \\int \\dfrac{\\textrm du}{u^2 + 9} \\\\[6pt]\n%& =  \\dfrac{1}{9} \\int \\dfrac{\\textrm du}{\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)^2 + 1} \\\\[6pt]\n& =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{u}{3}\\right) + C_1\\right] \\\\[8pt]\n& \\hspace{.275em} =  \\dfrac{1}{3}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{e^x - 4}{3}\\right) + C_2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  where $C_2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}C_1$."},{"ref":"AA497","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA497\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Show that $\\cos \\theta = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\theta\\right)$\n    for $0 < \\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find\n    $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sin \\theta}^{\\cos \\theta} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\,\\textrm dx$\n    where $0 < \\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\theta\\right)\n    & =   \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\cos\\theta -  \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\sin \\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  1\\cdot\\cos\\theta - 0\\cdot\\sin \\theta \\\\[8pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}=  \\cos \\theta\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using part (a), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_{\\sin \\theta}^{\\cos \\theta} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\,\\textrm dx\n    & =  (\\arcsin x)\\Big|_{\\sin \\theta}^{\\cos \\theta} \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\theta\\right) - \\theta \\\\[10pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - 2\\theta\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA508","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA508\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nBy using the substitution $s = \\tan x$, find\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} x}{2+\\cos 2x}$.\n\nExpress your answer in the form $A \\arctan (B \\tan x) + C$, where $A, B$\nare constants to be determined.","markScheme":"Letting $s = \\tan x$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx   & =  \\arctan s \\\\[11pt]\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} x}{\\mathrm{d} s} & =  \\dfrac{1}{1 + s^2} \\\\[11pt]\n\\mathrm{d} x & =  \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} s}{1 + s^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the triangle\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/AA508a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA508a1\">\n\nwe deduce that $\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + s^2}}$\n\nHence making the substitution $s = \\tan x$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} x}{2+\\cos 2x}\n& =  \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} x}{1+2\\cos^2 x} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{using $\\cos 2x = 2\\cos^2 x - 1$}] \\\\[8pt]\n& =  \\int \\dfrac{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} s}{1 + s^2}}{1+2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + s^2}}\\right)^2} \\\\[12pt]\n& =  \\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}s}{1 + s^2 + 2} \\\\[12pt]\n& =  \\int \\dfrac{1}{3 + s^2} \\,\\mathrm{d} s \\\\[12pt]\n& =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) + C \\\\[14pt]\n&  =   \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\tan x\\right) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA509","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA509\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nConsider the functions $f(x) = \\sin x$,\n$-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and\n$g(x) = \\dfrac{2x\\sqrt{1-x^2}}{1 - 2x^2}$,\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, x \\neq \\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n1.  Find an expression for $(g \\circ f)(x)$, stating its domain.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence show that $(g \\circ f)(x) = \\tan 2 x$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the exact value of\n    $(g\\circ f)'(x)$ at $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n    [6 marks]\n\n4.  Show that the area bounded by the graph of $y = (g\\circ f)(x)$, the\n    $x$-axis and the lines $x = 0$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ is\n    $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln 2$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g \\circ f)(x)  & =  g\\big(f(x)\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  g(\\sin x) \\\\[10pt]\n    (g \\circ f)(x)&= \\dfrac{2\\sin x\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2 x}}{1 - 2\\sin^2 x}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and its domain is\n     $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\,\\, x \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$\n\n2.  Simplifying the above expression for $(g \\circ f)(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g \\circ f)(x)  & =  \\dfrac{2\\sin x\\sqrt{\\cos^2 x}}{1 - 2\\sin^2 x} \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{2 \\sin x |\\cos x|}{1 - 2\\sin^2 x} \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{2 \\sin x \\cos x}{1 - 2\\sin^2 x} \\quad \\quad \\quad [\\text{since $\\cos x > 0$}] \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\tan 2x  \\hspace{0.2em} \\text{... as required.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g \\circ f)(x) &= \\tan 2x \\\\[8pt]\n    (g \\circ f)'(x) &= 2 \\sec ^2 2x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{\\cos ^2 2x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into the above expression for\n    $(g \\circ f)'(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g \\circ f)'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n    & =  \\dfrac{2}{\\left(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)^2} \\\\[5pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &=  8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Area}     & =  \\int_0^\\frac{\\pi}{6} (g \\circ f)(x)\\,\\mathrm{d} x \\\\[10pt]\n    & =  \\int_0^\\frac{\\pi}{6}  \\tan 2x \\,\\mathrm{d} x \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\int_0^\\frac{\\pi}{6}  \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x} \\,\\mathrm{d} x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let $u = \\cos 2x$. Therefore $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x$.\n\n    We can substitue $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{d}u$ in place of $\\sin 2x \\, \\mathrm{d}x$. Hence we can write\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^\\frac{\\pi}{6} (g \\circ f)(x)\\,\\mathrm{d} x &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\, \\mathrm{d} u \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\bigg(\\ln |u| \\bigg)\\bigg|_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\bigg(\\ln |\\cos 2x| \\bigg)\\bigg|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)- \\ln \\cos 0\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln \\frac{1}{2} - \\ln 1\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln 2^{-1}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{-7.35em}\\displaystyle{\\int_0^\\frac{\\pi}{6}} (g \\circ f)(x)\\,\\mathrm{d} x = \\frac{1}{2} \\ln 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA527","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA527\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{1+e^{3x}}\\, \\mathrm{d}x$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int \\sin 5x \\cos 5x\\, \\mathrm{d}x$ [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Substituting $u =1 + e^{3x}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{1+e^{3x}}\\, \\mathrm{d}x\n    & = \\dfrac 1 3\\int \\dfrac 1 {u}\\,\\textrm du \\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac 1 3 \\ln( u) + C\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.25em} = \\dfrac 1 3 \\ln(1 + e^{3x}) + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Since $\\sin 5x \\cos 5 x = \\dfrac 1 2\\sin 10x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\sin 5x \\cos 5x\\, \\mathrm{d}x\n    & = \\dfrac 1 2\\int \\sin 10 x \\, \\textrm d x\\hspace{1.1cm}\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{.25em} = -\\dfrac 1 {20} \\cos 10 x + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA541","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA541\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^x\\sin (\\pi x) + 4$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$. The graph of\n$f$ is given below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/8d8079cd938bd3fee8ac06dc5fe6de258f4e43d9.svg\" alt=\"8d8079cd938bd3fee8ac06dc5fe6de258f4e43d9.svg\">\n\nThere is an $x$-intercept at the point P, a local maximum point at A,\nwhere $x = a$, and a local minimum point at B, where $x = b$.\n\n1.  Write down the $x$-coordinate of P.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\mathrm{d}x$. Explain why this\n    is not the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    e^x\\sin (\\pi x) + 4 & = 0\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2mm} x = 1.42\n    \\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\hspace{1cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We know that $a$ and $b$ are $x$-coordinates of the min/max. Hence\n    we can graph the function and find the minimum and maximum points on\n    the curve.\n\n    We find that\n      $a = 0.598 \\mbox{ and } b = 1.60$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\int_{0.598}^{1.60} (e^x\\sin (\\pi x) + 4)\\,\\textrm d x  = 2.81\n    \\qquad\\quad \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    $$\n\n    The shaded area is given by the integral\n    $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b |f(x)|\\, \\textrm d x$ which is not equal to\n    $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x$."},{"ref":"AA557","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA557\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph of $f$ passes through the point\n$\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {12},2\\right)$.\n\nGiven that $f'(x) = 2 \\cos (2x)$, find $f(x)$.","markScheme":"We integrate\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)        & = \\int 2 \\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm dx\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\sin (2x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's determine $C$. Substituting $\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {12},2\\right)$\ninto the expression for $f$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5mm}\n2       & = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac \\pi {6}\\right) + C\\\\[6pt]\n2       & = \\dfrac 1 2 + C\\\\[6pt]\nC       & = \\dfrac 3 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\n  $f(x) = \\sin (2x) + \\dfrac 3 2$"},{"ref":"AA567","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA567\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f'(x) = \\sin(2x) \\cos (2x)$. Find $f(x)$, given that $f(\\pi) = 3$.","markScheme":"Substituting $u = \\sin(2x)$ and $\\textrm d u = 2 \\cos (2x) \\textrm d x$,\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)        & = \\int \\sin(2x) \\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm dx\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\int \\dfrac u 2 \\,\\textrm du\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\dfrac {u^2} 4 + C\\\\[6pt]\n& = \\dfrac {\\sin^2(2x)} 4 + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's determine $C$. Substituting $f(\\pi) = 3$ into the expression for\n$f$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3       & = \\dfrac {\\sin^2(2\\pi)} 4+ C\\hspace{.8cm}\\\\[6pt]\nC       & = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\n  $f(x) = \\dfrac {\\sin^2(2x)} 4 + 3$"},{"ref":"AA807","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA807\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nConsider the functions $f$ and $g$ defined on the domain $0< x< 2\\pi$ by\n\n$$\nf(x)=4\\cos 2x \\quad\\text{and}\\quad g(x)=2-8\\cos x.\n$$\nThe following\ndiagram shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/e700b521092372413c6cbd43f59c1c9797a22356.svg\" alt=\"e700b521092372413c6cbd43f59c1c9797a22356.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection of the two\n    graphs.[7 marks]\n\n2.  Find the exact area of the shaded region, giving your answer in the\n    form $a\\pi + b\\sqrt{3}$, where $a$, $b\\in \\mathbb{Q}$.[6 marks]\n\nAt the points P and Q on the diagram, the gradients of the two graphs\nare equal.\n\n3.  Determine the $y$-coordinate of P on the graph of\n    $g$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $x$ be an intersection point of the two graphs, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4\\cos(2x) &= 2 - 8\\cos x \\\\[12pt]\n    4\\left(2\\cos^2 x - 1 \\right)  &= 2-8\\cos x\\\\[12pt]\n    8\\cos^2 x - 4 &= 2 - 8\\cos x \\\\[12pt]\n    8\\cos^2 x + 8\\cos x -6 &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    4\\cos^2 x + 4\\cos x -3 &= 0\\\\\n    \\intertext{Using the quadratic formula, we get}\n    \\cos x &= \\dfrac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{16-4(4)(-3)}}{2(4)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{64}}{8} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\\n    \\intertext{Using that $-1\\leq \\cos x \\leq 1$ the solutions are}\n    \\cos x &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    & \\hspace{-0.52em}x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The area is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_{{\\pi}/{3}}^{{5\\pi}/{3}} \\big[\\hspace{0.1em} g(x)-f(x) \\hspace{0.1em}] \\, \\mathrm{d}x\n    &= \\int_{{\\pi}/{3}}^{{5\\pi}/{3}}  \\big[\\hspace{0.1em} 2-8\\cos x - 4\\cos 2x \\hspace{0.1em}\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\bigg[\\hspace{0.1em} 2x-8\\sin x-2\\sin 2x \\hspace{0.1em}\\bigg]_{\\pi/3}^{5\\pi/3}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10\\pi}{3} - 8\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) - 2\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\[9pt]\n    & \\qquad -\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + 8\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{8\\pi}{3} + 10\\sqrt{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $x$ be the point at which the gradients are equal, thus, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= g'(x) \\\\[12pt]\n    -8\\sin 2x &= 8\\sin x \\\\[13pt]\n    -16\\sin x\\cos x &= 8 \\sin x \\\\[10pt]\n    \\cos x &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{3em}[\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{since } \\sin x \\neq 0\\hspace{0.1em}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, using (1), the $y$-coordinate of P is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(x) &= 2-8 \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA871","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA871\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ defined by $f(x) = 12 - 12\\sin x$, for\n$0 \\leq x \\leq 3\\pi$.\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/bc2e839433d2b4b92bb9023f3a84ace75deaa6a1.svg\" alt=\"bc2e839433d2b4b92bb9023f3a84ace75deaa6a1.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ touches the $x$-axis at points A and B, as shown. The\nshaded region is enclosed by the graph of $y = f(x)$ and the $x$-axis,\nbetween the points A and B.\n\n1.  Find the $x$-coordinate of A and B. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Show that the area of the shaded region is $24\\pi$. [4 marks]\n\nThe following right cone has a total surface area of $24\\pi$, equal to\nthe shaded area in the previous diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/b92d7c53920c6d4b2c99b29a92442bcb40021d46.svg\" alt=\"b92d7c53920c6d4b2c99b29a92442bcb40021d46.svg\">\n\nThe cone has a base radius of $3$, height $h$, and slant height $l$.\n\n3.  Find the value of $l$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Hence, find the volume of the cone. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    12 - 12\\sin x &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\sin x &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{2} \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, the $x$-coordinate of A is\n     $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n      and the $x$-coordinate of B is\n     $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{2}$\n\n2.  The area of the shaded region is defined by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} A &= \\displaystyle \\int_{\\pi/2}^{5\\pi/2} (12-12\\sin x)\\, \\mathrm{d}{x} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\left[12x + 12\\cos x\\right]_{\\pi/2}^{5\\pi/2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\left[12\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{2}\\right) + 12\\cos \\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right] - \\left[12\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + 12\\cos \\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right] \\\\[8pt]\n    &= (30\\pi + 0) - (6\\pi + 0) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 24\\pi \\hspace{3em} \\text{[Shown]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the total surface area of a right cone formula\n    $A = \\pi rl + \\pi r^2$ with $r = 3$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\pi (3)l + \\pi (3)^2 &= 24\\pi \\\\[6pt]\n    3l + 9 &= 24 \\\\[6pt]\n    3l &= 15 \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}l = 5 \\hspace{2.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h^2 + r^2 &= l^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    h^2 + 3^2 &= 5^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    h^2 + 9 &= 25 \\\\[6pt]\n    h^2 &= 16 \\\\[6pt]\n    h &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the volume of a right cone\n    formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.5em} V &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2h \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi (3)^2(4) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 12\\pi \\text{ unit}^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA985","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA985\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined as $f(x)=\\sin{(x^2\\ln (1.5-x) + 2)}+x^3$, for $-1.3\\leq x \\leq 1.3$. The graph of $f$ is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/aa985a.PNG\" alt=\"aa985a\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ intersects the $x$-axis at point A, and has a local maximum at point B.\n\n1. 1.  Find the coordinates of B.\n\n    2. Find the $x$-coordinate of A.[3 marks]\n\n2. Find the total area enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-1.3$ and $x=1.3$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the G.D.C. we find that\n\n    1.  $$\\boxed{\\text{The coordinates of B are $(1.12,2.11)$.}}$$\n\n    2.  $$\\boxed{\\text{The $x$-coordinate of A is $-0.818$.}}$$\n\n2. The area enclosed by $f$, the $x$-axis, $x=-1.3$ and $x=1.3$ is shown in the following diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Trigonometric%20Integration/images/aa985b.PNG\" alt=\"aa985b\">\n\n    Using the G.D.C. we can evaluate the definite integrals. Hence,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{Total Area} &=  R_1 + R_2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=  \\int_{-1.3}^{1.3} f(x) \\,dx  \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=  -\\int_{-1.3}^{-0.818} f(x) \\,dx  + \\int_{-0.818}^{1.3} f(x) \\,dx\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\approx 0.5763 + 2.330 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-4.75em}\\boxed{\\text{Total Area} \\approx 2.91}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA1281","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1281\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Use mathematical induction to prove that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^n\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{n-1}x}&=\\frac{\\sin{2^nx}}{\\sin{x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  1.  Find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin series for $\\sin{8x}$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin series for $\\dfrac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}$.\n\n    3.  Hence find an estimate for $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\cos{x}\\cos{2x}\\cos{4x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.[12 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We start by showing the base case is true.\n\n    This is when $n=1$.\n\n    Let's first consider the value of the L.H.S. of the proposition\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^1\\times \\cos{2^{1-1}x}&=\\textcolor{red}{2\\cos{x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    And the value of the R.H.S. is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\sin{2^1x}}{\\sin{x}}&=\\frac{2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}}{\\sin{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\textcolor{red}{2\\cos{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{L.H.S.}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we have shown the proposition is true when $n=1$.\n\n    The next step is to assume the proposition is true when $n=k$ where $k \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^k\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{k-1}x}&=\\frac{\\sin{2^kx}}{\\sin{x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next we need to use the assumption to show that the proposition is true  when $n=k+1$.\n\n    Hence we need to prove\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^{k+1}\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{k}x}&=\\frac{\\sin{2^{k+1}x}}{\\sin{x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let's start by rewriting the L.H.S. so it looks similar to the L.H.S. of the assumption\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{L.H.S.}&=2\\,\\textcolor{blue}{\\times2^{k}\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{k-1}x}}\\times\\cos{2^{k}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Notice the blue part is now the same as the L.H.S. of the assumption, hence we can replace it with the R.H.S. of the assumption\n    $$4\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=2\\left(\\textcolor{blue}{\\frac{\\sin2^kx}{\\sin{x}}}\\right)\\times\\cos{2^{k}x}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    This is known as the _inductive_ step.\n\n    We can rearrange the trigonometric identity $\\sin{2\\theta}=2\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}$ to\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}&=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin{2\\theta}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and use it to rewrite our L.H.S. giving\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=2 \\times \\frac{\\sin2^kx}{\\sin{x}}\\times\\cos{2^{k}x}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=2\\times \\frac{\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin{(2\\times2^kx})}{\\sin{x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\frac{\\sin{(2\\times2^kx})}{\\sin{x}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{\\sin{2^{k+1}x}}{\\sin{x}}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{...as required.}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\text{R.H.S.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We have shown the proposition is true when $n=1$ and we have shown it to be true when $n=k+1$, assuming $n=k$ is true. Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction the proposition is true for all positive integer values of $n$.\n\n2.  1.  We begin by noting that $\\sin{8x}$ is a composite function.\n\n        Hence we can use the formula for the Macluarin series for sine but replace $x$ with $8x$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\sin{x}&=x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}+\\frac{x^5}{5!}-...\\\\[6pt]\n            \\sin{8x}&=8x-\\frac{(8x)^3}{3!}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We only need the first two non-zero terms hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=8x-\\frac{512x^3}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=8x-\\dfrac{256}{3}x^3\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Let's begin by using a general power series to represent the result of the quotient\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Where $a_i$ are real numbers we must determine.\n\n        We can use the result from part (a) and the general formula for sine to write the numerator and denominator as a Maclaurin series up to the $x^3$ term\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{8x-\\frac{256}{3}x^3}{x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Recall, we only need the first two non-zero terms which will mean up to and including the $x^3$ term.\n\n        It can be challenging to attempt to use long division to rewrite the L.H.S. Often a better strategy, when facing problems like this, is to rewrite the division as a multiplication\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                8x-\\frac{256}{3}x^3&=\\left(x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}\\right)\\left(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We can now expand the R.H.S. until we have all of the terms required to give the $x$ and $x^3$ terms.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                8x-\\frac{256}{3}x^3&=a_0x-\\frac{a_0x^3}{6}+\\cancel{a_1x^2}-\\cancel{\\frac{a_1x^4}{6}}+a_2x^3-\\cancel{\\frac{a_2x^5}{6}}\\\\[6pt]\n                &=a_0x-\\frac{a_0x^3}{6}++a_2x^3\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        There is no $x^2$ term therefore $a_1$ must be zero. Also we don't need any power of $x$ higher than $3$. So we can cancel out another two terms.\n\n        We can now equate the coefficients of the $x-$terms on the left and right side giving\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            8&=a_0\\hspace{6em}\\text{for $x$}\\\\[6pt]\n            -\\frac{256}{3}&=-\\frac{a_0}{6}+a_2\\hspace{3em}\\text{for $x^3$}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can see that $a_0=8$ hence we can use this to find $a_2$\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -\\frac{256}{3}&=-\\frac{8}{6}+a_2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-\\frac{8}{6}+\\frac{6a_2}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n            -512&=-8+6a_2\\\\[6pt]\n            6a_2&=-504\\\\[6pt]\n            a_2&=-84\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore, referring back to the series expansion at the start of the solution, we can see that the first two non-zero terms are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=8-84x^2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  The integral given is a very difficult integral to perform directly.\n\n        However, notice that it is of the form of the proposition from part (a).\n\n        If we take the proposition and let $n=3$ we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            2^3\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} &=\\frac{\\sin{2^3x}}{\\sin{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n            \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x}&=\\frac{\\sin{8x}}{8\\sin{x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we can rewrite the integral as\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\cos{x}\\cos{2x}\\cos{4x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{0.1}\\frac{\\sin{8x}}{8\\sin{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\tfrac{1}{8}\\int_{0}^{0.1}\\frac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The result is still a difficult integral to perform but the key word in the question is _estimate_. This suggests we should use the series expansion from part (b)(ii).\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\cos{x}\\cos{2x}\\cos{4x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&\\approx \\tfrac{1}{8} \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\left(8-84x^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Integrating each term we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\tfrac{1}{8}\\bigg[8x-28x^3\\bigg]_{0}^{0.1}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Substituting in the limits we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\tfrac{1}{8}\\left(8(0.1)-28(0.1)^3\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.1-3.5\\times0.001\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.1-0.0035\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.0965\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1319","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1319\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nLet $f(x)=(2x+1)e^{2x}$.\n\n1.    Show that $f'(x)=4e^{2x}(x+1)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.    Use mathematical induction to prove that $f^{(n)}(x)=2^{n+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\right)$\n    for $n\\geq 1$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [6 marks]\n\nLet another function $g$ be such that $g(x)=\\cos{kx}$, where $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nConsider the function $h$ which is defined by $h(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{ f(x)}$.\n\nIt is known that there is no $x^2$ term in the Maclaurin series for $h(x)$.\n\n3.    Find the possible values of $k$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. The function $f$ is a product of two functions of $x$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&=\\textcolor{red}{(2x+1)}\\textcolor{blue}{e^{2x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We must use the product rule to differentiate it.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{If }& &\\hspace{-5em}&y&=\\textcolor{red}{u}\\textcolor{blue}{v}&\\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{then }&&\\hspace{-5em}&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&= v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}+u\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u&=2x+1&v&=e^{2x}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2e^{2x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting these into the product rule formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)&=e^{2x} \\times 2 + (2x+1) \\times 2e^{2x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and factorising the result we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=2e^{2x}(1 + 2x+1)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=2e^{2x}(2x+2)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=2e^{2x}\\times 2(x+1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=4e^{2x}(x+1)\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Let's begin with the base case. As we are told that $n$ is an integer, for which $n \\geq 1$, then the base case (smallest $n$ value) occurs when $n=1$.\n\n    We substitute $n=\\textcolor{red}{1}$ into the given formula to verify it matches the result from part (a).\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(1)}(x)&=2^{\\textcolor{red}{1}+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{1}+1}{2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^2e^{2x}\\left(x+\\frac{2}{2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=4e^{2x}(x+1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{answer from part (a)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The formula works for the base case.\n    Next we will make the assumption.\n    We will assume that when $n=k$ the formula is true.\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(k)}(x)&=2^{k+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In the next step we must prove that the formula is true when $n=k+1$ by using the assumption (when $n=k$).\n    Let's begin by writing down the formula for when $n=\\textcolor{red}{k+1}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(\\textcolor{red}{k+1})}(x)&=2^{\\textcolor{red}{k+1}+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\textcolor{red}{k+1}+1}{2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+2}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+2}{2}\\right)\\hspace{2em}[1]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Differentiating the assumed case for $n=k$ should produce the expression on the R.H.S. of $[1]$, confirming the formula is true.\n\n    Let's factorise out the constant from the assumption and then use the product rule, in a similar way to part (a), to differentiate it\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[f^{(k)}(x)\\big]&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[2^{k+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)\\bigg]\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+1}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)\\bigg]\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+1}\\bigg[(e^{2x})'\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)+\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)'(e^{2x})\\bigg]\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+1}\\bigg[2e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)+e^{2x}\\bigg]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Don't forget, we are differentiating with respect to $x$ and we treat $k$ as a constant. Hence why $\\left(x+\\frac{k+1}{2}\\right)'=1$.\n\n    Now look at the result, $[1]$, which we are trying to prove. It looks like we should try to factorise our result as much as possible.\n\n    To best match the answer we should take out a factor of $2e^{2x}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=2^{k+1}\\times2e^{2x}\\bigg[\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\bigg]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next we rewrite the factor as $2^{k+1}\\times 2 = 2^{k+2}$ and simplify inside the bracket\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=2^{k+2}e^{2x}\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{k+1+1}{2}\\bigg)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=2^{k+2}e^{2x}\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{k+2}{2}\\bigg)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=[1]\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We must finish the proof with a summary sentence.\n\n    We verified the base case, when $n=1$ and we proved that the formula works for $n=k+1$ if we assume it to be true for $n=k$\n    $\\text{Therefore, we conclude by mathematical induction, that the result is true for all }n\\text{ where }n\\text{ is a positive integer.}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3. In this question, we need to write the Maclaurin expansions for both $f$ and $g$ so that we can attempt to find their quotient. Then using the fact that the coefficient of $x^2$ is zero we can form and solve an equation to find $k$.\n\n    Let's start with $g$. For this, we can use the formula given in the formula booklet\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos{x} &= 1 - \\frac{x^2}{2!} + \\frac{x^4}{4!} - \\dots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall that $g(x) = \\cos{kx}$. We can rewrite the formula using the substitution $kx$ as $x$, giving\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(x) &= \\cos{kx} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\frac{(kx)^2}{2!} + \\frac{(kx)^4}{4!} - \\dots \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now onto $f$. Let's recall the general formula for the Maclaurin series\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= f(0) + xf'(0) + \\frac{x^2}{2!}f''(0) + \\dots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To write the expansion for $f$, we need to evaluate $f(0)$, $f'(0)$, $f''(0)$ and $f'''(0)$.\n    We can use the formula we proved in part (b), choosing $n = 1$ (which we already have), $n = 2$, and $n = 3$.\n    First, evaluate the function $f$ at $n=0$ and $x = 0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= (2(0) + 1)e^{2(0)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next, for $n = 1$ and $x = 0$, we use part (a) to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(1)}(0) &= 4e^{2(0)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Then, for $n = \\textcolor{red}{2}$ and $x = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(2)}(0) &= 2^{\\textcolor{red}{2}+1}e^{2(0)}\\left(0 + \\frac{\\textcolor{red}{2}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8 \\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, for $n = \\textcolor{blue}{3}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(3)}(0) &= 2^{\\textcolor{blue}{3}+1}e^{2(0)}\\left(0 + \\frac{\\textcolor{blue}{3}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 16 \\times 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 32\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note, we are only looking for terms up to and including $x^2$, so you can find the answer without completing the $n=3$ step.\n    Now we can put these results together to get the expansion for $f$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= (2x + 1)e^{2x} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(0) + xf'(0) + \\frac{x^2}{2!}f''(0) + \\frac{x^3}{3!}f'''(0) + \\dots \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 + 4x + \\frac{12x^2}{2!} + \\frac{32x^3}{3!} + \\dots \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next we form $h(x)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(x) &= \\frac{g(x)}{f(x)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\frac{\\left(1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots \\right)}{\\left(1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots \\right)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To find the result when dividing two infinite series in this way it is often better practice to rewrite the expression as a multiplication.\n\n    To do this, let's assume the result is a new infinite series with coefficients of $a_n$, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\left(1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots \\right)}{\\left(1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots \\right)}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now rewrite the quotient as a multiplication\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\left(1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots \\right)=\\left(1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots \\right)(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...) \\hspace{2em}[1]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next, as we need to find the $x^2$ term, we will write the terms up to $x^2$ for the R.H.S. of [1].\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    =&&+a_0&&+4a_0x&&+6a_0x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    &&&&+a_1x&&+4a_1x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    &&&&&&+a_2x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note, by writing the terms in columns like this it makes it easier to combine the coefficients for each $x$ term.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=a_0+(4a_0+a_1)x+(6a_0+4a_1+a_2)x^2+...\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now compare the coefficients of the series above with the L.H.S. of [1]\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &&\\textcolor{red}{a_0}&&\\textcolor{blue}{+(4a_0+a_1)}x&&\\textcolor{green}{+(6a_0+4a_1+a_2)}x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    &&\\textcolor{red}{1}&&\\textcolor{blue}{0}x&&\\textcolor{green}{-\\frac{k^2}{2}}x^2+...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and form the following equations\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        a_0&=1&&&&4a_0+a_1=0&&&&6a_0+4a_1+a_2=-\\frac{k^2}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using $a_0=1$ with the second equation we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4(1)+a_1&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    a_1&=-4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, using those values for $a_0$ and $a_1$, we can find $a_2$ in terms of $k$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    6(1)+4(-4)+a_2&=-\\frac{k^2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    6-16+a_2&=-\\frac{k^2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    a_2&=10-\\frac{k^2}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In the question we are told that the coefficient of the expansion of $h$ is zero. Therefore $a_2=0$. Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0&=10-\\frac{k^2}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{k^2}{2}&=10\\\\[12pt]\n    k&=\\pm\\sqrt{20}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\pm2\\sqrt{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA456","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA456\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA continuous random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f$\ngiven by\n$$\nf(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\n\\dfrac{\\pi x}{16}\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big), & 0 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\\\[9pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nFind $\\mathrm{P}(0 \\leq X \\leq 2)$.","markScheme":"Using the integration by parts formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(0 \\leq X \\leq 2) &= \\int_0^2 \\dfrac{\\pi x}{16}\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\int_0^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\hspace{2em} \\Big[u = \\dfrac{\\pi x}{16},\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{d}v = \\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x\\Big] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\Big[uv\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 - \\int_0^2 v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\hspace{2em} \\Big[v = -\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big),\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\pi}{16}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\Big] \\\\[8pt]\n&= \\Big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\dfrac{x}{4}\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\int_0^2 \\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[10pt]\n&= \\Big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\dfrac{x}{4}\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 + \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\Big[\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 0 + \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\Big[1 - 0\\Big] \\\\[12pt]\n\\mathrm{P}(0 \\leq X \\leq 2)&=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA901","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA901\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x}}$,$x < 1$.\n\n1.  Show that $f''(x) = \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{\\frac{-5}{2}}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Use mathematical induction to prove that[9 marks]\n    $$\n    f^{(n)}(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^n \\dfrac{(2n)!}{n!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-n} \\quad n\\in \\mathbb{Z},\\enskip n\\geq 2.\n    $$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\cos (mx)$, $m\\in \\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nConsider the function $h$ defined by $h(x)=f(x) \\times g(x)$ for $x<1$.\n\nThe $x^2$ term in the Maclaurin series for $h(x)$ has a coefficient of\n$-\\dfrac{3}{4}$.\n\n3.  Find the possible values of $m$.[9 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Taking the derivative with respect to $x$ twice, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)\n    %&= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left(1-x\\right)^{\\frac{-1}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &=  \\dfrac{1}{2} (1-x)^{\\frac{-3}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n    f''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\dfrac{1}{2} (1-x)^{\\frac{-3}{2}}\\right] \\\\[11pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{\\frac{-5}{2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $\\mathrm{P}(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^n \\dfrac{(2n)!}{n!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-n} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$,} \\enskip n\\geq 2]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that $\\mathrm{P}(n)$\n    holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $n\\geq2$.\n\n    :u[Base case]. For $n = 2$, from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2f}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{\\frac{-5}{2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence P$(2)$ holds.\n\n    :u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that $\\mathrm{P}(k)$\n    holds for some $k \\geq 2$. In other words, we assume that\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^kf}{\\mathrm{d}x^k} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^k \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k} \\hspace{0.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    for some $k \\geq 2$.\n\n    :u[Inductive step]. To show that $\\mathrm{P}(k+1)$ holds,\n    we need to show that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k+2)!}{(k+1)!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we differentiate both sides of the equation (IH), we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.55em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^k \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k}\\right] \\\\[14pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^k \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} \\left(k+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1} \\\\[16pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} \\left(4k+2\\right)(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1} \\\\[16pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} \\left(\\dfrac{(2k+2)(2k+1)}{k+1}\\right)(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1} \\\\[16pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k+2)!}{(k+1)!}(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence $P(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n    :u[Conclusion]. P$(2)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 2$, if\n    P$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\n    Hence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for\n    all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $n\\geq2$.\n\n3.  First, we obtain the second derivative of $h$, using the product\n    rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\big[f(x)\\times g(x)\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x) \\\\[10pt]\n    h''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\big[f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x)\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f''(x)g(x)+2f'(x)g'(x)+f(x)g''(x) \\\\[13pt]\n    h''(0) &= f''(0)g(0)+2f'(0)g'(0)+f(0)g''(0)  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    From part (a), we have the following evaluations at $x=0$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x}} & f(0) &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(1-x)^{-3/2} & f'(0) &=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    f''(x) &= \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{-5/2} & f''(0) &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and the respective evaluations for $g(x)$ and its derivatives are\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(x) &= \\cos(mx) & g(0) &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    g'(x) &= -m\\sin(mx) & g'(0) &=0 \\\\[12pt]\n    g''(x) &= -m^2\\cos(mx) & g''(0) &= -m^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting the respective values into equation (1), we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h''(0) &= \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)(1) + 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)(0)+(1)(-m^2)\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{4}-m^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, using the formula\n    for the $x^2$ term in the Maclaurin series of $h(x)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{h''(0)}{2} &= -\\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{4}-m^2 &= -\\dfrac{6}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    m^2 &= \\dfrac{9}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-3mm}m = \\pm \\dfrac{3}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Volumes of Revolution","questions":[{"ref":"AA1280","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1280\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}{2x}$ for $x> 0$.\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the curves $y=f(x)$ and the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ as shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/AA1280q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1280q\">\n\nThe shape of a solid clay sculpture can be modeled by rotating the region $R$ though $2\\pi$ radians about the $y-$axis.\n\nThe top edge of the sculpture has coordinates of $\\left(\\tfrac{1}{6},k\\right)$.\n\nThe volume of clay used to make the sculpture is $a\\pi^2\\,\\text{units}^2$. Where $a \\in \\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nFind a.","markScheme":"In this question we are rotating region $R$ about the $y-$axis.\n\nAccording to the formula booklet rotation about the $y-$axis requires the following formula\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    V&=\\pi \\int_a^b x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThis means we need to find $x=f(y)$ hence we need to rewrite $y=f(x)$ to make $x$ the subject.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    y&=f(x)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    y&=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}{2x}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nLet's begin by squaring both sides and then rewriting the expression\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    y^2&=\\frac{1-9x^2}{4x^2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    4x^2y^2&=1-9x^2\\\\[12pt]\n\n    1&=4x^2y^2+9x^2\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow we can factorise the R.H.S. and make $x$ the subject\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    1&=x^2(4y^2+9)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    x^2&=\\frac{1}{4y^2+9}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    x&=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4y^2+9}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=f(y)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow substitute this expression into the formula mentioned to get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    V&=\\pi \\int_a^{b} \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4y^2+9}}\\right)^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\pi \\int_a^{b} \\frac{1}{4y^2+9}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNext let's consider the limits.\n\nWe have been given the $x-$coordinate of the edge of the vase but, as we are integrating up the $y-$axis, we need to find $k$, the $y-$coordinate.\n\nHence, we find this by evaluating $f$ at $x=\\tfrac{1}{6}$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    k&=f(\\tfrac{1}{6})\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-9\\left(\\tfrac{1}{6}\\right)^2}}{2\\left(\\tfrac{1}{6}\\right)}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-9\\times\\tfrac{1}{36}}}{\\tfrac{1}{3}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=3\\sqrt{1-\\tfrac{1}{4}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=3\\sqrt{\\tfrac{3}{4}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\tfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nHence our expression for the volume with the correct limits is\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    V&=\\pi \\int_0^{\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{4y^2+9}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe now have an expression, with limits in terms of $y$, which we are integrating with respect to $y$. \n\nNotice that the integral looks similar to the following standard integral in the formula booklet\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\int \\frac{1}{a^2+x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{1}{a}\\arctan{\\frac{x}{a}}+C\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nHowever, there are some differences, the first one is that our integral is a function of $y$ but, as we are integrating with respect to $y$ then we can ignore this difference.\n\nThe other difference is we have a composite function for $y$. As we need to be more careful here, we should use a substitution to make sure we perform the integral correctly.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    V&=\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{3^2+(2y)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nLet $u=2y$ therefore $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}y}=2$\n\nIgnoring the limits for now and using $\\mathrm{d}y=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathrm{d}u$ we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ to get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     V&=\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{3^2+u^2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{d}u\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\int \\frac{1}{3^2+u^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nUsing the standard integral with $a=3$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{3}\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)}+C\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWriting our answer back in terms of $y$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2y}{3}\\right)}+C\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nLet's now find the volume by re-introducing the limits.\n\n$$ \n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\left[\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2y}{3}\\right)}\\right]_0^{\\tfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{3}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nRecall, the constant of $C$ will cancel out and we can factorise out any common factors.\n\nSubstituting in the upper and lower limits we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\left[\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2(\\tfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{3})}{3}\\right)}-\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2(0)}{3}\\right)}\\right]\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\arctan{\\sqrt{3}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\times \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n\n     &=\\frac{\\pi^2}{18}\\text{ units}^3\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{a=\\dfrac{1}{18}}$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA293","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA293\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe following table shows the probability distribution of a discrete\nrandom $\\text{variable}$ $Z$,\nin terms of an angle $\\theta$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/00e66067cc36a0da32be98bbfb87aea3442f8386.svg\" alt=\"00e66067cc36a0da32be98bbfb87aea3442f8386.svg\">\n\n1.  Show that $\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{3}{4}$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\tan \\theta$, given that $\\tan \\theta > 0$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$, for $0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ between $x = \\theta$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\nrotated $\\ang{360}$ about the $x$-axis.\n\n3.  Find the volume of the solid formed. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the double angle identity for cosine, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\mathrm{P}(Z = 0) + \\mathrm{P}(Z = 1) &= 1 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{the axiom of probability}] \\\\[10pt]\n    2\\cos 2\\theta + \\cos \\theta &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    2(2\\cos^2 \\theta - 1) + \\cos \\theta &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    4\\cos^2 \\theta + \\cos \\theta - 3 &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    (\\cos \\theta + 1)(4\\cos \\theta - 3) &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    4\\cos \\theta - 3 &= 0 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $\\mathrm{P}(Z = 1) = \\cos \\theta \\geq 0$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.4em} \\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n    $$\n\n2.  Since $\\tan \\theta > 0$ and $\\cos \\theta = 3/4 > 0$, we have\n    $\\sin \\theta > 0$.\n\n    Hence, using the Pythagorean identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} \\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta &= 1 \\\\[11pt]\n    \\dfrac{9}{16} + \\sin^2 \\theta &= 1 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\sin \\theta &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{1-\\dfrac{9}{16}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tan \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}/4}{3/4} \\hspace{1.7em}  \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the volume of revolution formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} V_{\\text{solid}} &= \\int_{\\theta}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\pi y^2 \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\pi \\int_{\\theta}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos^2 x} \\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\pi\\Big[\\tan x\\Big]\\Big|_{\\theta}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\pi\\Big[\\tan \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\tan \\theta\\Big] \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\pi\\bigg[1 - \\dfrac{\\raisebox{-0.1em}{$\\sqrt{7}$}}{3}\\hspace{0.05em}\\bigg] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi\\sqrt{7}}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA470","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA470\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the ellipse defined by the equation $x^2 + 3y^2  = 12$.\n\n1.  Find the equation of the normal to the ellipse at the point\n    P$(3,1)$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Find the volume of the solid formed when the region bounded by the\n    ellipse, the $x$-axis for $x\\geq0$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\geq0$ is\n    rotated through $2\\pi$ about the $y$-axis. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using implicit differentiation, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.3em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[x^2 + 3y^2\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[12\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    2x + 6yy' &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by chain rule}] \\\\[9pt]\n    6yy' &= -2x \\\\[6pt]\n    y' &= -\\dfrac{x}{3y} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $y \\neq 0$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Evaluating the gradient of the tangent to the ellipse at P, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg|_{\\substack{x=3\\\\[1pt]\\hspace{0.025em}y=1}} &= -\\dfrac{3}{3(1)} \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the gradient of the normal to the\n    ellipse at P is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} m &= -\\dfrac{1}{(-1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the point-gradient formula with\n    P$(3,1)$ and $m = 1$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 1 &= (1)(x - 3) \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 1 &= x - 3 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.1em} y = x - 2\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the region bounded by the ellipse, the $x$-axis for\n    $x\\geq0$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\geq0$, we\n    have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/419ccb0adc60ba05e9238d66ea775e2ae02b3750.svg\" alt=\"419ccb0adc60ba05e9238d66ea775e2ae02b3750.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the volume of revolution formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.3em} V &= \\int_0^2 \\pi x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\pi \\int_0^2 \\big(12- 3y^2\\big)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\pi(16) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[17pt]\n    &= 16\\pi\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA479","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA479\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nConsider the function\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\cos x}$,\n$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Show that the $x$-coordinate of the maximum point on the curve\n        $y = f(x)$ satisfies the equation $1 + 2x\\tan x = 0$.\n\n    2.  Determine the values of $x$ for which $f(x)$ is an increasing\n        function. [7 marks]\n\n2.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, showing clearly the maximum point\n    and any asymptotic behaviour. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of the point on the curve $y = f(x)$ where the\n    normal to the curve is perpendicular to the line $y = x$. Give your\n    answers correct to two decimal places. [4 marks]\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis and\nthe lines\n\n$x = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$, $x = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$.\n\n4.  The region is now rotated through $2\\pi$ radians about the\n    $x$-axis. Find the volume of\n    revolution, giving your answer correct to two\n    decimal places. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the quotient rule, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\cos x}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\big[\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\big](2\\cos x) - (\\sqrt{x})\\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-2\\sin x\\big]}{4\\cos^2 x} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\cos x + 2x\\sin x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\cos^2x}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n- Hence, solving the equation $f'(x) = 0$ for $x$ in the\n    interval $\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\Big)$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7em} \\dfrac{\\cos x + 2x\\sin x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\cos^2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos x + 2x \\sin x &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    1 + 2x \\tan x &= 0  \\\\[12pt]\n    x &\\approx 2.98 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence $2.98$ is the only critical number of $f$.\n    Using the ID Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/99904fa2a1440839a3eeb2c7207c2691008819a6.svg\" alt=\"99904fa2a1440839a3eeb2c7207c2691008819a6.svg\">\n\n    The sign of $f'$ changes from positive to negative at $2.98$.\n    Therefore, by the First Derivative Test, $f$ has a local maximum at\n    $2.98$.\n    Hence $x = 2.98$ is the $x$-coordinate of the maximum point that\n    satisfies the equation $(1)$.\n\n    2. By part (a) (i), we have\n     $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < x < 2.98$\n\n2.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 2.98$, we have\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     y &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2.98}}{2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2.98)} \\hspace{1em} \\\\[8pt]\n     &\\approx -0.875 \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n     Hence the maximum point on the\n    curve $y = f(x)$ is $\\mathrm{P}(2.98,-0.875)$.\n    If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, indicating the point P and any\n    asymptotic behaviour, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/9a55367c593466d423486ac263bcb2f605b65bf8.svg\" alt=\"9a55367c593466d423486ac263bcb2f605b65bf8.svg\">\n\n3.\n    Hence, solving the equation $f'(x) = 1$ for $x$ in the interval\n    $\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\Big)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7em} \\dfrac{\\cos x + 2x\\sin x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\cos^2x} &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos x + 2x\\sin x&= 4\\sqrt{x}\\cos^2x \\\\[12pt]\n    1 + 2x\\tan x &= 4\\sqrt{x}\\cos x \\\\[12pt]\n    x &\\approx 2.31 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 2.31$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(2.31) &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2.31}}{2\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2.31)} \\hspace{2em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx -1.13 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of such point\n    are  $(2.31,-1.13)$\n\n4.  Using the volume of revolution formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.9em} V &= \\int_\\frac{3\\pi}{4}^\\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\pi y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\int_\\frac{3\\pi}{4}^\\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\pi \\bigg[\\dfrac{x}{4\\cos^2 x}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[15pt]\n    &\\approx 7.28  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA482","marks":"19","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA482\n\n**Maximum mark: 19**\n\nThe following graph shows the relation\n$x = 5\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi y}{30}\\right) + 10$,  $0 \\leq y \\leq 60$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/a07598c5c0c0ff67d90ad4f1942f075b64d82b6c.svg\" alt=\"a07598c5c0c0ff67d90ad4f1942f075b64d82b6c.svg\">\n\nThe curve is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis to form a volume of\nrevolution.\n\n1.  Calculate the value of the volume generated. [8 marks]\n\nA vase with this shape is made with a solid base of diameter $20$ cm.\nThe vase is filled with water from a faucet at a constant rate of\n$150$ cm$^3$ sec$^{-1}$.\nAt time $t$ sec, the water depth is $h$ cm, $0 \\leq h \\leq 60$ and the\nvolume of water in the vase is $V$ cm$^3$.\n\n2.\n    1.  Given that\n        $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dh} = \\pi \\Big[5\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\Big) + 10\\right]^2$,\n        find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ when\n        $h = 45$ cm. [4 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2h}{\\textrm dt^2}$.\n\n    2.  Find the values of $h$ for which\n        $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2h}{\\textrm dt^2} = 0$.\n\n    3.  By making specific reference to the shape of the vase, interpret\n        $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ at the values of $h$ found in\n        part (c) (ii). [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Calculating the value of the volume of revolution, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V & =  \\pi\\int_0^{60} x^2\\, \\textrm dy \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  \\pi\\int_0^{60} \\Big[5\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi y}{30}\\right) + 10\\Big]^2 \\textrm dy \\quad \\quad \\quad \\Big[\\text{let $u = \\dfrac{\\pi y}{30}$}\\Big] \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  (30\\cdot25)\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\left[\\sin u + 2\\right]^2 \\textrm du  \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  750 \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\left[\\sin^2 u + 4\\sin u + 4\\right]\\,\\textrm du  \\quad \\quad \\quad \\Big[\\text{replace $\\sin^2 u = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(1 - \\cos(2u)\\big)$}\\Big] \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  750 \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\Big[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(1-\\cos(2u)\\big) + 4\\sin u + 4\\Big]\\,\\textrm du \\\\[3pt]\n    & =  375 \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\left[9-\\cos(2u) + 8\\sin u\\right]\\,\\textrm du \\\\[6pt]\n    & =  375\\Big[9u - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2u) - 8\\cos u\\Big]\\Big|_0^{2\\pi} \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  375\\Big[(18\\pi-8) - (-8)\\Big] \\\\[12pt]\n    & =  6750 \\pi \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm$^3$}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} & =  \\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dV} \\\\[6pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{150}{\\pi \\Big[5\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) + 10\\Big]^2} \\\\[8pt]\n        &  =  \\dfrac{6}{\\pi \\Big[\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) + 2\\Big]^2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ at height $h = 45$\n        cm, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} & =  \\dfrac{6}{\\pi \\Big[\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{45\\pi}{30}\\right) + 2\\Big]^2} \\\\[6pt]\n        & =  \\dfrac{6}{\\pi \\big[(-1)+ 2\\big]^2} \\\\[8pt]\n        &  =  \\dfrac{6}{\\pi}\\mbox{\\hspace{0.25em}cm \\hspace{0.1em}sec}^{-1}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  Calculating the $2$nd derivative of $h$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} & = \\dfrac{6}{\\pi}\\Big[\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) + 2\\Big]^{-2} \\\\[10pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\textrm d^2h}{\\textrm dt^2} & = -\\dfrac{12}{\\pi}\\Big[\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) + 2\\Big]^{-3}\\Big[\\dfrac{\\pi}{30}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}\\right)\\Big] \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{12}{\\pi}\\Big[\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) + 2\\Big]^{-3}\\Bigg[\\dfrac{\\pi}{30}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right)\\dfrac{6}{\\pi}\\Big[\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) + 2\\Big]^{-2}\\Bigg]\\\\[10pt]\n        &  =  -\\dfrac{12\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right)}{5\\pi\\Big[\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) + 2\\Big]^5}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Solving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2h}{\\textrm dt^2} = 0$ for $h$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi h}{30}\\right) & =  0 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\pi h}{30} & =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{26mm} h  =  15\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}, \\ 45\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Considering the shape of the vase and part (c) (ii), we can make\n        the following conclusions:\n\n        -   $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ is a minimum at $h = 15$ cm\n            and the vase is widest at this height.\n\n        -   $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ is a maximum at $h = 45$ cm\n            and the vase is narrowest at this height."},{"ref":"AA510","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA510\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is obtained from the graph of $y = \\ln x$ by a\nhorizontal translation $3$ units in the negative $x$ direction and a\nvertical translation $\\ln 6$ units in the positive $y$ direction.\n\n1.  Find $f(x)$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  The region bounded by the graph of $y = f(x)$ and the two axes is\n    rotated through $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis. Find the volume\n    generated. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)        & = \\ln\\big(x - (-3)\\big) + \\hspace{0.05em}\\ln 6 \\\\[8pt]\n    & =  \\ln\\big(6(x + 3)\\big) \\\\[10pt]\n    &  = \\ln\\big(6x + 18\\big)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y       & =  \\ln\\big(6x + 18\\big) \\\\[8pt]\n    e^y         & = 6x + 18 \\\\[8pt]\n    x       & = \\dfrac{1}{6}(e^y - 18).\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    When $x = 0$,\n    $y = \\ln 18$. Hence the volume generated is given by\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V       & = \\pi\\int_0^{\\ln 18} x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\[8pt]\n    & = \\dfrac{\\pi}{36}\\int_0^{\\ln 18} \\big(e^y - 18\\big)^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\[8pt]\n    &  \\approx  42.4 \\qquad \\quad  [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA535","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA535\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n**All lengths in this question are in metres.**\n\nConsider the function\n$f(x) = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16-4x^2}{7}}$,\nfor $-2 \\leq x \\leq 2$. In the following diagram, the shaded\n region is enclosed by the graph of $f$ and\nthe $x$-axis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/b44e497d31bf5071c65617d06bb35c85c800702b.svg\" alt=\"b44e497d31bf5071c65617d06bb35c85c800702b.svg\">\n\nA rainwater collection tank can be modelled by revolving this region by\n$360$ $\\text{\\textdegree{}}$ about the\n$x$-axis.\n\n1.  Find the volume of the tank. [2 marks]\n\nRainwater in the tank is used for drinking, cooking, bathing and other\nneeds during the week.\n\nThe volume of rainwater in the tank is given by the function $g(t)$, for\n$0 \\leq t \\leq 7$, where $t$ is measured in days and $g(t)$ is measured\nin m$^3$. The rate of change of the volume of rainwater in the tank is\ngiven by $g'(t) = 1.5-4\\cos(0.12\\hspace{0.025em}t^2)$.\n\n2.  The volume of rainwater in the tank is increasing only when\n    $a < t < b$.\n\n    1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n    2.  During the interval $a < t < b$, the volume of rainwater in the\n        tank increases by $\\text{\\(d\\)\\hspace{0.25em}m\\(^3\\).}$ Find\n        the value of $d$. [4 marks]\n\nWhen $t=0$, the volume of rainwater in the tank is\n$8.2$ m$^3$. It is known that the tank is never\ncompletely full of rainwater during the $7$ day period.\n\n3.  Find the minimum volume of empty space in the tank during\n    the $7$ day period. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The volume is given by the following integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V   & = \\pi\\int_{-2}^2 \\dfrac {16 - 4 x^2} 7 \\,\\textrm d x\\\\[10pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}  = 19.1\\,\\mbox{m}^3 \\qquad\\quad [\\mbox{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The volume of rainwater is increasing when $g'(t) \\geq 0$,\n        $0\\leq t \\leq 7$. First, let's solve $g'(t) = 0$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        1.5 - 4 \\cos (0.12 t^2)     & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n        t                   & = 3.14,\\,6.52\\,\\mbox{days}\\qquad\\quad [\\mbox{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using the ID Test, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/84db364e5158f46802db700af9ea7e270c462097.svg\" alt=\"84db364e5158f46802db700af9ea7e270c462097.svg\">\n\n        Hence\n          $\\text{a = 3.14} \\mbox { days and } \\text{b = 6.52} \\mbox{ days}$\n    2.  The value of $d$ is given by the integral\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        d   & = \\int_{3.14}^{6.52} (1.5 - 4 \\cos(.12 t^2))\\textrm d t\\\\[8pt]\n        &\\hspace{.275em}  = 11.1\\,\\mbox{m}^3\\qquad\\quad\n        \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  The initial volume is $V(0) = 8.2$ m$^3$. Then the volume decreases\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V(3.14) & = 8.2 + \\int_{0}^{3.14} (1.5 - 4 \\cos(0.12 t^2))\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 8.2 - 6.2\\\\[8pt]\n    & = 2\\,\\mbox{m}^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    After that the volume\n    increases by $11.1$ (part (ii))\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V(6.52) & = 2 + 11.1\\hspace{3.7cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 13.1\\,\\mbox{m}^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Then the volume\n    decreases\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3mm}\n    V(7)        & = 13.1 + \\int_{6.52}^{7} (1.5 - 4 \\cos(0.12 t^2))\\textrm d t\\\\[6pt]\n    & = 13.1 - 0.588\\\\[8pt]\n    & = 12.512\\,\\mbox{m}^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the minimum\n    volume of empty space is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\min}    & = 19.1 - 13.1\\hspace{2.9cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}   = 6\\,\\mbox{m}^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA540","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA540\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3x-6}}$,\nfor  $x > 2$.\n\n1.  Find $\\displaystyle \\int \\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.[2 marks]\n\nPart of the graph of $f$ is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/f2933739f5f260e67ff6bfcc6c9e334c39baed29.svg\" alt=\"f2933739f5f260e67ff6bfcc6c9e334c39baed29.svg\">\n\nThe shaded region $R$ is enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis, and\nthe lines $x = 3$ and $x = 11$.\n\n2.  Find the exact volume of the solid formed when $R$ is\n    rotated $360\\text{\\textdegree}$ about the $x$-axis.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int \\left( \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3x-6}}\\right)^2\\textrm d x\n    & = \\int \\dfrac 1 {3x - 6}\\,\\textrm d x\\hspace{1.5cm}\\\\[9pt]\n    & \\hspace{.275em}= \\dfrac 1 3 \\ln |3x - 6| + C\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The volume is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7cm}\n    V   & = \\pi \\int_3^{11} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3x-6}}\\right)^2\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\pi \\left[\\dfrac 1 3 \\ln(3x - 6)\\right]_3^{11}\\\\[6pt]\n    & = \\dfrac \\pi 3 \\left[\\ln(27) - \\ln (3)\\right]\\\\[9pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = \\dfrac \\pi 3 \\ln 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA546","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA546\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = 9 - 2\\ln(x^2 + 4)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The graph of $f$\npasses through the point $(p,3)$, where\n$p > 0$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$. [2 marks]\n\nThe following diagram shows part of the graph of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/6016001b65155100f0dc6eb98ff244252ca74b02.svg\" alt=\"6016001b65155100f0dc6eb98ff244252ca74b02.svg\">\n\nThe region enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis and the lines\n$x = -p$ and $x = p$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis.\n\n2.  Find the volume of the solid formed. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(p)                & = 3\\\\[6pt]\n    9 - 2\\ln(p^2 + 4)   & = 3\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{-2mm} p = 4.01\\qquad\\quad\n    \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\\hspace{1cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The volume of revolution is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1cm}\n    V       & = \\pi \\int_{-4.01}^{4.01} (9 - 2 \\ln(x^2 + 4))^2\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em} = 605\\qquad\\quad\n    \\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA579","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA579\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = x(x - 3)^2$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 4$. The following graph\nshows $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/73357d47f8356c984a1f6f8487656309e75d281e.svg\" alt=\"73357d47f8356c984a1f6f8487656309e75d281e.svg\">\n\nLet $R$ be the region enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curve of $f$.\n\n1.  Find the area of $R$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the volume of the solid formed when $R$ is rotated $\\ang{360}$\n    about the\\\n    $x$-axis.[2 marks]\n\n3.  The diagram below shows part of the graph of the quadric function\\\n    $g(x) = x(a - x)$. The graph of $g$ crosses the $x$-axis when\n    $x = a$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/24a3dcfcd5f84e0e10dbbdadd1ce6ea632e48932.svg\" alt=\"24a3dcfcd5f84e0e10dbbdadd1ce6ea632e48932.svg\">\n\n    The area enclosed by the graph of $g$ is equal to the area of $R$.\n    Find the\\\n    value of $a$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The area of $R$ is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A       & = \\int_0^3 x (x - 3)^2\\,\\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}= 6.75\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The volume is given by the integral\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V   & = \\pi \\int_0^3 (x(x - 3)^2)^2\\, \\textrm d x\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{.275em}= 65.4\\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^a x(a - x)\\,\\textrm d x  & = 6.75\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.5mm} a  = 3.43\n    \\qquad\\quad\\mbox{[by using G.D.C.]}\n    \\hspace{2cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA934","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA934\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{x^2}-1}$, where $1 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{10}$.\n\n1. Sketch the curve $y=f(x)$, indicating the coordinates of the endpoints. [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. Show that $f^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{x^2+1}}$.\n\n    2. State the domain and range of $f^{-1}$. [5 marks]\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is rotated through $2\\pi$ about the $y$-axis to form a solid of revolution that is used to model a vase.\n\n3. 1. Show that the volume $V$ cm$^3$, of liquid in the vase when it is filled to a height of $h$ centimetres is given by $V=10\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(h)$.\n\n    2. Hence, determine the volume of the vase. [5 marks]\n\nAt $t=0$, the vase is filled to its maximum volume with water. Water is then removed from the vase at a constant rate of $4\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{cm}^3\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{s}^{-1}$.\n\n4. Find the time it takes to completely empty the vase. [2 marks]\n\n5. Find the rate of change of the height of the water when half of the water has been emptied from the vase. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using G.D.C., we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/AA934a.PNG\" alt=\"AA934a\">\n\n2. 1. If we interchange $x$ and $y$, we have $x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{y^2}-1}$.\n\n        Rearranging this equation to make $y$ the subject we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x^2 &= \\dfrac{10}{y^2}-1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n        x^2 + 1 &= \\dfrac{10}{y^2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        y^2 &= \\dfrac{10}{x^2+1} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        y &= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{x^2+1}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-3em}\\boxed{f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{x^2+1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. From part (a), we note that the domain of $f$ is $1 \\leq x \\ \\leq \\sqrt{10}$ and the range of $f$ is $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 3$. \n\n        Given that the range of a function is equal to the domain of its inverse, the domain of $f^{-1}$ is $0 \\leq x \\leq 3$\n\n        Given that the domain of a function is equal to the range of its inverse, the range of $f^{-1}$ is $1 \\leq f^{-1}(x) \\leq \\sqrt{10}$\n\n3. 1. The volume of the solid formed by rotating $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        V &= \\int_a^b\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Rearranging $f(x)$ to make $x^2$ the subject, we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        y &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{x^2}-1} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        y^2 &= \\dfrac{10}{x^2}-1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n        y^2 + 1 &= \\dfrac{10}{x^2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        x^2 &= \\dfrac{10}{y^2+1}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Substituting this expression for $x^2$ into the volume formula, with $a=0$ and $b=h$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        V     & = \\int_0^h \\dfrac{10\\pi}{y^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\[8pt]\n\n        & = 10\\pi  \\int_0^h \\dfrac{1}{y^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y  \\\\[8pt]\n\n        & =  10\\pi \\bigg[\\hspace{0.15em} \\mathrm{arctan}(y) \\hspace{0.15em}\\bigg]_{0}^{h}\\\\[8pt]\n\n        & = 10\\pi \\big[\\hspace{0.15em} \\mathrm{arctan}(h) -  \\mathrm{arctan}(0)\\hspace{0.15em}\\big] \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &  \\hspace{-1.1em}\\boxed{V=10\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(h)}\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. The volume of the vase is found by substituting the value $h=3$ into the equation found in part (c)(i).\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        V & = 10\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(3)  \\\\[8pt]\n\n        & \\approx  39.2\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The volume of the vase is approximately $39.2\\,\\text{cm}^3$\n\n4. The time taken to empty the vase is found by dividing the volume by the rate at which it is emptying. We have $V\\approx39.2$ and rate $=4$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    t &= \\dfrac{39.2}{4}\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 9.8\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    It takes $9.8\\, \\text{seconds}\\,$ to completely empty the vase.\n\n5. When half the water has been emptied, there is $19.6\\,\\text{cm}^3$ remaining. The height of the water at this time can be found using the volume equation from part (c)(i).\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    V & = 10\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(h)  \\\\[8pt]\n\n    19.6 &= 10\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(h)  \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{19.6}{10\\pi} &= \\text{arctan}(h) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    h &= \\text{tan}\\bigg(\\dfrac{19.6}{10\\pi}\\bigg) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 0.7197...\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ when $h=0.7197...$. We have $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-4$, given that the volume of water in the vase is reducing. \n\n    By differentiating our volume equation we can obtain an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    V & = 10\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{arctan}(h)  \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h} &= \\dfrac{10\\pi}{1 + h^2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Relating $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h}$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t}$, we obtain an expression for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t}$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}V} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{1+h^2}{10\\pi} \\times -4\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    When $h=0.7198$ we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} &= \\dfrac{1+(0.7197...)^2}{10\\pi} \\times -4\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= -0.1932...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=-0.193\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    At the time when the vase has been half emptied of water, the rate of change of the height of the water is approximately $-0.193\\, \\text{cm}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"AA937","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA937\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the curve $\\dfrac{x^2}{400}+\\dfrac{(y+5)^2}{225}=1$, where $0 \\leq y \\leq h$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/AA937a.PNG\" alt=\"AA937a\">\n\nThe curve from point C to point P is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis to form a lamp shade. The rectangle ABCD, of height $(10-h)$ cm, is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis to form a solid ceiling fixture.\n\nThe lamp shade is assumed to have a negligible thickness.\n\nGiven that the interior volume of the lamp shade is to be $6\\hspace{0.15em}000\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{cm}^3$, determine the height of the ceiling fixture, length AD in the diagram.","markScheme":"The volume of the solid formed by rotating $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\nV &= \\int_a^b\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nRearranging the given equation to make $x^2$ the subject, we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\dfrac{x^2}{400}+\\dfrac{(y+5)^2}{225} &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\dfrac{x^2}{400} &= 1 - \\dfrac{(y+5)^2}{225} \\\\[10pt]\n\nx^2 &= 400\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{(y+5)^2}{225}\\bigg)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $x^2$ into the volume formula, with $a=0$ and $b=h$, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\nV &= \\int_0^h 400\\pi\\hspace{0.15em}\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{(y+5)^2}{225}\\bigg)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThe interior volume of the lampshade is required to be $6\\hspace{0.15em}000\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{cm}^3$, hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n400\\pi\\,\\int_0^h \\hspace{0.15em}1 - \\dfrac{(y+5)^2}{225}\\,\\mathrm{d}y &= 6\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\int_0^h \\hspace{0.15em}1 - \\dfrac{(y+5)^2}{225}\\,\\mathrm{d}y &= \\dfrac{6\\hspace{0.15em}000}{400\\pi} \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\bigg[\\hspace{0.15em} y - \\dfrac{(y+5)^3}{675} \\hspace{0.15em}\\bigg]_{y=0}^{y=h} & = \\dfrac{15}{\\pi} \\\\[10pt]\n\nh - \\dfrac{(h+5)^3}{675} - \\bigg(0 - \\dfrac{(0+5)^3}{675}\\bigg) &= \\dfrac{15}{\\pi} \\\\[10pt]\n\nh - \\dfrac{(h+5)^3}{675} + \\dfrac{5}{27} &= \\dfrac{15}{\\pi}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nUsing the G.D.C. to sketch the graphs of $y=h - \\dfrac{(h+5)^3}{675} + \\dfrac{5}{27}$ and $y=\\dfrac{15}{\\pi}$, we get\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/AA937b.PNG\" alt=\"AA937b\">\n\nThe height of the lampshade is approximately $7.445$ cm and therefore the height of the ceiling fixture is\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n10 - 7.445 &= 2.555\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThe height of the ceiling fixture is approximately $2.56\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\,$."},{"ref":"AA993","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA993\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=-\\dfrac{2^x}{x+1}+8$, where $x \\geq 0$.\n\n1. Find $f'(x)$. [4 marks]\n\n2. Find the gradient of $f(x)$ when $x=3$. [1 mark]\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis and the $y$-axis is rotated through $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis to form a solid of revolution.\n\n3. Find the volume of this solid. [4 marks]\n\nConsider a function $g$ such that $g(6)=2$ and $g'(6)=7$.\n\n4. Find the value of\n\n    $$\\\\[1em]$$\n\n    1. $(g\\circ f)(3)$\n\n    2. $(g \\circ f)'(3)$ [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. Use the quotient rule\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(x)&=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}(-\\frac{2^x}{x+1}+8)}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\[10pt]\n        &=-\\dfrac{\\ln 2 \\times 2^x\\times(x+1)-2^x}{(x+1)^2}\\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2^x-\\ln 2 \\times 2^x\\times(x+1)}{(x+1)^2}\\\\[10pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{2^x(1-(x+1)\\ln2)}{(x+1)^2}\\hspace{0.525em}\\\\[10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2. Use the function from (a)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f'(3) &= -0.886294...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The gradient at $x=3$ is $-0.886$.\n\n3. Consider the following diagram which shows the part of the curve which will be rotated.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Calculus%20II/Volumes%20of%20Revolution/images/ans.png\" alt=\"ans\">\n\n    We need to find, $b$, the $x$-axis intercept.\n    Using G.D.C. the value of $b=5.75621...$\n    Now use the formula for the volume of revolution\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V&=\\pi\\int_{0}^{5.75621...} \\left(-\\dfrac{2^x}{x+1}+8\\right)^2 \\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[6pt]\n        &=578.460...\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The volume of the rotated shape is $578$ units$^3$.\n4. 1. First use G.D.C. to find the value of $f(3)=6$. Then use composite functions\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            (g\\circ f)(3)&=g(f(3))\\\\[3pt]\n            &=g(6)\\\\[3pt]\n            &=2\\\\[3pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2. Use chain rule and the answer from part (b)\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            (g \\circ f)'(3)&=g'(f(3))\\times f'(3)\\\\[3pt]\n            &=g'(6)\\times  -0.886294...\\\\[3pt]\n            &=7\\times  -0.886294...\\\\[3pt]\n            &=-6.20\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"}]}]},{"name":"Functions","subtopics":[{"name":"Composite & Inverse Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA1060","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1060\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe table below shows some values of the domain and range for the function $f(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/aa1060a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1060a\">\n\nThe graph below shows $y=g(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/aa1060b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1060b\">\n\n1. Evaluate\n\n    1. $f(2)$\n\n    2. $g(f(2))$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Find $f^{-1}(g(2))$ [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Using the table for $f(x)$ we can write\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &\\boxed{f(2)=5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n    2. Using our previous answer and the point $(5, 3)$ on $g(x)$ we can write\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &g(f(2))=g(5) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{g(f(2))=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. We can evaluate $g(2)=4$ using the graph.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall the definition of an inverse function if $f(a)=b$ then $f^{-1}(b)=a$.\n\n    From the table we can see that $f(3)=4$, therefore \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f^{-1}(g(x))=f^{-1}(4)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\boxed{f^{-1}(4)=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA125","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA125\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{2x+1}$, for\n$-14 \\leq x \\leq 13$.\n\n1.  Write down the range of $f$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for $f^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the domain and range of $f^{-1}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    1 $f$ is continuous and increasing;\n\n    2 $f(-14) = -3$ and $f(13) = 3$.\n\n    Hence the range of $f$ is\n     $[-3,3]$\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[13pt]\n    f\\left(y\\right) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    \\sqrt[3]{2y+1} &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    2y+1 &= x^3 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x^3 - 1}{2} \\hspace{1.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence an\n    expression for $f^{-1}$ is\n     $f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x^3 - 1}{2}$\n\n3.  The domain and range of $f^{-1}$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{domain}: & \\hspace{0.75em} \\hspace{0.525em}[-3,3] \\\\[8pt]\n    \\text{range}: & \\hspace{0.75em} \\hspace{0.525em}[-14,13]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1269","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1269\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ for $-3\\leq x\\leq 4$ is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-q\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $f(2)$. [1 mark]\n\nLet $g(x)=2f(x)-1$ for $-3\\leq x\\leq 4$. \n\n2.  On the axes above, sketch the graph of $g$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence determine the value of $(g\\circ f)(2) $. [1 mark]\n\n4.  Hence solve the equation $(f\\circ g)(x)=0$ when $x>0$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x)$ when $x=2$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a1\">\n\n    Hence $\\boxed{f(2)=1}$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n       \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  The function $f(x)$ is mapped to $g(x)$ by two transformations.\n\n    By considering $g(x)=\\textcolor{blue}{2}f(x)\\textcolor{green}{-1}$, we can see there is a $\\text{\\textcolor{blue}{vertical stretch by a scale factor of}}\\,\\,\\textcolor{blue}{2}$ and a $\\text{\\textcolor{green}{vertical shift down}}\\,\\textcolor{green}{1}\\,\\text{\\textcolor{green}{unit}}$.\n\n    The vertical stretch ($\\textcolor{blue}{y=2f(x)}$) is shown in blue below. \n\n    Note that the vertical distance from the $y$-axis of every point on the curve is doubled. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a2\">\n\n    Then the vertical shift is shown in green ($\\textcolor{green}{y=2f(x)-1}$) .\n\n    Note that every point on the blue curve is shifted vertically down $1$ unit.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a3\">\n\n    Correct local minimum at $(-2,1)$\n\n    $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Correct $y$-intercept at $(0,5)$\n\n    $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3.  From part (a) we know that $f(2)=1$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (g\\circ f)(2)&=g(f(2))\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=g(1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence $\\boxed{(g\\circ f)(2)=3}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Here is the graph of $g(x)$ showing that $g(1)=3$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a4\">\n\n4.  We have the equation $(f\\circ g)(x)=0$. The L.H.S. is a composite function which can be rewritten\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (f\\circ g)(x)&= f(\\textcolor{red}{g(x)})\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Here we can see that $\\textcolor{red}{g(x)}$ is the inner function and $f(x)$ is the outer function, meaning that the output of $g$ will be the input of $f$.\n\n    By considering the graph of $f(x)$ we see that when $x=3$ then $f(x)=0$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/AA1269-a5.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1269-a5\">\n\n    This means in order to have an output of $0$, the input must be $3$.\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(\\textcolor{red}{g(x)})=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n        f(\\textcolor{red}{3})=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we need to find the value of $x$, where $x>0$, such that $g(x)=3$. \n\n    From part (c), we know that $g(1)=3$, and we can see that this is the only possible solution when $x>0$.\n\n    Hence if $x>0$ and $(f\\circ g)(x)=0$ then $x=1$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA130","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA130\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined as $f(x) = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{6+2x}{6-2x}}$, for\n$-3 \\leq x < 3$.\n\nFind the inverse function of $f$, stating its domain and range.","markScheme":"If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for $y = f^{-1}(x)$,\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[16pt]\nf(y) &= x \\\\[11pt]\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6+2y}{6-2y}} &= x \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{6+2y}{6-2y} &= x^2 \\\\[12pt]\n6 + 2y &= 6x^2-2x^2y \\\\[16pt]\n3 + y &= 3x^2-x^2y \\\\[16pt]\n(x^2 + 1)y &= 3x^2 - 3 \\\\[12pt]\ny &= \\dfrac{3x^2 - 3}{x^2 + 1} \\hspace{3em}  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the\ninverse function of $f$ is\n $f^{-1}(x) =  \\dfrac{3x^2 - 3}{x^2 + 1}$\n\nThe domain of $f^{-1}$ is\n $[0,\\infty)$\nand range of $f^{-1}$ is\n $[-3,3)$"},{"ref":"AA132","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA132\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 5x^2 + 3x + 2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the coordinates of the local\n    maximum point. Round your answers to **three significant figures**.\n    [3 marks]\n\nThe domain of $f$ is now restricted to $[a,b]$ where\n$a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Write down the smallest value of $a < 0$ and the largest value\n        of $b > 0$ for which $f$ has an inverse. Give your answers\n        correct to **three significant figures**.\n\n    2.  For these values of $a$ and $b$, sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$\n        and $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same set of axes, showing clearly the\n        coordinates of the end points of each curve.\n\n    3.  Solve the equation $f^{-1}(x) = -1$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/9444e78d503e38688d4c8ecfe5c29f2d0705556f.svg\" alt=\"9444e78d503e38688d4c8ecfe5c29f2d0705556f.svg\">\n\n    From the diagram, we see that $f$ is positive on the intervals\n    $(-\\infty,-2)$, $(-0.414,1)$ and $(2.41,\\infty)$, and negative on\n    $(-2,-0.414)$ and $(1,2.41)$.\n\n    Hence the set of solutions to the inequality $f(x) < 0$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (-2,-0.414)\\cup(1,2.41)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  From the diagram, we see that the graph of $y = f(x)$ has a local\n    maximum at point B.\n\n    Hence the coordinates of the local maximum point are\n     $(0.285, 2.43)$\n\n3.\n    1.  From the diagram, we see that $f$ is a bijection if its domain\n        is restricted to $[-1.14,0.285]$ or $[0.285,1.87]$.\n        Hence $f$ has an inverse if\n         $a = -1.41$\n        and  $b = 0.285$\n\n    2.  The graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$ across the line $y = x$.\n\n        Hence, if we sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = f^{-1}(x)$\n        on the same set of axes, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/b6185b0f42f1522008f7c0707456fd536c854029.svg\" alt=\"b6185b0f42f1522008f7c0707456fd536c854029.svg\">\n\n    3.  If we solve the equation $f^{-1}(x) = -1$ for $x$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(f^{-1}(x)) &= f(-1) \\hspace{4em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{10.7em} x = -4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(f^{-1}(x)) = x$}]\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA160","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA160\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3 + 1$ and $g(x) = x-2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f(2)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Solve $(f\\circ g)(x) = 0$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x) = x^3 + 1$ for $x = 2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} f(2) &= 2^3 + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8 + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for $y = f^{-1}(x)$,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    f(y) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y^3 + 1 &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y^3 &= x - 1 \\\\[5pt]\n    y &= \\sqrt[3]{x-1} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2em} f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x-1}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $(f\\circ g)(x) = 0$ for $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(g(x)) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n    f(x-2) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{4.6em} (x-2)^3 + 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x-2 &= \\sqrt[3]{-1} \\hspace{3em} [=-1] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.7em} x = 1\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA161","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA161\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ and $g(x) = x - 3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f(3)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $(g\\circ f)(3)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $g^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ for $x = 3$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} f(3) &= 3^2 + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9 + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $(g\\circ f)(x) = g(f(x))$ for $x = 3$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.4em} (g\\circ f)(3) &= g(f(3)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(11) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11 - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $(g\\circ g^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = g^{-1}(x)$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(g^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    g(y) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 3 &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= x + 3 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g^{-1}(x) = x+3 \\hspace{0.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA165","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA165\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3$ and $g(x) = 2x - 1$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $g^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $(g\\circ f)(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Solve the equation $(g\\circ f)(x) = 0$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(g\\circ g^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = g^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(g^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    g(y) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    2y - 1 &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    2y &= x + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x + 1}{2} \\hspace{1.9em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{2} \\hspace{0.6em}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ f)(x) &= g(f(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(x^3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2x^3-1 \\hspace{1.1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $(g\\circ f)(x) = 0$ for $x$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.5em} 2x^3-1 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b)}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{12.2em} x \\approx 0.794 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA169","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA169\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sqrt{x-3}$, for $x \\geq 3$.\n\n1.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = x^2 +1$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2.  Find $(g\\circ f)(28)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the range of:\n    1.  $f^{-1}$;\n\n    2.  $g$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[7pt]\n    f(y) &= x \\\\[5pt]\n    \\sqrt{y - 3} &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y - 3 &= x^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= x^2 + 3 \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    f^{-1}(x) = x^2 + 3 \\hspace{0.4em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $f(x) = \\sqrt{x - 3}$ for $x = 28$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} f(28) &= \\sqrt{28-3} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{25} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ f)(28) &= g(f(28)) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= g(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5^2 +1 \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 26\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.\n        $[3,\\infty)$ or $y \\geq 3$\n\n    2.\n        $[1,\\infty)$ or $y \\geq 1$"},{"ref":"AA170","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA170\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = 4x - 3$ and $g(x) = 2x$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $g(5)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find $(f\\circ g)(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $(f\\circ g)^{-1}(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $g(5) = 10$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ g)(x) &= f(g(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(2x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4(2x) - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8x - 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $h(x) = (f\\circ g)(x)$.\n    Hence $h^{-1}(x) = (f\\circ g)^{-1}(x)$.\n\n    If we solve the equation $(h\\circ h^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = h^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(h^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    h(y) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y - 3 &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y &= x + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x + 3}{8} \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    (f\\circ g)^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x + 3}{8} \\hspace{2.7em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA171","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA171\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sqrt{x+7}$, for $x \\geq -7$.\n\n1.  Find $f^{-1}(3)$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g$ be a function such that $g^{-1}$ exists for all real numbers.\n\n2.  Given that $g(9) = 4$, find $(f\\circ g^{-1})(4)$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(3) = 3$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(3)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(3)) &= 3 \\hspace{3.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    f(y) &= 3 \\\\[4pt]\n    \\sqrt{y + 7} &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    y + 7 &= 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 2 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    f^{-1}(3) = 2 \\hspace{2.4em}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4 &= g(9) \\\\[6pt]\n    g^{-1}(4) &= g^{-1}(g(9)) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{11em} g^{-1}(4) &= 9 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{since $(g^{-1}\\hspace{-0.1em}\\circ g)(9) = 9$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ g^{-1})(4) &= f(g^{-1}(4)) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= f(9) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{9+7} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{16} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA174","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA174\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x+2)^3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g$ be a function so that $(f\\circ g)(x) = 27x^6$.\n\n2.  Find $g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[7pt]\n    f(y) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    (y+2)^3 &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    y+2 &= \\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\sqrt[3]{x} - 2 \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.3em} f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x} - 2\n    $$\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ g)(x) = 27x^6$ for $y = g(x)$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(g(x)) &= 27x^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    f(y) &= 27x^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    (y+2)^3 &= 27x^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    y+2 &= 3x^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 3x^2 - 2 \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.5em} g(x) = 3x^2 - 2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA175","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA175\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$, for\n$-4 \\leq x \\leq 5$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/8dc73726e7704b5f5e31e8ab0e8d2ebbdc109321.svg\" alt=\"8dc73726e7704b5f5e31e8ab0e8d2ebbdc109321.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of:\n    1.  $f(1)$;\n\n    2.  $f^{-1}(2)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the domain of $f^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same grid above.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $f(1) = 3$\n\n    2.\n        $f^{-1}(2) = 0$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{domain of $f^{-1}$} &= \\text{range of $f$} \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [-2,4]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$ across the line $y = x$.\n\n    Hence, if we sketch the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/90bb640268e36201860be09ad192280e600a64a2.svg\" alt=\"90bb640268e36201860be09ad192280e600a64a2.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA197","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA197\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nTwo functions, $f$ and $g$, are defined in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/f16cb92d520a6fc6d08cc849fc6b256348e1335f.svg\" alt=\"f16cb92d520a6fc6d08cc849fc6b256348e1335f.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $f(2)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of $(g\\circ f)(2)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $g^{-1}(5)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $f(2) = 0$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ f)(2) &= g(f(2)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (g\\circ g^{-1})(5) &= 5 \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    g(g^{-1}(5)) &= 5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.7em} g^{-1}(5) = -4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $g(-4) = 5$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA200","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA200\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2x$, $g(x) = 4x + 6$ and $h(x) = (f\\circ g)(x)$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $h(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $h^{-1}(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.3em} h(x) &= (f\\circ g)(x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(g(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(4x+6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(4x+6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8x+12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(h\\circ h^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = h^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(h^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    h(y) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y+12 &= x \\\\[12pt]\n    8y &= x-12 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{x-12}{8} \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    h^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x-12}{8}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA207","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA207\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe functions $f$ and $g$ are defined such that $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{3}$\nand $g(x) = 12x+4$.\n\n1.  Show that $(g\\circ f)(x) = 4x-4$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $(g\\circ f)^{-1}(a) = 10$, find the value of $a$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= g\\hspace{0.05em}(f(x)) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= g\\Big(\\dfrac{x-2}{3}\\Big) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 12\\Big(\\dfrac{x-2}{3}\\Big)+4 \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 4\\hspace{0.05em}(x-2) + 4 \\\\[13pt]\n    &= 4x-4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.25em} (g\\circ f)^{-1}(a) &= 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    (g\\circ f)(10) &= a \\\\[6pt]\n    4(10)-4 &= a \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part $(a)$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    a = 36\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA742","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA742\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe area, $A$, of a given square can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(P) &= \\dfrac{P^2}{16}, \\hspace{0.5em} P \\geq 0,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere\n$P$ is the perimeter of the square.\n\nThe graph of the function $A$, for $0 \\leq P \\leq 20$, is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/8c2c1a298c1b79e92e64f94fe2b4a4d452e488ad.svg\" alt=\"8c2c1a298c1b79e92e64f94fe2b4a4d452e488ad.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $A(20)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  On the grid above, draw the graph of the inverse function, $A^{-1}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  In the context of the question, explain the meaning of\n    $A^{-1}(4) = 8$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $A(P)$ for $P = 20$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A(P) &= \\dfrac{20^2}{16} \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of the inverse function, $A^{-1}$, on the grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/508588bd8662a40575516587374d1e3379be6249.svg\" alt=\"508588bd8662a40575516587374d1e3379be6249.svg\">\n\n3.\n    $\\text{For a given square, when the area is \\(4\\), the perimeter is \\(8\\)}$"},{"ref":"AA743","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA743\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe circumference, $C$, of a given circle can be represented by the\nfunction\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(A) &= 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{A}{\\pi}}, \\hspace{0.5em} A \\geq 0,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is the area of the circle.\n\nThe graph of the function $C$, for $0 \\leq A \\leq 24$, is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/3388e210bb4d6f02426816cb2651396f2822c4c1.svg\" alt=\"3388e210bb4d6f02426816cb2651396f2822c4c1.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $C(24)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  On the grid above, draw the graph of the inverse function, $C^{-1}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  In the context of the question, explain the meaning of\n    $C^{-1}(2\\pi) = \\pi$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $C(A)$ for $A = 24$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    C(A) &= 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{24}{\\pi}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 17.4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of the inverse function, $C^{-1}$, on the grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/fae26733051cc1a7c15c3478abb0d0697e69d7fa.svg\" alt=\"fae26733051cc1a7c15c3478abb0d0697e69d7fa.svg\">\n\n3.\n    $\\text{For a given circle, when the circumference is \\(2\\pi\\), the area is \\(\\pi\\)}$"},{"ref":"AA746","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA746\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA town is planning to construct a jogging path in a grass field\n$170$ m long and $70$ m\nwide. The path is to be the shape of a rectangle with two semicircles of\nradius $x$, as shown in the diagram. The sides of the rectangle\nconnecting the circles are to be $100$ m long.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/22448faee8b9c6574b3fdefa8505f67030a94583.svg\" alt=\"22448faee8b9c6574b3fdefa8505f67030a94583.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down a function, $P$, (in metres) for the perimeter of the\n    jogging path, in terms of the radius, $x$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Determine the domain and range of $P$, taking into consideration the\n    dimensions of the grass field.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Find an equation for the inverse function $P^{-1}(x)$. Express your\n    answer in the form $P^{-1}(x)=mx+c$.[2 marks]\n\nThe designers of the path are deciding whether the total length of the\npath should be $300$ m,\n$400$ m, or $500$ m. The\ndesigners want to maximise the perimeter of the path, but fit the path\nin the grass field.\n\n4.  Determine which length is most suitable, given the\n    dimensions of the grass field.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The perimeter for the path is the sum of the two straight sides\n    connecting the circles and the perimeter of a complete circle of\n    radius $x$. We get\n    $$\n    P(x) = 200+2\\pi x\n    $$\n\n2.  The length of the path will be $100+2x$ and its width $2x$.\n    Therefore we require $100+2x\\leq 170$ and $2x\\leq 70$. Hence\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Domain: } 0 < x \\leq 35.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    For\n    the range, the function $P(x)$ is increasing and $P(0)=200$ and\\\n    $P(35)=200+70\\pi$. We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Range: } 200\\leq P(x) \\leq 200+70\\pi.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let's denote $P(x)$ by $y$. To find the inverse we can interchange\n    the variables $x$ and $y$ and rearrange for $y$, as follows\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 200+2\\pi x\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    x &= 200+2\\pi y\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    y &= \\dfrac{x-200}{2\\pi}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-3.2em}P^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}x - \\dfrac{100}{\\pi}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5.5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  For each of the proposed perimeters $P$, we can find the radius\n    value $x$ as $x=P^{-1}(P)$, then the length and width of the running\n    track can be calculated, as shown in the following table.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/8985560368df68be350c8eca5afbe5b19a733963.svg\" alt=\"8985560368df68be350c8eca5afbe5b19a733963.svg\">\n\n    From the table, we can see that the largest perimeter of the options\n    that still fit inside the grass field dimensions is\n\n    $$\n    P=400\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}.\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA747","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA747\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nHarry is planning on constructing a glass window in one of the outer\nwalls of his house. The dimensions of the wall space available are 2m x\n2m. Harry wants the window to be in the shape shown in the diagram\nbelow. The bottom section is a rectangle and the top part is a\nsemicircle of radius $x$ m. Harry wants the\nheight of the rectangle to be fixed at 1 m.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/4b81f49038bd3a70fed3ed55e752da67f426bdf2.svg\" alt=\"4b81f49038bd3a70fed3ed55e752da67f426bdf2.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down a function $P$ (in metres) for the perimeter of the\n    window in terms of the radius, $x$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Determine the domain and range of $P$, taking into consideration the\n    dimensions of the available wall.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Find an equation for the inverse function $P^{-1}(x)$.[2 marks]\n\nHarry wants to maximise the size of the window, however the window frame\nthat covers the perimeter of the window can only be 5, 6, or 7 metres\nlong, due to manufacturing restrictions.\n\n4.  Determine which perimeter length is the best option for\n    Harry.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The perimeter for the window is the sum of the three sides of the\n    rectangle and the semicircle, thus we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    P(x) &= \\text{Rectangle width}+2\\times\\text{Rectangle height}+\\text{Semicircle} \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &= 2x+2(1)+\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}\\pi x}{\\cancel{2}} \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{8mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-2.15em}P(x) = 2+(2+\\pi)x\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The width of the window will be $2x$ and the height $1+x$.\n    Therefore, we require $2x\\leq 2$ and $1+x\\leq 2$.\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Domain: } 0\\leq x \\leq 1.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now\n    for the range, the function $P(x)$ is increasing and $P(0)=2$ and\n    $P(1)=4+\\pi$. Therefore,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Range: } 2\\leq P(x) \\leq 4+\\pi.\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let's denote $P(x)$ by $y$. To find the inverse we can interchange\n    the variables $x$ and $y$ and rearrange for $y$, as follows\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 2+(2+\\pi)x\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{3mm}\\\\\n    x &= 2+(2+\\pi)y\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    y &= \\dfrac{x-2}{(2+\\pi)}\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-3.2em}P^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{(2+\\pi)}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{6mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  For each of the proposed perimeters $P$, we can find the radius\n    value $x$ as $x=P^{-1}(P)$, then the length and width of the window\n    can be calculated, as shown in the following table\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Composite%20%26%20Inverse%20Functions/images/95900dceeb57876cf3bec3ce67dc138811471933.svg\" alt=\"95900dceeb57876cf3bec3ce67dc138811471933.svg\">\n\n    From the table, we can see that the perimeter that maximizes the\n    dimension of the window that fits in the wall is\n\n    $$\n    P=7\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}.\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Exponential and Logarithmic Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA044","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA044\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIt has been suggested that the number of visits (the number of hits) to\na newly launched website can be modelled by an equation of the form\n$H = aN^b$, where $H$ is the number of hits in each month,\n$N$ is the month number, $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nTo test this model, a new website was created and the number of hits in\neach of the first $10$ months were recorded.\n\nThe results are shown in the following table for $N = 1$ and $N = 2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/bc28e82540342d957840e8bc6479dc4995148f25.svg\" alt=\"bc28e82540342d957840e8bc6479dc4995148f25.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $a$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $b$, giving your answer to five significant\n        figures. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Use this model to estimate the number of hits in the\n    $8$th month. Give\\\n    your answer to the nearest integer. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Substituting $H = 32$ and $N = 1$ in the equation $H = aN^b$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        32 &= a(1)^b \\hspace{2.45em}  \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = 32 \\hspace{3.2em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Substituting $H = 100$ and $N = 2$ in the equation $H = 32N^b$\n        and solving the resulting equation for $b$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        100 &= 32(2)^b \\\\[6pt]\n        b &= \\log_2\\left(\\dfrac{100}{32}\\right) \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        b \\approx 1.6439 \\hspace{1.3em}\n        $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $H = 32\\hspace{0.05em}Y^{1.6439}$ for $N = 8$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.25em} H &= 32(8)^{1.6439} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 977\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1042","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1042\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe brightness of a star can be measured in terms of its apparent magnitude as seen from Earth. The apparent magnitude $M$ of a star is given by the formula\n$$\nM = -2.5 \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F}{F_0}\\right)\n$$\nwhere $F$ is the observed flux density and $F_0$ is a reference flux density.\n\n1. Solve for $F$ in terms of $M$.[2 marks]\n\n2. Suppose that $M(F_1)=1$ and $M(F_6)=6$. Find the value of $\\dfrac{F_1}{F_6}$.[1 mark]\n\nNow, let's look at the difference in magnitude of two stars. Suppose that star $1$ has observed flux density of $F_1$ with magnitude $M_1$ while star $2$ has flux density $F_2$ with magnitude $M_2$.\n\n3. Find an expression for the difference in magnitude $M_2-M_1$.[2 marks]\n\nThe apparent magnitude of the sun is $M_s=-26.8$ and the apparent magnitude of the full moon is $M_m=-12.7$.\n\n4. Their brightness factor is $\\dfrac{F_s}{F_m}=10^{\\tfrac{a}{b}}$ where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Q^+}$. Find $a$ and $b$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. By applying logarithm and exponential properties, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    M &= -2.5\\big[\\log(F)-\\log(F_0)\\big] \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[10pt]\n    M &= -2.5\\log(F)+2.5\\log(F_0) \\\\[11pt]\n    2.5\\log(F) &= 2.5\\log(F_0) - M \\\\[8pt]\n    \\log(F) &= \\log(F_0)- \\dfrac{M}{2.5} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.2em}F = F_0 \\cdot 10^{-\\frac{M\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Using the previous answer, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{F_1}{F_6} &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{F_0} \\cdot 10^{-\\frac{1\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}}{\\cancel{F_0}\\cdot  10^{-\\frac{6\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10^{-\\frac{1\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}}{10^{-\\frac{6\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 10^{\\frac{6\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}-\\frac{1\\rule[-1pt]{0pt}{6pt}}{2.5}}\\\\[10pt]\n    &= 10^{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{F_1}{F_6} &=100 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Using logarithm properties again, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    M_2-M_1 &= -2.5\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\right) - \\left[-2.5\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -2.5\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right]\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -2.5\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\div\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -2.5\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_0}\\div\\dfrac{F_1}{F_0}\\right)\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n    M_2-M_1 &=-2.5 \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_2}{F_1}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. From part (c), we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -2.5 \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_s}{F_m}\\right) &= M_s-M_m \\\\[10pt]\n    \\log\\left(\\dfrac{F_s}{F_m}\\right) &= \\dfrac{-26.8-(-12.7)}{-2.5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{F_s}{F_m} &=10^{\\tfrac{-14.1}{-2.5}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{-2.8em}a=14.1 \\text{ and }b=2.5 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1055","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1055\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe temperature of a chocolate is given by the equation\n$$\n%60 \\hspace{0.1em} e^{-0.1t} + 20\nT(t) = A \\left( 2^{-kt} \\right) + B\n$$\nwhere $A$ and $B$ and $k$ are constants, $k>0$, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t$ is the number of minutes since the chocolate was served.\n\nIt is known that the initial temperature is $84^\\circ$ and the temperature at time $t=\\dfrac{1}{k}$ is $52^\\circ$.\n\n1. Show that $A=64$ and $B=20$. [2 marks]\n\nIt took $40$ minutes to get a temperature of $23^\\circ$.\n\n2. Find the value of $k$.[2 marks]\n\n3. Find the number of minutes it takes to get a temperature of $21^\\circ$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Since the initial temperature is $84^\\circ$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    T(0) &= A \\left(2^{-k(0)}\\right) +  B \\\\[7pt]\n    84 &= A + B \\hspace{2em}\\textcolor{red}{[1]} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $T$ at $t=\\dfrac{1}{k}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    T\\left(\\dfrac{1}{k}\\right) &= A \\left(2^{-k/k}\\right) +  B \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[7pt]\n    52 &= \\dfrac{A}{2} + B \\hspace{2em}\\textcolor{red}{[2]} \\\\[-10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To find $A$ take the difference $\\textcolor{red}{[2]}-\\textcolor{red}{[1]}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-0.75em} A = 64  \\\\[-10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, using $\\textcolor{red}{[1]}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em} B = 20\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Solving for $k$ in the equation $T(40)=23$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    64 \\left(2^{-k(40)}\\right) + 20 &= 23 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[7pt]\n    &=0.11037...\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{ k = 0.110} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Solving for $t$, $T(t)=21$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    64 \\left( 2^{-0.110t} \\right) + 20 &= 21 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[9pt]\n    &=54.545...\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{ t = 54.5\\,\\text{minutes}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1169","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1169\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=6x^2-7x+2$.\n\n1.  Fully factorise $f(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n\\\nAnother function is defined as $g(x)=\\ln x$.\n\nA third function is defined as $h(x)=(g \\circ f)(x)$.\n\n2.  Hence, determine the domain of $h(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n\\\nThe value $a$ is added to $f(x)$, where $a \\in \\mathrm{R}$.\n\nFor the domain of $h(x)$ to be $x \\in \\mathrm{R}$ then $a>k$.\n\nThe minimum value of $k$ is $a_{\\text{min}}$.\n\n3.   Find $a_{\\text{min}}$ [3 marks]\n\n\\\nNow the value $b$ is added to $f(x)$, where $b \\in \\mathrm{R}$ and $b > a_{\\text{min}}$.\n\n4.  Find the value of $b$ such that the vertex of $h(x)$ lies on the $x-$axis. Give your answer to $4$ significant figures. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We will use the grouping method to factorise.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        a \\times c &= 2 \\times 6\\\\[6pt]\n         &=12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we need to find two numbers that sum to $-7$ and multiply to give $12$. These are $-3$ and $-4$.\n\n    Therefore, we can then rewrite $-7x$ as $-3x-4x$. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        6x^2-7x+2&=6x^2-3x-4x+2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We then factorise the resulting expression into two groups\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=3x(2x-1)-2(2x-1)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=(2x-1)(3x-2) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  This is a composite function in which we form $h(x)$ by substituting $f(x)$ into $g(x)$. Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        h(x)&=\\ln{\\left(6x^2-7x+2\\right)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now sketch $h(x)$ on our G.D.C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_a%20%282%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_a (2)\">\n\n    $h(x)$ has two vertical asymptotes and these occur when $h(x)=\\ln{0}$. Hence $f(x)=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the answer from part (a) we can find their equations.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (2x-1)(3x-2)&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        x&=\\tfrac{1}{2}, \\tfrac{2}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The red arrows on the image above show the domain of $h(x)$.\n\n    Therefore the $\\text{domain of }h(x)\\text{ is }x<\\tfrac{1}{2}\\text{ and }x>\\tfrac{2}{3}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The following image shows $f(x)$ and $h(x)$ together on the same axes.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_c%20%283%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_c (3)\">\n\n    Notice that $h(x)$ is undefined when $f(x)<0$, this is the dark blue part of $f(x)$.\n\n    Hence we need to shift $f(x)$ vertically up $a$ units such that the vertex of $f(x)$ touches the $x-$axis at a single point.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    To do this we must find the minimum value of $f(x)$.\n\n    Using a G.D.C. we can find the minimum point of $f(x)$ to be $(0.5833...,-0.04166...)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence $f(x)+0.04166...$ will ensure $f(x)$ intersects the $x-$axis at one point. Ensuring that $f(x)\\geq 0$ for all $x$. This is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_d.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_d\">\n\n    Therefore if $a>0.04166...$ then $f(x)>0$ for all $x$ and the domain of $h(x)$ is $x \\in \\mathrm{R}$.\n\n    Hence, $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{a_{\\text{min}}=0.0417}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Notice from the image in the previous part, that the vertex of $h(x)$ lies on the same vertical line as $f(x)$.\n\n    Therefore the equation of the vertical line is $x=0.5833...$ and the final position of the vertex of $h(x)$ must be on the $x-$axis. This is shown below.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1169_d2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1169_d2\">\n\n    Therefore we need to solve this equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        h(0.5833...)&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        (g \\circ (f(0.5833...)+b))&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        \\ln{(f(0.5833...)+b)}&=0 \\hspace{2em} (\\ln{1}=0) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        f(0.5833...)+b&=1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    From part (c) we know that $f(0.5833...)=-0.04166...$ at the vertex of $f(x)$ therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        b&=1-(-0.04166...)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1.04166...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{b=1.042}\\hspace{0.525em}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1180","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1180\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined as $f(x)=3e^{\\frac{x}{2}}$ where $x \\in \\mathrm{R}$ and is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1180_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1180_q\">\n\n1.  Write down the equation of the asymptote of $f(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Write down the $y-$intercept of $f(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Hence, or otherwise, sketch the inverse of $f(x)$ on the image above. Make sure any asymptotes and/or intercepts are clearly marked. [2 marks]\n\n4. Determine the domain of the inverse of $f(x)$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  $y=0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Occurs when $x=0$, $f(0)=3\\times e^0$ therefore $3$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The inverse is a reflection in the line $y=x$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1180_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1180_a\">\n\n    __A1__ Correct shape/asymptotes\\\n    __A1__ Correct intercept\n\n4.  $\\text{The domain of the inverse is }x>0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1284","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1284\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe amount, in milligrams, of a medicinal drug in the body $t$ hours after it is injected is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    D(t)=240e^{-kt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $k>0$ and $t\\geq 0$. Before the injection, it is assumed the amount of drug in the body is zero.\n\nA patient is to be injected with the drug and, for this patient, it is known that it takes $5$ hours for the amount of drug remaining in the body to have decreased by $65\\%$ of the initial dose.\n\nThe patient is regularly checked and is allowed to go home when the amount of drug remaining in the body is $10\\%$, or less, of the initial dose.\n\nThe initial dose is given to the patient at $9:00\\,$am.\n\nUse this model to estimate, to the nearest hour, the earliest time the patient will be allowed to go home.","markScheme":"This model has a fixed value of $k$. In order to use the model we must find $k$.\n\nFirst, we can find the initial amount of the drug injected into the patient, this occurs when $t=0$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(0)&=240e^{-k(0)}\\\\[6pt]\nD(0)&=240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFive hours later, $t=5$, we are told that $100\\%-65\\%=35\\%$ of the initial $240\\text{ mg}$ remains.\n\nWe can use this to find $k$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.35\\times240&=240e^{-5k}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n0.35&=e^{-5k}\\\\[6pt]\n\\ln{0.35}&=\\ln e^{-5k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the log law $\\log_ax^m=m\\log_ax$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln{0.35}&=-5k\\ln e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\ln e=1$, we can simplify the equation and solve for $k$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln{0.35}&=-5k\\\\[6pt]\nk&=\\frac{\\ln{0.35}}{-5}\\\\[6pt]\n&=0.2099\\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write out the model with out value of $k$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    D(t)&=240e^{-0.2099...t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $t$ when the drug remaining in the body is equal to $0.10\\times240=24\\text{ mg}$.\n\nHence we need to solve the following equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24&=240e^{-0.2099\\dots t}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can do this graphically by sketching the function $y=D(t)$ and the horizontal line $y=24$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA1284.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1284\">\n\nThe $x-$coordinate of the intersection point is the solution.\n\nAlthough a calculator method is preferred, an algebraic approach is also included in case you attempted it that way\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.1&=e^{-0.2099\\dots t}\\\\[6pt]\n\\ln{0.1}&=-0.2099\\dots t\\\\[6pt]\nt&=\\frac{\\ln{0.1}}{-0.2099\\dots}\\\\[6pt]\nt&=10.96\\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\nt&=11 \\hspace{1em}\\text{to the nearest hour}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the answer to a time we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{9:00 + 11 hours = 20:00}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the earliest time, to the nearest hour, the patient will be allowed to go home is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{20:00 or 8:00\\,pm}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA131","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA131\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nConsider $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2} - \\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x^2-4}\\right)$.\n\n1.  Find the largest possible domain $D$ for $f$ to be a function.\n    [2 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2} - \\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x^2-4}\\right)$, for $x \\in D$.\n\n2.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, showing clearly the\n    equations of asymptotes and the coordinates of any intercepts with\n    the axes. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Explain why $f$ is an even function. [1 mark]\n\n4.  Explain why the inverse function $f^{-1}$ does not exist.\n    [1 mark]\n\nThe function $g$ is defined by\n$g(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2} - \\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x^2-4}\\right)$, for\n$x \\in (2,\\infty)$.\n\n5.  Find the inverse function $g^{-1}$ and state its domain.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The square root function is defined on $[0,\\infty)$ and natural\n    logarithm function is defined on $(0,\\infty)$.\n\n    Hence $f$ is a function if\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textstyle \\sqrt{x^2-4} &> 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    x^2-4 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x^2 &> 4 \\\\[6pt]\n    |x| &> 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    x < -2 \\hspace{0.5em}&\\hspace{0.2em}\\text{or}\\hspace{0.7em} x > 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the largest possible domain for $f$ to be a function is\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     D = (-\\infty,-2)\\cup(2,\\infty)\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/c292af76cbb30669709b70ec8dbfa255472cb0f7.svg\" alt=\"c292af76cbb30669709b70ec8dbfa255472cb0f7.svg\">\n\n3.\n    $\\text{The function \\(f\\) is even because its graph is symmetric about the \\(y\\)-axis.}$\n\n    OR\n\n    $\\text{The function \\(f\\) is even because it satisfies \\(f(x) = f(-x)\\) for all \\(x \\in D\\).}$\n\n4.\n    $\\text{The inverse function \\(f^{-1}\\) does not exist because \\(f\\) is not bijective.}$\n\n5.  If we solve the equation $(g\\circ g^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = g^{-1}(x)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(g^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[15pt]\n    g(y) &= x \\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{2} - \\textstyle \\ln\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\sqrt{y^2-4}\\big) &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\textstyle \\ln\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\sqrt{y^2-4}\\big) &= \\dfrac{1}{2} - x  \\\\[9pt]\n    \\textstyle \\sqrt{y^2-4} &= e^{\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - x\\right)} \\\\[12pt]\n    y^2-4 &= e^{2\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - x\\right)} \\\\[14pt]\n    y^2 &= e^{1 - 2x} + 4 \\\\[13pt]\n    y &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{e^{1 - 2x} + 4} \\hspace{2.7em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence an expression for $g^{-1}$ is\n     $g^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{e^{1 - 2x} + 4}$\n\n    The domain of $g^{-1}$ is\n     $\\mathbb{R}$"},{"ref":"AA151","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA151\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Write down the domain and range of the logarithmic function\n    $y = \\log_a x$\\\n    where $a > 0$ and $a \\neq 1$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\log_{x^2} y = 9 \\log_y (x^2)$, find all the possible\n    expressions\\\n    of $y$ as a function of $x$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{domain}:\\hspace{0.75em}&\\hspace{0.525em}(0,\\infty) \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\text{range}:\\hspace{0.75em}&\\hspace{0.525em}\\mathbb{R}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{x^2} y &= 9 \\log_y (x^2) \\\\[11pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{\\ln y}{\\ln (x^2)} &= \\dfrac{9\\ln (x^2)}{\\ln y} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\ln y}{2\\ln x} &= \\dfrac{18\\ln x}{\\ln y} \\\\[12pt]\n    (\\ln y)^2 &= (6\\ln x)^2 \\\\[16pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\pm 6\\ln x \\\\[16pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln (1/x^6)\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.25em}\\ln (x^6) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.2em} y = \\dfrac{1}{x^6}\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.25em}x^6\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA178","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA178\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^x - 2$ and $g(x) = 3x + k$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$k$ is a constant.\n\n1.  Find $(g\\circ f)(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that\n    $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} (g\\circ f)(x) = -4$,\n    find the value of $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= g(f(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(e^x - 2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(e^x - 2) + k \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3e^x - 6 + k\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} (g\\circ f)(x) &= -4 \\hspace{6.9em} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty}\\hspace{-0.2em}\\big[3e^x - 6 + k\\big] &= -4 \\\\[4pt]\n    3(0) - 6 + k &= -4 \\\\[9pt]\n    - 6 + k &= -4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.2em} k = 2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA182","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA182\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = 0.2e^{x+2} - 4$, for $-3 \\leq x \\leq 2$.\n\n1.  On the following grid, sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/219ede634589aee775d4d590dd79b30ff5c63914.svg\" alt=\"219ede634589aee775d4d590dd79b30ff5c63914.svg\">\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of:\n    1.  the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ is reflected in the $x$-axis, then translated 1 unit in the direction of the positive $x$-axis and 2 units in the direction of the positive $y$-axis to obtain the graph of a function $g$.\n\n3.  Find $g(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graph of $y = f(x)$ for $-3 \\leq x \\leq 2$ \\[by using\n    G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/a0ce7feb5a9182c2d088b3711cfba75a324b8033.svg\" alt=\"a0ce7feb5a9182c2d088b3711cfba75a324b8033.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.2em} 0.2e^{x+2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &\\approx 0.996 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are\n         $(0.996,0)$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.2e^{0+2} - 4 \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[7pt]\n        &\\approx -2.52\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the\n        $y$-intercept are\n         $(0,-2.52)$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.5em} g(x) &= -f(x-1) + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\big[0.2e^{(x-1)+2} - 4\\big] + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -0.2e^{x+1} + 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA202","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA202\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA population of endangered snow leopards, $P$, can be modelled by the\nequation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_t = P_0e^{kt},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial population,\nand $t$ is measured in years.\n\n1.  After one year, it is estimated that $\\dfrac{P_1}{P_0} = 0.93$.\n    1.  Find the value of $k$.\n\n    2.  Interpret the meaning of the value of $k$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the least number of **whole** years for which\n    $\\dfrac{P_t}{P_0} < 0.50$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Substituting $t = 1$ in the equation $P_t = P_0e^{kt}$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        P_1 &= P_0e^{k(1)} \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{P_1}{P_0} &= e^k \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{0.1em} 0.93 &= e^k \\\\[11pt]\n        k &= \\ln 0.93 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{0.8em} k \\approx -0.0726\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $\\text{The rate at which the population of leopards exponentially decrease}$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{P_t}{P_0} &< 0.50 \\\\[8pt]\n    e^{-0.0726t} &< 0.50 \\\\[12pt]\n    -0.0726t &< \\ln 0.50 \\\\[6pt]\n    t &> -\\dfrac{\\ln 0.50}{0.0726} \\\\[8pt]\n    t &> 9.55 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the least number of whole years\n    for which $\\dfrac{P_t}{P_0} < 0.50$ is\n     $t = 10$"},{"ref":"AA734","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA734\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAtmospheric pressure, $P$, in kPa, decreases exponentially with\nincreasing height above sea level, $h$. The atmospheric pressure can be\nmodelled by the function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(h) &= 101\\times\\bigg(\\dfrac{25}{22}\\bigg)^{-h},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere\n$h$ is the height above sea level in kilometres.\n\n1.  Write down the exact atmospheric pressure at sea level, in kPa.\n    [1 mark]\n\nMount Kosciuszko is the highest mountain in Australia with a height of\n$2228$ $\\text{metres}$ above sea level at the top.\n\n2.  Calculate the atmospheric pressure at the top of the Mount\n    Kosciuszko. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the height where the atmospheric pressure is\n    equal to $10$ kPa. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $101 \\text{ kPa}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $P(h)$ for $h = 2.228$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} P(2.228) &= 101\\times\\bigg(\\dfrac{25}{22}\\bigg)^{-2.228} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{\\approx 76.0 \\text{ kPa}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Writing the equation $P(h) = 10$ for $h$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    101\\times\\bigg(\\dfrac{25}{22}\\bigg)^{-h} &= 10 \\hspace{8.1em} \\\\\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solve this equation [by using G.D.C.]\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11em} h = 18.1\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km} \\hspace{3em} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA735","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA735\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe number of French words, $N$, that are remembered by students after\nthe completion of a French language course decreases exponentially over\ntime. This data can be modelled by the function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) = a\\times b^{-t} + 450,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are\npositive constants, and $t$ is the time in years since a student\ncompleted the French language course.\n\nImmediately after completion, a student remembers $4200$ French words.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$. [2 marks]\n\nAfter $4$ years a student remembers only $1600$ French words.\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\nThe number of French words a student remembers never decreases below a\ncertain number of words, $n$.\n\n3.  Write down the value of $n$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Solving the equation $N(0) = 4200$ for $a > 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a\\times b^{-0} + 450 &= 4200 \\hspace{5.4em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n    a + 450 &= 4200 \\\\[-16pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    a = 3750 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $N(4) = 1600$ for $b > 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3750\\times b^{-4} + 450 &= 1600 \\hspace{6.9em} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solve this equation [by using G.D.C.]\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{10.6em} \\boxed{b \\approx 1.34} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we draw the graph of $y = N(t)$, for $t \\geq 0$ \\[by using\n    G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/5f0266262d56defec1ffa52f191a9228b568fd3e.svg\" alt=\"5f0266262d56defec1ffa52f191a9228b568fd3e.svg\">\n\n    Hence the value of $n$ is\n    $450$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA736","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA736\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe number of users on a new social network on January 1st $2018$ was $320\\hspace{0.15em}000$. One year later, on January 1st $2019$, the number of users on this network is estimated to be $350\\hspace{0.15em}000$. The number\nof the users on this network, $N$, can be modelled by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) = 320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times b^t, \\hspace{0.5em} t\\geq0,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since January 1st $2018$ and $b$ is a constant.\n\n1.  Find the **exact** value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Estimate the number of the users on this network there will be in\n    $2023$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine the year during which the number of the social network users\n    reaches one million. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $N(1) = 350\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $b$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times b &= 350\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\hspace{4.4em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[9pt]\n    b &= \\dfrac{350\\hspace{0.15em}000}{320\\hspace{0.15em}000} \\\\[-9pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{2.7em}\\boxed{b = \\dfrac{35}{32} \\text{ or } 1.09375} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we evaluate $N(t)$ for $t = 5$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} N(5) &= 320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times \\left(\\dfrac{35}{32}\\right)^5 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[4pt]\n    &\\approx 500\\hspace{0.15em}887.6... \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{\\approx 501\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\text{ users}}\\hspace{1em}\\text{(3 s.f.)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Solving the equation $N(t) = 1\\hspace{0.15em}000\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $t$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.75em} 320\\hspace{0.15em}000\\times \\left(\\dfrac{35}{32}\\right)^t &= 1\\hspace{0.15em}000\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    t &\\approx 12.71... \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we determine the years corresponding to $t = 12$ and $t=13$, we find that the number of network users reaches one million $\\text{during } 2030$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA739","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA739\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA population of goldfish decreases exponentially. The size of the\npopulation, $P$, after $t$ days is modelled by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(t) = 8000\\times2^{-t} + 100, \\hspace{0.5em} t \\geq 0. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Write down the **exact** size of the initial population. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the size of the population after $5$ days. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the time it will take for the size of the population to decrease to $120$.[2 marks]\n\nThe population will stabilize when it reaches a size of $n$.\n\n4.  Write down the value of $n$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $P(t)$ for $t = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.7em} P(0) &= 8000\\times2^0 + 100 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8000 + 100 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}= 8100 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $P(t)$ for $t = 5$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.3em} P(5) &= 8000\\times2^{-5} + 100 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}= 350 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $P(t) = 120$ for $t$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    8000\\times2^{-t} + 100 &= 120 \\hspace{6.1em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{14.2em} t = 8.64 \\text{ days} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11.9em} \\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty} P(t) &= \\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty} \\big[8000\\times2^{-t} + 100\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0+100 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $2^{-t} \\rightarrow 0$ as $t \\rightarrow \\infty$}] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 100 \\text{ fish}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the value of $n$ is $\\hspace{0.525em}100$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA740","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA740\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA population of $50$ hamsters was introduced to a new town. One month\nlater, the number of hamsters was $62$. The number of hamsters, $P$, can\nbe modelled by the function\n\n$$\nP(t) = 50\\times b^t,\\hspace{0.5em} t\\geq0, \\\\[3pt]\n$$\nwhere $t$ is the\ntime, in months, since the hamsters were introduced to the town.\n\n1.  Find the value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the number of hamsters in the town after $6$ months.\n    [2 marks]\n\nA wildlife specialist estimates that the town has enough water and food\nto support a maximum population of $2000$ hamsters.\n\n3.  Calculate the number of months it takes for the hamster\n    population to reach this maximum. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Solving the equation $P(1) = 62$ for $b$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    50\\times b^1 &= 62 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    b &= \\dfrac{62}{50} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.4em}b = 1.24\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $P(t)$ for $t = 6$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} P(6) &= 50\\times(1.24)^6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 182\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $P(t) = 2000$ for $t$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    50\\times(1.24)^t &= 2000 \\hspace{3.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{15.6em} t \\approx 17.1 \\text{ months} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA930","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA930\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{2}$, $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. Show that $f$ is an odd function. [2 marks]\n\nNow, consider the function $g$ given by $g(x) = \\dfrac{x^4+2}{2x}$ , $x\\in \\mathbb{R}, x \\neq 0$.\n\n2. By considering the graph of $y=f(x)-g(x)$, solve $f(x)>g(x)$ for $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Evaluating $f(-x)$, we get \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(-x) &= \\dfrac{e^{-x}-e^{-(-x)}}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{e^{-x}-e^{x}}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= -\\dfrac{e^{x}-e^{-x}}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= -f(x) \\\\[-8pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    $$\\text{Therefore, $f(x)$ is an odd function}$$\n\n2. If $f(x)-g(x)>0$, then $f(x)>g(x)$. Using G.D.C., we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Exponential%20and%20Logarithmic%20Functions/images/AA930a.PNG\" alt=\"AA930a\">\n\n    We see that $f(x)-g(x)>0$ for $-4.55 < x < 0$ and $x > 4.55$.\n\n    Therefore $f(x)>g(x)$ over the intervals\n\n    $-4.55 < x < 0 \\text{ and } x > 4.55$"},{"ref":"AA987","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA987\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA species of bird, thought to be extinct, was rediscovered on 14 March 1973. Through a careful protection and breeding program, the population has continued to increase since then.\n\nThe Department of conservation proposes that the population of birds $t$ years after 14 March 1973 can be modelled by the function $P(t)=10e^{kt}$, where $0 < k < 1$ and $t \\geq 0$.\n\nOn 14 March 1993 the known population of birds was 50.\n\n1. Use the model to estimate the population of birds on 14 March 2023.[4 marks]\n\nIn fact, the known population of birds on 14 March 2023 was 502. A new model is proposed in which\n\n* The population on March 14 1973 was 10.\n\n* The population on March 14 2023 was 502.\n\n* The population increased at a constant $r\\%$ per year.\n\n2. Find the value of $r$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 14 March 1993 is 20 years after 14 March 1973, therefore $P(20)=50$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(20) &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    50 &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^{20k} &= 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $k\\approx 0.08047$. Hence our model is $P(t)=10e^{0.08047t}$.\n\n    14 March 2023 is 50 years after 14 March 1973, hence we calculate $P(50)$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(50) &= 10e^{0.08047 \\times 50} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-2.4em}\\boxed{P(50)\\approx559}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. If something increases at a constant rate, the compound interest formula applies. Using the formula for compound interest with $PV=10$, $FV=502$, $k=1$ and $n=50$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    FV &= PV \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\right)^{kn} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    502 &= 10 \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100}\\right)^{50}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $\\boxed{r\\approx 8.15}$"}]},{"name":"Linear and Quadratic Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA1268","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1268\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nConsider the line $L_1$ which passes through the points $A (-4,-4)$ and $B(8,-1)$.\n\n1. Find the gradient of the line $L_1$. [1 mark]\n\nLine $L_2$ is perpendicular to $L_1$ and passes through the point $(-a,2a)$ where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2. Given that $L_2$ intersects the $y$-axis at $y=2a-3$, find the value of $a$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula for the gradient of a line\n\n    $$\n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m&=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    we can find the gradient of $L_1$\n\n    $$\n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m_1&=\\frac{-1-(-4)}{8-(-4)}\\\\[9pt]\n\n       &=\\frac{3}{12}\\\\[9pt]\n\n       &=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  As $L_2$ is perpendicular to $L_1$ we can find the gradient of $L_2$ using the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m_1\\times m_2&=-1\\\\[9pt]\n\n        \\frac{1}{4}\\times m_2&=-1\\\\[9pt]\n\n        m_2&=-4\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can form an equation, using the gradient formula, and solve for $a$.\n\n    Substituting in $m_2$ and the points $(-a,2a)$ and $(0,2a-3)$ we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    m_2&=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    -4&=\\frac{(2a-3)-2a}{0-(-a)}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[9pt]\n\n      -4 &=\\frac{-3}{a}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    -4a&=-3\\\\[6pt] \n\n    a&=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1285","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1285\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the equations of the two tangents to the curve $y=2x^2-x+\\tfrac{9}{2}$ that pass through the origin.","markScheme":"To better understand the scenario we can make an approximate sketch.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/AA1285.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1285\">\n\nThe dashed lines are the two tangent lines whose equations we need to find.\n\nThe equation of a tangent line, through the origin, will be of the form\n\n$$ \n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    y&=mx\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTo find the intersection points of the curve and line we can solve both equations simultaneously, hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    mx&=2x^2-x+\\frac{9}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0&=2x^2-x-mx+\\frac{9}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0&=2x^2-(1+m)x+\\frac{9}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nIf the lines are tangent to the curve then there is only one point of intersection i.e. only $1$ solution.\n\nAs we have a quadratic equation then we can say that the discriminant must be equal to zero. Hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\Delta&=b^2-4ac\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0&=[-(1+m)]^2-4(2)\\left(\\tfrac{9}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSolving for $m$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    36&=[-(1+m)]^2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    -(1+m)&=\\pm6\\\\[6pt]\n\n    m&=-7 \\text{ and }5\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore the two equations are \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{y=-7x}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{y=5x}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1322","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1322\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=x^2+2bx+3$, where $b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $f$ has two distinct real roots.\n\n1.    Find the range of values of $b$. [2 marks]\n\nConsider another function $g(x)=x^2+2Ax+3$, where $A$ is a continuous random variable such that $A \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n2.    Show that the probability that $g$ has two distinct real roots is $0.08326$ to four significant figures. [3 marks]\n\nThe two roots of $g$ are $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nGiven that $g$ has two distinct real roots, where $|x_1|<\\sqrt{3}$ and $|x_2|>\\sqrt{3}$,\n\n3.    find the probability that $|x_1|<1$. [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. Recall the discriminant, $\\Delta$, of a quadratic equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{If}\\hspace{1em}\\,ax^2+bx+c&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{then}\\hspace{4.7em}\\Delta&=b^2-4ac\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This tells us how many real roots the equation has.\n\n    Consider the equation  $f(x)=0$. If $f$ has two distinct real roots then $\\Delta > 0$.\n\n    Hence, using the parameter values of $a = 1$, $b$ is  $2b$ and $c = 3$ we can form the inequality\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2b)^2-4(1)(3)& > 0\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    which we can solve to find the range of values of $b$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4b^2-12&> 0\\\\[6pt]\n    4b^2&> 12\\\\[6pt]\n    b^2&> 3\\\\[6pt]b < -\\sqrt{3} &\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }b> \\sqrt{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Function $g$ is closely related to $f$ the only difference is that parameter $b$ is now $A$.\n\n    Therefore using our previous answer we can write down the range of values of $A$ for which $g$ has real roots\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &< -\\sqrt{3}&A &> \\sqrt{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall that $A$ is the continuous random variable such that $A \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n    Hence we need to find the probability that $A <-\\sqrt{3}$ or $A > \\sqrt{3}$.\n\n    This is a region on the standard normal distribution and is shown as the shaded regions below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/AA1322-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1322-a1\">\n\n    Hence, the probability $g$ has real distinct roots is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\mathrm{P}(A < -\\sqrt{3}) + \\mathrm{P}(A> \\sqrt{3})\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{normCDF}(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{3},0,1)+ \\text{normCDF}(\\sqrt{3},\\infty,0,1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.041632...+0.041632...\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.083264...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.08326\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Consider the given inequality, we can write it without the absolute value signs\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |x_1|&<1\\\\[6pt]\n    -1<x_1&<1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This means we need to find the value(s) of $A$ such that the root $x_1$ is in the range described above.\n\n    To find the roots, $x_1$ and $x_2$, of $g$ we can use the quadratic formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{If}\\hspace{1em}\\,ax^2+bx+c&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{then}\\hspace{4.7em}x&=\\dfrac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $a$ is  $1$, $b$ is $2A$ and $c$ is $3$ which gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x_{1,2}&=\\dfrac{-(2A)\\pm \\sqrt{(2A)^2-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{-2A\\pm \\sqrt{4A^2-12}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{-2A\\pm \\sqrt{4(A^2-3)}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{-\\cancel{2}A\\pm \\cancel{2}\\sqrt{A^2-3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=-A\\pm \\sqrt{A^2-3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the two roots of $g$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &&\\text{root $1$}&=-A - \\sqrt{A^2-3} &\\text{ and }&&\\text{root $2$}&=-A + \\sqrt{A^2-3}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    However, we can't be sure which one is $x_1$ and which is $x_2$!\n\n    But we can use a sketch of the graph of root $1$ and root $2$ against $A$ to learn more.\n\n    The following image shows both root $1$ and root $2$ against $A$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/AA1322-double.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1322-double\">\n\n    Recall, $x_1$ is defined as the root which has a value of $-\\sqrt{3}<x_1<\\sqrt{3}$.\n\n    Therefore, if $-\\sqrt{3}<\\text{root }1<\\sqrt{3}$ or $-\\sqrt{3}<\\text{root }2<\\sqrt{3}$ then that root is $x_1$.\n\n    Hence when the curve is between the two purple horizontal lines then that root represents $x_1$.\n\n    This question is asking us to find when $-1<x_1<1$. Hence we need to find when either root $1$ or root $2$ is between the two $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$ horizontal lines.\n\n    The boundary values are $A_1$ for root $1$ and $A_2$ for root $2$.\n\n    And we can see that $0<x_1<1$ when $A<A_1$ (shown by the $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$ arrow above) and $-1<x_1<0$ when $A>A_2$ (shown by the $\\textcolor{green}{\\text{green}}$ arrow above).\n\n    Using a G.D.C. we find $A_1=-2$ and $A_2=2$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's combine these two regions by splitting the double inequality\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -1<x_1<1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    into two single inequalities. This gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &0<x_ 1<1&&&-1<x_1<0&\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\text{occurs when }\\textcolor{blue}{A<-2}&&&\\text{occurs when }\\textcolor{green}{A>2}&\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}\\big(|x_1|<1\\big)&=\\mathrm{P}\\big( -1<x_1<1\\big)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\mathrm{P}(A < -2) + \\mathrm{P}(A> 2)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{normCDF}(-\\infty,-2,0,1)+ \\text{normCDF}(2,\\infty,0,1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.02275...+0.02275...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.04550...\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    However, we haven't quite finished yet. We were also given some extra information that $g$ has two distinct roots.\n    This is conditional probability for which we use the formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(R1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A|B)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Where event $A$ is $|x_1|<1$ and event $B$ is $g$ has two distinct roots.\n\n    Replacing $A$ and $B$ in the formula we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\,\\bigg|\\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\, \\cap \\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the answer from part (b) as the denominator\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\, \\cap \\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}{0.08326...}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To evaluate the numerator, we can use our answer from part (a), from which we can write down the range of values of $A$ for which $g$ has two distinct roots\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &< -\\sqrt{3}&A &> \\sqrt{3}\\\\[6pt]\n    A &< -1.732...&A &> 1.732...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall our values for $A$ when $|x_1|<1$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &< -2&A &> 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Notice that the set of values of $A$ for when $|x_1|<1$ fall entirely within the set of values for which $g$ has two distinct roots.\n\n    Hence the intersection of both events is simply the event the set of values when $|x_1|<1$, which we found earlier in this part.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, the numerator becomes\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\, \\cap \\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}{0.08326...}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\bigg)}{0.08326...}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{0.04550...}{0.08326...}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=0.5464...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.546\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA134","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA134\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Express $2x^2 - 8x + 9$ in the form $a(x+b)^2 + c$ where\n    $a,b,c \\in\\mathbb{Z}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $f(x) = x - 2$ and $(g\\circ f)(x) = 2x^2 - 8x + 9$, find\n    $g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the method of completing the square, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x^2 - 8x + 9 &= 2\\big[x^2 - 4x\\big] + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[(x-2)^2 - 4\\big] + 9 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x-2)^2 - 8 + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x-2)^2 + 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.3em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= 2x^2 - 8x + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    g(f(x)) &=  2(x-2)^2 + 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    g(x-2) &= 2(x-2)^2 + 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(x) = x - 2$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g(x) = 2x^2 + 1 \\hspace{3.4em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA145","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA145\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe equation $x^2 - kx + 1 = 0$ has two distinct real roots.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Since the quadratic function $q(x) = x^2 - kx + 1$ has two distinct real\nroots, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(-k)^2 - 4(1)(1) &> 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 - 4 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 &> 4 \\\\[7pt]\n|k| &> 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} k < \\hspace{-0.1em}-2 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k > 2  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $k$ is\n $(-\\infty,-2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$"},{"ref":"AA146","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA146\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe equation $x^2 + (k-3)x - 3k = 0$ has two distinct real solutions.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Since the quadratic function $q(x) = x^2 + (k-3)x - 3k$ has two distinct\nreal roots, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k-3)^2 - 4(1)(-3k) &> 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n(k^2 - 6k + 9) + 12k &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 + 6k + 9 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k + 3)^2 &> 0 \\\\[7pt]\n|k + 3| &> 0 \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.7em} k + 3 < 0 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k + 3 > 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk < \\hspace{-0.1em} -3 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k > -3   \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $k$ is\n $k\\in\\mathbb{R}\\,;\\,k\\neq-3$"},{"ref":"AA147","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA147\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 + kx$ and $g(x) = x + k$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$k$ is a constant.\n\nThe graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x)$ intersect at two distinct\npoints.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = g(x)$ in the form\n$ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2 + kx &= x + k \\\\[6pt]\nx^2 + (k-1)x - k &= 0 \\hspace{3.1em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the quadratic\nfunction $q(x) = x^2 + (k-1)x - k$ has two distinct real roots.\n\nTherefore we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k-1)^2 - 4(1)(-k) &> 0 \\hspace{3em}  [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n(k^2 - 2k + 1) + 4k &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2  + 2k + 1 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(k + 1)^2 &> 0 \\\\[7pt]\n|k+1| &> 0 \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.7em} k + 1 < 0 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k + 1 > 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk < \\hspace{-0.1em} -1 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} k > -1   \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $k$ is\n $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{-1\\}$"},{"ref":"AA148","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA148\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = p - \\dfrac{16}{x}$, for $x \\neq 0$, where $p$ is a constant.\n\nThe line $y = x - p$ intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at two distinct\npoints.\n\nFind the possible values of $p$.","markScheme":"If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = x - p$ in the form\n$ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np - \\dfrac{16}{x} &= x - p \\\\[6pt]\n2p - \\dfrac{16}{x} &= x \\\\[11pt]\n2px - 16 &= x^2 \\\\[14pt]\n\\hspace{0.2em} x^2 - 2px + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the quadratic\nfunction $q(x) = x^2 - 2px + 16$ has two distinct real roots.\n\nTherefore we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.8em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(-2p)^2 - 4(1)(16) &> 0 \\hspace{3em}  [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n4p^2 - 64 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\np^2 - 16 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\np^2 &> 16 \\\\[7pt]\n|p| &> 4 \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the inequality $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} p < \\hspace{-0.1em}-4 \\hspace{0.525em}&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.525em} p > 4  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the set of possible values of $p$ is\n $(-\\infty,-4)\\cup(4,\\infty)$"},{"ref":"AA155","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA155\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Express $3x^2 + 18x + 20$ in the form $a(x+b)^2 + c$ where\n    $a,b,c \\in\\mathbb{Z}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $f(x) = x + 3$ and $(g\\circ f)(x) = 3x^2 + 18x + 20$,\n    find $g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the method of completing the square, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3x^2 + 18x + 20 &= 3\\big[x^2 + 6x\\big] + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\big[(x+3)^2 - 9\\big] + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(x+3)^2 - 27 + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(x+3)^2 - 7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.8em} (g\\circ f)(x) &= 3x^2 + 18x + 20 \\\\[6pt]\n    g(f(x)) &= 3(x+3)^2 - 7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    g(x+3) &= 3(x+3)^2 - 7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(x) = x + 3$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    g(x) = 3x^2 - 7 \\hspace{0.6em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA162","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA162\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 - 2x - 8$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Write down the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Solve the equation $f(x) = 0$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry of the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0^2 - 2(0) - 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{P}(0,-8)$\n    is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 - 2x - 8 &= 0 \\hspace{4.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    (x + 2)(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.8em} x = -2,\\hspace{0.25em} 4\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.7em} f(x) &= x^2 - 2x - 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[(x - 1)^2 - 1\\big] - 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x - 1)^2 - 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the axis of symmetry of the\n    graph of $y = f(x)$ is\n     $x = 1$"},{"ref":"AA163","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA163\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows part of the graph of a quadratic function\n$f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/1964f1685a23a24125bfe5568ac8db4fc2c5ff72.svg\" alt=\"1964f1685a23a24125bfe5568ac8db4fc2c5ff72.svg\">\n\nThe vertex is at point A and the $x$-intercepts are at points B and C.\n\nThe function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = (x - h)^2+k$.\n\n1.  Write down the values of $h$ and $k$. [2 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ can also be written in the form\n$f(x) = (x + p)(x - q)$, where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$.\n\n2.  Write down the values of $p$ and $q$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the $y$-intercept of the graph of $f$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $h = 2$  and\n     $k = -9$\n\n2.\n    $p = 1$  and\n     $q = 5$\n\n3.  Evaluating $f(x) = (x+1)(x-5)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= (0+1)(0-5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1)(-5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{D}(0,-5)$\n    is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $f$."},{"ref":"AA164","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA164\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 1)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nFor the graph of $f$, find:\n\n1.  the $y$-intercept; [2 marks]\n\n2.  the $x$-intercepts; [2 marks]\n\n3.  the coordinates of the vertex. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $f(x) = (x - 3)(x + 1)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} f(0) &= (0 - 3)(0 + 1) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-3)(1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the $y$-intercept is\n     $\\mathrm{A}(0,-3)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (x - 3)(x + 1) &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -1,\\hspace{0.25em} 3 \\hspace{1.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the\n    $x$-intercepts are\n     $\\mathrm{B}(-1,0)$\n    and\n     $\\mathrm{C}(3,0)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing the square,\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.8em} f(x) &= (x - 3)(x + 1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= x^2 - 2x - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[(x-1)^2 - 1\\big] - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x-1)^2 - 4 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex\n    are  $(1,-4)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA166","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA166\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find:\n    1.  the $y$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $x$-intercepts. [3 marks]\n\n2.  The function $f$ can be expressed in the form $f(x) = (x-h)^2 + k$.\n    Find\\\n    the value of $h$ and the value of $k$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ on the grid below. Clearly label\\\n    the intercepts with the axes, and the vertex. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/aefa19917a8714e460d7087874916779ea719f3e.svg\" alt=\"aefa19917a8714e460d7087874916779ea719f3e.svg\">","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.8em} y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0^2 - 4(0) + 3 \\\\[7pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $y$-intercept is\n         $\\mathrm{A}(0,3)$\n\n    2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x^2 - 4x + 3 &= 0 \\hspace{8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        (x-1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= 1,\\hspace{0.25em} 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $x$-intercepts\n        are\n         $\\mathrm{B}(1,0)$\n        and\n         $\\mathrm{C}(3,0)$\n\n2.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.8em} f(x) &= x^2 - 4x + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[(x-2)^2 - 4\\big] + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x-2)^2 - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $h = 2$\n    and  $k = -1$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ on the grid below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/e4bab816ede5f709b1664609afdc53c6db3be4ab.svg\" alt=\"e4bab816ede5f709b1664609afdc53c6db3be4ab.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA167","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA167\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe equation $x^2 + kx + 4 = 0$ has two equal roots.\n\nFind the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Since the quadratic function $q(x) = x^2 + kx + 4$ has two equal roots,\nwe have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.9em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 - 4(1)(4) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac] \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 - 16 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^2 &= 16 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.9em} k = \\pm 4\n$$"},{"ref":"AA168","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA168\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = a(x-h)^2 + k$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe vertex of the graph of $f$ is at P$(3,4)$ and the graph passes\nthrough Q$(1,-4)$.\n\n1.  Write down the values of $h$ and $k$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $h = 3$  and\n     $k = 4$\n\n2.  Using the coordinates of Q, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(1) &= -4 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} a(1-h)^2 + k &= -4 \\\\[6pt]\n    a(1-3)^2 + 4 &= -4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[7pt]\n    4a &= -8 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    a = -2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA177","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA177\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider $f(x) = x^2 + bx + c$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. The graph of $f$ has a local minimum when $x = 2.5$.\nThe distance between the two $x$-intercepts of the graph of $f$ is $7$.\n\n1.  Find the coordinates of the two $x$-intercepts. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$ and the value of $c$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The axis of symmetry of the graph of $f$ is $x = 2.5$.\n    Hence the coordinates of the two $x$-intercepts are\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bigg(2.5-\\frac{7}{2},0\\bigg) = \\hspace{0.525em}(-1,0)\\hspace{0.525em} \\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\n    \\bigg(2.5+\\frac{7}{2},0\\bigg) = \\hspace{0.525em}(6,0)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The roots of the quadratic function $f(x) = x^2 + bx + c$ are\n    $x = -1$ and $x = 6$.\n\n    Hence, using the factor theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.4em} f(x) &= (x + 1)(x - 6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= x^2 - 5x - 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $b = -5$\n    and  $c = -6$"},{"ref":"AA179","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA179\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = ax^2 - 24x + c$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$a, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  A horizontal line, $L$, intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at\n    $x = 1$ and $x = 7$.\n\n    1.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that $a = 3$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  The equation of $L$ is $y = 6$. Find the value of $c$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The equation of the axis of symmetry is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= \\dfrac{1 + 7}{2} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        x= 4 \\hspace{2.2em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n        4 &= -\\dfrac{(-24)}{2a} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[12pt]\n        \\hspace{8.8em} 8a &= 24 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = 3 \\hspace{2.1em}\n        $$\n\n2.  Since the line $y = 6$ intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at\n    $x = 1$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(1) &= 6 \\hspace{6.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3(1)^2 - 24(1) + c &= 6 \\\\[7pt]\n    3 - 24 + c &= 6 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.8em} c = 27\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA186","marks":"15","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA186\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 3x^2 + 12x + 9$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $f$, find:\n    1.  the $y$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $x$-intercepts. [4 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = a(x-h)^2+k$.\n\n2.  Find the values of $a$, $h$ and $k$. [5 marks]\n\n3.  For the graph of $f$, write down:\n    1.  the coordinates of the vertex;\n\n    2.  the equation of the axis of symmetry. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a translation of 4 units in the direction of the positive $y$ axis.\n\n4.  Find $g(x)$, giving your answer in the form\n    $g(x) = px^2 + qx + r$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Evaluating $f(x) = 3x^2 + 12x + 9$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{5em} f(0) &= 3(0)^2 + 12(0) + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 9\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $y$-intercept is\n         $\\mathrm{A}(0,9)$\n\n    2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.5em} 3x^2 + 12x + 9 &= 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        x^2 + 4x + 3 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n        (x+3)(x+1) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n        x &= -3,\\hspace{0.25em}-1 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the\n        $x$-intercepts are\n         $\\mathrm{B}(-3,0)$\n        and\n         $\\mathrm{C}(-1,0)$\n\n2.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} f(x) &= 3x^2 + 12x + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\big[x^2 + 4x\\big] + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\big[(x + 2)^2 - 4\\big] + 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(x + 2)^2 - 3 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = 3,\\hspace{0.25em} h = -2$\n    and  $k = -3$\n\n3.\n    1.\n        $(-2,-3)$\n\n    2.\n        $x = -2$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} g(x) &= -f(x)+4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -(3x^2 + 12x + 9)+4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -3x^2 - 12x - 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA187","marks":"15","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA187\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2x^2 + 4x + p$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$p \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  The equation $f(x) = 0$ has two equal roots.\n    1.  Write down the value of the discriminant of $f$.\n\n    2.  Show that $p = 2$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $f$, find:\n    1.  the equation of the axis of symmetry.\n\n    2.  the coordinates of the vertex; [4 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the solution to the equation $f(x) = 0$. [1 mark]\n\n4.  The function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = a(x-h)^2 + k$.\n    Find\\\n    the values of $a$, $h$ and $k$. [5 marks]\n\n5.  The graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a\n    reflection\\\n    in the $x$-axis. Find the coordinates of the vertex of the graph of\n    $g$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $\\triangle  = 0$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} \\triangle  &= b^2 - 4ac \\\\[4pt]\n        0 &= 4^2 - 4(2)(p) \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[5pt]\n        0 &= 16 - 8p \\\\[5pt]\n        16 &= 8p \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        p = 2 \\hspace{2.9em}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -\\dfrac{4}{2(2)} \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        x = -1 \\hspace{2.9em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $f(x)=2x^2 + 4x + 2$ for $x = -1$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.2em} f(-1) &= 2(-1)^2 + 4(-1) + 2 \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the vertex are\n         $(-1,0)$\n\n3.\n    $x=-1$\n\n4.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} f(x) &= 2x^2 + 4x + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[x^2 + 2x\\big] + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[(x+1)^2 - 1\\big] + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x+1)^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = 2,\\hspace{0.25em} h = -1$\n    and  $k = 0$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em}  g(x) &= -f(x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x+1)^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex of\n    the graph of $g$ are\n     $(-1,0)$"},{"ref":"AA188","marks":"15","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA188\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2x^2-8x+6$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $f(0)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Solve the equation $f(x) = 0$. [3 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ can be written in the form $f(x) = a(x-h)^2+k$.\n\n3.  Find the values of $a$, $h$ and $k$. [5 marks]\n\n4.  For the graph of $f$, write down:\n    1.  the coordinates of the vertex;\n\n    2.  the equation of the axis of symmetry. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a translation of 1 unit to the right and 3 units up.\n\n5.  Find $g(x)$, giving your answer in the form\n    $g(x) = px^2+qx+r$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $f(0) = 6$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x^2-8x+6 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x^2-4x+3 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n    (x-1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6.8em} x = 1,\\hspace{0.25em}3\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing of square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} f(x) &= 2x^2-8x+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[x^2-4x\\big]+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\big[(x-2)^2-4\\big]+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x-2)^2-2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = 2,\\hspace{0.25em}h=2$\n    and  $k=-2$\n\n4.\n    1.\n        $(2,-2)$\n\n    2.\n        $x = 2$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.4em} g(x) &= -f(x-1)+3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\big[2((x-1)-2)^2-2\\big]+3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x-3)^2+5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x^2-6x+9)+5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2x^2+12x-13\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA190","marks":"15","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA190\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = a(x+1)(x+5)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$a \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. The following diagram shows part of the graph of\n$f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/c560b93b90031104353dd8752d2c6976f60f1751.svg\" alt=\"c560b93b90031104353dd8752d2c6976f60f1751.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ has $x$-intercepts at $(p,0)$ and $(q,0)$, and a\n$y$-intercept at $(0,-10)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $p$ and the value of $q$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $a$. [6 marks]\n\n2.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of the vertex. [3 marks]\n\nThe graph of a function $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a\nreflection in the $y$-axis, followed by a translation of 2 units in the direction of the positive $y$-axis. The point P$(-2,6)$ on the graph of $f$ is mapped to point Q on the graph of\n$g$.\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of Q. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $p = -5$  and\n         $q = -1$\n\n    2.  Using the coordinates of the $y$-intercept, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(0) &= -10 \\hspace{5.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        a(0+1)(0+5) &= -10  \\\\[6pt]\n        5a &= -10  \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = -2\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} f(x) &= -2(x+1)(x+5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x^2+6x+5) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -2x^2-12x-10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the axis of symmetry\n    formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.5em} x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -\\dfrac{(-12)}{2(-2)} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.2em} x = -3\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the method of completing the square, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} f(x) &= -2x^2-12x-10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2\\big[x^2+6x\\big] - 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2\\big[(x+3)^2 - 9\\big] - 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2(x+3)^2 + 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex\n    are  $(-3,8)$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} \\text{transformation $1$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{reflection in the $y$-axis} \\\\\n    \\text{transformation $2$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{translation of two units upwards}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we apply the transformations $1$ and $2$ to P, we find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/e1df7f8211664f00389891c42cbdf0bb7d3cdd9a.svg\" alt=\"e1df7f8211664f00389891c42cbdf0bb7d3cdd9a.svg\">\n\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(2,8)$"},{"ref":"AA191","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA191\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}x^2 - 2$ and $g(x) = x^2 - 4$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that $(f\\circ g)(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}x^4 - 2x^2 + 2$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  On the following grid, sketch the graph of $y = (f \\circ g)(x)$, for\n    $0 \\leq x \\leq 3$. [3 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/b4ef7d37b745cb564b996b9d79dff67b636cf799.svg\" alt=\"b4ef7d37b745cb564b996b9d79dff67b636cf799.svg\">\n\n3.  The equation $(f\\circ g)(x) = k$ has exactly two solutions,\n    for $0 \\leq x \\leq 3$. Find\\\n    the possible values of $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ g)(x) &= f(g(x)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(x^2 - 4) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} &= \\dfrac{1}{4}(x^2 - 4)^2 - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{4}(x^4 - 8x^2+16) - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{4}x^4 - 2x^2 + 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of $y = (f \\circ g)(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we\n    have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/35e596bf685bd72ab7dad8286b0830ba0c592c52.svg\" alt=\"35e596bf685bd72ab7dad8286b0830ba0c592c52.svg\">\n\n3.  The line $y = k$ intersects the graph of $y = (f \\circ g)(x)$\n    exactly at two points when $-2 < k \\leq 2$.\n\n    Hence the equation $(f \\circ g)(x) = k$ has exactly two solutions\n    when  $-2 < k \\leq 2$"},{"ref":"AA196","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA196\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nA quadratic function $f$ can be written in the form\n$f(x) = p(x-1)(x-q)$.\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ has axis of symmetry $x = 2$ and $y$-intercept\nat $(0,-3)$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $p$. [3 marks]\n\nThe line $y = kx + 6$ is a tangent to the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n3.  Find the possible values of $k$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The $x$-coordinates of the $x$-intercepts are $x=1$ and $x=q$.\n    Hence, using the equation of the axis of symmetry, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1+q}{2} &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    1+q &= 4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2em} q = 3\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the coordinates of the $y$-intercept, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= -3 \\hspace{3.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    p(0-1)(0-3) &= -3 \\\\[6pt]\n    3p &= -3 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.7em} p = -1\n    $$\n\n3.  If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = kx+6$ in the form\n    $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} -(x-1)(x-3) &= kx+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    -x^2+4x-3 &= kx+6 \\\\[6pt]\n    0 &= x^2+(k-4)x+9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the quadratic function\n    $q(x) = x^2+(k-4)x+9$ has two equal roots.\n\n    Therefore we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.2em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    (k-4)^2 - 4(1)(9) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n    (k-4)^2 &= 36 \\\\[7pt]\n    k-4 &= \\pm 6 \\\\[7pt]\n    k &= 4 \\pm 6 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4.75em} k=-2,\\hspace{0.25em} 10\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA201","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA201\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = 2(x-1)^2-8$, for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that $f(x) = 2x^2-4x-6$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $f$:\n    1.  write down the coordinates of the vertex;\n\n    2.  write down the $y$-intercept;\n    3.  find both $x$-intercepts. [7 marks]\n\n3.  Hence sketch the graph of $f$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = 6x^2$, for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe graph of $f$ may be obtained from the graph of $g$ using the\nfollowing three transformations:\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{1em} & \\bullet\\text{ a compression of scale factor $a$ in the $y$-direction, followed by} \\\\\n & \\bullet\\text{ a horizontal translation of $h$ units, followed by} \\\\\n & \\bullet\\text{ a vertical translation of $k$ units.}\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n4.  Find the values of $a, h$ and $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} f(x) &= 2(x-1)^2-8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2(x^2-2x+1)-8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2x^2-4x-6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $(1,-8)$\n\n    2.\n        $\\text{P}(0,-6)$\n\n    3.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        2x^2-4x-6 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x^2-2x-3 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{0.8em} (x+1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -1,\\hspace{0.25em}3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $x$-intercepts\n        are\n         $\\text{A}(-1,0)$\n        and\n         $\\text{B}(3,0)$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $f$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/8374016b8a888aa9fc9466667cfab6ca4babdde2.svg\" alt=\"8374016b8a888aa9fc9466667cfab6ca4babdde2.svg\">\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= ag(x-h)+k \\\\[6pt]\n    2(x-1)^2 - 8 &= a\\big[6(x-h)^2\\big]+k \\\\[6pt]\n    2(x-1)^2 - 8 &= 6a(x-h)^2+k \\hspace{2.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we\n    find\n     $a = 1/3,\\hspace{0.25em} h = 1$\n    and  $k = -8$"},{"ref":"AA725","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA725\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe equation of a line $L_1$ is $2x + 3y = -5$.\n\n1.  Find the gradient of $L_1$. [2 marks]\n\nA second line, $L_2$, is perpendicular to $L_1$.\n\n2.  State the gradient of $L_2$. [1 mark]\n\nThe point P$(4,0)$ lies on $L_2$.\n\n3.  Find the equation of $L_2$, giving your answer in the form\n    $ax + by + d = 0$, where $a, b, d \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [2 marks]\n\nThe point Q is the intersection of $L_1$ and $L_2$.\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of Q. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we rewrite the equation of $L_1$ in the form $y = mx + c$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x + 3y &= -5 \\\\[12pt]\n    3y &= -2x - 5 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}x-\\dfrac{5}{3} \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the gradient of $L_1$ is\n     $m_{L_1} = -\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n2.  The gradient of $L_2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} m_{L_2} &= -\\dfrac{1}{m_{L_1}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $L_2 \\perp L_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{(-2/3)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the point-gradient formula with P$(4,0)$ and $m = 3/2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[5pt]\n    y - 0 &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}(x - 4) \\\\[6pt]\n    2y &= 3x - 12 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.45em} 0 = 3x - 2y - 12\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    L_1&:\\hspace{1.0em} 2x + 3y = -5 \\hspace{3em}{(1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    L_2&:\\hspace{1.0em} 3x - 2y = 12 \\hspace{3.25em}{(2)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving\n    the system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $x$ and $y$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{-0.8em} \\begin{array}{rcl}\n    x &=& 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &=& -3\n    \\end{array}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(2,-3)$"},{"ref":"AA728","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA728\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows the graph of a quadratic function\n$f(x) = 2x^2 + bx + c$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/75e0c507b59edc93cc9f79e429c580e90c045817.svg\" alt=\"75e0c507b59edc93cc9f79e429c580e90c045817.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $c$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$ and write down $f(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the graph of $f$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $c = -30$\n\n2.  The graph of $f$ passes through the point P$(3,0)$.\n    Hence, using the coordinates of P, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(3) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2(3)^2 + b(3) - 30 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    18 + 3b - 30 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    3b - 12 &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4em} b = 4\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} f(x) = 2x^2 + 4x - 30\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The axis of symmetry of the graph of $f$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em}  x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -\\dfrac{4}{2(2)} \\\\[8pt]\n    x &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $f(x)$ for $x = -1$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.65em} f(-1) &= 2(-1)^2 + 4(-1) - 30 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -32\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the vertex are\n     $(-1,-32)$"},{"ref":"AA729","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA729\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA farmer is going to fence two equal adjacent parcels of land. These\nparcels share one side (which also requires fencing) as shown in the\nfollowing diagram. The farmer has only $80$ metres of fence.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/580f23d360211ad7dd2c45e79d30c7d9c7d24f56.svg\" alt=\"580f23d360211ad7dd2c45e79d30c7d9c7d24f56.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the equation for the total length of the fence,\n    $80$ m, in terms of $x$ and $y$.\n    [1 mark]\n\n2.  Write down the total area of both parcels of land in terms of $x$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the maximum area, in m$^2$, of one parcel of land. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $80 = 3x + 4y$\n\n2.  The total area of both parcels of land in terms of $x$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} A(x) &= x(2y) \\\\[18pt]\n    &= 2xy \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 2x\\left(\\dfrac{80 - 3x}{4}\\right) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{from part (a)}] \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 40x - \\dfrac{3}{2}x^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $y = A(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/d34855549a850484b1041b3153951956e33882fa.svg\" alt=\"d34855549a850484b1041b3153951956e33882fa.svg\">\n\n    Hence the maximum area of one parcel of land is\n    $\\dfrac{266.67}{2} \\approx$\n     $133 \\text{ m}^2$"},{"ref":"AA730","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA730\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe axis of symmetry of the graph of a quadratic function has equation\n$x = \\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\n1.  Draw the axis of symmetry on the grid below. [1 mark]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/095afe930760d162dfc8b9c36b42836af719a0ca.svg\" alt=\"095afe930760d162dfc8b9c36b42836af719a0ca.svg\">\n\nThe graph of the quadratic function intersects the $x$-axis at the point\nP$(-1,0)$. There is a second point, Q , at\nwhich the graph of the quadratic function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n2.  Mark and label P and Q on the grid above. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of the quadratic function has equation $y = -x^2+bx+c$, where\n$b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Find the value of $b$ and the value of $c$.\n\n    2.  Find the coordinates of the vertex, M.\n    3.  Draw the graph of the quadratic function on the grid above.\n        [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the axis of symmetry on the grid below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/251642e55ba01124027bed7ddba72f11d48d8750.svg\" alt=\"251642e55ba01124027bed7ddba72f11d48d8750.svg\">\n\n2.  The $x$-coordinate of $Q$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= \\dfrac{3}{2} + \\left[\\dfrac{3}{2}-(-1)\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if we mark and label P and Q on the grid\n    below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/1fa563586fbd8e498bbc0f6a90199f2a3a99e7f7.svg\" alt=\"1fa563586fbd8e498bbc0f6a90199f2a3a99e7f7.svg\">\n\n3.\n    1.  Using the coordinates of P, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} 0 &= -(-1)^2 + b(-1) + c \\\\[6pt]\n        0 &= -1 - b + c \\\\[6pt]\n        1 &= -b + c \\hspace{6em}(1)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using the coordinates of\n        Q, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.6em} 0 &= -(4)^2 + b(4) + c \\\\[6pt]\n        0 &= -16 + 4b + c \\\\[6pt]\n        16 &= 4b + c \\hspace{7.5em}(2)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, solving the system\n        of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $b$ and $c$, we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{10em} b = 3\\hspace{0.75em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.75em}c = 4\\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = -x^2 + 3x + 4$ for $x = 3/2 = 1.5$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.2em} y &= -(1.5)^2 + 3(1.5) + 4 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 6.25\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of M are\n         $(1.5,6.25)$\n\n    3.  If we draw the graph of $y = -x^2 + 3x + 4$ on the grid below,\n        we obtain\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/db963f9fd832653990678f1ba763db2a3974d991.svg\" alt=\"db963f9fd832653990678f1ba763db2a3974d991.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA732","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA732\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph shows the curve of a quadratic function of the form\n$f(x) = ax^2 + bx + 90$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/e02d6931ce58420fa7c9cbe422473aefce55e151.svg\" alt=\"e02d6931ce58420fa7c9cbe422473aefce55e151.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the equation for the axis of symmetry of the curve.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of the curve. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  The axis of symmetry intersects the $x$-axis midway between the\n    $x$-intercepts.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.15em} x &= \\dfrac{-5+9}{2} \\\\\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.4em} x = 2\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substitute the given coordinates into $f(x)=ax^2+bx+90.$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the coordinates of A, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(-5) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    a(-5)^2 + b(-5) + 90 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    25a - 5b &= -90 \\hspace{1em} \\hspace{6em}(1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the\n    coordinates of B, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(9) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{0.6em} a(9)^2 + b(9) + 90 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    81a + 9b &= -90 \\hspace{7.5em}(2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the system\n    of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$ and $b$ [by using G.D.C.].\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.4em} a = -2\\hspace{0.75em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.75em}b = 8\\hspace{3em}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The vertex of the curve lies on the axis of symmetry.\\\n    Hence, evaluating $f(x)$ for $x = 2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.8em} f(2) &= -2(2)^2 + 8(2) + 90 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 98\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA733","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA733\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph of a quadratic function has a $y$-intercept at A$(0,24)$ and\n**one** of its $x$-intercepts is B$(2,0)$.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is $4$.\n\nThe equation of the quadratic function is in the form $y = ax^2+bx+c$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $c$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Write down the coordinates of the second $x$-intercept of the\n    function. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  $c = 24$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.4em} 4 &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\hspace{3em} \\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\\n    \\\\\n    b &= -8a \\hspace{7em}(1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the coordinates of B, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0 &= a(2)^2 + b(2) + c  \\\\[6pt]\n    [\\text{from $(1)$ \\& part (a)}] \\hspace{2em} 0 &= a(2)^2 + (-8a)(2) + 24 \\hspace{3.6em} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.1em} \\boxed{a = 2}   \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, substituting $a = 2$ in the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    b &= -8(2) \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[-15pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    b = -16 \\hspace{1.7em}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    $(6,0)$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA744","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA744\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe ocean pressure, $P$, under sea level can be modelled by the function\n\n$$\nP(D) = \\dfrac{D}{10}+1\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the distance in metres below sea\nlevel and $P$ is measured in atmospheres.\\\nA submarine cruising near the surface is submerged according to the\nfunction\n$$\nD(t) = 10+5t\n\n$$\nwhere $t$ is measured in minutes and $D$ is\nthe distance the submarine is below sea level, measured in\nmetres.\n\n1.  Find the composite function $P\\circ D$ and explain what it means in\n    the\\\n    context of this question[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find and interpret $(P\\circ D)(10)$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The composite function can be expressed as\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ D) (t) &= P(D(t)) \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &= P(10+5t)\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &= \\dfrac{10+5t}{10}+1 \\rule[-4.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{0.28em}= 2+\\dfrac{t}{2}\\rule[-5.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\hspace{6em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{This composite function represents the ocean pressure (in atmospheres) outside the submarine at time $t$ minutes.}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substituting $t=10$ in (1), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ D)(10)&=2+\\dfrac{10}{2}\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{0.28em}=7\\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{This result means that after 10 minutes, the ocean pressure outside the submarine is 7 atmospheres.}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA745","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA745\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA tyre manufacturing company has found that the number of tyres it\nproduces, $N$, can be modelled by the function\n$$\nN(t)=3t-9\n\n$$\nwhere $t$\nis the number of hours the factory operates per day, with a minimum of 3\nhours.\\\nThe profit the company makes, $P$, in dollars, depends on the number of\ntyres produced, and is modelled by the function\n$$\nP(N)=60N-850\n\n$$\nwhere\n$N$ is the number of tyres produced.\n\n1.  Find the company's profit or loss if it operates for $6$ hours per\n    day.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the company's profit in terms of the hours of operation per\n    day, $t$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine the number of hours the company needs to operate the\n    factory per day in order to earn a positive profit. Give your answer\n    to the nearest hour.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Substituting $t=6$ in $N(t)$, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     N(6) &= 3(6)-9 = 9\\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n    Thus, the profit is given by P(9)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n     P(9) &= 60(9)-850 = -310\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n     Therefore, operating $6$\n    hours a day, the company has a loss:\n    $$\\$\\hspace{0.07em}-310$$\n\n2.  The profit in terms of the hours of operation per day can be found\n    by the composite function $P\\circ N$. Hence, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ N)(t) &= P(N(t)) \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    &= P(3t-9) \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n    &= 60(3t-9)-850\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{7mm}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-5em}(P\\circ N)(t) = 180\\hspace{0.05em}t-1390\\hspace{5em}{(1)}\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{4mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The company has a positive profit if (1) is greater than 0. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (P\\circ N)(t) &>0\\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n    180t-1390 &> 0 \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    t &> \\dfrac{1390}{180} \\rule[-3mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n    t &> 7.72.\\rule[0mm]{0pt}{5.5mm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, the\n    factory needs to operate\n    $8$\n    hours per day in order for the company to earn a positive profit."},{"ref":"AA759","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA759\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe line $y = x + 1$ touches the graph of the function\n$g(x) = 2x^2 + kx + 3$ at one point. Find the possible values of $k$.","markScheme":"Letting $g(x) = y$ we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^2 + kx + 3 &= x + 1   \\\\[10pt]\n2x^2 + (k-1)x + 2 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n[\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{discriminant of quadratic above}\\hspace{0.1em}] &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n(k-1)^2 - 4(2)(2) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac] \\\\[10pt]\nk^2 - 2k + 1 - 16 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\nk^2 - 2k - 15 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n(k - 5)(k + 3) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.5em} k = -3, \\, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA763","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA763\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = -6x^2 + bx + c$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  A horizontal line, $L$, intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at\n    $(-1,h)$ and $(5,h)$, where $h \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n    1.  Find the equation of the axis of symmetry of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that $b = 24$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given $h=-8$, find the value of $c$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The equation of the axis of symmetry is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= \\dfrac{-1 + 5}{2} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}x= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[12pt]\n        \\hspace{10em} 2 &= -\\dfrac{b}{2(-6)} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)(i)}] \\\\[12pt]\n        & \\hspace{-0.5em} b = 24\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Since the line $L$ intersects the graph of $y = f(x)$ at $(-1,-8)$,\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(-1) &= -8  \\\\[12pt]\n    -6(-1)^2 + 24(-1) + c &= -8 \\\\[12pt]\n    -6 - 24 + c &= -8 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.45em} c = 22\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA932","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA932\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the quadratic polynomial $p(x)=2kx^2 + 15kx + 3k + 6$, where $k\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. Find an expression for the product of the roots of $p(x)$ in terms of $k$. [1 mark]\n\n2. Find the values of $k$ for which $p(x)$ has two distinct negative real roots. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. Let $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ be the two roots of $p(x)$. The product of the roots of a polynomial is given by\n\n    $\\dfrac{(-1)^na_0}{a_n}$\n\n    In this case we have $n=2$, $a_0=3k+6$ and $a_n=2k$. Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\alpha\\beta &= \\dfrac{(-1)^2(3k+6)}{2k} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-1.2em}\\boxed{\\alpha\\beta = \\dfrac{3k+6}{2k}} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using the quadratic formula, we can find an expression for the roots of the polynomial:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= \\dfrac{-15k \\pm \\sqrt{(15k)^2 - 4(2k)(3k+6)}}{4k} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{-15}{4} \\pm \\dfrac{\\sqrt{201k^2 - 48k}}{4k}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This implies that there must be at least one negative root, and it is therefore not possible to have two positive roots.\n\n    If the product of the roots is positive, then the polynomial will have two negative roots.\n\n    Using G.D.C. to plot $\\alpha\\beta$ as a function of $k$, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/AA932a.PNG\" alt=\"AA932a\">\n\n    Hence, we can see that the product $\\alpha\\beta$ is greater than zero when $k<-2$ and when $k>0$. Therefore, for values of $k$ in these intervals, the polynomial has two negative roots.\n\n    For both roots to be distinct and real, the discriminant must be greater than zero. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    b^2 - 4ac &> 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    201k^2 - 48k &> 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    k(201k - 48) &> 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    k &<0 \\text{ , } k > \\dfrac{48}{201}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    For the roots to be distinct, real, and both negative, we must have $k<-2$ or $k>\\dfrac{48}{201}$.\n\n    Hence, $p(x)$ has two distinct negative real roots for $k<-2 \\text{ and } k>\\dfrac{48}{201}$"},{"ref":"AA982","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA982\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x)=4x-2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. The graph of a linear function $g$ is perpendicular to the graph of $f$ and passes through the origin. Find an expression for $g(x)$.[2 marks]\n\n2. The graph of a linear function $h$ is parallel to the graph of $f$ and passes through the point $(3,4)$. Find an expression for $h(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The slope of $f$ is $m_f=4$, then $m_g=-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ is the slope of the function $g$ that is perpendicular to $f$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n     y-0&=-\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-0)\\\\[6pt]\n\n     y&=-\\dfrac{1}{4}x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{g(x)=-\\dfrac{1}{4}x}.$\n\n2. The slope of the function $h$ is $m_h=4$ because $h$ is parallel to the graph $f$. We have, \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y-4&=4(x-3)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y-4&=4x-12\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y=4x-8\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{h(x)=4x-8}.$"},{"ref":"AA983","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA983\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nThe graph of a function $f$ has its vertex at the point $(1,7)$ and intersects the $x$-axis at $x=-3$. \n\n1. Find $f$ in the form $f(x)=a\\left(x-h\\right)^2+k$. [3 marks]\n\nThe function $g$ is defined by $g(x)=mx^2-2nx+\\dfrac{2n^2}{m}$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2. In the case where $g(-2)=g(1)=3$,\n\n    1. find the value of $m$ and the value of $n$;\n\n    2. Find the range of $g$.[7 marks]  \n\nThe linear function $h$ is defined by $h(x)=nx+1$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3. Show that the graphs of $h$ and $g$ have two distinct points of intersection for every possible value of $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{R}$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. In the form $f(x)=a\\left(x-h\\right)^2+k$, $h$ is the $x$-coordinate of the vertex, and $k$ is the $y$-coordinate. Hence $h=1$ and $k=7$, and $f(x)=a\\left(x-1\\right)^2+7$.\n\n    The graph of $f$ passes through the point $(-3,0)$, so we have $f(-3)=0$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(-3) &= a\\left(-3-1\\right)^2+7 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    0 &= 16a + 7 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a &= -\\dfrac{7}{16}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\boxed{f(x)=-\\dfrac{7}{16}\\left(x-1\\right)^2+7}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. 1. We have that $g(-2)=g(1)=3$, so $g$ passes through the points $(-2,3)$ and $(1,3)$. Given that $g$ is a quadratic and hence its graph is a parabola, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/aa983a.PNG\" alt=\"aa983a\">\n\n        The axis of symmetry must be halfway between these points and has equation $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$. Using the formula for the axis of symmetry, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x &= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        -\\dfrac{1}{2} &= -\\dfrac{-2n}{2m} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        m &= -2n\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n        Substituting $-2n$ in place of $m$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x) &= -2nx^2 -2nx + \\dfrac{2n^2}{-2n} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= -2nx^2 - 2nx - n\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        And given that $g(1)=3$,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(1) &= -2n(1)^2 - 2n(1) - n \\\\[8pt]\n\n        3 &= -5n \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{n = -\\dfrac{3}{5}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        And calculating the value of $m$,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        m &= -2n \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= -2\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{m = \\dfrac{6}{5}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. As stated in part (b)(i), the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$. The $y$-coordinate will therefore be equal to $g\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$:\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x) &= \\dfrac{6}{5}x^2 + \\dfrac{6}{5}x + \\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        g\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) &= \\left(\\dfrac{6}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{6}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) + \\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{10} - \\dfrac{6}{10} + \\dfrac{6}{10} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{10}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The vertex is at $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{3}{10}\\right)$ and the graph of $g$ is as follows:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Linear%20and%20Quadratic%20Functions/images/aa983b.PNG\" alt=\"aa983b\">\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\boxed{\\text{The range of $g$ is $g(x) \\geq \\dfrac{3}{10}$.}}  \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n3. If $g$ and $h$ always have two distinct points of intersection, then the equation $g(x)=h(x)$ must have two unique solutions. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    g(x) &= h(x) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    mx^2-2nx+\\dfrac{2n^2}{m} &= nx + 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    mx^2 - 3nx + \\dfrac{2n^2}{m} -1 &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can use the discriminant to check the number of solutions. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\Delta &= b^2 - 4ac \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= (-3n)^2 - 4(m)\\left(\\dfrac{2n^2}{m} -1\\right)\\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=(-3n)^2 - 4(m)\\left(\\dfrac{2n^2}{m} -1\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=9n^2 - 8n^2 + 4m \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=n^2 + 4m \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$, $4m>0$. $n^2 \\geq 0$. Hence $\\Delta>0$. Thus\n\n    $$\\boxed{\\text{The graphs of $h$ and $g$ have two distinct points of intersection for all $m \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{R}$.}}$$"}]},{"name":"Modulus Equations and Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA1027","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1027\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{4-ax}{5x - 2}$.\n\n1. Write down the equation of the vertical asymptote of the graph of $y = f(x)$.[1 mark]\n\nThe line $y = -1.4$ is a horizontal asymptote to the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n2. Find the value of $a$.[2 marks]\n\n3. The line $y = x - c$, where $c \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$, intersects the graph of $y = \\big|f(x)\\big|$ at exactly one point. Find the possible values of $c$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Solving the equation $5x-2=0$, we have $\\boxed{x = \\dfrac{2}{5}}$\n\n2. The horizontal asymptote will be equal to $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty}f(x)$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\displaystyle\\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty}f(x) &= \\displaystyle\\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty}\\dfrac{4-ax}{5x-2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    -1.4 &= -\\dfrac{a}{5} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{a=7}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. If we draw the graph of $y = |f(x)|$ [by using G.D.C.], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/aa1027a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1027a\">\n\n    A line of the form $y = x - c$ where $c \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ has a gradient of $m=1$ and a $y$-intercept that is less than zero. The following diagram shows the graph of $y=x$, which represents an absolute upper limit for the $y$-intercept.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/aa1027b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1027b\">\n\n    The line $y=x$ intersects the graph of $y = \\big|f(x)\\big|$ at three points.\n\n    The following diagram shows the graph of $y=x-0.571$, which passes through the point A$(0.571,0)$ and has a $y$-intercept of $-0.571$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/aa1027c.PNG\" alt=\"aa1027c\">\n\n    The line $y=x-0.571$ intersects the graph of $y = \\big|f(x)\\big|$ at two points. Any line of gradient $m=1$ and $y$-intercept of less than $-0.571$ will intersect the graph of $y = \\big|f(x)\\big|$ exactly once.\n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{c > 0.571}$"},{"ref":"AA124","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA124\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the graphs of $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}|x|$ and $y = 2|x| - a$, where\n$a \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n1.  Sketch the graphs on the same set of axes. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that the graphs enclose a region of area 40 square units,\\\n    find the value of $a$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graphs of $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}|x|$ and\n    $y = 2|x| - a$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/68ec0f82b42dd94d75c48aa4929409d0a553c4d8.svg\" alt=\"68ec0f82b42dd94d75c48aa4929409d0a553c4d8.svg\">\n\n2.  The two lines in the $4$th quadrant intersect when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.4em} -\\dfrac{1}{2}x &= 2x -a \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x > 0$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    -x &= 4x - 2a \\\\[10pt]\n    2a &= 5x \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= \\dfrac{2a}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the area enclosed between\n    the two graphs is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{base}\\times\\text)\\bigg] &= 2\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big(a\\times\\dfrac{2a}{5}\\Big)\\bigg] \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2a^2}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, given that the area is $40$\n    square units, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{2a^2}{5} &= 40 \\\\[6pt]\n    a^2 &= 100 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.7em} a = 10\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA128","marks":"17","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA128\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 1+ \\dfrac{4x}{x+3}$, for\n$x \\neq -3$.\n\n1.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, indicating clearly any asymptotes\n    and\\\n    points of intersection with the $x$ and $y$ axes. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for $f^{-1}$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find all values of $x$ for which $f(x) = f^{-1}(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Solve the inequality $|f(x)| < 2$. [4 marks]\n\n5.  Solve the inequality $f(|x|) < 2$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we solve the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1+ \\dfrac{4x}{x+3} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{5x+3}{x+3} &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\n    5x + 3 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    x &= -\\dfrac{3}{5} \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n    P$(-3/5,0)$ is the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point Q$(0,1)$ is the $y$-intercept of\n    the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow\\pm\\infty} f(x) &= 1 + \\dfrac{4}{1} \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the line $y = 5$ is a horizontal asymptote\n    of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    The denominator of $f$ is zero when $x = -3$. Hence the line\n    $x = -3$\\\n    is a vertical asymptote of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    If we sketch of the graph of $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/a4ee5024679811f4302b4cf52d17f68170c60827.svg\" alt=\"a4ee5024679811f4302b4cf52d17f68170c60827.svg\">\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n    $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\hspace{3.7em}  \\\\[8pt]\n    f\\left(y\\right) &= x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} 1+ \\dfrac{4y}{y+3} &= x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{5y+3}{y+3} &= x \\\\[10pt]\n    5y+3 &= xy+3x \\\\[16pt]\n    (5-x)y &= 3x-3 \\\\[10pt]\n    y &= \\dfrac{3x-3}{5-x} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence an expression for\n    $f^{-1}$ is\n     $f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{3x-3}{5-x}$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $f(x) = f^{-1}(x)$ for $x$, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     f(x) &= f^{-1}(x) \\\\[9pt]\n     1 + \\dfrac{4x}{x+3} &= \\dfrac{3x-3}{5-x} \\\\[6pt]\n     \\dfrac{5x+3}{x+3} &= \\dfrac{3x-3}{5-x} \\\\[10pt]\n     (5x+3)(5-x) &= (3x-3)(x+3) \\\\[12pt]\n     \\hspace{1.6em} 25x - 5x^2 + 15 -3x &= 3x^2 + 9x -3x - 9 \\\\[12pt]\n     -8x^2 + 16x + 24 &= 0\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 - 2x - 3 &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    (x+1)(x-3) &= 0 \\hspace{4em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.6em} x = -1,\\hspace{0.25em}3\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{8.1em} \\begin{array}{rcccl}\n    && |f(x)| &<& 2 \\\\[13pt]\n    -2 &<& f(x) &<& 2 \\\\[12pt]\n    f^{-1}(-2) &<& x &<& f^{-1}(2) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $f^{-1}(f(x)) = x$}]\n    \\end{array}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $y = f^{-1}(x)$ for $x = -2$,\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} f^{-1}(-2) &= \\dfrac{3(-2)-3}{5-(-2)} \\\\[3pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{9}{7} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $y = f^{-1}(x)$ for\n    $x = 2$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} f^{-1}(2) &= \\dfrac{3(2)-3}{5-2}  \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the set of solutions to the inequality\n    $|f(x)| < 2$ is\n     $-\\frac{9}{7}<x<1$\n\n5.  If we solve the inequality $f(|x|) < 2$ for $|x|$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} 1+ \\dfrac{4|x|}{|x|+3} &< 2 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\dfrac{4|x|}{|x|+3} &< 1 \\\\[10pt]\n    4|x| &< |x|+3 \\\\[16pt]\n    3|x| &< 3 \\\\[16pt]\n    |x| &< 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the set of solutions to the\n    inequality $f(|x|) < 2$ is\n     $-1<x<1$"},{"ref":"AA129","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA129\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Sketch the curve $y = -\\left|\\dfrac{5}{x-2}\\right|$ and line\n    $y = -x - 4$ on the same axes,\n\n    clearly indicating any $x$ and $y$ intercepts and any asymptotes.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the exact solutions to the equation $x + 4 = \\dfrac{5}{|x-2|}$.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $f(x) = -\\left|\\dfrac{5}{x-2}\\right|$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$,\n    $x \\neq 2$.\n\n    Since $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\neq 2$, the curve\n    $y = f(x)$ has no $x$-intercepts.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} y &= f(0) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -\\left|-\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point A$(0,-5/2)$ is the\n    $y$-intercept of the curve $y = f(x)$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\lim_{x \\rightarrow\\pm\\infty} f(x) = 0 \\hspace{3.9em}\n    $$\n    Hence the $x$-axis is a horizontal asymptote of the curve\n    $y = f(x)$.\n\n    The denominator of $f$ is zero when $x = 2$. Hence the line $x = 2$\\\n    is a vertical asymptote of the curve $y = f(x)$.\n\n    The $x$ and $y$ intercepts of the line $y = -4-x$ are B$(-4,0)$ and\n    C$(0,-4)$.\n\n    If we sketch the curve $y = f(x)$ and line $y = -x - 4$ on the same\n    axes, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/38ce371d4e9621963b29074adf6413c8b9f71c7c.svg\" alt=\"38ce371d4e9621963b29074adf6413c8b9f71c7c.svg\">\n\n2.  If we solve the equation $x + 4 = \\dfrac{5}{|x-2|}$ for $x < 2$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x + 4 &= -\\dfrac{5}{x-2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x - 2 < 0$}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\hspace{7.7em} (x+4)(x-2) &= -5 \\\\[12pt]\n    x^2 + 2x - 8 &= -5 \\\\[12pt]\n    x^2 + 2x - 3 &= 0 \\\\[13pt]\n    (x + 3)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\[13pt]\n    x &= -3,\\hspace{0.25em} 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we simplify the equation\n    $x + 4 = \\dfrac{5}{|x-2|}$ for $x > 2$, we have\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     x + 4 &= \\dfrac{5}{x-2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x - 2 > 0$}] \\\\[9pt]\n     \\hspace{10em} (x+4)(x-2) &= 5 \\\\[13pt]\n     x^2 + 2x - 8 &= 5 \\\\[13pt]\n     x^2 + 2x - 13 &= 0\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n     Hence, using the quadratic\n    formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{12em} x &= \\dfrac{-2+\\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(-13)}}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x > 2$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-2+2\\sqrt{14}}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -1 + \\sqrt{14} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the\n    exact solutions to the equation $\\text{\\(x + 4 = \\dfrac{5}{|x-2|}\\)}$ are  $-3,\\hspace{0.25em}1,\\hspace{0.25em}\\textstyle -1 + \\sqrt{14}$"},{"ref":"AA149","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA149\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = x^2\\arcsin(x)$, for $-1 \\leq x \\leq 1$.\n\n1.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Write down the range of $f$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Solve the inequality $\\left|x^2\\arcsin(x)\\right| > 0.5$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/116e48b3daf01abd56171298ff9c296be1c29e27.svg\" alt=\"116e48b3daf01abd56171298ff9c296be1c29e27.svg\">\n\n2.\n    $\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $|x^2\\arcsin(x)| = 0.5$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.4em} x^2\\arcsin(x) &= \\pm 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= \\pm 0.761 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclcrcl}\n    -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq x^2\\arcsin(x) &<& -0.5 \\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& 0.5 &<& x^2\\arcsin(x) \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[9pt]\n    -1 \\leq x &<& -0.761 \\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& 0.761 &<& x \\leq 1 \\\\\n    \\end{array} \\hspace{0.9em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the set of solutions\n    to the inequality $|x^2\\arcsin(x)| > 0.5$ is\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     [-1,-0.761)\\cup(0.761,1]\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA153","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA153\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Sketch the graph of $y = (x-2)^2-4|x-2|-1$, for $-3 \\leq x \\leq 7$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve the equation $(x-2)^2-4|x-2|-1 = 0$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = (x-2)^2-4|x-2|-1$ for\n    $-3 \\leq x \\leq 7$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/0ab2fa40cd74cdb2dd31cab2dedea2512bfb0bd0.svg\" alt=\"0ab2fa40cd74cdb2dd31cab2dedea2512bfb0bd0.svg\">\n\n2.  The solutions to the equation $(x-2)^2-4|x-2|-1 = 0$ are the\n    $x$-coordinates of the points C and D.\n\n    Hence the solutions to the equation are\n     $x \\approx -2.24,\\hspace{0.25em}6.24$"},{"ref":"AA195","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA195\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{9-12x}{cx - 20}$, for $x \\neq \\dfrac{20}{c}$, where\n$c \\neq 0$.\n\n1.  The line $x = 5$ is a vertical asymptote to the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n    1.  Find the value of $c$.\n\n    2.  Write down the equation of the horizontal asymptote to the graph\n        of $y = f(x)$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  The line $y = h$, where $h \\in \\mathbb{R}$, intersects the\n    graph of $y = \\big|f(x)\\big|$ at exactly one point. Find the\n    possible values of $h$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the equation of the vertical asymptote, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &= 0 \\hspace{8.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n        5c - 20 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        5c &= 20 \\\\[4pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.67em} c = 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $y = -3$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of $y = |f(x)|$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Modulus%20Equations%20and%20Functions/images/05e9ba9d7a31d35d6e66b11e5f0ab06197d7db87.svg\" alt=\"05e9ba9d7a31d35d6e66b11e5f0ab06197d7db87.svg\">\n\n    A line of the form $y = h$ can intersect the graph of $y = |f(x)|$\n    exactly at one point either at A or at B, as shown on the diagram\n    above.\n\n    Hence we find\n     $h = 0,\\hspace{0.25em}3$"}]},{"name":"Polynomials","questions":[{"ref":"AA1277","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1277\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nWhen the resulting product of $3x^2+7x-6$ multiplied by $ax+1$ is divided by $x-1$ the remainder is $-4$.\n\nFind the integer $a$.","markScheme":"Although not essential, we may notice that we can factorise the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    3x^2+7x-6&=(3x-2)(x+3)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe will let the the product of the quadratic and linear expression be $p(x)$, hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    p(x)&=(3x-2)(x+3)(ax+1)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow let's consider dividing $p(x)$ by $x-1$.\n\nUsing the remainder theorem we can form an equation\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    p(1)&=-4\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nHence substituting $x=1$ into $p(x)$ the L.H.S. of above becomes\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    (3(1)-2)(1+3)(a(1)+1)&=-4\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this for $a$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    1\\times 4 \\times (a+1)&=-4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    a+1&=-1\\\\[6pt]\n\n    a&=-2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA135","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA135\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx  +  d$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where\n$a,b,c,d \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. The diagram below shows part of the graph of\n$y = f(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Polynomials/images/f2e9d58912ae2f10d7fdf9d12ee3b7594c2c45fb.svg\" alt=\"f2e9d58912ae2f10d7fdf9d12ee3b7594c2c45fb.svg\">\n\n1.  Using the information shown in the diagram, find the values of $a$,\n    $b$, $c$ and $d$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Let $g(x) = -\\dfrac{3}{4}f(-2x+1)$.\n    1.  Find the coordinates of the points where the graph of $y = g(x)$\n        intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\n    2.  Find the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  From the diagram, we see that the roots of the polynomial equation\n    $f(x) = 0$ are $-3$, $-1$ and $3$.\n\n    Hence, using the factor theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= a(x-3)(x+1)(x+3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= a(x^2-9)(x+1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= a(x^3 + x^2 - 9x - 9)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we substitute\n    $x = 0$ in the expression $(1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} f(0) &= a(0^3 + 0^2 - 9(0) - 9) \\\\[12pt]\n    -3 &= -9a \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(0) = -3$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    a &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we substitute $a = 1/3$ in the\n    expression $(1)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{3}(x^3 + x^2 - 9x - 9) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + \\dfrac{1}{3}x^2 - 3x - 3 \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find\n     $a = b = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n    and  $c = d = -3$\n\n2.\n    1.  Solving the equation $g(x) = 0$ for $x$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        -\\dfrac{3}{4}f(-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        f(-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        -2x + 1 &= -3,\\hspace{0.25em} -1,\\hspace{0.25em} 3 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[12pt]\n        -2x &= -4,\\hspace{0.25em} -2,\\hspace{0.25em} 2 \\\\[12pt]\n        x &= -1,\\hspace{0.25em}1,\\hspace{0.25em}2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence\n        the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts of the graph of $y = g(x)$\n        are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (-1,0),\\hspace{0.25em}(1,0),\\hspace{0.25em}(2,0)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y=g(x)$ for $x = 0$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{10.9em} y &= g(0) \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{3}{4}f(2(0)+1) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{3}{4}f(1) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{3}{4}\\hspace{-0.2em}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{3}(1)^3 + \\dfrac{1}{3}(1)^2 - 3(1) - 3\\right] \\\\[4pt]\n        &=-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\hspace{-0.2em}\\left[-\\dfrac{16}{3}\\right] \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the $y$-intercept of the graph of\n        $y = g(x)$ is\n         $\\mathrm{P}(0,4)$"},{"ref":"AA136","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA136\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe quadratic equation $x^2 - kx + (k - 3) = 0$ has roots $\\alpha$ and\n$\\beta$ such that $\\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 = 6$. Without solving the\nequation, find the possible values of the real number $k$.","markScheme":"Using Vieta's formulas, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha+\\beta &=-\\dfrac{a_1}{a_2} \\\\[4pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{(-k)}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n&= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.9em} \\alpha\\beta &= (-1)^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_2}\\bigg] \\\\[4pt]\n&= \\dfrac{k-3}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n&= k-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\alpha + \\beta)^2  &= k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{5.2em} \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 + 2\\alpha\\beta &= k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n6 + 2(k - 3)  &=  k^2  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 = 6$}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\cancel{6} + 2k - \\cancel{6}  &=  k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n0 &= k^2 - 2k \\\\[7pt]\n0 &= k(k - 2)\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.5em} k = 0,\\hspace{0.25em}2\n$$"},{"ref":"AA137","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA137\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x) = x^3 - 2x^2 + ax + b$ and that\ndivision of $f(x)$ by $(x+2)$ leaves a remainder of $18$, find the value\nof $a$ and the value of $b$.","markScheme":"Using the factor theorem, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(4) &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n4^3 - 2(4)^2 + a(4) + b &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n32 + 4a + b &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n4a + b &= -32 \\hspace{2em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the remainder\ntheorem, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= 18 \\\\[5pt]\n(-2)^3 - 2(-2)^2 + a(-2) + b &= 18 \\\\[6pt]\n-16 - 2a + b &= 18 \\\\[6pt]\n-2a + b &= 34  \\hspace{6em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, solving the\nsystem of two linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$ and $b$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na = -11\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.525em}b = 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA139","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA139\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the equation $32x^3 - 144x^2 + 214x - 105 = 0$.\n\n1.  Find the sum and product of the roots of this equation. [1 mark]\n\n2.  The roots of this equation are three consecutive terms of an\n    arithmetic\\\n    sequence. Taking the roots to be $a - d, a, a + d$, solve the\n    equation. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using Vieta's formulas, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{sum of roots} &= -\\dfrac{a_2}{a_3} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{(-144)}{32} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{9}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{product of roots} &= (-1)^3\\bigg[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_3}\\bigg] \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{(-105)}{32} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{105}{32}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (a-d)+a+(a+d) &= \\dfrac{9}{2} \\hspace{9.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3a &= \\dfrac{9}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    a &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a(a-d)(a+d) &= \\dfrac{105}{32} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2}\\bigg[\\dfrac{9}{4}-d^2\\bigg] &= \\dfrac{105}{32} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{9}{4}-d^2 &= \\dfrac{35}{16} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{9}{4}-\\dfrac{35}{16} &= d^2\\\\[6pt]\n    d^2 &= \\dfrac{1}{16} \\\\[6pt]\n    d &= \\dfrac{1}{4} \\hspace{3em} \\bigg[\\text{or $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$}\\bigg]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the roots of the equation $32x^3 - 144x^2 + 214x - 105 = 0$\n    are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} a - d &= \\dfrac{3}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\hspace{0.525em}\\dfrac{5}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n    a &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n    a + d &= \\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\hspace{0.525em}\\dfrac{7}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA140","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA140\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nIt is given that $f(x) = 2x^4 + 5x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 4$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.  Given that $x^2 + x - 2$ is a factor of $f(x)$, find the values of\n    $a$ and $b$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Factorise $f(x)$ into a product of linear factors. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, labeling the maximum and minimum\n    points and the $x$ and $y$ intercepts. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Using your graph, state the range of values of $c$ for which\n    $f(x) = c$ has exactly four real roots. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Factorising $x^2 + x - 2$ into a product of linear factors, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 + x - 2 = (x-1)(x+2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $(x-1)$ and $(x+2)$\n    are both factors of $f(x)$.\n\n    Hence, using the factor theorem, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    2(1)^4 + 5(1)^3 + a(1)^2 + b(1) + 4 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    2 + 5 + a + b + 4 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    a + b &= -11 \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the factor theorem again, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(-2) &= 0 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2(-2)^4 + 5(-2)^3 + a(-2)^2 + b(-2) + 4 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n    32 - 40 + 4a - 2b + 4 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n    4a - 2b &= 4 \\\\[7pt]\n    2a - b &= 2 \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the system of\n    two linear equations $(1)-(2)$ for $a$ and $b$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a = -3\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.525em}b = -8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we divide $2x^4 + 5x^3 - 3x^2 - 8x + 4$ by $x^2 + x - 2$, we have\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= 2x^4 + 5x^3 - 3x^2 - 8x + 4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x^2 + x - 2)(2x^2 + 3x - 2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x-1)(x+2)(2x-1)(x+2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x-1)(2x-1)(x+2)^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Polynomials/images/fee6121260f114c0cc689969551419dd8b20d6fc.svg\" alt=\"fee6121260f114c0cc689969551419dd8b20d6fc.svg\">\n\n4.  From the diagram, we see that the equation $f(x) = c$ has exactly\n    four real\n\n    roots when\n     $0 < c < 6.92$"},{"ref":"AA141","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA141\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nConsider the polynomial $p(z) = z^5 + z^4 - z^3 + z^2 + 4z + 2$, for\n$z \\in \\mathbb{C}$.\n\n1.  Write down the sum and product of the roots of $p(z) = 0$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $(z+1)$ is a factor of $p(z)$. [2 marks]\n\nThe polynomial can be written in the form\n$p(z) = (z+1)^3(z^2 + cz + d)$.\n\n3.  Find the value of $c$ and the value of $d$. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Hence find the complex roots of $p(z) = 0$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using Vieta's formulas, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{sum of roots} &= -\\dfrac{a_4}{a_5} \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{product of roots} &= (-1)^5\\bigg[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_5}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{2}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $p(z)$ for $z = -1$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} p(-1) &= (-1)^5 + (-1)^4 - (-1)^3 + (-1)^2 + 4(-1) + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, by factor theorem, $(z+1)$ is a factor of\n    $p(z)$.\n\n3.  Evaluating $p(z)$ for $z = 0$ and $z = 1$, we have $p(0) = 2$ and\n    $p(1) = 8$.\n\n    Hence, substituting $z = 0,\\hspace{0.1em}1$ in the equation\n    $p(z) = (z+1)^3(z^2 + cz + d)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} p(0) &= (0+1)^3(0^2 + c(0) + d) \\\\[6pt]\n    2 &= d \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 2 \\hspace{6.1em}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} p(1) &= (1+1)^3(1^2 + c(1) + 2) \\\\[6pt]\n    8 &= 8(c+3) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    c = -2 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$\n\n4.  The complex roots of $p(z) = 0$ are the complex roots of\n    $z^2 - 2z + 2 = 0$.\n\n    Hence, using the quadratic formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} z &= \\dfrac{-(-2)\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(2)}}{2(1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2 \\pm 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 \\pm {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA144","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA144\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe quadratic equation $x^2 - kx + (k-1) = 0$ has roots $\\alpha$ and\n$\\beta$. Without solving the equation, find the possible values of the\nreal number $k$ given that $\\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 = 17$.","markScheme":"Using Vieta's formulas, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha+\\beta &=-\\dfrac{a_1}{a_2} \\\\[4pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{(-k)}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n&= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.9em} \\alpha\\beta &= (-1)^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_2}\\bigg] \\\\[4pt]\n&= \\dfrac{k-1}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n&= k-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\alpha + \\beta)^2  &= k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{5.8em} \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 + 2\\alpha\\beta &= k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n17 + 2(k - 1)  &=  k^2  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 = 17$}] \\\\[6pt]\n2k + 15  &=  k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n0 &= k^2 - 2k - 15 \\\\[7pt]\n0 &= (k+3)(k-5)\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{1.4em} k = -3,\\hspace{0.25em}5\n$$"},{"ref":"AA150","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA150\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nWhen $p(x) = x^2 - 2x + p$ is divided by $x - r$, the remainder is $4$.\\\nGiven that $p,r \\in \\mathbb{R}$, find the largest possible value for\n$p$.","markScheme":"Using the remainder theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(r) &= 4 \\\\[6pt]\nr^2 - 2r + p &= 4 \\hspace{6em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we rewrite the equation\n$(1)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.4em} p &= -r^2 + 2r + 4 \\\\[6pt]\np &= -\\big[r^2 - 2r\\big] + 4 \\\\[6pt]\np &= -\\big[(r-1)^2 - 1\\big] + 4 \\\\[6pt]\np &= -(r-1)^2 + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the largest possible value for\n$p$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{10.8em} p_{\\mathrm{max}} &= -(1-1)^2 + 5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{when $r = 1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n&= 0 + 5 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA157","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA157\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nWhen $p(x) = x^2 - 2x - c$ is divided by $(x-r)$, the remainder is\n$-5$.\\\nGiven that $c, r \\in \\mathbb{R}$, find the smallest possible value for\n$c$.","markScheme":"Using the remainder theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(r) &= -5 \\\\[6pt]\nr^2 - 2r - c &= -5 \\hspace{6em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we rewrite\nthe equation $(1)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.8em} c &= r^2 - 2r + 5 \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\big[(r - 1)^2 - 1\\big] + 5 \\\\[6pt]\n&= (r - 1)^2 + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the smallest possible value for\n$c$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{9.4em} c_{\\mathrm{min}} &= (1-1)^2 + 4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{when $r = 1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n&= 0 + 4 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA158","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA158\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nTwo distinct roots for the polynomial equation\n$z^4 - 10z^3 + cz^2 + dz + 170$ are\n$a + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$1 + b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n$a,b,c,d \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $b > 0$.\n\n1.  Write down the other two roots in terms of $a$ and $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu},\\hspace{0.25em}1 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b$\n\n2.  Using Vieta's formulas, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{sum of roots} &= -\\dfrac{a_3}{a_4} \\\\[6pt]\n    (a + \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}) + (a - \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}) + (1 + \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b}) + (1 - \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b}) &= -\\dfrac{(-10)}{1} \\hspace{7.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n    2a + 2 &= 10 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5em} a = 4\n    $$\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{product of roots} &= (-1)^4\\bigg[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_4}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    (4 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(4 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b)(1 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b) &= (-1)^4\\bigg[\\dfrac{170}{1}\\bigg] \\hspace{2em} \\\\[12pt]\n    17(1 + b^2) &= 170 \\\\[16pt]\n    1 + b^2 &= 10 \\\\[16pt]\n    b^2 &= 9 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.4em} b = 3  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $b > 0$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA203","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA203\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^4+5x^3+ax^2+bx+2$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $a,b$ are\nconstants. The remainder when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)$ is $6$, and\nthe remainder when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2)$ is $-6$. Find the value\nof $a$ and the value of $b$.","markScheme":"Using the remainder theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 6 \\\\[6pt]\n1^4+5(1)^3+a(1)^2+b(1)+2 &= 6 \\\\[7pt]\n1 + 5 + a + b + 2 &= 6 \\\\[7pt]\na + b &= -2 \\hspace{3.9em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= -6 \\\\[6pt]\n(-2)^4+5(-2)^3+a(-2)^2+b(-2)+2 &= -6 \\\\[7pt]\n16 - 40 + 4a - 2b + 2 &= -6 \\\\[7pt]\n4a -2b &= 16 \\hspace{8em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, solving the\nsystem of linear equations (1)-(2) for $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.8em} a = 2\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}b=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA204","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA204\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe polynomial $p(x) = 2x^4 + a_3x^3 + a_2x^2 + a_1x  - 12$ is divisible\nby each of $(x + 1)$, $(x - 1)$ and $(x - 2)$.\n\nFind the values of $a_1$, $a_2$ and $a_3$.","markScheme":"Using the factor theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(-1) = p(1) = p(2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence $-1$, $1$ and $2$ are\nroots of $p(x)$. Let $x_4$ be the fourth root of $p(x)$.\n\nUsing Vieta's formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1)(1)(2)x_4 &= (-1)^4\\left[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_4}\\right] \\\\[5pt]\n-2x_4 &= \\dfrac{(-12)}{2} \\\\[13pt]\n-2x_4 &= -6 \\\\[17pt]\nx_4 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we obtain\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n p(x) &= 2(x+1)(x-1)(x-2)(x-3) \\\\[6pt]\n &= 2(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 5x + 6) \\\\[6pt]\n &= 2x^4 - 10x^3 + 10x^2  + 10x - 12\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n Hence we find\n $a_1 = a_2 = 10$\nand  $a_3 = -10$"},{"ref":"AA981","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA981\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nConsider the family of polynomials of the form $P(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ where coefficients $a$, $b$, $c$ and $d$ belong to the set $\\{2,6,8,24\\}$.\n\n1. Find the number of possible polynomials if\n\n    \n\n    1. each coefficient value can be repeated;\n\n    \n\n    2. each coefficient must be different.[4 marks]\n\n    \n\nConsider the case where $P(x)$ has $x+3$ as a factor, two purely imaginary roots, and all the coefficients are different.\n\n2. 1. By considering the sum of the roots, find the two possible combinations for coefficients $a$ and $b$.\n\n    \n\n    2. Show that there is only one way to assign the values $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$ if $P(0)=24$.[7 marks]\n\nNow, consider the polynomial with the coefficients found in part (b) (ii).\n\n3. 1. Express $P(x)$ as a product of one linear and one quadratic factor.\n\n    \n\n    2. Determine the three roots of $P(x)$.[7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Let $X$ be the total number of polynomials.\n\n    1. Given that each coefficient value can be repeated, we have \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        X&=4 \\times 4 \\times 4 \\times 4\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=4^4\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=256\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Therefore, there are $\\boxed{256}$ possible polynomials if the coefficients can be repeated.\n\n    2. Given that each coefficient must be different, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        X&=4!\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=24\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Therefore, there are $\\boxed{24}$ possible polynomials if each coefficient must be different.\n\n2. 1. Let the roots be $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$. Given that $x+3$ is a factor, $x_1=-3$ is a root. Complex roots occur in conjugate pairs, therefore if the other two roots are imaginary, we have $x_2 = \\alpha \\mathrm{i}$ and $x_3 = -\\alpha \\mathrm{i}$, where $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Hence, the sum of the roots is given by\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -3 + \\alpha \\mathrm{i} - \\alpha \\mathrm{i} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= -3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using the formula for the sum of the roots, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -\\dfrac{b}{a} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        -3 &= -\\dfrac{b}{a} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        3a &= b\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We have that $a$ and $b$ belong to the set $\\{2,6,8,24\\}$ and $3a=b$. Therefore,\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{The two possible combinations are $a=2,\\,b=6$ and $a=8,\\,b=24$.}}$$\n\n    2. If $P(0)=24$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        P(0) &= a(0)^3 + b(0)^2 + c(0) + d \\\\[6pt]\n\n        24 &= d\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The coefficients must all be different. If $d=24$, $b \\neq 24$, so we have from part (b)(i) that $a=2$ and $b=6$. Therefore $c=8$.\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{If $P(0)=24$, we must have $a=2,\\,b=6,\\,c=8$ and $d=24$.}}$$\n\n3. 1. Our polynomial is $P(x) = 2x^3 + 6x^2 + 8x + 24$. Given that $x+3$ is a factor, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2x^3 + 6x^2 + 8x + 24 &= (x+3)(2x^2 + ex + 8) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 2x^3 +ex^2 + 8x + 6x^2 +3ex + 24 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        2x^3 + 6x^2 + 8x + 24 &= 2x^3 + (e+6)x^2 + (8+3e)x +24\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Equating the coefficients of $x^2$ and $x$ on the LHS and RHS, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        6 &= e + 6 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        8 &= 8 + 3e\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We must have $e=0$. Therefore\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        P(x) &= (x+3)(2x^2 + 8) \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-2.1em}\\boxed{P(x) = 2(x+3)(x^2 + 4)}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. We have from part (b)(i) that $x_1=-3$. To find the other two roots, we solve $x^2+4=0$:\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x^2+4&=0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x^2 &= -4 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x &= \\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x &= \\pm 2\\mathrm{i}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{The roots of $P(x)$ are $x_1=-3,\\,x_2=-2\\mathrm{i}$, and $x_3=2\\mathrm{i}$.}}$$"}]},{"name":"Rational Functions","questions":[{"ref":"AA1013","marks":"17","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1013\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x+4}{2x-5}$, for $x\\neq\\dfrac{5}{2}$. \n\n1. For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the coordinates of:\n\n    1. the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2. the $y$-intercept. [3 marks]\n\n2. For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the equation of:\n\n    1. the horizontal asymptote;\n\n    2. the vertical asymptote. [3 marks]\n\n3. Hence sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$.  [2 marks]\n\n4. Find $f^{-1}(x)$. [3 marks]\n\nThe graphs of $f(x)$ and $f^{-1}(x)$ have two points of intersection.\n\n5. Find the coordinates of the two points of intersection. **Give your answers to two decimal places**. [3 marks]\n\n6. Hence find the equation of the perpendicular bisector to the two points of intersection. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\dfrac{3x+4}{2x-5} &= 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        3x+4 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n        x &= -\\dfrac{4}{3}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{3},0\\right)$\n\n    \n\n    2. Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{3(0)+4}{2(0)-5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= -\\dfrac{4}{5}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $\\left(0,-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)$\n\n2. 1. We have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty} f(x) &= \\dfrac{3}{2} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\n    2. We have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        [\\text{denominator of $f$}] &= 0 \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        2x-5 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x &= \\dfrac{5}{2}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x = \\dfrac{5}{2}$\n\n3. If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/aa1013a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1013a\">\n\n4. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y &= \\dfrac{3x+4}{2x-5} \\hspace{5.5em} \\left[\\text{where } y = f(x)\\right]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= \\dfrac{3y+4}{2y-5} \\hspace{3em} \\left[\\text{where } y = f^{-1}(x)\\right] \\\\[8pt]\n\n    x(2y-5) &= 3y+4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2xy - 5x &= 3y +4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2xy - 3y &= 5x + 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y(2x - 3) &= 5x + 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y &= \\dfrac{5x+4}{2x-3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3em}\\boxed{f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{5x+4}{2x-3}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. Sketching $f(x)$ and $f^{-1}(x)$ on the same axes, we can find the points of intersection using the G.D.C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/aa1013b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1013b\">\n\n6. The midpoint of AB is given by\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(\\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2} , \\dfrac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right) &= \\left(\\dfrac{-0.45+4.45}{2} , \\dfrac{-0.45+4.45}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= (2,2)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Point A and point B both lie on the line $y=x$, which has gradient $m=1$. Therefore the perpendicular bisector has gradient $m=-1$.\n\n    The equation of the perpendicular bisector is given by\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y - 2 &= -(x - 2) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{y = -x + 4}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1149","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1149\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the function $\\displaystyle f(x)=b+\\frac{4}{x+a}$, $\\hspace{1em}$ where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThe equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$ is $x=2$ and $f(\\tfrac{2}{3})=0$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $a$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Hence, find the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Hence, write down the equation of the horizontal asymptote.[1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  The vertical asymptote occurs when the denominator of the fraction is equal to zero.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2+a&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{a=-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  From the information given we know that the point $(\\tfrac{2}{3},0)$ lies on the curve.\n\n    Hence, we can create an equation and solve it for $b$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=b+\\frac{4}{\\tfrac{2}{3}-2} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        b&=-\\frac{4}{-\\tfrac{4}{3}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{b=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The horizontal asymptote occurs as $x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty$. We can see that as this happens the fractional part of $f(x)$ will tend to $0$.\n\n    Hence the equation of the horizontal asymptote is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        y&=3+\\frac{4}{\\pm \\infty-2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{y=3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The answer must be given as an equation, either $y=3$ or $f(x)=3$."},{"ref":"AA126","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA126\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nA rational function is defined by $f(x) = a + \\dfrac{b}{x-c}$, for\n$x \\neq c$, where $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThe following diagram represents the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/174d0bc6234e556e44492ded9dca1f7d93a7be93.svg\" alt=\"174d0bc6234e556e44492ded9dca1f7d93a7be93.svg\">\n\nUsing the information on the graph,\n\n1.  state the value of $a$ and the value of\n    $c$ ; [2 marks]\n\n2.  find the value of $b$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a = -4$  and\n     $c = 4$\n\n2.  The point P lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n    Hence, using the coordinates of P, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} 0 &= f(6) \\\\[6pt]\n    0 &= -4 + \\dfrac{b}{6-4} \\\\[2pt]\n    4 &= \\dfrac{b}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    b = 8 \\hspace{1.9em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1263","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1263\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nConsider the functions $f$ and $g$ such that $f(x)=x^2-5x+4$ and $g(x)=\\dfrac{x-2}{f(x)}$.\n\n1. Evaluate $g(3)$.[2 marks]\n\nFor the graph of $g$,\n\n2. Determine the equations of the vertical asymptotes.[2 marks]\n\n3. Find the coordinates of any axes intercepts.[2 marks]\n\n4. Determine the equation of the horizontal asymptote.[1 mark]\n\n5. Hence sketch the graph of $g$, labelling any asymptotes and any axes intercepts.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Substituting $f(x) = x^2-5x+4$ into $g(x)$, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x) &= \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-5x+4}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Now substituting $x=3$,\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(3)&=\\dfrac{3-2}{(3)^2-5(3)+4}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        g(3)&=\\boxed{-\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Factorising the denominator of $g(x)$ from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x) &= \\dfrac{x-2}{(x-1)(x-4)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The vertical asymptotes occur at $x$-values that make the denominator equal to zero.\n\n    \n\n    Setting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (x-1)(x-4) &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By the null factor theorem, this leads to $x=1$ and $x=4$ and therefore the equations of the vertical asymptotes are \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\boxed{x=1} \\hspace{1em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{1em}\\boxed{x=4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. The $y$-intercept occurs when $x=0$. Evaluating $g(x)$ when $x=0$, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(0)&=\\dfrac{0-2}{(0)^2-5(0)-4}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        g(0)&=\\dfrac{-2}{-4}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        g(0)&=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Hence the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $\\left(0,-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The $x$-intercepts occur when the function value is 0. Solving the equation $g(x)=0$ for $x$, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        0&=\\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-5x-4}\\\\\n\n        0&=x-2\\\\\n\n        x&=2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $\\left(2,0\\right)$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.7em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n  \n\n4. To find the equation of the horizontal asymptote, we examine the behaviour of the function as $x$ approaches $\\pm \\infty$. As $x\\rightarrow\\infty$, $g(x)\\rightarrow0^+$. As $x\\rightarrow-\\infty$, $g(x)\\rightarrow0^-$.\n\n  \n\n    Therefore the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=0$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.7em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. The left branch of the equation passing through $\\big(0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\big)$ is bounded by the asymptotes and easy to graph. \n\n  \n\n    The middle and right branches require additional consideration.\n\n    As $x\\rightarrow1^+$, $g(x)\\rightarrow+\\infty$.\n\n  \n\n    As $x\\rightarrow4^-$, $g(x)\\rightarrow-\\infty$.\n\n    When $x=5$, $g(x)=\\frac{3}{4}$. This branch is above the $x$-axis and bounded by the asymptotes.\n\n  \n\n    Now we have enough information to accurately construct the following graph, and we can use our answer to part (a) to help us sketch the correct shape.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/1263-1.png\" alt=\"1263-1\">\n\n    $\\text{Correct asymptotes}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.7em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Correct intercepts}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.7em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Correct shape}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.7em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA127","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA127\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is of the form $f(x) = \\dfrac{ax+b}{2x+c}$, for\n$x \\neq -\\dfrac{c}{2}$, where $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\nGiven\n\nthat the graph of $y = f(x)$ has asymptotes $x = -5$ and $y = 2$, and\nthat the point\n\nP$\\left(1,-\\dfrac{1}{12}\\right)$ lies on the graph, find the values of\n$a, b$ and $c$.","markScheme":"Using the vertical asymptote $x = -5$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{[denominator of $f$ at $-5$]} &= 0 \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n2(-5) + c &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5em} c = 10\n$$\n\nUsing the horizontal asymptote $y = 2$, we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.1em} \\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\pm\\infty} f(x) &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{a}{2} &= 2 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{4.4em} a = 4\n$$\n\nUsing the coordinates of the point P, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= -\\dfrac{1}{12} \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{3.4em} \\dfrac{4+b}{2+10} &= -\\dfrac{1}{12} \\\\[10pt]\n4+b &= -1 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.2em} b = -5\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1283","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1283\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{3x+4}{2x+k}$.\n\n1.  Write down the domain of $f$ in terms of $k$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find $f^{-1}(x)$ in terms of $k$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence write down the value of $k$ such that $f(x)$ is a self-inverse function.[1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  A rational function is undefined when the denominator is equal to zero.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2x+k&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    x&=-\\frac{k}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Any other value for $x$ is valid.\n\n    Hence we get the domain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{x \\in \\mathbb{R}, x\\neq-\\frac{k}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  We are trying to find the function $y=f^{-1}(x)$.\n\n    The definition of an inverse states that\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (f \\circ f^{-1})(x)&=x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we can rewrite this to get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(f^{-1}(x))&=x\\\\[6pt]\n\n        f(y)&=x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(y)&=x\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{3y+4}{2y+k}&=x\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall, we want $y=f^{-1}(x)$ therefore we should rearrange this to make $y$ the subject.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x(2y+k)&=3y+4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2xy+kx&=3y+4\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    To isolate $y$, we move all of the $y$ terms to one side and then factorise out $y$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2xy-3y&=-kx+4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y(2x-3)&=-kx+4\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    y&=\\frac{-kx+4}{2x-3}\\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3.2em}f^{-1}(x)=\\dfrac{-kx+4}{2x-3}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3.  A self-inverse function is such that $f(x)\\equiv f^{-1}(x)$.\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{3x+4}{2x+k}&\\equiv \\frac{-kx+4}{2x-3}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Comparing either the numerators or denominators,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3x+4&=-kx+4\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{k=-3}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1316","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1316\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=\\dfrac{a-3x}{x-2}$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x\\neq 2$ and $a \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.    For the graph of $y=f(x)$, write down the equation of\n\n    1.    the vertical asymptote; \n\n    2.    the horizontal asymptote. [2 marks]\n\nIt is known that $f(1)=f^{-1}(1)$.\n\n2.    Find the value of $a$. [3 marks]\n\n3.    Hence, sketch the graph of $f$ on the axes below. On your sketch, indicate the values of any axis intercepts and label any asymptotes with their equations.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/AA1316q1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1316q1\">\n\n     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  $f$ is a rational function. This means it can be written as a fraction with a function of $x$ in the numerator and the denominator.\n\n    1. The vertical asymptote occurs when $f$ is undefined.\n\n        $f$ is undefined for any $x$-value that causes the denominator to equal zero.\n\n        Hence, we can form and solve an equation to find the value of $x$ that makes $f$ undefined.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x-2&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n        x&=2\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The answer must be written as an equation. Hence  $x=2$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. The horizontal asymptote of $f$ can be found by considering extremely large positive and negative values.\n\n        Let's consider the numerator $a-3x$ to begin with.\n\n        If $x$ becomes extremely large in the positive or negative direction, then the value of $a-3x$ becomes closer to $-3x$. The value of $a$ is less and less significant to the value of $a-3x$ as the size of $x$ grows.\n\n        \n\n        Similarly, considering the denominator, the value of $x-2$ approaches $x$ as the size of $x$ grows.\n\n        Hence, if we put that together, for large $x$ values we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        f(x)&=\\frac{-3\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=-3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Notice that, as the $x$'s cancel out, we don't need to worry about $x$ being positively or negatively large.\n\n        Therefore the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. We know that a function and its inverse will be equivalent on the line $y=x$. \n\n    It is known that the inverse functions are equivalent when $x=1$. Hence we also know that $f(x)=1$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can substitute $1$ in place of $x$ and $f(x)$ to solve for $a$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{a-3(1)}{(1)-2}&=1\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    -a+3&=1\\\\[6pt]\n\n    a&=2\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. First, we can find the axis intercepts.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y&\\text{-intercept}&&&x&\\text{-intercept}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    f(0)&=\\frac{2-3(0)}{(0)-2}&&&0&=\\frac{2-3x}{x-2}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=-1&&&&=\\frac{2}{3}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the answers from part (a) we can draw the asymptotes and also plot the intercepts.\n\n    We can then complete the sketch.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/AA1316a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1316a1\">\n\n    $\\text{Correct intercepts and asymptotes}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Correct function shape}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note, the function must not touch the asymptotes."},{"ref":"AA156","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA156\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe function $f$ is given by $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 128}{x^2 - 16}$, for\n$x \\neq \\pm 4$.\n\n1.  Prove that $f$ is an even function. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Write down the range of $f$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  For any $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\neq \\pm 4$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(-x) &= \\dfrac{4(-x)^2 - 128}{(-x)^2 - 16} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4x^2 - 128}{x^2 - 16} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $f$ is an even function.\n\n2.\n    1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we\n        have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/6c198522156a50253fdb14e877863a1523046369.svg\" alt=\"6c198522156a50253fdb14e877863a1523046369.svg\">\n\n    2.\n        $(-\\infty,4) \\cup [8,\\infty)$"},{"ref":"AA185","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA185\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x-6}{x+1}$, for $x\\neq-1$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the coordinates of:\n    1.  the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$, find the equation of:\n    1.  the horizontal asymptote;\n\n    2.  the vertical asymptote. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Hence sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{3x-6}{x+1} &= 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        3x-6 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n        x &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the $x$-intercept\n        are  $(2,0)$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.8em} y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3(0)-6}{0+1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -6\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of the $y$-intercept\n        are  $(0,-6)$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty} f(x) &= \\dfrac{3}{1} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the equation of the horizontal\n        asymptote is\n         $y = 3$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &= 0 \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n        x+1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the equation of the vertical\n        asymptote is\n         $x = -1$\n\n3.  Hence, if we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/5aa8339f9ee09bb9f920e735960c8b3bddfc8e2c.svg\" alt=\"5aa8339f9ee09bb9f920e735960c8b3bddfc8e2c.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA189","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA189\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x-a} + b$, for $x \\neq a$.\n\nThe line $x = 1$ is a vertical asymptote of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n1.  Write down the value of $a$. [1 mark]\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ passes through the point P$(0,3)$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a=1$\n\n2.  Using the coordinates of P, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= 3 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{2}{0-1} + b &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    -2+b &= 3 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    b = 5 \\hspace{0.3em}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty}\\left[\\dfrac{2}{x-1} + 5\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0 + 5 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA192","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA192\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{5}{x+1}$, for $x \\neq -1$.\n\n1.  For the graph of $f$, find the coordinates of:\n    1.  the $x$-intercept;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept;\n\n    3.  the equation of the vertical asymptote. [5 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = x-3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The graphs of $f$ and $g$\nintersect at points A and B.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of A and B. [5 marks]\n\nLine $L$ is the perpendicular bisector of A and B.\n\n3.  Find the equation of $L$, giving your answer in the form $y = mx + c$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the $x$-intercept of $L$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Since the equation $f(x) = 0$ has no solutions, there are\n         $\\text{no \\(x\\)-intercepts}$\n        $\\\\[-20pt]$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $f(x) = \\dfrac{5}{x+1}$ for $x = 0$, we have\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         f(0) &= \\dfrac{5}{0+1} \\hspace{3.6em} \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n         &= 5\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n         Hence the $y$-intercept is\n         $\\mathrm{P}(0,5)$\n        $\\\\[-20pt]$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x+1 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        x &= -1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the equation of the vertical\n        asymptote is\n         $x = -1$\n        $\\\\[-20pt]$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = g(x)$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{5}{x+1} &= x-3 \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}} \\\\[12pt]\n        5 &= (x+1)(x-3) \\\\[9pt]\n        5 &= x^2 - 2x - 3 \\\\[9pt]\n        0 &= x^2 - 2x - 8 \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[10pt]\n        0 &= (x+2)(x-4) \\\\[10pt]\n        x &= -2,4 \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $g(x) = x-3$\n    for $x = -2$ and $x = 4$, we find\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.1em} \\begin{array}{rcccl}\n    y &=& g(-2) &=& -5 \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\\\\[9pt]\n    y &=& g(4) &=& 1.\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $\\mathrm{A}(-2,-5)$\n    and\n     $\\mathrm{B}(4,1)$\n\n3.  As A and B lie on $g$ then the gradient of line AB is the gradient of $g$, therefore $m_{AB}=1$.\n\n    Hence, the gradient of $L$, the perpendicular bisector, is $m_L=-1$.\n\n    The midpoint of A$(-2,-5)$ and B$(4,1)$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    M_{AB}&= \\left(\\frac{-2+4}{2},\\frac{-5+1}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= (1,-2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the point-gradient form of the equation of a line (__2.1__)with  $M_{AB}(1,-2)$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y-(-2) &= -1(x-1)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    y+2 &= -x+1\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}y=-x-1\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.\n    At the $x$-intercept, $y=0$.\n\n    Substituting this into the equation of $L$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0 &= -x-1\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -1.\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA927","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA927\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nConsider the function defined by $f(x) = \\dfrac{7}{x^2+6x-7}$ for $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x\\neq -7$, $x\\neq 1$.\n\n1. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$, showing the values of any axes intercepts, the coordinates of any local maxima and minima, and the graphs of any asymptotes. [6 marks]\n\nNext, consider the function $g$ defined by $g(x)=\\dfrac{7}{x^2+6x-7}$ for $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x> 1$.\n\n2. Show that $g^{-1}(x)=-3 + \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x+7}{x}}$. [6 marks]\n\n3. State the domain of $g^{-1}$. [1 mark]\n\nNow, consider the function $h$ defined by $h(x)=\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{7}\\right)$.\n\n4. Given that $\\left(h\\circ g\\right)(a) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$, find the value of $a$. Give your answer in the form $p+q\\sqrt{2}$ where $p$, $q\\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. First we note that the function is undefined for $x=-7$ and $x=1$. Therefore there are two vertical asymptotes, $x=-7$ and $x=1$.\n\n    To determine the horizontal asymptote, we examine the behaviour of the function as $x \\to \\pm \\infty$.\n\n    $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty}}f(x)=0$\n\n    Therefore the horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.\n\n    By calculating $f(0)$, we find that the $y$-intercept is $(0,-1)$.\n\n    Next, we find the $x$-intercepts by solving the equation $f(x)=0$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{7}{x^2+6x-7} &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    7 &\\neq 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    There are no $x$-intercepts.\n\n    Now we need to determine any maxima or minima. To do this, we note that the reciprocal function is \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{f(x)} &= \\dfrac{x^2+6x-7}{7} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{7}(x+7)(x-1)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The graph of the reciprocal function is a parabola:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/AA927a.PNG\" alt=\"AA927a\">\n\n    The reciprocal function has a minimum at $\\left(-3,-\\dfrac{16}{7}\\right)$ and therefore $f(x)$ has a maximum at $\\left(-3,-\\dfrac{7}{16}\\right)$.\n\n    Finally, we need to determine the behaviour of the function around the vertical asymptotes. Again, we can use the reciprocal function to determine this.\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to -7^-$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^+$. Therefore $f(x) \\to +\\infty$.$\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to -7^+$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^-$. Therefore $f(x) \\to -\\infty$.$\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to 1^-$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^-$. Therefore $f(x) \\to -\\infty$. $\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to 1^+$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^+$. Therefore $f(x) \\to +\\infty$.$\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    Therefore the graph of $y=f(x)$ is as follows:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Rational%20Functions/images/AA927b.PNG\" alt=\"AA927b\">\n\n2. By switching $x$ and $g(x)$, we create the inverse function:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= \\dfrac{7}{y^2+6y-7} \\hspace{3em}[\\text{where }y=g^{-1}(x)]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that the domain of $g$ is $x>1$, the range of $g^{-1}$ is $g^{-1}(x)>1$. Therefore $y>1$. \n\n    Rearranging to make $y$ the subject, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y^2+6y-7 &= \\dfrac{7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y^2 + 6y  &= 7+\\dfrac{7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    (y + 3)^2 - 9 &= 7+\\dfrac{7}{x} \\hspace{3em}[\\text{By completing the square}]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    (y + 3)^2 &= 16 + \\dfrac{7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{16x + 7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y + 3 &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x + 7}{x}} \\hspace{3em}[\\text{Since $y>1$}]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    y &= -3 + \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x + 7}{x}} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3em}\\boxed{g^{-1}(x) = -3 + \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x+7}{x}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. We could note that $g(x)=f(x)$ for $x>1$, and therefore using our graph from part (a), the range is $g(x)>0$. Therefore the domain of $g^{-1}(x)$ is $x>0$\n\n4. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    g(a)&=\\dfrac{7}{a^2+6a-7}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    And\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(h\\circ g\\right)(a) &= \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{g(a)}{7}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7}\\right)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7}\\right) &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\cos\\left(\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7}\\right)\\right) &= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7} &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{3em}\\bigg[\\text{since }\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    a^2+6a-7 &= 2\\\\[8pt]\n\n    a^2+6a &= 9 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    (a+3)^2-9 &= 9 \\hspace{3em}[\\text{By completing the square}]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    (a+3)^2 &= 18 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a+3 &= \\pm\\sqrt{18} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a+3 &= \\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.55em}\\boxed{ a = -3 + 3\\sqrt{2}} \\hspace{3em} [\\hspace{0.05em}\\text{since }a>1\\hspace{0.05em}]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Transformations","questions":[{"ref":"AA1050","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1050\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nPart of the graph of a function, $f$, is shown in the following diagram. The graph of $y=f(x)$ has two horizontal asymptotes, $y=4$ and $y=-2$.\n\nThe $x-$intercept of $f(x)$ is $a$ and the $y-$intercept is $-b$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/question.png\" alt=\"question\">\n\nConsider the function $g(x)=|f(|x|)|$.\n\n1. Sketch the graph of $y=g(x)$ on top of the image above, labelling any new axis intercepts, in terms of $a$ and $b$, and giving the equation of any new asymptotes. [3 marks]\n\n2. Find the possible values of $r$ such that $(g(x))^2=r$ has exactly two solutions.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The inner absolute value, $f(|x|)$, has the effect of reflecting the part of $f(x)$ to the right of the $y-$axis in the $y$-axis.\n\n    The outer absolute value, $|f(x)|$, reflects the part of the graph below the $x$-axis in the $x$-axis and combines it with the existing positive part of the function. Therefore, we have:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/ans1v2.png\" alt=\"ans1v2\">\n\n2. A sketch of $(g(x))^2$ is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/ans2.png\" alt=\"ans2\">\n\n    The $y-$intercept is now at $b^2$ and the new asymptote is at $y=4^2$. $(g(x))^2$ has two solutions between the two dashed lines, hence $b^2<r<16$.\n\n    Additionally, there are two solutions when $r=0$. Hence there are two solutions to the equation when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &b^2<r<16 \\text{ and }r=0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1272","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1272\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider $f(x)=2\\cos\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3$ and $g(x)=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2$.\n\nThe function $f$ is mapped onto $g$ \n\n by three transformations.\n\n1.  Fully describe each of the transformations and the order in which they must be applied. [3 marks]\n\nA new function is such that $h(x)=g(x)+k$ where $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2.  Find the minimum value of $k$ such that $h(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  By considering the differences between the functions we can work out the transformations needed to map $f(x)$ to $g(x)$.\n\n    First we notice that the inner functions are different.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x)=2\\cos\\left(x\\textcolor{blue}{\\,-\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\right)+3\\\\[6pt]\n\n    g(x)=4\\cos\\left(x\\textcolor{blue}{\\,+\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\right)+2\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    If we add $\\pi$ units to the inner function of $f(x)$, it will equal the inner function of  $g(x)$.   \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x+\\pi)&=2\\cos\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\pi\\right)+3\\\\[6pt]\n\n    f(x+\\pi)&=2\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Comparing the function $f(x+\\pi)$ to $g(x)$, we notice that the coefficient of cosine has been doubled.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x+\\pi)=\\textcolor{green}{2}\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3\\\\[6pt]\n\n    g(x)=\\textcolor{green}{4}\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Hence, if we multiply $f(x+\\pi)$ by $2$ we would have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)=2(2\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+3)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+6\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    If we continue comparing the function $2f(x+\\pi)$ to $g(x)$, we see there is a difference of $4$ units. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\textcolor{red}{\\,+\\,6}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    g(x)&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\textcolor{red}{\\,+\\,2}\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    Hence, if we apply a vertical shift of $-4$ to $2f(x+\\pi)$, we will obtain $g(x)$.    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2f(x+\\pi)-4&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+6-4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=g(x)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$ \n\n    Summarising the $3$ transformations, there is\n\n    a horizontal shift $\\textbf{left}$ of $\\pi$ units, followed by a vertical stretch by a scale factor of $2$ , followed by a vertical shift of $-4$ units.\n\n    Note: The horizontal shift could also come after the vertical transformations, however the two vertical transformations must be applied in the order given. \n\n2.  In order to apply a vertical translation such that $g(x)>0$ for all $x$, we need to know the minimum value of $g(x)$.\n\n    The minimum value of cosine is $-1$. Hence we can determine the minimum of $g(x)$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        g(x)&=4\\cos\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=4(-1)+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=-2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence, we need to translate the graph vertically upwards at least $2$ units. \n\n    \n\n    Therefore, the minimum value is $k=2$.    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1279","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1279\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ for $0\\leq x \\leq 6$ is shown below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-q1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-q1\">\n\nThe odd function $h(x)$ has the domain $-6\\leq x \\leq 6$ and $h(x)=2f(x)$ for $0\\leq x \\leq 6$.\n\n1.  Sketch $h(x)$ on the axes above.[2 marks]\n\n$f(x)$ is shown again below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-q2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-q2\">\n\n2.  Sketch the graph of $y=[f(x)]^2$ on the axes above.[3 marks]\n\n$f(x)$ is shown one more time below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-q3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-q3\">\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{f(x)}$ on the axes above.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let's start with graphing $y=2f(x)$.\n\n    This is a vertical stretch by a scale factor of 2.\n\n    The distance of all $y-$coordinates from the $x-$axis are multiplied by two.\n\n    The key point $(2, 2)$ will become $(2, 4)$.  This is shown in the graph below in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a1\">\n\n    The definition of an odd function is $f(-x)=-f(x)$. This transformation represents rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin which is equivalent to performing two reflections, one in each axis, in any order.\n\n    Therefore, to complete the rest of the graph of $h(x)$, we can either take the graph of $y=2f(x)$ and rotate it $180^\\circ$ about the origin or perform two reflections, one in each axis. Both result in the same final position.\n\n    If we consider the reflection approach. First, we perform $f(x) \\rightarrow f(-x)$, this is a reflection in the $y-$axis. This is the dotted blue line shown in the following graph.\n\n    Then we consider $f(-x)\\rightarrow-f(-x)$, this is a reflection in the $x-$axis.\n\n    This fully transformed part is shown in solid $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$ in the following graph.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a2\">\n\n    The final graph of $y=h(x)$ is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a3\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Correct points at }(-2, -4)\\text{ and }(-6,0)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n         \\text{Correct transformation(s)}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  First, let's look at the section of the graph $0\\leq x \\leq 2$.\n\n    We can use the properties of the transformation $[f(x)]^2$ to identify some key points.\n\n    We know that if $f(x)=0$ then $[f(x)]^2=0$.\n\n    We also know that if $f(x)=1$ then $[f(x)]^2=1$.\n\n    It is important to also realise that if $-1<f(x)<1$ then $[f(x)]^2<f(x)$.\n\n    Therefore between $0<x<1$ then $[f(x)]^2<f(x)$.\n\n    Using the end point of the first part of the curve we can see that $[f(2)]^2=4$.\n\n    Using the key points $(0,0)$, $(1,1)$ and $(2,4)$ we can sketch the transformed curve, shown in $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a4\">\n\n    Now, let's look at the section of the graph $2\\leq x \\leq 6$.\n\n    We know the second section will start at $(2,4)$ and finish at $(6,0)$.\n\n    Using the property if $f(x)=1$ then $[f(x)]^2=1$ we know the curve will intersect the original function at $f(x)=1$.\n\n    Which we can see is at $(4,1)$.\n\n    It is important to clearly show on the graph that $[f(x)]^2<f(x)$ when $0<f(x)<1$.\n\n    With this in mind, using the key points $(2,4)$, $(1,4)$ and $(6,0)$ we can sketch the second part, shown in $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a5.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a5\">\n\n    The final graph of $y=[f(x)]^2$ is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a6.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a6\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\text{Correct shape}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\text{$f(x)=[f(x)]^2$ when $f(x)=0,1$}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\text{$[f(x)]^2$} \\text{ is less than } f(x) \\text{ when } 0<f(x)<1\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let's start by drawing the vertical asymptotes.\n\n    They will occur at the $x$-intercepts of $f(x)$ which are $x=0$ and $x=6$. Shown below as the dotted red vertical lines\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a7.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a7\">\n\n    Let's consider the key point $(2,2)$, after the transformation this point will become $(2,\\tfrac{1}{2})$.\n\n    Considering the asymptotes, we can see that as $x\\rightarrow 0$ and $x\\rightarrow 6$ then $f(x)\\rightarrow 0$ (from the positive direction) therefore $\\frac{1}{f(x)}\\rightarrow \\infty^+$.\n\n    Hence we get the graph below in $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a8.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a8\">\n\n    The final graph of $y=\\frac{1}{f(x)}$ is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/AA1279-a9.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1279-a9\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\text{Correct asymptotes}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\text{Correct shape}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\text{Correct minimum point at } (2,\\tfrac{1}{2})\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA133","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA133\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe function $f$ is defined by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n4x-1 &\\text{if}&  x \\geq 1, \\\\[6pt]\n-x^2+x+3  &\\text{if}&  x<1.\n\\end{array}\\right.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Determine whether or not $f$ is continuous at $x = 1$. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of the function $g$ is obtained by applying the following\ntransformations\\\nto the graph of $f$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\hspace{-5em}\\text{a horizontal translation $2$ units to the left, followed by}\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-5em}\\text{a reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by}\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-5em}\\text{a vertical stretch by a factor of $3$.}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n2.  Find $g(x)$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rcl}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} f(1) &=& 4(1)-1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &=& 3\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{array}{rcl}\n    \\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 1^-} f(x) &=& -1^2 + 1 + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &=& 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $\\text{\\(f\\) is continuous at \\(x = 1\\)}$\n\n2.  If we translate the graph of $y = f(x)$ horizontally $2$ units to\n    the left, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} f(x+2) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    4x+7      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    -(x+2)^2+x+5  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we reflect the graph of\n    $y = f(x+2)$ in the $x$-axis, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -f(x+2) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    -4x-7      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    (x+2)^2-x-5  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we stretch the graph of\n    $y = -f(x+2)$ vertically by a factor of $3$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} -3f(x+2) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    -12x-21      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    3(x+2)^2-3x-15  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the function $g$ is defined\n    by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.3em} \\answer{g(x) = \\left\\{\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{array}{rcl}\n    -12x-21      &\\text{if}&  x \\geq -1,  \\\\[5pt]\n    3(x+2)^2-3x-15  &\\text{if}&  x<-1.\n    \\end{array}\\right.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA173","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA173\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = x^3$ and $g(x) = 5(x-2)^3$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe graph of $g$ can be obtained from the graph of $f$ using two transformations.\n\n1.  Give a full geometric description of each of the transformations. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph of $g$ is translated by 4 units in the direction of the positive $x$-axis, and 1 unit in the direction of the negative $y$-axis to give the graph of a\nfunction $h$.\n\nThe point P$(1,1)$ on the graph of $f$ is translated to point Q on the\ngraph of $h$.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of Q. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    Transformation $1$: $$\\text{vertical stretch by a scale factor of $5$}$$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Transformation $2$: $$\\text{translation of 2 units in the direction of the positive $x$-axis.}$$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. If we apply the transformations $1$ and $2$ from part (a), and transformation $3$ from part (b) to P, we find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/577066d1f46b6880207621796d732b7e8ac43d46.svg\" alt=\"577066d1f46b6880207621796d732b7e8ac43d46.svg\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(7,4)$\n     $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA180","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA180\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$, for\n$-1 \\leq x \\leq 2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/ccc9d5942ccfefc2e6313a34922fed1ada13594f.svg\" alt=\"ccc9d5942ccfefc2e6313a34922fed1ada13594f.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of:\n    1.  $f(1)$;\n\n    2.  $f^{-1}(-2)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $(f\\circ f)(1)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(-x)$ on the same grid above. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $f(1) = 2$\n\n    2.\n        $f^{-1}(-2) = -1$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (f\\circ f)(1) &= f(f(1)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= f(2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The graph of $y = f(-x)$ is a reflection of the graph of $y = f(x)$\n    in the $y$-axis.\n\n    Hence, if we sketch the graph of $y = f(-x)$ on the same grid below,\n    we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/d2b3518aa3484d1ffd19b0a1dd3f20d6f92c6046.svg\" alt=\"d2b3518aa3484d1ffd19b0a1dd3f20d6f92c6046.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA181","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA181\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that $g(x) = 3f(x-2)+1$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe graph of $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ after the following\ntransformations:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{a vertical stretch by a factor of $k$, followed by} \\\\\n&\\text{a horizontal translation of $a$ units, followed by} \\\\\n&\\text{a vertical translation of $b$ units}. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Write down the value of:\n    1.  $k$;\n\n    2.  $a$;\n    3.  $b$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $h(x) = -2g(x)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe point P$(3,4)$ on the graph of $g$ is mapped to point Q on the graph\nof $h$.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of Q. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $k = 3$\n\n    2.\n        $a = 2$\n\n    3.\n        $b = 1$\n\n2.  The graph of $h$ is obtained from the graph of $g$ using two\n    transformations.\n\n    Their geometric descriptions are:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical stretch by a factor of $2$;} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{reflection in the $x$-axis.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we apply the transformations $1$ and $2$ to P, we find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/1510a052325892668888784527ddbb81323a45d9.svg\" alt=\"1510a052325892668888784527ddbb81323a45d9.svg\">\n\n    Hence the coordinates of Q are\n     $(3,-8)$"},{"ref":"AA205","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA205\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$. The graph has a\nhorizontal asymptote at $y = -2$. The graph crosses the $x$-axis at\n$x = -2$ and $x = 2$, and the $y$-axis at $y = 2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/c78f9f086a8acc010784a1b850302f69d14d6493.svg\" alt=\"c78f9f086a8acc010784a1b850302f69d14d6493.svg\">\n\nOn the following set of axes, sketch the graph of $y = [f(x)]^2-1$,\nclearly showing any asymptotes with their equations and the coordinates\nof any local maxima or minima.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/188aa0d6105478da37f4d68a7d60a3e5ae3b83f9.svg\" alt=\"188aa0d6105478da37f4d68a7d60a3e5ae3b83f9.svg\">","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/b5c75dc830aab19d64d4bd831fabe672cddf4748.svg\" alt=\"b5c75dc830aab19d64d4bd831fabe672cddf4748.svg\">\n\nHence, if we sketch the graph of $y = [f(x)]^2-1$, clearly showing the\nhorizontal asymptote and the coordinates of local min/max, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/277cab337794fd9e0454e3cf353e39380f546c58.svg\" alt=\"277cab337794fd9e0454e3cf353e39380f546c58.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA206","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA206\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$. The graph has a\nhorizontal asymptote at $y = -2$. The graph crosses the $x$-axis at\n$x = -1$ and $x = 1$, and the $y$-axis at $y = 2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/78cc89164062caf98085797a78fe4274df588839.svg\" alt=\"78cc89164062caf98085797a78fe4274df588839.svg\">\n\nOn the following set of axes, sketch the graph of $y = [f(x)]^2-2$,\nclearly showing any asymptotes with their equations and the coordinates\nof any local maxima or minima.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/43ee184d47f5a2762efb07289d20445bdfc20e52.svg\" alt=\"43ee184d47f5a2762efb07289d20445bdfc20e52.svg\">","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/11e2fd65ba5146c5df4d12c427fa9a95cb663451.svg\" alt=\"11e2fd65ba5146c5df4d12c427fa9a95cb663451.svg\">\n\nHence, if we sketch the graph of $y = [f(x)]^2-2$, clearly showing the\nhorizontal asymptote and the coordinates of local min/max, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/215026c8b5e8deb9359d6dcee93a236d90ce4a55.svg\" alt=\"215026c8b5e8deb9359d6dcee93a236d90ce4a55.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA770","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA770\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nConsider the function $g(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $a \\neq 0$ and\n$a$, $b$, $c$, $d \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down an expression for $g'(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, given that $g^{-1}$ does not exist, show that\n        $b^2-3ac>0.$[3 marks]\n\nConsider the function\n$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2+6x+\\dfrac{9}{2}$\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that $f^{-1}$ exists.\n\n    2.  $f(x)$ can be written in the form $p\\hspace{0.05em}(x+2)^3+q$,\n        where $p$, $q\\in \\mathbb{R}$. Find the value of $p$ and the\n        value of $q$.\n\n    3.  Hence, find $f^{-1}(x)$.[7 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f(x)$ may be obtained by transforming the graph of $y=x^3$\nusing a sequence of three transformations.\n\n3.  State each of the transformations in the order in which\n    they are applied.[3 marks]\n\n4.  Sketch the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=f^{-1}(x)$ on the same\n    set of axes, indicating the points where each graph crosses the\n    coordinate axes.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Taking the derivative with respect to $x$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        g'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\hspace{0.1em} \\big[ ax^3+bx^2+cx+d \\big] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 3ax^2+2bx+c\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Since $a \\neq 0$, $g(x)$ is a cubic polynomial. For $g^{-1}$ not to exist, $g(x)$ cannot be one-to-one. Thus, $g'(x)$ must have **two distinct real roots** (so that the graph has two stationary points, a local maximum and a local minimum) which requires the discriminant of $g'(x)$ to be strictly positive $\\left(\\triangle > 0 \\right)$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\big[\\text{discriminant of $g'$}\\big] &> 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        (2b)^2 - 4(3a)(c) &> 0 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac] \\\\[12pt]\n        4b^2 - 12ac &> 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-1.25cm}b^2-3ac > 0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using part (a) (ii), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (3)^2-3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(6\\right) & \\ngtr 0.\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore, by the contrapositive statement to part (a) (ii), we\n        deduce $f^{-1}$ exists.\n\n    2.  Factorizing the expression for $y=f(x)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(x^3+6x^2+12x+9\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(x^3+6x^2+12x+8+1\\right)  \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(x+2\\right)^3 + \\dfrac{1}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore\n        $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\, q=\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n    3.  If we solve the equation $(f\\circ f^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n        $y = f^{-1}(x)$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(f^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[12pt]\n        f(y) &= x \\\\[10pt]\n        \\dfrac{1}{2}(y+2)^3+\\dfrac{1}{2} &= x \\\\[10pt]\n        (y+2)^3 &= 2x - 1 \\\\[12pt]\n        y+2 &= \\sqrt[3]{2x-1} \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\hspace{-10.5mm}f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt[3]{2x-1} - 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We get the following transformations\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}&: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{horizontal translation $2$ units left} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}&:\n    \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical stretch by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$}\\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{transformation $3$}&: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical translation $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ units up}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    *Note:* The horizontal translation can be performed in any step but\n    the\\\n    vertical stretch should be performed before the vertical\n    translation.\n\n4.  Using the shape of a cubic function, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Transformations/images/79b23cf27cdbbbaba59cb5e2eee4c41cd47b395a.svg\" alt=\"79b23cf27cdbbbaba59cb5e2eee4c41cd47b395a.svg\">"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA028","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA028\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe cubic polynomial equation $x^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0$ has three roots\n$x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$. By expanding the product $(x-x_1)(x-x_2)(x-x_3)$,\nshow that\n\n1.\n    1.  $b = -(x_1+x_2+x_3)$;\n\n    2.  $c = x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3$;\n    3.  $d = -x_1x_2x_3$. [3 marks]\n\nIt is given that $b = -9$ and $c = 45$ for parts (b) and (c) below.\n\n2.\n    1.  In the case that the three roots $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$ form an\n        arithmetic\\\n        sequence, show that one of the roots is $3$.\n\n    2.  Hence determine the value of $d$. [5 marks]\n\n3.  In another case the three roots form a geometric sequence.\n    Determine\\\n    the value of $d$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Given the three roots $x_1$, $x_2$ and $x_3$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^3 + bx^2 + cx + d &= (x-x_1)(x-x_2)(x-x_3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x^2 -(x_1+x_2)x + x_1x_2)(x-x_3) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= x^3 - (x_1+x_2+x_3)x^2 + (x_1x_2+x_1x_3+x_2x_3)x - x_1x_2x_3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, comparing the coefficients, we get\n\n    1.\n        $b = -(x_1+x_2+x_3)$\n\n    2.\n        $c = x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3$\n\n    3.\n        $d = -x_1x_2x_3$\n\n2.\n    1.  Letting $\\alpha = x_1 + \\delta$, where $\\delta$ is the common\n        difference of the arithmetic sequence $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_3$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.8em} x_1  &=  \\alpha - \\delta \\\\[6pt]\n        x_2  &=  \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n        x_3  &=  \\alpha + \\delta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using part (a)\n        (i), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -b \\\\[6pt]\n        (\\alpha - \\delta) + \\alpha + (\\alpha + \\delta) &= -(-9) \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n        3\\alpha &= 9 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\alpha &= 3 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        x_2 = 3\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using part (a) (ii), we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3 &= c \\\\[6pt]\n        (3 - \\delta)(3) + (3 - \\delta)(3 + \\delta) + (3)(3 + \\delta) &= 45 \\\\[6pt]\n        27 - \\delta^2 &= 45 \\\\[6pt]\n        -\\delta^2 &= 18 \\\\[5pt]\n        \\delta &= \\sqrt{18}\\hspace{0.05em}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\hspace{2em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, using part (a) (iii), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4em} d &= -x_1x_2x_3 \\\\[5pt]\n        &= -(3-\\sqrt{18}\\hspace{0.05em}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(3)(3+\\sqrt{18}\\hspace{0.05em}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -81\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Letting $\\alpha = x_1$ and $r$ denote the common ratio of the\n    geometric sequence $x_1, x_2, x_3$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} x_1  &=  \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n    x_2  &=  \\alpha r \\\\[6pt]\n    x_3  &=  \\alpha r^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.25em} x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -b \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a) (i)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha + \\alpha r + \\alpha r^2 &= -(-9) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha (1+r+r^2) &= 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3 &= c \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a) (ii)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha (\\alpha r) + \\alpha (\\alpha r^2) + (\\alpha r)(\\alpha r^2) &= 45 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha^2r(1+r+r^2) &= 45\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Taking the ratio\n    of the expressions $(1)$ and $(2)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\alpha^2r(\\cancel{1+r+r^2})}{\\alpha(\\cancel{1+r+r^2})} &= \\dfrac{45}{9} \\hspace{4em} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\alpha^2r}{\\alpha} &= \\dfrac{45}{9} \\\\[15pt]\n    \\alpha r &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the expression $(3)$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.5em} d &= -x_1x_2x_3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (a) (iii)}] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -\\alpha (\\alpha r)(\\alpha r^2) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -(\\alpha r)^3 \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -5^3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -125\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1037","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1037\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe arithmetic sequence has the sixth term of $u_6=\\ln{2}$ and the eighth term of $u_8=\\frac{3}{2}\\ln{4}$.\n\n1.  1.  Show the common difference is $\\ln2$.\n\n    2.  Find $u_1$.\n\n    3.  Hence, show that the sum of $n$ terms is given by $S_n=\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}} $. Where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$. [5 marks]\n\nA new function, $f(x)$, is formed using $S_n$ with the discrete variable $n$ being replaced with the continuous variable $x$, such that $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Hence $f(x)=\\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n2.  1.  Write down the equation of the axis of symmetry of $f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Sketch $f(x)$ on the grid below, identify the axis of symmetry and any axis intercepts.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/q1037.png\" alt=\"q1037\">\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the minimum value of $S_n$ in the form $a\\ln b$. Where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ [5 marks]\n\nConsider the linear function $g(x)=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$.\n\n3.  1.  Solve $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, solve the inequality $f(x)<g(x)$.\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the range of values of $n$ that satisfy $S_n<g(x)$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  As the sequence is arithmetic we can write $u_8=u_6+2d$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\tfrac{3}{2}\\ln{4}&=\\ln{2}+2d \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            2d&=3\\ln{(\\sqrt{4})}-\\ln2\\\\\n            2d&=2\\ln{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\ln2}\\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  We can use $u_6$ to find the first term\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            u_6&=u_1+5d\\\\\n            \\ln2&=u_1+5\\ln2\\\\\n            u_1&=\\hspace{0.525em}-4\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\left(2\\times -4 \\ln2 +(n-1)\\times \\ln2\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            &=\\frac{n^2}{2}\\ln2-\\frac{9n}{2}\\ln2\\\\\n            &=\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\left(n^2-9n\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln{\\sqrt{2}} \\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n2.  1.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x&=-\\frac{-9\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}\\\\\n            &\\hspace{-0.525em}x=\\tfrac{9}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  Here is the sketch of $f(x)$ with the desired features identified.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/a1037-1.png\" alt=\"a1037-1\">\n\n        $\\text{Correct intercepts}$ **A1** and $\\text{axis of symmetry}$ **A1**.\n\n    3.  Using the symmetry of $f(x)$ we can identify the minimum value(s) of $f(x)$ which has integer $x$ coordinates.\n\n        Both points are equidistant from the axis of symmetry and they represent the minimum value of $S_n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/a1037-2.png\" alt=\"a1037-2\">\n\n        We can use the $x-$value from the graph to pick either $n=4$ or $n=5$ as both will yield the same minimum value. This solution will pick $n=4$, hence, the minimum value of $S_n$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_4&=\\left(4^2-9(4)\\right)\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\\n            &=-20\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}-10\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  1.  Equate the RHS of each function\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}&=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Cancel out the factor $\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$ and solve for $x$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x^2-9x&=6-3x\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n            0&=x^2-6x-6\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can solve this by completing the square.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            (x-3)^2-9-6&=0\\\\\n            x-3&=\\pm\\sqrt{15}\\\\\n            x&=\\hspace{0.525em}3\\pm\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  By making a rough sketch of $g(x)$ on the graph we can solve the inequality\\\\\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/a1037-3.png\" alt=\"a1037-3\">\n\n        We can identify the region the $f(x)<g(x)$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &\\hspace{0.525em}3-\\sqrt{15}<x<3+\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  $n$ must be a positive integer and lie entirely within the region identified in part (c)(ii).\n\n        We know that $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}$, hence we can identify the minimum and maximum values of $n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        The minimum value must be an integer larger than $3-\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -1<3-\\sqrt{15}<0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The minimum value of $n$ is $1$.\n\n        Similarly, the maximum value must be an integer smaller than $3+\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            6<3+\\sqrt{15}<7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The maximum value of $n$ is $6$.\n\n        Hence\n        $1\\leq n\\leq 6$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$"},{"ref":"AA1047","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1047\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the range of possible values for $p$ such that $$e^{2x}+2\\ln p = 4e^x$$ has at least one real solution.","markScheme":"The equation is a hidden quadratic. Let $k=e^x$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^2 - 4k + 2 \\ln p &= 0  \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the discriminant we can say a quadratic equation has at least one real solution if $ b^2-4ac\\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, we have at least one real solution for $k$ if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-4)^2-4(2\\ln p) &\\geq 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[7pt]\n16-8\\ln p &\\geq 0 \\\\[7pt]\n8\\ln p &\\leq 16  \\\\[7pt]\n\\ln p &\\leq 2 \\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-2.33em}\\boxed{0 < p \\leq e^2} \\hspace{3em} \\text{($\\ln p > 0$)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\\\\[-11pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1063","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1063\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the real polynomial $f(x)=x^5-5.5x^4-...-604.662$.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial form a geometric sequence with first term $u_1$ and common ratio $r$.\n\nWhere $u_1>0$ and $r<-1$.\n\n1. Find two distinct functions for $u_1$ in terms of $r$. [3 marks]\n\n2. Hence find $u_1$ and $r$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The roots of $f(x)$ form the following geometric sequence $u_1, u_1r, u_1r^2, u_1r^3, u_1r^4$.\n\n    Hence we can write the sum of the roots as $u_1\\tfrac{r^5-1}{r-1}$ and the product of the roots as $u_1^5r^{10}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can find the sum and product of the roots of $f$ using the formula, hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        -\\dfrac{(-5.5)}{1}&=u_1\\left(\\frac{r^5-1}{r-1}\\right) &(-1)^5\\dfrac{(-604.662)}{1}=u_1^5r^{10} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_1&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{5.5(r-1)}{r^5-1}} &u_1=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{604.662}{r^{10}}}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Draw the functions on a G.D.C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/graph.png\" alt=\"graph\">\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the conditions given in the question\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\boxed{u_1=1.6 \\text{ and } r=-1.5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1264","marks":"17","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1264\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nConsider the function $f$ such that $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^2-10x+20}{x-3}$.\n\n1. Write down the equation of the vertical asymptote.[1 mark]\n\n2. Find the coordinates of any maxima and minima on the graph of $f$. [6 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ approaches an oblique asymptote as $x\\rightarrow \\pm \\infty$.\n\n3. Find the equation of the oblique asymptote.[4 marks]\n\n4. Sketch the graph of $f$ on the axes provided, clearly indicating any asymptotes, axes intercepts, maxima and minima. [5 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/1264-1.png\" alt=\"1264-1\">\n\n5. State the range of $f$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Since the function is undefined when the denominator is zero, and the numerator is non-zero at this value, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x-3 &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Therefore the equation of the vertical asymptote is\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\boxed{x = 3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using **the quotient rule (5.6)**, with $u=2x^2-10x+20$ and $v=x-3$, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\frac{v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}-u\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}}{v^2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(x) &= \\frac{(x-3)(4x-10)-(2x^2-10x+20)(1)}{(x-3)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\frac{4x^2-22x+30-2x^2+10x-20}{(x-3)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(x) &= \\frac{2x^2-12x+10}{(x-3)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Setting $f'(x)=0$ to find the stationary points, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2x^2-12x+10 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    x^2-6x+5 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n    (x-1)(x-5) &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    So the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points are\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= 1 \\text{ or } x = 5 \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting these values into $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(1) &= \\frac{2(1)^2-10(1)+20}{1-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= -6 \\\\[12pt]\n\n    f(5) &= \\frac{2(5)^2-10(5)+20}{5-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= 10\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We therefore have two stationary points, $(1,-6)$ and $(5,10)$. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By performing a sign-change test, we can determine whether these are maxima or minima. Let's find the value of the gradient at $x=0$, $x=2$, $x=4$ and $x=6$. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f'(0)&= \\dfrac{2(0)^2-12(0)+10}{9} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(0)>0\\right]} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(2) &= \\dfrac{2(2)^2-12(2)+10}{1} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(2)<0\\right]}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    f'(4) &= \\dfrac{2(4)^2-12(4)+10}{1} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(4)<0\\right]}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    f'(6) &= \\dfrac{2(6)^2-12(6)+10}{9} \\hspace{3em}\\bm{\\left[f'(6)>0\\right]} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Before $(1,-6)$, the gradient is **positive** and immediately after, the gradient is **negative**. Before $(5,10)$, the gradient is **negative** and immediately after, the gradient is **positive**. Therefore we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\text{maximum at } (1,\\,-6) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\text{minimum at } (5,\\,10)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Because we are dividing a degree 2 polynomial by a degree 1 polynomial, we know that $f(x)$ can be written in the form $f(x)=ax+b+\\dfrac{c}{x-3}$, where $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{R}$. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(R1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{2x^2-10x+20}{x-3} &= ax+b+\\dfrac{c}{x-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    2x^2 - 10x + 20 &= ax(x-3) + b(x-3) + c \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= ax^2 -3ax + bx -3b + c \\\\[6pt]\n\n    2x^2 - 10x + 20 &= ax^2 - (3a-b)x + (c - 3b)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Equating the coefficients on the LHS with the RHS, we must have $a=2$.\n\n    We must have $3a-b=10$ and therefore $b=-4$.\n\n    We must have $c-3b = 20$ and therefore $c=8$.\n\n    Hence, $f(x)=2x-4+\\dfrac{8}{x-3}$.\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    As $x\\rightarrow\\pm\\infty$, we have $\\dfrac{8}{x-3}\\rightarrow0$, so the graph approaches the line $y=2x-4$.\n\n    Therefore the equation of the oblique asymptote is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{y = 2x-4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. To find the $y$-intercept, we set $x=0$, so we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(0) &= \\frac{2(0)^2-10(0)+20}{0-3} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= -\\frac{20}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    To find any $x$-intercepts, we set $f(x)=0$, which requires the numerator to be zero. Using \\textbf{the formula for the discriminant (2.7)} with $a=2$, $b=-10$ and $c=20$, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    b^2-4ac &= (-10)^2-4(2)(20) \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= -60\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Since the discriminant is negative, the graph has no $x$-intercepts. \n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the asymptotes, the maximum and minimum from the previous parts, and the $y$-intercept, we obtain the following sketch.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/1264-2.png\" alt=\"1264-2\">\n\n    $\\text{Correct asymptotes}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\\text{Correct maximum, minimum and $y$-intercept}$$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Correct curve}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. From the sketch in part (d), the maximum value on the left-hand branch is $-6$ and the minimum value on the right-hand branch is $10$, so the range is\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{f(x)\\leq-6 \\text{ or } f(x)\\geq10} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA159","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA159\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nLet\n$f(x) = x^4 - 0.4\\hspace{0.05em}x^3 - 2.85\\hspace{0.05em}x^2 + 0.9\\hspace{0.05em}x + 1.35$,\nfor $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find the solutions for $f(x) > 0$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$,\n    1.  find the coordinates of local minimum and maximum points.\n\n    2.  find the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflexion. [5 marks]\n\nThe domain of $f$ is now restricted to $[a,b]$ where\n$a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Write down the smallest value of $a > 0$ and the largest value\n        of $b > 0$ for which $f$ has an inverse. Give your answers\n        correct to **three significant figures**.\n\n    2.  For these values of $a$ and $b$, sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$\n        and $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same set of axes, showing clearly the\n        coordinates of the end points of each curve.\n\n    3.  Solve $f^{-1}(x) = 0.5$. [6 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\sin(2x - 1) + \\dfrac{1}{2}$,\n $\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\n4.\n    1.  Find an expression for $g^{-1}$ and state its domain.\n\n    2.  Solve $(f^{-1}\\circ g)(x) < 0.5$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/245a2a1979fce06233cc54e60878b1e96f11b9d1.svg\" alt=\"245a2a1979fce06233cc54e60878b1e96f11b9d1.svg\">\n\n    From the diagram, we see that $f$ is positive on the intervals\n    $(-\\infty,-1.5)$, $(-0.6,1)$ and $(1.5,\\infty)$, and negative on\n    $(-1.5,-0.6)$ and $(1,1.5)$.\n\n    Hence the set of solutions to the inequality $f(x) > 0$ is\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     (-\\infty,-1.5)\\cup(-0.6,1)\\cup(1.5,\\infty)\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.\n    1.  From the diagram, we see that the graph of $y = f(x)$ has a\n        local\\\n        maximum at point A and local minimums at points B and C.\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the local max and min points are\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{local max}:&\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}(0.155, 1.42)  \\\\[10pt]\n        \\text{local min}:&\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}(-1.13, -1.10)\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.525em}(1.28, -0.322)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x) &= x^4 - 0.4x^3 - 2.85x^2 + 0.9x + 1.35 \\\\[6pt]\n        f'(x) &= 4x^3 - 1.2x^2 - 5.7x + 0.9 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{1.5em} f''(x) &= 12x^2 - 2.4x - 5.7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n         Hence, if we solve the equation\n        $f''(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        12x^2 - 2.4x - 5.7 &= 0 \\hspace{9.8em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{12.95em} x \\approx -0.596, \\hspace{0.25em} 0.796 \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  From the diagram, we see that $f$ is a bijection if its domain\n        is restricted to $[-1.13, 0.155]$ or $[0.155,1.28]$.\n\n        As it is given that $a,b > 0$, hence $f$ has an inverse if\n         $a = 0.155$\n        and  $b = 1.28$\n\n    2.  The graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is the reflection of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$ across the line $y = x$.\n\n        Hence, if we sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = f^{-1}(x)$\n        on the same set of axes, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/524a320d1d2563d4a110444319e1f709eb5ee6c6.svg\" alt=\"524a320d1d2563d4a110444319e1f709eb5ee6c6.svg\">\n\n    3.  If we solve the equation $f^{-1}(x) = 0.5$ for $x$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(f^{-1}(x)) &= f(0.5) \\hspace{4em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{10.8em} x = 1.1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(f^{-1}(x)) = x$}]\n        $$\n\n4.\n    1.  Solving the equation $(g\\circ g^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n        $y = g^{-1}(x)$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        g(g^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[14pt]\n        g(y) &= x \\\\[10pt]\n        \\hspace{1.7em} \\dfrac{2}{3}\\sin(2y - 1) + \\dfrac{1}{2} &= x \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{2}{3}\\sin(2y - 1) &= \\dfrac{2x-1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\sin(2y - 1) &= \\dfrac{6x-3}{4} \\\\[4pt]\n        2y - 1 &= \\arcsin\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{6x-3}{4}\\bigg) \\\\[3pt]\n        y &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\arcsin\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{6x-3}{4}\\bigg) + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence an expression for $g^{-1}$ is\n         $g^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\arcsin\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{6x-3}{4}\\bigg) + \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n        The range of $g$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left[-\\dfrac{2}{3}+\\dfrac{1}{2},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{2}{3}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right] = \\left[-\\dfrac{1}{6},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{7}{6}\\right] \\hspace{4.7em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the domain of $g^{-1}$ is\n         $\\bigg[-\\dfrac{1}{6},\\dfrac{7}{6}\\bigg]$\n\n    2.  Solving the inequality $(f^{-1}\\circ g)(x) < 0.5$ for $x$, we\n        get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{9.5em} f^{-1}(g(x)) &< 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n        g(x) &> f(0.5) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f$ is decreasing}] \\\\[6pt]\n        g(x) &> 1.1 \\\\[5pt]\n        x &> g^{-1}(1.1) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $g^{-1}$ is increasing}] \\\\[6pt]\n        x &> 1.06\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the set of solutions to the\n        inequality $(f^{-1}\\circ g)(x) < 0.5$ is\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\left(1.06,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right] \\hspace{2em}\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$"},{"ref":"AA199","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA199\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{3\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)}$, for $x > 0$.\n\nThe $k$th maximum point on the graph of $f$ has $x$-coordinate $x_k$,\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n1.  Given that $x_{k+1} = x_k + d$, find $d$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the value of $n$ such that\n    $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k = 992$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graph of $y= f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\" alt=\"97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the $x$-coordinates of the points A$(2,20.1)$ and\n    B$(10,20.1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} x_2 &= x_1 + d \\\\[6pt]\n    10 &= 2 + d \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 8\n    $$\n\n2.  We have an arithmetic sequence with the first term $x_1 = 2$ and the\n    common difference $d = 8$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2x_1+(n-1)d)$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k &= 992 \\\\[6pt]\n    S_n &= 992 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{with $x_1 = 2$ \\& $d = 8$}] \\\\[13pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(2)+(n-1)(8)) &= 992 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.1em} n = 16 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA234","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA234\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nLet $g(x) = -4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $h(x) = 5\\cos x - 1$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $(h\\circ g)(x) = 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 1$.\n     [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the range of $h\\circ g$.  [2 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $(h\\circ g)\\Big(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}\\Big) = 4$, find the next\n    value of $x$, greater than $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}$,\n\n    for which $(h\\circ g)(x)=4$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  The graph of $y = (h\\hspace{0.075em}\\circ\\hspace{0.075em}g)(x)$ can\n    be obtained by applying five $\\text{transformations}$ to the\n    graph of $y = \\cos x$. State what the five transformations represent\n    $\\text{geometrically}$ and give the order in which they are\n    applied. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (h\\circ g)(x) &= h(g(x)) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= h\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.25em} -1 &\\leq \\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\leq 1 \\\\[3pt]\n    -5 &\\leq 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\leq 5 \\\\[3pt]\n    -6 &\\leq 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-1 \\leq 4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the range of $h\\circ g$ is\n     $[-6,4]$\n\n3.  The period of the function $h\\circ g$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{|-4|} \\hspace{1.05em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the next value of $x$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= \\dfrac{5\\pi}{12} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{compression parallel to the $x$-axis by a factor of $1/4$} \\hspace{1.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{reflection in the $y$-axis} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $3$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{stretch parallel to the $y$-axis by a factor of $5$} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $4$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{horizontal translation $\\pi/12$ units to the left} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $5$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{vertical translation $1$ unit down}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA922","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA922\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the functions $f(x)=3\\cos(x)+\\dfrac{9}{2}$ and $g(x)=3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+A$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $A< \\dfrac{9}{2}$.\n\n1. Describe a sequence of two transformations that transforms the graph of $f$ to the graph of $g$. [3 marks]\n\nThe $y$-intercept of the graph $g$ is at the point $\\left(0\\,,\\dfrac{9}{2}\\right)$\n\n2. Find the range of $g$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Applying a translation of $a$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis to a function $f$ is shown as $f(x+a)$, and a translation of $b$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis is shown as $f(x)-b$. \n\n    Therefore a translation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis would be $f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)+\\dfrac{9}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that $A < \\dfrac{9}{2}$, if we then apply a translation of $\\dfrac{9}{2} - A$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) - \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{2} - A\\bigg) &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)+\\dfrac{9}{2} - \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{2} - A\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) + A \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= g(x)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we have the following transformations in any order\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    T_1 &: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\text{translation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    T_2 &: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\text{translation of $\\dfrac{9}{2} - A$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. We have $g(0)=\\dfrac{9}{2}$. Solving for $A$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3\\cos\\left(0+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+A &= \\dfrac{9}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) + A & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    A & = 3 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $g(x)=3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+3$ , and the following inequalities hold:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    -1\\leq \\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &\\leq 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    -3\\leq 3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &\\leq 3 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    0\\leq 3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+3 &\\leq 6 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    0\\leq g(x) &\\leq 6 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the range of $g$ is $0\\leq g(x) \\leq 6$"},{"ref":"AA979","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA979\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sin(x+c)$, where $0\\leq x \\leq a$ and $a,c \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\n1. By sketching a suitable graph, or otherwise, find the largest value of $a$ for which the inverse function $f^{-1}$ exists in the case where $c=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Find the largest value of $a$ for which the inverse function $f^{-1}$ exists in the case where $c=\\pi$. [1 mark]\n\n3. Find the largest value of $a$ for which the inverse function $f^{-1}$ exists in the case where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < c < \\pi$. Give your answer in terms of $c$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. By sketching the graph of $\\sin x$ under a horizontal shift $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ units to the left, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/aa979a.PNG\" alt=\"aa979a\">\n\n    Note that a function must be one-to-one for its inverse to exist. The diagram shows that $f$ is one-to-one if its domain is restricted to $\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$.\n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{a = \\pi}.$\n\n2. By sketching the graph of $\\sin x$ under a horizontal shift $\\pi$ units to the left, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/aa979b.PNG\" alt=\"aa979b\">\n\n    The diagram shows that $f$ is one-to-one if its domain is restricted to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$. \n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{a = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$\n\n3. This time the graph of $f$ is an intermediate graph between the previous two:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Functions/Unassigned/images/aa979c.PNG\" alt=\"aa979c\">\n\n    Hence, we can see that $f$ is one-to-one over the interval $\\left[0,\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}-c\\right]$. \n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{a=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}-c}$"}]}]},{"name":"Geometry & Trigonometry","subtopics":[{"name":"Circle Geometry","questions":[{"ref":"AA1054","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1054\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA gardener is designing a flower decoration around a fountain shown by the shaded region of the figure below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/AA1054.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1054\">\n\nThe inner circle, of radius $r$ m represents the fountain. The outer circle has a radius of $8$ m and $\\theta =2.4$ radians.\n\n1. Show that the area to be decorated is $(\\pi-1.2)\\left(64-r^2\\right)$.[2 marks]\n\nThe area to be decorated is $32$ metres squared.\n\n2. Find the value of $r$.[2 marks]\n\n3. Find the perimeter of the shaded area.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using **the formula for the area of a sector (3.4)**, $A=\\frac{1}{2}r^2 \\theta$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Area of shaded region} &= \\text{Outer region} - \\text{Inner region} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\left(\\dfrac{\n    2\\pi-2.4}{2}\\right)(8^2)- \\left(\\dfrac{\n    2\\pi-2.4}{2}\\right)(r^2) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\left(\\pi-1.2\\right)\\left(8^2\\right)- \\left(\\pi-1.2\\right)\\left(r^2\\right)\\\\[8pt]\n        &=\n    \\left(\\pi-1.2\\right)\\left(64-r^2\\right) \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. By solving the corresponding equation, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left(\\pi-1.2\\right)\\left(64-r^2\\right) &= 32  \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        r&=6.8933...\\\\[6pt]\n    r&=\\boxed{6.89\\,\\text{m}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Using **the formula for the length of an arc (3.4)**, $l = r \\theta$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Perimeter} &= \\text{Outer arc} + \\text{Inner arc} + 2(8-6.893...)  \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left(2\\pi-2.4\\right)(8) + \\left(2\\pi-2.4\\right)(6.893...) + 2(8-6.893...) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[8pt]\n    &=60.04...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\boxed{60.0\\, \\text{m}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1334","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1334\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nAn architect is designing a concert hall in the shape of a sector with radius, $r\\,\\text{metres}$, as shown in the following diagram. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/AA1334qX.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1334qX\">\n\nThe shaded region, $X$, is where the audience sits.\n\nSector $OAD$, with radius $a$ metres and subtending an angle of $\\theta$ radians, represents the stage, where $0 < \\theta < \\pi$.\n\n1.    Find an expression for the area of $X$ in terms of $r, a$ and $\\theta$. [2 marks]\n\nA fence of length $b\\,$metres will enclose $X$.\n\nThe fence consists of the straight edges $AB$ and $CD$ and the arcs $\\overset{\\large\\frown}{AD}$ and $\\overset{\\large\\frown}{BC}$.\n\nThe architect is considering a number of different designs for the concert hall.\n\nFor the first design,\n\n2.    $a=15\\,$m and $b=200\\,$m.\n\n    1.    Find $r$ in terms of $\\theta$. \n\n    2.    Hence find the maximum area of $X$ for the first design. [4 marks]\n\nFor the second design,\n\n3.    $r=90\\,$m and the area of $X=5000\\,$m$^2$.\n\n    1.    Show that $\\theta=\\dfrac{10\\,000}{8\\,100-a^2}$. \n\n    2.    Hence, find the value of $a$ that minimises $b$ for the second design. [5 marks]\n\nFor the final design it is decided that the area of sector $OAD$ and the area of $X$ must be in the ratio $1:p$ where $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $p>65$.\n\nGiven that $a=12\\,$m, $\\theta=1.3$ radians, and $r \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$,\n\n4.    find the minimum value of $r$ for the final design. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. To find the area of $X$, $A_X$, we need to subtract the area of the smaller sector from the area of the larger sector.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A_X&=\\frac{1}{2}r^2(\\theta)-\\frac{1}{2}a^2\\theta\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &A_X=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\theta\\,(r^2-a^2)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  1. We need an equation which contains only $r$ and $\\theta$ so let's set up a formula for the length of the fence, $b$.  \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        b&=AB+\\overset{\\large\\frown}{BC}+CD+\\overset{\\large\\frown}{AD}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using the arc length formula and known values we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        b&=(r-a)+r\\theta+(r-a)+a\\theta\\\\[6pt]\n\n        200&=2(r-15)+\\theta(r+15)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can rearrange this equation to make $r$ the subject.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        200&=2r-30+r\\theta+15\\theta\\\\[6pt]\n\n        230-15\\theta&=r(2+\\theta)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        r&=\\dfrac{230-15\\theta}{2+\\theta}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. To maximise $A_X$ we need to eliminate one of the variables. \n\n        Substituting the expression for $r$ from part (b)(i) into the formula from part (a) we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A_X&=\\frac{1}{2}\\theta\\left(\\left(\\frac{230-15\\theta}{2+\\theta}\\right)^2-15^2\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can find the maximum point of this function using our G.D.C.\n\n        Note that it is useful to restrict the graph to the given domain of $0\\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/AA1334a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1334a1\">\n\n        Hence we obtain the answers\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\theta&=1.25\\\\[6pt]\n\n        A&=2500\\text{ m}^2\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n3.  1. In the second design we know that $r=90$ and $A_X=5\\,000$.\n\n        We can substitute these values into the same formula from part (a) and make $\\theta$ the subject.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A_X&=\\frac{1}{2}\\theta\\,(r^2-a^2)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        5\\,000&=\\frac{1}{2}\\theta\\,(90^2-a^2)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        10\\,000&=\\theta\\,(8\\,100-a^2)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\theta&=\\dfrac{10\\,000}{8\\,100-a^2}\\hspace{2em}\\text{as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. We want to find the minimum value of $b$ so let's again use the formula from part (b).\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        b&=2(r-a)+\\theta(r+a)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=2(90-a)+\\theta(90+a)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Substituting the expression from part (d) we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        b&=2(90-a)+\\left(\\frac{10\\,000}{8\\,100-a^2}\\right)(90+a)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[16pt]\n\n        &=180-2a+\\frac{900\\,000+10\\,000a}{8\\,100-a^2}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can find the minimum point of this function using our G.D.C.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/AA1334a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1334a2\">\n\n        Hence we obtain the answers\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a&=19.3\\text{ m}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        b&=283\\text{ m}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n4. In the last scenario we are given that the ratio of $A_{OAD}:A_X=1:p$.\n\n    If we divide both ratios by the element on the right we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{A_{OAD}}{A_x}:1&=\\frac{1}{p}:1\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    therefore we can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\frac{A_{OAD}}{A_x}:1&=\\frac{1}{p}:1\\\\[12pt]\n\n    p \\times A_{OAD}&=A_x\\\\[9pt]\n\n    p\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{2}a^2\\theta\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\theta(r^2-a^2)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We are given $a=12$ and $\\theta=1.3$, hence \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    p\\times\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(12)^2(1.3)\\right)&= \\frac{1}{2}(1.3)(r^2-12^2)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    p\\times(93.6)&=0.65(r^2-144)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Next we can write $p$ as the subject and then use the G.D.C. to graph the equation.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    p&=\\frac{0.65(r^2-144)}{93.6}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall that $p>65$ and $r\\in\\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n    \n\n    Hence we will need to use the intersection of the function and $y=65$ to find our answer.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/AA1334a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1334a3\">\n\n    We need to find the first integer value of $r$ which is larger than $97.48...$.\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}r=98\\text{ m}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    If you aren't sure this is the correct value you can always check the value on either side of your answer to see which one results in an answer where  $p>65$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    p(97)&=\\frac{97^2-144}{144}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=64.34...\\\\[10pt]\n\n    p(98)&=\\frac{98^2-144}{144}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=65.69...\\hspace{2em}\\checkmark\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA209","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA209\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe diagram below shows a sector of a circle with radius $r$ where\n$\\text{A\\^OB} = x$ radians\n\nand the length of the arc AB is $\\dfrac{4}{x}$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/396fb6507d7c9bef4b6d659a48ea8869f9e85fd4.svg\" alt=\"396fb6507d7c9bef4b6d659a48ea8869f9e85fd4.svg\">\n\nGiven that the area of the sector is\n$27$ cm$^2$, find the length of the arc AB.","markScheme":"Using the length of an arc formula, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.5em} l_{\\text{arc}} &= (\\text{A\\^OB})(r) \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{4}{x} &= xr \\\\[4pt]\nr &= \\dfrac{4}{x^2} \\text{ cm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the area of a\nsector formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.6em} A &= \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2(\\text{A\\^OB}) \\\\[6pt]\n27 &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{16}{x^4} \\cdot x \\\\[6pt]\n27 &= \\dfrac{8}{x^3} \\\\[6pt]\nx^3 &= \\dfrac{8}{27} \\\\[4pt]\nx &=  \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{8}{27}} \\\\[6pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{2}{3} \\text{ radians}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the length of the\narc AB is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.9em} l &= \\dfrac{4}{x}  \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{4}{(2/3)} \\\\[8pt]\n&= 6 \\text{ cm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA210","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA210\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe app logo, for a mobile arcade game, is a sector of a circle of\nradius $3$ cm, shown as shaded in the diagram\nbelow. The area of the logo is $6 \\pi$ cm$^2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/78e72d0c99835aa59c5c4bbe3bca760a063b6070.svg\" alt=\"78e72d0c99835aa59c5c4bbe3bca760a063b6070.svg\">\n\n1.  Find, in radians, the measure of the angle AOB. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total length of the perimeter of the logo. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a circle and the area of a sector formulas, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.05em} \\text{area of the logo} &= \\pi r^2 - \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2 \\theta \\hspace{3em} [\\text{where $\\theta = \\text{A\\^OB}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    6\\pi &= \\pi(3)^2 - \\dfrac{1}{2}(3)^2 \\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    2\\pi &= 3\\pi - \\dfrac{3\\theta}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3\\theta}{2} &= \\pi \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\hspace{0.7em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the length of an arc formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{length of the (major) arc AB} &= r\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi - \\text{A\\^OB}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\Big(2\\pi - \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 4\\pi \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{perimeter of the logo} &= 4\\pi + 2r \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\\pi + 2(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\\pi + 6 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{6.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA211","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA211\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe following diagram shows a radioactivity warning symbol made out of a\ncircle in the centre and three equal blades.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/cbb464d0bf450f25830c94376d8c375dc107d08d.svg\" alt=\"cbb464d0bf450f25830c94376d8c375dc107d08d.svg\">\n\nGiven that $\\text{OA} = 2$ cm,\n$\\text{AB} = 1$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 4$ cm, and\n$\\text{C\\^OD} = \\ang{30}$, find the area of the symbol.","markScheme":"We have $\\text{C\\^OD} = \\ang{30} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\nUsing the area of a sector formula, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.8em} A_{\\text{blade}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OC}^2 - \\text{OB}^2\\big](\\text{C\\^OD}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[7^2 - 3^2\\big]\\Big[\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{10\\pi}{3} \\text{ cm}^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the area of the\nsymbol is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.2em} A_{\\text{symbol}} &= \\pi \\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big] + 3A_{\\text{blade}} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\pi\\big[2^2\\big] + 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{10\\pi}{3}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n&= 4\\pi + 10\\pi \\\\[12pt]\n&= 14\\pi \\text{ cm}^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA212","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA212\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe diagram below shows two circles with centres at the points A and B\nand radii $3r$ and $2r$, respectively. The point B lies on the circle\nwith centre A. The circles intersect at the points C and D.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/afd6e0672a0258953fa2b0338cc01b6683d850f0.svg\" alt=\"afd6e0672a0258953fa2b0338cc01b6683d850f0.svg\">\n\nLet $\\alpha$ be the measure of the angle CAD and $\\theta$ be the measure\nof the angle CBD in radians.\n\n1.  Find an expression for the shaded area in terms of $\\alpha, \\theta$\n    and $r$.  [3 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $\\displaystyle\\alpha = 4\\arcsin\\frac{1}{3}$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence find the value of $r$ given that the shaded area is equal to\n    $16$ cm$^2$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The chord CD divides the shaded region into two segments:\n    1\\. Segment A: the segment of the circle with centre A;\n\n    2\\. Segment B: the segment of the circle with centre B.\n\n    The following diagram shows the segment A.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/caa3e42d91c8eb082c4acfe1110b1d04276d5890.svg\" alt=\"caa3e42d91c8eb082c4acfe1110b1d04276d5890.svg\">\n\n    Using the area of a triangle and the area of a sector formulas, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.3em} A_{\\text{segment\\hspace{0.2em}A}} &= A_{\\text{sector}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{ACD}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(3r)^2\\alpha - \\dfrac{1}{2}(3r)(3r)\\sin \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\frac{9}{2}(\\alpha - \\sin\\alpha)r^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Similarly, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.05em} A_{\\text{segment\\hspace{0.2em}B}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(2r)^2\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{2}(2r)(2r)\\sin\\theta \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2(\\theta - \\sin\\theta)r^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the shaded area is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} A_{\\text{segment\\hspace{0.2em}A}} + A_{\\text{segment\\hspace{0.2em}B}} &=  \\hspace{0.525em}\\bigg[\\frac{9}{2}(\\alpha - \\sin\\alpha) + 2(\\theta - \\sin\\theta)\\bigg]r^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let M be the midpoint of \\[BD\\].\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/dda7937ee8d436b10422ba275de3a8ff4ebf9444.svg\" alt=\"dda7937ee8d436b10422ba275de3a8ff4ebf9444.svg\">\n\n    Using the right triangle ABM, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin \\dfrac{\\alpha}{4} &= \\dfrac{\\text{BM}}{\\text{AB}} \\\\[7pt]\n    \\sin \\dfrac{\\alpha}{4} &= \\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[7pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\alpha}{4} &= \\arcsin \\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.2em} \\alpha = 4\\arcsin \\frac{1}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\approx 1.3593 \\text{ radians}]\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the right triangle ABM, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.4em} \\dfrac{\\alpha}{4} + \\dfrac{\\theta}{2} &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\theta &= \\pi - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}  \\\\[5pt]\n    \\theta &= \\pi - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg[4\\arcsin \\frac{1}{3}\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n    \\theta &= \\pi - 2\\arcsin \\frac{1}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\approx 2.4619 \\text{ radians}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation\n    $\\bigg[\\dfrac{9}{2}(\\alpha - \\sin\\alpha) + 2(\\theta - \\sin\\theta)\\bigg]r^2 = 16$\n    for $r$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bigg[\\frac{9}{2}(1.3593 - \\sin 1.3593) + 2(2.4619 - \\sin 2.4619)\\bigg]r^2 &= 16 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.5em} r \\approx 1.72 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA253","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA253\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circle with the centre O and radius\n$8$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/4701a00e1152364c7844fcad6bd746938b6e55aa.svg\" alt=\"4701a00e1152364c7844fcad6bd746938b6e55aa.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle and $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = 1.4$ radians.\n\n1.  Find the length of the minor arc AB. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the length of an arc formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} l_{\\text{arc}} &= (\\text{A\\^{O}B})(\\text{OB}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1.4)(8) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11.2 \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OB}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[8^2\\big](1.4) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 44.8 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA255","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA255\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circle with centre O and radius\n$10$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/d680d624c5579097af69d19f4c53f5a964e7e9c9.svg\" alt=\"d680d624c5579097af69d19f4c53f5a964e7e9c9.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle and $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = 2.2$ radians.\n\n1.  Find:\n    1.  the length of the minor arc AB;\n\n    2.  the perimeter of the shaded region. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the length of an arc formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        l_{\\text{arc}} &= (\\text{A\\^{O}B})(\\text{OA}) \\hspace{1.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (2.2)(10) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 22 \\text{ cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence the perimeter of the shaded region is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        P_{\\text{shaded}} &= 2(\\text{OA}) + l_{\\text{arc}} \\hspace{2.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2(10) + 22 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 42 \\text{ cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[10^2\\big](2.2) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 110 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA261","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA261\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circle with centre O and radius\n$8$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/d599dde5f666e8b2b70a0d2ef18e3de9f1744b7f.svg\" alt=\"d599dde5f666e8b2b70a0d2ef18e3de9f1744b7f.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle and $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = 2.7$ radians.\n\n1.  Find:\n    1.  the length of the major arc AB;\n\n    2.  the perimeter of the shaded region. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the length of an arc formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.3em} l_{\\text{arc}} &= (2\\pi - \\text{A\\^{O}B})(\\text{OA}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (2\\pi - 2.7)(8) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 28.7 \\text{ cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence the perimeter of the shaded region is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        P_{\\text{shaded}} &= 2(\\text{OA}) + l_{\\text{arc}} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 2(8) + 28.7 \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 44.7 \\text{ cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.05em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](2\\pi-\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[8^2\\big](2\\pi-2.7) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 115 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA267","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA267\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circle with centre O and radius\n$5$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/7502343217aad3149465f358ed929a0bf497a477.svg\" alt=\"7502343217aad3149465f358ed929a0bf497a477.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle and $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = 2.1$ radians.\n\n1.  Find the length of:\n    1.  minor arc AB;\n\n    2.  chord AB. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the length of an arc formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        l_{\\text{arc}} &= (\\text{A\\^{O}B})(\\text{OA}) \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (2.1)(5) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 10.5 \\text{ cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.2em} \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{OA}^2 + \\text{OB}^2 - 2(\\text{OA})(\\text{OB})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\text{AB} &= \\sqrt{5^2 + 5^2 - 2(5)(5) \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2.1)} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\text{AB} \\approx 8.67 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{11.9em}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector and the area of a triangle formulas, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{sector}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{OAB}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^{O}B}) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{OA})(\\text{OB})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[5^2\\big](2.1) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(5)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(2.1) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 15.5 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA268","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA268\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circle with centre O and radius\n$7.2$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/d4977f00fb021efbd366ac34dea773c85923bdcd.svg\" alt=\"d4977f00fb021efbd366ac34dea773c85923bdcd.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle and $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = 1.3$ radians.\n\n1.  Find the length of:\n    1.  minor arc AB;\n\n    2.  chord AB. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the length of an arc formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        l_{\\text{arc}} &= (\\text{A\\^{O}B})(\\text{OA}) \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (1.3)(7.2) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 9.36 \\text{ cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.5em} \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{OA}^2 + \\text{OB}^2 - 2(\\text{OA})(\\text{OB})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\text{AB} &= \\sqrt{7.2^2 + 7.2^2 - 2(7.2)(7.2) \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(1.3)} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\text{AB} \\approx 8.71 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{11.6em}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector and the area of a triangle formulas, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.95em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{sector}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{OAB}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^{O}B}) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{OA})(\\text{OB})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[7.2^2\\big](1.3) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(7.2)(7.2)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(1.3) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 8.72 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA283","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA283\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circle with centre O and radius $r$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/4a2de1c9425e4d830bbf4132ba8ee6e58293cd69.svg\" alt=\"4a2de1c9425e4d830bbf4132ba8ee6e58293cd69.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle and $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = 1.2$ radians.\n\n1.  Show that $\\text{OC} = r\\cos 1.2$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  The area of the shaded region is\n    $35$ cm$^2$. Find the value of $r$.\n    [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the right triangle OAC, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos 1.2 &= \\dfrac{\\text{OC}}{r} \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\text{OC} = r\\cos 1.2\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector and the area of a triangle formulas, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{sector}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{OAC}} \\\\[10pt]\n    A_{\\text{shaded}}  &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^{O}B}) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{OA})(\\text{OC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    35 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[r^2\\big](1.2) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(r)(r\\cos 1.2)(\\sin 1.2) \\\\[6pt]\n    35 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2(1.2- \\cos 1.2\\times\\sin 1.2) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    r \\approx 9.01 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{0.7em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA289","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA289\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe diagram below shows the cross section of a cylindrical pipe,\n$80$ cm in length, carrying water. The pipe has\na radius of $15$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/4719558002970c7289015f8defc8959d46793d7a.svg\" alt=\"4719558002970c7289015f8defc8959d46793d7a.svg\">\n\nThe pipe is not at full capacity, such that the chord length of the\nwater level \\[AB\\] is $20$ cm. Find the volume\nof water in the pipe.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/15b78589197cdc906193828635817a7d6cacb10e.svg\" alt=\"15b78589197cdc906193828635817a7d6cacb10e.svg\">\n\nUsing the cosine rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{AO}^2 + \\text{BO}^2 - 2(\\text{AO})(\\text{BO})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{O}A}) \\\\[13pt]\n20^2 &= 15^2 + 15^2 - 2(15)(15)\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{O}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{O}A}) &= \\dfrac{15^2 + 15^2 - 20^2}{2(15)(15)} \\\\[7pt]\n\\text{B\\^{O}A} &\\approx 1.46 \\text{ radians} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{3.65em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the area of a sector and the area of a triangle formulas, we\nobtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.65em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{circle}} - (A_{\\text{sector}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{AOB}}) \\\\[10pt]\n&= \\pi\\big[\\text{AO}^2\\big] - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{AO}^2\\big](\\text{B\\^{O}A}) + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AO})(\\text{BO})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{O}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\pi\\big[15^2\\big] - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[15^2\\big](1.46) + \\dfrac{1}{2}(15)(15)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(1.46) \\\\[10pt]\n&\\approx 654.42 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the volume of water in\nthe pipe is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.8em} V_{\\text{pipe}} &= A_{\\text{shaded}}\\times l_{\\text{pipe}} \\\\[8pt]\n&= (654.42)(80) \\\\[6pt]\n&\\approx 52\\hspace{0.15em}400 \\text{ cm}^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA301","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA301\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA bronze sphere has a radius of $10.5$ cm.\n\n1.  Find the volume of the sphere, expressing your answer in the form\n    $a\\times10^k$, $1 \\leq a < 10$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. [3 marks]\n\nThe sphere is to be melted down and remoulded into the shape of a\n**cone**\\\nwith a height of $11.9$ cm.\n\n2.  Find the radius of the base of the cone, giving your answer\n    correct to $3$ significant\n    figures. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the volume of a sphere formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{sphere}} &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (10.5)^3 \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 4849.05 \\text{ cm}^3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 4.85\\times10^3 \\text{ cm}^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the volume of a cone formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{cone}} &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2h \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{9.7em} 4849.05 &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2(11.9) \\hspace{2.4em} [\\text{since $V_{\\text{cone}} = V_{\\text{sphere}}$}] \\\\[10pt]\n    r &\\approx 19.73 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{3.35em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    r \\approx 19.7 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{1.5em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA302","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA302\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA sphere made out of gold has a radius of\n$13.2$ cm.\n\n1.  Find the volume of the sphere, expressing your answer in the form\n    $a\\times10^k$, $1 \\leq a < 10$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. [3 marks]\n\nThe sphere is to be melted down and remoulded into the shape of a\n**cylinder**\\\nwith a height of $18.4$ cm.\n\n2.  Find the radius of the base of the cylinder, giving your\n    answer correct to $2$ significant figures.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the volume of a sphere formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{sphere}} &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (13.2)^3 \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 9634.08 \\text{ cm}^3 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 9.63\\times10^3 \\text{ cm}^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the volume of a cylinder formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V_{\\text{cylinder}} &= \\pi r^2h \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{10.5em} 9634.08 &= \\pi r^2(18.4) \\hspace{2.8em} [\\text{since $V_{\\text{cylinder}} = V_{\\text{sphere}}$}] \\\\[7pt]\n    r &\\approx 12.91 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    r \\approx 13 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{1.6em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA731","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA731\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA right cylinder has height $h$ mm and diameter $x$ mm. The volume of\nthis cylinder is equal to $45$ mm$^3$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/9aebb1d3e12c9fb1c552ed279ae0c28d1ca125c3.svg\" alt=\"9aebb1d3e12c9fb1c552ed279ae0c28d1ca125c3.svg\">\n\nThe total surface area, $A$, of the cylinder can be expressed as\n$A = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}x^2 + \\dfrac{k}{x}$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $k$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $x$ that makes the total surface area a minimum.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the volume of a right cylinder formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V &= \\pi r^2h \\\\[6pt]\n    45 &= \\pi\\bigg[\\dfrac{x}{2}\\bigg]^2\\hspace{-0.2em}h \\\\[3pt]\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n    45 &= \\dfrac{\\pi x^2h}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n    h &= \\dfrac{180}{\\pi x^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the total surface area is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} A(x) &= [\\text{top \\& bottom area}] + [\\text{curved surface area}] \\\\[16pt]\n    &=  2\\pi r^2 + 2\\pi rh \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 2\\pi\\bigg[\\dfrac{x}{2}\\bigg]^2 + 2\\pi\\bigg[\\dfrac{x}{2}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{180}{\\pi x^2}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}x^2 + \\dfrac{180}{x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find$\\hspace{0.3\n    em}k = 180$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we sketch the graph of $y = A(x)$, for $x > 0$ \\[by using\n    G.D.C.\\], we have\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/10ad2caa42e46f8e9db41f9ae6642694c0b81ac1.svg\" alt=\"10ad2caa42e46f8e9db41f9ae6642694c0b81ac1.svg\">\n\n    Hence\n     $\\hspace{0.3\n    em}x \\approx 3.86 \\text{  mm}$\n    makes the total surface area a minimum.\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA738","marks":"13","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA738\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA loaf pan is made in the shape of a cylinder. The pan has a diameter of\n$24\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ and a height of\n$5\\,\\mathrm{cm}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Circle%20Geometry/images/a347288bee589088e6dce052611dad40b73c72e7.svg\" alt=\"a347288bee589088e6dce052611dad40b73c72e7.svg\">\n\n1.  Calculate the volume of this pan. [2 marks]\n\nGloria prepares enough bread dough to exactly fill the pan. The dough\nwas in the shape of a sphere.\n\n2.  Find the radius of the sphere in cm, correct to one decimal place. [3 marks]\n\nThe bread was cooked in a hot oven. Once taken out of the oven, the\nbread was left in the kitchen.\n\nThe temperature, $T$, of the bread, in degrees\nCelsius, $\\text{\\textdegree{C}}$, can be\nmodelled by the function\n\n$$\nT(t) = a\\times(1.51)^{-\\frac{t}{3}} + 21, \\hspace{0.5em} t \\geq 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is a constant and $t$ is the time, in minutes, since the bread\nwas taken out of the oven.\n\nWhen the bread was taken out of the oven its temperature was\n$205$ $\\text{\\textdegree{C}}$.\n\n3.  Find the value of $a$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Find the temperature that the bread will be $10$ minutes\n    after it is taken out of the oven. [2 marks]\n\nThe bread can be eaten once its temperature drops to\n$35$ $\\text{\\textdegree{C}}$.\n\n5.  Calculate, to the nearest minute, the time since the bread\n    was taken out\\\n    of the oven until it can be eaten. [3 marks]\n\n6.  In the context of this model, state what the value of $21$\n    represents. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Using the volume of a cylinder formula with $r = 12$ and $h = 5$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.05em} V_{\\text{pan}} &= \\pi r^2h \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\pi(12)^2(5) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{= 720\\pi \\approx 2260 \\text{ cm}^3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the volume of a sphere formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.4em} V_{\\text{dough}} &= V_{\\text{pan}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 &= 720\\pi \\hspace{3em} [\\text{from  part (a)}] \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving this equation for $r$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{12.9em} \\boxed{r \\approx 8.1 \\text{ cm}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $T(0) = 205$ for $a$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a\\times(1.51)^0 + 21 &= 205 \\hspace{5.65em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n    a + 21 &= 205 \\\\[-12pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} \\boxed{a = 184} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n4.  Evaluating $T(t)$ for $t=10$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} T(10) &= 184\\times(1.51)^{-\\frac{10}{3}} + 21 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[9pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{\\approx 67.6 \\text{\\hspace{0.15em}\\textdegree{C}}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Solving the equation $T(t) = 35$ for $t$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} 184\\times(1.51)^{-\\frac{t}{3}} + 21 &= 35 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    t &\\approx 18.8 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[-16pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\boxed{t \\approx 19 \\text{ minutes}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n6.  $\\text{21 is the room  temperature of the kitchen in degrees celsius.}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA847","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA847\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe diameter of a spherical object is $3 \\times 10^{8}\\hspace{0.2em}$cm.\n\n1.  Write down the radius of the object. [1 mark]\n\nThe volume of the object can be expressed in the form\n$\\pi (a \\times 10^k)$ cm$^3$ where\n$1 \\leq a < 10$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $1.5 \\times 10^8 \\hspace{0.2em}\\text{cm}$\n\n2.  Using the volume of a sphere formula with $r = 1.5 \\times 10^8$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    V &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi \\left( 1.5 \\times 10^8 \\right)^3 \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi \\left( \\dfrac{27}{8} \\times 10^{24} \\right) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{9}{2} \\times 10^{24} \\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence,\n     $a = 4.5$\n    and   $k = 24$"}]},{"name":"Mixed Geometry","questions":[{"ref":"AA1167","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1167\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nTwo circles, with centres $A$ and $B$, intersect at points $S$ and $T$.\n\nThe radii of the circles are $5\\,$cm and $2\\,$cm and angle $\\angle SAT = 30^\\circ$. This is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/AA1167_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1167_q\">\n\nThe shaded region $A$ is enclosed by the arcs of the two circles from their intersection points.\n\n1.  Find $\\overline{ST}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the angle $\\angle SBT$. [2 marks]\n\nMorris is making a sign for his ice cream shop based on the circles shown above and he wants to paint region $A$.\n\n3. What is the area of region $A$? [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule we can write a formula $\\overline{ST}$ and then use our G.D.C.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\overline{ST}^2&=5^2+5^2-2\\times 5 \\times 5 \\times \\cos{30^\\circ} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=2.5881...\\\\[6pt]\n        \\overline{ST}&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{2.59\\,\\text{cm}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.\n    Consider the triangle $SBT$. We can use the cosine rule and the answer from part(b) to find $\\angle SBT$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos{\\angle SBT}&=\\frac{2^2+2^2-(2.5881...)^2}{2\\times 2^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\angle SBT&=80.63...^\\circ\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{80.6^\\circ} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  For this part of the question we need to break region $A$ into two parts. This is shown below as the dark blue and dark red regions.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/AA1167_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1167_a\">\n\n    We will first find the dark blue region ($A_B$) by subtracting the area of the blue triangle ($A_t$) from the area of the blue sector ($A_s$). Using the formula booklet\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_B&=A_s - A_t\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{30}{360}\\times \\pi \\times 5^2 - \\frac{1}{2}\\times 5^2 \\times \\sin{30^\\circ}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.29498...\\text{cm}^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using a similar method we can find the area of the red region.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_R&=A_s - A_t\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{80.63...}{360}\\times \\pi \\times 2^2 - \\frac{1}{2}\\times 2^2 \\times \\sin{80.63...^\\circ}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.8414...\\text{cm}^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we can find the total area of $A$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=A_b+A_r\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.29498...+0.8414...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}1.14\\,\\text{cm}^2  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA213","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA213\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nFarmer Edward owns a triangular field with ratio of the sides\n$15:15:15\\sqrt{2}$. Edward attaches a rope to a wooden post at the right\nangle corner of his field, and attaches the other end to his cow Gertie.\n\n1.  Given that the rope is $10$ m long,\n    calculate the percentage of Edward's field that Gertie is able to\n    graze. Give your answer correct to **the nearest percent**.\n    [3 marks]\n\nEdward replaces the rope with another one, this time of length $b$\nmetres, $10 < b < 15$, so that Gertie can graze exactly $90$% of his\nfield.\n\n2.  Show that $b$ satisfies the equation\n    $$\n    \\hspace{-2em} b^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\arccos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2b}\\bigg)\\bigg] + \\bigg(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\bigg)\\sqrt{b^2 - \\dfrac{225}{2}} = 101.25. \\\\\n    $$\n    [7 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $b$. Give your answer correct to **two\n    decimal places**. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch Edward's field and the region that his cow Gertie is\n    able to graze (single sector, unshaded), we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/142acc37a1d81643a131037b3368808fab24ad5d.svg\" alt=\"142acc37a1d81643a131037b3368808fab24ad5d.svg\">\n\n    Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.4em} A_{\\text{field}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{OB})(\\text{OC}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(15)(15) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 112.5 \\text{ m$^2$}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the area of a sector\n    formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} A_{\\text{sector}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](\\text{B\\^{O}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[10^2\\big]\\Big[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\Big] \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 25\\pi \\text{ m$^2$}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the percentage of\n    Edward's field that Gertie is able to graze is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{A_{\\text{sector}}}{A_{\\text{field}}}\\times100\\% &= \\dfrac{25\\pi}{112.5}\\times100\\% \\hspace{2.65em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 70\\%\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we sketch Edward's field and the region that his cow Gertie is\n    able to graze ($2 \\text{ sectors} + 2 \\text{ triangles}$, unshaded),\n    we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/c656b73af5e41c0e3158f9fbdcc110f74db38ea1.svg\" alt=\"c656b73af5e41c0e3158f9fbdcc110f74db38ea1.svg\">\n\n    where:\n\n    1\\. $\\text{OA} = 15$ m and\n        $\\text{AE} = 15\\sqrt{2}$ m;\n\n    2\\.\n        $\\text{OC} = \\text{AC} = \\text{CE} = \\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ m;\n\n    3\\. $\\text{OB} = b$ m;\n\n    4\\.\n        $\\text{BC} = \\sqrt{b^2 - \\dfrac{225}{2}}$ m;\n\n    5\\. $\\theta = \\text{B\\^{O}C}$;\n\n    6\\. $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\theta$.\n\n    Using the right triangle BOC, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\text{OC}}{\\text{OB}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2b} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\theta &= \\arccos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2b}\\bigg)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the area of a sector and the area of a triangle\n    formulas, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &=  2\\bigg[ \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OB}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^OB}) + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{OC})(\\text{BC})\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big[\\text{OB}^2\\big]\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\theta\\bigg] + (\\text{OC})(\\text{BC}) \\\\[3pt]\n    &= b^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\arccos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2b}\\bigg)\\bigg] + \\bigg(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\bigg)\\sqrt{b^2 - \\dfrac{225}{2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    On the other hand, $90\\%$ of the area of Edward's field is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\text{shaded}} &= (112.5)(0.9) \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 101.25 \\text{ m$^2$}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n\n    $$\n    b^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\arccos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2b}\\bigg)\\bigg] + \\bigg(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\bigg)\\sqrt{b^2 - \\dfrac{225}{2}} = 101.25\n    $$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $(1)$ for $b$\n    \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\], we find\n     $b \\approx 11.83 \\text{ m}$"},{"ref":"AA246","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA246\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nPeggy owns a rectangular field, $12$ m by\n$5$ m. Peggy attaches a rope to a metal post at\nthe upper left corner of her field, and attaches the other end to her\ncow Daisy.\n\n1.  Given that the rope is $6$ m long, calculate\n    the percentage of Peggy's field that Daisy is able to graze. Give\n    your answer correct to **the nearest percent**. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Peggy replaces Daisy's rope with another one of length $\\alpha$ m,\n    $5 < \\alpha < 12$, so that Daisy can now graze **exactly three\n    quarters** of Peggy's field.\n\n    Show that $\\alpha$ satisfies the equation\n\n    $$\n    \\textstyle 5\\sqrt{\\alpha^2 - 25} + \\alpha^2\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{5}{\\alpha}\\bigg) =  90.\n    $$\n    [5 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $\\alpha$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch Peggy's field and the region that her cow Daisy is able\n    to graze ($\\text{right triangle} +\\text{sector}$, unshaded), we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/874caa3225f5635c09acdbdf186647f4983800de.svg\" alt=\"874caa3225f5635c09acdbdf186647f4983800de.svg\">\n\n    Using the area of a rectangle formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\text{field}} &= (\\text{OB})(\\text{OE}) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= (12)(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 60 \\text{ m}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the area of a triangle\n    formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.2em} A_{\\triangle\\text{DOE}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{DE})(\\text{OE}) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\textstyle \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\sqrt{6^2-5^2}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\text{ m}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the area\n    of a sector formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{12em} A_{\\text{sector}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OD}^2\\big](\\text{D\\^{O}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[6^2\\big](\\theta) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 18\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{5}{6}\\bigg) \\text{ m}^2 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $\\sin \\theta = 5/6$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the percentage of Peggy's field that Daisy is able to graze is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bigg[\\dfrac{A_{\\triangle\\text{DOE}} + A_{\\text{sector}}}{A_{\\text{field}}}\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.05em}\\times100\\% &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{2} + 18\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\frac{5}{6}\\big)}{60}\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.05em}\\times100\\% \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 43\\%\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we sketch Peggy's field and the region that her cow Daisy is able\n    to graze ($\\text{right triangle} +\\text{sector}$, unshaded), we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/5d7c138857cb64746d28b7646c6a98bd09037be8.svg\" alt=\"5d7c138857cb64746d28b7646c6a98bd09037be8.svg\">\n\n    where:\n\n    1\\. $\\text{DE} = \\sqrt{\\alpha^2 - 25}$ m and\n        $\\theta = \\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{5}{\\alpha}\\bigg)$\n        radians;\n\n    2\\.\n        $A_{\\triangle\\text{DOE}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\sqrt{\\alpha^2 - 25})$\n        m$^2$;\n\n    3\\.\n        $A_{\\text{sector}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\alpha^2\\big](\\theta)$\n        m$^2$;\n\n    4\\. $A_\\text{field} = 60$ m$^2$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\triangle\\text{DOE}} + A_{\\text{sector}} &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\\hspace{0.05em}A_\\text{field} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\textstyle \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\sqrt{\\alpha^2 - 25}) + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\alpha^2\\big](\\theta) &= \\dfrac{3}{4}(60) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\textstyle 5\\sqrt{\\alpha^2 - 25} + \\alpha^2\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{5}{\\alpha}\\bigg) =  90 \\hspace{2.15em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $(1)$ for $\\alpha$\n    \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\], we get\n     $\\alpha \\approx 9.46 \\text{ m}$"},{"ref":"AA249","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA249\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows a fenced triangular enclosure in the middle of a\ngrassy field where $\\mathrm{AC} = 3$ m,\n$\\mathrm{DC} = 2$ m, $\\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\alpha$\nradians and $\\text{A\\^{C}B} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$ radians.\n\nOne end of a rope is attached at point D on the outside edge of the\nenclosure, and the other end is attached to a goat G.\n\nGiven that the rope is $6$ m long and the area of field outside the\nenclosure that the goat is able to graze is\n$74$ m$^2$, find the value of $\\alpha$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/8da8003bc3b284d5b66ba703992b3a876529d9a6.svg\" alt=\"8da8003bc3b284d5b66ba703992b3a876529d9a6.svg\">","markScheme":"If we sketch the region outside the fenced triangular enclosure that the\ngoat is able to graze (three sectors, shaded in light blue), we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/e56faebedfc9bba1814ecb333696d1a613652900.svg\" alt=\"e56faebedfc9bba1814ecb333696d1a613652900.svg\">\n\nwhere:\n\n1\\. $\\mathrm{CE} = 4$ m and\n    $\\mathrm{AH} = 1$ m;\n\n2\\. $\\text{E\\^{C}F} = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ and\n    $\\text{F\\^{A}H} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\alpha$.\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{\\text{sector\\hspace{0.2em}1}}+A_{\\text{sector\\hspace{0.2em}2}}+A_{\\text{sector\\hspace{0.2em}3}} &= 74 \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[6^2\\big]\\big[\\pi\\big] + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[4^2\\big]\\bigg[\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\bigg] + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[1^2\\big]\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\alpha\\bigg] &= 74 \\\\\n%18\\pi + \\dfrac{16\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} &= 74 \\\\[10pt]\n%\\dfrac{47\\pi}{2} + \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} &= 74 \\\\[9pt]\n%\\alpha &= 2\\bigg[74 - \\dfrac{47\\pi}{2}\\bigg] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{4.6em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{3em} \\alpha \\approx 0.345\n$$"},{"ref":"AA252","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA252\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/48262080f0126eb0c2b6a75a97f728bd1bfa9403.svg\" alt=\"48262080f0126eb0c2b6a75a97f728bd1bfa9403.svg\">\n\nThe sides \\[AB\\] and \\[BC\\] of the isosceles triangle ABC have lengths\n$5$ cm and the third\nside \\[AC\\] has length $6$ cm. The midpoint of\n\\[AC\\] is denoted by M. The circular arc AC has\ncentre, G, the midpoint of \\[BM\\].\n\n1.\n    1.  Find AG.\n\n    2.  Find MĜA in radians. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the right triangle ABM, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{8em} \\mathrm{BM} &= \\sqrt{\\mathrm{AB}^2 - \\mathrm{AM}^2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{5^2 - 3^2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since M is a midpoint}] \\\\[9pt]\n        &= 4 \\text{ cm}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using the right triangle\n        AGM, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{8em} \\mathrm{AG} &= \\sqrt{\\mathrm{GM}^2 + \\mathrm{AM}^2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{2^2 + 3^2} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since G is a midpoint}] \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{13} \\text{ cm} \\hspace{2.65em} [\\approx 3.61 \\text{ cm}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tan\\hspace{0.05em}(\\mathrm{M\\hat{G}A}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{AM}}{\\mathrm{GM}} \\\\[8pt]\n        \\tan\\hspace{0.05em}(\\mathrm{M\\hat{G}A}) &= \\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\[11pt]\n        \\mathrm{M\\hat{G}A} &= \\tan^{-1}(1.5) \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{3.5em} \\mathrm{M\\hat{G}A} \\approx 0.983 \\text{ radians}\n        $$\n\n2.  Since M is a midpoint of \\[AC\\] and $\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{BC}$, we\n    have $\\mathrm{M\\hat{G}A} = \\mathrm{C\\hspace{0.025em}\\hat{G}M}$.\n\n    Using the area of a sector and the area of a triangle formulas,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\mathrm{AG}^2\\big](\\mathrm{C\\hat{G}A}) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathrm{AC})(\\mathrm{GM}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(13)(2\\tan^{-1}(1.5)) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(6)(2) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since }\\mathrm{C\\hat{G}A} = 2\\mathrm{M\\hat{G}A}] \\\\[11pt]\n    &\\approx 6.78 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA279","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA279\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circle with centre O and radius\n$r$ cm.\n\nThe circle is divided into three equal sectors.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/7f5a64927561552456b4327a3478b469df993141.svg\" alt=\"7f5a64927561552456b4327a3478b469df993141.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle and $\\text{A\\^{O}B} = \\theta$ radians.\n\n1.  Find the exact value of $\\theta$, giving your answer in terms of\n    $\\pi$. [2 marks]\n\nThe area of the shaded sector AOB is\n$3\\pi$ cm$^2$.\n\n2.  Find the radius of the circle, $r$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the length of the chord AB. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\theta &= \\dfrac{1}{3}(2\\pi) \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.7em} A_{\\text{sector}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OB}^2\\big](\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    3\\pi &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[r^2\\big]\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) \\\\[10pt]\n    9 &= r^2 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    r = 3 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{1.3em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{OA}^2 + \\text{OB}^2 - 2(\\text{OA})(\\text{OB})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= 3^2 + 3^2 - 2(3)(3) \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\pi/3) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= 9 + 9 - 18(-1/2) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= 27 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AB} = 3\\sqrt{3} \\text{ cm} \\hspace{11em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA280","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA280\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC and sector ACD of a circle with\ncentre C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/a8aa4bb770e086d30cb351efe48b6aa3488058dd.svg\" alt=\"a8aa4bb770e086d30cb351efe48b6aa3488058dd.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AC} = 5$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 8$ cm,\n$\\text{the area of triangle ABC} = 10\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}^2$.\n\n1.  Find AĈB. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the exact area of sector ACD. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.6em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AC})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[8pt]\n    10\\sqrt{3} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(8)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} &= \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.8em} \\text{A\\^{C}B} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since \\text{A\\^{C}B} is acute}]\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{D\\^{C}A} &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\text{A\\^{C}B} \\hspace{0.75em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the area of a sector\n    formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.9em} A_{\\text{sector}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{AC}^2\\big](\\text{D\\^{C}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[5^2\\big]\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{25\\pi}{12} \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA286","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA286\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe diagram below shows a circle of radius\n$9$ cm. The points A, B, C and D lie on the\ncircle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/e3afdde75d5d2e0f844a6a5d7274cc20537c886c.svg\" alt=\"e3afdde75d5d2e0f844a6a5d7274cc20537c886c.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AD} = 9\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm},\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{DC} = 12\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm},\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{BC} = 16\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm},\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{A\\^{D}C} = \\ang{108},\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{D\\^{C}B} = \\ang{75}$.\n\n1.  Find AC. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find DĈA.\n\n    2.  Hence find AĈB. [5 marks]\n\n3.  Find the area of triangle ACD. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Hence, or otherwise, find the total area of the shaded region.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AD}^2 + \\text{CD}^2 - 2(\\text{AD})(\\text{CD})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{D}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{9^2 + 12^2 - 2(9)(12)\\cos \\ang{108}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AC} \\approx 17.1 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{12em}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the sine rule, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{D\\^{C}A})}{\\text{AD}} &= \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{D}C})}{\\text{AC}} \\hspace{3.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{D\\^{C}A})}{9} &= \\dfrac{\\sin\\ang{108}}{17.1} \\\\[8pt]\n        \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{D\\^{C}A}) &= \\dfrac{9\\sin\\ang{108}}{17.1} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{6.7em }\\text{D\\^{C}A} \\approx \\ang{30.0} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{A\\^{C}B} &= \\ang{75} - \\text{D\\^{C}A} \\hspace{3.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\ang{75} - \\ang{30.0} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\ang{45.0}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.25em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ACD}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AD})(\\text{CD})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{D}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(9)(12)\\sin \\ang{108} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 51.4 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AC})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(17.1)(16)\\sin \\ang{45.0} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 96.7 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{ACD}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 81\\pi - 51.4 - 96.7 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 106 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA290","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA290\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe following diagram shows a circular crop field.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/d32147c4e7101351ca3a3ac5d91bed2a551229ea.svg\" alt=\"d32147c4e7101351ca3a3ac5d91bed2a551229ea.svg\">\n\nThe circle has centre O and a radius of $400$ m,\nand the points A, B, and C lie on the circle. The angle AOB is $1.6$\nradians.\n\n1.  Find the length of chord AB.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of triangle AOB. [2 marks]\n\nThe angle BOC is $2.5$ radians.\n\n3.  Find the length of the minor arc AC. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]\n\nThe shaded region is to be planted with corn. Corn seeds are sold in\nbags which cost $$\\$140$$ each. One bag is enough for seeding\n$8960$ m$^2$.\n\n5.  Find the cost of the corn seeds. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{AO}^2 + \\text{BO}^2 - 2(\\text{AO})(\\text{BO})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AB} &= \\sqrt{400^2 + 400^2 - 2(400)(400)\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(1.6)} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AB} \\approx 574 \\text{ m} \\hspace{12.9em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} A_{\\triangle\\text{AOB}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AO})(\\text{BO})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(400)(400)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(1.6) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 80\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\text{ m}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} \\text{C\\^{O}A} &= 2\\pi - (\\text{A\\^{O}B} + \\text{B\\^{O}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\pi - (1.6 + 2.5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 2.1832 \\text{ radians}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the\n    length of an arc formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    l_{\\text{arc}} &= (\\text{C\\^{O}A})(\\text{CO}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (2.1832)(400) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 873 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the area of a sector formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{sector}} + A_{\\triangle\\text{AOB}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](\\text{C\\^{O}A}) + 80\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[400^2\\big](2.1832) + 80\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 255\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\text{ m}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{\\# bags needed} &= \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{A_{\\text{shaded}}}{8960}\\right\\rceil \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\left\\lceil\\dfrac{255\\hspace{0.15em}000}{8960}\\right\\rceil \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 29\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the cost of the corn seeds is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{cost} &= (29)(140) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$4060\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA296","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA296\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC, with\n$\\text{AB} = 9\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$,\n$\\text{BC} = 6\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$, and\n$\\text{A\\^{B}C} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/2bbd2aea52569ece11b343d5214200f61ca7f323.svg\" alt=\"2bbd2aea52569ece11b343d5214200f61ca7f323.svg\">\n\n1.  Show that $\\text{AC} = 3\\sqrt{7}\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n2.  The shape in the following diagram is formed by adding a semicircle\n    with diameter \\[AC\\] to the triangle.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/36f59d82426967dc80b1fcd4a0a947b096d711ec.svg\" alt=\"36f59d82426967dc80b1fcd4a0a947b096d711ec.svg\">\n\n    Find the exact perimeter of this shape. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{9^2 + 6^2 - 2(9)(6)\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/3)} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{81 + 36 - 108\\hspace{0.05em}(1/2)} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{63} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AC} = 3\\sqrt{7} \\text{ cm} \\hspace{10.3em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the circumference of a (semi) circle formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    P_{\\text{shape}} &=  \\text{AB} + \\text{BC} + \\pi\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9 + 6 + \\pi\\bigg[\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 15 + \\pi\\bigg[\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\bigg] \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA300","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA300\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows part of a circle with centre O and radius\n$5$ cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/d3a02f29895c6ce0b991606f98b7b99a2c21d836.svg\" alt=\"d3a02f29895c6ce0b991606f98b7b99a2c21d836.svg\">\n\nPoints A, B lie on the circle, chord AB has a length of $8$ cm and\n$\\text{A\\^{O}B} = \\theta$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $\\theta$, giving your answer in radians.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the shaded region. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{OA}^2 + \\text{OB}^2 - 2(\\text{OA})(\\text{OB})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{O}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    8^2 &= 5^2 + 5^2 - 2(5)(5)\\cos \\theta \\\\[7pt]\n    64 &= 50 - 50\\cos \\theta \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.5em} \\theta \\approx 1.85 \\text{ radians} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a sector formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\text{shaded}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{OA}^2\\big](\\text{B\\^{O}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[5^2\\big](2\\pi - 1.85) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 55.4 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA656","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA656\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows a right-angled triangle ABC and a sector ABD\nof a circle with centre A. The point E lies on [AC] such that [BE] is perpendicular to [AC]. The region $R$ is bounded by [DE], [BE] and arc BFD.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/587218ebb535066e62e4c39fa3e3aee886495568.svg\" alt=\"587218ebb535066e62e4c39fa3e3aee886495568.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AB} = 5$ cm,\n$\\text{AC} = 13$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 12$ cm.\n\n1.  Find BÂC, giving your answer in radians. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of $R$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) &= \\dfrac{5}{13} \\hspace{4.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.25em} \\text{B\\^{A}C} \\approx 1.18 \\text{ radians} \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) &= \\dfrac{\\text{AE}}{5}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AE} &= 5\\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\\hspace{-0.15em}\\left[\\dfrac{5}{13}\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 1.92 \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the area of a\n    sector and the area of a triangle formulas, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} A_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{sector}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{ABE}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{AB}^2\\big](\\text{B\\^{A}C}) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AE})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[5^2\\big](1.18) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(1.92)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(1.18) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 10.3 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA686","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA686\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe following diagram shows a right-angled triangle ABC and a sector CBE\nof a circle with centre C. The point D lies on [AC] such that [BD] is perpendicular to [AC]. The region $R$ is bounded by [BD], [DE] and arc BFE.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/bb277c6f5a06c81f51a26fa62ffabd73438e8bbe.svg\" alt=\"bb277c6f5a06c81f51a26fa62ffabd73438e8bbe.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AB} = 8$ cm,\n$\\text{AC} = 10$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 6$ cm.\n\nFind the area of the shaded region $R$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}E}) &= \\dfrac{6}{10} \\hspace{4.1em} \\\\[8pt]\n\\text{B\\^{C}E} &\\approx 0.927295 \\text{ radians} \\hspace{1.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}E}) &= \\dfrac{\\text{CD}}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n\\text{CD} &= 6\\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}E}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= 6\\hspace{-0.15em}\\left[\\dfrac{6}{10}\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n&= 3.6 \\text{ cm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the area of a sector and\nthe area of a triangle formulas, we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{\\text{shaded}} &= A_{\\text{sector}} - A_{\\triangle\\text{BCD}} \\\\[9pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[\\text{BC}^2\\big](\\text{B\\^{C}E}) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{BC})(\\text{CD})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}D}) \\\\[8pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big[6^2\\big](0.927295) - \\dfrac{1}{2}(6)(3.6)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(0.927295) \\\\[10pt]\n&\\approx 8.05 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA975","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA975\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the following cone inscribed in a cylinder.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/aa975a.PNG\" alt=\"aa975a\">\n\nThe coordinates of points P and Q are P$(-5,7,-2)$ and Q$(3,-1,2)$, and are the endpoints of the diameter of the circular base.\n\n1. Find the coordinates of the centre of the circular base of the cone.[2 marks]\n\n2. Find the radius, $r$, of the circular base of the cone.\n\n[2 marks]\n\nThe cylinder has a volume of $96\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3$.\n\n3. Find the volume of the inscribed cone.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the formula for the coordinates of the midpoint of a segment, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{Centre} &= \\text{Midpoint} (\\mathrm{P},\\mathrm{Q}) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{-5+3}{2},\\dfrac{7+(-1)}{2},\\dfrac{-2+2}{2}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\boxed{= (-1,3,0)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. The radius is the length of the segment joining point P or Q with the centre. \n\n    Using the distance formula \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d=\\sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2+(z_1-z_2)^2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        r &= \\sqrt{(-5-(-1))^2+(7-3)^2+(-2-0)^2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &= \\sqrt{(-4)^2+(4)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &= \\sqrt{16+16+4} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &= \\sqrt{36} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{r = 6}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using the volume formula for a cone and a cylinder, we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{Volume of the cylinder} &= \\pi r^2 h \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\text{Volume of the cone} &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h \\\\[10pt] \n\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\\times\\text{Volume of the cylinder} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(96\\pi\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    & \\hspace{-0.75em}\\boxed{V=32\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA996","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA996\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA circle, with centre $O$, has a radius of $r\\,$cm.\n\nThe triangle $OAB$ is drawn such that the vertices $A$ and $B$ lie on the circumference of the circle.\n\n$\\theta$ is the angle $\\angle{AOB}$.\n\nThe segment formed is shaded as shown below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/Screenshot%202023-09-15%20at%2012.50.10%20PM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-09-15 at 12.50.10 PM\">\n\n1. Show that the area of the shaded segment is given by $A_{\\text{segment}}=\\frac{1}{2}r^2(\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$.\n[2 marks]\n2. Show that the length of $AB$ can be written as $AB=r\\sqrt{2-2\\cos\\theta}$.\n[4 marks]\n\nA semi-circle is drawn with a diameter equal to the length of $AB$ with its centre at the point $C$, which is the mid-point of $AB$. This is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Mixed%20Geometry/images/Screenshot%202023-09-15%20at%2012.50.19%20PM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-09-15 at 12.50.19 PM\">\n\nThe blue area is the area of the semi-circle with the area of the segment removed.\n\nThe red area is the area of the triangle.\n\n3. Find the value of $\\theta$, where $0<\\theta<\\pi$, which makes the red area equal to the blue area. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the area of a triangle and area of a sector formula.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{\\text{shaded}}&=A_{\\text{sector}}-A_{\\text{triangle}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta-\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\sin\\theta\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}r^2(\\theta-\\sin\\theta)}\\hspace{0.525em}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As required.\n\n2. Use the Cosine rule to find the side length $AB$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        AB^2&=r^2+r^2-2\\times r\\times r\\times \\cos\\theta\\\\[6pt]\n        &=2r^2-2r^2 \\cos\\theta\\\\[6pt]\n        &=r^2(2-2\\cos\\theta)\\\\[6pt]\n        AB&=\\sqrt{r^2(2-2\\cos\\theta)}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{r\\sqrt{(2-2\\cos\\theta)}}\\hspace{0.525em}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As required.\n\n3. We need to first find the radius of the semi-circle in terms of $r$ and $\\theta$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        r_{\\text{semi}} &= \\dfrac{AB}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{r\\sqrt{(2-2\\cos\\theta)}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now write the area of the semi-circle in terms of $r$ and $\\theta$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{\\text{semi}}&=\\dfrac{\\pi r_{\\text{semi}}^2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi r^2(2-2\\cos\\theta)}{8}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi r^2(1-\\cos\\theta)}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now subtract the area of the segment you found in part (a) to get the blue area.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{\\text{blue}}&=\\dfrac{\\pi r^2(1-\\cos\\theta)}{4} - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}r^2(\\theta-\\sin\\theta)\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Form an equation using the area of the red triangle.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{\\text{red}}&=A_{\\text{blue}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\sin\\theta}&=\\dfrac{\\pi r^2(1-\\cos\\theta)}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta+\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\sin\\theta}\\\\[6pt]\n        0&=\\dfrac{\\pi r^2(1-\\cos\\theta)}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta&=\\dfrac{\\pi r^2(1-\\cos\\theta)}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n        2\\cancel{r^2}\\theta&=\\pi \\cancel{r^2}(1-\\cos\\theta)\\\\[6pt]\n        2\\theta&=\\pi(1-\\cos\\theta)\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The above equation has one unknown and can be solved on the G.D.C.\n\n    The value of $\\theta$, in the region $0<\\theta<\\pi$, for which the red and blue areas are equal is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians."}]},{"name":"Triangle Geometry","questions":[{"ref":"AA1052","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1052\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA cyclist leaves from a town situated at point $A$, she cycles $4$ kilometres on a bearing of $330^\\circ$, to another town at point $B$.\n\nShe then cycles $6$ kilometres on a bearing of $220^\\circ$, to a village at point $C$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/q1052.png\" alt=\"q1052\">\n\n1.  Determine the size of angle $\\angle ABC$. [1 mark]\n\nThe cyclist travels in a straight line from the village at point $C$ back to the town at point $A$ at an average speed of $22$ kilometres per hour.\n\n2.  1.  Calculate how long will it take her to get home. Give your answer to the nearest minute.\n\n    2.  Determine the bearing on which she will need to travel. [5 marks]","markScheme":"<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/a1052-1.png\" alt=\"a1052-1\">\n\n1.  We can add the bearings to the diagram and through angle reasoning\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\angle ABC\\ &=220-150\\\\\n        &=70^\\circ \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  1.  Using **the cosine rule (3.2)** we can find the length $AC$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        AC^2&=6^2+4^2-2\\times 6 \\times 4 \\times \\cos{70} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n        AC&=5.965... \\text{km} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next we can calculate the time, $T$, to the nearest minute, to go from $C$ to $A$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        T&=\\frac{5.965...}{22}\\times 60\\\\[6pt]\n        &=16 \\text{ minutes} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    2.  We can use **the sine rule (3.2)** to find angle $$\\angle ACB$$ ($=\\theta$). We must then add $40^\\circ$ to this to get the bearing the cyclist must head on.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/a1052-2.png\" alt=\"a1052-2\">\n\n        We calculated $\\angle ABC = 70^{\\circ}$ in part (a), and length $AC = 5.965...$ in part (b)(i).\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\sin{\\theta}}{4}&=\\frac{\\sin{70}}{5.965...}  \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n        \\theta&=39.059...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using **the sine rule (3.2)**, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{a}{\\sin A} &= \\frac{b}{\\sin B} = \\frac{c}{\\sin C}\\\\[8pt]\n        \\Rightarrow \\frac{\\sin A}{a} &= \\frac{\\sin B}{b} = \\frac{\\sin C}{c}\\\\[8pt]\n        \\frac{\\sin \\angle ACB}{AB} &= \\frac{\\sin \\angle ABC}{AC} \\\\[8pt]\n        \\frac{\\sin{\\theta}}{4}&=\\frac{\\sin{70}}{5.965...}  \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[8pt]\n        \\theta&=39.059...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore the bearing of $A$ from $C$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=39.059...+40\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\boxed{079^\\circ}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1164","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1164\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThere is a triangle $ABC$ such that $AB=7\\,$cm and $\\angle{BAC}=50^\\circ$.\n\nFind the lengths of $BC$ such that it is possible to draw two triangles $ABC$.","markScheme":"We begin by making a sketch of the information we have been given\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1164_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1164_a\">\n\nFirst begin by attempting to sketch triangles when $BC>7$.\n\nWe can see from the image below that only one is possible.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textcolor{white}{place}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1164_b.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1164_b\">\n\nNow we reduce the size of $BC$ until $BC=7$. We can see in the image below that still only $1$ triangle is possible.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1164_c.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1164_c\">\n\nHowever, if $BC<7$ then we can see it is possible to draw $2$ triangles.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textcolor{white}{place}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1164_d.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1164_d\">\n\nAs we continue to decrease the length of $BC$ we still observe $2$ triangles until $BC$ is perpendicular to $AC$ and then we go back to only being able to draw $1$ triangle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1164_e.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1164_e\">\n\nWe can use trigonometric ratios to find $BC$ at this time.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin{50^\\circ}&=\\frac{BC}{7}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    BC&=5.3623...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=5.36\\,\\text{cm}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if $BC<5.36...$ then we cannot draw any triangles.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1164_f.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1164_f\">\n\nHence, we can draw two triangles when $\\hspace{0.5252em}\\boxed{5.36\\,\\text{cm}<BC<7\\,\\text{cm}}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textcolor{white}{place}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1172","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1172\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA company is designing an animated logo for its website.\n\nThe logo is created by drawing a rectangle around a sector with radius $r$ centimetres and angle $\\theta$ radians. This is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1172_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1172_q\">\n\nLet the area of rectangle $RSTU$ be $A$.\n\n1.  Show that $A=r\\sqrt{2r^2(1-\\cos{\\theta})}$ [2 marks]\n\nTo animate the logo the designer increases the angle $\\theta$ by a constant rate of $0.5$ radians per second.\n\nFor the final logo design $r$ is fixed at $4\\,$cm.\n\n2.  If\n    $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}=a\\sqrt{b}$ where $a, b\\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n    Find $a$ and $b$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Recognise that the area of the rectangle is given by\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=VW\\times r \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the Cosine rule to find $VW$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        VW^2&=r^2+r^2-2r^2\\cos{\\theta}\\\\[6pt]\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        VW&=\\sqrt{2r^2(1-\\cos{\\theta})}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A&=r\\sqrt{2r^2(1-\\cos{\\theta})} \\hspace{2em} \\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We can recognise that the three quantities $A, \\theta$ and $t$ are changing.\n\n    Hence we can relate them using the chain rule.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\\times \\frac{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We know from the question that $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t}=0.5$ for all $\\theta$.\n\n    We need to find the value of $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n    Recall that $r=4$ hence $A=4\\sqrt{32(1-\\cos{\\theta})}$\n\n    We can use the power and chain rule to differentiate $A$ with respect to $\\theta$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A&=4(32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))^{\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}&=4(32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times (32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))' \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The derivative of the inner function of $A$ shown above is $(32-32\\cos{\\theta})'=32\\sin{\\theta}$\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}&=4(32(1-\\cos{\\theta}))^{-\\tfrac{1}{2}}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times 32\\sin{\\theta}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{64\\sin{\\theta}}{\\sqrt{32(1-\\cos{\\theta})}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We must evaluate this expression when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}&=\\frac{64\\sin{\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}}}{\\sqrt{32(1-\\cos{\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}})}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{64\\times \\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\sqrt{32(1-\\tfrac{1}{2})}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=8\\sqrt{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Recall\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\\times \\frac{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=8\\sqrt{3}\\times 0.5\\\\[6pt]\n        &=4\\sqrt{3} \\,\\text{cm}^2\\text{s}^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $a=4 \\text{ and } b=3$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1286","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1286\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA cyclist leaves town $A$ on a bearing of $240^\\circ$ and rides $11$ kilometers to town $B$.\n\nThe cyclist then travels $d\\,$km on a bearing of $090^\\circ$ until he is exactly $6\\,$km from town $A$.\n\nFind the possible values of $d$.","markScheme":"First let's sketch a diagram to better understand the given information.\n\nWe can sketch the approximate positions of towns $A$ and $B$ and also indicate the stopping places of the cyclist by $D_1$ and $D_2$.\n\nAny bearing measures start from the North and turn clockwise.\n\nWith all lengths in kilometres, our diagram will look like this.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1286-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1286-a1\">\n\nNotice that the dashed red circle, with radius $6$ centred on $A$, indicates that there are two places where the cyclist can stop that are $6$ kilometers from town $A$.\n\nUsing angle reasoning, we can determine $\\angle ABD$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1286-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1286-a2\">\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    240^\\circ-180^\\circ&=60^\\circ\\\\[6pt]\n    90^\\circ-60^\\circ&=30^\\circ\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhen we add this to the diagram we notice that there are $2$ triangles which have sides of $11$ and $6$ and a non-included angle of $30^\\circ$. This is an example of the ambiguous case of the sine rule.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1286-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1286-a3\">\n\nUsing the sine rule\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin A}{a}&=\\frac{\\sin B}{b}=\\frac{\\sin C}{c}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\sin D}{11}&=\\frac{\\sin 30}{6}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin D&=0.9166...\\\\[6pt]\n    D&=66.44...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nAs $D<90^\\circ$, this must be the angle associated with triangle $2$.\n\nLet's complete this triangle to find the distance $d_2$ associated with stopping at $D_2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1286-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1286-a4\">\n\nFinding angle $BAD_2$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    BAD_2&=180^\\circ-30^\\circ-66.44...^\\circ\\\\[6pt]\n    &=83.55...^\\circ\n\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing sine rule again\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{d_2}{\\sin 83.55...^\\circ}&=\\frac{6}{\\sin 30^\\circ}\\\\[6pt]\n    d_2&=11.92...\\\\[6pt]\n&=11.9\\text{ km}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that triangle $AD_1D_2$ is isosceles.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1286-a5.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1286-a5\">\n\nHence we can find angle $BD_1A$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    &=180^\\circ-66.44...^\\circ\\\\[6pt]\n    &=113.5...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinding angle $BAD_1$ in triangle $1$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\angle BAD_1&=180^\\circ-30^\\circ-113.55...^\\circ\\\\[6pt]\n    &=36.44...^\\circ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing sine rule again to find the distance associated with stopping at $D_1$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{d_1}{\\sin 36.44...^\\circ}&=\\frac{6}{\\sin 30^\\circ}\\\\[6pt]\n    d_1&=7.128...\\\\[6pt]\n&=7.13\\text{ km}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1295","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1295\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA group of designers are creating a knife holder.\n\nThe diagram on the left shows a knife inside the holder and the diagram on the right shows the empty holder.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/1295q.JPEG\" alt=\"1295q\">\n\nThe lengths of the holder, $AB=28$, $AD=20$ and $CD=20$ are measured in centimeters and $\\angle CXB = \\theta $ radians.\n\nIt is also given that $AB$ is parallel to $CD$ and that both are perpendicular to $AD$.\n\nThe designers can move the vertex $X$ anywhere along the line $AD$.\n\nMarket research suggests that the most appealing design is such that the value of $\\theta$ is maximised.\n\nFind the length $AX$ which maximises the value of $\\theta$.","markScheme":"This is an optimisation question. \n\nWe need to use the information given to obtain the angle $\\theta$ as a function of the length $AX$. We can then sketch the function and find the value of $x$ for which $\\theta$ is maximised.\n\nWe can begin by introducing a variable for the length $AX$, we'll call this $x$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/1295a1.JPEG\" alt=\"1295a1\">\n\nThe trick to this question, and ones similar, is to use the diagram to spot a way to relate the two variables. In our case $\\theta$ and $x$.\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle BXC$ contains the angle $\\theta$. We can write the side lengths of this triangle in terms of in terms of $x$. \n\nHence we can use the cosine rule to relate the variables.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\cos{A}&=\\frac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2 b c}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNote that angle $A$ and side length $a$ are opposite each other.\n\nIn our example $a=BC$ and $A=\\theta$. The other two sides, $b$ and $c$, are $BX$ and $CX$.\n\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\cos{\\theta}&=\\frac{BX^2+CX^2-BC^2}{2\\times BX \\times CX}\\\\[12pt]\n\n\\theta&=\\arccos{\\left(\\frac{BX^2+CX^2-BC^2}{2\\times BX \\times CX}\\right)}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow we need to write the side lengths in terms of $x$.\n\nLet's first consider triangle $\\triangle ABX$. Using Pythagoras' theorem we can write\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    BX^2&=AX^2+AB^2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    BX^2&=28^2+x^2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSimilarly, using the length $AX=20-x$, we get for triangle $\\triangle CDX$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    CX^2&=CD^2+XD^2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    CX^2&=20^2+(20-x)^2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nAnd finally, to find length $BC$, using the diagram below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/1295a2.JPEG\" alt=\"1295a2\">\n\nWe can see that \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    BC^2&=8^2+20^2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    BC^2&=464\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of these results into the cosine rule we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\theta&=\\arccos{\\left(\\frac{BX^2+CX^2-BC^2}{2\\times BX \\times CX}\\right)}\\\\[12pt]\n\n&=\\arccos{\\left(\\frac{28^2+x^2+20^2+(20-x)^2-464}{2 \\sqrt{28^2+x^2}\\sqrt{20^2+(20-x)^2}}\\right)}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNotice, we haven't simplified/expanded anything. We don't need to, we can simply let the calculator do the work.\n\nThis type of technique not only reduces the chance of making mistakes it also saves time! So it is an important exam technique.\n\nWe should now sketch the function on our G.D.C. and find the maximum point.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/1295a3.JPEG\" alt=\"1295a3\">\n\nHence we can see that when $AX=6.28\\,\\text{cm}$ the value of $\\theta$ is maximised.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNote, the maximum value of $\\theta$ is $0.822$ radians, however the question does not require us to state this.\n\nFinal note. You may've used this alternate (simpler?) method\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1295-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1295-a3\">\n\nWe can see that $\\alpha + \\beta +\\theta = \\pi$.\n\nIf we let the length $AX=x$ then we can use the tangent ratio to write \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    &&\\tan{\\alpha}&=\\dfrac{28}{x}&&\\hspace{2em}&&&\\tan{\\beta}&=\\dfrac{20}{20-x}&\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &&\\alpha&=\\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{28}{x}\\right)}&&\\hspace{2em}&&&\\beta&=\\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{20}{20-x}\\right)}&\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore using the relation above we get a function for $\\theta$ in terms of $x$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{28}{x}\\right)} + \\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{20}{20-x}\\right)} +\\theta &= \\pi\\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\theta &=\\pi-\\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{28}{x}\\right)} - \\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{20}{20-x}\\right)}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nOptimising the above function yields the correct answer."},{"ref":"AA1333","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1333\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe diagram shows a right-pyramid with vertex $A$ and triangular base $BCD$. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1333q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1333q\">\n\nPoint $B$ has coordinates $(2,-1,4)$ and point $D$ has coordinates $(6,0,1)$.\n\n1.    Find $BD$. [2 marks]\n\nThe volume of pyramid $ABCD$ is $39.2$ units$^3$.\n\n2.    Given that triangle $BCD$ is an equilateral triangle, find the height of the pyramid $AX$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. We are given coordinates of $B$ and $D$. Let's use the distance between two points formula to find the length of edge [BD]. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    d&=\\sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2+(z_1-z_2)^2}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    BD &=\\sqrt{(2-6)^2+(-1-0)^2+(4-1)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=5.099...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=5.10\\text{ units}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. The formula for the volume of a right pyramid is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    V&=\\frac{1}{3}Ah\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    where $A=$ area of the base and $h=$ height of the pyramid.\n\n    We are given volume and we need to find height. This tells us that we first need to find a value for $A$, the area of the base.\n\n    We know the base is equilateral so all sides are the same length and we found the length of $BD$ in part (a).\n\n    We also know that in an equilateral triangle, all angle measures are also the same $(\\frac{180^\\circ}{3}=60^\\circ)$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA1333a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1333a\">\n\n    Hence we can use the area of a triangle formula to calculate the area.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A&=\\frac{1}{2}ab\\sin C\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\frac{1}{2}(5.099...)^2\\sin 60^\\circ\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=11.25...\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $V=39.2$ and $A=11.25...$ into the volume formula we can solve for $h$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    39.2&=\\frac{1}{3}(11.25...)h\\\\[6pt]\n\n    h&=10.44...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=10.4\\text{ units}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA215","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA215\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nCabin boy Jim is at the top of a cliff on Tortuga Island, standing\n$100$ m above the sea level, and observes two\nships in the Caribbean sea.\n\nThe Black Pearl (P) is at an angle of depression of $\\ang{25}$ and the\nHispaniola (H) is at an angle of depression of $\\ang{50}$.\n\nThe following three dimensional diagram shows the foot of the cliff at\nO, Jim at J, two ships at P and H, and the angle\n$\\text{POH} = \\ang{75}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/e810f0e7222fd0cb0521ed78d1f929940c404119.svg\" alt=\"e810f0e7222fd0cb0521ed78d1f929940c404119.svg\">\n\nFind the distance between the two ships, giving your answer correct to\n$3$ significant figures.","markScheme":"Using the right triangle JOP, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.6em} \\tan\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{O\\^{P}J}) &= \\dfrac{\\text{JO}}{\\text{OP}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\tan \\ang{25} &= \\dfrac{100}{\\text{OP}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\text{OP} &= \\dfrac{100}{\\tan \\ang{25}} \\\\[10pt]\n\\text{OP} &\\approx 214.45 \\text{ m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the right\ntriangle JOH, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.6em} \\tan\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{O\\^{H}J}) &= \\dfrac{\\text{JO}}{\\text{OH}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\tan \\ang{50} &= \\dfrac{100}{\\text{OH}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\text{OH} &= \\dfrac{100}{\\tan \\ang{50}} \\\\[10pt]\n\\text{OH} &\\approx 83.910 \\text{ m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the\ncosine rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} \\text{PH}^2 &= \\text{OP}^2 + \\text{OH}^2 - 2(\\text{OP})(\\text{OH}) \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{P\\^{O}H}) \\\\[6pt]\n\\text{PH} &= \\sqrt{(214.45)^2 + (83.910)^2 - 2(214.45)(83.910)\\cos\\ang{75}} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{PH} \\approx 209 \\text{ m} \\hspace{15em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA216","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA216\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn a triangle ABC, $\\text{AB} = 6$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 10$ cm and\n$\\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\ang{70}$.\n\n1.  Find the area of the triangle. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\text{AC}$. [3 marks]\n\nGive your answers correct to $3$ significant figures.","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(6)(10)\\sin \\ang{70} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 28.2 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{6^2 + 10^2 - 2(6)(10)\\cos \\ang{70}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AC} \\approx 9.74 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{9.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA217","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA217\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nABCD is a quadrilateral where\n$\\text{AB} = 9$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 12$ cm,\n$\\text{CD} = 11$ cm, $\\text{\\(\\text{DA} = 8.5\\)\\hspace{0.25em}cm}$\nand $\\text{A\\^{B}C} = \\ang{90}$. Find $\\text{A\\^{D}C}$, giving your\nanswer correct to **the nearest degree**.","markScheme":"If we sketch quadrilateral ABCD, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/7201405f218b4c00163aa096cd77a2c58d3f16b6.svg\" alt=\"7201405f218b4c00163aa096cd77a2c58d3f16b6.svg\">\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1em} \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{9^2 + 12^2} \\\\[8pt]\n\\text{AC} &= 15 \\text{ cm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the cosine rule,\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.8em} \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AD}^2 + \\text{DC}^2 - 2(\\text{AD})(\\text{DC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{D}C})  \\\\[6pt]\n15^2 &= 8.5^2 + 11^2 - 2(8.5)(11)\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{D}C}) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{A\\^DC} \\approx \\ang{100} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{1em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA218","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA218\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Find the set of values of $h$ that satisfy the inequality\n    $2h^2 - 3h - 14 > 0$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  The triangle ABC is shown in the following diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/280703425496e906bb7cacafeebe346ebdda7cce.svg\" alt=\"280703425496e906bb7cacafeebe346ebdda7cce.svg\">\n\n    Given that $\\cos \\theta > 0.25$, find the range of possible values\n    of $a$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2h^2 - 3h - 14 &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    (2h-7)(h+2) &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n    h &= -2,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{7}{2} \\hspace{2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, if we make a sign table, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/634f8d4b56a7defcf1dea745f8a9eb0cd21e5df5.svg\" alt=\"634f8d4b56a7defcf1dea745f8a9eb0cd21e5df5.svg\">\n\n    Hence the set of values of $h$ satisfying $2h^2 - 3h - 14 > 0$ is\n     $\\textstyle (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (\\frac{7}{2},\\infty)$\n\n2.  Using the cosine rule, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{AC}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AC})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[12pt]\n    4^2 &= a^2 + 3^2 - 2(3)(a)\\cos\\theta \\\\[12pt]\n    16 &= a^2 + 9 - 6a\\cos\\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos\\theta &= \\dfrac{a^2 - 7}{6a} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\dfrac{a^2 - 7}{6a} &> \\dfrac{1}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\cos \\theta > 0.25$}]  \\\\[9pt]\n    2a^2 - 14 &> 3a \\\\[12pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} 2a^2 - 3a - 14 &> 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    a &> \\dfrac{7}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Since ABC is a triangle, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} \\text{BC} &< \\text{AB} + \\text{AC} \\\\[6pt]\n    a &< 4 + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    a &< 7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $\\dfrac{7}{2} < a < 7$"},{"ref":"AA219","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA219\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe triangle ABC is equilateral of side $5$ cm.\nThe point D lies on \\[BC\\] such that\n$\\text{DC} = 2$ cm. Find\n$\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{D}A})$.","markScheme":"If we sketch the equilateral triangle ABC, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/34344a6ec2ffed1263f1efd0cd9e6fde9a8a3c10.svg\" alt=\"34344a6ec2ffed1263f1efd0cd9e6fde9a8a3c10.svg\">\n\nUsing the cosine rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} \\text{AD}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BD}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BD})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[7pt]\n\\text{AD}^2 &= 5^2 + 3^2 - 2(5)(3)\\cos \\ang{60} \\\\[6pt]\n\\text{AD} &= \\sqrt{25 + 9 - 30(1/2)} \\\\[6pt]\n\\text{AD} &= \\sqrt{19} \\text{ cm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using the cosine rule, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6em} \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AD}^2 + \\text{DC}^2 - 2(\\text{AD})(\\text{DC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{D}A}) \\\\[13pt]\n5^2 &= (\\sqrt{19})^2 + 2^2 - 2(\\sqrt{19})(2)\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{D}A}) \\\\[13pt]\n25 &= 19 + 4 - 4\\sqrt{19}\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{D}A}) \\\\[9pt]\n\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{D}A}) &= \\dfrac{19 + 4 - 25}{4\\sqrt{19}} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{D}A}) = -\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{19}} \\hspace{9.15em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA221","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA221\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIn a triangle ABC, $\\text{AB} = 3$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 5$ cm and\n$\\text{A\\^{C}B} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\n1.  Find, to three significant figures, the two possible lengths of\n    \\[AC\\]. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Find the difference between the areas of the two possible triangles\n    ABC.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{AC}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AC})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[7pt]\n    3^2 &= \\text{AC}^2 + 5^2 - 2(\\text{AC})(5)\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n    9 &= \\text{AC}^2 + 25 - 5\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{AC}) \\\\[10pt]\n    0 &=  \\text{AC}^2 - 5\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{AC}) + 16 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5.65em} \\text{AC} \\approx 2.67\\text{ cm}, \\hspace{0.25em} 5.99\\text{ cm} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.4em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AC})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AC})(5)\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{4}(\\text{AC}) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{where $\\text{AC} \\approx 2.67,\\hspace{0.25em} 5.99$}] \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 3.34 \\text{ cm}^2, \\hspace{0.5em} 7.49 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the difference b/n the areas of the two possible triangles ABC\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\triangle A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= 7.49 - 3.34 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4.15 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA222","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA222\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nIn a triangle XYZ, $\\text{XY} = 9$ cm,\n$\\text{YZ} = x$ cm,\n$\\text{XZ} = y$ cm and\n$\\text{X\\^{Y}Z} = \\ang{45}$.\n\n1.  Using the cosine rule, show that\n    $x^2 - 9\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}x + 81 - y^2 = 0$. [2 marks]\n\nConsider the possible triangles with\n$\\text{XZ} = 7$ cm.\n\n2.  Calculate the two corresponding values of YZ. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Hence find the area of the smaller triangle. [3 marks]\n\nConsider the case where $y$, the length of \\[XZ\\], is not fixed at\n$7$ cm.\n\n4.  Find the range of values of $y$ for which it is possible to\n    form two triangles. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11em} \\text{XZ}^2 &= \\text{XY}^2 + \\text{YZ}^2 - 2(\\text{XY})(\\text{YZ})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{X\\^{Y}Z}) \\\\[7pt]\n    y^2 &= 9^2 + x^2 - 2(9)(x)\\cos \\ang{45} \\\\[6pt]\n    y^2 &= 81 + x^2 - 9\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}x \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    x^2 - 9\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}x + 81 - y^2 = 0  \\hspace{11.4em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substituting $y = 7$ in the equation $(1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^2 - 9\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}x + 81 - 7^2 &= 0 \\hspace{11.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    x^2 - 9\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}x + 32 &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{14em} x \\approx 3.45 \\text{ cm},\\hspace{0.5em} 9.28 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.5em} A_{\\triangle\\text{XYZ}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{XY})(\\text{YZ})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{X\\^{Y}Z}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(9)(3.45) \\sin \\ang{45} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 11.0 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The quadratic polynomial\n    $p(x) = x^2 - 9\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}x + 81 - y^2$ has two roots\n    when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &> 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\hspace{2.35em} (-9\\sqrt{2})^2 - 4(1)(81-y^2) &> 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac] \\\\[12pt]\n    4y^2 - 162 &> 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    y^2 &> \\dfrac{81}{2} \\\\[3pt]\n    y &> \\dfrac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $y > 0$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using Vieta's formulas, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{sum of roots} &= 9\\sqrt{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{product of roots} &= 81-y^2 \\hspace{1.1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence both roots of $p(x)$ are positive when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    81 - y^2 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &< 9 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence it is possible to form two triangles\n    when\n     $\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} < y < 9$"},{"ref":"AA223","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA223\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nIn a triangle ABC,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C}) - 6\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}A}) &= 7, \\\\[6pt]\n6\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}A}) - 5\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C}) &=  \\sqrt{2}. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A}) = \\dfrac{1}{6}$.\n    [6 marks]\n\nJames claims that CÂB can have two possible values.\n\n2.  Show that James is wrong by proving that CÂB has only one\n    possible value.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    25\\sin^2(\\text{A\\^BC}) - 60\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^CA}) + 36\\cos^2(\\text{B\\^CA}) &= 49 \\\\[6pt]\n    36\\sin^2(\\text{B\\^CA}) - 60\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^CA})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^BC}) + 25\\cos^2\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^BC}) &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the Pythagorean identity and compound angle identity,\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} 25 + 36 - 60\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A}) &= 51 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{sum of $(1)$ and $(2)$}] \\\\[10pt]\n    10 &= 60\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A}) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A}) =\\dfrac{1}{6} \\hspace{4.3em}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.85em} \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}B}) &= \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\ang{180} - (\\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A})) \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}B}) &= \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A}) \\\\[5pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}B}) &= \\dfrac{1}{6} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{C\\^{A}B} &= \\arcsin\\hspace{0.05em}(1/6), \\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{180} - \\arcsin\\hspace{0.05em}(1/6) \\\\[9pt]\n    \\text{C\\^{A}B} &\\approx \\ang{10}, \\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{170} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{two possible values}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    For $\\text{C\\^{A}B} = \\ang{170}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.3em} \\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A} &= \\ang{180} - \\text{C\\^{A}B} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A} &= \\ang{10} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{B\\^{C}A} &< \\ang{10}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} 7 &= 5\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C}) - 6\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}A}) \\\\[6pt]  &< 5 - 6\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &< 5 - 6\\cos \\ang{10} \\\\[6pt]\n    &< 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{contradiction!}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence there\n    can be only one possible value of CÂB and James is wrong."},{"ref":"AA224","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA224\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Expand and simplify $(1 + \\sqrt{3})^2$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  By writing $\\ang{75}$ as $\\ang{30} + \\ang{45}$, find the value of\n    $\\cos \\ang{75}$. [3 marks]\n\nThe following diagram shows the triangle ABC where\n$\\text{BC} = \\sqrt{6}$, $\\text{CA} = 2$ and\\\n$\\text{A\\^{C}B} = \\ang{75}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/eb9eb28a6c1d96f4f66bea5d31bd0d663c466f82.svg\" alt=\"eb9eb28a6c1d96f4f66bea5d31bd0d663c466f82.svg\">\n\n3.  Find AB in the form $a + \\sqrt{b}$ where\n    $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textstyle (1 + \\sqrt{3})^2 &= \\textstyle 1 + 2\\sqrt{3} + (\\sqrt{3})^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\textstyle 4 + 2\\sqrt{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the compound angle identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} \\cos \\ang{75} &= \\cos (\\ang{30} + \\ang{45}) \\\\[17pt]\n    &=  \\cos \\ang{30} \\cos \\ang{45} - \\sin \\ang{30}\\sin \\ang{45} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} \\text{AB}^2 &= \\text{AC}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AC})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[12pt]\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= \\textstyle 2^2 + (\\sqrt{6})^2 - 2(2)(\\sqrt{6})\\cos \\ang{75} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= 4 + 6 - 4\\sqrt{6}\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\bigg] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AB}^2 &= \\textstyle 10 - (6 - 2\\sqrt{3}) \\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{AB} &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{4 + 2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AB} = \\textstyle 1 + \\sqrt{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\hspace{1em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA237","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA237\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider a triangle with sides\n$\\text{AB} = 10$ mm and\n$\\text{AC} = 8$ mm. Given that the area of the\ntriangle is $24$ mm$^2$, find the possible\nvalues for the length of \\[BC\\].","markScheme":"Using the cosine rule, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6em} \\text{BC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{AC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n\\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{10^2 + 8^2 - 2(10)(8)\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}\\smash{\\raisebox{-0.05em}{$(\\text{B\\^{A}C})$}}} \\hspace{8.5em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the area of a triangle formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.55em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n24 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(10)(8) \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[9pt]\n\\text{B\\^{A}C} &\\approx \\ang{36.870},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{143.13} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{3em}{(2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, substituting $(2)$ in the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{BC} \\approx 6.00 \\text{ mm}, \\hspace{0.25em} 17.1 \\text{ mm} \\hspace{1em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA238","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA238\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn a triangle ABC, $\\text{AB} = 12$ cm and\n$\\text{AC} = 15$ cm. The area of the\ntriangle is $\\text{\\(80\\)\\hspace{0.25em}cm\\(^2\\)}$.\n\n1.  Find the two possible values for BÂC. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that BÂC is obtuse, find BC. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\hspace{3.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n    80 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(12)(15) \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.6em} \\text{B\\^{A}C} \\approx \\ang{62.7},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{117} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.2em} \\text{BC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{AC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{12^2 + 15^2 - 2(12)(15)\\cos\\ang{117}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{BC} \\approx 23.1 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{7.6em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA240","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA240\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC. Point D lies on \\[AC\\] so that\n\\[DB\\]\\\nbisects CB̂A. The area of the triangle ABC is\n$3$ cm$^2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/9bed4f14a5fbef1e1ee63e96d70de815c633ae41.svg\" alt=\"9bed4f14a5fbef1e1ee63e96d70de815c633ae41.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AB} = 2\\sqrt{7}$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = x$ cm, and\n$\\text{C\\^{B}D} = \\theta$, where $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{3}{4}$\n\nFind the value of $x$ in the form of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$ where $a$ and $b$\nare positive integers.","markScheme":"Using the Pythagorean identity, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta &= 1 \\\\[14pt]\n(3/4)^2 + \\cos^2 \\theta &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\\cos^2 \\theta &= 1 - \\dfrac{9}{16} \\hspace{2em} \\\\[7pt]\n\\cos^2 \\theta &= \\dfrac{7}{16} \\\\[4pt]\n\\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the area\nof a triangle formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.85em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[7pt]\n3 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{7})(x)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\theta) \\\\[10pt]\n3 &= (\\sqrt{7}x) (2\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta) \\\\[8pt]\n3 &= (2\\sqrt{7}x) \\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{4}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n3 &= \\dfrac{21x}{8} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{8}{7} \\hspace{4.35em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA245","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA245\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nIn a triangle ABC, $\\text{AB} = 4$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 10$ cm and\n$\\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\ang{105}$.\n\n1.  Find the area of the triangle ABC. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find AC. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(10) \\sin \\ang{105} \\\\[11pt]\n    &\\approx 19.3 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{4^2 + 10^2 - 2(4)(10)\\cos \\ang{105}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AC} \\approx 11.7 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{9.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA248","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA248\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Given that $\\theta = \\ang{18}$ satisfies the equation\n    $4\\sin^2\\theta + 2\\sin \\theta - 1 = 0$, find the value of\n    $\\sin \\ang{18}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the value of $\\cos \\ang{36}$. [3 marks]\n\nThe following diagram shows the triangle ABC where\n$\\text{AB} = 4$ cm,\n$\\text{BC} = 5$ cm and\n$\\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\ang{36}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/4bb160f97a0ceec9cc59915b7c9a8a2c358dd84c.svg\" alt=\"4bb160f97a0ceec9cc59915b7c9a8a2c358dd84c.svg\">\n\n3.  Find AC in the form $\\sqrt{a + b\\sqrt{5}}$ cm where\n    $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the quadratic formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.6em} \\sin \\ang{18} &= \\dfrac{-2+\\sqrt{2^2-4(4)(-1)}}{2(4)} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $\\sin \\ang{18} > 0$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-2 + \\sqrt{20}}{8} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Hence, using the double angle identity for cosine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\cos \\ang{36} &= \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\hspace{0.05em}(\\ang{18})) \\\\[19pt]\n    &= 1 - 2\\sin^2 \\ang{18} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 - 2\\bigg[\\dfrac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\bigg]^2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - 2\\bigg[\\dfrac{6-2\\sqrt{5}}{16}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\dfrac{3-\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the cosine rule, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[8pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{4^2 + 5^2 - 2(4)(5)\\cos \\ang{36}} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{16 + 25 - 40[(1+\\sqrt{5})/4]} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b)}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AC} = \\textstyle \\sqrt{31 - 10\\sqrt{5}} \\hspace{11.25em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA259","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA259\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/95a5a986a0e57f71e696c7f03164eaaf921eab87.svg\" alt=\"95a5a986a0e57f71e696c7f03164eaaf921eab87.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AC} = 14$ cm,\n$\\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\ang{115}$, $\\text{B\\^{A}C} = \\ang{38}$.\n\n1.  Find BC. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of the triangle ABC. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the sine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A})} &= \\dfrac{\\text{BC}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C})} \\hspace{2.65em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{14}{\\sin\\ang{115}} &= \\dfrac{\\text{BC}}{\\sin \\ang{38}} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\text{BC} &= \\dfrac{14\\sin \\ang{38}}{\\sin\\ang{115}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{BC} \\approx 9.51 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{0.7em}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.6em} \\text{A\\^{C}B} &= \\ang{180} - (\\text{C\\^{B}A} + \\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{180} - (\\ang{115} + \\ang{38}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{27}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the area of a triangle\n    formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AC})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(14)(9.51)\\sin \\ang{27} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 30.2 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA264","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA264\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/4ccf804b6fad9991f97515916077867f3bac6aa8.svg\" alt=\"4ccf804b6fad9991f97515916077867f3bac6aa8.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AB} = 12$ cm,\n$\\text{AC} = 16$ cm,\n$\\text{B\\^{A}C} = \\ang{34}$.\n\n1.  Find BC. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find CB̂A, given that it is obtuse. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the area of the triangle ABC. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\text{BC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{AC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{12^2 + 16^2 - 2(12)(16)\\cos \\ang{34}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.15em} \\text{BC} \\approx 9.04 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{3em} [\\approx 9.0360 \\text{ cm}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A})} &= \\dfrac{\\text{BC}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C})} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{16}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A})} &= \\dfrac{9.0360}{\\sin \\ang{34}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= \\dfrac{16\\sin \\ang{34}}{9.0360} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{C\\^{B}A} &\\approx \\ang{82.0}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $\\textbf{Note:}$ This calculation produces the solution $\\text{C\\^{B}A} \\approx \\ang{82.0}$, however we have been told that $\\text{C\\^{B}A}$ is obtuse. This is an ambiguous case of the sine rule. Given that $\\sin{\\theta} = \\sin{180^{\\circ}-\\theta}$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{C\\^{B}A} &\\approx 180^{\\circ}-82^{\\circ}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8.1em} \\text{C\\^{B}A} \\approx \\ang{98.0} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.9em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(12)(16)\\sin \\ang{34} \\\\[11pt]\n    &\\approx 53.7 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA269","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA269\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/75c0aac3e03cc79c60f3a1a4e03229068b9a63fc.svg\" alt=\"75c0aac3e03cc79c60f3a1a4e03229068b9a63fc.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AB} = 10$ cm,\n$\\text{AC} = 8$ cm, $\\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\ang{52}$.\n\n1.  Find AĈB, given that it is acute. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find BC. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the sine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\text{AB}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B})}  &= \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A})} \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{10}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B})} &= \\dfrac{8}{\\sin \\ang{52}} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) &= \\dfrac{10\\sin \\ang{52}}{8} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\text{A\\^{C}B} &= 80.0681...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.2em} \\text{A\\^{C}B}  = \\ang{80.1}\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{(3 s.f.)} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.9em} \\text{B\\^{A}C} &= \\ang{180} - (\\text{C\\^{B}A} + \\text{A\\^{C}B}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{180} - (\\ang{52} + \\ang{80.1}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{47.9}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the cosine rule, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.3em} \\text{BC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{AC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{10^2 + 8^2 - 2(10)(8)\\cos \\ang{47.9}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{BC} \\approx 7.53 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{.5em} [\\text{accept BC} =7.54 \\text{ cm} ]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA274","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA274\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/93335bfd8c3cbe4d6c4bbc0674016f78c5337789.svg\" alt=\"93335bfd8c3cbe4d6c4bbc0674016f78c5337789.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AC} = 10$ cm,\n$\\text{B\\^{A}C} = \\ang{35}$.\n\nThe area of the triangle ABC is $22$ cm$^2$.\n\n1.  Find AB. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find BC. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find CB̂A. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[7pt]\n    22 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(10)\\sin \\ang{35} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{22}{5\\sin \\ang{35}} &= \\text{AB} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.9em} \\text{AB} \\approx 7.67 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{3em} [\\approx 7.6712 \\text{ cm}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{BC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{AC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{(7.6712)^2 + 10^2 - 2(7.6712)(10)\\cos \\ang{35}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{BC} \\approx 5.76 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{3em} [\\approx 5.7593 \\text{ cm}] \\hspace{2.95em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Again using the cosine rule, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= \\dfrac{\\text{AB}^2+\\text{BC}^2-\\text{AC}^2}{2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})}\\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7.67^2 + 5.76^2 - 10^2}{2(7.67)(5.76)} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx -0.09047 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.6em} \\text{C\\^{B}A} \\approx \\ang{95.2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA275","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA275\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe following diagram shows three cities A, B and C.\n\nCity B is $70$ km from A, on a bearing of\n$\\ang{130}$. City C is $40$ km from City\nB, on bearing of $\\ang{75}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/5713f313b248309218955735d1649078b8a3cc98.svg\" alt=\"5713f313b248309218955735d1649078b8a3cc98.svg\">\n\n1.  Find CB̂A. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the distance from City A to C. [3 marks]\n\n3.  If you wanted to travel from city A directly to City C, find the\n    bearing you would need to travel.\n    [4 marks]\n\n4.  Find the area enclosed by connecting the three cities in a triangle\n    ABC. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.1em} \\text{C\\^{B}A} &= (\\ang{180}-\\ang{130}) + \\ang{75} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{125}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{70^2 + 40^2 - 2(70)(40)\\cos \\ang{125}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{AC} \\approx 98.5 \\text{ km} \\hspace{11.4em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sine rule, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.9em} \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C})}{\\text{BC}} &= \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A})}{\\text{AC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C})}{40} &= \\dfrac{\\sin \\ang{125}}{98.5} \\\\[12pt]\n    %\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) &= \\dfrac{40\\sin \\ang{125}}{98.5} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\text{B\\^{A}C} &\\approx \\ang{19.4196} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.45em} \\text{bearing} &= \\ang{130} - \\text{B\\^{A}C} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{130} - \\ang{19.4196} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx \\ang{111}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(70)(40)\\sin \\ang{125} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 1150 \\text{ km}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA288","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA288\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe following diagram shows quadrilateral ABCD.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/36057acad3a985fe731526cd66cbe2dc1378d991.svg\" alt=\"36057acad3a985fe731526cd66cbe2dc1378d991.svg\">\n\n$\\text{AB} = 12\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm},\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{AD} = 4\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm},\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{A\\^{C}B} = \\ang{85},\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\ang{38}$.\n\n1.  Find AC. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of triangle ABC. [4 marks]\n\nThe area of triangle ABC is three times bigger than the area of triangle\nACD.\n\n3.  Find the acute angle CÂD. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Find CD. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the sine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.2em} \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A})}{\\text{AC}} &= \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{C}B})}{\\text{AB}} \\hspace{5.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\sin \\ang{38}}{\\text{AC}} &= \\dfrac{\\sin\\ang{85}}{12} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\text{AC} &= \\dfrac{12\\sin\\ang{38}}{\\sin \\ang{85}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.4em} \\text{AC} \\approx 7.42 \\text{ cm}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} \\text{B\\^{A}C} &= \\ang{180} - (\\text{A\\^{C}B} + \\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{180} - (\\ang{85} + \\ang{38}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{57}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the area of a triangle\n    formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.9em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(12)(7.42)\\sin \\ang{57} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 37.3 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= 3A_{\\triangle\\text{ACD}} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AC})(\\text{AD})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}D})\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{2}(12)(7.42)\\sin \\ang{57} &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(7.42)(4)\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}D})\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n    \\sin \\ang{57} &= \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}D}) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{C\\^{A}D} = \\ang{57} \\hspace{2.6em}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{CD}^2 &= \\text{AC}^2 + \\text{AD}^2 - 2(\\text{AC})(\\text{AD})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}D}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{CD} &= \\sqrt{7.42^2 + 4^2 - 2(7.42)(4)\\cos \\ang{57}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{CD} \\approx 6.22 \\text{ cm} \\hspace{12em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA294","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA294\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows a cell tower AB\n$6.4$ m tall on the roof of a commercial\nbuilding. The angle of depression from A to a point C on the horizontal\nground is $\\ang{49}$. The angle of elevation of the top of the building\nfrom C is $\\ang{45}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/AA294.PNG\" alt=\"AA294\">\n\nFind the height of the building.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/7a8a205b1328ef343ffbd24e4ecabed6176ebc59.svg\" alt=\"7a8a205b1328ef343ffbd24e4ecabed6176ebc59.svg\">\n\nwhere:\n\n1\\.\n    $\\text{AB} = 6.4$ m,\n    $\\text{BD} = \\text{CD} = h$ m;\n\n2\\. $\\text{C\\^{A}D} = \\ang{41}$,\n    $\\text{C\\^{B}D} = \\ang{45}$,\n    $\\text{D\\^{C}A} = \\ang{49}$.\n\nHence, using the sine rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{D\\^{C}A})}{\\text{AB} + \\text{BD}} &= \\dfrac{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{A}D})}{\\text{CD}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{\\sin \\ang{49}}{6.4+h} &= \\dfrac{\\sin \\ang{41}}{h} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{13.05em} h \\approx 42.6 \\text{ m} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA297","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA297\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA triangle ABC has $a = 10.2$ cm,\n$b= 17.5$ cm and area\n$32$ cm$^2$. Find the largest possible perimeter\nof triangle ABC.","markScheme":"Using the area of a triangle formula, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8.4em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(a)(b)\\sin\\hspace{-0.05em}\\text{\\^{C}} \\\\[6pt]\n32 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(10.2)(17.5)\\sin\\hspace{-0.05em}\\text{\\^{C}} \\\\[10pt]\n\\text{\\^{C}} &\\approx \\ang{21.0},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{159} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using the cosine rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} c^2 &= a^2 + b^2 - 2(a)(b)\\cos\\hspace{-0.05em}\\text{\\^{C}} \\\\[6.5pt]\nc &= \\sqrt{(10.2)^2 + (17.5)^2 - 2(10.2)(17.5)\\cos\\hspace{-0.05em}\\smash{\\raisebox{-0.05em}{$\\text{\\^{C}}$}}} \\\\[8pt]\nc &\\approx 8.78\\text{ cm},\\hspace{0.25em} 27.3\\text{ cm} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $\\text{\\^{C}} \\approx \\ang{21.0},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{159}$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the (largest possible) perimeter of triangle ABC is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1em} P_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= a + b + c_{\\text{max}} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 10.2 + 17.5 + 27.3 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 55 \\text{ cm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA303","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA303\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nJack sets out for a horse ride from his family ranch at point A. He\nrides at an average speed of\n$6.3$ km$/$h for $28$ minutes, on a bearing of\n$\\ang{40}$ from the ranch, until he\nstops for a break near a spring at point B.\n\n1.  Find the distance from point A to point B. [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/895ff3d50c5e85f43580cba54d0b885fd3fc2664.svg\" alt=\"895ff3d50c5e85f43580cba54d0b885fd3fc2664.svg\">\n\nJack leaves point B on a bearing of $\\ang{112}$ and continues to ride\nfor a distance of\n$5.8$ km until he reaches a river at point C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/9719e0168f5f8056b390bc6270e68e32d772fab3.svg\" alt=\"9719e0168f5f8056b390bc6270e68e32d772fab3.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that AB̂C is $\\ang{108}$.\n\n    2.  Find the distance from point A to point C. [5 marks]\n\n3.  Find BĈA. [3 marks]\n\nJack's brother John wants to ride a horse directly from the ranch to\nmeet Jack at point C.\n\n4.  Find the bearing that John must take to point C.\n    [3 marks]\n\nJohn rides at an average speed of $12.9$ km$/$h.\n\n5.  Find, to the nearest minute, the time it takes for John to\n    reach point C. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula\n    \\\"$\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}$\\\", we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{AB} &= 6.3\\times\\dfrac{28}{60} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2.94 \\text{ km}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{A\\^{B}C} &= \\ang{40} + (\\ang{180} - \\ang{112}) \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\ang{108}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the cosine rule, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{AC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{BC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{BC})\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C}) \\\\[7pt]\n        \\text{AC} &= \\sqrt{(2.94)^2 + (5.8)^2 - 2(2.94)(5.8)\\cos \\ang{108}} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\text{AC} \\approx 7.27 \\text{ km} \\hspace{13.65em}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using the sine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\text{AB}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}A})} &= \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{A\\^{B}C})} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{2.94}{\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{C}A})} &= \\dfrac{7.27}{\\sin \\ang{108}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.6em} \\text{B\\^{C}A} \\approx \\ang{22.6} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.85em} \\text{C\\^{A}B} &= \\ang{180} - (\\text{A\\^{B}C} + \\text{B\\^{C}A}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{180} - (\\ang{108} + \\ang{22.6}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{49.4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{bearing} &= \\ang{40} + \\text{C\\^{A}B} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{40} + \\ang{49.4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ang{89.4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Using the formula \\\"$\\text{time} = \\text{distance}/\\text{speed}$\\\",\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.7em} \\text{time} &= \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7.27}{12.9} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.564 \\text{ hours} \\hspace{3em} [\\approx 0.564\\times 60 \\text{ minutes}] \\\\[15pt]\n    &\\approx 34 \\text{ minutes}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA653","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA653\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle ABC, with\n$\\text{AB}=7\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$,\n$\\text{AC}=5\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$,\n\n$\\ang{0} < \\text{B\\^{A}C} < \\ang{90}$ and\n$\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C})\\hspace{-0.05em} = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/04f131b9a7ba33f696cab0509a4218f2b57360a1.svg\" alt=\"04f131b9a7ba33f696cab0509a4218f2b57360a1.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the area of triangle ABC. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C})$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find BC. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(7)(5)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[\\dfrac{4}{5}\\bigg] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 14 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos^2(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) + \\sin^2(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\cos^2(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) + \\bigg[\\dfrac{4}{5}\\bigg]^2 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos^2(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) &= 1 - \\dfrac{16}{25} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) &= \\pm \\textstyle\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{25}} \\hspace{6em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.8em} \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) = \\dfrac{3}{5} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since B\\^{A}C is acute}]\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the cosine rule, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} \\text{BC}^2 &= \\text{AB}^2 + \\text{AC}^2 - 2(\\text{AB})(\\text{AC}) \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6.5pt]\n    \\text{BC}^2 &= 7^2 + 5^2 - 2(7)(5)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{BC}^2 &= 49 + 25 - 42 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\text{BC} &= \\sqrt{32} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{BC} = 4\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm} \\hspace{6.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA683","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA683\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows triangle PQR, with\n$\\text{PQ}=10\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$,\n$\\text{PR}=6\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$,\n\n$\\ang{0} < \\text{Q\\^{P}R} < \\ang{90}$ and\n$\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{Q\\^{P}R})\\hspace{-0.05em} = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Triangle%20Geometry/images/785f0c71e31141f8770621b1b3ca397532c130be.svg\" alt=\"785f0c71e31141f8770621b1b3ca397532c130be.svg\">\n\n1.  Find $\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{Q\\^{P}R})$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the area of triangle PQR. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find QR. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos^2(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) + \\sin^2(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\bigg[\\dfrac{4}{5}\\bigg]^2 + \\sin^2(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin^2(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) &= 1 - \\dfrac{16}{25} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) &= \\pm \\textstyle\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{25}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\therefore \\hspace{1em} &\\hspace{-4.7em}\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) = \\dfrac{3}{5} \\hspace{1.5em} [\\text{since Q\\^{P}R is acute}]\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\triangle\\text{PQR}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\text{PQ})(\\text{PR})\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(10)(6)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 18 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the cosine rule, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} \\text{QR}^2 &= \\text{PQ}^2 + \\text{PR}^2 - 2(\\text{PQ})(\\text{PR}) \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{Q\\^{P}R}) \\\\[6.5pt]\n    \\text{QR}^2 &= 10^2 + 6^2 - 2(10)(6)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[\\dfrac{4}{5}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{QR}^2 &= 100 + 36 - 96 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\text{QR} &= \\sqrt{40} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{QR} = 2\\sqrt{10} \\text{ cm} \\hspace{6.8em}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Trigonometric Functions & Graphs","questions":[{"ref":"AA1048","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1048\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows part of the graph of $f(x)=\\cos(bx)$ for $-p\\leq x \\leq 5p$ expressed in radians.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/question.png\" alt=\"question\">\n\n1. Find an expression for $p$ in terms of $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2. On the same axes, sketch the graph of $-3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{bx}{2}\\right)$ for $-2p\\leq x \\leq 10p$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the period formula for a cosine function, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\\\\[-12pt]\n        b &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[7pt]\n        %b &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6p} \\\\[7pt]\n        b &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{4p} \\\\[7pt]\n        %3pb &= \\pi \\\\[7pt]\n        & \\hspace{-0.49em}p = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2b} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. The graph of $-3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{bx}{2}\\right)$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ after the following transformations:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\\\\[-14pt]\n        \\text{transformation 1\\,:} & \\text{ horizontal stretch by a factor of 2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[3pt]\n        \\text{transformation 2\\,:} & \\text{ reflection in the $x$-axis} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[3pt]\n        \\text{transformation 3\\,:} & \\text{ vertical stretch by a factor of 3}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[-12pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, we get the following graph:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/answer.png\" alt=\"answer\">"},{"ref":"AA1154","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1154\n\n[Maximum marks: 10]\n\nThe function $f(x)=a\\sin{(bx)}+c$ where $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$ and $a,b,c \\in \\mathrm{R}$ is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1154.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1154\">\n\nThe coordinates of the first two stationary points are $(\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}, -1)$ and $(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2},3)$ respectively.\n\n1.  Find\n    1.  a.\n\n    2.  c.\n\n    3.  b. [5 marks]\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is restricted to $\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\leq x \\leq k$, where $k \\in \\mathrm{R}$. This restriction allows an inverse to exist.\n\n2.  Find the largest possible value of $k$. [2 marks]\n\nAnother function is defined as $g(x)=-2f(\\tfrac{1}{2}x)+3$.\n\n3.  Determine the coordinates of the __first__ maximum value of $g(x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  To find the value of $a$, which is known as the amplitude, we must find half of the vertical distance between the maximum and minimum values.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1154a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1154a\">\n\n        We can see from the diagram that the curve is a reflection of $\\sin{x}$ in the $x-$axis, therefore the value of $a$ must be negative.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            a&=-\\frac{3-(-1)}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}-2  \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  To find the  value of $c$ we must find the $y-$value half way between the maximum and minimum values.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1154b.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1154b\">\n\n        Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            c&=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{1}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  To find $b$ we use the formula $T=\\frac{2\\pi}{b}$.\n\n        We can use the diagram to find $T$ and see that $\\frac{T}{2}$ is the $x-$distance between the maximum and minimum values.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1154c%20%281%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1154c (1)\">\n\n        Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{T}{2}&=\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            T&=2\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            T&=\\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using this value of $T$ and the formula stated above we can find $b$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{2\\pi}{3}&=\\frac{2\\pi}{b}\\\\[6pt]\n            b&=\\hspace{0.525em}3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  For an inverse to exist the function must be one-to-one, this means it must pass the horizontal line test. We can see this is satisfied by the red part of $f(x)$  shown below\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1154inv.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1154inv\">\n\n    We can see that if we add one complete time period to $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$ we will get $k$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        k&=\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}+\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  First we split the vertical and horizontal transformations.\n\n    The inner function of $f$ is the horizontal transformations and everything else is vertical.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &-2f(\\tfrac{1}{2}x)+3\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\text{Vertical}& \\hspace{1em} & \\text{Horizontal}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\times -2 & \\hspace{1em} & \\div 2\\\\[6pt]\n        &+3 & \\hspace{1em} &\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Lets do the vertical transformations first. We don't need to transform the entire function. It is easier to identify a single point and transform that.\n\n    Notice that we are multiplying by a negative number, that means $g$ will be a vertical reflection of $f$.\n\n    Therefore the first maximum point of $g$ will be the image of the transformation of the first minimum point of $f$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we need to apply our transformations to the point $(\\tfrac{\\pi}{6},-1)$.\n\n    We transform the $y$-coordinate by $\\times -2$ and then $+3$, therefore the transformed $y$-coordinate is $-1\\times-2+3=5$.\n\n    We transform the $x$-coordinate by $\\times\\tfrac{1}{1/2}$.therefore the transformed $x$-coordinate is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\times2=\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n    Therefore $\\text{the first maximum of }g(x)\\text{ has coordinates }(\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}, 5).$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1309","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1309\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Consider the sine function $y=-4\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(x-2)\\right)}-3,\\,0\\leq{x}\\leq{8}$.\n\n    Write down\n\n    1. the amplitude[1 mark]\n\n    2. the period[1 mark]\n2.  Sketch the function on the axes below, labelling the coordinates of the first minimum.[3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/Screenshot%202026-10-20%20at%2012.13.01.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-10-20 at 12.13.01\">\n\n3.  Calculate the value of $x$ when $y=-3$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The general form for a sinusoidal function is $y=a\\sin{\\left(b(x-c)\\right)}+d$.\n    1. Written in this form, the amplitude is equal to $\\left|a\\right|$. Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{amplitude} &= \\left|-4\\right| \\\\[6pt]\n                         &= \\boxed{4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2. Written in this form, the period of a sine function is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{b}$. Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n                      &= \\boxed{4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n2.  Compare with the parent function $y=-\\sin{\\theta}$, whose first minimum is at $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},-1\\right)$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/Screenshot%202026-10-20%20at%2012.05.19.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-10-20 at 12.05.19\">\n\n    Two of the transformations in $y=a\\sin{\\left(b(x-c)\\right)}+d$ that affect the $x$-coordinates are $b$ and $c$. The factor $b$ is a horizontal stretch that multiplies all $x$-coordinates by its reciprocal, and $c$ is a horizontal translation that moves the entire graph $2$ units to the right.\n\n    Applying these transformations to the first minimum of the parent graph, we take the $x$-coordinate $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, multiply by $\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}$ and add $2$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\right)+2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n      &= 1+2 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= 3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This means the first minimum at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ has moved to $x=3$.\n\n    Substituting $x=3$ into the sinusoidal function to find the corresponding $y$-coordinate.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= -4\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(3-2)\\right)}-3 \\\\[6pt]\n      &= -4\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}-3 \\\\[6pt]\n      &= -4(1)-3 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= -7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The coordinates of the first minimum are $\\boxed{(3,-7)}$.\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/Screenshot%202026-10-20%20at%2012.08.01.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-10-20 at 12.08.01\">\n\n3.  Substituting $y=-3$ into the sinusoidal function, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -3 &= -4\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(x-2)\\right)}-3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n    0 &= -4\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(x-2)\\right)} \\\\[6pt]\n    0 &= \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(x-2)\\right)} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(x-2) &= \\arcsin{0} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since the function is sinusoidal, there is likely more than one $x$ value giving $y=-3$ on $0\\leq{x}\\leq{8}$. Finding several values of $\\arcsin{0}$ in each direction.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(x-2) &= -2\\pi,\\,-\\pi,\\,0,\\,\\pi,\\,2\\pi,\\,3\\pi,\\,4\\pi \\\\[6pt]\n    x-2 &= -4,\\,-2,\\,0,\\,2,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -2,\\,0,\\,2,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Rejecting any values outside the interval $0\\leq{x}\\leq{8}$, we have\n\n    $x = \\boxed{0,\\,2,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8}$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1329","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1329\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA ceiling fan with centre $A$ is located $5$ metres from a beam $[PQ]$.\n\nThis is shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1329q1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1329q1\">\n\n$C$ represents the tip of a blade on the fan where $AC=3$ metres.\n\nThe shortest distance from $C$ to $[PQ]$ is $h_C$, measured in metres.\n\n1.    Find\n\n    1.    the maximum value of $h_C$; \n\n    2.    the minimum value of $h_C$. [2 marks]\n\n$h_C$ can be modelled by the function $h_C(t)=a\\cos(bt)+c$ where $a$, $b$, $c\\,\\in\\mathbb{R}$ and $t$ is time in seconds.\n\n2.    Write down the value of\n\n    1.    $a$ \n\n    2.    $c$ [2 marks]\n\nThe fan rotates clockwise at a rate of $1$ rotation per second.\n\n3.    Write down the value of $b$. [1 mark]\n\nAt $t=0$ the value of $h_C$ is at a maximum value.\n\n4.    Sketch the graph of $h_C(t)$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1$ on the given axis. [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1329q2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1329q2\">\n\n5.    Show that $\\sin4\\theta=2\\sin2\\theta\\cos2\\theta$. [1 mark]\n\nAnother ceiling fan, centred at $B$, is installed on the other side of $[PQ]$ and $D$ represents the tip of a blade on fan $B$.\n\nThe shortest distance from $D$ to $[PQ]$ is $h_D(t)$, measured in metres. It can be modelled by the function $h_D(t)=-\\sqrt\n\n3\\sin(4\\pi t)+5$ for $0\\leq t\\leq1$.\n\n6.    Find the values of $t$ when $h_C(t)=h_D(t)$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We know that point $A$ is $5$ metres from $[PQ]$.\n\n    We know that the blade on fan $A$ is $3$ metres long and that to be at a maximum (or minimum) the blade ($[AC]$) must be perpendicular to $[PQ]$.\n\n    1. Adding values to the diagram for the maximum distance\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1329a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1329a1\">\n\n        we see that\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        h_\\text{C\\,max}&=\\textcolor{red}{3}+\\textcolor{green}{5}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=8\\text{ metres}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. Showing values when $C$ is the minimum distance from $[PQ]$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1329a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1329a2\">\n\n        we obtain\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        h_\\text{C\\,min}&=\\textcolor{green}{5}-\\textcolor{red}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=2\\text{ metres}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2.  We can use the information from part (a) to solve for $a$ and $c$.\n\n    1. The amplitude, $a$, is found using the formula \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a&=\\frac{\\text{maximum}-\\text{minimum}}{2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{8-2}{2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=3\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. The vertical shift of the cosine curve, $c$, can be found with the formula\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        c&=\\frac{\\text{maximum}+\\text{minimum}}{2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{8+2}{2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=5\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n3. To find $b$ for a sine or cosine function we can use the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{period}=\\frac{2\\pi}{b}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The period is the length of time it takes for the fan to complete a single rotation.\n\n    We are told that fan $A$ rotates at a rate of $1$ rotation per second.\n\n    Substituting this into the formula we can solve for $b$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    1&=\\frac{2\\pi}{b}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    b&=2\\pi\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4. Using the information from previous parts we can construct the following diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1329a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1329a3\">\n\n    For both marks our diagram should show\n\n    $\\text{Correct position of maximum and minimum points}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $1\\text{ complete rotation of cosine for }0\\leq x\\leq1$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n5. To show that $\\sin(4\\theta)=2\\sin(2\\theta)\\cos(2\\theta)$ we can use the double angle identity.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\sin(4\\theta)&=\\sin(2(2\\theta))\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=2\\sin(2\\theta)\\cos(2\\theta)\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n6. We are told that $h_C(t)=h_D(t)$ so we can use functions, $h_C$ and $h_D$, to form an equation.\n\n    Using our values for $a$, $b$, and $c$ we have $h_C(t)=3\\cos(2\\pi t)+5$.\n\n    \n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    h_C(t)&=h_D(t)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    3\\cos(2\\pi t)+5&=-\\sqrt{3}\\sin(4\\pi t)+5\\\\[6pt]\n\n    3\\cos(2\\pi t)&=-\\sqrt{3}\\sin(4\\pi t)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Notice the angle measures are different.\n\n    However, we can use the relationship from part (e) to rewrite the angle measure of $4\\theta$ in terms of $2\\theta$.\n\n    Hence we can rewrite the R.H.S.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3\\cos(2\\pi t)&=-2\\sqrt{3}\\sin(2\\pi t)\\cos(2\\pi t)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We don't want to divide by $\\cos(2\\pi t)$ as there are values of $t$ where this expression is $0$ and we cannot divide by $0$.\n\n    Instead, let's rearrange the equation so that it equals $0$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3\\cos(2\\pi t)+2\\sqrt{3}\\sin(2\\pi t)\\cos(2\\pi t)&=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can factorise the common term of $\\cos(2\\pi t)$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    [\\cos(2\\pi t)][3+2\\sqrt{3}\\sin(2\\pi t)]&=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By the null factor theorem we can set each factor equal to $0$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\cos(2\\pi t)&=0&&3+2\\sqrt{3}\\sin(2\\pi t)=0\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    First let's work with the equation on the left.\n\n    We know that $\\cos\\theta=0$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\,,\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\pi t&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}&&2\\pi t=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    t_1&=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\text{ s}&&t_2=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\text{ s}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now let's consider the equation on the right. We can start by rearranging it.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\sqrt{3}\\sin(2\\pi t)&=-3\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\sin(2\\pi t)&=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    At first glance this doesn't look like a familiar trigonometric ratio but let's multiply by $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}$ to remove the root from the denominator.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\sin(2\\pi t)&=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    After simplifying, this now this looks more familiar.\n\n    We know that the $\\textcolor{green}{\\text{principal}}$ angle for $\\sin{\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n    Using a sketch (or the unit circle) of the sine curve we can identify the solutions for when $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/AA1329a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1329a4\">\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\pi t&=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}&&2\\pi t=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    t_3&=\\dfrac{2}{3}\\text{ s}&&t_4=\\dfrac{5}{6}\\text{ s}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA230","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA230\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the curve\n$y = a\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(k(x - d)) + c$ where\n$a, k, d$ and $c$ are all positive constants. The\ncurve has a minimum point at $(1.5,2)$ and\na $\\text{maximum}$ point at $(3.5,7)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/3729611ba3a439e48776fd9a8da5e25aa14a08a8.svg\" alt=\"3729611ba3a439e48776fd9a8da5e25aa14a08a8.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $a$ and the value of $c$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $k$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the smallest possible value of $d$, given $d > 0$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} a &= \\dfrac{y_{\\text{max}} - y_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7-2}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} c &= \\dfrac{y_{\\text{max}} + y_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7+2}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 4.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The period of the curve is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\text{period} &= 2(3.5 - 1.5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} k &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Substituting $x = 3.5$ & $y = 7$ in the equation\n    $y = a\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(k(x-d))+c$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} y &= 2.5\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi/2)(x - d)) + 4.5 \\\\[12pt]\n    7 &= 2.5\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi/2)(3.5 - d)) + 4.5 \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi/2)(3.5 - d)) &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(3.5 - d) &= 2n\\pi \\\\[7pt]\n    d &= 3.5 - 4n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the smallest possible (positive) value of $d$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} d &= 3.5 - 4(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA231","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA231\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA function is defined by $f(x) = a\\cos(kx) + c$, for\n$-\\pi \\leq x \\leq \\pi$, where $k > 0$.\n\nPart of the graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown on the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/332ebe7e7e1639f2dc5953bac5a0772eedc61176.svg\" alt=\"332ebe7e7e1639f2dc5953bac5a0772eedc61176.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of:\n    1.  $a$;\n\n    2.  $k$;\n    3.  $c$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Solve $f(x) = 2$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Since the curve has a minimum point at $(0,-1)$, we know\n        $a < 0$.\n\n        Hence we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.6em} a &= -\\bigg[\\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\bigg[\\dfrac{3-(-1)}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The period of the curve is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= 2\\Big[\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - 0\\Big] \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        k &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2\\pi\\hspace{-0.15em}/\\hspace{-0.05em}3} \\hspace{1.45em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.8em} c &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} + f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3+(-1)}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 2$ for $x \\in [0,\\pi]$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} -2\\cos 3x + 1 &= 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\cos 3x &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    3x &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi,\\hspace{0.4em}  \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= \\dfrac{2\\pi + 6n\\pi}{9},\\hspace{0.4em}  \\dfrac{4\\pi+6n\\pi}{9} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.4em} x = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{9}, \\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{4\\pi}{9},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{8\\pi}{9} \\hspace{3.4em} [\\text{since $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA234","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA234\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nLet $g(x) = -4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $h(x) = 5\\cos x - 1$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $(h\\circ g)(x) = 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 1$.\n     [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the range of $h\\circ g$.  [2 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $(h\\circ g)\\Big(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}\\Big) = 4$, find the next\n    value of $x$, greater than $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}$,\n\n    for which $(h\\circ g)(x)=4$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  The graph of $y = (h\\hspace{0.075em}\\circ\\hspace{0.075em}g)(x)$ can\n    be obtained by applying five $\\text{transformations}$ to the\n    graph of $y = \\cos x$. State what the five transformations represent\n    $\\text{geometrically}$ and give the order in which they are\n    applied. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (h\\circ g)(x) &= h(g(x)) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= h\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.25em} -1 &\\leq \\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\leq 1 \\\\[3pt]\n    -5 &\\leq 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\leq 5 \\\\[3pt]\n    -6 &\\leq 5\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left(-4x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-1 \\leq 4 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the range of $h\\circ g$ is\n     $[-6,4]$\n\n3.  The period of the function $h\\circ g$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{|-4|} \\hspace{1.05em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the next value of $x$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= \\dfrac{5\\pi}{12} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{compression parallel to the $x$-axis by a factor of $1/4$} \\hspace{1.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{reflection in the $y$-axis} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $3$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{stretch parallel to the $y$-axis by a factor of $5$} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $4$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{horizontal translation $\\pi/12$ units to the left} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{transformation $5$}:& \\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{vertical translation $1$ unit down}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA247","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA247\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the curve\n$y = a\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(k(x - d)) + c$ where\n$a, k, d$ and $c$ are all positive constants. The\ncurve has a minimum point at $(1.5,1)$ and\na $\\text{maximum}$ point at $(3,7)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/7052ffd5de84afb1800aac020deb2608b5dacd1d.svg\" alt=\"7052ffd5de84afb1800aac020deb2608b5dacd1d.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $a$ and the value of $c$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $k$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the smallest possible value of $d$, given $d > 0$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} a &= \\dfrac{y_{\\text{max}} - y_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7-1}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} c &= \\dfrac{y_{\\text{max}} + y_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7+1}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The period of the curve is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.7em} \\text{period} &= 2(3 - 1.5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} k &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Substituting $x = 3$ & $y = 7$ in the equation\n    $y = a\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(k(x-d))+c$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} y &= 3\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((2\\pi/3)(x - d)) + 4 \\\\[13pt]\n    7 &= 3\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((2\\pi/3)(3 - d)) + 4 \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[13pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((2\\pi/3)(3 - d)) &= 1 \\\\[9pt]\n    \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}(3 - d) &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi \\\\[5pt]\n    3 - d &= \\dfrac{3}{4} + 3n \\\\[10pt]\n    d &= 2.25 - 3n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the smallest possible (positive) value of $d$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.6em} d &= 2.25 - 3(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2.25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA254","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA254\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = a\\sin bx$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The following diagram\nshows part of the graph of $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/906d6d1d20c7e7bd0c948dab43e1239ca11f38db.svg\" alt=\"906d6d1d20c7e7bd0c948dab43e1239ca11f38db.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the amplitude of $f$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $a$. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Write down the period of $f$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $b$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $\\text{amplitude} = 3$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        a &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3-(-3)}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $\\text{period} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        b &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi\\hspace{-0.075em}/2} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA256","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA256\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = a\\cos bx$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $b > 0$.\n\nPart of the graph of $f$ is shown on the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/efa784ef04c29864a818e047d0aa0869e63c18b0.svg\" alt=\"efa784ef04c29864a818e047d0aa0869e63c18b0.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find:\n    1.  the period of $f$;\n\n    2.  the value of $b$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Since the graph of $f$ has a minimum point at $(0,-2)$, we know\n    $a < 0$.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} a &= -\\bigg[\\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\bigg[\\dfrac{2-(-2)}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The period of $f$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\hspace{4.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\pi\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        b &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA258","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA258\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = a\\cos bx + d$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $b > 0$.\n\nPart of the graph of $f$ is shown on the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/bbd6f0daac341d4d5a3032afd8b59e2c8111e4ad.svg\" alt=\"bbd6f0daac341d4d5a3032afd8b59e2c8111e4ad.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Write down the period of $f$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $d$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} a &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4-(-2)}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $\\text{period} = \\pi$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        b &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} d &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} + f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4+(-2)}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA265","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA265\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe height, $h$ metres, of a seat on a Ferris wheel after $t$ minutes is\ngiven by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(t) &= -23.5\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(0.4t) + 25, \\hspace{0.25em} \\text{for} \\hspace{0.4em} t \\geq 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Find the initial height of the seat. [2 marks]\n\nOnce a passenger's seat is more than $30$ m\nabove the ground, there are no trees in view and\nthey can take unobstructed photographs of a nearby city.\n\n2.  Given that passengers only complete one rotation on the\n    Ferris wheel, $\\text{calculate}$ how\n    long they can take unobstructed photographs of the\n    nearby city. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Evaluating $h(t)$ for $t = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} h(0) &= -23.5\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(0) + 25 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -23.5(1) + 25 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1.5 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the graph of $y = h(t)$ for $0 \\leq t \\leq 5\\pi$ \\[by\n    using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/7704e8c4b43167e1de2ba1b2edf0c396041c3afd.svg\" alt=\"7704e8c4b43167e1de2ba1b2edf0c396041c3afd.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the $t$-coordinates of points A and B, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    t_{\\text{unobstructed}} &= 11.2450-4.4630 \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6.7820 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6.78 \\text{ min}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA270","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA270\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $f(x) = 3\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi x)+1$.\n\n1.  Write down the amplitude of $f$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the period of $f$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  On the following grid, sketch the graph of $f$, for\n    $0 \\leq x \\leq 4$. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/23829a6d409ec126b334226dd1cafe7918c8984a.svg\" alt=\"23829a6d409ec126b334226dd1cafe7918c8984a.svg\">","markScheme":"1.\n    $a = 3$\n\n2.  The period of $f$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{period} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi} \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 4$, on the\n    grid below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/787cbf0d432b923e16dea50bdcac8f9011ce25c0.svg\" alt=\"787cbf0d432b923e16dea50bdcac8f9011ce25c0.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA271","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA271\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = p\\sin(qx) + r$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nPart of the graph of $f$ is shown on the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/e58978d4cb9da418d75b3e727bb726aa22d90f25.svg\" alt=\"e58978d4cb9da418d75b3e727bb726aa22d90f25.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ has a minimum at A$(3,-1)$ and a maximum at B$(9,5)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find the period of $f$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the values of:\n    1.  $p$;\n\n    2.  $r$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Solve $f(x) = 4$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 15$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The period of $f$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= 2(9-3) \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 12\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        q &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{12} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Since the graph of $f$ has a minimum point at $(3,-1)$, we know\n        $p < 0$.\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.1em} p &= -\\bigg[\\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\bigg[\\dfrac{5-(-1)}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.3em}  r &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} + f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5+(-1)}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 4$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 15$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -3\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{6}\\Big) + 2 &= 4 \\hspace{6.9em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{14em} x \\approx 7.39, \\hspace{0.25em}10.6 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA276","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA276\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big) + q$. The\ngraph of $f$ passes through the point $\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},5\\Big)$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the maximum value of $f$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = \\sin x$. The graph of $g$ is translated to the graph of $f$\nby\n$\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\n3.  Write down the values of $a$ and $b$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(\\pi/3) &= 5 \\hspace{6.8em} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big) + q &= 5 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\Big) + q &= 5 \\\\[9pt]\n    1 + q &= 5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    q = 4 \\hspace{0.2em}\n    $$\n\n2.  The maximum occurs when $\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(x+\\pi/6) = 1$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f_{\\text{max}} &= 1 + 4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} f(x) &= \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big) + 4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= g(x + \\pi/6) + 4 \\\\[10pt]\n    &= g(x - (-\\pi/6)) + 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n     $a = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$\n    and  $b = 4$"},{"ref":"AA277","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA277\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe Singapore Flyer is a giant observation wheel in Singapore with\ndiameter of $150$ metres. The wheel rotates at a\nconstant speed and completes one rotation in $32$\nminutes. The bottom of the wheel is $d$ metres above the ground.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/cd3a2b0940f065b11cf681392f0c1e722b522460.svg\" alt=\"cd3a2b0940f065b11cf681392f0c1e722b522460.svg\">\n\nA seat starts at the bottom of the wheel.\n\n1.  After $16$ minutes, the seat is $165$ metres above the ground. Find\n    $d$. [2 marks]\n\nAfter $t$ minutes, the height of the seat above the ground is given by\n\n$$\nh(t) = 90 + a\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}\\hspace{0.05em}t\\Big), \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{for}\\hspace{0.65em} 0\\leq t \\leq 64.\n$$\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find when the seat is $60$ metres above the ground for the\n    third time. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The wheel completes $1/2$ a rotation in $16$ minutes.\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    150+d &= 165 \\hspace{2em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.35em} d = 15 \\text{ m}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(0) &= 15 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    90 + a &= 15 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.9em} a = -75\n    $$\n\n3.  If we draw the graph of $y = h(t)$ for $0 \\leq t \\leq 64$\n    \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/fb7adc5561dbacfc841595d13e8910816224a82b.svg\" alt=\"fb7adc5561dbacfc841595d13e8910816224a82b.svg\">\n\n    Hence the seat is $60$ m above the ground\n    for the $3$rd time when\n     $t = 37.9 \\text{ min}$"},{"ref":"AA281","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA281\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nLet $f(x) = a\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(k(x-d)) + c$, for $1 \\leq x \\leq 7$.\n\nThe graph of $f$ is shown on the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/281a6e2c15162a8657e750c0b5180c5816112269.svg\" alt=\"281a6e2c15162a8657e750c0b5180c5816112269.svg\">\n\nThe graph of $f$ has a maximum at A$(2,5)$ and a minimum at B$(4,1)$.\n\n1.  Find the value of:\n    1.  $a$;\n\n    2.  $c$. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the value of $k$.\n\n    2.  Find the smallest possible value of $d$, given $d > 0$.\n        [4 marks]\n\nThe graph of $f$ is translated vertically by $b$ units to give the graph of a function $g$ such that $g(x) = 3$ has only one solution in the given domain.\n\n3.  Find the value of $b$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        a &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5-1}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        c &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} + f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5+1}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The period of $f$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= 2(4-2) \\hspace{4.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        k &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{4} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the coordinates of A, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(2) &= 5 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\hspace{1em} 2\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2)(2-d)) + 3 &= 5 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2)(2-d)) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}(2-d) &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi \\hspace{2.05em} [\\sin^{-1} (1) = \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2] \\\\[6pt]\n        2 - d &= 1 + 4n \\\\[10pt]\n        d &= 1 - 4n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the smallest possible (positive) value of $d$ is\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} d &= 1-4(0) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  If we sketch the graphs of $f$ and $g$ on the same set of axes, we\n    have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/731a1c7f3f185db0dcd4bfe3c99dc9bd68b0d00d.svg\" alt=\"731a1c7f3f185db0dcd4bfe3c99dc9bd68b0d00d.svg\">\n\n    Hence we find\n     $b = 2$"},{"ref":"AA282","marks":"13","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA282\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nLet\n$f(x) = \\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}x\\Big) + \\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}x\\Big)$,\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 8$.\n\n1.  Find the values of $x$ where $f$ has a positive rate of change.\n    [4 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ can be written in the form\n$f(x) = a\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}(x - d)\\Big)$ where\n$6 \\leq d \\leq 9$.\n\n2.  Find the value of:\n    1.  $a$;\n\n    2.  $d$. [5 marks]\n\n3.  Solve $f(x) = 1$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 8$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graph of $y = f(x)$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 8$\n    \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/9fe0dd0e572db3dc3a194ed91d5987e07db745d4.svg\" alt=\"9fe0dd0e572db3dc3a194ed91d5987e07db745d4.svg\">\n\nHence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has positive gradient (or $f$ has positive\nrate of change)\n\nwhen\n $0 \\leq x < 0.75$\nand  $3.75 < x < 6.75$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the $y$-coordinates of A and B, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} a &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} - f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\\\[4pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1.41-(-1.41)}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 1.41\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the coordinates of A, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(0.75) &= 1.41 \\\\[9pt]\n        1.41\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/3)(0.75 - d)) &= 1.41 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/3)(0.75 - d)) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}(0.75 - d) &= 2n\\pi \\\\[6pt]\n        0.75 - d &= 6n \\\\[10pt]\n        d &= 0.75 - 6n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}] \\hspace{4.1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        d &= 0.75 - 6(-1) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 6.75\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $f(x) = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 8$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1.41\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/3)(x - 6.75)) &= 1 \\hspace{13.1em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{12.95em} x = 0,\\hspace{0.25em} 1.5,\\hspace{0.25em} 6,\\hspace{0.25em} 7.5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA292","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA292\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = \\sin x$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $x$\nis in radians.\n\n1.  Write down:\n    1.  the maximum value of $f$;\n\n    2.  the smallest positive value of $x$ for which the maximum of $f$\n        occurs. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = 2\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\Big)$, for\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $x$ is in radians.\n\n2.\n    1.  Determine the two transformations the graph of $f$ undergoes to\n        form the graph of $g$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the maximum value of $g$ and the smallest positive\n        value of $x$ for which this maximum occurs. [4 marks]\n\nLet\n$h(x) = \\dfrac{4}{2\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\big(x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\big)-3}$,\nfor $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $x$ is in radians.\n\n3.  Determine if the graph of $h$ has a vertical asymptote.\n    Justify your answer. [4 marks]\n\n4.  Find the range of $h$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $f_{\\text{max}} = 1$\n\n    2.\n        $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} g(x) &= 2\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2f(x + \\pi/4) \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 2f(x - (-\\pi/4))\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the two transformations\n        are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.75em} \\text{transformation $1$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical stretch by a factor of $2$} \\\\[8pt]\n        \\text{transformation $2$}: &\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{translation by the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence the maximum value of $g$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        g_{\\text{max}} &= 2f_{\\text{max}} \\hspace{1.85em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2(1) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        and this\n        maximum occurs when\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{denominator of $h(x)$} &=  2\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\Big)-3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\leq 2(1) - 3 \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\leq -1 \\hspace{3.05em} [\\text{for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{denominator of $h(x)$} \\neq 0 \\hspace{3.8em} [\\text{for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$}] \\\\[2pt]\n    $$\n\n    $\\text{Hence the graph of \\(h\\) has no vertical asymptotes.}$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.8em} h_{\\text{min}} &\\leq h(x) \\leq h_{\\text{max}} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{4}{2(1)-3} &\\leq h(x) \\leq \\dfrac{4}{2(-1)-3} \\\\[6pt]\n    -4 &\\leq h(x) \\leq -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the range of $h$ is\n     $[-4,-4/5]$"},{"ref":"AA298","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA298\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nThe London Eye is an observation wheel in England with diameter of $120$\nmetres. The wheel rotates at a constant speed and completes $2.5$\nrotations every hour. The bottom of the wheel is $15$ metres above the\nground.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/c6172973a9b2e90bb590b73a948d81d6dd74a43f.svg\" alt=\"c6172973a9b2e90bb590b73a948d81d6dd74a43f.svg\">\n\nA seat starts at the bottom of the wheel.\n\n1.  Find the maximum height above the ground of the seat.[2 marks]\n\nAfter $t$ minutes, the height $h$ metres above the ground of the seat is\ngiven by\n\n$$\nh(t) = 75 + a\\cos (b\\hspace{0.01em}t), \\hspace{0.25em} b > 0.\n$$\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that the period of $h$ is $24$ minutes.\n\n    2.  Write down the **exact** value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $a$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Sketch the graph of $h$, for $0 \\leq t \\leq 48$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n5.  In one rotation of the wheel, find the probability that a\n    randomly selected seat is at least $110$ metres above the ground.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.2em} h_{\\text{max}} &= h_{\\text{min}} + 120 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 15 + 120 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 135 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The period of $h$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= \\dfrac{60}{2.5} \\hspace{4.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 24 \\text{ minutes}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        b &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{24} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(0) &= 15 \\hspace{6.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    75 + a\\cos (0) &= 15 \\\\[6pt]\n    75 + a(1) &= 15 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $$\n     a = - 60 \\hspace{0.8em}\n     $$\n\n4.  If we sketch the graph of $y = h(t)$ for $0 \\leq t \\leq 48$\n    \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/10da9121704edbb1872be3c6e29a562b67474a27.svg\" alt=\"10da9121704edbb1872be3c6e29a562b67474a27.svg\">\n\n5.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/0cbd6c39865885e30c0daf23446059842b7dea86.svg\" alt=\"0cbd6c39865885e30c0daf23446059842b7dea86.svg\">\n\n    Hence $h(t) \\geq 110$ when $8.379 \\leq t \\leq 15.62$.\n    Therefore we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{seat} \\geq 110 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}) &= \\dfrac{15.62 - 8.379}{24} \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.302\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA304","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA304\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA function, $f$, is defined by\n$f(x)= 6.2\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}(x-7.5)\\Big)\\hspace{-0.15em}+c$,\nfor $0\\leq x \\leq 15$,\n\nwhere $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find the period of $f$. [2 marks]\n\nThe function $f$ has a minimum at A$(3,11.8)$ and a maximum at\nB$(12,24.2)$.\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the value of $c$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the value of $f(9)$. [4 marks]\n\nA second function, $g$, is defined by\n$g(x) = a\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{15}(x+2.25)\\Big)\\hspace{-0.15em}+b$,\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 15$,\n\nwhere $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe function $g$ passes through the points P$(1.5,14.5)$ and\nQ$(14,10.2)$.\n\n3.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Find the value of $x$ for which the functions have the\n    greatest difference. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\dfrac{\\pi}{9} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\text{period}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\text{period} = 18 \\hspace{1.95em}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        c &= \\dfrac{f_{\\text{max}} + f_{\\text{min}}}{2} \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{24.2 + 11.8}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 18\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $f(x)$ for $x = 9$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.6em} f(9) &= 6.2\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/9)(9-7.5)) + 18 \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 21.1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using the coordinates of points P and Q, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(1.5) &= 14.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    g(14) &= 10.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    or\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((2\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/15)(1.5+2.25)) + b &= 14.5  \\\\[6pt]\n    a\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((2\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/15)(\\hspace{0.135em}14\\hspace{0.135em}+2.25)) + b &= 10.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$\n    and $b$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{12em} a = 8.6 \\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.525em} b = 5.9 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.25em} h(x) &= |f(x) - g(x)| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= |(6.2\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/9)(x-7.5)) + 18) - (8.6\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}((2\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/15)(x+2.25)) + 5.9)| \\\\[1pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we sketch the graph of $y = h(x)$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 15$ \\[by\n    using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/8c38b2efba89a030c57601dc7e7f553e8facba37.svg\" alt=\"8c38b2efba89a030c57601dc7e7f553e8facba37.svg\">\n\n    Hence the (absolute) maximum value of $h$ occurs when\n     $x \\approx 9.96$"},{"ref":"AA669","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA669\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nLet $f(x) = 3\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2x) + 5$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe range of $f$ is $p \\leq f(x) \\leq q$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$ and the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = 4\\hspace{0.05em}f(3x)$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2.  Find the range of $g$. [3 marks]\n\nThe function $g$ can be written in the form\n$g(x) = 12\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(kx) + c$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Find the value of $k$ and the value of $c$.\n\n    2.  Find the period of $g$. [5 marks]\n\nThe equation $g(x) = 10$ has two solutions for\n$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\leq x \\leq \\pi$.\n\n4.  Find both solutions. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The min and max values of cos($2x$) are $-1$ and $+1$. Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.7em} p &= f_\\text{min}\\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(-1)+5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} q &= f_\\text{max} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(+1)+5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g_\\text{min} &= 4\\hspace{0.05em}f_\\text{min} = 4\\hspace{0.05em}(2) = 8\\\\[10pt]\n    g_\\text{max} &= 4\\hspace{0.05em}f_\\text{max} = 4\\hspace{0.05em}(8) = 32\\\\[10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the range of $g$ is\n     $8 \\leq g(x) \\leq 32$\n\n3.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} g(x) &= 4\\hspace{0.05em}f(3x) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 4\\hspace{0.05em}[3\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(2(3x)) + 5] \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 12\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(6x) + 20\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we obtain\n         $k = 6$\n        and  $c = 20$\n\n    2.  The period of $g$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{period} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{k} = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} \\hspace{0.975em}= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\hspace{5em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  Solving the equation $g(x) = 10$ for\n    $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\leq x \\leq \\pi$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    12\\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(6x) + 20 &= 10 \\hspace{8.9em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13em} x \\approx 2.52,\\hspace{0.25em}2.72 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA809","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA809\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe following diagram shows a ball attached to the end of a spring,\nwhich is suspended from a ceiling.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/e7dfdc0e552808c20b66447ca8a440bb4c1183bf.svg\" alt=\"e7dfdc0e552808c20b66447ca8a440bb4c1183bf.svg\">\n\nThe height, $h$ metres of the ball above the ground at time $t$ seconds\nafter being released can be modelled by the function\n$h(t)=0.5\\cos(\\pi t)+2.2$, where $t\\geq0$.\n\n1.  Find the height of the ball above the ground when it is released.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the minimum height of the ball above the ground.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Show that the ball takes $2$ seconds to return to its initial height\n    above the ground for the first time. [2 marks]\n\n4.  For the first $2$ seconds of its motion, determine the amount of the\n    time that the ball is less than $2.2+0.25\\sqrt{2}$ meters above the\n    ground. [5 marks]\n\n5.  Find the rate of change of the ball's height above the ground when\n    $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$.\\\n    Give your answer in the form $p\\pi\\sqrt{q} \\text{ ms}^{-1}$, where\n    $p\\in \\mathbb{Q}$ and $q \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}$[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the fact that the ball is released at $t=0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(0)&=0.5\\cos(\\pi\\cdot 0)+2.2 \\\\[12pt]\n    &=0.5(1)+2.2\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2.7\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The minimum height occurs when $\\cos(\\pi t)=-1$. We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h_{\\text{min}}&=0.5(-1)+2.2 \\hspace{1cm}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=1.7\\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The ball returns to its initial height after a time period $T$. We\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}&=\\pi \\hspace{2cm}\\\\[12pt]\n    T&=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{s}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Calculating the time stamps at which the ball gains the height of\n    $2.2+0.25\\sqrt{2}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0.5\\cos(\\pi t)+2.2&=2.2+0.25\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{2cm}\\\\[12pt]\n    0.5\\cos(\\pi t)& = 0.25\\sqrt{2} \\\\[11pt]\n    \\cos(\\pi t)&=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\[11pt]\n    \\pi t &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\\\[12pt]\n    t &= \\dfrac{1}{4},\\, \\dfrac{7}{4} \\hspace{4em} [\\hspace{0.1em}0\\leq t\\leq 2\\hspace{0.1em}]\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The amount of the time that the ball is less than $2.2+0.25\\sqrt{2}$\n    meters above the ground is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    t&=\\dfrac{7}{4}-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.5cm}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=1.5\\text{ s}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    %h'(t) &= -0.5\\pi\\sin(\\pi t)\\\\[6pt]\n    h'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right)&= -0.5\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi \\sin\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=-0.5\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=-0.25\\hspace{0.05em}\\pi\\sqrt{3} \\text{ ms}^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA922","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA922\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the functions $f(x)=3\\cos(x)+\\dfrac{9}{2}$ and $g(x)=3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+A$, where $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $A< \\dfrac{9}{2}$.\n\n1. Describe a sequence of two transformations that transforms the graph of $f$ to the graph of $g$. [3 marks]\n\nThe $y$-intercept of the graph $g$ is at the point $\\left(0\\,,\\dfrac{9}{2}\\right)$\n\n2. Find the range of $g$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Applying a translation of $a$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis to a function $f$ is shown as $f(x+a)$, and a translation of $b$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis is shown as $f(x)-b$. \n\n    Therefore a translation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis would be $f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)+\\dfrac{9}{2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that $A < \\dfrac{9}{2}$, if we then apply a translation of $\\dfrac{9}{2} - A$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) - \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{2} - A\\bigg) &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)+\\dfrac{9}{2} - \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{2} - A\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 3\\cos\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg) + A \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= g(x)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we have the following transformations in any order\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    T_1 &: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\text{translation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units in the direction of the negative $x$-axis}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    T_2 &: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\text{translation of $\\dfrac{9}{2} - A$ units in the direction of the negative $y$-axis}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. We have $g(0)=\\dfrac{9}{2}$. Solving for $A$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3\\cos\\left(0+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+A &= \\dfrac{9}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) + A & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    A & = 3 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $g(x)=3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+3$ , and the following inequalities hold:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    -1\\leq \\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &\\leq 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    -3\\leq 3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &\\leq 3 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    0\\leq 3\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+3 &\\leq 6 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    0\\leq g(x) &\\leq 6 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the range of $g$ is $0\\leq g(x) \\leq 6$"},{"ref":"AA979","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA979\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sin(x+c)$, where $0\\leq x \\leq a$ and $a,c \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\n1. By sketching a suitable graph, or otherwise, find the largest value of $a$ for which the inverse function $f^{-1}$ exists in the case where $c=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Find the largest value of $a$ for which the inverse function $f^{-1}$ exists in the case where $c=\\pi$. [1 mark]\n\n3. Find the largest value of $a$ for which the inverse function $f^{-1}$ exists in the case where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < c < \\pi$. Give your answer in terms of $c$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. By sketching the graph of $\\sin x$ under a horizontal shift $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ units to the left, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/aa979a.PNG\" alt=\"aa979a\">\n\n    Note that a function must be one-to-one for its inverse to exist. The diagram shows that $f$ is one-to-one if its domain is restricted to $\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$.\n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{a = \\pi}.$\n\n2. By sketching the graph of $\\sin x$ under a horizontal shift $\\pi$ units to the left, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/aa979b.PNG\" alt=\"aa979b\">\n\n    The diagram shows that $f$ is one-to-one if its domain is restricted to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$. \n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{a = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$\n\n3. This time the graph of $f$ is an intermediate graph between the previous two:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Functions%20%26%20Graphs/images/aa979c.PNG\" alt=\"aa979c\">\n\n    Hence, we can see that $f$ is one-to-one over the interval $\\left[0,\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}-c\\right]$. \n\n    Therefore, $\\boxed{a=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}-c}$"}]},{"name":"Trigonometric Identities & Equations","questions":[{"ref":"AA1025","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1025\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the equation $\\sin x - \\cos x = \\pm\\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n\n1. Show that the equation can be expressed as $\\sin 2x = \\dfrac{8}{9}$[4 marks]\n\n2. Hence or otherwise, solve the equation $\\sin x - \\cos x = \\pm\\dfrac{1}{3}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. By squaring both sides of the equation, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(\\sin x - \\cos x\\right)^2 &= \\left(\\pm\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\sin^2x - 2\\sin x \\cos x + \\cos^2x &= \\dfrac{1}{9} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    1 - 2\\sin x \\cos x &= \\dfrac{1}{9} \\hspace{3em}\\text{[by the Pythagorean identity]} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2\\sin x \\cos x &= 1 - \\dfrac{1}{9} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-2.5em}\\boxed{\\sin 2x = \\dfrac{8}{9}} \\hspace{3em}\\text{[by the double angle identity]}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Sketching the graphs of $y= \\sin 2x$ and $y = \\dfrac{8}{9}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi$, we find that there are four points of intersection:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/aa1025.PNG\" alt=\"aa1025\">\n\n    Hence, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi$, the solutions to $\\sin x - \\cos x = \\pm\\dfrac{1}{3}$ are \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\boxed{x = 0.547, 1.02, 3.69, 4.16}  \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1155","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1155\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\n1.  Show that $\\cos{3\\theta}=4\\cos^3{\\theta}-3\\cos{\\theta}$. [4 marks]\n\nConsider the following function\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&=4x^3-3x-\\cos{3k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $k$ is an angle in degrees such that $0 \\leq k \\leq 360^\\circ$.\n\n2.  Hence, verify that $\\cos{k}$ is one of the roots of $f(x)$.[2 marks]\n\nLet the remaining two roots of $f(x)$ be $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$.\n\n3.  1.  Find $\\alpha\\beta$ in terms of $\\cos{k}$.\n\n    2.  Show that $\\beta=-\\alpha-\\cos k$.[3 marks]\n\n4.  Using your answers from part (c) show that $\\alpha^2+\\alpha \\cos k +\\cos^2 k -\\tfrac{3}{4}=0$. [2 marks]\n\n5.  Hence show that $\\alpha = \\frac{-\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}$ and $\\beta = \\frac{-\\cos k - \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}$. [4 marks]\n\nIt is known that for $0 \\leq k \\leq 360^\\circ$ $f(x)$ has either $3$ distinct roots or a single distinct root and a repeated root.\n\n6.  Hence, find all of the values of $k$ for which $f(x)$ intersects the $x-$axis exactly $2$ times. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the compound angle identity from the formula booklet we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos{(A+B)}&=\\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B\\\\[6pt]\n        \\cos{(2\\theta+\\theta)}&=\\cos 2\\theta \\cos \\theta - \\sin 2\\theta \\sin \\theta\\\\[6pt]\n        \\cos{3\\theta}&=\\cos 2\\theta \\cos \\theta - \\sin 2\\theta \\sin \\theta \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now the use the double angle formula to rewrite $\\cos {2\\theta}$ and $\\sin {2\\theta}$ in terms of single angle functions.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos{3\\theta}&=(2\\cos^2{\\theta}-1) \\cos \\theta - (2\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}) \\sin \\theta \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=2\\cos^3\\theta-\\cos \\theta - 2\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now rewrite $\\sin^2{\\theta}$ in terms of $\\cos{\\theta}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos{3\\theta}&=2\\cos^3\\theta-\\cos \\theta - 2(1-\\cos^2{\\theta})\\cos{\\theta}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=2\\cos^3\\theta-\\cos \\theta - 2\\cos{\\theta}+2\\cos^3{\\theta}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos \\theta \\hspace{2em}\\text{... as required.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If $\\cos{k}$ is a root of $f(x)$ then $f(\\cos{k})=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(\\cos{k})&=4(\\cos{k})^3-3(\\cos{k})-\\cos{3k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using part (a)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(\\cos{k})&=\\cos{3k}-\\cos{3k} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, $\\cos k \\text{ is a root of }f(x)$\n\n3.  1.  Using the formula for the product of the roots of a polynomial\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\alpha\\beta \\cos{k} &=-\\frac{(-1)^3\\cos{3k}}{4}  \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using part (a) we can rewrite $\\cos{3k}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\alpha\\beta \\cos{k} &=\\frac{4\\cos^3{k}-3\\cos{k}}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n            \\alpha\\beta &= \\frac{4\\cos^2{k}-3}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n            &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\cos^2{k}-\\tfrac{3}{4}}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  Using the formula for the sum of the roots of a polynomial (with the coefficient of $x^2$ being $0$)\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\alpha + \\beta + \\cos k &=-\\frac{0}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n            \\beta= \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{-\\alpha - \\cos k}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  Combining our answers from part (c)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\alpha \\beta &= \\cos^2{k}-\\tfrac{3}{4} \\hspace{4em}\\beta= -\\alpha - \\cos k\\\\[6pt]\n        \\alpha(-\\alpha - \\cos k) &= \\cos^2{k}-\\tfrac{3}{4} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        -\\alpha^2-\\alpha \\cos{k} &= \\cos^2{k}-\\tfrac{3}{4}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.4em}\\boxed{0=\\alpha^2+\\alpha \\cos{k}+\\cos^2{k}-\\tfrac{3}{4}} \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Use the quadratic formula with $a=1, b=\\cos k$ and $c=\\cos^2 k - \\tfrac{3}{4}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\alpha &=\\frac{-\\cos k \\pm \\sqrt{\\cos^2 k - 4\\times 1 \\times (\\cos^2 k - \\tfrac{3}{4})}}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-\\cos k \\pm \\sqrt{\\cos^2 k - 4\\cos^2 k +3)}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-\\cos k \\pm \\sqrt{-3\\cos^2 k +3)}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-\\cos k \\pm \\sqrt{3(1-\\cos^2 k)}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-\\cos k \\pm \\sqrt{3\\sin^2 k}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-\\cos k \\pm \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let $\\alpha = \\frac{-\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}$, using the answer from part (c) (ii) we can find $\\beta$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\beta &= -\\alpha - \\cos{k}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\frac{-\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2} - \\cos{k}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\frac{\\cos k - \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2} - \\frac{2\\cos{k}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-\\cos k - \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $\\alpha = \\frac{-\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2} \\text{ and } \\beta = \\frac{-\\cos k - \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note: also accept vice versa solutions for $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$.\n\n6.  $f(x)$ must have a single distinct root and a repeated root to intersect the $x-$axis exactly twice.\n\n    Recall the three roots to $f(x)$ are $\\alpha, \\beta$ and $\\cos k$.\n\n    We have a repeated root when any of these conditions are met.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\alpha &= \\beta &[1]\\\\[6pt]\n        \\alpha &= \\cos k &[2]\\\\[6pt]\n        \\beta &= \\cos k &[3]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let's consider condition $[1]$ first\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\alpha &= \\beta\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{-\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}&=\\frac{-\\cos k - \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\sin k&=0 \\hspace{2em} \\text{ where $0 \\leq k \\leq 360^\\circ$}\\\\[6pt]\n        k&=0, 180^\\circ, 360^\\circ \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Now consider condition $[2]$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\alpha &= \\cos k\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{-\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}&=\\cos k \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{-\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}&=\\frac{2\\cos k}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{-3\\cos k + \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        \\sqrt{3}\\sin k&=3\\cos k\\\\[6pt]\n        \\tan k&= \\sqrt{3}\\hspace{2em} \\text{ where $0 \\leq k \\leq 360^\\circ$}\\\\[6pt]\n        k&=60^\\circ, 240^\\circ \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally consider condition $[3]$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\beta &= \\cos k\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{-\\cos k - \\sqrt{3}\\sin k}{2}&=\\cos k \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\tan k&= -\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{2em} \\text{ where $0 \\leq k \\leq 360^\\circ$}\\\\[6pt]\n        k&=120^\\circ, 300^\\circ \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $f(x)$ will intersect the $x-$axis exactly $2$ times when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\boxed{k=0^\\circ, 60^\\circ, 120^\\circ, 180^\\circ, 240^\\circ, 300^\\circ, 360^\\circ}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1271","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1271\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Show that $4-3\\cos2x=6\\sin^2x+1$.[1 mark]\n\n2.  Hence or otherwise solve $4-3\\cos(4\\theta+\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3})-9\\sin (2\\theta+\\tfrac{\\pi}{3})=-2$ for $0\\leq \\theta<\\pi$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  In a $'$show that$'$ question we should work from the L.H.S. to the R.H.S.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n       \\text{L.H.S.}&=4-3\\cos2x&\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The cosine double angle identity that contains only $\\sin \\theta$ is $\\cos2\\theta=1-2\\sin^2\\theta$.\n\n    Substituting this we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=4-3(1-2\\sin^2x)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=4-3+6\\sin^2x\\\\[6pt]   &=6\\sin^2x+1\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\text{R.H.S.}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  By considering a substitution $x=2\\theta+\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}$ we can write the equation in part (b) so that contains the expression from part (a).\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    4-3\\cos(4\\theta+\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3})-9\\sin(2\\theta+\\tfrac{\\pi}{3})=-2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\textcolor{red}{4-3\\cos2x}-9\\sin x=-2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we can substitute the R.H.S. of the equation from part (a) so that the equation is in terms of sine.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\textcolor{red}{6\\sin^2x+1}-9\\sin x&=-2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    6\\sin^2x-9\\sin x+3&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2\\sin^2x-3\\sin x+1&=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Notice that this is a quadratic equation. We can factorise it using grouping which gives\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\sin^2x-3\\sin x+1&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2\\sin^2x-2\\sin x-\\sin x+1&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2\\sin x(\\sin x-1)-1(\\sin x - 1)&=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    (2\\sin x-1)(\\sin x-1)&=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Applying the null factor theorem we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n      2\\sin x-1&=0&&\\sin x-1=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n       \\sin x&=\\tfrac{1}{2}&&\\sin x=1\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the unit circle, we know that $\\sin x=1$ when $\\textcolor{red}{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$ and $\\sin x=\\frac{1}{2}$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\textcolor{blue}{x=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}}$.  This is represented in the diagram below. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/AA1271.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1271\">\n\n    Hence the answers for $x$ are $\\frac{\\pi}{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\text{, and }\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall that we are solving for $\\theta$ and we used a substitution.  \n\n    Hence we can find values of $\\theta$ that satisfy the equation\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\theta+\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}&=\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\theta&=-\\tfrac{\\pi}{12}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    The first value we have found is outside of the given domain $(0 \\leq \\theta < \\pi)$ hence we need to add (or subtract!) $\\textcolor{red}{2\\pi}$ to the value we found from the unit circle to obtain other values that could be in the domain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\theta+\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}&=\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}+\\textcolor{red}{2\\pi}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\tfrac{13\\pi}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2\\theta&=\\tfrac{13\\pi}{6}-\\tfrac{2\\pi}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\theta&=\\tfrac{11\\pi}{12}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This value is now in the given domain.\n\n    Let's find the remaining values\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2\\theta+\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}&=\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\theta&=\\tfrac{\\pi}{12}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    2\\theta+\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}&=\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\theta&=\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We have found three solutions. Although not required by the question we can view the values on a graph.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/AA1274-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1274-a2\">\n\n    Notice three solutions, one in red and two in blue.\n\n    The solution identified with the black dot is the value we found which was not in the desired domain."},{"ref":"AA1308","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1308\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $\\sin{x},\\tan{x},\\tan{x} \\sec{x}\\dots$\n\n1.  Find an expression for the common ratio, $r$, as a single trigonometric ratio.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Find an expression for $u_4$, expressing your answer in terms of only $\\tan x$.[3 marks]\n3.  Find an expression for $u_9$, expressing your answer in terms of $\\tan{x}$ and $\\sec{x}$.[2 marks]\n4.  Show that the sum of the first four terms is $\\displaystyle S_4=-\\tan^3{x}\\left(\\dfrac{\\cos^2{x}+1}{\\cos{x}-1}\\right)$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio, $r$, is equal to any term divided by the previous term.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n      &= \\dfrac{\\tan{x}}{\\sin{x}} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the **identity for $\\tan\\theta$ (3.5)**, to rewrite $\\tan x$, $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$, together with the **reciprocal trig identity (3.9)**, to express the result as a single ratio, $\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\left(\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n      &= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}} \\\\[6pt]\n    r &= \\boxed{\\sec{x}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  Using the **geometric sequence formula (1.3)**, to find $u_4$ with $u_1 = \\sin x$ and $r = \\sec x$, $u_n = u_1 r^{n-1}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_4 &= \\sin{x}(\\sec{x})^{4-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sin{x}\\sec^{3}{x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To write the answer in terms of $\\tan x$, factorise $\\sec^3 x$ and apply the **Pythagorean identity (3.9)**, to replace $\\sec^2 x$, $1 + \\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta$, together with the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_4 &= \\sin{x}\\sec{x}(\\sec^{2}{x}) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sin{x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}\\right)(\\sec^{2}{x}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}(\\sec^{2}{x}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\tan{x}(1 + \\tan^2{x}) \\\\[6pt]\n    u_4 &= \\boxed{\\tan{x} + \\tan^3{x}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3.  Using the **geometric sequence formula (1.3)** again with $n = 9$, $u_n = u_1 r^{n-1}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_9 &= \\sin{x}(\\sec{x})^{9-1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sin{x}(\\sec{x})^{8}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To write the expression in terms of $\\tan x$ and $\\sec x$, factorise a single $\\sec x$ and apply the **identity for $\\tan\\theta$ (3.5)**, $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_9 &= \\sin{x}(\\sec x)(\\sec x)^7 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\sin{x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}\\right)\\sec^7{x} \\\\[10pt]\n    u_9 &= \\boxed{\\tan{x}\\sec^7{x}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n4.  Using the **geometric sum formula (1.3)**, to find the sum of the first four terms with $u_1 = \\sin x$, $r = \\sec x$ and $n = 4$, $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_4 &= \\dfrac{\\sin{x}(1-\\sec^4{x})}{1-\\sec{x}} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sin{x}\\left(1 - \\tfrac{1}{\\cos^4{x}}\\right)}{1 - \\tfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Writing $1 - \\tfrac{1}{\\cos^4{x}}$ and $1 - \\tfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ with common denominators.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_4 &= \\dfrac{\\sin{x}\\left(\\tfrac{\\cos^4{x}-1}{\\cos^4{x}}\\right)}{\\tfrac{\\cos{x}-1}{\\cos{x}}} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\sin{x}\\left(\\dfrac{\\cos^4{x}-1}{\\cos^4{x}}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\cos{x}-1}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n    S_4 &= \\sin{x}\\left(\\dfrac{\\cos^4{x}-1}{\\cos^3{x}(\\cos{x}-1)}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the difference of two squares, $\\cos^4{x}-1 = (\\cos^2{x}-1)(\\cos^2{x}+1)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_4 &= \\sin{x}\\left(\\dfrac{(\\cos^2{x}-1)(\\cos^2{x}+1)}{\\cos^3{x}(\\cos{x}-1)}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Applying the **Pythagorean identity (3.6)**, to replace $\\cos^2 x - 1$ with $-\\sin^2 x$, $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_4 &= \\sin{x}\\left(\\dfrac{(-\\sin^2{x})(\\cos^2{x}+1)}{\\cos^3{x}(\\cos{x}-1)}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{-\\sin^3{x}(\\cos^2{x}+1)}{\\cos^3{x}(\\cos{x}-1)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\boxed{S_4 = -\\tan^3{x}\\left(\\dfrac{\\cos^2{x}+1}{\\cos{x}-1}\\right)}\\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required.} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA159","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA159\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nLet\n$f(x) = x^4 - 0.4\\hspace{0.05em}x^3 - 2.85\\hspace{0.05em}x^2 + 0.9\\hspace{0.05em}x + 1.35$,\nfor $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find the solutions for $f(x) > 0$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  For the graph of $y = f(x)$,\n    1.  find the coordinates of local minimum and maximum points.\n\n    2.  find the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflexion. [5 marks]\n\nThe domain of $f$ is now restricted to $[a,b]$ where\n$a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Write down the smallest value of $a > 0$ and the largest value\n        of $b > 0$ for which $f$ has an inverse. Give your answers\n        correct to **three significant figures**.\n\n    2.  For these values of $a$ and $b$, sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$\n        and $y = f^{-1}(x)$ on the same set of axes, showing clearly the\n        coordinates of the end points of each curve.\n\n    3.  Solve $f^{-1}(x) = 0.5$. [6 marks]\n\nLet $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\sin(2x - 1) + \\dfrac{1}{2}$,\n $\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\n4.\n    1.  Find an expression for $g^{-1}$ and state its domain.\n\n    2.  Solve $(f^{-1}\\circ g)(x) < 0.5$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/245a2a1979fce06233cc54e60878b1e96f11b9d1.svg\" alt=\"245a2a1979fce06233cc54e60878b1e96f11b9d1.svg\">\n\n    From the diagram, we see that $f$ is positive on the intervals\n    $(-\\infty,-1.5)$, $(-0.6,1)$ and $(1.5,\\infty)$, and negative on\n    $(-1.5,-0.6)$ and $(1,1.5)$.\n\n    Hence the set of solutions to the inequality $f(x) > 0$ is\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     (-\\infty,-1.5)\\cup(-0.6,1)\\cup(1.5,\\infty)\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.\n    1.  From the diagram, we see that the graph of $y = f(x)$ has a\n        local\\\n        maximum at point A and local minimums at points B and C.\n\n        Hence the coordinates of the local max and min points are\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{local max}:&\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}(0.155, 1.42)  \\\\[10pt]\n        \\text{local min}:&\\hspace{0.75em}\\hspace{0.525em}(-1.13, -1.10)\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{0.525em}(1.28, -0.322)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x) &= x^4 - 0.4x^3 - 2.85x^2 + 0.9x + 1.35 \\\\[6pt]\n        f'(x) &= 4x^3 - 1.2x^2 - 5.7x + 0.9 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{1.5em} f''(x) &= 12x^2 - 2.4x - 5.7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n         Hence, if we solve the equation\n        $f''(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        12x^2 - 2.4x - 5.7 &= 0 \\hspace{9.8em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{12.95em} x \\approx -0.596, \\hspace{0.25em} 0.796 \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  From the diagram, we see that $f$ is a bijection if its domain\n        is restricted to $[-1.13, 0.155]$ or $[0.155,1.28]$.\n\n        As it is given that $a,b > 0$, hence $f$ has an inverse if\n         $a = 0.155$\n        and  $b = 1.28$\n\n    2.  The graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is the reflection of the graph of\n        $y = f(x)$ across the line $y = x$.\n\n        Hence, if we sketch the graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = f^{-1}(x)$\n        on the same set of axes, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/524a320d1d2563d4a110444319e1f709eb5ee6c6.svg\" alt=\"524a320d1d2563d4a110444319e1f709eb5ee6c6.svg\">\n\n    3.  If we solve the equation $f^{-1}(x) = 0.5$ for $x$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        f(f^{-1}(x)) &= f(0.5) \\hspace{4em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{10.8em} x = 1.1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f(f^{-1}(x)) = x$}]\n        $$\n\n4.\n    1.  Solving the equation $(g\\circ g^{-1})(x) = x$ for\n        $y = g^{-1}(x)$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        g(g^{-1}(x)) &= x \\\\[14pt]\n        g(y) &= x \\\\[10pt]\n        \\hspace{1.7em} \\dfrac{2}{3}\\sin(2y - 1) + \\dfrac{1}{2} &= x \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{2}{3}\\sin(2y - 1) &= \\dfrac{2x-1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\sin(2y - 1) &= \\dfrac{6x-3}{4} \\\\[4pt]\n        2y - 1 &= \\arcsin\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{6x-3}{4}\\bigg) \\\\[3pt]\n        y &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\arcsin\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{6x-3}{4}\\bigg) + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence an expression for $g^{-1}$ is\n         $g^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\arcsin\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{6x-3}{4}\\bigg) + \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n        The range of $g$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left[-\\dfrac{2}{3}+\\dfrac{1}{2},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{2}{3}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right] = \\left[-\\dfrac{1}{6},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{7}{6}\\right] \\hspace{4.7em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the domain of $g^{-1}$ is\n         $\\bigg[-\\dfrac{1}{6},\\dfrac{7}{6}\\bigg]$\n\n    2.  Solving the inequality $(f^{-1}\\circ g)(x) < 0.5$ for $x$, we\n        get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{9.5em} f^{-1}(g(x)) &< 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n        g(x) &> f(0.5) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $f$ is decreasing}] \\\\[6pt]\n        g(x) &> 1.1 \\\\[5pt]\n        x &> g^{-1}(1.1) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $g^{-1}$ is increasing}] \\\\[6pt]\n        x &> 1.06\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the set of solutions to the\n        inequality $(f^{-1}\\circ g)(x) < 0.5$ is\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\left(1.06,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right] \\hspace{2em}\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$"},{"ref":"AA226","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA226\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $\\sin x - \\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{4}$, find $\\cos 8x$, rounding\nyour answer to $3$ significant\n\nfigures.","markScheme":"Using the double angle identity, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin x - \\cos x)^2 &= \\dfrac{1}{16} \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n(\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x) - 2\\sin x \\cos x &= \\dfrac{1}{16} \\\\[6pt]\n1 - \\sin 2x &= \\dfrac{1}{16} \\\\[6pt]\n\\sin 2x &= \\dfrac{15}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the double angle\nidentities, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.8em} \\cos 8x &= 1 - 2\\sin^2 4x \\\\[12pt]\n&= 1 - 2(2\\sin 2x \\cos 2x)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 1 - 8(\\sin 2x)^2(\\cos 2x)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 1 - 8(\\sin 2x)^2\\big[1 - (\\sin 2x)^2\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n&= 1 - 8(\\sin 2x)^2 + 8(\\sin 2x)^4 \\\\[4pt]\n&= 1 - 8\\bigg(\\dfrac{15}{16}\\bigg)^2 + 8\\bigg(\\dfrac{15}{16}\\bigg)^4 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 0.149\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA227","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA227\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind all solutions to the equation $\\tan 2x - 3\\tan x  = 0$ where\n$\\ang{0} \\leq x < \\ang{360}$.","markScheme":"Using the double angle identity, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan 2x - 3\\tan x  &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{2\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^2 x} - 3\\tan x &=  0 \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{2.1em} 2\\tan x - 3\\tan x\\hspace{0.05em}(1 - \\tan^2 x) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\\tan x\\hspace{0.05em}(2 - 3 + 3\\tan^2 x) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\\tan x\\,(3\\tan^2 x - 1) &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\tan x &=  0,\\hspace{0.25em} \\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.5em} x &= \\arctan(0),\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{180}+\\arctan(0) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\ang{0},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{180}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.2em} x &= \\arctan(1/\\sqrt{3}),\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{180}+\\arctan(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\ang{30},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{210}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.7em} x &= \\arctan(-1/\\sqrt{3}),\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{180}+\\arctan(-1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\ang{150},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{330}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA228","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA228\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nSolve the equation $\\cos 2x + \\cos x = 1 + \\sin 2x - \\sin x$, for\n$x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.","markScheme":"Using double angle identities, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos 2x + \\cos x &= 1 + \\sin 2x - \\sin x \\\\[6pt]\n(1- 2\\sin^2x) + \\cos x &= 1 + 2\\sin x \\cos x - \\sin x \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\sin x - 2\\sin^2x + \\cos x - 2\\sin x \\cos x &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n\\sin x\\hspace{0.05em}(1 - 2\\sin x) + \\cos x\\hspace{0.05em}(1 - 2\\sin x) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(\\sin x + \\cos x)(1 - 2\\sin x) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.8em} \\sin x + \\cos x &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\n\\tan x &= -1 \\\\[11pt]\nx &= \\arctan(-1),\\hspace{0.25em} \\pi+\\arctan(-1) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{3em} x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{7.8em} 1 - 2\\sin x &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\\sin x &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\nx &=\\arcsin(1/2),\\hspace{0.25em}\\pi-\\arcsin(1/2) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2.25em}  x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA229","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA229\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}-1}}{\\sin x} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}+1}}{\\cos x}  = 2\\sqrt[4]{8}, \\hspace{0.5em} 0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Given that\n    $\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}$\n    and\n    $\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}$,\n    verify that $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ is a solution to the equation.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the other solution to the equation. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Substituting $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ in the left hand side of the\n    equation $(1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}-1}}{\\raisebox{-0.5em}{$\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big)$}} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}+1}}{\\raisebox{-0.5em}{$\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big)$}} &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}-1}}{\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}+1}}{\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}} \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}-1}}{\\dfrac{\\sqrt[4]{2}}{2}\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2} - 1}} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}+1}}{\\dfrac{\\sqrt[4]{2}}{2}\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2} + 1}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[4]{2}} + \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[4]{2}} \\\\[13pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt[4]{8}}{\\sqrt[4]{8}} \\\\[21pt]\n    &= 2\\sqrt[4]{8} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ is a\n    solution to the equation $(1)$.\n\n2.  Solving the equation $(1)$ for $x \\neq \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}-1}}{\\sin x} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}+1}}{\\cos x} &= 2\\sqrt[4]{8} \\\\[3pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.5em}{$\\dfrac{\\sqrt[4]{2}}{2}\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}-1}$}}{\\sin x} + \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.5em}{$\\dfrac{\\sqrt[4]{2}}{2}\\sqrt{\\sqrt{2}+1}$}}{\\cos x} &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt[4]{2}}{2}(2\\sqrt[4]{8}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.5em}{$\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}$}}{\\sin x} + \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.5em}{$\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{2}}$}}{\\cos x} &= 2 \\\\[9pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.5em}{$\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big)$}}{\\sin x} + \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.5em}{$\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big)$}}{\\cos x} &= 2 \\\\[19pt]\n    \\cos x \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) + \\sin x \\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) &= 2\\sin x \\cos x \\\\[19pt]\n    \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) &= \\sin 2x \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by trig identities}] \\\\[19pt]\n    x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{8} &= \\pi - 2x \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x\\neq\\pi\\hspace{-0.1em}/8$}] \\\\[19pt]\n    3x &= \\dfrac{7\\pi}{8} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.65em} x = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{24}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA232","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA232\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\sqrt{2}\\cos x,\\hspace{0.4em} \\sin 2x,\\hspace{0.4em} 2\\sqrt{2}\\sin^2 x \\cos x,\\hspace{0.4em} -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}. \\\\\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n1.  Find the common ratio. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the set of values of $x$ for which the geometric series below\n    converges.\n\n    $$\n    \\sqrt{2}\\cos x + \\sin 2x + 2\\sqrt{2}\\sin^2 x \\cos x + \\cdots\n\n    $$\n    [3 marks]\n\nConsider $x = \\arcsin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg)$,\n$0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n3.  Show that the sum to infinity of this series is $\\sqrt{7}$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = \\sqrt{2}\\cos x$ and $u_2 = \\sin 2x$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{14.5em} r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\sqrt{2}\\cos x} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\sin x \\cos x}{\\sqrt{2}\\cos x} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by double angle identity}] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{2}\\sin x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The geometric series converges when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |r| &< 1 \\\\[15pt]\n    |\\sqrt{2}\\sin x| &< 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[12pt]\n    |\\sin x| &< \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{2.4em} -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} <  \\sin x  &< \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    -\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} <  x  < \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{3.5em}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} u_1 &= \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.2em}\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{2}\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} r &= \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.2em}\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{2}\\times\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the sum of an infinite\n    geometric sequence formula $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$ with\n    $u_1 = \\sqrt{7}/2$ and $r = 1/2$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}/2}{1 - 1/2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{7}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA236","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA236\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind all solutions to the equation\n$\\dfrac{1}{\\tan x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\tan 2x}  = 0$ where $0 < x < 2\\pi$ and\n$x \\neq \\pi$.","markScheme":"Using the double angle formula for tangent, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{1}{\\tan x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\tan 2x} &=  0 \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{2em} \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}  + \\dfrac{1 - \\tan^2 x}{2\\tan x} &=  0 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{3 - \\tan^2 x}{2\\tan x} &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n3 - \\tan^2 x &= 0 \\\\[14pt]\n\\tan x &= \\pm \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} x &= \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}), \\hspace{0.25em} \\pi + \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.55em} x &= \\arctan(-\\sqrt{3}), \\hspace{0.25em} \\pi + \\arctan(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA239","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA239\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve the equation $\\cos 2x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x + 3\\cos x$, for\n$0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi$.","markScheme":"Using the Pythagorean identity and double angle identity for cosine, we\nget\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos 2x - \\sin^2 x &= \\cos^2 x + 3\\cos x \\\\[12pt]\n\\cos 2x - 3\\cos x &= \\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x  \\\\[13pt]\n\\cos 2x - 3\\cos x &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n(2\\cos^2 x - 1) - 3\\cos x &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n2\\cos^2 x - 3\\cos x - 2 &= 0 \\\\[13pt]\n\\hspace{2em} (2\\cos x + 1)(\\cos x - 2) &= 0 \\\\[13pt]\n2\\cos x + 1 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\cos x - 2 \\neq 0$}] \\\\[8pt]\n\\cos x &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2.5em} x = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA243","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA243\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind all solutions to the equation $\\tan x - \\sin 2x = 0$ where\n$\\ang{0} \\leq x \\leq \\ang{360}$.","markScheme":"Using the double angle identity, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan x - \\sin 2x &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n\\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} - 2\\sin x \\cos x &=  0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\hspace{2em} \\dfrac{\\sin x - 2 \\sin x \\cos^2 x}{\\cos x} &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\n\\sin x - 2 \\sin x \\cos^2 x &= 0 \\\\[14pt]\n\\sin x \\hspace{0.05em}(1 - 2\\cos^2 x) &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\\sin x =  0 \\hspace{0.55em}&\\hspace{0.3em}\\text{or}\\hspace{0.6em} \\cos x = \\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.1em} \\sin x =  0 &\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{when}\\hspace{0.5em} x =\\hspace{0.525em}\\ang{0},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{180},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{360}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} &\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{when}\\hspace{0.5em} x =\\hspace{0.525em}\\ang{45},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{315} \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} &\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{when}\\hspace{0.5em} x =\\hspace{0.525em}\\ang{135},\\hspace{0.25em} \\ang{225} \\hspace{0.4em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA257","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA257\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nGiven that $\\sin x = \\dfrac{3}{5}$, where $x$ is an acute angle, find:\n\n1.  $\\cos x$; [3 marks]\n\n2.  $\\sin 2x$; [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\cos 2x$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the Pythagorean identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\cos^2 x + \\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^2 &= 1 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= 1 - \\dfrac{9}{25} \\hspace{6.1em} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= \\dfrac{16}{25} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.3em} \\cos x = \\dfrac{4}{5} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\pi\\hspace{-0.1em}/2$}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the double angle identity for sine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin 2x &= 2\\sin x \\cos x \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 2\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\bigg) \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{24}{25}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the double angle identity for cosine, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos 2x &= 1 - 2\\sin^2 x \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 1 - 2\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\dfrac{18}{25} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7}{25}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA262","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA262\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan x + 1 = 0$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\" alt=\"eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\">\n\nUsing the right triangle above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8em} \\sqrt{3}\\tan x + 1 &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n\\tan x &= -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\[6pt]\nx &= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + n\\pi \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}] \\\\[9pt]\nx &= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\pi \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.4em} x = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA263","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA263\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $\\sin x = \\dfrac{2}{3}$, where $x$ is obtuse.\n\n1.  Find $\\cos x$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\cos 2x$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the Pythagorean identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\cos^2 x + \\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg)^2 &= 1 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= 1 - \\dfrac{4}{9} \\hspace{6.4em} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= \\dfrac{5}{9} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.2em} \\cos x = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\pi\\hspace{-0.1em}/2 < x < \\pi$}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the double angle identity for cosine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos 2x &= 1 - 2\\sin^2 x \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 1 - 2\\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg)^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\dfrac{8}{9} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{9}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA266","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA266\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve $\\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos x} - 1 = 0$, for\n$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < x \\leq \\pi$.","markScheme":"Applying the **double angle identity (3.6)**, to rewrite $\\sin 2x$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos x} - 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{2\\sin x \\hspace{0.1em}\\cancel{\\cos x}}{\\cancel{\\cos x}} - 1 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n> In this case, it is valid to cancel $\\cos x$, because in the specified domain, $\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x \\leq \\pi$, $\\cos x \\neq 0$. Hence we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    2\\sin x - 1 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\hspace{6.5em} [\\text{for } n \\in \\mathbb{Z}] \\hspace{3em} x &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2n\\pi, \\hspace{1em} x = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} + 2n\\pi \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[9pt]\n    x &= \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} + 2n\\pi \\hspace{3em} \\left[\\text{since } \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < x \\leq \\pi\\right] \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n x=   \\boxed{ \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA272","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA272\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nGiven that $\\sin x = \\dfrac{4}{5}$, where $x$ is an obtuse angle, find\nthe value of:\n\n1.  $\\cos x$; [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\cos 2x$; [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\tan 2x$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the Pythagorean identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x &= 1 \\\\[9pt]\n    \\cos^2 x + \\bigg(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\bigg)^2 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= 1 - \\dfrac{16}{25} \\hspace{3.8em} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= \\dfrac{9}{25} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.5em} \\cos x = -\\dfrac{3}{5} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\pi\\hspace{-0.1em}/2 < x < \\pi$}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the double angle identity for cosine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos 2x &= 1 - 2\\sin^2 x \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 1 - 2\\bigg(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\bigg)^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\dfrac{32}{25} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{7}{25}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the double angle identity for sine, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.1em} \\tan 2x &= \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos 2x} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\big(\\frac{4}{5}\\big)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\frac{3}{5}\\big)}{\\big(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\frac{7}{25}\\big)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{24}{7}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA278","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA278\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nGiven that $\\sin x = \\dfrac{2}{3}$, for\n$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\pi$, find the value of:\n\n1.  $\\cos x$; [3 marks]\n\n2.  $\\tan 2x$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the Pythagorean identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x &= 1 \\\\[9pt]\n    \\cos^2 x + \\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg)^2 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= 1 - \\dfrac{4}{9} \\hspace{6.9em} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= \\dfrac{5}{9} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8.7em} \\cos x = -\\dfrac{\\raisebox{-0.15em}{$\\sqrt{5}$}}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\pi\\hspace{-0.1em}/2 < x < \\pi$}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the double angle identities for sine and cosine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tan 2x &= \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{1 - 2\\sin^2 x} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\big(\\frac{2}{3}\\big)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\big)}{1 - 2\\big(\\frac{2}{3}\\big)^2} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\[2pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{9}}{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -4\\sqrt{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA285","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA285\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe expression $8\\sin x \\cos x$ can be written in the form $p\\sin qx$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$ and the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve the equation\n    $8\\sin x \\cos x = 2\\sqrt{3}$, for\n    $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the double angle identity for sine, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin 2x &= 2\\sin x \\cos x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.3em} 4\\sin 2x &= 8\\sin x \\cos x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we\n    find\n     $p = 4$\n    and  $q = 2$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $8\\sin x \\cos x = 2\\sqrt{3}$ for\n    $\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/4 \\leq x \\leq \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{8.1em} 4\\sin 2x &= 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\[7pt]\n     \\sin 2x &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n     2x &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi,\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi \\\\[9pt]\n     x &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + n\\pi,\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}] \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.1em} x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/4 \\leq x \\leq \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA291","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA291\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $\\cos x = \\frac{3}{4}$, where $0 < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find\nthe value of $\\sin 4x$.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/681b1162a7884afd1f69dcb04042193ca0edb27d.svg\" alt=\"681b1162a7884afd1f69dcb04042193ca0edb27d.svg\">\n\nUsing the right triangle above & the double angle identities  $(\\sin 2\\theta=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ and $\\cos 2\\theta=2\\cos^2\\theta-1)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} \\sin 4x &= \\sin(2(2x))\\\\[12pt]\n&=2\\sin 2x\\cos 2x \\\\[16pt]\n&= 2(2\\sin x\\cos x)(2\\cos^2\\hspace{-0.05em}x - 1) \\\\[8pt]\n&= 4\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg[2\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\bigg)^2\\hspace{-0.2em}-1\\bigg] \\\\[16pt]\n&= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{9}{8} - 1\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n\\sin 4x \\hspace{0.525em}&= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{7}}{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA822","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA822\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Show that $2x-9+\\dfrac{5}{x+1} = \\dfrac{2x^2-7x-4}{x+1}$,\n    $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x\\neq -1$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, solve the equation\n    $2\\sin 2\\theta - 9 + \\dfrac{5}{\\sin 2\\theta + 1} =  0$ for\n    $0\\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$, $\\theta \\neq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Expanding the left hand side, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x - 9 + \\dfrac{5}{x+1} &= \\dfrac{(2x - 9)(x+1)+5}{x+1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2x^2-7x-9+5}{x+1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2x^2-7x-4}{x+1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using part (a), we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2\\sin 2\\theta - 9 + \\dfrac{5}{\\sin 2\\theta + 1} &=  0 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{2\\sin^2 2\\theta - 7 \\sin 2\\theta - 4}{\\sin 2\\theta + 1} &=  0 \\\\[12pt]\n    %2\\sin^2 2\\theta - 7 \\sin 2\\theta - 4 &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    (2\\sin 2\\theta + 1) (\\sin 2\\theta - 4) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    (2\\sin 2\\theta + 1) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since }\\sin 2\\theta \\neq 4] \\\\[12pt]\n    \\sin 2\\theta &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    2\\theta &= \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\theta = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA882","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA882\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nIt is given that $\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta=-\\dfrac{5}{3}$, where\n$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}<\\theta<\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$. Find the exact value of\n$\\cot \\theta$.","markScheme":"Using the definition of $\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n%\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta &= -\\dfrac{5}{3} \\\\[10pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta} &= -\\dfrac{5}{3} \\\\[11pt]\n\\sin\\theta &= -\\dfrac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, by the Pythagorean\nidentity, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos \\theta &= \\pm \\sqrt{1-\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^2} \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\pm \\sqrt{1-\\dfrac{9}{25}}\\\\[11pt]\n&= \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16}{25}} \\\\[11pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\hspace{3em}  \\left[\\text{ since }\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}<\\theta<\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute in the definition of $\\cot \\theta$, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cot \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin\\theta} \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\dfrac{-4/5}{-3/5} \\\\[11pt]\n&\\hspace{0.35em}= \\dfrac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA883","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA883\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nIt is given that $\\sec\\theta=-\\frac{6}{5}$, where $\\pi<\\theta<2\\pi$.\nFind the exact value of $\\tan \\theta$.","markScheme":"Using the definition of $\\sec\\theta$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n%\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta &= -\\dfrac{5}{3} \\\\[10pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta} &= -\\dfrac{6}{5} \\\\[11pt]\n\\cos\\theta &= -\\dfrac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, by the Pythagorean\nidentity, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin \\theta &= \\pm \\sqrt{1-\\left(-\\dfrac{5}{6}\\right)^2} \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\pm \\sqrt{1-\\dfrac{25}{36}}\\\\[11pt]\n&= \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{11}{36}} \\\\[11pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{11}}{6} \\hspace{3em}  \\left[\\text{ since }\\pi<\\theta<2\\pi \\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute in the definition of $\\tan \\theta$, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta} \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\dfrac{-\\sqrt{11}/6}{-5/6} \\\\[11pt]\n&\\hspace{0.35em}= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA941","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA941\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}-\\dfrac{x}{4}\\right)$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. Show that the $y$-intercept of $f(x)$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Find the least positive value of $x$ for which $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. Substituting $x=0$ into $f(x)$, we find that the $y$-intercept is at $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)$. \n\n    Noting that $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, and using the compound angle identity for sine, we have \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\sin(A - B) &= \\sin\\,A\\,\\cos\\,B - \\cos\\,A\\,\\sin\\,B \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) &= \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\[10pt] \n\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The $y$-intercept of $f(x)$ is $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}}\\,\\,$ as required.\n\n2. Let $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{12} - \\dfrac{x}{4}$ so that the equation becomes $\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{12} - \\dfrac{x}{4} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{x}{4} &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{12} - \\theta \\\\[10pt]\n\n    x &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - 4\\theta\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We want $x$ to be as small as possible while still being positive, hence we need the largest value for $\\theta$ such that $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - 4\\theta > 0$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - 4\\theta &> 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    - 4\\theta &> - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\theta &< \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we require the largest value of $\\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ that satisfies the equation $\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. \n\n    Using the fact that $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we have a principal value for $\\theta$ in quadrant 1, $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$, as shown in the following diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Trigonometric%20Identities%20%26%20Equations/images/AA941a.PNG\" alt=\"AA941a\">\n\n    But $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} > \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$. \n\n    Using the properties of the sine function, the largest value of $\\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ for which $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is $-\\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = -\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$. Hence $\\theta = -\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - 4\\theta \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - 4\\left(-\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{16\\pi}{3} \\\\[10pt]\n\n   &\\hspace{-0.535em}\\boxed{x = \\dfrac{17\\pi}{3}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA149","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA149\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $f(x) = x^2\\arcsin(x)$, for $-1 \\leq x \\leq 1$.\n\n1.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Write down the range of $f$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Solve the inequality $\\left|x^2\\arcsin(x)\\right| > 0.5$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Unassigned/images/116e48b3daf01abd56171298ff9c296be1c29e27.svg\" alt=\"116e48b3daf01abd56171298ff9c296be1c29e27.svg\">\n\n2.\n    $\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $|x^2\\arcsin(x)| = 0.5$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.4em} x^2\\arcsin(x) &= \\pm 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= \\pm 0.761 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclcrcl}\n    -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq x^2\\arcsin(x) &<& -0.5 \\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& 0.5 &<& x^2\\arcsin(x) \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[9pt]\n    -1 \\leq x &<& -0.761 \\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\text{ or }\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& 0.761 &<& x \\leq 1 \\\\\n    \\end{array} \\hspace{0.9em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the set of solutions\n    to the inequality $|x^2\\arcsin(x)| > 0.5$ is\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     [-1,-0.761)\\cup(0.761,1]\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA284","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA284\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve $\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\sin x) - \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\cos x) = 1$, for\n$0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Unassigned/images/eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\" alt=\"eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\">\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms and the right triangle above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.1em} \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\sin x) - \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\cos x) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}\\hspace{-0.2em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\Big) &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\tan x) &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\\tan x &= \\sqrt{3} \\\\[10pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi \\hspace{2.3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}] \\\\[6pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2.1em} x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA299","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA299\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Show that $\\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) = \\log_4 \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve\n    $\\log_4(\\sqrt{10}\\cos x) = \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7)$, for\n    $0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) &= \\dfrac{\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7)}{\\log_4 16} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7)}{2\\log_4 4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\log_4 \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\hspace{2.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the double angle formula for cosine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.75em} \\log_4(\\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x) &= \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) \\\\[14pt]\n    \\log_4(\\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x) &= \\log_4 \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\hspace{3em} \\\\[14pt]\n    \\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\\\[14pt]\n    10\\cos^2 x &= \\cos 2x + 7 \\\\[14pt]\n    10\\cos^2 x &= (2\\cos^2x - 1) + 7  \\\\[14pt]\n    8\\cos^2 x &= 6 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= \\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\cos x &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\hspace{1em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA646","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA646\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Show that $\\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} = \\log_4 (3-\\cos 2x)$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve\n    $\\log_4 (3\\sin x) \\hspace{0.15em}+\\hspace{0.15em} \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}$,\n    for\n    $0 \\hspace{-0.05em}<\\hspace{-0.05em} x \\hspace{-0.05em}<\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} &= \\dfrac{\\log_4 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}}{\\log_4 2} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log_4 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}}{\\frac{1}{2}\\log_4 4} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2 \\log_4 \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\log_4 4 = 1$}] \\\\[18pt]\n    &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\dfrac{1}{4} &= \\textstyle \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{3.5em} \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\log_4 4 &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[7pt]\n    \\textstyle \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\log_4\\sqrt{2} &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{since $\\textstyle 4^{1/4} = \\sqrt{2}$}\\hspace{0.05em}] \\\\[10pt]\n    \\textstyle \\log_4 (3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x)\\hspace{-0.1em} &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\\\[10pt]\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 3-\\cos 2x  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $0 < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 3 - (1 - 2\\sin^2 x) \\hspace{1.3em} [\\text{by double angle identity}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 2 + 2\\sin^2 x \\\\[9pt]\n    \\textstyle 2\\sin^2 x - 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + 2 &= 0 \\hspace{2.1em} [\\text{rearranging terms to form quadratic}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2} \\pm \\sqrt{18-4\\cdot2\\cdot2}}{4} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\sin x \\neq \\sqrt{2}$}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.1em} x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{2}$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA927","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA927\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nConsider the function defined by $f(x) = \\dfrac{7}{x^2+6x-7}$ for $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x\\neq -7$, $x\\neq 1$.\n\n1. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$, showing the values of any axes intercepts, the coordinates of any local maxima and minima, and the graphs of any asymptotes. [6 marks]\n\nNext, consider the function $g$ defined by $g(x)=\\dfrac{7}{x^2+6x-7}$ for $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x> 1$.\n\n2. Show that $g^{-1}(x)=-3 + \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x+7}{x}}$. [6 marks]\n\n3. State the domain of $g^{-1}$. [1 mark]\n\nNow, consider the function $h$ defined by $h(x)=\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{7}\\right)$.\n\n4. Given that $\\left(h\\circ g\\right)(a) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$, find the value of $a$. Give your answer in the form $p+q\\sqrt{2}$ where $p$, $q\\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. First we note that the function is undefined for $x=-7$ and $x=1$. Therefore there are two vertical asymptotes, $x=-7$ and $x=1$.\n\n    To determine the horizontal asymptote, we examine the behaviour of the function as $x \\to \\pm \\infty$.\n\n    $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to \\pm \\infty}}f(x)=0$\n\n    Therefore the horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.\n\n    By calculating $f(0)$, we find that the $y$-intercept is $(0,-1)$.\n\n    Next, we find the $x$-intercepts by solving the equation $f(x)=0$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    f(x) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{7}{x^2+6x-7} &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    7 &\\neq 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    There are no $x$-intercepts.\n\n    Now we need to determine any maxima or minima. To do this, we note that the reciprocal function is \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{f(x)} &= \\dfrac{x^2+6x-7}{7} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{7}(x+7)(x-1)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The graph of the reciprocal function is a parabola:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Unassigned/images/AA927a.PNG\" alt=\"AA927a\">\n\n    The reciprocal function has a minimum at $\\left(-3,-\\dfrac{16}{7}\\right)$ and therefore $f(x)$ has a maximum at $\\left(-3,-\\dfrac{7}{16}\\right)$.\n\n    Finally, we need to determine the behaviour of the function around the vertical asymptotes. Again, we can use the reciprocal function to determine this.\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to -7^-$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^+$. Therefore $f(x) \\to +\\infty$.$\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to -7^+$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^-$. Therefore $f(x) \\to -\\infty$.$\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to 1^-$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^-$. Therefore $f(x) \\to -\\infty$. $\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\bullet$ As $x \\to 1^+$, $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)} \\to 0^+$. Therefore $f(x) \\to +\\infty$.$\\\\[10pt]$\n\n    Therefore the graph of $y=f(x)$ is as follows:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Unassigned/images/AA927b.PNG\" alt=\"AA927b\">\n\n2. By switching $x$ and $g(x)$, we create the inverse function:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x &= \\dfrac{7}{y^2+6y-7} \\hspace{3em}[\\text{where }y=g^{-1}(x)]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that the domain of $g$ is $x>1$, the range of $g^{-1}$ is $g^{-1}(x)>1$. Therefore $y>1$. \n\n    Rearranging to make $y$ the subject, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    y^2+6y-7 &= \\dfrac{7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y^2 + 6y  &= 7+\\dfrac{7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    (y + 3)^2 - 9 &= 7+\\dfrac{7}{x} \\hspace{3em}[\\text{By completing the square}]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    (y + 3)^2 &= 16 + \\dfrac{7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{16x + 7}{x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    y + 3 &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x + 7}{x}} \\hspace{3em}[\\text{Since $y>1$}]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    y &= -3 + \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x + 7}{x}} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3em}\\boxed{g^{-1}(x) = -3 + \\sqrt{\\dfrac{16x+7}{x}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. We could note that $g(x)=f(x)$ for $x>1$, and therefore using our graph from part (a), the range is $g(x)>0$. Therefore the domain of $g^{-1}(x)$ is $x>0$\n\n4. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    g(a)&=\\dfrac{7}{a^2+6a-7}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    And\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(h\\circ g\\right)(a) &= \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{g(a)}{7}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7}\\right)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7}\\right) &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\cos\\left(\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7}\\right)\\right) &= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{1}{a^2+6a-7} &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{3em}\\bigg[\\text{since }\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    a^2+6a-7 &= 2\\\\[8pt]\n\n    a^2+6a &= 9 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    (a+3)^2-9 &= 9 \\hspace{3em}[\\text{By completing the square}]\\\\[8pt]\n\n    (a+3)^2 &= 18 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a+3 &= \\pm\\sqrt{18} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a+3 &= \\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.55em}\\boxed{ a = -3 + 3\\sqrt{2}} \\hspace{3em} [\\hspace{0.05em}\\text{since }a>1\\hspace{0.05em}]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Vectors & Planes","questions":[{"ref":"AA1024","marks":"19","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1024\n\n**Maximum mark: 19**\n\nConsider a plane $\\Pi_1$ that passes through the points A$(1,-5,1)$, B$(2,9,-2)$ and C$(4,-3,2)$.\n\n1. Find the cartesian equation of plane $\\Pi_1$. [5 marks]\n\nA second and third plane have equations\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pi_2&: \\hspace{2em} x +5y -2z = -1 \\\\[6pt]\n\\Pi_3&: \\hspace{0.75em}-2x + ay +4z = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n2. 1. Determine the value of $a$ for which the system of equations formed by the three planes has multiple solutions.\n\n    2. In this case, describe geometrically the relationship between the three planes.\n\n    3. Find the equation of the line of intersection in parametric form. [7 marks]\n\n3. Show that the three planes can never have a unique point of intersection. [3 marks]\n\n4. Show that, for $a>1$, the planes will always intersect in three pairs of parallel lines. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. We find $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ and $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$, two vectors parallel to plane $\\Pi_1$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\textbf{b} - \\textbf{a} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 9\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -2\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} - \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -5\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 14\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -3\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\vec{\\text{AC}} &= \\textbf{c} - \\textbf{a} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 4\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -3\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 2\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} - \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -5\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the vector product formula, we find a common perpendicular to $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ and $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$, which is the normal to plane $\\Pi_1$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\times \\vec{\\text{AC}} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 14\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -3\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] (14)(1) - (-3)(2) \\\\ (-3)(3) - (1)(1) \\\\ (1)(2) - (14)(3) \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 20\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -10\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -40\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= 10\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -4\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Plane $\\Pi_1$ can be expressed as $2x-y-4z=d$. Substituting A$(1,-5,1)$, we find\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    2(1)-(-5)-4(1) &= d \\\\[8pt]\n\n    d &= 3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The cartesian equation of $\\Pi_1$ is $\\boxed{2x-y-4z=3}$\n\n2. 1. If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented matrix, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/aa1024a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1024a\">\n\n        Hence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination, we get\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/aa1024b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1024b\">\n\n        From row 3, we note that if $a+10=0$, we have a row of zeroes, indicating multiple solutions.\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{When $a=-10$, the system has multiple solutions.}}$$\n\n    2. When $a=-10$, the system becomes\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\Pi_1&: \\hspace{2.35em} 2x - y -4z = 3 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\Pi_2&: \\hspace{2.5em} x +5y -2z = -1 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        \\Pi_3&: \\hspace{0.75em} -2x -10y +4z = 2\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The equation of $\\Pi_3$ is a linear multiple of the equation of $\\Pi_2$. They are therefore the same plane.\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{$\\Pi_2$ and $\\Pi_3$ are coincidental, $\\Pi_1$ intersects both planes in a straight line.}}$$\n\n    \n\n    3. From the final augmented matrix in part (b) (i), we note from row 2 that $11y = -5$.\n\n        Therefore $y=-\\dfrac{5}{11}$. Substituting this value into row 1 of the same matrix, we form an expression for $x$ in terms of $z$:\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2x + \\dfrac{5}{11} - 4z &= 3 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        x &= \\dfrac{14}{11} + 2z\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Let $z = \\lambda$. The parametric equation of the line is\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{x = \\dfrac{14}{11}+2\\lambda \\text{ , } y = -\\dfrac{5}{11}+0\\lambda \\text{ , } z = 0 + \\lambda}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n3. We know from part (b) (i) that the system is not unique when $a=-10$. We now consider the case where $a \\neq -10$.\n\n    Referring back to the final augmented matrix from part (b) (i), we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/aa1024c.PNG\" alt=\"aa1024c\">\n\n    From row 3 we have that $(a+10)y = 0$. Given that $a \\neq -10$, we must have $y=0$.\n\n    But from row 2, $y = -\\dfrac{5}{11}$. Hence we have a contradiction. If $a \\neq -10$, the system is inconsistent.\n\n    $$\\boxed{\\text{For any value of $a$, the system does not have a unique solution.}}$$\n\n4. The line of intersection of two planes is parallel to the cross product of their normal vectors. Using the vector product formula, we first find the cross product of the normal vectors to planes $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -4\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 5\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -2\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] (-1)(-2) - (-4)(5) \\\\ (-4)(1) - (2)(-2) \\\\ (2)(5) - (-1)(1) \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 11 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= 11\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Next, we find the cross product of the normal vectors to planes $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_3$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -4\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ a\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 4\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] (-1)(4) - (-4)(a) \\\\ (-4)(-2) - (2)(4) \\\\ (2)(a) - (-1)(-2) \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4a-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2a-2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= (2a-2)\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Finally, we find the cross product of the normal vectors to planes $\\Pi_2$ and $\\Pi_3$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 5\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -2\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ a\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 4\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] (5)(4) - (-2)(a) \\\\ (-2)(-2) - (1)(4) \\\\ (1)(a) - (5)(-2) \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2a+20 \\\\ 0 \\\\ a+10 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[18pt]\n\n    &= (a+10)\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    For $a>1$, the direction vectors of all three lines of intersection are positive multiples of the vector $2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{k}$. Hence,\n\n    $$\\boxed{\\text{For $a>1$, the planes always intersect in three pairs of parallel lines.}}$$"},{"ref":"AA1065","marks":"18","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1065\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nConsider the following three points $A(4,-2,-2)$, $B(-3,-1,2)$ and $C(1,-3,-1)$.\n\n1.  1.  Find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$.\n\n    2. Find  $\\overrightarrow{AB} \\times \\overrightarrow{AC}$.\n\n    3. Hence, show that the area of the triangle formed by $A, B$ and $C$ is $\\tfrac{5}{2}\\sqrt{6}$.[7 marks]\n\nPlane $\\Pi_1$ contains the points $A$, $B$ and $C$.\n\n2. Find the Cartesian equation of plane $\\Pi_1$. [2 marks]\n\nPlane $\\Pi_2$ has the Cartesian equation $-2x+2y=8+4z$.\n\n3. Describe the geometrical relationship between $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$. [1 mark]\n\nPoint $D$ is a reflection of point $A$ in $\\Pi_2$.\n\nThe pyramid $ABCD$ is such that its base is formed using the points $A, B$ and $C$ with its vertex being at point $D$.\n\n4. Find the volume of pyramid $ABCD$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Consider\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\overrightarrow{OB} - \\overrightarrow{OA} &\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\overrightarrow{OC} - \\overrightarrow{OA}\\\\[3pt]\n            &=\\begin{pmatrix}-3-(4) \\\\-1-(-2) \\\\2-(-2)\\\\\\end{pmatrix} &\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\begin{pmatrix}1-(4) \\\\-3-(-2) \\\\-1-(-2)\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[12pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix}-7 \\\\1 \\\\4\\\\\\end{pmatrix} &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix}-3 \\\\-1 \\\\1\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2. Using the formula for the vector cross product\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n            \\overrightarrow{AB} \\times \\overrightarrow{AC}&=\\begin{pmatrix}1(1)-4(-1) \\\\4(-3)-(-7)(1) \\\\-7(-1)-1(-3)\\\\\\end{pmatrix} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix}5\\\\-5\\\\10\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    3. The area of triangle $ABC$ is half of the parallelogram formed from $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, using the formula for the area of a parallelogram we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        A_{\\triangle ABC}&=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left|\\overrightarrow{AB} \\times \\overrightarrow{AC}\\right|\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(5)^2+(-5)^2+(10)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{150}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\tfrac{5}{2}\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2. The direction of the answer from (a)(ii) is the normal to the plane, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textbf{n}&=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{pmatrix}5\\\\-5\\\\10\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\\\2\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the normal plane equation and choosing point $A$ as a point on the plane (note: you can choose point $B$ or $C$ and get the same result)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\\\z\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\\\2\\\\\\end{pmatrix}&=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\\\2\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\-2\\\\-2\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-4.525em}x-y+2z=2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3. Rearrange $\\Pi_2$ and then divide by $-2$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -2x+2y-4z&=8\\\\[3pt]\n        x-y+2z&=-4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    By comparing the coefficients of $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ we can see that\n\n    $\\Pi_1 \\text{ and } \\Pi_2 \\text{ are parallel}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n4. We know that $D$ is a reflection in $\\Pi_2$ therefore $AD$ is twice the distance between the planes $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To find the distance between the two planes we must find the line through $A$ which is perpendicular to $\\Pi_1$, the line intersects $\\Pi_2$ at $X$. This is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/vec1.png\" alt=\"vec1\">\n\n    We can write the relationship $AD=2AX$.\n\n    The equation of the line through $A$ and parallel to $\\textbf{n}$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textbf{r}&=\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\-2\\\\-2\\\\\\end{pmatrix}+\\lambda \\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\\\2\\\\\\end{pmatrix} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the Cartesian form of $\\Pi_2$ to find the value of $\\lambda$ at point $X$, where the line intersects the plane.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (4+\\lambda)-(-2-\\lambda)+2(-2+2\\lambda)&=-4\\\\\n        \\lambda&=-1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textbf{r}&=\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\-2\\\\-2\\\\\\end{pmatrix}+(-1) \\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\\\2\\\\\\end{pmatrix}\\\\\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\-1\\\\-4\\\\\\end{pmatrix} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we can write the coordinates $X(3, -1, -4)$.\n\n    Next we need to find the distance between points $A$ and $X$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        D_{AX}&=\\sqrt{(4-3)^2+(-2-(-1))^2+(-2-(-4))^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n        &=\\sqrt{6}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can find the length of $AD$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        AD&=2AX\\\\\n        &=2\\times\\sqrt{6}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the formula for the volume of a pyramid\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_{p}&=\\frac{1}{3}Ah\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Where $A$ is the base area and $h$ is the height.\n\n    We can recognise that the height of the pyramid is the length $AD$ and the base area is the answer we found in (a)(iii), this is shown below (not to scale)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/Screenshot%202024-02-06%20at%206.00.32%20PM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-02-06 at 6.00.32 PM\">\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_p&=\\frac{1}{3}\\times 2\\sqrt{6}\\times \\tfrac{5}{2}\\sqrt{6}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\boxed{10 \\text{ units}^3} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1069","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1069\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nFind the minimum distance between the point $A(-5, 0, 1)$ and the plane\n\n$\\textbf{\\textit{r}}= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\lambda\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\mu \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}$\n\nin the form $a\\sqrt{b}$, where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.","markScheme":"The vector equation of the plane provides us with a point on the plane, $(1, 1, 2)$, and two direction vectors in the plane.\n\nTo begin with we must find the Cartesian form of the plane. This will allow us to find the coordinates of any points of intersection of the plane and a line.\n\nIf we cross the two direction vectors in the plane we will find the normal to the plane. Hence\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\textbf{n}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[15pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1\\times 2 - (-4) \\times (3) \\\\ 3 \\times (-3) - (-1)\\times 2 \\\\ (-1)\\times(-4) - 1\\times(-3) \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[15pt]\n\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 14\\\\ -7\\\\ 7\\end{pmatrix*}\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nAs we just need the normal as a direction we can divide the components by $7$, hence \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\textbf{n}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe can use the normal equation of a plane with the point $(1, 1, 2)$ given in the question.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\textbf{r} \\cdot \\textbf{n}&=\\textbf{n} \\cdot \\textbf{a} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[15pt]\n\n    2x-y+z&=3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThe point $B$ is the point on the plane closest to point $A$. The line through $A$ and $B$ is parallel to $\\textbf{n}$. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/aa1069a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1069a\">\n\nHence we can write the vector equation of the line through $A$ and $B$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n   \\textbf{r}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} + t\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWhere $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nNext we find the coordinates of point $B$ by substituting the vector equation of the line into the Cartesian form of the plane and solving for $t$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    2(-5+2t)-(-t)+(1+t)&=3\\\\[3pt]\n\n    -10+4t+t+1+t&=3\\\\[3pt]\n\n    t&=2 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nHence the position vector of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n   &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} + 2\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[15pt]\n\n   &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nFinally we must find the distance between $A(-5, 0, 1)$ and $(-1, -2, 3)$\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    D^2&=(-5-(-1))^2+(0-(-2))^2+(1-3)^2) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n\n    D&=\\sqrt{24}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore the shortest distance between the point and plane is $\\boxed{ 2\\sqrt{6}}$.\n\n$$                    \n\n\\begin{aligned}            \n\n\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}    \n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1070","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1070\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nTwo remote controlled toy planes, taking part in an organised display, took off from a field some time ago.\n\nThe planes, $A$ and $B$, have position vectors with respect to an origin $O$ given respectively as\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n   \\mathbf{r_A}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} + t\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[15pt]\n\n   \\mathbf{r_B}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0.5 \\end{pmatrix*} + t\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWhere $t$ is measured in seconds and $0\\leq t\\leq 2$.\n\nEntries in each column vector give the displacement east of $O$, the displacement north of $O$ and the distance above the ground, all measured in metres.\n\n1. At time $t=k$ seconds the toy planes are closest together.\n\n    1. Find $k$.\n\n    \n\n    2. Hence, write down the distance between the planes at this time.[5 marks]\n\nThe directions of the two planes are known to be non-parallel.\n\n2. 1. Show that the lines describing the flight paths of the planes are skew.\n\n    \n\n    2. Find the minimum distance between the flight paths of the planes. [12 marks]\n\n    \n\nDue to safety regulations, the distance between the toy planes at any time $t$ must always exceed $4$ metres.\n\n3. Comment on your answers with regards to the safety regulations. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Consider the diagram below showing the flight paths of the two planes and the distance between them\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/aa1070a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1070a\">\n\n    At any particular time we can find the distance, $D$, between points $A$ and $B$.\n\n    1. Hence, we can create the function $D(t)$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            D(t)&=\\sqrt{(1-3t-(-3+2t))^2+(4+2t-(2-t))^2+(3+t-(0.5+1.5t))^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[8pt]\n\n            &=\\sqrt{(4-5t)^2+(2+3t)^2+(2.5-0.5t)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We don't need to simplify this expression any further, we can use the G.D.C. to sketch the function and find the minimum point.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/aa1070b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1070b\">\n\n        $$                    \n\n        \\begin{aligned}            \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}    \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence, $\\boxed{k=0.445 \\text{ seconds}.}$\n\n        $$                    \n\n        \\begin{aligned}            \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}    \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. $\\boxed{D=4.41 \\text{ metres}.}$\n\n        $$                    \n\n        \\begin{aligned}            \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}    \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. 1. We are no longer concerned with the relative position of the planes at a particular time as we are  comparing the flight paths. To represent this we need to introduce different parameters for each line.\n\n        $$                    \n\n        \\begin{aligned}            \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}    \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n        Hence, the two flight paths can be written with the parameters $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ as follows\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n       \\mathbf{r_A}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\lambda\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[15pt]\n\n       \\mathbf{r_B}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0.5 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\mu\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        To show the lines are skew we attempt to solve for the $x$ and $y$ components\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        1-3\\lambda&=-3+2\\mu\\\\\n\n        4+2\\lambda&=2-\\mu \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Solving on a G.D.C.  $\\lambda=-8$ and $\\mu=14$.\n\n        $$                    \n\n        \\begin{aligned}            \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}    \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We now find the $z-$component for both flight paths using these values.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        z_A&=3+(-8)1 &z_B=0.5+(14)1.5\\\\\n\n        &=-5    &=21.5\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The directions of the planes are known to be non-parallel and $\\boxed{z_A \\neq z_B \\text{ therefore the lines are skew.}}$\n\n        $$                    \n\n        \\begin{aligned}            \n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}    \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. Consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and the direction vectors for each of the two lines, $\\textbf{d}_A$ and $\\textbf{d}_B$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/aa1070c.PNG\" alt=\"aa1070c\">\n\n        We can write $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ in terms of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\overrightarrow{BA}&=\\mathbf{r}_B - \\mathbf{r}_A \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[15pt]\n\n        &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0.5 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\mu\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix*}-\\left(\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\lambda\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\right)\\\\[15pt]\n\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4+2\\mu +3\\lambda \\\\ -2-\\mu-2\\lambda \\\\ -2.5+1.5\\mu-\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        When the skew lines are closest $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ is perpendicular to both $\\textbf{d}_A$ and $\\textbf{d}_B$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(R1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence we can form two equations, in terms of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$, using the directions of each line.\n\n    \n\n        $\\hspace{11em}\\overrightarrow{BA} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}_A=0 \\hspace{11em} \\overrightarrow{BA} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}_B=0$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        $\\hspace{2em }\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4+2\\mu +3\\lambda \\\\ -2-\\mu-2\\lambda \\\\ -2.5+1.5\\mu-\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}=0 \\hspace{2em } \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4+2\\mu +3\\lambda \\\\ -2-\\mu-2\\lambda \\\\ -2.5+1.5\\mu-\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix*}=0$ \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Expanding and simplifying we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        -3(-4+2\\mu +3\\lambda) + 2(-2-\\mu-2\\lambda) + (-2.5+1.5\\mu-\\lambda)&=0 \\\\\n\n        12 - 6\\mu - 9\\lambda - 4 - 2\\mu - 4\\lambda - 2.5 + 1.5\\mu - \\lambda&=0  \\\\\n\n        5.5 - 6.5\\mu - 14\\lambda &= 0  \\tag*{\\textcolor{red}{[1]}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        And\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2(-4+2\\mu +3\\lambda) - (-2-\\mu-2\\lambda) + 1.5(-2.5+1.5\\mu-\\lambda)&=0 \\\\\n\n        -8 + 4\\mu + 6\\lambda + 2 + \\mu + 2\\lambda - 3.75 + 2.25\\mu - 1.5\\lambda&=0 \\\\\n\n        -9.75 + 7.25\\mu + 6.5\\lambda &= 0  \\tag*{\\textcolor{red}{[2]}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        By solving equations $\\textcolor{red}{[1]}$ and $\\textcolor{red}{[2]}$ using a G.D.C. we find that $\\lambda=-0.3966...$ and $\\mu=1.7004...$ \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We can use the values of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ to find the coordinates of points $A$ and $B$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathbf{r_A}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\lambda\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n       &\\mathbf{r_B}=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0.5 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\mu\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[15pt]\n\n       &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 -3\\times-0.3966...  \\\\ 4 + 2\\times-0.3966...   \\\\ 3+1\\times-0.3966...  \\end{pmatrix*} \n\n       &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3+2\\times 1.7004... \\\\ 2-1\\times 1.7004... \\\\ 0.5+1.5 \\times 1.7004... \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Simplifying\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathbf{r_A}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2.1898...  \\\\3.2067...\\\\ 2.6033...\\end{pmatrix*} \n\n       &\\mathbf{r_B}=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0.4008... \\\\ 0.2995...\\\\ 3.0506... \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We have now found the coordinates of $A$ and $B$ (to 3sf)\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &A(2.19, 3.21, 2.60) \n\n        &B(0.401,0.300, 3.05) \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The minimum distance between the two flight paths is the distance between these two points.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        D_{\\text{min}}&=\\sqrt{(2.1898...-0.4008...)^2+(3.2067...-0.2995...)^2+(2.6033...-3.0506...)^2}\\\\\n\n        &=3.443... \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The minimum distance between the two flight paths is $\\boxed{3.44 \\text{ metres}}$.\n\n3. Referring to answer (b)(ii) the minimum distance between the flight paths is smaller than $4$m, however,\n\n    Answer (a)(ii) tells us that the distance between the two planes is always greater than $4$m for all times $t$ therefore adhering to the safety guidelines.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1161","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1161\n\nConsider the points $A(1,0,-1)$, $B(-1,2,1)$ and $C(3,-1,-1)$.\n\nThe plane $\\Pi$ contains the points $A$, $B$ and $C$.\n\n1.  1.  Find the column vectors $\\vec{AB}$ and $\\vec{AC}$.\n    2.  Hence, show that the  vector $\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}$  is normal to plane $\\Pi$.\n    3.  Hence, show that the Cartesian equation of  plane $\\Pi$ is $x+2y-z=2$. [7 marks]\n\nA fourth point $D(3,1,-3)$ does **not** lie in plane $\\Pi$.\n\n2.  Write down a vector equation of the line through $D$ which is perpendicular to plane $\\Pi$. [1 mark]\n\nPoint $D$ is reflected in plane $\\Pi$. The reflected point is labelled $E$.\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of point $E$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  First find $\\vec{AB}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\vec{AB} &= \\vec{OB} - \\vec{OA}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}-\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[12pt]\n            \\vec{AB}\\hspace{1em}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Next find $\\vec{AC}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\vec{AC} &= \\vec{OC} - \\vec{OA}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}-\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[12pt]\n            \\vec{AC}\\hspace{1em}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  As both\n        $\\vec{AB}$ and $\\vec{AC}$ are vectors in plane $\\Pi$ then the normal vector to the plane will be their vector cross product, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\bm{n}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[12pt]\n            &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2(0)-2(-1) \\\\ 2(2)-(-2)(0) \\\\ -2(-1)-2(2) \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[12pt]\n            &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        3.  To use formula for the equation of a plane we need the normal to the plane and the position vector of any point in the plane. In this answer we will use point $A$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot \\bm{n}&=\\vec{OA}\\cdot \\bm{n}\\\\[12pt]\n            \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n            2x+4y-2z&=1(2)+0(4)+(-1)(-2)\\\\[6pt]\n            2x+4y-2z&=4\\\\[6pt]\n            &\\hspace{-4.6em}x+2y-z=2 \\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  The correct answer can have any multiple of the normal vector. Here is the most likely answer\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\bm{r}=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*}+\\lambda\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  First we realise that point $E$ lies on the line found in part (b).\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/1161_a.png\" alt=\"1161_a\">\n\n    We can now state the steps to finding the coordinates of $E$.\n    - First find the point $X$ which is the intersection point of the line and plane.\n    - Find the vector $\\vec{DX}$.\n    - Note that $\\vec{DX}=\\vec{XE}$.\n    \\item Find the coordinates of point $E$.\n\n    Let's begin by finding the intersection point of the plane and line, $X$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/1161_b.png\" alt=\"1161_b\">\n\n    Recall, from part (b) we know that any point on the line can be represented using a single parameter. Therefore we can write the general coordinates of any point on the line as $(3+\\lambda, 1+2\\lambda, -3-\\lambda)$.\n\n    To find the intersection of the plane and line we substitute this into the Cartesian plane equation found in part (a)(iii) and solve for $\\lambda$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        3+\\lambda + 2(1+2\\lambda) - (-3-\\lambda)&=2\\\\[6pt]\n        3+\\lambda+2+4\\lambda+3+\\lambda&=2\\\\[6pt]\n        8+6\\lambda&=2\\\\[6pt]\n        6\\lambda&=-6\\\\[6pt]\n        \\lambda&=-1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting this into the general point we find $X$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (3+(-1), 1+2(-1), -3-(-1))\\\\[6pt]\n        X(2, -1, -2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next we find the vector $\\vec{DX}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{DX}&=\\vec{OX}-\\vec{OD}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}-\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/1161_c.png\" alt=\"1161_c\">\n\n    As shown above we notice that $\\textcolor{red}{\\vec{DX}}$ is equal to $\\textcolor{blue}{\\vec{XE}}$ as $E$ is a reflection in the plane, hence we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{DX}&=\\vec{XE}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\vec{DX}&=\\vec{OE}-\\vec{OX}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}&=\\vec{OE}-\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[12pt]\n        \\vec{OE}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}+\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\hspace{0.525em}E(1,-3,-1)$"},{"ref":"AA1297","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1297\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nConsider the planes $\\Pi_1$, $\\Pi_2$ and  $\\Pi_3$ with the following equations.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\Pi_1&:\\hspace{1.8em}x-y+z=-4\\\\[3pt]\n    \\Pi_2&:\\hspace{1.3em}2x+y-z=-1\\\\[3pt]\n    \\Pi_3&:\\hspace{1em}-x+y+kz=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe system of equations that represents the three planes is inconsistent.\n\n1.  1.  Find $k$.\n\n    2.  Describe the geometrical relationship of the three planes.[3 marks]\n\n$L$ is the line of intersection between $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ and it crosses the $xy-$plane at point $D$.\n\n2.  1.  Verify that the vector equation of $L$ can be written as\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\bm{r}=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -\\tfrac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}+ \\lambda \\begin{pmatrix*}[c]0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence find the coordinates of point $D$.[4 marks]\n\nThe point $D'$ is found by reflecting $D$ in $\\Pi_3$.\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of $D'$.  [9 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  A system is inconsistent when there is no solution of any type - either a single solution or infinite solutions.\n\n        We should use Guassian Elimination to attempt to solve the system in terms of $k$\n\n        Let's begin by adding the equations for $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_3$ this gives\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x-y+z+(-x+y+kz)&=-4+(-3)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n            z+kz&=-7\\\\[6pt]\n            z(k+1)&=-7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can see here that if $k=-1$ then we have $z \\times 0=-7$ which leads to an impossible (inconsistent) equation to solve.\n\n        Therefore when $k=-1$ the system is inconsistent.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  There are a number of different geometrical arrangements of planes that could lead to an inconsistent solution.\n\n        Let's check for parallelism first.\n\n        If the L.H.S. of one plane is a multiple of the L.H.S. of another plane and the constants on the R.H.S.'s are different then the planes are parallel.\n\n        Using $k=-1$ and rewriting $\\Pi_3$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\Pi_1&:\\hspace{1.8em}x-y+z=-4\\\\[3pt]\n            \\Pi_3&:\\hspace{1em}-(x-y+z)=-3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can't do a similar manipulation with $\\Pi_2$.\n\n        $\\text{Hence plane }\\Pi_1\\text{ and }\\Pi_3\\text{ are parallel}$\n\n        and\n\n        $\\Pi_2\\text{ is oblique as it is distinct to }\\Pi_1\\text{ and }\\Pi_3\\text{ and parallel to neither.}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Although not required by the question, here is a visual showing the two parallel planes and the oblique plane.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA1297-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1297-a1\">\n\n2.  1.  There are a number of ways to answer this question.\n\n        One way to check that the line is the intersection of both planes is to verify that the line lies in both planes for any value of $\\lambda$.\n\n        Hence we will substitute each component of line $L$ into the L.H.S. of $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ and ensure the result equals the R.H.S.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        First let's try $\\Pi_1$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\Pi_1:\\,\\, \\text{L.H.S.}&= x-y+z\\\\[6pt]\n            &= \\left(-\\tfrac{5}{3}\\right)-(\\lambda)+\\left(-\\tfrac{7}{3}+\\lambda\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-\\tfrac{12}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-4\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\text{R.H.S.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        As the L.H.S. equals the R.H.S. for any value of $\\lambda$ this means $L$ lies in $\\Pi_1$.\n\n        Let's do the same again for $\\Pi_2$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\Pi_2:\\,\\, \\text{L.H.S.}&=  2x+y-z\\\\[6pt]\n            &= 2\\left(-\\tfrac{5}{3}\\right)+(\\lambda)-\\left(-\\tfrac{7}{3}+\\lambda\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-\\tfrac{3}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-1\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\text{R.H.S.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $L$ also lies in $\\Pi_2$.\n\n        $\\text{As }L\\text{ lies in both }\\Pi_1\\text{ and }\\Pi_2$\n        and as\n        $\\Pi_1\\text{ and }\\Pi_2\\text{ are not parallel it must be the line of intersection of both planes.}$\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The value of the $z-$component of a line that intersects the $xy-$plane is zero.\n\n        This diagram can help you visualise it\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA1297-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1297-a2\">\n\n        We need to find the value of $\\lambda$ that makes the $z-$component of $L$ equal zero.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            r_z: \\, -\\tfrac{7}{3}+\\lambda&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n            \\lambda&=\\tfrac{7}{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of $D$ when $\\lambda=\\tfrac{7}{3}$ are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &D\\left(-\\tfrac{5}{3}+0,0+ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\times 1, -\\tfrac{7}{3}+\\tfrac{7}{3}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n           &\\boxed{D\\left(-\\tfrac{5}{3},\\tfrac{7}{3},0 \\right)}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Before we begin answering the question let's outline a method.\n\n    We can make a rough sketch of the information to help us.\n\n    The diagram below shows $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_3$.\n\n    $\\Pi_2$ is not shown.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA1297-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1297-a3\">\n\n    In order to find $D'$ we need to use point $D$.\n\n    Due to the symmetry of reflection, the point $D'$ will lie on the line which is normal to plane $\\Pi_1$ and goes through $D$.\n\n    This is shown as the red line above.\n\n    The point $X$ is the point on $\\Pi_3$ which lies on the red line.\n\n    Note that the vector from $D$ to $X$, shown in red, is equal to the vector from $X$ to $D'$, shown in blue.\n\n    Hence, we can form an equation using displacement vectors such that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\overrightarrow{DX}&=\\overrightarrow{XD'}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The will allow us to find the coordinates of $D'$.\n\n    We can summarise this information into four steps.\n\n    - Find the equation of the red line.\n\n    - Find the position vector of $\\overrightarrow{OX}$.\n\n    - Solve the displacement vector equation above to find the coordinates of $D'$.\n\n    Let's begin by finding the equation of the red line.\n\n    Recall the Cartesian form of plane $1$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\Pi_1&:\\hspace{1.8em}x-y+z=-4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Written in this form, the coefficients of the L.H.S. give the normal, $\\bm{n_1}$, to $\\Pi_1$. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\bm{n_1}=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore using the coordinates of $D$ and $\\bm{n_1}$ as the direction we can write down the vector equation of the red line, $\\bm{r_R}$, as\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bm{r_R}=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -\\tfrac{5}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}+\\mu\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $\\mu \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n    The next step is to find the coordinates of $X$.\n\n    For this we substitute the components of $\\bm{r_R}$ into the plane equation for $\\Pi_3$ and solve for $\\mu$. Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -x+y-z&=-3\\\\[6pt]\n        -\\left(-\\tfrac{5}{3}+\\mu\\right)+\\left(\\tfrac{7}{3}-\\mu\\right)-(\\mu)&=-3\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\mu&=\\tfrac{7}{3}\\hspace{2em}\\text{[solved on G.D.C.]}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the position vector of $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\overrightarrow{OX}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -\\tfrac{5}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}+\\left(\\tfrac{7}{3}\\right)\\times\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -\\tfrac{5}{3}+\\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3}-\\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] \\tfrac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the coordinates of $D\\left(-\\tfrac{5}{3},\\tfrac{7}{3},0 \\right)$ we can write down the position vector $\\overrightarrow{OD}$ but first we can rewrite the equation mentioned earlier in terms of position vectors\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\overrightarrow{DX}&=\\overrightarrow{XD'}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\overrightarrow{OX}-\\overrightarrow{OD}&=\\overrightarrow{OD'}-\\overrightarrow{OX}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting in what we have found we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] \\tfrac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}-\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -\\tfrac{5}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}&=\\overrightarrow{OD'}-\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] \\tfrac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can simplify and rearrange to find $\\overrightarrow{OD'}$, giving\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] \\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}&=\\overrightarrow{OD'}-\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] \\tfrac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\overrightarrow{OD'}&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] \\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}+\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] \\tfrac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\tfrac{7}{3} \\end{pmatrix*}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -\\tfrac{7}{3} \\\\ \\tfrac{14}{3}\\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of point $D'$ are $\\left(3,-\\tfrac{7}{3},\\frac{14}{3}\\right)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1346","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1346\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the three planes\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\Pi_1&:-x+y-2z=-1\\\\[6pt]\n    \\Pi_2&:2x-y-z=d\\\\[6pt]\n    \\Pi_3&:-x-y+cz=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $c,d \\in \\mathbb{Q^+}$.\nThe three planes intersect at the line $L$.\n\n1.    Show that $c=8$ and $d=0.5$. [4 marks]\n\n2.    Determine the shortest distance from $L$ to the origin $(0,0,0)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. There are several approaches to solve this system of equations.\n\n    In this solution, we will use Gaussian elimination.\n\n    We start by combining $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ to eliminate $x$.\n\n    $2 \\times \\textcolor{red}{\\Pi_1} + \\textcolor{blue}{\\Pi_2}$ gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2\\textcolor{red}{(-x+y-2z)}+\\textcolor{blue}{(2x-y-z)}&=2\\textcolor{red}{(-1)}+\\textcolor{blue}{d}\\\\[6pt]\n    y-5z&=-2+d \\hspace{2em}[1]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next we will combine $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_3$ to eliminate $x$.\n\n    $\\textcolor{red}{\\Pi_1} -\\textcolor{green}{\\Pi_3}$ gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{red}{-x+y-2z}-\\textcolor{green}{(-x-y+cz)}&=\\textcolor{red}{-1}-\\textcolor{green}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    -x+y-2z+x+y-cz)&=-3\\\\[6pt]\n    2y-(2+c)z&=-3 \\hspace{2em}[2]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We now have a system of two equations with two unknowns.\n\n    Next, we combine [1] and [2] to eliminate $y$.\n\n    $2 \\times \\textcolor{red}{[1]} - \\textcolor{blue}{[2]}$ gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2 \\times \\textcolor{red}{(y-5z)}-\\textcolor{blue}{(2y-(2+c)z)}&=2\\times\\textcolor{red}{(-2+d)}-\\textcolor{blue}{(-3)}\\\\[6pt]\n    -10z+(2+c)z&=-4+2d+3\\\\[6pt]\n    cz-8z&=-1+2d\\\\[6pt]\n    (c-8)z&=2d-1\\hspace{2em}[3]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    For all three planes to intersect at a line, there must be an infinite number of solutions for $z$ in equation $[3]$.\n\n    Therefore $[3]$ must be of the form\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{red}{0}z&=\\textcolor{blue}{0}\\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\textcolor{red}{(c-8)}z&=\\textcolor{blue}{2d-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, we can find $c$ and $d$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    c-8&=0&&&2d-1&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    c&=8&&&d&=0.5\\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Using the values of $\\textcolor{red}{c=8}$ and $\\textcolor{blue}{d=0.5}$ we can use a G.D.C. to solve the following system of equations.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -x+y-2z&=-1\\\\[6pt]\n    2x-y-z&=\\textcolor{blue}{0.5}\\\\[6pt]\n    -x-y+\\textcolor{red}{8}z&=2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The output from our G.D.C. will look something like this\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (3\\cdot s-0.5,5 \\cdot s-1.5, s )\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This output represents a line with a parameter of $s$.\n\n    We could also express this answer as a vector equation of a line as such\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bm{r}&=\\begin{pmatrix} -0.5 \\\\ -1.5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}+s\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we let the distance from a point on $\\textcolor{blue}{L}$ to the origin be $\\textcolor{red}{D}$ then we are looking to find the minimum value of $D$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA1346-b1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1346-b1\">\n\n    Using the distance formula to find the distance between $(3\\cdot s-0.5,5 \\cdot s-1.5, s )$ and the origin, we can generate a function for $D$ in terms of the parameter $s$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    D&=\\sqrt{(3s-0.5)^2+(5s-1.5)^2+s^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Sketching this on a G.D.C., using $x$ for $s$, we find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA1346-b2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1346-b2\">\n\n    the minimum value of $D$ to be $0.431\\,\\text{units}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textcolor{black}{\\textbf{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note, this minimum value of $D$ (the $y$-coordinate) occurs when $s=0.2571...$ (the $x$-coordinate)."},{"ref":"AA307","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA307\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe points A and B are given by A$(-1,2,0)$ and B$(2,4,1)$.\n\n1.  Find a vector equation of the line $\\mathit{L}$ passing through the\n    points A and B. [3 marks]\n\nThe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ is defined by the equation $x + 2y - 2z = 18$.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of the point of intersection of the\n    line $\\mathit{L}$ with the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.4em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} - \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the vector equation of a line formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathbf{r} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.4em} \\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.2em} + \\hspace{0.1em} t\\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3em} [t \\in \\mathbb{R}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Substituting $x = -1 + 3t$, $y = 2 + 2t$, $z = t$ in the equation of\n    $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x + 2y - 2z &= 18 \\hspace{4.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    (-1+3t) + 2(2+2t) - 2t &= 18 \\\\[6pt]\n    3 + 5t &= 18 \\\\[6pt]\n    t &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, evaluating $x = -1 + 3t$, $y = 2 + 2t$,\n    $z = t$ for $t = 3$, we obtain\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rcrcl}\n    x &=& -1+3(3) &=& 8 \\\\[7pt]\n    y &=& 2 + 2(3) &=& 8 \\\\[7pt]\n    && z &=& 3\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the point of intersection of\n    $\\mathit{L}$ and $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ are\n     $(8, 8, 3)$"},{"ref":"AA308","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA308\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the vectors\n$\\mathbf{u} = 3\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} - 4\\textbf{k}$ and\n$\\mathbf{v} = \\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the Cartesian equation of the plane containing the\n    vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$, and passing through the point\n    P$(1,-2,2)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the vector product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.3em} \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} &= (3\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} - 4\\textbf{k})\\times(\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}1 & 2 & 0  \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= [0-(-8)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [0-(-4)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [6-(-1)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 8\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 7\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11.2em} \\mathbf{n} &= 8\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 7\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{normal vector to the plane}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{p} &= \\phantom{8}\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.5em} [= \\vec{\\text{OP}}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the scalar product formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.55em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{p} &= (8)(1) + (-4)(-2) + (7)(2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 30\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the equation of a plane formula,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{p} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    8x- 4y + 7z = 30 \\hspace{2.7em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA309","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA309\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ containing the\npoints A$(3,-1,3)$ and B$(4,1,-1)$, and\nperpendicular to the plane $2x - 5y + z = 10$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n&= (4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}) - (3\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} - 4\\textbf{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nA normal vector\nto the plane $2x - 5y + z = 10$ is\n$\\textbf{n}_1 = 2\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k}$.\n\nHence, using the vector product formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.5em} \\mathbf{n}_2 &= \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\times \\mathbf{n}_1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$}] \\\\[8pt]\n&= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & -5 & 1 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n&= [2-20]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [1-(-8)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [-5-4]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[8pt]\n&= -18\\textbf{i} - 9\\textbf{j} - 9\\textbf{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using\nthe scalar product formula, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.2em} \\mathbf{n}_2\\cdot \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= (-18)(3) + (-9)(-1) + (-9)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n&= -72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the equation of a plane formula, we\nfind\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{n}_2\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}_2\\cdot \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{0.5em} -18x- 9y - 9z &= -72 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n2x + y + z = 8\n$$"},{"ref":"AA311","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA311\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the following two planes:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathit{\\Pi}_1 &:\\hspace{0.5em} x - 5y + 3z = 10, \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathit{\\Pi}_2 &:\\hspace{0.5em} 2x + 6y - z = 12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFind\nthe acute angle between $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$ and $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$, giving\nyour answer correct to the nearest degree.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} \\mathbf{n}_1 &=  \\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathbf{n}_2 &=  2\\textbf{i} + 6\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3.8em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{n}_1\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}_2}{|\\mathbf{n}_1||\\mathbf{n}_2|} \\\\[6pt]\n\\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{(1)(2) + (-5)(6) + (3)(-1)}{\\big[\\sqrt{1^2+(-5)^2+3^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{2^2+6^2+(-1)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[10pt]\n\\theta &\\approx \\ang{145}  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the acute angle between $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$ and $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$ is\n$\\ang{180} - \\ang{145} = \\hspace{0.525em}\\ang{35}$"},{"ref":"AA312","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA312\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe lines $\\mathit{L}_1$ and $\\mathit{L}_2$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathit{L}_1 &:\\hspace{0.5em} \\dfrac{x+2}{-3} = \\dfrac{y-1}{4} = \\dfrac{z+6}{2}, \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathit{L}_2 &:\\hspace{0.5em} \\dfrac{x+2}{6} = \\dfrac{y-1}{2} = \\dfrac{z+6}{-4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ contains both $\\mathit{L}_1$ and\n$\\mathit{L}_2$.\n\n1.  Find the Cartesian equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}$. [4 marks]\n\nThe line $\\mathit{L}_3$ is passing through the point P$(1,-2,5)$ and\nperpendicular to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$.\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of the point where $\\mathit{L}_3$\n    intersects $\\mathit{\\Pi}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathit{L}_1 &:\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathbf{r}_1 = \\mathbf{a}_0 + t\\mathbf{b}_1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathit{L}_2 &:\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathbf{r}_2 = \\mathbf{a}_0 + t\\mathbf{b}_2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    where\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathbf{a}_0 &= -2\\textbf{i}+\\phantom{1}\\textbf{j}-6\\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{b}_1 &= -3\\textbf{i}+4\\textbf{j}+2\\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{b}_2 &= \\phantom{-}6\\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}-4\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the vector product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.9em} \\mathbf{n} &= \\mathbf{b}_1 \\hspace{-0.1em}\\times \\mathbf{b}_2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$}] \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\hspace{0.75em}\\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -4 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= [-16-4]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [12-12]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [-6-24]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -20\\textbf{i}-30\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the scalar\n    product formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} \\mathbf{n} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}_0 &= (-20)(-2) + (0)(1) + (-30)(-6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 220\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the equation of a plane formula,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathbf{n} \\cdot \\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}_0 \\hspace{1.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    -20x - 30z &= 220 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    2x + 3z = -22\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the vector equation of a line formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathit{L}_3 &:\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.1em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3em} [t \\in \\mathbb{R}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $x = 1 + 2t$, $y = -2$, $z = 5 + 3t$ in the equation of\n    $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x + 3z &= -22 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2(1+2t) + 3(5+3t) &= -22 \\\\[6pt]\n    17 + 13t &= -22 \\\\[6pt]\n    13t &= -39 \\\\[6pt]\n    t &= -3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, evaluating $x = 1 + 2t$, $y = -2$,\n    $z = 5 + 3t$ for $t = -3$, we obtain\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rcrcl}\n    x &=& 1+2(-3) &=& -5 \\\\[7pt]\n    && y &=& -2 \\\\[7pt]\n    z &=& 5 + 3(-3) &=& -4\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the point where\n    $\\mathit{L}_3$ meets $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ are\n     $(-5, -2, -4)$"},{"ref":"AA313","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA313\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe points A and B have coordinates A$(1,0,4)$ and B$(2,2,3)$ relative\nto an origin O.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{OA}}\\times\\vec{\\text{OB}}$.\n\n    2.  Determine the area of the triangle OAB, giving your answer\n        correct to two decimal\n        places.\n\n    3.  Find the Cartesian equation of the plane OAB. [5 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the vector equation of the line $\\mathit{L}_1$ containing\n        the points A and B.\n\n    The line $\\mathit{L}_2$ has vector equation\n    $\\mathbf{r} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -9 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}s\\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix*}$,\n    $s\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n    2.  Determine whether the lines $\\mathit{L}_1$ and\n        $\\mathit{L}_2$ are parallel, skew or intersecting. If they\n        intersect, find the coordinates of the point of intersection.\n        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the vector product formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.4em} \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OB}} &= (\\textbf{i} + 4\\textbf{k})\\times(2\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[c] \\hspace{0.25em}\\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & 2 & 3 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n        &= [0-8]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [3-8]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [2-0]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -8\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} A_{\\triangle\\text{OAB}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big|\\vec{\\text{OA}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OB}}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big|\\hspace{-0.15em}-8\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(-8)^2 + 5^2 + 2^2} \\\\[10pt]\n        &\\approx 4.82\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{9.3em} \\mathbf{n} &= -8\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{normal vector to the plane}] \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathbf{b} &= \\phantom{-}2\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2em} [= \\vec{\\text{OB}}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using the scalar product formula, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{b} &= (-8)(2) + (5)(2) + (2)(3) \\hspace{0.9em}  \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using the equation of a plane\n        formula, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        -8x + 5y + 2z = 0 \\hspace{5em}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} - \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}  \\hspace{0.35em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, using the vector equation of a line formula, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathbf{r} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{7.4em} \\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.2em} + \\hspace{0.1em} t\\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3em} [t \\in \\mathbb{R}]\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the direction vectors of $\\mathit{L}_1$ and\n        $\\mathit{L}_2$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{10em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix*} \\neq \\hspace{0.1em}k\\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for any $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence $\\mathit{L}_1$ and $\\mathit{L}_2$ are not parallel. They\n        are either skew or intersecting.\n\n        Using the vector equations of $\\mathit{L}_1$ and $\\mathit{L}_2$,\n        we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        -3s &= 1 + t \\hspace{1.55em} \\\\[6pt]\n        12 + s &= 2t  \\\\[6pt]\n        -9-4s &= 4 - t\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Solving the system of\n        linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{9.9em} s = -2 \\hspace{0.5em}\\&\\hspace{0.5em} t = 5 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        If we try to verify the equation $(3)$ for values $(4)$, we\n        obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        -9 - 4(-2) &= 4 - 5 \\hspace{2.95em} \\\\[6pt]\n        -1 &= -1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $\\mathit{L}_1$ and $\\mathit{L}_2$\n        are indeed intersecting.\n\n        Substituting $t = 5$ in the vector equation of $\\mathit{L}_1$,\n        we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathbf{r} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*} + \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the point of intersection of $\\mathit{L}_1$ and\n        $\\mathit{L}_2$ has coordinates\n         $(6,10,-1)$"},{"ref":"AA315","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA315\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nThe points A, B and C have the following position vectors with respect\nto an origin O.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}+3\\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n\\vec{\\text{OB}} &= 3\\textbf{i}-2\\textbf{j}+\\textbf{k}  \\\\[6pt]\n\\vec{\\text{OC}} &=  3\\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}-\\textbf{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Find the vector equation of the line (AB). [3 marks]\n\n2.  Determine whether the lines (AB) and (OC) are parallel, skew or\n    intersecting.[6 marks]\n\n3.  Find the Cartesian equation of the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$, that\n    passes through the point A and is perpendicular to $\\vec{\\text{OC}}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n4.  Show that the line (AB) lies in the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$.\n    [2 marks]\n\nThe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$ contains the points O, A and C and the plane\n$\\mathit{\\Pi}_3$ contains the points O, B and C.\n\n5.  Verify that $4\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}$ is\n    perpendicular to the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$. [3 marks]\n\n6.  Find a vector perpendicular to the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_3$.\n    [1 mark]\n\n7.  Find the acute angle between the planes $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$\n    and $\\mathit{\\Pi}_3$, giving your answer correct to the nearest\n    degree. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.3em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (3\\textbf{i}-2\\textbf{j}+\\textbf{k}) - (\\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}+3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\textbf{i}-4\\textbf{j}-2\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the vector equation of a line formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathbf{r} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} + s\\hspace{0.05em}\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\hspace{3.5em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.25em} \\mathbf{r} = \\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}+3\\textbf{k} + s\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\textbf{i}-4\\textbf{j}-2\\textbf{k}) \\hspace{3em} [s \\in \\mathbb{R}]\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{(OC)}:&\\hspace{0.75em} \\mathbf{r} = t\\hspace{0.05em}(3\\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}-\\textbf{k}) \\hspace{3em} [t \\in \\mathbb{R}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the direction vectors of (AB) and (OC), we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.55em} 2\\textbf{i}-4\\textbf{j}-2\\textbf{k} \\neq p\\hspace{0.05em}(3\\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}-\\textbf{k}) \\hspace{2.8em} [\\text{for any $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence (AB) and (OC) are not parallel. They are either skew or\n    intersecting.\n\n    Using the vector equations of (AB) and (OC), we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 + 2s &= 3t  \\\\[6pt]\n    2 - 4s &= 2t  \\\\[6pt]\n    3 - 2s &= -t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving the system of linear\n    equations $(2)$-$(3)$ for $s$ and $t$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} s = 1 \\hspace{0.5em}\\&\\hspace{0.5em} t = -1 \\hspace{9.9em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we try to verify the equation $(1)$ for values $(4)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 + 2(1) &\\neq 3(-1) \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3 &\\neq -3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the system of linear equations\n    $(1)$-$(2)$-$(3)$ has no solution.\n\n    Therefore (AB) and (OC) are not intersecting. They are skew.\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.5em} \\mathbf{n}_1 &= 3\\textbf{i}+2\\textbf{j}-\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{a} &= \\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2em} [= \\vec{\\text{OA}}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathbf{n}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{a} &= (3)(1) + (2)(2) + (-1)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the equation of a plane formula, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} \\mathbf{n}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    3x + 2y - z = 4  \\hspace{3.6em}\n    $$\n\n4.  If we substitute $x = 1+2s$, $y = 2-4s$, $z = 3-2s$ in the left hand\n    side of the equation $(5)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.35em} 3x + 2y - z &= 3(1+2s) + 2(2-4s) - (3-2s) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (3 + 4 - 3) + (6s - 8s + 2s) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for any  $s \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence (AB) lies in the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$.\n\n5.  Using the scalar product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (4\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}) \\cdot \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= (4\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}) \\cdot (\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (4)(1) + (-5)(2) + (2)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the scalar product formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (4\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}) \\cdot \\vec{\\text{OC}} &= (4\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}) \\cdot (3\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (4)(3) + (-5)(2) + (2)(-1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $4\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}$\n    is perpendicular to the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$.\n\n6.  Using the vector product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} \\vec{\\text{OB}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OC}} &= (3\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k})\\times(3\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\hspace{0.25em}\\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}3 & 2 & -1 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= [2-2]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [-3-3]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [6-(-6)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 6\\textbf{j} + 12\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}$\n    is perpendicular to the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}_3$.\n\n7.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.25em} \\mathbf{n}_2 &= 4\\textbf{i} - 5\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{n}_3 &= \\phantom{4}\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}\\phantom{ - 5\\textbf{j}} \\hspace{3.415em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_3$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{n}_2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}_3}{|\\mathbf{n}_2||\\mathbf{n}_3|} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{(4)(0) + (-5)(1) + (2)(2)}{\\big[\\sqrt{4^2+(-5)^2+2^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{0^2+1^2+2^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\theta &\\approx \\ang{94}  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the acute angle between $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$ and $\\mathit{\\Pi}_3$\n    is $\\ang{180} - \\ang{94} = \\hspace{0.525em}\\ang{86}$"},{"ref":"AA318","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA318\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nTwo planes have equations\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathit{\\Pi}_1:\\hspace{0.5em} 2x + 4y + z = 9 \\hspace{0.75em}\\text{and}\\hspace{0.75em} \\mathit{\\Pi}_2:\\hspace{0.5em} 2x + y - z = 1. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Find the cosine of the angle between the two planes, giving your\n    answer\n\n    in the form $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{p}}{q}$ where $p, q \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n     [4 marks]\n\nLet $\\mathit{L}$ be the line of intersection of the two planes.\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that $\\mathit{L}$ has direction\n        $5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}$.\n\n    2.  Show that the point P$(0,2,1)$ lies on both planes.\n    3.  Write down the vector equation of $\\mathit{L}$. [6 marks]\n\nQ is the point on $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$ with coordinates $(a,1,b)$.\n\n3.  Given that the vector $\\vec{\\text{PQ}}$ is perpendicular to\n    $\\mathit{L}$, find the value of $a$ and the\n    value of $b$. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Show that $\\text{PQ} = \\sqrt{33}$. [1 mark]\n\nThe point R lies on $\\mathit{L}$ and $\\text{P\\^QR} = \\ang{30}$.\n\n5.  Find the coordinates of the two possible positions of R.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} \\mathbf{n}_1 &= 2\\textbf{i}+4\\textbf{j}+\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{n}_2 &= 2\\textbf{i}+\\phantom{4}\\textbf{j}-\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{n}_1\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}_2}{|\\mathbf{n}_1||\\mathbf{n}_2|} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(2)(2) + (4)(1) + (1)(-1)}{\\big[\\sqrt{2^2+4^2+1^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{2^2+1^2+(-1)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4 + 4 - 1}{\\big[\\sqrt{4 + 16 + 1}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{4 + 1 + 1}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7}{\\big[\\sqrt{21}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7}{3\\sqrt{14}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{14}}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the vector product formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{n}_1\\times\\mathbf{n}_2 &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\hspace{0.25em} \\textbf{i} & \\hspace{0.8em}\\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & 1 & -1 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n        &= [-4-1]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [-2-2]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [2-8]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -5\\textbf{i} + 4\\textbf{j} - 6\\textbf{k} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the direction of $L$ is\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        -(\\mathbf{n}_1\\times\\mathbf{n}_2) &= -(-5\\textbf{i} + 4\\textbf{j} - 6\\textbf{k}) \\hspace{5.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the coordinates of P$(0,2,1)$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathit{\\Pi}_1 :&\\hspace{0.75em} 2(0) + 4(2) + 1 = 9 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathit{\\Pi}_2 :&\\hspace{0.75em} 2(0) + 2 - 1 = 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence P lies on both planes.\n\n    3.\n        $\\mathbf{r} = 2\\textbf{j}+\\textbf{k} + t\\hspace{0.05em}(5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}),\\hspace{0.25em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} \\vec{\\text{PQ}} &= \\vec{\\text{OQ}} - \\vec{\\text{OP}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (a\\textbf{i}+\\textbf{j}+b\\textbf{k}) - (2\\textbf{j}+\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= a\\textbf{i}-\\textbf{j}+(b-1)\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{PQ}} \\cdot (5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}) &= 0 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{PQ}} \\perp L$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{0.8em} (a\\textbf{i}-\\textbf{j}+(b-1)\\textbf{k}) \\cdot (5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    (a)(5) + (-1)(-4) + (b-1)(6) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    5a + 6b &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting the coordinates\n    of Q$(a,1,b)$ in the equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11em} 2(a) + 4(1) + b &= 9 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since Q lies on $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    2a + b &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if we solve the system\n    of linear equations $(2)$-$(3)$ for $a$ and $b$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a = 4\\hspace{0.75em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.75em}b = -3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Substituting $a = 4$ and $b = -3$ in the expression $(1)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.4em} \\vec{\\text{PQ}} &= 4\\textbf{i}-\\textbf{j}+(-3-1)\\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\\textbf{i}-\\textbf{j}-4\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    evaluating the length of $\\vec{\\text{PQ}}$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{PQ} &= |4\\textbf{i}-\\textbf{j}-4\\textbf{k}| \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{4^2 + 1^2 + 4^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{33}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/6fbc0cff0e0fb28260be5afe58baf5846511d2d8.svg\" alt=\"6fbc0cff0e0fb28260be5afe58baf5846511d2d8.svg\">\n\n    Since R lies on $L$, the vector $\\vec{\\text{PR}}$ is parallel to\n    $5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}$.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.2em} \\vec{\\text{PR}} &= \\lambda\\hspace{0.05em}(5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for some $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Evaluating the length of $\\vec{\\text{PR}}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} \\text{PR} &= |\\lambda\\hspace{0.05em}(5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k})| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= |\\lambda|\\textstyle \\sqrt{5^2 + (-4)^2 + 6^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= |\\lambda|\\textstyle \\sqrt{25 + 16 + 36} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= |\\lambda|\\sqrt{77} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the triangle PQR, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tan \\ang{30} &= \\dfrac{\\text{PR}}{\\text{PQ}} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} &= \\dfrac{|\\lambda|\\sqrt{77}}{\\sqrt{33}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} &= \\dfrac{|\\lambda|\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\[8pt]\n    |\\lambda| &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\lambda &= \\pm \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\vec{\\text{OR}} &= \\vec{\\text{OP}} + \\vec{\\text{PR}} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= (2\\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k}) + \\pm\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\hspace{0.05em}(5\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\pm\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\hspace{0.1em}\\textbf{i} + \\bigg(2 \\mp\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\bigg)\\textbf{j} + \\bigg(1 \\pm \\dfrac{6\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\bigg)\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the two possible positions of R have coordinates\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.525em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{7}}{7}, 2 - \\dfrac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}, 1 + \\dfrac{6\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\bigg)\\hspace{0.75em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.75em}\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{7}}{7}, 2 + \\dfrac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}, 1 - \\dfrac{6\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\bigg)\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA319","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA319\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nA line $\\mathit{L}$ has equations\n$\\dfrac{x-a}{2} = \\dfrac{y-1}{b} = - \\dfrac{z - 1}{2}$ where\n$a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nA plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ has equation $2x + 3y - z = 6$.\n\n1.  Show that $\\mathit{L}$ is not perpendicular to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\mathit{L}$ lies in the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, find the\n    value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [4 marks]\n\nConsider the different case where the acute angle between $\\mathit{L}$\nand $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ is $\\alpha$\n\nwhere $\\alpha = \\arcsin\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}\\bigg)$.\n\n3.\n    1.  Show that $b = 1$.\n\n    2.  Given that $\\mathit{L}$ intersects $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ at $z = -3$,\n        find the value of $a$. [11 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{12em} \\mathbf{n} &= 2\\textbf{i}+3\\textbf{j}-\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3.8em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{d} &= 2\\textbf{i}+b\\textbf{j}-2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Since $\\mathbf{n} \\neq \\lambda\\hspace{0.05em} \\mathbf{d}$, for any\n    $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are not\n    proportional.\n\n    Hence $\\mathit{L}$ is not perpendicular to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$.\n\n2.  Substituting $x = a + 2t$, $y = 1 + bt$, $z = 1 - 2t$ in the\n    equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x + 3y - z &= 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{2em} 2(a + 2t) + 3(1 + bt) - (1 - 2t) &= 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    2a - 4 + (6 + 3b)\\hspace{0.025em}t &= 0 \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{for any $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $t = 0$ in the equation $(1)$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2a - 4 + (6 + 3b)(0) &= 0 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2a - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6.1em} a = 2 \\\\[3pt]\n    $$\n    Hence, substituting\n    $a = 2$ and $t = 1$ in the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2(2) - 4 + (6 + 3b)(1) &= 0 \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    6 + 3b &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7em} b = -2\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.  Letting $\\theta$ be the acute angle between $\\mathbf{n}$ and\n        $\\mathit{L}$, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/a5d6777ef92f4b78f76e57bd13967f09f9481ca1.svg\" alt=\"a5d6777ef92f4b78f76e57bd13967f09f9481ca1.svg\">\n\n        where:\n\n        1\\. $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha$;\n\n        2\\. $\\sin \\alpha = \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}$.\n\n- Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.6em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{n}\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}}{|\\mathbf{n}||\\mathbf{d}|} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha\\Big) &= \\dfrac{(2)(2) + (3)(b) + (-1)(-2)}{\\big[\\sqrt{2^2+3^2+(-1)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{2^2+b^2+(-2)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin \\alpha &= \\dfrac{4 + 3b + 2}{\\big[\\sqrt{4 + 9 + 1}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{4 + b^2 + 4}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} &= \\dfrac{3(2 + b)}{\\big[\\sqrt{14}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{8+b^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[7pt]\n    1 &= \\dfrac{2 + b}{\\sqrt{8 + b^2}} \\\\[14pt]\n    \\textstyle \\sqrt{8 + b^2} &= 2 + b \\\\[15pt]\n    8 + b^2 &= 4 + 4b + b^2 \\\\[16pt]\n    4 &= 4b \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    b = 1 \\hspace{5em}\n    $$\n\n    2.  Substituting $b = 1$ and $z = -3$ in the Cartesian\n    equations of $\\mathit{L}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\dfrac{x-a}{2} &= \\dfrac{y-1}{1} = - \\dfrac{(-3) - 1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{x-a}{2} &= \\hspace{0.125em}y-1\\hspace{0.125em} = 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equations above for $x$ and $y$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= a + 4 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, substituting $x = a + 4$, $y = 3$,\n    $z = -3$ in the equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x + 3y - z &= 6 \\hspace{3.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2(a + 4) + 3(3) - (-3) &= 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    2a + 20 &= 6 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6.9em} a = -7\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA320","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA320\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the vectors\n$\\mathbf{a} = 2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 4\\textbf{k}$ and\n$\\mathbf{b} = \\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + 5\\textbf{k}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the Cartesian equation of the plane containing the\n    vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ and passing through the point\n    P$(2,-1,0)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the vector product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} &= (2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 4\\textbf{k})\\times(\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + 5\\textbf{k}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}1 & -2 & 5 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= [5-8]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [10-(-4)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [-4-1]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -3\\textbf{i} - 14\\textbf{j} -5\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.25em} \\mathbf{n} &= -3\\textbf{i} - 14\\textbf{j} - 5\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{normal vector to the plane}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{p} &= \\phantom{-}2\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j}\\phantom{14 - 5\\textbf{k}} \\hspace{2.5em} [= \\vec{\\text{OP}}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the scalar product formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{p} &= (-3)(2) + (-14)(-1) + (-5)(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the equation of a plane formula, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{p} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    -3x- 14y - 5z = 8 \\hspace{2.7em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA321","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA321\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ has equation $2x - y  + z = 15$.\n\nThe line $L$ is perpendicular to the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ and has a\nvector equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -p \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} +\\lambda\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ p \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\hspace{0.75em} \\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\n1.  Determine the value of $p$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of the point where $L$ meets the plane\n    $\\mathit{\\Pi}$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  The point P$(3,-1,2)$ does not lie on the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$.\n    Find:\n\n    1.  $\\vec{\\text{PA}}$ if A is a general point on $L$;\n    2.  $\\lambda$ such that $\\vec{\\text{PA}}$ is perpendicular to $L$;\n\n    3.  the coordinates of the foot of the perpendicular from P to $L$;\n    4.  the shortest distance from P to $L$. [7 marks]\n\n4.  Given two planes $2x - y  +z = 15$ and $x + 3y - 2z = 8$, find the\n    equation of a third plane which is perpendicular to both of these\n    planes and which cuts the $x$-axis at $-5$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we write down a normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ and a direction\n    vector of $L$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathbf{n} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{0.65em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1.0em} \\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ p \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Since $L$ is perpendicular to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, the vectors\n    $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are parallel.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.5em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} &= \\alpha\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ p \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for some $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the vector equation $(1)$, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -1 &= 2\\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n    1 &= p\\hspace{0.05em}\\alpha \\hspace{1.7em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, if we solve the system of equations $(2)$-$(3)$ for $\\alpha$\n    and $p$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.9em} \\alpha &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} & \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    p = -2 \\hspace{0.85em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substituting $x = 1 - 4\\lambda$, $y = 2+2\\lambda$,\n    $z = 3 - 2\\lambda$ in the equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2x - y  + z &= 15 \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    2(1 - 4\\lambda) - (2+2\\lambda) + (3 - 2\\lambda) &= 15 \\\\[6pt]\n    3 - 12\\lambda &= 15 \\\\[6pt]\n    -12\\lambda &= 12 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\lambda &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coordinates of the point\n    where $L$ meets $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} (1 - 4\\lambda, 2+2\\lambda, 3 - 2\\lambda) &= (1-4(-1), 2+2(-1), 3-2(-1)) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= (5,0,5)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{7em} \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1-4\\lambda \\\\ 2+2\\lambda \\\\ 3-2\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for some $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.2em} \\vec{\\text{PA}} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} - \\vec{\\text{OP}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1-4\\lambda \\\\ 2+2\\lambda \\\\ 3-2\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*} - \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2-4\\lambda \\\\ 3+2\\lambda \\\\ 1-2\\lambda  \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the scalar product formula, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{PA}} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2-4\\lambda \\\\ 3+2\\lambda \\\\ 1-2\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        (-2-4\\lambda)(-4) + (3+2\\lambda)(2) + (1-2\\lambda)(-2) &= 0 \\\\[15pt]\n        12 + 24\\lambda &= 0 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{16.8em} \\lambda = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/c3b98b2066eee6e28559368b830512468bd37c7a.svg\" alt=\"c3b98b2066eee6e28559368b830512468bd37c7a.svg\">\n\n        Evaluating the expression $(4)$ for $\\lambda = -1/2$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3.2em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of A are\n         $(3,1,4)$\n\n    4.  Evaluating the expression $(5)$ for $\\lambda = -1/2$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{PA}} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3.05em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the shortest distance from P to $L$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.8em} \\text{PA} &= |2\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{0^2 + 2^2 + 2^2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2\\sqrt{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathit{\\Pi}_1&:\\hspace{0.5em} 2x - y  + z = 15 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathit{\\Pi}_2&:\\hspace{0.5em} x + 3y - 2z = 8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.5em} \\mathbf{n}_1 &= 2\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k} \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{n}_2 &= \\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the vector product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.8em} \\mathbf{n}_3 &= \\mathbf{n}_1\\times\\mathbf{n}_2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{$\\mathbf{n}_3 \\perp \\mathbf{n}_1$ \\& $\\mathbf{n}_3 \\perp \\mathbf{n}_2$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}1 & 3 & -2 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [2-3]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [-4-1]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [6-(-1)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 7\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the\n    scalar product formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{n}_3\\cdot(-5\\textbf{i}) &= (-1)(-5) + (5)(0) + (7)(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the equation of a plane formula, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{n}_3\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}_3\\cdot(-5\\textbf{i}) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    -x + 5y + 7z = 5 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA322","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA322\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the vectors given by\n$\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j} - 4\\mathbf{k}$ and\n$\\mathbf{v} = a\\mathbf{i} + c\\hspace{0.025em}\\mathbf{k}$, where $a$ and\n$c$ are constants.\n\nIt is given that\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times\\mathbf{v} = 3\\mathbf{i} + b\\mathbf{j} - 6\\mathbf{k}$,\nwhere $b$ is a constant.\n\n1.  Find the value of each of the constants $a, b$ and $c$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the Cartesian equation of the plane containing the\n    vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ and passing through the point\n    P$(3,1,0)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the vector product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[c] \\hspace{0.25em}\\textbf{i} & \\hspace{0.75em}\\textbf{j} & \\hspace{0.7em}\\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}1 & \\hspace{0.75em}3 & -4 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}a & \\hspace{0.75em}0 & \\hspace{0.7em}c \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{6em} 3\\mathbf{i} + b\\mathbf{j} - 6\\mathbf{k} &= [3c-0]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [c-(-4a)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [0-3a]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k} \\\\[12pt]\n    3\\mathbf{i} + b\\mathbf{j} - 6\\mathbf{k} &= 3c\\hspace{0.025em}\\textbf{i} - (c+4a)\\textbf{j} - 3a\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3 &= 3c \\\\[6pt]\n    b &= -(c+4a) \\hspace{1.1em}  \\\\[6pt]\n    -6 &= -3a  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving the equations $(1)$\n    and $(3)$ for $a$ and $c$, we get\n     $a = 2$\n    and  $c = 1$\n\n    Hence, substituting $a = 2$ and $c = 1$ in the equation $(2)$, we\n    find  $b = -9$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11.2em} \\mathbf{n} &= 3\\textbf{i} - 9\\textbf{j} - 6\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{normal vector to the plane}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{p} &= 3\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} \\hspace{5.325em} [= \\vec{\\text{OP}}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.55em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{p} &= (3)(3) + (-9)(1) + (-6)(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the equation of a plane formula, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{p} \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3x- 9y - 6z &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    x- 3y - 2z = 0 \\hspace{2.5em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA325","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA325\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nTwo planes have equations\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathit{\\Pi}_1:\\hspace{0.5em} 2x - 2y + z = 1 \\hspace{0.75em}\\text{and}\\hspace{0.75em} \\mathit{\\Pi}_2:\\hspace{0.5em} x - 4y - z = 5. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Find the cosine of the angle between the two planes, giving your\n    answer\n\n    in the form $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{p}}{q}$ where $p, q \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n     [4 marks]\n\nLet $\\mathit{L}$ be the line of intersection of the two planes.\n\n2.\n    1.  Show that $\\mathit{L}$ has direction\n        $2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}$.\n\n    2.  Show that the point A$(4,1,-5)$ lies on both planes.\n    3.  Write down the vector equation of $\\mathit{L}$. [6 marks]\n\nB is the point on $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$ with coordinates $(a,b,3)$.\n\n3.  Given that the vector $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ is perpendicular to\n    $\\mathit{L}$, find the value of $a$ and the\n    value of $b$. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Show that $\\text{AB} = 12$. [1 mark]\n\nThe point C lies on $\\mathit{L}$ and $\\text{A\\^BC} = \\ang{60}$.\n\n5.  Find the coordinates of the two possible positions of C.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} \\mathbf{n}_1 &= 2\\textbf{i}-2\\textbf{j}+\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{n}_2 &= \\phantom{4}\\textbf{i}-4\\textbf{j}-\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}_2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{n}_1\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}_2}{|\\mathbf{n}_1||\\mathbf{n}_2|} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(2)(1) + (-2)(-4) + (1)(-1)}{\\big[\\sqrt{2^2+(-2)^2+1^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{1^2+(-4)^2+(-1)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2 + 8 - 1}{\\big[\\sqrt{4 + 4 + 1}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{1 + 16 + 1}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{9}{\\big[\\sqrt{9}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{18}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{9}}{\\cancel{9}\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the vector product formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.9em} \\mathbf{n}_1\\times\\mathbf{n}_2 &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\hspace{0.25em} \\textbf{i} & \\hspace{0.8em}\\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}1 & -4 & -1 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n        &= [2-(-4)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [-2-1]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [-8-(-2)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 6\\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{j} - 6\\textbf{k} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the direction of $L$ is\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{1}{3}(\\mathbf{n}_1\\times\\mathbf{n}_2) &= \\dfrac{1}{3}(6\\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{j} - 6\\textbf{k}) \\hspace{5em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the coordinates of A$(4,1,-5)$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathit{\\Pi}_1 :&\\hspace{0.75em} 2(4) - 2(1) - 5 = 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathit{\\Pi}_2 :&\\hspace{0.75em} 4 - 4(1) - (-5) = 5 \\hspace{1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence A lies on both planes.\n\n    3.\n        $\\mathbf{r} = 4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j}-5\\textbf{k} + t\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}),\\hspace{0.25em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.3em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (a\\textbf{i}+b\\textbf{j}+3\\textbf{k}) - (4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j}-5\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (a-4)\\textbf{i}+(b-1)\\textbf{j}+8\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot (2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\perp L$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.1em} ((a-4)\\textbf{i}+(b-1)\\textbf{j}+8\\textbf{k}) \\cdot (2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    (a-4)(2) + (b-1)(1) + (8)(-2) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    2a + b &= 25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting the coordinates\n    of B$(a,b,3)$ in the equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} 2(a) - 2(b) + 3 &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since B lies on $\\mathit{\\Pi}_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    2a - 2b &= -2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if we solve the\n    system of linear equations $(2)$-$(3)$ for $a$ and $b$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a = 8\\hspace{0.75em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.75em}b = 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Substituting $a = 8$ and $b = 9$ in the expression $(1)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.15em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= (8-4)\\textbf{i}+(9-1)\\textbf{j}+8\\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\\textbf{i}+8\\textbf{j}+8\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    evaluating the length of $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{AB} &= |4\\textbf{i}+8\\textbf{j}+8\\textbf{k}| \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{4^2 + 8^2 + 8^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{144} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/2a10c66bfb9743b0d8821a4e9ee0fd42d4140d87.svg\" alt=\"2a10c66bfb9743b0d8821a4e9ee0fd42d4140d87.svg\">\n\n    Since C lies on $L$, the vector $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$ is parallel to\n    $2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}$.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.2em} \\vec{\\text{AC}} &= \\lambda\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for some $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Evaluating the length of $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} \\text{AC} &= |\\lambda\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k})| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= |\\lambda|\\textstyle \\sqrt{2^2 + 1^2 + (-2)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= |\\lambda|\\sqrt{9} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3|\\lambda| \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the triangle ABC, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tan \\ang{60} &= \\dfrac{\\text{AC}}{\\text{AB}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sqrt{3} &= \\dfrac{3|\\lambda| }{12} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sqrt{3} &= \\dfrac{|\\lambda|}{4} \\\\[10pt]\n    |\\lambda| &= 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\[13pt]\n    \\lambda &= \\pm 4\\sqrt{3} \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\vec{\\text{OC}} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} + \\vec{\\text{AC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j}-5\\textbf{k}) \\pm 4\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.05em}(2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} - 2\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (4 \\pm 8\\sqrt{3})\\textbf{i} + (1 \\pm 4\\sqrt{3})\\textbf{j} + (-5 \\mp 8\\sqrt{3})\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the two possible positions of C have coordinates\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.525em}(4 + 8\\sqrt{3}, 1 + 4\\sqrt{3}, -5 - 8\\sqrt{3})\\hspace{0.75em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.75em}(4 - 8\\sqrt{3}, 1 - 4\\sqrt{3}, -5 + 8\\sqrt{3})\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA327","marks":"23","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA327\n\n**Maximum mark: 23**\n\nThe points A, B, C and D have position vectors\n$\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$, relative\nthe $\\text{origin}$ O.\n\nIt is given that $\\vec{\\mathrm{AD}} = \\vec{\\mathrm{BC}}$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Explain why ABCD is a parallelogram.\n\n    2.  Using vector algebra, show that\n        $\\vec{\\mathrm{AB}} = \\vec{\\mathrm{DC}}$. [4 marks]\n\nThe position vectors $\\vec{\\mathrm{OA}}$, $\\vec{\\mathrm{OB}}$,\n$\\vec{\\mathrm{OC}}$ and $\\vec{\\mathrm{OD}}$ are given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} &= 5\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + 3\\mathbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathbf{b} &= p\\mathbf{i} - 6\\mathbf{j} + 5\\mathbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathbf{c} &= 2\\mathbf{i} + q\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathbf{d} &= 6\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j} + r\\mathbf{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $p$, $q$ and $r$ are constants.\n\n2.  Show that $p = 1$, $q = 2$ and $r = 0$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find the area of the parallelogram ABCD. [4 marks]\n\nThe point where the diagonals of ABCD intersect is denoted by M.\n\n4.  Find the vector equation of the straight line $L$ passing\n    through M and normal to the plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ containing ABCD.\n    [4 marks]\n\n5.  Find the Cartesian equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}$. [3 marks]\n\nThe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ cuts the $x$, $y$ and $z$ axes at P, Q and R\nrespectively.\n\n6.\n    1.  Find the coordinates of P, Q and R.\n\n    2.  Find PR. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Given $\\vec{\\mathrm{AD}} = \\vec{\\mathrm{BC}}$, we have\n        1\\. \\[AD\\] and \\[BC\\] is a pair of opposite sides of\n            ABCD;\n\n        2\\. \\[AD\\] and \\[BC\\] are parallel and have the same\n            length.\n\n        Hence ABCD is a parallelogram.\n\n    2.  Using vector algebra, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\mathrm{AD}} &= \\vec{\\mathrm{BC}} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\vec{\\mathrm{OD}} -\\vec{\\mathrm{OA}} &= \\vec{\\mathrm{OC}} -\\vec{\\mathrm{OB}} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\vec{\\mathrm{OB}} -\\vec{\\mathrm{OA}} &= \\vec{\\mathrm{OC}} -\\vec{\\mathrm{OD}} \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\vec{\\mathrm{AB}} = \\vec{\\mathrm{DC}} \\hspace{2.2em}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\mathrm{AD}} &= \\vec{\\mathrm{BC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{d} - \\mathbf{a} &= \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b} \\\\[6pt]\n    (6\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j} + r\\mathbf{k}) - (5\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + 3\\mathbf{k}) &= (2\\mathbf{i} + q\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k}) - (p\\mathbf{i} - 6\\mathbf{j} + 5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{i} + 8\\mathbf{j} + (r-3)\\mathbf{k} &= (2-p)\\mathbf{i} + (q+6)\\mathbf{j} - 3\\mathbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n\n    \\hspace{0.525em} \\begin{array}{rclcl}\n    1 &=& 2 - p &\\hspace{0.5em}\\Rightarrow& \\hspace{0.5em} \\hspace{0.525em}p = 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    8 &=& q + 6 &\\hspace{0.5em}\\Rightarrow& \\hspace{0.5em} \\hspace{0.525em}q = 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    r - 3 &=& -3 &\\hspace{0.5em}\\Rightarrow& \\hspace{0.5em} \\hspace{0.525em}r = 0\n    \\end{array} \\hspace{3.6em}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.9em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (\\mathbf{i} - 6\\mathbf{j} + 5\\mathbf{k}) - (5\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + 3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -4\\mathbf{i} - 4\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} \\vec{\\text{AD}} &= \\mathbf{d} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (6\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j}) - (5\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + 3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\mathbf{i} + 8\\mathbf{j} - 3\\mathbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    using the vector product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\times \\vec{\\text{AD}} &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\hspace{0.75em}\\textbf{k} \\\\ -4 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -3  \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [12-16]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [12-2]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [-32-(-4)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -4\\textbf{i} - 10\\textbf{j} - 28\\textbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    using the area of a parallelogram formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} A_{\\square\\text{ABCD}} &= \\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\times \\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + (-10)^2 + (-28)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{16 + 100 + 784} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{900} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 30\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{12em} \\mathbf{d} &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\times \\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}(-4\\textbf{i} - 10\\textbf{j} - 28\\textbf{k}) \\\\[10pt]\n    &=  2\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 14\\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*} 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 14 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\vec{\\text{OM}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}[\\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{d}] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{position vector of M}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}[(\\mathbf{i} - 6\\mathbf{j} + 5\\mathbf{k}) + (6\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j})] \\hspace{0.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7}{2}\\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{5}{2}\\mathbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the vector equation of a line formula, we find\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\mathbf{r} &= \\vec{\\text{OM}} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathbf{d} \\hspace{3em} \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4.6em} \\mathbf{r} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*} 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 14 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2em} [t \\in \\mathbb{R}]\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11.7em} \\mathbf{n} &= 2\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 14\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{a} &= 5\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + \\phantom{1}3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{A lies on $\\mathit{\\Pi}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{a} &= (2)(5) + (5)(-2) + (14)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 42\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the equation of a plane formula,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.175em} \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{r} &= \\mathbf{n}\\cdot\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    2x + 5y + 14z = 42 \\hspace{4em}\n    $$\n\n6.\n    1.  Substituting $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of $\\mathit{\\Pi}$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        2x + 5(0) + 14(0) &= 42 \\hspace{2em}\\\\[6pt]\n        2x &= 42 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &= 21 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of P are\n         $(21,0,0)$\n\n        Similarly, substituting $x=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of\n        $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        2(0) + 5y + 14(0) &= 42 \\hspace{1.75em} \\\\[6pt]\n        5y &= 42 \\\\[6pt]\n        y &= 8.4 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of Q are\n         $(0,8.4,0)$\n        Similarly, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ in the equation of\n        $\\mathit{\\Pi}$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        2(0) + 5(0) + 14z &= 42 \\hspace{2em}\\\\[6pt]\n        14z &= 42 \\\\[6pt]\n        z &= 3 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the coordinates of R are\n         $(0,0,3)$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{PR}} &= \\vec{\\text{OR}} - \\vec{\\text{OP}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\\textbf{k} - 21\\textbf{i} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= - 21\\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the length of $\\vec{\\text{PR}}$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.9em} \\mathrm{PR} &= |\\hspace{-0.1em}-21\\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{k}| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(-21)^2 + 0^2 + 3^2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{441 + 0 + 9} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{450} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 15\\sqrt{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA351","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA351\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ has the Cartesian equation $x + y + 3z = 5$.\nThe line $L$ has the vector\n\nequation\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}\\lambda\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ p \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.25em},\\hspace{0.25em} \\lambda, p \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nThe acute angle between the line $L$ and\n\nthe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ is $\\ang{60}$. Find the possible values of $p$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{11.1em} \\mathbf{n} &= \\phantom{-}\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathbf{d} &= -\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + p\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ and the line $L$\nis $\\ang{90} - \\ang{60} = \\ang{30}$.\n\nHence, using the (acute) angle between two vectors formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{7.45em} \\cos \\ang{30} &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{n}\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}}{|\\mathbf{n}||\\mathbf{d}|} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} &= \\dfrac{(1)(-1) + (1)(1) + (3)(p)}{\\big[\\sqrt{1^2+1^2+3^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2+p^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[3pt]\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} &= \\dfrac{3p}{\\big[\\sqrt{11}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{p^2 + 2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[9pt]\n\\sqrt{33p^2+66} &= 6p \\\\[12pt]\n33p^2+66 &= 36p^2 \\\\[12pt]\n66 &= 3p^2 \\\\[12pt]\np^2 &= 22 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\np = \\pm \\sqrt{22} \\hspace{3.7em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA648","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA648\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe plane $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ has the Cartesian equation $x - y + pz = 10$,\nwhere $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $p<0$.\n\nThe line $L$ has the vector equation\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.15em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}$,\n$t \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathit{\\Pi}$ and $L$ is $\\ang{30}$. Find the\nvalue of $p$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{12em} \\mathbf{n} &= \\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + p\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.8em} [\\text{normal vector to $\\mathit{\\Pi}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathbf{d} &= \\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.9em} [\\text{direction vector of $L$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ and the direction\nvector $\\mathbf{d}$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta &= \\ang{90} - \\ang{30} \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\ang{60}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the (acute) angle between two\nvectors formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{10.6em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{n}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}}{|\\mathbf{n}||\\mathbf{d}|} \\\\[8pt]\n\\cos \\ang{60} &= \\dfrac{(\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + p\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k})\\cdot(\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k})}{|\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + p\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}||\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k}|} \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2} &= \\dfrac{(1)(1) + (-1)(1) + (p)(1)}{\\big[\\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+p^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{1^2+1^2+1^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2} &= \\dfrac{p}{\\big[\\sqrt{2+p^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[8pt]\n\\textstyle \\sqrt{6 + 3p^2} &= 2p \\\\[10pt]\n6 + 3p^2 &= 4p^2 \\\\[6pt]\n6 &= p^2 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{6.4em} p = - \\sqrt{6} \\hspace{2.8em} [\\text{since $p<0$}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA924","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA924\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider the planes $\\Pi_1$, $\\Pi_2$, $\\Pi_3$ given by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\Pi_1: &\\hspace{6.27mm} 2x + y - z = -3 \\\\[6pt]\n\n\\Pi_2: &\\hspace{4.52mm} x + 5y - 5z = -6 \\\\[6pt]\n\n\\Pi_3: &\\enskip\\hspace{1mm} 3x + 5y - 5z = -7\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n1. Show that the three planes do not intersect. [4 marks]\n\nIt is given that the point Q$(-1,-1,0)$ lies on both $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$.\n\nLet $\\ell$ be the line of intersection of $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$.\n\n2. Find a vector expression for $\\ell$. [4 marks]\n\n3. Show that $\\ell$ is parallel to plane $\\Pi_3$. [2 marks]\n\n4. Hence or otherwise, find the distance between $\\ell$ and $\\Pi_3$ Express your answer in the form $\\dfrac{p}{\\sqrt{q}}$, where $p$, $q\\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented matrix, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA924a.PNG\" alt=\"AA924a\">\n\n    Hence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA924b.PNG\" alt=\"AA924b\">\n\n    Since $0\\neq -0.1$ we conclude that the system has no solution and therefore $\\text{the three planes do not intersect.}$\n\n2. Any vector parallel to $\\Pi_1$ is, by definition, perpendicular to its normal vector. Similarly, any vector parallel to $\\Pi_2$ is perpendicular to its normal vector.\n\n    The line of intersection of $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ has a direction vector that is parallel to both planes and hence is simultaneously perpendicular to both normal vectors.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA924c.PNG\" alt=\"AA924c\">\n\n    We can find a common perpendicular to the normal vectors using the vector product formula.\n\n    The normal vectors to planes $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$ are $n_1 =\\hspace{-0.15em} \\left(\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{matrix} \\,\\,\\,2 \\\\[2pt] \\hspace{0.6em}1 \\\\[2pt] -1 \\end{matrix}\\right)$ and $n_2\\hspace{-0.15em} = \\left(\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{matrix} \\hspace{0.6em}1 \\\\[2pt] \\hspace{0.6em}5 \\\\[2pt] -5 \\end{matrix}\\right)$.\n\n    Using the vector product formula we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    v \\times w &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\left(\\begin{matrix} v_2w_3 - v_3w_2 \\\\[2pt]  v_3w_1 - v_1w_3 \\\\[2pt] v_1w_2 - v_2w_1 \\end{matrix}\\right)\\\\[18pt]\n\n    n_1 \\times n_2 &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\left(\\begin{matrix} (1)(-5) - (-1)(5) \\\\[2pt]  (-1)(1) - (2)(-5) \\\\[2pt] (2)(5) - (1)(1) \\end{matrix}\\right)\\\\[18pt]\n\n    n_1 \\times n_2 &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\left(\\begin{matrix} 0 \\\\[2pt]  9 \\\\[2pt] 9 \\end{matrix}\\right)\\\\[18pt]\n\n    n_1 \\times n_2 &= 9\\hspace{-0.15em}\\left(\\begin{matrix} 0 \\\\[2pt]  1 \\\\[2pt] 1 \\end{matrix}\\right)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the direction vector of line $\\ell$ is $\\hspace{-0.15em} \\left(\\begin{matrix} \\hspace{0em}0 \\\\[2pt] \\hspace{0em}1 \\\\[2pt] 1 \\end{matrix}\\right)$.\n\n    We have that point Q$(-1,-1,0)$ lies on both $\\Pi_1$ and $\\Pi_2$.\n\n    Therefore $\\hspace{-0.15em} \\left(\\begin{matrix} \\hspace{0em}-1 \\\\[2pt] \\hspace{0em}-1 \\\\[2pt] \\hspace{0.6em}0 \\end{matrix}\\right)$ is the position vector of a point on line $\\ell$.\n\n    Using the vector equation of a line, $r = a + \\lambda b$, the vector equation of the line is \n\n    $\n\n    \\ell =\n\n    \\begin{pmatrix}\n\n    -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\n\n    \\end{pmatrix} + \\lambda \n\n    \\begin{pmatrix}\n\n    0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\n\n    \\end{pmatrix}\n\n    $\n\n3. If line $\\ell$ is parallel to plane $\\Pi_3$, it must be perpendicular to its normal vector.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA924d.PNG\" alt=\"AA924d\">\n\n    Therefore we calculate the scalar product of $n_3$ and the direction vector of $\\ell$ (vector $b$ from part (b)).\n\n    $b$, the direction vector of $\\ell$ is $\\hspace{-0.15em} \\left(\\begin{matrix} \\hspace{0em}0 \\\\[2pt] \\hspace{0em}1 \\\\[2pt] 1 \\end{matrix}\\right)$ and $n_3 =\\hspace{-0.15em} \\left(\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{matrix} \\hspace{0.6em}3 \\\\[2pt] \\hspace{0.6em}5 \\\\[2pt] -5 \\end{matrix}\\right)$\n\n    Using the scalar product formula, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    v \\cdot w &= v_1w_1 + v_2w_2 + v_3w_3 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    b \\cdot n_3 &= (0)(3) + (1)(5) + (1)(-5) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Given that the scalar product is zero, line $\\ell$ is perpendicular to the normal to plane $\\Pi_3$.\n\n    $$\\text{Therefore, we conclude that line $\\ell$ is parallel to plane $\\Pi_3$.}$$\n\n4. Since $\\ell$ is parallel to the plane $\\Pi_3$, it is enough to find the distance between any point on $\\ell$ to the plane $\\Pi_3$. We have the point Q$(-1,-1,0)$ on $\\ell$. Following the normal vector will take us to point P on $\\Pi_3$ as shown in the following diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/AA924e.PNG\" alt=\"AA924e\">\n\n    The distance from point Q to point P will be our solution.\n\n    The line passing through points P and Q has equation $r = \\begin{pmatrix}\n\n-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\n\n\\end{pmatrix} + \\mu \n\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\n3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\n\n\\end{pmatrix}$.\n\n    Therefore, any point on this line has coordinates $(-1+3\\mu,-1+5\\mu,-5\\mu)$. If we substitute these coordinates into the plane equation of $\\Pi_3$, we will find the coordinates of point P and hence the value of $\\mu$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    3x + 5y - 5z &= -7 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    3(-1+3\\mu) + 5(-1+5\\mu) - 5(-5\\mu) &= -7 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    -3 +9\\mu -5 +25\\mu +25\\mu &= -7 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    59\\mu &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\mu &= \\dfrac{1}{59}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n   Hence the vector from point Q to point P is the vector $\\dfrac{1}{59} \n\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\n3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\n\n\\end{pmatrix}$.\n\n    The magnitude of this vector is the distance from point Q to point P and therefore the distance between $\\ell$ and $\\Pi_3$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    |QP| &= \\Bigg|\\dfrac{1}{59} \n\n    \\begin{pmatrix}\n\n    3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\n\n    \\end{pmatrix}\\Bigg| \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3^2 + 5^2 + (-5)^2}{59^2}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{59}{59^2}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{59}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The distance between $\\ell$ and $\\Pi_3$ is $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{59}} $"},{"ref":"AA994","marks":"22","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA994\n\n\\[Maximum mark 22\\]\n\nConsider the points $A(5,\\,1,\\,0)$, $B(3,\\,2,\\,-1)$ and $C(1,\\,2,\\,3)$.\n\n1. 1. Find the vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$.\n   2. Hence, find a vector equation, $\\mathbf{r_1}$, of the line, $L_1$, that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and passes through $C$. Use the parameter $\\lambda$ in your answer. [3 marks]\n\nA second line, $L_2$, is skew to $L_1$ and has a Cartesian equation given by\n$$\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\dfrac{x+1}{3}=\\dfrac{y}{2}=\\dfrac{z-1}{-2}\n\\end{equation*}\n$$\n2. Show that a vector equation of line $L_2$ could be written as\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\mathbf{r_2} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 0\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\mu\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 3\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -2\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} \\mu \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n    [2 marks]\n\nThe vector $\\bm d = \\overrightarrow{DE}$. Where $D$ is any point on $L_1$ and $E$ is any point on $L_2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/Screenshot%202023-09-15%20at%2011.45.45%20AM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-09-15 at 11.45.45 AM\">\n\n3.  1. Show that $\\bm d$, in terms of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$, is\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\mathbf{\\bm d} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2+2\\lambda+3\\mu \\\\ -2-\\lambda+2\\mu \\\\ -2+\\lambda-2\\mu \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n    2. Hence or otherwise, find the minimum distance between $L_1$ and $L_2$. [9 marks]\n\nPlane $\\Pi$ contains the points $A$, $B$ and $C$.\n\n4. 1. Find the Cartesian equation of the plane $\\Pi$.\n\n   2. A triangle is formed from the origin and the $y$ and $z$ intercepts of plane $\\Pi$. Find the area of the triangle.\n    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\overrightarrow{AB} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 3\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 3-5\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 2-1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1-0\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    2. As it passes through $C(1, 2, 3)$\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\boxed{\\mathbf{r_1} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\lambda \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} \\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}}.\n        \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n\n2. Use the parameter $\\mu$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\dfrac{x+1}{3}=\\dfrac{y}{2}=\\dfrac{z-1}{-2}=\\mu\n    \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n    We can then write each component in terms of $\\mu$.\n    &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $x=-1+3\\mu$ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;   $y= 2\\mu$   &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$z=1-2\\mu$\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\boxed{\\mathbf{r_2} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 0\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\mu\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 3\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -2\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} \\mu \\in \\mathbb{R}}.\n        \\end{equation*}\n    $$\n    As required.\n3. 1. Parametrically write the coordinates of points $D$ and $E$.\n\n        $D = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1-2\\lambda \\\\ 2+\\lambda \\\\ 3-\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}$\n        &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;\n        $E = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1+3\\mu \\\\2\\mu \\\\ 1-2\\mu \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}$\n\n        Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\mathbf{\\bm d} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1+3\\mu \\\\2\\mu \\\\ 1-2\\mu \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1-2\\lambda \\\\ 2+\\lambda \\\\ 3-\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\\\[6pt]\n            &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1+3\\mu -1+2\\lambda\\\\2\\mu- 2-\\lambda\\\\ 1-2\\mu-3+\\lambda \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2+2\\lambda+3\\mu \\\\ -2-\\lambda+2\\mu \\\\ -2+\\lambda-2\\mu \\end{pmatrix*}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n     As required.\n\n    2. The vector $\\bm d$ is perpendicular to both directions of the line when the distance between the lines is minimised. As shown below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%20%26%20Planes/images/Screenshot%202023-09-15%20at%2011.45.52%20AM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-09-15 at 11.45.52 AM\">\n\n    We can use the dot product to form two equations which have the parameters $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$. Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2+2\\lambda+3\\mu \\\\ -2-\\lambda+2\\mu \\\\ -2+\\lambda-2\\mu \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 3\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -2\\color{white}{1}\\end{pmatrix*}=0 \\text{  and  }\n        &\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2+2\\lambda+3\\mu \\\\ -2-\\lambda+2\\mu \\\\ -2+\\lambda-2\\mu \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1}\\end{pmatrix*}=0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    That leads to\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &6-2\\lambda-17\\mu=0 &-4+6\\lambda+2\\mu=0\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\lambda=\\dfrac{4}{7}, \\mu=\\dfrac{2}{7} &\\text{   [Solved on G.D.C.]}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can find the vector $\\bm d$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n            \\bm d&=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2+2\\times\\frac{4}{7}+3\\times\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -2-\\frac{4}{7}+2\\times\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -2+\\frac{4}{7}-2\\times\\frac{2}{7} \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 0\\color{white}{1}\\\\ -2\\color{white}{1}\\\\ -2\\color{white}{1}\\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the length of $\\bm d$ is the minimum distance.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |\\bm d| &= \\sqrt{0^2+(-2)^2+(-2)^2}\\\\[3pt]\n    &=2.8284271...\\\\[3pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The minimum distance between the two lines is $2.83$units.\n\n4.  1. We can find the normal to the plane using the cross product.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\bm n &= \\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1-5 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 3-0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -2\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ -1\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*} \\times \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -4\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 1\\color{white}{1} \\\\ 3\\color{white}{1} \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1\\times3-(-1)\\times1 \\\\ -1\\times(-4)-(-2)\\times(3) \\\\ -2\\times1-(1)\\times(-4) \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4\\\\ 10 \\\\2 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The normal is $\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4\\\\ 10 \\\\2 \\end{pmatrix*}$ however, as we only need a direction to represent the normal we can write the normal ($\\div2$) as $\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2\\\\ 5 \\\\1 \\end{pmatrix*}$.\n\n    Using the scalar product plane equation with point $A(5, 1, 0)$ which lies on the plane\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] x\\\\ y \\\\z \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2\\\\ 5 \\\\1 \\end{pmatrix*} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5\\\\ 1 \\\\0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\cdot\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2\\\\ 5 \\\\1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\\\[6pt]\n        2x+5y+z=5\\times2+1\\times5+0\\times1\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The Cartesian equation of the plane is $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{2x+5y+z=15}$\n\n    2. The plane has a $y$-intercept when $x, z=0$. Therefore the $y=3$.\n\n        The plane has a $z$-intercept when $x, y=0$. Therefore the $z=15$.\n\n        The triangle has a base width of $3$ and a height of $15$.\n\n        Therefore the area of the triangle is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\dfrac{3\\times15}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=22.5\\text{ units$^2$}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"}]},{"name":"Vectors, Points & Lines","questions":[{"ref":"AA1051","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1051\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe following diagram shows parallelogram ABCD with $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{AB}}=\\boldsymbol{u}$, $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{AD}}=\\boldsymbol{v}$ and $|\\boldsymbol{u}|=\\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|}{2}$, where $|\\boldsymbol{u}|\\neq 0$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/1051aaa%20-%20Copy.JPEG\" alt=\"1051aaa - Copy\">\n\nThe angle between sides $\\mathrm{AB}$ and $\\mathrm{AD}$ is $\\alpha$, where $0< \\alpha < \\pi$.\n\nPoint $\\mathrm{X}$ is on side $\\mathrm{BC}$ such that $\\mathrm{BX}=c\\mathrm{BC}$ for $0\\leq c \\leq 1$.\n\n1.  A vector expression for $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XA}}$ is $-c\\boldsymbol{v}-\\boldsymbol{u}$. Find a similar expression for $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}$. [3 marks]\n\nThe angle between $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XA}}$ and $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}$ is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n2.  Use the scalar product to show that $\\lvert \\boldsymbol{v} \\lvert^2(2c-1)\\left[2\\cos\\alpha + (2c-1)\\right]=0$. [4 marks]\n\n3. Find the range of possible values for $\\alpha$ such that there are two possible positions for $\\mathrm{X}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Because we have a parallelogram, $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{AB}}=\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{DC}}=\\boldsymbol{u}$ and $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{AD}}=\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BC}}=\\boldsymbol{v}$.\n\n    Further, we must have that $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BC}}=\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BX}}+\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}}$.\n\n    Substituting in $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BC}}=\\boldsymbol{v}$ and the given information that $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BX}}=c\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BC}}$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BC}}&=\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BX}}+\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= c\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{BC}}+\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\boldsymbol{v} &= c\\boldsymbol{v} + \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\boldsymbol{v} - c\\boldsymbol{v} &= \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}} &= (1-c)\\boldsymbol{v} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We require a vector expression for $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}$, and from the diagram, we can see that $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}=\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}}+\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{CD}}$. We have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}&=\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}}+\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{CD}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XC}}-\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{DC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}&= \\boxed{(1-c)\\boldsymbol{v} - \\boldsymbol{u}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Given that the angle between $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XA}}$ and $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}$ is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$, their scalar product must be 0.\n\n    Using our expressions for $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XA}}$ and $\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}}$ from part (a), we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XA}} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{XD}} &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[7pt]\n    (-c\\boldsymbol{v}-\\boldsymbol{u})\\cdot ((1-c)\\boldsymbol{v}-\\boldsymbol{u}) &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the property of the scalar product that $\\boldsymbol{u}\\cdot(\\boldsymbol{v}+\\boldsymbol{w})=\\boldsymbol{u}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{v} + \\boldsymbol{u} \\cdot \\boldsymbol{w}$, we can expand this expression.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -c\\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot (1-c)\\boldsymbol{v} + c\\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{u}-\\boldsymbol{u}\\cdot(1-c)\\boldsymbol{v} + \\boldsymbol{u}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{u} &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Now using the properties that $(k\\boldsymbol{v})\\cdot\\boldsymbol{w}=k(\\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{w})$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{v}=|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2$, we can simplify further.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -c(1-c)|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2 + (c-(1-c))\\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{u} + |\\boldsymbol{u}|^2 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next, we note that $|\\boldsymbol{u}|=\\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|}{2}$, and the question has asked for an expression in terms of $|\\boldsymbol{v}|$, $c$ and $\\alpha$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -c(1-c)|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2 + (c-(1-c))\\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{u} + \\left(\\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|}{2}\\right)^2 &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Finally, we need a substitution for $\\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{u}$. Using **the scalar product formula (3.13)**\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\boldsymbol{v}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{u} &= |\\boldsymbol{v}||\\boldsymbol{u}|\\cos \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n    &= |\\boldsymbol{v}|\\left(\\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|}{2}\\right)\\cos \\alpha\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can manipulate our expression to the form required. We have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -c(1-c)|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2 + (c-(1-c))|\\boldsymbol{v}|\\left(\\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|}{2}\\right)\\cos \\alpha + \\left(\\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|}{2}\\right)^2 &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    -c(1-c)|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2 + (c-(1-c))\\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2}{2}\\cos \\alpha + \\dfrac{|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2}{4} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    -4c(1-c)|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2 + 2(c-(1-c))|\\boldsymbol{v}|^2\\cos \\alpha + |\\boldsymbol{v}|^2 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    |\\boldsymbol{v}|^2\\left(-4c+4c^2+2(2c-1)\\cos \\alpha +1\\right) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    |\\boldsymbol{v}|^2\\left(2(2c-1)\\cos \\alpha + 4c^2-4c+1\\right) &= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    |\\boldsymbol{v}|^2\\left(2(2c-1)\\cos \\alpha + (2c-1)^2\\right) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-13.5em}\\lvert \\boldsymbol{v} \\lvert^2(2c-1)\\left[2\\cos\\alpha + (2c-1)\\right] = 0\\hspace{0.5em}...\\text{ as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Firstly, we note that the equation found in part (b) must be satisfied for the angle at X to be a right angle. Since $\\boldsymbol{u}\\neq \\boldsymbol{0}$, we have $|\\boldsymbol{v}|=2|\\boldsymbol{u}|\\neq0$, so we require\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2c-1=0 \\hspace{2em}\\text{or}\\hspace{2em}2\\cos{\\alpha}+(2c-1)=0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Solving each equation for $c$ gives the two values of $c$ for which the angle at X is a right angle.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    c=\\tfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{2em}\\text{and}\\hspace{2em}c=\\frac{1-2\\cos{\\alpha}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    For $\\mathrm{X}$ to have two distinct positions, these two values of $c$ must be different.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tfrac{1}{2}&\\neq\\frac{1-2\\cos{\\alpha}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    1&\\neq1-2\\cos{\\alpha}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos{\\alpha}&\\neq0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha&\\neq\\tfrac{\\pi}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Both values of $c$ must also lie in the given range $0\\leq c \\leq 1$. The first value, $c=\\tfrac{1}{2}$, always satisfies this. For the second value, this gives an inequality in $\\alpha$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0\\leq&\\frac{1-2\\cos{\\alpha}}{2}\\leq1 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    0\\leq&1-2\\cos{\\alpha}\\leq2\\\\[6pt]\n    -\\tfrac{1}{2}\\leq&\\cos{\\alpha}\\leq\\tfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/1051bbb.JPEG\" alt=\"1051bbb\">\n\n    Recall that $0< \\alpha < \\pi$, this is shown in red in the above sketch. Hence\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\leq \\alpha \\leq \\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Combining this with the earlier requirement that $\\alpha\\neq\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\hspace{-0.65em}\\boxed{\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\leq \\alpha \\leq \\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\hspace{1em}\\alpha \\neq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1174","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1174\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn this question, all distances are measured in metres and time in seconds.\n\nA seagull, positioned at $(5,9,15)$, is looking at a fish positioned at $(9, -23, -1)$.\n\nAt $t=0$, the seagull dives toward the fish with a speed of $12$ metres per second.\n\n1.  Find the velocity vector of the seagull. [2 marks]\n\nA duck, positioned at the point $(8, -10, 2)$, is also looking at the fish.\n\n2.  At what time during the seagull's dive is it closest to the duck? [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We begin by working out the direction the seagull must fly. This can be done by subtracting the position vector of the seagull ($S$) from the fish ($F$)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\overrightarrow{SF}&=\\begin{pmatrix}\n            9 \\\\\n            -23 \\\\\n            -1\n        \\end{pmatrix} -\n        \\begin{pmatrix}\n            5 \\\\\n            9 \\\\\n            15\n        \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix}\n            4 \\\\\n            -32 \\\\\n            -16\n        \\end{pmatrix}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $\\overrightarrow{SF}$ is shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/AA1174_a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1174_a1\">\n\n    However, this vector does not take into account the speed of the seagull.\n\n    Lets work out the distance between $S$ and $F$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        |\\overrightarrow{SF}|&=\\left|\n            \\begin{array}{c}\n            4 \\\\\n            -32 \\\\\n            -16\n            \\end{array}\n            \\right|\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\sqrt{4^2+(-32)^2+(-16)^2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=36 \\text{ metres}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We know the speed of the seagull is $12\\,$ms$^{-1}$, therefore in $1$ second the velocity vector of the seagull is $\\tfrac{1}{36}\\times 12$ multiplied by the direction of $\\overrightarrow{SF}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{1}{36}\\times \\overrightarrow{SF}\\times 12\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{pmatrix}\n            4 \\\\\n            -32 \\\\\n            -16\n        \\end{pmatrix}   \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix}\n            4/3 \\\\\n            -32/3 \\\\\n            -16/3\n        \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix}\n            1.33 \\\\\n            -10.7 \\\\\n            -5.33\n        \\end{pmatrix}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The seagull moves in the direction of the vector above every second, therefore\n\n    $\\text{The velocity vector of the seagull is } \\begin{pmatrix}\n            1.33 \\\\\n            -10.7 \\\\\n            -5.33\n        \\end{pmatrix}t$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The following diagram shows the approximate position of the duck ($D$) relative to $S$ and $F$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/AA1174_a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1174_a2\">\n\n    When $t>0$ the seagull moves toward the fish. The position of the seagull after some time is represented by $S'$.\n\n    The direction between $S'$ and $D$, $\\overrightarrow{S'D}$, is shown as the red vector below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/AA1174_a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1174_a3\">\n\n    The seagull is closest to the duck when $\\overrightarrow{S'D}$ is perpendicular to the direction of the seagulls travel, $\\overrightarrow{SF}$. Shown below\n\n     <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/AA1174_a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1174_a4\">\n\n    Therefore we need to solve the following equation for $t$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=\\overrightarrow{SF} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{S'D} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We already have $\\overrightarrow{SF}$ but we need $\\overrightarrow{S'D}$ in terms of $t$.\n\n    To find $\\overrightarrow{S'D}$ we need to use the position vectors for $S'$ and $D$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\overrightarrow{S'D}&=\\overrightarrow{OD} - \\overrightarrow{OS'}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We know $\\overrightarrow{OD}$, we were given that in the question. Using the answer to part(a), $\\overrightarrow{v_S}$, we can write $\\overrightarrow{OS'}$ relative to the origin after $t$ seconds as\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\overrightarrow{OS'}&=\\overrightarrow{OS} + \\overrightarrow{v_S}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\overrightarrow{OS'}&=\\begin{pmatrix}\n        5 \\\\\n        9 \\\\\n        15\n    \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix}\n        1.33 \\\\\n        -10.7 \\\\\n        -5.33\n    \\end{pmatrix}t\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\begin{pmatrix}\n        5+1.33t \\\\\n        9-10.7t \\\\\n        15-5.33t\n    \\end{pmatrix} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore we can find $\\overrightarrow{S'D}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\overrightarrow{S'D}&=\\overrightarrow{OD} - \\overrightarrow{OS'}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\begin{pmatrix}\n        8 \\\\\n        -10 \\\\\n        2\n    \\end{pmatrix}-\\begin{pmatrix}\n        5+1.33t \\\\\n        9-10.7t \\\\\n        15-5.33t\n    \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\begin{pmatrix}\n        3-1.33t \\\\\n        -19+10.7t \\\\\n        -13+5.33t\n    \\end{pmatrix} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Recall we need to solve the following and can use the result we have just found\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=\\overrightarrow{SF} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{S'D}\\\\[6pt]\n        0&=\\begin{pmatrix}\n        4 \\\\\n        -32 \\\\\n        -16\n    \\end{pmatrix}  \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix}\n        3-1.33t \\\\\n        -19+10.7t \\\\\n        -13+5.33t\n    \\end{pmatrix}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can solve this directly using the numerical solver on the G.D.C.\n\n    Or we can expand the dot product and then solve on the G.D.C. however it is always advisable to solve on the G.D.C. at the earliest opportunity\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=12-4t+608-\\tfrac{1024}{3}t+208-\\tfrac{256}{3}t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore using a G.D.C.\n    $\\text{The seagull is closest to the duck when } t=1.92\\,\\text{seconds}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA305","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA305\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe points A and B have position vectors\n$\\vec{\\text{OA}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12 \\end{pmatrix*}$\nand\n$\\vec{\\text{OB}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{OA}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OB}}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the area of the triangle OAB. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the vector product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.9em} \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OB}} &= (2\\textbf{i} + 4\\textbf{j} + 12\\textbf{k})\\times(\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[c] \\hspace{0.25em}\\textbf{i} & \\hspace{0.25em}\\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}2 & \\hspace{0.25em}4 & 12 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}0 & \\hspace{0.25em}1 & 2  \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= [8-12]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [4-0]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [2-0]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -4\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] -4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.3em} A_{\\triangle\\text{OAB}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\big|\\vec{\\text{OA}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OB}}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\big|\\hspace{-0.2em}-4\\textbf{i} - 4\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(-4)^2 + (-4)^2 + 2^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{36} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA306","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA306\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nABCD is a parallelogram where\n$\\vec{\\text{AB}} = -2\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}$ and\n$\\vec{\\text{AD}} = -4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}$.\n\n1.  Find the area of the parallelogram ABCD. [3 marks]\n\n2.  By using a suitable scalar product of two vectors, determine whether\n    AB̂C is acute or obtuse. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the vector product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.8em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\times \\vec{\\text{AD}} &= (-2\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k})\\times(-4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\hspace{0.65em}\\textbf{k} \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}-2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ \\hspace{0.25em}-4 & 1 & 3  \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= [-3-6]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [-6-(-24)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [-2-4]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -9\\textbf{i} - 18\\textbf{j} - 6\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the area of a parallelogram formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.1em} A_{\\square \\text{ABCD}} &= \\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\times \\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= |\\hspace{-0.2em}-9\\textbf{i} - 18\\textbf{j} - 6\\textbf{k}| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{(-9)^2 + (-18)^2 + (-6)^2} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 21\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the scalar product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{AD}} &= (-2\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{j} + 6\\textbf{k}) \\cdot (-4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-2)(-4) + (-1)(1) + (6)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors\n    formula, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{D\\^{A}B}) &= \\dfrac{\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{AD}}}{\\raisebox{-0.35em}{$\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big|$}} \\hspace{4.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{25}{\\raisebox{-0.35em}{$\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big|$}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &> 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $\\text{D\\^{A}B}$ is acute and\n    $\\text{A\\^{B}C} = \\ang{180} - \\text{D\\^{A}B}$ is\n     $\\text{obtuse}$"},{"ref":"AA310","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA310\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nGiven that\n$2(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}) = - 3(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})$, prove\nthat $2\\mathbf{a} - 3\\mathbf{c} = t\\mathbf{b}$ where $t$ is a scalar.","markScheme":"Using the properties of vector product, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}) &= - 3(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}) \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n2(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}) + 3(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n2(\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}) - 3(\\mathbf{c}\\times\\mathbf{b}) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n[(2\\mathbf{a})\\times\\mathbf{b}] + [(-3\\mathbf{c})\\times\\mathbf{b}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(2\\mathbf{a} - 3\\mathbf{c})\\times\\mathbf{b} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence\n$2\\mathbf{a} - 3\\mathbf{c}$ is parallel (or anti-parallel) to\n$\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nTherefore, we deduce $2\\mathbf{a} - 3\\mathbf{c} = t\\mathbf{b}$, for some\n$t \\in \\mathbb{R}$."},{"ref":"AA314","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA314\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe position vectors of the points A, B and C are\n$\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$,\nrespectively, relative to an origin O.\n\nThe following diagram shows the triangle ABC and points N, R, S and T.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/cb7aed8c5ba2f3296562ebe80fd7f4a0d37ebcea.svg\" alt=\"cb7aed8c5ba2f3296562ebe80fd7f4a0d37ebcea.svg\">\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccl}\n\\text{N} & \\text{is a point on} &\\text{[AB]}& \\text{such that } \\vec{\\text{AN}} &=& \\dfrac{3}{7}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{AB}}. \\\\[9pt]\n\\text{R} & \\text{is a point on} &\\text{[BC]}& \\text{such that } \\vec{\\text{BR}} &=& \\dfrac{2}{5}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{BC}}. \\\\[9pt]\n\\text{S} & \\text{is a point on} &\\text{[CA]}& \\text{such that } \\vec{\\text{CS}} &=& \\dfrac{2}{5}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{CA}}. \\\\[9pt]\n\\text{T} & \\text{is a point on} &\\text{[RS]}& \\text{such that } \\vec{\\text{RT}} &=& \\dfrac{2}{3}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{RS}}.\n\\end{array} \\hspace{20em}\n$$\n\n1.\n    1.  Express $\\vec{\\text{AN}}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a}$ and\n        $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that\n        $\\vec{\\text{CN}} = \\dfrac{4}{7}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{a} + \\dfrac{3}{7}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{c}$.\n        [4 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Express $\\vec{\\text{RC}}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{b}$ and\n        $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\vec{\\text{CS}}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a}$ and\n        $\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that\n        $\\vec{\\text{RT}} = \\dfrac{4}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{a} - \\dfrac{6}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{b} + \\dfrac{2}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{c}$.\n        [5 marks]\n\n3.  Prove that T lies on \\[CN\\]. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AN}} &= \\dfrac{3}{7}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{7}(\\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{7}(\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a})\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using part (a) (i), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} \\vec{\\text{CN}} &= \\vec{\\text{CA}} + \\vec{\\text{AN}} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= (\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{c}) + \\dfrac{3}{7}(\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4}{7}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{a} + \\dfrac{3}{7}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{c}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{RC}} &= \\vec{\\text{BC}} - \\vec{\\text{BR}} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\vec{\\text{BC}} - \\dfrac{2}{5}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{BC}} \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{5}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{BC}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{5}(\\vec{\\text{OC}} - \\vec{\\text{OB}}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{3}{5}(\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b})\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Similarly, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.2em} \\vec{\\text{CS}} &= \\dfrac{2}{5}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{CA}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2}{5}(\\vec{\\text{OA}} - \\vec{\\text{OC}}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2}{5}(\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{c})\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using part (b) (i), we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{5.1em} \\vec{\\text{RT}} &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{RS}} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2}{3}(\\vec{\\text{RC}} + \\vec{\\text{CS}}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{5}(\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b}) + \\dfrac{2}{5}(\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{c})\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{a} - \\dfrac{6}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{b} + \\dfrac{2}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{c}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{CT}} &= \\vec{\\text{CR}} + \\vec{\\text{RT}} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by part (b)}] \\hspace{3em} &= \\bigg[\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{3}{5}(\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b})\\bigg] + \\bigg[\\dfrac{4}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{a} - \\dfrac{6}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{b} + \\dfrac{2}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{c}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{a} + \\dfrac{3}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{7}{15}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{c} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7}{15}\\bigg[\\dfrac{4}{7}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{a} + \\dfrac{3}{7}\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{c}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    [\\text{by part (a)}] \\hspace{3em} &= \\dfrac{7}{15}\\hspace{0.2em}\\vec{\\text{CN}} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence T is common to $\\vec{\\text{CT}}$ and $\\vec{\\text{CN}}$.\n\n    Therefore T lies on \\[CN\\]."},{"ref":"AA316","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA316\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the lines $\\mathit{L}_1$ and $\\mathit{L}_2$ defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathit{L}_1:\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathbf{r} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -6 \\\\ 10 \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} +  \\hspace{0.1em}\\lambda\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{0.55em}\\text{and}\\hspace{0.8em} \\mathit{L}_2:\\hspace{0.5em} -\\dfrac{x}{2} = y - 1 = \\dfrac{z -13}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $c$ is a constant. Given that the\nlines intersect at a point A,\n\n1.  find the value of $c$ ; [4 marks]\n\n2.  determine the coordinates of A. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the Cartesian equations of $\\mathit{L}_2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -x &= 2(y - 1)  \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.1em} 3(y-1) &= z - 13\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    substituting $x = -6 + 3\\lambda$ and $y = 10 - 3\\lambda$ in the\n    equation $(1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} -(-6 + 3\\lambda) &= 2[(10 - 3\\lambda) - 1] \\\\[6pt]\n    6 - 3\\lambda &= 18 - 6\\lambda \\\\[6pt]\n    3\\lambda &= 12 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\lambda &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, substituting $y = 10-3(4)$ and\n    $z = c + 4$ in the equation $(2)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3[(10-3(4))-1] &= (c+4)-13 \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3(-2-1) &= c - 9 \\\\[6pt]\n    - 9 &= c - 9 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.1em} c = 0\n    $$\n\n2.  The coordinates of A are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} (-6+3\\lambda, 10-3\\lambda, c+\\lambda) &= (-6+3(4), 10-3(4), 0+4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (6,-2,4)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA317","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA317\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nO, A, B and C are distinct points such that\n$\\vec{\\text{OA}} = \\mathbf{a}$, $\\vec{\\text{OB}} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n$\\vec{\\text{OC}} = \\mathbf{c}$.\n\nIt is given that $\\mathbf{a}$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf{b}$ and\n$\\mathbf{c}$.\n\nProve that $\\mathbf{a}$ is perpendicular to $\\vec{\\text{BC}}$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1em} \\vec{\\text{BC}} &= \\vec{\\text{OC}} -\\vec{\\text{OB}} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the properties of\nscalar product, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{10.95em} \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{BC}} &= \\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b})  \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 0 - 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\mathbf{a}\\perp\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{a}\\perp\\mathbf{c}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence $\\mathbf{a}$ is perpendicular to\n$\\vec{\\text{BC}}$."},{"ref":"AA323","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA323\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nThe equations of the lines $L_1$ and $L_2$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &:\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathbf{r}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}s\\hspace{-0.2em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\hspace{0.5em} s\\hspace{-0.05em} \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\\\[20pt]\nL_2 &:\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathbf{r}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\hspace{0.5em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Show that the lines $L_1$ and $L_2$ are skew. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the acute angle between $L_1$ and $L_2$, giving your answer\n    correct to the nearest degree. [4 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Find a vector perpendicular to both lines.\n\n    2.  Hence determine the equation of the line $L_3$ that is\n        perpendicular to both $L_1$ and $L_2$ and\n        intersects both lines. [10 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the direction vectors of $L_1$ and $L_2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.2em} \\neq k\\hspace{-0.1em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for any $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence $L_1$ and $L_2$ are not parallel. They are either skew or\n    intersecting.\n\n    Using the vector equations of $L_1$ and $L_2$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.7em} 2 + 4s &= 1 - 2t  \\\\[6pt]\n    3 - 2s &= 3 + t   \\\\[6pt]\n    1 + 4s &= 1 + 2t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving the system of\n    linear equations $(2)$-$(3)$ for $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.75em} s = 0\\hspace{0.75em}\\&\\hspace{0.75em} t = 0 \\hspace{9.75em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we try to verify the equation $(1)$ for values $(4)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} 2 + 4(0) &\\neq 1 - 2(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    2 &\\neq 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the system of linear equations\n    $(1)$-$(2)$-$(3)$ has no solution.\n\n    Therefore $L_1$ and $L_2$ are not intersecting. They are skew.\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.25em} \\mathbf{d}_1 &= \\phantom{-}4\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + 4\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{d}_2 &= -2\\textbf{i} + \\phantom{2}\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L_2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.15em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{d}_1\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}_2}{|\\mathbf{d}_1||\\mathbf{d}_2|} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{(4)(-2) + (-2)(1) + (4)(2)}{\\big[\\sqrt{4^2+(-2)^2+4^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{(-2)^2+1^2+2^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\theta &\\approx \\ang{96} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the acute angle between $L_1$ and $L_2$ is\n    $\\ang{180} - \\ang{96} =  \\hspace{0.525em}\\ang{84}$\n\n3.\n    1.  Using the vector product formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{5.1em} \\mathbf{d}_1 \\times \\mathbf{d}_2 &= (4\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + 4\\textbf{k})\\times(-2\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\textbf{i} & \\textbf{j} & \\hspace{0.7em}\\textbf{k} \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= [-4-4]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [8-(-8)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [4-4]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= -8\\mathbf{i} - 16\\mathbf{j}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{10em} \\mathbf{d}_3 &= 8\\mathbf{i} + 16\\mathbf{j}\\phantom{-} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L_3$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        and\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/52470a552fb1b070c5e32f42c88ee3747a6d6ab6.svg\" alt=\"52470a552fb1b070c5e32f42c88ee3747a6d6ab6.svg\">\n\n        The points A and B lie on $L_1$ and $L_2$ respectively.\n\n        Hence, using the equations of $L_1$ and $L_2$, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{6em} \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2+4s \\\\ 3-2s \\\\ 1 + 4s \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{0.75em} \\& \\hspace{1em} \\vec{\\text{OB}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1-2t \\\\ 3+t \\\\ 1+2t \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{1.9em} [\\text{for some $s, t \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        The vectors $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}}$\n        and $\\mathbf{d}_3 = 8\\mathbf{i} + 16\\mathbf{j}$ are parallel.\n\n        Hence we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= p\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}  \\\\[3pt]\n        \\hspace{2.85em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1-4s-2t \\\\ \\hspace{0.3em}2s+t \\\\ -4s+2t \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} &= p\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for some $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Rewriting the vector equation $(6)$ as a system of linear\n        equations, we get\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{array}{rcccccl}\n        8p &+& 4s &+& 2t &=&-1 \\\\[6pt]\n        16p &-& 2s &-& t &=& 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        && 4s &-& 2t &=& 0\n        \\end{array}\n        $$\n        Hence, solving this system of linear equations for\n        $p$, $s$ and $t$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{7.4em} p = -0.025,\\hspace{0.25em} s = -0.1,\\hspace{0.5em} t = -0.2 \\hspace{1.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1.6 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 0.6 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{0.9em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, using the vector equation of a line formula, we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathbf{r}_3 &=  \\vec{\\text{OA}} + \\lambda\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathbf{d}_3 \\hspace{3em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{6.1em} \\mathbf{r}_3 = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1.6 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 0.6 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} +  \\lambda\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3em} [\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}]\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA324","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA324\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe position vectors of the points A, B and C are $\\mathbf{a}$,\n$\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$, respectively,\nrelative to the origin O.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/266be74a9d66d8ad28b4a59082e625f3c9478eac.svg\" alt=\"266be74a9d66d8ad28b4a59082e625f3c9478eac.svg\">\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{a}$ is perpendicular to $\\vec{\\text{BC}}$ and\n$\\mathbf{b}$ is perpendicular to\n $\\vec{\\text{CA}}$, prove\nthat $\\mathbf{c}$ is perpendicular to\n$\\vec{\\text{AB}}$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\n\\begin{array}{rcccl}\n\\vec{\\text{AB}} &=& \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} &=& \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\[6pt]\n\\vec{\\text{BC}} &=& \\vec{\\text{OC}} - \\vec{\\text{OB}} &=& \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b} \\\\[6pt]\n\\vec{\\text{CA}} &=& \\vec{\\text{OA}} - \\vec{\\text{OC}} &=& \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{c}\n\\end{array}\n$$\nHence, using the properties of scalar product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{BC}} &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{b}) &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\hspace{0.15em} \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} &= \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} \\hspace{0.9em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{CA}} &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{b} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{c}) &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} &= 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} &= \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} \\hspace{0.9em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using the properties of scalar product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{13em} \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} &= \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} \\\\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} &=  \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} \\hspace{4em} [\\text{by $(1)$ and $(2)$}] \\\\[5pt]\n0 &= \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} \\\\[5pt]\n(\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c} &= 0 \\\\[4pt]\n\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence $\\mathbf{c}$\nis perpendicular to $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$."},{"ref":"AA326","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA326\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe points P and R have position vectors\n$\\vec{\\text{OP}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}$ and\n $\\vec{\\text{OR}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{OP}}\\times\\vec{\\text{OR}}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the area of the triangle OPR. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the vector product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.75em} \\vec{\\text{OP}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OR}} &= (2\\textbf{i} - \\textbf{k})\\times(-2\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\left|\\begin{matrix*}[r] \\hspace{0.25em}\\textbf{i} & \\hspace{0.75em}\\textbf{j} & \\textbf{k} \\\\  2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1  \\end{matrix*}\\hspace{0.25em}\\right| \\\\[8pt]\n    &= [0-(-2)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{i} - [2-2]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{j} + [4-0]\\hspace{0.05em}\\textbf{k}  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2\\textbf{i} + 4\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\triangle\\text{OPR}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\big|\\vec{\\text{OP}} \\times \\vec{\\text{OR}}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\big|2\\textbf{i} + 4\\textbf{k}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2^2 + 0^2 + 4^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{20} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA329","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA329\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}$\nand $\\vec{\\text{AC}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8 \\end{pmatrix*}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{BC}}$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the unit vector in the direction of $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  Show that $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ is perpendicular to $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11.8em} \\vec{\\text{BC}} &= \\vec{\\text{BA}} + \\vec{\\text{AC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.2em} + \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{BA}} = - \\vec{\\text{AB}}$}] \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 9 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big| &= |4\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{4^2 + 2^2 + (-1)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{21}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the unit vector in the direction of $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\vec{\\text{AB}}}{\\raisebox{-0.3em}{$\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|$}} &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{21}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\[3pt] \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\[3pt] \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{21}} \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the scalar product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.8em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{AC}} &= (4\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k})\\cdot(3\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} + 8\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (4)(3) + (2)(-2) + (-1)(8) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12-4-8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ is perpendicular to\n    $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$."},{"ref":"AA330","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA330\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $\\textbf{u} = 6\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}$ and\n$\\textbf{v} = 3\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}$.\n\n1.  Find:\n    1.  $\\textbf{u} + \\textbf{v}$;\n\n    2.  $|\\textbf{u}|$;\n    3.  $|\\textbf{v}|$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\textbf{u}\\cdot\\textbf{v}$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the angle between $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.2em} \\textbf{u} + \\textbf{v} &= (6\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}) + (3\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 9\\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The magnitude of $\\textbf{u}$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.2em} |\\textbf{u}| &= |6\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{6^2 + (-2)^2 + (-1)^2} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{41}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  The magnitude of $\\textbf{v}$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        |\\textbf{v}| &= |3\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{3^2 + 5^2 + 3^2} \\hspace{2.15em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{43}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\textbf{u}\\cdot\\textbf{v} &= (6\\textbf{i} - 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k})\\cdot(3\\textbf{i} + 5\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (6)(3) + (-2)(5) + (-1)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the angle between two vectors formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.15em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}}{|\\mathbf{u}||\\mathbf{v}|} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{5}{(\\sqrt{41})(\\sqrt{43})} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by parts (a) \\& (b)}]\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5.1em} \\theta \\approx \\ang{83.2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA331","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA331\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $\\textbf{u} = 5\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}$ and\n$\\textbf{v} = 2\\textbf{i} - 3\\textbf{j} + n\\textbf{k}$ where\n$n \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Given that $\\textbf{u}$ is perpendicular to $\\textbf{v}$, find the\n    value of $n$. [3 marks]\n\nLet $\\textbf{w} = 7\\textbf{i} + p\\textbf{j} - 3\\textbf{k}$ where\n$p \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2.  Given that $|\\textbf{w}| = \\sqrt{74}$, find the possible\n    values for $p$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the scalar product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textbf{u} \\cdot \\textbf{v} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\textbf{u} \\perp \\textbf{v}$}] \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    (5\\textbf{i} + 2\\textbf{j} - \\textbf{k}) \\cdot (2\\textbf{i} - 3\\textbf{j} + n\\textbf{k}) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    (5)(2) + (2)(-3) + (-1)(n) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    4 - n &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.15em} n = 4\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |\\textbf{w}| &= \\sqrt{74} \\hspace{3.75em} \\\\[6pt]\n    |7\\textbf{i} + p\\textbf{j} - 3\\textbf{k}| &= \\sqrt{74} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\textstyle \\sqrt{7^2 + p^2 + (-3)^2} &= \\sqrt{74} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\textstyle 49 + p^2 + 9 &= 74 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\textstyle p^2 &= 16 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3em} p = \\pm 4\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA332","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA332\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the parallelogram ABCD.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/c83a023b0ba0a590fcaaa9dff5b2c31f1012ca2a.svg\" alt=\"c83a023b0ba0a590fcaaa9dff5b2c31f1012ca2a.svg\">\n\nLet E denote the midpoint of \\[BD\\], $\\textbf{p} = \\vec{\\text{BA}}$ and\n$\\textbf{q} = \\vec{\\text{BC}}$.\n\nFind each of the following vectors in terms of $\\textbf{p}$ and\n$\\textbf{q}$.\n\n1.  $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$ ; [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\vec{\\text{BE}}$ ; [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\vec{\\text{AE}}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AC}} &= \\vec{\\text{AB}} + \\vec{\\text{BC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-\\textbf{p}) + \\textbf{q} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\textbf{q} - \\textbf{p}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\vec{\\text{BE}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}\\vec{\\text{BD}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\big(\\vec{\\text{BA}} + \\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\big(\\vec{\\text{BA}} + \\vec{\\text{BC}}\\big) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\textbf{p} + \\textbf{q})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.6em} \\vec{\\text{AE}} &= \\vec{\\text{AB}} + \\vec{\\text{BE}} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= (-\\textbf{p}) + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\textbf{p} + \\textbf{q}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(-2\\textbf{p}) + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\textbf{p} + \\textbf{q}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\textbf{q} - \\textbf{p})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA333","marks":"11","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA333\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nA line $L_1$ passes through the points A$(-2,0,1)$ and B$(1,4,1)$.\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}$\n    [1 mark]\n\n2.  Hence write down:\n    1.  a direction vector for $L_1$;\n\n    2.  a vector equation for $L_1$ in the form\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{a} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathbf{b}$.[3 marks]\n\nAnother line $L_2$ is perpendicular to $L_1$ and has vector equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + s\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} s \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\n3.  Find the value of $k$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Show that the point C$(-4,8,-5)$ lies on $L_2$. [2 marks]\n\n5.  Let D be the point such that ABCD is a parallelogram. Find\n    $\\vec{\\text{OD}}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} = \\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}$\n\n    2.\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n3.  Using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\cdot \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $L_1 \\perp L_2$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3.1em} (k)(3) + (-3)(4) + (1)(0) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    3k - 12 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    3k &= 12 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.9em} k = 4\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the vector equation of $L_2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + s\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{0.5em}\\Longleftrightarrow\\hspace{0.5em} s = -2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence C$(-4,8,-5)$ lies on $L_2$.\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{CD}} &= - \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.3em} \\vec{\\text{OD}} &= \\vec{\\text{OC}} + \\vec{\\text{CD}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.2em} + \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} = \\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA334","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA334\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA line $L_1$ passes through the points A$(2,1,-1)$ and B$(5,-5,-4)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Show that\n        $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n    2.  Write down the vector equation of $L_1$ in the form\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathbf{d}$.\n        [2 marks]\n\nA line $L_2$ has equation\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}s\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.2em},\\hspace{0.25em} s \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2.  Find the acute angle between $L_1$ and $L_2$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  The lines $L_1$ and $L_2$ intersect at point C. Find\n    $\\vec{\\text{OC}}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.7em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[4pt]\n        &=\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4  \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[10pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.25em} \\mathbf{d}_1 &= \\phantom{-}3\\textbf{i} - 6\\textbf{j} - 3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L_1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{d}_2 &= -3\\textbf{i} + 4\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{direction vector of $L_2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{5.7em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{d}_1\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}_2}{|\\mathbf{d}_1||\\mathbf{d}_2|} \\\\[12pt]\n     \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{(3)(-3) + (-6)(4) + (-3)(2)}{\\big[\\sqrt{3^2+(-6)^2+(-3)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{(-3)^2+4^2+2^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[16pt]\n     \\theta &= \\ang{170.2...} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    Hence the acute angle between $L_1$ and $L_2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ang{180} - \\ang{170.2...} &=9.759...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\ang{9.76}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the vector equations of $L_1$ and $L_2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    5 - 3s &= 2 + 3t \\hspace{0.2em}  \\\\[6pt]\n    5 + 4s &= 1 - 6t\n    %1 + 2s &= -1 - 3t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving the system of\n    linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $s$ and $t$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.75em} s = 5\\hspace{0.75em}\\&\\hspace{0.75em} t = -4 \\hspace{9.7em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $s = 5$ in the equation of $L_2$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.65em} \\vec{\\text{OC}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}5\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -10 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 11 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA335","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA335\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn the following diagram, $\\vec{\\text{CA}} = \\mathbf{p}$,\n$\\vec{\\text{CB}} = \\mathbf{q}$ and\n$\\vec{\\text{AD}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\vec{\\text{AB}}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/c071bff1f572ad731d80b239235e4ab30d7ec1aa.svg\" alt=\"c071bff1f572ad731d80b239235e4ab30d7ec1aa.svg\">\n\nExpress each of the following vectors in terms of $\\mathbf{p}$ and\n$\\mathbf{q}$.\n\n1.  $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ ; [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\vec{\\text{CD}}$ . [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{AC}} + \\vec{\\text{CB}} \\hspace{1.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-\\mathbf{p}) + \\mathbf{q} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\mathbf{q} - \\mathbf{p}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.2em} \\vec{\\text{CD}} &= \\vec{\\text{CA}} + \\vec{\\text{AD}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\vec{\\text{CA}} + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\mathbf{p} + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{q} - \\mathbf{p}) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(2\\mathbf{p}) + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{q} - \\mathbf{p}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{p} + \\mathbf{q})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA336","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA336\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe line $L_1$ passes through the points A$(4,7,-4)$ and B$(4,6,-3)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Show that\n        $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n    2.  Write down the vector equation of $L_1$ in the form\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathbf{d}$.\n        [2 marks]\n\nAnother line $L_2$ has equation\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}s\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.2em},\\hspace{0.25em} s \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nThe lines $L_1$ and $L_2$\n\nintersect at point C.\n\n2.  Find $\\vec{\\text{OC}}$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the acute angle between $\\vec{\\text{OC}}$ and $L_1$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3  \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix*} =\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{0.7em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n2.  Using the vector equations of $L_1$ and $L_2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} 2 - s &= 4   \\\\[6pt]\n    1 &= 7 - t\n    %4 + s &= -4 + t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving the system of\n    linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.75em} s = -2\\hspace{0.75em}\\&\\hspace{0.75em} t = 6 \\hspace{9.6em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $s = -2$ in the equation of $L_2$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.65em} \\vec{\\text{OC}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{0.1em}2\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.15em} \\mathbf{c} &= \\hspace{0.31em}4\\textbf{i} + \\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\hspace{3em} [= \\vec{\\text{OC}}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathbf{d} &= - \\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k} \\hspace{5.075em} [\\text{direction vector of $L_1$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we find\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{5.7em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}}{|\\mathbf{c}||\\mathbf{d}|} \\\\[5pt]\n     \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{(4)(0) + (1)(-1) + (2)(1)}{\\big[\\sqrt{4^2+1^2+2^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{0^2+(-1)^2+1^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[9pt]\n     \\theta &\\approx \\ang{81.1} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    Hence the acute angle between $\\vec{\\text{OC}}$ and $L_1$ is\n     $\\ang{81.1}$"},{"ref":"AA337","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA337\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFive equilateral triangles, each with side length\n$4$ cm, are arranged to form a truss bridge\nmodel.\n\nThis is shown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/ed76e097fdbcfa4a334e429debc3794ef7294dc7.svg\" alt=\"ed76e097fdbcfa4a334e429debc3794ef7294dc7.svg\">\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are shown on the\ndiagram.\n\nFind $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b} + 2\\mathbf{c})$.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/72b03b21af49b6b96e653ed87e3fec1251aa4940.svg\" alt=\"72b03b21af49b6b96e653ed87e3fec1251aa4940.svg\">\n\nHence, using the scalar product formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b} + 2\\mathbf{c}) &= \\mathbf{a}\\cdot(3\\mathbf{c}) \\\\[10pt]\n&= |\\mathbf{a}||3\\mathbf{c}|\\cos \\ang{60} \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(12) \\\\[6pt]\n&= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA338","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA338\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nLet A and B be points such that\n$\\vec{\\text{OA}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}$\nand\n$\\vec{\\text{OB}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n    [2 marks]\n\nLet C and D be points such that ABCD is a rectangle.\n\n2.  Given that\n    $\\vec{\\text{AD}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] q \\\\ -4 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix*}$ ,\n    show that $q = 3$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of point C. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the area of the rectangle ABCD. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[9pt]\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/f442ac6be90e1a4f1865bfcdc42b6af4c70e81f6.svg\" alt=\"f442ac6be90e1a4f1865bfcdc42b6af4c70e81f6.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the scalar product formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{AD}} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\perp \\vec{\\text{AD}}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\cdot \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] q \\\\ -4 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix*} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3.8em} (-2)(q) + (-3)(-4) + (1)(-6) &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    -2q + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6.2em} q = 3\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.7em} \\vec{\\text{OC}} &=  \\vec{\\text{OB}} + \\vec{\\text{BC}} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} + \\vec{\\text{AD}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\perp \\vec{\\text{AD}}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of C are\n     $(4,-6,-7)$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.9em} \\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big| &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-3)^2 + 1^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{4 + 9 + 1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{14}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.9em} \\big|\\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big| &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2 + (-6)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{9 + 16 + 36} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{61}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the area of a rectangle\n    formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\square\\text{ABCD}} &= \\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\big(\\sqrt{14}\\big)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\big(\\sqrt{61}\\big) \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{854}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA339","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA339\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following diagram shows the triangle ABC.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/5b86518022b4b342c8c9fbb7f26177d16d5c984d.svg\" alt=\"5b86518022b4b342c8c9fbb7f26177d16d5c984d.svg\">\n\nGiven that $\\vec{\\text{BA}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{BC}} = -6$ and\n$\\big|\\vec{\\text{BA}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{BC}}\\big| = 12$, find the area\nof triangle ABC.","markScheme":"Using the angle between two vectors formula, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= \\dfrac{\\vec{\\text{BA}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{BC}}}{\\raisebox{-0.35em}{$\\big|\\vec{\\text{BA}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{BC}}\\big|$}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= -\\dfrac{6}{12} \\\\[11pt]\n\\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[11pt]\n\\text{C\\^{B}A} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the area\nof a triangle formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big|\\vec{\\text{BA}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{BC}}\\big|\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) \\\\[9pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}(12)\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}(12)\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[11pt]\n&= 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA340","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA340\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet\n$\\mathbf{u} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}$\nand\n$\\mathbf{v} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] k \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix*}$\nwhere $k > 0$. The angle between $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\nFind the value of $k$.","markScheme":"Using the angle between two vectors formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{10.65em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}}{|\\mathbf{u}||\\mathbf{v}|} \\\\[9pt]\n\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\Big)\\hspace{-0.15em} &= \\dfrac{(1)(k) + (3)(2) + (-2)(-4)}{\\big[\\sqrt{1^2+3^2+(-2)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{k^2+2^2+(-4)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} &= \\dfrac{k + 14}{\\big[\\sqrt{14}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{k^2+20}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\textstyle \\big[\\sqrt{14}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{k^2+20}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\hspace{-0.15em} &= k + 14 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{6.7em} k \\approx 6.18 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA341","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA341\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nTwo points A and B have coordinates $(2,3,1)$ and $(5,9,3)$\nrespectively.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$.\n\n    2.  Find $\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|$. [4 marks]\n\nLet $\\vec{\\text{AC}} = 4\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} - 4\\mathbf{k}$.\n\n2.  Find the angle between $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$ and\n    $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find the area of triangle ABC. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Hence, or otherwise, find the shortest distance from C to\n    the line passing through A and B.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= 2\\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\vec{\\text{OB}} &= 5\\textbf{i} + 9\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k} \\hspace{2em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{5.55em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (5\\textbf{i} + 9\\textbf{j} + 3\\textbf{k}) - (2\\textbf{i} + 3\\textbf{j} + \\textbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\\textbf{i} + 6\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big| &= \\big|3\\textbf{i} + 6\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{3^2 + 6^2 + 2^2} \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= 7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= 3\\textbf{i} + 6\\textbf{j} + 2\\textbf{k} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\vec{\\text{AC}} &= 4\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} - 4\\mathbf{k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the angle between two vectors formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{AC}}}{\\raisebox{-0.35em}{$\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{AC}}\\big|$}} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{(3)(4) + (6)(2) + (2)(-4)}{\\big[\\sqrt{3^2+6^2+2^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{4^2+2^2+(-4)^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.2em} \\theta \\approx \\ang{67.6} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.9em} \\big|\\vec{\\text{AC}}\\big| &= \\big|4\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} - 4\\mathbf{k}\\big| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{4^2 + 2^2 + (-4)^2}  \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the area of a triangle formula, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} A_{\\triangle\\text{ABC}} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{AC}}\\big|\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}C}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(7)(6)\\sin \\ang{67.6} \\\\[11pt]\n    &\\approx 19.4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  If we sketch triangle ABC, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/eafe1ba171cbc18d6717423f09cd26875612d70c.svg\" alt=\"eafe1ba171cbc18d6717423f09cd26875612d70c.svg\">\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d_{\\text{min}} &= 6\\sin \\ang{67.6} \\hspace{1.15em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 5.55\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA342","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA342\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $\\mathbf{u} = 3\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}$ and\n$\\mathbf{v} = 4\\mathbf{i} - 5\\mathbf{j} + 6\\mathbf{k}$. The vector\n$\\mathbf{v} - p\\hspace{0.025em}\\mathbf{u}$ is\nperpendicular to $\\mathbf{u}$.\n\nFind the value of $p$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} \\mathbf{v} - p\\hspace{0.025em}\\mathbf{u} &= (4\\mathbf{i} - 5\\mathbf{j} + 6\\mathbf{k}) - p(3\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= (4-3p)\\mathbf{i} + (-5+2p)\\mathbf{j} + (6-p)\\mathbf{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using the scalar product formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathbf{v} - p\\hspace{0.025em}\\mathbf{u})\\cdot\\mathbf{u} &= 0 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $(\\mathbf{v} - p\\hspace{0.025em}\\mathbf{u}) \\perp \\mathbf{u}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{2em} ((4-3p)\\mathbf{i} + (-5+2p)\\mathbf{j} + (6-p)\\mathbf{k})\\cdot(3\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(4-3p)(3) + (-5+2p)(-2) + (6-p)(1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(12-9p) + (10-4p) + (6-p) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n28 - 14p &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{12.8em} p= 2\n$$"},{"ref":"AA343","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA343\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nThe diagram shows a parallelogram ABCD.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/ed67c1c8e1a3dbf0935691e5a18e4790b5f71757.svg\" alt=\"ed67c1c8e1a3dbf0935691e5a18e4790b5f71757.svg\">\n\nThe coordinates of A, B and D are A$(2,3,4)$, B$(8,5,7)$ and D$(4,7,8)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Show that\n        $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}$\n         .\n\n    2.  Find $\\vec{\\text{AD}}$.\n    3.  Hence show that\n        $\\vec{\\text{AC}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n        [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of point C. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\cdot\\vec{\\text{AD}}$.\n\n    2.  Hence find BÂD. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Hence, or otherwise, find the area of the parallelogram. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} =  \\hspace{0.525em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AD}} &= \\vec{\\text{OD}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} =  \\hspace{0.525em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{1.1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AC}} &= \\vec{\\text{AB}} + \\vec{\\text{AD}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} =  \\hspace{0.525em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\vec{\\text{OC}} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} + \\vec{\\text{AC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 10 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 11 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of C are\n     $(10,9,11)$\n\n3.\n    1.  Using the scalar product formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AB}}\\cdot\\vec{\\text{AD}} &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (6)(2) + (2)(4) + (3)(4) \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 32\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        |\\vec{\\text{AB}}| &= \\sqrt{6^2 + 2^2 + 3^2} \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        and\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        |\\vec{\\text{AD}}| &= \\sqrt{2^2 + 4^2 + 4^2} \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 6\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using the angle between two vectors\n        formula, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}D}) &= \\dfrac{\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{AD}}}{\\raisebox{-0.35em}{$\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big|$}} \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}D}) &= \\dfrac{32}{(7)(6)} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\text{B\\^{A}D} \\approx \\ang{40.4} \\hspace{2.05em}\n        $$\n\n4.  Using the area of a triangle formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.75em} A_{\\square\\text{ABCD}} &= 2A_{\\triangle\\text{ABD}} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big|\\vec{\\text{AB}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{AD}}\\big|\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{B\\^{A}D})\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(7)(6)\\sin \\ang{40.4}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 27.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA344","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA344\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n**In this question, distance is in metres.**\n\nJack and John are flying airplanes in a straight line at a constant\nspeed.\n\nJack's airplane passes through a point P. Its position, $t$ seconds\nafter it passes\n\nthrough P, is given by\n$\\mathbf{r}_1 = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}$ ,\n$t \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the coordinates of P.\n\n    2.  Find the speed of Jack's airplane in ms$^{-1}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  After six seconds, Jack's airplane passes through a point Q.\n    1.  Find the coordinates of Q.\n\n    2.  Find the distance the airplane has travelled during the six\n        seconds. [4 marks]\n\nJohn's airplane passes through a point R. Its position, $s$ seconds\nafter it passes\n\nthrough R, is given by\n$\\mathbf{r}_2 = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}s\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}$ ,\n$s \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3.  Find the coordinates where the two airplanes intersect.\n    [4 marks]\n\n4.  Is Jack or John flying faster? Justify your answer.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $\\vec{\\text{OP}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n        Hence the coordinates of P are\n         $(5,8,2)$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Jack's speed} &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(-1)^2 + 2^2 + 3^2} \\hspace{2.25em}  \\\\[10pt]\n        &\\approx 3.74 \\text{ ms}^{-1}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Evaluating $\\mathbf{r}_1$ for $t= 6$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.5em} \\vec{\\text{OQ}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}6\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 20 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the coordinates of Q are\n         $(-1,20,20)$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{PQ}} &= \\vec{\\text{OQ}} - \\vec{\\text{OP}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 20 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{1.15em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix*}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} \\big|\\vec{\\text{PQ}}\\big| &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 12^2 + 18^2} \\\\[8pt]\n        &\\approx 22.4 \\text{ m}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using the vector equation $\\mathbf{r}_1 = \\mathbf{r}_2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    5 - t &= 4 - s  \\\\[6pt]\n    8 + 2t &= 4 + 5s  \\\\[6pt]\n    2 + 3t &= 5 + 3s\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the system\n    of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.95em} t = 3 \\hspace{0.75em} \\& \\hspace{0.65em} s = 2 \\hspace{2.25em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we try to verify the equation $(3)$ for values $(4)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2 + 3(3) &= 5 + 3(2) \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    11 &= 11\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Evaluating $\\mathbf{r}_2$ for $s= 2$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.3em} \\mathbf{r}_2 &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}2\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 11 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates where the two airplanes intersect are\n     $(2,14,11)$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{John's speed} &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(-1)^2 + 5^2 + 3^2} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 5.92 \\text{ ms}^{-1} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $\\text{Since \\(5.92 \\text{ ms}^{-1} > 3.74 \\text{ ms}^{-1}\\), John is flying faster.}$"},{"ref":"AA345","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA345\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nTwo lines $L_1$ and $L_2$ are represented by the vector equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1:&\\hspace{0.75em} \\mathbf{r}_1 = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.1em},\\hspace{0.25em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\\\[6pt]\nL_2:&\\hspace{0.75em} \\mathbf{r}_2 = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 11 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}s\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ k \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.1em},\\hspace{0.25em} s \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe lines $L_1$ and $L_2$ are perpendicular to each other.\n\n1.  Show that $k = -1$. [2 marks]\n\nThe lines $L_1$ and $L_2$ intersect at the point A.\n\n2.  Find $\\vec{\\text{OA}}$. [3 marks]\n\nLet\n$\\vec{\\text{OB}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix*}$\nand\n$\\vec{\\text{BC}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n\n3.\n    1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{BA}}$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the angle CB̂A. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the scalar product formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\cdot \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ k \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.25em} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $L_1 \\perp L_2$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.5em} (0)(1) + (1)(2) + (2)(k) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    2 + 2k &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.3em} k = -1\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the equations of $L_1$ and $L_2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2 &= -1 + s  \\\\[6pt]\n    1 + t &= -3 + 2s \\hspace{1.1em}\n    %4 + 2t &= 11 - s\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we solve the system\n    of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$, we get $s = 3$ and $t = 2$.\n\n    Hence, substituting $s = 3$ in the equation of $L_2$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.5em} \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 11 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}3\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{BA}} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} - \\vec{\\text{OB}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2.7em} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the angle between two vectors formula, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.5em} \\cos\\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= \\dfrac{\\vec{\\text{BA}} \\cdot \\vec{\\text{BC}}}{\\raisebox{-0.35em}{$\\big|\\vec{\\text{BA}}\\big|\\big|\\vec{\\text{BC}}\\big|$}} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= \\dfrac{(0)(1) + (1)(0) + (1)(1)}{\\big[\\sqrt{0^2 + 1^2 + 1^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\\big[\\sqrt{1^2 + 0^2 + 1^2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]} \\\\[9pt]\n        \\cos \\hspace{0.05em}(\\text{C\\^{B}A}) &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\text{C\\^{B}A} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\hspace{7.8em}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA346","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA346\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA line $L$ passes through the points A$(6,5,0)$ and B$(8,6,1)$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Show that\n        $\\vec{\\text{AB}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n    2.  Write down a vector equation of $L$ in the form\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathbf{d}$.\n        [2 marks]\n\nThe line $L$ also passes through the point C$(2,b,-2)$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\nThe point D has coordinates $(p^2,2,5p)$.\n\n3.  Given that $\\vec{\\text{CD}}$ is perpendicular to $L$, find\n    the possible values of $p$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} =  \\hspace{0.525em} \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\hspace{1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.\n        $\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.1em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ b \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{1.7em} [\\text{for some $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the vector equation $(1)$ for $t$ and $b$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    t &= -2 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{2.05em} b &= 5 + (-2) = \\hspace{0.575em}3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{CD}} &= \\vec{\\text{OD}} - \\vec{\\text{OC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] p^2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5p \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] p^2-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5p+2 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the scalar product formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] p^2-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5p+2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\cdot \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.25em} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{CD}} \\perp L$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3.5em} (p^2-2)(2) + (-1)(1) + (5p+2)(1) &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\n    2p^2 + 5p - 3 &= 0 \\\\[11pt]\n    (p+3)(2p-1) &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11em} p = -3,\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA347","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA347\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet\n$\\mathbf{u} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix*}$\nand\n$\\mathbf{v} = \\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] m \\\\ n \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\nGiven that $\\mathbf{v}$ is a unit vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf{u}$,\n\nfind the possible values of $m$ and the possible values of $n$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{v}| &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\mathbf{v}$ is a unit vector}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\textstyle \\hspace{9.8em} \\sqrt{m^2 + n^2 + 0^2} &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\nm^2 + n^2 & =1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n(2)(m) + (-1)(n) + (5)(0) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n2m - n &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n2m &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the equations $(1)$ and\n$(2)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^2 + (2m)^2 & =1 \\\\[10pt]\n5m^2 & =1 \\\\[6pt]\nm^2 &= \\dfrac{1}{5} \\\\[3pt]\nm &= \\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\hspace{0.55em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the\npossible values of $m$ and $n$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&m = \\dfrac{-1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\hspace{0.25em} n = \\dfrac{-2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\[9pt]\n&m = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\hspace{0.25em} n = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA348","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA348\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nConsider the points A$(3,2,-5)$ and B$(-3,6,-5)$.\n\n1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$. [2 marks]\n\nLet C be a point such that\n$\\vec{\\text{AC}} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}$ .\n\n2.  Find the coordinates of C. [2 marks]\n\nThe line $L$ passes through B and is parallel to $\\vec{\\text{AC}}$.\n\n3.  Write down a vector equation of $L$ in the form\n    $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathbf{d}$. [1 mark]\n\n4.  Given that $|\\vec{\\text{AB}}| = k|\\vec{\\text{AC}}|$, find\n    $k$. [3 marks]\n\nThe point D lies on $L$ such that $|\\vec{\\text{AB}}| = |\\vec{\\text{BD}}|$.\n\n5.  Find the possible coordinates of D. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=  \\hspace{0.525em} \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{OC}} &= \\vec{\\text{OA}} + \\vec{\\text{AC}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of C are\n     $(6,2,-3)$\n\n3.\n    $\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\\hspace{0.5em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |\\vec{\\text{AB}}| &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 4^2 + 0^2} = \\sqrt{52} \\\\[6pt]\n    |\\vec{\\text{AC}}| &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{3^2 + \\hspace{-0.35em}\\phantom{(}0^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{13}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} |\\vec{\\text{AB}}| &= k|\\vec{\\text{AC}}| \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sqrt{52} &= k\\sqrt{13} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.6em} k = 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/efbc3d2593cbb7ee3d35f1d9edfc31a76ed4fc37.svg\" alt=\"efbc3d2593cbb7ee3d35f1d9edfc31a76ed4fc37.svg\">\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.4em} \\vec{\\text{BD}} &= \\lambda\\hspace{0.05em}\\vec{\\text{AC}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{BD}} \\parallel \\vec{\\text{AC}}$}\\hspace{0.025em}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\lambda\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}  \\hspace{2.05em} [\\text{for some $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |\\vec{\\text{AB}}| &= |\\vec{\\text{BD}}| \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{4.25em} 2|\\vec{\\text{AC}}| &= |\\lambda\\hspace{0.05em}\\vec{\\text{AC}}| \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (d)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    2|\\vec{\\text{AC}}| &= |\\lambda||\\vec{\\text{AC}}| \\\\[8pt]\n    2 &= |\\lambda| \\\\[8pt]\n    \\lambda &= \\pm 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.4em} \\vec{\\text{OD}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} + \\vec{\\text{BD}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} \\pm \\hspace{0.05em}2\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{0.15em} \\text{or} \\hspace{0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the possible coordinates of D are\n     $(3,6,-1)$\n    and  $(-9,6,-9)$"},{"ref":"AA349","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA349\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe line $L$ has equation\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\hspace{0.25em} t \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe point A has coordinates $(4,8,-3)$. The point B lies on $L$ such\nthat $\\vec{\\text{AB}}$\\\nis perpendicular to $L$.\n\n1.  Find $\\vec{\\text{OB}}$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the shortest distance from A to $L$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of the reflection of the point A in the line\n    $L$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.5em} \\vec{\\text{OB}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for some $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} - \\vec{\\text{OA}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[r] 4 + t\\phantom{\\hspace{0.1em}} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.25em} -1 + t\\phantom{\\hspace{0.1em}} \\\\ 0 - t\\phantom{\\hspace{0.1em}} \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{1.75em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] t \\\\ t-9 \\\\ 3-t \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] t \\\\ t-9 \\\\ 3-t \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\vec{\\text{AB}} \\perp L$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{4.8em} (t)(1) + (t-9)(1) + (3-t)(-1) &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    3t - 12 &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n    t &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, substituting $t = 4$ in the equation\n    $(1)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} \\vec{\\text{OB}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}4\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/9202e38aec1b6f80eafe90905f697bf0d80eab98.svg\" alt=\"9202e38aec1b6f80eafe90905f697bf0d80eab98.svg\">\n\n    Substituting $t = 4$ in the equation $(2)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\vec{\\text{AB}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 4-9 \\\\ 3-4 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} d_{\\text{min}} &= |\\vec{\\text{AB}}| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{4^2 + (-5)^2 + (-1)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{42}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textstyle  \\vec{\\text{OA}'} &= \\vec{\\text{OB}} + \\vec{\\text{AB}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 12 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coordinates of $\\text{A}'$ are\n     $(12,-2,-5)$"},{"ref":"AA352","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA352\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**In this question, distances are measured in kilometres.**\n\nTwo boats, A and B, are observed from an origin O. Relative to O, their\nposition vectors at time $t$ hours after midday\nare given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}_\\text{A} &= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\\\[16pt]\n\\mathbf{r}_\\text{B} &= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 9 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the minimum distance between the two boats.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} \\mathbf{r}_\\text{B} - \\mathbf{r}_\\text{A} &= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\left[\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ 9 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\right]\\hspace{-0.1em}- \\left[\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2+t \\\\ 6-3t \\end{pmatrix*}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the distance between the boats at time $t$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} d &= |\\mathbf{r}_\\text{B} - \\mathbf{r}_\\text{A}| \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(2+t)^2 + (6-3t)^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we draw the\ngraph of $d$ vs $t$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we get\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/c713f2118510c91d01f0e46439229cbba7703bd1.svg\" alt=\"c713f2118510c91d01f0e46439229cbba7703bd1.svg\">\n\nHence the minimum distance between the boats is\n $3.79\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km}$"},{"ref":"AA353","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA353\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**In this question, distances are measured in kilometres.**\n\nTwo tankers, A and B, are observed from an origin O. Relative to O,\ntheir position vectors at time $t$ hours after 6:00pm are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}_\\text{A} &= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 7 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\\\[16pt]\n\\mathbf{r}_\\text{B} &= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 9 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the time when the distance between the two tankers is at a minimum.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5em} \\mathbf{r}_\\text{B} - \\mathbf{r}_\\text{A} &= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\left[\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 6 \\\\ 9 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\right]\\hspace{-0.1em}- \\left[\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 7 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5 \\\\ 8 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.05em}t\\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\hspace{-0.1em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 5-2t \\\\ 8-t \\end{pmatrix*}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the distance b$/$n the tankers at time $t$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} d &= |\\mathbf{r}_\\text{B} - \\mathbf{r}_\\text{A}| \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(5-2t)^2 + (8-t)^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we draw the\ngraph of $d$ vs $t$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we get\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/0a9045a2c8ff75e4cf565b8206c94e84658fadde.svg\" alt=\"0a9045a2c8ff75e4cf565b8206c94e84658fadde.svg\">\n\nHence the time when the distance b$/$n the two tankers is at a minimum\nis  $\\text{9:36pm}$"},{"ref":"AA662","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA662\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nTwo lines have vector equations given by\n\n$\\mathit{L}_1$ :\n $\\mathbf{r}_1 = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}t\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\hspace{0.25em}t \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n  $\\mathit{L}_2$ :\n $\\mathbf{r}_2 = \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\hspace{0.1em}s\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em}, \\hspace{0.25em}s \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nPoint A is the point on $\\mathit{L}_1$ that is closest to the origin.\n\n1.  Find the coordinates of A. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the shortest distance between $\\mathit{L}_1$ and\n    $\\mathit{L}_2$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.9em} \\mathbf{d}_1 &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{direction vector of $\\mathit{L}_1$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Point A lies on $\\mathit{L}_1$.\n\n    Hence, using the equation of $\\mathit{L}_1$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.5em} \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 + t \\\\ 2t-1 \\\\ 2 + t \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{for some $\\hspace{0.05em}t \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Point A is closest to the origin when\n    $\\vec{\\text{OA}} \\perp \\mathbf{d}_1$.\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $\\vec{\\text{OA}} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}_1 = 0$\n    for $t$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 3 + t \\\\ 2t-1 \\\\ 2 + t \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3.8em} (3 + t)(1) + (2t - 1)(2) + (2 + t)(1) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    t &= -0.5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $t = -0.5$ in the expression $(1)$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7em} \\vec{\\text{OA}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 2.5 \\\\ \\hspace{-0.25em}-2 \\\\ 1.5 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the coordinates of A are\n     $(2.5, -2, 1.5)$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10.9em} \\mathbf{d}_2 &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{direction vector of $\\mathit{L}_2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we draw the shortest distance PQ between $\\mathit{L}_1$ and\n    $\\mathit{L}_2$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Geometry%20%26%20Trigonometry/Vectors%2C%20Points%20%26%20Lines/images/d1298c64cb8be88af8fbc9efe70dd34fbb2aea76.svg\" alt=\"d1298c64cb8be88af8fbc9efe70dd34fbb2aea76.svg\">\n\n    Using the equations of $\\mathit{L}_1$ and $\\mathit{L}_2$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} \\text{P}(3+t, 2t-1,2+t)& \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for some $\\hspace{0.05em}t \\in \\mathbb{R}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{Q}(4+s,3s-1,s)& \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for some $s\\hspace{-0.06em} \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the coordinates of P and Q, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.15em} \\vec{\\text{PQ}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] s-t+1 \\\\ 3s-2t \\\\ s-t-2 \\end{pmatrix*}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have $\\vec{\\text{PQ}} \\perp \\mathbf{d}_1$ and\n    $\\vec{\\text{PQ}} \\perp \\mathbf{d}_2$.\n\n    Hence, using the equation $\\vec{\\text{PQ}} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}_1 = 0$,\n    we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] s-t+1 \\\\ 3s-2t \\\\ s-t-2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot \\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} &= 0 \\hspace{8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    (s-t+1)(1) + (3s-2t)(2) + (s-t-2)(1) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    8s-6t &= 1  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Similarly, using the\n    equation $\\vec{\\text{PQ}} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}_2 = 0$, we deduce\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] s-t+1 \\\\ 3s-2t \\\\ s-t-2 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot \\hspace{-0.15em} \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix*}\\hspace{-0.15em} &= 0 \\hspace{8em} \\\\[6pt]\n     (s-t+1)(1) + (3s-2t)(3) + (s-t-2)(1) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n     11s-8t &= 1  \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n     Hence, solving the system\n    of linear equations $(3)$-$(4)$ for $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.4em} s &= -1\\hspace{0.75em}\\&\\hspace{0.75em} t = -1.5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting $t=-1.5$ & $s = -1$ in the expression $(2)$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.2em} \\vec{\\text{PQ}} &= \\hspace{-0.15em}\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 1.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1.5 \\end{pmatrix*} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the shortest distance between $\\mathit{L}_1$ and\n    $\\mathit{L}_2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.4em} \\text{PQ} &= \\sqrt{(1.5)^2 + 0^2 + (-1.5)^2} \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 2.12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA891","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA891\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nGiven any two non-zero vectors, $u$ and $v$, show that\n$|u\\times v|^2 = |u|^2|v|^2 - (u\\cdot v)^2$.","markScheme":"Using the vector product formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|u\\times v| &= |u||v|\\sin \\theta \\\\[12pt]\n|u\\times v|^2 &= |u|^2|v|^2\\sin^2\\theta \\\\[12pt]\n&= |u|^2|v|^2\\left(1-\\cos^2\\theta\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n&= |u|^2|v|^2-|u|^2|v|^2\\cos^2\\theta \\\\[12pt]\n&= |u|^2|v|^2-\\left(|u||v|\\cos\\theta\\right)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n&= |u|^2|v|^2-\\left(u\\cdot v\\right)^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have\nshowed$|u\\times v|^2 = |u|^2|v|^2-\\left(u\\cdot v\\right)^2$"},{"ref":"AA931","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA931\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -6 \\\\ 8 \\end{pmatrix*}$ and $|\\mathbf{v}|=20$.\n\n1. Find the possible range of values for $|\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}|$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Given that $\\mathbf{v}=k \\mathbf{u}$ for some $k\\in\\mathbb{R}$, find $\\mathbf{v}$ when $|\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}|$ is a minimum. [2 marks]\n\n3. Find the vector $\\mathbf{w}=\\begin{pmatrix*}[c] a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix*}$ such that $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$, $|\\mathbf{w}|=|\\mathbf{v}|$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf{u}$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. First we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf{u}$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    |\\mathbf{u}| &= \\sqrt{(-6)^2+8^2} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\sqrt{100} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 10\\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the triangle inequality, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\big||\\mathbf{u}| - |\\mathbf{v}|\\big| &\\leq |\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}| \\leq |\\mathbf{u}| + |\\mathbf{v}| \\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\big|10 - 20\\big| &\\leq |\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}| \\leq 10 + 20 \\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\big|-10\\big| &\\leq |\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}| \\leq 30\\\\[12pt]\n\n    & \\hspace{-0.99em}\\boxed{10\\leq |\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}| \\leq 30}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Let $\\mathbf{v} = k\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] -6k \\\\ 8k \\end{pmatrix*}$, then $\\mathbf{v}$ satisfies:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\big|\\mathbf{v}\\big| &= 20 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\sqrt{(-6k)^2 + (8k)^2} &= 20\\\\[10pt]\n\n    100k^2 &= 400 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    k^2 &= 4 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    k &= \\pm 2 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now, since $|\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}| = 10$ we must have $k = -2$. Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-2.16mm}\\boxed{\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 12 \\\\ -16 \\end{pmatrix*}} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using that the scalar product of two perpendicular vectors is zero, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    -6a+8b &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    8b &= 6a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    b &= \\dfrac{3}{4}a\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathbf{w} &= \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] a \\\\[4pt] \\dfrac{3}{4}a \\end{pmatrix*}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now, using that $|\\mathbf{w}| = 20$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\sqrt{a^2+\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}a\\right)^2} &= 20 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a^2 + \\dfrac{9}{16}a^2 &= 400 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{25}{16}a^2 &= 400 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    a^2 &= 256 \\\\[12pt]\n\n    a &= 16 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since }a>0]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-3mm}\\boxed{\\mathbf{w} = \\begin{pmatrix*}[c] 16 \\\\[4pt] 12 \\end{pmatrix*}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA990","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA990\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the vectors $\\bm{u}=\\sqrt{3}\\bm{i}+\\bm{j}$ and $\\bm{v}=\\tan(k)\\bm{i}+\\bm{j}$, where $k \\in \\mathbb{R}^+, k \\neq\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nLet $\\theta$ be the angle between $u$ and $v$.\n\n1. Find an expression for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of $k$. [3 marks]\n\n2. Find the exact value of $\\theta$ as $k \\rightarrow \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Use the dot product formula and the Pythagorean identity formula to write an expression for $\\cos{\\theta}$ in terms of $k$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos{\\theta} &= \\dfrac{\\bm{u}\\cdot\\bm{v}}{|\\bm{u}||\\bm{v}|}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}\\tan{k}+1}{\\sqrt{4}\\times\\sqrt{\\tan^2{k}+1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}\\tan{k}+1}{2\\times\\sec k}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2. Multiply the top and bottom of the fraction by $\\cos{k}$ and then find the limit as $k \\rightarrow \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\sin{k}+\\cos{k}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\lim_{k \\to \\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\sin{k}+\\cos{k}\\right)\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\lim_{k \\to \\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\sin{k}+\\cos{k}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Apply the limit of $k$ to each term in the expression\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\lim_{k \\to \\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\sin{k}\\right)+\\lim_{k \\to \\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\left(\\cos{k}\\right)\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}+0)\\\\[6pt]\n        \\cos{\\theta}&=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\theta&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]}]},{"name":"Number & Algebra","subtopics":[{"name":"Complex Numbers- Algebraic Operations","questions":[{"ref":"AA093","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA093\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the complex number $z = \\dfrac{w_1}{w_2}$ where\n$w_1 = \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{6}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$w_2 = 3 + \\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$.\n\n1.  Express $w_1$ and $w_2$ in modulus-argument form and write down\n    1.  the modulus of $z$;\n\n    2.  the argument of $z$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the smallest positive integer value of $n$ such that $z^n$ is a\n    real number. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we express $w_1$ and $w_2$ in modulus-argument form, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w_1 = 2\\sqrt{2}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\hspace{1.5em} \\text{and} \\hspace{1.5em}  w_2 = 2\\sqrt{3}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    1.  Hence the modulus of $z$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        |z| &= \\dfrac{|w_1|}{|w_2|} \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}\\sqrt{2}}{\\cancel{2}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence the argument of $z$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} \\arg z &= \\arg w_1 - \\arg w_2 \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  The $n$th power of $z$ is a real number if\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} \\sin \\left(\\arg \\left(z^n\\right)\\right) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin \\left(n\\arg z\\right) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by de Moivre's theorem}] \\\\[3pt]\n    \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{6}\\right) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a) (ii)}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the smallest $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$ such that $z^n$ is a real\n    number is  $n = 6$"},{"ref":"AA094","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA094\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nConsider the complex numbers $z_1 = 3 \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}(\\ang{120})$\nand $z_2 = 2 + 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$.\n\n1.  Calculate $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$ giving your answer both in\n    modulus-argument form and\n\n    Cartesian form. [7 marks]\n\n2.  Use your results from part (a) to find the exact value of\n    $\\sin \\ang{15}\\cdot\\,\\sin \\ang{45} \\cdot\\,\\sin \\ang{75}$,\n\n    giving your answer in the form $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{a}}{b}$ where\n    $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we express $z_1$ in Cartesian form, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_1 &= 3 \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}(\\ang{120}) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3 \\cos (\\ang{120}) + 3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (\\ang{120}) \\\\[4pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{2} \\\\[4pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{2}(-1+\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we express $z_2$ in modulus-argument form, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.4em} z_2 &= 2 + 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2(1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}(\\cos(\\ang{45}) +{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin(\\ang{45})) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\sqrt{2}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}(\\ang{45})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, if we calculate $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$ giving answer in\n    modulus-argument form, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{z_1}{z_2} &= \\dfrac{3 \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}(\\ang{120})}{2\\sqrt{2}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}(\\ang{45})} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}(\\ang{75}) \\hspace{2.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, if we calculate $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$ giving answer in Cartesian\n    form, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\dfrac{z_1}{z_2} &= \\dfrac{\\frac{3}{2}(-1+\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})}{2 + 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\frac{3}{2}(-1+\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})}{2 + 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}\\cdot \\dfrac{2-2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{2-2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3(-1+\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(1 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})}{(2 + 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(2 - 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{-3 + 3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}+ 3\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}+ 3\\sqrt{3}}{4 + 4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3(\\sqrt{3} - 1)}{8} + \\dfrac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{8}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we equate the two expressions for $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$ from part\n    (a), we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cos(\\ang{75}) + \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin(\\ang{75}) &= \\dfrac{3(\\sqrt{3} - 1)}{8} + \\dfrac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{8}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos \\ang{75} &= \\dfrac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot\\dfrac{3(\\sqrt{3} - 1)}{8} \\hspace{0.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin \\ang{75} &= \\dfrac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot\\dfrac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{8} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\sin \\ang{15}\\cdot\\, \\sin \\ang{45} \\cdot\\, \\sin \\ang{75} &= \\cos \\ang{75}\\cdot\\, \\sin \\ang{45} \\cdot\\, \\sin \\ang{75} \\\\[9pt]\n    &=  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(3-1)\\sqrt{2}}{16} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA095","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA095\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\n1.  Solve $2\\sin(x+\\ang{120}) = \\sqrt{3}\\cos(x + \\ang{60})$, for\n    $x \\in [0,\\ang{180}]$.  [5 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $\\sin \\ang{75} + \\cos \\ang{75} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  Let\n    $z = \\sin 4\\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(1 - \\cos 4\\theta)$,\n    for $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$, $\\theta \\in [0,\\ang{90}]$.\n\n    1.  Find the modulus and argument of $z$ in terms of $\\theta$.\n    2.  Hence find the fourth roots of $z$ in modulus-argument form.\n        [14 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the compound angle formulas for sine and cosine, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2\\sin(x+\\ang{120}) &= \\sqrt{3}\\cos(x + \\ang{60}) \\\\[12pt]\n    2\\hspace{0.05em}(\\sin x \\cos \\ang{120} + \\cos x \\sin \\ang{120}) &= \\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.05em}(\\cos x \\cos \\ang{60} - \\sin x \\sin \\ang{60}) \\\\[12pt]\n    2\\sin x \\hspace{0.05em}(- \\sin \\ang{30}) + 2\\cos x \\cos \\ang{30} &= \\sqrt{3}\\cos x \\cos \\ang{60} - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x \\sin \\ang{60} \\\\[6pt]\n    2\\sin x \\bigg(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg) + 2\\cos x\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg) &= \\sqrt{3}\\cos x \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg) - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg)  \\\\[6pt]\n    -2\\sin x + 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos x &= \\sqrt{3}\\cos x - 3\\sin x  \\\\[12pt]\n    \\sin x &= -\\sqrt{3}\\cos x \\\\[12pt]\n    \\tan x &= -\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.2em} x = \\ang{120}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the compound angle formula for sine, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (\\sin \\ang{75} + \\cos \\ang{75})^2 &= (\\sin \\ang{75})^2 + (\\cos \\ang{75})^2 + 2\\sin \\ang{75}\\cos \\ang{75}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 + \\sin \\left(\\ang{75} + \\ang{75}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 + \\cos \\ang{60} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 1 + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[4pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{6}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if we take the\n    square root of both sides of the equality $(1)$, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\sin \\ang{75} + \\cos \\ang{75} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n3.\n    1.  The modulus of $z$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} |z| &= \\left[(\\sin 4\\theta)^2  + (1 - \\cos 4 \\theta)^2\\right]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\left[(\\sin 4\\theta)^2 + (\\cos 4 \\theta)^2 + 1 - 2\\cos 4\\theta\\right]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\left[2 - 2\\cos 4 \\theta\\right]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\left[2\\hspace{0.05em}(1 - \\cos 4\\theta)\\right]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\left[2\\big(1-(1 - 2\\hspace{0.05em}(\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\big)\\right]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by double-angle formula}] \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\left[4\\hspace{0.05em}(\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\right]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 2\\hspace{0.05em}|\\hspace{-0.05em}\\sin 2\\theta\\hspace{0.05em}| \\\\[10pt]\n        &= 2\\sin 2\\theta \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\sin 2\\theta \\geq 0$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\ang{90}]$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using the double-angle formulas for sine and cosine, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tan \\left(\\arg z\\right) &= \\dfrac{1- \\cos 4\\theta}{\\sin 4\\theta} \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[4pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1-\\big[1 - 2(\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\big]}{2\\sin 2\\theta\\cos 2\\theta} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\cos 2\\theta} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\tan 2\\theta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the argument of $z$ is\n         $\\arg z = 2 \\theta$\n\n    2.  Hence, using part (c) and de Moivre's theorem, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} z^\\frac{1}{4} &= (2\\sin 2\\theta \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}2\\theta)^\\frac{1}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\sqrt[4]{2\\sin 2\\theta}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\left(\\dfrac{2\\theta + 2k\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sqrt[4]{2\\sin 2\\theta}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta + k\\pi}{2}\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        for $k = 0,1,2,3$."},{"ref":"AA096","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA096\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\n1.  Solve the equation $z^3 = 27$, $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$, giving your\n    answer in the form\\\n    $z = r(\\cos \\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)$\n    and in the form $z = a + b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$\n    where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.  [6 marks]\n\n2.  Consider the complex numbers\n    $z_1 = -1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu} \\text{ and } z_2 =\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg[\\mathrm{cos}\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)+\\mathrm{i}\\,\\mathrm{sin}\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\bigg)\\bigg]$\n    .\n\n    1.  Write $z_1$ in the form\n        $r(\\cos \\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)$.\n\n    2.  Calculate $z_1z_2$ and write in the form\n        $a + b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n        $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n    3.  Hence find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)$ in\n        the form $c + d\\sqrt{3}$ where $c,d \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n    4.  Find the smallest $p \\in \\mathbb{Q}^+$ such that $(z_1z_2)^p$ is\n        a positive real number. [11 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^3 &= 27\\hspace{0.05em}(\\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0) \\hspace{5.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using de Moivre's theorem, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.6em} z &= 27^{\\frac{1}{3}}(\\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0)^\\frac{1}{3} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2k\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{2k\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $k = 0,1,2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the roots of the equation $z^3 = 27$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k = 0 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = 3\\hspace{0.05em}(\\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0) = 3 \\\\[9pt]\n    k = 1 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = 3\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] = -\\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu} \\\\[10pt]\n    k = 2 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = 3\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] = -\\dfrac{3}{2} - \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  If we write $z_1$ in modulus-argument form, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.8em} z_1 &= -1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{2}\\bigg[\\hspace{-0.25em}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{2}\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\Big)\\Big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  If we write $z_2$ in Cartesian form, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z_2 &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, if we calculate $z_1z_2$ giving answer in Cartesian form,\n        we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z_1z_2 &= (-1  + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\bigg] \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} - \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}+ \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}- \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{-\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  If we calculate $z_1z_2$ giving answer in modulus-argument form,\n        we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} z_1z_2 &= \\bigg[\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\Big)\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}} \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &=  \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, comparing the expressions $(1)$ and $(2)$, we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big) &= \\dfrac{-\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big) &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}}{4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tan\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\Big) &= \\tan\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big) \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big) \\div \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{12}\\Big) \\hspace{2em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}}{\\cancel{4}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{4}}{-\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{8 - 4\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 2 - \\sqrt{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    4.  The $p$ th power of $z_1z_2$ is a real\n        number if\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{7.4em} \\sin \\left(\\arg \\left((z_1z_2)^p\\right)\\right) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        \\sin \\left(p\\arg \\left(z_1z_2\\right)\\right) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by de Moivre's theorem}] \\\\[6pt]\n        \\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{13p\\pi}{12}\\Big) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (b) (iii)}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the smallest $p \\in \\mathbb{Q}^+$ such that $(z_1z_2)^p$\n        is a positive real number is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.4em} p = \\dfrac{24}{13}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        **Note:**\n\n        Solving $\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{13p\\pi}{12}\\Big) = 0$ gives $p = \\dfrac{12k}{13}$, but this only ensures $(z_1z_2)^p$ is $\\emph{real}$. Odd multiples of $\\pi$ give a $\\emph{negative}$ real number, so these must be rejected.\n\n        For example, $k = 1$ gives\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (z_1z_2)^{\\frac{12}{13}} &= \\cos \\pi + i \\sin \\pi \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we require $\\dfrac{13p\\pi}{12} = 2k\\pi$, giving $p = \\dfrac{24}{13}$."},{"ref":"AA097","marks":"19","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA097\n\n**Maximum mark: 19**\n\n1.\n    1.  Expand\n        $(\\cos \\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)^4$\n        by using the binomial theorem.\n\n    2.  Hence use de Moivre's theorem to prove that\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos 4\\theta = \\cos^4 \\theta - 6\\cos^2 \\theta\\sin^2 \\theta + \\sin^4 \\theta. \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  State a similar expression for $\\sin 4 \\theta$ in terms of\n        $\\cos \\theta$ and $\\sin \\theta$. [6 marks]\n\nLet\n$z = r(\\cos \\alpha + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)$,\nwhere $\\alpha$ is measured in degrees, be the solution\\\nof $z^4 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}= 0$ which has the\nsmallest positive argument.\n\n2.  Find the modulus and argument of $z$.\n     [4 marks]\n\n3.  Use (a) (ii) and your answer from (b) to show that\n    $8\\cos^4\\alpha - 8\\cos^2 \\alpha + 1 = 0$.  [4 marks]\n\n4.  Hence express $\\cos \\ang{22.5}$ in the form\n    $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{a + b\\sqrt{c}}}{d}$ where $a,b,c,d \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the binomial theorem, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &(\\cos \\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)^4 =  \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.375em}\\cos^4 \\theta + 4{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\cos^3\\theta \\sin \\theta - 6\\cos^2 \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta - 4{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\cos \\theta \\sin^3 \\theta + \\sin^4 \\theta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using de Moivre's theorem, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (\\cos \\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)^4 = \\cos 4\\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 4 \\theta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, if we compare real parts of the expressions $(1)$ and\n        $(2)$, we deduce\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos 4\\theta = \\cos^4 \\theta - 6\\cos^2 \\theta\\sin^2 \\theta + \\sin^4\\theta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  If we compare imaginary parts of the expressions $(1)$ and\n        $(2)$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sin 4\\theta = 4\\cos^3 \\theta\\sin \\theta - 4\\cos\\theta \\sin^3 \\theta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using de Moivre's theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} z^4 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}&= 0 \\\\[13pt]\n    z^4 &= {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[11pt]\n    z^4 &= \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n    z &= \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8} + \\dfrac{k\\pi}{2}\\Big) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $k = 0,1,2,3$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the modulus of $z$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r=1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and the argument of $z$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\alpha = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8} = \\ang{22.5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Substituting $\\theta = \\alpha$ in the equation $(3)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos^4 \\alpha - 6\\cos^2 \\alpha\\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^4 \\alpha &= \\cos 4\\alpha \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos^4 \\alpha - 6\\cos^2 \\alpha\\hspace{0.05em}(1- \\cos^2 \\alpha) + (1 - \\cos^2 \\alpha)^2 &= \\cos \\ang{90} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos^4 \\alpha - (6\\cos^2 \\alpha - 6\\cos^4 \\alpha) + (1 - 2\\cos^2 \\alpha + \\cos^4 \\alpha) &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8.55em} 8\\cos^4 \\alpha - 8\\cos^2\\alpha + 1 = 0\n    $$\n\n4.  Hence, using the quadratic formula to solve the\n    equation $(4)$ for $\\cos^2\\alpha$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.5em} \\cos^2 \\alpha &= \\dfrac{-(-8) \\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^2-4(8)(1)}}{2(8)} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos^2 \\alpha &= \\dfrac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n    |\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cos \\alpha\\hspace{0.05em}| &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 \\pm \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos \\ang{22.5} > \\cos \\ang{45} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} > 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the inequalities $(5)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos \\ang{22.5}  =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA098","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA098\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the complex numbers\n$u = 1 + 2 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$v = 2 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$.\n\n1.  Given that $\\dfrac{1}{u} + \\dfrac{1}{v} = \\dfrac{6\\sqrt{2}}{w}$,\n    express $w$ in the form\n    $a + b {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n    $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.  [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $w^\\ast$ and express it in the form\n    $re^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\theta}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Substituting $u = 1+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n    $v = 2+{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ in the given equation,\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} \\dfrac{6\\sqrt{2}}{w} &= \\dfrac{1}{u} + \\dfrac{1}{v} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=  \\dfrac{1}{1 + 2 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} + \\dfrac{1}{2 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=  \\dfrac{1}{1 + 2 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}\\cdot\\dfrac{1 - 2 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{1 - 2 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} + \\dfrac{1}{2 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}\\cdot\\dfrac{2 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{2 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1 - 2 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{5} + \\dfrac{2 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3 - 3 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $w$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w &= \\dfrac{30\\sqrt{2}}{3 - 3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10\\sqrt{2}}{1 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}\\cdot\\dfrac{1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}(1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})}{2} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 5\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}(1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) \\\\[14pt]\n    &= 5\\sqrt{2} + 5\\sqrt{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu} \\hspace{4em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The conjugate of $w$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.1em} w^\\ast &= 5\\sqrt{2} - 5\\sqrt{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[8pt]\n    &= 10\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 10\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right] \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 10\\left[\\cos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 10\\hspace{0.05em}e^{-{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by Euler's formula}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA099","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA099\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nConsider $w = \\dfrac{z - 1}{z + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}$\nwhere $z = x + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}y$ and\n${\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}= \\sqrt{-1}$.\n\n1.  If $z = {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$,\n    1.  write $w$ in the form $r\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\theta$;\n\n    2.  find the value of $w^{14}$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Show that in general,\n    $$\n    w = \\dfrac{(x^2 - x + y^2 + y) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(y - x + 1)}{x^2 + (y + 1)^2}\n    $$\n    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find condition under which $\\mathrm{Re}(w) = 1$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  State condition under which $w$ is:\n    1.  real;\n\n    2.  purely imaginary. [2 marks]\n\n5.  Find the modulus of $z$ given that $\\arg w = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Substituting $z = {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ in the\n        expression for $w$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        w &= \\dfrac{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}- 1}{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}+ {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}- 1}{2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}\\cdot\\dfrac{-{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{-{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{2} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, if we express $w$ given by $(1)$ in modulus-argument\n        form, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        w &= \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using de Moivre's theorem, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        w^{14} &= \\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right]^{14} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2^7} \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{128}\\left(-{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= - \\dfrac{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{128}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Substituting $z = x + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}y$ in the\n    expression for $w$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w &= \\dfrac{(x + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}y) - 1}{(x + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}y) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(x-1) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}y}{x  + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(y + 1)} \\cdot \\dfrac{x  - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(y + 1)}{x  - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(y + 1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{x(x - 1) + y(y + 1) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}[xy - (x-1)(y+1)]}{x^2 + (y+1)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(x^2 - x) + (y^2 + y) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}[\\cancel{xy} - \\cancel{xy} - x + y + 1]}{x^2 + (y+1)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(x^2 - x + y^2 + y) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(y - x + 1)}{x^2 + (y+1)^2} \\hspace{3em} [(x,y) \\neq (0,-1)]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Hence $\\mathrm{Re}(w) = 1$ if\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{x^2 - x + y^2 + y}{x^2 + (y+1)^2} &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by $(2)$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    x^2 - x + y^2 + y &= x^2 + (y+1)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n    x^2 - x + y^2 + y &= x^2 + y^2 + 2y + 1 \\hspace{1.2em} \\\\[12pt]\n    -x &= y + 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{12.9em}y = - x - 1 \\hspace{3em} [(x,y) \\neq (0,-1)]\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  $w$ is real if\n         $y - x + 1 = 0$\n        where $(x,y) \\neq (0,-1)$.\n\n    2.  $w$ is purely imaginary if\n         $x^2 - x + y^2 + y  = 0 \\text{ and } y - x + 1 \\neq 0$\n\n5.  Given that $\\arg w = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tan \\hspace{0.05em}(\\arg w) &= \\dfrac{y - x + 1}{x^2 - x + y^2 + y} \\\\[3pt]\n    1 &= \\dfrac{y - x + 1}{x^2 - x + y^2 + y} \\\\[7pt]\n    x^2 - x + y^2 + y &= y - x + 1 \\\\[13.5pt]\n    x^2 + y^2 &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    |z| = 1 \\hspace{0.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA100","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA100\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve the equation $z^3 = 1$, giving your answers in Cartesian form.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^3 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\nz^3 &= \\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0 \\hspace{5.2em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using de Moivre's theorem, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{9.9em} z &= (\\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0)^\\frac{1}{3} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2k\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{2k\\pi}{3}\\Big) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $k = 0,1,2$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the roots of the equation $z^3 = 1$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk = 0 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = \\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0 = 1 \\\\[7pt]\nk = 1 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu} \\\\[10pt]\nk = 2 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\Big) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA101","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA101\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\n1.  Solve the equation $\\sin (x + \\ang{90}) = 2\\cos(x - \\ang{60})$,\n    $\\ang{0} < x < \\ang{360}$.  [5 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $\\sin \\ang{15} + \\cos \\ang{15} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$.\n     [4 marks]\n\n3.  Let\n    $z = 1 - \\cos 4\\theta - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 4\\theta$,\n    for $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$, $0 < \\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n    1.  Find the modulus and argument of $z$. Express each answer\\\n        in its simplest form.\n\n    2.  Hence find the fourth roots of $z$ in modulus-argument form.\n        [11 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the compound angle formulas for sine and cosine, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin\\left(x + \\ang{90} \\right) &= 2\\cos\\left(x - \\ang{60}\\right) \\\\[14pt]\n    \\sin x \\cos \\ang{90} + \\cos x \\sin \\ang{90} &= 2\\cos x \\cos \\ang{60} + 2\\sin x \\sin \\ang{60} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\sin x \\left(0\\right) + \\cos x \\left(1\\right) &= 2\\cos x \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg) + 2\\sin x \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\cos x &= \\cos x + \\sqrt{3}\\sin x \\\\[14pt]\n    0 &= \\sqrt{3}\\sin x \\\\[14pt]\n    \\sin x &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11.8em} x = \\ang{180}  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\ang{0} < x < \\ang{360}$}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the compound angle formula for sine, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.1em} \\sin \\ang{15} &=\\sin\\left(\\ang{45} - \\ang{30}\\right) \\\\[14pt]\n    &= \\sin\\ang{45}\\cos\\ang{30} - \\cos \\ang{45}\\sin \\ang{30} \\\\[8pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[4pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the compound\n    angle formula for cosine, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos \\ang{15} &=\\cos (\\ang{45} - \\ang{30}) \\\\[14pt]\n    &= \\cos\\ang{45}\\cos\\ang{30} + \\sin \\ang{45}\\sin \\ang{30} \\\\[8pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[4pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sin \\ang{15} + \\cos \\ang{15} &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.\n    1.  The modulus of $z$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{5em} |z| &= \\big[(1-\\cos 4\\theta)^2 + (\\sin 4\\theta)^2\\big]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\big[1 - 2\\cos 4\\theta + (\\cos 4\\theta)^2 + (\\sin 4\\theta)^2\\big]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\big[2 - 2\\cos 4\\theta\\big]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\big[2 - 2\\big(1 - 2\\hspace{0.05em}(\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\big)\\big]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by double-angle formula}] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\big[4\\hspace{0.05em}(\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\big]^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[11pt]\n        &= 2\\hspace{0.05em}|\\hspace{-0.05em}\\sin 2\\theta\\hspace{0.05em}| \\\\[11pt]\n        &= 2\\sin 2\\theta \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\sin 2\\theta > 0$ for $0 < \\theta < \\ang{90}$}] \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using the double-angle formulas for sine and cosine, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tan \\left(\\arg z \\right)&= \\dfrac{(-\\sin 4\\theta)}{1 - \\cos 4\\theta} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{2\\sin 2\\theta \\cos 2\\theta}{1 - \\big[1 - 2\\hspace{0.05em}(\\sin 2\\theta)^2\\big]} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{\\cos 2\\theta}{\\sin 2\\theta} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -\\cot 2\\theta \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - 2\\theta\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n        &=\\tan\\left(2\\theta - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the argument of $z$ is\n         $\\arg z = 2\\theta - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n    2.  Hence, using part c (i) and de Moivre's theorem, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z^\\frac{1}{4} &= \\left[2\\sin 2\\theta \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\Big(2\\theta - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\Big)\\right]^\\frac{1}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\sqrt[4]{2\\sin 2\\theta} \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\bigg(\\dfrac{2\\theta - \\pi/2 + 2k\\pi}{4}\\bigg) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sqrt[4]{2\\sin 2\\theta} \\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{8} + \\dfrac{k\\pi}{2}\\bigg)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        for $k = 0,1,2,3$."},{"ref":"AA103","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA103\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\n1.  Use de Moivre's theorem to find the value of\n    $\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right]^{12}$.\n     [2 marks]\n\n2.  Use mathematical induction to prove that\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.5em} (\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^n = \\cos (n\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (n\\alpha) \\hspace{1em} \\text{for all } n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+.\n    $$\n    [6 marks]\n\nLet\n$w = \\cos \\alpha + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha$.\n\n3.  Find an expression in terms of $\\alpha$ for\n    $w^n - (w^\\ast)^n$, $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$, where $w^\\ast$ is\n    the complex conjugate of $w$. [2 marks]\n\n4.\n    1.  Show that $ww^\\ast = 1$.\n\n    2.  Write down and simplify the binomial expansion of\n        $(w - w^\\ast)^3$ in terms of $w$ and $w^\\ast$.\n\n    3.  Hence show that\n        $\\sin (3\\alpha) = 3\\sin \\alpha - 4 \\sin^3 \\alpha$. [5 marks]\n\n5.  Hence solve\n    $4\\sin^3\\alpha + (2 \\cos \\alpha - 3) \\sin \\alpha = 0$ for\n    $0 \\leq \\alpha \\leq \\pi$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using de Moivre's theorem, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right]^{12} &= \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{12\\pi}{6}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{12\\pi}{6}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\cos 2\\pi + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 2\\pi \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.6em} (\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^n = \\cos (n\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (n\\alpha) \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for\n    all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.3em} (\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^1 &= \\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\cos (1\\cdot\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (1\\cdot\\alpha)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^k = \\cos (k\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (k\\alpha)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} (\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^{k+1} = \\cos ((k+1)\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin ((k+1)\\alpha)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the left hand side of the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5em} &(\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^{k+1} = \\\\[5pt]\n&= (\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^k\\cos \\alpha - (\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^k ({\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha) \\\\[6pt]\n&= [\\cos (k\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (k\\alpha)]\\cos \\alpha - [\\cos (k\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (k\\alpha)] ({\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)  \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\cos (k\\alpha)\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (k\\alpha) \\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\cos (k\\alpha) \\sin \\alpha - \\sin (k\\alpha)\\sin \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n&= [\\cos (k\\alpha)\\cos \\alpha - \\sin (k\\alpha)\\sin \\alpha] - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}[\\sin (k\\alpha) \\cos \\alpha + \\cos (k\\alpha) \\sin \\alpha]  \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\cos((k+1)\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin((k+1)\\alpha) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by compound angle formulas}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n3.  Using de Moivre's theorem and the identity $(2)$ from part\n    (b), we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w^n - (w^\\ast)^n &= (\\cos \\alpha + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^n - (\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^n \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [\\hspace{0.05em}\\cos (n\\alpha) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (n\\alpha)] - [\\hspace{0.05em}\\cos (n\\alpha) - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (n\\alpha)] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (n\\alpha)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  The product of $w$ and its conjugate is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.2em} ww^\\ast &= (\\cos \\alpha + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)(\\cos \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\cos^2 \\alpha - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\cos^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  If we simplify the binomial expansion of $(w - w^\\ast)^3$, we\n        get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (w - w^\\ast)^3 &= w^3 - 3w^2w^\\ast + 3w(w^\\ast)^2 - (w^\\ast)^3 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= w^3 - 3w(ww^\\ast) + 3w^\\ast(ww^\\ast) - (w^\\ast)^3 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= w^3 - 3w + 3w^\\ast - (w^\\ast)^3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (d) (i)}] \\hspace{0.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= w^3 - (w^\\ast)^3 - 3(w - w^\\ast)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Substituting $n = 1$ and $n = 3$ in the identity $(3)$ from part\n        (c), we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        n=1 &: \\hspace{0.5em} w - (w^\\ast) = 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n        n=3 &: \\hspace{0.5em} w^3 - (w^\\ast)^3 = 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (3\\alpha)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, from part d (ii), we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.3em} (w - w^\\ast)^3 &= w^3 - (w^\\ast)^3 - 3\\hspace{0.05em}(w - w^\\ast) \\\\[6pt]\n        (2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha)^3 &= 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (3\\alpha) - 3\\hspace{0.05em}(2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha) \\\\[6pt]\n        -8{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin^3 \\alpha &= 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin (3\\alpha) - 6{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n        4\\sin^3 \\alpha &= -\\sin (3\\alpha) + 3 \\sin \\alpha \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\sin (3\\alpha) = 3 \\sin \\alpha -4\\sin^3 \\alpha \\hspace{0.9em}\n        $$\n\n5.  If we rewrite the given equation, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4\\sin^3\\alpha + (2 \\cos \\alpha - 3) \\sin \\alpha &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    2\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha  &= 3\\sin \\alpha - 4\\sin^3\\alpha \\\\[7pt]\n    \\sin (2\\alpha) &= \\sin (3\\alpha) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (d) (iii)}] \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $(4)$ for $\\alpha$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.7em} \\alpha &= 0 \\hspace{0.75em}\\text{or}\\hspace{0.75em} 3\\alpha = k\\pi - 2\\alpha \\hspace{2.2em} [\\text{where $k$ is an odd integer}] \\\\[7pt]\n    \\hspace{8.7em} \\alpha &= 0 \\hspace{0.75em}\\text{or}\\hspace{0.75em} \\alpha = \\dfrac{k\\pi}{5} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{where $k = 1,3,5$ since $\\alpha \\in [0,\\pi]$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\alpha = 0,\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{\\pi}{5},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{3\\pi}{5},\\hspace{0.25em} \\pi \\hspace{8.55em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1039","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1039\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the complex numbers $z_1=-1+\\sqrt{3}ai$ and $z_2=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}(a-1)}{3a+1}+i$.  Where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}, a\\neq-\\tfrac{1}{3}$.\n\nAnother complex number is defined such that $z_3=\\dfrac{z_2}{z_1}$.\n\nLet $\\theta=\\arg({z_3})$, where $-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$.\n\n1. Show that $\\tan{\\theta}$ is constant for $a \\in \\mathbb{R}, a\\neq-\\tfrac{1}{3}$. [4 marks]\n\n2. Hence, find the two possible values of $\\theta$ and the intervals of $a$ for which they will occur.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Begin by dividing the two complex numbers\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_3&=\\frac{\\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}(a-1)}{3a+1}+i}{-1+\\sqrt{3}ai}\\\\[3pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Multiply the top and bottom by the complex conjugate of $z_1$, then simplify\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{\\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}(a-1)}{3a+1}+i}{-1+\\sqrt{3}ai}\\times\\frac{{-1-\\sqrt{3}ai}}{{-1-\\sqrt{3}ai}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{\\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}(a-1)(-1-\\sqrt{3}ai)}{3a+1}+(-1-\\sqrt{3}ai)i}{3a^2+1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[3pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Write the numerator as a single fraction and simplify\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{3a^2+1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}(a-1)(-1-\\sqrt{3}ai)+(-i+\\sqrt{3}a)(1+3a)}{3a+1}\\right)\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{1}{3a^2+1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}(-a-\\sqrt{3}a^2i+1+\\sqrt{3}ai)-i-3ai+\\sqrt{3}a+3\\sqrt{3}a^2}{3a+1}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Cancel out terms.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{3a^2+1}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{-\\sqrt{3}a}-3a^2i +\\sqrt{3}+\\cancel{3ai}-i-\\cancel{3ai}+\\cancel{\\sqrt{3}a}+3\\sqrt{3}a^2}{3a+1}\\right)\\\\[3pt]\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{3a^2+1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{3}a^2-(3a^2+1)i}{3a+1}\\right)\\\\[3pt]\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{\\cancel{3a^2+1}}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{(3a^2+1)}-\\cancel{(3a^2+1)i}}{3a+1}\\right)\\\\\n    &=\\tfrac{1}{3a+1}\\left(\\sqrt{3}-i\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we can find $\\tan\\theta$ independent of $a$\n    $$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\\\[3pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $ \\tan{\\theta}\\text{ is constant for a }\\in \\mathbb{R}, a\\neq-\\tfrac{1}{3}$\n\n2. First we can find the two values of $\\theta$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\theta&=\\arctan{\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}\\\\[3pt]\n        &=-\\frac{\\pi}{6},\\,\\frac{5\\pi}{6} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can now sketch two representations of $z_3=\\frac{1}{3a+1}(\\sqrt{3}-i)$ on Argand diagrams which both have the same value for $\\tan \\theta$.\n    $$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    $$\n\n    When $\\frac{1}{3a+1}>0$ then $\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and when $\\frac{1}{3a+1}<0$ then $\\theta = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Algebraic%20Operations/images/ans1.png\" alt=\"ans1\">\n\n    We can use a sign diagram (or a sketch) to determine the conditions for the sign of $\\tfrac{1}{3a+1}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Algebraic%20Operations/images/ans2.png\" alt=\"ans2\">\n\n    Therefore $\\tfrac{1}{3a+1}>0$ when $a>-\\tfrac{1}{3}$ and $\\tfrac{1}{3a+1}<0$ when $a<-\\tfrac{1}{3}$\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\theta = -\\tfrac{\\pi}{6} \\text{ for } a>-\\tfrac{1}{3} \\text{ and } \\theta = \\tfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\text{\n    for } a<-\\tfrac{1}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1058","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1058\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. Find three distinct roots of the equation $z^3 + 27i  = 0$, $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$, giving your answers in exact cartesian form. [3 marks]\n\nLet $w$ be the sum of two of the roots of $z$, from part (a), which have non-zero real parts and $\\dfrac{c}{c+w}=2i$.\n\n2. Show that $c=\\dfrac{6}{5} + \\dfrac{12}{5} i$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using de Moivre's theorem, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^3 + 27i &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    z^3 &= -27i \\\\[8pt]\n    z &= 27^{\\tfrac{1}{3}}\\left(\\cos \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} + i \\sin \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)^{\\tfrac{1}{3}} \\\\[8pt]\n    z_k &= 3 \\left(\\cos \\dfrac{3\\pi/2+2k\\pi}{3} + i \\sin \\dfrac{3\\pi/2+2k\\pi}{3}\\right)   \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    for $k = 0,1,2$.\n\n    Hence the roots of the equation $z^3 + 27 i = 0$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        z_0 &= 3 \\left(\\cos \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + i \\sin \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        z_1 &= 3 \\left(\\cos \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6} + i \\sin \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n        z_2 &= 3 \\left(\\cos \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6} + i \\sin \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\\right)   \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Converting the roots to its Cartesian form, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k = 0 : \\hspace{0.525em}z_0 = 3i \\hspace{2em} k = 1 : \\hspace{0.525em}z_1 = -\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\dfrac{3i}{2} \\hspace{2em} k = 2 : \\hspace{0.525em}z_2 = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\dfrac{3i}{2}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. First, the sum of the roots described is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        w &= z_1 + z_2 \\\\[8pt]\n        &= -3i \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Working out the complex equation, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{c}{c+w} &= 2i \\\\[8pt]\n        c &= 2i(c-3i)  \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        c &= 2i c + 6 \\\\[8pt]\n        c-2i c &= 6 \\\\[8pt]\n        c(1-2i) &= 6 \\\\[8pt]\n        c &= \\dfrac{6}{1-2i} \\\\[8pt]\n        c &= \\dfrac{6}{1-2i} \\cdot \\dfrac{1+2i}{1+2i}  \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        c &= \\dfrac{6+12i}{1+4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.5em} z = \\dfrac{6}{5} + \\dfrac{12}{5} i \\hspace{2em} \\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA113","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA113\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe complex numbers $w$ and $z$ satisfy the equations\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{z}{w} &= {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}, \\\\[6pt]\nw^\\ast + 2z &= 4 + 5{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $w$ and $z$ in the form\n$a + b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n$a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\hspace{1.5em} z = {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}w \\\\\n$$\nand\n\n$$\nw^\\ast + 2z = 4 + 5{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $w = x + y{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$z = {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(x + y{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})$\nin the equation $(1)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + y{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})^\\ast + 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(x + y{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) &= 4 + 5{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[6pt]\n(x - y{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) + \\left(-2y + 2x{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\right) &= 4 + 5{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[6pt]\n(x - 2y) + (2x - y){\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}&= 4 + 5{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, comparing real and imaginary parts of the equation $(2)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rcl}\nx - 2y &=& 4 \\\\[8pt]\n2x - y &=& 5\n\\end{array} \\hspace{1.5em}\n$$\nSolving the system of equations\n$(3)$ for $x$ and $y$, we obtain $x = 2$ and $y = -1$.\n\nHence we find\n $w = 2-{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$\nand\n $z = 1+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$"},{"ref":"AA1148","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1148\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the complex conjugates $z$ and $z^*$.\n\n1.  Show that $(x-z)(x-z^*)=x^2-2\\text{Re}(z)x+|z|^2$. [3 marks]\n\n$z_1$ and $z_2$ are two of the roots of the real polynomial $z^4+az^3+bz^2+cz+d$, where $a,b,c,d \\in \\mathrm{Z}$.\n\n2. Given that $z_1=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cis}{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}$ and $z_2=2-i$, find the values of $a, b, c$ and $d$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Expand the expression\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (x-z)(x-z^*)&=x^2-zx-z^*x+zz^*\\\\[6pt]\n        &=x^2-(z+z^*)x+zz^* \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we let $z=a+bi$ then $z^*=a-bi$.\n\n    We can now simplify $z+z^*$ and $zz^*$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z+z^*&=a+bi+a-bi\\\\[6pt]\n        &=2a\\\\[6pt]\n        &=2\\text{Re}(z) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        zz^*&=(a+bi)(a-bi)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=a^2-b^2i^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=a^2+b^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=|z|^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting these expressions into the expansion above we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (x-z)(x-z^*)&=x^2-(z+z^*)x+zz^*\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{x^2-2\\text{Re}(z)x+|z|^2} \\hspace{0.5em} \\text{... as required.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  Recognise that a real polynomial has complex roots in  conjugate pairs.\n\n    The first two roots given were\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_1 = \\sqrt{2} \\,\\text{cis} \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{5em} z_2 = 2 - i\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    So we can write the remaining roots of the polynomial\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        z_3=z_1^*&=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cis}\\,{\\tfrac{-\\pi}{4}} &\\hspace{2em} & z_4=z_2^*=2+i\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can now write the polynomial in factorised form using the roots\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        z^4+az^3+bz^2+cz+d&=(z-z_1)(z-z_2)(z-z_3)(z-z_4)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=(z-z_1)(z-z_1^*)(z-z_2)(z-z_2^*)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can use the identity we proved in part(a) to rewrite the factors\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{red}{(z-z_1)(z-z_1^*)}\\textcolor{blue}{(z-z_2)(z-z_2^*)}&=\\textcolor{red}{(z^2-2\\text{Re}{(z_1)}z+|z_1|^2)}\\textcolor{blue}{(z^2-2\\text{Re}{(z_2)}z+|z_2|^2)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next we can identify the elements we need complete the expansion\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Re}(z_1)&=\\sqrt{2}\\times \\cos{\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}} &\\hspace{2em} & |z_1|^2=\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^2\\\\[6pt]\n    \\textcolor{white}{\\text{Re}(z_1)}&=\\sqrt{2}\\times \\tfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} &\\hspace{2em} &  \\textcolor{white}{|z_1|^2}=\\textcolor{orange}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\textcolor{white}{\\text{Re}(z_1)}&=\\textcolor{red}{1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{Re}(z_2)&=\\textcolor{blue}{2} &\\hspace{2em} & |z_2|^2=(\\sqrt{2^2+1^2})^2\\\\[6pt]\n    \\textcolor{white}{\\text{Re}(z_2)}& &\\hspace{2em} &  \\textcolor{white}{|z_2|^2}=\\textcolor{green}{5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now substitute these values into the expansion above\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &(z^2-2\\text{Re}{(z_1)}z+|z_1|^2)(z^2-2\\text{Re}{(z_2)}z+|z_2|^2)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=(z^2-2\\times \\textcolor{red}{1}z+\\textcolor{orange}{2})(z^2-2\\times \\textcolor{blue}{2}z+\\textcolor{green}{5})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now expand this expression and simplify\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &(z^2-2z+2)(z^2-4z+5)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=z^4-4z^3+5z^2-2z^3+8z^2-10z+2z^2-8z+10\\\\[6pt]\n        &=z^4-6z^3+15z^2-18z+10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, $a=-6,\\, b=15,\\, c=-18 \\text{ and } d=10$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA115","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA115\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the equation $2z^4 + az^3 + bz^2 +cz + d = 0$, where $a, b, c, d \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$.\\\nTwo of the roots of the equation are $\\log_2 10$ and $i\\sqrt{5}$ and the sum of all the roots is $4 + \\log_2 5$.\n\nShow that $15a = -(d + 90)$.","markScheme":"Since $\\im\\sqrt{5}$ is a root of the equation with real coefficients, its conjugate $-\\im\\sqrt{5}$ is also a root.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet $z_4$ denote the fourth root. Using the sum of all four roots.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cancel{\\im\\sqrt{5}} + (-\\cancel{\\im\\sqrt{5}}) + \\log_2 10 + z_4 &= 4 + \\log_2 5 \\\\[6pt]\nz_4 &= 4 + \\log_2 5 - \\log_2 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nApplying the **laws of logarithms (1.7)**, to simplify $\\log_2 10$, $\\log_a xy = \\log_a x + \\log_a y$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_4 &= 4 + \\log_2 5 - (\\log_2 2 + \\log_2 5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4 - 1 \\\\[6pt]\nz_4 &= 3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the **sum and product of roots of polynomial equations (2.12)**, to express $a$ and $d$ in terms of the four roots, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=0}^{n} a_r x^r = 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{sum is } \\dfrac{-a_{n-1}}{a_n}\\,;\\ \\text{product is } \\dfrac{(-1)^n a_0}{a_n}$, with $n = 4$, $a_n = 2$, $a_{n-1} = a$ and $a_0 = d$.\n\nFrom the sum of the roots.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\dfrac{a}{2} &= 4 + \\log_2 5 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\na &= -8 - 2\\log_2 5 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the product of the roots.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{d}{2} &= (\\im\\sqrt{5})(-\\im\\sqrt{5})(\\log_2 10)(3) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n             &= 5(3)\\log_2 10 \\\\[6pt]\nd &= 30\\log_2 10 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nComputing $15a + d$ and applying the laws of logarithms to $\\log_2 10 = \\log_2 2 + \\log_2 5$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15a + d &= 15(-8 - 2\\log_2 5) + 30\\log_2 10 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -120 - \\cancel{30\\log_2 5} + 30\\log_2 2 + \\cancel{30\\log_2 5} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -120 + 30 \\\\[6pt]\n15a + d &= -90\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nRearranging the terms.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15a &= -d - 90 \\\\[6pt]\n&\\boxed{15a = -(d + 90)}\\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required.} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA116","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA116\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Find the roots of $z^{16} = 1$ which satisfy the condition\n    $0 < \\arg(z) < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$,\n\n    expressing your answer in the form\n    $re^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\theta}$, where\n    $r, \\theta \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Let $S$ be the sum of the roots found in part (a).\n    1.  Show that $\\mathrm{Re}(S) = \\mathrm{Im}(S)$.\n\n    2.  By writing $\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ as\n        $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, find the value of\n        $\\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big)$ in the form\n        $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{a + \\sqrt{b}}}{c}$,\n\n        where $a, b$ and $c$ are integers to be determined.\n\n    3.  Hence, or otherwise, show that\n        $S = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\hspace{-0.1em}\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}} + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big)\\big(1+{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\big)$.\n        [11 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^{16} &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    z^{16} &= \\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using de Moivre's theorem, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} z &= (\\cos 0 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 0)^\\frac{1}{16} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2k\\pi}{16}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{2k\\pi}{16}\\Big) \\\\[4pt]\n    &= \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{k\\pi}{8}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{k\\pi}{8}\\Big) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{k\\pi}{8}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by Euler's formula}] \\hspace{0.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    for $k = 0,1,2, \\dots, 15$.\n\n    Hence the roots of the equation $z^{16} = 1$ that satisfy\n    $0 < \\arg(z) < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k = 1 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z_1 = e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\\\[6pt]\n    k = 2 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z_2 = e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\[6pt]\n    k = 3 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z_3 = e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{3\\pi}{8}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  If we let $S = z_1 + z_2 + z_3$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{Re}(S) &= \\cos \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) + \\cos \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) + \\cos \\Big(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{Im}(S) &= \\sin \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) + \\sin \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) + \\sin \\Big(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}\\Big)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using the reflection law for cosine and sine, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) &= \\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{3\\pi}{8}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sin \\Big(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}\\Big) \\\\[15pt]\n        \\cos \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) &= \\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sin \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) \\\\[15pt]\n        \\cos \\Big(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}\\Big) &= \\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\sin \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\Big)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we deduce\n         $\\mathrm{Re}(S) = \\mathrm{Im}(S)$\n\n    2.  Using the half-angle formula for cosine, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.5em} \\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) &= \\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+\\cos(\\pi/4)}{2}} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big[1 + \\dfrac{\\raisebox{-0.25em}{$\\sqrt{2}$}}{2}\\Big]} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Similarly, using the half-angle formula for sine, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8}\\Big) &= \\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1-\\cos(\\pi/4)}{2}} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big[1 - \\dfrac{\\raisebox{-0.25em}{$\\sqrt{2}$}}{2}\\Big]} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{Re}(S) &= \\cos \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\Big) + \\cos \\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\Big) + \\cos \\Big(\\frac{3\\pi}{8}\\Big) \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\hspace{-0.1em}\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}} + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.8em} S &= \\mathrm{Re}(S) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\mathrm{Im}(S) \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\mathrm{Re}(S) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\mathrm{Re}(S) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\mathrm{Re}(S) = \\mathrm{Im}(S)$}] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\mathrm{Re}(S)(1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\hspace{-0.1em}\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}} + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big)\\big(1+{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\big)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1282","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1282\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe complex number $z$ is a root of the equation $|z+4i|=|z-10i|$.\n\n1.  Show that the imaginary part of $z$ is $3$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Let $\\omega_1$ and $\\omega_2$ be two possible values of $z$ such that $|z|=6$.\n\n    1.  If $\\omega_1$ is in the first quadrant sketch both solutions on an Argand diagram.\n\n    2.  Hence find the arguments of $\\omega_1$ and $\\omega_2$.[4 marks]\n\nA different complex number, $v$, is defined such that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    v&=\\dfrac{\\omega_{1}^k\\,\\omega_2}{-i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $k$ is a real number that can take any value in the interval $-10\\leq k \\leq 10$ and $-\\pi \\leq \\arg{(-i)} \\leq \\pi$.\n\n3.  1.  Find $\\arg{(v)}$ in terms of $k$ and $\\pi$.\n\n    2.  Hence find all possible values of $k$ such that $v$ is a real number.[8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We are told $z$ is a complex number, therefore $z$ is of the form $a+bi$ where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n    Hence we can rewrite the equation in terms of $a$ and $b$ to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        |a+bi+4i|&=|a+bi-10i|\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we collect together the real and imaginary parts it is easier to consider the magnitude of the number\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        |a+i(b+4)|&=|a+i(b-10)|\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall the magnitude of a complex number $|a+bi|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sqrt{a^2+(b+4)^2}&=\\sqrt{a^2+(b-10)^2}\\\\[6pt]\n        a^2+(b+4)^2&=a^2+(b-10)^2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\cancel{a^2}+\\cancel{b^2}+8b+16&=\\cancel{a^2}+\\cancel{b^2}-20b+100\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can find $b$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        28b&=84\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        b&=3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\text{Im}\\,(z)=3$ as required.\n\n2.  1.  The red dashed circle of radius $6$ represents the fact that $|z|=6$. Therefore $z$ must lie somewhere on this circle.\n\n        The blue horizontal line shows the possible values of $z$ when $\\text{Im}\\,(z)=3$, with $\\omega_1$ being in the first quadrant.\n\n        $z$ must lie on both the red circle and blue line\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Algebraic%20Operations/images/AA1282-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1282-a1\">\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &\\text{$\\omega_1$ and $\\omega_2$ both have imaginary values of $3$}.\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &\\text{Correct quadrants for $\\omega_1$ and $\\omega_2$}.\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using trigonometric ratios we can find $\\arg{(\\omega_1)}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Algebraic%20Operations/images/AA1282-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1282-a2\">\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\sin{[\\arg{(\\omega_1)}]}&=\\frac{3}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n            {[\\arg{(\\omega_1)}]}&=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            \\textcolor{red}{\\arg{(\\omega_1)}}&=\\textcolor{black}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using the symmetries of the diagram\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\sin{[\\textcolor{blue}{\\arg{(\\omega_2)}}]}&=\\pi-\\sin{[\\textcolor{red}{\\arg{(\\omega_1)}}]}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\textcolor{black}{\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  1.  In this question we are combining complex numbers and working with their arguments.\n\n        For questions of this nature it is most efficient to use Euler's form of a complex number.\n\n        Recall\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            z&=re^{\\normalsize i\\theta}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Where $r$ is the magnitude of the number and $\\theta$ the argument.\n\n        Hence, using our answers from part (b) we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\omega_1&=6e^{\\normalsize i\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}}\\\\[6pt]\n            \\omega_2&=6e^{\\normalsize i\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        If we raise $\\omega_1$ to the power $k$, using De Moivre's theorem, we get\n        $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n            \\omega_1&=6^{\\normalsize k}e^{\\normalsize i\\tfrac{k\\pi}{6}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Next we need to consider how to write $-i$ in Euler's form.\n\n        Here is a sketch of $-i$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Algebraic%20Operations/images/AA1282-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1282-a3\">\n\n        We can see that the magnitude is $1$ and the argument is $-\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$, angles measured in the clockwise direction are negative and we are told in the question that $-\\pi \\leq \\arg{(-i)} \\leq \\pi$.\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -i&=e^{\\normalsize -i\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Putting these results together for $v$ we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            v&=\\dfrac{\\omega_{1}^k\\,\\omega_2}{-i}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\dfrac{6^ke^{\\normalsize i\\textcolor{red}{\\tfrac{k\\pi}{6}}} \\times 6e^{\\normalsize i\\textcolor{blue}{\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}}}}{e^{\\normalsize -i\\textcolor{green}{\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}}}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Recall we want to find the argument of $v$, hence we need to simplify our expression so that it is in standard Euler's form $\\rightarrow z=re^{i\\theta}$.\n\n        We can now see the advantage of using Euler's form. To combine the angles we need to resolve the powers of $e$ using basic index laws\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=6\\times6^k e^{\\normalsize i\\textcolor{red}{\\tfrac{k\\pi}{6}}+\\textcolor{blue}{\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}}-\\left(\\textcolor{green}{-\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}}\\right)}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n             &=6^{k+1} e^{\\normalsize i\\textcolor{red}{\\tfrac{k\\pi}{6}}+\\textcolor{blue}{\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}}+\\textcolor{green}{\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}}}\\\\[6pt]\n             &=6^{k+1} e^{\\normalsize i\\textcolor{red}{\\tfrac{k\\pi}{6}}+\\textcolor{blue}{\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}}+\\textcolor{green}{\\tfrac{3\\pi}{6}}}\\\\[6pt]\n             &=6^{k+1} e^{\\normalsize i\\textcolor{black}{\\tfrac{k\\pi}{6}}+\\textcolor{black}{\\tfrac{8\\pi}{6}}}\\\\[6pt]\n             &=6^{k+1} e^{\\normalsize i\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}(k+8)}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        By looking at the exponent of $e$ we can find the argument of $v$ in terms of $k$ and $\\pi$, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\arg{(v)}&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\left(k+8\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  $v$ is a real number when it lies entirely on the real axis (Re $z$).\n\n        Therefore its argument must be a multiple of $\\pi$. Note $0$ is a multiple of $\\pi$.\n\n        We can form an equation for the argument. We aren't sure how many multiples of $\\pi$ we'll need so we can pick a few to get us started\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\left(k+8\\right)&=... -2\\pi,\\hspace{1em}-\\pi,\\hspace{1em}0,\\hspace{1em}\\pi,\\hspace{1em}2\\pi...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can now multiply by $6$ and divide by $\\pi$ to get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            k+8&=... -12,\\hspace{1em}-6,\\hspace{1em}0,\\hspace{1em}6,\\hspace{1em}12...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Subtracting $8$ from each answer we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            k&=...-20,\\hspace{1em}-14,\\hspace{1em}-8,\\hspace{1em}-2,\\hspace{1em}4...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Recall that $-10\\leq k \\leq 10$.\n\n        Hence we reject the first two answers. We may have missed one on the positive side.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{\\pi}{6}(k+8)&=3\\pi\\\\[6pt]\n            k+8&=18\\\\[6pt]\n            k&=10\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence all answers in the interval for $k$ are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            k&=-8, -2, 4, 10\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1293","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1293\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the real polynomial\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    p(z)=2z^3+az^2+bz-75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nOne of the roots of $p(z)$ is $3+4i$.\n\n1.  Find the remaining roots of $p(z)$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence determine the values of $a$ and $b$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We know, due to the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra, that a polynomial of order $3$ must have $3$ roots.\n\n    Also, $p(z)$ is a real polynomial, this means its coefficients are real numbers and that any complex roots __must__ come in conjugate pairs.\n\n    Hence we can write down the second root as $3-4i$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We have one root remaining, it must be real.\n\n    Looking at the polynomial we can see we have been given the first and last coefficient.\n\n    Consider the formulae for the sum and products of the roots of a polynomial\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Sum of roots}&=\\frac{-a_{n-1}}{a_n} \\hspace{3em}\\text{Product of roots}=\\frac{(-1)^na_{0}}{a_n}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $n$ is the order of the polynomial, which in this case is $3$.\n\n    $a_n$, where $n=3$ refers to the coefficient of the $z^3$ term and $a_0$ refers to the constant term.\n\n    We can write down the values\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        a_3&=2\\\\[6pt]\n        a_0&=-75\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    This is enough information to find the value of the product of the roots.\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Product of roots}&=\\frac{(-1)^na_{0}}{a_n}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{(-1)^3\\times (-75)}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{75}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we call the third root $z_3$ we can form an equation which we can solve on our G.D.C.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (3+4i)(3-4i)z_3&=\\frac{75}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        z_3&=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We can use the roots from part (a) to write out $p(z)$ in factorised form\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (z-(3+4i))(z-(3-4i)(z-\\tfrac{3}{2})&=0\\\\[6pt]\n         \\textcolor{red}{(z-3-4i)(z-3+4i)}(z-\\tfrac{3}{2})&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    There is a very useful identity, worth memorising, that is helpful when expanding brackets of $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{this}}$ form\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (z-w)(z-w^*)&=z^2-2\\text{Re}(w)(z)+|w|^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    In our case $w=3+4i$ therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        |w|^2&=(\\sqrt{3^2+4^2})^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=5^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    And\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Re}(w)&=\\text{Re}(3+4i)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Putting these results together we can expand the $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$ part of the factorised form to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n         \\textcolor{red}{(z-3-4i)(z-3+4i)}(z-\\tfrac{3}{2})&=0\\\\[6pt]\n         \\textcolor{red}{(z^2-2 \\times 3 z+25)}(z-\\tfrac{3}{2})&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can expand the rest of the expression to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        z^3-6z^2+25z-\\tfrac{3}{2}z^2+9z-\\tfrac{75}{2}&=0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can multiply throughout by $2$ and simplify to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2z^3-12z^2+50z-3z^2+18z-75&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        2x^3-15z^2+68z-75&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        p(z)=2x^3-15z^2+68z-75\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $a=-15$ and $b=68\\hspace{0.525em}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{placeholder}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1325","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1325\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe complex number $z_1$ has a modulus of $2$ and an argument of $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians.\n\n1.    Find $z_1$ in Cartesian form. [2 marks]\n\nConsider the complex numbers $z_2$ and $w$ such that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    z_2&=-2\\sqrt{3}-2\\mathrm{i}\\\\[6pt]\n    w&=z_1+z_2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe angle $\\alpha$ is such that $\\alpha=\\arg{(w)}$ where $-\\pi \\leq \\alpha < \\pi$.\n\n2.    Find the value of $\\alpha$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  First, let's write $z_1$ in the form $r\\,\\text{cis}\\,{\\theta}$ where $r$ is the modulus of $z_1$ and $\\theta$ is the argument of $z_1$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_1&=2\\,\\mathrm{cis}{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=2\\left(\\cos{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}+\\mathrm{i}\\sin{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The argument $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ is in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore cosine is positive and sine is negative.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_1&=2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{i}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\sqrt{3}-\\mathrm{i}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  It is best practice to use Cartesian form to add or subtract complex numbers.\n    We already have $z_1$ and $z_2$ in the correct form so we can go ahead and find $w$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w&=z_1+z_2\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\sqrt{3}-\\mathrm{i}+(-2\\sqrt{3}-2\\mathrm{i})\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=(\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{3})+(-\\mathrm{i}-2\\mathrm{i})\\\\[6pt]&=-\\sqrt{3}-3\\mathrm{i}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let's make a __rough__ sketch of $w$ on an Argand diagram.\n    As both the real and imaginary part are negative $w$ lies in the third quadrant. We can also (optionally) sketch approximate positions for $z_1$ and $z_2$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Algebraic%20Operations/images/AA1325.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1325\">\n\n    In the question we are told that the argument is in the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$. Therefore it must be the clockwise angle from the positive  real axis shown in $\\textcolor{green}{\\text{green}}$.\n\n    $\\beta$ is the acute angle shown above.\n\n    Using the tangent ratio we can find $\\beta$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tan{\\beta}&=\\frac{\\textcolor{blue}{3}}{\\textcolor{red}{\\sqrt{3}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\sqrt{3}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\beta&=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note that the angle we have found is the acute angle $\\beta$, as shown in the third quadrant of the diagram.\n\n    However, as mentioned the argument of $w$ is the clockwise angle from the positive horizontal axis.\n    This is why drawing an Argand diagram is so important as it helps us to find the correct argument.\n\n    Therefore the argument of $w$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arg{(w)}&=-\\left(\\pi-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n     &=-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\,\\text{radians}\\hspace{1em}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA241","marks":"19","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA241\n\n**Maximum mark: 19**\n\nLet\n$z = \\cos \\theta  + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta$,\nfor $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} < \\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find $z^3$ using the binomial theorem.\n\n    2.  Use de Moivre's theorem to show that\n        $\\cos 3\\theta = 4\\cos^3\\theta - 3\\cos \\theta$ and\n        $\\sin 3\\theta = 3\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^3\\theta$. [8 marks]\n\n2.  Hence show that\n    $\\dfrac{\\sin 3\\theta - \\sin \\theta}{\\cos 3\\theta + \\cos \\theta}  = \\tan \\theta$.\n     [6 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{1}{3}$, find the exact value of\n    $\\tan 3\\theta$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the binomial theorem, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z^3 &= (\\cos \\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)^3 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\cos^3 \\theta + 3\\cos^2 \\theta\\hspace{0.1em}({\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta) + 3 \\cos \\theta\\hspace{0.1em}({\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)^2 + ({\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)^3 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\cos^3 \\theta + 3 {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\cos^2 \\theta \\sin \\theta - 3 \\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin^3 \\theta \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (\\cos^3 \\theta - 3 \\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(3 \\cos^2 \\theta \\sin \\theta - \\sin^3 \\theta)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using de Moivre's theorem, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z^3 &= (\\cos \\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\theta)^3 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\cos 3\\theta + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin 3\\theta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, using part (a) (i), we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{6.5em} \\cos 3\\theta &= \\cos^3 \\theta - 3 \\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta \\hspace{3em} \\big[=\\mathrm{Re}(z^3)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        \\cos 3\\theta &= \\cos^3 \\theta - 3 \\cos \\theta\\hspace{0.1em}(1 - \\cos^2 \\theta) \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\cos 3\\theta = 4\\cos^3 \\theta - 3\\cos \\theta \\hspace{3.45em}\n        $$\n        and\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{6.5em} \\sin 3\\theta &= 3 \\cos^2 \\theta \\sin \\theta - \\sin^3 \\theta \\hspace{3em} \\big[=\\mathrm{Im}(z^3)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        \\sin 3\\theta &= 3(1 - \\sin^2 \\theta) \\sin \\theta - \\sin^3 \\theta \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\sin 3\\theta = 3\\sin \\theta - 4\\sin^3 \\theta \\hspace{3.55em}\n        $$\n\n2.  Hence, using the identities $(1)$ & $(2)$ from part (a) (ii), we\n    deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\sin 3\\theta - \\sin \\theta}{\\cos 3\\theta + \\cos \\theta} &= \\dfrac{(3\\sin \\theta - 4\\sin^3\\theta) - \\sin \\theta}{(4\\cos^3 \\theta - 3\\cos\\theta) + \\cos \\theta} \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\sin \\theta - 4\\sin^3 \\theta}{4\\cos^3 \\theta - 2\\cos \\theta} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\sin \\theta\\hspace{0.1em}(1 - 2\\sin^2 \\theta)}{2\\cos \\theta\\hspace{0.1em}(2\\cos^2 \\theta - 1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}\\sin \\theta \\hspace{0.1em}\\cancel{\\cos 2\\theta}}{\\cancel{2}\\cos \\theta \\hspace{0.1em}\\cancel{\\cos 2\\theta}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\[14pt]\n    &= \\tan \\theta\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{1}{3}$, for\n    $0 < \\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\hspace{3.6em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the identities $(1)$ & $(2)$ from part (a) (ii), we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.85em} \\tan 3 \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\sin 3\\theta}{\\cos 3\\theta} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\sin \\theta - 4\\sin^3 \\theta}{4\\cos^3 \\theta - 3\\cos \\theta} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3(1/3) - 4(1/3)^3}{4(2\\sqrt{2}/3)^3 - 3(2\\sqrt{2}/3)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1 - 4/27}{64\\sqrt{2}/27 - 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{27 - 4}{64\\sqrt{2} - 54\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{23}{10\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{23\\sqrt{2}}{20}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA721","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA721\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $z = re^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{\\pi}{3}}$ where\n$r \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\n1.  For $r = \\sqrt{2}$, express $z^2$ and $z^3$ in the form\n        $a+b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n        $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Given that the integer powers of\n    $w = (3-3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})\\hspace{0.05em}z$ lie\n    on a unit circle centred at the origin, find the value of $r$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To express $z^2$ and $z^3$ in Cartesian form, we need to use **the Polar form of a complex number (1.13)**, $z=r(\\cos\\theta+\\mathrm{i} \\sin\\theta)=re^{\\mathrm{i}\\theta}=r\\,\\text{cis}\\,\\theta$\n\n    and **De Moivre's Theorem (1.14)**, $[r(\\cos\\theta+\\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)]^n=r^ne^{\\mathrm{i} n\\theta}$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^2 &= (\\sqrt{2})^2e^{\\mathrm{i}(2\\times\\frac{\\pi}{3})} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2e^{\\mathrm{i}\\frac{2\\pi}{3}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos\\Big(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\Big(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(-\\frac{1}{2}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\Big(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[9pt]\n    z^2&=\\boxed{-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\mathrm{i}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^3 &= (\\sqrt{2})^3e^{\\mathrm{i}(3\\times\\frac{\\pi}{3})} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2\\sqrt{2}e^{\\mathrm{i} \\pi} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2\\sqrt{2}\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos \\pi + \\mathrm{i}\\sin \\pi \\Big] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2\\sqrt{2}\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}(-1) + \\mathrm{i}(0)\\Big] \\\\[9pt]\n    z^3&=\\boxed{-2\\sqrt{2} + 0\\mathrm{i}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  First, we find the modulus of $3-3\\mathrm{i}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |3-3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}| &= \\sqrt{3^2 + (-3)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{18} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the properties of modulus,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |w^k| &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for all $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}]  \\\\[13pt]\n    |w|^k &= 1 \\\\[14pt]\n    |w| &= 1 \\\\[14pt]\n    \\hspace{3.1em} |(3-3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})z| &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [w = (3-3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})z] \\\\[14pt]\n    |3-3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}||z| &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    3\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.05em}r &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    r = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\hspace{1.4em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA722","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA722\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $z = re^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{\\pi}{6}}$ where\n$r \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\n1.  For $r = \\sqrt{3}$, express $z^2$ and $z^3$ in the form\n        $a+b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n        $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Given that the integer powers of\n    $w = \\dfrac{z}{6+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}$ lie on a\n    unit circle centred at the origin, find the value of $r$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To express $z^2$ and $z^3$ in Cartesian form, we need to use **the Polar form of a complex number (1.13)**, $z=r(\\cos\\theta+\\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)=re^{\\mathrm{i}\\theta}=r\\,\\text{cis}\\,\\theta$\n\n    and **De Moivre's Theorem (1.14)**, $[r(\\cos\\theta+\\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)]^n=r^ne^{\\mathrm{i} n\\theta}$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^2 &= (\\sqrt{3})^2e^{\\mathrm{i}(2\\times\\frac{\\pi}{6})} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3e^{\\mathrm{i}\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos\\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\frac{1}{2}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\Big(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[9pt]\n    z^2 &=\\boxed{\\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\mathrm{i}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^3 &= (\\sqrt{3})^3e^{\\mathrm{i}(3\\times\\frac{\\pi}{6})} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{3}e^{\\mathrm{i}\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{3}\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos\\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\Big(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Big)\\Big]\\\\[5pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{3}\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}(0) + \\mathrm{i}(1)\\Big]\\\\[9pt]\n    z^3&=\\boxed{0+3\\sqrt{3}\\mathrm{i}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Finding the modulus of $6+2\\mathrm{i}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |6+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}| &= \\sqrt{6^2 + 2^2} \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{40} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\sqrt{10}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the properties of modulus,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |w^k| &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for all $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}]  \\\\[16pt]\n    |w|^k &= 1 \\\\[17pt]\n    |w| &= 1 \\\\[15pt]\n    \\hspace{3.9em} \\Big|\\dfrac{z}{6+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}\\Big| &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [w = z/(6+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})] \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{|z|}{|6+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}|} &= 1 \\\\[9pt]\n    \\dfrac{r}{2\\sqrt{10}} &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    r = 2\\sqrt{10} \\hspace{1.3em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA723","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA723\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $w = 2e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}$.\n\n1.  Write $w$, $w^2$ and $w^3$ in the form\n        $a + b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n        $a, b \\in\\mathbb{R}$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the smallest integer $k > 3$ such that $w^k$ is a real number.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To express $w$, $w^2$ and $w^3$ in Cartesian form, we need to use\n**the Polar form of a complex number (1.13)**,\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z=r(\\cos\\theta+\\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)=re^{\\mathrm{i}\\theta}=r\\,\\text{cis}\\,\\theta\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and **De Moivre's Theorem (1.14)**,\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    [r(\\cos\\theta+\\mathrm{i}\\sin\\theta)]^n=r^ne^{\\mathrm{i} n\\theta}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w &= 2e^{\\mathrm{i}\\frac{2\\pi}{3}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos\\Big(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\Big(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(-\\frac{1}{2}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\Big(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[9pt]\n    w&=\\boxed{-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\mathrm{i}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\\\\[3em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w^2 &= (2)^2e^{\\mathrm{i}(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\times2)} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 4e^{\\mathrm{i}\\frac{4\\pi}{3}} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 4\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos\\Big(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin\\Big(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 4\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(-\\frac{1}{2}\\Big) + \\mathrm{i}\\Big(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[9pt]\n    w^2&=\\boxed{-2 - 2\\sqrt{3}\\mathrm{i}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\\\\[3em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    w^3 &= (2)^3e^{\\mathrm{i} (\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\times3)} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 8e^{\\mathrm{i} 2\\pi} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 8\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos (2\\pi) + \\mathrm{i}\\sin (2\\pi)\\Big] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 8\\Big[\\hspace{-0.1em}(1) + \\mathrm{i}(0)\\Big] \\\\[9pt]\n    w^3&=\\boxed{8 + 0\\mathrm{i}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Considering the argument of $w^k$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arg (w^k) &= \\dfrac{2k\\pi}{3} \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The $k$th\n    power of $w$ is real if $\\arg(w^k)$ is a multiple of $\\pi$.\n\n    Hence the smallest such integer $k > 3$ is\n     $k = 6$"},{"ref":"AA779","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA779\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the equation $\\dfrac{3z}{5-z^{*}}=\\text{i}$, where\n$z=x+\\text{i}y$ and $x$, $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\\\nFind the value of $x$ and the value of $y$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{3z}{5-z^{*}}&=\\text{i} \\\\[12pt]\n\\dfrac{3(x+\\text{i}y)}{5-(x-\\text{i}y)}&= \\text{i}\\\\[12pt]\n\\dfrac{3x+3y\\text{i}}{5-x+y\\text{i}}&=\\text{i}\\\\[12pt]\n3x+3y\\text{i}&=\\text{i}(5-x+y\\text{i}) \\\\[12pt]\n3x+3y\\text{i}&=5\\text{i}-x\\text{i}-y\\\\[12pt]\n3x+3y\\text{i}&=-y+(5-x)\\text{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nComplex numbers can be\nequal only if their real and imaginary parts are equal.\n  Equating the real\nparts, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x&=-y \\\\[8pt]\ny&=-3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nEquating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y&=5-x \\\\[8pt]\n3(-3x)&=5-x \\hspace{4em} [\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{by equation (1)}\\hspace{0.1em}]\\\\[8pt]\n-9x&=5-x\\\\[8pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.55em}x=-\\dfrac{5}{8}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $x$ in equation $(1)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=-3\\left(-\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.55em}y=\\dfrac{15}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA977","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA977\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the equation $z^4-6z^3+cz^2-30z+13=0$ where $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$ and $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThree of the roots of the equation are $2-3i$, $\\alpha$ and $\\alpha^4$, where $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. Find the value of $\\alpha$.[4 marks]\n\n2. Hence, or otherwise, find the value of $c$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Since $2-3i$ is a root its conjugate $2+3i$ is a root too.\n\n    Now, using the formula for the product of the roots, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{product of roots}&=(-1)^4\\dfrac{a_0}{a_4}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    (2-3i)\\cdot(2+3i)\\cdot \\alpha \\cdot \\alpha^4 &=(1)\\dfrac{13}{1}\\\\[8pt]\n\n    13\\cdot \\alpha^5&=13\\\\[8pt]  \n\n    &\\hspace{-0.65em}\\boxed{\\alpha =1} \\hspace{4em}[\\text{since $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$}]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. The complex polynomial can be factorised as\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned} \n\n     p(x)&=(x-(2+3i))(x-(2-3i))(x-1)(x-1)\\\\[8pt]\n\n     &=(x^2-4x+13)(x-1)^2\\\\[8pt]\n\n     &=(x^2-4x+13)(x^2-2x+1)\\\\[8pt]\n\n     &=x^4-6x^3+22x^2-30x+13\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, we conclude that $\\hspace{0.29em}\\boxed{c=22}$"}]},{"name":"Complex Numbers- Geometric Representations","questions":[{"ref":"AA102","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA102\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1.  Find three distinct roots of the equation $z^3 + 64 = 0$,\n    $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$, giving your answers in modulus-argument form.\n    [6 marks]\n\nThe roots are represented by the vertices of a triangle in an Argand\ndiagram.\n\n2.  Show that the area of the triangle is $12\\sqrt{3}$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the equality\n    $-1 = \\cos \\pi + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\pi$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z^3 &= -64 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 64(\\cos \\pi + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\pi)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using de Moivre's theorem, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} z &= 64^\\frac{1}{3}(\\cos \\pi + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\pi)^\\frac{1}{3} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 4\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi + 2k\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi + 2k\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    for $k = 0,1,2$.\n\n    Hence the roots of the equation $z^3 = -64$, in modulus-argument form, are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k = 0 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = 4\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[10pt]\n    k = 1 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = 4\\Big[\\cos \\pi + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\pi \\Big]  \\\\[10pt]\n    k = 2 &: \\hspace{0.525em}z = 4\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\Big(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we sketch the triangle whose vertices represent the roots $(1)$\n    on an Argand diagram, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/7ba8fa24617bc7505a9ecf65b0f77c82f33a610b.svg\" alt=\"7ba8fa24617bc7505a9ecf65b0f77c82f33a610b.svg\">\n\n    Hence the area of the triangle ABC is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Area} &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(4)\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\bigg)\\bigg] \\\\[4pt]\n    &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(4)\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg)\\bigg]  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 12\\sqrt{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA112","marks":"18","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA112\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\n1.  Express $-4 + 4\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ in the\n    form $re^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\theta}$, where\n    $r > 0$ and $- \\pi < \\theta \\leq \\pi$. [5 marks]\n\nLet the roots of the equation\n$z^3 = -4 + 4\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ be $z_1$,\n$z_2$ and $z_3$.\n\n2.  Find $z_1$, $z_2$ and $z_3$ expressing your answers in the\n    form $re^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\theta}$, where\n    $r > 0$ and $-\\pi < \\theta \\leq \\pi$. [5 marks]\n\nOn an Argand diagram, $z_1$, $z_2$ and $z_3$ are represented by the\npoints A, B and C, respectively.\n\n3.  Find the area of the triangle ABC. [4 marks]\n\n4.  By considering the sum of the roots $z_1$, $z_2$ and $z_3$,\n    show that\n\n    $$\n    \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{9}\\Big) + \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{9}\\Big) + \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{9}\\Big) = 0\n\n    $$\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we express $-4 + 4\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$\n    in the form $re^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\theta}$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} -4 + 4\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}&= 8\\bigg[\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 8\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 8e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{2\\pi}{3}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by Euler's formula}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using de Moivre's theorem, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.2em} z &= \\sqrt[3]{8}\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big]^{\\frac{1}{3}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.6em} &= \\sqrt[3]{8}\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi/3 + 2k\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi/3 + 2k\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\Big[\\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{9} + \\dfrac{2k\\pi}{3}\\Big) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{9} + \\dfrac{2k\\pi}{3}\\Big)\\Big]  \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 2e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}(\\frac{2\\pi}{9} + \\frac{2k\\pi}{3})} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by Euler's formula}] \\hspace{0.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    for $k = -1,0,1$.\n\n    Hence the roots of the equation\n    $z^3 = -4 + 4\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_1 = 2e^{-{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{4\\pi}{9}},\\hspace{0.5em}z_2 = 2e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{2\\pi}{9}},\\hspace{0.5em}z_3 = 2e^{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\frac{8\\pi}{9}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we sketch the triangle ABC on an Argand diagram, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/be310f824ce127a4f24f53c5e5e7cb15aa1f5774.svg\" alt=\"be310f824ce127a4f24f53c5e5e7cb15aa1f5774.svg\">\n\n    Hence the area of the triangle ABC is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Area} &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(2)(2)\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\bigg)\\bigg] \\\\[4pt]\n    &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}(2)(2)\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg)\\bigg]  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The sum of the roots of the equation\n    $z^3 = -4 + 4\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ is zero.\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{Re}\\left(z_1 + z_2 + z_3\\right) &= 0 \\hspace{3em} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\cos\\Big(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{4\\pi}{9}\\Big) + \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{9}\\Big) + \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{9}\\Big) &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{9}\\Big) + \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{9}\\Big) + \\cos\\Big(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{9}\\Big) = 0 \\hspace{2.05em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1331","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1331\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nConsider the equation $z^4+4=0$, where $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$.\n\nThe roots of the equation are $z_1, z_2, z_3$ and $z_4$.\n\nWhere $\\text{Im}(z_2)>0$ and $\\text{Re}(z_3)<\\text{Re}(z_4)$.\n\n1.\n    1.    Verify that $z_1=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i}\\frac{\\pi}{4}}$ is a root of the equation.\n\n    2.    Find $z_2, z_3$ and $z_4$ expressing each in the form $re^{\\text{i}\\theta}$ where $r,\\theta>0$.\n\n    3.    Express $z_1$ in the form $a+b\\text{i}$, where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n    4.    Plot the point Z, which represents the root $z_1$, on an Argand diagram.\n\n    5.    Hence, write down $z_2, z_3$ and $z_4$ in Cartesian form. [7 marks]\n\nConsider the equation $\\left(\\dfrac{w+1}{w-1}\\right)^4+4=0$ where $w \\in \\mathbb{C}$ and $w \\neq 1$.\n\n2.\n    1.    Using $z_1$, show that one of the roots of the equation is $w_1=1-2\\text{i}$.\n\n    2.    Hence, find the remaining roots of the equation in Cartesian form. [8 marks]\n\nOn an Argand diagram, the roots of the equation are represented by the points A, B, C and D.\n\n3.    Find the area of the quadrilateral ABCD. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. The command term _verify_ means we can substitute the given root into the equation to confirm it is a solution.\n        Hence, we need to show that\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left(\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i}\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\right)^4+4&=0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Let's simplify the L.H.S.\n        Rewriting the indices, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left(\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i}\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\right)^4+4&=(\\sqrt{2})^4e^{\\text{i}\\pi}+4\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The number $e^{\\text{i}\\pi}$ has an argument of $\\pi$ radians and a modulus of $1$.\n        By making a sketch of $e^{\\text{i}\\pi}$ on an Argand diagram, we can see that  $e^{\\text{i}\\pi}=-1$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/AA1331-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1331-a1\">\n\n        Using this, we can simplify further to obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=(\\sqrt{2})^4(-1)+4\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=2^2 \\times (-1)+4\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-4+4\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Thus, we have verified that $\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i}\\frac{\\pi}{4}}$ is a root.\n\n    2. To find the other roots, we can rearrange the equation and solve for $z$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z^4+4&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        z^4&=-4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Let's write $-4$ in Euler's form, using the method we described in part (a)(i)\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z^4+4&=0\\\\[6pt]\n        z^4&=-4\\\\[6pt]\n        &=4e^{\\text{i}\\pi}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The fundamental theorem of algebra tells us that a complex number of the form $z^n=0$ has $n$ roots.\n\n        Therefore, to find all $4$ roots of $z$ we take our previous result and add $2n\\pi$ to the argument, where $n=0,1,2,3$, to represent each of the symmetrically distributed roots of $z$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z^4&=4e^{\\text{i}(\\pi+2n\\pi)}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Taking the fourth root of both sides and using index laws we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z&=\\left(4e^{\\text{i}(\\pi+2n\\pi)}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=4^{\\frac{1}{4}} \\left(e^{\\text{i} (\\pi+2n\\pi)}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i}\\frac{(\\pi+2n\\pi)}{4}}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Notice when $n=0$ we get the root $z_1$,  previously verified in part (a).\n\n        Hence, we can find the remaining three roots by substituting in $n=1,2$ and $3$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}    z_2&=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i} \\frac{3\\pi}{4} }&&&z_3&=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i} \\frac{5\\pi}{4} }&&&z_4&=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i} \\frac{7\\pi}{4} }\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3. We have expressed the number in Euler's form\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        re^{\\text{i}\\theta} \\rightarrow \\sqrt{2}e^{\\text{i}\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        where $r$ is the modulus and $\\theta$ the argument, therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{modulus}&=\\sqrt{2}&&&\\text{argument}&=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we can write the number in $r\\,\\text{cis}\\theta$ form which simplifies to the desired form.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z_1&=\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}+\\text{i}\\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1+\\text{i}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    4. As $\\text{Re}(z_1)=1$ and the $\\text{Im}(z_1)=1$ we can plot the point $\\mathrm{Z}(1,1)$ on an Argand diagram\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/AA1331-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1331-a2\">\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}z_1\\text{ plotted at }\\mathrm{Z}(1,1)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    5.  Note, writing a number in Cartesian form means expressing it as $a+b\\text{i}$, where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$. This is the same form as our answer from part (a)(iii).\n\n        As we know the roots are symmetrically distributed about the origin on an Argand diagram we can (optionally) add them to our diagram from the previous part\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/AA1331-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1331-a3\">\n\n        this visual helps us write down the Cartesian form of the remaining roots\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z_2&=-1+\\text{i}&z_3&=-1-\\text{i}&z_4&=1-\\text{i}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  1.  In this question we must __show that__ $1-2\\text{i}$ is a root. We cannot verify (by substitution) like in part (a)!\n\n        Make sure you understand the A.A. command terms.\n\n        Note, that the given equation\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left(\\frac{w+1}{w-1}\\right)^4+4&=0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        is of the form $z^4+4=0$.\n        Hence we can say that\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{w+1}{w-1}&=z\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We have the $4$ roots to the equation $z^4+4=0$ from part (a) hence, if we can write $w$ in terms of $z$, we can find the roots of $w$.\n        Let's make $w$ the subject of the above\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        w+1&=zw-z\\\\[6pt]\n        1+z&=zw-w\\\\[6pt]\n        1+z&=w(z-1)\\\\[6pt]\n        w&=\\frac{z+1}{z-1}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        As instructed, we use $z_1=1+\\text{i}$\n        Substituting $z=1+\\text{i}$ we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        w&=\\frac{1+\\text{i}+1}{1+\\text{i}-1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{2+\\text{i}}{\\text{i}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        To rewrite $w$ into the desired form we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator $\\dfrac{-\\text{i}}{-\\text{i}}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{2+\\text{i}}{\\text{i}}\\times \\frac{-\\text{i}}{-\\text{i}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-2\\text{i}-\\text{i}^2}{-(-1)}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{-2\\text{i}-(-1)}{1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1-2\\text{i}\\hspace{2em}\\text{... as required}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2. Note, before starting this part recall the equation we are solving\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\left(\\frac{w+1}{w-1}\\right)^4+4&=0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        By multiplying throughout by $(w-1)^4$ we can (optionally) rewrite it as\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (w+1)^4+4(w-1)^4&=0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        This is useful as we can see, when fully expanded, we would get a real polynomial in $w$, i.e. all of the coefficients of $w$ are real, this means we can expect the roots to be in conjugate pairs.\n        If we let the root we found in the previous part be $w_1$ then we can find the remaining roots of $w$ by using the remaining roots of $z$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        w_2&=\\frac{z_2+1}{z_2-1}&&&w_3&=\\frac{z_3+1}{z_3-1}&&&w_4&=\\frac{z_4+1}{z_4-1}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\frac{-1+\\text{i}+1}{-1+\\text{i}-1}&&&&=\\frac{-1-\\text{i}+1}{-1-\\text{i}-1}&&&&=\\frac{1-\\text{i}+1}{1-\\text{i}-1}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\frac{\\text{i}}{-2+\\text{i}}&&&&=\\frac{-\\text{i}}{-2-\\text{i}}&&&&=\\frac{2-\\text{i}}{-\\text{i}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        In a similar fashion to part (b)(i) we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator giving\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{\\text{i}}{-2+\\text{i}}\\times \\frac{-2-\\text{i}}{-2-\\text{i}}&&&&=\\frac{-\\text{i}}{-2-\\text{i}}\\times \\frac{-2+\\text{i}}{-2+\\text{i}} &&&&=\\frac{2-\\text{i}}{-\\text{i}}\\times \\frac{-\\text{i}}{-\\text{i}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\frac{-2\\text{i}-\\text{i}^2}{(-2)^2-\\text{i}^2}&&&&=\\frac{2\\text{i}-\\text{i}^2}{(-2)^2-\\text{i}^2} &&&&=\\frac{-2\\text{i}+\\text{i}^2}{(-\\text{i})^2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Recall $\\text{i}^2=-1$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{1-2\\text{i}}{4-(-1)}&&&&=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{4-(-1)^2} &&&&=\\frac{-2\\text{i}+(-1)}{-1}\\\\[12pt]\n        w_2&=\\dfrac{1-2\\text{i}}{5}&&&w_3&=\\dfrac{1+2\\text{i}}{5} &&&w_4&=1+2\\text{i}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Notice, $w_2=(w_3)^*$ and $w_1=(w_4)^*$, thus, as expected, the roots are in conjugate pairs.\n        This is one way to verify your answers.\n\n3.  Note, it doesn't matter which roots we label A, B, C or D as long as they are in the correct order to form a quadrilateral.\n    Making a sketch of the roots on an Argand diagram and labelling the points ABCD we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/AA1331-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1331-a4\">\n\n    Notice that the points form a trapezoid (trapezium).\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(R1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The area of a trapezoid is calculated using the formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A&=\\frac{a+b}{2}\\times h\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the two parallel sides and $h$ is the perpendicular distance between them.\n    Hence we need to find the lengths of AD and BC.\n    Using the Imaginary (vertical) value for these points we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{AD}&=\\frac{2}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)&\\text{BC}&=2-(-2)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{4}{5}&&=4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The height $h$ is the horizontal distance between points A or D and B or C, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h&=1-\\frac{1}{5}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{4}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, the area of ABCD is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A_{\\text{ABCD}}&=\\frac{\\frac{4}{5}+4}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\left(\\frac{4}{5}+4\\right)\\times \\frac{2}{5}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{24}{5}\\times \\frac{2}{5}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{48}{25}\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{units}^2\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\bigg(&=1\\tfrac{23}{25}\\bigg)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA712","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA712\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**In this question give all angles in radians.**\n\nLet $z = 1 + 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$w = 4 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$.\n\n1.  Find $z+w$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find:\n    1.  $|z+w|$;\n\n    2.  $\\arg(z+w)$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\theta$, the angle shown on the diagram below. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/9f1b9b167af97e4b2c9ee84b7c9014f2cdaf63c7.svg\" alt=\"9f1b9b167af97e4b2c9ee84b7c9014f2cdaf63c7.svg\">","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z+w &= (1+2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) + (4 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1+4) + (2+1){\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5 + 3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The modulus of $z+w$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        |z+w| &= \\sqrt{5^2 + 3^2} \\hspace{6.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 5.83\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The argument of $z+w$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\arg(z+w) &= \\tan^{-1}(3/5) \\hspace{7.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.540\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.7em} \\theta &= \\arg z - \\arg (z+w) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\tan^{-1}(2/1) - \\tan^{-1}(3/5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1.107148\\dots) - (0.540419\\dots) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.567\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA713","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA713\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $z = 2+{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$w = 1-2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$.\n\n1.  Find $zw$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Illustrate $z$, $w$ and $zw$ on the same Argand diagram. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Let $\\theta$ be the angle between $zw$ and $w$. Find $\\theta$,\n    giving your answer in radians.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The product of $z$ and $w$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    zw &= (2+{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(1-2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2-4{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}+{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}+2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4-3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we illustrate $z$, $w$ and $zw$ on the same Argand diagram, we\n    have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/96f14dbc4defd6873946ae02a45c2e643f791342.svg\" alt=\"96f14dbc4defd6873946ae02a45c2e643f791342.svg\">\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arg w &= \\tan^{-1}((-2)/1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -1.107148\\dots \\hspace{1.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arg zw &= \\tan^{-1}((-3)/4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -0.643501\\dots \\hspace{1.8em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\theta &= |\\arg w| - |\\arg zw| \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1.107148\\dots) - (0.643501\\dots) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.464\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA714","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA714\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn an Argand diagram, the complex numbers\n$z_1 = 2 + 2\\sqrt{3}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$,\n$z_2 = 1 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and $z_3 = z_1z_2$ are\nrepresented by the vertices of a triangle.\n\nThe exact area of the triangle can be expressed in the form\n$p+\\sqrt{q}$. Find the value of $p$ and of $q$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.1em} |z_1| &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{2^2+(2\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 4 \\\\[12pt]\n|z_2| &= \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg z_1 &= \\tan^{-1}(2\\sqrt{3}/2) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\ang{60} \\\\[12pt]\n\\arg z_2 &= \\tan^{-1}(-1/1) \\\\[6pt]\n&= -\\ang{45}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the property of modulus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|z_3| &= |z_1z_2| \\\\[6pt]\n&= |z_1||z_2| \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg z_3 &= \\arg(z_1z_2) \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\arg z_1 + \\arg z_2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\ang{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we sketch the triangle whose vertices represent $z_1$, $z_2$ and\n$z_3$ on an Argand diagram, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/e1e467a933ff283d4afc5581ecd27d102258be6b.svg\" alt=\"e1e467a933ff283d4afc5581ecd27d102258be6b.svg\">\n\nHence the area of the triangle ABC is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{area(A\\^{B}C)} &= \\text{area(A\\^{O}C)} + \\text{area(B\\^{O}C)} - \\text{area(A\\^{O}B)} \\\\[10pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(4\\sqrt{2})\\sin \\ang{45} + \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2})(4\\sqrt{2})\\sin \\ang{60} - \\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(\\sqrt{2})\\sin \\left(\\ang{60}+\\ang{45}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n&= (8\\sqrt{2})\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) + 4 \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) - 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\Big(\\sin \\left(\\ang{60}\\right)\\cos(\\ang{45})+\\cos(\\ang{60})\\sin(\\ang{45}) \\Big)\\\\[8pt]\n&= 8+2\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\\\[10pt]\n&= 7+\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$p=7$and$q=3$"},{"ref":"AA715","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA715\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nOn the Argand diagram below, the point A represents the complex number\n$4{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and the point B represents the\ncomplex number $-5+{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$. The shape\nABCD is a square.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/fed41c2307d3c10825aed04db2b46a9169006d2c.svg\" alt=\"fed41c2307d3c10825aed04db2b46a9169006d2c.svg\">\n\nDetermine the complex number represented by:\n\n1.  the point C; [2 marks]\n\n2.  the point D. [2 marks]","markScheme":"Using the coordinates of the points A and B, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/0bb5140f7bfb03dc770a8940b3da94e0b2a73825.svg\" alt=\"0bb5140f7bfb03dc770a8940b3da94e0b2a73825.svg\">\n\nFrom the Argand diagram above, we have:\n\n1.  the point C represents\n     $-2-4{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$\n\n2.  the point D represents\n     $3-{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$"},{"ref":"AA716","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA716\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe complex numbers $z$ and $w$ correspond to the points A and B as\nshown on the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/0c46fa70e78de8ef6fa11a8d28566cc1742d4a83.svg\" alt=\"0c46fa70e78de8ef6fa11a8d28566cc1742d4a83.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the exact value of $|z - w|$. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the exact perimeter of triangle AOB.\n\n    2.  Find the exact area of triangle AOB. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the cosine rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} |z - w|^2 &= |z|^2 + |w|^2 - 2|z||w|\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    |z - w|^2 &= 5^2 + 5^2 - 2(5)(5)\\hspace{-0.1em} \\bigg[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[10pt]\n    |z - w|^2 &= 25(2-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    |z - w| = \\textstyle 5\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt{3}} \\hspace{4.3em}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  The exact perimeter of triangle AOB is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{perimeter} &= |z| + |w| + |z-w| \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 5 + 5 + \\textstyle 5\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\textstyle 10 + 5\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt{3}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The exact area of triangle AOB is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.1em} \\text{area} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}|z||w|\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(5)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{25}{4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA717","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA717\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $z = 2\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}2\\theta$ where $0 < \\theta < \\ang{45}$.\nFind the modulus and argument of $z + 2$, expressing your answers in\nterms of $\\theta$.","markScheme":"Illustrating $z$ and $z+2$ on an Argand diagram, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/1d7aa7a6ad70d5559224e1ad164664c7c8b18e90.svg\" alt=\"1d7aa7a6ad70d5559224e1ad164664c7c8b18e90.svg\">\n\nSince $\\mathrm{OA} = \\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{BC} = \\mathrm{OC} = 2$, the\nshape OABC is a rhombus.\n\nLetting M be the midpoint of OB, we further have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/ab69dba55452ae052577c8e1dbf5234f80b40234.svg\" alt=\"ab69dba55452ae052577c8e1dbf5234f80b40234.svg\">\n\nSince OB bisects the angle AOC, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg(z+2) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\arg z \\\\[6pt]\n\\arg(z+2) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(2\\theta) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg(z+2) = \\theta \\hspace{2.45em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing\nthe right triangle AOM, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{15.2em} \\cos \\theta &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{OM}}{|z|} \\\\[8pt]\n\\mathrm{OM} &= 2\\cos \\theta \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $|z|=2$}] \\\\[14pt]\n\\mathrm{OB} &= 4\\cos \\theta \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since M is a midpoint}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{1.3em} |z+2| = 4\\cos \\theta\n$$"},{"ref":"AA718","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA718\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA circle of radius 3 and centre (0,3) is drawn on an Argand diagram. The tangent to\nthe circle from the point B$(0,9)$ meets the circle at the point A as\nshown. Let $w = \\vec{\\mathrm{OA}}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/756bc10d49856365edc8188e18631d432452e02d.svg\" alt=\"756bc10d49856365edc8188e18631d432452e02d.svg\">\n\n1.  Show that $|w| = 3\\sqrt{3}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\arg w$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence write $w$ in the form\n    $a+b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n    $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Given that the angle between a tangent and a radius is a right angle, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/e572903f13ea5ca7d33e50badc186e738f21ec73.svg\" alt=\"e572903f13ea5ca7d33e50badc186e738f21ec73.svg\">\n\n    Using the right triangle ABC, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.4em} \\cos(\\text{A\\^{C}B}) &= \\dfrac{3}{6} \\hspace{3em} \\left(=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{A\\^{C}B} &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{A\\^{C}O} &= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using **the cosine rule (3.2)**, $$c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab \\cos C$$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.4em} |w|^2 &= 3^2 + 3^2 - 2(3)(3)\\cos \\Big(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\Big) \\\\[3pt]\n    |w|^2 &= 18 - 18\\hspace{-0.1em}\\left[-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    |w|^2 &= 27 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |w| = 3\\sqrt{3} \\hspace{0.9em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/c956da10fdd9a0dd23ce23a1defc574f970f2c67.svg\" alt=\"c956da10fdd9a0dd23ce23a1defc574f970f2c67.svg\">\n\n2.  Using the isosceles triangle OAC, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\text{A\\^{O}C} &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.05em}(\\pi - \\text{A\\^{C}O}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg[\\pi - \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the argument of $w$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\arg w &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\text{A\\^{O}C} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  As we have the modulus and argument of $w$ we can first write $w$ using **the Polar form of a complex number (1.13)**,\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z = r\\left(\\cos \\theta + \\mathrm{i} \\sin \\theta\\right) = re^{\\mathrm{i}\\theta} = r\\mathrm{cis}\\theta\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Then change $w$ into **Cartesian form (1.12)**\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z = a + b\\mathrm{i}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\\\\[2em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.4em} w &= 3\\sqrt{3}\\mathop{\\mathrm{cis}}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{3}\\bigg[\\hspace{-0.1em}\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{3}\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2} + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\bigg] \\\\[7pt]\n    w&=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\dfrac{9}{2}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA720","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA720\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nPoints A and B represent the complex numbers\n$z_1 = \\sqrt{3} - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$z_2 = -3 - 3{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ as shown on the\nArgand diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Complex%20Numbers-%20Geometric%20Representations/images/b9acbcc9be3dbe232f939a960c2a1907744b9b96.svg\" alt=\"b9acbcc9be3dbe232f939a960c2a1907744b9b96.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the angle AOB. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the argument of $z_1z_2$. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Given that the real powers of $pz_1z_2$, for $p > 0$, all lie on a\n    unit circle centred at the origin, find the exact value of $p$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arg z_1 &= \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\hspace{-0.1em}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arg z_2 &= \\tan^{-1}(1) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\pi \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.3em} \\text{A\\^{O}B} &= |\\arg z_2| - |\\arg z_1| \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The argument of $z_1z_2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arg(z_1z_2) &= \\arg z_1 + \\arg z_2 \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} |z_1| &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^2 + (-1)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{4} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} |z_2| &= \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-3)^2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{18} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3\\sqrt{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the properties of modulus,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} |(pz_1z_2)^x| &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$}] \\\\[12pt]\n    |pz_1z_2|^x &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    |pz_1z_2| &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    |p||z_1||z_2| &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    p\\hspace{0.05em}(2)(3\\sqrt{2}) &= 1  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $p > 0$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    p = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{12} \\hspace{2.6em}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Counting Principles","questions":[{"ref":"AA078","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA078\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nFind the number of ways in which twelve different baseball cards can be\ngiven to Emily, Harry, John and Olivia, if Emily is to receive $5$\ncards, Harry is to receive $3$ cards, John is to receive $3$ cards and\nOlivia is to receive $1$ card (in that order).","markScheme":"Choose $5$ from $12$ baseball cards for Emily, then choose $3$ from $7$\nremaining cards for Harry, then choose $3$ from $4$ remaining cards for\nJohn and give the last card which is left to Olivia.\n\nHence the total number of ways is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\binom{12}{5}\\binom{7}{3}\\binom{4}{3}\\binom{1}{1} &= \\dfrac{12!}{5!\\cdot\\cancel{7!}}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\cancel{7!}}{3!\\cdot\\cancel{4!}}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\cancel{4!}}{3!\\cdot1!}\\cdot 1  \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{12!}{5!\\cdot3!\\cdot3!\\cdot1!} \\\\[15pt]\n&= 110\\hspace{0.15em}880\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA079","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA079\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA school basketball team of $5$ students is selected from $8$ boys and\n$4$ girls.\n\n1.  Determine how many possible teams can be chosen. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Determine how many teams can be formed consisting of $3$ boys and\n    $2$ girls? [2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine how many teams can be formed consisting of at most $3$\n    girls? [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We choose $5$ students from $8+4 = 12$ boys and girls.\n    Hence the number of possible teams is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{12}{5} &= \\dfrac{12!}{5!\\cdot7!} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 792\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We choose $3$ students from $8$ boys and then we choose $2$ more\n    students from $4$ girls. Hence the number of teams consisting of $3$\n    boys and $2$ girls is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\binom{8}{3}\\binom{4}{2} &= \\dfrac{8!}{3!\\cdot5!}\\cdot\\dfrac{4!}{2!\\cdot2!} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 336\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  A team of $5$ students can either have $0,1,2,3$ or $4$ girls. The\n    number of teams consisting of $1$ boy and $4$ girls is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{8}{1}\\binom{4}{4} = 8 \\hspace{3.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the\n    number of teams consisting of at most $3$ girls is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    792 - 8 &= \\hspace{0.525em}784 \\hspace{1.25em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA080","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA080\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA police department has $4$ male and $7$ female officers. A special\ngroup of $5$ officers is to be assembled for an undercover operation.\n\n1.  Determine how many possible groups can be chosen. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Determine how many groups can be formed\n    consisting of $2$ males and $3$ $\\text{females.}$[2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine how many groups can be formed consisting of at least one\n    male. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We choose $5$ people from $4+7 = 11$ males and females.\n    Hence the number of possible groups is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{11}{5} &= \\dfrac{11!}{5!\\cdot6!} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 462\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We choose $2$ officers from $4$ males and then we choose $3$ more\n    officers from $7$ females. Hence the number of groups consisting of\n    $3$ males and $2$ females is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\binom{4}{2}\\binom{7}{3} &= \\dfrac{4!}{2!\\cdot2!}\\cdot\\dfrac{7!}{3!\\cdot4!} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 210\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The number of groups consisting of $0$ males and $5$ females is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\binom{4}{0}\\binom{7}{5} &= \\dfrac{4!}{0!\\cdot4!}\\cdot\\dfrac{7!}{5!\\cdot2!} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 21\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the number of groups consisting of at\n    least $1$ male is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    462 - 21 &= \\hspace{0.525em}441 \\hspace{1.65em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA081","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA081\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nPeter needs to decide the order in which to schedule $14$ exams for his\nschool. Two of these exams are Chemistry ($1$ SL and $1$ HL).\n\nFind the number of different ways Peter can schedule the $14$ exams\ngiven that the two Chemistry subjects must not be consecutive.","markScheme":"Let $n$ be the number of different ways in which the $14$ exams can be\nscheduled such that the two Chemistry subjects are not consecutive. To\nfind $n$, subtract from $14!$ the number of possible ways to order the\n$14$ exams such that the two Chemistry subjects are consecutive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 14! - 12!\\times\\binom{2}{2}\\times2!\\times\\binom{13}{1}\\times1!  \\\\[6pt]\n&= 14! - 12!\\times2!\\times13 \\\\[12pt]\n&\\approx 7.47\\times10^{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA082","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA082\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThere are $11$ players on a football team who are asked to line up in one straight line for a team photo. Three of the team members named Adam, Brad and Chris refuse to stand next to each other. There is no restriction on the order in which the other team members position themselves.\n\nFind the number of different orders in which the $11$ team members can be positioned for the photo.","markScheme":"To ensure Adam, Brad and Chris are not next to each other, arrange the other $8$ players first and then slot Adam, Brad and Chris into the gaps between them.\n\nThe $8$ unrestricted players can be arranged in $8!$ ways.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis creates $9$ gaps in which Adam, Brad and Chris can stand.\n$$\\_\\,P\\,\\_\\,P\\,\\_\\,P\\,\\_\\,P\\,\\_\\,P\\,\\_\\,P\\,\\_\\,P\\,\\_\\,P\\,\\_$$\nAdam, Brad and Chris must occupy three different gaps, and since they are distinct people the order in which they fill the gaps matters, so we use permutations.\n\nUsing the **permutations formula (1.10)**, to count the number of ordered arrangements of $3$ people chosen from $9$ gaps, $^nP_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!}$.\n\nChoosing and arranging Adam, Brad and Chris in the $9$ gaps gives $^9P_3$.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the permutation, $^9P_3 = \\dfrac{9!}{6!} = 504$.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying this with the number of arrangements of the $8$ unrestricted players to count the total number of orderings.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 8! \\times {}^9P_3 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n  &= 8! \\times \\dfrac{9!}{6!} \\\\[6pt]\n  &= 8! \\,(504) \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}} \\\\[6pt]\nn &= \\boxed{20\\hspace{0.15em}321\\hspace{0.15em}280} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n**Alternative method**\n\nTo find $n$, subtract from $11!$ the number of orderings in which exactly two, and exactly three, of Adam, Brad and Chris stand next to each other.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 11! - 8!\\times\\binom{3}{2}\\times2!\\times\\binom{9}{2}\\times2! - 8!\\times\\binom{3}{3}\\times3!\\times\\binom{9}{1}\\times1! \\\\[6pt]\n  &= 11! - 8!\\times3\\times2!\\times9\\times8 - 8!\\times3!\\times9 \\\\[6pt]\nn &= \\boxed{20\\hspace{0.15em}321\\hspace{0.15em}280}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA083","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA083\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe barcode strings of a new product are created from four letters A, B,\nC, D and ten digits $0,1,2,\\dots,9$. No three of the letters may be\nwritten consecutively in a barcode string. There is no restriction on\nthe order in which the numbers can be written.\n\nFind the number of different barcode strings that can be created.","markScheme":"Let $n$ denote the number of different barcode strings that can be\ncreated. Four letters and ten digits can be ordered in $14!$ ways. To\nfind $n$, subtract from $14!$ the number of possible barcode strings\nwith exactly three and exactly four consecutive letters.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 14! - 10!\\times\\binom{4}{3}\\times3!\\times\\binom{11}{2}\\times2! - 10!\\times\\binom{4}{4}\\times4!\\times\\binom{11}{1}\\times1! \\\\[6pt]\n&= 14! - 10!\\times4\\times3!\\times11\\times10 - 10!\\times4!\\times11 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 7.66\\times10^{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1010","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1010\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn an art museum, there are 8 different paintings by Picasso, 5 different paintings by Van Gogh, and 3 different paintings by Rembrandt. The curator of the museum wants to hold an exhibition in a hall that can only display a maximum of 7 paintings at a time.\n\nThe curator wants to include at least two paintings from each artist in the exhibition.\n\n1. Given that 7 paintings will be displayed, determine how many ways they can be selected. [4 marks]\n\n2. Find the probability that more Rembrandt paintings will be selected than Picasso paintings or Van Gogh paintings. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Given that there must be at least 2 paintings by each artist, there are three cases to consider:\n\n    1. 3 Picasso, 2 Van Gogh, 2 Rembrandt\n\n    2. 2 Picasso, 3 Van Gogh, 2 Rembrandt\n\n    3. 2 Picasso, 2 Van Gogh, 3 Rembrandt\n\n    Let $k_1$ be the total number of combinations from option 1, $k_2$ the total from option 2 and $k_3$ the total from option 3.\n\n    In the first case, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k_1 &= {}^8C_3 \\times {}^5C_2 \\times {}^3C_2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 1680\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In the second case, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k_2 &= {}^8C_2 \\times {}^5C_3 \\times {}^3C_2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 840\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In the third case, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k_3 &= {}^8C_2 \\times {}^5C_2 \\times {}^3C_3 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 280\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Overall, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k &= k_1 + k_2 + k_3 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 1680 + 840 + 280\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In total there are $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{2800}\\hspace{0.525em}$ ways in which the paintings can be selected.\n\n2. For there to be more Rembrandt paintings selected than Picasso or Van Gogh, there must be 3 by Rembrandt, 2 by Picasso and 2 by Van Gogh. From part (a), there are 280 possible combinations that fit this criteria, and 2800 combinations overall. Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{P}(3\\mathrm{R}\\,2\\mathrm{P}\\,2\\mathrm{V}) &= \\dfrac{280}{2800} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-5.5em}\\boxed{\\mathrm{P}(3\\mathrm{R}\\,2\\mathrm{P}\\,2\\mathrm{V}) = \\dfrac{1}{10}} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1011","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1011\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAn arts and crafts store is offering a special package on personalized keychains. \n\nThe store has a selection of $6$ distinct types of charms.\n\nCustomers can personalize their keychains with between 1 to $3$ distinct charms from the selection mentioned above.\n\nDetermine how many ways a customer can personalize a keychain if\n\n1. The order of the selections is important. [3 marks]\n\n2. The order of the selections is **not** important. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. A customer can select either 1, 2 or 3 unique charms, all of which are different. Therefore we have \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k &= {}^6P_1 + {}^6P_2 + {}^6P_3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula for permutations, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k &= \\dfrac{6!}{5!} + \\dfrac{6!}{4!} + \\dfrac{6!}{3!} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{6\\times\\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} + \\dfrac{6\\times5\\times\\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}} + \\dfrac{6\\times5\\times4\\times\\cancel{3!}}{\\cancel{3!}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &= 6 + 30 + 120\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In total there are $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{156}\\hspace{0.525em}$ ways in which a customer can personalize a keychain if the order is important.\n\n2. A customer can select either 1, 2 or 3 unique charms, all of which are different. Therefore we have \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k &= {}^6C_1 + {}^6C_2 + {}^6C_3\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula for combinations, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        k &= \\dfrac{6!}{1!5!} + \\dfrac{6!}{2!4!} + \\dfrac{6!}{3!3!} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{6\\times\\cancel{5!}}{1\\times\\cancel{5!}} + \\dfrac{6\\times5\\times\\cancel{4!}}{2\\times\\cancel{4!}} + \\dfrac{\\cancel{6}\\times5\\times4\\times\\cancel{3!}}{\\cancel{3!}\\times\\cancel{3!}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &= 6 + 15 + 20\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In total there are $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{41}\\hspace{0.525em}$ ways in which a customer can personalize a keychain if the order is not important."},{"ref":"AA1159","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1159\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA coed soccer team consists of $2$ boys and $3$ girls.\n\nThey are standing in a line posing for a team picture.\n\nFind the number of arrangements such that\n\n1.  All the boys stand next to each other and all the girls stand next to each other. [1 mark]\n\n2.  All the boys stand next to each other. [1 mark]\n\n3.  All the boys are apart and all the girls are apart.[1 mark]\n\n4.  Just the boys are apart. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can treat the boys as a single entity and the girls as a single entity.\n\n    There are $2!$ ways to arrange the boys and $3!$ ways to arrange the girls.\n\n    We can arrange each entity in $2$ ways, i.e.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\underline{B} \\hspace{1em}\\underline{G}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\underline{G} \\hspace{1em}\\underline{B}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2! \\times 3! \\times 2&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{24} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  In this case we treat the boys as a single entity.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_1} \\hspace{1em} \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}}  \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_2} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_3} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}} \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can see above that this single entity has a choice of $4$ positions. Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2! \\times 3! \\times 4&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{48} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3.  The only way to keep all of the girls apart is place the boys between them\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\underline{G_1} \\hspace{1em} \\underline{\\textcolor{black}{B_1}}  \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_2} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{\\textcolor{black}{B_2}} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    There is only one way to do this, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2! \\times 3! \\times 1&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{12} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n4.  We can use a similar diagram as part(b)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_1} \\hspace{1em} \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}}  \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_2} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{G_3} \\hspace{1em}  \\underline{\\textcolor{white}{B}} \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can see $4$ positions to place the $2$ boys. Therefore there are ${}_{4}C_{2}$ ways to place the boys.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula ${}_{4}C_{2}=\\tfrac{4!}{2!2!}=6$, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2! \\times 3! \\times 6&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{72} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA118","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA118\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTen students are to be arranged in a new chemistry lab. The chemistry\nlab is set out in two rows of five desks as shown in the following\ndiagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/42b953ec648c17bd92a6ba9406f0ca05241c501c.svg\" alt=\"42b953ec648c17bd92a6ba9406f0ca05241c501c.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the number of ways the ten students may be arranged in the lab.\n    [1 mark]\n\nTwo of the students, Hugo and Leo, often talk to each other\nduring previous lab sessions.\n\n2.  Find the number of ways the students may be arranged if\n    Hugo and Leo must sit so that one is directly behind the other. For\n    example, Desk $1$ and Desk $6$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the number of ways the students may be arranged if\n    Hugo and Leo must **not** sit next to each other in the same row.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $10! = 3\\hspace{0.15em}628\\hspace{0.15em}800$\n\n2.  Hugo and Leo may be arranged in the lab first in $(5)(2) = 10$ ways\n    so that one is directly behind the other. Then the rest of the\n    students may be arranged in $8! = 40\\hspace{0.15em}320$ ways.\n    In this case, the number of ways the ten students may be\n    arranged in the lab is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (10)(8!) = 403\\hspace{0.15em}200\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Hugo and Leo may be arranged in the lab first in $(8)(2) = 16$ ways\n    so that they sit next to each other. Then the rest of the students\n    may be arranged in $8! = 40\\hspace{0.15em}320$ ways.\n    In this case, the number of ways the ten students may be\n    arranged in the lab is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (16)(8!) = 645\\hspace{0.15em}120\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if Hugo and\n    Leo must **not** sit next to each other, the number of ways is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} 10! - (16)(8!) &= 3\\hspace{0.15em}628\\hspace{0.15em}800 - 645\\hspace{0.15em}120 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\hspace{0.15em}983\\hspace{0.15em}680\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1330","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1330\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAt a school fair, there are $15$ booths in the auditorium. These booths are arranged in $3$ rows of $5$ booths each, as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA1330-q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1330-q\">\n\nThere are $15$ volunteers, including Arman and Bojanna. Each volunteer  is assigned to one booth.\n\n$5$ of the volunteers are wearing a red shirt, $5$ are wearing a blue shirt and $3$ plus Arman and Bojanna are wearing a green shirt.\n\nFor planning purposes, other than Arman and Bojanna, those volunteers wearing the same colour shirt are indistinguishable.\n\nThe committee initially decides that Arman and Bojanna must both be assigned to a booth in the front row.\n\n1.    Find the total number of ways the $15$ volunteers can be assigned to booths. [3 marks]\n\nAfter some deliberation, the committee revises its plan and decides that Arman and Bojanna must both be assigned to booths in the same row, either the middle row or the back row.\n- If they are in the middle row, they must not be seated next to each other.\n- If they are in the back row, there must be at least two booths between them.\n\n2.    Find the total number of ways the $15$ volunteers can be assigned to booths. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The front row has $5$ booths.\n    This means there are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{5}{2} = 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    ways to choose $2$ booths.\n    There are $2!$ ways for Arman and Bojanna to occupy these two booths.\n    Therefore, there are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    10 \\times 2! \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    ways for Arman and Bojanna to choose two booths in the front row.\n\n    There are $13$ volunteers remaining which means, without restriction, there are $13!$ ways to seat them.\n\n    However, as the volunteers with the same shirt colour are indistinguishable then, if we use $13!$, we have over counted.\n\n    For example, if we sat $5$ of the red shirted volunteers in the middle row then the following two arrangements, though different, are indistinguishable\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{arrangement 1}: R_1, R_2, R_3, R_4, R_5\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{arrangement 2}: R_2, R_1, R_3, R_4, R_5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we have over counted by $5!$ ways for the red shirts.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Similarly, we have over counted $5!$ for the blue.\n\n    With the green, there are $3$ volunteers left, we have already counted booths for Arman and Bojanna, therefore we have over counted by $3!$ ways.\n\n    Therefore, the total number of ways to assign all $15$ volunteers is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{5}{2} \\times 2! \\times \\frac{13!}{5!5!3!} &= 1\\,441\\,440\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Let’s first consider the middle row.\n    As in part (a), the middle row has $5$ booths, so there are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{5}{2} = 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    ways to choose $2$ booths.\n\n    However, Arman and Bojanna cannot occupy adjacent booths.\n\n    In this case, it is easier to count how many combinations are not allowed and subtract this from the total.\n\n    If we number the booths in the middle row\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA1330-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1330-a1\">\n\n    the pairings\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &(1,2) && (2,3) && (3,4) && (4,5)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    are not allowed.\n\n    Therefore, $4$ of the $10$ combinations are not allowed.\n\n    Hence, there are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (10 - 4) \\times 2!\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    valid ways to assign Arman and Bojanna to booths in the middle row.\n    Now let’s consider the back row.\n\n    Similarly, there are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{5}{2} = 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    ways to choose $2$ booths.\n\n    However, there must be at least two booths between Arman and Bojanna.\n    Since there are only a few possibilities, we can count them directly.\n\n    If we number the booths in the back row\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA1330-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1330-a2\">\n\n    the valid pairings are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &(1,4) && (1,5) && (2,5)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, there are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3 \\times 2!\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    valid ways to assign Arman and Bojanna to booths in the back row.\n\n    Therefore, using a similar concept to part (a), the total number of ways to assign the pair under these conditions is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\big[(10 - 4) \\times 2! + 3 \\times 2!\\big] \\times  \\frac{13!}{5!5!3!} &= 1\\,297\\,296\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA693","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA693\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nJulie works at a book store and has nine books to display on the main\nshelf of the store. Four of the books are non-fiction and five are\nfiction. Each book is different. Determine the number of possible ways\nJulie can line up the nine books on the main shelf, given that\n\n1.  the non-fiction books should stand together; [2 marks]\n\n2.  the non-fiction books should stand together on either end;\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  the non-fiction books should stand together and do not stand on\n    either end. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $k$ be the number of possible ways that the 9 books can be\n    ordered with the 4 non-fiction books being arranged together.\n\n    To find $k$, we can consider a two steps process. First, we can\n    treat the 4 non-fiction books as a single item, and so there are\n    $6!$ permutations of the 5 fiction books and the block of\n    non-fiction books. After that, we can arrange the non-fiction books\n    in $4!$ different ways. Thus, by the multiplication rule, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k &= 6!\\times 4! \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 17\\hspace{0.15em}280\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $m$ be the number of possible ways that the non-fiction books\n    can stand together on either end. There are $2$ ways in which the\n    non-fiction books can be on one end, and $5!$ ways to order the\n    fiction books next to them. Finally, the non-fiction books can be\n    ordered in $4!$ ways. Hence, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m &=  2\\times 5! \\times 4! \\hspace{1.0em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\\hspace{0.15em}760\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $n$ be the number of possible ways that the non-fiction books\n    can stand together and do not stand on either end.\n\n    Using the results found in parts (a)-(b), we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} n &= k-m \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11\\hspace{0.15em}520\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA750","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA750\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nTyler needs to decide the order in which to schedule $11$ exams for his\nschool. Two of these exams are Chemistry ($1$ SL and $1$ HL).\n\nFind the number of different ways Tyler can schedule the $11$ exams\ngiven that the two Chemistry subjects must not be consecutive.","markScheme":"Let $n$ be the number of different ways in which the $11$ exams can be scheduled such that the two Chemistry subjects are not consecutive.\n\nTo find $n$, we can subtract the number of possible ways to order the $11$ exams such that the two Chemistry subjects **are** consecutive from $11!$, which is the total number of arrangements without considering Chemistry.\n\nIf we consider the two chemistry subjects as a single entity, then with the other 9 exams, we have a total of $10!$ ways that the exams can be ordered with both Chemistry exams staying together.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tag*{\\textbf{(R1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then consider that within these arrangements, the Chemistry exams could be in either order, so we must also multiply by $2!$.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tag*{\\textbf{(R1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 11! - 10! \\times 2!  \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n&= 32659200 \\\\[6pt]\nn &=\\boxed{ 3.27\\times10^{7}} \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{(3 s.f.)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA878","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA878\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSophia and Zoe compete in a freestyle swimming race where there are no\ntied finishes and there is a total of $10$ competitors.\n\nFind the total number of possible ways in which the ten swimmers can\nfinish if Zoe finishes\n\n1.  in the position immediately after\n    Sophia;[2 marks]\n\n2.  in any position after Sophia.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $n$ be the number of possible ways in which Zoe finishes\n    immediately after Sophia. To find $n$, we can treat Sophia followed\n    by Zoe as one item. We can then order the 9 items in any order.\n    Hence, the value of $n$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    n &= 9! \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= 362\\hspace{0.15em}880\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $m$ be the number of possible ways in which Zoe finishes in any\n    position after Sophia. Hence, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    m &= \\binom{9}{1}\\times8!+\\binom{8}{1}\\times8!+\\binom{7}{1}\\times8!+\\binom{6}{1}\\times8!+\\binom{5}{1}\\times8!+.....\\binom{1}{1}\\times8! \\\\[10pt]\n    &= (9 + 8 + 7 + 6 + 5 + \\cdots + 1) \\times 8!\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}= 1\\hspace{0.15em}814\\hspace{0.15em}400\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA880","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA880\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThere are six office cubicles arranged in a grid with two rows and three\ncolumns as shown in the following diagram. Aria, Bella, Charlotte,\nDanna, and Emma are to be stationed inside the cubicles to work on\nvarious company projects.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA880_q.png\" alt=\"AA880_q\">\n\nFind the number of ways of placing the team members in the cubicles in\neach of the following cases.\n\n1.  Each cubicle is large enough to contain the five team members, but\n    Danna and Emma must not be placed in the same cubicle.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Each cubicle may only contain one team member. But Aria and Bella\n    must not be placed in cubicles which share a boundary, as they tend\n    to get distracted by each other.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We start counting the number of ways we can place each of the team\n    members. Aria, Bella, Charlotte and Danna can be placed in any of\n    the six cubicles, but once Danna is placed, Emma can be placed in\n    the remaining five cubicles. Hence, the total number of ways of\n    placing the team members in this case is\n\n    $$\n    6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 5  = \\enskip  6\\hspace{0.15em}480\n    $$\n\n2.  Let's label the cubicles as follows\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/46e01a12bceb8a96908234fb3cd05cae223de337.svg\" alt=\"46e01a12bceb8a96908234fb3cd05cae223de337.svg\">\n\n    We have several ways of placing Aria and\n    Bella:\n\n    | Aria's cubicle | Bella's cubicle |\n    |:--------------:|:---------------:|\n    |       1        |     3, 5, 6     |\n    |       2        |      4, 6       |\n    |       3        |     1, 4, 5     |\n    |       4        |     2, 3, 6     |\n    |       5        |      1, 3       |\n    |       6        |     1, 2, 4     |\n\n    Thus, there are $16$ ways of placing Aria and Bella in cubicles that\n    do not share a boundary.\n\n    After that, Charlotte can be placed in any of the 4 remaining\n    cubicles, Danna in any of the 3 remaining cubicles, and Emma in any\n    of the 2 remaining cubicles.\n\n    Therefore, the total number of arrangements in this case is\n\n    $$\n    16 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 = \\enskip384\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA933","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA933\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA professor and five of his students attend a talk given in a lecture series. They have a row of 8 seats to themselves.\n\nFind the number of ways the professor and his students can sit if\n\n1. the professor and his students sit together. [3 marks]\n\n2. the students decide to sit at least one seat apart from their professor. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. We can consider the 8 seats as three \"blocks\", where one block consists of the 6 adjacent seats that the professor and his students occupy, and the other two blocks are the remaining two single seats. There are three possible arrangements of these blocks:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA933a.PNG\" alt=\"AA933a\">\n\n    Within the block of six seats occupied by the professor and his students, they can arrange themselves in $6!$ different ways. Let $k$ be the number of possible ways the six people can sit. The total number of arrangements is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    k &= 3 \\times 6! \\\\[11pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}\\boxed{k = 2\\hspace{0.15em}160}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Let $k$ be the number of possible ways the students can sit at least one seat apart from their professor. We have two cases:\n\n    \n\n    $\\bullet$ If the professor sits at one of the ends, then the students have $6$ seats available to them. There are two possibilities: \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA933b.PNG\" alt=\"AA933b\">\n\n    The students can arrange themselves in any order among the six seats. So we have \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA933d.PNG\" alt=\"AA933d\">\n\n    $\\bullet$ If the professor sits in one of the six seats in the middle, the students have only $5$ seats available to them, noting that the five students do not all need to sit together. There are six possible seats where the professor can sit, one example of which is shown here:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Counting%20Principles/images/AA933c.PNG\" alt=\"AA933c\">\n\n    The students can arrange themselves in any order among the five seats. So we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}  \n\n    k_2 &= 6 \\times 5! \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 720\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$  \n\n    The total will be $k = k_1 + k_2$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    k &= 1\\hspace{0.15em}440 + 720 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{k = 2\\hspace{0.15em}160}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Exponents & Logarithms","questions":[{"ref":"AA029","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA029\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve $\\log_6(x) + \\log_6(x-5) = 2$, for $x > 5$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_6(x) + \\log_6(x-5) &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_6(x(x-5)) &= \\log_6 6^2 \\\\[5pt]\n\\log_6 (x^2-5x) &= \\log_6 36 \\\\[5pt]\nx^2-5x &= 36 \\\\[5pt]\nx^2 - 5x - 36 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(x + 4)(x - 9) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nx - 9 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x + 4 > 0$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = 9 \\hspace{1.6em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA031","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA031\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_3 x - \\log_3 5 = 1 + \\log_3 4$ for $x$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_3 x - \\log_3 5 &= 1 + \\log_3 4 \\\\[10pt]\n\\log_3 x - \\log_3 5 &= \\log_3 3 + \\log_3 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\\log_3\\dfrac{x}{5} &= \\log_3 (3\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot4)  \\\\[5pt]\n\\log_3\\dfrac{x}{5} &= \\log_3 12 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x}{5} &= 12 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.55em}x = 60\n$$"},{"ref":"AA032","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA032\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_5 x - \\log_5 4 = 2 + \\log_5 3$ for $x$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_5 x - \\log_5 4 &= 2 + \\log_5 3 \\\\[10pt]\n\\log_5 x - \\log_5 4 &= \\log_5 5^2 + \\log_5 3 \\\\[8pt]\n\\log_5\\dfrac{x}{4} &= \\log_5 (5^2\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot3)  \\\\[5pt]\n\\log_5\\dfrac{x}{4} &= \\log_5 75 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x}{4} &= 75 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.55em}x = 300\n$$"},{"ref":"AA034","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA034\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider\n$a = \\log_{63}64\\times\\log_{62}63\\times\\log_{61}62\\times\\dots\\times\\log_{2}3$.\nGiven that $a\\in\\mathbb{Z}$, find the value of $a$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\log_{63}64\\times\\log_{62}63\\times\\log_{61}62\\times\\dots\\times\\log_{2}3 \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\dfrac{\\log64}{\\cancel{\\log63}}\\times\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\log63}}{\\cancel{\\log62}}\\times\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\log62}}{\\cancel{\\log61}}\\times\\cdots\\times\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\log3}}{\\log2} \\\\[6pt]\n&=  \\dfrac{\\log64}{\\log2} \\\\[6pt]\n&=  \\dfrac{\\log2^6}{\\log2} \\\\[6pt]\n&=  \\dfrac{6\\hspace{0.05em}\\cancel{\\log2}}{\\cancel{\\log2}} \\\\[12pt]\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA035","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA035\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) = 1 + \\log_2(x-1)$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) &= 1 + \\log_2(x-1) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) &= \\log_2 2 + \\log_2(x-1) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_2(x^2-2x+1) &= \\log_2(2x-2) \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-2x+1 &= 2x-2 \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-4x+3 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(x-1)(x-3) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\nx-3 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{$x\\neq1$, since $\\log_20$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = 3 \\hspace{3.15em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA036","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA036\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) = 1 + \\log_3(x-2)$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) &= 1 + \\log_3(x-2) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) &= \\log_3 3 + \\log_3(x-2) \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_3(x^2-4x+4) &= \\log_3(3x-6) \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-4x+4 &= 3x-6 \\\\[6pt]\nx^2-7x+10 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(x-2)(x-5) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\nx-5 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $x - 2 > 0$}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx=5 \\hspace{3.15em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA037","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA037\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $\\log_a 2 = 5$.\n\n1.  Find the exact value of $\\log_a 32$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the exact value of $\\log_{\\sqrt{a}} 2$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $a$, giving your answer correct to $3$ significant\n    figures. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_a 32 &= \\log_a 2^5 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5 \\log_a 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{\\sqrt{a}} 2 &= \\dfrac{\\log 2}{\\log \\sqrt{a}} \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[2pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log 2}{\\frac{1}{2}\\log a} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_a 2 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2(5) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 10\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the definition of logarithm, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_a 2 &= 5 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    a^5 &= 2 \\\\[4pt]\n    a &= 2^\\frac{1}{5} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    a \\approx 1.15 \\hspace{0.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA038","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA038\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven that $\\log_b 3 = 10$.\n\n1.  Find the exact value of $\\log_b 81$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the exact value of $\\log_{b^2} 3$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the value of $b$, giving your answer correct to $3$ significant\n    figures. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_b 81 &= \\log_b 3^4 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4 \\log_b 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4(10) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 40\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{b^2} 3 &= \\dfrac{\\log 3}{\\log b^2} \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log 3}{2\\log b} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_b 3 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(10) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the definition of logarithm, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_b 3 &= 10 \\hspace{3.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    b^{10} &= 3 \\\\[4pt]\n    b &= 3^\\frac{1}{10} \\\\[4pt]\n    &=1.116...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    b \\approx 1.12 \\hspace{0.6em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA039","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA039\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $15^{4a} = 81^{a+2}$ for $a$. Express your answer in\nterms of $\\ln 3$ and $\\ln 5$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15^{4a} &= 81^{a+2} \\\\[10pt]\n(3\\cdot5)^{4a} &= 3^{4(a+2)} \\\\[9pt]\n\\cancel{3^{4a}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot5^{4a} &= \\cancel{3^{4a}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot3^8 \\\\[10pt]\n5^{4a} &= 3^8 \\\\[10pt]\n\\ln 5^{4a} &= \\ln 3^8 \\\\[10pt]\n4a \\ln 5 &= 8\\ln 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2.25em}a = \\dfrac{2\\ln 3}{\\ln 5}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA040","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA040\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $14^{6x} = 64^{x+3}$ for $x$. Express your answer in\nterms of $\\ln 2$ and $\\ln 7$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14^{6x} &= 64^{x+3} \\\\[10pt]\n(2\\cdot7)^{6x} &= 2^{6(x+3)} \\\\[9pt]\n\\cancel{2^{6x}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot7^{6x} &= \\cancel{2^{6x}}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cdot2^{18} \\\\[10pt]\n7^{6x} &= 2^{18} \\\\[10pt]\n\\ln 7^{6x} &= \\ln 2^{18} \\\\[10pt]\n6x\\ln 7 &= 18\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{1.9em}x = \\dfrac{3\\ln 2}{\\ln 7}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA041","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA041\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the values of $x$ when\n$25^{x^2-2x} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{125}\\right)^{4x+2}$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25^{x^2-2x} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{125}\\right)^{4x+2} \\\\[3pt]\n\\left(5^2\\right)^{x^2-2x} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5^3}\\right)^{4x+2} \\\\[9pt]\n5^{2x^2-4x} &= 5^{-12x-6} \\\\[16pt]\n2x^2 - 4x &= -12x-6 \\\\[16pt]\nx^2 - 2x &= -6x - 3 \\\\[16pt]\nx^2 + 4x + 3 &= 0 \\\\[17pt]\n(x+3)(x+1) &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{3.45em}x = -3,-1\n$$"},{"ref":"AA042","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA042\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the value(s) of $x$ when\n$27^{x+2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right)^{2x+4}$.","markScheme":"Using the properties of exponents, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27^{x+2} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right)^{2x+4} \\\\[3pt]\n\\left(3^3\\right)^{x+2} &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3^2}\\right)^{2x+4} \\\\[9pt]\n3^{3x+6} &= 3^{-4x-8} \\\\[16pt]\n3x+6 &= -4x-8 \\\\[16pt]\n7x &= -14 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = -2 \\hspace{0.5em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA045","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA045\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the integer values of $a$ and $b$ for which\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b\\log_4 7 + 60\\log_{8} 14 &= 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b\\log_4 7 + 60\\log_{8} 14 &= 0 \\\\[13pt]\na + \\dfrac{b\\ln 7}{2\\ln 2} + \\dfrac{60\\ln 14}{3\\ln 2} &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\na\\ln 2 + \\dfrac{b}{2}\\ln 7 + 20\\ln (2\\cdot7) &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\na\\ln 2 + \\dfrac{b}{2}\\ln 7 + 20(\\ln 2 + \\ln 7) &= 0 \\\\[9pt]\n(a + 20)\\ln 2 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{b}{2}+20\\bigg)\\ln 7 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n $a = -20$\nand  $b = -40$"},{"ref":"AA046","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA046\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind the value of each of the following, giving your answer as an\ninteger.\n\n1.  $\\log_6 6$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_6 9 + \\log_6 4$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_6 72 - \\log_6 2$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\log_6 6 = 1$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_6 9 + \\log_6 4 &= \\log_6 (9\\cdot4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\log_6 36 \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\log_6 \\big(6^2\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_6 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_6 72 - \\log_6 2 &= \\log_6 \\bigg(\\dfrac{72}{2}\\bigg) \\hspace{0.15em} \\\\[5pt]\n     &= \\log_6 36 \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA047","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA047\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nFind the value of each of the following, giving your answer as an\ninteger.\n\n1.  $\\log_{10} 100$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_{10} 50 + \\log_{10} 2$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_{10} 4 - \\log_{10} 40$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.65em} \\log_{10} 100 &= \\log_{10} \\big(10^2\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_{10} 10 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{10} 50 + \\log_{10} 2 &= \\log_{10} (50\\cdot2) \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\log_{10} 100 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_{10} 4 - \\log_{10} 40 &= \\log_{10} \\bigg(\\dfrac{4}{40}\\bigg) \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[3pt]\n     &= \\log_{10} \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{10}\\bigg) \\\\[9pt]\n     &= \\log_{10} \\big(10^{-1}\\big) \\\\[16pt]\n     &= (-1) \\log_{10} 10 \\\\[9pt]\n     &= -1\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA048","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA048\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $p=\\ln 2$ and $q = \\ln 6$. Write down the following expressions in\nterms of $p$ and $q$.\n\n1.  $\\ln 12$ [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\ln 3$ [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\ln 48$ [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of natural logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln 12 &= \\ln (2\\cdot6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ln 2 + \\ln 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= p + q\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of natural logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\ln 3 &= \\ln \\bigg(\\dfrac{6}{2}\\bigg) \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\ln 6 - \\ln 2 \\\\[10pt]\n    &= q - p\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of natural logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.6em} \\ln 48 &= \\ln \\big(2^3\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot6\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n     &= 3\\ln2 +  \\ln 6 \\\\[6pt]\n     &= 3p + q\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA050","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA050\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $\\log_2 a = p$, $\\log_2 b = q$, $\\log_2 c = r$. Write down the\nfollowing expressions in terms of $p$, $q$ and $r$.\n\n1.  $\\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{ab}{c}\\Big)$ [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{a^2c}{b^3}\\Big)$\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_a b$ [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{ab}{c}\\Big) &= \\log_2 (ab) - \\log_2 c \\\\[6pt]\n     &= (\\log_2 a + \\log_2 b) - \\log_2 c  \\\\[10pt]\n     &= p + q - r\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.9em} \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{a^2c}{b^3}\\Big) &= \\log_2 \\big(a^2c\\big) - 3\\log_2 b \\\\[6pt]\n     &= (2\\log_2 a + \\log_2 c) - 3\\log_2 b \\\\[10pt]\n     &= 2p + r - 3q\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_a b &= \\dfrac{\\log_2 b}{\\log_2 a} \\hspace{2em}  \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{q}{p}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA052","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA052\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write down the value of\n    1.  $\\log_2 8$;\n\n    2.  $\\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big)$;\n    3.  $\\log_9 3$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence solve\n    $\\log_2 8 + \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big) + \\log_9 3 = \\log_{16} x$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_2 8 &= \\log_2 \\big(2^3\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\\log_2 2 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big) &= \\log_5\\big(5^{-2}\\big) \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (-2)\\log_5 5 \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_9 3 &= \\dfrac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 9} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 3}{\\ln \\big(3^2\\big)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 3}{2\\ln 3} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_2 8 + \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{25}\\Big) + \\log_9 3 &= \\log_{16} x \\hspace{6.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    3 + (-2) + \\dfrac{1}{2} &= \\log_{16} x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2} &= \\log_{16} x \\\\[10pt]\n    16^{3/2} &= x \\\\[14pt]\n    4^3 & = x \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $$\n     \\hspace{2.7em} x = 64\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA053","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA053\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write down the value of\n    1.  $\\log_3 81$;\n\n    2.  $\\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big)$;\n    3.  $\\log_{25} 5$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence solve\n    $\\log_3 81 + \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big) + \\log_{25} 5 = \\log_{9} x$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_3 81 &= \\log_3\\big(3^4\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 4\\log_3 3 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big) &= \\log_2\\big(2^{-3}\\big) \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (-3)\\log_2 2 \\\\[10pt]\n        &= -3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\log_{25} 5 &= \\dfrac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 25} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 5}{\\ln \\big(5^2\\big)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\ln 5}{2\\ln 5} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_3 81 + \\log_2\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\Big) + \\log_{25} 5 &= \\log_{9} x \\hspace{7.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    4 + (-3) + \\dfrac{1}{2} &= \\log_{9} x \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{2} &= \\log_{9} x \\\\[10pt]\n    9^{3/2} &= x \\\\[14pt]\n    3^3 & = x \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $$\n     \\hspace{2.4em} x = 27\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA055","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA055\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind the value of\n\n1.  $\\log_7 98 - \\log_7 2$; [2 marks]\n\n2.  $49^{\\log_7 6}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_7 98 - \\log_7 2 &= \\log_7\\Big(\\dfrac{98}{2}\\Big) \\hspace{3.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\log_7 49 \\\\[10pt]\n     &= \\log_7\\big(7^2\\big) \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2 \\log_7 7 \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    49^{\\log_7 6} &= \\big(7^2\\big)^{\\log_7 6} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 7^{2\\log_7 6} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7^{\\log_7 (6^2)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 7^{\\log_7 36} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 36\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA056","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA056\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nFind the value of\n\n1.  $\\log_5 75 - \\log_5 3$; [2 marks]\n\n2.  $25^{\\log_5 8}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\log_5 75 - \\log_5 3 &= \\log_5\\Big(\\dfrac{75}{3}\\Big) \\hspace{3.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\log_5 25 \\\\[10pt]\n     &= \\log_5\\big(5^2\\big) \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2 \\log_5 5 \\\\[12pt]\n     &= 2\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of exponents and logarithms, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    25^{\\log_5 8} &= \\big(5^2\\big)^{\\log_5 8} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 5^{2\\log_5 8} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5^{\\log_5 (8^2)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 5^{\\log_5 64} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 64\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1037","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1037\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe arithmetic sequence has the sixth term of $u_6=\\ln{2}$ and the eighth term of $u_8=\\frac{3}{2}\\ln{4}$.\n\n1.  1.  Show the common difference is $\\ln2$.\n\n    2.  Find $u_1$.\n\n    3.  Hence, show that the sum of $n$ terms is given by $S_n=\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}} $. Where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$. [5 marks]\n\nA new function, $f(x)$, is formed using $S_n$ with the discrete variable $n$ being replaced with the continuous variable $x$, such that $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Hence $f(x)=\\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n2.  1.  Write down the equation of the axis of symmetry of $f(x)$.\n\n    2.  Sketch $f(x)$ on the grid below, identify the axis of symmetry and any axis intercepts.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/q1037.png\" alt=\"q1037\">\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the minimum value of $S_n$ in the form $a\\ln b$. Where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ [5 marks]\n\nConsider the linear function $g(x)=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$.\n\n3.  1.  Solve $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n    2.  Hence, solve the inequality $f(x)<g(x)$.\n\n    3.  Hence or otherwise, find the range of values of $n$ that satisfy $S_n<g(x)$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  As the sequence is arithmetic we can write $u_8=u_6+2d$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\tfrac{3}{2}\\ln{4}&=\\ln{2}+2d \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            2d&=3\\ln{(\\sqrt{4})}-\\ln2\\\\\n            2d&=2\\ln{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\ln2}\\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  We can use $u_6$ to find the first term\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            u_6&=u_1+5d\\\\\n            \\ln2&=u_1+5\\ln2\\\\\n            u_1&=\\hspace{0.525em}-4\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\left(2\\times -4 \\ln2 +(n-1)\\times \\ln2\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\n            &=\\frac{n^2}{2}\\ln2-\\frac{9n}{2}\\ln2\\\\\n            &=\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\left(n^2-9n\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\left(n^2-9n\\right)\\ln{\\sqrt{2}} \\hspace{2.525em} \\text{...as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n2.  1.  Using the axis of symmetry formula, we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x&=-\\frac{-9\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\ln{\\sqrt{2}}}\\\\\n            &\\hspace{-0.525em}x=\\tfrac{9}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  Here is the sketch of $f(x)$ with the desired features identified.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/a1037-1.png\" alt=\"a1037-1\">\n\n        $\\text{Correct intercepts}$ **A1** and $\\text{axis of symmetry}$ **A1**.\n\n    3.  Using the symmetry of $f(x)$ we can identify the minimum value(s) of $f(x)$ which has integer $x$ coordinates.\n\n        Both points are equidistant from the axis of symmetry and they represent the minimum value of $S_n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/a1037-2.png\" alt=\"a1037-2\">\n\n        We can use the $x-$value from the graph to pick either $n=4$ or $n=5$ as both will yield the same minimum value. This solution will pick $n=4$, hence, the minimum value of $S_n$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S_4&=\\left(4^2-9(4)\\right)\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\\n            &=-20\\frac{\\ln2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\hspace{0.525em}-10\\ln2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  1.  Equate the RHS of each function\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\left(x^2-9x\\right)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}&=(6-3x)\\ln {\\sqrt 2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Cancel out the factor $\\ln {\\sqrt 2}$ and solve for $x$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            x^2-9x&=6-3x\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n            0&=x^2-6x-6\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can solve this by completing the square.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            (x-3)^2-9-6&=0\\\\\n            x-3&=\\pm\\sqrt{15}\\\\\n            x&=\\hspace{0.525em}3\\pm\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  By making a rough sketch of $g(x)$ on the graph we can solve the inequality\\\\\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/a1037-3.png\" alt=\"a1037-3\">\n\n        We can identify the region the $f(x)<g(x)$. Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &\\hspace{0.525em}3-\\sqrt{15}<x<3+\\sqrt{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  $n$ must be a positive integer and lie entirely within the region identified in part (c)(ii).\n\n        We know that $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}$, hence we can identify the minimum and maximum values of $n$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n        The minimum value must be an integer larger than $3-\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -1<3-\\sqrt{15}<0\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The minimum value of $n$ is $1$.\n\n        Similarly, the maximum value must be an integer smaller than $3+\\sqrt{15}$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            6<3+\\sqrt{15}<7\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The maximum value of $n$ is $6$.\n\n        Hence\n        $1\\leq n\\leq 6$.\n        $$\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        $$\n        $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$"},{"ref":"AA1047","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1047\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nFind the range of possible values for $p$ such that $$e^{2x}+2\\ln p = 4e^x$$ has at least one real solution.","markScheme":"The equation is a hidden quadratic. Let $k=e^x$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^2 - 4k + 2 \\ln p &= 0  \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the discriminant we can say a quadratic equation has at least one real solution if $ b^2-4ac\\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, we have at least one real solution for $k$ if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-4)^2-4(2\\ln p) &\\geq 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[7pt]\n16-8\\ln p &\\geq 0 \\\\[7pt]\n8\\ln p &\\leq 16  \\\\[7pt]\n\\ln p &\\leq 2 \\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{-2.33em}\\boxed{0 < p \\leq e^2} \\hspace{3em} \\text{($\\ln p > 0$)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\\\\[-11pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1179","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1179\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the following equation for $a$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=a}^{a+35} \\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"First we will unpack the sigma notation.\n\nThe smallest value of $k$ is $\\textcolor{red}{a}$. This value is the increased by $1$ for each subsequent term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=a}^{a+35} \\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)&=\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a}}\\right)+\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+1}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+1}}\\right)+\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{1.2em}\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+2}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+2}}\\right)+...\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis continues until we reach the largest value of $k$ which is $a+35$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    +...\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+34}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+34}}\\right)+\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{a+35}+1}{\\textcolor{red}{a+35}}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we simplify the fractions and then apply the following log law\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{a}\\tfrac{x}{y}&=\\log_{a}x-\\log_{a}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=a}^{a+35} \\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)&=\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{a+1}{a}\\right)+\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{a+2}{a+1}\\right)+...\\log_{13} \\left(\\frac{a+36}{a+35}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+1)}}-\\textcolor{blue}{\\log_{13}{a}}+\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+2)}}-\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+1)}}+\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{1.2em}-\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+2)}}+...+\\textcolor{blue}{\\log_{13}{(a+36)}}-\\cancel{\\log_{13}{(a+35)}} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNotice how the terms cancel each other out, leaving only the two $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$ terms. Recall, from the question, this is equal to $1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    -\\log_{13}{a}+\\log_{13}{(a+36)}&=1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can now solve the equation by rewriting the L.H.S. as a single logarithm\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{13}{\\left(\\frac{a+36}{a}\\right)}&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the definition of a logarithm, we rewrite the equation into exponential form\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{a+36}{a}&=13^1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow we can solve for $a$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    a+36&=13a\\\\[6pt]\n    12a&=36\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}a=3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA121","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA121\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $a = \\log_5b$, where $b > 0$. Write down each of the following\nexpressions\\\nin terms of $a$.\n\n1.  $\\log_5b^4$ [2 marks]\n\n2.  $\\log_5 (25b)$ [2 marks]\n\n3.  $\\log_{25}b$ [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_5b^4 &= 4\\log_5b \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4a\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} \\log_5 (25b) &= \\log_5 \\left(5^2\\hspace{-0.2em}\\cdot b\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\log_5 5 + \\log_5 b \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2 + a\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we obtain\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{0.3em} \\log_{25}b &= \\dfrac{\\log_5 b}{\\log_5 25} \\\\[6pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{\\log_5 b}{2\\log_5 5} \\\\[10pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{a}{2}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA1291","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1291\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Show that $12\\log_x{2}=\\dfrac{12}{\\log_2{x}}$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Hence solve the equation $\\log_2{x}=8-12\\log_x{2}$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the change of base formula we can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{L.H.S.}&=12\\log_{x}2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=12\\left(\\frac{\\log_22}{\\log_2x}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    As $\\log_n{n}=1$, we can replace $\\log_2{2}$ with 1 and simplify\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=12\\left(\\frac{1}{\\log_2x}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{12}{\\log_2x}\\hspace{1em}\\text{ ...as required}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\text{R.H.S.}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  We can replace $12\\log_x2$ with the R.H.S. of the identity from part (a)\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_2x&=8-12\\log_x2\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\log_2x&=8-\\frac{12}{\\log_2x}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We now multiply each side by $\\log_2x$ and then rearrange such that the R.H.S. is equal to $0$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    (\\log_2x)^2&=8\\log_2x-12\\\\[6pt]\n\n    (\\log_2x)^2-8\\log_2x+12&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This is a hidden quadratic equation.  If we replace $\\log_2$ with a variable, say $a$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a^2-8a+12&=0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Let's solve this by factorising.  \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    (a-2)(a-6)&=0    \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore the solutions are\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a-2&=0&\\text{and}&&a-6=0\\\\[6pt]\n\n    a&=2&&& a=6\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall $a=\\log_2x$, therefore the solutions become\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_2x&=2&&&\\log_2x=6\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Converting each to exponential form, we get \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x&=2^2&&&x=2^6\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}x=4&\\hspace{0.3em}&&x=64\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1315","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1315\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nIt is given that $\\log_k{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}=\\dfrac{1}{5}$, where $k>0$.\n\nFind the value of\n\n1.    $\\log_k{16}$; [2 marks]\n\n2.    $\\log_{k^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{16}\\right)}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Let's begin by looking at the given relationship and expression\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_k{\\left(\\textcolor{red}{\\dfrac{1}{4}}\\right)}&=\\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_k{\\textcolor{red}{16}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Notice that $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and $16$ share the same base of $4$, this means we can rewrite $\\log_k{16}$ in the terms of $\\log_k{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}$.\n\n    \n\n    To do that we first write down\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    16&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{a}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    where $a$ is to be determined.\n\n    Using index laws we can simplify the L.H.S. and the R.H.S. of above to get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    4^2&=4^{-a}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $a=-2$.\n\n    Using that we can rewrite the given expression in terms of $\\log_k{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_k{16}&= \\log_k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{-2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the logarithmic power law we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &= -2\\log_k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=-2 \\times \\frac{1}{5}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Note that the base of the expression is now $k^2$, hence we need to use the change of base formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_{a}x&=\\frac{\\log_{b}x}{\\log_{b}a}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    to change the base to $k$ so it matches the given relation\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\log_{k^2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}&=\\frac{\\log_{k}\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}{\\log_{k}k^2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that $\\frac{1}{16}=16^{-1}$ and $\\log_{k}k^2=2$ hence we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\frac{\\log_{k}\\left(16\\right)^{-1}}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the logarithmic power law and our answer from part (a) we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{k}\\left(16\\right)\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\times -\\frac{2}{5}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{1}{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA143","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA143\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nSolve the equation $9^x + 2\\cdot3^{x+1} = 1$.","markScheme":"If we substitute $y = 3^x$ in the equation $9^x + 2\\cdot3^{x+1} = 1$, we\nhave\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(3^x\\right)^2 + 6\\cdot3^x - 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\ny^2 + 6y - 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the quadratic formula, we\nget\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{13em} y &= \\dfrac{-6 + \\sqrt{6^2 - 4(1)(-1)}}{2(1)} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{since $y = 3^x > 0$}] \\\\[4pt]\n&= \\dfrac{-6 + 2\\sqrt{10}}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n&= -3 + \\sqrt{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the properties of\nlogarithms, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^x &= -3 + \\sqrt{10} \\hspace{2.6em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{0.3em} x = \\log_3(-3 + \\sqrt{10})\n$$"},{"ref":"AA193","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA193\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider $f(x) = \\log_k(8x-2x^2)$, for $0 < x < 4$, where $k > 0$.\n\nThe equation $f(x) = 3$ has exactly one solution. Find the value of $k$.","markScheme":"If we rewrite the equation $f(x) = 3$ in the form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$,\nwe have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} \\log_k(8x-2x^2) &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n8x-2x^2 &= k^3 \\\\[6pt]\n0 &= 2x^2 - 8x + k^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the quadratic function\n$q(x) = 2x^2 - 8x + k^3$ has two equal roots.\n\nTherefore we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.1em} [\\text{discriminant of $q$}] &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(-8)^2 - 4(2)(k^3) &= 0 \\hspace{3em}  [\\triangle  = b^2 - 4ac]  \\\\[6pt]\n64 - 8k^3 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n8 - k^3 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nk^3 &= 8 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{3.3em} k=2\n$$"},{"ref":"AA284","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA284\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSolve $\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\sin x) - \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\cos x) = 1$, for\n$0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.","markScheme":"We have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Exponents%20%26%20Logarithms/images/eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\" alt=\"eea28ea6dc26969cf5c008612e865ae5046a04ef.svg\">\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms and the right triangle above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.1em} \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\sin x) - \\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\cos x) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}\\hspace{-0.2em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\Big) &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\\log_{\\sqrt{3}}(\\tan x) &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n\\tan x &= \\sqrt{3} \\\\[10pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi \\hspace{2.3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$}] \\\\[6pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$}] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{2.1em} x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA299","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA299\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Show that $\\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) = \\log_4 \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve\n    $\\log_4(\\sqrt{10}\\cos x) = \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7)$, for\n    $0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) &= \\dfrac{\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7)}{\\log_4 16} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7)}{2\\log_4 4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_4(\\cos 2x + 7) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\log_4 \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\hspace{2.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the double angle formula for cosine, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.75em} \\log_4(\\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x) &= \\log_{16}(\\cos 2x + 7) \\\\[14pt]\n    \\log_4(\\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x) &= \\log_4 \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\hspace{3em} \\\\[14pt]\n    \\textstyle \\sqrt{10}\\cos x &= \\textstyle \\sqrt{\\cos 2x + 7} \\\\[14pt]\n    10\\cos^2 x &= \\cos 2x + 7 \\\\[14pt]\n    10\\cos^2 x &= (2\\cos^2x - 1) + 7  \\\\[14pt]\n    8\\cos^2 x &= 6 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\cos^2 x &= \\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\cos x &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\pi\\hspace{-0.05em}/2$}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\hspace{1em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA646","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA646\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Show that $\\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} = \\log_4 (3-\\cos 2x)$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve\n    $\\log_4 (3\\sin x) \\hspace{0.15em}+\\hspace{0.15em} \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}$,\n    for\n    $0 \\hspace{-0.05em}<\\hspace{-0.05em} x \\hspace{-0.05em}<\\hspace{-0.05em}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} &= \\dfrac{\\log_4 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}}{\\log_4 2} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\log_4 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x}}{\\frac{1}{2}\\log_4 4} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2 \\log_4 \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\log_4 4 = 1$}] \\\\[18pt]\n    &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the properties of logarithms, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\dfrac{1}{4} &= \\textstyle \\log_2 \\sqrt{3-\\cos 2x} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{3.5em} \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\log_4 4 &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[7pt]\n    \\textstyle \\log_4 (3\\sin x) + \\log_4\\sqrt{2} &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{since $\\textstyle 4^{1/4} = \\sqrt{2}$}\\hspace{0.05em}] \\\\[10pt]\n    \\textstyle \\log_4 (3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x)\\hspace{-0.1em} &= \\log_4(3-\\cos 2x) \\\\[10pt]\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 3-\\cos 2x  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $0 < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 3 - (1 - 2\\sin^2 x) \\hspace{1.3em} [\\text{by double angle identity}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\textstyle 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x &= 2 + 2\\sin^2 x \\\\[9pt]\n    \\textstyle 2\\sin^2 x - 3\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + 2 &= 0 \\hspace{2.1em} [\\text{rearranging terms to form quadratic}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2} \\pm \\sqrt{18-4\\cdot2\\cdot2}}{4} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $\\sin x \\neq \\sqrt{2}$}] \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sin x &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3.1em} x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < x < \\textstyle \\frac{\\pi}{2}$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA751","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA751\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write the expression $4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8$ in the form of $\\ln k$, where\n    $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, solve $4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8 = -\\ln (2x)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8 &= \\ln \\big(2^4\\big) - \\ln 8 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\ln 16 - \\ln 8 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\ln\\Big(\\dfrac{16}{8}\\Big) \\\\[9pt]\n    & = \\ln 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Replacing the left hand side of the equation with $\\ln 2$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4\\ln 2 - \\ln 8 &= -\\ln (2x) \\\\[12pt]\n    \\ln 2  &= -\\ln(2x) \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[12pt]\n    -\\ln 2 &= \\ln(2x) \\\\[12pt]\n    \\ln \\big(2^{-1}\\big) &= \\ln(2x) \\\\[12pt]\n    2x &= 2^{-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    2x &= \\dfrac 1 2 \\\\[13pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em} x = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA820","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA820\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nSolve the simultaneous equations:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 2\\log_5 x &= \\log_5 7y \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_7 (6x-1) &= 1 + \\log_7 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"Using the properties of logarithms, we rewrite the given equations as\nfollows.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_5 5 + \\log_5 x^2 &= \\log_5 7y \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_5 5x^2 &= \\log_5 7y \\\\[6pt]\n5x^2 &= 7y \\hspace{5em}{(1)} \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_7 (6x-1) &= 1 + \\log_7 y \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{2.75em} \\log_7 (6x-1) &= \\log_7 7 + \\log_7 y \\\\[6pt]\n\\log_7 (6x-1) &= \\log_7 7y \\\\[6pt]\n6x - 1 &= 7y \\hspace{5em}{(2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the equations (1) and (2),\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^2 &= 6x - 1 \\\\[6pt]\n5x^2 - 6x + 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n(5x - 1)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{1}{5} \\text{ or } x = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = \\dfrac{1}{5}$ into the equation (1), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5} \\right)^2 &= 7y \\hspace{2.5em}\\\\[12pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{5} &= 7y \\\\[12pt]\ny &= \\dfrac{1}{35}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting $x = 1$ into the\nequation (1), we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(1)^2  &= 7y \\\\[12pt]\n5 &= 7y \\\\[12pt]\ny &= \\dfrac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\hspace{-2.15em}x = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\, y = \\dfrac{1}{35} \\\\[12pt]\n& \\hspace{-1.9em}x = 1, \\,\\, y = \\dfrac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA984","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA984\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe equation $e^{2x} - 12e^x = -32$ has two solutions, $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\n1. Find the value of $x_1$ and the value of $x_2$.[5 marks]\n\nA second equation, $2\\left(\\log_{\\,9}x\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 6\\log_{\\,9}x - 2\\log_{\\,3}x = -6$, also has two solutions, $x_3$ and $x_4$. \n\n2. 1.  Show that this second equation can be expressed as\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right)^2 -5\\log_{\\,3}x + 6 = 0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. Hence find the value of $x_3$ and the value of $x_4$. [7 marks]\n\n3. Given that $x_1 + x_2 = a(x_3 + x_4)$, find the value of $a$. Give your answer in the form $b\\ln c$, where $b,c \\in \\mathbb{R}$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The equation can be written as a quadratic in $e^x$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(e^x\\right)^2 - 12e^x + 32 = 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The equation can then be factorised:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\left(e^x - 4\\right)\\left(e^x - 8\\right) = 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    From the first bracket, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    e^x - 4 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^x &= 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    x &= \\ln 4 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 2\\ln 2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    And from the second bracket,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    e^x - 8 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^x &= 8 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    x &= \\ln 8 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 3\\ln 2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence, $$\\boxed{\\text{$x_1 = 2\\ln 2$ and $x_2 = 3\\ln 2$.}}$$\n\n2. 1. We are asked to write the second equation in terms of $\\log_{\\,3}x$, and currently the equation is expressed in terms of $\\log_{\\,3}x$ and $\\log_{\\,9}x$. Using the formula for changing the base, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\log_{\\,9}x &= \\dfrac{\\log_{\\,3}x}{\\log_{\\,3}9} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{\\log_{\\,3}x}{\\log_{\\,3}3^2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{\\log_{\\,3}x}{2\\log_{\\,3}3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\log_{\\,9}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_{\\,3}x\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Substituting this expression into our original equation, we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2\\left(\\log_{\\,9}x\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 6\\log_{\\,9}x - 2\\log_{\\,3}x &= -6 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 6\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right) - 2\\log_{\\,3}x &= -6 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-10em}\\boxed{\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x\\right)^2 - 5\\log_{\\,3}x + 6 = 0}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. The equation can be factorised as follows:\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\left(\\log_{\\,3}x - 2\\right)\\left(\\log_{\\,3}x - 3\\right) = 0\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        From the first bracket, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x - 2 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x &= 2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        x &= 9\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        And from the second bracket,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x - 3 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\log_{\\,3}x &= 3 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        x &= 27\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence, $$\\boxed{\\text{$x_3 = 9$ and $x_4 = 27$.}}$$\n\n3. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    x_1 + x_2 &= a(x_3 + x_4) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    2\\ln 2 + 3\\ln 2 &= a(9+27) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5\\ln 2 &= 36a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{a = \\dfrac{5}{36}\\ln 2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Financial and Growth Models","questions":[{"ref":"AA1053","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1053\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn the $1$st of January  $2024$ Fabian bought a new car for \\$23,000. He knows from research that the car will depreciate at a rate of $8\\%$ for every complete year.\n\n1.  Find the value ($V_f$) of Fabian's car on the $1$st of January $2030$.[3 marks]\n\nFabian's colleague Sven also bought a new car on $1$st of January $2024$. Sven knows from research what the value (in \\$) of his new car will be on the $1$st of January for the following years.\n\n$$\\\\[1em]$$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/q1053.png\" alt=\"q1053\">\n\n2.  Assuming the value of Sven's car can be approximately modelled by the equation $V_s=ax+b$, find out the difference in value of Fabian and Sven's cars on $1$st of January $2030$, according to the model. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can use a geometric sequence to model the value of Fabian's car ($V_f$).\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    The common ratio, $r$, will be $1-0.08=0.92$, and the first term, $u_1$, will be $23\\,000$. There are $6$ complete years between $1$st of January $2024$ and $1$st of January $2030$. We are looking for $u_7$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_f &= u_1 \\times r^{7-1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=23\\,000\\times 0.92^6\\\\[6pt]\n        &=13\\,946.16...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\$13\\,900 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We can find a linear model using the G.D.C. Such that $a=-3\\,228.84...$ and $b=6\\,574\\,789.23... $, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s&=-3228.84... x+6574789.23... \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can predict the value ($V_s$) with $x=2030$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s&=-3228.84...\\times 2030+6574789.23...\\\\\n        &=20\\,231.53...\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore the difference in value of the two cars is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s-V_f&=20\\,231.53...- 13\\,946.16...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\$6\\,290 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA108","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA108\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn $1$st of January $2022$, Grace invests $$\\$P$$ in an account that pays\na nominal annual interest rate of $6$ %,\n**compounded quarterly**.\n\nThe amount of money in Grace's account **at the end of each year**\nfollows a geometric sequence with common ratio, $\\alpha$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $\\alpha$, giving your answer to four significant\n    figures. [3 marks]\n\nGrace makes no further deposits or withdrawals from the account.\n\n2.  Find the year in which the amount of money in Grace's\n    account will become **triple** the amount she invested. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/98fa6420aaf44673cb775b3bfb7054ff59483e48.svg\" alt=\"98fa6420aaf44673cb775b3bfb7054ff59483e48.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    P\\alpha &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{4\\times 1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the value of $\\alpha$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\alpha &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{400}\\bigg)^4 \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 1.061\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/22a91089c8626c046d977bf7f58b143ef817f639.svg\" alt=\"22a91089c8626c046d977bf7f58b143ef817f639.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} 3P &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{4\\times n} \\\\[5pt]\n    3 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{400}\\bigg)^{4n} \\\\[12pt]\n    n &\\approx 18.4 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence Grace will have triple the amount she invested during\n     $2040$"},{"ref":"AA109","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA109\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn Gary's $50$th birthday, he invests $$\\$P$$ in an account that pays a\nnominal annual interest rate of $5$ %,\n**compounded monthly**.\n\nThe amount of money in Gary's account **at the end of each year**\nfollows a geometric sequence with common ratio, $\\alpha$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $\\alpha$, giving your answer to four significant\n    figures. [3 marks]\n\nGary makes no further deposits or withdrawals from the account.\n\n2.  Find the age Gary will be when the amount of money in his\n    account will be **double** the amount he invested. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/41a3f8a185c5c4fff3e200802b215cea41fb13e6.svg\" alt=\"41a3f8a185c5c4fff3e200802b215cea41fb13e6.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    P\\alpha &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the value of $\\alpha$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\alpha &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{1200}\\bigg)^{12} \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 1.051\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/3a174f6b31ae114bd45f642a60b4cf2b3ffba841.svg\" alt=\"3a174f6b31ae114bd45f642a60b4cf2b3ffba841.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} 2P &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times n} \\\\[5pt]\n    2 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[12pt]\n    n &\\approx 13.9 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence Gary will have double the amount he invested at age\n     $63$"},{"ref":"AA110","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA110\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nBill takes out a bank loan of $$\\$100\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to buy a premium\nelectric car, at an annual interest rate of\n$5.49$%. The interest is calculated at the end\nof each year and added to the amount outstanding.\n\n1.  Find the amount of money Bill would owe the bank after $10$ years.\n    Give your answer to the nearest dollar. [3 marks]\n\nTo pay off the loan, Bill makes quarterly deposits of $$\\$P$$ at the end\nof every quarter in a savings account, paying a nominal annual interest\nrate of $3.2$%. He makes his first deposit at\nthe end of the first quarter after taking out the loan.\n\n2.  Show that the total value of Bill's savings after $10$\n    years is $P\\bigg[\\dfrac{1.008^{40}-1}{1.008-1}\\bigg]$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Given that Bill's aim is to own the electric car after $10$\n    years, find the value for $P$ to the nearest dollar. [3 marks]\n\nMelinda visits a different bank and makes a single deposit of\n$$\\$\\hspace{0.05em}Q$$, the annual $\\text{interest}$ rate being\n$3.5$%.\n\n4.\n    1.  Melinda wishes to withdraw $$\\$8000$$ at the end of each year for\n        a period of $n$ years. Show that an expression for the minimum\n        value of $Q$ is\n\n        $$\n        \\dfrac{8000}{1.035} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^2} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^3} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^n}.\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence, or otherwise, find the minimum value of $Q$ that would\n        permit Melinda to withdraw annual amounts of $$\\$8000$$\n        indefinitely. Give your answer to the nearest dollar.\n        [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/8d43689548a3d16e00609e684b76b38f90c0c20a.svg\" alt=\"8d43689548a3d16e00609e684b76b38f90c0c20a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 100\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5.49}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times10} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx \\$170\\hspace{0.15em}653 \\hspace{8em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The number of time periods in $10$ years is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    n &= (4)(10) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 40\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The total value of Bill's savings after $10$\n    years is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} &= P + P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.2}{100\\times4}\\bigg) + P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.2}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^2 + \\dots + P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.2}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{39} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= P + P(1.008) + P(1.008)^2 + \\cdots + P(1.008)^{39} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = P$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 1.008$.\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n - 1)}{r - 1}$ with $\\text{\\(n = 40\\)}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} &= S_{40} \\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\hspace{0.525em}P\\bigg(\\dfrac{1.008^{40} - 1}{1.008 - 1}\\bigg) \\hspace{8em}{(2)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $(1) = (2)$ for $P$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.45em} 170\\hspace{0.15em}653 &= P\\bigg(\\dfrac{1.008^{40} - 1}{1.008 - 1}\\bigg)  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    P \\approx \\$3637\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/7fa99aa4ecafd8974b214fde355c840e109f39ef.svg\" alt=\"7fa99aa4ecafd8974b214fde355c840e109f39ef.svg\">\n\n        Hence the amount of money in Melinda's account after $n$ years\n        is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= Q\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times n} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= Q(1.035)^n\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we deduce\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        Q(1.035)^n &= 8000 + 8000(1.035) + 8000(1.035)^2 + \\cdots + 8000(1.035)^{n-1} \\\\[12pt]\n        Q &= \\dfrac{8000\\big[1 + 1.035 + 1.035^2 + \\cdots + 1.035^{n-1}\\big]}{1.035^n} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        Q = \\dfrac{8000}{1.035} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^2} + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^3} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{8000}{1.035^n} \\hspace{4em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have an infinite geometric sequence with the first term\n        $u_1 = \\dfrac{8000}{1.035}$ and the common ratio\n        $r = \\dfrac{1}{1.035}$.\n        Hence, using the sum of an infinite geometric\n        sequence $\\text{formula}$ $\\text{\\(S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}\\)}$,\n        we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} Q &= \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\bigg[\\dfrac{8000}{1.035}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{1.035}\\bigg]^k \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\frac{8000}{1.035}}{1 - \\frac{1}{1.035}} \\\\[15pt]\n        &= \\$228\\hspace{0.15em}571\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA117","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA117\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIt is known that the number of trees in a small forest will decrease by\n$5$ % each year unless some new trees are\nplanted. At the end of each year, $600$ new trees are planted to the\nforest. At the start of $2021$, there are $8200$ trees in the forest.\n\n1.  Show that there will be roughly $9060$ trees in the forest at the\n    start of $2026$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the approximate number of trees in the forest at the start of\n    $2041$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of trees in the forest at the start of $2022$ will be\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T}_1 &= 8200\\times0.95 + 600 \\hspace{2.8em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The number of trees in the forest at the start of $2023$ will be\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.65em} \\mathrm{T}_2 &= \\mathrm{T}_1\\times0.95 + 600 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\left(8200\\times0.95 + 600\\right)0.95 + 600 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8200\\times0.95^2 + 600(0.95 + 1)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the number\n    of trees in the forest at the start of $2026$ will be\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T}_5 &= 8200\\times0.95^5 + 600\\hspace{0.05em}(0.95^4 + 0.95^3 + 0.95^2 + 0.95 + 1) \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 9060\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The approximate number of trees in the forest at the start of $2041$\n    will be\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{T}_{20} &= 8200\\times0.95^{20} + 600\\bigg[\\sum_{k=0}^{19}0.95^k\\bigg] \\\\[2pt]\n    &= 8200\\times0.95^{20} + 600\\bigg[\\dfrac{1 - 0.95^{20}}{1 - 0.95}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 10\\hspace{0.15em}638\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1175","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1175\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nJuanita wants to borrow some money to buy an apartment.\n\nShe finds an offer allowing her to borrow \\$480\\,000 over $10$ years with an interest rate of $r\\%$ P.A. compounded monthly. She repays the loan with a fixed amount $p$ every month.\n\nJuanita takes the loan out at the beginning of the month. At the end of the month, the interest is added __and then__ she makes the monthly payment of $p$.\n\nThis continues until after $10$ complete years, she has repaid the loan in its entirety.\n\nJuanita wants to analyse three different scenarios in which she could repay the loan.\n\n1.  In the first scenario her monthly payment is $$p=\\,\\$5\\,000$$.\n\n    If $k=1+\\dfrac{r}{1200}$\n    1.  Write down the number of payments that will be made over the entire $10$ year term of the loan.\n\n    2.  Show that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    96k^{120}&=\\frac{k^{120}-1}{k-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    3.  Hence, or otherwise, find $r$.[6 marks]\n\n2.  In the second scenario Juanita uses the same values for $p$ and $r$ as part (a).  She makes the monthly payments of $p$ for $7$ years and $4$ months.\n\n    She then makes a final payment to clear the remaining balance of the loan.\n    1.  Find the number of payments she makes __before__  the final payment.\n\n    2.  Hence, find the final payment required to clear the remaining balance to $4$ significant figures. [3 marks]\n\n3.  In the third scenario Juanita pays $p$ per month for $5$ complete years and then she increases her monthly loan repayment to $2p$ for the remaining $5$ years.\n\n    Find the value of $p$, to the nearest dollar, for the third scenario. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  There are $12$ payments per year for $10$ years\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=10 \\times 12\\\\[6pt]\n        &=120 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    2.  Note: Multiplying by $k$ represents an increase of $r\\%$.\n\n        We can build up an expression which represents the remaining value, $FV$, of the loan. After the first month, this remaining value is increased by $r\\%$ and then the payment \\$$5\\,000$ is subtracted\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=480\\,000\\times k -5\\,000\\\\[6pt]\n            &=480\\,000k -5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We then continue into the second month, this process is repeated. The remaining value is increased by $r\\%$ and the payment \\$$5\\,000$ is subtracted\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=(480\\,000k -5\\,000)\\times k -5\\,000\\\\[6pt]\n            &=480\\,000k^2 -5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Similarly for the third month\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=(480\\,000k^2 -5\\,000k-5\\,000)\\times k -5\\,000\\\\[6pt]\n            &=480\\,000k^3 -5\\,000k^2-5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        This continues for all $120$ payments and can be shown using the following series\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            FV&=480\\,000k^{120} -5\\,000k^{119}-5\\,000k^{118}-5\\,000k^{117}-...-5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        As the loan has been repaid in full we can replace $FV$ with $0$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            0&=480\\,000k^{120} -5\\,000k^{119}-5\\,000k^{118}-5\\,000k^{117}-...-5\\,000k-5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can then rearrange the equation above to get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            480\\,000k^{120}&=5\\,000k^{119}+5\\,000k^{118}+5\\,000k^{117}+...+5\\,000k+5\\,000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Recognise that the R.H.S. of the above is a geometric series with a first term of $5\\,000$ and a common ratio of $k$. Hence we can use the sum of a geometric sequence formula to write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            480\\,000k^{120}&=5\\,000\\left(\\frac{k^{120}-1}{k-1}\\right) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n            &\\hspace{-2.8em}96k^{120}=\\dfrac{k^{120}-1}{k-1}\\hspace{2em} \\text{... as required.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    3.  Using a G.D.C. we can solve the equation found above.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                96k^{120}&=\\frac{k^{120}-1}{k-1} \\\\[6pt]\n                k&=1.0038412... \\hspace{3.3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                1.0038412...&=1+\\tfrac{r}{1200} \\\\[6pt]\n                r&=4.609419...\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n                &=4.61\\% \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n2.  1.  She makes $7$ full years of payments plus an additional $4$ months, which gives\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                &=7 \\times 12 + 4\\\\[6pt]\n                &=88\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $\\text{She makes }88\\text{ payments before the final payment.}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  You could use the Financial Solver on the G.D.C. with the following values\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/AA1175_table.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1175_table\">\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Solving for $FV$ gives a remaining value of $150\\,286.301...$.\n\n        Hence the final payment is $$\\boxed{\\$150\\,300}$$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Juanita pays $p$ per month for $60$ months, she then pays $2p$ per month for the remaining $60$ months.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Lets consider the first $60$ payments of  $p$ using a similar method to part (a) where $k=1+\\tfrac{4.61}{1200} = 1.0038...$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        FV&=((480\\,000 \\times k - p)\\times k - p)...\\\\[6pt]\n        FV&=480\\,000k^{60}-pk^{59}-pk^{58}...-pk-p \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Now let's consider the next $60$ payments of $2p$ which will clear the remaining balance, which means the future value is \\$$0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n         \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=((480\\,000k^{60}-pk^{59}-pk^{58}...-p)\\times k - 2p)\\times k - 2p)...\\\\[6pt]\n        0&=480\\,000k^{120}-pk^{119}...-pk^{60}-2pk^{59}-2pk^{58}-...-2pk-2p\\\\[6pt]\n        480\\,000k^{120}&=pk^{119}...+pk^{60}+2pk^{59}+2pk^{58}+...+2pk+2p \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    This equation has a single unknown ($p$). However in its current form we cannot enter it into the calculator as there are too many terms!\n\n    We need to find a way to rewrite the sum in a simpler form.\n\n    We can do this by summing the RHS of the equation in two parts. We will let the first $60$ terms be part $A$ and the last $60$ terms be part $B$\n\n    To find the sum of part $A$ we can use the formula directly, pasrt $A$ has parameters $u_1=2p$, $r=1.0038...$ and $n=60$, therefore\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_A&=2p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's reconsider the original equation and replace part $A$ with the simpler form found above\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        480\\,000k^{120}&=\\underbrace{pk^{119}...+pk^{60}}_{\\mathrm{B}}+\\underbrace{2pk^{59}+2pk^{58}+...+2pk+2p}_{\\mathrm{A}}\\\\[6pt]\n        480\\,000k^{120}&=\\underbrace{pk^{119}...+pk^{60}}_{\\mathrm{B}}+\\underbrace{2p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1}}_{\\mathrm{A}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Part $B$ is more complicated. We can't simply sum $120$ terms as we only want to sum the last $60$ terms of the sequence.\n\n    Lets find the sum of the full $120$ terms and then subtract the sum of the first $60$ terms.\n\n    Part $B$ has parameters $u_1=p$, $r=1.0038...$ and $n=120$, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_B&=p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{120}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} - p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's now rewrite the original equation and replace $S_B$ with the formula above\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        480\\,000(1.0038...)^{120}&=\\underbrace{p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{120}-1}{(1.0038...)-1} - p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1}}_{\\mathrm{B}}+\\underbrace{2p\\frac{(1.0038...)^{60}-1}{(1.0038...)-1}}_{\\mathrm{A}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now solve this on the G.D.C.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        p&=3465.61...\\\\[6pt]\n        p&=\\$3466 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1332","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1332\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn $1$st January $2021$, Nyla buys a yacht.\n\nThe price of the yacht depreciates at $7\\%$ per annum.\n\nOn $1$st January $2025$, she sells the yacht for \\$23\\,564 .\n\n1.    Find the price of the yacht when Nyla bought it. [3 marks]\n\nNyla wants to buy a speedboat.\n\nThe speedboat is priced at \\$45\\,000 on $1$st January $2021$, and it depreciates at $0.2\\%$ per month.\n\nOn $1$st June $2025$, Nyla buys the speedboat.\n\nIn addition to the money she received for selling the yacht she was required to add \\$$K$ to buy the speedboat.\n\n2.    Find the value of $K$ to the nearest dollar. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. We can determine the price of the boat by using the compound depreciation formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}&=\\mathrm{PV}\\left(1-\\frac{r}{100}\\right)^{n}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}&=\\text{ Future Value}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{PV}&=\\text{ Present Value}\\\\[6pt]\n    r&=\\text{ annual rate of depreciation, as a percentage}\\\\[6pt]\n    n&=\\text{ number of compounding periods}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In this case we are told the future value and are expected to find the present value.\n\n    This means we have $\\mathrm{FV}=$\\$23\\,564, $k=1$, $r=7$, and $n=4$.\n\n    Hence we can substitute these values into the formula to solve for the initial value (PV).\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    23\\,564&=\\mathrm{PV}\\times \\left(1-\\frac{7}{100}\\right)^4\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1A1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    23\\,564&=\\mathrm{PV}(0.93)^4\\\\[6pt]\n    31\\,500.4...&=\\mathrm{PV}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{PV}&=\\$31\\,500\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. We are told that Nyla will use the money from the sale of the boat and an additional amount $K$ to buy the speedboat.\n\n    Hence, letting $\\mathrm{FV}_S$ represent the future value of the speedboat we can write the formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    23\\,564+K&=\\mathrm{FV}_S\\\\[6pt]\n    K&=\\mathrm{FV}_S-23\\,564\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To determine $\\mathrm{FV}_S$ at the time when Nyla buys the speedboat we can use the formula in part (a).\n\n    We know that $\\mathrm{PV}=$ \\$45\\,000 and $r=0.2$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}&=45\\,000\\left(1-\\frac{0.2}{100}\\right)^{n}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The value of $n$ is the number of compounding periods so we need to determine how many months are between $1$ January $2021$ and $1$ June $2025$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    n&=4\\times12+5\\\\[6pt]\n    &=53\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting $n=53$ we can solve for the value of the speedboat.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV}_S&=45\\,000\\left(1-\\frac{0.2}{100}\\right)^{53}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\$40\\,469.813...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can use this value to solve for $K$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    K&=\\mathrm{FV}_S-23\\,564\\\\[6pt]\n    &=40\\,469.813-23\\,564\\\\[6pt]\n    &=16\\,905.813...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\$16\\,906\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA630","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA630\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe Australian Koala Foundation estimates that there are about\n$45\\hspace{0.15em}000$ koalas left in the wild in $2019$. A year before,\nin $2018$, the population of koalas was estimated as\n$50\\hspace{0.15em}000$. Assuming the population of koalas continues to\ndecrease by the same percentage each year, find:\n\n1.  the exact population of koalas in $2022$; [3 marks]\n\n2.  the number of years it will take for the koala population to reduce\n    to half of its number in $2018$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have a geometric sequence with\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2018:\\hspace{0.5em} u_1 &= 50\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[6pt]\n    2019:\\hspace{0.5em} u_2 &= 45\\hspace{0.15em}000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The\n    common ratio of the sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{45\\hspace{0.15em}000}{50\\hspace{0.15em}000} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 5$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.5em} 2022:\\hspace{0.5em} u_5 &= u_1r^{5-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 50\\hspace{0.15em}000(0.9)^{5-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 32\\hspace{0.15em}805\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $u_n = 25\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $n$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r^{n-1} &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    50\\hspace{0.15em}000(0.9)^{n-1} &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.5em} n \\approx 7.58 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[2pt]\n    $$\n\n    Because our starting year, 2018, is considered to be year 1 ($n=1$), it will take 6.58 years from then for the population to halve from $50\\,000$ to $25\\,000$.\n\n    Hence it will take\n     $6.58 \\text{ years}$\n    for the koala population to reduce to half of its number in $2018$."},{"ref":"AA635","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA635\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**In this question give all answers correct to the nearest whole\nnumber.**\n\nA population of goats on an island starts at $232$. The population is\nexpected to increase by $15$ % each year.\n\n1.  Find the expected population size after:\n    1.  $10$ years;\n\n    2.  $20$ years. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the number of years it will take for the population to reach\n    $15\\hspace{0.15em}000$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/537894a1e06c34454736a91f01647c46ca2f4fa3.svg\" alt=\"537894a1e06c34454736a91f01647c46ca2f4fa3.svg\">\n\n    Hence the population size is expected to increase according to a\n    geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 232(1.15)$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 1.15$.\n\n    1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 10$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} u_{10} &= u_1r^{10-1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= [232(1.15)](1.15)^{10-1} \\\\[7pt]\n        &\\approx 939\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 20$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} u_{20} &= u_1r^{20-1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= [232(1.15)](1.15)^{20-1} \\\\[7pt]\n        &\\approx 3797\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Solving the equation $u_n = 15\\hspace{0.15em}000$ for $n$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r^{n-1} &= 15\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.5em} [232(1.15)](1.15)^{n-1} &= 15\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[7pt]\n    n &\\approx 29.8 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence it will take\n     $30 \\text{ years}$\n    for the population of goats to reach $15\\hspace{0.15em}000$."},{"ref":"AA657","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA657\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn $1$st of January $2021$, Fiona decides to take out a bank loan to\npurchase a new Tesla electric car. Fiona takes out a loan of $$\\$P$$ with\na bank that offers a nominal annual interest rate of\n$2.6\\hspace{0.05em}\\%$, **compounded monthly**.\n\nThe size of Fiona's loan **at the end of each year** follows a geometric\nsequence with common ratio, $\\alpha$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $\\alpha$, giving your answer to five significant\n    figures. [3 marks]\n\nThe bank lets the size of Fiona's loan increase until it becomes\n**triple** the size of the original loan. Once this happens, the bank\ndemands that Fiona pays the entire amount back to close the loan.\n\n2.  Find the year during which Fiona will need to pay back the\n    loan. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/5f2f8a1ae6bc63ae761ba90ac49b1bb0bd1bd144.svg\" alt=\"5f2f8a1ae6bc63ae761ba90ac49b1bb0bd1bd144.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we deduce\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    P\\alpha &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the value of $\\alpha$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} \\alpha &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{1200}\\bigg)^{12} \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 1.0263\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/a4fdabe8dee8262e3ad5b7a5f6550e756265ae9a.svg\" alt=\"a4fdabe8dee8262e3ad5b7a5f6550e756265ae9a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.75em} 3P &= P\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times n} \\\\[5pt]\n    3 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{2.6}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[12pt]\n    n &\\approx 42.3 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence Fiona will need to pay back the loan during\n     $2063$"},{"ref":"AA702","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA702\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nJeremy invests $$\\$8000$$ into a savings account that pays an annual\ninterest rate of $5.5$ %, **compounded\nannually**.\n\n1.  Write down a formula which calculates that total value of the\n    investment after $n$ years. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the amount of money in the savings account after:\n    1.  $1$ year;\n\n    2.  $3$ years. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Jeremy wants to use the money to put down a $$\\$10\\hspace{0.15em}000$$\n    deposit on an apartment. Determine if Jeremy will be able to do this\n    within a $5$-year timeframe.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/eaedd006beb5ba238d9ad76a3a6f6101e049be71.svg\" alt=\"eaedd006beb5ba238d9ad76a3a6f6101e049be71.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 8000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5.5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times n} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055)^n \\hspace{3em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Substituting $n = 1$ in the formula $(1)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{FV} &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055) \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\$8440\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Substituting $n = 3$ in the formula $(1)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{FV} &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055)^3 \\\\[8pt]\n        &=9393.9...\\\\[8pt]\n        &\\approx \\$9390\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Substituting $n = 5$ in the formula $(1)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} \\mathrm{FV} &= 8000\\hspace{0.05em}(1+0.055)^5 \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx \\$10\\hspace{0.15em}456\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $\\text{Yes, Jeremy will be able to do this within a \\(5\\)-year timeframe.}$"},{"ref":"AA703","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA703\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nMaria invests $$\\$25\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ into a savings account that pays\na nominal annual interest rate of $4.25$%,\n**compounded monthly**.\n\n1.  Calculate the amount of money in the savings account after $3$\n    years. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the number of years it takes for the account to reach\n    $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/987f4c1d6bf2f65199be87193b7aea8bd2861a05.svg\" alt=\"987f4c1d6bf2f65199be87193b7aea8bd2861a05.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times3} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx \\$28\\hspace{0.15em}400\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/f2cb8aa057458cfd9f39f532ecc8e0e8adc7b0a4.svg\" alt=\"f2cb8aa057458cfd9f39f532ecc8e0e8adc7b0a4.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} 40\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 25\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[6pt]\n    1.6 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Sketching the graphs of $y=1.6$ and $y=\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4.25}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n}$ we get:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/AA703a.PNG\" alt=\"AA703a\">\n\n    Hence it takes\n     $11.1 \\text{ years}$\n    for the account to reach $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$."},{"ref":"AA704","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA704\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**In this question give all answers correct to two decimal places.**\n\nMia deposits $4000$ Australian dollars (AUD) into a bank account. The\nbank pays a nominal annual interest rate of\n$6$ %, **compounded semi-annually**.\n\n1.  Find the amount of **interest** that Mia will earn over the next\n    $2.5$ years. [3 marks]\n\nElla also deposits AUD into a bank account. Her bank pays a nominal\nannual $\\text{interest}$ rate of $4$ %,\n**compounded monthly**. In $2.5$ years, the total amount in Ella's\naccount will be $4000$ AUD.\n\n2.  Find the amount that Ella deposits in the bank account.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/dc425b29b9e196ccc51470e8966f83917d4179b9.svg\" alt=\"dc425b29b9e196ccc51470e8966f83917d4179b9.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 4000\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{6}{100\\times2}\\bigg)^{2\\times2.5} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 4637.10 \\text{ AUD}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the amount of interest earned is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} - \\text{PV} &\\approx 4637.10 - 4000 \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 637.10 \\text{ AUD}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/bc195cd8b953f93121c2542be7b715a98cf4c62e.svg\" alt=\"bc195cd8b953f93121c2542be7b715a98cf4c62e.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} 4000 &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{4}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times2.5} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9em} \\text{PV} \\approx 3619.95 \\text{ AUD} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA705","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA705\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGreg has saved $2000$ British pounds (GBP) over the last six months. He\ndecided to deposit his savings in a bank which offers a nominal annual\ninterest rate of $\\text{\\(8\\)\\hspace{0.05em}\\%}$, **compounded\nmonthly**, for two years.\n\n1.  Calculate the total amount of interest Greg would earn over the two\n    years. Give your answer correct to **two decimal places**.\n    [3 marks]\n\nGreg would earn the same amount of interest, **compounded\nsemi-annually**, for two years if he deposits his savings in a second\nbank.\n\n2.  Calculate the nominal annual interest rate the second bank\n    offers. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/d94b9efaa433da997063a9b0927befd8e7a68df2.svg\" alt=\"d94b9efaa433da997063a9b0927befd8e7a68df2.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{8}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2345.78 \\text{ GBP}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the total amount of interest earned is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{FV} - \\text{PV} &= 2345.78 - 2000 \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 345.78 \\text{ GBP}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/218c768ecef0bd0485a005a319e86a9adb263fd0.svg\" alt=\"218c768ecef0bd0485a005a319e86a9adb263fd0.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} 2345.78 &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100\\times2}\\bigg)^{2\\times 2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8.2em} r \\approx 8.13\\hspace{0.05em}\\% \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA706","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA706\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nEmily deposits $2000$ Australian dollars (AUD) into a bank account. The\nbank pays a nominal annual interest rate of\n$4$ %, **compounded monthly**.\n\n1.  Find the amount of money that Emily will have in her bank account\n    after $5$ years. Give your answer correct to **two decimal places**.\n    [3 marks]\n\nEmily will withdraw the money back from her bank account when the amount\nreaches $3000$ AUD.\n\n2.  Find the time, **in years and months**, until Emily withdraws the\n    money from her bank account. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/adb2e5a5727d896793f414aff36c01c8cf4261d3.svg\" alt=\"adb2e5a5727d896793f414aff36c01c8cf4261d3.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 5} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2441.99 \\text{ AUD}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/07a5668b1bebdc2755dde33ca8b99bce2a25d25a.svg\" alt=\"07a5668b1bebdc2755dde33ca8b99bce2a25d25a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} 3000 &= 2000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12n} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\hspace{3.7em} 1.5 &= \\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Sketching the graphs of $y=1.5$ and $y=\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{4}{1200}\\bigg)^{12n}$ we get:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/AA706a.PNG\" alt=\"AA706a\">\n\n    $10.15$ years $\\approx 10$ years and 2 months.\n\n    Hence Emily will withdraw the money from her bank account after\n     $10 \\text{ years and }2 \\text{ months.}$"},{"ref":"AA707","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA707\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nJulia wants to buy a house that requires a deposit of\n$74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ Australian dollars (AUD).\n\nJulia is going to invest an amount of AUD in an account that pays a\nnominal annual interest rate of $5.5$ %,\n**compounded monthly**.\n\n1.  Find the amount of AUD Julia needs to invest to reach\n    $74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ AUD after $8$ years. Give your answer correct\n    to **the nearest dollar**. [3 marks]\n\nJulia's parents offer to add $5000$ AUD to her initial investment from\npart (a), however, only if she invests her money in a more reliable bank\nthat pays a nominal annual interest rate only of\n$3.5$ %, **compounded quarterly**.\n\n2.  Find the number of years it would take Julia to save the\n    $74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ AUD if she accepts her parents money and\n    follows their advice. Give your answer correct to **the nearest\n    year**. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/3787235f52ce0a9532d1fb499061fa26f27dacb9.svg\" alt=\"3787235f52ce0a9532d1fb499061fa26f27dacb9.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    74\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5.5}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 8} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{9.1em} \\text{PV} \\approx 47\\hspace{0.15em}707 \\text{ AUD} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/c0f56a142d7776c6858b94a3b97c9a56ff5d69a7.svg\" alt=\"c0f56a142d7776c6858b94a3b97c9a56ff5d69a7.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.6em} 74\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 52\\hspace{0.15em}707\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{3.5}{100\\times4}\\bigg)^{4n} \\\\[8pt]\n    n &\\approx 9.74 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence it would take Julia\n     $10 \\text{ years}$\n    to save the $74\\hspace{0.15em}000$ AUD."},{"ref":"AA709","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA709\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nHannah buys a car for $$\\$24\\hspace{0.15em}900$$. The value of the car\ndepreciates by $16$ %\neach year.\n\n1.  Find the value of the car after $10$ years. [3 marks]\n\nPatrick buys a car for $$\\$12\\hspace{0.15em}000$$. The car depreciates by\na fixed percentage each year, and after $6$ years it is worth $$\\$6200$$.\n\n2.  Find the annual rate of depreciation of the car.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/848a0a2395b69bf10b5ffc75fbbed6c738b8c64e.svg\" alt=\"848a0a2395b69bf10b5ffc75fbbed6c738b8c64e.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{r}{100}\\bigg)^n \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 24\\hspace{0.25em}900\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{16}{100}\\bigg)^{10}  \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 4355.04...\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx \\$4360\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/80839ebacd99f943925e1048218639215d638a8d.svg\" alt=\"80839ebacd99f943925e1048218639215d638a8d.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.9em} 6200 &= 12\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{r}{100}\\bigg)^6  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.7em} r \\approx 10.4\\hspace{0.05em}\\% \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA710","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA710\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAli bought a car for $$\\$18\\hspace{0.15em}000$$. The value of the car\ndepreciates by $10.5$ %\neach year.\n\n1.  Find the value of the car at the end of the first year. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of the car after $4$ years. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the number of years it will take for the car to be worth\n    exactly half its original value. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/86d0641572fd7d8ca13b40e061d1374bca7391bc.svg\" alt=\"86d0641572fd7d8ca13b40e061d1374bca7391bc.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\text{PV}\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{r}{100}\\bigg)^n \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 18\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{10.5}{100}\\bigg)  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\$16\\hspace{0.15em}110 \\\\[18pt]\n    &\\approx \\$16\\hspace{0.15em}100\\hspace{2em}\\text{correct to 3 s.f.} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/8adbc1baad593f942992356c96995063b6051eee.svg\" alt=\"8adbc1baad593f942992356c96995063b6051eee.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{FV} &= 18\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{10.5}{100}\\bigg)^4  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\$11\\hspace{0.15em}549.5 \\\\[18pt]\n    &\\approx \\$11\\hspace{0.15em}500\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/22cbaefd6c6344e284fd948d4606858070563a4e.svg\" alt=\"22cbaefd6c6344e284fd948d4606858070563a4e.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound depreciation formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    9000 &= 18\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 - \\dfrac{10.5}{100}\\bigg)^n \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8em} n \\approx 6.25 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA839","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA839\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nAlex and Julie each have a goal of saving $$\\$30\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to\nput towards a house deposit. They each have $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to\ninvest.\n\n1.  Alex chooses his local bank and invests his $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$\n    in a savings account that offers an interest rate of $5\\%$ per annum\n    compounded **annually**.\n\n    1.  Find the value of Alex's investment after $7$ years, to the\n        nearest hundred dollars.\n\n    2.  Alex reaches his goal after n years, where n is an integer.\n        Determine the value of n. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Julie chooses a different bank and invests her\n    $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ in a savings account that offers an\n    interest rate of $r\\%$ per annum compounded **monthly**, where $r$\n    is set to two decimal places.\n\n    Find the minimum value of $r$ needed for Julie to reach her goal\n    after $10$ years. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Xavier also wants to reach a savings goal of\n    $$\\$30 \\hspace{0.15em}000$$. He doesn't trust his local bank so he\n    decides to initially put his money into a safety deposit box where it does not\n    earn any interest. His system is to add more money into the safety\n    deposit box at the end of each year. At the end of each year he will add one third of the amount\n    he added at the end of the previous year.\n\n    1.  Show that Xavier will never reach his goal if his initial\n        deposit into the safety deposit box is $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$.\n\n    2.  Find the amount Xavier needs to initially deposit in order to\n        reach his goal after $7$ years. Give your answer to the nearest\n        dollar. [7 marks]\n\n    $\\\\[-2em]$","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/9dbbca0f5e685431fe27e5f6d20cddfa41909806.svg\" alt=\"9dbbca0f5e685431fe27e5f6d20cddfa41909806.svg\">\n\n        Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times 7} \\\\[8pt]\n        &\\approx\\$22\\hspace{0.15em}500\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/bb1b286c0aec001397762ed487c09e553b272c3a.svg\" alt=\"bb1b286c0aec001397762ed487c09e553b272c3a.svg\">\n\n        Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n        30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{5}{100\\times1}\\bigg)^{1\\times n} \\\\[8pt]\n        n & =13 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/990e9181ddb4f41f6ff2aaf08aa718b7d5031131.svg\" alt=\"990e9181ddb4f41f6ff2aaf08aa718b7d5031131.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 10} \\\\[6pt]\n    30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{1200}\\bigg)^{120} \\\\[8pt]\n    r&=6.302...\\hspace{5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\\\[8pt]\n    r&=6.31\\,(\\text{2 d.p.})\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    **Note:** Even though we generally round down in this case, here we need to round up to 6.31 to ensure Julie reaches her goal.\n\n3.\n    1.  The total amount of money in Xavier's safe after an infinite\n        number of years is expressed by\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        A &= 16\\hspace{0.15em}000 + 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right) + 16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^2 +  16\\hspace{0.15em}000\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^{3} + \\dots\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We have an infinite geometric series with\n        the first term $u_1 = 16\\hspace{0.15em}000$ and the common ratio\n        $r = \\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n\n        Hence, using **the formula for the sum of an infinite geometric sequence (1.8)**,\n        $\\text{\\(S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}\\)}$, we obtain\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        A &=  \\dfrac{16\\hspace{0.15em}000}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} \\hspace{5.8em}\\\\[8pt]\n        A &=\\$24\\hspace{0.15em}000\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence he will never reach the\n        $$\\$30\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ target if his initial deposit is\n        $$\\$16\\hspace{0.15em}000$$.\n\n    2.  Let $P$ represent the initial deposit. Note that after the initial deposit, there will be a further $7$ deposits, one at the end of each year.\n\n        The amount of money that Xavier saves after $7$ years, $A$, is\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        A &= P + P\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right) + P\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^2 + \\dots + P\\left( \\dfrac 1 3 \\right)^{7} \\hspace{2em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We have a geometric series with the first\n        term $u_1 = P$, common ratio $r = \\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $8$ terms in total.\n\n        Hence, using the formula **for the sum of $\\bm{n}$ terms of a finite geometric sequence (1.3)**, $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\n        with $\\text{\\(n = 8\\)}$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{A} &= S_{8} \\\\[6pt]\n        30\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= \\dfrac{P\\left(1-\\left( \\frac 1 3 \\right)^8\\right)}{1-\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\[12pt]\n        P &= \\dfrac{20\\,000}{1-\\left( \\frac 1 3 \\right)^8}\\\\[12pt]\n        P&=\\$20\\hspace{0.15em}003 \\hspace{1em}\\text{ [to the nearest dollar]}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA840","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA840\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nGrant wants to save $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ over 5 years to help his\nson pay for his college tuition. He deposits $$\\$20\\hspace{0.15em}000$$\ninto a savings account that has an interest rate of $6\\%$ per annum\ncompounded **monthly** for $5$ years.\n\n1.  Show that Grant will not be able to reach his target. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the minimum amount, to the nearest dollar, that Grant would\n    need to deposit initially for him to reach his target. [3 marks]\n\nGrant only has $$\\$20\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ to invest, so he asks his\nsister, Caroline, to help him accelerate the saving process. Caroline is\nhappy to help and offers to contribute part of her income each year. Her\nannual income is $$\\$37 \\hspace{0.15em}500$$ per year. She starts by\ncontributing one fifth of her annual income, and then decreases her\ncontributions by half each year until the target is reached. Caroline's\ncontributions do not yield any interest.\n\n3.  Show that Grant and Caroline together can reach the target\n    in 5 years. [4 marks]\n\nGrant and Caroline agree that Caroline should stop contributing once she\ncontributes enough to complement the deficit of Grant's investment.\n\n4.  Find the **whole** number of years after which Caroline\n    will stop contributing. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/64fb25df045f9e0b7a416f15dde7a9fce22438d5.svg\" alt=\"64fb25df045f9e0b7a416f15dde7a9fce22438d5.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 20\\hspace{0.15em}000\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 5} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx \\$26\\hspace{0.15em}977\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therfore, Grant\n    will not reach his target as the value of his investment after $5$\n    years is $$\\$26\\hspace{0.15em}977$$ which is less than his\n    $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$ goal.\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Financial%20and%20Growth%20Models/images/aaeeec80128922b9372aa176b0353a0a146271f3.svg\" alt=\"aaeeec80128922b9372aa176b0353a0a146271f3.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the compound interest formula, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{FV} &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\bigg)^{kn} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\hspace{4.25em} 40\\hspace{0.15em}000 &= \\mathrm{PV}\\bigg(1 + \\dfrac{6}{100\\times12}\\bigg)^{12\\times 5} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.5em}\\mathrm{PV} \\approx \\$29\\hspace{0.15em}655 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The amount that Caroline contributes in the first year is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P} &= \\dfrac{1}{5}(37\\hspace{0.15em}500) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$7\\hspace{0.15em}500\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The total\n    amount that Caroline contributes after $5$ years is expressed by\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &= 7500 + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right) + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right)^2 +  + \\dots + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right)^{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We have a geometric series with the first term\n    $u_1 = 7500$ and $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Hence, using the formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n - 1)}{r - 1}$ with\n    $\\text{\\(n = 5\\)}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{A} &= S_{5} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\hspace{2.75em} &= 7\\hspace{0.15em}500\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\left( \\frac 1 2 \\right)^5 - 1}{\\frac{1}{2} - 1}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx \\$14\\hspace{0.15em}531\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, the total amount that Grant and Caroline can save in $5$\n    years is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.5em} \\text{Total amount} &\\approx 26\\hspace{0.15em}977 + 14\\hspace{0.15em}531 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.35em} \\approx \\$41\\hspace{0.15em}508\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, Grant and Caroline together can reach the target as the total\n    amount saved exceeds the target of $$\\$40\\hspace{0.15em}000$$.\n\n4.  The amount Caroline must contribute to help Grant reach the target\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.75em} C &= 40\\hspace{0.15em}000 - 26\\hspace{0.15em}977 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$13\\hspace{0.15em}023\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore, we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    7500 + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right) + 7500\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right)^2 +  \\dots \\hspace{1em} &> 13\\hspace{0.15em}023\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have a geometric series with the first term $u_1 = 7500$ and\n    $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n - 1)}{r - 1}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    7500\\bigg[\\dfrac{\\left( \\frac 1 2 \\right)^n - 1}{\\frac{1}{2} - 1}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.2em} &> 13\\hspace{0.15em}023 \\\\[12pt]\n    n &> 2.92358 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, Caroline will stop contributing after\n     $3\\hspace{0.3em} \\text{years}$"}]},{"name":"Proof by Deduction, Contradiction, and Counterexample","questions":[{"ref":"AA111","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA111\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Show that $(2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 = 16n^3+12n$ for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, prove that the sum of the cubes of any two\n    consecutive odd integers is divisible by four. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n-1)^3 &= (2n)^3 - 3(2n)^2 + 3(2n) - 1 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8n^3 - 12n^2 + 6n - 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n+1)^3 &= (2n)^3 + 3(2n)^2 + 3(2n) + 1 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8n^3 + 12n^2 + 6n + 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Summing our expressions for $(2n-1)^3$ and $(2n+1)^3$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 &= 8n^3 - \\cancel{12n^2} + 6n - \\cancel{1} + 8n^3 + \\cancel{12n^2} + 6n + \\cancel{1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 = 16n^3 + 12n \\hspace{2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $2n-1$ and $2n+1$ be two consecutive odd integers for some\n    $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n    The sum of the cubes of $2n-1$ and $2n+1$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.4em} (2n-1)^3 + (2n+1)^3 &= 16n^3 + 12n \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4n(4n^2 + 3) \\hspace{2.22em} [\\text{multiple of $4$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the sum of the cubes of any two consecutive odd integers is\n    divisible by four."},{"ref":"AA1157","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1157\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUse proof by contradiction to show that if $(n-6)^3=(n+6)^3-n^3, n\\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$, is true then $n$ must be even.","markScheme":"First expand and simplify the given equality\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^3+\\binom{3}{1}(-6)n^2+\\binom{3}{2}(-6)^2n+(-6)^3&=n^3+\\binom{3}{1}(6)n^2+\\binom{3}{2}6^2n+6^3-n^3\\\\[6pt]\nn^3-18n^2+108n-216&= 18n^2+108n+216\\\\[6pt]\nn^3-36n^2-432&= 0 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow we make the contradiction by assuming $n$ is odd. Let $n=2k+1$, where $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2k+1)^3-36(2k+1)^2-432&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nConsider the term $36(2k+1)^2$, we can rewrite this as $2\\times18(2k+1)^2$, therefore this term is even.\n\nHence we can write $36(2k+1)^2$ as $2a$, where $a \\in     \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe term $-432$ is also even.\n\nLet's expand the first term\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2k+1)^3&=8k^3+12k^2+6k+1\\\\[6pt]\n&=2(4k^3+6k^2+3k)+1\\\\[6pt]\n&=2b+1 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $b \\in     \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nTherefore the original simplified equation can be written in the following form\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&= 2b+1-2a-432\\\\[6pt]\n0&=2(b-a-216)+1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet $c=b-a-216$ where where $c \\in     \\mathbb{Z}$. Hence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&= 2c+1 \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is a contradiction as $0$ is an even number and $2c+1$ is odd.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1245","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1245\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the 3 digit number $m=134$.\n\nThe **digits** of $m$ are 1, 3 and 4.\n\nThe sum of the digits of $m$ is $1+3+4=8$, and we could write the number $m$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    m &= 100 \\times 1 + 10 \\times 3 + 1 \\times 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow consider a 4 digit number $n$.\n\nProve that if the sum of the digits of $n$ is divisible by 3, then $n$ is also divisible by 3.","markScheme":"Let the digits of $n$ be $p, q, r$ and $s$, where $p,q,r,s\\in\\mathbb{Z^+}$, $1 \\leq p \\leq 9$, and $0 \\leq q,r,s\\leq9$.\n\nWe can therefore rewrite $n$ as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn=1000p+100q+10r+s \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the sum of the digits is divisible by three, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{p+q+r+s}{3} &= k \\hspace{1em}(k\\in\\mathbb{Z^+})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    p+q+r+s=3k\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging to make $s$ the subject, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    s=3k-p-q-r \\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation for $n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    n&=1000p+100q+10r+3k-p-q-r \\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=999p+99q+9r+3k\\\\[6pt]\n    &=3(333p+33q+3r+k)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=3v \\hspace{1em}[\\text{where } v=333p+33q+3r+k \\text{ and }v \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}]\\\\[6pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\\text{if the digits of $n$ are divisible by 3, then $n$ is also divisible by $3$}$$ ... as required.\n\n$$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA600","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA600\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nUsing the method of proof by contradiction, prove that $\\sqrt{3}$ is\nirrational.","markScheme":"Let's assume that it is not true that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational. Then\n$\\sqrt{3}$\n\nis rational, so there are positive integers $a$ and $b$ ($b\\neq 0$) such\nthat\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{a}{b}\n\n$$\nwe can also assume that this fraction has\nbeen written in lowest terms, so $a$ and $b$ have no common factors.\n\nSquaring both sides,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{3})^2 &= \\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right)^2 \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n3 &= \\dfrac{a^2}{b^2} \\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{10mm}\\\\\na^2 &= 3b^2 \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{8mm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom (1) we\ndeduce that $a^2$ is divisible by $3$ and therefore $a$ is divisible by\n$3$. Then we can write $a=3p$ for some positive integer $p$.\nSubstituting $a=3p$ in (1), we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3p)^2 &= 3b^2 \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{5mm}\\\\\n9p^2 &= 3b^2 \\rule[-3.5mm]{0pt}{9mm}\\\\\n3p^2&= b^2 \\rule[-2mm]{0pt}{6.5mm}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore $b$ is\nalso divisible by $3$. This is a contradiction, as $a$ and $b$ have no\ncommon factors.\n\nThus our original assumption is false, and by the method of proof by\ncontradiction, we conclude $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational."},{"ref":"AA834","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA834\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nConsider two consecutive positive integers, $k$ and $k+1$.\n\nShow that the difference of their squares is equal to the sum of the two\nintegers.","markScheme":"The difference of the squares of $k$ and $k+1$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{9.5em}(k+1)^2 - k^2 &= k^2 + 2k + 1 - k^2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2k + 1 \\\\[6pt]\n&= k + (k + 1) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{sum of $k$ and $k+1$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the difference of the squares of two consecutive positive integers\nis equal to their sum."},{"ref":"AA835","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA835\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nProve that the sum of three consecutive positive integers is divisible\nby $3$.","markScheme":"Let $n, \\, n + 1$ and $n+2$ be three consecutive positive integers for\nsome $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^{+}}$.\n\nThe sum of the three integers is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8em} n + (n + 1) + (n+2) &= 3n + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 3(n+1) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{multiple of $3$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the sum of three consecutive positive integers is divisible by\n$3$."},{"ref":"AA836","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA836\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe product of three consecutive integers is increased by the middle\ninteger.\n\nProve that the result is a perfect cube.","markScheme":"Let $n-1,\\, n$ and $n+1$ be three consecutive integers for some\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThe product of these three integers is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(n-1) \\times n \\times (n + 1) &=  n \\times (n^2 - 1) \\\\[6pt]\n&= n^3 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf this product is\nincreased by the middle integer, $n$, then the result would be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.25em} (n^3 - n) + n &= n^3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{perfect cube}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the result of increasing the product of three consecutive integers\nby the middle integer is a perfect cube."},{"ref":"AA926","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA926\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nProve by contradiction that the equation $3x^3-7x^2+5=0$ has no integer roots.","markScheme":"Assume that the equation $3x^3-7x^2+5=0$ has at least one integer root, $n$.\n\nTherefore, $n$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    3n^3-7n^2+5 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    3n^3+5 &= 7n^2 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nConsider first the case where $n$ is odd. $3n^3$ is odd and hence $3n^3 + 5$ is even. $7n^2$ is odd, therefore\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    3n^3+5 &\\neq 7n^2 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow consider the case where $n$ is even. $3n^3$ is even and hence $3n^3 + 5$ is odd. $7n^2$ is even, therefore\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    3n^3+5 &\\neq 7n^2 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe have shown that for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, whether $n$ is odd or even, the equation is not satisfied. Hence there is a contradiction.\n\nThus our original assumption is false, and by the method of proof by contradiction, we conclude that $3x^3-7x^2+5=0$ has no integer roots."},{"ref":"AA950","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA950\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1.  Prove that $\\dfrac{5}{x^2} = \\dfrac{5}{x(x-2)}-\\dfrac{10}{x^2(x-2)}$. [3 marks]\n\n2. Determine the set of numbers $x$ for which the proof in part (a) is valid. [2 marks]\n$$","markScheme":"1. By working out the right side of the equation, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{5}{x(x-2)}-\\dfrac{10}{x^2(x-2)} &= \\dfrac{5x}{x^2(x-2)}-\\dfrac{10}{x^2(x-2)} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5x-10}{x^2(x-2)} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5\\ \\cancel{(x-2)}}{x^2\\ \\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{5}{x^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore $\\boxed{\\text{LHS} = \\text{RHS}}$, as required.\n\n2. We cannot have a denominator equal to zero, and therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        x^2 &\\neq 0 \\Rightarrow x \\neq 0 \\hspace{3.95em} \\text{[from L.H.S.]} \\\\[8pt]\n        x(x-2) &\\neq 0 \\Rightarrow x \\neq 0 , 2 \\hspace{3em} \\text{[from R.H.S.]} \\\\[8pt]\n        x^2(x-2) &\\neq 0 \\Rightarrow x \\neq 0 , 2 \\hspace{3em} \\text{[from R.H.S.]}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore the proof is valid for all values of $x$ except $x = 0$ and $x=2$.\\\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}, x \\neq 0 \\text{  and  } x\\neq 2$"}]},{"name":"Proof by Induction- Differentiation & Integration","questions":[{"ref":"AA087","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA087\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $y = x^2 e^x$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Prove by mathematical induction that\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^n}{\\mathrm{d}x^n}\\big(x^2e^x\\big) = \\big(n(n-1) + 2nx + x^2\\big)e^x \\hspace{1.5em} \\text{for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$, $n\\geq2$.}\n    $$\n    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the product rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big(x^2 e^x\\big) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2xe^x + x^2e^x \\hspace{1.95em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.65em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^n}{\\mathrm{d}x^n}\\big(x^2e^x\\big) = \\big(n(n-1) + 2nx + x^2\\big)e^x \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n\\geq2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using induction, we will prove that P$(n)$\n    $\\text{holds for all \\(n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+\\), \\(n\\geq2\\).}$\n\n    :u[Base case]. For $n = 2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.4em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}x^2}\\big(x^2e^x\\big) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big(x^2e^x\\big)\\right] \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\big(2xe^x + x^2e^x\\big) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2e^x + 2xe^x + 2xe^x + x^2e^x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.4em} &= \\big(2 + 4x + x^2\\big)e^x \\\\[13pt]\n    &= \\big(2(2-1) + 2(2)x + x^2\\big)e^x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\nHence P$(2)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 2$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^k}{\\mathrm{d}x^k}\\big(x^2e^x\\big) = \\big(k(k-1) + 2kx + x^2\\big)e^x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 2$.\n\n:u[Inductive Step]: To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}}\\big(x^2e^x\\big) = \\big((k+1)k + 2(k+1)x + x^2\\big)e^x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the left hand side of the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}}\\big(x^2e^x\\big) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^k}{\\mathrm{d}x^k}\\big(x^2e^x\\big)\\right] \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\big(k(k-1) + 2kx + x^2\\big)e^x \\right] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\big(2k + 2x\\big)e^x + \\big(k(k-1) + 2kx + x^2\\big)e^x \\\\[14pt]\n&= \\big((k+1)k + 2(k+1)x + x^2\\big)e^x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(k+1)$\nholds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(2)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 2$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that\nP$(n)$ holds for all $\\text{\\(n\\hspace{-0.2em}\\in\\hspace{-0.2em}\\mathbb{Z}^+\\hspace{-0.2em},\\hspace{0.05em}n\\hspace{-0.2em}\\geq\\hspace{-0.2em}2\\)}$."},{"ref":"AA088","marks":"23","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA088\n\n**Maximum mark: 23**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x-1)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}$, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$.  [2 marks]\n\n2.  Prove by induction that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\bigg(\\dfrac{3n + x - 1}{3^n}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}$\n    for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.  [7 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of any local maximum and minimum points on the\n    graph of $y = f(x)$. Justify whether such point is a maximum or a\n    minimum. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of any points of inflexion on the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$. Justify whether such point is a point of inflexion.\n    [5 marks]\n\n5.  Hence sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, indicating clearly the points\n    found in parts (c) and (d) and any intercepts with the axes.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the product rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[(x-1)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= e^{\\frac{x}{3}} + \\dfrac{1}{3}(x-1)e^{\\frac{x}{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{x+2}{3}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.7em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\bigg(\\dfrac{3n + x - 1}{3^n}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for\n    all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, from part (a), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\bigg(\\dfrac{3 + x - 1}{3}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.1em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^kf}{\\mathrm{d}x^k} = \\bigg(\\dfrac{3k + x - 1}{3^k}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.2em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} = \\bigg[\\dfrac{3(k+1) + x - 1}{3^{k+1}}\\bigg]e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we differentiate both sides of the equation (IH), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4.9em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[\\bigg(\\dfrac{3k + x - 1}{3^k}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{3^k}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg(\\dfrac{3k + x - 1}{3^k}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3^k} + \\dfrac{3k + x - 1}{3^{k+1}}\\bigg]e^{\\frac{x}{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\bigg[\\dfrac{3(k+1) + x - 1}{3^{k+1}}\\bigg]e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $f'(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bigg(\\dfrac{x + 2}{3}\\bigg)e^{\\frac{x}{3}} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x + 2 &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    x &= -2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $-2$ is the only critical number of\n    $f$.\n\n    Using the ID Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Proof%20by%20Induction-%20Differentiation%20%26%20Integration/images/41f59f91431e030c029eab143139497c30f1df01.svg\" alt=\"41f59f91431e030c029eab143139497c30f1df01.svg\">\n\n    The sign of $f'$ changes from negative to positive at $-2$.\n\n    Hence, by the First Derivative Test, $f$ has a local minimum at\n    $-2$.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = -2$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= f(-2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-2-1)e^{-\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -3e^{-\\frac{2}{3}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$\n    has a local minimum at\n     $\\mathrm{A}\\big(\\hspace{-0.2em}-2,-3e^{-\\frac{2}{3}}\\big)$.\n\n4.  If we solve the equation $f''(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bigg[\\dfrac{3(2) + x - 1}{3^2}\\bigg]e^{\\frac{x}{3}} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    3(2) + x - 1 &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    x &= -5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence there is at most one point of inflexion\n    on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    Using the Concavity Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Proof%20by%20Induction-%20Differentiation%20%26%20Integration/images/1c4ca00263d0aa013c934479ae539a797857f949.svg\" alt=\"1c4ca00263d0aa013c934479ae539a797857f949.svg\">\n\n    The graph of $y = f(x)$ changes concavity from downward to upward at\n    $-5$.\n\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has an inflexion point at $-5$.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = -5$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= f(-5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-5-1)e^{-\\frac{5}{3}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -6e^{-\\frac{5}{3}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$\n    has the inflexion point at\n     $\\mathrm{B}\\big(\\hspace{-0.2em}-5,-6e^{-\\frac{5}{3}}\\big)$.\n\n5.  We first note that the domain of the function\n    $f(x) = (x-1)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\n    Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (x-1)e^{\\frac{x}{3}} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x-1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{C}(1,0)$\n    is the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0-1)e^{0} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{D}(0,-1)$\n    is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\big[(x-1)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\big[(x-1)e^{\\frac{x}{3}}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\infty\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the $x$-axis is a horizontal\n    asymptote of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ indicating the points A, B, C &\n    D, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Proof%20by%20Induction-%20Differentiation%20%26%20Integration/images/2fc0df9fcb42d39dca43193d481d73ca1795c4bb.svg\" alt=\"2fc0df9fcb42d39dca43193d481d73ca1795c4bb.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA089","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA089\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x+1)e^{-2x}$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $f'(x)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Prove by induction that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\big[n(-2)^{n-1} + (-2)^n(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}$\n    for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.[7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the product rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[(x+1)e^{-2x}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= e^{-2x} - 2(x+1)e^{-2x} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $\\mathrm{P}(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.9em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\big[n(-2)^{n-1} + (-2)^n(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that $\\mathrm{P}(n)$\n    holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, from part (a), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that $\\mathrm{P}(k)$ holds\nfor some $k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^kf}{\\mathrm{d}x^k} = \\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\hspace{0.5em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that $\\mathrm{P}(k+1)$ holds, we\nneed to show that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.6em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} = \\big[(k+1)(-2)^k + (-2)^{(k+1)}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we differentiate both sides of the equation (IH), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.55em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\Big[\\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\\Big] \\\\[14pt]\n&= \\big[(-2)^k\\big]e^{-2x} - 2\\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\[16pt]\n&= \\big[(-2)^k\\big]e^{-2x} + \\big[k(-2)^k + (-2)^{k+1}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\[16pt]\n&= \\big[(k+1)(-2)^k + (-2)^{(k+1)}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $P(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$."},{"ref":"AA1319","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1319\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nLet $f(x)=(2x+1)e^{2x}$.\n\n1.    Show that $f'(x)=4e^{2x}(x+1)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.    Use mathematical induction to prove that $f^{(n)}(x)=2^{n+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\right)$\n    for $n\\geq 1$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. [6 marks]\n\nLet another function $g$ be such that $g(x)=\\cos{kx}$, where $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nConsider the function $h$ which is defined by $h(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{ f(x)}$.\n\nIt is known that there is no $x^2$ term in the Maclaurin series for $h(x)$.\n\n3.    Find the possible values of $k$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1. The function $f$ is a product of two functions of $x$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)&=\\textcolor{red}{(2x+1)}\\textcolor{blue}{e^{2x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We must use the product rule to differentiate it.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{If }& &\\hspace{-5em}&y&=\\textcolor{red}{u}\\textcolor{blue}{v}&\\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{8em}&\\text{then }&&\\hspace{-5em}&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&= v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}+u\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u&=2x+1&v&=e^{2x}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2&\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2e^{2x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Substituting these into the product rule formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)&=e^{2x} \\times 2 + (2x+1) \\times 2e^{2x}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and factorising the result we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=2e^{2x}(1 + 2x+1)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=2e^{2x}(2x+2)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=2e^{2x}\\times 2(x+1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=4e^{2x}(x+1)\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Let's begin with the base case. As we are told that $n$ is an integer, for which $n \\geq 1$, then the base case (smallest $n$ value) occurs when $n=1$.\n\n    We substitute $n=\\textcolor{red}{1}$ into the given formula to verify it matches the result from part (a).\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(1)}(x)&=2^{\\textcolor{red}{1}+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{1}+1}{2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^2e^{2x}\\left(x+\\frac{2}{2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=4e^{2x}(x+1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{answer from part (a)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The formula works for the base case.\n    Next we will make the assumption.\n    We will assume that when $n=k$ the formula is true.\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(k)}(x)&=2^{k+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In the next step we must prove that the formula is true when $n=k+1$ by using the assumption (when $n=k$).\n    Let's begin by writing down the formula for when $n=\\textcolor{red}{k+1}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(\\textcolor{red}{k+1})}(x)&=2^{\\textcolor{red}{k+1}+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\textcolor{red}{k+1}+1}{2}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+2}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+2}{2}\\right)\\hspace{2em}[1]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Differentiating the assumed case for $n=k$ should produce the expression on the R.H.S. of $[1]$, confirming the formula is true.\n\n    Let's factorise out the constant from the assumption and then use the product rule, in a similar way to part (a), to differentiate it\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[f^{(k)}(x)\\big]&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[2^{k+1}e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)\\bigg]\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+1}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\bigg[e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)\\bigg]\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+1}\\bigg[(e^{2x})'\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)+\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)'(e^{2x})\\bigg]\\\\[12pt]\n    &=2^{k+1}\\bigg[2e^{2x}\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)+e^{2x}\\bigg]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Don't forget, we are differentiating with respect to $x$ and we treat $k$ as a constant. Hence why $\\left(x+\\frac{k+1}{2}\\right)'=1$.\n\n    Now look at the result, $[1]$, which we are trying to prove. It looks like we should try to factorise our result as much as possible.\n\n    To best match the answer we should take out a factor of $2e^{2x}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=2^{k+1}\\times2e^{2x}\\bigg[\\left(x+\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\bigg]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next we rewrite the factor as $2^{k+1}\\times 2 = 2^{k+2}$ and simplify inside the bracket\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=2^{k+2}e^{2x}\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{k+1+1}{2}\\bigg)\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=2^{k+2}e^{2x}\\bigg(x+\\dfrac{k+2}{2}\\bigg)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=[1]\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We must finish the proof with a summary sentence.\n\n    We verified the base case, when $n=1$ and we proved that the formula works for $n=k+1$ if we assume it to be true for $n=k$\n    $\\text{Therefore, we conclude by mathematical induction, that the result is true for all }n\\text{ where }n\\text{ is a positive integer.}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3. In this question, we need to write the Maclaurin expansions for both $f$ and $g$ so that we can attempt to find their quotient. Then using the fact that the coefficient of $x^2$ is zero we can form and solve an equation to find $k$.\n\n    Let's start with $g$. For this, we can use the formula given in the formula booklet\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos{x} &= 1 - \\frac{x^2}{2!} + \\frac{x^4}{4!} - \\dots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall that $g(x) = \\cos{kx}$. We can rewrite the formula using the substitution $kx$ as $x$, giving\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(x) &= \\cos{kx} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\frac{(kx)^2}{2!} + \\frac{(kx)^4}{4!} - \\dots \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now onto $f$. Let's recall the general formula for the Maclaurin series\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= f(0) + xf'(0) + \\frac{x^2}{2!}f''(0) + \\dots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To write the expansion for $f$, we need to evaluate $f(0)$, $f'(0)$, $f''(0)$ and $f'''(0)$.\n    We can use the formula we proved in part (b), choosing $n = 1$ (which we already have), $n = 2$, and $n = 3$.\n    First, evaluate the function $f$ at $n=0$ and $x = 0$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(0) &= (2(0) + 1)e^{2(0)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next, for $n = 1$ and $x = 0$, we use part (a) to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(1)}(0) &= 4e^{2(0)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Then, for $n = \\textcolor{red}{2}$ and $x = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(2)}(0) &= 2^{\\textcolor{red}{2}+1}e^{2(0)}\\left(0 + \\frac{\\textcolor{red}{2}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8 \\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, for $n = \\textcolor{blue}{3}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f^{(3)}(0) &= 2^{\\textcolor{blue}{3}+1}e^{2(0)}\\left(0 + \\frac{\\textcolor{blue}{3}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 16 \\times 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 32\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note, we are only looking for terms up to and including $x^2$, so you can find the answer without completing the $n=3$ step.\n    Now we can put these results together to get the expansion for $f$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= (2x + 1)e^{2x} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f(0) + xf'(0) + \\frac{x^2}{2!}f''(0) + \\frac{x^3}{3!}f'''(0) + \\dots \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 + 4x + \\frac{12x^2}{2!} + \\frac{32x^3}{3!} + \\dots \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next we form $h(x)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h(x) &= \\frac{g(x)}{f(x)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\frac{\\left(1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots \\right)}{\\left(1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots \\right)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To find the result when dividing two infinite series in this way it is often better practice to rewrite the expression as a multiplication.\n\n    To do this, let's assume the result is a new infinite series with coefficients of $a_n$, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\left(1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots \\right)}{\\left(1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots \\right)}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now rewrite the quotient as a multiplication\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &\\left(1 - \\frac{k^2x^2}{2} + \\frac{k^4x^4}{24} - \\dots \\right)=\\left(1 + 4x + 6x^2 + \\frac{16}{3}x^3 + \\dots \\right)(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...) \\hspace{2em}[1]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next, as we need to find the $x^2$ term, we will write the terms up to $x^2$ for the R.H.S. of [1].\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    =&&+a_0&&+4a_0x&&+6a_0x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    &&&&+a_1x&&+4a_1x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    &&&&&&+a_2x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note, by writing the terms in columns like this it makes it easier to combine the coefficients for each $x$ term.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=a_0+(4a_0+a_1)x+(6a_0+4a_1+a_2)x^2+...\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now compare the coefficients of the series above with the L.H.S. of [1]\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &&\\textcolor{red}{a_0}&&\\textcolor{blue}{+(4a_0+a_1)}x&&\\textcolor{green}{+(6a_0+4a_1+a_2)}x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n    &&\\textcolor{red}{1}&&\\textcolor{blue}{0}x&&\\textcolor{green}{-\\frac{k^2}{2}}x^2+...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and form the following equations\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        a_0&=1&&&&4a_0+a_1=0&&&&6a_0+4a_1+a_2=-\\frac{k^2}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using $a_0=1$ with the second equation we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4(1)+a_1&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    a_1&=-4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, using those values for $a_0$ and $a_1$, we can find $a_2$ in terms of $k$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    6(1)+4(-4)+a_2&=-\\frac{k^2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    6-16+a_2&=-\\frac{k^2}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    a_2&=10-\\frac{k^2}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In the question we are told that the coefficient of the expansion of $h$ is zero. Therefore $a_2=0$. Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0&=10-\\frac{k^2}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{k^2}{2}&=10\\\\[12pt]\n    k&=\\pm\\sqrt{20}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\pm2\\sqrt{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA901","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA901\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x}}$,$x < 1$.\n\n1.  Show that $f''(x) = \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{\\frac{-5}{2}}$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Use mathematical induction to prove that[9 marks]\n    $$\n    f^{(n)}(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^n \\dfrac{(2n)!}{n!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-n} \\quad n\\in \\mathbb{Z},\\enskip n\\geq 2.\n    $$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\cos (mx)$, $m\\in \\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nConsider the function $h$ defined by $h(x)=f(x) \\times g(x)$ for $x<1$.\n\nThe $x^2$ term in the Maclaurin series for $h(x)$ has a coefficient of\n$-\\dfrac{3}{4}$.\n\n3.  Find the possible values of $m$.[9 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Taking the derivative with respect to $x$ twice, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f'(x)\n    %&= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left(1-x\\right)^{\\frac{-1}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n    &=  \\dfrac{1}{2} (1-x)^{\\frac{-3}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n    f''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\dfrac{1}{2} (1-x)^{\\frac{-3}{2}}\\right] \\\\[11pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{\\frac{-5}{2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $\\mathrm{P}(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^n \\dfrac{(2n)!}{n!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-n} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$,} \\enskip n\\geq 2]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that $\\mathrm{P}(n)$\n    holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $n\\geq2$.\n\n    :u[Base case]. For $n = 2$, from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2f}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{\\frac{-5}{2}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence P$(2)$ holds.\n\n    :u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that $\\mathrm{P}(k)$\n    holds for some $k \\geq 2$. In other words, we assume that\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^kf}{\\mathrm{d}x^k} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^k \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k} \\hspace{0.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    for some $k \\geq 2$.\n\n    :u[Inductive step]. To show that $\\mathrm{P}(k+1)$ holds,\n    we need to show that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k+2)!}{(k+1)!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we differentiate both sides of the equation (IH), we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.55em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left[\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^k \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} (1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k}\\right] \\\\[14pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^k \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} \\left(k+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1} \\\\[16pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} \\left(4k+2\\right)(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1} \\\\[16pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k)!}{k!} \\left(\\dfrac{(2k+2)(2k+1)}{k+1}\\right)(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1} \\\\[16pt]\n    &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{k+1} \\dfrac{(2k+2)!}{(k+1)!}(1-x)^{\\frac{-1}{2}-k-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence $P(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n    :u[Conclusion]. P$(2)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 2$, if\n    P$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\n    Hence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for\n    all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $n\\geq2$.\n\n3.  First, we obtain the second derivative of $h$, using the product\n    rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\big[f(x)\\times g(x)\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x) \\\\[10pt]\n    h''(x) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\big[f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x)\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= f''(x)g(x)+2f'(x)g'(x)+f(x)g''(x) \\\\[13pt]\n    h''(0) &= f''(0)g(0)+2f'(0)g'(0)+f(0)g''(0)  \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    From part (a), we have the following evaluations at $x=0$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    f(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x}} & f(0) &= 1 \\\\[10pt]\n    f'(x) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(1-x)^{-3/2} & f'(0) &=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    f''(x) &= \\dfrac{3}{4} (1-x)^{-5/2} & f''(0) &= \\dfrac{3}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and the respective evaluations for $g(x)$ and its derivatives are\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    g(x) &= \\cos(mx) & g(0) &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    g'(x) &= -m\\sin(mx) & g'(0) &=0 \\\\[12pt]\n    g''(x) &= -m^2\\cos(mx) & g''(0) &= -m^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, substituting the respective values into equation (1), we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h''(0) &= \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)(1) + 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)(0)+(1)(-m^2)\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3}{4}-m^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Finally, using the formula\n    for the $x^2$ term in the Maclaurin series of $h(x)$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{h''(0)}{2} &= -\\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{4}-m^2 &= -\\dfrac{6}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    m^2 &= \\dfrac{9}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-3mm}m = \\pm \\dfrac{3}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Proof by Induction- Series, Divisibility, and Inequalities","questions":[{"ref":"AA085","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA085\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $r \\in \\mathbb{R}, r\\neq 1$. Use the method of mathematical\ninduction to prove that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8.3em} 1+r+r^2+\\cdots+r^n=\\frac{1-r^{n+1}}{1-r} \\hspace{2em} \\text{for all } n\\in \\mathbb{Z}^+. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{7.2em} 1+r+r^2+\\cdots+r^n=\\frac{1-r^{n+1}}{1-r} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1-r^{1+1}}{1-r}  &= \\frac{1-r^2}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\frac{(1+r)\\cancel{(1-r)}}{\\cancel{1-r}} \\\\[13pt]\n&= 1+ r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1+r+r^2+\\ldots+r^k=\\frac{1-r^{k+1}}{1-r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfor\nsome $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]: To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1+r+r^2+\\ldots + r^k +r^{k+1} = \\frac{1-r^{(k+1)+1}}{1-r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the left hand side of the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1+r+r^2+\\ldots + r^k +r^{k+1} &= (1+r+r^2+\\ldots + r^k) + r^{k+1} \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\frac{1-r^{k+1}}{1-r} + r^{k+1} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\frac{1-r^{k+1}}{1-r} + \\frac{r^{k+1}(1-r)}{1-r} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\frac{1-\\cancel{r^{k+1}} + \\cancel{r^{k+1}} - r^{k+2}}{1-r}\\\\[6pt]\n&= \\frac{1 - r^{(k+1)+1}}{1-r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as\nwell.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$."},{"ref":"AA086","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA086\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe Fibonacci sequence is defined as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_0 &= 0,\\hspace{0.25em} a_1 = 1,\\hspace{0.25em} a_2 = 1, \\\\[6pt]\na_n &= a_{n-1}+a_{n-2} \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{for}\\hspace{0.5em} n \\geq 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProve by mathematical induction that\n$a_1^2+a_2^2+\\cdots+a_n^2=a_na_{n+1}$, where $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^+$.","markScheme":"Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8.3em} a_1^2+a_2^2+...+a_n^2=a_na_{n+1} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_1^2 &= 1^2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1\\cdot 1 \\\\[6pt]\n&= a_1a_2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_1^2+a_2^2+...+a_k^2 = a_ka_{k+1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfor some\n$k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_1^2+a_2^2+...+a_k^2 + a_{k+1}^2= a_{k+1}a_{k+2} \\hspace{2.2em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the left hand side of the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_1^2+a_2^2+...+a_k^2 + a_{k+1}^2 &= \\big(a_1^2+a_2^2+...+a_k^2\\big) + a_{k+1}^2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= a_ka_{k+1} + a_{k+1}^2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[7pt]\n&= \\big(a_k + a_{k+1}\\big)a_{k+1} \\\\[7pt]\n&= a_{k+2}a_{k+1} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (FS)}] \\\\[7pt]\n&= a_{k+1}a_{k+2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$."},{"ref":"AA090","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA090\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\n1.  Show that\n    $\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{n+1}} < \\sqrt{n+1} - \\sqrt{n}$,\n    where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},\\hspace{0.1em} n\\geq 0$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence show that $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} < 2\\sqrt{2} - 2$.\n     [2 marks]\n\n3.  Prove by mathematical induction that\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4em} \\sum_{r = 2}^n \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r}} < 2\\sqrt{n} - 2 \\hspace{2em} \\text{for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$, $n \\geq 2$.}\n\n    $$\n    [9 marks]","markScheme":"1.  For any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},\\hspace{0.1em} n \\geq 0$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\sqrt{n+1} - \\sqrt{n} &= \\left[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{n+1} - \\sqrt{n}}{1}\\right] \\left[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{n+1} + \\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n+1} + \\sqrt{n}}\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(\\cancel{n}+1) - \\cancel{n}}{\\sqrt{n+1} + \\sqrt{n}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1} + \\sqrt{n}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.8em} \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{n+1}} < \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1} + \\sqrt{n}} \\hspace{2em} \\big[\\text{since $\\sqrt{n+1} > \\sqrt{n}$}\\hspace{0.1em}\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{n+1}} < \\sqrt{n+1} - \\sqrt{n} \\hspace{3.8em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Hence, substituting $n = 1$ in the inequality $(1)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} &< \\sqrt{2} - 1 \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} < 2\\sqrt{2} - 2\n    $$\n\n3.  Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} \\sum_{r = 2}^n \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r}} < 2\\sqrt{n} - 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n\\geq2$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using math induction, we will prove that P$(n)$\n    $\\text{holds for all \\(n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+\\), \\(n\\geq2\\).}$\n\n    :u[Base case]. For $n = 2$, from part (b), we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} < 2\\sqrt{2} - 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence P$(2)$\n    holds.\n\n    :u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for\n    some $k \\geq 2$. In other words, we assume that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{r = 2}^k \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r}} < 2\\sqrt{k} - 2 \\hspace{1.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    for some $k \\geq 2$.\n\n    :u[Inductive step]. To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need\n    to show that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} \\sum_{r = 2}^{k+1} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r}} < 2\\sqrt{k+1} - 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\nFrom the left hand side of the inequality $(2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8em} \\sum_{r = 2}^{k+1} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r}} &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k+1}} + \\sum_{r = 2}^k \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{r}} \\\\[6pt]\n&< \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k+1}} + \\big(2\\sqrt{k} - 2\\big) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[12pt]\n&< \\big(2\\sqrt{k+1} - \\cancel{2\\sqrt{k}}\\big) + \\big(\\cancel{2\\sqrt{k}} - 2\\big) \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[18pt]\n&= 2\\sqrt{k+1} -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(2)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 2$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that\nP$(n)$ holds for all $\\text{\\(n\\hspace{-0.2em}\\in\\hspace{-0.2em}\\mathbb{Z}^+\\hspace{-0.2em},\\hspace{0.05em}n\\hspace{-0.2em}\\geq\\hspace{-0.2em}2\\)}$."},{"ref":"AA091","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA091\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUsing mathematical induction, prove that\n$1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + n^2 = \\dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}$ for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.","markScheme":"Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{8.3em} 1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + n^2 = \\dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{(1)(1+1)(2(1)+1)}{6} &= \\dfrac{(1)(2)(3)}{6} \\hspace{4.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 1^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + k^2 = \\dfrac{k(k+1)(2k+1)}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + k^2 + (k+1)^2 = \\dfrac{(k+1)(k+2)(2k+3)}{6} \\hspace{2.2em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the left hand side of the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} 1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + k^2 + (k+1)^2 &= \\big[1^2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + k^2\\big] + (k+1)^2 \\\\[11pt]\n&= \\dfrac{k(k+1)(2k+1)}{6} + (k+1)^2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[7pt]\n&= \\dfrac{k(k+1)(2k+1) + 6(k+1)^2}{6} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{(k+1)\\big[k(2k+1) + 6(k+1)\\big]}{6} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{(k+1)\\big[2k^2 + 7k + 6\\big]}{6} \\\\[7pt]\n&= \\dfrac{(k+1)(k+2)(2k+3)}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as\nwell.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$."},{"ref":"AA092","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA092\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUsing the principle of mathematical induction, prove that $n(n^2+5)$ is\ndivisible by $6$ for all integers $n \\geq 1$.","markScheme":"Notation: For $m, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $m \\mid n$ means $m$ divides $n$ or\n$n$ is divisible by $m$.\n\nLet P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.1em} 6 \\mid n(n^2+5) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\geq 1$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\mid (1)(1^2+5) = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\mid k(k^2+5) \\hspace{2.5em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfor some\n$k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.55em} 6 \\mid (k+1)\\big[(k+1)^2+5\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we rewrite the expression on the right hand side of the statement\n$(1)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(k+1)\\big[(k+1)^2+5\\big] &= (k+1)\\big[(k^2+5)+(2k+1)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n&= k(k^2+5) + (k^2+5) + (k+1)(2k+1) \\\\[6pt]\n&= k(k^2+5) + (3k^2 + 3k + 6) \\\\[6pt]\n&= k(k^2+5) + 3\\big[k(k+1) + 2\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince either $k$ or $k+1$ is even, $k(k+1)$ is divisible by $2$.\n\nHence we deduce\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6em} 2 &\\mid k(k+1) + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n6 &\\mid 3\\big[k(k+1) + 2\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n6 &\\mid k(k^2+5) + 3\\big[k(k+1) + 2\\big] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[6pt]\n6 &\\mid (k+1)\\big[(k+1)^2+5\\big] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by $(2)$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$."},{"ref":"AA1038","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1038\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUse mathematical induction to prove that $n^3-9n^2+26n-24$ is divisible by $6$ for all $n\\geq 5$.","markScheme":"Let $P(n)$ be the proposition that $n^3-9n^2+26n-24$ is divisible by $6$.\n\nThe first step is to verify the base case ($n=5$).\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(5)&=(5)^3-9(5)^2+26(5)-24\\\\\n    &=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$P(5)$ is divisible by $6$ therefore it is true.\n$$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n$$\n$\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\nNow we will form the assumption.\n\nAssume $P(k)$ is true.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(k)&=k^3-9k^2+26k-24\\\\\n    &=6A  \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhere $k, A\\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$ and $k\\geq5$.\n\nNow we will consider the $(k+1)^{\\text{th}}$ case and by using the assumption we will show that $P(k+1)$ is divisible by $6$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(k+1)&=(k+1)^3-9(k+1)^2+26(k+1)-24\\\\\n    &=\\bm{k^3}+3k^2+3k+1-\\bm{9k^2}-18k-9+\\bm{26k}+26-\\bm{24} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe terms in bold above are equivalent to the assumption, $P(k)$. Hence we can replace them with $6A$ and then simplify. This is known as the **inductive** step as we are inducting the assumption into the proof.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(k+1)&=3k^2+3k+1-18k-9+26+6A \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n    &=6A+3k^2-15k+18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe now need to show the remaining terms involving $k$ are divisible by $6$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(k+1)&=6A+3(k-2)(k-3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nAfter factorising we can see that the expression $(k-2)(k-3)$ is the product of two consecutive numbers (recall $k\\geq5$). This will always be an even number, hence we can replace it with $2B$, Where $B \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(k+1)&=6A+3\\times 2B\\\\\n    &=6A + 6B\\\\\n    &=6(A+B)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$P(k+1)$ is divisible by $6$.\n$$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n$$\n$\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\nWe must write a concluding sentence.\n\n$$\n\\text{$P(5)$ is true and $P(k)$ being true implies $P(k+1)$ is true. We can therefore say, by the principle of mathematical induction, that $P(n)$ is true for all $n\\geq 5$.}\n$$\n$$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n$$\n$\\textcolor{white}{gap}$"},{"ref":"AA114","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA114\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nUse the principle of mathematical induction to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{4em} 1\\cdot2^1 + 2\\cdot2^2 + 3\\cdot2^3 + \\cdots + n\\cdot2^n &= 2 + (n-1)2^{n+1} \\hspace{2em} \\text{for all } n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$","markScheme":"Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.3em} 1\\cdot2^1 + 2\\cdot2^2 + 3\\cdot2^3 + \\cdots + n\\cdot2^n =  2 + (n-1)2^{n+1} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1\\cdot2^1 &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2 + (1-1)2^{1+1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1\\cdot2^1 + 2\\cdot2^2 + 3\\cdot2^3 + \\cdots + k\\cdot2^k &=  2 + (k-1)2^{k+1} \\hspace{1.8em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1\\cdot2^1 + 2\\cdot2^2 + 3\\cdot2^3 + \\cdots + k\\cdot2^k + (k+1)2^{k+1} &=  2 + k\\cdot2^{k+2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the left hand side of the equation $(1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\hspace{1.25em}1\\cdot2^1 + 2\\cdot2^2 + 3\\cdot2^3 + \\cdots + k\\cdot2^k + (k+1)2^{k+1} = \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\big[1\\cdot2^1 + 2\\cdot2^2 + 3\\cdot2^3 + \\cdots + k\\cdot2^k\\big] + (k+1)2^{k+1} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\big[2 + (k-1)2^{k+1}\\big] +(k+1)2^{k+1} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (IH)}]  \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2 + (k-\\cancel{1} + k+\\cancel{1})2^{k+1} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2 + (2k)2^{k+1} \\\\[6pt]\n&= 2 + k\\cdot2^{k+2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$."},{"ref":"AA1281","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1281\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Use mathematical induction to prove that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^n\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{n-1}x}&=\\frac{\\sin{2^nx}}{\\sin{x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  1.  Find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin series for $\\sin{8x}$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin series for $\\dfrac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}$.\n\n    3.  Hence find an estimate for $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\cos{x}\\cos{2x}\\cos{4x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.[12 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We start by showing the base case is true.\n\n    This is when $n=1$.\n\n    Let's first consider the value of the L.H.S. of the proposition\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^1\\times \\cos{2^{1-1}x}&=\\textcolor{red}{2\\cos{x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    And the value of the R.H.S. is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\sin{2^1x}}{\\sin{x}}&=\\frac{2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}}{\\sin{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\textcolor{red}{2\\cos{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{L.H.S.}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we have shown the proposition is true when $n=1$.\n\n    The next step is to assume the proposition is true when $n=k$ where $k \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^k\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{k-1}x}&=\\frac{\\sin{2^kx}}{\\sin{x}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next we need to use the assumption to show that the proposition is true  when $n=k+1$.\n\n    Hence we need to prove\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2^{k+1}\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{k}x}&=\\frac{\\sin{2^{k+1}x}}{\\sin{x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let's start by rewriting the L.H.S. so it looks similar to the L.H.S. of the assumption\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{L.H.S.}&=2\\,\\textcolor{blue}{\\times2^{k}\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} \\times ... \\times \\cos{2^{k-1}x}}\\times\\cos{2^{k}x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Notice the blue part is now the same as the L.H.S. of the assumption, hence we can replace it with the R.H.S. of the assumption\n    $$4\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=2\\left(\\textcolor{blue}{\\frac{\\sin2^kx}{\\sin{x}}}\\right)\\times\\cos{2^{k}x}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    This is known as the _inductive_ step.\n\n    We can rearrange the trigonometric identity $\\sin{2\\theta}=2\\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}$ to\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}&=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin{2\\theta}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and use it to rewrite our L.H.S. giving\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=2 \\times \\frac{\\sin2^kx}{\\sin{x}}\\times\\cos{2^{k}x}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=2\\times \\frac{\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sin{(2\\times2^kx})}{\\sin{x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\frac{\\sin{(2\\times2^kx})}{\\sin{x}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{\\sin{2^{k+1}x}}{\\sin{x}}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{...as required.}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\text{R.H.S.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We have shown the proposition is true when $n=1$ and we have shown it to be true when $n=k+1$, assuming $n=k$ is true. Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction the proposition is true for all positive integer values of $n$.\n\n2.  1.  We begin by noting that $\\sin{8x}$ is a composite function.\n\n        Hence we can use the formula for the Macluarin series for sine but replace $x$ with $8x$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\sin{x}&=x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}+\\frac{x^5}{5!}-...\\\\[6pt]\n            \\sin{8x}&=8x-\\frac{(8x)^3}{3!}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We only need the first two non-zero terms hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=8x-\\frac{512x^3}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=8x-\\dfrac{256}{3}x^3\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Let's begin by using a general power series to represent the result of the quotient\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Where $a_i$ are real numbers we must determine.\n\n        We can use the result from part (a) and the general formula for sine to write the numerator and denominator as a Maclaurin series up to the $x^3$ term\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{8x-\\frac{256}{3}x^3}{x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Recall, we only need the first two non-zero terms which will mean up to and including the $x^3$ term.\n\n        It can be challenging to attempt to use long division to rewrite the L.H.S. Often a better strategy, when facing problems like this, is to rewrite the division as a multiplication\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                8x-\\frac{256}{3}x^3&=\\left(x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}\\right)\\left(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We can now expand the R.H.S. until we have all of the terms required to give the $x$ and $x^3$ terms.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                8x-\\frac{256}{3}x^3&=a_0x-\\frac{a_0x^3}{6}+\\cancel{a_1x^2}-\\cancel{\\frac{a_1x^4}{6}}+a_2x^3-\\cancel{\\frac{a_2x^5}{6}}\\\\[6pt]\n                &=a_0x-\\frac{a_0x^3}{6}++a_2x^3\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        There is no $x^2$ term therefore $a_1$ must be zero. Also we don't need any power of $x$ higher than $3$. So we can cancel out another two terms.\n\n        We can now equate the coefficients of the $x-$terms on the left and right side giving\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            8&=a_0\\hspace{6em}\\text{for $x$}\\\\[6pt]\n            -\\frac{256}{3}&=-\\frac{a_0}{6}+a_2\\hspace{3em}\\text{for $x^3$}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can see that $a_0=8$ hence we can use this to find $a_2$\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            -\\frac{256}{3}&=-\\frac{8}{6}+a_2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-\\frac{8}{6}+\\frac{6a_2}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n            -512&=-8+6a_2\\\\[6pt]\n            6a_2&=-504\\\\[6pt]\n            a_2&=-84\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore, referring back to the series expansion at the start of the solution, we can see that the first two non-zero terms are\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}&=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=8-84x^2\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  The integral given is a very difficult integral to perform directly.\n\n        However, notice that it is of the form of the proposition from part (a).\n\n        If we take the proposition and let $n=3$ we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            2^3\\times \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x} &=\\frac{\\sin{2^3x}}{\\sin{x}}\\\\[6pt]\n            \\cos{x} \\times \\cos{2x} \\times \\cos{4x}&=\\frac{\\sin{8x}}{8\\sin{x}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence we can rewrite the integral as\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\cos{x}\\cos{2x}\\cos{4x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{0.1}\\frac{\\sin{8x}}{8\\sin{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\tfrac{1}{8}\\int_{0}^{0.1}\\frac{\\sin{8x}}{\\sin{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        The result is still a difficult integral to perform but the key word in the question is _estimate_. This suggests we should use the series expansion from part (b)(ii).\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\cos{x}\\cos{2x}\\cos{4x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&\\approx \\tfrac{1}{8} \\int_{0}^{0.1}\\left(8-84x^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Integrating each term we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\tfrac{1}{8}\\bigg[8x-28x^3\\bigg]_{0}^{0.1}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Substituting in the limits we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\tfrac{1}{8}\\left(8(0.1)-28(0.1)^3\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.1-3.5\\times0.001\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.1-0.0035\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.0965\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA664","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA664\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1.  Solve the inequality $x^2 \\geq 2x + 3$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Use mathematical induction to prove that $2^n > n^2 - 2$ for all\n    $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$, $n \\geq 3$.[6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graphs of $y = x^2$ and $y = 2x + 3$ \\[by using\n    G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Proof%20by%20Induction-%20Series%2C%20Divisibility%2C%20and%20Inequalities/images/8559be520206e999f46f83d313584ee1a94282b7.svg\" alt=\"8559be520206e999f46f83d313584ee1a94282b7.svg\">\n\n    Using the $x$-coordinates of A and B, we see that the graph of\n    $y = x^2$ is above the graph of $y = 2x+3$ when $x < -1$ and\n    $x > 3$, and the graphs intersect at $x = -1,3$.\n\n    Hence the solution set to the inequality $x^2 \\geq 2x+3$ is\n     $(-\\infty,-1] \\cup [3, \\infty)$\n\n2.  Let P$(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.2em} 2^n &> n^2 - 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n\\geq3$}] \\hspace{1.9em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\nUsing mathematical induction, we will prove that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$, $n \\geq 3$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 3$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^3 = 8 &> 3^2 - 2 = 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(3)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that P$(k)$ holds for some\n$k \\geq 3$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^k &> k^2 - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfor some $k \\geq 3$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]: To show that P$(k+1)$ holds, we need to\nshow that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5em} 2^{k+1} &> (k+1)^2 - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom the\nleft hand side of the inequality $(1)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.8em} 2^{k+1} &= 2\\cdot2^k \\\\[6pt]\n&>2\\hspace{0.05em}[k^2 - 2] \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (IH)}] \\\\[6pt]\n&= k^2 + [k^2 - 4] \\\\[6pt]\n[\\text{by part (a)}] \\hspace{3em} &\\geq 2k+3 + [k^2 - 4] \\\\[6pt]\n&= [k^2 + 2k + 1] - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n&= (k+1)^2 - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence P$(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(3)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 3$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that\nP$(n)$ holds for all $\\text{\\(n\\hspace{-0.2em}\\in\\hspace{-0.2em}\\mathbb{Z}^+\\hspace{-0.2em},\\hspace{0.05em}n\\hspace{-0.2em}\\geq\\hspace{-0.2em}3\\)}$."},{"ref":"AA903","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA903\n\n**Maximum mark: 17**\n\nThe following diagram shows the graph of\n$y=\\arctan(2x-3)+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ for $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$,\\\nwith asymptotes at $y=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $y=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Proof%20by%20Induction-%20Series%2C%20Divisibility%2C%20and%20Inequalities/images/081334c23d135e25efc729fff5cf10e0f4fe7363.svg\" alt=\"081334c23d135e25efc729fff5cf10e0f4fe7363.svg\">\n\n1.  Describe a sequence of transformations that transforms the graph of\\\n    $y=\\arctan x$ to the graph of $y=\\arctan(2x-3)+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ for\n    $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Show that\n    $\\arctan p - \\arctan q \\equiv \\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{p-q}{1+pq}\\right)$.[3 marks]\n\n3.  Verify that\n    $\\arctan(x+2)-\\arctan(x+1) = \\arctan\\left( \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2+(x+1)+1}\\right)$.[3 marks]\n\n4.  Using mathematical induction and the results from **part (b)** and\n    **(c)**, prove that[8 marks]\n\n    $$\n    \\sum_{r=1}^n \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{r^2+r+1}\\right) = \\arctan(n+1)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{0.8em} \\text{for } n\\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}.\n    $$","markScheme":"1.  The function can be expressed as\n    $y=\\arctan\\left(2\\left(x-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$,\n    hence we get the following transformations in order\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{transformation $1$}&: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{horizontal stretch by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{transformation $2$}&:\n    \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{horizontal translation $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ units right}\\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{transformation $3$}&: \\hspace{0.5em}\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{vertical translation $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ units up}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Note that the vertical shift, which is transformation $3$, can also be performed before the horizontal transformations.\n\n2.  Using the compound angle identity, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tan\\left(\\arctan p - \\arctan q \\right) &= \\dfrac{\\tan(\\arctan p)-\\tan(\\arctan q)}{1+\\tan(\\arctan p)\\tan(\\arctan q)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{p-q}{1+pq} \\\\[12pt]\\\\\n    \\intertext{Finally, taking the $\\arctan$ on both sides}\\\\\n    &\\hspace{-2.85cm}\\arctan p - \\arctan q \\equiv \\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{p-q}{1+pq}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the formula from part (b), we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arctan(x+2)-\\arctan(x+1) &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{(x+2)-(x+1)}{1+(x+2)(x+1)}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{((x+1)+1)(x+1)+1}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.2em}=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2+(x+1)+1}\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $\\mathrm{P}(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{r=1}^n \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{r^2+r+1}\\right) &= \\arctan(n+1)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{3em} \\big[\\,\\text{for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}$}\\,\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that $\\mathrm{P}(n)$\n    holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}$.\n\n    :u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{(1)^2+1+1}\\right) &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\arctan(2)-\\arctan(1) \\hspace{3em} \\big[\\text{by part (b,c)}\\big]\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\arctan(2)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n    :u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that $\\mathrm{P}(k)$\n    holds for some $k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{r=1}^k \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{r^2+r+1}\\right) = \\arctan(k+1)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{0.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    for some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n    :u[Inductive step]. To show that $\\mathrm{P}(k+1)$ holds,\n    we need to show that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{r=1}^{k+1} \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{r^2+r+1}\\right) = \\arctan(k+2)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we add the $k+1$ term to both sides of the equation (IH), we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{r=1}^{k+1} \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{r^2+r+1}\\right) &= \\arctan(k+1)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{1}{(k+1)^2+(k+1)+1}\\right)\\\\[10pt]\n    \\big[\\text{by part (c)}\\big]\\hspace{3em} &= \\arctan(k+2) - \\arctan(k+1) + \\arctan(k+1)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\arctan(k+2)-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $P(k+1)$ holds\n    as well.\n\n    :u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\n    P$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\n    Hence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for\n    all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}$."}]},{"name":"Sequences & Series","questions":[{"ref":"AA001","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA001\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider an arithmetic sequence $2,6,10,14,\\dots$\n\n1.  Find the common difference, $d$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $10$th term in the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the sum of the first $10$ terms in the sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 2$ and $u_2 = 6$.\n    Hence the common difference is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6 - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 10$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} u_{10} &= u_1 + (10-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2 + (10-1)(4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 38\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=10$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{10} &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(u_1 + u_{10}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2 + 38) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 200\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA002","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA002\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAn arithmetic sequence has $u_1= 40$, $u_2 = 32$, $u_3 = 24$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference, $d$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_8$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $S_8$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common difference of an arithmetic sequence is found by subtracting any term in the sequence from the next term in the sequence. We could subtract $u_1$ from $u_2$, or subtract $u_2$ from $u_3$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 32 - 40 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    d&=-8\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 8$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} u_8 &= u_1 + (8-1)d   \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 40 + (8-1)(-8) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    u_8&=-16\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=8$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} S_8 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(u_1 + u_8) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(40 + (-16)) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[11pt]\n    S_8 &=96 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA003","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA003\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the infinite geometric sequence $4480$, $-3360$, $2520$,\n$-1890,\\dots$\n\n1.  Find the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $20$th term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the **exact** sum of the infinite sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 4480$ and $u_2 = -3360$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{3360}{4480} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -0.75\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 r^{n-1}$ with $n = 20$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} u_{20} &= u_1 r^{20-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (4480)(-0.75)^{20-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx -18.9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.3em} S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{4480}{1-(-0.75)} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2560\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA004","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA004\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nAn arithmetic sequence is given by $3$, $5$, $7,\\dots$\n\n1.  Write down the value of the common difference, $d$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find\n    1.  $u_{10}$;\n\n    2.  $S_{10}$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $u_n = 253$, find the value of $n$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 3$ and $u_2 = 5$.\n    Hence the value of $d$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5 - 3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 10$,\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.1em} u_{10} &= u_1 + (10-1)d  \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3 + (10-1)(2) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 21\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n        $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n=10$, we find\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         S_{10} &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(u_1 + u_{10}) \\\\[6pt]\n         &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(3 + 21) \\\\[11pt]\n         &= 120\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n3.  Substituting $u_n = 253$, $u_1 = 3$ and $d = 2$ in\n    $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ and solving the resulting equation for $n$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} 253 &= 3 + (n-1)(2) \\\\[6pt]\n    253 &= 2n+1 \\\\[6pt]\n    252 &= 2n\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    n = 126\\hspace{1.1em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA005","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA005\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, $u_5 = 24$, $u_{13} = 80$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the first term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the sum of the first $20$ terms in the sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n = 13$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{13} &= u_5 + (13-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    80 &= 24 + (13-5)d \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    56 &= 8d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 7 \\hspace{6.35em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_5 + (1-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 + (1-5)(7) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n    $n=20$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} S_{20} &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(2u_1 + (20-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(2(-4) + (20-1)(7)) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 1250\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA006","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA006\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $u_1 = 32$,\n$u_2 = -16$, $u_3 = 8$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_6$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $S_{\\infty}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The value of $r$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{16}{32} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{3.6em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 r^{n-1}$ with $n = 6$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_6 &= u_1 r^{6-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 32\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.3em} -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)^{6-1} \\\\[4pt]\n    &= 32\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{1}{32}\\bigg) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.405em} S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{32}{1-(-1/2)} \\hspace{5.45em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{32}{3/2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{64}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA007","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA007\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, $u_4 = 12$, $u_{11} = -9$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the first term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the sum of the first $11$ terms in the sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_4 + (n-4)d$ with $n = 11$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{11} &= u_4 + (11-4)d \\\\[6pt]\n    -9 &= 12 + (11-4)d \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    -21 &= 7d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = -3 \\hspace{5.5em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_4 + (n-4)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_4 + (1-4)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12 + (1-4)(-3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 21\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=11$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{11} &= \\dfrac{11}{2}(u_1 + u_{11}) \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11}{2}(21 + (-9)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11}{2}(12) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 66\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA008","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA008\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the following sequence of figures.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA008.PNG\" alt=\"AA008\">\n\nFigure 1 contains $6$ line segments.\n\n1.  Given that Figure $n$ contains $101$ line segments, show that\n    $n = 20$.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total number of line segments in the first $20$ figures.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have an arithmetic sequence with $u_1 = 6$, $u_2 = 11$ and\n    $u_3 = 16$.\n\n    The common difference is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= u_2 - u_1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 11 - 6 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, if we substitute $u_n = 101$, $u_1 = 6$\n    and $d = 5$ in $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ and solve the resulting equation\n    for $n$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.9em} 101 &= 6 + (n-1)(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    101 &= 5n+1 \\\\[6pt]\n    100 &= 5n\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    n = 20\\hspace{2.3em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$\n    with $n=20$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} S_{20} &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(u_1 + u_{20}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{20}{2}(6 + 101) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 10(107) \\\\[16pt]\n    &= 1070\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA009","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA009\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, the third term is $41$ and the ninth term is\n$23$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the first term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the smallest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 0$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_3 = 41$ and $u_9 = 23$.\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with\n    $n = 9$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_9 &= u_3 + (9-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n    23 &= 41 + (9-3)d \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    -18 &= 6d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = -3 \\hspace{5em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} u_1 &= u_3 + (1-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 41 + (1-3)(-3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 47\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &< 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d) &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{0.7em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(47) + (n-1)(-3)) &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{n}{2}(97-3n) &< 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    32.333 &< n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the smallest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 0$ is\n     $n=33$"},{"ref":"AA010","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA010\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $u_1 = 0.8$,\n$u_2 = 2.4$, $u_3 = 7.2$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $S_8$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the least value of $n$ such that $S_n > 35\\hspace{0.15em}000$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The value of $r$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2.4}{0.8} \\\\[13pt]\n    &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$\n    with $n=8$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} S_8 &= \\dfrac{u_1(r^8 - 1)}{r-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.8(3^8 - 1)}{3-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2624\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1} &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{8.1em} \\dfrac{0.8(3^n-1)}{3-1} &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[8pt]\n    0.4(3^n-1) &> 35\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\\\[13pt]\n    n &> 10.358 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the least value of $n$ such that $S_n > 35\\hspace{0.15em}000$\n    is  $n=11$"},{"ref":"AA011","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA011\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $u_1 = 0.4$,\n$u_2 = 0.6$, $u_3 = 0.9$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the sum of the first ten terms in the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the greatest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 650$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The value of $r$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.6}{0.4} \\\\[13pt]\n    &= 1.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$\n    with $n=10$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.6em} S_{10} &= \\dfrac{u_1(r^{10} - 1)}{r-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.4(1.5^{10} - 1)}{1.5-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 45.3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &< 650 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1} &< 650 \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{7.7em} \\dfrac{0.4(1.5^n-1)}{1.5-1} &< 650 \\\\[8pt]\n    0.8(1.5^n-1) &< 650 \\\\[13pt]\n    n &< 16.528 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the greatest value of $n$ such that $S_n < 650$ is\n     $n=16$"},{"ref":"AA012","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA012\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe third term of an arithmetic sequence is equal to $7$ and the sum of\nthe first $8$ terms is $20$.\n\nFind the common difference and the first term.","markScheme":"Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 1$ and\n$n = 8$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_3 + (1-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 7-2d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_8 &= u_3 + (8-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 7+5d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n$S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n=8$, we get\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{4em}  S_8 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(u_1 + u_8) \\\\[6pt]\n 20 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}((7-2d) + (7+5d)) \\\\[6pt]\n 20 &= \\dfrac{8}{2}(14 + 3d) \\\\[10pt]\n 20 &= 56 + 12d \\\\[14pt]\n -36 &= 12d \\\\\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n$$\nd = -3 \\hspace{4em}\n$$\nHence\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_3-2d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 7 - 2(-3) \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA013","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA013\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe first term and the common ratio of a geometric series are denoted,\nrespectively, by $u_1$ and $r$, where $u_1,r \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. Given that\nthe fourth term is $64$ and the sum to infinity is $625$, find the value\nof $u_1$ and the value of $r$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_4 &= 64 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\nu_1r^{4-1} &= 64 \\\\[6pt]\nu_1r^3 &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\infty} &= 625 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 625 \\\\[5pt]\nu_1 &= 625(1-r)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1r^3 &= 64 \\\\[6pt]\n625(1-r)r^3 &= 64  \\hspace{6.45em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{14.4em}r = 0.8 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{0.5em} [\\text{since $r\\in \\mathbb{Q}$}]\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.15em} u_1 &= 625(1-r) \\\\[7pt]\n&= 625(1-0.8) \\\\[7pt]\n&= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Solving $625(1-r)r^3 = 64$ on the G.D.C. gives two solutions, $r = 0.8$ and $r = 0.695\\ldots$, both of which satisfy $|r| < 1$. However, only $r = 0.8$ is rational, so the second solution must be rejected since we are given $r \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ in the question."},{"ref":"AA014","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA014\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn a geometric sequence, $u_2 = 6$, $u_5 = 20.25$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the greatest value of $n$ such that $u_n < 200$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_2r^{n-2}$ with $n = 5$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_5 &= u_2r^{5-2} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    20.25 &= 6r^{5-2} \\\\[6pt]\n    r^3 &= 3.375\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.75em} r = 1.5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_2r^{n-2}$ with $n = 1$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_2r^{1-2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6(1.5)^{1-2}  \\hspace{0.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.7em} u_n &< 200 \\\\[6pt]\n    u_1r^{n-1} &< 200 \\\\[6pt]\n    4(1.5)^{n-1} &< 200 \\\\[6pt]\n    n &< 10.648 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the greatest value of $n$ such that $u_n < 200$ is\n     $n=10$"},{"ref":"AA016","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA016\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSarah walks to school each morning. During the first minute, she travels\n$130$ $\\text{metres}$. In each subsequent minute, she travels\n$5$ metres less than the distance she travelled during the previous\nminute. The distance from her home to school is $950$ metres. Sarah\nleaves her house at $8$:$00$ am and must be at\nschool by $8$:$10$ am.\n\nWill Sarah arrive to school on time? Justify your answer.","markScheme":"We have an arithmetic sequence with the first term $u_1 = 130$ and the\ncommon difference $d = -5$.\n\nIf we attempt to solve $S_n = 950$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_n &= 950 \\\\[9pt]\n\\dfrac{n}{2}(2(130)+(n-1)(-5)) &= 950 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{n}{2}(265-5n) &= 950 \\\\[8pt]\nn &\\approx 8.55 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Hence it takes approximately \\(8.55\\) minutes for Sarah to travel \\(950\\) metres.}$\n\n$\\text{Therefore, Sarah \\textbf{will} arrive at school on time.}$"},{"ref":"AA017","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA017\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nJack rides his bike to work each morning. During the first minute, he\ntravels $160$ metres. In each subsequent minute, he travels\n$80$ % of the distance travelled during the\nprevious minute.\n\nThe distance from his home to work is $750$ metres. Jack leaves his\nhouse at $8$:$30$ am and must be at work at\n$8$:$40$ am.\n\nWill Jack arrive to work on time? Justify your answer.","markScheme":"We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 160$ and the\ncommon ratio $r = 0.8$.\n\nIf we use the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\nwith $n=10$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{10} &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^{10})}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{160(1-0.8^{10})}{1-0.8} \\\\[12pt]\n&\\approx 714\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Jack only travels \\(714\\) metres by \\(8\\):\\(40\\)\\hspace{0.2em}am. Therefore, Jack \\textbf{will not} arrive to work on time.}$"},{"ref":"AA018","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA018\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe sum of the first three terms of a geometric sequence is $81.3$, and\nthe sum of the infinite sequence is $300$. Find the common ratio.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_3 &= 81.3 \\hspace{4.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 81.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\infty} &= 300 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 300 \\\\[5pt]\nu_1 &= 300(1-r)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 81.3 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{300(1-r)(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 81.3 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n300(1-r^3) &= 81.3 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{9.3em} r = 0.9 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA019","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA019\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe first two terms of an infinite geometric sequence, in order, are\n\n$$\n3\\log_3x,\\,\\, 2\\log_3x,\\,\\, \\text{where $x > 0$.}\n$$\n\n1.  Find the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Show that the sum of the infinite sequence is $9\\log_3 x$.\n    [3 marks]\n\nThe first three terms of an arithmetic sequence, in order, are\n\n$$\n\\log_3x,\\,\\, \\log_3 \\dfrac{x}{3},\\,\\, \\log_3\\dfrac{x}{9},\\,\\, \\text{where $x > 0$.}\n$$\n\n3.  Find the common difference $d$, giving your answer as an\n    integer. [3 marks]\n\nLet $S_6$ be the sum of the first $6$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n\n4.  Show that $S_6 = 6\\log_3 x - 15$. [3 marks]\n\n5.  Given that $S_6$ is equal to one third of the sum of the\n    infinite geometric\\\n    sequence, find $x$, giving your answer in the form\n     $a^p$ where $a,p \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\log_3 x}{3\\log_3 x} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{3\\log_3x}{1 - 2/3} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\log_3x}{1/3} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 9\\log_3x \\hspace{5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.9em} d &= u_2 - u_1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\log_3 \\dfrac{x}{3} - \\log_3x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (\\cancel{\\log_3x} - \\log_33) - \\cancel{\\log_3x} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n    $n=6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.8em} S_6 &= \\dfrac{6}{2}(2u_1 + (6-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{6}{2}(2(\\log_3 x) + (6-1)(-1)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{6}{2}(2\\log_3 x - 5) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 6\\log_3 x - 15\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  If we solve the equation $S_6 = \\dfrac{1}{3}S_{\\infty}$ for $x$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    6\\log_3 x - 15 &= \\dfrac{1}{3}(9\\log_3x) \\hspace{3.6em} \\\\[8pt]\n    6\\log_3 x - 15 &= 3\\log_3x \\\\[12pt]\n    3\\log_3x &= 15 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\log_3x &= 5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    x = 3^5 \\hspace{2.5em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA020","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA020\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe $1$st, $5$th and $13$th terms of an arithmetic sequence, with common\ndifference $d$, $d \\neq 0$, are the first three terms of a geometric\nsequence, with common ratio $r$, $r \\neq 1$. Given that the $1$st term\nof both sequences is $12$, find the value of $d$ and the value of $r$.","markScheme":"The $1$st, $5$th and $13$th terms of the arithmetic sequence are $12$,\n$12 + 4d$ and $12 + 12d$.\n\nThe first three terms of the geometric sequence are $12$, $12r$ and\n$12r^2$ .\n\nHence we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 4d &= 12r  \\hspace{2.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n12 + 12d &= 12r^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nDividing the equation $(1)$\nby $4$ and the equation (2) by $12$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + d &= 3r  \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n1 + d &= r^2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we subtract the equation $(4)$\nfrom the equation $(3)$ and solve the resulting\n$\\text{equation}$ for $r$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 + d) - (1 + d) &= 3r - r^2 \\\\[6pt]\n2 &= 3r - r^2 \\hspace{4.55em} \\\\[6pt]\nr^2 - 3r + 2 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(r - 1)(r - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{7.9em} r = 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $r \\neq 1$}]\n$$\n\nHence, if we substitute $r = 2$ in the equation (4) and solve the\nresulting equation for $d$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + d &= 2^2 \\\\[6pt]\nd &= 2^2 -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = 3 \\hspace{0.45em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA021","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA021\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe first three terms of an infinite geometric sequence are\n$k-4,\\,\\, 4,\\,\\, k+2$, where $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down an expression for the common ratio, $r$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that $k$ satisfies the equation\n        $k^2 - 2k - 24 = 0$.[5 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the possible values for $k$.\n\n    2.  Find the possible values for $r$. [5 marks]\n\n3.  The geometric sequence has an infinite sum.\n\n    1.  Which value of $r$ leads to this sum. Justify your answer.\n\n    2.  Find the sum of the sequence. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The common ratio is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4}{k-4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        or\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_3}{u_2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{k+2}{4}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.5em} \\dfrac{4}{k-4} &= \\dfrac{k+2}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n        16 &= (k+2)(k-4) \\\\[10pt]\n        16 &= k^2 - 2k - 8 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        k^2 - 2k - 24 = 0 \\hspace{7.05em}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Solving the equation $k^2 - 2k - 24 = 0$ for $k$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        k^2 - 2k - 24 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        (k+4)(k-6) &= 0 \\hspace{7.65em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        k = -4,\\, 6 \\hspace{0.25em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Evaluating $r = \\dfrac{4}{k-4}$ for $k = -4,\\, 6$, we obtain\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\hspace{1.5em} r &= \\dfrac{4}{(-4)-4} \\\\[6pt]\n         &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n        and\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{4}{6-4} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= 2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.\n    1.  The infinite sum exists when $|r| < 1$.\n        We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\bigg|\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.1em}\\bigg| &= \\dfrac{1}{2} < 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence\n         $r = - 1/2$\n        leads to this sum.\n\n    2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n        $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$,\n\n        we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{(-8)}{1 - (-1/2)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{(-8)}{3/2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -\\dfrac{16}{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA022","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA022\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe first two terms of an infinite geometric sequence are $u_1 = 20$ and\n$u_2 = 16\\sin^2 \\theta$, where $\\text{\\(0 < \\theta < 2\\pi\\)}$, and\n$\\theta \\neq \\pi$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Find an expression for $r$ in terms of $\\theta$.\n\n    2.  Find the possible values of $r$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Show that the sum of the infinite sequence is\n    $\\dfrac{100}{3 + 2\\cos 2\\theta}$.\n    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find the values of $\\theta$ which give the greatest value of the\n    sum. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  The common ratio is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{16\\sin^2 \\theta}{20} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4\\sin^2 \\theta}{5}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Since $0 < \\theta < 2\\pi$ and $\\theta \\neq \\pi$, we have\n        $\\sin \\theta \\neq 0$.\n\n        Hence we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        0 < \\sin^2 \\theta &\\leq 1 \\\\[9pt]\n        0 < \\dfrac{4\\sin^2 \\theta}{5} &\\leq \\dfrac{4}{5} \\hspace{2.8em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        0 < r \\leq \\dfrac{4}{5}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the double-angle formula for cosine, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\cos 2\\theta &= 1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    2\\cos 2\\theta &= 2 - 4\\sin^2\\theta \\\\[6pt]\n    3 + 2\\cos 2\\theta &= 5 - 4\\sin^2\\theta \\hspace{4em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$,\n\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{20}{1 - \\dfrac{4\\sin^2 \\theta}{5}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{100}{5-4\\sin^2 \\theta} \\\\[15pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{100}{3 + 2\\cos 2\\theta}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The greatest value of the sum is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{100}{3 + 2(-1)} &= 100 \\hspace{6.8em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    solving the equation $\\cos 2\\theta = -1$ for $\\theta$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2\\theta &= \\pi,\\, 3\\pi \\hspace{2.5em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\hspace{1.5em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA023","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA023\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are $\\ln x^9$, $\\ln x^3$,\n$\\ln x$, for $x > 0$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, solve $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty 3^{3-k}\\ln x = 27$.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 9\\ln x$ and $u_2 = 3\\ln x$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{3\\ln x}{9\\ln x} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_n &= u_1r^{n-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9\\ln x\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg)^{n-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 3^{3-n}\\ln x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty 3^{3-k}\\ln x \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $S_{\\infty} = 27$ for $x$, we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 27 \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n     \\dfrac{9\\ln x}{1 - 1/3} &= 27 \\\\[6pt]\n     \\dfrac{27\\ln x}{3-1} &= 27 \\\\[12pt]\n     \\ln x &= 2 \\\\\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    $$\n    x = e^2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA024","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA024\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe seventh term of an arithmetic sequence is equal to $1$ and the sum\nof the first $16$ terms is $52$.\n\nFind the common difference and the first term.","markScheme":"Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_7 + (n-7)d$ with $n = 1$ and\n$n = 16$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_7 + (1-7)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1-6d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{16} &= u_7 + (16-7)d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1+9d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n$S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n=16$, we get\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\hspace{4em}  S_{16} &= \\dfrac{16}{2}(u_1 + u_{16}) \\\\[6pt]\n 52 &= \\dfrac{16}{2}((1-6d) + (1+9d)) \\\\[6pt]\n 52 &= \\dfrac{16}{2}(2 + 3d) \\\\[10pt]\n 52 &= 16 + 24d \\\\[14pt]\n 36 &= 24d \\\\\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n$$\nd = 1.5 \\hspace{4em}\n$$\nHence\nwe obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 &= u_7-6d \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1 - 6(1.5) \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n&= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA026","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA026\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGiven a sequence of integers, between $20$ and $300$, which are\ndivisible by $9$.\n\n1.  Find their sum. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Express this sum using sigma notation. [2 marks]\n\nAn arithmetic sequence has first term $-500$ and common difference of\n$8$. The sum of the first $n$ terms of this sequence is negative.\n\n3.  Find the greatest value of $n$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have an arithmetic sequence $27,\\, 36,\\, 45,\\,\\dots,\\, 297$ with\n    $u_1 = 27$ and $d = 9$.\n\n    The number of terms in the sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.8em} n &= \\floor*{\\dfrac{300}{9}} - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    & = 31\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(u_1 + u_n)$ with $n = 31$, $u_1 = 27$ and\n    $u_n = 297$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(27 + 297) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 5022\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Alternatively,\n    using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $n = 31$, $u_1 = 27$ and\n    $d = 9$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.6em} S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(2(27) + (31-1)(9)) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= 5022\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Since $u_1 = 27$, $d = 9$ and $n = 31$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} S_{31}  &= 27 + (27 + 9) + (27 + 2\\cdot9) + \\cdots + (27 + 30\\cdot9) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^{31}} (27 + (n-1)(9)) \\\\[5pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.3em}\\text{or} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^{31}} 9(n+2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $u_1 = -500$ and $d = 8$,\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_n &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(-500) + (n-1)(8)) &< 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    n(-500 + (n-1)(4)) &< 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    n &< 126 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    the greatest value of $n$ is\n     $125$"},{"ref":"AA028","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA028\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nThe cubic polynomial equation $x^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0$ has three roots\n$x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$. By expanding the product $(x-x_1)(x-x_2)(x-x_3)$,\nshow that\n\n1.\n    1.  $b = -(x_1+x_2+x_3)$;\n\n    2.  $c = x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3$;\n    3.  $d = -x_1x_2x_3$. [3 marks]\n\nIt is given that $b = -9$ and $c = 45$ for parts (b) and (c) below.\n\n2.\n    1.  In the case that the three roots $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$ form an\n        arithmetic\\\n        sequence, show that one of the roots is $3$.\n\n    2.  Hence determine the value of $d$. [5 marks]\n\n3.  In another case the three roots form a geometric sequence.\n    Determine\\\n    the value of $d$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Given the three roots $x_1$, $x_2$ and $x_3$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x^3 + bx^2 + cx + d &= (x-x_1)(x-x_2)(x-x_3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (x^2 -(x_1+x_2)x + x_1x_2)(x-x_3) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= x^3 - (x_1+x_2+x_3)x^2 + (x_1x_2+x_1x_3+x_2x_3)x - x_1x_2x_3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, comparing the coefficients, we get\n\n    1.\n        $b = -(x_1+x_2+x_3)$\n\n    2.\n        $c = x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3$\n\n    3.\n        $d = -x_1x_2x_3$\n\n2.\n    1.  Letting $\\alpha = x_1 + \\delta$, where $\\delta$ is the common\n        difference of the arithmetic sequence $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_3$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.8em} x_1  &=  \\alpha - \\delta \\\\[6pt]\n        x_2  &=  \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n        x_3  &=  \\alpha + \\delta\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using part (a)\n        (i), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -b \\\\[6pt]\n        (\\alpha - \\delta) + \\alpha + (\\alpha + \\delta) &= -(-9) \\hspace{6em} \\\\[6pt]\n        3\\alpha &= 9 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\alpha &= 3 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        x_2 = 3\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using part (a) (ii), we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3 &= c \\\\[6pt]\n        (3 - \\delta)(3) + (3 - \\delta)(3 + \\delta) + (3)(3 + \\delta) &= 45 \\\\[6pt]\n        27 - \\delta^2 &= 45 \\\\[6pt]\n        -\\delta^2 &= 18 \\\\[5pt]\n        \\delta &= \\sqrt{18}\\hspace{0.05em}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\\hspace{2em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, using part (a) (iii), we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4em} d &= -x_1x_2x_3 \\\\[5pt]\n        &= -(3-\\sqrt{18}\\hspace{0.05em}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(3)(3+\\sqrt{18}\\hspace{0.05em}{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= -81\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Letting $\\alpha = x_1$ and $r$ denote the common ratio of the\n    geometric sequence $x_1, x_2, x_3$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} x_1  &=  \\alpha \\\\[6pt]\n    x_2  &=  \\alpha r \\\\[6pt]\n    x_3  &=  \\alpha r^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.25em} x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -b \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a) (i)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha + \\alpha r + \\alpha r^2 &= -(-9) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha (1+r+r^2) &= 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3 &= c \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a) (ii)}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha (\\alpha r) + \\alpha (\\alpha r^2) + (\\alpha r)(\\alpha r^2) &= 45 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\alpha^2r(1+r+r^2) &= 45\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Taking the ratio\n    of the expressions $(1)$ and $(2)$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{\\alpha^2r(\\cancel{1+r+r^2})}{\\alpha(\\cancel{1+r+r^2})} &= \\dfrac{45}{9} \\hspace{4em} \\\\[5pt]\n    \\dfrac{\\alpha^2r}{\\alpha} &= \\dfrac{45}{9} \\\\[15pt]\n    \\alpha r &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the expression $(3)$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.5em} d &= -x_1x_2x_3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by (a) (iii)}] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -\\alpha (\\alpha r)(\\alpha r^2) \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -(\\alpha r)^3 \\\\[5pt]\n    &= -5^3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -125\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1023","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1023\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is equal to $13$ and the sum of the first $10$ terms is $55$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference and the first term. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Determine the greatest value of $n$ such that the $n$th term is positive. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 4$ and $u_4 = 13$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_4 &= u_1 + 3d \\\\[6pt]\n    13 &= u_1 + 3d \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[1]}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $n=10$ and $S_{10}=55$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{10} &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2u_1 + 9d) \\\\[6pt]\n    55 &= 5(2u_1 + 9d) \\\\[6pt]\n    11 &= 2u_1 + 9d \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[2]}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Solving the system formed by equations [1] and [2] we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2u_1 + 6d &= 26 \\hspace{3em} \\text{(equation \\textcolor{green}{[1]} $\\times$ 2)} \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[3]}}\\\\[6pt]\n    2u_1 + 9d &= 11 \\tag*{\\textcolor{green}{[4]}}\\\\[6pt]\n    3d &= -15 \\hspace{2.5em}\\text{(equation \\textcolor{green}{[4]} - equation \\textcolor{green}{[3]})}\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.8em}d = -5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= 13-3d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 13 - 3(-5) \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.2em}u_1 = 28\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We must have $u_n > 0$. Using the $n$th term formula with $u_1=28$ and $d=-5$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_n &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    u_1 + (n-1)d &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    28 - 5(n-1) &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    28 - 5n + 5 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    33 &> 5n \\\\[6pt]\n    n &< \\dfrac{33}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $n$ must be an integer, and the greatest integer less than $\\dfrac{33}{5}$ is $6$.\n\n    Hence, the greatest value of $n$ such that $u_n$ is positive is $n=6$."},{"ref":"AA1063","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1063\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the real polynomial $f(x)=x^5-5.5x^4-...-604.662$.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial form a geometric sequence with first term $u_1$ and common ratio $r$.\n\nWhere $u_1>0$ and $r<-1$.\n\n1. Find two distinct functions for $u_1$ in terms of $r$. [3 marks]\n\n2. Hence find $u_1$ and $r$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The roots of $f(x)$ form the following geometric sequence $u_1, u_1r, u_1r^2, u_1r^3, u_1r^4$.\n\n    Hence we can write the sum of the roots as $u_1\\tfrac{r^5-1}{r-1}$ and the product of the roots as $u_1^5r^{10}$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can find the sum and product of the roots of $f$ using the formula, hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        -\\dfrac{(-5.5)}{1}&=u_1\\left(\\frac{r^5-1}{r-1}\\right) &(-1)^5\\dfrac{(-604.662)}{1}=u_1^5r^{10} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_1&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{5.5(r-1)}{r^5-1}} &u_1=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{604.662}{r^{10}}}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Draw the functions on a G.D.C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/graph.png\" alt=\"graph\">\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the conditions given in the question\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\boxed{u_1=1.6 \\text{ and } r=-1.5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1150","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1150\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nA geometric series has a common ratio of $3^x$.\n\n1.  State the domain of $x$ such that the sum to infinity of the series exists. [2 marks]\n\nThe sum to infinity of the series is found to be $54$.\n\n2. Find the value of $x$, given that the first term of the series is $52$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $\\bm{S_{\\infty}}\\bm{=}\\dfrac{\\bm{u_1}}{\\bm{1-r}}\\,,\\,\\bm{|r|<1}$ **(1.8)**.\n\n    That is, the common ratio ($r$) must be between $-1$ and $1$ for the sum to infinity to exist. Therefore,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -1<3^x<1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $3^x$ is larger than zero for all $x$, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0<3^x<1\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/3%5Ex.png\" alt=\"3^x\">\n\n    From the sketch of $3^x$ we can see that when $x<0$ then $0<3^x<1$.\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The domain such that the sum to infinity exists is $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{x<0}$\n    $$\n    \\\\[-2.5em]\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  Using the formula for the sum of an infinite geometric series with $S_{\\infty}=54$, $r=3^x$ and $u_1=52$ we can write the equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        54&=\\frac{52}{1-3^x}\\\\[6pt]\n        1-3^x&=\\frac{26}{27}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        3^x&=\\frac{1}{27}\\\\[6pt]\n        3^x&=3^{-3}\\\\[6pt]\n        x&=-3\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1163","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1163\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAn arithmetic progression has a common difference of $d$, where $d \\neq 0$.\n\nThe $7^{\\text{th}}$ term is equal to the $3^{\\text{rd}}$ term squared and the $5^{\\text{th}}$ term is $1$.\n\n1.  Find the common difference. [4 marks]\n\n$S_n$ is the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence.\n\n2.  Find the largest value of $n$ such that $S_n<300$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can use the information given to form an equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        u_7&=(u_3)^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the general term of an arithmetic progression, we can write the equation in terms of the first term, $u_1$, and $d$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        u_1+(7-1) \\times d&=(u_1 + (3-1) \\times d)^2\\\\[6pt]\n        u_1+6d&=(u_1 + 2d)^2\\\\[6pt]\n        u_1+6d&=u_1^2+4u_1d+4d^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the given fact $u_5=1$ we can write $u_1$ in terms of $d$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1&=u_1+4d\\\\[6pt]\n    u_1&=1-4d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now write the original equation in terms of $d$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        1-4d+6d&=(1-4d)^2+4(1-4d)d+4d^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now simplify both sides of this equation and then solve the equation graphically.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        1+2d&=(1-4d)^2+4d-16d^2+4d^2\\\\[6pt]\n        1+2d&=(1-4d)^2+4d-12d^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We do this by sketching the L.H.S. and R.H.S. of the above equation, as two seperate functions, using a G.D.C. and then finding the points of intersection\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        f(x)=&=1+2x\\\\[6pt]\n        g(x)&=(1-4x)^2+4x-12x^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA1163_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1163_a\">\n\n    Hence we can see that the intersection points tell us that $d=0$ and $d=\\tfrac{3}{2}$.\n    As $d\\neq0$ then the common difference is $d=\\tfrac{3}{2}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the sum $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with $d=\\tfrac{3}{2}$ we can write $S_n$ in terms of $u_1$ and $n$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2u_1+\\tfrac{3}{2}(n-1))\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can find $u_1$ by using the 5$^{th}$ term\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        1&=u_1+4\\times \\tfrac{3}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        u_1&=-5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we can now write $S_n$ in terms of $n$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2(-5)+\\tfrac{3}{2}(n-1)) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the G.D.C. to produce a table of values for $S_n$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/table_2.png\" alt=\"table_2\">\n\n    We can see that for $S_n<300$ the largest value of $n$ is $24$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA119","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA119\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1.  The following diagram shows \\[PQ\\], with length\n    $4$ cm. The line is divided into an infinite\n    number of line segments. The diagram shows the first four\n    segments.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/2ecea1f156846207a208a3fc02a1af87be7132ca.svg\" alt=\"2ecea1f156846207a208a3fc02a1af87be7132ca.svg\">\n\n    The length of the line segments are $m$ cm,\n    $m^2$ cm, $m^3$ cm,\n    $\\dots$, where $0 < m < 1$.\n\n    Show that $m = \\dfrac{4}{5}$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  The following diagram shows \\[RS\\], with length\n    $l$ cm, where $l > 1$. Squares with side\n    lengths $n$ cm, $n^2$ cm,\n    $n^3$ cm, $\\dots$, where $0 < n < 1$, are\n    drawn along \\[RS\\]. This process is carried on indefinitely. The\n    diagram shows the first four squares.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/282d818bda418427e2f4b47fb94d3fce3af0ad9c.svg\" alt=\"282d818bda418427e2f4b47fb94d3fce3af0ad9c.svg\">\n\n    The **total** sum of the areas of all the squares is\n    $\\dfrac{25}{11}$. Find the value of $l$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$ with $u_1 = m = r$, we have\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{2.5em} 4 &= \\dfrac{m}{1-m} \\hspace{5em}[1]\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $[1]$ for $m$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4 - 4m &= m \\\\[12pt]\n    4 &= 5m \\hspace{1.6em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    m = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$ with $u_1 = n^2 = r$, we have\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{1.9em} \\dfrac{25}{11} &= \\dfrac{n^2}{1-n^2} \\hspace{5em}[2]\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $[2]$ for $n$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    25 - 25n^2 &= 11n^2 \\\\[14pt]\n    25 &= 36n^2 \\\\[10pt]\n    n^2 &= \\dfrac{25}{36} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    n &= \\dfrac{5}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the sum of an infinite\n    geometric sequence formula $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$\n    with\n\n    $u_1 = r = n = \\dfrac{5}{6}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.3em} l &= \\dfrac{5/6}{1 - 5/6} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA122","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA122\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe sum of an infinite geometric sequence is $27$. The second term of\nthe sequence is $6$. Find the possible values of $r$.","markScheme":"We have $S_{\\infty} = 27$ and $u_2 = 6$.\n\nUsing the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_2 &= u_1r^{2-1} \\\\[12pt]\n6 &= u_1r \\\\[6pt]\nu_1 &= \\dfrac{6}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the sum of an infinite\ngeometric sequence formula\n$S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27 &= \\dfrac{6/r}{1-r} \\\\[5pt]\n27-27r &= \\dfrac{6}{r} \\\\[10pt]\n9r - 9r^2 &= 2 \\\\[10pt]\n0 &= 9r^2 - 9r + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the quadratic formula to find the roots of the equation, we\nobtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{9.6em} r &= \\dfrac{-(-9)\\pm\\sqrt{(-9)^2 - 4(9)(2)}}{2(9)} \\\\[9pt]\nr &= \\dfrac{6}{18},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{12}{18} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{1}{3},\\hspace{0.25em}\\dfrac{2}{3} \\hspace{0.3em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1275","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1275\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA geometric sequence, with common ratio $r$, has a first term of $81$. The sum of the first four terms of the sequence is $195$.\n\n1.  1.  Find $r$.\n\n    2.  Hence, find $G_n$, the sum of the first $n$ terms of the geometric sequence. [3 marks]\n\nThe first three terms of an arithmetic sequence, with a common difference of $d$, are $\\log 96, \\log 48$ and $\\log24$.\n\n2.  Find $d$ in the form $p\\log q$ where $p, q \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.[2 marks]\n\n$A_n$ is the sum of the first $n$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n\n3.  Show that $A_n=\\log{\\left(3^n\\times\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)^{11n-n^2}\\right)}$.[5 marks]\n\n4.  Find the maximum value of $n$ such that $\\left|G_n\\right|>\\left|A_n\\right|$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  From the given information we know $u_1=81$ and $S_4=195$. \n\n        \n\n        Using the formula for the sum of a geometric sequence we can form an equation and then solve for $r$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            S_n&=\\frac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            S_4&=\\frac{u_1(1-r^4)}{1-r}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            195&=\\frac{81(1-r^4)}{1-r}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using a G.D.C. we get\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            r&=0.6666...\\\\[6pt]\n\n            r&=\\dfrac{2}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n    2.  Using the answer from (a) (i) and the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric series we can write the general term for $G_n$ as \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            G_n&=\\frac{81(1-(\\frac{2}{3})^n)}{1-\\frac{2}{3}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            &=\\frac{81(1-(\\frac{2}{3})^n)}{\\frac{1}{3}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n            &=81\\left(1-\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right)\\times3\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &=243\\left(1-\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2.  In an arithmetic sequence, there is a common difference between terms. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    d&=u_2 - u_1\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting the given values for $u_1$ and $u_2$ we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    d&=\\log 48-\\log96\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    d&=\\log\\left(\\frac{48}{96}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    d&=\\log \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    d&=\\log 2^{-1}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    d&=-\\log 2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n3.  The formula for sum of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2u_1+(n-1)d)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $u_1=\\log 96$ and $d=-\\log 2$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        A_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2\\log 96+(n-1)(-\\log 2))\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{n}{2}(\\log96^2-(n-1)\\log 2)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Applying logarithm rules we get     \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n\n    &=\\frac{n}{2}(\\log96^2-\\log2^{(n-1)})\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\frac{n}{2}\\log \\frac{96^2}{2^{(n-1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log\\left(\\frac{96^2}{2^{(n-1)}}\\right)^{\\frac{n}{2}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{96^2}{2^{(n-1)}}\\right)^n}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{96^2}}{\\sqrt{2^{(n-1)}}}\\right)^n\\\\[12pt]\n\n    &=\\log{\\left(\\frac{96^n}{(\\sqrt{2})^{n^2-n}}\\right)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Looking at the form of the answer we can attempt to rewrite the denominator using by negating its exponent \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log(96^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$ \n\n    Let's rewrite $96$ as a product of prime factors\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log((2^5\\times3)^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Then we'll rewrite $2^5$ as a base of $\\sqrt{2}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log(((\\sqrt{2})^{10}\\times3)^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Now we can distribute the power of $n$ and then use exponent laws again to simplify to the required form.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &=\\log((\\sqrt{2})^{10n}\\times3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{n-n^2})\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\log(3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{10n+n-n^2})\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=\\log(3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{11n-n^2})\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required.}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the functions from (a) (ii) and (c) we can form an inequality which we can solve for $n$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\textcolor{red}{\\left|243\\left(1-\\left(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right)\\right|}>\\textcolor{blue}{\\left|\\log(3^n\\times(\\sqrt{2})^{11n-n^2})\\right|}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can sketch the $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{L.H.S.}}$ and the $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{R.H.S.}}$ on a G.D.C. to get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA1275.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1275\">\n\n    Hence when\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        n<47.88...\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    then $|G_n|>|A_n|$.\n\n    As $n$ represents the term value, we therefore round down to the nearest integer value. \n\n    Hence $n=47$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1324","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1324\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA geometric sequence has a first term of $81$, and the sum of the first $4$ terms is $195$.\n\n1.    Find the value of the common ratio, $r$. [2 marks]\n\nThe fourth term of this geometric sequence is the sixth term of an arithmetic sequence.\n\nLet $S_n$ denote the sum of the first $n$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n\nIt is also known that $S_{20}=S_{11}$.\n\n2.    Find the largest possible value of $S_n$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. We can use the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric sequence where $S_4=195$, $u_1=81$ and $n=4$, to form an equation.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_n&=u_1\\left(\\frac{r^n-1}{r-1}\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n\n    195&=81\\left(\\frac{r^4-1}{r-1}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    By graphing the L.H.S. and R.H.S. as two functions\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA1324-a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1324-a\">\n\n    we find\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    r&=0.6666...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=0.667\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\bigg(&=\\frac{2}{3}\\bigg)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note, you could have also solved the equation $81+81r+81r^2+81r^3=195$ on your G.D.C.\n\n2. As we need to find the maximum value of the sum of the arithmetic sequence, we must find the first term of the arithmetic sequence, $a$, and the common difference, $d$.\n\n    We will begin by finding the fourth term of the geometric sequence.\n\n    Using the general term of a geometric sequence we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_4&=81 \\times \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{4-1}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=24\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can use the result above to form an equation for the sixth term of the arithmetic sequence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    24&=a+(6-1)\\times d\\\\[6pt]\n\n    24&=a+5d \\hspace{2em}[1]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence, $S_n=\\frac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d)$ we can rewrite the given relation in terms of $a$ and $d$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_{20}&=S_{11}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\frac{20}{2}(2a+19d)&=\\frac{11}{2}(2a+10d)  \\hspace{2em}[2]\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We now have two linear equations $[1]$ and $[2]$, which we can solve using our G.D.C.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a&=36&d=&-\\frac{12}{5}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can now find $S_n$ in terms of $n$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\bigg[2(36)+(n-1)\\left(-\\frac{12}{5}\\right)\\bigg]\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We could simplify this expression. However, as this is a paper $2$ question, it is better practice to not simplify unless you are required to.\n\n    \n\n    We need to find the maximum value of $S_n$ remembering the fact that this represents a sequence and is only valid for positive integer values of $n$.\n\n    \n\n    Using a table of values is a better approach than graphing, to find the maximum value.\n\n    | $n$ | $S_n$ |\n\n    | --- | --- |\n\n    |&nbsp; $13$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $280.8$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $14$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $285.6$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $15$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $\\textcolor{red}{288}$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $16$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $\\textcolor{red}{288}$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $17$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $285.6$ &nbsp;|\n\n    |&nbsp; $18 $ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $280.8$ &nbsp;|\n\n    We can see that the $15$th and $16$th terms both give the same maximum value of $S_n$.\n\n    Hence the maximum value of $S_n$ is $288$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note that graphing the function $y=\\dfrac{x}{2}\\bigg[2(36)+(x-1)\\left(-\\frac{12}{5}\\right)\\bigg]$ on your G.D.C. gives the maximum point $(15.5, 288.3)$. If you gave the answer as $288.3$ then you would not receive the final A$1$ mark as this is not a valid value for the sum of the sequence as $n$ must be a whole number.\n\n    \n\n    This is why, often when working with sequences, it is better practice to use a table of values."},{"ref":"AA1452","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1452\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe $m$th, $n$th and $k$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $a$, $b$ and $c$ respectively, where $a,b,c>0$. The common difference of the arithmetic sequence is $d$.\n\n1. Show that $b-c=(n-k)d$.[2 marks]\n\n2. Hence show that $(b-c)(m-1)+(c-a)(n-1)+(a-b)(k-1)=0$. [2 marks]\n\n    \n\nThe $m$th, $n$th and $k$th terms of a geometric sequence, with first term $v_1>0$ and common ratio $r>0$, are also $a$, $b$ and $c$ respectively.\n\n3. Hence show that $a^{\\,b-c}\\,b^{\\,c-a}\\,c^{\\,a-b}=1$.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using **the formula for the $\\bm{n}$th term of an arithmetic sequence (1.2)**, $u_n = u_1+(n-1)d$, let's first write expressions for $u_m, u_n$ and $u_k$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_m &= u_1+(m-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n\n    u_n &= u_1+(n-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n\n    u_k &= u_1+(k-1)d\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We have that $u_m = a, u_n=b$ and $u_k=c$ and therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a &= u_1+(m-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{[1]}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    b &= u_1+(n-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{[2]}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    c &= u_1+(k-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{[3]}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Subtracting equation **[3]** from equation **[2]**, we can form an expression for $b-c$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    b-c &= \\bigl[u_1+(n-1)d\\bigr]-\\bigl[u_1+(k-1)d\\bigr] \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= (n-1)d-(k-1)d \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= d(n-1-(k-1))\\\\[6pt]\n\n    b-c &= \\boxed{(n-k)d}\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using the method of part (a), we can form expressions for $a-b$ and $c-a$. This yields\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a-b&=(m-n)d \\\\[6pt]\n\n    c-a&=(k-m)d\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting these into the left-hand side of the given expression, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &(b-c)(m-1)+(c-a)(n-1)+(a-b)(k-1) \\\\[6pt]\n\n    =&(n-k)d(m-1)+(k-m)d(n-1)+(m-n)d(k-1) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    =&d\\left[(n-k)(m-1)+(k-m)(n-1)+(m-n)(k-1)\\right]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Expanding the brackets, we obtain\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{LHS} &= d\\bigl[(mn-n-mk+k) \\\\\n\n    &\\quad+(kn-k-mn+m) \\\\\n\n    &\\quad+(mk-m-nk+n)\\bigr] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\boxed{0} \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using **the $\\bm{n}$th term of a geometric sequence (1.3)**, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_n &= u_1r^{n-1} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    a &= v_1r^{m-1} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    b &= v_1r^{n-1} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    c &= v_1r^{k-1}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Substituting into the left-hand side of the given expression, we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a^{\\,b-c}\\,b^{\\,c-a}\\,c^{\\,a-b} &= \\left(v_1r^{m-1}\\right)^{b-c}\\left(v_1r^{n-1}\\right)^{c-a}\\left(v_1r^{k-1}\\right)^{a-b} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= v_1^{b-c}r^{(m-1)(b-c)} \\cdot v_1^{c-a}r^{(n-1)(c-a)} \\cdot v_1^{a-b}r^{(k-1)(a-b)}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= v_1^{(b-c)+(c-a)+(a-b)} \\cdot r^{(m-1)(b-c)+(n-1)(c-a)+(k-1)(a-b)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= v^0 \\cdot r^{(b-c)(m-1)+(c-a)(n-1)+(a-b)(k-1)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Using our result from part (b), the exponent of $r$ is $0$. Therefore we have\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    a^{\\,b-c}\\,b^{\\,c-a}\\,c^{\\,a-b} &= v_1^{0}\\,r^{0}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= \\boxed{1} \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{... as required} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA199","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA199\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{3\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)}$, for $x > 0$.\n\nThe $k$th maximum point on the graph of $f$ has $x$-coordinate $x_k$,\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n1.  Given that $x_{k+1} = x_k + d$, find $d$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the value of $n$ such that\n    $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k = 992$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw the graph of $y= f(x)$ \\[by using G.D.C.\\], we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\" alt=\"97f38ce996ffbe7f9b884196663bd4ac3db522d5.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the $x$-coordinates of the points A$(2,20.1)$ and\n    B$(10,20.1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} x_2 &= x_1 + d \\\\[6pt]\n    10 &= 2 + d \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 8\n    $$\n\n2.  We have an arithmetic sequence with the first term $x_1 = 2$ and the\n    common difference $d = 8$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2x_1+(n-1)d)$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k=1}^n x_k &= 992 \\\\[6pt]\n    S_n &= 992 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{with $x_1 = 2$ \\& $d = 8$}] \\\\[13pt]\n    \\hspace{4em} \\dfrac{n}{2}(2(2)+(n-1)(8)) &= 992 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.1em} n = 16 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA623","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA623\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOnly one of the following four sequences is arithmetic and only one of\nthem is geometric.\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rcccccl}\na_n &=& 1,\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& c_n &=& 1.5,\\,3,\\,4.5,\\,6,\\,\\dots \\\\[12pt]\nb_n &=& \\dfrac{1}{2},\\,\\dfrac{2}{3},\\,\\dfrac{3}{4},\\,\\dfrac{4}{5},\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& d_n &=& 2,\\,1,\\,\\dfrac{1}{2},\\,\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,\\dots\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n1.  State which sequence is arithmetic and find the common difference of\n    the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  State which sequence is geometric and find the common ratio of the\n    sequence.[2 marks]\n\n3.  For the **geometric** sequence find the **exact** value of the\n    eighth term. Give your answer as a fraction. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    a_2-a_1 &\\neq& a_3-a_2 &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& c_2-c_1 &=& c_3-c_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    5-1 &\\neq& 10-5 &&& 3-1.5 &=& 4.5-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    4 &\\neq& 5 &&& 1.5 &=& 1.5 \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    b_2-b_1 &\\neq& b_3-b_2 &&& d_2-d_1 &\\neq& d_3-d_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{2}{3}-\\dfrac{1}{2} &\\neq& \\dfrac{3}{4}-\\dfrac{2}{3} &&& 1-2 &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{2}-1 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{6} &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{12} &&& -1 &\\neq& -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $c_n$\n    is arithmetic and\n     $d = 1.5$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{a_3}{a_2} &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& \\dfrac{c_2}{c_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{c_3}{c_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{5}{1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{10}{5} &&& \\dfrac{3}{1.5} &\\neq& \\dfrac{4.5}{3} \\\\[12pt]\n    5 &\\neq& 2 &&& 2 &\\neq& 1.5 \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    \\dfrac{b_2}{b_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{b_3}{b_2} &&& \\dfrac{d_2}{d_1} &=& \\dfrac{d_3}{d_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{2/3}{1/2} &\\neq& \\dfrac{3/4}{2/3} &&& \\dfrac{1}{2} &=& \\dfrac{1/2}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{4}{3} &\\neq& \\dfrac{9}{8} &&& \\dfrac{1}{2} &=& \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $d_n$\n    is geometric and\n     $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n3.  Using the $n$th term formula $d_n = d_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 8$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d_8 &= d_1r^{8-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{8-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{64}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA624","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA624\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOnly one of the following four sequences is arithmetic and only one of\nthem is geometric.\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rcccccl}\na_n &=& \\dfrac{1}{3},\\,\\dfrac{1}{4},\\,\\dfrac{1}{5},\\,\\dfrac{1}{6},\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& c_n &=& 3,\\,1,\\,\\dfrac{1}{3},\\,\\dfrac{1}{9},\\,\\dots \\\\[12pt]\nb_n &=& 2.5,\\,5,\\,7.5,\\,10,\\,\\dots &\\,\\hspace{4em}\\,& d_n &=& 1,\\,3,\\,6,\\,10,\\,\\dots\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n1.  State which sequence is arithmetic and find the common difference of\n    the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  State which sequence is geometric and find the common ratio of the\n    sequence.[2 marks]\n\n3.  For the **geometric** sequence find the **exact** value of the sixth\n    term. Give your answer as a fraction. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    a_2-a_1 &\\neq& a_3-a_2 &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& c_2-c_1 &\\neq& c_3-c_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{4}-\\dfrac{1}{3} &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{5}-\\dfrac{1}{4} &&& 1-3 &\\neq& \\dfrac{1}{3}-1 \\\\[12pt]\n    -\\dfrac{1}{12} &\\neq& -\\dfrac{1}{20} &&& -2 &\\neq& -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    b_2-b_1 &=& b_3-b_2 &&& d_2-d_1 &\\neq& d_3-d_2 \\\\[12pt]\n    5-2.5 &=& 7.5-5 &&& 3-1 &\\neq& 6-3 \\\\[12pt]\n    2.5 &=& 2.5 &&& 2 &\\neq& 3\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $b_n$\n    is arithmetic and\n     $d = 2.5$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{array}{rclc|crcl}\n    \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{a_3}{a_2} &\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,&\\,\\hspace{0.5em}\\,& \\dfrac{c_2}{c_1} &=& \\dfrac{c_3}{c_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1/4}{1/3} &\\neq& \\dfrac{1/5}{1/4} &&& \\dfrac{1}{3} &=& \\dfrac{1/3}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{4} &\\neq& \\dfrac{4}{5} &&& \\dfrac{1}{3} &=&\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[12pt] \\hline\n    &&&&&&& \\\\\n    \\dfrac{b_2}{b_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{b_3}{b_2} &&& \\dfrac{d_2}{d_1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{d_3}{d_2} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{5}{2.5} &\\neq& \\dfrac{7.5}{5} &&& \\dfrac{3}{1} &\\neq& \\dfrac{6}{3} \\\\[12pt]\n    2 &\\neq& 1.5 &&& 3 &\\neq& 2\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $c_n$\n    is geometric and\n     $r = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n3.  Using the $n$th term formula $c_n = c_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    c_6 &= c_1r^{6-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{6-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{81}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA625","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA625\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA tennis ball is dropped from a height of $h_0\\,\\text{cm}$ and bounces on the ground $n$ times. The height the ball reaches after each\nbounce, $h_1, h_2, h_3, \\dots,h_n,$ form a geometric sequence. The\nheight that the ball reaches after the first bounce, $h_1$, is\n$80$ cm, and after the second bounce, $h_2$, is\n$60$ cm.\n\n1.  Find the value of the common ratio for the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2. Hence, calculate the value of $h_0$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Determine the height that the ball reaches after the tenth bounce, $h_{10}$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n4.  Find the **total distance** travelled by the ball at the moment it bounces on the ground for the sixth time. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio can be found by taking any term in a geometric sequence, and dividing it by the previous term. We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{h_2}{h_1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{60}{80} \\\\[12pt]\n    r &= 0.75 \\hspace{2.75em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. In any geometric sequence, the second term, $u_2$, is equal to the first term multiplied by the common ratio. Therefore, we can write\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r &= u_2 \\\\[6pt]\n    u_1 &= \\frac{u_2}{r} \\\\[6pt]\n    h_0 &= \\frac{h_1}{r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\frac{80}{0.75} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 106.6...\\\\[6pt]\n    h_0 &= 107\\,\\text{cm}\\,\\text{(3 s.f.)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the $n$th term formula $h_n = h_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 10$ and $h_1=80$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    h_{10} &= h_1r^{10-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 80(0.75)^{10-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &=6.006...\\\\[7pt]\n    h_{10}&= 6.01\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}\\,\\text{(3 s.f.)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  When the ball bounces on the ground for the 6th time, it has travelled **downwards** 6 times, with a total distance of\n\n    $h_0+h_1+h_2+h_3+h_4+h_5$\n\n    and **upwards** 5 times, with a total distance of\n\n    $h_1+h_2+h_3+h_4+h_5$\n\n    this is shown in the following diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/bounce.png\" alt=\"bounce\">\n\n    We therefore have $h_0$ plus 2 times the sum of the first 5 terms of a geometric sequence with first term $h_1$ and common ratio $r=0.75$. We have\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Total distance} &= h_0 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{h_1\\left(1-0.75^5\\right)}{1-0.75}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 106.6...+2\\left(\\dfrac{80\\left(1-0.75^5\\right)}{0.25}\\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 594.7...\\\\[10pt]\n    \\text{Total distance} &= 595\\,\\text{cm}\\,\\text{(3 s.f.)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA626","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA626\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe third term, $u_3$, of an arithmetic sequence is $7$. The common\ndifference of\\\nthe sequence, $d$, is $3$.\n\n1.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_{60}$, the $60$th term of sequence. [2 marks]\n\nThe first and fourth terms of this arithmetic sequence are the first two\nterms\\\nof a geometric sequence.\n\n3.  Calculate the sixth term of the **geometric** sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 1$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_3 + (1-3)d \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7 + (1-3)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_3 + (n-3)d$ with $n = 60$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{60} &= u_3 + (60-3)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7 + (60-3)(3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 178\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/ad62ab3f954984b454acd9a772d836d707c86a2f.svg\" alt=\"ad62ab3f954984b454acd9a772d836d707c86a2f.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 6$,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_6 &= u_1r^{6-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1(10)^{6-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 100\\hspace{0.15em}000 \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA627","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA627\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fifth term, $u_5$, of a geometric sequence is $125$. The sixth term,\n$u_6$, is $156.25$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the sum of the first $12$ terms of the sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_6}{u_5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{156.25}{125} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 1.25 \\hspace{3.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5r^{n-5}$ with $n = 1$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_5r^{1-5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 125(1.25)^{1-5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 51.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$\n    with $n = 12$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} S_{12} &= \\dfrac{u_1(r^{12}-1)}{r-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{51.2((1.25)^{12}-1)}{1.25-1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &=2775.4...\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 2780\\hspace{3em}\\text{rounded to 3 s.f.}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA628","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA628\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fourth term, $u_4$, of a geometric sequence is $135$. The fifth\nterm, $u_5$, is $81$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the sum of the first $20$ terms of the sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_5}{u_4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{81}{135} \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 0.6 \\hspace{3.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_4r^{n-4}$ with $n = 1$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 &= u_4r^{1-4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 135(0.6)^{1-4} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 625\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\n    with $n = 20$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.7em} S_{20} &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^{20})}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{625(1-(0.6)^{20})}{1-0.6} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 1560\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA629","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA629\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe fifth term, $u_5$, of an arithmetic sequence is $25$. The eleventh\nterm, $u_{11}$, of the same sequence is $49$.\n\n1.  Find $d$, the common difference of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $u_1$, the first term of the sequence. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $S_{100}$, the sum of the first $100$ terms of the sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n=11$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{11} &= u_5 + (11-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    49 &= 25 + (11-5)d\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5.8em}d = 4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_5 + (n-5)d$ with $n=1$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.7em} u_1 &= u_5 + (1-5)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 25 + (1-5)(4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1+(n-1)d)$\n    with $n = 100$,\n\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} S_{100} &= \\dfrac{100}{2}(2u_1+(100-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{100}{2}(2(9)+(100-1)(4)) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 20\\hspace{0.15em}700\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA631","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA631\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLandmarks are placed along the road from London to Edinburgh and the\ndistance between each landmark is $16.1$ km. The\nfirst landmark placed on the road is $124.7$ km\nfrom London, and the last landmark is near Edinburgh. The length of the\nroad from London to Edinburgh is $667.1$ km.\n\n1.  Find the distance between the fifth landmark and London. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Determine how many landmarks there are along the road. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have an arithmetic sequence with $u_1 = 124.7$ and $d = 16.1$.\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with\n    $n = 5$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.6em} u_5 &= u_1 + (5-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 124.7 + (5-1)(16.1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 189\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the distance between the fifth\n    landmark and London is\n     $189\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{km}$\n\n2.  If we solve the inequality $u_n < 667.1$ for the largest value of\n    $n$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1 + (n-1)d &< 667.1 \\\\[6pt]\n    124.7 + (n-1)(16.1) &< 667.1 \\\\[6pt]\n    n &< 34.7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    n &= 34 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence there are\n     $34$\n    landmarks along the road."},{"ref":"AA632","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA632\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe first term of an arithmetic sequence is $24$ and the common\ndifference is $16$.\n\n1.  Find the value of the $62$nd term of the\n    sequence. [2 marks]\n\nThe first term of a geometric sequence is $8$. The $4$th term of the\ngeometric sequence is equal to the $13$th term of the arithmetic\nsequence given above.\n\n2.  Write down an equation using this information. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the common ratio of the geometric sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 24$ and $d = 16$.\n    Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 62$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} u_{62} &= u_1 + (62-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 + (62-1)(16) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have $v_1 = 8$ and $v_4 = u_{13}$.\n    Using the $n$th term formula $v_n = v_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 4$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v_4 &= v_1r^{4-1} \\hspace{1.15em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8r^{4-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8r^3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$ with $n = 13$, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} u_{13} &= u_1 + (13-1)d \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 + (13-1)(16) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 216\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    v_4 = u_{13} \\hspace{2.6em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    8r^3 = 216 \\hspace{3.05em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $8r^3 = 216$ for $r$ using G.D.C., we\n    obtain  $r = 3$"},{"ref":"AA633","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA633\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the sum $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_{k = 4}^l (2k-3)$, where $l$ is\na positive integer greater than $4$.\n\n1.  Write down the first three terms of the series. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Write down the number of terms in the series. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Given that $S = 725$, find the value of $l$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $5,\\, 7,\\, 9$\n\n2.\n    $l-3$\n\n3.  From part (a), we have an arithmetic sequence with the first term\n    $u_1 = 5$ and the common difference $d = 2$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with $n = l-3$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S &= S_{l-3} \\\\[11pt]\n    \\hspace{4.5em}725 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{l-3}{2}\\bigg](2u_1 + ((l-3)-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    725 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{l-3}{2}\\bigg](2(5) + (l-4)(2)) \\\\[6pt]\n    725 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{l-3}{2}\\bigg](2 + 2l) \\\\[12pt]\n    725 &= (l-3)(l+1) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{4.65em} l = 28 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA634","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA634\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nLet $u_n = 5n-1$, for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Using sigma notation, write down an expression for\n        $u_1 + u_2 + u_3 + \\dots + u_{10}$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of the sum from **part (a) (i)**. [4 marks]\n\nA geometric sequence is defined by $v_n = 5\\times 2^{n-1}$, for\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n2.  Find the value of the sum of the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{k = 1}^6 \\hspace{0.1em}v_k$.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using sigma notation, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        u_1 + u_2 + u_3 + \\dots + u_{10} &= \\sum_{k=1}^{10} u_k \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^{10} (5k-1) \\hspace{8em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  If we rewrite $u_n = 5n-1$ in the form $u_n = u_1 + (n-1)d$, we\n        have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} u_n &= 4 + (n-1)(5)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $u_n$ is\n        an arithmetic sequence with the first term $u_1 = 4$ and\\\n        the common difference $d = 5$.\n\n        Using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n        $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n        $n=10$\n\n        we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sum_{k=1}^{10} (5k-1) &= S_{10} \\\\[3pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2u_1 + (10-1)d) \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{10}{2}(2(4) + (10-1)(5)) \\\\[14pt]\n        &= 265\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.3em} v_1 &= 5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{the first term}] \\\\[9pt]\n    r &= 2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{the common ratio}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{v_1(r^n-1)}{r-1}$ with\n    $n=6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum_{k = 1}^6 5\\times 2^{k-1} &= S_6 \\\\[3pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{v_1(r^6-1)}{r-1} \\hspace{3.3em}  \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5\\hspace{0.05em}(2^6-1)}{2-1} \\\\[15pt]\n    &= 315\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA636","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA636\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe table shows the first four terms of three sequences: $u_n$, $v_n$,\nand $w_n$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/c39694c1cf7513ffce115791e6b0f1c54c230963.svg\" alt=\"c39694c1cf7513ffce115791e6b0f1c54c230963.svg\">\n\n1.  State which sequence is\n    1.  arithmetic;\n\n    2.  geometric. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the sum of the first $50$ terms of the arithmetic sequence.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the exact value of the $13$th term of the geometric sequence.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $v_n$\n\n    2.\n        $w_n$\n\n2.  The common difference of the arithmetic sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    d &= v_2 - v_1 \\hspace{0.75em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 24 - 12 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}(2v_1 + (n-1)d)$ with\n    $n=50$,\n\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} S_{50} &= \\dfrac{50}{2}(2v_1 + (50-1)d) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{50}{2}(2(12) + (50-1)(12)) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 15\\hspace{0.15em}300\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The common ratio of the geometric sequence is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{w_2}{w_1} \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{24}{12} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula\n    $w_n = w_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 13$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.1em} w_{13} &= w_1r^{13-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12\\hspace{0.05em}(2)^{13-1} \\hspace{3.8em} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 49\\hspace{0.15em}152\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA637","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA637\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA bouncy ball is dropped from a height of $2$ metres onto a concrete floor. After hitting the floor, the ball rebounds back up to $80$\\% of it's previous height, and this pattern continues on repeatedly, until coming to rest.\n\n1.  Show that the total distance travelled by the ball until coming to rest can be expressed by\n\n    $$\n    2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots\n    $$\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total distance travelled by the ball.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    | |$n$|$\\hspace{1.5em}0\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}1\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}2\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}3\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n    |:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n    |distance travelled in metres|$n$th fall|2|$2(0.8)$|$2(0.8)^2$|$2(0.8)^3$|\n    | |$n$th rebound|0|$2(0.8)$|$2(0.8)^2$|$2(0.8)^3$|\n\n    $$\\\\[-1.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Combining the fall distances (top row) with the non-zero rebound distances (bottom row) gives the total distance travelled by the ball.\n    $$\\\\[-2.5em]$$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\boxed{2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots}\\hspace{1em}\\text{... as required.} \\tag*{\\textbf{AG}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Applying the **sum of infinite geometric series (1.3)**, with $u_1 = 4$ and $r = 0.8$, to find the total distance travelled by the ball.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S &= 2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Adding and subtracting $2$ regroups the leading term as $4$, allowing the series to be rewritten as a single geometric series starting from $n=0$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S &= \\textcolor{blue}{(-2+2)} + 2 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -2 + \\left[4 + 4(0.8) + 4(0.8)^2 + 4(0.8)^3 + \\cdots\\right] \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= -2 + \\left[\\dfrac{4}{1 - 0.8}\\right] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[12pt]\n        S &= \\boxed{18 \\text{ metres}} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA638","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA638\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the infinite geometric sequence $9000$, $-7200$, $5760$,\n$-4608$, ...\n\n1.  Find the common ratio. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the $25$th term. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find the **exact** sum of the infinite sequence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = 9000$ and $u_2 = -7200$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{7200}{9000} \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{4}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 25$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{25} &= u_1r^{25-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 9000\\left(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^{25-1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 42.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{9000}{1-(-4/5)} \\hspace{1.9em} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 5000\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA639","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA639\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nPeter is playing on a swing during a school lunch break. The height of\nthe first swing was $2$ m and every subsequent\nswing was $84$ % of the previous one. Peter's\nfriend, Ronald, gives him a push whenever the height falls below\n$1$ m.\n\n1.  Find the height of the third swing. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the number of swings before Ronald gives Peter a push.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Calculate the total height of swings if Peter is left to swing until\n    coming\\\n    to rest. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 2$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 0.84$.\n\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n = 3$,\n    we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_3 &= u_1r^{3-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\hspace{0.05em}(0.84)^{3-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 1.41 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Solving the inequality $u_n < 1$ for $n$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_1r^{n-1} &< 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} 2\\hspace{0.05em}(0.84)^{n-1} &< 1 \\\\[7pt]\n    4.98 &< n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n       Hence\n    the swing needs a push after\n     $5$\n    swings.\n\n3.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{2}{1-0.84} \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 12.5 \\text{ m}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA640","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA640\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe sides of a square are $8$ cm long. A new\nsquare is formed by joining the midpoints of the adjacent sides and two\nof the resulting triangles are shaded as shown. This process is repeated\n$5$ more times to form the right hand diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA640.PNG\" alt=\"AA640\">\n\n1.  Find the total area of the shaded region in the right hand diagram\n    above. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the total area of the shaded region if the process is repeated\n    indefinitely.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The side length of the first pair of shaded triangles is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{side length} &= \\dfrac{8}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm} \\hspace{2.9em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    their total area is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.9em} \\text{area} &= 2\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(4)\\right] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 16\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Similarly, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/e6f45d593aa89a3c2cdb821ce1c78f8d901f645f.svg\" alt=\"e6f45d593aa89a3c2cdb821ce1c78f8d901f645f.svg\">\n\n    Hence the area of successive pairs of shaded triangles form a\n    geometric $\\text{sequence}$ with the first term $u_1 = 16$\n    and the common ratio $r = 1/2$.\n\n    Hence, using the sum of $n$ terms formula\n    $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$ with $n = 6$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} S_6 &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^6)}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{16\\hspace{0.05em}(1-(1/2)^6)}{1-1/2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 31.5 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{16}{1-1/2} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 32 \\text{ cm}^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA668","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA668\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, the sum of the 2nd and 6th term is $32$.\n\nGiven that the sum of the first six terms is $120$, determine the first\nterm and common difference of the sequence.","markScheme":"Let $u_1$ be the first term of the arithmetic sequence and $d$ be the\ncommon difference. The first six terms are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1 \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+2d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+3d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+4d \\hspace{0.15em},\\hspace{0.15em} u_1+5d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_2 + u_6= 32\\qquad s_6=120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nthus, we can form two\nequations\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2u_1+6d &= 32 \\\\[5pt]\n6u_1+15d &= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can solve this system of\nlinear equations by using the elimination method or using our G.D.C. We\nget\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_1=40 \\qquad d = -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA724","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA724\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA $3$D printer builds a set of $49$ Ei$\\text{f}$fel Tower\nReplicas in different sizes. The height of the largest tower in this set\nis $64$ cm. The heights of successive smaller\ntowers are $95$ % of the preceding larger tower,\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/AA724a.PNG\" alt=\"AA724a\">\n\n1.  Find the height of the smallest tower in this set. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the **total** height if all $49$ towers were placed one on top\n    of another. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have a geometric sequence with the first term $u_1 = 64$ and the\n    common ratio $r = 0.95$.\n\n    Hence, using the $n$th term formula $u_n = u_1r^{n-1}$ with $n=49$,\n    we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    u_{49} &= u_1r^{49-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 64(0.95)^{49-1} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 5.46 \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the sum of $n$ terms formula $S_n = \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^n)}{1-r}$\n    with $n=49$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.9em} S_{49} &= \\dfrac{u_1(1-r^{49})}{1-r} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{64\\hspace{0.05em}(1-0.95^{49})}{1-0.95} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 1176.33 \\\\[16pt]\n    &\\approx 1180 \\text{ cm}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA748","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA748\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe sum of the first three terms of a geometric sequence is $92.5$, and\nthe sum of the infinite sequence is $160$. Find the common ratio.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_3 &= 92.5 \\hspace{4.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 92.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{\\infty} &= 160 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} &= 160 \\\\[5pt]\nu_1 &= 160(1-r)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{u_1(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 92.5 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{160(1-r)(1-r^3)}{1-r} &= 92.5 \\hspace{8.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n160(1-r^3) &= 92.5 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{9.3em} r = 0.75 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA832","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA832\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nAn arithmetic sequence has first term $45$ and common difference $-1.5$.\n\n1.  Given that the $k$th term of the sequence is zero, find the value of\n    $k$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $S_n$ denote the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence.\n\n2.  Find the maximum value of $S_n$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The $k$th term in the sequence is given by $u_k = u_1 + (k-1)d$ with\n    $u_1 = 45$ and $d = -1.5$.\n\n    Since $u_k = 0$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    45 + (k-1)(-1.5) &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}k = 31 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Since the common difference $d$ is negative, the terms in the\n    sequence are decreasing. From part (a), we found that the $31$st\n    term in the sequence is zero. Hence, any term after this would be\n    negative. Therefore, the sum $S_n$ is maximised when $n = 30$ or\n    $n = 31$ (as $u_{31}=0$).\n\n    Using the formula $S_n = \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(u_1 + u_n \\right)$ with\n    $n = 31$, we find that the maximum value of $S_n$ is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(u_{1} + u_{31}) \\hspace{3em}\\\\[10pt]\n    S_{31} &= \\dfrac{31}{2}(45 + 0) \\hspace{3em}\\\\[10pt]\n    &= 697.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA923","marks":"18","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA923\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nThe first three terms of an infinite sequence, in order, are \n\n$$2\\ln x,\\,\\, q\\ln x,\\,\\, \\ln \\sqrt{x}\\,\\,\\,  \\text{ where $\\ x > 0$.} $$\n\nFirst consider the case in which the series is geometric.\n\n1. 1. Find the possible values of $q$.\n\n    2. Hence or otherwise, show that the series is convergent. [3 marks]\n\n2. Given that $q>0$ and $S_\\infty=8\\ln{3}$, find the value of $x$. [3 marks]\n\nNow suppose that the series is arithmetic.\n\n3. 1. Show that $q=\\dfrac{5}{4}$.\n\n    2. Write down the common difference in the form $m\\ln x$, where $m \\in \\mathbb{Q}$. [4 marks]\n\n4. Given that the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence is $\\ln \\sqrt{x^5}$, find the value of $n$. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Given that the sequence is geometric, we have for the common ratio\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{q\\ln x}{2\\ln x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &= \\dfrac{q}{2}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Similarly,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        r &= \\dfrac{u_3}{u_2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{q}{2} &= \\dfrac{\\ln \\sqrt{x}}{q\\ln x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{q}{2} &= \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{2}\\ln {x}}{q\\ln x} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        \\dfrac{q}{2} &= \\dfrac{1}{2q} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        q^2 &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.49em}\\boxed{q= \\pm 1}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. Given that $r=\\dfrac{q}{2}$, and $q= \\pm 1$, we have $r = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}$. Hence $|r| < 1$.\n\n        $$\\text{In both cases the series converges.}$$\n\n2. Given that $q>0$, $r = \\dfrac{1}{2}$. \n\n    Using the formula for the sum of an infinite geometric sequence, we~get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    8 \\ln 3 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln x}{1-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    8 \\ln 3 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln x}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    8 \\ln 3 &= 4\\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    2\\ln 3 &= \\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\ln 3^2 &= \\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\ln 9 &= \\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.54em}\\boxed{x = 9} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. 1. First we find the common difference $d$ as follows\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d &= u_2 - u_1\\\\[10pt]\n\n        d &= q\\ln x - 2\\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n        d &= (q-2)\\ln x\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Similarly,\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d &= u_3 - u_2\\\\[10pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &= \\ln\\sqrt{x} - q\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &= \\ln x^{1/2} - q\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln x - q\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        (q-2)\\ln x &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2} - q\\bigg)\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n        q-2 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}-q \\\\[8pt]\n\n        2q &= \\dfrac{1}{2} + 2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n        2q &= \\dfrac{5}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.535em}\\boxed{q = \\dfrac{5}{4}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. Given that $d = (q-2)\\ln x$ and $q = \\dfrac{5}{4}$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        d &= \\left(\\dfrac{5}{4}-2\\right)\\ln x \\\\[10pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.535em}\\boxed{d = -\\dfrac{3}{4}\\ln x}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n4. Using the formula for the sum of $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S_n &= \\dfrac{n}{2}(2u_1 + (n-1)d) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\ln \\sqrt{x^5} &= \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(2\\hspace{0.1em}(2\\ln x) + (n-1)\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\ln x \\right)\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\ln x^{5/2} &= \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(\\left(4\\ln x + \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)(n-1)\\ln x\\right) \\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{5}{2}\\ln x &= \\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(4 - \\dfrac{3(n-1)}{4}\\right)\\ln x \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5 &= n\\left(4 - \\dfrac{3(n-1)}{4}\\right) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5 &= 4n - \\dfrac{3n(n-1)}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    20 &= 16n - 3n(n-1) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    20 &= 16n - 3n^2 + 3n \\\\[8pt]\n\n    3n^2 -19n +20 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    3n^2 -15n -4n +20 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    3n(n-5) - 4(n-5) &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    (3n-4)(n-5) &= 0\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The solutions to the quadratic equation are $n=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ and $n=5$.\n\n    Since $n$ must be an integer, we have $n = 5$"},{"ref":"AA986","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA986\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ such that $0 < r < 1$.\n\n1. Show that $u_n - u_{n+1} = u_n(1-r)$.[2 marks]\n\nA geometric sequence has a first term of 150 and a second term of 120.\n\n2. Find the smallest value of $n$ such that $u_n - u_{n+1} < 1$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_n - u_{n+1} &= u_1r^{n-1} - u_1r^{n} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= u_1r^{n-1}(1-r) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-4.5em}\\boxed{u_n - u_{n+1} = u_n(1-r)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. For a geometric sequence, we have $r=\\dfrac{u_2}{u_1}$. Therefore $r = \\dfrac{120}{150}$.\n\n    Hence $u_1 = 150$ and $r=0.8$.\n\n    We are solving the inequality $u_n - u_{n+1} < 1$. Using our result from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    u_n(1-r) &< 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    u_n(1-0.8) &< 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    150(0.8)^{n-1}(0.2) &< 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    150(0.8)^{n-1} &< 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Sketching the graphs of $y=150(0.8)^{x-1}$ and $y=5$ on the G.D.C. we look for the smallest integer value of $x$ for which $y<5\\,$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Sequences%20%26%20Series/images/aa986.PNG\" alt=\"aa986\">\n\n    The smallest integer greater than $16.2$ is $17$. Hence\n\n    $$\\boxed{\\text{The smallest value of $n$ such that $u_n - u_{n+1}<1$ is $n=17$.}}$$"}]},{"name":"Systems of Linear Equations","questions":[{"ref":"AA1012","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1012\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nConsider three planes represented by the following system of equations: \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\Pi_1: -2x + ay + 4z &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n\\Pi_2: x + by - 2z &= -1 \\\\[6pt]\n\n\\Pi_3: 2x - y + cz &= 3\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWhere $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. State the values of $a$, $b$ and $c$ for which\n\n    1. The system has infinite solutions.\n\n    2. The system is inconsistent. [7 marks]\n\n2. In the case where the system has infinite solutions, describe the geometric relationship between the three planes. [2 marks]\n\n3. In the case where the system is inconsistent, identify one of the geometric relationships that could exist between the three planes.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented matrix, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/aa1012a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1012a\">\n\n    Hence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/aa1012b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1012b\">\n\n    1. From row 2, if the system has infinite solutions, we must have $a + 2b = 0$. \n\n        This implies that if $a = -2b$, there are infinite solutions to the system.\n\n        However, we also note from row 3, that if $a - 1 = 0$ and $c + 4 = 0$, then the system is inconsistent. In this case we have $a = 1$ and $c = -4$.\n\n        There are infinite solutions to the system if $a = -2b$, $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$ unless $a=1$, $b=-0.5$ and $c=-4$.\n\n    2.  As noted in part (i), if $a = 1$ and $c = -4$, the system is inconsistent. \n\n        However, we also note that if $a \\neq 1$ and $c = -4$, Gaussian Elimination leads to\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/aa1021b.png\" alt=\"aa1021b\">    \n\n        In this case, we can also have an inconsistent system. \n\n        From part (a), if $a = -2b$ then the system is consistent (has infinite solutions), however consider when $a \\neq -2b$.\n\n        It follows that $a+2b =k$, where $k \\neq 0$, this leads to\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/aa1012a2.png\" alt=\"aa1012a2\">\n\n        Using row 2\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        ky &=0 \\\\\n\n        \\intertext{As $k \\neq 0$ then}\n\n        y &= 0 \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using row 3, this value of $y$ shows an inconsistency as shown below.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (a-1)y &=5 \\\\\n\n        (a-1)\\times0 &= 5 \\\\\n\n        0 &= 5 \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence...\n\n        $\\text{The system is inconsistent if } a = 1 \\text{ and } c = -4, b \\in \\mathbb{R} \\text{ or if } a \\neq 1, a \\neq -2b \\text{ and } c = -4.$\n\n2. From (a) (i), we note that $\\Pi_1$ is a multiple of $\\Pi_2$. They are therefore equations representing the same plane.\n\n    $\\Pi_1 \\text{ and } \\Pi_2 \\text{ are coincidental, }\\Pi_3 \\text{ intersects both planes in a straight line.}$\n\n3. In total there are 4 different geometric realtionships between the planes when the system is inconsistent.\n\n    First, from part (a) (i) we note that the system is inconsistent if $a=1$, $b=-0.5$ and $c=-4$. In this case,\n\n    $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\Pi_1 \\text{ and } \\Pi_2 \\text{ are coincidental and } \\Pi_3 \\text{ is parallel to both planes.}}\\hspace{0.525em}$\n\n    Second, from (a) (ii), we have one scenario where $a=1$, $b \\neq -0.5$ and $c=-4$. In this case, \n\n    $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\Pi_1 \\text{ and } \\Pi_3 \\text{ are parallel and } \\Pi_2 \\text{ intersects both planes.}}\\hspace{0.525em}$\n\n    Thirdly, if $a \\neq 1$, $b=-0.5$ and $c=-4$,\n\n    $\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\Pi_2 \\text{ and } \\Pi_3 \\text{ are parallel and } \\Pi_1 \\text{ intersects both planes.}}\\hspace{0.525em}$    \n\n    Finally, if $a \\neq 1$, $a \\neq -2b$ and $c=-4$, then $\\Pi_1$ intersects $\\Pi_2$ on a line, $\\Pi_1$ intersects $\\Pi_3$ on a different line and $\\Pi_2$ intersects $\\Pi_3$ on another different line. Additionally,$\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\text{All three of these lines of intersection are parallel.}}\\hspace{0.525em}$"},{"ref":"AA104","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA104\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe system of equations given below represents three planes in space.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 5z &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n-2x + y - 6z &= -1 \\\\[6pt]\n2y + 8z &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Show that this system of equations has an infinite number of\n    solutions. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the parametric equations of the line of intersection of the\n    three planes. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented\n    matrix, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/e92843ccd123af5e4a99f63d9b0726fa3e606968.svg\" alt=\"e92843ccd123af5e4a99f63d9b0726fa3e606968.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/e2d7661b43a236c006ef48f0c2b01b8969087034.svg\" alt=\"e2d7661b43a236c006ef48f0c2b01b8969087034.svg\">\n\n    The last row of zeros tells us that the system is consistent. As there are only two pivot positions corresponding to $x$ and $y$, the variable $z$ is free. Hence the system has an infinite number of solutions.\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/9e7ed3301e98f940e211c9d9825320385d1b6446.svg\" alt=\"9e7ed3301e98f940e211c9d9825320385d1b6446.svg\">\n\n    If we express $x$ and $y$ in term of $z = t$, we obtain $x = 2 - 5t$\n    and $y = 3 - 4t$.\n\n    Hence the parametric equations of the line of intersection of the\n    $3$ planes are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x = 2-5t,\\hspace{0.5em}y = 3-4t,\\hspace{0.5em}z = t\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    where $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$."},{"ref":"AA105","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA105\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3z &= -2 \\\\[6pt]\n-3x + y + 6z &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n2x - 2y + (a-4)z &= b-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find conditions on $a$ and $b$ for which\n    1.  the system has no solutions;\n\n    2.  the system has only one solution;\n    3.  the system has an infinite number of solutions. [6 marks]\n\n2.  In the case where the number of solutions is infinite, find the\n    general\\\n    solution of the system of equations in Cartesian form. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented\n    matrix, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/fc1722646826b747a78d8a717d7f3e24269c7335.svg\" alt=\"fc1722646826b747a78d8a717d7f3e24269c7335.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/d5e5b20529826e7a89602916ebcc5c019737e1ea.svg\" alt=\"d5e5b20529826e7a89602916ebcc5c019737e1ea.svg\">\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/846a88889458337ab2aeba1c647b2fdea4de2812.svg\" alt=\"846a88889458337ab2aeba1c647b2fdea4de2812.svg\">\n\n    The system has no solutions if\n\n    1.\n        $a = 4 \\text{ and } b \\neq 5$\n\n    The system has only one solution if\n\n    2.\n         $a \\neq 4 \\text{ and } b \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n    The system has an infinite number of solutions if\n\n    3.\n         $a = 4 \\text{ and } b = 5$\n\n2.  For $a = 4$ and $b = 5$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/8dacc6559227ad5fef6e1aeaeb78b17ac463976c.svg\" alt=\"8dacc6559227ad5fef6e1aeaeb78b17ac463976c.svg\">\n\n    Hence the general solution of the system of equations in Cartesian\n    form is\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{x + 2}{3} = \\dfrac{y + 3}{3} = z\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA106","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA106\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= -1 \\\\[6pt]\n4x + 2y + z &= 3 \\\\[6pt]\n9x + 3y &= p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Show that this system does not have a unique solution for any value\n    of $p$. [4 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Determine the value of $p$ for which the system is consistent.\n\n    2.  For this value of $p$, find the general solution of the system.\n        [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented\n    matrix, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/9f7fa9719e458a709cb1464a249710dcc108a8a6.svg\" alt=\"9f7fa9719e458a709cb1464a249710dcc108a8a6.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/ba6fbb5338a0222b47ef58fe6256b45e3ba64074.svg\" alt=\"ba6fbb5338a0222b47ef58fe6256b45e3ba64074.svg\">\n\n    If $p \\neq 12$, the system is inconsistent and has no solutions. For otherwise, the~system is consistent and $z$ is a free variable. Hence, for any given value of $p$, the system either has no solutions or it has infinite number of solutions.\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $p = 12$\n\n    2.  For $p = 12$, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/bd86febfa147c1cf21b65e965d8b671ce0dbd029.svg\" alt=\"bd86febfa147c1cf21b65e965d8b671ce0dbd029.svg\">\n\n        Letting $z = t$ and using the method of back-substitution, we\n        obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        -2y - 3z &= 7 \\\\[11pt]\n        -2y - 3t &= 7 \\\\[5pt]\n        y &= -\\dfrac{7}{2} - \\dfrac{3t}{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        and\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        x + y + z &= -1 \\\\[5pt]\n        x +\\left[-\\dfrac{7}{2} - \\dfrac{3t}{2}\\right] + t &= -1 \\\\[1pt]\n        x &= \\dfrac{5}{2} + \\dfrac{t}{2} \\hspace{5.5em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence the general solution of the system in parametric form is\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         &x = \\dfrac{5}{2} + \\dfrac{t}{2}, \\hspace{0.5em} y = -\\dfrac{7}{2} - \\dfrac{3t}{2}, \\hspace{0.5em} z = t\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n        where $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$."},{"ref":"AA107","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA107\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + 4z &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n3x + 2y + 16z &= 5 \\\\[6pt]\n4x + 2y + (a-1)z &= b-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find conditions on $a$ and $b$ for which\n    1.  the system has no solutions;\n\n    2.  the system has only one solution;\n    3.  the system has an infinite number of solutions. [6 marks]\n\n2.  In the case where the number of solutions is infinite, find the\n    general\\\n    solution of the system of equations in Cartesian form. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented\n    matrix, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/71754659bebe16a79ad946ef4aafc3d04e0565c7.svg\" alt=\"71754659bebe16a79ad946ef4aafc3d04e0565c7.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/038e3e50a616d564956e685040a3632159834164.svg\" alt=\"038e3e50a616d564956e685040a3632159834164.svg\">\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/87fa5baaa0e247b59e3412d3ed2d320e73681f15.svg\" alt=\"87fa5baaa0e247b59e3412d3ed2d320e73681f15.svg\">\n\n    1.  The system has no solutions if\n         $a = 25 \\text{ and } b \\neq 12$\n\n    2.  The system has only one solution if\n         $a \\neq 25 \\text{ and } b \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n    3.  The system has an infinite number of solutions if\n         $a = 25 \\text{ and } b = 12$\n\n2.  For $a = 25$ and $b = 12$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/6539968a8c5ba7b87ecfd3a27ed70dbb2c225401.svg\" alt=\"6539968a8c5ba7b87ecfd3a27ed70dbb2c225401.svg\">\n\n    Using the method of back-substitution, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -y + 4z &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 4z - 2 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x + (4z - 2) + 4z &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= 3 - 8z \\hspace{4em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the general solution\n    of the system of equations in Cartesian form is\n\n    $$\n    \\dfrac{3-x}{8} = \\dfrac{y + 2}{4} = z\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA644","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA644\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe system of equations given below represents three planes in space.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y - 2z &= 4 \\\\[6pt]\n3x + 5y - 4z &= 9 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{8em} 4x + 6y + az &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFind the set of values of\n$a$ and $b$ such that the three planes have no points of\\\nintersection.","markScheme":"If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented matrix, we\nhave\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/a778d4ec606218beb371bd39dc87b29995594e1e.svg\" alt=\"a778d4ec606218beb371bd39dc87b29995594e1e.svg\">\n\nHence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination (row reduction), we get\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/6253468268c59bd2ef1a8710c34fc0674230f053.svg\" alt=\"6253468268c59bd2ef1a8710c34fc0674230f053.svg\">\n\nTherefore, the three planes have no points of intersection if\n $a = -4 \\text{ \\& } b \\neq 10$"},{"ref":"AA674","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA674\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe system of equations given below represents three planes in space.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3y + 2z &= 5 \\\\[6pt]\n3x + 5y + az &= b \\hspace{3em} [\\text{where $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{8em} 4x + 2y - 3z &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFind the set of values of\n$a$ and $b$ such that the three planes have exactly one intersection\npoint.","markScheme":"If we convert the given system of equations into an augmented matrix, we\nhave\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/80fc0ab3f375c653f4f6b65da0848111f53434b1.svg\" alt=\"80fc0ab3f375c653f4f6b65da0848111f53434b1.svg\">\n\nHence, using the method of Gaussian Elimination (row reduction), we get\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Systems%20of%20Linear%20Equations/images/4d136862891148012690049e8033042f467da733.svg\" alt=\"4d136862891148012690049e8033042f467da733.svg\">\n\nThe three planes have exactly one intersection point if\n $a \\neq -5 \\text{  \\&  } b \\in \\mathbb{R}$"}]},{"name":"The Binomial Theorem","questions":[{"ref":"AA057","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA057\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nExpand $(2x - 3)^4$ in descending powers of $x$ and simplify your\nanswer.","markScheme":"Using Pascal triangle\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/The%20Binomial%20Theorem/images/d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\" alt=\"d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\">\n\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} (2x - 3)^4 &= (2x)^4 + 4(2x)^3(-3) + 6(2x)^2(-3)^2 + 4(2x)(-3)^3 + (-3)^4 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 + 4(8x^3)(-3) + 6(4x^2)(9) + 4(2x)(-27) + 81 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 - 96x^3 + 216x^2 - 216x + 81\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA058","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA058\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nExpand $(2x + 1)^4$ in descending powers of $x$ and simplify your\nanswer.","markScheme":"Using Pascal triangle\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/The%20Binomial%20Theorem/images/d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\" alt=\"d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\">\n\nwe get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} (2x + 1)^4 &= (2x)^4 + 4(2x)^3(1) + 6(2x)^2(1)^2 + 4(2x)(1)^3 + (1)^4 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 + 4(8x^3) + 6(4x^2) + 4(2x) + 1 \\\\[9pt]\n&= 16x^4 + 32x^3 + 24x^2 + 8x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA059","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA059\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe third term, in descending powers of $x$, in the expansion of $(x+p)^8$ is $252x^6$. Find the\npossible values of $p$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} 252x^6 &= \\binom{8}{2}\\big(x^{8-2}\\big)\\big(p^2\\big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $p$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n252x^6 &= 28\\big(x^6\\big)\\big(p^2\\big) \\\\[9pt]\n252 &= 28p^2 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{9.05em} p = \\pm 3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA060","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA060\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the expansion of\n$\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{2} + \\dfrac{a}{x}\\right)^6$. The constant term is\n$960$.\n\nFind the possible values of $a$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.5em} 960 &= \\binom{6}{4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\bigg]^{6-4}\\bigg[\\dfrac{a}{x}\\bigg]^4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $a$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n960 &= 15\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^4}{4}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{a^4}{x^4}\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n960 &= \\dfrac{15a^4}{4} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{7.3em} a = \\pm 4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA062","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA062\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $(2x-1)^9$.\n\n1.  Write down the number of terms in this expansion. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the coefficient of the term in $x^2$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of terms in this expansion is\n     $10$\n\n2.  The term in $x^2$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{9}{7} \\big[(2x)^{9-7}\\big]\\big[(-1)^7\\big] &= 36\\big[4x^2\\big]\\big[-1\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -144x^2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^2$\n    is  $-144$"},{"ref":"AA063","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA063\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Write down the quadratic expression $3x^2 + 5x - 2$ in the form\n    $(ax-b)(x+c)$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, find the coefficient of the term in $x^9$ in\n    the expansion\\\n    of $(3x^2+5x-2)^5$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $(3x - 1)(x + 2)$\n\n2.  Using the binomial theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (3x^2+5x-2)^5 &= (3x-1)^5(x+2)^5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\left[\\sum_{k = 0}^5 \\binom{5}{k}\\big[(3x)^{5-k}\\big]\\big[(-1)^k\\big]\\right]\\left[\\sum_{k=0}^5\\binom{5}{k}\\big(x^{5-k}\\big)\\big(2^k\\big)\\right]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^9$ in the expansion of\n    $(3x^2+5x-2)^5$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    a_9 &= \\left[\\binom{5}{0}\\big[3^{5-0}\\big]\\big[(-1)^0\\big]\\right]\\left[\\binom{5}{1}\\big(2^1\\big)\\right] + \\left[\\binom{5}{1}\\big[3^{5-1}\\big]\\big[(-1)^1\\big]\\right]\\left[\\binom{5}{0}\\big(2^0\\big)\\right] \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\big[(1)(3^5)(1)\\big]\\big[(5)(2)\\big] + \\big[(5)(3^4)(-1)\\big]\\big[(1)(1)\\big] \\\\[15pt]\n    &= 2025\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA066","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA066\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.  Write down and simplify the expansion of $(3-x)^5$ in descending\n    order of powers of $x$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Hence find the exact value of $(2.9)^5$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the binomial theorem, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (3-x)^5 &= ((-x) + 3)^5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sum_{k = 0}^5 \\binom{5}{k}\\big[(-x)^{5-k}\\big]\\big[3^k\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -x^5 + 15x^4 - 90x^3 + 270x^2 - 405x + 243\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Hence, substituting $x = 0.1$ in the expression $(1)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2.9)^5 &=  -(0.1)^5 + 15(0.1)^4 - 90(0.1)^3 + 270(0.1)^2 - 405(0.1) + 243 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -0.00001 + 0.0015 - 0.09 + 2.7 - 40.5 + 243 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -40.59001 + 245.7015 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 205.11149\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA067","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA067\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn the expansion of $(2x + 1)^n$, the coefficient of the term in $x^2$\nis $40n$, where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. Find $n$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40nx^2 &= \\binom{n}{2}\\big[1^{n-2}\\big]\\big[(2x)^2\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $n$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40nx^2 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{n!}{2!(n-2)!}\\bigg]\\big[4x^2\\big] \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[18pt]\n40n &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\\bigg]\\big[4\\big] \\\\[18pt]\n20n &= n(n-1) \\\\[18pt]\n0 &= n(n - 21) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nn =21 \\hspace{5.1em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA068","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA068\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nIn the expansion of $x(2x + 1)^n$, the coefficient of the term in $x^3$\nis $20n$, where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. Find $n$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20nx^3 &= x\\binom{n}{2}\\big[1^{n-2}\\big]\\big[(2x)^2\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $n$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20nx^3 &= x\\bigg[\\dfrac{n!}{2!(n-2)!}\\bigg]\\big[4x^2\\big] \\hspace{0.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n20nx^3 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\\bigg]\\big[4x^3\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n10n &= n(n-1) \\\\[18pt]\n0 &= n(n - 11) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nn =11 \\hspace{5.7em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA069","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA069\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nGiven that $(1 + x)^3(1 + px)^4 = 1 + qx + 93x^2 + \\dots + p^4x^7$, find\nthe possible values of $p$ and $q$.","markScheme":"Using Pascal triangle\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/The%20Binomial%20Theorem/images/d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\" alt=\"d6143401ad9783a3554c63b7ab804d89d1d7b5f1.svg\">\n\nwe have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + qx + 93x^2 + \\dots + p^4x^7 &= (1 + x)^3(1 + px)^4 \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\big(1 + 3x + 3x^2\\hspace{-0.15em} + x^3\\big)\\hspace{-0.15em}\\big(1 + 4px + 6p^2x^2\\hspace{-0.15em} + 4p^3x^3\\hspace{-0.15em} + p^4x^4\\big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nqx &= 4px + 3x \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[6pt]\nq &= 4p + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} 93x^2 &= 6p^2x^2 + 12px^2 + 3x^2 \\\\[6pt]\n93 &= 6p^2 + 12p + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(2)$ for $p$, we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6p^2 + 12p + 3 &= 93 \\\\[6pt]\n6p^2 + 12p - 90 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\np^2 + 2p - 15 &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\n(p + 5)(p - 3) &= 0 \\\\[7pt]\np &= -5,\\,3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, substituting $p = -5,\\,3$ in the\nequation $(1)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.1em} q &= 4(-5) + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n&= -17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.3em} q &= 4(3) + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n $p = -5,\\,q=-17$\nand  $p = 3,\\,q=15$"},{"ref":"AA071","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA071\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\bigg(x^3+\\dfrac{2}{x}\\bigg)^8$.\n\n1.  Write down the number of terms in this expansion. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the coefficient of the term in $x^4$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of terms in this expansion is\n     $9$\n\n2.  The term in $x^4$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{8}{5} \\big[x^3\\big]^{8-5} \\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{x}\\bigg]^5&= 56\\big[x^9\\big]\\bigg[\\dfrac{32}{x^5}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1792x^4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^4$\n    is  $1792$"},{"ref":"AA072","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA072\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\bigg(3x + \\dfrac{p}{x}\\bigg)^8$, where\n$p > 0$. The coefficient of the term\n\nin $x^4$ is equal to the coefficient of the term in $x^6$. Find $p$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigg(3x+\\dfrac{p}{x}\\bigg)^8 &= \\sum_{k=0}^8 \\binom{8}{k} \\big[3x\\big]^{8-k}\\bigg[\\dfrac{p}{x}\\bigg]^k \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sum_{k=0}^8 \\binom{8}{k} \\big[3^{8-k}p^k\\big]\\big[x^{8-2k}\\big] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^4$}\\big] &= \\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^6$}\\big] \\hspace{5.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\binom{8}{2}\\big[3^{8-2}p^2\\big] &= \\binom{8}{1}\\big[3^{8-1}p\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n28\\big[3^6p^2\\big] &= 8\\big[3^7p\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n20\\hspace{0.15em}412\\hspace{0.05em}p^2 - 17\\hspace{0.15em}496\\hspace{0.05em}p &= 0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.1em} p = \\dfrac{6}{7}\n \\approx 0.857... \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$\n\n <!-- $$\n \\text{or} \\hspace{5.7em}\n $$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{6.6em} p \\approx 0.857 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$ -->"},{"ref":"AA073","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA073\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn the expansion of $px^2(5 + px)^8$, the coefficient of the term in\n$x^6$ is $3402$. Find the value of $p$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3402x^6 &= px^2\\binom{8}{4}\\big[5^{8-4}\\big]\\big[(px)^4\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, solving the equation $(1)$ for $p$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3402x^6 &= px^2\\big[70\\big]\\big[5^4\\big]\\big[p^4x^4\\big] \\hspace{1.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n3402 &= 43\\hspace{0.15em}750\\hspace{0.05em}p^5 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{5.3em} p =0.6 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA075","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA075\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nUse the extension of the binomial theorem for $n \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ to show that\n$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}} \\approx 1 + x + \\dfrac{x^2}{2}$,\n$|x| < 1$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}} &= (1+x)^{\\frac{1}{2}}(1-x)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fractional binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} (1+x)^{\\frac{1}{2}} &= \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\binom{\\scriptstyle{1/2}}{k} x^k \\hspace{3em} \\big[|x| < 1\\big] \\\\[3pt]\n&= 1 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)x + \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}-1\\bigg)\\dfrac{x^2}{2!} + \\cdots \\\\[7pt]\n&= 1 + \\dfrac{x}{2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{8} + \\cdots\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the\nfractional binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{6.8em} (1-x)^{-\\frac{1}{2}} &= \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\binom{\\scriptstyle{-1/2}}{k} (-x)^k \\hspace{3em} \\big[|x| < 1\\big] \\\\[3pt]\n&= 1 + \\left(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\hspace{-0.2em}(-x) + \\left(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\left(\\hspace{-0.3em}-\\dfrac{1}{2}-1\\right)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\dfrac{(-x)^2}{2!} + \\cdots \\\\[7pt]\n&= 1 + \\dfrac{x}{2} + \\dfrac{3x^2}{8} + \\cdots\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.7em} (1+x)^{\\frac{1}{2}}(1-x)^{-\\frac{1}{2}} &= \\bigg[1 + \\dfrac{x}{2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{8} + \\cdots\\bigg]\\bigg[1 + \\dfrac{x}{2} + \\dfrac{3x^2}{8} + \\cdots\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\left\\{\n\n\\begin{array}{rccccl}\n1 &+& \\dfrac{x}{2} &+& \\dfrac{3x^2}{8} & + \\cdots \\\\[12pt]\n&+& \\dfrac{x}{2} &+& \\dfrac{x^2}{4} & + \\cdots \\\\[12pt]\n&  &                    &-& \\dfrac{x^2}{8} & + \\cdots \\\\[12pt]\n\\end{array}\\right. \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1 + x + \\dfrac{x^2}{2} + \\cdots\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor $|x| < 1$, the\nterm in $x^k$ is small for $k \\geq 3$.\n\nHence we deduce\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}} \\approx 1 + x + \\dfrac{x^2}{2} \\hspace{3em} \\big[|x| < 1\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA076","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA076\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nUse the extension of the binomial theorem for $n \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ to show that\n$\\dfrac{x}{(1+x)^2} \\approx x - 2x^2 + 3x^3$,\n$|x| < 1$.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{x}{(1+x)^2} &= x(1+x)^{-2} \\hspace{2.3em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing\nthe fractional binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{3em} (1+x)^{-2} &= \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\binom{-2}{k} x^k \\hspace{3em} \\big[|x| < 1\\big] \\\\[3pt]\n&= 1 + (-2)x + \\dfrac{(-2)(-3)}{2!}x^2 + \\dfrac{(-2)(-3)(-4)}{3!}x^3 + \\cdots \\\\[15pt]\n&= 1 - 2x + 3x^2 - 4x^3 + \\cdots\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1em} x(1+x)^{-2} &= x(1 - 2x + 3x^2 - 4x^3 + \\cdots) \\\\[6pt]\n&= x - 2x^2 + 3x^3 - 4x^4 + \\cdots\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor $|x| < 1$, the\nterm in $x^k$ is small for $k \\geq 4$.\n\nHence we deduce\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.6em} \\dfrac{x}{(1+x)^2} \\approx x - 2x^2 + 3x^3 \\hspace{3em} \\big[|x| < 1\\big]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA084","marks":"25","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA084\n\n**Maximum mark: 25**\n\nJack and John have decided to play a game. They will be rolling a die\nseven times. One roll of a die is considered as one round of the game.\nOn each round, John agrees to pay Jack \\$4 if $1$ or $2$ is rolled, Jack\nagrees to pay John \\$2 if $3,4,5$ or $6$ is rolled, and who is paid wins\nthe round. In the end, who earns money wins the game.\n\n1.  Show that the probability that Jack wins exactly two rounds is\n    $\\dfrac{224}{729}$.  [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Explain why the total number of outcomes for the results of the\n        seven rounds is $128$.\n\n    2.  Expand $(1 + y)^7$ and choose a suitable value of $y$ to prove\n        that\n\n        $$\n        128 = \\binom{7}{0} + \\binom{7}{1} + \\binom{7}{2} + \\binom{7}{3} + \\binom{7}{4} + \\binom{7}{5} + \\binom{7}{6} + \\binom{7}{7}.\n        $$\n\n    3.  Give a meaning of the equality above in the context of the\n        seven\\\n        rounds.[4 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Find the expected amount of money earned by each player in the\n        game.\n\n    2.  Who is expected to win the game?\n    3.  Is this game fair? Justify your answer. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Jack and John have decided to play the game again.\n    1.  Find an expression for the probability that John wins five\n        rounds on the first game and two rounds on the second game. Give\n        your answer in the form\n\n        $$\n        \\binom{7}{r}^2\\bigg[\\frac{1}{3}\\bigg]^s\\bigg[\\frac{2}{3}\\bigg]^t\n        $$\n        where the values of $r,s$ and $t$ are to be found.\n\n    2.  Use your answer to (d) (i) and seven similar expressions to\n        write down the probability that John wins a total of seven\n        rounds over two games as the sum of eight probabilities.\n\n    3.  Hence prove that\n        $$\n        \\binom{14}{7} = \\sum_{k = 0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}^2.\n        $$\n        [9 marks]\n\n5.  Now Jack and John roll a die $12$ times. Let $A$ denote the number\n    of rounds Jack wins. The expected value of $A$ can be written as\n\n    $$\n    \\mathrm{E}[A] = \\sum_{r=0}^{12} r\\binom{12}{r} \\left[\\dfrac{a^{12-r}}{b^{12}}\\right]\n    $$\n\n    1.  Find the value of $a$ and $b$.\n    2.  Differentiate the expansion of $(1 + y)^{12}$ to prove that the\n        expected\\\n        number of rolls Jack wins is $4$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The probability that Jack wins a round is $\\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n    Hence the probability that Jack wins exactly two rounds is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{7}{2}\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg]^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg]^5 &= \\bigg[\\dfrac{7\\cdot6}{2}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3^2}\\bigg]\\bigg[\\dfrac{2^5}{3^5}\\bigg]  \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{7\\cdot2^5}{3^6} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{224}{729}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  :::answer\n        On each round, either Jack wins or John wins. Hence in seven rounds, there are $2^7 = 128$ possible outcomes.\n\n    2.  Using the binomial theorem, we have\n        $$\n        (1 + y)^7  =  \\sum_{k = 0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}\\hspace{0.05em}y^k \\hspace{0.3em}\n        $$\n        Hence, if we substitute $y = 1$ in the equation $(1)$, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{0.7em} 128 = \\sum_{k = 0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}\n        $$\n\n    3.  :::answer\n        The total number of outcomes equals to the sum of the number of ways Jack can win $k$ rounds over $k = 0,1,2,\\dots,7$.\n\n3.\n    1.  The expected amount of money earned by Jack in the game is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.2em} \\mathrm{E}[\\$ \\text{ earned by Jack}] &= 7\\left[(\\$\\hspace{0.05em}4)\\cdot\\frac{1}{3} + (-\\$\\hspace{0.05em}2)\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}\\right] \\\\[3pt]\n        &= \\$\\hspace{0.05em}0.00\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        The expected amount of money earned by John in the game is\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[\\$ \\text{ earned by John}] &= 7\\left[(-\\$\\hspace{0.05em}4)\\cdot\\frac{1}{3} + (\\$\\hspace{0.05em}2)\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}\\right] \\\\[3pt]\n        &= \\$\\hspace{0.05em}0.00\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  :::answer\n        Since no one is expected to earn money in the game, no one is expected to win the game.\n\n    3.  :::answer\n        Yes, this game is fair because no one is expected to win or lose the~game.\n\n4.\n    1.  The probability that John wins a round is $\\dfrac{2}{3}$.\n        Hence the probability that John wins five rounds on the first\n        game and two rounds on the second game is\n\n        $$\n        \\left[\\binom{7}{5}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg]^5\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg]^2\\right]\\left[\\binom{7}{2}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg]^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg]^5\\right]  =  \\hspace{0.525em}\\binom{7}{5}^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg]^7\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg]^7\n        $$\n\n    2.  Similarly as in (d) (i), the probability that John wins a total\n        of seven rounds over two games is\n\n        $$\n        \\sum_{k = 0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg]^7\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg]^7 = \\hspace{0.525em}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2^7}{3^{14}}\\bigg]\\sum_{k = 0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}^2\n        $$\n\n    3.  Equivalently, the probability that John wins $7$ rounds out of\n        $14$ is\n\n        $$\n        \\binom{14}{7}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg]^7\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg]^7 = \\binom{14}{7}\\bigg[\\dfrac{2^7}{3^{14}}\\bigg] \\hspace{0.75em}\n        $$\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\binom{14}{7}\\cancel{\\bigg[\\dfrac{2^7}{3^{14}}\\bigg]} &= \\cancel{\\bigg[\\dfrac{2^7}{3^{14}}\\bigg]}\\sum_{k = 0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}^2 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\binom{14}{7} = \\sum_{k = 0}^7 \\binom{7}{k}^2 \\hspace{0.15em}\n        $$\n\n5.\n    1.  The expected value of $A$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{E}[A] &= \\sum_{r=0}^{12} r\\binom{12}{r}\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigg]^r\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{3}\\bigg]^{12-r} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=  \\sum_{r=0}^{12} r\\binom{12}{r}\\left[\\dfrac{2^{12-r}}{3^{12}}\\right]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence\n         $a = 2$\n        and  $b = 3$\n\n    2.  Using the binomial theorem, we have\n        $$\n        (1 + y)^{12}  =  \\sum_{k = 0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k}\\hspace{0.05em}y^k \\hspace{1.2em}\n        $$\n        Hence, if we differentiate both sides of the equation $(2)$, we\n        get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}y}\\left[(1 + y)^{12}\\right] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}y}\\left[\\sum_{r=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{r} y^{r}\\right] \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n        12(1 + y)^{11} &= \\sum_{r=0}^{12} r\\binom{12}{r} y^{r-1}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        To obtain $\\dfrac{3^{12}}{2^{11}}$ on the left hand side and\n        $2^{1-r}$ on the right hand side of\n\n        the equation $(3)$, we need to substitute $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ in\n        this equation.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        12\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{11} &= \\sum_{r=0}^{12} r\\binom{12}{r} \\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg]^{r-1} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        4\\cdot\\dfrac{3^{12}}{2^{11}} &= \\sum_{r=0}^{12} r\\binom{12}{r} \\big[2^{1-r}\\big]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, if we rewrite the equation $(4)$, we obtain\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.5em} 4 &= \\sum_{r=0}^{12} r\\binom{12}{r} \\left[\\dfrac{2^{12-r}}{3^{12}}\\right] \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        4 = \\mathrm{E}[A] \\hspace{1.9em}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1045","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1045\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe coefficient of the variable $x^5$ in the expansion of $(x+2a)^8$, where $a\\in \\mathbb{R}$, is $56$.\n\nFind the value of $a$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    (x+2a)^8 &=x^8+\\binom{8}{1}(x)^7(2a)^1+...+\\binom{8}{r}(x)^{(8-r)}(2a)^r+...+(2a)^8 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to identify the $r^{\\text{th}}$ term which includes $x^5$, hence, $r=8-5=3$,\n$$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\binom{8}{3}\\big(x^{5}\\big)\\big(2a\\big)^3 &= 56x^5 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUsing the formula we can evaluate the binomial coefficient\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{8}{3}&=\\frac{8!}{3!5!}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=56\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cancel{56})(\\cancel{x^5})(8a^3) &= \\cancel{56}\\cancel{x^5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[9pt]\n8a^3 &= 1 \\\\[7pt]\na^3 &= \\dfrac{1}{8} \\\\[7pt]\n&\\hspace{-0.535em}\\boxed{a = \\dfrac{1}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1152","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1152\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn the expansion of $(a+bx)^6$, the coefficient of the $x^3$ term is $-2$ times the coefficient of the $x^4$ term.\n\nDetermine the value of $\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}$.","markScheme":"From the formula booklet we can find the formula for a general term of the expansion\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    (a+b)^n&=...+\\binom{n}{r}a^{n-r}b^r+...\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the general term above, we can write an expression for the $x^3$ term and the $x^4$ term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    &\\binom{6}{3}(a)^3(bx)^3& \\binom{6}{4}(a)^2(bx)^4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use the information given to write an equation linking the coefficients of the two expressions above.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    &\\binom{6}{3}a^3b^3=-2\\times \\binom{6}{4}a^2b^4 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula we can evaluate the binomial coefficients and simplify\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{6!}{3!3!}a^3b^3&=-2\\times \\frac{6!}{4!2!}a^2b^4\\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{6\\times 5 \\times 4\\times \\cancel{3!}}{\\cancel{3!}\\times3\\times 2\\times 1}a^3b^3&=-2\\times \\frac{6\\times 5 \\times  \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}\\times 2 \\times 1}a^2b^4\\\\[6pt]\n    20a^3b^3&=-30a^2b^4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe need to find the value of $\\tfrac{a}{b}$ therefore we can rearrange the expression above\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{a^3b^3}{a^2b^4}&=\\frac{-30}{20}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\frac{a}{b}&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{\\frac{-3}{2}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=-1.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA120","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA120\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\bigg(2x^6+\\dfrac{x^2}{q}\\bigg)^{10}$, \n$q \\neq 0$. The coefficient of the term\n\nin $x^{40}$ is twelve times the coefficient of the term in $x^{36}$.\nFind $q$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.5em} \\bigg(2x^6+\\dfrac{x^2}{q}\\bigg)^{10} &= \\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k} \\big[2x^6\\big]^{10-k}\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^2}{q}\\bigg]^k \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k} \\big[2^{10-k}q^{-k}\\big]\\big[x^{60-4k}\\big] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^{40}$}\\big] &= 12\\big[\\text{coefficient of the term in $x^{36}$}\\big] \\hspace{2.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n\\binom{10}{5}\\big[2^{10-5}q^{-5}\\big] &= 12\\binom{10}{6}\\big[2^{10-6}q^{-6}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n252\\big[2^5q^{-5}\\big] &= 12(210)\\big[2^4q^{-6}\\big] \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{6.9em} q = 5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA123","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA123\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nGiven that\n$(5+nx)^2\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{3}{5}x\\bigg)^n\\hspace{-0.25em}=\\hspace{0.05em}25+100x+\\cdots$,\nfind the value of $n$.","markScheme":"Using the binomial theorem, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5+nx)^2\\bigg(1+\\dfrac{3}{5}x\\bigg)^n &= \\big[25 + 10nx + n^2x^2\\big]\\left[\\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom{n}{k}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3x}{5}\\bigg]^k\\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe term in $x$ in the expansion of the product $(1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25\\binom{n}{1}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3x}{5}\\bigg]^1 + 10nx\\binom{n}{0}\\bigg[\\dfrac{3x}{5}\\bigg]^0 &= 15nx + 10nx  \\\\[6pt]\n&= 25nx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25n &= 100 \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nn = 4 \\hspace{2.1em}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1273","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1273\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  1.  Consider the following equation $2\\binom{n}{r}=\\binom{n}{r+1}$.\n\n        Show that it can be written as $3r+2=n$. \n\n    2.  Now consider the following equation $7\\binom{n}{r-1}=2\\binom{n}{r}$.\n\n    \n\n        Show that it can be written as $9r-2=2n$.  [4 marks]\n\nConsider the expansion\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n(1+x)^n=1+a_1x+...+a_{k-1}x^{k-1}+a_{k}x^{k}+a_{k+1}x^{k+1}+...+x^n\n\n\\end{aligned} \n\n$$\n\nWhere $a_i \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n    \n\nThe coefficients of three consecutive terms of the expansion are such that\n\n$$ \n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n7\\times a_{k-1}&=2\\times a_{k} \\hspace{1em}\\text{ and }\\hspace{1em} 14\\times a_{k}=7\\times a_{k+1}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n2.  Find $n$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Using the combinations formula $\\binom{n}{r}=\\tfrac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}$ we can rewrite both the LHS and the RHS of the equation\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            2\\left(\\dfrac{\\cancel{n!}}{r!(n-r)!}\\right)&=\\dfrac{\\cancel{n!}}{(r+1)!(n-(r+1))!}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can simplify and rearrange the equation\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\dfrac{2(r+1)!}{r!}&=\\dfrac{(n-r)!}{(n-r-1)!}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Rewrite the numerators using the concept $n!=n(n-1)!$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\dfrac{2(r+1)r!}{r!}&=\\dfrac{(n-r)(n-r-1)!}{(n-r-1)!} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We can cancel out the factorial terms\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\dfrac{2(r+1)\\cancel{r!}}{\\cancel{r!}}&=\\dfrac{(n-r)\\cancel{(n-r-1)!}}{\\cancel{(n-r-1)!}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            2(r+1)&=n-r\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &\\hspace{-2.8em} \\boxed{3r+2=n} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As required.\n\n        \n\n    2.  Rewrite the equation using the combinations formula and simplify in a similar way to part (a)\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            7\\left(\\dfrac{n!}{(r-1)!(n-(r-1))!}\\right)&=2\\left(\\dfrac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n\n            \\dfrac{7}{(r-1)!(n-r+1)!}&=\\dfrac{2}{r!(n-r)!}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            \\dfrac{7r!}{(r-1)!}&=\\dfrac{2(n-r+1)!}{(n-r)!}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            \\dfrac{7r\\cancel{(r-1)!}}{\\cancel{(r-1)!}}&=\\dfrac{2(n-r+1)\\cancel{(n-r)!}}{\\cancel{(n-r)!}} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            7r&=2n-2r+2\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &\\hspace{-2.8em} \\boxed{9r-2=2n} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As required.\n\n2.  We can begin by expanding $(1+x)^n$, using the binomial theorem, in terms of $n$ and $r$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        (1+x)^n&=1+\\binom{n}{1}x+\\binom{n}{2}x^2+...\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &\\textcolor{red}{+\\binom{n}{r-1}x^{r-1}+\\binom{n}{r}x^r+\\binom{n}{r+1}x^{r+1}+...}  \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &+x^n\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The terms in red represent any three consecutive terms in the expansion.\n\n    We are told in the question the way in which the coefficients of three consecutive terms are related.\n\n    $$ \n\n     \\begin{aligned}\n\n    (1+x)^n=1+a_1x+...+\\textcolor{red}{a_{k-1}x^{k-1}+a_{k}x^{k}+a_{k+1}x^{k+1}}+...+x^n\n\n    \\end{aligned} \n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Hence we can use the information given to write the following two equations in terms of binomial coefficients \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &&7\\times a_{k-1}&=2\\times a_{k}& &&14\\times a_{k}&=7\\times a_{k+1}&  \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &&7\\binom{n}{r-1}&=2\\binom{n}{r} &\\hspace{-1em} && 14\\binom{n}{r}&=7\\binom{n}{r+1}& \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &&&&\\hspace{-1em} && 2\\binom{n}{r}&=\\binom{n}{r+1}& \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In part (a) we already rewrote these equations without the combination notation, lets call them $[1]$ and $[2]$. \n\n    We can now solve them simultaneously and find $n$ and $r$. \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\\n\n        3r+2&=n \\hspace{4.4em} [1]\\\\\n\n        9r-2&=2n \\hspace{4em} [2]\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Multiply $[1]$ by $3$ and subtract equation $[2]$ from this $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{result}}$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\textcolor{red}{9r}-9r+\\textcolor{red}{6}-(-2)&=\\textcolor{red}{3n}-2n\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-0.6em}\\boxed{n=8} \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1292","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1292\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1.  Write $\\dfrac{3x-7}{3x^2+x-2}$ in form $\\dfrac{A}{x+1}+\\dfrac{B}{3x-2}$, where $x\\neq -1,\\,\\tfrac{2}{3}.$[2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence determine the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $\\dfrac{3x-7}{3x^2+x-2}$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The process of rewriting a single fraction as two (or more!) fractions is known as partial fractions.\n\n    Let's setup an identity using the information given, this means the identity is true for all valid values of $x$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{3x-7}{3x^2+x-2} &\\equiv \\dfrac{A}{x+1}+\\dfrac{B}{3x-2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can then multiply throughout by $3x^2+x-2$ to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        3x-7&= (3x-2)A+(x+1)B\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next expand the R.H.S. and then collect together the coefficients of $x$ and the constant\n    $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n        3x-7&=3Ax-2A+Bx+B\\\\[6pt]\n        3x-7&=(3A+B)x-2A+B\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now equate the coefficients of $x$ and the constant term, to form two equations\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        3A+B&=3\\\\[6pt]\n        -2A+B&=-7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Subtracting the two equations gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        5A&=10\\\\[6pt]\n        A&=2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    And\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        3(2)+B&=3\\\\[6pt]\n        B&=-3\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{3x-7}{3x^2+x-2}&=\\dfrac{2}{x+1}-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}\\\\[8pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{2}{x+1}+\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}\\right)\\hspace{1em}\\text{[In the form required]}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  First we can rewrite both quotients in index form\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2(x+1)^{-1}- 3(3x-2)^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Next consider the given formula for the extension of the binomial theorem\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (a+b)^n&=a^n\\left(1+n\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right)+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right)^2+...\\right)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Let's begin with the first quotient\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2(x+1)^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can see that $a=1$, $b=x$ and $n=-1$, hence the first three terms are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2(x+1)^{-1}&=2\\times 1^{-1}\\left(1+(-1)\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)+\\frac{-1((-1)-1)}{2!}\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)^2\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n        &=2-2x+2x^2\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now lets consider the other quotient\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -3(3x-2)^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This one is not as straight forward.\n\n    In order to use the formula effectively it is best to try and rewrite the quotient by factorising out the $a^n$ term, this is the constant term inside the bracket\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -3(3x-2)^{-1}&=-3(-2\\left(-\\tfrac{3}{2}x+1\\right))^{-1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=-3 \\times (-2)^{-1}\\left(1-\\tfrac{3}{2}x\\right)^{-1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{3}{2}\\left(1-\\tfrac{3}{2}x\\right)^{-1}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In this form we can now see that $a=1$, $b=-\\tfrac{3}{2}x$ and $n=-1$, hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tfrac{3}{2}\\left(1-\\tfrac{3}{2}x\\right)^{-1}&=\\tfrac{3}{2}\\left(1+(-1)\\left(-\\tfrac{3}{2}x\\right)+\\frac{-1((-1)-1)}{2!}\\left(-\\tfrac{3}{2}x\\right)^2\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{3}{2}\\left(1+\\tfrac{3}{2}x+\\tfrac{9}{4}x^2\\right)\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{3}{2}+\\tfrac{9}{4}x+\\tfrac{27}{8}x^2\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we need to sum the two results which gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2(x+1)^{-1}- 3(3x-2)^{-1}&=2-2x+2x^2+\\tfrac{3}{2}+\\tfrac{9}{4}x+\\tfrac{27}{8}x^2\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{7}{2}+\\tfrac{1}{4}x+\\tfrac{43}{8}x^2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1328","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1328\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn the expansion $(2x+1)^n$, where $n\\in\\mathbb Z^+$, the coefficient of $x^3$ is equal to six times the difference between of the coefficient of $x^2$ and the coefficient of $x$.\n\nFind the value of $n$.","markScheme":"Let's use the binomial theorem from the formula booklet, to start expanding this binomial. \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n(a+b)^n&= a^n+\\binom{n}{1}a^{n-1}b +...+ \\binom{n}{r}a^{n-r}b^r +...\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThe given information relates to coefficients of the $x$, $x^2$, and $x^3$ terms so we want to expand in ascending powers of $x$. For that we need to switch the terms of the binomial so that $a=1$ and $b=2x$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n(1+2x)^n &= (1)^n + \\binom{n}{1}(1)^{n-1}(2x)+\\binom{n}{2}(1)^{n-2}(2x)^2+\\binom{n}{3}(1)^{n-3}(2x)^3 + ...\\\\[12pt]\n\n&=1+\\binom{n}{1}2x+\\binom{n}{2}4x^2+\\binom{n}{3}8x^3+...\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe are told that six times the difference of the coefficients of the $x^2$ term and $x$ term is equal to the coefficient of the $x^3$ term.\n\nUsing this relationship we can form an equation.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n6\\left[4\\binom{n}{2}-2\\binom{n}{1}\\right]&=8\\binom{n}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n24\\binom{n}{2}-12\\binom{n}{1}&=8\\binom{n}{3}\\\\[12pt]\n\n6\\binom{n}{2}-3\\binom{n}{1}&=2\\binom{n}{3}\\hspace{5em}[1]\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow let's use the formula for the binomial coefficient.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\binom{n}{r}&=\\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nEvaluating the formula when $r=1$, $r=2$, and $r=3$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\binom{n}{1}&=\\frac{n!}{1!(n-1)!}=n\\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\binom{n}{2}&=\\frac{n!}{2!(n-2)!}=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\\\[12pt]\n\n    \\binom{n}{3}&=\\frac{n!}{3!(n-3)!}=\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{6}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nNow we substitute these into equation [1] and solve for $n$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n 6\\left(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\right)-3n&=2\\left(\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{6}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[12pt]\n\n3n(n-1)-3n&=\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3}\\\\[9pt]\n\n9n(n-1)-9n&=n(n-1)(n-2)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nFrom here we can factorise $9n$ on the L.H.S.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n9n(n-1)-9n&=n(n-1)(n-2)\\\\[6pt]\n\n9n(n-1-1)&=n(n-1)(n-2)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n9n(n-2)&=n(n-1)(n-2)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nAs we know that $n\\in\\mathbb Z^+$ we know that $n\\neq0$. Also, because the $x^3$ term exists, $n>2$. Therefore we can divide both sides by $n$ and by $n-2$.\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n 9\\cancel{n}\\cancel{(n-2)}&=\\cancel{n}(n-1)\\cancel{(n-2)}\\\\[6pt]\n\n 9&=n-1\\\\[6pt]\n\nn&=10\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"},{"ref":"AA749","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA749\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider the expansion of $(x-3)^8$.\n\n1.  Write down the number of terms in this expansion. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the coefficient of the term in $x^6$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The number of terms in this expansion is\n     $9$\n\n2.  The term in $x^6$ is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\binom{8}{2} \\big[(x)^{8-2}\\big]\\big[(-3)^2\\big] &= 28\\big[x^6\\big]\\big[9\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 252x^6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the coefficient of the term in $x^6$\n    is  $252$"},{"ref":"AA988","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA988\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider the expansion of $\\dfrac{(x+a)^7}{bx}$, where $a > 0$. The coefficient of the term in $x^5$ is $2$, and the coefficient of the term in $x^3$ is $1690$.\n\nFind the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.","markScheme":"Because each term in the expansion of $(x+a)^7$ will be divided by $bx$, we require the terms in $x^6$ and $x^4$. Using the Binomial Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{Term in $x^6$} &= \\binom{7}{1}x^6a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 7x^6a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\text{Term in $x^4$} &= \\binom{7}{3}x^4a^3 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 35x^4a^3\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nDividing both these terms by $bx$ we get\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{7x^6a}{bx} &= \\dfrac{7x^5a}{b} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{35x^4a^3}{bx} &= \\dfrac{35x^3a^3}{b}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nGiven that the coefficient of $x^5$ is $2$, and the coefficient of $x^3$ is $1690$, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{7a}{b} &= 2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\dfrac{35a^3}{b} &= 1690\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nAnd therefore \n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    7a &= 2b \\tag*{[1]}\\\\[8pt] \n\n    35a^3 &= 1690b \\tag*{[2]}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nDividing equation [2] by equation [1] we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\dfrac{35a^3}{7a} &= \\dfrac{1690b}{2b} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    5a^2 &= 845 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a^2 &= 169 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    a &= \\pm 13\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nGiven that $a>0$, $\\boxed{a = 13}$. Substituting into equation [1],\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    7a &= 2b \\\\[8pt]\n\n    7(13) &= 2b \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{b = 45.5}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA141","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA141\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nConsider the polynomial $p(z) = z^5 + z^4 - z^3 + z^2 + 4z + 2$, for\n$z \\in \\mathbb{C}$.\n\n1.  Write down the sum and product of the roots of $p(z) = 0$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Show that $(z+1)$ is a factor of $p(z)$. [2 marks]\n\nThe polynomial can be written in the form\n$p(z) = (z+1)^3(z^2 + cz + d)$.\n\n3.  Find the value of $c$ and the value of $d$. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Hence find the complex roots of $p(z) = 0$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using Vieta's formulas, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{sum of roots} &= -\\dfrac{a_4}{a_5} \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{1}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{product of roots} &= (-1)^5\\bigg[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_5}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\dfrac{2}{1} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= -2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Evaluating $p(z)$ for $z = -1$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} p(-1) &= (-1)^5 + (-1)^4 - (-1)^3 + (-1)^2 + 4(-1) + 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, by factor theorem, $(z+1)$ is a factor of\n    $p(z)$.\n\n3.  Evaluating $p(z)$ for $z = 0$ and $z = 1$, we have $p(0) = 2$ and\n    $p(1) = 8$.\n\n    Hence, substituting $z = 0,\\hspace{0.1em}1$ in the equation\n    $p(z) = (z+1)^3(z^2 + cz + d)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} p(0) &= (0+1)^3(0^2 + c(0) + d) \\\\[6pt]\n    2 &= d \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    d = 2 \\hspace{6.1em}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} p(1) &= (1+1)^3(1^2 + c(1) + 2) \\\\[6pt]\n    8 &= 8(c+3) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    c = -2 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$\n\n4.  The complex roots of $p(z) = 0$ are the complex roots of\n    $z^2 - 2z + 2 = 0$.\n\n    Hence, using the quadratic formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} z &= \\dfrac{-(-2)\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(2)}}{2(1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2 \\pm 2{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 \\pm {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA158","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA158\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nTwo distinct roots for the polynomial equation\n$z^4 - 10z^3 + cz^2 + dz + 170$ are\n$a + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ and\n$1 + b{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}$ where\n$a,b,c,d \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $b > 0$.\n\n1.  Write down the other two roots in terms of $a$ and $b$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu},\\hspace{0.25em}1 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b$\n\n2.  Using Vieta's formulas, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{sum of roots} &= -\\dfrac{a_3}{a_4} \\\\[6pt]\n    (a + \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}) + (a - \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}}) + (1 + \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b}) + (1 - \\cancel{{\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b}) &= -\\dfrac{(-10)}{1} \\hspace{7.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n    2a + 2 &= 10 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5em} a = 4\n    $$\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{product of roots} &= (-1)^4\\bigg[\\dfrac{a_0}{a_4}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    (4 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(4 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu})(1 + {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b)(1 - {\\mathrm{\\hspace{0.05em}i}\\mkern 1mu}b) &= (-1)^4\\bigg[\\dfrac{170}{1}\\bigg] \\hspace{2em} \\\\[12pt]\n    17(1 + b^2) &= 170 \\\\[16pt]\n    1 + b^2 &= 10 \\\\[16pt]\n    b^2 &= 9 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.4em} b = 3  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $b > 0$}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA232","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA232\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe first three terms of a geometric sequence are\n\n $$\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\sqrt{2}\\cos x,\\hspace{0.4em} \\sin 2x,\\hspace{0.4em} 2\\sqrt{2}\\sin^2 x \\cos x,\\hspace{0.4em} -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}. \\\\\n \\end{aligned}\n $$\n\n1.  Find the common ratio. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the set of values of $x$ for which the geometric series below\n    converges.\n\n    $$\n    \\sqrt{2}\\cos x + \\sin 2x + 2\\sqrt{2}\\sin^2 x \\cos x + \\cdots\n\n    $$\n    [3 marks]\n\nConsider $x = \\arcsin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg)$,\n$0 < x < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n3.  Show that the sum to infinity of this series is $\\sqrt{7}$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $u_1 = \\sqrt{2}\\cos x$ and $u_2 = \\sin 2x$.\n    Hence the common ratio is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{14.5em} r &= \\dfrac{u_2}{u_1} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\sqrt{2}\\cos x} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2\\sin x \\cos x}{\\sqrt{2}\\cos x} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by double angle identity}] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{2}\\sin x\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The geometric series converges when\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |r| &< 1 \\\\[15pt]\n    |\\sqrt{2}\\sin x| &< 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[12pt]\n    |\\sin x| &< \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[4pt]\n    \\hspace{2.4em} -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} <  \\sin x  &< \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    -\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} <  x  < \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\hspace{3.5em}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} u_1 &= \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.2em}\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{2}\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.4em} r &= \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\hspace{-0.2em}\\arcsin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{2}\\times\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the sum of an infinite\n    geometric sequence formula $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$ with\n    $u_1 = \\sqrt{7}/2$ and $r = 1/2$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S_{\\infty} &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}/2}{1 - 1/2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{7}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA364","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA364\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nSophie and Ella play a game. They each have five cards showing roman\nnumerals I, V, X, L, C. Sophie lays her cards face\nup on the table in order I, V, X, L, C as shown in\nthe following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Unassigned/images/8c2117a8771870dcd1b10ba85dbc123753724df8.svg\" alt=\"8c2117a8771870dcd1b10ba85dbc123753724df8.svg\">\n\nElla shuffles her cards and lays them face down on the table. She then\nturns them over one by one to see if her card\nmatches with Sophie's card directly above. Sophie wins if **no**\nmatches occur; otherwise Ella wins.\n\n1.  Show that the probability that Sophie wins the game is\n    $\\dfrac{11}{30}$.[6 marks]\n\nSophie and Ella repeat their game so that they play a total of $90$\ntimes. Let the discrete random\nvariable $X$ represent the number of times Sophie wins.\n\n2.  Determine:\n    1.  the mean of $X$;\n\n    2.  the variance of $X$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Unassigned/images/5557f2430f2b6a1d2ae2b382374192f4abd645a4.svg\" alt=\"5557f2430f2b6a1d2ae2b382374192f4abd645a4.svg\">\n\n    There are $5! = 120$ possible ways to lay down five cards in order\n    on the table.\n\n    Hence the number of possible ways to lay down five cards with no\n    matches is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    120 - (45+20+10+0+1) &= 120 - 76 \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 44\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the probability that Sophie wins the game\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Sophie wins}) &= \\dfrac{44}{120} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11}{30}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Since $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(90,11/30)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 90\\times\\dfrac{11}{30} \\hspace{3.3em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 33\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the formula for the variance of\n        $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(90,11/30)$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.75em} \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= np(1-p) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 90\\times\\dfrac{11}{30}\\times\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[1 - \\dfrac{11}{30}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 20.9\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA365","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA365\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nAn ice cream company advertises free gifts to customers who collect four\ncoupons. The coupons are placed at random into\n$20\\%$ of all bags of ice cream sticks. Oliver buys\nice cream sticks and he opens them one at a time to see if\nthey contain a coupon.\n\nLet $\\mathrm{P}(X = n)$ be the probability that Oliver obtains his $4$th\ncoupon on the $n$th ice cream stick opened.\n\nIt is assumed that the probability that an ice cream stick contains a\ncoupon stays at $20\\%$ throughout the question.\n\n1.  Show that $\\mathrm{P}(X = 4) = 0.0016$ and\n    $\\mathrm{P}(X = 5) = 0.00512$. [3 marks]\n\nIt is given that\n$\\mathrm{P}(X = n) = \\dfrac{1}{3750}(n-1)(n^2+an+b)(0.8)^{n-4}$ for\n$n \\geq 4$, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$,\n\nwhere $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n2.  Find the values of $a$ and $b$.\n    [5 marks]\n\n3.  Deduce that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} = \\dfrac{4(n-1)}{5(n-4)}$\n    for $n > 4$. [4 marks]\n\n4.\n    1.  Hence show that $X$ has two modes $m_1$ and $m_2$.\n\n    2.  Write down the values of $m_1$ and $m_2$. [5 marks]\n\nOliver's mother goes to the market and buys $N$ ice cream sticks. She\ntakes the ice cream sticks home for Oliver\nto open.\n\n5.  Determine the minimum value of $N$ such that the\n    probability Oliver receives at least one\n    free gift is greater than $0.5$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.25em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{C}) &= 0.2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{C\\hspace{0.025em}:\\hspace{0.25em} Coupon}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{E}) &= 0.8 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{E\\hspace{0.025em}:\\hspace{0.25em} Empty}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the independent events formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(X = 4) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CCCC}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [\\mathrm{P}(\\text{C})]^4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0.2)^4 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 0.0016 \\\\[16pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 5) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{ECCCC}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CECCC}) + \\cdots \\\\[8pt]\n    &+ \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CCECC}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CCCEC}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4[\\mathrm{P}(\\text{E})][\\mathrm{P}(\\text{C})]^4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4(0.8)(0.2)^4 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 0.00512\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 4) &= 0.0016 \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{3750}(4-1)(4^2+a(4) + b)(0.8)^{4-4} &= 0.0016 \\\\[6pt]\n    16+4a + b &= 2 \\\\[10pt]\n    4a + b &= -14\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 5) &= 0.00512 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{3750}(5-1)(5^2+a(5) + b)(0.8)^{5-4} &= 0.00512 \\\\[6pt]\n    25+5a + b &= 6 \\\\[10pt]\n    5a + b &= -19\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the system of\n    linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$ and $b$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} a = -5\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}b = 6 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.9em} \\mathrm{P}(X = n) &= \\dfrac{1}{3750}(n-1)(n^2-5n+6)(0.8)^{n-4} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3750}(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)(0.8)^{n-4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3750}}(n-1)\\cancel{(n-2)}\\cancel{(n-3)}(0.8)^{n-4}}{\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3750}}\\cancel{(n-2)}\\cancel{(n-3)}(n-4)(0.8)^{n-5}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(n-1)(0.8)}{(n-4)} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4(n-1)}{5(n-4)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  Solving the equation\n        $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} = 1$ for\n        $n > 4$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{9.3em} \\dfrac{4(n-1)}{5(n-4)} &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n        n &= 16 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} < 1 & \\hspace{0.75em}\\Rightarrow\\hspace{0.75em} n > 16 \\hspace{3.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} > 1 & \\hspace{0.75em}\\Rightarrow\\hspace{0.75em} n < 16\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence $X$ has two modes; let's call them $m_1$ and $m_2$.\n\n    2.\n        $m_1 = 15$  and\n         $m_2 = 16$\n\n5.  Let $Y$ be the number of coupons among $N$ ice cream sticks.\n    Hence we have:\n\n    1\\. $Y \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sim \\mathrm{B}(N,0.2)$;\n    2\\. Oliver gets at least one free gift if $Y \\geq 4$.\n\n    Hence, solving the inequality $\\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 4) > 0.5$ for $N$,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 - \\mathrm{P}(Y < 4) &> 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(Y < 4) &< 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{7.525em} \\text{binomCdf}(N,0.2,0,3) &<  0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    N &= 19,\\hspace{0.25em} 20,\\hspace{0.25em} 21,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dots \\hspace{1em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the minimum value of $N$ is\n     $19$"},{"ref":"AA410","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA410\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe following table shows values of $\\ln x$ and $\\ln y$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Unassigned/images/8c64f77af5ec93279ceb96452d0a87f353ec5871.svg\" alt=\"8c64f77af5ec93279ceb96452d0a87f353ec5871.svg\">\n\nThe relationship between $\\ln x$ and $\\ln y$ can be modelled by the\nregression equation $\\ln y = a \\ln x + b$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Use the regression equation to estimate the value of\n     $y$  when\n     $x = 4.12$. [3 marks]\n\nThe relationship between $x$ and $y$ can be modelled using the formula\n$y = px^q$, where $p\\neq 0$ and $q \\neq 0$, $q\\neq 1$.\n\n3.  Expressing $\\ln y$ in terms of $\\ln x$, find the value of\n    $p$ and the value of $q$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a \\approx -0.564$\n    and  $b \\approx 4.74$\n      \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.  Substituting $x = 4.12$ in the equation\n    $\\ln y = -0.564\\ln x + 4.74$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} \\ln y &= -0.564\\ln (4.12) + 4.74 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= e^{-0.564\\ln (4.12) + 4.74} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    y \\approx 51.5 \\hspace{6em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln y &= -0.564\\ln x + 4.74 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln x^{-0.564} + \\ln e^{4.74} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln\\hspace{-0.15em}\\big([x^{-0.564}][e^{4.74}]\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= [e^{4.74}][x^{-0.564}] \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 114\\hspace{0.05em}x^{-0.564} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n     $p \\approx 114$\n    and\n     $q \\approx -0.564$"},{"ref":"AA478","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA478\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nLet $f(x) = (x+1)e^{-2x}$, $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1.  Find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Prove by induction that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\big[n(-2)^{n-1} + (-2)^n(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}$\n    for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.[7 marks]\n\n3.  Find the coordinates of any local minimum and maximum points on the\n    graph of $y = f(x)$. Justify whether any such point is a minimum or\n    a maximum. [5 marks]\n\n4.  Find the coordinates of any points of inflexion on the graph of\n    $y = f(x)$. Justify whether any such point is a point of inflexion.\n    [5 marks]\n\n5.  Hence sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, indicating clearly the points\n    found in parts (c) and (d) and any intercepts with the axes.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the product rule, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big[(x+1)e^{-2x}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= e^{-2x} - 2(x+1)e^{-2x} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $\\mathrm{P}(n)$ be the following statement:\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.9em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^nf}{\\mathrm{d}x^n} = \\big[n(-2)^{n-1} + (-2)^n(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{for $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using mathematical induction, we will prove that $\\mathrm{P}(n)$\n    holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n:u[Base case]. For $n = 1$, from part (a), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence P$(1)$ holds.\n\n:u[Inductive hypothesis]. We assume that $\\mathrm{P}(k)$ holds\nfor some $k \\geq 1$. In other words, we assume that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^kf}{\\mathrm{d}x^k} = \\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\hspace{0.5em}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\geq 1$.\n\n:u[Inductive step]. To show that $\\mathrm{P}(k+1)$ holds, we\nneed to show that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.6em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} = \\big[(k+1)(-2)^k + (-2)^{(k+1)}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we differentiate both sides of the equation (IH), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{5.55em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^{k+1}f}{\\mathrm{d}x^{k+1}} &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\Big[\\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x}\\Big] \\\\[14pt]\n&= \\big[(-2)^k\\big]e^{-2x} - 2\\big[k(-2)^{k-1} + (-2)^k(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\[16pt]\n&= \\big[(-2)^k\\big]e^{-2x} + \\big[k(-2)^k + (-2)^{k+1}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\[16pt]\n&= \\big[(k+1)(-2)^k + (-2)^{(k+1)}(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $P(k+1)$ holds as well.\n\n:u[Conclusion]. P$(1)$ holds, and for every $k \\geq 1$, if\nP$(k)$ holds then P$(k+1)$ also holds.\n\nHence, by mathematical induction, we conclude that P$(n)$ holds for all\n$n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$.\n\n3.  If we solve the equation $f'(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\big[1 - 2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    1 - 2(x+1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    - 2x - 1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -0.5 \\hspace{0.6em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence $-0.5$ is the only\n    critical number of $f$.\n\n    Using the ID Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Unassigned/images/6db5903b56fcbc8c908bce990a2d28a2f4d84f81.svg\" alt=\"6db5903b56fcbc8c908bce990a2d28a2f4d84f81.svg\">\n\n    The sign of $f'$ changes from positive to negative at $-0.5$.\n    Hence, by the First Derivative Test, $f$ has a local maximum at\n    $-0.5$.\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = -0.5$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} y &= f(-0.5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-0.5+1)e^{-2(-0.5)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 0.5e\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has a local\n    maximum at\n     $\\mathrm{A}(-0.5,0.5e)$\n\n4.  If we solve the equation $f''(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\big[2(-2) + (-2)^2(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} &= 0 \\hspace{3.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-4 + 4(x+1)\\big]e^{-2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    -4 + 4(x+1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence there is at most one point of inflexion\n    on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    Using the Concavity Test, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Unassigned/images/4ab9272c6eeca47267a3b6db4da801aa3659585d.svg\" alt=\"4ab9272c6eeca47267a3b6db4da801aa3659585d.svg\">\n\n    The graph of $y = f(x)$ changes concavity from downward to upward at\n    $0$.\n\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has an inflexion point at $0$.\n\n    Evaluating $y = f(x)$ for $x = 0$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= f(0) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0+1)e^{-2(0)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the graph of $y = f(x)$ has the inflexion\n    point at\n     $\\mathrm{B}(0,1)$\n\n5.  We first note that the domain of the function\n    $f(x) = (x+1)e^{-2x}$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\n    Solving the equation $f(x) = 0$ for $x$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (x+1)e^{-2x} &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x+1 &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n    x &= -1 \\hspace{1.7em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{C}(-1,0)$\n    is the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    From part (d), we know $y = 1$ when $x = 0$.\n\n    Hence the point\n     $\\mathrm{B}(0,1)$\n    is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\big[(x+1)e^{-2x}\\big] \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -\\infty\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\big[(x+1)e^{-2x}\\big] \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the $x$-axis is a horizontal asymptote of\n    the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\n    If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ indicating the points A, B, &\n    C, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Number%20%26%20Algebra/Unassigned/images/109a725ff8e3517bb8bd68e412ea7f4366d78a1c.svg\" alt=\"109a725ff8e3517bb8bd68e412ea7f4366d78a1c.svg\">"},{"ref":"AA981","marks":"18","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA981\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nConsider the family of polynomials of the form $P(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ where coefficients $a$, $b$, $c$ and $d$ belong to the set $\\{2,6,8,24\\}$.\n\n1. Find the number of possible polynomials if\n\n    \n\n    1. each coefficient value can be repeated;\n\n    \n\n    2. each coefficient must be different.[4 marks]\n\n    \n\nConsider the case where $P(x)$ has $x+3$ as a factor, two purely imaginary roots, and all the coefficients are different.\n\n2. 1. By considering the sum of the roots, find the two possible combinations for coefficients $a$ and $b$.\n\n    \n\n    2. Show that there is only one way to assign the values $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$ if $P(0)=24$.[7 marks]\n\nNow, consider the polynomial with the coefficients found in part (b) (ii).\n\n3. 1. Express $P(x)$ as a product of one linear and one quadratic factor.\n\n    \n\n    2. Determine the three roots of $P(x)$.[7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Let $X$ be the total number of polynomials.\n\n    1. Given that each coefficient value can be repeated, we have \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        X&=4 \\times 4 \\times 4 \\times 4\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=4^4\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=256\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Therefore, there are $\\boxed{256}$ possible polynomials if the coefficients can be repeated.\n\n    2. Given that each coefficient must be different, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        X&=4!\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=24\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Therefore, there are $\\boxed{24}$ possible polynomials if each coefficient must be different.\n\n2. 1. Let the roots be $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$. Given that $x+3$ is a factor, $x_1=-3$ is a root. Complex roots occur in conjugate pairs, therefore if the other two roots are imaginary, we have $x_2 = \\alpha \\mathrm{i}$ and $x_3 = -\\alpha \\mathrm{i}$, where $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Hence, the sum of the roots is given by\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -3 + \\alpha \\mathrm{i} - \\alpha \\mathrm{i} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= -3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using the formula for the sum of the roots, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= -\\dfrac{b}{a} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        -3 &= -\\dfrac{b}{a} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        3a &= b\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We have that $a$ and $b$ belong to the set $\\{2,6,8,24\\}$ and $3a=b$. Therefore,\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{The two possible combinations are $a=2,\\,b=6$ and $a=8,\\,b=24$.}}$$\n\n    2. If $P(0)=24$, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        P(0) &= a(0)^3 + b(0)^2 + c(0) + d \\\\[6pt]\n\n        24 &= d\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The coefficients must all be different. If $d=24$, $b \\neq 24$, so we have from part (b)(i) that $a=2$ and $b=6$. Therefore $c=8$.\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{If $P(0)=24$, we must have $a=2,\\,b=6,\\,c=8$ and $d=24$.}}$$\n\n3. 1. Our polynomial is $P(x) = 2x^3 + 6x^2 + 8x + 24$. Given that $x+3$ is a factor, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        2x^3 + 6x^2 + 8x + 24 &= (x+3)(2x^2 + ex + 8) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 2x^3 +ex^2 + 8x + 6x^2 +3ex + 24 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        2x^3 + 6x^2 + 8x + 24 &= 2x^3 + (e+6)x^2 + (8+3e)x +24\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Equating the coefficients of $x^2$ and $x$ on the LHS and RHS, we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        6 &= e + 6 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        8 &= 8 + 3e\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We must have $e=0$. Therefore\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        P(x) &= (x+3)(2x^2 + 8) \\\\[8pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-2.1em}\\boxed{P(x) = 2(x+3)(x^2 + 4)}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. We have from part (b)(i) that $x_1=-3$. To find the other two roots, we solve $x^2+4=0$:\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x^2+4&=0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x^2 &= -4 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x &= \\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        x &= \\pm 2\\mathrm{i}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        $$\\boxed{\\text{The roots of $P(x)$ are $x_1=-3,\\,x_2=-2\\mathrm{i}$, and $x_3=2\\mathrm{i}$.}}$$"},{"ref":"AA987","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA987\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA species of bird, thought to be extinct, was rediscovered on 14 March 1973. Through a careful protection and breeding program, the population has continued to increase since then.\n\nThe Department of conservation proposes that the population of birds $t$ years after 14 March 1973 can be modelled by the function $P(t)=10e^{kt}$, where $0 < k < 1$ and $t \\geq 0$.\n\nOn 14 March 1993 the known population of birds was 50.\n\n1. Use the model to estimate the population of birds on 14 March 2023.[4 marks]\n\nIn fact, the known population of birds on 14 March 2023 was 502. A new model is proposed in which\n\n* The population on March 14 1973 was 10.\n\n* The population on March 14 2023 was 502.\n\n* The population increased at a constant $r\\%$ per year.\n\n2. Find the value of $r$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 14 March 1993 is 20 years after 14 March 1973, therefore $P(20)=50$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(20) &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    50 &= 10e^{20k} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    e^{20k} &= 5\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $k\\approx 0.08047$. Hence our model is $P(t)=10e^{0.08047t}$.\n\n    14 March 2023 is 50 years after 14 March 1973, hence we calculate $P(50)$:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(50) &= 10e^{0.08047 \\times 50} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-2.4em}\\boxed{P(50)\\approx559}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. If something increases at a constant rate, the compound interest formula applies. Using the formula for compound interest with $PV=10$, $FV=502$, $k=1$ and $n=50$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    FV &= PV \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100k}\\right)^{kn} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    502 &= 10 \\times \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{100}\\right)^{50}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the numerical solver on the G.D.C. we find that $\\boxed{r\\approx 8.15}$"}]}]},{"name":"Statistics & Probability","subtopics":[{"name":"Bivariate Statistics","questions":[{"ref":"AA1162","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1162\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn Andy's athletics club, $8$ runners are training for the $100\\,$ metre sprint.\n\nAndy decided to analyse the training data of the runners to see if he could improve his own performance.\n\nHe decided to record the number of hours per day each athlete trained and their personal best time for the $100\\,$metres.\n\nThe following data shows a summary of his findings.\n\n|$\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{Average hours per day training }(h)\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}1.51\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.915\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}1.30\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.511\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}1.09\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.711\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}1.96\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}1.78\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n|Personal best time in seconds $(t)$|11.2|12.0|11.6|12.9|11.6|11.8|10.8|11.0|\n\n1.  1.  Find Pearson's product-moment correlation coefficient $r$, for this data.\n\n    2.  Comment on the correlation of the two variables.  [2 marks]\n\nAndy wants to use the data to make a prediction about his own training.\n\nHe finds the regression equation for $t$ on $h$.\n\nThe gradient of this line is $m$.\n\n2.  Find $m$. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Andy uses this value of $m$ to predict how much he can reduce his personal best by.\n    1.  He trains an extra $30$ minutes per day. Using the value of $m$, predict how much time he can reduce his personal best by.\n\n    2.  Briefly describe one factor that could cause an error with his prediction?  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  Using the G.D.C. we find $r=-0.929$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    2.  There is $\\text{strong negative}$ correlation between the variables.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the G.D.C. we find $m=-1.20$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  1.  The gradient of $-1.20$ tells us that the personal best ($t$) should fall by $1.2\\,$s for every additional $1\\,$hour of training per day. Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\frac{-1.20}{2}&=-0.60\\,\\text{s} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $\\text{He predicts his PB will fall by }0.60\\text{ seconds}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  $\\text{The model for }t\\text{ on }h\\text{ is likely to not be linear.} $\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We would expect to see diminishing returns as the amount of training time increases."},{"ref":"AA1339","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1339\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following table shows the results of a study measuring the amount of sleep, $S$ hours, and blood pressure, $B$ mmHg.\n\n| $\\textrm{Hours of sleep (S)}$ &nbsp; |&nbsp; $4.2$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $a$ &nbsp;| &nbsp;$6.3$&nbsp; | &nbsp;$7.8$&nbsp; | &nbsp;$7.4$&nbsp; | &nbsp;$8.2$&nbsp; | &nbsp;$8.7$&nbsp; | &nbsp;$9.0$&nbsp; |\n\n| --- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n\n| Blood Pressure (B)|&nbsp; $142$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $138$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $134$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $\\,\\,\\,b\\,\\,\\,$ &nbsp;| &nbsp; $128$ &nbsp; | &nbsp; $118$ &nbsp; | &nbsp; $116$ &nbsp; | &nbsp; $\\,111\\,$ &nbsp; |\n\nThe mean point is found to be $(\\,7.125,\\,126\\,)$.\n\n1.    Find the value of\n\n    1.    $a$. \n\n    2.    $b$. [3 marks]\n\n2.    Hence find the equation of the regression line $S$ on $B$. [2 marks]\n\nA person is considered to have high blood pressure if their blood pressure measures $130$ mmHg or higher.\n\n3.    Find an estimate for the maximum number of hours of sleep for an adult with a high blood pressure. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. 1.   The mean point tells us that $\\large\\bar{x}=7.125$ and $\\large\\bar{y}=126$.\n\n        Hence we can add all the $x$ values and divide by the number of terms to find the mean.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            7.125&=\\frac{4.2+a+6.3+7.8+7.4+8.2+8.7+9.0}{8}\\\\[9pt]\n\n            57&=51.6+a\\\\[6pt]\n\n            a&=5.4\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2.  In a similar fashion we can find $b$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            126&=\\frac{142+138+134+b+128+118+116+111}{8}\\\\[9pt]\n\n            1008&=887+b\\\\[6pt]\n\n            b&=121\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. Now that we have all the data points we can use the G.D.C. to find the regression line.\n\n    We are asked for the regression line of $S$ on $B$ (sleep _on_ blood pressure).\n\n    This means we want to have a line that estimates sleep $(S)$ given a known value for blood pressure.\n\n    So $B$ is the independent variable and $S$ is the dependent variable. When entering our lists we want to make $B$ the $x$ list and $S$ the $y$ list.\n\n    Hence we find $S=-0.147B+25.6$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A2}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. We can use the equation found in (b) to estimate the maximum number of hours of sleep for a person with high blood pressure. \n\n    Let's substitute $130$ into the equation to find $S$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    S&=-0.1467...(130)+25.61...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=6.538...\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=6.54\\text{ hours}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA398","marks":"13","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA398\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nEach day, a factory records the number $(x)$ of memory foam pillows it\nproduces and the total production cost $(y)$ dollars. The results for\nten days are shown in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/ccbb2774e6db3c56443c9907e3979b82f8681a91.svg\" alt=\"ccbb2774e6db3c56443c9907e3979b82f8681a91.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the equation of the regression line in the form $y=ax+b$\n    [2 marks]\n\nUse your regression line as a model to answer the following.\n\n2.  Interpret the meaning of:\n    1.  the gradient;\n\n    2.  the $y$-intercept. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Estimate the cost of producing $100$ pillows. [2 marks]\n\nThe factory sells the pillows for \\$ $29.99$\neach.\n\n4.  Find the least number of pillows that the factory should\n    produce in one day in order to make a profit. [3 marks]\n\n5.  Comment on the appropriateness of using your model to\n    estimate:\n\n    1.  the cost of producing $1000$ pillows;\n    2.  the number of pillows produced when the total production cost is\n        $$\\$\\hspace{0.02em}1940$$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $y = 18.2x + 410$\n    \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $$\\text{\\(18.2\\)\\hspace{0.025em};\\hspace{0.25em} the cost to produce each additional pillow is \\(\\$18.20\\)}$$\n    2.\n        $$\\text{\\(410\\);\\hspace{0.25em} the initial cost before any pillows have been produced is \\(\\$410\\)}$$\n\n3.  Evaluating $y = 18.2x + 410$ for $x = 100$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 18.2(100) + 410 \\hspace{0.6em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\$2230\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Solving the equation $y = \\text{\"sales revenue\"}$ for $x$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5em} 18.2x + 410 &= 29.99x \\\\[8pt]\n    x &\\approx 34.8 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the least number of pillows that the factory should produce is\n     $35$\n\n5.\n    1.\n        $\\text{not appropriate, \\(x = 1000\\) is far outside of the domain (extrapolation)}$\n\n    2.\n        $\\text{not appropriate, this regression line cannot predict \\(x\\) from \\(y\\)}$"},{"ref":"AA405","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA405\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe maximum temperature $T$, in degrees Celsius, on six randomly\nselected days is shown in the following table. The table also shows the\nnumber of soda cans purchased, $N$ , from a\nvending machine on those six days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/b5d0bcdbf9120dbd7d7dee1dd6d718bafc18d83b.svg\" alt=\"b5d0bcdbf9120dbd7d7dee1dd6d718bafc18d83b.svg\">\n\n1.  The relationship between the variables can be modelled by the\n    regression equation $N = aT+b$.\n\n    1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n    2.  Estimate the number of soda cans purchased on a day when\\\n        the maximum temperature is $23$ $\\text{\\textdegree{C}}$.\n        [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $r$, the correlation coefficient. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $a \\approx 0.702$\n        and\n         $b \\approx 22.9$\n          \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n    2.  Evaluating $N = 0.702\\hspace{0.05em}T + 22.9$ for $T = 23$, we\n        get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        N &= 0.702(23) + 22.9 \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 39.046 \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 39 \\text{ cans}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    $r \\approx 0.931 \\hspace{3em}\\text{[by using G.D.C.]}$"},{"ref":"AA406","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA406\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nExtended power failure occurred in John's neighbourhood last\nwinter. He $\\text{measured}$ the temperature of his house as air\ninside cooled.\n\nThe following table shows air temperature $T$, in degrees Celsius, $t$\nhours after power failed.\n\n|$\\hspace{0.5em}t\\text{ (hours)}\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}4\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}8\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}12\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}16\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}20\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n|$T\\,\\,(^\\circ C)$|$\\hspace{0.5em}22.0\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}18.2\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}12.5\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}9.84\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}4.48\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}1.15\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the independent variable.\n\n    2.  Write down the warmest air temperature of the house. [2 marks]\n\nJohn believes that the relationship between $T$ and $t$ can be modelled\nby a linear regression equation.\n\n2.  John describes the correlation as **very strong**. Circle\n    the value below which best represents the correlation coefficient.\n    [2 marks]\n\n    |$\\hspace{2.5em}0.997\\hspace{3em}0.272\\hspace{3em}0\\hspace{2.5em}-0.272\\hspace{2.5em}-0.997\\hspace{2.5em}$|\n    |:---:|\n\n3.  John's model is $T = -1.06\\hspace{0.05em}t + 22.0$, for\n    $0 \\leq t \\leq 20$. Use this model to predict the decrease in\n    temperature for any $2$ hour interval. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $t$\n\n    2.\n        $22\\hspace{0.05em}\\text{\\textdegree{C}}$\n\n2.  If we circle the value which best represents the correlation\n    coefficient, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/cdea833eb6f5ac39164a02e7f54e9aa1f8bde1dc.svg\" alt=\"cdea833eb6f5ac39164a02e7f54e9aa1f8bde1dc.svg\">\n\n3.  We get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\triangle T &= -1.06\\times2 \\hspace{6.275em}\\\\[6pt]\n    &= -2.12\\text{\\hspace{0.05em}\\textdegree{C}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA410","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA410\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe following table shows values of $\\ln x$ and $\\ln y$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/8c64f77af5ec93279ceb96452d0a87f353ec5871.svg\" alt=\"8c64f77af5ec93279ceb96452d0a87f353ec5871.svg\">\n\nThe relationship between $\\ln x$ and $\\ln y$ can be modelled by the\nregression equation $\\ln y = a \\ln x + b$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Use the regression equation to estimate the value of\n     $y$  when\n     $x = 4.12$. [3 marks]\n\nThe relationship between $x$ and $y$ can be modelled using the formula\n$y = px^q$, where $p\\neq 0$ and $q \\neq 0$, $q\\neq 1$.\n\n3.  Expressing $\\ln y$ in terms of $\\ln x$, find the value of\n    $p$ and the value of $q$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a \\approx -0.564$\n    and  $b \\approx 4.74$\n      \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.  Substituting $x = 4.12$ in the equation\n    $\\ln y = -0.564\\ln x + 4.74$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} \\ln y &= -0.564\\ln (4.12) + 4.74 \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= e^{-0.564\\ln (4.12) + 4.74} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    y \\approx 51.5 \\hspace{6em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the properties of logarithms, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\ln y &= -0.564\\ln x + 4.74 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln x^{-0.564} + \\ln e^{4.74} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\ln y &= \\ln\\hspace{-0.15em}\\big([x^{-0.564}][e^{4.74}]\\big) \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= [e^{4.74}][x^{-0.564}] \\\\[6pt]\n    y &= 114\\hspace{0.05em}x^{-0.564} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n     $p \\approx 114$\n    and\n     $q \\approx -0.564$"},{"ref":"AA417","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA417\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA coffee shop owner believes that the number of coffees they sell on a\ngiven day is related to the average temperature of\nthat day. The average temperature $T$, in degrees\nCelsius, on seven randomly selected days is shown in the\nfollowing table. The table also shows the number\nof coffees, $N$, the coffee shop sells on those\nseven days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/e182b3471e48ce381f17d81a5fd34ed73e64dd78.svg\" alt=\"e182b3471e48ce381f17d81a5fd34ed73e64dd78.svg\">\n\n1.  The relationship between the variables can be modelled by the\n    regression equation $N = aT+b$.\n\n    1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n    2.  To the nearest integer, estimate the number of coffees sold on a day when    the average temperature is\n        $16$ $\\text{\\textdegree{C}}$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the value of $r$, the correlation coefficient. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $a \\approx -9.93$\n        and\n         $b \\approx 222$\n          \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n    2.  Evaluating $N = -9.93\\hspace{0.05em}T + 222$ for $T = 16$, we\n        get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        N &= -9.93(16) + 222 \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 63.12 \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 63 \\text{ coffees}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    $r \\approx -0.955 \\hspace{5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]$"},{"ref":"AA418","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA418\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following table shows the average weights\n($y$ kg) for given heights\n($x$ cm) in a population\nof high school males.\n\n|$\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{Heights }(x\\text{ cm})\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}160\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}165\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}170\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}175\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}180\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}185\\hspace{1em}$|\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n|Weights ($y$ kg)|62.0|65.9|68.4|71.8|74.6|76.3|\n\n1.  The relationship between the variables can be modelled by the\n    regression $\\text{equation}$\n    $y = ax+b$.\n\n    1.  Write down the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n    2.  Hence estimate the weight of a male whose height is\n        $167$ cm. [4 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Write down the correlation coefficient.\n\n    2.  State which **two** of the following describe the correlation\n        between the variables.\n        $$\n        \\begin{array}{lll}\n        \\text{negative} & \\text{zero} & \\text{no correlation} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\text{strong} & \\text{weak} & \\text{positive}\n        \\end{array} \\hspace{1em}\n        $$\n        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $a \\approx 0.577$\n        and\n         $b \\approx -29.7$\n          \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n    2.  Evaluating $y = 0.577x - 29.7$ for $x = 167$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        y &= 0.577(167) - 29.7 \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 66.7 \\text{ kg}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $r \\approx 0.995$\n        \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n    2.\n        $\\text{strong}$\n        and\n         $\\text{positive}$"},{"ref":"AA421","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA421\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following table shows the amount of fuel ($y$ litres) used by a car\nto travel certain distances ($x$ km).\n\n|$\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{Distance travelled }(x\\text{ km})\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}14\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}35\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}80\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}115\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}145\\hspace{1em}$|$\\hspace{1em}170\\hspace{1em}$|\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n|Fuel used ($y$) litres|2.5|6.2|10.8|13.6|15.4|19.7|\n\n1.  Find the correlation coefficient. [2 marks]\n\nThe data can be modelled by the regression line with equation\n$y = ax + b$.\n\n2.  Write down the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Use the model to estimate the amount of fuel the car would\n    use if it has driven\n    $55$ km. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $r \\approx 0.991$\n    \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.\n    $a \\approx 0.101$\n    and  $b \\approx 1.96$\n      \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n3.  Evaluating $y = 0.101x + 1.96$ for $x = 55$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 0.101(55) + 1.96 \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 7.52 \\text{ litres}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA436","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA436\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following table shows the number of skateboards $y$ that are sold\n$x$ days after the shop opened.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/8ca41bbf1b72b153da5e126adf69caff34abe2e2.svg\" alt=\"8ca41bbf1b72b153da5e126adf69caff34abe2e2.svg\">\n\nThis data can be modelled by the regression line with equation\n$y = ax + b$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\n\n    2.  Explain what the gradient $a$ represents. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Use the model to estimate the number of skateboards sold in $5$\n    days after the shop opened. Round\n    your answer to the nearest integer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $a \\approx 4.44$\n        and\n         $b \\approx 1.07$\n          \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n    2.  :::answer\n        $4.44\\hspace{0.025em};\\hspace{0.25em}$ each day after the shop opened the number of skateboards sold increases by $4.44$\n\n2.  Evaluating $y = 4.44x + 1.07$ for $x = 5$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 4.44(5) + 1.07 \\hspace{1.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 23.27 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 23 \\text{ skateboards}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA453","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA453\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA study records the number of chickens and ducks on a farm after $t$\nyears, starting on $1$ January, $1997$.\n\nLet $c$ be the number of chickens on the farm after $t$ years. The\nfollowing table shows the number of chickens after $t$ years.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/445a139e443a870563b0c4df38d9119c2a8c93c1.svg\" alt=\"445a139e443a870563b0c4df38d9119c2a8c93c1.svg\">\n\nThe relationship between the variables can be modelled by the regression\nequation $c = at + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Use the regression equation to estimate the number of chickens on\n    the farm when $t = 6$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $d$ be the number of ducks on the farm after $t$ years. The number\nof ducks can be modelled by the equation $d = 400e^{-kt}$, where $k$ is\na constant.\n\n3.  Find the number of ducks on the farm on $1$ January,\n    $1997$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  After six years, there are $377$ ducks on the farm. Find\n    the value of $k$. [3 marks]\n\n5.  Find the year during which the number of chickens and ducks\n    were the same.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a \\approx 14.9$  and\n     $b \\approx 119$\n      \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.  Evaluating $c(t) = 14.9t + 119$ for $t = 6$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} c(6) &= 14.9(6) + 119 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 208 \\text{ chickens}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Evaluating $d(t) = 400e^{-kt}$ for $t = 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.2em} d(0) &= 400e^{-k(0)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 400 \\text{ ducks}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Solving the equation $d(6) = 377$ for $k$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    400e^{-k(6)} &= 377 \\hspace{4.9em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{11.1em} k \\approx 0.00987 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n5.  Solving the equation $c(t) = d(t)$ for $t$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.9em} 14.9t + 119 &= 400e^{-0.00987t} \\\\[6pt]\n    t &\\approx 15.1 \\text{ years} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the number of chickens and ducks were the same during\n     $2012$"},{"ref":"AA458","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA458\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following table below shows the points scored by eight basketball\nplayers in two games.\n\n|$\\hspace{0.5em}\\text{Game 1 }(x)\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}5\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}6\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}9\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}12\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}12\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}16\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}20\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}25\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n|Game 2 $(y)$|6|8|12|13|15|17|24|22|\n\nLet $L_1$ be the regression line of $x$ on $y$. The equation of the line\n$L_1$ can be written in the form $x = ay + b$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $L_2$ be the regression line of $y$ on $x$. The lines $L_1$ and\n$L_2$ both pass through the same point with coordinates $(p,q)$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $p$ and the value of $q$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Andrew's injury didn't allow him to play in the first game\n    but he played and scored $10$ points in the second game. Use an\n    appropriate regression equation to estimate the number of points\n    that Andrew would have scored in the first game. Round your answer\n    to the nearest point. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Carefully solving for $x$ on $y$ we find\n    $a \\approx 1.05$  and\n     $b \\approx -2.16$\n      \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.  Both lines pass through the mean point of the data given in the\n    table.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} p &= \\bar{x} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 13.1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[12pt]\n    q &= \\bar{y} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 14.6 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Evaluating $x = 1.05y - 2.16$ for $y = 10$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x &= 1.05(10) - 2.16 \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8.34 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 8 \\text{ points}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA459","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA459\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following table below shows the marks scored by six job candidates\non two aptitude tests.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/4385fab6f0a22eb1414db34b1a91764663e8e9c3.svg\" alt=\"4385fab6f0a22eb1414db34b1a91764663e8e9c3.svg\">\n\nLet $L_1$ be the regression line of $x$ on $y$. The equation of the line\n$L_1$ can be written in the form $x = ay + b$.\n\n1.  Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$. [2 marks]\n\nLet $L_2$ be the regression line of $y$ on $x$. The lines $L_1$ and\n$L_2$ both pass through the same point with coordinates $(p,q)$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $p$ and the value of $q$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Chelsea missed the first aptitude test but scored $101$\n    marks on the second aptitude test. Use an appropriate regression\n    equation to estimate Chelsea's mark on the first aptitude test.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $a \\approx 0.800$\n    and  $b \\approx 28.2$\n      \\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n\n2.  Both lines pass through the mean point of the data given in the\n    table.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.1em} p &= \\bar{x} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 105 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[12pt]\n    q &= \\bar{y} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 95.8 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Evaluating $x = 0.800y + 28.2$ for $y = 101$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.6em} x &= 0.800(101) + 28.2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 109 \\text{ marks}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA726","marks":"12","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA726\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe following table shows the total revenue, $y$, in Australian dollars\n(AUD), $\\text{obtained}$ daily during the first week of January\n$2020$, by Peppy's Pizza $\\text{restaurant}$ and the number of\nguests, $x$, served.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/9736e8eb9c9cfe8a065bfa8f5344721e7887b704.svg\" alt=\"9736e8eb9c9cfe8a065bfa8f5344721e7887b704.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Calculate the Pearson's product-moment correlation coefficient,\n        $r$, for this data.\n\n    2.  Comment on the result. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Write down the equation of the regression line $y$ on $x$.\n    [1 mark]\n\n3.  Use the line of the regression to estimate the revenue of serving\n    $70$ guests. Give your answer correct to **the nearest AUD**.\n    [2 marks]\n\nThe daily maintenance cost for the restaurant is $300$ AUD. Additionally,\nthe cost of serving one guest is $5$ AUD.\n\n4.  Determine if the restaurant expects to make a profit when serving\n    $50$ guests on a $\\text{particular}$ day. [2 marks]\n\n5.\n    1.  Find an expression for the profit of the restaurant when serving\n        $x$ guests on a particular day.\n\n    2.  Find the least number of guests required to be served to result\n        in a profit for the day. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $r = 0.991$ $\\hspace{3em}$\\[$\\text{by using G.D.C.}$\\]\n    2.\n        $\\text{Strong, positive correlation between the number of guests and revenue}$\n\n2.\n    By using G.D.C.,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 10.41...(x) - 14.255... \\\\[6pt]\n    &y = 10.4x - 14.3\\hspace{0.5em} \\text{(3 s.f.)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Evaluating $y = 10.4x - 14.3$ for $x = 70$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    y &= 10.4(70) - 14.3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 713.7 \\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\boxed{714 \\text{ AUD}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The expected profit of the restaurant when serving $50$ guests on a\n    particular day is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Profit} &= \\text{Revenue} - \\text{Costs} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 10.4(50) - 14.3 - (300 + 5(50)) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= -44.3 \\text{ AUD} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $\\text{Hence the restaurant expects to make a loss when serving only \\(50\\) guests.}$\n\n5.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.4em} \\mathrm{Profit} &= \\text{Revenue} - \\text{Costs} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (10.4x - 14.3) - (300 + 5x) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 10.4x - 14.3 - 300 - 5x \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 5.4x - 314.3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Solving the inequality $\\text{Profit} > 0$ for $x$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.1em} 5.4x - 314.3 &> 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        x &> 58.2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence\n         $\\text{at least \\(59\\) guests}$\n        are required to be served to result in a profit."},{"ref":"AA920","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA920\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA real estate company keeps a register of the monthly cost of rent, $R$, of their apartments and their corresponding area, $A$, in m$^2$. \n\nThe areas of the apartments registered are summarised in the following box and whisker diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/AA920a.PNG\" alt=\"AA920a\">\n\n1. Find the smallest area $A$ that would not be considered an outlier. [3 marks]\n\nThe regression line $A$ on $R$ is $A=\\dfrac{5}{12}R-50$.\n\nMeanwhile, the regression line $R$ on $A$ is $R=\\dfrac{5}{2}A+100$.\n\n2. One of the apartments has a monthly rent of $$\\$480$$. Estimate the area of the rental. [2 marks]\n\n3. Find the mean rental cost of all the real estate company's apartments. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Bivariate%20Statistics/images/AA920b.PNG\" alt=\"AA920b\">\n\n    Hence we obtain\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 276 - 176 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 100\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    In general, the smallest value in a data set that is not considered an outlier is $q_1 - 1.5 \\times \\text{IQR}$. Therefore, the smallest area $A$ that would not be considered an outlier is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A &= 176 - 1.5\\times 100  \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.75em}\\boxed{A = 26\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{m}^2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Substituting $R = 480$ into the regression equation $A$ on $R$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    A &= \\dfrac{5}{12}(480)-50  \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= 150\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    An apartment with a rent of \\$480 has an area of approximately $150\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{m}^2$\n\n3. We need to find the value of $\\bar{R}$. By definition, the mean point $(\\bar{R},\\bar{A})$, or $(\\bar{A},\\bar{R})$, lies on both regression lines. Hence the mean point will be found at the intersection of the regression lines. \n\n    By substituting $A=\\dfrac{5}{12}R-50$ into the regression equation $R$ on $A$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    R &= \\dfrac{5}{2}\\bigg(\\dfrac{5}{12}R-50\\bigg)+100  \\\\[10pt]\n\n    R &= \\dfrac{25R}{24} -\\dfrac{250}{2} + 100 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    R &= \\dfrac{25R}{24} -25 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    25 &= \\dfrac{25R}{24} - R \\\\[10pt]\n\n    25 &= \\dfrac{R}{24} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    R &= 25(24)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The mean rental cost is $$\\$600$$"}]},{"name":"Continuous Random Variables","questions":[{"ref":"AA1181","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1181\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nSeb commutes to work every day.\n\nFor the first part of his journey he walks to the train station and for the second part he waits for the train and the third part he rides the train to work.\n\nThe continuous random variable $W$, in minutes, is the time it takes him to walk to the train station.\n\n1.  It is known that $W$ is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu_{W}$ and a standard deviation of $\\sigma_{W}$.\n    1.  If $P(W<6)=0.07529)$ and $P(W>9)=0.3309)$ to $4$ significant figures. Find $\\mu_{W}$ and $\\sigma_{W}$.\n\n    2.  Hence find $\\text{E}(W)$ and $\\text{Var}(W)$. [4 marks]\n\nWhen Seb reaches the train station he must wait for the train.\n\nThe continuous random variable $T$, in minutes, is the time he waits for the train.\n\n2.  It is known that $T=f(t)$ where\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/piece.png\" alt=\"piece\">\n\n    Where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n    1.   Find $a$.\n\n    2.   Hence find $\\text{E}(T)$ and $\\text{Var}(T)$. [5 marks]\n\nSeb is analysing the total time it takes him to get to work.\n\nTo do this he adds all $3$ parts of the journey together.\n\nThe train ride always takes exactly $10$ minutes.\n\nThe continuous random variable $X$ is the total time it takes Seb to travel to work, therefore $X=W+T+10$.\n\nOne day there is a problem with the train and the wait time, $T$, changes to $T_X$ such that $T_X=4T-2$ minutes.\n\n3.  Use the following two facts\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{E}(X)&=\\text{E}(W)+\\text{E}(T_X)+ \\text{E}(10)\\\\[6pt]\n        \\text{Var}(X)&=\\text{Var}(W)+\\text{Var}(T_X)+ \\text{Var}(10)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    to find $\\text{E}(X)$ and $\\text{Var}(X)$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  As we need to find both parameters that define the distribution we will need to use the standard normal distribution.\n\n        The image below shows the two given regions of $W$ and the corresponding position on the standard normal distribution curve.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/AA1181_s1%20%281%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1181_s1 (1)\">\n\n        Where $W=6$ corresponds to $z_1$ and $W=9$ corresponds to $z_2$.\n\n        Hence, from the image we can say\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            P(W<6)&=P(Z<z_1) &\\hspace{2em} & P(W>9)=P(Z>z_2)\\\\[6pt]\n            P(Z<z_1)&=0.07529 &\\hspace{2em} & P(Z>z_2)=0.3309\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Any normal distribution can be standardised to a standard normal distribution using the formula $\\displaystyle Z=\\frac{X-\\mu}{\\sigma}$.\n\n        To find the $Z-$scores that correspond to the given probabilities we need use the inverse standard normal function.\n\n        The inverse normal function is generally defined in the form $P(Z<z)$, therefore we use the symmetry of the normal distribution to rewrite the inequality for $z_2$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            P(W<6)&=P(Z<z_1) &\\hspace{2em} & P(W>9)=P(Z>z_2)\\\\[6pt]\n            P(Z<z_1)&=0.07529 &\\hspace{2em} & P(Z\\textcolor{red}{>}z_2)=0.3309\\\\[6pt]\n            & &\\hspace{2em} & P(W<9)=1-P(Z>z_2)\\\\[6pt]\n            & &\\hspace{2em} & P(W<9)=1-0.3309\\\\[6pt]\n            & &\\hspace{2em} & P(Z\\textcolor{red}{<}z_2)=0.6691\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can now use the inverse normal function to find $z_1$ and $z_2$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            z_1&=\\text{invNorm}(0.07529,0,1)&\\hspace{2em}&z_2=\\text{invNorm}(0.6691,0,1)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=-1.4374...&\\hspace{2em}&=0.4374...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Next we use the standardised normal variable formula to form two equations in terms of $\\mu_{W}$ and $\\sigma_{W}$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            z_1&=\\frac{X_1-\\mu_{W}}{\\sigma_{W}} & \\hspace{2em} & z_2=\\frac{X_2-\\mu_{W}}{\\sigma_{W}}\\\\[6pt]\n            -1.4374...&=\\frac{6-\\mu_{W}}{\\sigma_{W}} & \\hspace{2em} & 0.4374...=\\frac{9-\\mu_{W}}{\\sigma_{W}}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            -1.4374...\\sigma_{W}&=6-\\mu_{W} & \\hspace{2em} & 0.4374...\\sigma_{W}=9-\\mu_{W}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can simultaneously solve the equations above to get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &\\mu_{W}=8.30 \\hspace{2em}\\sigma_{W}=1.60 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  We can find the expected value and variance\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\text{E}(W)&=\\mu_{W} &\\hspace{2em} &\\text{Var}(W)=(\\sigma_{W})^2\\\\[6pt]\n            \\text{E}(W)&=8.30 \\, \\text{minutes} &\\hspace{2em} &\\text{Var}(W)=(1.60)^2\\\\[6pt]\n            & &\\hspace{2em} &\\text{Var}(W)=2.56 \\, \\text{minutes}^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  1.  The area under a probability distribution curve must be equal to $1$.\n\n        Hence, we can form an equation\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\int_{0}^{8}f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=1\\\\[6pt]\n            \\int_{0}^{8}\\frac{a(8-t)}{t+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=1 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can solve the above equation using a G.D.C.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            a=0.0849 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We can check our answer by sketching the function on a G.D.C. and verifying the area under the curve from $x=0$ to $x=8$ is equal to $1$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/AA1181_s2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1181_s2\">\n\n    2.  Using the formula for the expected value of a CRV we can write\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\text{E}(T)&=\\int_{0}^{8}tf(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\int_{0}^{8}t\\times\\frac{0.0849(8-t)}{t+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\[6pt]\n            &=1.717... \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\\\[6pt]\n            &=1.72\\, \\text{minutes} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        However, for this we need to evaluate $\\text{E}(T^2)$\n\n        We can find this using a G.D.C.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\text{E}(\\textcolor{red}{T^2})&=\\int_{0}^{8}\\textcolor{red}{t^2}f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\int_{0}^{8}t^2\\times\\frac{0.0849(8-t)}{t+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\[6pt]\n            &=5.527...  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\\\[6pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\text{Var}(T)&=\\text{E}(T^2)-[(\\text{E}(T)]^2\\\\[6pt]\n            &=5.527...-(1.717...)^2\\\\[6pt]\n            &=2.5792...\\\\[6pt]\n            &=2.58\\,\\text{minutes}^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n3.  Let's first find $\\text{E}(T_X)$ and $\\text{Var}(T_X)$.\n\n    We need to use the formula for the linear transform of a RV\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{E}(aX+b)&=a\\text{E}(X)+b &\\hspace{2em} &\\text{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\text{Var}(X)\\\\[6pt]\n        \\text{E}(T_X)&=\\text{E}(4T-2) &\\hspace{2em} &\\hspace{1.8em}\\text{Var}(T_X)=\\text{Var}(4T-2)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=4\\text{E}(T)-2 &\\hspace{2em} &\\hspace{5.5em}=4^2\\text{Var}(T) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now use the answers from part (b)(ii)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=4\\times 1.717..-2 &\\hspace{2em} &\\hspace{5.5em}=16\\times 2.579...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=4.8684... &\\hspace{2em} &\\hspace{5.5em}=41.268... \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We aso realise that if C is a constant then $\\text{E}(C)=C$ and $\\text{Var}(C)=0$, hence, using our answers from part (a)(ii) and (b)(ii)\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\text{E}(X)=\\text{W}(X)+\\text{E}(T_X)+\\text{E}(10)&\\hspace{2em} &\\text{Var}(X)=\\text{Var}(X)+\\text{Var}(T_X)+\\text{Var}(10)\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\text{E}(X)=8.300...+4.8684...+10&\\hspace{2em} &\\hspace{3.2em}=2.560...+ 41.268...+0\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{2.3em}=23.168...\n        ... &\\hspace{2em} &\\hspace{3.2em}=43.828...\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.4em}\\text{E}(X)=23.2\\,\\text{minutes}\n        &\\hspace{2em} &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\text{Var}(X)=43.8\\,\\text{minutes}^2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1296","marks":"21","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1296\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nA continuous random variable, $X$, has a probability density function defined by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    f(x)=\\begin{cases}\n        \\dfrac{1}{4}x, & 0 \\leq x \\leq a \\\\[12pt]\n        \\dfrac{2}{7}-\\dfrac{1}{28}x, & a \\leq x \\leq 8\\\\[12pt]\n        0, & \\text{otherwise}\n    \\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  It is known that $\\text{P}(X \\leq a)=0.125$.\n\n    1.  Show that $a=1$.\n\n    2.  Hence show that $\\mathrm{E}(X)=3$.[4 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\text{P}(0.5\\leq X \\leq 2)$.[3 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $\\mathrm{E}(X^2)=\\frac{73}{6}$ find $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$.[1 mark]\n\nAnother continuous random variable, $Y$, is normally distributed with a mean of $1.5$ and a standard deviation of $\\sigma_Y$.\n\nFor this distribution it is known that $\\text{P}(Y \\geq 1.8367)=0.2$. This information is shown below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/AA1296-q2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1296-q2\">\n\n4.  Find $\\sigma_Y$.[3 marks]\n\n5.  Hence find $\\text{P}(0.5\\leq Y \\leq 2)$.[1 mark]\n\nA water utility company serves a large number of households. $90\\%$ of their households are classed as __regular__ households. The rest are classed as __premium__ households.\n\nThe water usage per day, in $\\text{m}^3$, of the regular households is modeled by the random variable $X$.\n\nThe water usage per day, in $\\text{m}^3$, of the premium households is modeled by the random variable $Y$.\n\nIf a household uses between $0.5\\,\\text{m}^3$ and $2\\,\\text{m}^3$ of water per day they are eligible for a special deal.\n\n6.  A household is chosen at random. It is found that they are eligible for a special deal. What is the probability they are a premium household?[3 marks]\n\nThe water company charges the households a daily fee based on how much water is used in that day.\n\nThe charge per day, $C_X$, in \\$US, for the regular households is calculated using the following formula\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    C_X&=1.5X+0.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n7.  Find\n    1.  $\\text{E}(C_X)$.\n\n    2.  $\\text{Var}(C_X)$.[3 marks]\n\nPremium households are charged in a different way to regular households.\n\nThe charge per day, $C_Y$, in \\$US, for the premium households is calculated using the following formula\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    C_Y&=sY+t \\hspace{2em}\\text{where } s,t \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf the value of $\\text{E}(C_Y)$ is  \\$$1$ less than the value of $\\text{E}(C_X)$ and $\\text{Var}(C_Y)=\\tfrac{32}{57}\\text{Var}(C_X)$ then\n\n8.  Given that $s>0$, find $s$ and $t$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  The probability density function (P.D.F.) of $X$ is a piecewise function consisting of two straight lines.\n\n        We can sketch the first part of the P.D.F., from $x=0$ to $x=a$, noting that the end-point has coordinates $(a, \\tfrac{1}{4}a)$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/AA1296-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1296-a1\">\n\n    From the information given we know the area under the graph for this part is $0.125$.\n\n    Hence we can form an equation for the area of the triangle\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0.125&=\\frac{a \\times \\tfrac{1}{4}a}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n        a^2&=1\\\\[6pt]\n        a&=1\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    From the information given we can see that $a>0$.\n\n    2.  We can make a rough sketch of the rest of the P.D.F., noting that the two lines do intersect when $x=1$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/AA1296-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1296-a2\">\n\n        We can use the formula for the expected value of a continuous random variable but we must be careful to evaluate the integral over both parts and sum the results\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\mathrm{E}(X)&=\\int_{0}^{1}x\\times\\tfrac{1}{4}x\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int_{1}^{8}x\\times\\left[\\tfrac{2}{7}-\\tfrac{1}{28}x\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can integrate each term to get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\bigg[\\tfrac{1}{12}x^3\\bigg]_{0}^{1}+\\bigg[\\tfrac{1}{7}x^2-\\tfrac{1}{84}x^3\\bigg]_{1}^{8}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n            &=\\tfrac{1}{12}+\\left(\\tfrac{64}{7}-\\tfrac{512}{84}\\right)-\\left(\\tfrac{1}{7}-\\tfrac{1}{84}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{35}{12}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=3\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required.}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We can identify on our sketch the region whose area we need to find\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/AA1296-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1296-a3\">\n\n    In a similar fashion to the previous part we need to evaluate the different parts of the function and then sum them together.\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(0.5\\leq X \\leq 2)&=\\int_{0.5}^{1}\\tfrac{1}{4}x\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int_{1}^{2}\\left[\\tfrac{2}{7}-\\tfrac{1}{28}x\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1A1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=0.3258...\\\\[12pt]\n        &=0.326\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the formula for variance and the value for $\\mathrm{E}(X)$ from part (a) we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{Var}(X)&=\\mathrm{E}(X^2)-\\left(\\mathrm{E}(X)\\right)^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{73}{6}-3^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{19}{6}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=3.166....\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  To find an unknown standard deviation we need to use the $Z-$distribution along with the standardisation formula.\n\n    This distribution is defined as $Z \\sim N(0,1^2)$.\n\n    Recall, $\\text{P}(Y \\geq 1.8367)=0.2$, we want to find the corresponding $Z-$score.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{inverseNorm(0.8, 0, 1)}&=0.8416...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Notice we used $0.8$, this is because the $0.2$ region is in the upper tail of the distribution.\n\n    We can now use the standardised normal variable formulae, with $Y$ replacing $X$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        Z&=\\frac{Y-\\mu}{\\sigma}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We know that $\\mu=1.5$ and when $Y=1.8367$ then $Z=0.8416...$, hence we can form an equation and solve for $\\sigma_Y$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0.8416...&=\\frac{1.8367-1.5}{\\sigma_Y}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\sigma_Y&=0.4000...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.400\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We can now fully define the distribution for $Y$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        Y &\\sim N(1.5, 0.400^2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using a G.D.C. we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{P}(0.5\\leq Y \\leq 2)&=0.8878...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.888\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  In this question we have been given a condition. We are told that the chosen customer is eligible for the special deal.\n\n    Consider the conditional probability formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A|B)=\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}}{\\textcolor{blue}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    If we assign event $A$ as $'$the household is a premium customer$'$ and event $B$ as $'$the household is eligible for the special deal$'$, then $ \\mathrm{P}(A|B)$ is the probability that the household is premium __given__ they are eligible.\n\n    First consider $\\mathrm{P}(B)$. This represents the probability that the chosen customer is eligible for a special deal.\n\n    The calculation for this includes __both__ the regular households who are eligible and the premium households who are eligible.\n\n    We can use our answers from part (b) and (e) and the given information about the proportion of household type to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(B)&=0.9 \\times 0.3258... + 0.1 \\times 0.8878...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\textcolor{blue}{0.3820...}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    To find $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)$, the household is premium __and__ eligible, we calculate\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)&=0.1 \\times 0.8878...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\textcolor{red}{0.08878...}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Therefore putting these results together we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A|B)&=\\frac{\\textcolor{red}{0.08878...}}{\\textcolor{blue}{0.3820...}}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.2323...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.232\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n6.  This part of the questions concerns the linear transform of a random variable.\n\n    We will be using the following formulae\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}(aX+b)&=a\\mathrm{E}(X)+b\\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{Var}(aX+b)&=a^2\\mathrm{Var}(X)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    1.  Using the formula above we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}(C_X)&=\\mathrm{E}(1.5X+0.5)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1.5\\,\\mathrm{E}(X)+0.5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        From part (a) we know that $\\mathrm{E}(X)=3$, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=1.5\\times 3+0.5\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\$5\\hspace{1em} \\text{per day}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Similarly we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{Var}(C_X)&=\\mathrm{Var}(1.5X+0.5)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=1.5^2\\,\\mathrm{Var}(X)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Using our answer from part (c) we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{Var}&=1.5^2\\,\\ \\times \\frac{19}{6} \\\\[6pt]\n        &=7.125\\hspace{1em}\\text{dollars}^2\\text{ per day}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\tfrac{57}{8}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n7.  We can begin by forming two equations from the information provided\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &&\\mathrm{E}(C_Y)&= \\mathrm{E}(C_X)-1&\\hspace{-1em} &&\\mathrm{Var}(C_Y)&= \\tfrac{32}{57}\\text{Var}(C_X)&\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the answers from part (g) we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &&\\mathrm{E}(C_Y)&= 5-1&\\hspace{-1em} &&\\mathrm{Var}(C_Y)&= \\tfrac{32}{57}\\times \\tfrac{57}{8}&\\\\[6pt]\n        &&&= 4&\\hspace{-1em} &&&= 4&\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall $C_Y=sY+t$, hence, using the results above, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &&\\mathrm{E}(C_Y)&= \\mathrm{E}(sY+t)&\\hspace{-1em} &&\\mathrm{Var}(C_Y)&= \\mathrm{Var}(sY+t)&\\\\[6pt]\n        &&4&= s\\,\\mathrm{E}(Y)+t&\\hspace{-1em} &&4&= s^2\\,\\mathrm{Var}(Y)&\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    In part (d) we found $\\sigma_Y=0.4000...$, therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{Var}(Y)&=\\sigma_Y^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.4000...^2\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.1600...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Recall, we know that $\\mu_Y=1.5$, hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &&4&= s \\times 1.5+t&\\hspace{-1em} &&4&= s^2 \\times 0.1600...&\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving the equation on the right we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        s^2&=\\frac{4}{0.1600...}\\\\[6pt]\n        s&=4.999...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=5.00\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As $s>0$ we take only the positive value.\n\n    Using the other equation we can find $t$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        4&=1.5\\times 4.999...+t\\\\[6pt]\n        t&=-3.498...\\\\[6pt]\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        &=-3.50\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA374","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA374\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA continuous random variable $T$ has a probability density function $f$\ndefined by\n$$\nf(t) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\n\\dfrac{1}{4}|t-3|, & 1 \\leq t \\leq 5, \\\\[9pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n1.  Sketch the graph of $y = f(t)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the lower quartile of $T$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the interquartile range of $T$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(t)$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/83f779aeb4857017028b8c532ae03336a4d552a9.svg\" alt=\"83f779aeb4857017028b8c532ae03336a4d552a9.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Let $q_1$ be the lower quartile of $T$.\n        If we solve the equation $\\mathrm{P}(T \\leq q_1) = 0.25$ for\n        $q_1$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{1}{4}\\int_1^{q_1} (3-t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= 0.25 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $1 < q_1 < 3$}] \\\\[4pt]\n        \\bigg[3t - \\dfrac{t^2}{2}\\bigg]\\bigg|_1^{q_1} \\hspace{-0.15em}&= 4(0.25) \\\\[4pt]\n        \\bigg[3q_1 - \\dfrac{q_1^2}{2}\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\hspace{-0.15em}\\bigg[3 - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg] \\hspace{-0.15em}&= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n        6q_1-q_1^2 - 5 &= 2 \\\\[8pt]\n        0 &= q_1^2 - 6q_1  + 7 \\\\[9pt]\n        [\\text{by quadratic formula}] \\hspace{2.25em} q_1 &= \\dfrac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 4(1)(7)}}{2(1)} \\hspace{5.5em} \\\\[4pt]\n        q_1 &= \\dfrac{6-\\sqrt{8}}{2} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $q_1 < 3$}] \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        q_1 = 3 - \\sqrt{2} \\hspace{1.75em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Let $q_3$ be the upper quartile of $T$.\n        Using the symmetry of the graph of $y =f(t)$, we have\n        $q_3 = 3 + \\sqrt{2}$.\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.1em} \\text{IQR} &= q_3  - q_1 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (3 + \\sqrt{2}) - (3 - \\sqrt{2}) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2\\sqrt{2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA375","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA375\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA continuous random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f$\ngiven by\n$$\n\\hspace{8.1em} f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n\\dfrac{x^3}{a} + \\dfrac{1}{b}, & 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\hspace{1em} \\text{where $a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}^+$}, \\\\[9pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nIt is given that $\\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 2) = 0.31481$,\ncorrect to five decimal places.\n\n1.  Show that $a = 27$ and $b = 12$, correct to the nearest integer.\n    [5 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[X]$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Find the median of $X$. [3 marks]\n\nTwelve independent observations of $X$ are now taken.\n\n5.  A random variable $Y$ is the number of observations of $X$\n    such that $X \\geq 2$.\n\n    1.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[Y]$.\n    2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(Y\\geq4)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_0^3 \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^3}{a} + \\dfrac{1}{b}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x &= 1 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^4}{4a} + \\dfrac{x}{b}\\bigg]\\bigg|_0^3 &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{81}{4a} + \\dfrac{3}{b} &= 1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 2) &= 0.31481 \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\int_0^2 \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^3}{a} + \\dfrac{1}{b}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x &= 0.31481 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^4}{4a} + \\dfrac{x}{b}\\bigg]\\bigg|_0^2 &= 0.31481 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{4}{a} + \\dfrac{2}{b} &= 0.31481\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, solving the system of equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$ and $b$,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8.4em} a \\approx 27\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}b \\approx 12 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9.1em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} xf(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\int_0^3 x\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^3}{27} + \\dfrac{1}{12}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\int_0^3 \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^4}{27} + \\dfrac{x}{12}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[14pt]\n    &= 2.175 \\quad \\quad [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X^2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.2em} \\mathrm{E}[X^2] &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\int_0^3 x^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^3}{27} + \\dfrac{1}{12}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\int_0^3 \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^5}{27} + \\dfrac{x^2}{12}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[14pt]\n    &= 5.25 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    using the formula for the variance of $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= \\mathrm{E}[X^2] - (\\mathrm{E}[X])^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5.25 - (2.175)^2 \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 0.519 \\hspace{3em} [= 0.519375] \\hspace{0.2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  If we solve the equation $\\mathrm{P}(X \\leq m) = 0.5$ for $m$, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.5em} \\int_0^m \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^3}{27} + \\dfrac{1}{12}\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x &= 0.5  \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $0 < m < 3$}] \\\\[4pt]\n    \\bigg[\\dfrac{x^4}{108} + \\dfrac{x}{12}\\bigg]\\bigg|_0^m &= 0.5  \\\\[4pt]\n    \\dfrac{m^4}{108} + \\dfrac{m}{12} &= 0.5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{12.9em} m \\approx 2.39 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 2) &= 1- \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 2) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - 0.31481 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.68519\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    $Y\\hspace{-0.1em}\\sim \\mathrm{B}(12,0.68519)$.\n    1.  Using the formula for the expected value of $Y$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[Y] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (12)(0.68519) \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 8.22\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3.2em} \\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 4) &= \\text{binomCdf}(12,0.68519,4,12) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.998\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA380","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA380\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA continuous random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f$\ngiven by\n$$\nf(x) = \\left\\{\\begin{array}{cl}\n\\dfrac{1}{72}(-x^2 + 12x - 20), & 4 < x < 10, \\\\[9pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[X]$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(\\mu - \\sigma < X < \\mu)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} xf(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &=  \\int_4^{10} x\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{72} (-x^2+12x-20)\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{72}\\int_4^{10} \\hspace{-0.2em}\\big[\\hspace{-0.2em}-x^3+12x^2-20x\\big]\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[14pt]\n    &= 6.5 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X^2$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.3em} \\mathrm{E}[X^2] &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &=  \\int_4^{10} x^2\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{72} (-x^2+12x-20)\\bigg]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{72}\\int_4^{10} \\hspace{-0.2em}\\big[\\hspace{-0.2em}-x^4+12x^3-20x^2\\big]\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[14pt]\n    &= 44.4 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    using the formula for the variance of $X$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.55em} \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= \\mathrm{E}[X^2] - (\\mathrm{E}[X])^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 44.4 - (6.5)^2 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 2.15\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have $\\mu = 6.5$ and\n    $\\mu - \\sigma = 6.5 - \\sqrt{2.15} \\approx 5.034$. Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathrm{P}(\\mu - \\sigma < X < \\mu) &= \\mathrm{P}(5.034 < X < 6.5) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= \\int_{5.034}^{6.5} f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &=  \\int_{5.034}^{6.5} \\dfrac{1}{72}(-x^2+12x-20)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.321 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA382","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA382\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nThe continuous random variable $X$ has a probability density function\ngiven by\n$$\nf(x) = \\left\\{\\begin{array}{ccl}\nh\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{12}\\Big)\\hspace{-0.1em}    &\\text{if}& |x| \\leq 6, \\\\[9pt]\n0    &\\text{if}& |x| > 6.\n\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\n1.  Find the value of $h$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  By considering the graph of $f$, write down the mean, median and\n    mode of $X$.[3 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.  Show that $\\mathrm{P}(-2 \\leq X \\leq 0) = 0.25$.\n\n    2.  Hence state the interquartile range of $X$. [6 marks]\n\n4.  Calculate\n    $\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq -2 \\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} X \\leq 2)$.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= 1 \\hspace{6em} \\\\[3pt]\n    \\int_{-6}^6 h\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{12}\\Big)\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathrm{d}x &= 1 \\\\[4pt]\n    \\dfrac{12h}{\\pi} \\Big[\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{12}\\Big)\\Big] \\Big|_{-6}^6 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{12h}{\\pi}\\Big[\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\Big)\\hspace{-0.15em} - \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\Big)\\Big] \\hspace{-0.15em} &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{12h}{\\pi}\\left[1 - (-1)\\right]&= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{5.6em} h = \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Continuous%20Random%20Variables/images/4d0ce004711da07012e065ef6acf79ba30c52d7b.svg\" alt=\"4d0ce004711da07012e065ef6acf79ba30c52d7b.svg\">\n\n    Hence we get\n     $\\text{mean} = 0,\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{median} = 0,\\hspace{0.25em} \\text{mode} = 0$\n\n3.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(-2 \\leq X \\leq 0) &= \\int_{-2}^0 \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\cos\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{12}\\Big)\\hspace{0.1em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[3pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big[\\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{12}\\Big)\\Big] \\Big|_{-2}^0 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\Big[\\sin\\hspace{0.05em}(0) - \\sin\\hspace{-0.1em}\\Big(\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\Big)\\Big] \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{2}[0 - (-1/2)] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{4} \\\\[11pt]\n        &= 0.25\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using part (c) (i) and the symmetry of the graph of $y = f(x)$,\n        we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{8.7em} q_1 &= -2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{lower quartile of $X$}] \\\\[6pt]\n        q_3 &= 2\\phantom{-} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{upper quartile of $X$}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2 - (-2) \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  Using the conditional probability formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq -2 \\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} X \\leq 2) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq -2 \\hspace{0.15em}\\cap\\hspace{0.15em} X \\leq 2)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 2)} \\hspace{0.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(-2 \\leq X \\leq 2)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 2)} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.5}{0.75} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA393","marks":"13","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA393\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nThe continuous random variable $X$ has probability density function $f$\ngiven by\n$$\n\nf(x) = \\left\\{\\begin{array}{cl}\n3p\\hspace{0.025em}x, & 0 \\leq x < 1, \\\\[6pt]\np(4-x), & 1 \\leq x < 4, \\\\[6pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n1.  Show that $p = \\dfrac{1}{6}$.  [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(X < 2)$.  [3 marks]\n\n3.  Given that\n    $\\mathrm{P}(a < X < 2) = 3\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(3\\hspace{0.02em}a < X < 2)$,\n    where $\\dfrac{1}{3} < a < \\dfrac{2}{3}$,\n\n    find the value of $a$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\,\\mathrm{d}x &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\int_0^1 3px\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\int_1^4p(4-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    p\\bigg[\\int_0^1 3x\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\int_1^4(4-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.2em} &= 1 \\\\[13pt]\n    6p &= 1 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{1.6em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    p = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} \\mathrm{P}(X < 2) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 2) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 1 - \\dfrac{1}{6}\\int_2^4 (4-x)\\, \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{5.8em} \\mathrm{P}(a < X < 2) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_a^1x\\, \\mathrm{d}x + \\dfrac{1}{6}\\int_1^2(4-x)\\, \\mathrm{d}x \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{since $a < 1$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg[\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\bigg]\\bigg|_a^1\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\dfrac{5}{12} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac{a^2}{2}\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.15em} + \\dfrac{5}{12} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac{a^2}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.6em} \\mathrm{P}(3a < X < 2) &= \\dfrac{1}{6}\\int_{3a}^2(4-x)\\, \\mathrm{d}x \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $1 < 3a < 2$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{6}\\bigg[4x - \\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\bigg]\\bigg|_{3a}^2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{6}\\bigg[6 - \\bigg(12a - \\hspace{-0.25em}\\dfrac{9a^2}{2}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - 2a + \\dfrac{3a^2}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the\n    equation\n    $\\mathrm{P}(a < X < 2) = 3\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(3a < X < 2)$ for\n    $a$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac{a^2}{4} &= 3\\bigg[1 - 2a + \\dfrac{3a^2}{4}\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n    \\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac{a^2}{4} &= 3 - 6a + \\dfrac{9a^2}{4} \\\\[7pt]\n    \\hspace{1.1em} \\dfrac{5a^2}{2} - 6a + \\dfrac{7}{3} &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{3em} a \\approx 0.488 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $a < 1$}] \\hspace{0.8em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA456","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA456\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA continuous random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f$\ngiven by\n$$\nf(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\n\\dfrac{\\pi x}{16}\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big), & 0 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\\\[9pt]\n0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nFind $\\mathrm{P}(0 \\leq X \\leq 2)$.","markScheme":"Using the integration by parts formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(0 \\leq X \\leq 2) &= \\int_0^2 \\dfrac{\\pi x}{16}\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\int_0^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\hspace{2em} \\Big[u = \\dfrac{\\pi x}{16},\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{d}v = \\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x\\Big] \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\Big[uv\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 - \\int_0^2 v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\hspace{2em} \\Big[v = -\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big),\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\pi}{16}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\Big] \\\\[8pt]\n&= \\Big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\dfrac{x}{4}\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\int_0^2 \\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[10pt]\n&= \\Big[\\hspace{-0.15em}-\\dfrac{x}{4}\\cos\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 + \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\Big[\\sin\\hspace{-0.15em}\\Big(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\Big)\\Big]\\Big|_0^2 \\\\[12pt]\n&= 0 + \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\Big[1 - 0\\Big] \\\\[12pt]\n\\mathrm{P}(0 \\leq X \\leq 2)&=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA677","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA677\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA continuous random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f$\ngiven by\n$$\nf(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x^2}, & \\hspace{0.5em}1 \\leq x < \\infty, \\\\[6pt]\n0, & \\hspace{0.5em}\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(X < e^3)$. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, or otherwise, find $\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq e^3)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the integration by parts formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X < e^3) &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{e^3} f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\int_1^{e^3} \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\int_1^{e^3} u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\hspace{2em} \\big[u = \\ln x,\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{d}v = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\Big[uv\\Big]\\bigg|_1^{e^3} - \\int_1^{e^3} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\hspace{2em} \\big[\\textstyle v = -\\dfrac{1}{x},\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\Big[\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\Big]\\bigg|_1^{e^3} - \\int_1^{e^3} \\left[\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right]\\hspace{0.025em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\Big[\\hspace{-0.2em}-\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\Big]\\bigg|_1^{e^3} - \\left[\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right]\\bigg|_1^{e^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &= \\left[-\\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}-\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right]\\bigg|_1^{e^3} \\\\[14pt]\n    &= \\left[-\\dfrac{3}{e^3}-\\dfrac{1}{e^3}\\right] - \\Big[-1\\Big] \\\\[13pt]\n    &= 1-\\dfrac{4}{e^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq e^3) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(X < e^3) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 1 - \\left(1-\\dfrac{4}{e^3}\\right) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{e^3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA989","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA989\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nConsider the function $f$ such that\n\n$$ \n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n     f(x) = \\left\\{\n\n           \\begin{array}{ll}\n\n         \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x\\, ,      & a \\leq x \\leq 3 \\\\[8pt]\n\n         \\hspace{0.8em}0\\hspace{1.8em}, & \\mathrm{otherwise} \n\n           \\end{array}\n\n         \\right.\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n   where $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$.\n\n1. Given that $f$ defines a probability density function for a continuous random variable $X$, find the value of $a$. Give your answer to four significant figures.[5 marks]\n\n2. Hence find $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Given that $f$ defines a probability density function, we must have \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\int_{a}^{3} f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\int_{a}^{3} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= 1\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula for the integral of $a^x$, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\int a^x\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}a^x + C \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\int_{a}^{3} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x\\,\\Bigg|_{a}^{3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    1 &= \\dfrac{1}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x\\,\\Bigg|_{a}^{3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 - \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^a \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^a &= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 - \\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\[24pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{a \\approx 0.2896} \\hspace{3em}\\text{[Using G.D.C.]}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. First we must find $\\mathrm{E}(X)$. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{E}(X) &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} xf(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\int_{0.2896}^{3} x\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\mathrm{E}(X) &\\approx 1.243\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we can find the variance:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x - \\left(\\mathrm{E}(X)\\right)^2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\int_{0.2896}^{3} x^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x \\mathrm{d}x - (1.243)^2 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3.7em}\\boxed{\\mathrm{Var}(X) \\approx 0.519}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Descriptive Statistics","questions":[{"ref":"AA1022","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1022\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThere are $20$ players in local basketball club who have a competition to see how many free throws they can score from five attempts. The scores obtained by the 20 players are shown in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/Screenshot%202023-08-30%20at%2011.55.39%20AM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2023-08-30 at 11.55.39 AM\">\n\n1. State the value of $x$. [1 mark]\n\n2. One of the players is chosen at random. Find the probability that this player's score was $3$ or more. [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the mean score. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Given that $\\Sigma f = 20$, $x=4$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$3$ or more}) &= \\dfrac{4+2+1}{20} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{= \\dfrac{7} {20}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Using the mean formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\bar{x} &= \\dfrac{\\sum_{i=1}^6 f_ix_i}{n} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(2)(0)+(7)(1)+(4)(2)+(4)(3)+(2)(4)+(1)(5)}{20} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{40}{20} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\boxed{\\bar{x} = 2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1068","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1068\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA study of the retirement age of a number of professional soccer players was conducted.\n\nThe following boxplot was produced from the results.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1068Q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1068Q\">\n\n1. Describe the distribution of the plot. [1 mark]\n\n2. What is the interquartile range? [2 marks]\n\n3. Due to an injury, a player is forced to retire early at age $18$. Justify whether or not this player would be classified as an outlier. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The plot has a long tail to the left of the $x-$axis, this is known as\n\n    $\\text{negative skew}$.\n\n    $$                    \n\n    \\begin{aligned}            \n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}    \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. The interquartile range is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        IQR &=Q_3-Q_1\\\\\n\n        &=34-27 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{7} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using the formula we can find the lower fence for outliers\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        &=Q_1-1.5\\times IQR\\\\\n\n        &=27-1.5\\times 7 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\\n\n        &=16.5 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The player's age of $18$ is larger than the lower fence for outliers. Therefore the player would $\\boxed{ \\text{not be an outlier}}$.\n\n    $$                    \n\n    \\begin{aligned}            \n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}    \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1147","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1147\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA dataset has a minimum value of $12$, a lower quartile of $18$ and a median of $22$.\n\nA partially drawn boxplot of the dataset is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1147_q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1147_q\">\n\nIt is known that the lower boundary for outliers is $9$.\n\n1. Find the interquartile range. [2 marks]\n\nThe maximum value is known to be $26$.\n\n2.  Hence, complete the sketch of\n    the boxplot on the grid above. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Describe the distribution of the dataset.[1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  To find the interquartile range ($IQR$) we can use the following formula for the lower boundary ($LB$) of outliers\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        LB&=Q_1-1.5\\times IQR\\\\[6pt]\n        9&=18-1.5\\times IQR\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        1.5\\times IQR&=9\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2.2em}IQR=6\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  Using the formula for the $IQR$ we can find $Q_3$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        IQR&=Q_3-Q_1\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        6&=Q_3-18\\\\[6pt]\n        Q_3&=24 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the provided maximum value and the value for $Q_3$ we can complete the boxplot\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1147_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1147_a\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The boxplot has a longer tail to the left, the median is also closer to $Q_3$ therefore the boxplot has$\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{negative (or left) skew.}$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1303","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1303\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA market research survey asked $200$ teenagers for the number of minutes\n\nthey spend on their phones on a daily basis. The results were recorded\n\nin the following box-and-whisker diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1303q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1303q\">\n\n1.  Write down the median. [1 mark]\n\n \n\nThe following incomplete table shows the distribution of the responses\n\nfrom these $200$ teenagers. \n\n|Number of minutes spent on phones daily ($x$)|Number of teenagers|\n\n| :---: | :---: |\n\n|$0\\leq x \\leq 40$|5|\n\n|$40< x \\leq 80$||\n\n|$80< x \\leq 120$|42|\n\n|$120 < x \\leq 160$|58|\n\n|$160 < x \\leq 200$|30|\n\n|$200 < x \\leq 240$||\n\n2.  Complete the table. [2 marks]\n\n3.  \n\n    1.  Write down the mid-interval value for the $80 < x \\leq 120$\n\n        group.\n\n    2.  Using the table, calculate an estimate for the mean number of\n\n        minutes spent on phones daily by these $200$ teenagers.\n\n        [3 marks]\n\n    \n\nThe 200 teenages were chosen for the survey by randomly selecting\n\nstudent ID numbers in a school, using a random number generator.\n\n4.  Identify the sampling method used. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Let's label the five summary numbers on the given box-and-whisker\n\n    diagram. Note that these numbers divides the whole range into four\n\n    intervals of the same percentage.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1303q1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1303q1\">\n\n    The median is always the vertical line in the center of the box which separates the lower $50\\%$ of the data from the upper $50\\%$ of the data.\n\n    Hence the median $145\\text{ minutes.}$\n\n2.  We can see from the labeled box-and-whisher diagram that the\n\n    interval $0 < x \\leq 80$ makes up $25\\%$ of the sample. This\n\n    interval includes the first two rows of the table. Hence the number\n\n    of teenagers in the $40 < x \\leq 80$ group is \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{[number of teenagers, $40 < x \\leq 80$]} &= 0.25 \\times 200 - 5\\\\\n\n    &= 45\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    \n\n    Similarly, the interval $160 < x \\leq 240$ makes up $25\\%$ of the\n\n    sample. This interval includes the last two rows of the table. Hence\n\n    the number of teenagers in the $200 < x \\leq 240$ group is\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{[number of teenagers, $200 < x \\leq 240$]} &= 0.25 \\times 200 - 30\\\\\n\n    &= 20\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    \n\n    Therefore, the complete table is as follows.\n\n    |Number of minutes spent on phones daily ($x$)|Number of teenagers|\n\n    | :---: | :---: |\n\n    |$0\\leq x \\leq 40$|5|\n\n    |$40< x \\leq 80$|$\\boxed{45}$|\n\n    |$80< x \\leq 120$|42|\n\n    |$120 < x \\leq 160$|58|\n\n    |$160 < x \\leq 200$|30|\n\n    |$200 < x \\leq 240$|$\\boxed{20}$|\n\n    \n\n3.  \n\n    1.  We calculate the mid-interval value by taking the average of the upper and lower boundaries. \n\n    \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\text{Mid-interval } &= \\dfrac{80+120}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n            & \\hspace{0.525em}= 100\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2.  Finding the mid-interval values of the other classes we have the following table. \n\n    |Number of minutes spent on phones daily ($x$)|Number of teenagers|\n\n    | :---: | :---: |\n\n    |$20$|5|\n\n    |$60$|45|\n\n    |$100$|42|\n\n    |$140$|58|\n\n    |$180$|30|\n\n    |$220$|20|\n\n    \n\n    By entering these values into the G.D.C. we can find the mean.\n\n    Hence $\\mkern 1.5mu\\overline{\\mkern-1.5mux\\mkern-1.5mu}\\mkern 1.5mu \\approx 125 \\text{ minutes}$\n\n    \n\n4.  As all members of the population have an equal chance of being selected, this is an example of $\\text{Simple Random Sampling}$."},{"ref":"AA1304","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1304\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe histogram below shows the number of working hours recorded by $145$\n\nemployees in a company during a particular week, each measured correct\n\nto the nearest hour and then rounded to the nearest units of $10$ hours.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1304q.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1304q\">\n\n1.  Write down the modal working time of the employees. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the median working time. [3 marks]\n\n \n\nThe upper quartile is $50$ working hours.\n\n3.  Calculate \n\n    1.  the lower quartile;\n\n    2.  the interquartile range. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The modal value is the one that occurs at the highest frequency.\n\n    From the histogram, we can see that the highest column is $40$,\n\n    which is the frequency of $40$ working hours. Hence,\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\hspace{0.525em}\\text{Modal working time} = 40 \\text{ hours}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the histogram, we can create a cumulative frequency table for\n\n    the data, as follows\n\n    |$\\hspace{0.5em}$Working time, hours$\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}$Frequency$\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}$Cumulative frequency$\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}$Grouped $n$-th value$\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n\n    | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n\n    |10|10|10|1-10|\n\n    |20|15|25|11-25|\n\n    |30|25|50|26-50|\n\n    |40|40|90|51-90|\n\n    |50|30|120|91-120|\n\n    |60|20|140|121-140|\n\n    |70|5|145|141-145|\n\n    Using the formula for\n\n    $\\text{Median} = \\dfrac 1 2 (n+1) \\text{ th value}$ with $n=145$, we\n\n    get \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{[Median]} &= \\dfrac 1 2 (145+1) \\text{ th value} \\\\\n\n    &= 73 \\text{ rd value} \\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The\n\n    Median is the $73$rd value. The cumulative frequency table shows\n\n    that this is in the row of $40$ hours. Hence,\n\n    \n\n    $$\n\n    \\hspace{0.525em}\\text{Median working time} = 40 \\text{ hours}.\n\n    $$\n\n3.  \n\n    1.  Using the formula for $Q_1 = \\dfrac 14(n+1) \\text{ th value}$,\n\n        we get \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        Q_1 &= \\dfrac 1 4 (145+1) \\text{ th value} \\\\\n\n        &= 36.5 \\,\\text{th value} \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        So this is the average of the $36$th\n\n        and $37$th values. The cumulative frequency table shows that\n\n        both these values are $30$ hours. Hence, \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        Q_1 &= \\dfrac{30+30}2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{0.525em}= 30 \\text{ hours}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n    2.  Using the interquartile range formula with $Q_3=50$ and\n\n        $Q_1=30$, we get \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{IQR} &= Q_3 - Q_1 \\\\\n\n        &= 50 - 30\\\\[6pt]\n\n        & \\hspace{0.525em}=20\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1326","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1326\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA teacher gives a class a short quiz and records the change in student score compared to their previous score.\nThe teacher noted that $3$ students had their score increase by $1$ point.\nThe full results are shown in the following table, where $a \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$.\n\n| $\\textrm{Change in score}$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | &nbsp;$0$&nbsp; | &nbsp;$1$&nbsp; |\n| --- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| Frequency | $2$ | $a$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\n1.\n    1.    Find an expression for the mean of the change in score in terms of $a$.\n\n    2.    Hence, show that the variance of the change in score is $\\dfrac{5(13+3a)}{(6+a)^2}$. [4 marks]\n\nIt is known that the mean and variance of the change in score are in the ratio $-2:5$.\n\n2.    Show that $a=4$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. Consider the formula for the mean which is in the formula booklet\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\bar{x}&=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{f_ix_i}{n}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        In this example\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\bar{x}  \\text{ is the mean of the change in score}\\\\[6pt]\n        &x_i  \\text{ refers to each individual value of the change in score}\\\\[6pt]\n        &f_i  \\text{ refers to the associated frequency value}\\\\[6pt]\n        &n  \\text{ is the total frequency}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Substituting the values from the table into the formula we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{mean}&=\\frac{-2 \\times 2+ (-1)\\times a+0\\times 1+1\\times 3}{2+a+1+3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{-1-a}{6+a}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2. Let's consider the formula for variance\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sigma^2&=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{f_ix_i^2}{n}-\\mu^2\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        In addition to part(a), $\\mu $ is the mean ($\\bar{x}$) of the change in score.\n\n        Note that in this example $\\bar{x}$ and $\\mu$ represent the same thing.\n\n        Substituting the values from the table into the formula and using our answer from part (a) we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{variance}&=\\frac{(-2)^2 \\times 2+ (-1)^2\\times a+0^2\\times 1+1^2\\times 3}{2+a+1+3}-\\left(\\frac{-1-a}{6+a}\\right)^2\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{8+a+3}{6+a}-\\frac{(-1-a)^2}{(6+a)^2}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Finding a common denominator and simplifying we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{(11+a)(6+a)-(-1-a)^2}{(6+a)^2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{66+17a+a^2-(1+2a+a^2)}{(6+a)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{66+17a+a^2-1-2a-a^2}{(6+a)^2}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{5(13+3a)}{(6+a)^2}\\hspace{1em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2. We are told that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{mean}:\\text{variance}&=-2:5\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we divide both ratios by the element on the right we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\text{mean}}{\\text{variance}}:1&=\\frac{-2}{5}:1\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    therefore we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{\\text{mean}}{\\text{variance}}&=\\frac{-2}{5}\\\\[12pt]\n    5 \\times \\text{mean}&=-2 \\times \\text{variance}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using parts (a)(i) and (a)(ii) we can rewrite the equation in terms of $a$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    5\\times \\left(\\frac{-1-a}{6+a}\\right)&=-2 \\times \\left(\\dfrac{5(3a+13)}{(a+6)^2}\\right)\\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Simplifying we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\frac{-(-1-a)}{6+a}&=\\dfrac{2(3a+13)}{(a+6)^2}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{1+a}{6+a}&=\\dfrac{2(3a+13)}{(a+6)^2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[12pt]\n    \\frac{(1+a)(6+a)^2}{6+a}&=2(3a+13)\\\\[12pt]\n    (1+a)(6+a)&=6a+26\\\\[6pt]\n    6+7a+a^2&=6a+26\\\\[6pt]\n    a^2+a-20&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    (a+5)(a-4)&=0\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall, $a \\in \\mathbb{Z^+}$, it represents a frequency and must be positive.\n\n    Therefore $a=4$...as required.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1338","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1338\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe average daily water usage, $w$ (in litres), of 200 households in a city was recorded. The households were grouped based on their average daily consumption. The data is shown in the following table.\n\n| **Water usage ($w$ litres)** | **Frequency** |\n\n|:---:|:---: |\n\n| $100 < w \\leq 150$ | 10 |\n\n| $150 < w \\leq 200$ | 20 |\n\n| $200 < w \\leq 250$ | 88 |\n\n| $250 < w \\leq 300$ | 46 |\n\n| $300 < w \\leq 350$ | $p$ |\n\n| $350 < w \\leq 400$ | 8 |\n\n1.    \n\n    1.    Find the value of $p$. \n\n    2.    Write down the modal class. [2 marks]\n\nThe table has been used to create the following cumulative frequency curve.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1338q1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1338q1\">\n\n2.    Use the cumulative frequency curve to estimate the interquartile range. [2 marks]\n\n3.    Given that $15\\%$ of these families use more than $q$ litres per day, find the value of $q$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  As the table shows data for $200$ households, the total frequency must be $200$.\n\n        Hence, using the frequencies for each class, we can form an equation to solve for $p$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        200&=10+20+88+46+p+8\\\\[6pt]\n\n        200&=172+p\\\\[6pt]\n\n        p&=28\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. The modal class is the class with the highest frequency.\n\n        \n\n        We can see that\n\n        | **Water usage ($w$ litres)** | **Frequency** |\n\n        | :---: | :---: |\n\n        | $100 < w \\leq 150$ | 10 |\n\n        | $150 < w \\leq 200$ | 20 |\n\n        | $\\textcolor{red}{200 < w \\leq 250}$ | $\\textcolor{red}{88}$ |\n\n        | $250 < w \\leq 300$ | 46 |\n\n        | $300 < w \\leq 350$ | $p$ |\n\n        | $350 < w \\leq 400$ | 8 |\n\n        Since $88$ is the class with the highest frequency, the modal class is\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}200<w \\leq 250\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Note, you must give the full inequality as your answer, not the associated frequency!\n\n2. The interquartile range (IQR) is given by\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{IQR}&=Q_3-Q_1\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we need to find the lower quartile ($Q_1$) and the upper quartile  ($Q_3$).\n\n    Let’s start with $Q_1$, which is the 25th percentile — the value of $w$ below which $25\\%$ of the data lies.\n\n    \n\n    Working out $25\\%$ of 200 we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    0.25 \\times 200 &=50\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    which means that $Q_1$ is the $50$th household's usage.\n\n    \n\n    Similarly, $Q_2$ is the 75th percentile — the value of $w$ below which $75\\%$ of the data lies.\n\n    \n\n    Finding $75\\%$ of 200 we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    0.75 \\times 200 &=150\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    which means that $Q_3$ is the $150$th household's usage\n\n    \n\n    We can identify these two values on our graph and then read off the corresponding values of $w$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1338a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1338a1\">\n\n    $Q_1=210$ and  $Q_3=280$.\n\n    \n\n    Using the formula for I.Q.R. we get an estimate of\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\text{I.Q.R.}&=Q_3-Q_1\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=280-210\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=70\\,L\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Let's begin by working out $15\\%$ of 200\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    0.15 \\times 200 &= 30\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    This means the top $30$ households represent $15\\%$.\n\n    We draw a horizontal line at $200-30=170$ getting\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA1338a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1338a2\">\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    q&=310\\,L\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA354","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA354\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThere are $40$ players in a clay pigeon shooting club who take part in a\nlocal tournament. The scores obtained after the\nfirst round of shootings are shown in the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/4b291e250d6c5c0e77239c566e5e7ce2032b760e.svg\" alt=\"4b291e250d6c5c0e77239c566e5e7ce2032b760e.svg\">\n\n1.  One of the players is chosen at random. Find the probability that\n    this player's score was $3$ or more.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate the mean score. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$3$ or more}) &= \\dfrac{10+6+3}{40} = \\dfrac{19} {40}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.475\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the mean formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\bar{x} &= \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^6 f_ix_i\\bigg]\\bigg/ \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^6 f_i\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(2)(0)+(7)(1)+(12)(2)+(10)(3)+(6)(4)+(3)(5)}{40} \\\\[15pt]\n    &= 2.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA355","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA355\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe data of the goals scored by players in a futsal club during the\nwinter games are given in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/aa570444b72f1299be96ea27144d1264429491cc.svg\" alt=\"aa570444b72f1299be96ea27144d1264429491cc.svg\">\n\n1.  Given that the mean number of goals scored per player is $4.8$,\n    find the value of $k$. [3 marks]\n\nIt is discovered that there is a mistake in the data and that two\nplayers, who scored $2$ and $13$ goals, have not\nbeen included in the table.\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the correct mean number of goals scored per player.\n\n    2.  Find the correct standard deviation of the numbers of goals\n        scored per player. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the mean formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\bar{x} &= \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^7 f_ix_i\\bigg] \\bigg/ \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^7 f_i\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n    4.8 &= \\dfrac{(2)(0)+(3)(2)+(6)(3)+(k)(5)+(3)(7)+(2)(11)+(1)(13)}{2+3+6+k+3+2+1} \\\\[10pt]\n    \\hspace{4.0em} 4.8 &= \\dfrac{5k+80}{k+17} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    k = 8 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{10.3em}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the mean formula, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\bar{x}_{\\text{correct}} &= \\dfrac{(5(8) + 80) + (1)(2) + (1)(13)}{(8+17) + 1 + 1} \\hspace{11.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 5\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using G.D.C. we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\hspace{-12.275em}\\sigma_{\\text{correct}} \\approx 3.45\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA386","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA386\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nAt a sprinting competition the mean time of the first three sprinters is\n$10.5$ $\\text{seconds}$. The time for the fourth sprinter is\nthen recorded and the mean time of the first four\nsprinters is $11.0$ seconds.\n\nFind the time achieved by the fourth sprinter.","markScheme":"Using the mean formula, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bar{x}_{\\text{three}} &= \\dfrac{x_1 + x_2 + x_3}{3} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n10.5 &= \\dfrac{x_1 + x_2 + x_3}{3} \\\\[10pt]\nx_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= (10.5)(3) \\\\[15pt]\nx_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= 31.5 \\text{ seconds}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bar{x}_{\\text{four}} &= \\dfrac{x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4}{4} \\\\[6pt]\n11.0 &= \\dfrac{31.5 + x_4}{4} \\\\[10pt]\n\\hspace{0.75em} 31.5 + x_4 &= (11.0)(4) \\\\[15pt]\nx_4 &= 44.0 - 31.5 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{1.15em} x_4 = 12.5 \\text{ seconds}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA402","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA402\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe cumulative frequency curve below represents the time spent by $120$\nstudents to solve a challenging mathematics problem in class.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/a2c4f9406907d2f9018f779304bfc2c8dd701ec1.svg\" alt=\"a2c4f9406907d2f9018f779304bfc2c8dd701ec1.svg\">\n\nUse the graph to answer the following.\n\n1.  Write down the median time. [1 mark]\n\n2.  A student is chosen at random. Find the probability that this\n    student spent less than $30$ minutes.\n    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $90$ % of the students spent\n    greater than $k$ minutes, find the value\n    of $k$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\text{median} = 34 \\text{ min}$\n\n2.  Using the cumulative frequency diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(t < 30) &= \\dfrac{F(30)}{F(60)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{32}{120} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.267 \\hspace{3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We know $10$% of the students spent less than $k$ minutes.\n    Hence we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.3em} F(k) &= 10\\text{th percentile} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.1(120) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 12\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the cumulative frequency diagram,\n    we find  $k = 20$"},{"ref":"AA404","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA404\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe following box-and-whisker plot represents the commute time, in\nminutes, for a group of workers.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/9e28a9e7a6be31076ba32a58199c00ff7ac4e0ab.svg\" alt=\"9e28a9e7a6be31076ba32a58199c00ff7ac4e0ab.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the median commute time. [1 mark]\n\nThe range is $23$ minutes, and the interquartile range is $10$ minutes.\n\n2.  Find the value of:\n    1.  $p$;\n\n    2.  $q$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\text{median} = 22 \\text{ min}$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/5afececa95307fe0573e845ed475d6688879b302.svg\" alt=\"5afececa95307fe0573e845ed475d6688879b302.svg\">\n\n    1.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{range} &= \\text{max} - \\text{min} \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        23 &= 37 - p \\\\[6pt]\n        p &= 37 - 23 \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.75em}p = 14\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\hspace{4.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n        10 &= q - 18 \\\\[6pt]\n        10+18 &= q \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.75em}q = 28\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA408","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA408\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following box-and-whisker plot shows the number of tweets sent by\npeople in a coffee shop on a particular day.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/ebae3d0b08e3f773d62c15865c46eb9c6cd7747a.svg\" alt=\"ebae3d0b08e3f773d62c15865c46eb9c6cd7747a.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of the interquartile range. [2 marks]\n\n2.  One person sent $k$ tweets, where $k > 7$. Given that $k$ is an\n    outlier, find the least value of $k$.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/e7dbe926c0ed4173f21a424ed08eaffe27133857.svg\" alt=\"e7dbe926c0ed4173f21a424ed08eaffe27133857.svg\">\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7 - 2 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{upper inner fence} &= q_3 + 1.5(\\text{IQR}) \\hspace{3.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 7 + 1.5(5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 14.5 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the least value of $k$ is\n     $15$"},{"ref":"AA413","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA413\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nConsider the following frequency table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/f12fa3382f663003ac2acc8d1303a6373769b9fd.svg\" alt=\"f12fa3382f663003ac2acc8d1303a6373769b9fd.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the mode.\n\n    2.  Find the value of the range. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the mean.\n\n    2.  Find the variance. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $\\text{mode} = 9$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{range} &= \\text{max} - \\text{min} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 11 - 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 10\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the mean formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.45em} \\bar{x} &= \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^5 f_ix_i\\bigg]\\bigg/ \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^5 f_i\\bigg] \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{(12)(1)+(20)(3)+(26)(6)+(32)(9)+(5)(11)}{12+20+26+32+5} \\\\[15pt]\n        &\\approx 6.01\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using our G.D.C. to calculate the variance, $\\sigma^2$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sigma^2 &= (3.06592...)^2 \\hspace{5.275em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &\\approx 9.40\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA423","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA423\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA school conducts a research into how many minutes each day their\nstudents spend browsing the internet. Jake's class is studied first. The\nnumber of minutes spent browsing the internet by each student in Jake's\nclass is shown in the following stem and leaf plot.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/3d3da4e0e583b1c9fd9eef0d86d8f4e2d11cbc6e.svg\" alt=\"3d3da4e0e583b1c9fd9eef0d86d8f4e2d11cbc6e.svg\">\n\n**Key:\n$\\bm{2}$ \\| $\\bm{3}$\nrepresents $\\bm{23}$ minutes**\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the number of students in Jake's class.\n\n    2.  Find the median number of minutes spent browsing the internet.\n        [3 marks]\n\nThe following box-and-whisker plot also displays the number of\nminutes spent browsing the internet by\nstudents in Jake's class.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/cf76cc1c5a44d16ab1d972adbcd564f5afe0b2ba.svg\" alt=\"cf76cc1c5a44d16ab1d972adbcd564f5afe0b2ba.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Write down the value of $q$.\n\n    2.  The interquartile range is $13$. Find the value of $p$.\n        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $\\text{\\# of students} = \\text{\\# of leafs} = 16$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{med} &= \\text{middle leaf} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{8\\text{th leaf} + 9\\text{th leaf}}{2} \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{31 + 33}{2} \\\\[11pt]\n        &= 32 \\text{ min}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/1446e39b0f014ccb70f21ba0d91c1a4eb128361e.svg\" alt=\"1446e39b0f014ccb70f21ba0d91c1a4eb128361e.svg\">\n\n    1.\n        $q = \\text{max} = 16\\text{th leaf} = 45$\n\n    2.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        13 &= 37 - p \\\\[6pt]\n        p &= 37 - 13 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        p = 24 \\hspace{2.65em}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA426","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA426\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThere are $8$ numbers in a data set. The sum of the numbers is $48$.\n\n1.  Find the mean. [2 marks]\n\nThe variance of this data set is $2$. Each number in the set is\nmultiplied by $3$.\n\n2.  Find the value of:\n    1.  the new mean;\n\n    2.  the new variance. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{mean} &= \\dfrac{48}{8} \\hspace{3.85em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{new mean} &= 3\\times(\\text{old mean}) \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\\times6 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 18\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{new variance} &= 3^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times(\\text{old variance}) \\hspace{2.55em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times2 \\\\[7pt]\n        &= 18\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA427","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA427\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA data set has a mean of $15$ and a standard deviation of $4$.\n\nEach element in the data set has $5$ added to it.\n\n1.  Find the value of:\n    1.  the new mean;\n\n    2.  the new standard deviation. [3 marks]\n\nEach element in the original data set is multiplied by $5$.\n\n2.  Find the value of:\n    1.  the new mean;\n\n    2.  the new variance. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{new mean} &= \\text{old mean} + 5 \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 15 + 5 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 20\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{new SD} &= \\text{old SD} \\hspace{5.35em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{new mean} &= 5\\times(\\text{old mean}) \\hspace{2.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 5\\times15 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 75\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{new variance} &= 5^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times(\\text{old variance}) \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 5^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times(\\text{old SD})^2 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 5^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times4^2 \\\\[7pt]\n        &= 400\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA431","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA431\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following cumulative frequency diagram shows the distance $d$, in\nkilometres, students need to travel to get to school.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/3a843b422c1f5b2908abeaa8d4be3c920695dff9.svg\" alt=\"3a843b422c1f5b2908abeaa8d4be3c920695dff9.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the median distance a student travels to school.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the number of students who travel between\n    $2$ km and\n    $4$ km to get\n    to school. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the percentage of students who travel more than\n    $4.5$ km to get\n    to school. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\text{median} = 3 \\text{ km}$\n\n2.  Using the cumulative frequency diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.1em} f(2 \\leq d \\leq 4) &= 130 - 30 \\hspace{2.75em} [\\text{$f$: frequency}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 100 \\text{ students}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the cumulative frequency diagram, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(d > 4.5) &= \\dfrac{20}{160} \\hspace{5.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.125\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the percentage of students who travel\n    more than $4.5$ km is\n     $12.5\\%$"},{"ref":"AA444","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA444\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe following cumulative frequency diagram shows the lengths of $80$\nflower stems in a garden, in centimetres.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/921b64fb9e6a89f74d566714ee1961c1829f99b3.svg\" alt=\"921b64fb9e6a89f74d566714ee1961c1829f99b3.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the median length.\n\n    2.  Find the percentage of flower stems that are\n        $60$ cm or greater. [4 marks]\n\nThe same data is presented in the following frequency table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/f143e487c5fd5118594467694ae06b43fae4188f.svg\" alt=\"f143e487c5fd5118594467694ae06b43fae4188f.svg\">\n\n2.  Find the value of:\n    1.  $p$;\n\n    2.  $q$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Use the values from the frequency table above to estimate\n    the mean and standard deviation of\n    the lengths. [3 marks]\n\nA flower with stem length of $60$ cm or greater\nis considered as mature flower.\n\n4.  Given that a randomly selected flower is mature, find the\n    probability that its stem length\n    is $90$ cm or greater. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $\\text{median} = 50\\hspace{0.25em}\\text{cm}$\n    2.  Using the cumulative frequency diagram, we have\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{P}(x \\geq 60) &= \\dfrac{F(120) - F(60)}{F(120)} \\\\[5pt]\n         &= \\dfrac{80-55}{80} \\\\[12pt]\n         &= 0.3125\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n    Hence the percentage of flower stems that are\n    $60$ cm or greater is\n     $31.25\\%$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        10 + p &= F(60) \\hspace{0.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n        10 + p &= 55 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        p = 45\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        10 + 45 + 20 + q &= F(120) \\hspace{4.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n        75 + q &= 80 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        q = 5 \\hspace{0.5em}\n        $$\n\n3.  Evaluating the midpoints of the length intervals, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/0838315d2ce8123458abe04d067cfba49f85e835.svg\" alt=\"0838315d2ce8123458abe04d067cfba49f85e835.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using G.D.C., we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1em} \\bar{x} = 52.5 \\text{ cm}&  \\\\[9pt]\n    \\sigma = 22.5 \\text{ cm}&\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the frequency table, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{P}(x \\geq 90) &= \\dfrac{q}{80} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{80} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.0625 \\hspace{2em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the conditional\n    probability formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(x \\geq 90 \\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{mature flower}) &= \\mathrm{P}(x \\geq 90 \\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}x \\geq 60) \\hspace{3.05em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(x \\geq 90)}{\\mathrm{P}(x \\geq 60)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.0625}{0.3125} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA463","marks":"16","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA463\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nA primary school surveyed $120$ of its students to find out how much\ntime they spend using digital devices on a given day. The results of the\nsurvey are shown in the following cumulative frequency diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/e07ae0d3554141d985a49db0473a55cc80fdc5e7.svg\" alt=\"e07ae0d3554141d985a49db0473a55cc80fdc5e7.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the median number of minutes spent using digital devices.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the number of students whose screen time is within $30$\n    minutes of the median. [3 marks]\n\nOnly $5$% of the students spent more than $k$ minutes using digital\ndevices.\n\n3.  Find the value of $k$. [3 marks]\n\nThe results of the survey can also be displayed on the following\nbox-and-whisker diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/08577a2480ca259edd11aced36c54e86044abea1.svg\" alt=\"08577a2480ca259edd11aced36c54e86044abea1.svg\">\n\n4.  Write down the value of $q$. [1 mark]\n\n5.\n    1.  Find the value of $p$.\n\n    2.  Hence find the interquartile range. [4 marks]\n\nScreen times of less than $r$ minutes are considered outliers.\n\n6.  Find the value of $r$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    F(\\text{median}) &= 50\\text{th percentile} \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.5(120) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 60\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the cumulative frequency diagram,\n    we find\n     $\\text{median} = 180 \\text{ min}$\n\n2.  Using the cumulative frequency diagram, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    F(180-30) &= F(150) = 26 \\\\[6pt]\n    F(180+30) &= F(210) = 98 \\hspace{1.75em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    F(210) - F(150) &= 98 - 26 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 72 \\text{ students} \\hspace{4.7em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We know $95$% of the students spent less than $k$ minutes using\n    digital devices.\n\n    Hence we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} F(k) &= 95\\text{th percentile} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.95(120) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 114\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the cumulative frequency diagram,\n    we find  $k = 250$\n\n4.\n    $q = 300$\n\n5.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/36458c76fb6f9a2eadb240150c50ee9502065b89.svg\" alt=\"36458c76fb6f9a2eadb240150c50ee9502065b89.svg\">\n\n    1.  Hence we deduce\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.3em} F(p) &= 75\\text{th percentile} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.75(120) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 90\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using the cumulative frequency\n        diagram, we find\n         $p = 200$\n\n    2.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 200 - 155 \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 45\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n7.  Using the lower inner fence formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} r &= q_1 - 1.5(\\text{IQR}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 155 - 1.5(45) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 155 - 67.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 87.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA684","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA684\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nPaul records the duration, in minutes, of 10 customer service calls he\nreceives between 1 and 6 pm on Wednesday. The results are as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.4\\hspace{1.6em} 8.5\\hspace{1.6em} 10\\hspace{1.6em} 11.6\\hspace{1.6em} 14\\hspace{1.6em} 16.2\\hspace{1.6em} 19\\hspace{1.6em} 21.4\\hspace{1.6em} 25.8\\hspace{1.6em} 37.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n1.  Find the mean duration, in minutes, of the calls he received.\n    [2 marks]\n\nThe following box-and-whisker plot represents the results listed above.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/0cc2d89607279da637de2689f3d3b23d6df2a35b.svg\" alt=\"0cc2d89607279da637de2689f3d3b23d6df2a35b.svg\">\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the value of $p$.\n\n    2.  Find the interquartile range. [3 marks]\n\nCalls greater than $r$ minutes are considered\noutliers.\n\n3.  Find the value of $r$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the mean formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.1em} \\bar{x} &= \\dfrac{1}{10}\\sum_{i = 1}^{10} x_i \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{10}(3.4+8.5+10+11.6+14+16.2+19+21.4+25.8+37.1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{10}(167) \\hspace{1em}= 16.7 \\text{ min}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  $p$ is the median, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        p &= \\dfrac{14+16.2}{2} \\hspace{1.1em} = 15.1 \\hspace{10em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/403a79bf5b933151bd212cbd918dba6588db15cb.svg\" alt=\"403a79bf5b933151bd212cbd918dba6588db15cb.svg\">\n\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 21.4 - 10 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 11.4 \\text{ min} \\hspace{2.8em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    r &= q_3 + 1.5(\\text{IQR})  \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 21.4 + 1.5(11.4) \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 38.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA928","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA928\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe following table shows the number of overtime hours worked by employees in a company.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Descriptive%20Statistics/images/AA928a.PNG\" alt=\"AA928a\">\n\nIt is known that the mean number of overtime hours is $1$.\n\n1. Find the value of $x$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Find the standard deviation of the data. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using the mean formula, we get \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\bar{x} &= \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^5 f_ix_i\\bigg]\\bigg/ \\bigg[\\sum_{i=1}^5 f_i\\bigg] \\\\[7pt]\n\n    1 &= \\dfrac{(17)(0)+(4)(1)+(3)(2)+(x)(3)+(2)(4)}{17+4+3+x+2} \\\\[7pt]\n\n    1 &= \\dfrac{18+3x}{26+x} \\\\[10pt]\n\n    26+x &= 18+3x \\\\[12pt]\n\n    2x &= 8 \\\\[12pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.57em}\\boxed{x = 4} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. Using our G.D.C. to calculate the standard deviation, $\\sigma$, we get \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.58em}\\boxed{\\sigma \\approx 1.34} \n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Discrete Random Variables","questions":[{"ref":"AA1044","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1044\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider a geometric sequence with $a_1 = 8$ and $a_4=1$.\n\n1.  Find the common ratio, $r$.[2 marks]\n\nConsider the following probability function for a random variable $X$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/table.png\" alt=\"table\">\n\n2. Find the value of $k$.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the $n$th term formula, $a_n = a_1 r^{n-1}$, with $n=4$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        a_4 &= a_1 r^3 \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\\\\\[8pt]\n        r^3 &= \\dfrac{a_4}{a_1} \\\\[7pt]\n        r^3 &= \\dfrac{1}{8} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.475em}r = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Since the sum of probabilities must add up to 1, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        %ka_1+ka_2+ka_3+ka_4 &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n        k(a_1+a_2+a_3+a_4) &= 1   \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[7pt]\n        a_1+a_2+a_3+a_4 &= \\dfrac{1}{k} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{a_1(r^4-1)}{r-1} &= \\dfrac{1}{k} \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{8\\left(\\frac{1}{16}-1\\right)}{\\frac{1}{2}-1} &= \\dfrac{1}{k} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{2}-8}{-\\frac{1}{2}} &= \\dfrac{1}{k} \\\\[6pt]\n        15 &= \\dfrac{1}{k} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.535em}k = \\dfrac{1}{15} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1238","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1238\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe discrete random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution\n\n|$\\textrm{x}$|$\\textrm{1}$|$\\textrm{2}$|$\\textrm{3}$|\n\n|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n\n|$\\hspace{0.5em}\\mathrm{P}(X=x)\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.6-2a\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.4-2a\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}4a\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n\n1. Find the range of possible values of $a$.[2 marks]\n\n2. In the case where $a=0.1$, determine $\\mathrm{Var}(3-2X)$.[6 marks]","markScheme":"1. Since probabilities must be non-negative, we can form 3 inequalities\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        0.6-2a &\\geq 0&&&0.4-2a &\\geq 0 &&&0& \\leq 4a \\leq 1\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Solving those we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        a &\\leq 0.3 &&&a &\\leq 0.2 &&&0& \\leq a \\leq 0.25\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, solving all inequalities simultaneously we find the range of possible values of $a$ is \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        0 \\leq a \\leq 0.2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. When $a=0.1$, the distribution becomes\n\n    |$\\textrm{x}$|$\\textrm{1}$|$\\textrm{2}$|$\\textrm{3}$|\n\n    |:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n\n    |$\\hspace{0.5em}\\mathrm{P}(X=x)\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.4\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.2\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.4\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n\n    Using the formula for the **linear transform of a single random variable (4.14)**\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\mathrm{Var}(X)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can write\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{Var}(3-2X)&=(-2)^2\\mathrm{Var}(X)\\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=4\\mathrm{Var}(X) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore, we need to find $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$.\n\n    For this we can use the formula for the **variance (4.14)** \n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{Var}(X)=\\mathrm{E}(X^2)-[\\mathrm{E}(X)]^2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Hence we need to find the expected value of $x$ and $x^2$.\n\n    Let's add a row to our table for $x^2$\n\n    |$\\textrm{x}$|$\\textrm{1}$|$\\textrm{2}$|$\\textrm{3}$|\n\n    |:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n\n    |$x^2$|$1$|$4$|$9$|\n\n    |$\\hspace{0.5em}\\mathrm{P}(X=x)\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.4\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.2\\hspace{0.5em}$|$\\hspace{0.5em}0.4\\hspace{0.5em}$|\n\n    Let's first find the expected value of $X$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{E}(X) &= 1(0.4)+2(0.2)+3(0.4) \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 2 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Next let's find the expected value of $X^2$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{E}(X^2) &= 1^2(0.4)+2^2(0.2)+3^2(0.4) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 0.4+0.8+3.6 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &= 4.8 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Putting those results together we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= \\mathrm{E}(X^2)-[\\mathrm{E}(X)]^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= 4.8 - 2^2 \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= 0.8 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Finally, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{Var}(3-2X) &= 4 \\times 0.8 \\\\[6pt]\n\n        &=\\hspace{0em}3.2\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA364","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA364\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nSophie and Ella play a game. They each have five cards showing roman\nnumerals I, V, X, L, C. Sophie lays her cards face\nup on the table in order I, V, X, L, C as shown in\nthe following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/8c2117a8771870dcd1b10ba85dbc123753724df8.svg\" alt=\"8c2117a8771870dcd1b10ba85dbc123753724df8.svg\">\n\nElla shuffles her cards and lays them face down on the table. She then\nturns them over one by one to see if her card\nmatches with Sophie's card directly above. Sophie wins if **no**\nmatches occur; otherwise Ella wins.\n\n1.  Show that the probability that Sophie wins the game is\n    $\\dfrac{11}{30}$.[6 marks]\n\nSophie and Ella repeat their game so that they play a total of $90$\ntimes. Let the discrete random\nvariable $X$ represent the number of times Sophie wins.\n\n2.  Determine:\n    1.  the mean of $X$;\n\n    2.  the variance of $X$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/5557f2430f2b6a1d2ae2b382374192f4abd645a4.svg\" alt=\"5557f2430f2b6a1d2ae2b382374192f4abd645a4.svg\">\n\n    There are $5! = 120$ possible ways to lay down five cards in order\n    on the table.\n\n    Hence the number of possible ways to lay down five cards with no\n    matches is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    120 - (45+20+10+0+1) &= 120 - 76 \\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 44\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the probability that Sophie wins the game\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Sophie wins}) &= \\dfrac{44}{120} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11}{30}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Since $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(90,11/30)$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 90\\times\\dfrac{11}{30} \\hspace{3.3em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 33\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the formula for the variance of\n        $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(90,11/30)$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.75em} \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= np(1-p) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 90\\times\\dfrac{11}{30}\\times\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[1 - \\dfrac{11}{30}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 20.9\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA365","marks":"20","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA365\n\n**Maximum mark: 20**\n\nAn ice cream company advertises free gifts to customers who collect four\ncoupons. The coupons are placed at random into\n$20\\%$ of all bags of ice cream sticks. Oliver buys\nice cream sticks and he opens them one at a time to see if\nthey contain a coupon.\n\nLet $\\mathrm{P}(X = n)$ be the probability that Oliver obtains his $4$th\ncoupon on the $n$th ice cream stick opened.\n\nIt is assumed that the probability that an ice cream stick contains a\ncoupon stays at $20\\%$ throughout the question.\n\n1.  Show that $\\mathrm{P}(X = 4) = 0.0016$ and\n    $\\mathrm{P}(X = 5) = 0.00512$. [3 marks]\n\nIt is given that\n$\\mathrm{P}(X = n) = \\dfrac{1}{3750}(n-1)(n^2+an+b)(0.8)^{n-4}$ for\n$n \\geq 4$, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$,\n\nwhere $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\n2.  Find the values of $a$ and $b$.\n    [5 marks]\n\n3.  Deduce that\n    $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} = \\dfrac{4(n-1)}{5(n-4)}$\n    for $n > 4$. [4 marks]\n\n4.\n    1.  Hence show that $X$ has two modes $m_1$ and $m_2$.\n\n    2.  Write down the values of $m_1$ and $m_2$. [5 marks]\n\nOliver's mother goes to the market and buys $N$ ice cream sticks. She\ntakes the ice cream sticks home for Oliver\nto open.\n\n5.  Determine the minimum value of $N$ such that the\n    probability Oliver receives at least one\n    free gift is greater than $0.5$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.25em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{C}) &= 0.2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{C\\hspace{0.025em}:\\hspace{0.25em} Coupon}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{E}) &= 0.8 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{E\\hspace{0.025em}:\\hspace{0.25em} Empty}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the independent events formula, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(X = 4) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CCCC}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [\\mathrm{P}(\\text{C})]^4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0.2)^4 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 0.0016 \\\\[16pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 5) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{ECCCC}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CECCC}) + \\cdots \\\\[8pt]\n    &+ \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CCECC}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{CCCEC}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4[\\mathrm{P}(\\text{E})][\\mathrm{P}(\\text{C})]^4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4(0.8)(0.2)^4 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 0.00512\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 4) &= 0.0016 \\hspace{2.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{3750}(4-1)(4^2+a(4) + b)(0.8)^{4-4} &= 0.0016 \\\\[6pt]\n    16+4a + b &= 2 \\\\[10pt]\n    4a + b &= -14\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 5) &= 0.00512 \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{3750}(5-1)(5^2+a(5) + b)(0.8)^{5-4} &= 0.00512 \\\\[6pt]\n    25+5a + b &= 6 \\\\[10pt]\n    5a + b &= -19\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the system of\n    linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $a$ and $b$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} a = -5\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}b = 6 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.9em} \\mathrm{P}(X = n) &= \\dfrac{1}{3750}(n-1)(n^2-5n+6)(0.8)^{n-4} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{3750}(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)(0.8)^{n-4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} &= \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3750}}(n-1)\\cancel{(n-2)}\\cancel{(n-3)}(0.8)^{n-4}}{\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3750}}\\cancel{(n-2)}\\cancel{(n-3)}(n-4)(0.8)^{n-5}} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(n-1)(0.8)}{(n-4)} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4(n-1)}{5(n-4)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  Solving the equation\n        $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} = 1$ for\n        $n > 4$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{9.3em} \\dfrac{4(n-1)}{5(n-4)} &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n        n &= 16 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} < 1 & \\hspace{0.75em}\\Rightarrow\\hspace{0.75em} n > 16 \\hspace{3.5em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X = n)}{\\mathrm{P}(X = n-1)} > 1 & \\hspace{0.75em}\\Rightarrow\\hspace{0.75em} n < 16\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence $X$ has two modes; let's call them $m_1$ and $m_2$.\n\n    2.\n        $m_1 = 15$  and\n         $m_2 = 16$\n\n5.  Let $Y$ be the number of coupons among $N$ ice cream sticks.\n    Hence we have:\n\n    1\\. $Y \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sim \\mathrm{B}(N,0.2)$;\n    2\\. Oliver gets at least one free gift if $Y \\geq 4$.\n\n    Hence, solving the inequality $\\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 4) > 0.5$ for $N$,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 - \\mathrm{P}(Y < 4) &> 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(Y < 4) &< 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{7.525em} \\text{binomCdf}(N,0.2,0,3) &<  0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    N &= 19,\\hspace{0.25em} 20,\\hspace{0.25em} 21,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dots \\hspace{1em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the minimum value of $N$ is\n     $19$"},{"ref":"AA366","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA366\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nTen balls numbered $2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 5, 5, 5, 5, 5$ are placed in a bag.\nBalls are taken one at a time from the bag at random and the number\nnoted.\n\nThroughout the question a ball is always replaced before the new ball is\ntaken.\n\n1.  A single ball is taken from the bag. Let $X$ denote the value shown\n    on the ball. Find\n    $\\mathrm{E}[X]$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Three balls are taken from the bag. Find the probability that:\n    1.  the sum of the three numbers is $9$;\n\n    2.  the median of the three numbers is $3$. [6 marks]\n\n3.  Twelve balls are taken from the bag. Find the probability that more\n    than five of the balls are numbered $2$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Determine the least number of balls that must be taken from the\n    bag for the probability of taking out at\n    least one ball numbered $5$ to be greater\n    than $0.9$. [3 marks]\n\n5.  Another bag also contains balls numbered $2,3$ or $5$. Nine balls\n    are to be taken from this bag at random. It is calculated that the\n    expected number of balls numbered $2$ is $4.5$, and the variance of\n    the number of balls numbered $3$ is $1.25$. Find the least possible\n    number of balls numbered $5$ in this bag. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The probability distribution for $X$ is\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/741d9f36f264ba93930b7595245f74e299aad97a.svg\" alt=\"741d9f36f264ba93930b7595245f74e299aad97a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\times\\dfrac{2}{10} + 3\\times\\dfrac{3}{10} + 5\\times\\dfrac{5}{10} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 3.8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the independent events formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{sum is $9$}) &= 3\\mathrm{P}(225) + \\mathrm{P}(333) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{5}{10}\\bigg] + \\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.087\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the independent events formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{median is $3$}) &= 3\\mathrm{P}(233) + \\mathrm{P}(333) + 3\\mathrm{P}(335) + 6\\mathrm{P}(235)  \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\bigg] + \\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n        &+ 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{5}{10}\\bigg] + 6\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{5}{10}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 0.396\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Let $Y$ be the number of balls numbered $2$ among the twelve balls\n    taken from the bag. We have\n    $Y \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sim \\mathrm{B}(12,0.2)$.\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.1em} \\mathrm{P}(Y > 5) &= \\text{binomCdf}(12,0.2,6,12) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0194\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $N$ be the number of balls taken from the bag and $Z$ be the\n    number of balls numbered $5$ among them. We have\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{B}(N,0.5)$.\n    Solving the inequality $\\mathrm{P}(Z \\geq 1) > 0.9$ for $N$, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 - \\mathrm{P}(Z = 0) &> 0.9 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(Z = 0) &< 0.1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.025em} \\text{binomPdf}(N,0.5,0) &< 0.1 \\\\[6pt]\n    N &= 4,\\hspace{0.25em} 5,\\hspace{0.25em} 6,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dots \\hspace{1em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the least number of balls that must be taken from the bag is\n     $4$\n\n5.  Let $S$ be the number of balls numbered $2$ and $T$ be the number of\n    balls $\\text{numbered}$ $3$ among the nine balls taken from\n    the bag. We have $S \\sim \\mathrm{B}(9,p)$ and\n    $T \\sim \\mathrm{B}(9,q)$, where $p$ is the probability of taking out\n    a ball numbered $2$ and $q$ is the probability of taking out a ball\n    numbered $3$.\n\n    Using the formula for the expected value of $S$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}[S] &= 9p \\\\[12pt]\n    4.5 &= 9p \\\\[6pt]\n    p &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula for the variance of $T$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{12.1em} \\mathrm{Var}(T) &= 9q(1-q) \\\\[16pt]\n    1.25 &= 9q(1-q) \\\\[10pt]\n    q &= \\dfrac{1}{6},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{5}{6} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[6pt]\n    q &= \\dfrac{1}{6},\\hspace{0.25em} \\cancel{\\cancel{\\hspace{0.075em}\\dfrac{5}{6}\\hspace{0.075em}}} \\hspace{2.85em} [\\text{since $1/2 + 5/6 > 1$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$2$ or $3$}) &= \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{6} \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    1 - \\mathrm{P}(5) &= \\dfrac{4}{6} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(5) &= \\dfrac{2}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the minimum total\n    number of balls in the bag is $6$ and the least possible\n\n    number of balls numbered $5$ among them is\n     $2$"},{"ref":"AA367","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA367\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA random variable $X$ has a probability distribution given in the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/f6a399c4b7f03138d76ff9f0562ae2dafad093bc.svg\" alt=\"f6a399c4b7f03138d76ff9f0562ae2dafad093bc.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Determine the value of $\\mathrm{E}[X^2]$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence find the value of $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the probability distribution table for $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum \\mathrm{P}(X=x) &= 1 \\hspace{11em} \\\\[6pt]\n    0.1 + 0.2 + 0.3 + p + 0.1 + 0.05 &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.95em} p = 0.25 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X^2$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{E}[X^2] &= \\sum x^2\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X=x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-2)^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.1 + (-1)^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.2 + 0^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.3 + 1^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.25 + \\cdots \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\phantom{= (-2)^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.1 + (-1)^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.2 \\hspace{0.275em}} + 2^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.1 + 3^2\\hspace{-0.1em}\\times0.05 \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 1.7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.85em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X=x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-2)(0.1) + (-1)(0.2) + (0)(0.3) + (1)(0.25) + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\phantom{= (-2)(0.1) + (-1)(0.2) \\hspace{0.275em}} + (2)(0.1) + (3)(0.05) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 0.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the formula for the variance of\n    $X$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= \\mathrm{E}[X^2] - (\\mathrm{E}[X])^2 \\hspace{0.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1.7 - (0.2)^2 \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 1.66\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA368","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA368\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe faces of a fair six-sided die are numbered $2,4,4,4,6,6$. Let $X$ be\nthe discrete random variable that models the sum of scores obtained when\ntwo such dice are rolled.\n\n1.  Complete the probability distribution table for $X$. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/18726b7af4542fa7241b38af8ca727b997fa4361.svg\" alt=\"18726b7af4542fa7241b38af8ca727b997fa4361.svg\">\n\n2.  Find the expected value of $X$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we complete the probability distribution table for $X$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/5994e14c75ae690d1ed197ac998f85a082c071f2.svg\" alt=\"5994e14c75ae690d1ed197ac998f85a082c071f2.svg\">\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X=x) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 4\\times\\dfrac{1}{36} + 6\\times\\dfrac{6}{36} + 8\\times\\dfrac{13}{36} + 10\\times\\dfrac{12}{36} + 12\\times\\dfrac{4}{36} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4 + +36 + 104 + 120 + 48}{36} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{312}{36} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{26}{3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA390","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA390\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo unbiased dice are thrown. Let the\nrandom variable $X$ be the smaller number on either die. The probability distribution of $X$ is\ngiven in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/d971114c54499352c44b9022cbbf37bc186c22e3.svg\" alt=\"d971114c54499352c44b9022cbbf37bc186c22e3.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$ and the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the expected value of $X$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The possible outcomes with the minimum score of $2$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{array}{rcccl}\n    (2,2) & (2,3) & (2,4) & (2,5) & (2,6) \\\\[6pt]\n    & (3,2) & (4,2) & (5,2) & (6,2)\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} p &= \\mathrm{P}(X = 2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{9}{36}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The possible outcomes with the minimum score of $4$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{array}{rcccl}\n    (4,4) & (4,5) & (4,6) \\\\[6pt]\n    & (5,4) & (6,4)\n    \\end{array}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} q &= \\mathrm{P}(X = 4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{36}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 1\\times\\dfrac{11}{36} + 2\\times\\dfrac{9}{36} + 3\\times\\dfrac{7}{36} + \\cdots \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\phantom{= 1\\times\\dfrac{11}{36}}\\hspace{0.28em} + 4\\times\\dfrac{5}{36} + 5\\times\\dfrac{3}{36} + 6\\times\\dfrac{1}{36} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{11 + 18 + 21 + 20 + 15 + 6}{36} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{91}{36}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA394","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA394\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA bag contains $n$ marbles, two of which are green. Claudia plays a game\nin which she randomly draws marbles out of the bag,\none after another, without $\\text{replacement}$. The game ends\nwhen Claudia draws a green marble.\n\n1.  Find the probability, in terms of $n$, that the game will end on\n    her:\n\n    1.  $1$st draw;\n    2.  $2$nd draw. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $n = 6$, find the probability that the game will end on\n    her:\n\n    1.  $3$rd draw;\n    2.  $5$th draw. [4 marks]\n\nClaudia plays the game when $n = 6$. She pays $$\\$\\hspace{0.025em}11$$ to\nplay and can earn money back depending on the number of draws it takes\nto obtain a green marble. She earns no money back if she obtains a green\nmarble on her $5$th draw. Let $M$ be the amount of money that she earns\nback playing the game. This information is shown in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/c9928dc318007f1d69bf16b87eaae3ebf61563c0.svg\" alt=\"c9928dc318007f1d69bf16b87eaae3ebf61563c0.svg\">\n\n3.  Find the value of $k$ so that this is a fair game.\n    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $1$st draw}) &= \\hspace{0.525em}\\dfrac{2}{n} \\hspace{5em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $2$nd draw}) &= \\dfrac{n-2}{n}\\times\\dfrac{2}{n-1} \\hspace{4em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2(n-2)}{n(n-1)}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on \\hspace{0.1em}$3$rd\\hspace{0.1em} draw}) &= \\dfrac{6-2}{6}\\times\\dfrac{5-2}{5}\\times\\dfrac{2}{4} \\hspace{2.3em}  \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4}{6}\\times\\dfrac{3}{5}\\times\\dfrac{2}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{5}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.8em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $5$th draw}) &= \\dfrac{6-2}{6}\\times\\dfrac{5-2}{5}\\times\\dfrac{4-2}{4}\\times\\dfrac{3-2}{3}\\times\\dfrac{2}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{4}{6}\\times\\dfrac{3}{5}\\times\\dfrac{2}{4}\\times\\dfrac{1}{3}\\times\\dfrac{2}{2} \\\\[8pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{15}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $4$th draw}) &= \\dfrac{6-2}{6}\\times\\dfrac{5-2}{5}\\times\\dfrac{4-2}{4}\\times\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{6}\\times\\dfrac{3}{5}\\times\\dfrac{2}{4}\\times\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{2}{15} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the probability distribution\n    of $M$ is\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/cb57502ef1e7cec1ea8f48d31c36a9b4c601320a.svg\" alt=\"cb57502ef1e7cec1ea8f48d31c36a9b4c601320a.svg\">\n\n    The game is fair if $\\mathrm{E}[M] = 11$.\n\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $M$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{E}[M] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[7pt]\n    11 &= 15\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{5}{15}+4k\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{4}{15}+10\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{3}{15}+2k\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{2}{15}+0\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{1}{15} \\\\[6pt]\n    11 &= \\dfrac{75 + 16k + 30 + 4k + 0}{15} \\\\[6pt]\n    11 &= \\dfrac{105 + 20k}{15} \\\\[6pt]\n    k &= \\dfrac{165 - 105}{20} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k = 3 \\hspace{13.3em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA399","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA399\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA random variable $X$ has a probability distribution given in the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/07354adced4a4b4d082c64348179d6c79f0b6ca7.svg\" alt=\"07354adced4a4b4d082c64348179d6c79f0b6ca7.svg\">\n\n1.  Given that $\\mathrm{P}(X > 2) = 0.6$, find the value of $r$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\mathrm{E}[X] = 2.9$, find the value of $p$ and the\n    value of $q$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the probability distribution table for $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X > 2) &= 0.6 \\hspace{4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 3) + \\mathrm{P}(X = 4) + \\mathrm{P}(X = 5) &= 0.6 \\\\[6pt]\n    0.2 + 0.1 + r &= 0.6 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.55em} r = 0.3\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[10pt]\n    2.9 &= (0)(p) + (1)(q) + (2)(0.1) + (3)(0.2) + \\cdots \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\phantom{= (0)(p) + (1)(q)\\hspace{0.28em}} + (4)(0.1) + (5)(0.3) \\\\[12pt]\n    2.9 &= q + 2.7 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    q = 0.2 \\hspace{6.9em}\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum \\mathrm{P}(X = x) &= 1 \\hspace{2.7em} \\\\[10pt]\n    p + 0.2 + 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.1 + 0.3 &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n    p + 0.9 &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.55em} p = 0.1\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA409","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA409\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following diagram shows an archery target which is divided into\nthree regions A, B and C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/091cb5327e00b25c5b64048cb35e8bab5848f215.svg\" alt=\"091cb5327e00b25c5b64048cb35e8bab5848f215.svg\">\n\nA contest consists of an archer shooting one arrow at the target. The\nprobability of hitting each region is given in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/6313a406c3aa82abc8eb508c344ca936eb2e4293.svg\" alt=\"6313a406c3aa82abc8eb508c344ca936eb2e4293.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the probability that the arrow does **not** hit the target.\n    [3 marks]\n\nAn archer scores points on the contest as shown in the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/06e27f959c8785ee9c798034c83014c48759f00d.svg\" alt=\"06e27f959c8785ee9c798034c83014c48759f00d.svg\">\n\n2.  Given that the contest is fair, find the value of $k$.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{A}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{B}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{C}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Out-of-Target}) &= 1 \\hspace{3.6em} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\dfrac{1}{24} + \\dfrac{4}{24} + \\dfrac{7}{24} + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Out-of-Target}) &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{12}{24} + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Out-of-Target}) &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6.9em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Out-of-Target}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $X$ be the number of points scored by an archer on the contest.\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}(X) &= 0 \\hspace{2em} [\\text{fair contest}] \\\\[12pt]\n    \\hspace{5em}10\\times\\dfrac{1}{24} + 6\\times\\dfrac{4}{24} + k\\times\\dfrac{7}{24} + (-4)\\times\\dfrac{12}{24} &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{10 + 24 + 7k - 48}{24} &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n    7k - 14 &= 0 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.65em} k = 2\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA415","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA415\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA random variable $X$ has a probability distribution given in the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/9d61612e1bea6f48ec0f65485811595250c87ab0.svg\" alt=\"9d61612e1bea6f48ec0f65485811595250c87ab0.svg\">\n\n1.  Given that $\\text{E}(X) = 0.8$, find the exact value of:\n    1.  $q$;\n\n    2.  $p$. [4 marks]\n\nA bag contains black and red balls, with at least two of each colour.\nTwo balls are drawn from the bag without replacement. The number of red\nballs drawn is given by the random variable $X$.\n\n2.\n    1.  Write down the probability of drawing two red balls.\n\n    2.  Explain why the probability of drawing two black balls is\n        $\\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n\n    3.  The bag contains a total of ten balls of which $k$ are black.\n        Find $k$. [5 marks]\n\nA game is played in which two balls are drawn from the bag of ten balls,\nwithout replacement. A player wins a prize if two black balls are drawn.\n\n3.  Olivia plays the game twelve times. Find the probability\n    that she wins $\\text{exactly}$ three prizes. [2 marks]\n\n4.  William plays the game until he wins three prizes. Find the\n    probability that he wins his third prize on his tenth attempt.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[8pt]\n        0.8 &= 0\\times p + 1\\times\\dfrac{8}{15} + 2\\times q \\\\[6pt]\n        0.8 &= \\dfrac{8}{15} + 2q \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{2.9em} \\dfrac{8}{10} - \\dfrac{8}{15} &= 2q \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{2.9em} \\dfrac{24}{30} - \\dfrac{16}{30} &= 2q \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg[\\dfrac{8}{30}\\bigg] & = q \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        q = \\dfrac{2}{15} \\hspace{2.5em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sum \\mathrm{P}(X = x) &= 1 \\hspace{8.6em} \\\\[8pt]\n        p + \\dfrac{8}{15} + \\dfrac{2}{15} &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        p + \\dfrac{2}{3} &= 1 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        p = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\hspace{3em}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $\\mathrm{P}(X = 2) = q = \\dfrac{2}{15}$\n\n    2.  Because:\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{two black balls}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{zero red balls}) \\hspace{3em} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= \\mathrm{P}(X = 0) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{two black balls}) &= \\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[7pt]\n        \\dfrac{k}{10} \\times \\dfrac{k-1}{9} &= \\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[10pt]\n        k^2 - k & = 30 \\hspace{9.35em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{13.1em} k = 6 \\hspace{2.1em} [\\text{since $k > 0$}] \\hspace{1.4em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        $$\n\n3.  Let $S$ be the number of prizes that she wins while playing the game\n    $12$ times.\n\n    We have $S \\sim \\mathrm{B}(12,1/3)$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.8em} \\mathrm{P}(S = 3) &= \\text{binomPdf}(12,1/3,3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.212\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $T$ be the number of prizes that he wins while playing the game\n    $9$ times.\n\n    We have $T \\sim \\mathrm{B}(9,1/3)$.\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.8em} \\mathrm{P}(T = 2) &= \\text{binomPdf}(9,1/3,2) \\hspace{1.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.23411\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the independence of the\n    games, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$3$rd prize on $10$th game}) &=  \\mathrm{P}(T = 2)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{wins $10$th game}) \\hspace{0.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.23411\\times\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0780\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA424","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA424\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA random variable $X$ has a probability distribution given in the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/32fd72e5f84528f6d3185f34f3568275a692567d.svg\" alt=\"32fd72e5f84528f6d3185f34f3568275a692567d.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $q$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[X]$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum \\mathrm{P}(X = x) &= 1 \\hspace{10.3em} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{3}{10} + q + \\dfrac{1}{10} + \\dfrac{3}{20} + \\dfrac{1}{4} &= 1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.5em} q = 0.2 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= 0\\times\\dfrac{3}{10} + 1\\times0.2 + 2\\times\\dfrac{1}{10} + 3\\times\\dfrac{3}{20} + 4\\times\\dfrac{1}{4} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1.85\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA430","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA430\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA biased coin has a probability of showing \"heads\" as $0.7$. Jim pays\n$$\\$20$$ to play a game where he flips the coin. He gets no money if he\nflips a \"heads\" and gets $$\\$k$$ if he flips a \"tails\". The game is fair.\nFind the value of $k$.","markScheme":"Let $X$ be the amount of money that Jim gets after playing the game.\n\nThe probability distribution for $X$ is\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/95c2cac3708dedfb2db80aba740685e5ede9c8b3.svg\" alt=\"95c2cac3708dedfb2db80aba740685e5ede9c8b3.svg\">\n\nHence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{E}[X] &= 20 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{fair game}] \\\\[6pt]\n(0)(0.7) + (k)(0.3) &= 20 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{11.7em} k \\approx 66.7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA439","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA439\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA random variable $X$ has a probability distribution given in the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/35887a4c5f30670cad47400b994b9e045edaade9.svg\" alt=\"35887a4c5f30670cad47400b994b9e045edaade9.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[X]$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum \\mathrm{P}(X = x) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    p + 5p^2 + 0.35 + 0.15 + 0.1 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    5p^2 + p - 0.4 &= 0 \\hspace{9.15em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{3em} p = 0.2 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{since $p > 0$}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (-2)(0.2) + (-1)\\big[5(0.2)^2\\big] + (0)(0.35) + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\phantom{= (-2)(0.2) + (-1)\\big[5(0.2)^2\\big] \\hspace{0.28em}} + (1)(0.15) + (2)(0.1) \\\\[9pt]\n    &= -0.25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA454","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA454\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nTom enters a tennis competition where he plays three matches every\nday. The $\\text{results}$ of each match are independent of each\nother. Let $A$ be the number of matches Tom wins on any given day of the\ncompetition. The probability distribution for $A$ can be modelled by the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/bcea20887fef54f59a1eb9bf81a3037ac9d140cd.svg\" alt=\"bcea20887fef54f59a1eb9bf81a3037ac9d140cd.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  A day is chosen at random. Write down the probability that Tom\n        wins every match he plays.\n\n    2.  The competition goes for four days. Find the probability that\n        Tom wins every match on exactly three of these days. [4 marks]\n\nClare enters the same tennis competition. Let $B$ be the number of\nmatches Clare wins on any given day of the competition. The probability\ndistribution for $B$ can be modelled by the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/41a45f0a5743c5ece137c6e8cc5decc7aeafa42a.svg\" alt=\"41a45f0a5743c5ece137c6e8cc5decc7aeafa42a.svg\">\n\n3.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[B]$. [3 marks]\n\nOn the final day of the competition, both Tom and Clare play their three\nrespective matches and their results are independent. The number of wins\nTom and Clare obtain are then added together to form a total of six.\n\n4.\n    1.  Find the probability that they win more than four matches\n        combined.\n\n    2.  Given that they win more than four matches combined, find the\n        probability that Clare won all of her matches. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\sum \\mathrm{P}(A = a) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    0.15 + 0.2 + p + 0.25 &= 1 \\\\[9pt]\n    p &= 1 - 0.6 \\hspace{4.2em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{2.6em} p = 0.4\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.\n        $\\mathrm{P}(A = 3) = 0.25$\n    2.  Let $X$ be the number of days of the competition Tom wins every\n        match.\n\n        We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(4,0.25)$.\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 3) &= \\text{binomPdf}(4,0.25,3) \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.0469\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using the formula for the expected value of $B$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{E}[B] &= \\sum b\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B = b) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0)(0.05) + (1)(0.1) + (2)(0.35) + (3)(0.5) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2.3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  Using the independence of Tom's and Clare's results, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A + B > 4) &= \\mathrm{P}(A = 2, B = 3) + \\mathrm{P}(A = 3, B = 2) + \\mathrm{P}(A = 3, B = 3) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (0.4)(0.5) + (0.25)(0.35) + (0.25)(0.5) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.4125\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the conditional probability formula, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.75em} \\mathrm{P}(B = 3\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}A+B > 4) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A = 2, B = 3) + \\mathrm{P}(A = 3, B = 3)}{\\mathrm{P}(A + B > 4)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{(0.4)(0.5) + (0.25)(0.5)}{0.4125} \\\\[13pt]\n        &\\approx 0.788\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA460","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA460\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn an airport terminal, the number of passengers, $X$, who ride in an\nairport cab has the following probability distribution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/b198afeefb785c9a3864342d8822a0937eb44207.svg\" alt=\"b198afeefb785c9a3864342d8822a0937eb44207.svg\">\n\nAfter the opening of a new ring road around the urban centre that\ncharges a toll, an airport transfer company introduces a charge for\npassengers who use the new ring road. The charge is $$\\$4.80$$ per airport\ncab plus $$\\$1.25$$ per passenger. Let $Y$ represent the amount, in\ndollars, that is charged by the airport transfer company per ride.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[Y]$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 0.9144$, find $\\mathrm{Var}(Y)$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1)(0.25) + (2)(0.46) + (3)(0.19) + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\phantom{= (1)(0.25)\\hspace{0.28em} } + (4)(0.08) + (5)(0.02) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2.16\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathrm{E}[Y] &= \\mathrm{E}[1.25X + 4.80] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1.25\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{E}[X] + 4.80 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1.25(2.16) + 4.80 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$7.50\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} \\mathrm{Var}(Y) &= \\mathrm{Var}(1.25X + 4.80) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1.25)^2\\mathrm{Var}(X) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1.25)^2(0.9144) \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx \\$1.43\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA461","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA461\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn a river city, the number of passengers, $X$, who ride in a taxi cab\nhas the following probability distribution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/464f93413f3f930783f890ac749cb5aaf4a150ee.svg\" alt=\"464f93413f3f930783f890ac749cb5aaf4a150ee.svg\">\n\nAfter the closing of the central river bridge for reconstruction, a taxi\ncab $\\text{company}$ introduces a charge for passengers who use\ncar ferries to cross the river. The charge is $$\\$8.00$$ per taxi cab plus\n$$\\$1.60$$ per passenger. Let $Y$ represent the amount, in dollars, that\nis charged by the taxi cab company per ride.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{E}[Y]$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 1.0275$, find $\\mathrm{Var}(Y)$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1)(0.55) + (2)(0.25) + (3)(0.12) + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\phantom{= (1)(0.55)\\hspace{0.28em} } + (4)(0.06) + (5)(0.02) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1.75\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathrm{E}[Y] &= \\mathrm{E}[1.60X + 8.00] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1.60\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{E}[X] + 8.00 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1.60(1.75) + 8.00 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$10.8\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.1em} \\mathrm{Var}(Y) &= \\mathrm{Var}(1.60X + 8.00) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1.60)^2\\mathrm{Var}(X) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (1.60)^2(1.0275) \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx \\$2.63\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA691","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA691\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA new video streaming service has a monthly subscription cost of $$\\$15$$,\nwhich allows access to the content on only one device. Customers can add\nup to 5 additional devices on their account, each additional device\nhaving an extra fee of $$\\$4.10$$ per month. Let $X$ be the number of\n**additional** devices purchased by a randomly selected customer. The\nvariable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/77dd5cd32e19a31cc59e2186bf0a672ae5a6702e.svg\" alt=\"77dd5cd32e19a31cc59e2186bf0a672ae5a6702e.svg\">\n\n1.  Given that $\\mathrm{P}(X < 4) = 0.85$, find\n    1.  the value of $p$.\n\n    2.  the value of $q$.[4 marks]\n\nIt is known that $\\mathrm{E}[X]=2.13$, and $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 1.3131$.\nLet the variable $Y$ represent the total amount, in dollars, charged to\na customer per month. Find\n\n2.\n    1.  $\\mathrm{E}[Y]$ to two decimal places.\n\n    2.  $\\mathrm{Var}(Y)$ to two decimal places. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Using the probability distribution table for $X$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X < 4) &= 0.85  \\\\[8pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 1) + \\mathrm{P}(X = 2) + \\mathrm{P}(X=3) &= 0.85 \\\\[8pt]\n        p+0.27+0.19 &= 0.85 \\\\[9pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.45em}p = 0.39 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Given that the sum of probabilities of all possible values is\n        $1$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        1 &= 0.39 + 0.27 + 0.19 + q + 0.03 \\\\[9pt]\n        1 &= 0.88 + q \\\\[9pt]\n        & \\hspace{-0.5em}q = 0.12 \\\\[9pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[Y] &= \\mathrm{E}[4.10\\hspace{0.05em}X + 15]  \\\\[9pt]\n        &= 4.10\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{E}[X] + 15 \\\\[9pt]\n        &= 4.10(2.13) + 15 \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\[9pt]\n        &\\approx \\$23.73 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{Var}(Y) &= \\mathrm{Var}(4.10\\hspace{0.05em}X + 15) \\\\[9pt]\n        &= (4.10)^2\\mathrm{Var}(X) \\\\[9pt]\n        &= (4.10)^2(1.3131) \\\\[9pt]\n        &\\approx \\$22.07 \\hspace{3.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA775","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA775\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA random variable $X$ has a probability distribution given in the\nfollowing table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/573473f006b35710dd8b353664972d8f3d4c90c7.svg\" alt=\"573473f006b35710dd8b353664972d8f3d4c90c7.svg\">\n\nGiven the expected value of $X$ is $2.65$, find the value of $a$ and of\n$b$.","markScheme":"Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[12pt]\n2.65&= (1)(5a) + (2)(4b) + (3)(0.3) \\\\[3pt]\n& \\qquad + (4)(0.15) + (5)(0.05) \\\\[12pt]\n2.65&=5a+8b+1.75 \\\\[12pt]\n5a+8b &= 0.9 \\hspace{10.5em}{(1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe also know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum \\hspace{0.055em} \\mathrm{P}(X = x) &= 1 \\\\[12pt]\n5a+4b+0.3+0.15+0.05 &= 1\n\\\\[12pt]\n5a+4b &= 0.5 \\hspace{2em}{(2)} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving equations $(1)$ and\n$(2)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&a= 0.02 \\quad \\text{ and} \\quad b= 0.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA867","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA867\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nBag A contains a large number of tokens. Each token is numbered 1, 2, 3\nor 4. A token is selected from the bag and the following table shows the\nprobability distribution of the score, $X$, obtained.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/8dd8bde68056b203a1b2f58c0da5310bf6cb4b04.svg\" alt=\"8dd8bde68056b203a1b2f58c0da5310bf6cb4b04.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $p$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence, find the value of $\\mathrm{E}(X)$. [2 marks]\n\nBag B also contains a large number of tokens, each numbered 1, 2, 3 or\n4. A token is selected from the bag and the following table shows the\nprobability distribution of the score, $Y$, obtained. It is given that $q>0$ and $s>0$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/ae133462540d402546323ba2a46a473c9b42e747.svg\" alt=\"ae133462540d402546323ba2a46a473c9b42e747.svg\">\n\n3.  State the range of possible values of $s$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Hence, find the range of possible values of\n    $\\mathrm{E}(Y)$. [3 marks]\n\nA token is selected simultaneously from Bag A and Bag B. The probability\nof obtaining tokens showing the same score is equal to $0.24$.\n\n5.  Calculate $\\mathrm{E}(Y)$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\displaystyle \\sum \\mathrm{P}(X = x) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    p + p + 0.3 - p^2 + 0.4 - 0.7p  &= 1 \\\\[8pt]\n    -p^2 + 1.3p - 0.3 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{multiplying by $10$}]\\\\[8pt]\n    -10p^2 + 13p - 3 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n    (10p - 3)(1-p) &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}p = 0.3 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $p < 1$}] \\hspace{8em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Substituting $p = 0.3$ in the probability distribution table, we\n    have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/121d7cd425024b8578b63329e29f87f2a80b01a9.svg\" alt=\"121d7cd425024b8578b63329e29f87f2a80b01a9.svg\">\n\n    Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{9em} \\mathrm{E}(X) &= \\displaystyle \\sum x \\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 \\times 0.3 + 2 \\times 0.3 + 3 \\times 0.21 + 4 \\times 0.19 \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 2.29\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\displaystyle \\sum \\mathrm{P}(Y = y) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    q + q + s + s &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    2q + 2s &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    q + s &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\hspace{1.75em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Since $q > 0$ and $s > 0$, we obtain\n     $0 < s < \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n4.  Using the formula for the expected value of $Y$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.75em} \\mathrm{E}(Y) &= \\displaystyle \\sum y \\mathrm{P}(Y = y) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 \\times q + 2 \\times q + 3 \\times s + 4 \\times s \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 3q + 7s \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3(q+s) + 4s \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 3 \\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right) + 4s \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by equation $(1)$}]\\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac 3 2 + 4s\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Since\n    $0 < s < \\dfrac{1}{2}$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\dfrac 3 2 + 4(0)  &< \\dfrac 3 2 + 4s < \\dfrac 3 2 + 4\\left( \\dfrac 1 2 \\right) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.7em}\\dfrac 3 2 < \\hspace{0.3em} \\mathrm{E}(Y) \\hspace{0.3em} < \\dfrac 7 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  We have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/8408cd9bad2f3692c5b4de2432ab7f0001289544.svg\" alt=\"8408cd9bad2f3692c5b4de2432ab7f0001289544.svg\">\n\n    Hence, the probability of obtaining equal scores on the two tokens\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    0.30q + 0.30q + 0.21s + 0.19s & = 0.24 \\\\[6pt]\n    0.6q + 0.4s &= 0.24 \\\\[6pt]\n    6q + 4s &= 2.4 \\hspace{10.25em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If we\n    solve the equations (1) and (2) for $q$ and $s$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em}q = 0.2  \\\\[6pt]\n    s = 0.3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.5em} \\mathrm{E}(Y) &= \\dfrac 3 2 + 4(0.3) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 2.7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA943","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA943\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nCarmen plays a game at a stall at a local fair. The stall owner lays $n$ cards face down on a table. Three of the cards say \"Win\" on the hidden side, the others say \"Try Again\". Carmen draws cards from the table, one after the other, without replacement. The game ends when Carmen draws a card that says \"Win\".\n\n1. Find the probability, in terms of $n$, that the game will end on her $2$nd draw. [2 marks]\n\n2. Given that $n = 8$, find the probability that the game will end on her $6$th draw. [2 marks]\n\nCarmen plays the game when $n = 8$. She pays $$\\$\\hspace{0.025em}6$$ to play and can earn money back depending on the number of draws it takes to obtain a card that says \"Win\". She earns no money back if she obtains a card that says \"Win\" on her $6$th draw. Let $M$ be the amount of money that she earns back playing the game. This information is shown in the following table. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/AA943a.PNG\" alt=\"AA943a\">\n\n3. Find the value of $k$ so that this is a fair game. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $2$nd draw}) &= \\dfrac{n-3}{n}\\times\\dfrac{3}{n-1} \\hspace{4em} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{= \\dfrac{3(n-3)}{n(n-1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{0.8em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $6$th draw}) &= \\dfrac{8-3}{8}\\times\\dfrac{7-3}{7}\\times\\dfrac{6-3}{6}\\times\\dfrac{5-3}{5}\\times\\dfrac{4-3}{4}\\times\\dfrac{3}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{8}\\times\\dfrac{4}{7}\\times\\dfrac{3}{6}\\times\\dfrac{2}{5}\\times\\dfrac{1}{4}\\times\\dfrac{3}{3} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{=\\dfrac{1}{56}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. We have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $1$st draw}) &= \\dfrac{3}{8} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{21}{56} \\\\[-13pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    And using the expression from part (a)\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $2$nd draw}) &= \\dfrac{3(8-3)}{8(8-1)} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{15}{56} \\\\[-13pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The probabilities of the game ending on the 3rd, 4th, or 5th draw are as follows:\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $3$rd draw}) &= \\dfrac{8-3}{8}\\times\\dfrac{7-3}{7}\\times\\dfrac{3}{6} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{8}\\times\\dfrac{4}{7}\\times\\dfrac{3}{6} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{10}{56} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $4$th draw}) &= \\dfrac{8-3}{8}\\times\\dfrac{7-3}{7}\\times\\dfrac{6-3}{6}\\times\\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{8}\\times\\dfrac{4}{7}\\times\\dfrac{3}{6}\\times\\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{6}{56} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{game ends on $5$th draw}) &= \\dfrac{8-3}{8}\\times\\dfrac{7-3}{7}\\times\\dfrac{6-3}{6}\\times\\dfrac{5-3}{5}\\times\\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{8}\\times\\dfrac{4}{7}\\times\\dfrac{3}{6}\\times\\dfrac{2}{5}\\times\\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &=\\dfrac{3}{56} \\\\[-13pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    From part (b) we have P(game ends on 6th draw) $=\\dfrac{1}{56}$.\n\n    Hence the probability distribution of $M$ is\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Discrete%20Random%20Variables/images/AA943b.PNG\" alt=\"AA943b\">\n\n    The game is fair if $\\mathrm{E}[M] = 6$.\n\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $M$, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{E}[M] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[7pt]\n\n    6 &= 10\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{21}{56}+5\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{15}{56}+4\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{10}{56}+\\dfrac{3k}{4}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{6}{56}+\\dfrac{k}{3}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{3}{56}+0\\hspace{-0.05em}\\cdot\\hspace{-0.1em}\\dfrac{1}{56} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    6 &= \\dfrac{210 + 75 + 40 + \\dfrac{9k}{2} + k + 0}{56} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    6 \\times 56 &= 210 + 75 + 40 + \\dfrac{9k}{2} + k + 0 \\\\[6pt]\n\n    336 &= 325 + \\dfrac{11k}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    11 &= \\dfrac{11k}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.5em}\\boxed{k = 2}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"}]},{"name":"Probability","questions":[{"ref":"AA025","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA025\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nMia and Oliver play a game with a box containing two green marbles and\nthree blue marbles. Each player in turn randomly selects a marble from\nthe box, notes its color and replaces it. Mia wins the game if she\nselects a green marble and Oliver wins the game if he selects a blue\nmarble. Mia starts the game.\n\nFind the probability that\n\n1.  Mia wins on her first turn; [1 mark]\n\n2.  Mia wins on her second turn; [2 marks]\n\n3.  Oliver wins on one of his first three turns; [4 marks]\n\n4.  Oliver eventually wins. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  To win on her $1$st turn, Mia has to select a green marble on her\n    $1$st turn.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\small{\\text{Mia wins on her $1$st turn}}) &= \\dfrac{2}{5} \\hspace{3.8em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  To win on her $2$nd turn, Mia has to select a blue marble on her\n    $1$st turn, then Oliver has to select a green marble on his $1$st\n    turn, and then Mia has to select a green marble on her $2$nd turn.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\small{\\text{Mia wins on her $2$nd turn}}) &= \\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{2}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{2}{5} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\bigg)^2\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 0.096\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  To win on one of his $3$ turns, Oliver has to select a blue marble\n    on his $1$st turn, or on his $2$nd turn, or on his $3$rd turn.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\small{\\text{Oliver wins on one of his 3 turns}}) &= \\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{3}{5} + \\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{2}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{3}{5} + \\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{2}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot  \\dfrac{2}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{3}{5}\\cdot\\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^2 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\bigg)\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^3 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\bigg)^2\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^4 \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.467\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\small{\\text{Oliver eventually wins}}) &= \\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^2 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\bigg)\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^3 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\bigg)^2\\bigg(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\bigg)^4 + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{9}{25} + \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\bigg)\\bigg(\\dfrac{6}{25}\\bigg) + \\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\bigg)\\bigg(\\dfrac{6}{25}\\bigg)^2 + \\cdots\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula\n    $S_{\\infty} = \\dfrac{u_1}{1-r}$ with $u_1 = \\dfrac{9}{25}$ and\n    $r = \\dfrac{6}{25}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\small{\\text{Oliver eventually wins}}) &= S_{\\infty} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{9/25}{1 - 6/25} \\\\[9pt]\n    &\\approx 0.474\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1026","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1026\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nConsider two events $A$ and $B$ such that $\\mathrm{P}(A)=k$, $\\mathrm{P}(B)=2k$, $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) = 4k^2-0.11$ and $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) = 0.65$.\n\nFind the value of $k$.","markScheme":"Using the formula for combined events, we have\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    0.65 &= k + 2k - (4k^2 - 0.11) \\\\[8pt]\n\n    0.65 &= 3k - 4k^2 + 0.11 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    4k^2 - 3k + 0.54 &= 0 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    k &= 0.3 \\text{ or } 0.45 \\hspace{3em} \\text{[using G.D.C.]}\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nIf $k=0.45$, we have $\\mathrm{P}(A)=0.45$ and\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) &= 4(0.45)^2-0.11 \\\\[8pt]\n\n    &= 0.7\n\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\n$\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)$ cannot be greater than $\\mathrm{P}(A)$ and therefore $k\\neq0.45$. $\\boxed{k=0.3}$"},{"ref":"AA1061","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1061\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nAt the $\\textit{SunDoe}$ coffee shop customers were asked if they like tea and/or coffee.\n\nFrom the survey results the manager was able to calculate the probability that customers like tea, P$(T)$, and/or like coffee, P$(C)$. She found the probability that a randomly chosen customer likes **only** tea is 0.18.\n\n1. The probability that customers like **only** coffee is $0.32$ and the probability that customers like **neither** tea **nor** coffee is $0.3$.\n\n    1. Find the probability customers like tea and coffee. \n\n    2. A randomly observed customer is known to not like tea. Find the probability they also do not like coffee.[6 marks]\n\n2. The manager realised an error in her calculations, she discovered that the probability  customers like **only** coffee is in fact $0.28$.\n\n    \n\n    1. When P$(T \\cup C)=0.58$ show that events $T$ and $C$ are independent.\n\n    2. Now P$(T \\cup C)=k$, where $k\\neq0.58$. If events $T$ and $C$ are independent. Find $k$. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. From the question we can ascertain the following\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{P}(C \\cap T')&=0.32\\\\\n\n        \\text{P}((T \\cup C)') &= 0.3\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        We can add this to a Venn Diagram using $x$ for the intersection.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/aa1061a.PNG\" alt=\"aa1061a\">\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        x&=1-0.3-0.18-0.32 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\\n\n        x&=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{0.2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    \n\n    2. Using the conditional probability formula\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\text{P}(C'|T')&=\\frac{\\text{P}(C' \\cap T')}{\\text{P}(T')} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &=\\frac{0.3}{0.3+0.32} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &=0.483870...\\\\[3pt]\n\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{0.484} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned} \n\n        $$\n\n2. 1. If events $T$ and $C$ are independent then we can say that\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\text{P}(T \\cap C) &= \\text{P}(T) \\times \\text{P}(C)\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        From the information given we can work out the intersection of $T$ and $C$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\text{P}(T \\cap C) &= \\text{P}(T \\cup C) - \\text{P}(T \\cap C') - \\text{P}(C \\cap T')\\\\[3pt]\n\n            &=0.58-0.18-0.28\\\\[3pt]\n\n            &=0.12 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Using this we can find $\\text{P}(T)$ and $\\text{P}(C)$ \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\text{P}(T) &= 0.18 + 0.12\\\\\n\n            &=0.3 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[3pt]\n\n            \\text{P}(C) &= 0.28 + 0.12\\\\\n\n            &=0.4\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Now we can show the events are independent\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\text{P}(T) \\times \\text{P}(C) &= 0.3 \\times 0.4\\\\\n\n            &=0.12\\\\\n\n            &= \\text{P}(T \\cap C) \\tag*{\\textbf{R1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        $\\text{As } \\text{P}(T \\cap C) = \\text{P}(T) \\times \\text{P}(C) \\text{ the events T and } C \\text{ are independent.}$\n\n    \n\n    2. Let the intersection be $x$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/aa1061b.PNG\" alt=\"aa1061b\">\n\n        From the Venn Diagram we can note that $\\text{P}(T)=0.18+x$ and $\\text{P}(C)=0.28+x$.\n\n        We are told that $T$ and $C$ are independent therefore we can write \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\text{P}(T \\cap C) &=  \\text{P}(T)\\times \\text{P}(C)\\\\[3pt]\n\n            x&=(0.18 + x)(0.28 + x) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)(A1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        This equation can be solved graphically by sketching $y=(0.18 + x)(0.28 + x)$ and $y=x$ on the same axes \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/aa1061c.PNG\" alt=\"aa1061c\">\n\n        We can see from the graph that there are two possible solutions for $x$, $0.12$. and $0.42$. This means we can find two values for $k$.\n\n        We can write $k$ in terms of $x$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            k&=0.18+x+0.28\\\\[3pt]\n\n            &=x+0.46\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Therefore the two values for $k$ are\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            k_1&=0.12+0.46 &k_2=0.42+0.46\\\\\n\n            &=0.58 &=0.88\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        As $k\\neq0.58$ then $k=0.88$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1165","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1165\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA class of university students can decide to take language electives as part of their degree. They can either choose to take _Russian_,\n_Spanish_, both of those languages or neither of them.\n\nIf a randomly selected student is chosen the probability they take both languages is $0.39$ and the probability they take __only__ _Russian_ is $0.05$ __more__ than if they take __only__ _Spanish_.\n\nLet event $R$ be the chosen student takes _Russian_ and event $S$ be they take _Spanish_.\n\n1.  Represent the information above on the following Venn diagram in terms of $x$.\n\n    You do __not__ need to complete the region which represents the students who take _neither_ subject. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/v_q.png\" alt=\"v_q\">\n\n2. Find the probability the chosen  student takes _Spanish_ in the case that $R$ and $S$ are independent events. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  From the information given we can write down $P(R \\cap S)=0.39$.\n\n    We can also let $P(S \\cap R')=x$ which means that $P(R \\cap S')=x+0.05$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{space} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Adding these to the Venn diagram we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/venn_a.JPEG\" alt=\"venn_a\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{space} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. We need to find $P(S)$ when $R$ and $S$ are independent.\n\n    If $R$ and $S$ are independent then, using the formula, we know that the following relationship must be true\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        P(R \\cap S)&=P(R)\\times P(S) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    From the Venn diagram we can write $P(R)=x+0.44$ and $P(S)=x+0.39$ in terms of $x$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{space} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Next substitute these expressions back into the formula for independent events and solve for $x$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0.39&=(x+0.44)(x+0.39)\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        x&=0.21 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\\\[6pt]\n        P(S)&=0.39+0.21\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-2.2em}P(S)=0.6 \\hspace{3em}  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1171","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1171\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA botanist grows a type of flower that has three known variations, $A$, $B$ and $C$.\n\nShe grows the flowers in a large field. The proportion of each type of flower,$A:B:C$, in the field, is in the ratio $2:1:\\tfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nThe stem length of each variation is known to be normally distributed.\n\nVariation $A$ has a stem length with a mean of $8.3\\,$cm and a standard deviation of $0.5\\,$cm.\n\nVariation $B$ has a stem length with a mean of $7.6\\,$cm and a standard deviation of $0.3\\,$cm.\n\nVariation $C$ has a stem length with a mean of $9.5\\,$cm and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,$cm.\n\nThe botanist randomly chooses a flower from the field and measures the stem length.\n\nThe length is between $8.1$ and $8.2\\,$cm.\n\nWhat is the probability the flower is of variation $A$?","markScheme":"This question involves Baye's theorem for $3$ events.\n\nWe can either use the formula from the formula booklet or use a tree diagram method. This solution will use the formula directly.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(B_1|A)&=\\frac{P(B_1)P(A|B_1)}{P(B_1)P(A|B_1) + P(B_2)P(A|B_2) +P(B_3)P(A|B_3)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst we will define the events used in the formula\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    A&=\\text{The length of the stem is between }8.1 \\text{ and } 8.2\\,\\text{cm}\\\\[6pt]\n    B_1&=\\text{ choosing variation A} \\\\[6pt]\n    B_2&=\\text{ choosing variation B} \\\\[6pt]\n    B_3&=\\text{ choosing variation C}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variations are in the ratio $2:1:\\tfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nIf we mutliply this ratio by $2$ we get $4:2:1$.\n\nThis tells us that $\\tfrac{4}{7}$ of the flowers are of type $A$, $\\tfrac{2}{7}$ of the flowers are of type $B$ and $\\tfrac{1}{7}$ of the flowers are of type $C$.\n\nTherefore $P(B_1)=\\frac{4}{7}$, $P(B_2)=\\frac{2}{7}$ and $P(B_3)=\\frac{1}{7}$.\n\nUsing the information given in the question we can use a G.D.C. to evaluate the following\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Variation $A$ having a length from $8.1$ to $8.2$\\,cm}\\\\[6pt]\n&P(A|B_1)=\\text{normalCDF(8.1, 8.2, 8.3, 0.5)}\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{3.8em}=0.07616...\\\\[6pt]\n&\\text{Variation $B$ having a length from $8.1$ to $8.2$\\,cm}\\\\[6pt]\n&P(A|B_2)=\\text{normalCDF(8.1, 8.2, 7.6, 0.3)}\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{3.8em}=0.02504...\\\\[6pt]\n&\\text{Variation $C$ having a length from $8.1$ to $8.2$\\,cm}\\\\[6pt]\n&P(A|B_3)=\\text{normalCDF(8.1, 8.2, 9.5, 0.7}\\\\[6pt]\n&\\hspace{3.8em}=0.008895..\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of these values into the formula we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    P(B_1|A)&=\\frac{\\tfrac{4}{7}\\times 0.07616...}{\\tfrac{4}{7}\\times 0.07616... + \\tfrac{2}{7}\\times 0.02504... +\\tfrac{1}{7}\\times 0.008895...}\\\\[18pt]\n    &=0.838\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA1288","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1288\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nChun Li has a bag with five 6-sided dice.\n\nFour of them are normal fair dice and one of them is biased with a $6$ showing on each of its faces.\n\nShe draws two out at random and rolls them.\n1.  Find the probability a six shows on both dice.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Given a six shows on both dice find the probability one of the dice is the biased dice.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can begin by defining two events.\n\n    Event $A$ is drawing two fair dice.\n\n    Event $B$ is rolling two $6$s.\n\n    Let's consider event $A$ first.\n\n    As Chun Li is drawing out two dice, she can either have $2$ fair dice or $1$ biased dice and $1$ fair dice.\n\n    The probability of her drawing (without replacement!) two fair dice is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A)&=\\frac{4}{5}\\times\\frac{3}{4}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{12}{20}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{3}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    As she can only hold $2$ fair dice or $1$ fair and $1$ biased this means that the probability she holds $1$ fair dice and $1$ biased is the compliment of the result we just found, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}{(A')}&=1-\\frac{3}{5}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{2}{5}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Now let's consider event B. Rolling two $6$'s.\n\n    If she rolls $2$ fair dice the probability of rolling two $6'$s is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{1}{6}\\times\\frac{1}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{1}{36}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    If she rolls $1$ fair dice and $1$ biased dice the probability of rolling two $6'$s is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{1}{6}\\times1 \\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{1}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We can now put this information into a tree diagram\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1288-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1288-a1\">\n\n    Notice we don't need to calculate the all possible outcomes.\n\n    The sum of both red branches will give the probability she rolls two $6's$.\n\n    Therefore we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &=\\frac{3}{5}\\times\\frac{1}{36}+\\frac{2}{5}\\times\\frac{1}{6}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1A1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{12}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=0.08333...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  In this part some we have been given some information about the roll.\n\n    This is known as conditional probability.\n\n    Consider this tree diagram\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1288-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1288-a2\">\n\n    We have been told that the roll is from either of the two red branches. This is now the universal set and is the denominator of the answer.\n\n    The blue dashed branches form the desired outcome.\n\n    Here is the conditional probability formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A|B)=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    The way we have defined the events in this solution means $A'$ is the event that $1$ fair and $1$ biased dice is chosen.\n\n    Hence we can replace $A$ with $A'$ to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A'|B)=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Our answer from part (a) is $\\mathrm{P}(B)$ and the numerator is the blue dashed branch seen on the previous tree diagram.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A'|B)&=\\frac{\\frac{2}{5}\\times\\frac{1}{6}}{\\frac{1}{12}}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{4}{5}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1313","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1313\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are such that $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)=0.1$, $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)'=0.3$ and $\\mathrm{P}(A\\,|\\,B) =0.2$.\n\n1.    Find $\\mathrm{P}(B)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.    Hence find $\\mathrm{P}(A)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.    Determine whether events $A$ and $B$ are independent. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. As we are given conditional probability,  let's use the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\,|\\,B)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting the given values\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    0.2&=\\frac{0.1}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(B)&=\\frac{0.1}{0.2}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    &=0.5\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. To find $\\mathrm{P}(A)$ lets use the formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)&=\\mathrm{P}(A)+\\mathrm{P}(B)-\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We were given $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)$ and we found $\\mathrm{P}(B)$ in part (a).\n\n    We also know that complementary events add up to one, so we can find $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)+\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)'&=1\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)+0.3&=1\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)&=0.7\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting the information into the formula, now we can solve for $\\mathrm{P}(A)$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    0.7&=\\mathrm{P}(A)+0.5-0.1\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0.7&=\\mathrm{P}(A)+0.4\\\\[6pt]\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A)&=0.3\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Note: You could also try to solve part (a) and (b) using a Venn diagram.\n\n    \n\n    Here is a Venn diagram with the given information  $\\textcolor{red}{\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)=0.1}$,    $\\textcolor{blue}{\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)'=0.3}$ and variables representing the unknown probabilities.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1313a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1313a1\">\n\n    We can also add the values for $m$ and $n$ after we have done the calculations.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    P(B)&=0.1+n&&P(A)=m+0.1\\\\\n\n    0.5&=0.1+n&&\\hspace{1em}0.3=m+0.1\\\\\n\n    n&=0.4&&\\hspace{1.4em}m=0.2\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1313a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1313a2\">\n\n3. If events $A$ and $B$ are independent then\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A)\\times \\mathrm{P}(B)&=\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting information into this formula\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A)\\times \\mathrm{P}(B)&=\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0.3\\times0.5&\\,\\,\\fbox{?}\\,\\,0.1\\\\[6pt]\n\n    0.15&\\neq0.1\\tag*{\\textbf{{R1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Therefore $$\\text{the events $A$ and $B$ are not independent}$$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1340","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1340\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nValeria sells jewellery online. There is an $p\\%$ chance that a customer returns the jewellery. \n\nIf the customer returns the jewellery there is an $87\\%$ chance that they will leave a review.\n\nIf the customer does not return the jewellery there is a $68\\%$ chance the customer will leave a review. \n\nThis is shown in the following tree diagram. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1340a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1340a1\">\n\n1.    Complete the tree diagram. [2 marks]\n\nThe probability that a customer returns the jewellery given that they left a review is $0.3$.\n\n2.    Find the value of $p$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. We know that the total probability of an event must add up to $1$.\n\n    Hence we can determine the missing values by subtracting the known values from $1$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1340a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1340a2\">\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{{A1A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2. The probability a customer returns the jewellery $\\textit{given}$ that they left a review is $0.3$.\n\n    This is a conditional probability so we need the conditional probability formula.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A|B)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Writing the formula in the context of the situation we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{return }|\\text{ review})&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{return }\\cap \\text{ review})}{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{review})}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    0.3&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{return }\\cap \\text{ review})}{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{review})}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We can use the tree diagram from part (a) to find the necessary probabilities.\n\n    The probability of a return $\\textbf{and}$ a review is the top branch so we multiply those probabilities.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{return }\\cap \\text{ review})&=0.87p\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    The probability that the customer leaves a review comes from two branches. We need to multiply both branches and then add the probabilities.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{review})&=\\mathrm{P}(\\text{return }\\cap \\text{ review})+\\mathrm{P}(\\text{no return }\\cap \\text{ review})\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=p(0.87)+(1-p)(0.68)\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=0.87p+0.68(1-p)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    As this is a calculator question we can substitute these results into the formula without simplifying and then use the G.D.C. to solve for $p$.\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    0.3&=\\frac{0.87p}{0.87p+0.68(1-p)}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[9pt]\n\n    p&=0.2509...\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Recall that $p$ is a percentage. Hence our final answer is\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    p&=0.2509...\\times100\\%\\\\[6pt]\n\n    &=25.1\\,\\%\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1444","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1444\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\n \n\nA class contains $30$ students. Each student studies chemistry ($C$), physics ($P$), both subjects, or neither subject. It is given that $18$ students study chemistry, $14$ students study physics and $4$ students study neither subject.\n\n \n\nThe Venn diagram below records the number of students in each region of $U$. The values $p$, $q$, $r$ and $s$ represent numbers of students.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1444a.png\" alt=\"AA1444a\">\n\n1.  1. Write down the value of $s$.\n\n    2. Find the value of $r$.\n\n    \n\n    3. Write down the value of $p$ and the value of $q$.[5 marks]\n\nA student is chosen at random from the class. \n\n2.  1. Given that the student does not study physics, find the probability that the student studies chemistry.\n\n    2. Hence show that studying chemistry and studying physics are not independent events.[5 marks]\n\nTwo students are chosen at random from the class, one after the other.\n\n3. Find the probability that at least one of the two students studies physics. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. In the diagram, $s$ is outside of both circles. Hence it represents the number of students who study neither subject.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            s &= \\hspace{0em}4 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n \n\n    2. We know that the total of all four regions of the Venn diagram is the class size, $30$. \n\n        Adding the three given totals we have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            n(C)+n(P)+n(C'\\cap P')&=18+14+4\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &=36.\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        This is greater than the number of students in the class. \n\n        Notice that $r$ lies inside both circles, so students in this region have been counted in both $n(C)$ and $n(P)$ ($r$ has been counted twice). The extra amount over the class size is therefore the value of $r$.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            r &= 36-30  \\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= \\hspace{0em}6 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n \n\n    3. To find $p$ and $q$ we need to subtract the overlap from each set total.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            p &= n(C) - r \\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= 18 - 6 \\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= \\hspace{0em}12 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n            q &= n(P) - r \\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= 14 - 6 \\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= \\hspace{0em}8 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n \n\n2.  1. We need to use the **conditional probability formula (4.6)**.\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\mathrm{P}(A \\mid B) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Considering the context of the question we have \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\mathrm{P}(C \\mid P') = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(C \\cap P')}{\\mathrm{P}(P')}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        The numerator counts students in the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$only chemistry\" region ($p = 12$). \n\n        The denominator counts students who do not study physics ($30 - n(P) = 16$).\n\n        Therefore\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\mathrm{P}(C \\mid P') &= \\dfrac{12}{16} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= \\hspace{0em}\\dfrac{3}{4} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n \n\n    2. This is a show that question with the word $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$hence'. Therefore we need to use the previous answer in our solution. \n\n        If two events were independent, conditioning on $P'$ would leave the probability of $C$ unchanged, so $\\mathrm{P}(C \\mid P') = \\mathrm{P}(C)$. \n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{{\\textbf{R1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        Hence we can compare the value of $\\mathrm{P}(C \\mid P')$ with $\\mathrm{P}(C)$. \n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{{\\textbf{M1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n        \n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\mathrm{P}(C) &= \\dfrac{18}{30}\\\\[6pt]\n\n            &= \\dfrac{3}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n\n            \\mathrm{P}(C \\mid P') &= \\dfrac{3}{4} \n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n \n\n        Since $\\dfrac{3}{5} \\neq \\dfrac{3}{4}$, the events $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$studies chemistry\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$studies physics\" are $\\hspace{0em}\\text{not independent}\\hspace{0.2em}$.\n\n        $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n            \\tag*{{\\textbf{A1}}}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n3. If we want to find $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$at least one student studies physics\" this is either $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one student studies physics\" or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$two students study physics\". \n\n    We could calculate both of these probabilities or, as this is a non-calculator question, we should recognize that we get an equivalent result if we find $1$ minus the probability that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$neither student studies physics\".\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{at least one studies }P) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(\\text{neither studies }P) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    We calculated $n(P')=16$ in part b(i). \n\n    The probability the first student selected does not study physics is $\\dfrac{16}{30}$.\n\n    The probability the second student selected does not study physics is $\\dfrac{15}{29}$.\n\n    (this is an example of drawing without replacement).\n\n    $$\\\\[-2.3em]$$\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\tag*{{\\textbf{(A1)}}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Then, multiplying these probabilities and subtracting from one\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{at least one studies }P) &=1- \\dfrac{16}{30} \\times \\dfrac{15}{29} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}} \\\\[9pt]\n\n        &=1-\\dfrac{8}{29} \\\\[9pt]\n\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{at least one studies }P) &= \\hspace{0em}\\dfrac{21}{29} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1651","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1651\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA factory uses three machines, $A$, $B$, and $C$, to manufacture items. The table below shows the percentage of the total output produced by each machine and the probability that an item produced by that machine is defective.\n\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}\\textbf{Machine}\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}\\textbf{Percentage of output}\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}\\textbf{P(defective)}\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n\n| $A$ | $50\\%$ | 0.02 |\n\n| $B$ | $30\\%$ | 0.05 |\n\n| $C$ | $20\\%$ | 0.08 |\n\nAn item is chosen at random from the factory's total output.\n\n1.  Find the probability that the item is defective.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Given that the item is defective, find the probability that it was produced by Machine $C$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  There are three ways an item can be defective, depending on whether it was produced by Machine $A$, Machine $B$, or Machine $C$. These possibilities are shown on the tree diagram below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1651.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1651\">\n\n    Hence using the law of total probability we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(D) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(D \\mid A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(D \\mid B) + \\mathrm{P}(C) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(D \\mid C) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n           &= 0.50 \\times 0.02 + 0.30 \\times 0.05 + 0.20 \\times 0.08 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(D) &= \\boxed{0.041} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  We are told that the item is defective and asked for the probability that it came from Machine $C$. This reverses the natural order of events, in which an item is first produced and then found to be defective. This tells us we can apply Bayes' theorem. Using the formula for **Bayes' theorem (4.13)** with the result from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(C \\mid D) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(D \\mid C) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(C)}{\\mathrm{P}(D)} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n                  &= \\dfrac{0.08 \\times 0.20}{0.041} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n                  &= 0.3902\\ldots \\\\[6pt]\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(C \\mid D) &= \\boxed{0.390}\\hspace{1em}\\text{(3 s.f.)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1652","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1652\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA manufacturer sources components from three suppliers $X$, $Y$, and $Z$. The table below shows the percentage of components provided by each supplier and the probability that a component from that supplier passes a quality inspection.\n\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}\\textbf{Supplier}\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}\\textbf{Percentage of components}\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}\\textbf{P(passes)}\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n\n| $X$ | $45\\%$ | 0.96 |\n\n| $Y$ | $35\\%$ | 0.88 |\n\n| $Z$ | $20\\%$ | 0.80 |\n\nA component is selected at random.\n\n1.  Find the probability that the component passes inspection.[3 marks]\n\n2.  Given that the component passes inspection, find the probability that it came from Supplier $Z$.[2 marks]\n\n3.  Given that the component fails inspection, find the probability that it came from Supplier $X$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  A component may come from any of the three suppliers $X$, $Y$, or $Z$, and from each it either passes or fails inspection. These possibilities are shown on the tree diagram below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/AA1652.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1652\">\n\n    Using the law of total probability, with $P$ representing the event that a component passes inspection, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(P) &= \\mathrm{P}(X) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(P \\mid X) + \\mathrm{P}(Y) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(P \\mid Y) + \\mathrm{P}(Z) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(P \\mid Z) \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n           &= 0.45 \\times 0.96 + 0.35 \\times 0.88 + 0.20 \\times 0.80 \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(P) &= \\boxed{0.900} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n2.  We are told that the component passes inspection and asked for the probability that it came from Supplier $Z$. This reverses the natural order of events, in which a component is first supplied and then inspected, so we can apply Bayes' theorem. \n\n    Applying **Bayes' theorem (4.13)** with the result from part (a), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(Z \\mid P) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(P \\mid Z) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(Z)}{\\mathrm{P}(P)} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n                  &= \\dfrac{0.80 \\times 0.20}{0.900} \\\\[6pt]\n\n                  &= 0.1778\\ldots \\\\[6pt]\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(Z \\mid P) &= \\boxed{0.178}\\hspace{1em}\\text{(3 s.f.)} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3.  Here the component fails inspection, so we work with the event $F$ that a component fails. \n\n    Since $\\mathrm{P}(P) = 0.900$, the probability of failure is $\\mathrm{P}(F) = 1 - 0.900 = 0.100$. \n\n    \n\n    The failure probability for each supplier is the complement of passing and we need to find $\\mathrm{P}(F \\mid X)$. Hence\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(F \\mid X) &= 1-\\mathrm{P}(P \\mid X) \\\\[6pt]\n\n    &= 1-0.96\\\\[6pt]\n\n     \\mathrm{P}(F \\mid X) &= 0.04\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Substituting values into our formula,\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(X \\mid F) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(F \\mid X) \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(X)}{\\mathrm{P}(F)} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n                  &= \\dfrac{0.04 \\times 0.45}{0.100} \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}} \\\\[6pt]\n\n      \\mathrm{P}(X \\mid F) &= \\boxed{0.18} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA357","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA357\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nConsider two events $A$ and $B$ such that $\\mathrm{P}(A) = 3k$,\n$\\mathrm{P}(B) = k$, $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) = \\dfrac{k^2}{2}$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) = 0.78$. Find:\n\n1.  the value of $k$; [3 marks]\n\n2.  $\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B)$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the combined events formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    0.78 &= 3k + k - \\dfrac{k^2}{2} \\hspace{2.9em} [0 < k < 1] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{6.2em} k = 0.2 \\hspace{4em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the equality\n    $A' \\cap B = B\\hspace{-0.05em}\\setminus\\hspace{-0.1em}(A \\cap B)$,\n    we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B) &= \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.2 - \\dfrac{(0.2)^2}{2} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 0.18\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA358","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA358\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are such that $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) = 0.78$,\n$\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) = 0.27$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.1em}B) = 0.45$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(B)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(A)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence show that events $A'$ and $B$ are independent. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the conditional probability formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.1em}B) &=  \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)} \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    0.45 &= \\dfrac{0.27}{\\mathrm{P}(B)} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{7.7em} \\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.6 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the combined events formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.9em} \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    0.78 &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + 0.6 - 0.27 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{8.2em} \\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.45 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the equality\n    $A' \\cap B = B\\hspace{-0.05em}\\setminus\\hspace{-0.1em}(A \\cap B)$,\n    we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.2em} \\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B) &= \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.6 - 0.27 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.33\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the complementary events formula, we\n    have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A')\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) &= [1 - \\mathrm{P}(A)]\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= [1 - 0.45](0.6) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.33 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n     $\\text{Since \\(\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B) = 0.33 = \\mathrm{P}(A') \\mathrm{P}(B)\\), events \\(A'\\) and \\(B\\) are independent.}$"},{"ref":"AA359","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA359\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are such that\n$\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B) = \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B') = 0.3$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) = 0.2$.\n\n1.  Calculate $\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B')$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Calculate $\\mathrm{P}(B')$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence find $\\mathrm{P}(A'\\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.1em}B')$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the complementary events formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B') &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $A' \\cap B' = (A \\cup B)'$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - [\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B) + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B')] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - [0.3 + 0.2 + 0.3] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the complementary events formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(B') &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - [\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B) + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1 - [0.3+0.2] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Hence, using the conditional probability formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A'\\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.1em}B') &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cap B')}{\\mathrm{P}(B')} \\hspace{1.2em} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.2}{0.5} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA360","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA360\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA box contains $8$ green and $4$ blue marbles. Two marbles are selected\nat random (without replacement). Find the probability that selected\nmarbles are:\n\n1.  of the same colour; [3 marks]\n\n2.  of different colours. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{of the same colour}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{both green})+\\mathrm{P}(\\text{both blue})\\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{8}{12}\\times\\dfrac{7}{11} + \\dfrac{4}{12}\\times\\dfrac{3}{11}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{17}{33}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the complementary events formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{of different colours}) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(\\text{of the same colour}) \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 1 -  \\dfrac{17}{33} \\hspace{2.9em} [\\text{by part (a)}] \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{16}{33}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA361","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA361\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nJack and John decided to run a total of $250$ km\nin $31$ days. To help them $\\text{decide}$ whether it is fine to\ngo running, they classify each day in August as rainy or dry. Given that\na day in August is rainy, the probability that the next day is dry is\n$0.25$. Given that a day in August is dry, the probability that the next\nday is dry is $0.8$. The weather forecast for the $1$st of August\npredicts that the probability that it will be dry is $0.9$.\n\n1.  Draw a tree diagram to display all the possible outcomes for the\n    first three days of August. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the probability that the $3$rd of August is dry. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the probability that the $1$st of August was dry given that the\n    $3$rd of August is rainy. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Drawing a tree diagram to display all the possible outcomes for the\n    first three days of August, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/0381b67154e9baaebf51e12b3773010451b2c167.svg\" alt=\"0381b67154e9baaebf51e12b3773010451b2c167.svg\">\n    D : Dry;\n    R : Rainy.\n\n2.  Using the tree diagram from part (a), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(3\\text{rd Aug Dry}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{DDD}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{DRD}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{RDD}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{RRD}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= (0.9)(0.8)(0.8)+(0.9)(0.2)(0.25)+ \\cdots \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\phantom{+(0.1)(0.25)(0.8)}+(0.1)(0.25)(0.8)+(0.1)(0.75)(0.25) \\\\[8pt]\n    & = 0.65975\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.45em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{$1$st Aug Dry}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{$3$rd Aug Rainy}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{$1$st Aug Dry and $3$rd Aug Rainy})}{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{$3$rd Aug Rainy})} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{DDR}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{DRR})}{1 - \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$3$rd Aug Dry})} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(0.9)(0.8)(0.2) + (0.9)(0.2)(0.75)}{1 - 0.65975} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.819985 \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 0.820\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA362","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA362\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nElizabeth and Georgia decided to cycle a total of\n$750$ km in $30$ days. To help them decide\nwhether it is safe to go cycling, they classify each day in June as\nrainy or dry. Given that a day in June is dry, the probability that the\nnext day is rainy is $0.25$. Given that a day in June is rainy, the\nprobability that the next day is rainy is $0.85$. The weather forecast\nfor the $1$st of June predicts that the probability that it will be dry\nis $0.8$.\n\n1.  Draw a tree diagram to display all the possible outcomes for the\n    first three days of June. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find the probability that the $3$rd of June is rainy. [3 marks]\n\n3.  $\\text{Find the probability that the \\(1\\)st of June}$ was dry\n    given that the $3$rd of June is dry.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Drawing a tree diagram to display all the possible outcomes for the\n    first three days of June, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/b1b8b3e1cbf17007422f3deb5a51c6ddd3d63a0f.svg\" alt=\"b1b8b3e1cbf17007422f3deb5a51c6ddd3d63a0f.svg\">\n\n    D : Dry;\n    R : Rainy.\n\n2.  Using the tree diagram from part (a), we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(3\\text{rd Jun Rainy}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{DDR}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{DRR}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{RDR}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{RRR}) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= (0.8)(0.75)(0.25)+(0.8)(0.25)(0.85)+ \\cdots \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\phantom{= (0.8)(0.75)(0.25)\\hspace{0.28em}}+(0.2)(0.15)(0.25)+(0.2)(0.85)(0.85) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= 0.472\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{$1$st Jun Dry}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{$3$rd Jun Dry}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{$1$st Jun Dry and $3$rd Jun Dry})}{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{$3$rd Jun Dry})} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{DDD}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{DRD})}{1 - \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$3$rd Jun Rainy})} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(0.8)(0.75)(0.75) + (0.8)(0.25)(0.15)}{1 - 0.472} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.909\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA379","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA379\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$A$ and $B$ are independent events with $\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.28$ and\n$\\text{\\(\\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) = 0.75\\)}$. Find $\\mathrm{P}(B)$.","markScheme":"Using the combined events formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{9.5em} \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) \\\\[8pt]\n\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\quad \\quad [\\text{by independence}] \\\\[8pt]\n0.75 &= 0.28 + \\mathrm{P}(B) - 0.28\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\hspace{3.2em} \\mathrm{P}(B) \\approx 0.653 \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n$$"},{"ref":"AA383","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA383\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n$A$ and $B$ are two events such that\n$\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.5, \\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.7$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) = 0.85$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Determine whether events $A$ and $B$ are independent. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the combined events formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    0.85 &= 0.5 + 0.7 - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) &= 0.5 + 0.7 - 0.85 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) = 0.35 \\hspace{7.1em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the conditional probability formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\\hspace{3em} \\\\[5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.35}{0.7} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\mathrm{P}(A) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n     $\\text{\\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are independent}$"},{"ref":"AA384","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA384\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIsabella studies at the University of Chicago and has relatives in\nDetroit and New York City. Each time she visits\nher relatives, she either drives or flies.\n\nWhen travelling to Detroit, the probability she drives is\n$\\dfrac{3}{4}$, and when\ntravelling\n\nto New York City, the probability she flies is\n$\\dfrac{7}{8}$.\n\nGiven that the probability she drives when visiting her relatives is\n$\\dfrac{5}{12}$, find the probability that for a\nparticular trip, she:\n\n1.  travels to Detroit, giving your answer as a fraction; [4 marks]\n\n2.  is on her way to Detroit, given that she is flying. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $p$ be the probability that Isabella travels to Detroit for a\n    particular trip.\n\n    Drawing a tree diagram to display all the possible outcomes of the\n    trip, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/90b21769236578a3a31ddf8fe047240e677f1ee0.svg\" alt=\"90b21769236578a3a31ddf8fe047240e677f1ee0.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the tree diagram, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{P}(\\text{Drives}) &= \\dfrac{5}{12} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\text{P}(\\text{DET})\\hspace{0.05em}\\text{P}(\\text{Drives\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}DET}) + \\text{P}(\\text{NYC})\\hspace{0.05em}\\text{P}(\\text{Drives\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}NYC}) &= \\dfrac{5}{12} \\\\[9pt]\n    p\\times\\dfrac{3}{4} + (1-p)\\times\\dfrac{1}{8} &= \\dfrac{5}{12} \\\\[9pt]\n    \\dfrac{5p}{8} + \\dfrac{1}{8} &= \\dfrac{5}{12} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{19.1em} p = \\dfrac{7}{15}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the conditional probability formula, we have\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\mathrm{P}(\\text{DET}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{Flies}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{DET} \\cap \\text{Flies})}{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{Flies})}\\\\[5pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.6em}{$\\dfrac{7}{15}\\times\\dfrac{1}{4}$}}{\\raisebox{-0.7em}{$\\dfrac{7}{15}\\times\\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{8}{15}\\times\\dfrac{7}{8}$}} \\hspace{3.4em} [\\text{using tree diagram}] \\\\[12pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.6em}{$\\dfrac{7}{60}$}}{\\raisebox{-0.7em}{$\\dfrac{7}{12}$}} \\\\[12pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{1}{5}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA387","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA387\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1.  On the Venn diagram below, shade in the region\n    $A' \\cup B^{\\prime}$. [1 mark]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/1fd0957270ac7c4fcc059cb50a5d91e2c9d3ed49.svg\" alt=\"1fd0957270ac7c4fcc059cb50a5d91e2c9d3ed49.svg\">\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are such that\n$\\mathrm{P}(A' \\cup B^{\\prime}) = 0.85$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) = 0.6$.\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B)$\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we shade in the region $A'\\cup B^{\\prime}$ on the\n    Venn diagram below, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/2636dd88f0e51ebfc727ddd0a040c82b706b1a28.svg\" alt=\"2636dd88f0e51ebfc727ddd0a040c82b706b1a28.svg\">\n\n2.  Using the complementary events formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) + \\mathrm{P}(A'\\cup B^{\\prime}) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) + 0.85 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) &= 0.15 \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(B) + \\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(B) + 0.6 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(B) &= 0.4 \\hspace{1em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the\n    conditional probability formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.15}{0.4} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.375\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA396","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA396\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are such that $\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.58$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) = 0.16$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B^{\\prime})$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\mathrm{P}(A'\\cap B^{\\prime}) = 0.28$,\n    find\n    $\\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime})$.\n    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we shade in the region $A\\cap B^{\\prime}$ on the\n    Venn diagram below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/e6eef933fb5ecc12a077b0f32950628643b6a12a.svg\" alt=\"e6eef933fb5ecc12a077b0f32950628643b6a12a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the Venn diagram above, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B^{\\prime}) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.58 - 0.16 \\\\[6pt]\n    & = 0.42\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the complementary events formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\mathrm{P}(A'\\cup B^{\\prime}) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) \\hspace{2.4em} [\\text{since $A' \\hspace{-0.1em}\\cup B^{\\prime} = (A\\cap B)^{\\prime}$}] \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(A') &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(A)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the\n    combined events formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A'\\cup B^{\\prime}) &= \\mathrm{P}(A') + \\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) - \\mathrm{P}(A'\\cap B^{\\prime}) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{2.4em} 1 - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) &= (1-\\mathrm{P}(A)) + \\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) - \\mathrm{P}(A'\\cap B^{\\prime}) \\\\[6pt]\n    1 - 0.16 &= (1 - 0.58) + \\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) - 0.28 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) &= 0.7 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence, using the conditional probability formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B^{\\prime})}{\\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime})} \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.42}{0.7} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA412","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA412\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA bag contains $7$ blue and $5$ red marbles. Two marbles are selected at\nrandom without replacement.\n\n1.  Complete the tree diagram below. [3 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/865fd050d3db84e24416b401670caec65ade51d0.svg\" alt=\"865fd050d3db84e24416b401670caec65ade51d0.svg\">\n\n2.  Find the probability that exactly one of the selected\n    marbles is blue. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we complete the tree digram below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/de30fd1a308a84cd300cf6c87259b0d420284f80.svg\" alt=\"de30fd1a308a84cd300cf6c87259b0d420284f80.svg\">\n\n2.  Hence, using the tree diagram from part (a), we get\n\n     $$\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\hspace{2.1em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{exactly one is blue}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{blue \\& red}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{red \\& blue}) \\\\[15pt]\n     &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{blue})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{red\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}blue}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{red})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{blue\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}red}) \\\\[10pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{7}{12}\\times\\dfrac{5}{11} + \\dfrac{5}{12}\\times\\dfrac{7}{11} \\\\[7pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{70}{132} \\\\[8pt]\n     &= \\dfrac{35}{66}\n     \\end{aligned}\n     $$"},{"ref":"AA414","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA414\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are such that $\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.25$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) = 0.7$.\n\nFind $\\mathrm{P}(B)$, given that $A$ and $B$ are:\n\n1.  mutually exclusive; [2 marks]\n\n2.  independent. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the mutually exclusive events formula, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[8pt]\n    0.7 &= 0.25 + \\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\[8pt]\n    0.7 - 0.25 &= \\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.45 \\hspace{4.2em}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{10em} \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\hspace{2.7em} [\\text{A \\& B are independent}] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.25\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using the\n    combined events formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[12pt]\n    0.7 &= 0.25 + \\mathrm{P}(B) - 0.25\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\[6pt]\n    0.45 &= 0.75\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{0.45}{0.75} &= \\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.6 \\hspace{4.7em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA416","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA416\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nSophie can randomly select one chocolate from a large box full of\nchocolates of different types and sizes.\n\nThe probability that a chocolate is:\n\n- sugar free is $0.6$;\n\n- king size is $0.25$;\n\n- sugar free or king size\n    is $0.8$.\n\nFind the probability that the chocolate chosen by Sophie is **not** a\nsugar free king size.","markScheme":"We have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF}) &= 0.6\\phantom{5} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{SF: sugar free}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathrm{P}(\\text{KS}) &= 0.25 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{KS: king size}] \\\\[6pt]\n\\hspace{7.6em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF} \\cup \\text{KS}) &= 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, using the combined events formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF} \\cup \\text{KS}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{KS}) - \\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF} \\cap \\text{KS}) \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n0.8 &= 0.6 + 0.25 - \\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF} \\cap \\text{KS}) \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF} \\cap \\text{KS}) &= 0.05\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we\nobtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(\\text{not ``sugar free king size''}) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(\\text{SF} \\cap \\text{KS}) \\\\[6pt]\n&= 1 - 0.05 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 0.95\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA419","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA419\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following Venn diagram shows two events $A$ and $B$, where\n$\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.3$, $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) = 0.2$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) = 0.7$. The values of $p$, $q$, $r$ and $s$ are\n$\\text{probabilities}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/8ca7dc69310d14eee80cfb8fc216080fc079bcf5.svg\" alt=\"8ca7dc69310d14eee80cfb8fc216080fc079bcf5.svg\">\n\n1.  Write down the value of $r$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find the value of:\n    1.  $p$;\n\n    2.  $q$;\n    3.  $s$. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(B)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $r = 0.2$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the Venn diagram, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A) &= p + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        0.3 &= p + 0.2 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        p = 0.1 \\hspace{5.1em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the Venn diagram, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + q \\hspace{6.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        0.7 &= 0.3 + q \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        q = 0.4 \\hspace{5.1em}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the Venn diagram, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(U) &= \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) + s \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        1 &= 0.7 + s \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        s = 0.3 \\hspace{5.1em}\n        $$\n\n3.  Using the Venn diagram, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) + q \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.2 + 0.4 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA428","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA428\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following Venn diagram shows two events $A$ and $B$, where\n$\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.6$, $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) = 0.1$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) = 0.9$. The values of $p$ and $q$ are\n$\\text{probabilities}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/26f9ec4aca9d307c31eec8ca219e6fb844c8d795.svg\" alt=\"26f9ec4aca9d307c31eec8ca219e6fb844c8d795.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $q$ and of $p$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime})$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find\n    $\\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime})$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    q = 0.1 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$\n    \\\n    Using the Venn diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A) &= p + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    0.6 &= p + 0.1 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    p = 0.5 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the Venn diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.8em} \\mathrm{P}(B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) - p \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.9 - 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the complementary events formula, we\n    obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(B) \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  If we shade in the region that represents\n    $A \\cap B^{\\prime}$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/4f45e013a0498a8d457ff1414a5e7d931bd2a791.svg\" alt=\"4f45e013a0498a8d457ff1414a5e7d931bd2a791.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the conditional probability formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B^{\\prime})}{\\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime})} \\hspace{1.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.5}{0.6} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{5}{6}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA429","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA429\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe following Venn diagram shows two events $A$ and $B$, where\n$\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.3$, $\\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.8$ and $\\mathrm{P}(A\\,\\cap\\,B) = 0.2$. The values of $p$, $q$, $r$ and $s$ are probabilities.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/6cea7543b52909d937298497fa90fc1b3f7389b8.svg\" alt=\"6cea7543b52909d937298497fa90fc1b3f7389b8.svg\">\n\n1.  Find the value of $r$, $p$, $q$, and $s$. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Find\n    $\\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime})$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    r = 0.2 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$\n    \\\n    Using the Venn diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A) &= p + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\hspace{2.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n    0.3 &= p + 0.2 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    p = 0.1 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$\n\n    Using the Venn diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) + q \\hspace{2.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n    0.8 &= 0.2 + q \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    q = 0.6 \\hspace{5.15em}\n    $$\n\n   $$\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\hspace{0.55em} \\mathrm{P}(U) &= \\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B) + s \\\\[6pt]\n   1 &= (p + r + q) + s \\\\[6pt]\n   1 &= (0.1 + 0.2 + 0.6) + s \\\\[10pt]\n   &\\hspace{-0.75em}s = 0.1\n   \\end{aligned}\n   $$\n\n2.  If we shade in the region that represents\n    $A \\cap B^{\\prime}$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/1e26e9ce5436705e1f13a9b121cf8501a3704284.svg\" alt=\"1e26e9ce5436705e1f13a9b121cf8501a3704284.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the conditional probability formula, we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B^{\\prime})}{\\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime})} \\hspace{0.5em}= \\dfrac{0.1}{0.2} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA435","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA435\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are independent with\n$\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) = \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B^\\prime) = 0.3$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(A)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(B)$.\n\n    2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cup B)$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} \\mathrm{P}(A) &= \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B^\\prime) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.3 + 0.3 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.6\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the independent events formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\hspace{5em} \\\\[12pt]\n        0.3 &= 0.6\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(B) &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.5 \\hspace{6.4em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B^\\prime) + \\mathrm{P}(B) \\hspace{4.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.3 + 0.5 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.8\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA445","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA445\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nJane commutes to work five days a week and travels by either bike or\ntrain. When it rains, the probability Jane rides her bike is $0.2$. When\nit does not rain, the probability she catches the train is $0.3$. The\nprobability it rains on any given day is $0.4$.\n\n1.  On a randomly selected day, find the probability Jane catches the\n    train to work. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that Jane catches the train, find the probability it was\n    raining that day. [3 marks]\n\n3.  In a randomly chosen work week, find the probability Jane catches\n    the train on exactly two out of the five days. [3 marks]\n\n4.  If Jane travels to work $48$ weeks a year, riding her bike is free\n    and the train costs $$\\$1.70$$ per day, calculate how much Jane would\n    expect to pay per year on her commute to work.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we draw a tree diagram that shows the probabilities for the\n    events, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/07648a103ad92692987791c0e75265c8b8c5c499.svg\" alt=\"07648a103ad92692987791c0e75265c8b8c5c499.svg\">\n    $R$ : rains;\n    $R^\\prime$ : does not\n    rain;  $B$ : rides\n    bike;  $T$ : catches\n    train.\n\n    Hence, using the tree diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(T) &= \\mathrm{P}(R)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(T\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}R) + \\mathrm{P}(R^\\prime)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(T\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}R^\\prime) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0.4)(0.8) + (0.6)(0.3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(R\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}T) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(R)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(T\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}R)}{\\mathrm{P}(T)} \\\\[5.5pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{(0.4)(0.8)}{0.5} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.64\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $X$ be the number of days Jane catches the train in the chosen\n    work week.\n\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(5,0.5)$.\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathrm{P}(X = 2) &= \\text{binomPdf}(5,0.5,2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.3125\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the expected value formula, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathrm{E}[\\text{pay per day}] &= 0\\times\\mathrm{P}(B) + 1.70\\times\\mathrm{P}(T) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0)(0.5) + (1.70)(0.5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$0.85\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathrm{E}[\\text{pay per year}] &= (\\#\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{weeks})(\\#\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{days})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{E}[\\text{pay per day}]  \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (48)(5)(0.85) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\$204\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA448","marks":"14","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA448\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nIn a group of $25$ students, $18$ students take chemistry and $12$\nstudents take physics. Three students take neither chemistry nor\nphysics. The Venn diagram below shows the events $C$ (studying\nchemistry) and $P$ (studying physics). The values of $p, q, r$ and $s$\nrepresent numbers of students.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/b392cf8c9ae567be0ff74befbb22c9570a52c927.svg\" alt=\"b392cf8c9ae567be0ff74befbb22c9570a52c927.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $s$.\n\n    2.  Find the value of $r$.\n    3.  Write down the value of $p$ and the value of $q$. [5 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  A student is selected at random from the group. Given that the\n        student studies chemistry, write down the probability they study\n        physics.\n\n    2.  Hence show that studying chemistry and physics are not\n        independent events. [5 marks]\n\n3.  Two students are selected at random one after the other. Find the\n    probability the first student studies only chemistry and the second\n    student studies only physics. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $s = 3$\n\n    2.  Using the inclusion-exclusion principle, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{3em} n(C \\cup P) &= n(C) + n(P) - n(C \\cap P) \\\\[6pt]\n        22 &= 18 + 12 - r \\\\[6pt]\n        r &= 30 - 22 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        r = 8 \\hspace{3.7em}\n        $$\n\n    3.\n        $p = 10$  and\n         $q = 4$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the Venn diagram, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(P\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}C) &= \\dfrac{n(C \\cap P)}{n(C)} \\hspace{3.05em} \\\\[9pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{8}{18}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(P) &= \\dfrac{12}{25} \\neq \\mathrm{P}(P\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}C) = \\dfrac{8}{18} \\hspace{2.4em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence $C$ and $P$ are not independent.\n\n3.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$1$st only chem \\& $2$nd only phys}) &= \\dfrac{10}{25}\\times\\dfrac{4}{24} \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{1}{15}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA450","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA450\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nBag $A$ contains $4$ red balls and $3$ green balls. Bag $B$ contains $4$\nred balls and $1$ green ball.\n\n1.  A ball is randomly chosen from both bags. Find the probability they\n    are of different colours. [4 marks]\n\nA biased coin is flipped. If heads is shown, a ball is randomly selected\nfrom bag $A$. If tails is shown, a ball is randomly selected from bag\n$B$.\n\n2.  Complete the tree diagram below. [4 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/b943e8259f3fec832b0d2065d55ebab352b7eca7.svg\" alt=\"b943e8259f3fec832b0d2065d55ebab352b7eca7.svg\">\n\n3.  The biased coin is flipped and a ball is randomly selected.\n    Find the probability the ball is red. [4 marks]\n\n4.  Given that a red ball is selected, find the probability the\n    biased coin showed heads. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{different colours}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{red}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.1em}A \\text{ \\& } \\text{green}\\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{green}\\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.15em}A \\text{ \\& } \\text{red}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.1em}B) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{7}\\times\\dfrac{1}{5} + \\dfrac{3}{7}\\times\\dfrac{4}{5} \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{35} + \\dfrac{12}{35} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{16}{35}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we complete the tree digram below, we get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/fa250012019075c6bf4a146817ce9c01f4cb174d.svg\" alt=\"fa250012019075c6bf4a146817ce9c01f4cb174d.svg\">\n\n3.  Using the tree diagram, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.5em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{red}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{heads})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{red}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{heads}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{tails})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{red}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{tails}) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\times\\dfrac{4}{7} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\times\\dfrac{4}{5} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{8}{21} + \\dfrac{4}{15} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{68}{105}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Using the conditional probability formula, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{heads}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{red}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{heads})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{red}\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{heads})}{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{red})} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.6em}{$\\dfrac{2}{3}\\times\\dfrac{4}{7}$}}{\\raisebox{-0.75em}{$\\dfrac{68}{105}$}} \\\\[9pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{10}{17}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA455","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA455\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nLet $A$ and $B$ be events such that $\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.35$,\n$\\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.5$ and $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) = 0.65$.\n\nFind $\\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B)$.","markScheme":"Using the combined events formula, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2.9em} \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n0.65 &= 0.35 + 0.5 - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) &= 0.85 - 0.65 \\\\[6pt]\n\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) &= 0.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, using the conditional\nprobability formula, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{0.8em} \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)} \\\\[6pt]\n&= \\dfrac{0.2}{0.5} \\\\[9pt]\n&= \\dfrac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA641","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA641\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo events $A$ and $B$ are such that $\\mathrm{P}(A) = 0.5$ and\n$\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) = 0.1$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B^{\\prime})$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that\n    $\\mathrm{P}(A'\\hspace{-0.075em}\\cap B^{\\prime}) = 0.2$,\n    find :\n\n    1.  $\\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B)$;\n    2.  $\\mathrm{P}(B)$;\n\n    3.  $\\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime})$.\n        [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we shade in the region $A\\cap B^{\\prime}$ on the\n    Venn diagram below, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/e6eef933fb5ecc12a077b0f32950628643b6a12a.svg\" alt=\"e6eef933fb5ecc12a077b0f32950628643b6a12a.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the Venn diagram above, we deduce\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.95em} \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B^{\\prime}) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.5 - 0.1 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        (A\\cup B)^{\\prime} &= A'\\hspace{-0.075em}\\cap B^{\\prime} \\hspace{5em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, using the complementary events formula, we get\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A'\\hspace{-0.075em}\\cap B^{\\prime}) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) \\hspace{3.2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        0.2 &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) &= 1 - 0.2 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) = 0.8 \\hspace{7.25em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the combined events formula, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} \\mathrm{P}(A\\cup B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A) + \\mathrm{P}(B) - \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B) \\\\[6pt]\n        0.8 &= 0.5 + \\mathrm{P}(B) - 0.1 \\\\[6pt]\n        0.8 - 0.4 &= \\mathrm{P}(B) \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\mathrm{P}(B) = 0.4 \\hspace{5.4em}\n        $$\n\n    3.  Using the complementary events formula, we know\n        $\\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) = 1 - \\mathrm{P}(B)$\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime}) &= 0.6 \\hspace{6em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, using the conditional probability formula, we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(A\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}B^{\\prime}) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B^{\\prime})}{\\mathrm{P}(B^{\\prime})} \\hspace{3.65em} \\\\[7pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{0.4}{0.6} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA670","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA670\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA burger shop serves veggie burgers and chicken burgers. Each customer\nwill choose one of the following three options; buy only a veggie\nburger, buy only a chicken burger or buy both a veggie burger and a\nchicken burger. The probability that a customer buys a veggie burger is\n$0.65$ and the probability that a customer buys a chicken burger is\n$0.54$.\n\n1.  Find the probability that a customer chosen at random will buy:\n    1.  both a veggie burger and a chicken burger;\n\n    2.\n        only a chicken burger. [5 marks]\n\nOn a typical day, $600$ customers come to the burger shop.\n\n2.  Find:\n    1.  the expected number of veggie burgers sold on a typical day;\n\n    2.  the probability that more than $400$ veggie burgers will be\n        sold on a typical day.\n        [4 marks]\n\nIt is known that $44\\hspace{0.05em}\\%$ of the customers who come to the\nburger shop are females, and that\n$75\\hspace{0.05em}\\%$ of the female customers buy a chicken burger.\n\n3.\n    1.  A customer is selected at random. Find the probability\n        that the customer is female\n        and buys a chicken burger.\n\n    2.  A male customer is selected at random. Find the\n        probability that he buys a\n        chicken burger. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $V$ be the event that a customer chosen at random buys a veggie\n    burger.\n\n    Let $C$ be the event that a customer chosen at random buys a chicken\n    burger.\n\n    1.  Using the combined events formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.5em} \\mathrm{P}(V\\cup C) &= \\mathrm{P}(V) + \\mathrm{P}(C) - \\mathrm{P}(V \\cap C) \\\\[6pt]\n        1 &= 0.65 + 0.54 - \\mathrm{P}(V \\cap C) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(V \\cap C) &= 0.65 + 0.54 - 1 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\mathrm{P}(V \\cap C) = 0.19 \\hspace{6.8em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  If we shade in the region\n        $V^{\\prime}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cap C$ on the Venn\n        diagram below, we have\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/ec6cd1e378af12c406602ea6d802eeb838c2903f.svg\" alt=\"ec6cd1e378af12c406602ea6d802eeb838c2903f.svg\">\n\n        Hence, using the Venn diagram above, we deduce\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(V^{\\prime}\\hspace{-0.15em}\\cap C) &= \\mathrm{P}(C) - \\mathrm{P}(V \\cap C) \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.54 - 0.19 \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 0.35\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Let $X$ be the number of veggie burgers sold on a typical day.\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(600,0.65)$.\n\n    1.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (600)(0.65) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 390 \\text{ veggie burgers}  \\hspace{0.7em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using G.D.C., we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{P}(X > 400) &= \\text{binomCdf}(600,0.65,401,600) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.185\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Let $M$ denote the event that a customer chosen at random is male.\n    Let  $F$  denote the\n    event that a customer chosen at random is female.\n\n    1.  Using the conditional probability formula, we find\n\n         $$\n         \\begin{aligned}\n         \\mathrm{P}(C\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}F) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(C\\cap F)}{\\mathrm{P}(F)} \\\\[5pt]\n         0.75 &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(C\\cap F)}{0.44} \\\\[12pt]\n         \\mathrm{P}(C\\cap F) &= (0.75)(0.44) \\\\\n         \\end{aligned}\n         $$\n\n        $$\n        \\mathrm{P}(C\\cap F) = 0.33 \\hspace{3.3em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using complementary events formula, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(C) &= \\mathrm{P}(C\\cap F) + \\mathrm{P}(C\\cap M) \\\\[6pt]\n        0.54 &= 0.33 + \\mathrm{P}(C\\cap M) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\hspace{0.6em} 0.54 - 0.33 &= \\mathrm{P}(C\\cap M) \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(C\\cap M) &= 0.21\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Similarly, using\n        complementary events formula, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(M) + \\mathrm{P}(F) &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(M) + 0.44 &= 1 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(M) &= 1 - 0.44 \\hspace{6.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(M) &= 0.56\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using the\n        conditional probability formula, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(C\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}M) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(C\\cap M)}{\\mathrm{P}(M)} \\hspace{3.6em} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{0.21}{0.56} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 0.375\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA929","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA929\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$A$ and $B$ are independent events such that $\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap {B\\,} ^\\prime) = 0.09$ and $\\mathrm{P}({A\\,}^\\prime \\cap B) = 0.49$. \n\nLet $x = \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)$.\n\n1. 1. Express $\\mathrm{P}(A)$ in terms of $x$.\n\n    2. Express $\\mathrm{P}(B)$ in terms of $x$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Find the value of $x$. [2 marks]\n\n3. Find $\\mathrm{P}(B\\,|\\,A)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. We have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{P}(A) &= \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) + \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap {B\\,}^\\prime) \\\\[11pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-8.1mm}\\boxed{\\mathrm{P}(A) = x + 0.09}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n    2. We have\n\n        $$\n\n        \\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\mathrm{P}(B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) + \\mathrm{P}({A\\,}^\\prime \\cap B) \\\\[11pt]\n\n        &\\hspace{-8.35mm}\\boxed{ \\mathrm{P}(B) = x + 0.49}\n\n        \\end{aligned}\n\n        $$\n\n2. Using the independent events formula, we get\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(B) &= \\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B) \\\\[10pt]\n\n    (x+0.09)(x+0.49) &= x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    x^2 + 0.58 x + 0.0441 &= x \\\\[10pt]\n\n    x^2 - 0.42 x + 0.0441 &= 0 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-0.55em}\\boxed{x = 0.21} \\hspace{3em} [\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{using G.D.C.}\\hspace{0.1em}]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n3. Using the result of previous part and part (a) (i), we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A) &= 0.21 + 0.09 \\\\[10pt]\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(A) &= 0.3 \\\\[-12pt]\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$\n\n    Using the conditional probability formula, we have\n\n    $$\n\n    \\begin{aligned}\n\n    \\mathrm{P}(B\\,|\\,A) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A\\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(A)}\\\\[10pt]\n\n    &= \\dfrac{0.21}{0.3} \\\\[11pt]\n\n    &\\hspace{-3.5em}\\boxed{\\mathrm{P}(B\\,|\\,A) = 0.7}\n\n    \\end{aligned}\n\n    $$"},{"ref":"AI332","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AI332\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAt the local supermarket, out of the $100$ cartons of eggs on sale, $8$\nhave at least one egg inside that has cracked.\n\nIf two cartons are chosen at random from the $100$ **without\nreplacement**, find the probabilities of:\n\n1.  **Neither** cartons having a cracked egg inside. [2 marks]\n\n2.  **Both** cartons having at least one cracked egg inside. [2 marks]\n\n3.  One carton **not** having a cracked egg inside, while the other\n    having at least one cracked egg inside. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $C_1$ = {$1$st carton has a cracked egg inside},\n    $C_2$ = {$2$nd carton has a cracked egg inside}.\n\n    The event that neither cartons have a cracked egg inside can be\n    illustrated by the paths highlighted in red on the tree diagram\n    below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/ebf59df4398674c730598565ce738e626c3b701b.svg\" alt=\"ebf59df4398674c730598565ce738e626c3b701b.svg\">\n\n    Hence the probability of neither cartons having a cracked egg inside\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Pr(neither cartons, crack)} &= \\text{Pr}(C_1') \\cdot \\text{Pr}(C_2'|C_1') \\\\\n    &= \\dfrac{92}{100} \\cdot \\dfrac{91}{99}\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{0.525em}\\approx 0.846\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The paths highlighted in red on the following tree diagram represent\n    the event that both cartons have at least one cracked egg inside.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/5ff48c85fdd43a88d2fd5358b5e4769beec88ad5.svg\" alt=\"5ff48c85fdd43a88d2fd5358b5e4769beec88ad5.svg\">\n\n    Based off this tree diagram, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Pr(both cartons, crack)} &= \\text{Pr}(C_1) \\cdot \\text{Pr}(C_2|C_1) \\\\\n    &= \\dfrac{8}{100} \\cdot \\dfrac{7}{99}\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{0.525em}\\approx 0.00566\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The paths highlighted in red on the following tree diagram represent\n    the event that one carton has a cracked egg inside while the other\n    has at least one cracked egg inside.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Probability/images/e312e81017a764be8813d6e9025ef817dd19e707.svg\" alt=\"e312e81017a764be8813d6e9025ef817dd19e707.svg\">\n\n    Hence,\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{Pr(one crack, one no crack)} &= \\text{Pr}(C_1) \\cdot \\text{Pr}(C_2'|C_1) + \\text{Pr}(C_1') \\cdot \\text{Pr}(C_2|C_1')\\\\\n    &=  (\\dfrac{8}{100} \\cdot \\dfrac{92}{99}) + (\\dfrac{92}{100} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{99})\\\\[6pt]\n    & \\hspace{0.525em}\\approx 0.149\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"The Binomial Distribution","questions":[{"ref":"AA1170","marks":"21","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1170\n\n**Maximum mark: 21**\n\nAlex and Ben are playing a game with a spinner.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Binomial%20Distribution/images/AA1170_q1%20%282%29.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1170_q1 (2)\">\n\nThe triangular shaped spinner has three equal sized regions with two regions labelled __LOSE__ and one labelled __WIN__.\n\nIn turn $1$ Alex spins the spinner first. If she lands on __WIN__ then the game ends and Alex has won in turn $1$.\n\nIf she lands on __LOSE__ then Ben spins. If he lands on __WIN__ then the game ends and Ben has won in turn $1$.\n\nIf they both land on __LOSE__ then the game continues into turn $2$.\n\nThe same process repeats until someone lands on __WIN__. Alex always spins first for each new turn.\n\nLet the turn that the game ends be the discrete random variable $T$. Where $T=1,2,3,4,...$.\n\n1.  Show that the probability that Alex wins and $T=2$ is $\\displaystyle\\frac{4}{27}$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Hence complete the missing values in the first four columns of the probability distribution for $T$ when Alex wins.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Binomial%20Distribution/images/AA1170_table.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1170_table\">\n\n[2 marks]\n\n3.  Show that when Alex wins the values of $\\text{P}(T=t)$  form a geometric sequence. [2 marks]\n\n4.  Justify that as $T \\rightarrow \\infty$ the sequence has a sum. [1 mark]\n\n5.  Hence show that the probability Alex wins the game is $\\displaystyle\\frac{3}{5}$. [2 marks]\n\n6.  Alex and Ben play the same game 10 times.\n\n    The discrete random variable $W$ is the number of times Alex wins.\n\n    1.  Determine the probability that $W$ is within one standard deviation of the mean.\n\n    2.  Determine the probability that $W$ is larger than $\\mathrm{E}(W)$ given $W$ is within one standard deviation of the mean. [7 marks]\n\nAlex and Ben want to keep playing the same game with only one change. They decide to create a new heptagonal spinner.\n\nThe spinner has $7$ equally sized regions.\n\nEach region must be labelled either __WIN__ or __LOSE__.\n\nThey want to label the spinner such that the probability of Alex winning the game is $\\frac{7}{10}$.\n\n7.  How __many__ of the regions should be labeled __WIN__? You must justify your answer. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  For any particular spin $\\mathrm{P}(\\text{Win})=\\tfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(\\text{Lose})=\\tfrac{2}{3}$.\n\n    For Alex to win in turn $2$, Alex must first lose in turn $1$, then Ben must lose in turn $1$ and Alex wins in turn $2$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Alex wins in turn 2})&=\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{4}{27} \\hspace{2em}\\text{... as required}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2.  We can calculate the probability when $T=1, 3$ and $4$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Alex wins $T=1$)}&=\\frac{1}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Alex wins $T=3$)}&=\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{3}\\hspace{1em}\\times \\hspace{1em}\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{3}\\hspace{1em}\\times\\frac{1}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{16}{243}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Alex wins $T=4$)}&=\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{3}\\hspace{1em}\\times \\hspace{1em} \\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{3}\\hspace{1em}\\times \\hspace{1em}\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{3}\\hspace{1em}\\times\\frac{1}{3}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{64}{2187}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Binomial%20Distribution/images/AA1170_table_a.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1170_table_a\">\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We can see that a geometric sequence is formed\n\n    with a first term of $u_1=\\tfrac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.525em}$ and $r=\\tfrac{4}{9}\\hspace{0.525em}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  We know that an infinite geometric sequence has a sum if $|r|<1$.\n\n    Therefore as $\\tfrac{4}{9}<1\\hspace{0.525em}$ then the series has a sum.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Using the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        S_\\infty&=\\frac{u_1}{1-r}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{\\tfrac{1}{3}}{1-\\tfrac{4}{9}}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\frac{3}{5} \\hspace{2em}\\text{... as required}\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n6.  1.  The DRV $W$ forms a binomial distribution, where $n=10$ and $p=\\tfrac{3}{5}$ therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            W \\sim \\text{Bin}\\left(10, \\tfrac{3}{5}\\right)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can use the formula booklet to find the expected value (mean) of the distribution\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n                \\mathrm{E}(W)&=np\\\\[6pt]\n            &=10 \\times \\tfrac{3}{5}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=6 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Similarly, we can find the variance\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\mathrm{Var}(W) &= np(1-p)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=10 \\times \\tfrac{3}{5} \\times \\left(1-\\tfrac{3}{5}\\right)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\tfrac{12}{5} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore we can find the standard deviation of the distribution\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\sigma&=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{Var}(W)}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=1.5491...\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence we need to find the value of the following\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\mathrm{P}(6 - 1.5491... < W < 6 + 1.5491...)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We can simplify the inequality and as the distribution is discrete we round to the next whole number\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &=\\mathrm{P}(4.4509... < W < 7.5491...)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\mathrm{P}(5 \\leq W \\leq 7) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence using the G.D.C.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &= \\mathrm{P}(5 \\leq W \\leq 7)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.66647...\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.666 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  This is a conditional probability problem therefore we can use the following formula\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\mathrm{P}(A|B)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Where event $A$ is when $W > \\mathrm{E}(W)$ and (from part(f)(i)) event $B$ is when $5 \\leq W \\leq 7$.\n\n        To find the intersection of events $A$ and $B$ we should first consider the possible outcomes of event $A$.\n\n        As $\\mathrm{E}(W)=6$ and $W>6$ then $W= 7, 8, 9, 10$.\n\n        Event $B$ occurs when $W=5, 6, 7$.\n\n        We can see that $A \\cap B$ is when $W=7$.\n\n        Therefore $(A \\cap B) =  \\mathrm{P}(W=7)$. Hence we can find $ \\mathrm{P}(A|B)$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n             \\mathrm{P}(A|B)&=\\frac{ \\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{ \\mathrm{P}(B)}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\frac{ \\mathrm{P}(W=7)}{ \\mathrm{P}(5 \\leq W \\leq 7)} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\frac{0.21499...}{0.66647...} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.32258...\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.323\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n7.  Let the probability of winning on any given spin be $w$, therefore the probability of losing on any given spin is $1-w$.\n\n    For Alex to win she must win in turn $1$ or turn $2$ or turn $3$ etc.\n\n    We can form an infinite geometric series for the probability that Alex wins\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{Alex wins})&= \\mathrm{P}(T=1)+P(T=2)+ \\mathrm{P}(T=3)+...\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{7}{10}&=w+(1-w)^2\\times w + (1-w)^4\\times w +...\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can now write a closed formula for the open sequence shown above. To do this we use the sum of an infinite geometric sequence formula from the booklet with $u_1=w$ and $r=(1-w)^2$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\frac{7}{10}&=\\frac{w}{1-(1-w)^2} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving this on a G.D.C. we find that $w=\\frac{4}{7}$.\n\n    Hence we need to label $4 \\text{ of the regions with} \\textbf{ WIN}$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA363","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA363\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA biased coin is tossed $10$ times. Let $X$ be the number of tails\nobtained.\n\nThe probability of obtaining a tail in any one throw is $p > 0$.\n\n1.  Find, in terms of $p$, an expression for $\\mathrm{P}(X = 8)$.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1.  Determine the value of $p$ for which $\\mathrm{P}(X = 8)$ is a\n        maximum.\n\n    2.  For this value of $p$, determine the expected number of tails.\n        [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Since $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(10,p)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.7em} \\mathrm{P}(X = 8) &= \\hspace{-0.075em}\\binom{10}{8}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\big[p^8\\big]\\big[(1-p)^{10-8}\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 45\\big[p^8\\big]\\big[1 - 2p + p^2\\big] \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 45\\big(p^8 - 2p^9 + p^{10}\\big)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Differentiating $\\mathrm{P}(X = 8)$ with respect to $p$, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{4.925em} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}p}\\big[\\mathrm{P}(X = 8)\\big] &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}p}\\big[45\\big(p^8 - 2p^9 + p^{10}\\big)\\big] \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 45\\big(8p^7 - 18p^8 + 10p^9\\big) \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by power rule}] \\\\[11pt]\n        &=\\hspace{-0.075em}\\big(90p^7\\big)\\big(4 - 9p + 5p^2\\big)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Hence, solving the equation\n        $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}p}\\big[\\mathrm{P}(X = 8)\\big] = 0$\n        for $p$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\big(90p^7\\big)\\big(4 - 9p + 5p^2\\big) &= 0 \\\\[12pt]\n        5p^2 - 9p + 4 &= 0 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $p > 0$}] \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[12pt]\n        (5p - 4)(p - 1) &= 0 \\\\[6pt]\n        p &= \\dfrac{4}{5},\\hspace{0.25em} 1\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence $4/5$\n        and $1$ are the only critical numbers of $\\mathrm{P}(X = 8)$.\\\n        Therefore, $\\mathrm{P}(X = 8)$ is a maximum for\n         $p = \\dfrac{4}{5}$\n\n    2.  Using the formula for the expected value of\n        $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(10,4/5)$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        4\n\n        \\mathrm{E}[X] &= 10\\times\\dfrac{4}{5} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 8\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA377","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA377\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe probability of obtaining tails when a biased coin is tossed is\n$0.6$. The coin is tossed $400$ times.\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the mean number of tails.\n\n    2.  Find the variance of the number of tails. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find the probability that the number of tails obtained is less than\n    one standard deviation away from the mean.\n    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $T$ be the number of tails obtained when the coin is tossed\n    $400$ times.\n\n    We have $T \\sim \\mathrm{B}(400,0.6)$.\n    1.  Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $T$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[T] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (400)(0.6) \\hspace{2.2em} \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 240\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence, using the formula for the variance of $T$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{Var}(T) &= np(1-p) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (400)(0.6)(1-0.6) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= 96\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the formula for the standard deviation of $T$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.5em} \\sigma &= \\sqrt{\\mathrm{Var}(T)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\sqrt{96} \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 9.80\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} \\mathrm{P}(|T - 240| < 9.80) &= \\mathrm{P}(240-9.80 < T < 240+9.80) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\mathrm{P}(230.2 < T < 249.8) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\mathrm{P}(231 \\leq T \\leq 249) \\\\[8pt]\n    &= \\text{binomCdf}(400,0.6,231,249) \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx 0.668\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA378","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA378\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe probability of obtaining heads when a biased coin is tossed is\n$0.65$. The coin is tossed seven times. Find the probability of\nobtaining:\n\n1.  at least four heads; [2 marks]\n\n2.  the fourth tail is on the seventh toss. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $H$ be the number of heads obtained when the coin is tossed\n    seven times.\n\n    We have $H \\sim \\mathrm{B}(7,0.65)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} \\mathrm{P}(H \\geq 4) &= \\text{binomCdf}(7,0.65,4,7) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.800\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $T$ be the number of tails obtained among the first six tosses\n    of the coin.\n\n    We have $T \\sim \\mathrm{B}(6,0.35)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} \\mathrm{P}(T = 3) &= \\text{binomPdf}(6,0.35,3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.23549\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, using independence of coin\n    tosses, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$4$th ``tails'' on $7$th toss}) &=  \\mathrm{P}(T = 3)\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{``tails'' on $7$th toss}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0.23549)(0.35) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0824\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA389","marks":"22","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA389\n\n**Maximum mark: 22**\n\nTen balls numbered $1,2,2,3,3,3,4,4,4,4$ are placed in a bag. Balls are\ntaken one at a time from the bag at random and the number noted.\n\nThroughout the question a ball is always replaced before the new ball is\ntaken.\n\n1.  A single ball is taken from the bag. Let $X$ denote the value shown\n    on the ball. Find\n    $\\mathrm{E}[X]$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Three balls are taken from the bag. Find the probability that:\n    1.  the sum of the three numbers is $8$;\n\n    2.  the median of the three numbers is $4$. [6 marks]\n\n3.  Fifteen balls are taken from the bag. Find the probability that less\n    than six of the balls are numbered $3$. [3 marks]\n\n4.  Find the least number of balls that must be taken from the\n    bag for the probability of taking out at\n    least one ball numbered $4$ to be greater\n    than $0.95$.[3 marks]\n\n5.  Another bag also contains balls numbered $1,2,3$ or $4$. Twenty\n    balls are to be taken from this bag at random. It is calculated that\n    the expected number of balls numbered $2$ is $2.5$, the expected\n    number of balls numbered $3$ is $5$, and the variance of the number\n    of balls numbered $1$ is $1.8$. Find the least possible number of\n    balls numbered $4$ in this bag. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The probability distribution for $X$ is\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Binomial%20Distribution/images/df56eabb855674e47aa0d3469d445f272214b9c9.svg\" alt=\"df56eabb855674e47aa0d3469d445f272214b9c9.svg\">\n\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= \\sum x\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X = x) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 1\\times\\dfrac{1}{10} + 2\\times\\dfrac{2}{10} + 3\\times\\dfrac{3}{10} + 4\\times\\dfrac{4}{10} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the independent events formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.3em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{sum is $8$}) &= 6\\mathrm{P}(134) + 3\\mathrm{P}(233) + 3\\mathrm{P}(224) \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 6\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\bigg] + 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{3}{10}\\bigg] + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n        &+ 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\bigg] \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 0.174\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the independent events formula, we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{median is $4$}) &= 3\\mathrm{P}(144) + 3\\mathrm{P}(244) + 3\\mathrm{P}(344) + \\mathrm{P}(444)  \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\bigg] + 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{2}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\bigg] + \\cdots \\\\[6pt]\n        &+ 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{3}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\bigg] + \\dfrac{4}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10}\\times\\dfrac{4}{10} \\\\[12pt]\n        &= 0.352\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n3.  Let $Y$ be the number of balls numbered $3$ among the fifteen balls\n    taken from the bag. We have\n    $Y \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sim \\mathrm{B}(15,0.3)$.\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.65em} \\mathrm{P}(Y < 6) &= \\text{binomCdf}(15,0.3,0,5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.722\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $N$ be the number of balls taken from the bag and $Z$ be the\n    number of balls numbered $4$ among them. We have\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{B}(N,0.4)$.\n    Solving the inequality $\\mathrm{P}(Z \\geq 1) > 0.95$ for $N$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    1 - \\mathrm{P}(Z = 0) &> 0.95 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(Z = 0) &< 0.05 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\hspace{1.025em} \\text{binomPdf}(N,0.4,0) &< 0.05 \\\\[6pt]\n    N &= 6,\\hspace{0.25em} 7,\\hspace{0.25em} 8,\\hspace{0.25em}\\dots \\hspace{1em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the least number of balls that must be taken from the bag is\n     $6$\n\n5.  Let $S$ be the number of balls numbered $1$, $T$ be the number of\n    balls numbered $2$ and $U$ be the number of balls numbered $3$ among\n    the twenty balls taken from the bag. We have\n    $S \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,p)$, $T \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,q)$ and\n    $U \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,r)$, where $p$, $q$ and $r$ are the\n    probabilities of taking out balls numbered $1$, $2$ and $3$,\n    respectively.\n\n    Using the formula for the expected value of $T$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}[T] &= 20q \\\\[10pt]\n    2.5 &= 20q \\\\[4pt]\n    q &= \\dfrac{1}{8}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the formula for the expected value of $U$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}[U] &= 20r \\hspace{2.1em} \\\\[12pt]\n    5 &= 20r \\\\[6pt]\n    r &= \\dfrac{1}{4}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the formula for the variance\n    of $S$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{12.15em} \\mathrm{Var}(S) &= 20p(1-p) \\\\[16pt]\n    1.8 &= 20p(1-p) \\\\[10pt]\n    p &= \\dfrac{1}{10},\\hspace{0.25em} \\dfrac{9}{10} \\hspace{2em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\[6pt]\n    p &= \\dfrac{1}{10},\\hspace{0.25em} \\cancel{\\cancel{\\hspace{0.075em}\\dfrac{9}{10}\\hspace{0.075em}}} \\hspace{1.85em} [\\text{since $9/10 +  1/8 + 1/4 > 1$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$1$ or $2$ or $3$}) &=  \\dfrac{1}{10} +   \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{1}{4} \\hspace{2.6em} \\\\[6pt]\n    1 - \\mathrm{P}(4) &= \\dfrac{19}{40} \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(4) &= \\dfrac{21}{40}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the minimum\n    total number of balls in the bag is $40$ and the least\n\n    possible number of balls numbered $4$ among them is\n     $21$"},{"ref":"AA392","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA392\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe random variable $X$ has a binomial distribution with parameters $n$\nand $p$.\n\nIt is given that $\\mathrm{E}(X) = 4.5$.\n\n1.  Find the least possible value of $n$. [2 marks]\n\nIt is further given that $\\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 1) = 0.03683$, correct to\n$4$ significant figures.\n\n2.  Determine the value of $n$ and the value of $p$.\n    [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Using the formula for the expected value of\n    $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(n,p)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.9em} \\mathrm{E}(X) &= np \\\\[6pt]\n    4.5 &= np \\\\[6pt]\n    4.5 &\\leq n \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $p \\leq 1$}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the least possible value of $n$ is\n     $5$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    np &= 4.5  \\\\[6pt]\n    n &= \\dfrac{4.5}{p} \\hspace{1.35em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using\n    the probability mass function of $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(n,p)$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 1) &= \\mathrm{P}(X = 0) + \\mathrm{P}(X = 1) \\\\[8pt]\n    0.03683 &= (1-p)^n + np\\hspace{0.05em}(1-p)^{n-1} \\\\[6pt]\n    (1-p)^{\\frac{4.5}{p}}\\hspace{-0.2em} + 4.5(1-p)^{\\frac{4.5}{p}-1} &= 0.03683 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by $(1)$ and $(2)$}] \\hspace{0.4em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.85em} p \\approx 0.281 \\hspace{3.1em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{11.1em} n &= \\dfrac{4.5}{p} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\hspace{-0.2em}\\bigg\\lfloor\\dfrac{4.5}{0.281}\\bigg\\rfloor\\hspace{-0.2em} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{since $p > 0.281$}]\\\\[12pt]\n    &= 16\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA434","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA434\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA multiple choice exam consists of $20$ questions. Each question has\nfour answers. Only one of the answers is correct. For each question,\nJack randomly chooses one of the four answers.\n\n1.  Find the expected number of questions Jack answers correctly.\n    [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the probability that Jack:\n\n    1.  answers exactly four questions correctly;\n\n    2.  answers more than four questions correctly. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $X$ be the number of questions Jack answers correctly.\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,1/4)$.\n\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 20\\times\\dfrac{1}{4} \\hspace{3em} \\\\[7pt]\n    &= 5\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,1/4)$.\n    1.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 4) &= \\text{binomPdf}(20,1/4,4) \\hspace{1.35em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.190\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.15em} \\mathrm{P}(X > 4) &= \\text{binomCdf}(20,1/4,5,20) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.585\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA437","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA437\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nJohn likes to play two games, $A$ and $B$.\n\nFor game $A$, the probability John wins is $0.8$. He plays game $A$ five\ntimes.\n\n1.  Find the probability that he wins exactly two games. [2 marks]\n\nFor game $B$, the probability John wins is $p$. He plays game $B$ six\ntimes.\n\n2.  Write down an expression, in terms of $p$, for the\n    probability that he wins exactly four games. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Hence find the values of $p$ such that the probability that\n    he wins exactly four games is $0.2$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $X$ be the number of games John wins when he plays game $A$ five\n    times.\n\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(5,0.8)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X = 2) &= \\text{binomPdf}(5,0.8,2) \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0512\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $Y$ be the number of games John wins when he plays game $B$ six\n    times.\n\n    We have $Y \\sim \\mathrm{B}(6,p)$.\n\n    Hence, using the probability mass function of $Y$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(Y = 4) &= \\hspace{-0.075em}\\binom{6}{4}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\big[p^4\\big]\\big[(1-p)^{6-4}\\big] \\hspace{0.3em}  \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 15\\big[p^4\\big]\\big[(1-p)^2\\big]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Solving the equation $\\mathrm{P}(Y = 4) = 0.2$ for $0 < p < 1$, we\n    find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    15\\big[p^4\\big]\\big[(1-p)^2\\big] &= 0.2 \\hspace{10.9em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{10.3em} p \\approx 0.464,\\hspace{0.25em} 0.834 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA446","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA446\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA factory makes toys. The probability that a toy is defective is\n$0.08$. A random sample of $50$ toys is\ntested.\n\n1.  Find the expected number of defective toys. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the probability that there is at least one defective toy\n    in the sample. [3 marks]\n\n3.  Given that there is at least one defective toy in the sample,\n    find the probability that there are at\n    most three defective toys. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $X$ be the number of defective toys in the sample.\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(50,0.08)$.\n\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{4.2em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (50)(0.08) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 4 \\text{ defective toys} \\hspace{3.6em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.5em} \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 1) &= \\text{binomCdf}(50,0.08,1,50) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.985\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 3\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} X \\geq 1) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(1 \\leq X \\leq 3)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 1)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\text{binomCdf}(50,0.08,1,3)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 1)} \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx \\dfrac{0.410}{0.985} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.416\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA451","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA451\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA bag contains $8$ blue marbles, $12$ green marbles and $m$ red marbles.\nA marble is selected at random and replaced. This\nis performed three times.\n\n1.  Write down the probability that the first marble selected is blue.\n    [1 mark]\n\nLet $X$ be the number of blue marbles selected.\n\n2.  Find the smallest value of $m$ for which\n    $\\mathrm{Var}(X) < 0.5$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\mathrm{P}(\\text{blue}) = \\dfrac{8}{20+m}$\n\n2.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(3, 8/(20+m))$.\n    Hence, using the formula for the variance of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{Var}(X) &= np(1-p) \\\\[11pt]\n    &= 3\\bigg[\\dfrac{8}{20+m}\\bigg]\\hspace{-0.1em}\\bigg[1-\\dfrac{8}{20+m}\\bigg] \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{24(12+m)}{(20+m)^2}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, solving the\n    inequality $\\mathrm{Var}(X) < 0.5$ for $m > 0$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{7.5em} \\dfrac{24(12+m)}{(20+m)^2} &< \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n    48(12+m) &< (20+m)^2 \\\\[12pt]\n    m &> 17.9 \\hspace{2.7em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the smallest value of $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$ for which\n    $\\mathrm{Var}(X) < 0.5$ is\n     $18$"},{"ref":"AA773","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA773\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nAB Technologies produces DC motors of which $5\\%$ are defective. The\nmotors are packaged into boxes, each box containing $60$ motors. A box\nis selected at random.\n\n1.  Find the probability that the box contains:\n    1.  exactly one defective motor;\n\n    2.  at least two defective motors. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Given that there are at least two defective motors in the box, find\n    the probability that there are at most four defective motors.\n    [2 marks]\n\nJames orders 5 boxes of motors for his company.\n\n3.  Find the probability that James will receive at least one\n    but no more than fifteen defective motors in his order. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Let $X$ be the number of defective motors in the box.\\\n        We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(60,0.05)$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm P(X=1) &= \\text{binomPdf}(60,0.05,1) \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\approx 0.145\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 2) &= \\text{binomCdf}(60,0.05,2,60) \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\approx 0.808\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 4\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} X \\geq 2) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(2 \\leq X \\leq 4)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 2)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\text{binomCdf}(60,0.05,2,4)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 2)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx \\dfrac{0.628}{0.808} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.777\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $Y$ be the number of defective motors in 5 boxes.\\\n    We have $Y \\sim \\mathrm{B}(300,0.05)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(1 \\leq Y \\leq 15) &= \\text{binomCdf}(300,0.05,1,15)\\hspace{1.8em} \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.568\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]},{"name":"The Normal Distribution","questions":[{"ref":"AA1057","marks":"18","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1057\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\nThe duration, $E$, expressed in months, of the $\\textit{Everlast}$ brand of car battery, follows a normal distribution with a mean of $42$ months and a standard deviation $\\sigma$ months.\n\nIt is known that $10\\%$ of the $\\textit{Everlast}$ batteries will have a larger duration than $60$ months.\n\n1. Find the value of $\\sigma$. [4 marks]\n\n2. Find the probability that an $\\textit{Everlast}$ battery lasts less than $12$ months.[2 marks]\n\nA battery is considered defective if it lasts less than $12$ months.\n\nA retailer has purchased a batch of $550$ $\\textit{Everlast}$ batteries.\n\n3.  1. What is the probability the retailer finds at least four defective batteries?\n\n    2. What is the probability the retailer finds at least $8$ defective batteries, given that at least four defective batteries have been found?\n\n    3. Find the expected number of defective batteries purchased by the retailer. Round your answer to the nearest integer.[7 marks]\n\nThe retailer has sold the entire batch of batteries and needs to buy new ones.\n\nThey have decided to try another brand of batteries called $\\textit{Permalast}$.\n\nLet the number of defective batteries in a batch be represented by the discrete random variable $D$. With $D_e$ being the number of defective $\\textit{Everlast}$ batteries in a batch and $D_p$ being the number of defective $\\textit{Permalast}$ batteries in a batch.\n\nThe probability that a single $\\textit{Permalast}$ battery is defective is $k$, where, $0.5<k<1$.\n\nThe retailler buys a new batch of $550$ $\\textit{Everlast}$ and $550$ $\\textit{Permalast}$ batteries.\n\nIt is known that $4\\text{Var}(D_e)<\\text{Var}(D_p)<5\\text{Var}(D_e)$.\n\n4. Find the range of possible values for $k$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. Using that $E\\sim \\mathrm{N}(42, \\sigma^2)$ and $Z\\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$, we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X > 60) &= 0.10 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[8pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}\\left(Z > \\dfrac{60-42}{\\sigma}\\right) &= 0.1 \\\\[8pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}\\left(Z < \\dfrac{60-42}{\\sigma}\\right) &= 1-0.1 \\\\[8pt]\n        \\dfrac{18}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.9, 0, 1) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1A1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        \\sigma &= \\frac{18}{1.28155...} \\hspace{5.52em}[\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{by using G.D.C.}\\hspace{0.1em}]\\\\[8pt]\n        &=14.04547...\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\sigma = 14.0  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Using that $E\\sim \\mathrm{N}(42, \\sigma^2)$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X < 12) &= \\text{normCdf}(-\\infty, 12, 42, 14.045...) \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        &=0.0163429...\\hspace{5.52em} [\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{by using G.D.C.}\\hspace{0.1em}]\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.29em} = 0.0163 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. 1. Let $X$ be the number of defective batteries among the purchased batteries.\n\n    Then, we have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(550,0.0163)$.\n    $$\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the probability of finding at least $4$ defective batteries is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 4) &=\\text{binomCdf}(550, 0.0163..., 4, 550) \\\\[8pt]\n        &=0.97933...\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.275em}= 0.979 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    2.  Using the conditional probability formula $\\mathrm{P}(A\\,|\\,B) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 8 \\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.1em} X \\geq 4) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}\\left(\\{X \\geq 8\\} \\cap \\{X \\geq 4\\}\\right)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 4)} \\hspace{1em} \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 8)}{\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 4)} \\\\[10pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\text{binomCdf}(550, 0.0163..., 8, 550)}{0.97933...} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\\\\[8pt]\n        &=0.690970...\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{0.275em}=0.691 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    3. Using the formula for the mean of a binomial distribution, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{E(X)} &= 550 \\times 0.0163... \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[9pt]\n        &=8.9886...\\\\[6pt]\n                        &\\hspace{0.275em}\\boxed{= 9} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n4. We can (re)define the two random variables\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &D_e\\sim \\mathrm{B}(550,0.0163...) &D_p\\sim \\mathrm{B}(550,k)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the formula for the variance of a binomial distribution we can evaluate\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Var}(D_e)&=550\\times 0.0163...(1-0.0163...)\\\\\n        &=8.8417... \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we can rewrite the inequality given in the question\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        4\\text{Var}(D_e)<\\text{Var}(D_p)&<5\\text{Var}(D_e)\\\\\n        4\\times 8.8417...<\\text{Var}(D_p)&<5 \\times 8.8417...\\\\\n        35.366...<\\text{Var}(D_p)&<44.208... \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Using the same formula again we can write\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\text{Var}(D_p)&=550k(1-k) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we need to solve the following inequality for $k$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        35.366...<550k(1-k)&<44.208... \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can sketch the above on a G.D.C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/ans.png\" alt=\"ans\">\n\n    As $k>0.5$ we can see from the intersection points\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        0.912<k<0.931  \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1168","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1168\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nA continuous random variable $X$ is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu$ and a standard deviation of $\\tfrac{1}{6}\\mu$.\n\nPart of the distribution of $X$ is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1168_q1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1168_q1\">\n\n1.  1.  Using the information given in the diagram, show that $\\mu=8.316$ to four significant figures.\n\n    2.  Hence find the standard deviation of $X$ to four significant figures. [4 marks]\n\nCharles is designing a game for the school fair in which players must balance on a tightrope for as long as possible.\n\nPlayers win money to donate to charity.\n\nDepending on how long they stay on the rope, they either receive money from Charles or give it to him. The game is free to play.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1168_q2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1168_q2\">\n\nThe CRV $X$, from part (a),  is the time a player stays on the rope and is measured in seconds.\n\nThe discrete random variable $D$ is the money a player gives or receives and is rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nCharles decides to have $3$ different payout categories.\n\nFor category $1$, if a player stays on the rope for less than $8$ seconds, they must __give__ Charles \\$_a_.\n\nFor category $2$, if a player stays on the rope for between $8$ and $10$ seconds, they __receive__ \\$_b_ from Charles.\n\nFor category $3$, if a player stays on the rope for more than $10$ seconds, they __receive__ \\$_c_ from Charles.\n\nWhere $a,b,c \\in \\mathrm{R}$.\n\n2.  1.  Complete the two missing spaces, to $4$ significant figures, in the probability distribution table below\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/table_q.JPEG\" alt=\"table_q\">\n\n    2.  When designing the game, Charles is deciding what values to assign to $a, b$ and $c$. For category $1$ players give money to charles, therefore $a<0$.\n\n        He wants the game to be fair.\n\n        If $c=5-b^2$ and $a<0$, what is the maximum value of $|a|$? [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  We can use the standardised normal distribution formula to write an equation in terms of $\\mu$.\n\n        We need to find the $Z-$score corresponding to $10$ for this. We will call this value $z_1$.\n\n        We use the inverse normal function to find $z_1$. This function is generally defined as P$(Z<z)$, therefore we need to find P$(X<10)$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\text{P}(X<10)&=1-\\text{P}(X>10)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=1-0.1122\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.8878\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now we can use the inverse normal function to find $z_1$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            z_1&=\\text{invNorm}(0.8878,0,1)\\\\[6pt]\n            &=1.2149...\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Now, we can substitute the known information into the standardised normal distribution formula\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            Z&=\\frac{X-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\\\[6pt]\n            1.2149...&=\\frac{10-\\mu}{\\tfrac{1}{6}\\mu}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n            1.2149...\\tfrac{1}{6}\\mu&=10-\\mu\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Solving this using a G.D.C. gives $\\mu=8.316\\hspace{0.525em}$ to four significant figures as required.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  The standard deviation ($\\sigma_X$) of $X$ is\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\sigma_X&=\\frac{8.316}{6}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=1.386 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  1.  We can use our values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ from part(a) and a G.D.C. to complete the table\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/table_a.JPEG\" alt=\"table_a\">\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n    2.  For the game to be fair the expected payout must be zero. Therefore\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\text{E}(D)&=0 \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n            0&=0.4098a+0.4780b+0.1122c \\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        We are told in the question that $c=5-b^2$. Therefore we can write the equation above in terms of $a$ and $b$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        0&=0.4098a+0.4780b+0.1122(5-b^2) \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        If we make $a$ the subject then we have $a=f(b)$\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}        0.4098a&=-0.4780b-0.1122(5-b^2)\\\\[6pt]\n        a&=\\frac{-0.4780b-0.1122(5-b^2)}{0.4098} \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We can now draw a graph of $a$ against $b$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/aa1168.JPG\" alt=\"aa1168\">\n\n        The questions states $a<0$ this is shown above by the red part of the curve, therefore we can see that the graph has a minimum point of $(2.130..., -2.611...)$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Therefore the maximum value of $|a|$ is $2.61$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\textcolor{white}{hack}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA1289","marks":"18","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1289\n\n**Maximum mark: 18**\n\n_Lesta Laboratory_ conducts experiments on different metals.\n\nA sample of a metal is taken, if the weight falls in a particular range then the sample is used in an experiment. Otherwise it is rejected.\n\nOne type of metal is _Alloy X_, for which the samples have a weight that is normally distributed with a mean of $19.6\\,$grams and a standard deviation of $2.1\\,$grams.\n\n_Lesta Laboratory_ will use a sample of _Alloy X_ in an experiment if the weight is between $17$ g and $23$ g.\n1.  Find the probability a randomly selected sample of _Alloy X_ will be used in an experiment.[2 marks]\n\n_Alloy Y_ is another type of metal used at _Lesta Laboratory_.\n\nThe weights, $W_Y$, of samples of _Alloy Y_ are  normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu_Y$ and a standard deviation of $\\sigma_Y$.\n\nIt is known that $3.061\\%$ of samples of _Alloy Y_ weigh less than $35\\,$g.\n\n2.  Show that $\\mu_Y=35+1.872\\sigma_Y$[2 marks]\n\nIt is also known that $\\mathrm{P}(W_Y>46)=0.1714$.\n\n3.  1.  Show that $\\mu_Y=46-0.9486\\sigma_Y$\n\n    2.  Hence write down the values of $\\mu_Y$ and $\\sigma_Y$.[3 marks]\n\nA sample of _Alloy Y_ is used in an experiment if the weight of the sample lies within $k$ standard deviations, $\\sigma_Y$, of its mean, $\\mu_Y$. The probability of this occurring is $54.67\\%$.\n\n4.  1.  Find $k$.\n\n    2.  If _Lesta Laboratory_ takes $8$ independent  samples of _Alloy Y_ what is the probability more than $5$ are used in an experiment?[6 marks]\n\n_Lesta Laboratory_ is testing some samples of metals for an experiment. They select $2$ samples of _Alloy X_ and $2$ samples of _Alloy Y_.\n\n5.  What is the probability there will be more samples of _Alloy X_ than _Alloy Y_ that can be used in the experiment?[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  When working with normal distribution questions it is helpful to sketch the information on a normal distribution curve.\n\n    From the given information we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1289-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1289-a1\">\n\n    where $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(19.6,2.1^2)$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    The area of the shaded region represents the probability of $X$ being between $17\\,$g and $23\\,$g.\n\n    Using the calculator we can find this area\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(17 \\leq X \\leq 23) &= \\text{normCdf}(17,23,19.6,2.1) \\hspace{4.1em}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=0.8394...\\\\[8pt]\n    &=0.839\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  The sketch below represents the region P$(Y<35)=0.03061$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1289-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1289-a2\">\n\n    When $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are unknown we will need to standardise the distribution. For this we will use the formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        z&=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall that the C.R.V. $Z$ follows the standard normal distribution such that $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1^2)$.\n\n    In the diagram below, the axis representing the $Z-$distribution is drawn below the distribution for $Y$. The $Z-$value is aligned with $Y=35$, which is the boundary of the region with a probability of $0.03601$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1289-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1289-a3\">\n\n    To find the $Z-$value associated with P$(Z<0.03061)$ we must use the inverse normal function.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z &= \\text{invNorm}(0.03061,0,1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &=-1.871...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Substituting $z$ and $35$ into the formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        -1.8719...&=\\frac{35-\\mu_Y}{\\sigma_Y}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        -1.872\\,\\sigma_Y&=35-\\mu_Y\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-1.5em}\\mu_Y=35+1.872\\sigma_Y\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  1.  As in part (b) we calculate the $Z$-value and use the standardisation formula.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1289-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1289-a4\">\n\n        We need to find the associated $Z-$value on the standard normal curve for $P(Z>z)=0.1714$.\n\n        $\\mathrm{P}(Z>0.1714)=\\mathrm{P}(Z<0.8286)$. Hence we can use the calculator to find $z$.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        z &= \\text{invNorm}(0.8286,0,1) \\hspace{4.1em}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=0.94864...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Substituting $z$ and $46$ into the formula\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            0.9486&=\\frac{46-\\mu_Y}{\\sigma_Y}\\\\[6pt]\n            0.9486\\,\\sigma_Y&=46-\\mu_Y\\\\[6pt]\n        &\\hspace{-1.5em}\\mu_Y=46-0.9486\\sigma_Y\\hspace{2em}\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{AG}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Now we have a system of equations that can be solved simultaneously.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mu_Y&=35+1.872\\sigma_Y\\\\[6pt]\n            \\mu_Y&=46-0.9486\\sigma_Y\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Using the G.D.C.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\sigma_Y&=3.899...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=3.90\\\\[12pt]\n        \\mu_Y&=42.30...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=42.3\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n4.  1.  The probability of using a sample of _Alloy Y_ is $54.67\\%$. Using $\\mu_Y$ and $\\sigma_Y$ from part (c)(ii) we can represent this on a normal distribution curve.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1289-a5.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1289-a5\">\n\n        $\\textcolor{red}{s}$ is the length between the boundaries of the shaded region and the mean.\n\n        Notice that the region is centred around $\\mu_Y(=42.3)$. With the lower boundary of the region being $42.3\\,\\textcolor{red}{-\\,s}$ and the upper boundary being $42.3\\,\\textcolor{red}{+\\,s}$\n\n        By subtracting from $1$ and dividing by $2$ we can find the area of the lower tail of the distribution.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        1 - 0.5467&=0.4533\\\\[6pt]\n        \\frac{0.4533}{2}&=0.22665\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1289-a6.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1289-a6\">\n\n        Now we can use the calculator to find the $Y-$value which is the lower boundary of the shaded region.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        y &= \\text{invNorm}(0.22665,42.30...,3.899...) \\\\[6pt]\n        &=39.37...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        We now have the lower boundary of the region, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            42.30...-s&=39.37...\\\\[6pt]\n            s&=2.924...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $k$ is the number of standard deviations from the mean, hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            k&=\\frac{s}{\\sigma_Y}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\frac{2.924...}{3.899...}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.7499...\\\\[6pt]\n            &=0.750\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  In this part of the question we can define a new discrete random variable $S$ as the number of samples used in an experiment.\n\n        Where $S=\\{0,1...,8\\}$.\n\n        Addtionally\n        - We are given a fixed number of trials $n=8$.\n\n        - he possible outcomes of each trial are the sample is $\\textit{used}$ or $\\textit{not used}$. i.e. each trial is a Bernoulli trial.\n\n        - The probability of success, $p=0.5467$, of an individual trial is independent of any other trial - note we can assume this.\n\n        Hence the conditions have been fulfilled such that $S$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\n        Therefore we can say that\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            S& \\sim B(n,p)\\\\[6pt]\n            S &\\sim B(8,0.5467)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Where $n$ is the number of trials and $p$ is the probability of success of a single trial.\n\n        When we are asked for $\\textbf{more than}$ $5$ selected we can use the G.D.C. to calculate\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(Y>5) &=\\text{binomCdf}(8,0.5467,6,8) \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\\\\[12pt]\n        &=0.2145...\\\\[8pt]\n        &=0.215\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n5.  The probability of a usable _Alloy X_ sample is $0.8394...$ from part (a).\n\n    The probability of a usable _Alloy Y_ sample is $0.5467$ from part (d).\n\n    We can use more of _Alloy X_ than _Alloy Y_ in the following ways\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2\\text{}\\textit{ Alloy X}\\text{ and }0\\textit{ Alloy Y}\\\\[6pt]\n        2\\textit{ Alloy X}\\text{ and }1\\textit{ Alloy Y}\\\\[6pt]\n        1\\textit{ Alloy X}\\text{ and }0\\textit{ Alloy Y}\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's calculate the probability of each of these outcomes.\n\n    There is only $1$ way to choose $2$ samples of $X$ (or $Y$) or $0$ samples of $X$ (or $Y$).\n\n    Hence for $2\\textit{ Alloy X}\\text{ and }0\\textit{ Alloy Y}$ we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (0.8394...)^2\\times(1-0.5467)^2&=0.1447...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    However there are $2$ ways to choose $1$ sample of $Y$. We can either choose the first and reject the second or reject the first and choose the second. Therefore we need to multiply the probability by $2$.\n\n    Hence for $2\\textit{ Alloy X}\\text{ and }1\\textit{ Alloy Y}$ we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (0.8394)^2\\times2(1-0.5467)(0.5467)&=0.3492...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and similarly for $1\\textit{ Alloy X}\\text{ and }0\\textit{ Alloy Y}$ we have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        2(0.8394...)(1-0.8394)\\times(1-0.5467)^2&=0.05540...\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Adding them all together we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        P(\\text{More samples of }\\textit{Alloy X}\\text{ than } \\textit{Alloy Y})&=0.1447...+0.3492...+0.05540...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.5494...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=0.549\\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1335","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1335\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nAt AeroLink Dynamics, a company that develops autonomous drones, the landing time, $T$ seconds, is defined as the time it takes a drone to land after receiving a command.\n\n$T$ is normally distributed with a mean of $4.8$ seconds and a standard deviation of $0.5$ seconds.\n\n1.    Find the percentage of drones that have a landing time of more than $5$ seconds. [2 marks]\n\nThe company classifies drones as _elite_ if their landing time is between $4.1$ and $4.4$ seconds.\n\n2.    Find the probability a randomly chosen drone is classified as _elite_. [2 marks]\n\nThe company sells the drones in boxes. Each box contains $5$ drones.\n\n3.    Find the probability a randomly chosen box contains exactly $1$ _elite_ drone. [2 marks]\n\n4.    If $20$ of these boxes are chosen, find the probability that at least half of them contain exactly $1$ _elite_ drone. [2 marks]\n\nIt is known that $\\text{P}(a<T<b)=0.6881$, and that $2\\text{P}(T<a)=5\\text{P}(T>b)$ where $a$ and $b$ are landing times.\n\nA drone which takes more than $b\\,$ seconds to land is classified as faulty.\n\n5.    Find the minimum landing time for a drone to be classified as faulty. [4 marks]\n\nAeroLink Dynamics is about to release a new drone. The new landing time, $S\\,$seconds, is normally distributed with a mean of $3.3$ seconds and a standard deviation of $k$ seconds. The company finds the interquartile range to be $0.3818$ seconds.\n\n6.    Find the value of $k$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. To begin with, we can define the continuous random variable using the parameters given\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    T \\sim N(4.8,0.5^2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We have been asked to find $\\mathrm{P}(T>5)$. This is represented by the following shaded region\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1335-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1335-a1\">\n\n    Hence, using a G.D.C., we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(T>5)&=\\text{normalCDF}(5, \\infty, 4.8, 0.5)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.3445...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=34.5\\%\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Note, your answer must be written as a percentage.\n\n2. We are required to find $\\mathrm{P}(4.1<T<4.4)$ which is the enclosed shaded region shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1335-a2.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1335-a2\">\n\n    Using a calculator, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(4.1<T<4.4)&=\\text{normalCDF}(4.1, 4.4, 4.8, 0.5)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.1310...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.131\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. In this part of the question we can define a new discrete random variable $E$ as the number of _elite_ drones in a box.\n\n    Where $E=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}$.\n\n    Additionally\n\n    - We are told there is a fixed number of drones (trials) in a box, $n=5$.\n\n    - The outcome of each trial is either the drone is an _elite_ drone or is not an _elite_ drone. i.e. each trial is a Bernoulli trial.\n\n    - The probability of success, $p=0.1310...$, of an individual trial, i.e. a drone being _elite_, is independent of any other trial and a fixed value- note we can assume this.\n\n    Hence the conditions have been fulfilled such that $E$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\n    Therefore we can say that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    E& \\sim B(n,p)\\\\[6pt]\n    E &\\sim B(5,0.1310...)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{P}(E=1)&=\\text{binomPDF(5,0.1310...,1)}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.3736...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.374\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4. In this question we are dealing with a binomial distribution but the random variable and the parameters have changed.\n\n    Let the new discrete random variable $D$ be the number of boxes that contain exactly $1$ _elite_ drone.\n\n    Where $D=\\{0,1,2,....19,20\\}$\n\n    - We are now considering $20$ boxes therefore $n=20$.\n\n    - The outcome of each trial is either the box contains $1$ _elite_ drone or it does not contain $1$ _elite_ drone i.e. each trial is still a Bernoulli trial.\n\n    - The probability of success is now $p=0.3736...$, this is the probability the box contains exactly $1$ _elite_ drone and it is a fixed value and independent.\n\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    D &\\sim B(20,0.3736...)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{P}(D \\geq 10)&=\\text{binomCDF(20,0.3736...,10,20)}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.1737...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.174\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5. The following rough sketch shows the normal distribution broken into $3$ regions\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1335-a3.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1335-a3\">\n\n    with $\\text{P}(T<a)=A$, $\\text{P}(a<T<b)=0.6881$ and $\\text{P}(T>b)=B$.\n\n    If $T>b$, then the drone is classified as faulty. Hence we need to find the value of $b$, which is the critical value for region $B$.\n\n    To do this we use the information given to attempt to find the value of $A$ and $B$.\n\n    Hence, we can form the following equations\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textcolor{black}{\\textbf{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    2A&=5B &A+B&=1-0.6881\\\\[6pt]\n    & &A+B&=0.3119\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using a G.D.C. to solve them simultaneously we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A&=0.2227...&B&=0.08911...\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we can say that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(T>b)&=0.08911...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To find $b$ we must use the inverse normal distribution with the parameter values $\\mu=4.8$ and $\\sigma=0.5$.\n\n    However, as region $B$ is the upper tail then we use the area $1-0.08911...=0.9108...$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    b&=\\text{inverseNorm}(0.9108..., 4.8, 0.5)\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=5.473...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=5.47\\,\\text{seconds}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n6. The continuous random variable $S$ is normally distributed such that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    S \\sim N(3.3, k^2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To help us find $k$ we can first use the information given and show the four quartiles on a sketch of the normal distribution curve for $S$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/AA1335-a4.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1335-a4\">\n\n    The area of each region is $0.25$ and the I.Q.R. is given by $Q_3-Q_1$.\n\n    Hence, due to this symmetry we can say that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3.3-Q_1&=\\frac{\\text{IQR}}{2}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the information given we can find the value of $Q_1$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    3.3-Q_1&=\\frac{0.3818}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    Q_1&=3.1091\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore we can say that\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(S<Q_1)&=0.25\\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(S<3.1091)&=0.25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can't directly use the inverse normal distribution as we don't know the value of $\\sigma_S$.\n\n    However, we can find the associated $Z-$value of the lower quartile.\n\n    Recall, that is the value of $Z$ which is the lower quartile, $z_1$, of a standard normal distribution.\n    If\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    Z \\sim N(0,1^2)\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    then\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(Z<z_1)&=0.25\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    and using the inverse normal distribution we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_1&=\\text{inverseNorm}(0.25,0,1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=-0.6744...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can transform this into $Q_1$ using the formula for the standardised normal variable\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    Z&=\\frac{X-\\mu}{\\sigma}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    to get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    z_1&=\\frac{Q_1-3.3}{\\sigma}\\\\[6pt]\n    -0.6744...&=\\frac{3.1091-3.3}{k}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Solving for $k$ we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k&=0.2830...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.283\\,\\text{seconds}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA370","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA370\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIt is given that three in ten men take less than $220$ minutes to run a\nmarathon. It is also known that one in five men\ntake more than $274$ minutes to run the same\nmarathon.\n\nAssume that the time taken, in minutes, for men to run a marathon is\nmodelled by a normal distribution.\n\nFind the mean and standard deviation.","markScheme":"Let $X$ be the time taken, in minutes, for a man to run the marathon.\n\nWe have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ and $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(X < 220) &= 0.3 \\\\[11pt]\n\\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{220-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.3 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{220-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.3) \\hspace{0.25em} \\\\[7pt]\n\\dfrac{220-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx -0.524 \\\\[11pt]\n\\mu - 0.524\\sigma &\\approx 220\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(X > 274) &= 0.2 \\\\[11pt]\n\\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z > \\dfrac{274-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.2 \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{274-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.8) \\hspace{0.25em} \\\\[7pt]\n\\dfrac{274-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx 0.842 \\\\[11pt]\n\\mu + 0.842\\sigma &\\approx 274  \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence,\nsolving the system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $\\mu$ and\n$\\sigma$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{10.4em} \\mu \\approx 241 \\text{ min}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}\\sigma \\approx 39.5 \\text{ min} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA371","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA371\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nDomino Sugar Bags are packaged by machines. The weights $X$, in grams,\nof bags of sugar can be modelled by a\nnormal distribution where $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$. A\nbag of sugar is considered to be\nunderweight if it weights less than $4480$ grams.\n\n1.  Given that $\\mu = 4536$ and $\\sigma = 25$, find the probability that\n    a randomly selected bag of sugar\n    is underweight. [2 marks]\n\nThe manufacturer makes the decision that the probability that a bag of\nsugar is underweight should be\n$0.001$. To do this $\\mu$ is increased and $\\sigma$ is kept unchanged.\n\n2.  Calculate the new value of $\\mu$, rounding your answer to\n    the nearest gram. [3 marks]\n\nThe manufacturer is happy with the decision that the probability that a\nbag of sugar is underweight should be\n$0.001,$ but is unhappy with the way in which\nthis was achieved. The machines are\nnow adjusted to reduce $\\sigma$ and return $\\mu$ back\nto $4536$ grams.\n\n3.  Calculate the new value of $\\sigma$, rounding your answer\n    to the nearest gram. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(4536,25^2)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(X < 4480) &= \\text{normCdf}(-\\infty, 4480, 4536, 25) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0125\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(\\mu,25^2)$ and $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X < 4480) &= 0.001 \\hspace{6.6em} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{4480-\\mu}{25}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.001 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{4480 - \\mu}{25} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.001) \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{4480 - \\mu}{25} &\\approx -3.09 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{14.3em} \\mu \\approx 4557 \\text{ grams} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$\n\n3.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(4536,\\sigma^2)$ and\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X < 4480) &= 0.001 \\hspace{8em} \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{4480 - 4536}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.001 \\\\[6pt]\n    -\\dfrac{56}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.001) \\\\[8pt]\n    -\\dfrac{56}{\\sigma} & \\approx -3.09 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{13.3em} \\sigma \\approx 18 \\text{ grams} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA372","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA372\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe battery lifetime, in hours, of a new model of a smartphone is\nmodelled by a normal distribution with mean $\\mu$\nand standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\n1.  Given that $12\\%$ of the smartphones have battery lifetime of more\n    than $18$ hours and $21\\%$ of the smartphones\n    have battery lifetime of less than $13$ hours,\n    calculate the value of $\\mu$ and the value of $\\sigma$, giving your\n    answers to two significant figures.\n    [6 marks]\n\n2.  A random sample of twenty smartphones of this new model is\n    selected. Let $X$ denote the\n    number of smartphones in the sample whose\n    batteries last for more than $18$ hours.\n\n    1.  Calculate $\\mathrm{E}[X]$.\n    2.  Calculate the probability that exactly five of these\n        smartphones have battery lifetime of more\n        than $18$ hours.\n\n    3.  Determine the most likely value of $X$. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $T$ be the battery lifetime, in hours, of a smartphone of the\n    new model.\n\n    We have $T \\sim \\mathrm{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ and\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(T < 13) &= 0.21 \\\\[11pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{13-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.21 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\dfrac{13-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.21) \\\\[7pt]\n    \\dfrac{13-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx -0.806 \\\\[11pt]\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mu - 0.806\\sigma &\\approx 13 \\hspace{5em}{(1)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    and\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(T > 18) &= 0.12 \\\\[11pt]\n    \\hspace{0.6em} \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z > \\dfrac{18-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.12 \\\\[5pt]\n    \\dfrac{18-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.88) \\\\[7pt]\n    \\dfrac{18-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx 1.175 \\\\[11pt]\n    \\mu + 1.175\\sigma &\\approx 18 \\hspace{5em}{(2)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence,\n    solving the system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $\\mu$ and\n    $\\sigma$, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{8em} \\mu \\approx 15 \\text{ hours}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}\\sigma \\approx 2.5 \\text{ hours} \\hspace{2.5em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Using the formula for the expected value of\n        $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,0.12)$, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (20)(0.12) \\hspace{4.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 2.4\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 5) &= \\text{binomPdf}(20,0.12,5) \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.0567\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 0) &= \\text{binomPdf}(20,0.12,0) \\hspace{1.3em} \\\\[3pt]\n        &\\approx 0.0776 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 1) &= \\text{binomPdf}(20,0.12,1) \\\\[3pt]\n        &\\approx 0.212 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 2) &= \\text{binomPdf}(20,0.12,2) \\\\[3pt]\n        &\\approx 0.274 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(X = 3) &= \\text{binomPdf}(20,0.12,3) \\\\[3pt]\n        &\\approx 0.224 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence the most likely value of\n        $X$ is  $2$"},{"ref":"AA373","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA373\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nA survey is conducted in a large technology company. It is found that\n$25\\%$ of the employees drink more than $30$ cups\nof coffee and $4\\%$ of the employees drink less\nthan $12$ cups of coffee during a five-day week. The number of cups\nof $\\text{coffee}$ consumed by the\nemployees of the company can be modelled by a\nnormal $\\text{distribution}$ with mean\n$\\mu$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\n1.\n    1.  Determine two linear equations satisfied by $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$.\n\n    2.  Find the values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$, giving your answers\n        correct to one decimal place. [6 marks]\n\n2.  Find the probability that an employee drinks more that $40$ cups of\n    coffee during a five-day week. [2 marks]\n\nOn Monday mornings, there are team meetings held by project managers.\n\n3.  There are $12$ employees attending a particular meeting.\n    1.  Find the probability that at least three of the employees drink\n        more than $40$ cups of coffee during a\n        five-day week.\n\n    2.  Given that at least one of the employees drinks more than $40$\n        cups of coffee, find the probability that\n        there are fewer than\n        three $\\text{employees}$ drinking\n        more than $40$ cups of coffee during a five-day week.\n        [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $X$ be the number of coffee cups consumed by an employee of the\n    company.\n\n    1.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ and\n        $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X < 12) &= 0.04 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{12-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.04 \\\\[4pt]\n        \\dfrac{12-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.04) \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{12-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx -1.751 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\mu - 1.751\\sigma \\approx 12 \\hspace{4.6em}\n        $$\n        and\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X > 30) &= 0.25 \\\\[10pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z > \\dfrac{30-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.25 \\\\[4pt]\n        \\dfrac{30-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.75) \\hspace{1.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{30-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx 0.674 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\mu + 0.674\\sigma \\approx 30  \\hspace{4.6em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Solving the system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $\\mu$ and\n        $\\sigma$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{5.6em} \\mu \\approx 25.0 \\text{ cups}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}\\sigma \\approx 7.4 \\text{ cups} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(25.0,7.4^2)$\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.3em} \\mathrm{P}(X > 40) &= \\text{normCdf}(40, \\infty, 25.0, 7.4) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0213\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $Y$ be the number of employees attending the meeting who drink\n    more than $40$ cups of coffee. We have\n    $Y \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sim \\mathrm{B}(12,0.0213)$.$\\\\[12pt]$\n\n    1.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.25em} \\mathrm{P}(Y\\geq3) &= \\text{binomCdf}(12,0.0213,3,12) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.00184\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence, using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(Y < 3 \\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} Y \\geq 1) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(Y < 3  \\hspace{0.1em}\\cap\\hspace{0.1em} Y \\geq 1)}{\\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 1)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(Y = 1 \\hspace{0.1em}\\cup\\hspace{0.1em} Y = 2)}{\\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 1)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(Y = 1) + \\mathrm{P}(Y = 2)}{1 - \\mathrm{P}(Y = 0)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\text{binomPdf}(12,0.0213,1) + \\text{binomPdf}(12,0.0213,2)}{1 - \\text{binomPdf}(12,0.0213,0)} \\hspace{0.5em} \\\\[11pt]\n        &\\approx 0.992\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA381","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA381\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe heights $X$, in metres, of trees in a forest are normally\ndistributed with a mean of $56.7$ m and a\nstandard deviation of $18.2$ m.\n\n1.  A tree is selected at random. Find the probability that the tree is:\n    1.  between $65$ m and\n        $85$ m;\n\n    2.  at least $70$ m. [3 marks]\n\nThe tallest $10\\%$ of trees are considered giant trees and the smallest\n$15\\%$ of trees are considered small trees.\n\n2.\n    1.  Calculate the minimum height for a tree to be considered giant.\n\n    2.  Calculate the maximum height for a tree to be considered small.\n        [4 marks]\n\nFour trees are selected at random.\n\n3.  Find the probability that two trees are giant and the other\n    two are small. [4 marks]\n\nConsider the case where twenty trees are selected at random.\n\n4.  Find the probability that:\n    1.  no trees are giant;\n\n    2.  at least three trees are giant. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(56.7,18.2^2)$.\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} \\mathrm{P}(65 \\leq X \\leq 85) &= \\text{normCdf}(65,85,56.7,18.2) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.264\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.3em} \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 70) &= \\text{normCdf}(70,\\infty,56.7,18.2) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.232\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Let $h_\\text{min}$ be the minimum height for a tree to be\n        considered giant.\n\n        Hence we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq h_{\\text{min}}) &= 0.1 \\\\[6pt]\n        h_{\\text{min}} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.9, 56.7,18.2) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        h_{\\text{min}} \\approx 80.0 \\text{ m} \\hspace{4.55em}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Let $h_\\text{max}$ be the maximum height for a tree to be\n        considered small.\n\n        Hence we have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq h_{\\text{max}}) &= 0.15 \\\\[6pt]\n        h_{\\text{max}} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.15, 56.7,18.2) \\hspace{0.55em} \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        h_{\\text{max}} \\approx 37.8 \\text{ m} \\hspace{4.7em}\n        $$\n\n3.  The possible ways to choose two giant trees and two small trees are:\n\n    1\\. GGSS;\n\n    2\\. GSGS;\n\n    3\\. GSSG;\n\n    4\\. SGSG;\n\n    5\\. SSGG;\n\n    6\\. SGGS.\n\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{$2$ giant \\& $2$ small}) &= 6\\big[0.1^2 \\times0.15^2\\big] \\hspace{3.7em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.00135\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $Y$ be the number of giant trees among the twenty selected\n    trees.\n\n    We have $Y \\hspace{-0.1em}\\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,0.1)$.\n    1.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(Y = 0) &= \\text{binomPdf}(20,0.1,0) \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.122\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 3) &= \\text{binomCdf}(20,0.1,3,20) \\hspace{0.5em}  \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.323\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA385","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA385\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nFarmer Hitoshi grows Kamo eggplants and the weights of his eggplants\nare normally distributed with a mean of\n$284$ g and standard deviation of\n$66$ g.\n\n1.\n    1.  Calculate the percentage of Hitoshi's eggplants that weigh\n        between $230$ g\n        and $320$ g.\n\n    2.  Hitoshi has $150$ eggplants to take to market. Find the\n        expected $\\text{number}$ of\n        eggplants weighing more than $320$ g.\n        Give your answer correct to the nearest\n        integer.\n\n    3.  Find the probability that at least $45$ of the $150$ eggplants\n        weigh more than\n        $320$ g. [6 marks]\n\nFarmer Antonio grows Barbarella eggplants and the weights of his\neggplants are normally distributed with a mean\nof $402$ g. Antonio only takes to\nmarket eggplants that weigh more than\n$320$ g. Over a period of time, Antonio finds\nthat he has to reject $1$ in $12$ eggplants due to them being\nunderweight.\n\n2.\n    1.  Find the standard deviation of the weights of Antonio's\n        eggplants.\n\n    2.  Antonio has $250$ eggplants to take to the market. Find the\n        expected $\\text{number}$ of\n        eggplants weighing more than $410$ g.\n        Give your answer correct to the nearest\n        integer. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  Let $X$ be the weights, in grams, of Hitoshi's eggplants.\n        We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(284, 66^2)$\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(230 \\leq X \\leq 320) &= \\text{normCdf}(230, 320, 284, 66) \\hspace{2em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.501 \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 50.1\\%\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{0.3em} \\mathrm{P}(X > 320) &= \\text{normCdf}(320, \\infty, 284, 66) \\\\[5pt]\n        &\\approx 0.293\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Let $S$ be the number of eggplants\n        for sale weighing more than $320$ g.\n\n        We have $S\\hspace{-0.1em} \\sim \\mathrm{B}(150, 0.293)$.\n        Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $S$ we\n        obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[S] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (150)(0.293) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 44 \\text{ eggplants} \\hspace{2.1em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    3.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2.6em} \\mathrm{P}(S \\geq 45) &= \\text{binomCdf}(150,0.293,45,150) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.456\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.\n    1.  Let $Y$ be the weights, in grams, of Antonio's eggplants.\n        We have $Y \\sim \\mathrm{N}(402,\\sigma^2)$ and\n        $Z \\sim  \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(Y \\leq 320) &= \\dfrac{1}{12} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z \\leq \\dfrac{320 - 402}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= \\dfrac{1}{12} \\\\[6pt]\n        -\\dfrac{82}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(1/12) \\hspace{5.4em} \\\\[6pt]\n        -\\dfrac{82}{\\sigma} &\\approx -1.383 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{9.2em} \\sigma \\approx 59.3 \\text{ g} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have $Y \\sim \\mathrm{N}(402,59.3^2)$.\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(Y > 410 \\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} Y > 320) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(Y > 410) }{\\mathrm{P}(Y > 320)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\text{normCdf}(410, \\infty, 402, 59.3)}{\\text{normCdf}(320, \\infty, 402, 59.3)}  \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx \\dfrac{0.4463}{0.9166} \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\approx 0.487\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        Let $T$ be the number of eggplants for sale weighing more than\n        $410$ g.\n\n        We have $T \\sim \\mathrm{B}(250,0.487)$.\n        Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $T$ we find\n\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[T] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (250)(0.487) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 122 \\text{ eggplants} \\hspace{1.5em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA388","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA388\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe heights $X$, in metres, of mature dwarf fruit trees in a tree garden\ncan be modelled by a normal distribution.\n\nIt is given that $1$% of the dwarf trees are shorter than\n$2.46$ m and $10$% are\ntaller than $2.91$ m.\n\nFind the mean and standard deviation of the heights of the dwarf trees.","markScheme":"We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ and $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(X < 2.46) &= 0.01 \\\\[12pt]\n\\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{2.46-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.01 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{2.46-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.01) \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{2.46-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx -2.326 \\\\[12pt]\n\\mu - 2.326\\sigma &\\approx 2.46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(X > 2.91) &= 0.1 \\\\[12pt]\n\\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.10em}\\bigg(Z > \\dfrac{2.91-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.1 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{2.91-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.9) \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{2.91-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx 1.282 \\\\[12pt]\n\\mu + 1.282\\sigma &\\approx 2.91\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, solving\nthe system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$, we\nobtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{11em} \\mu \\approx 2.75 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}\\sigma \\approx 0.125 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA391","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA391\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe heights of giraffes in a savanna can be modelled by a normal\ndistribution with mean $5.2$ metres and standard\ndeviation $\\sqrt{0.1}$ metres.\n\n1.  Find the probability that a giraffe selected at random is at least\n    $5$ metres tall.[2 marks]\n\nTwelve giraffes are independently selected at random and their heights\nrecorded.\n\n2.  Find the probability that more than nine of these giraffes\n    are at least $5$ metres tall.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $H$ be the height, in metres, of a giraffe in the savanna.\n    We have $H \\sim \\mathrm{N}(5.2, 0.1)$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.5em} \\mathrm{P}(H \\geq 5) &= \\text{normCdf}(5, \\infty, 5.2, \\sqrt{0.1}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.736\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $X$ be the number of selected giraffes whose height is at least\n    $5$ metres.\n\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(12, 0.736)$.\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} \\mathrm{P}(X > 9) &= \\text{binomCdf}(12, 0.736, 10, 12) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.349\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA397","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA397\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet the random variable $X$ be normally distributed with mean $16$, as\nshown in the following diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/ffdc26c8f7b3d1f4ea14a7214377676d556d4b31.svg\" alt=\"ffdc26c8f7b3d1f4ea14a7214377676d556d4b31.svg\">\n\nThe shaded region between $16$ and $20$ represents $35$% of the\ndistribution.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(X>20)$. [2 marks]\n\n2.  Find the standard deviation of $X$. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.4em} \\mathrm{P}(X > 20) &= \\mathrm{P}(X > 16) - \\mathrm{P}(16 < X \\leq 20) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.5 - 0.35 \\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.15\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(16,\\sigma^2)$ and\n    $Z  \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 20) &= 1 - \\mathrm{P}(X > 20) \\hspace{1.25em} \\\\[18pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 20) &= 1 - 0.15 \\\\[11pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z \\leq \\dfrac{20-16}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.85 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{4}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.85) \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{4}{\\sigma} &\\approx 1.036 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{1.05em} \\sigma \\approx 3.86\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA403","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA403\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA health study on body fat percentage is conducted on adult male\nparticipants in two dietary groups. The\nparticipants in Group A consume less than $52$\ngrams of sugar daily. Their body fat percentages\nare normally distributed with mean $15$% and\nstandard deviation $3$%.\n\n1.  A person is selected at random from Group A. Find the probability\n    that their body fat percentage is greater\n    than $20$%. [2 marks]\n\nThe participants in Group B consume more than $52$ grams of sugar daily.\nTheir body fat percentages are normally\ndistributed with mean $24$% and standard\ndeviation $\\sigma\\hspace{0.05em}\\%$.\n\n2.  The probability that the body fat percentage of a person in\n    Group B is less than $20$% is $0.0548$, find\n    the value of $\\sigma$. [4 marks]\n\nIn the study, there are $160$ participants in Group A and $80$\nparticipants in Group B.\n\n3.  A randomly selected participant has a body fat percentage\n    greater than $20$%. Find the probability that\n    the participant is in Group A. [6 marks]\n\n4.  Twelve of the participants with body fat percentages\n    greater than $20$% are selected at random.\n    Find the probability that at least two of them\n    are in Group A. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $X$ be the body fat percentage of a participant in Group A.\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(15,3^2)$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.1em} \\mathrm{P}(X > 20) &= \\text{normCdf}(20,\\infty,15,3) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0478\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  Let $Y$ be the body fat percentage of a participant in Group B.\n    We have $Y\\hspace{-0.1em} \\sim \\mathrm{N}(24,\\sigma^2)$ and\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$. Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(Y < 20) &= 0.0548 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{20-24}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.0548 \\\\[6pt]\n    -\\dfrac{4}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.0548) \\hspace{2em} \\\\[8pt]\n    -\\dfrac{4}{\\sigma} &= -1.6 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\sigma = 2.5\\% \\hspace{0.3em}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{A} \\hspace{0.15em} | \\hspace{0.15em} \\text{BFP} > 20) &=  \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{A})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X > 20)}{\\mathrm{P}(\\text{A})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(X > 20) + \\mathrm{P}(\\text{B})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(Y > 20)} \\\\[16pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\raisebox{0.65em}{$\\dfrac{160}{240}\\times0.0478$}}{\\raisebox{-0.75em}{$\\dfrac{160}{240}\\times0.0478 + \\dfrac{80}{240}\\times(1 - 0.0548)$}} \\\\[16pt]\n    &\\, \\\\\n    &\\approx 0.0919\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $S$ be the number of selected participants that are in Group A.\n    We have $S \\sim \\mathrm{B}(12,0.0919)$. Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(S \\geq 2) &= \\text{binomCdf}(12,0.0919,2,12) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.304\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA407","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA407\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe random variable $X$ is normally distributed with a mean of $90$. The\nfollowing diagram shows the normal curve for $X$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/970ac43c386b584507ca57023f99c8602810fc8b.svg\" alt=\"970ac43c386b584507ca57023f99c8602810fc8b.svg\">\n\nLet $R$ be the shaded region under the curve, to the left of $84$. The\narea of $R$ is $0.22$.\n\n1.  Write down $\\mathrm{P}(X < 84)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(84 < X < 90)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(84 < X < 96)$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\mathrm{P}(X < 84) = 0.22$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(84 < X < 90) &= \\mathrm{P}(X < 90) - \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 84) \\hspace{0.9em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.5 - 0.22 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.28\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the symmetry of the normal curve for $X$, we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(84 < X < 96) &= 2\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(84 < X < 90) \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 2\\times0.28 \\\\[6pt]\n    &= 0.56\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA411","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA411\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nThe mass $M$, in grams, of oranges is normally distributed with mean\n$\\mu$. The table below shows probabilities for values of $M$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/9c13f40db1a4c17af000f6c0b716cb019a80d38c.svg\" alt=\"9c13f40db1a4c17af000f6c0b716cb019a80d38c.svg\">\n\n1.\n    1.  Write down the value of $k$.\n\n    2.  Show that $\\mu = 165$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(M < 120)$. [5 marks]\n\nThe oranges are packed in boxes of fifteen.\n\nAny orange with a mass less than $120$ grams is classified as small.\n\n3.  Find the probability that a box of oranges selected at\n    random contains at most two small oranges.\n    [3 marks]\n\n4.  A wooden crate contains $20$ boxes of oranges. A crate is\n    selected at random.\n\n    1.  Find the expected number of boxes in this crate that contain at\n        most two small oranges.\n\n    2.  Find the probability that at least $18$ boxes in this crate\n        contain at most two small oranges. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        $k = 0.02$\n\n    2.  We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(M < 110) &= 0.02 \\hspace{3em} \\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(M > 220) &= 0.02\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        Hence, using the\n        symmetry of the normal curve for $M$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mu &= \\dfrac{110 + 220}{2} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 165 \\text{ grams} \\hspace{3em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  We have $M \\sim \\mathrm{N}(165,\\sigma^2)$ and\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(M < 110) &= 0.02 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\hspace{0.7em} \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{110-165}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.02 \\\\[6pt]\n    -\\dfrac{55}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.02) \\\\[8pt]\n    -\\dfrac{55}{\\sigma} &\\approx -2.05375 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\sigma &\\approx 26.7803 \\text{ grams}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence we find\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3em} \\mathrm{P}(M < 120) &= \\text{normCdf}(-\\infty,120,165,26.7803) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0464\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $X$ be the number of small oranges in the selected box of\n    fifteen.\n\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(15,0.0464)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.2em} \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq 2) &= \\text{binomCdf}(15,0.0464,0,2) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.970\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $Y$ be the number of boxes in the selected crate containing at\n    most two small oranges. We have\n    $Y\\hspace{-0.1em} \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,0.970)$.\n    1.  Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $Y$, we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[Y] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &=(20)(0.970) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 19.4 \\text{ boxes} \\hspace{3.6em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1em} \\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 18) &= \\text{binomCdf}(20,0.970,18,20) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.979\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA422","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA422\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA random variable $X$ is normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a\nstandard deviation of $10$.\n\n1.  On the following diagram, shade in the region representing\n    $\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 45)$. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/8f5acb14ecf70c9d048e4418c3f5098c2ce5e6cd.svg\" alt=\"8f5acb14ecf70c9d048e4418c3f5098c2ce5e6cd.svg\">\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 45)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Given that $\\mathrm{P}(X \\leq k) = 0.8$, find the value of $k$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  If we shade in the region representing $\\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 45)$, we\n    get\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/809f632e67f6c7263358cdcb66497105f451b0d4.svg\" alt=\"809f632e67f6c7263358cdcb66497105f451b0d4.svg\">\n\n2.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(50,10^2)$.\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 45) &= \\text{normCdf}(45,\\infty,50,10) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.691\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(50,10^2)$.\n    Hence we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq k) &= 0.8 \\\\[6pt]\n    k &= \\text{invNorm}(0.8, 50,10) \\hspace{0.8em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    k \\approx 58.4 \\hspace{4.1em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA432","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA432\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA random variable $X$ is normally distributed with $\\mu = 120$ and\n$\\sigma = 15$.\n\nFind the interquartile range of $X$.","markScheme":"If we draw the normal curve for $X$, we have\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/8a52d7a99181e065b38d9ab0218832e1b3b3402d.svg\" alt=\"8a52d7a99181e065b38d9ab0218832e1b3b3402d.svg\">\n\nHence, using $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(120,15^2)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_1 &= \\text{invNorm}(0.25,120,15) \\approx 109.9 \\\\[6pt]\nq_3 &= \\text{invNorm}(0.75,120,15) \\approx 130.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence we\nobtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} \\text{IQR} &= q_3 - q_1 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 130.1 - 109.9 \\\\[6pt]\n&= 20.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA438","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA438\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe time taken for a student to complete an exam is normally\ndistributed with a mean of $40$ minutes and a\nstandard deviation of $5.5$ minutes.\n\n1.  A student is selected at random. Find the probability that the\n    student competes the task in less than $48$\n    minutes. [3 marks]\n\n2.  The probability a student takes between $k$ and $48$ minutes is\n    $0.4$. Find the value of $k$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $X$ be the time taken for a student to complete the exam.\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(40,5.5^2)$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X < 48) &= \\mathrm{normCdf}(-\\infty,48,40,5.5) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.927\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  If we draw the normal curve for $X$, we have\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/0e9a12c9a3ca8ecdd2cee616c2d8ec39077026f9.svg\" alt=\"0e9a12c9a3ca8ecdd2cee616c2d8ec39077026f9.svg\">\n\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.85em} \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq k) &= \\mathrm{P}(X < 48) - \\mathrm{P}(k < X < 48) \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq k) &= 0.927 - 0.4 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq k) &= 0.527 \\\\[6pt]\n    k &= \\text{invNorm}(0.527,40,5.5) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    k \\approx 40.4 \\hspace{5.2em}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA440","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA440\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe weights $W$, in grams, of tea bags are normally distributed with a\nmean of $3.5$ grams and a standard\ndeviation of $0.53$ grams. A tea bag is\nconsidered small if it weights less\nthan $w$ grams.\n\n1.  Given that $5.2$ % of tea bags are small,\n    find $w$. [3 marks]\n\n2.  A selected tea bag is small. Find the probability that it weighs at\n    least $2.25$ grams. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $W \\sim \\mathrm{N}(3.5, 0.53^2)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(W < w) &= 0.052 \\\\[6pt]\n    w &= \\text{invNorm}(0.052,3.5,0.53) \\hspace{0.2em} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    w \\approx 2.64 \\text{ grams} \\hspace{2.4em}\n    $$\n\n2.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(W \\geq 2.25 \\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} W < 2.6383...) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(2.25 \\leq W < 2.6383...)}{\\mathrm{P}(W < 2.6383...)} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\text{normCdf}(2.25,2.6383...,3.5,0.53)}{0.052} \\hspace{2em} \\\\[8pt]\n    &\\approx \\dfrac{0.0428252...}{0.052} \\\\[13pt]\n    &\\approx 0.824\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA442","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA442\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nThe weights $W$, in grams, of frogs in an enclosure are normally\ndistributed with a mean of $230$ grams and a standard deviation of $15$\ngrams.\n\nA frog that weights less than $210$ grams is considered as a small frog.\n\nA frog is selected at random.\n\n1.  Find the probability that this frog is small. [3 marks]\n\nA frog that weights more than $250$ grams is considered as a big frog.\n\nAnother frog is selected at random.\n\n2.  Find the probability that the frog is neither small nor\n    big. [3 marks]\n\nFrogs that aren't small or big are considered as standard frogs.\n\nA sample of $450$ frogs are selected.\n\n3.  Find the expected number of standard frogs in this sample.\n    [3 marks]\n\nAnother sample of $N$ frogs are selected.\n\n4.  Find the minimum value for $N$ such that the expected\n    number of standard frogs in the sample is $100$.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $W \\sim \\mathrm{N}(230,15^2)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{2.7em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{small}) &= \\mathrm{P}(W < 210) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\text{normCdf}(-\\infty,210,230,15) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0912\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have $W \\sim \\mathrm{N}(230,15^2)$.\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.6em} \\mathrm{P}(\\text{not small \\& not big}) &= \\mathrm{P}(210 \\leq W \\leq 250) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\text{normCdf}(210,250,230,15) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.818\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $X$ be the number of standard frogs in the sample of $450$\n    frogs.\n\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(450,0.818)$.\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{0.1em} \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (450)(0.818) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 368 \\text{ frogs}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $Y$ be the number of standard frogs in the sample of $N$ frogs.\n    We have $Y \\sim \\mathrm{B}(N,0.818)$.\n\n    Hence, solving the equation $\\mathrm{E}[Y] = 100$ for $N$, we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{6.1em} (N)(0.818) &= 100 \\\\[6pt]\n    N &\\approx 122.249 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence we find\n     $N_{\\text{min}} = 123$"},{"ref":"AA443","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA443\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA garden bed has a large number of flowers. The heights of the flowers\nare $\\text{normally}$ distributed with a mean of\n$43$ cm and a standard deviation of\n$6$ cm. A flower is classified as tall if it is\ntaller than $48$ cm.\n\n1.  A flower is selected at random.\n    1.  Find the probability that this flower is tall.\n\n    2.  Given that the flower is tall, find the probability it is taller\n        than $55$ cm.[6 marks]\n\n2.  Two flowers are selected at random. Find the probability they are\n    both tall.[3 marks]\n\n3.  Five hundred flowers are selected at random.\n    1.  Find the probability that at least $100$ of these $500$ flowers\n        are tall.\n\n    2.  Find the expected number of tall flowers among these $500$\n        flowers. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $H$ be the height, in centimetres, of a flower in the garden\n    bed.\n\n    We have $H \\sim \\mathrm{N}(43,6^2)$.\n\n    1.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(\\text{tall flower}) &= \\mathrm{P}(H  > 48) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\text{normCdf}(48,\\infty,43,6) \\hspace{1.65em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.202\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(H > 55\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em}\\text{tall flower}) &= \\mathrm{P}(H > 55\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} H > 48) \\\\[8pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(H > 55)}{\\mathrm{P}(H > 48)} \\\\[6pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\text{normCdf}(55,\\infty,43,6)}{\\text{normCdf}(48,\\infty,43,6)} \\hspace{5.25em} \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx \\dfrac{0.0228}{0.2023} \\\\[10pt]\n        &\\approx 0.112\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n2.  Using the independence of flowers, we find\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\text{both tall flowers}) &= \\mathrm{P}(\\text{tall flower})\\hspace{0.05em}\\mathrm{P}(\\text{tall flower}) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= (0.202)^2 \\\\[7pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0408\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Let $X$ be the number of tall flowers among the selected $500$\n    flowers.\n\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(500,0.202)$.\n    1.  Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{1.55em} \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 100) &= \\text{binomCdf}(500,0.202,100,500) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.562\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{E}[X] &= np \\\\[6pt]\n        &= (500)(0.202) \\\\[6pt]\n        &= 101 \\text{ tall flowers} \\hspace{2em}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA447","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA447\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe lengths $L$, in millimetres, of caterpillars in an enclosure are\nnormally distributed with a mean of\n$12.5$ mm and a standard deviation of\n$\\sigma$ mm.\n\n1.  Write down the probability that a randomly selected\n    caterpillar has a length greater\n    than $12.5$ mm. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Given that $6.5$% of caterpillars have a length greater than\n    $14$ mm, find:\n\n    1.  the value of $\\sigma$;\n    2.  the probability that a randomly selected caterpillar has a\n        length less than\n        $11.5$ mm. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\mathrm{P}(L > 12.5) = 0.5$\n\n2.\n    1.  We have $L \\sim \\mathrm{N}(12.5,\\sigma^2)$ and\n        $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n        Hence we get\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(L > 14) &= 0.065 \\\\[12pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z > \\dfrac{14-12.5}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.065 \\\\[6pt]\n        \\dfrac{1.5}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.935) \\hspace{4em} \\\\[8pt]\n        \\dfrac{1.5}{\\sigma} &= 1.514 \\\\\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        \\hspace{1.1em} \\sigma \\approx 0.991 \\text{ mm}\n        $$\n\n    2.  We have $L \\sim \\mathrm{N}(12.5,0.991^2)$.\n        Hence we obtain\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\hspace{2em} \\mathrm{P}(L < 11.5) &= \\text{normCdf}(-\\infty,11.5,12.5,0.991) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.156\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA449","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA449\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe time taken for a student to finish a task is normally distributed\nwith a mean $\\mu$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$. It is found that $6\\%$\nof students take less than $7$ minutes to complete the task and $75$%\ntake less than $22$ minutes.\n\nFind the value of $\\mu$ and the value of $\\sigma$.","markScheme":"Let $T$ be the time, in minutes, it takes for a student to complete the\ntask.\n\nWe have $T \\sim \\mathrm{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ and $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\nHence we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(T < 7) &= 0.06 \\\\[12pt]\n\\hspace{0.5em} \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{7-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.06 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{7-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.06) \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{7-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx -1.555 \\\\[12pt]\n\\mu - 1.555\\sigma &\\approx 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSimilarly, we\nobtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{P}(T < 22) &= 0.75 \\\\[12pt]\n\\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{22-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.75 \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{22-\\mu}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.75) \\\\[8pt]\n\\dfrac{22-\\mu}{\\sigma} &\\approx 0.674 \\\\[12pt]\n\\mu + 0.674\\sigma &\\approx 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, solving\nthe system of linear equations $(1)$-$(2)$ for $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$, we\nfind\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{11.8em} \\mu \\approx 17.5 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m}\\hspace{0.525em} \\text{ and } \\hspace{0.525em}\\sigma \\approx 6.73 \\hspace{0.25em}\\text{m} \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"},{"ref":"AA452","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA452\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA pencil manufacturer measures the length, $L$ mm, of their new batch of\npencils. The lengths of the pencils are normally distributed, where\n$L \\sim \\mathrm{N}(110,\\sigma^2)$.\n\n1.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(110 - 2\\sigma < L < 110 + \\sigma)$. [3 marks]\n\nThe probability that the length of a pencil is less than\n$116$ mm is $0.95$.\n\n2.  Find the value of $\\sigma$. [3 marks]\n\nAll pencils with length at least $k$ mm are\nclassified as large pencils.\n\n3.  A pencil is chosen at random. The probability it is a large\n    pencil is $0.6$. Find the value of $k$. [3 marks]\n\n4.\n    1.  A pencil is chosen at random from the large pencils. Find the\n        probability that the length of this pencil is less than\n        $111$ mm.\n\n    2.  Twenty pencils are chosen at random from the large pencils.\n        Find the probability that at least three\n        pencils have a length less than\n        $111$ mm. [6 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have $L \\sim \\mathrm{N}(110,\\sigma^2)$ and\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$.\n\n    Hence we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(110 - 2\\sigma < L < 110 + \\sigma) &= \\mathrm{P}(- 2\\sigma < L-110 < \\sigma) \\hspace{1em} \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\mathrm{P}(- 2 < Z < 1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &= \\text{normCdf}(-2,1,0,1) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.819\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(L < 116) &= 0.95 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z < \\dfrac{116-110}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.95 \\\\[6pt]\n    \\dfrac{6}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.95) \\hspace{4.5em} \\\\[8pt]\n    \\dfrac{6}{\\sigma} &= 1.645 \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.85em} \\sigma \\approx 3.65 \\text{ mm}\n    $$\n\n3.  We have $L \\sim \\mathrm{N}(110,3.65^2)$.\n    Hence we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{3.7em} \\mathrm{P}(L > k) &= 0.6 \\\\[6pt]\n    k &= \\text{invNorm}(0.4,110,3.65) \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    $$\n    \\hspace{0.65em} k \\approx 109 \\text{ mm}\n    $$\n\n4.\n    1.  Using the conditional probability formula, we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(L < 111\\hspace{0.15em}|\\hspace{0.15em} L > 109) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(109 < L < 111)}{\\mathrm{P}(L > 109)} \\\\[5pt]\n        &= \\dfrac{\\text{normCdf}(109,111,110,3.65)}{0.6} \\hspace{1.1em} \\\\[9pt]\n        &\\approx \\dfrac{0.2159}{0.6} \\\\[12pt]\n        &\\approx 0.360\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2.  Let $X$ be the number of pencils with length less than\n        $111$ mm among the twenty large pencils.\n        We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(20,0.360)$.\n        Hence we find\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(X \\geq 3) &= \\text{binomCdf}(20,0.360,3,20) \\\\[6pt]\n        &\\approx 0.990\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$"},{"ref":"AA695","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA695\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be normally distributed with\n$X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(135,\\sigma^2)$ and\n$Y \\sim \\mathrm{N}(105,\\sigma^2)$.\n\n1.  Find $k$ such that $\\mathrm{P}(Y>k)=\\mathrm{P}(X<k)$.[2 marks]\n\n2.  Given that $\\mathrm{P}(X>130)=0.8944$, find\n    $\\mathrm{P}(90<Y<110)$.[5 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Recognizing that $X$ and $Y$ have the same standard deviation, $k$\n    is midway between the means of $X$ and $Y$.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    k = \\dfrac{135 + 105}{2} \\hspace{1em}= 120\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(135,\\sigma^2)$ and\n    $Z \\sim \\mathrm{N}(0,1)$. Hence we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X > 130) &= 0.8944 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\hspace{-0.05em}\\bigg(Z > \\dfrac{130-135}{\\sigma}\\bigg)\\hspace{-0.125em} &= 0.8944 \\\\[6pt]\n    -\\dfrac{5}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.1056) \\hspace{4em} \\\\[8pt]\n    -\\dfrac{5}{\\sigma} &\\approx -1.25027 \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.65em}\\sigma \\approx 4\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    We have $Y \\sim \\mathrm{N}(105,4^2)$. Hence we obtain\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(90<Y<110) &= \\text{normCdf}(90,110,105,4) \\hspace{1em}\\approx 0.894\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA777","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA777\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe random variable $X$ is normally distributed with a mean of $120$.\nThe following diagram shows the normal curve for $X$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/1f917f7113a014d3ccaee4eed959663d9ba5b3cb.svg\" alt=\"1f917f7113a014d3ccaee4eed959663d9ba5b3cb.svg\">\n\nLet $R$ be the shaded region under the curve between $105$ and $135$.\nThe area of $R$ is $0.4$.\n\n1.  Write down $\\mathrm{P}(105 < X < 135)$. [1 mark]\n\n2.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(X < 135)$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Find $\\mathrm{P}(X > 105\\hspace{0.25em}|\\hspace{0.25em} X < 135)$.\n    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\mathrm{P}(105 < X < 135) = 0.4$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X < 135) &= \\mathrm{P}(X < 120) + \\dfrac{R}{2} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.5 + 0.2 \\\\[12pt]\n    &= 0.7\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the conditional probability formula, we obtain\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X > 105\\hspace{0.25em}|\\hspace{0.25em} X < 135) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(105 < X < 135)}{\\mathrm{P}(X < 135)}  \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.4}{0.7} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{4}{7}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA794","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA794\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nAli works at an amusement park on a small island in Indonesia. Ali\ndrives from his home to the ferry depature terminal and then catches the\nferry to the island. The time that it takes him, $T_1$ minutes, to drive\nfrom his home to the ferry is normally distributed with\n$T_1 \\sim \\mathrm{N}(16, 4^2)$.\n\nAli always leaves his house at $07$:$40$. The first ferry that he can\ncatch departs at $08$:$00$.\n\n1.  Find the probability that it will take Ali between $20$ to $30$\n    minutes to drive to the ferry. Round your answer to $4$ significant\n    figures. [2 marks]\n\nThe length of time, $T_2$ minutes, of the ferry journey to the island is\nnormally distributed with $T_2 \\sim \\mathrm{N}(25, \\sigma^2)$. The\nprobability that the ferry journey takes less than $30$ minutes is\n$0.9520$.\n\n2.  Find $\\sigma$. Round your answer to $1$ significant figure.\n    [3 marks]\n\n3.  Find the probability that the ferry journey takes less than\n    $20$ minutes. [2 marks]\n\nIf Ali misses the first ferry, he can take another one which departs at\n$08$:$10$. He must arrive at work by $08$:$30$ to be on time. He will\nnot make it to the island if he misses both ferries. The variables $T_1$\nand $T_2$ are independent.\n\n4.  Find the probability that Ali will arrive to work on time.\n    Round your answer to $4$ significant figures. [4 marks]\n\nThis year, Ali will go to work on $214$ days.\n\n5.  Calculate the number of days Ali is expected to arrive at\n    work on time. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(20 < T_1 < 30) &= \\text{normCdf}(20, 30, 16, 4) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.1584 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(T_2 < 30) &= 0.9520 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}\\left( Z < \\dfrac{30-25}{\\sigma}\\right) &= 0.9520 \\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{30-25}{\\sigma} &= \\text{invNorm}(0.9520) \\\\[10pt]\n    \\dfrac{5}{\\sigma} &\\approx 1.66456 \\hspace{3.25em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{0.5em}\\\\[10pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em}\\sigma \\approx 3\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  The probability that the ferry journey takes less than $20$ minutes\n    is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(T_2 < 20) &= \\text{normCdf}(0, 20, 25, 3) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0478 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  Let $F_1$ represent the event that Ali gets on the first ferry,\\\n     $F_2$ represent the event that\n    Ali gets on the second ferry,\\\n    $O$\n    represent the event that Ali\n    arrives on time.\n\n    We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(F_1) &= \\mathrm{P}(T_1 < 20) \\hspace{3em} \\\\[10pt]\n    &= \\text{normCdf}(0, 20, 16, 4) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.841313... \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\hspace{1em}\\\\[10pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(F_2) &= \\mathrm{P}(20\\leq T_1 <30) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.1584 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{found in part (a)}] \\\\[10pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(O|F_1) &= \\mathrm{P}(T_2 < 30) \\\\[10pt]\n    &= 0.9520 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{given for part (b)}] \\\\[10pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(O|F_2) &= \\mathrm{P}(T_2 < 20) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.0478 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{found in part (c)}]\\\\\n    \\intertext{Therefore, we get}\n    \\mathrm{P}(O) &=  \\mathrm{P}(F_1) \\times \\mathrm{P}(O|F_1) + \\mathrm{P}(F_2) \\times \\mathrm{P}(O|F_2) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.841313 \\times 0.9520 + 0.1584 \\times 0.0478 \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.8085 \\\\[-2cm]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  Let $X$ be the number of days Ali arrives on time.\\\n    We have $X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(214,0.8085)$.\\\n    Hence, using the formula for the expected value of $X$, we get\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{E}(X) &= np \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx (214)(0.8085) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 173\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA799","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA799\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe volumes, in mL, of olive oil bottles can be modelled by a normal\ndistribution, with mean $508$ mL and standard\ndeviation $12$ mL.\n\n1.  Find the probability that a randomly selected bottle has a volume\n    less than $500$ mL. Round your answer to $3$\n    significant figures. [2 marks]\n\n2.  The probability that a randomly selected bottle has a volume greater\n    than $v$  mL is $0.366$. Find the value of\n    $v$. [2 marks]\n\n3.  A bottle is randomly selected. Given that the bottle has a volume\n    greater than $500$ mL, find the probability\n    that it has a volume greater than $520$ mL.\n    Round your answer to $3$ significant figures. [3 marks]\n\n4.  From a random sample of $200$ bottles, determine to the nearest\n    integer the number of bottles that would be expected to have a\n    volume within $2$ standard deviations of the mean. [4 marks]\n\n5.  The bottles of olive oil are delivered to supermarkets in packs of\n    $30$. Determine the probability that at least $5$ bottles from a\n    randomly selected pack have a volume less than\n    $495$ mL. Round your answer to $3$\n    significant figures. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Let $X$ represent the volume of an olive oil bottle.\n    We are given $X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(508, 12^2)$. Hence the probability\n    that a randomly selected bottle has a volume less than\n    $500$ mL is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X < 500) &= \\text{normCdf}(0, 500, 508, 12) \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.252 \\hspace{3em} [\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{by using G.D.C.}\\hspace{0.1em}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We have\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X > v) &= 0.366 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq v) &= 1 - 0.366 \\\\[12pt]\n    \\mathrm{P}(X \\leq v) &= 0.634 \\\\[12pt]\n    v &= \\text{invNorm}(0.634, 508, 12) \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\hspace{-0.525em}v \\approx 512 \\hspace{5.52em} [\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{by using G.D.C.}\\hspace{0.1em}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3.  Using the conditional probability formula\n    $\\mathrm{P}(A|B) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}$, we\n    get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X > 520 \\hspace{0.1em}|\\hspace{0.1em} X > 500) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}\\left(\\{X > 520\\} \\cap \\{X > 500\\}\\right)}{\\mathrm{P}(X > 500)} \\hspace{1em}\\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(X > 520)}{\\mathrm{P}(X > 500)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{\\text{normCdf}(520, \\infty, 508, 12)}{\\text{normCdf}(500, \\infty, 508, 12)} \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\dfrac{0.158655}{0.747508} \\hspace{3em} [\\hspace{0.1em}\\text{by using G.D.C.}\\hspace{0.1em}]\\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.212\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n4.  The probability that the volume of a randomly selected bottle lies\n    within $2$ standard deviations of the mean is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(\\mu - 2\\sigma < X < \\mu + 2\\sigma) &= \\mathrm{P}(508 - 24 < X < 508 + 24) \\\\[12pt]\n    &= \\mathrm{P}(484 < X < 532) \\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\text{normCdf}(484, 532, 508, 12) \\\\[12pt]\n    &\\approx 0.9545 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}] \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Hence the expected number of bottles that have a volume lying within\n    $2$ standard deviations of the mean is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{E(number of bottles)} &\\approx 200 \\times 0.9545\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 191\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n5.  The probability that the volume of a randomly selected bottle have a\n    volume less than $495$ mL is\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(X < 495) &= \\text{normCdf}(0,495,508, 12) \\\\[6pt]\n    &\\approx 0.13933 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let $Y$ be the number of bottles that have a volume less than\n    $495$ mL in the pack.\n\n    We have $Y \\sim \\mathrm{B}(30, 0.13933)$. Hence the probability that\n    at least $5$ bottles from the pack have a volume less than\n    $495$ mL is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(Y \\geq 5) &= \\text{binomCdf}(30, 0.13933, 5, 30) \\\\[10pt]\n    &\\approx 0.409 \\hspace{3em} [\\text{by using G.D.C.}]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA992","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA992\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nAbby sells pumpkins for Halloween, her stall is very popular and she always has a long line of customers.\n\nThe weight of each pumpkin she sells, $W$, in kilograms, is normally distributed with a mean of $5.7\\,$kg and a standard deviation of $0.85\\,$kg.\n\n1. Find the probability that a randomly bought pumpkin weighs more than $6.5\\,$kg. [2 marks]\n\n2. Five customers join the queue and one by one they buy a random pumpkin from Abbey. Find the probability that only the last three customers buy a pumpkin weighing more than $6.5\\,$kg. [3 marks]\n\nCustomers prefer pumpkins to be over $7\\,$kg, it makes them easier to carve.\n\n3.  1. One of the customers who purchased a pumpkin weighing over $6.5\\,$kg weighed their pumpkin when they got home. What is the probability it weighs over $7\\,$kg?\n\n    2. Out of the three customers who bought pumpkins weighing over $6.5\\,$kg what is the probability exactly two of them had pumpkins weighing over $7\\,$kg?[7 marks]\n\nCustomers sometimes prefer to eat the pumpkins they buy. Pumpkins that weigh less than $a\\,$kg are best for eating.\n\nIt is known that $P(W\\leq a)=\\dfrac{1}{10}P(a < W\\leq6.5)$.\n\n4. Find the maximum weight, in kilograms, of a randomly bought pumpkin from Abbey if it is considered to be in the weight range that is best for eating. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. $W$ follows a normal distribution.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        W \\sim \\mathrm{N}(5.7, 0.85^2)&\\\\[3pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(W > 6.5) &= \\text{normCdf}(6.5,\\,\\infty,\\, 5.7,\\, 0.85)\\\\[3pt]\n        &=0.173307...\\\\[3pt]\n        &=0.173\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n2. The first two customers must not buy a pumpkin over $6.5\\,$kg and the last three must buy one.\\\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (1-0.173307...)^2\\times(0.173307...)^3 &=0.003557444..\\\\[3pt]\n        &=\\hspace{0.525em}\\boxed{0.00356}\\hspace{0.525em}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n3. 1. This is conditional probability, we can use the formula.\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\mathrm{P}(W > 7 | W > 6.5) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(W > 7 \\cap W > 6.5)}{\\mathrm{P}(W > 6.5)}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n        $\\mathrm{P}(W > 7 \\cap W > 6.5) = \\mathrm{P}(W > 7)$ as $\\mathrm{P}(W > 7)$ is a subset of $\\mathrm{P}(W > 6.5)$.\n\n        Hence\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\mathrm{P}(W > 7 | W > 6.5) &= \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}(W > 7)}{\\mathrm{P}(W > 6.5)}\\\\[6pt]\n            &=\\dfrac{\\text{normCdf}(7,\\, \\infty,\\, 5.7,\\, 0.85)}{0.173307...}\\\\[6pt]\n            &= 0.364\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n\n    2. Let $T$ represent the total number of the three whose pumpkins weigh more than $7\\,$kg. We have\n\n        * A fixed number of trials (3 people bought pumpkins weighing over $6.5\\,$kg)\n\n        * Each trial is independent (weights of pumpkins are independent)\n        * Each trial has two outcomes ($W>7$ or $W \\leq 7$)\n        * Constant probability ($\\mathrm{P}(W>7|W>6.5)=0.364$)\n\n        Therefore $T \\sim \\mathrm{B}(3,0.364)$. We have\n        $$\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\mathrm{P}(T=2) &= \\text{binomPdf}(3,0.364,2) \\\\[6pt]\n            &= 0.2528... \\\\[6pt]\n            &= 0.253\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $$\n4. Here is the region $\\mathrm{P}(a < W \\leq 6.5)$ shown below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/norm%20ans.png\" alt=\"norm ans\">\n\n    If we consider both $\\mathrm{P}(W \\leq a)$ and $\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq6.5)$ individually\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/The%20Normal%20Distribution/images/double%20norm.png\" alt=\"double norm\">\n\n    We can see that  $\\mathrm{P}(a< W\\leq6.5)=\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq6.5) - \\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a )$ therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a)&=\\dfrac{1}{10}\\mathrm{P}(a< W\\leq6.5)\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{10}(\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq6.5) - \\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a ))\\\\[6pt]\n        10\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a)&=\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq6.5) - \\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a )\\\\[6pt]\n        11\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a)&=\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq6.5)\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall that $\\mathrm{P}(W>6.5)=0.173307...$ therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        11\\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a)&=1-0.173307...\\\\[6pt]\n        \\mathrm{P}(W\\leq a)&=0.075154...\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Using the known mean and standard deviation of the pumpkins we can use the inverse Normal function on the G.D.C. to find the weight.\n\n    Hence, when $a=0.075154...$, the maximum best weight for eating is $\\hspace{0.525em}W=4.48\\,\\text{kg.}\\hspace{0.525em}$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"AA1053","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# AA1053\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOn the $1$st of January  $2024$ Fabian bought a new car for \\$23,000. He knows from research that the car will depreciate at a rate of $8\\%$ for every complete year.\n\n1.  Find the value ($V_f$) of Fabian's car on the $1$st of January $2030$.[3 marks]\n\nFabian's colleague Sven also bought a new car on $1$st of January $2024$. Sven knows from research what the value (in \\$) of his new car will be on the $1$st of January for the following years.\n\n$$\\\\[1em]$$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Unassigned/images/q1053.png\" alt=\"q1053\">\n\n2.  Assuming the value of Sven's car can be approximately modelled by the equation $V_s=ax+b$, find out the difference in value of Fabian and Sven's cars on $1$st of January $2030$, according to the model. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  We can use a geometric sequence to model the value of Fabian's car ($V_f$).\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    The common ratio, $r$, will be $1-0.08=0.92$, and the first term, $u_1$, will be $23\\,000$. There are $6$ complete years between $1$st of January $2024$ and $1$st of January $2030$. We are looking for $u_7$.\n    $$\n     \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    $$\n    $\\textcolor{white}{gap}$\n\n    Hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_f &= u_1 \\times r^{7-1}\\\\[6pt]\n        &=23\\,000\\times 0.92^6\\\\[6pt]\n        &=13\\,946.16...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\$13\\,900 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2.  We can find a linear model using the G.D.C. Such that $a=-3\\,228.84...$ and $b=6\\,574\\,789.23... $, hence\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s&=-3228.84... x+6574789.23... \\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Now we can predict the value ($V_s$) with $x=2030$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s&=-3228.84...\\times 2030+6574789.23...\\\\\n        &=20\\,231.53...\\tag*{\\textbf{(A1)}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore the difference in value of the two cars is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        V_s-V_f&=20\\,231.53...- 13\\,946.16...\\\\[6pt]\n        &=\\$6\\,290 \\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"},{"ref":"AA1322","marks":"14","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# AA1322\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=x^2+2bx+3$, where $b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $f$ has two distinct real roots.\n\n1.    Find the range of values of $b$. [2 marks]\n\nConsider another function $g(x)=x^2+2Ax+3$, where $A$ is a continuous random variable such that $A \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n2.    Show that the probability that $g$ has two distinct real roots is $0.08326$ to four significant figures. [3 marks]\n\nThe two roots of $g$ are $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nGiven that $g$ has two distinct real roots, where $|x_1|<\\sqrt{3}$ and $|x_2|>\\sqrt{3}$,\n\n3.    find the probability that $|x_1|<1$. [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. Recall the discriminant, $\\Delta$, of a quadratic equation\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{If}\\hspace{1em}\\,ax^2+bx+c&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{then}\\hspace{4.7em}\\Delta&=b^2-4ac\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This tells us how many real roots the equation has.\n\n    Consider the equation  $f(x)=0$. If $f$ has two distinct real roots then $\\Delta > 0$.\n\n    Hence, using the parameter values of $a = 1$, $b$ is  $2b$ and $c = 3$ we can form the inequality\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    (2b)^2-4(1)(3)& > 0\\tag*{\\textbf{{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    which we can solve to find the range of values of $b$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    4b^2-12&> 0\\\\[6pt]\n    4b^2&> 12\\\\[6pt]\n    b^2&> 3\\\\[6pt]b < -\\sqrt{3} &\\hspace{0.525em}\\text{ and }b> \\sqrt{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n2. Function $g$ is closely related to $f$ the only difference is that parameter $b$ is now $A$.\n\n    Therefore using our previous answer we can write down the range of values of $A$ for which $g$ has real roots\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &< -\\sqrt{3}&A &> \\sqrt{3}\\tag*{\\textbf{{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall that $A$ is the continuous random variable such that $A \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n    Hence we need to find the probability that $A <-\\sqrt{3}$ or $A > \\sqrt{3}$.\n\n    This is a region on the standard normal distribution and is shown as the shaded regions below\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Unassigned/images/AA1322-a1.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1322-a1\">\n\n    Hence, the probability $g$ has real distinct roots is\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\mathrm{P}(A < -\\sqrt{3}) + \\mathrm{P}(A> \\sqrt{3})\\tag*{\\textbf{{M1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{normCDF}(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{3},0,1)+ \\text{normCDF}(\\sqrt{3},\\infty,0,1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.041632...+0.041632...\\tag*{\\textbf{{A1}}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.083264...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.08326\\text{...as required.}\\tag*{\\textbf{{AG}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n3. Consider the given inequality, we can write it without the absolute value signs\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    |x_1|&<1\\\\[6pt]\n    -1<x_1&<1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    This means we need to find the value(s) of $A$ such that the root $x_1$ is in the range described above.\n\n    To find the roots, $x_1$ and $x_2$, of $g$ we can use the quadratic formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\text{If}\\hspace{1em}\\,ax^2+bx+c&=0\\\\[6pt]\n    \\text{then}\\hspace{4.7em}x&=\\dfrac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    where $a$ is  $1$, $b$ is $2A$ and $c$ is $3$ which gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    x_{1,2}&=\\dfrac{-(2A)\\pm \\sqrt{(2A)^2-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)}\\tag*{\\textbf{M1}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{-2A\\pm \\sqrt{4A^2-12}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{-2A\\pm \\sqrt{4(A^2-3)}}{2}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\dfrac{-\\cancel{2}A\\pm \\cancel{2}\\sqrt{A^2-3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=-A\\pm \\sqrt{A^2-3}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence the two roots of $g$ are\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &&\\text{root $1$}&=-A - \\sqrt{A^2-3} &\\text{ and }&&\\text{root $2$}&=-A + \\sqrt{A^2-3}\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    However, we can't be sure which one is $x_1$ and which is $x_2$!\n\n    But we can use a sketch of the graph of root $1$ and root $2$ against $A$ to learn more.\n\n    The following image shows both root $1$ and root $2$ against $A$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Mathematics%20AA%20HL/Statistics%20%26%20Probability/Unassigned/images/AA1322-double.JPEG\" alt=\"AA1322-double\">\n\n    Recall, $x_1$ is defined as the root which has a value of $-\\sqrt{3}<x_1<\\sqrt{3}$.\n\n    Therefore, if $-\\sqrt{3}<\\text{root }1<\\sqrt{3}$ or $-\\sqrt{3}<\\text{root }2<\\sqrt{3}$ then that root is $x_1$.\n\n    Hence when the curve is between the two purple horizontal lines then that root represents $x_1$.\n\n    This question is asking us to find when $-1<x_1<1$. Hence we need to find when either root $1$ or root $2$ is between the two $\\textcolor{red}{\\text{red}}$ horizontal lines.\n\n    The boundary values are $A_1$ for root $1$ and $A_2$ for root $2$.\n\n    And we can see that $0<x_1<1$ when $A<A_1$ (shown by the $\\textcolor{blue}{\\text{blue}}$ arrow above) and $-1<x_1<0$ when $A>A_2$ (shown by the $\\textcolor{green}{\\text{green}}$ arrow above).\n\n    Using a G.D.C. we find $A_1=-2$ and $A_2=2$.\n\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(A1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n\n    Let's combine these two regions by splitting the double inequality\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    -1<x_1<1\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    into two single inequalities. This gives\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &0<x_ 1<1&&&-1<x_1<0&\\\\[6pt]\n    &\\text{occurs when }\\textcolor{blue}{A<-2}&&&\\text{occurs when }\\textcolor{green}{A>2}&\\\\[6pt]\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Therefore\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}\\big(|x_1|<1\\big)&=\\mathrm{P}\\big( -1<x_1<1\\big)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\mathrm{P}(A < -2) + \\mathrm{P}(A> 2)\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[6pt]\n    &=\\text{normCDF}(-\\infty,-2,0,1)+ \\text{normCDF}(2,\\infty,0,1)\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.02275...+0.02275...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.04550...\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    However, we haven't quite finished yet. We were also given some extra information that $g$ has two distinct roots.\n    This is conditional probability for which we use the formula\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(R1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}(A|B)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathrm{P}(B)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Where event $A$ is $|x_1|<1$ and event $B$ is $g$ has two distinct roots.\n\n    Replacing $A$ and $B$ in the formula we get\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\,\\bigg|\\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\, \\cap \\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    We can use the answer from part (b) as the denominator\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\, \\cap \\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}{0.08326...}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    To evaluate the numerator, we can use our answer from part (a), from which we can write down the range of values of $A$ for which $g$ has two distinct roots\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &< -\\sqrt{3}&A &> \\sqrt{3}\\\\[6pt]\n    A &< -1.732...&A &> 1.732...\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Recall our values for $A$ when $|x_1|<1$\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    A &< -2&A &> 2\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Notice that the set of values of $A$ for when $|x_1|<1$ fall entirely within the set of values for which $g$ has two distinct roots.\n\n    Hence the intersection of both events is simply the event the set of values when $|x_1|<1$, which we found earlier in this part.\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\tag*{\\textbf{\\textcolor{black}{(M1)}}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$\n    Hence, the numerator becomes\n    $$\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\, \\cap \\,g\\text{ has two distinct roots}\\bigg)}{0.08326...}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}\\bigg(|x_1|<1\\bigg)}{0.08326...}\\tag*{\\textbf{(M1)}}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=\\frac{0.04550...}{0.08326...}\\\\[12pt]\n    &=0.5464...\\\\[6pt]\n    &=0.546\\tag*{\\textbf{A1}}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $$"}]}]}]};
